\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for October 6, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jeff Pegues, Catherine Herridge, Charlie De Mar,  Mike Bettes, Scott MacFarlane, Jo Ling Kent, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the surprising jobs report blowing expectations out of the water. What does the workforce growth mean for interest rates, inflation and the overall U.S. economy?\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 An unexpected surge for employment in America, but still thousands of union workers across the country are on the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Despite the strong September jobs report, picketers and protesters like these in the Screen Actors Guild still aren`t feeling the gains.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Another weekend with rain in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Philippe is making its way through the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: It is going to be a soggy, windy mess along the coast.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Major American cities struggle to handle the near record levels of migrants.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: As asylum seekers continue to cross the Southern border, more than 17,000 have been bused here to Chicago, where resources are so limited, migrants have been sleeping outside on the ground in front of police stations.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Sharing nuclear submarine secrets? The new report alleging former President Trump revealed to an Australian businessman some of America`s most sensitive information. Why his own secretary of defense tells us it`s reckless.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The battle for speaker of the House heats up, as former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  weighs in. We will look at the path ahead.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: In total, how much did you send?\par \par \uc2 RICH BRUNE (Scam Victim): Over a four- or five-month period, $800,000.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A warning tonight from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about senior scams going after your retirement savings. What to look out for.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA KEITHLY (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Supervisory Special Agent): Older adults are disproportionately targeted and victimized.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA KEITHLY: Because they have the most money.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And it`s Friday, so we go On the Road with a story about an un-egg-pected meeting between two strangers.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Do you remember writing on that egg?\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS (92 Years Old): Oh, my goodness yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And you were hoping to find someone to be a pen pal?\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS: Well, who knows? We all dream.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with the economy and a booming new government report showing the jobs market is stronger than expected. Businesses added 336,000 jobs last month, nearly doubling expectations. The unemployment rate remained near a historic low of 3.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 President Biden hailed the news, crediting his economic policies for keeping the unemployment rate below 4 percent for 20 months in a row. That`s the longest stretch in 50 years.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street liked what it was hearing, with the Dow jumping nearly 300 points and the S&P rising more than 1 percent. While businesses are reporting record profits, some workers are not feeling the benefits, with ongoing strikes calling for more pay and better job protections.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent will start us off tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jo Ling.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 This strong jobs report could lead the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to hike interest rates again next month to try to further curb inflation. That, of course, would make borrowing more expensive when it comes to cars and mortgages and credit cards. And these are all things that striking workers out here are telling us they already can`t afford it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Coalition of Kaiser Permanente Unions)\par \par \uc2 STRIKER #1: What`s this about?\par \par \uc2 STRIKERS: Patient care!\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): It`s a tale of two economies. Strikers continue their walkouts nationwide, as a strong September jobs report shows the economy continues to grow; 336,000 jobs were added last month, smashing economists` expectations, posting a 33rd straight month of gains.\par \par \uc2 July and August jobs were revised up, adding 119,000 more than previously reported.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We have the highest share of working-age Americans in the workforce in 20 years. And it`s no accident. It`s Bidenomics.\par \par \uc2 When you hear good economic news like that --\par \par \uc2 MARI MORROW (\uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  Member): Right.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: -- but you`re here on strike --\par \par \uc2 MARI MORROW: Right.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: -- how do you feel?\par \par \uc2 MARI MORROW: Yes, I`m like, OK, can you bring it over here? I hear you. I hear what you`re saying, but, OK, we need to see it as well.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Four hundred and fifty-three thousand workers have walked off the job this year alone, including actors who`ve been on the picket lines for 84 days.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK (Chief Economist, \uc2 KPMG\uc2 ): If you put it in the context of understanding that a lot of union workers did not see their wages go up as rapidly as the rest of the economy, that`s why they`re striking in this moment.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: It`s been three weeks now for striking autoworkers. Today, their union president announced they would not expand their strike as negotiations continue with the Big Three automakers.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (President, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ): Our strike is working, but we`re not there yet.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: It`s a feeling shared among the 75,000 striking Kaiser health care workers.\par \par \uc2 STRIKER #2: I feel hope knowing that our people are going to do whatever it takes. We are making a little bit of progress, but we are not done.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: While workers pressed on, the official end to the Kaiser strike is tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 There has been no meaningful progress, certainly no resolution. Both sides are now telling us they will be back at the bargaining table next Thursday -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jo Ling Kent, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, the battle to be the next speaker of the House is heating up on Capitol Hill. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is throwing his weight behind Ohio`s Jim Jordan. But it`s not clear if either candidate has the votes to take the gavel.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane reports on what Trump`s endorsement means.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): In the battle to replace Kevin McCarthy, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is looking to play kingmaker, giving Ohio`s Jim Jordan a complete and total endorsement.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Jim Jordan, how great is he?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Seventy-seven U.S. House Republicans, including Jordan, have endorsed Trump`s reelection quest, so his backing carries enormous weight.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM JORDAN (R-Ohio): I think I can bring our team together.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Jordan, the founder of the conservative Freedom Caucus and a leader of the Biden impeachment inquiry, would be the most right- leaning speaker in decades.\par \par \uc2 Former Wyoming Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  said Jordan knew more about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had planned for January 6th than any other member of the U.S. House.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE LIZ CHENEY (R-Wyoming): Jim Jordan was involved, was part of the conspiracy in which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was engaged as he attempted to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Louisiana Republican Steve Scalise, the current House majority leader, is the other top contender, currently fighting blood cancer, but cleared to run by his doctor.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE STEVE SCALISE (R-Louisiana): The real question the members have is, how do we get things back on track?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: President Biden said he would work with any new speaker, but slammed the House GOP for creating more gridlock in Congress.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s time to stop fooling around. House Republicans, it`s time for you to do your job.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Meanwhile, late today, former Speaker McCarthy denied reports he was considering quitting.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): I`m still here finishing my job, all right?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Are you thinking about running for reelection?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The contenders to be the next speaker of the House make their pitch behind closed doors Tuesday here at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, in a divided race, there`s concern it could take weeks. And, Norah, that would risk the November 17th deadline to avoid a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 From the political storm in the House to the post-Tropical Cyclone Philippe as it barrels towards New England. Up to five inches of rain is forecast from New York to Southeast Canada through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 For details, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Norah, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Some of the roughest weather that we will see this weekend will be in the Northeast, heavy rain and wind in our forecast. We have got a very potent cold front that`s approaching now from the West. That combined with the remnants of Philippe moving its way northbound here will make a very rough weekend, especially on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Heavy rain forms for us later on tonight, really peaks tomorrow afternoon and evening, I would say from the tristate, New York, Philadelphia, northbound from there, and then in comes the really heavy rain and wind from Philippe as well. Some of the winds, especially near the coastline, could be 25 to 30 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 From there, that rain really hammers us in Maine, but everything exits by the time we get to Sunday. We do watch for some flooding, maybe not as bad as what we had last weekend, but from the tristate, Norah, northbound watching for some high water on the roadways.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the immigration crisis being felt far from the Southern border, more than 200,000 migrants illegally crossed the border in September. That`s the highest level this year. Mayors in major cities across the country are sounding the alarm about a growing humanitarian crisis.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie De Mar reports from an overwhelmed Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR (voiceover): Across Chicago, more than 2,500 migrants, including children, are sleeping on the ground in and outside of police stations waiting to be placed in overflowing city shelters. The airport floor is also where roughly 1,000 asylum seekers now call home.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Brandon Johnson has called for more federal help.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON (D-Mayor of Chicago, Illinois): This particular dynamic has created -- created an incredible amount of strain on the city, and we cannot do it alone.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Today, in Eagle Pass, Texas, as migrants continue to cross the Southern border, New York City Mayor Adams arrived in Ecuador as part of his three-country tour south of the border to discourage migrants from making the trek north.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D-Mayor of New York City): We are not going to be able to sustain this situation for migrants and asylum seekers and for long-term New Yorkers who are also dealing with real issues.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Backlash over the housing of migrants is increasing in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Laura Mendoza with The Resurrection Project has been dealing with the lack of shelter space.\par \par \uc2 What is the concern from -- from your point of view?\par \par \uc2 LAURA MENDOZA (The Resurrection Project): The concern is -- is, just how are they going to be able to withstand the cold?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: And to prepare for winter and to get asylum seekers off of the ground and out of police stations like this one, the city of Chicago recently signed a nearly $30 million contract for winterized base camps.\par \par \uc2 But, Norah, so far, a location has not been secured.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie De Mar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 America`s submarine fleet is known as the Silent Service. The technology and capabilities of the nuclear ships are among some of the country`s closest-kept secrets. And now, there is an explosive report that former \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  shared those secrets with a foreign citizen, Australian billionaire \uc2 Anthony Pratt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Catherine Herridge talks to Trump`s former defense secretary about the risks of discussing that kind of classified information.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from September 2019)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): And Anthony is one of the most successful men in the world, perhaps Australia`s most successful man, as they say.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE (voiceover): CBS News has learned that discussions between Australian billionaire \uc2 Anthony Pratt\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at Mar-a-Lago were probed by special counsel investigators after allegations that Trump may have shared potentially sensitive information about U.S. nuclear submarines.\par \par \uc2 First reported by ABC News, that information may have included the number of nuclear warheads and how close U.S. submarines can get to enemy subs without detection.\par \par \uc2 While some information is publicly available, former military officials tell CBS News the subs` locations are the crown jewels of U.S. national security.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2  (Former U.S. Secretary of Defense): It`s just another example of him being careless, if not reckless, with our most sensitive secrets.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: It`s also alleged Pratt may have then shared the submarine information with other Australians.\par \par \uc2 (Audio clip of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Except it is, like, highly confidential.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Earlier this year, CBS News obtained an audiotape where former President Trump discussed a classified military attack plan on Iran at his New Jersey golf club.\par \par \uc2 What`s the potential ripple effect for national security?\par \par \uc2 FORMER DEFENSE SECRETARY MARK ESPER: With each telling of the story, people get far less concerned about being discreet or about protecting information.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: On TRUTH Social, Trump called the new claims -- quote -- "false and ridiculous." A spokesman for Pratt`s company did not immediately respond.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell CBS News there`s no indication that Trump showed Pratt sensitive records and no charges have been brought by the special counsel - - Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Catherine Herridge, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is warning the public about a new scam called the Phantom Hacker that targets older Americans. The scammers trick people into thinking that their most valuable financial accounts have been compromised.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jeff Pegues goes in depth with one of the scam`s victims, who lost everything.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RICH BRUNE (Scam Victim): It shows the transactions into my account.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES (voiceover): Rich Brune was comfortably retired after a lifetime of saving.\par \par \uc2 RICH BRUNE: It`s just a routine habit -- pay my bills, save some money.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Last year, the 75-year-old Navy veteran and telecom executive got a pop-up warning on his laptop.\par \par \uc2 RICH BRUNE: They said they were trying to upload child porn, and they were also trying to tap into your financial records.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: He wasn`t being hacked, but he was about to be scammed.\par \par \uc2 Did they sound legit?\par \par \uc2 RICH BRUNE: Oh, yes, 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: The hackers convinced Brune to move his money to a so-called safe account.\par \par \uc2 How much did you send?\par \par \uc2 RICH BRUNE: Over a four- or five-month period, $800,000.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Eight hundred thousand dollars?\par \par \uc2 The safe account was part of the scam, and his $800,000 soon vanished. It was stolen. These scams are known as Phantom Hackers, fake tech support criminals.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA KEITHLY (FBI Supervisory Special Agent): They`re very sophisticated. They`re very good at what they do.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Rebecca Keithly is with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Who is usually targeted in these scams?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA KEITHLY: Older adults are disproportionately targeted and victimized.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA KEITHLY: Because they have the most money.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Several agents from the state.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents now visit senior centers to warn about scams ranging from cyber to fake kidnap ransom plots. According to the AARP, more than $28 billion is stolen annually from adults over the age of 60.\par \par \uc2 RICH BRUNE: I`m living, you know, close to the bone now.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: To add to his problems, Rich Brune is now dealing with the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . He may owe $200,000 in taxes because he withdrew the money from a retirement account.\par \par \uc2 What does the future look like for you?\par \par \uc2 RICH BRUNE: As soon as I pay off the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , I will be virtually penniless.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: He now wants to warn retirees to stay alert for scams, because, otherwise, it could cost them a comfortable retirement.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Pegues, CBS News, Arlington, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, we have a consumer alert about two candy recalls over potential choking hazards. What parents need to know.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to this important consumer alert, about two candy recalls just weeks before Halloween.\par \par \uc2 Over 145,000 Rolling Candy products are being recalled as a 7-year-old girl died from choking on a ball that got trapped in her throat. And about 70 million Slime Licker Sour Rolling Candy products are also being recalled because of a potential choking hazard. Customers can contact the companies for a refund.\par \par \uc2 This year`s Nobel Peace Prize is being awarded to Narges Mohammadi, a women`s rights activist now jailed in Iran. Mohammadi has been arrested 13 times and sentenced to more than 30 years in prison and 150 lashes. The Nobel Committee says her life embodies the Woman, Life, Freedom motto of the protests that erupted last year after a young woman died in custody after being arrested for wearing her headscarf incorrectly.\par \par \uc2 Her family today congratulated all the brave women and girls of Iran.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next with the story of an egg, an autograph and one man`s desire to crack the case.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s the age-old question of, what came first, the chicken or the egg?\par \par \uc2 Well, for one New Jersey man, the egg always came first.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman explains On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): For John Amalfitano, the past is ever-present. Everywhere you look in his Dunellen, New Jersey, home, there are relics from a bygone era.\par \par \uc2 JOHN AMALFITANO (Collector): I don`t know what it is with me. I just have a -- have a connection with old stuff.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And he says no connection runs deeper than the curio in this cabinet.\par \par \uc2 JOHN AMALFITANO: That is the oddest thing of all.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It`s a chicken egg bequeathed to John by a neighbor, who found it in a carton of eggs in 1951. The neighbor saved it because of the note. "Whoever gets this egg, please write," signed Ms. Mary Foss, Forest City, Iowa.\par \par \uc2 John says his neighbor held onto the egg for 50 years and never looked for her. Then John held onto it for another 20, before finally posting pictures on the Weird and Wonderful Secondhand Finds \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 To its three million members, he pondered: "Wonder if she might still be alive?"\par \par \uc2 So, all of those people who had egg on their \uc2 Facebook\uc2  hatched a plan, scrambled, fried hard to find this Mary Foss. After 72 years, they egg- spected an egg-austing search that would not be over-easy. But they cracked the case in less than a day.\par \par \uc2 And for those of you keeping track at home, that was eight puns in 15 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Do you remember writing on that egg?\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS (92 Years Old): Oh, my goodness yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And you were hoping to find someone to be a pen pal?\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS: Well, who knows? We all dream.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Mary is now 92. But as a teenager working in an egg-packing plant like this one -\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Eggs, 1946 Encyclopedia Britannica, Inc.)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Mary says she used to dream of meeting someone in a far-off place, that fragile little message in a bottle her way of reaching out.\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And now, 72 years later, she has finally made her connection.\par \par \uc2 JOHN AMALFITANO: And here it is.\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS: How are you, egg?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This week, they came face to face for the first time.\par \par \uc2 JOHN AMALFITANO: And I hope we get to see you again.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Would you want to meet John in person?\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS: Oh, I would love to meet God. Wouldn`t you?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: John.\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS: Oh, John. Not really.\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS: I have no desire to meet the guy. He`s got his problem keeping the egg that long.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Yeah, saving 70-year-old eggs. Yeah, you got a point there.\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS: Well, when you get to be my age, you meet a lot of kooks.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Sorry, John. Looks like the yolk`s on us.\par \par \uc2 There`s my uplifting ending.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman, On the Road, in Mason City, Iowa.\par \par \uc2 MARY FOSS: How you like your egg?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, she`s got an egg-cellent sense of humor.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Simone Biles flips, jumps and spins her way into the history books with another world title.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: American gymnastics superstar Simone Biles makes history again.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Peacock/NBC Olympics)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Biles now stands alone as the world`s most decorated gymnast after winning a record-tying sixth all-around title at the World Championships in Belgium. She has a record 34 medals across the world championships and the Olympics, more than any person, male or female. There`s a reason people call her the GOAT, the greatest of all time. She is simply the best.\par \par \uc2 Go, Simone.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night, and have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231007eja600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 McCarthy Out as House Speaker; Five Hurt in Campus Shooting; Health Care Strike; Trump Under Gag Order; Hunter Biden Pleads Not Guilty; Inside Secretive Ukrainian Unit; Robotaxi Fears; Inside the Battery Belt; Catholics Consider Change; Student Loan Scams; Liver Disease Warning; Making Waves of Change; The Poetry of Arlo Parks; "In the Form of a Question," \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Nikole Killion, John  Dickerson, Nicole Sganga, Catherine Herridge, Ramy Inocencio, Kris Van  Cleave, Ben Tracy, Chris Livesay, Anna Werner, Vladimir Duthiers, Dana  Jacobson \par \uc2 14,576 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy becomes the first House speaker in history to be voted out of a job after a revolt by some far-right Republicans.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Eight people can throw you out as speaker, I think you`ve got a real divide. My fear is the institution fell today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Multiple people wounded after gunfire erupts at \uc2 Morgan State University\uc2  in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Well, it`s hitting glass behind my head. It`s just -- it is another day here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: During his trial for fraud, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is hit with a partial gag order for attacking a court clerk with false claims on social media.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I think it`s going very well.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In Northern Italy, at least 21 people were killed and 18 others hurt when a passenger bus careened off an overpass.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A deadly plane crash near Portland, Oregon. This single engine plane falling out of the sky crashing into a home.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Jury selection is underway in the criminal fraud trial of fallen crypto king, Sam Bankman-Fried.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): Which is surprising because by the looks of him, he already got the electric chair.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Mere minutes before I walked out here on this stage, Republican Kevin McCarthy was ousted as speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 Back in January, Congress had to vote a record 15 times for McCarthy to get the speaker`s job, but to remove him, just one.\par \par \uc2 (Cheering and applause.)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: That`s progress, Kev. Feel good about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a stunning turn of events and that`s where we`re going to get right to the news today with history being made on Capitol Hill. The uncertainty over what is going to happen next.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy is now the first speaker of the House ever to be removed from leadership. Eight members of his own party went after him and ultimately took him down.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy said it was personal and now the race is on to pick a replacement. Till then, Congress is at a standstill.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is following all of this for us all.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning. What a day yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, pretty unprecedented, Gayle, and good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 You know, this leaves a huge leadership vacuum at a very critical time, the speaker is second in line to the presidency, and Congress has a lot of work to do, including trying to avoid a shutdown in the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Office of Speaker of the House of the \uc2 United States House of Representatives\uc2  is hereby declared vacant.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: This morning, a House divided, leaderless after Kevin McCarthy was forced out as speaker.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I don`t regret standing up for choosing governance over grievance.\par \par \uc2 It is my responsibility. It is my job.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: McCarthy was pushed out by eight members of his far-right flank led by Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): Chaos is Speaker McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Florida Republican introduced the motion to remove McCarthy and accused him of breaking promises to conservatives and cutting deals with Democrats on spending.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): The reason Kevin McCarthy went down today is because nobody trusts Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): This is a serious, solemn and sober moment.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: House Democrats joined GOP hardliners in part after circulating this clip in a meeting from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` "Face the Nation" when McCarthy blamed them for holding up a recent bill to prevent a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I wasn`t sure it was going to pass. You want to know why? Because the Democrats tried to do everything they can not to let it pass.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Democrats were the ones who voted for this.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Did you -- did you watch --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In a larger number than Republicans to keep the continuing resolution alive.\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR (D-MN): Democrats seized the day, and he goes on TV hours later and says Republicans did it. Republicans were leading, which is insane.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: McCarthy`s ouster marks a stunning sequence of events for the California Republican who battled his members at every turn and went through 15 rounds to secure the speaker`s gavel in January when he made this prediction.\par \par \uc2 How confident are you that you will have this job for a full two-year term?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): A thousand percent.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: With no consensus for a replacement, North Carolina Republican, \uc2 Patrick McHenry\uc2  was designated acting speaker. McCarthy`s deputies like Majority Leader Steve Scalise are being floated for the job and some posted last night, they nominate former President Trump for speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): If I lose my job over doing what I truly believe what`s right, I`m very at peace with it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Scalise is battling blood cancer, but says he feels great. He has been reaching out to members and a candidate forum is set for next week.\par \par \uc2 As for the Trump option, the speaker does not have to be a current member of the House, but whoever it is, will continue to face a slim five-vote majority, so every vote is going to count -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know who`s going to want the job at this point.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more on the fallout from all of this, we are joined now by CBS News chief political analyst, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 John, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 What is going on in this country? What is going on in Congress? What`s going on in the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well, when you gain power by criticizing what it takes to wield power, it`s really hard to govern and use that power, and that`s essentially the problem the Republican Party has.\par \par \uc2 Governing requires compromise, working with the other party, and believing in institutions. The energy of the Republican Party, not the majority of physical members, but in the base and a lot of -- and the age who voted out the speaker, they believe that compromise is capitulation, they believe the other party is evil, and they believe institutions are corrupt.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you fan those flames to get elected, then once you have to wield power by compromising working within institutions and working with the other party, this very powerful loud base says: Wait, you`ve sold out. And that`s what happened to Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 He often fomented concern about institutions. I mean, he denied the outcome of the 2020 election. So when he says \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  doesn`t care about the institution, McCarthy in part came to power by not trusting institutions.\par \par \uc2 So you can`t -- it makes a very big conflict, and in the end, it creates chaos, and the challenge for the Republican Party is what are the two biggest acts of chaos in American politics in this modern era? There`s this vacating of the speaker, and there is also the attack on the Capitol, which all of the top Republicans say the front runner of the party, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  encouraged and had his supporters commit that attack.\par \par \uc2 So the two last acts of chaos are on the doorstep of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, Kevin McCarthy also said bring it and they did. I think, even -- he seemed so confident that this was not going to happen, and that`s why it`s so stunning.\par \par \uc2 Was there not one Democrat, not one? Or clearly, they needed more than one that could come and help them at this particular time? Because they did work together to prevent a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : They worked -- they did. They worked together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he ticked people off on Margaret`s show.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And in fact -- we should -- still, it`s an irony that \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  who knocked the speaker for working with Democrats --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : -- needed Democrats to in the end undo the speaker.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To take down Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And why does that matter? It reinforces the idea that when you`re in a legislative body --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : -- with a small margin, you have to work with the other side, and that`s what Kevin McCarthy did. Democrats worked with him. Why didn`t they vote to save Kevin McCarthy? Because I think, you know, as Nikole mentioned in her piece, they worked with Kevin McCarthy, and then he turned around, and by their view, kneed them in the groin for having worked with him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On Margaret`s show, yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And also, I think they go back and see the way in which he`s done a number of other things that have been -- that the Democrats have --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So the House can`t do anything until they elect a new speaker. So what`s next? And what does it mean for the American people?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We`re stuck at the moment. I mean, it means that until that -- until there`s a new speaker, business will basically come to a standstill.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A halt.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I mean, they`ll be able to work on some stuff about -- but the thing is also, what`s going to be required to move forward on government funding and addressing any of the problems that America faces is compromise, working together, all of those things that this small number of Republicans kicked out a speaker for engaging.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But then, they take a week off, John. It seems like this ought to be an all hands on deck.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I think their hope, if this is even possible is to find a consensus leader and have one vote instead of 15.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know --\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I mean, given the way things are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I always bring up this name in moments like this, Dolly Parton for speaker of the House. I think she could bring everybody together. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Sending that East Tennessee wisdom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We need something here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Tupac Shakur or --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, this is why people are exhausted with Washington, right? We have problems in this country and they`re not getting solved.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes. Dolly Parton second in line to the presidency is perhaps some people`s vision.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: First in line in my heart.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or Taylor Swift seems to be working for unity.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Would be one as well, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sure. John, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Making the complicated digestible.\par \par \uc2 All right, turning now to a shooting overnight at a Historically Black College. Police say at least five people were shot at a dorm on the campus of \uc2 Morgan State University\uc2  in Baltimore. They are expected to survive, which is good news.\par \par \uc2 Authorities do not know how many shooters were involved.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Sganga is at Morgan State for us.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 This campus, home to about 9,000 students was on lockdown for three hours last night. Just hours before shots rang out, students gathered for their annual homecoming coronation ceremony, an event that kicks off homecoming week. Now instead, it would be remembered by students as a tragedy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 POLICE: I got a shot out window it appears.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: It`s the shattered windows at a dorm building that led police to believe chaos was unfolding on campus Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD WORLEY (Baltimore Police Commissioner): Multiple windows shattered made our officers believe possibly we had an active shooter.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Law enforcement says campus police heard the first shots around 9:25, Tuesday night. Minutes later they found five victims, four of those are students.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BRANDON SCOTT (D) (Baltimore, Maryland): This unfortunate and unacceptable tragedy joins as we know a long line of colleges and schools.\par \par \uc2 We have to stop saying not one more.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Police say the shooting took place outside of Thurgood Marshall Hall.\par \par \uc2 POLICE: Got to check and make sure.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: But inside, officers were going door to door scanning rooms in that dorm while students were sheltering in place.\par \par \uc2 While a shooter still remains on the loose, officials remain firm that the campus is safe.\par \par \uc2 COMMISSIONER RICHARD WORLEY: They cleared every single floor twice, and after that, we realized the campus was most licensed likely safe, the shooter was nowhere around that we could find.\par \par \uc2 NAISIR (Attendee): Families were just taking pictures with their children who won events. As soon as we were walking, we started just hearing the shots go off.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Naisir (sp?) was attending a packed coronation ceremony, part of a ritual in the run up to the school`s homecoming week. He says it was just across the street from the gunfire.\par \par \uc2 NAISIR: I was shaken up by it. Two bullet holes went through the glass windows. I heard them as they were going by, so I was really just praying that I would get inside safely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: And it`s a scene all too familiar for students here as there were shooting incidents during homecoming weekend last year and in 2021. Meanwhile, classes are canceled today. As for the rest of homecoming week, well, it`s still up in the air. We do know that \uc2 FBI\uc2  is assisting in this investigation -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nicole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Some more news breaking overnight: A strike involving more than 75,000 health care workers is indeed getting underway after \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  and a coalition of unions failed to strike a deal.\par \par \uc2 If the strike is fully implemented, hundreds of Kaiser medical facilities will be affected across five states and Washington, DC. The striking workers would include nurses, pharmacists, technicians, therapists, and cleaners. They are asking for higher wages. They`re asking for additional staffing. These have been major challenges lately across industries.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser says it has offered increases across the board saying its goal is a fair and equitable agreement. That`s a direct quote. Hospitals and emergency departments will remain open or are set to during this three-day strike, but there could be delays. There could be cancellations as well. So watch out.\par \par \uc2 The union say this is expected to be the largest strike of its kind in American history.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The judge in Donald Trump`s civil fraud trial has issued a limited gag order after the former president posted a phony and personal criticism of the judge`s law clerk.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s social media posts which has since been deleted, included a photo of the clerk with Democratic Senate Majority Leader, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  falsely calling her Schumer`s girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 The judge quickly called Trump and his lawyers to meet in private and then ordered all parties in the case to stop making remarks about court staff. Trump was in court for the first two days of testimony in a civil fraud trial.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has pleaded not guilty to charges of lying about his drug abuse to buy a handgun in 2018. The president`s son attended a court hearing in Delaware after the judge in that case refused to let him enter a plea over a video link.\par \par \uc2 Senior investigative correspondent, Catherine Herridge was inside the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 I was not more than five feet away from \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  as he entered the courtroom and took his seat when the hearing came to order. There was a nervous smile and some laughter with his attorneys.\par \par \uc2 The last time the president`s son was in the same federal courthouse, a plea deal for two misdemeanor tax charges and a felony gun offense collapsed opening the door to more serious charges. Asked by the judge on Tuesday if he understood the alleged crimes and penalties, which can include up to 25 years in prison if convicted, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  responded in a soft but determined voice, "Yes, Your Honor."\par \par \uc2 At his side, lead attorney, Abbe Lowell at one point arguing a statute Biden is accused of violating a ban on gun possession for drug users is unconstitutional. The ongoing special counsel investigation could still file tax charges in this case -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Catherine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s head overseas now to Ukraine this morning where we continue our look at special units in that country`s military seeking to push back on Russia`s invasion.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio met with some elite snipers who are playing a key role in the battlefields.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: American bullets from American rifles crack through the air near Ukraine`s frontline with Russia. This sniper unit granted CBS News special access as they keep their skill to kill sharp.\par \par \uc2 Always working in teams of two, a spotter checks windspeed. His sniper adjust angle, then between heartbeats, fires, hitting a target nearly a quarter of a mile away.\par \par \uc2 So is it easy to hit this target?\par \par \uc2 COMMISSAR (Sniper): Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Yes?\par \par \uc2 COMMISSAR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: You sound very confident. Yup.\par \par \uc2 COMMISSAR: Yup.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: This young man is codenamed Commissar.\par \par \uc2 How important is your job as a sniper in the Defense of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (COMMISSAR speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "We bridge the gaps where infantry can`t," he says. "We liquidate top targets like commanders and machine gunners."\par \par \uc2 With progress along the war`s more than 600-mile front slope, if not static, snipers have become more heralded.\par \par \uc2 And in contrast to the high tech war of high flying drones and high visibility hardware, the power of a sniper is low tech, low visibility, and relatively low cost, killing with a single bullet.\par \par \uc2 Everything about snipers is secretive. We can`t even tell you where we are. We can`t even show you their faces. That`s because they are high-value targets.\par \par \uc2 (NIKOLAI speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "An experienced sniper is priceless," says Nikolai, their commander. "A tank is just a bunch of metal and can be easily replaced, but it takes a lot of money and years to train a sniper."\par \par \uc2 Along with Ukrainian firearms, American scopes and American 338 caliber rifles, 90 percent of their ammunition they say is also American.\par \par \uc2 You have an American flag there. Why?\par \par \uc2 (COMMISSAR speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "Americans have helped Ukraine a lot. I wear this as a sign of respect."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: As for the US funding freeze on Ukraine, Tony, the White House says that existing aid will only last about another couple of months. President Biden has called allied leaders and told them he is confident that bipartisan support for this country will stay strong.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ramy Inocencio for us in Ukraine. Ramy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, stay with us, a woman is seriously injured after being hit by a driverless taxi, but was the self-driving vehicle really at fault in this case? We`ll tell you how this is reigniting the conversation around autonomous vehicles, but first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, how people repaying student loans can avoid online scams that claim to help them avoid big bills for a price.\par \par \uc2 And there`s panic in Paris ahead of the Summer Olympics over a guest that nobody wants to have at home. We`ll explain in a bit.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Police in San Francisco are investigating after a woman was hurt in an accident involving a self-driving rideshare and another car, but here`s the story: The car with a human driver hit the woman first and then she wound up in the path of the driverless robotaxi. This accident is putting new attention on the safety of those driverless vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: San Francisco Police are investigating an apparent hit- and-run that left a woman critically injured and pinned under a Cruise self-driving robotaxi in San Francisco Monday night.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The patient was transported within 10 minutes of the arrival of the first rescuers.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Video from the Cruise vehicle reviewed by CBS News shows the woman crossing against the light and getting hit by a sedan. She is then launched into the path of the self-driving car. Cruise says its vehicle braked aggressively to minimize the impact.\par \par \uc2 There is some chance that this self-driving car was just in the wrong place at the wrong time.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: The real question that needs to be answered is, could a human driver have avoided this person in the road?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Cruise says its vehicles are involved in 65 percent fewer collisions than rideshares driven by people, but in August, cut its Bay Area fleet by 50 percent after two accidents within hours of each other.\par \par \uc2 The autonomous taxis have drawn public outcry with some residents disabling them by putting traffic cones on their hoods.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BRANDENBURG (San Francisco Bay Area resident): We already have taxis, we already have Lyfts and Ubers. It doesn`t seem like we need them.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Cruise has about 400 of these self-driving vehicles on the roads in several cities across the US including here in Phoenix. The company has never had a fatal accident, and this morning says it is cooperating with police as they try to find this apparent hit-and-run driver.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And by hit-and-run driver, he is talking about the initial car that hit the lady.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not the self-driving car, which clearly was staying right there. I mean, I think we have to keep in mind that the data shows that the self-driving vehicles overall --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are safer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- are safer than human drivers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People make mistakes. The computers make fewer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but some people love them, some people don`t --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Nate --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s a ton of these vehicles in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they`re coming everywhere.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If they`re there, they`re going national.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the question they`re asking is, a human driver, they said probably couldn`t have done any better than the driverless vehicle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The human driver caused the incident.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would say so as well. At least, began anyway.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in our series, "Protecting the Planet," we will go to a part of the country being called the Battery Belt for the rapid growth of plants there.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us, local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Our series "Protecting the Planet" looks at the manufacturing boom driven by new electric vehicle battery plants.\par \par \uc2 As the auto worker strike enters its third week, a key sticking point is worker pay in EV plants. Many are being built in the south where the workforce is largely non-union.\par \par \uc2 Senior national and environmental correspondent, Ben Tracy shows us how the EV transition is transforming communities.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: This is the kind of town that assumed its best days had rolled by, but now Commerce, Georgia, population 7,700 and climbing finds itself back on track.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CLARK HILL (Commerce, Georgia): Well, I think money follows money.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Mayor Clark Hill says new businesses like this brewery are pouring into downtown. There`s even something approaching rush hour on once sleepy streets.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CLARK HILL: When you have a company investing more than two-and-a- half billion dollars in your community, it attracts a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: That company is just down the road inside this massive manufacturing plant where they`re building battery cells for electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 How big is this place and how many batteries can you actually produce?\par \par \uc2 JAEWON CHEY (SK On executive vice chairman): This place as large as 13 football fields, and this place we make battery for over 400,000 cars.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Jaewon Chey is executive vice chairman of SK On, SK stands for South Korea and the flagpole out front is a sign that after decades of US jobs being offshored to Asia, the script is being flipped.\par \par \uc2 Foreign battery manufacturers are opening dozens of new US plants to be close to the automakers they supply. SK builds many of its batteries for Ford.\par \par \uc2 Despite some recent job cuts, it currently employs more than 3,000 people at this one plant including Desmond Salmon.\par \par \uc2 DESMOND SALMON (SK On employee): I live, you know, 20 minutes from here. This is a great opportunity for me to be a part of this.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: SK plans to employ up to 15,000 people by 2026 in five battery plants across three US states.\par \par \uc2 JAEWON CHEY: One more in different location in Georgia; two in Kentucky, one in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: In the past three years, more than $90 billion in battery investments have been announced nationwide, creating an estimated 70,000 manufacturing jobs. Much of this is clustered into about eight states stretching from Michigan down into the southeast, a part of the country now known as the Battery Belt.\par \par \uc2 Many of these states are red ones, where Republican lawmakers rarely mention climate change and even oppose the climate legislation that is now supercharging US battery manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 Georgia, which has seen the most investment even imposed a new tax on public EV charging this summer.\par \par \uc2 MIKE O`KRONLEY\uc2  (Ascend Elements CEO): For right now, this plant just opened around six months ago. By year end, this plant will be at capacity and we`re going to need another plan.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: \uc2 Mike O`Kronley\uc2  is CEO of Ascend Elements.\par \par \uc2 Here in Covington, Georgia, it recycles used EV batteries, extracting about 98 percent of all the valuable metals inside them.\par \par \uc2 MIKE O`KRONLEY\uc2 : Specifically nickel, cobalt, lithium.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: This is another critical part of the new domestic EV supply chain.\par \par \uc2 MIKE O`KRONLEY\uc2 : I`m really proud to be part of that, and I know a lot of our team members are also very, very proud to be part of that.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And for towns like Commerce, where spotting an actual electric vehicle is still rare, being a notch on the Battery Belt means more than bragging rights.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CLARK HILL: We feel like it`s not only going to be good for our citizens, but it`s also going to provide a better quality of life for the whole region.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ben Tracy, in Commerce, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so interesting to see how far we`ve come with electric vehicles because remember in the beginning, people were like, whoever is going to drive one of those? And now, a lot of us want to drive one of them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do remember those days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now, they are building batteries and they`re really changing these little towns. They`re getting bigger. They`re getting a lot of energy. New bars, new restaurants, new folks coming in. It`s an amazing time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it is supposed to create more revenue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As people transition to more electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is doing that.\par \par \uc2 Moving on, the Roman Catholic church has opened a formal meeting that`s likely to address major controversies like female priests and the church`s response to its gay members.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis celebrated mass this morning to open that meeting saying the church needs to lower its barriers and welcome everyone.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay is in Rome covering this meeting.\par \par \uc2 Chris, good morning\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 It`s been called the Super Bowl of the Catholic Church. Bishops gathered from around the world to discuss hot button topics such as whether or not priests can get married or whether women can become deacons or whether or not divorced and remarried Catholics can have communion.\par \par \uc2 The Synod is already historic. It`s the first time women and lay people are being allowed to vote. However, 80 percent of participants are still bishops, but the biggest bombshell dropped earlier this week when Francis opened the door for priests to bless same sex unions.\par \par \uc2 Now, this came with caveats. Francis emphasized that blessings shouldn`t be seen as elevating same sex unions to the sacred place of heterosexual weddings.\par \par \uc2 Up until now, the Church`s position has been that same sex unions cannot be blessed because, "God cannot bless sin." But in his statement, Francis said, "We cannot be judges who only deny, reject, and exclude."\par \par \uc2 LGBTQ groups have welcomed the important change while conservative Catholics have blasted Pope Francis for what they say is diluting church doctrine and sowing confusion. All of this in the Synod has only just begun -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Chris Livesay for us in Rome.\par \par \uc2 Chris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next in just a moment, as student loan repayments resume, the government is warning borrowers to watch out for scammers.\par \par \uc2 Anna Werner will be here with what you need to know to avoid getting ripped off.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There are some new warnings this morning about scammers trying to take advantage of the millions of Americans now resuming payments on their student loans. We`re talking 27 million people who have federal student loan debt, and that totals approximately, $1.1 trillion. That`s a lot of people in a vulnerable position, a lot of scammers trying to get that money.\par \par \uc2 Our consumer investigative correspondent, Anna Werner has more.\par \par \uc2 Anna, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 And you will not be surprised to hear that government agencies and nonprofit groups say the scammers have been reaching out to people through robocalls, online ads, e-mails, even snail mail. They often ask for fees to provide a bogus service, or try to get your personal information -- surprise. Here`s what some of those robocalls sound like.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Press one now to see if you are eligible to get your loans completely forgiven. This recent announcement could potentially eliminate 100 percent of your federal student debt.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So many of these scammers offer to help borrowers avoid repayment, to lower their payments, get the loans forgiven. Some scammers provide some legitimate information, then they claim they`ll do the paperwork for you for a price, of course.\par \par \uc2 So you really have to be paying attention. I think a lot of people get caught because somebody calls and they just aren`t quite listening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, they also say "completely forgiven."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People want to -- you know, people --\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Either you don`t have the money or you`re just looking to save some money. That`s everybody, right? And so you`re susceptible, you want to hear them out. And then you feel like, oh, this is an easy, cheap path.\par \par \uc2 So what are the warning signs, because you want to walk down, but you`re walking down a bad path.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Right. Okay. So if they`re trying to get your personal information, first of all, they ask for your federal student aid loan ID, you want to watch out for that. Don`t give out personal information.\par \par \uc2 They might say they`re with the Department of Education. You should go to the Department of Education, or StudentAid.gov website makes sure that you`re talking to the real people. They might offer, as I said, to manage or process your paperwork. You don`t have to pay fees for that. You can go through the federal government, you don`t need that help, which will take a bunch of your money.\par \par \uc2 So you really -- I think a lot of it though, is people just paying attention and thinking for a minute about now, wait a minute, why is somebody wanting to help me so much?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`re talking to these people who have been scammed. How do they say they got tricked?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Well, so we spoke to one woman who said for example, they called and they said she had until the end of the month to submit an application, she would pay $1,000.00 and her loans would be dismissed, of course, and the urgency to complete by a deadline is part of the con.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Fortunately, she reached out to a nonprofit agency and they were like, no, no, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: It`s a scam. Don`t do it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, my momma said and I`m pretty sure we all heard this, if it sounds too good to be true --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- it probably is.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your Momma sounds like my momma.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anna, thank you so much. This is valuable information.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories you will be talking about today. But first, it is 7:42, times to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." There are bed bugs in Paris apparently?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh man. We`re going to get to that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can probably tell Katy, which is why we`re not going, Katy. This is why we`re not going.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Boy, all right --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s 