 is pretty obvious. I`m well ahead of my -- of the normal protocols when it comes to rehab for this kind of thing, but that`s you know -- that`s -- that was always what my mindset was, and there is common practices about six weeks in a boot and I was in a shoe in like 13 days.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So the 39-year-old says he is making his own timetables and they`ll see where he is in a couple of months.\par \par \uc2 If sticks to that four-month time schedule, that puts him in about middle of January, the season ends January 7th, but then you`ve got the postseason.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If the Jets are in the postseason.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Aren`t they like one in four? I mean, what are the chances of him coming back and they still have a season to play?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. That`s what a lot of people are asking the question, by this time the season will be over, but certain fans that are Jets fans say the season is already --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re calling --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They are in a rough year so far.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Talk about waving the white flag early.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Recall the acronym Jets "Just Endure The Suffering." J-E- T-S.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that he is highly motivated.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I agree.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He wants to do everything he can to come back and help the team.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s go with that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that. And on top of that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Setting his own timetable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is tough. Usually guys sit out for like a year.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So for him to fight back and want to get back on the field, I admire that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too. Making his own timetable. I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love it. Love it.\par \par \uc2 All right, this video is -- I mean, we`ve got to share this frightening encounter between a mom and a biker in Philadelphia. You`re not going to believe it.\par \par \uc2 So let`s show you what`s going on here.\par \par \uc2 Bikers were riding through their streets Sunday night, and you can see this one dude gets off his bike, right? And he smashes, kicks in the rear window of this vehicle, okay.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the thing. That mom`s kids were in the backseat. She gets out of the car. You see her confronting the biker. He then waves a gun at her. Head butts her, shoves her away, and gets back on the bike.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to show it again. So watch this. He gets off the bike, smashes her car window.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s awful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the gun, actually, folks take a look at the driver -- this dude here on the car. Watch the gun fall out of his waistband.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and then it hits the ground.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He picks it up. The mom gets out of the car. He then points the gun in her face.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She clearly sees it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She didn`t even flinch.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She sees -- oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She clearly sees it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But she don`t care.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She keeps advancing. He head butts her. It doesn`t matter. She`s back in his face confronting him, pushes him off the bike, and it`s because her kids were in the back seat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she is a mama bear for sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The whole thing churns my stomach.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, the mom, her name is Nikki Bullock, congratulations to you for surviving that, and for being as tough as you were.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She said she feared for her children`s safety. She spoke to our affiliate in Philadelphia. Police sources say there is a person of interest that has been identified in connection to that video.\par \par \uc2 Look, whenever we see these --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are going to get him. They are going to get him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They`re going to get him. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But guys, just think about as a parent now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She went into Mama Bear mode.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Totally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But still, I mean --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Totally.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Completely fearless.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Didn`t even flinch when she saw the gun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But she could have easily -- very easily been killed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She`s very lucky.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, and he tucked that gun away.\par \par \uc2 He needed -- he did the right thing, and he is a coward.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s talk about Paris Fashion Week. The biggest news isn`t who is walking down the runway, but what`s crawling nearly everywhere else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yikes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The city is bugging out over a widespread -- this is nasty --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that what a bed bug --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bed bug infestation. They`ve been found, guys on trains, in hotels, restaurants, movie theaters, even in the airports. The city`s Transport minister says extensive countermeasures are being taken. Companies specializing in the treatment report an overwhelming number of cases.\par \par \uc2 And this, of course, comes as Paris prepares to host the Summer Olympics next July.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of guys right now trying to save money on that Paris trip that their girlfriend, their wife has been asking about --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re like, I`m sorry, honey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is still one of the most incredible cities in the world.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, this is our moment. Can`t do it though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`d blame it. I`d blame it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Appreciate it, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just take precautions.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, actor, Bellamy Young is here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Always entertaining.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: On shows like "Scandal" here with a health warning that you need to hear. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in his eight o`clock hour, you know actor, Bellamy Young from shows like "Scandal," she is here only on CBS MORNINGS to share something very personal for a very good cause.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Lewis Pugh set out recently to swim the entire Hudson River, all 315 miles. Why did he do it? Well, Dana Jacobson got in the water to find out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson, 40-time "Jeopardy!" champion, Amy Schneider is here with a new memoir. It`s all about the difference between knowing and understanding.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she`s super smart, but first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I don`t regret standing up for choosing governance over grievance. It is my responsibility. It is my job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: An historic first in the House as Speaker Kevin McCarthy is removed from his position.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We are stuck at the moment. Until there`s a new speaker, business will basically come to a standstill.\par \par \uc2 NAISIR: I just saw everybody running. I heard the shots going off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Five people are hurt at a shooting at a Historically Black University in Baltimore\par \par \uc2 DAVID WILSON (\uc2 Morgan State University\uc2  president): \uc2 Morgan State University\uc2  will not be deterred. We will move forward.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: More than 20 people are dead after a tourist bus plunges off an overpass in Italy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The main question that the investigation will look to answer is exactly how this could have happened.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  pleads not guilty to federal firearms charges in a Delaware courtroom.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The ongoing special counsel investigation could still file tax charges in this case.\par \par \uc2 DOROTHY HOFFNER (World`s oldest skydiver at 104): It was wonderful. I had to keep myself awake so I could see the scenery.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`re never too old for fun. A Chicago woman goes tandem skydiving at the age of 104.\par \par \uc2 DOROTHY HOFFNER: I couldn`t understand why those people were there, but as I say, all I did was get old -- older. I`m not old. I`m older.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she`s not old, she is older.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And listen Bellamy is here. Hi, Bellamy. We all want to get older.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG (Actor): Yes. Oh, yes. The alternative is worse.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that something you would do?\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: I haven`t done it yet, but maybe at her age.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is not a bad time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s done it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate`s done it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He had the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I`ll do, I`ll do it at her age.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with an interview you`ll see only on CBS MORNINGS and we love when that can happen. So I`m going to say it again, only on CBS MORNINGS, actor, Bellamy Young, best known for shows like "Scandal" and "The Other Black Girl" is opening up for the very first time about the impact of chronic liver disease on her family.\par \par \uc2 So Bellamy`s father had cirrhosis, which led to a chronic liver condition known as HE. Now that had devastating effects on his brain. That`s a great picture.\par \par \uc2 Thirty million Americans have some form of liver disease, nearly one in five have a chronic condition, which for many leads to HE. It can produce symptoms like extreme personality changes, disorientation, confusion, and uncoordinated movements, and inappropriate behavior.\par \par \uc2 And Bellamy joins us at the table.\par \par \uc2 We`re glad you`re here, because I had never heard of HE until this meeting yesterday, when they told us that you were coming on.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: I`m so grateful to have this time with you guys, because that`s how it is. This affects so many people in America and across the world, and a lot has changed.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of information to know. We can find out how to handle this condition, and no one is having this conversation. So I`m very, very grateful to be here with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So how did it affect your family? We should say your dad died when you were 15.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: He did. So it was a bit of a time ago. But we felt very isolated and alone. There wasn`t a lot of information. Even our doctor couldn`t really help us.\par \par \uc2 So he was diagnosed very late because we didn`t know what to look for. We never imagined after a diagnosis of cirrhosis, which is a severe form of chronic liver disease, we never imagined that a liver disease could affect your cognition and your behavior. So we didn`t even know what to keep our eyes open for.\par \par \uc2 And now that`s why I partner with Salix and I`m hoping I can encourage everyone to look at a website called UnderstandingHE.com because you can find out about the symptoms, you can notice them early.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how did it show itself, Bellamy?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s talk about -- yes, let`s talk about --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I am not sure exactly what --\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still don`t understand what was happening in your house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Yes, so, well, at the time, I was young and so a lot of this annoyed me as things annoy teenagers. So maybe a change in the smell of his breath and I`d be like: Ugh, he`s drinking in the daytime, or he`d forget to pick me up from school and I`d be like: Oh, great, you know, he forgot to pick me up.\par \par \uc2 But when things became progressively worse, like one night, he forgot how to get home from the job he`d had forever, and we thought, okay, that`s something -- is something going on?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really bad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s bad.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: But at that point, he was in a very what they call overt hepatic encephalopathy and what happens with hepatic encephalopathy or HE is the toxins in your liver, they sort of back up in your bloodstream until they get to your brain, and they cause disorientation and they cause a lack of coordination and a lot of different, very obvious symptoms if you know what you`re looking for.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: But we didn`t know, so we didn`t catch it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Was this tied to alcohol use?\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: His diagnosis of cirrhosis was because of alcohol use. But you know, chronic -- severe chronic liver disease can be caused by so many things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. It seems like at one point, you came to a realization about these things that were going on because you weren`t aware.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When did you come to this, you know, realization? And how did you come to the point where you want to talk about it?\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Well, we came to the realization very late, and that is exactly why I want to talk about it because so much has changed.\par \par \uc2 Now there is information, people understand this disease and how to treat it and how to walk along the path. But most of all, how to recognize it when it`s happening to the person that you love. Because as a person who is sick, or as a caregiver, it`s just agonizing to watch and you can get in front of it.\par \par \uc2 If you talk to your doctor, you can plan a path forward and also to have community. We felt very alone. So just to feel like there are other people out there to talk to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Who can help you, have great conversations with your doctor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you think there`s a stigma because of the association with alcohol? Is that why you think people don`t talk about it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: I do think so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: I think people get embarrassed about liver disease, but it affects like 30 million people in America. That`s a lot of people, and we need to release the stigma.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Especially for people --\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: And enhance the education.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bellamy, like us who have never heard about it.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we have heard about you in "Scandal."\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Well, hello?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know it is running -- I know it is on another network, but I loved you in that show, and I love that show and what`s so interesting is that, you weren`t even supposed to be on the show for very long, right?\par \par \uc2 I read that you were only supposed to be in the first couple of episodes. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Gayle, I am so lucky. No, she came -- yes. She came -- Shonda came around, our first official table read once we`ve gotten picked up to tell everybody what their arc was going to be that first season and she kind of put her hand on my shoulder.\par \par \uc2 And I dressed up and I`m very southern, so I was very excited for the day. And she said: So you`re going to be here three episodes. I want to write a presidential divorce, and I tried to smile because you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Like, yay, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: I was grateful to be there and I was dying inside. But it didn`t turn out that way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you became such a key figure in that show.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: It was fun. It was fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Such a keeper --\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Madam President. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Bellamy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much for coming here.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. And that website again for people you want to check that out.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: UnderstandingHE.com.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, very good. Thank you very much for bringing awareness to this.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Appreciate it. Bellamy Young. Come back soon.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, our series "Pushing the Limits," Dana Jacobson goes into the water, the dirty water of the Hudson River.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say what?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, for the Hudson -- the Hudson River. It`s cloudy, can`t see very far in it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cloudy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is a story about one man`s aquatic adventure.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up on CBS MORNINGS, take a swim with us in the Hudson River and find out why this guy is swimming all 315 miles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, it`s pretty up there, I am not worried about that part of it. But anyway, okay, but first it is time anybody -- 8:08, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Purple Phase.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is Grammy-nominated artist, Arlo Parks, who will be here in studio with us. She will talk to us about her new book of poems, very personal, and why she decided to release them now after keeping them private.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our series, "Pushing the Limits" share stories of ordinary people accomplishing the truly incredible. In this case, it is Lewis Pugh, he is an endurance athlete. He just wrapped up a 315-mile aquatic adventure, swimming from the source of the Hudson River in New York`s Adirondack Mountains all the way down to New York Harbor, accompanied by a support team.\par \par \uc2 "CBS Saturday Morning" co-host, Dana Jacobson joined Pugh on the journey and later at New York`s Pier 57 to find out more about the inspirational message behind the mission.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Endurance swimmer, Lewis Pugh knows exactly how to make a splash. The 53-year-old is the only person to have completed a long- distance swim in every ocean in the world. He is willing to test the waters no matter the adventure.\par \par \uc2 Over the past decade, he has become the first athlete to swim across the North Pole, the English Channel, the Red Sea, even under the Antarctic ice sheet.\par \par \uc2 When did swimming come into your life?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH (Endurance swimmer): When I was about 17 years old.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You ready?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You`re feeling well? Good luck, my boy.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: When did it go from enjoying swimming to finding these endurance type of swims?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: In 2007, I went to the North Pole and I did a swim there, and this was across an open patch of sea.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: And I remember going into that swim and then coming out the other end, and actually feeling that I was a different person because I came out of that feeling, wow, you know, this place is melting so quickly, I now have a big responsibility to share this message with the whole world.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It changed your perspective, obviously.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: I think it changed me.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Since then, Pugh has been on a mission to complete record breaking swims in jaw-dropping places, not for the titles or accolades, but to make waves in the conversation about ocean and now river preservation.\par \par \uc2 His latest challenge turned quite a few heads as he expected it would. In mid-August, Pugh set out on a month-long journey that`s never been done before, swimming unassisted from the tip of the Hudson River in the Adirondacks down to the very bottom in Lower Manhattan. That`s 315 miles of the infamous river contaminated by toxic waste throughout the last century before cleanup efforts began in the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: I want people to know what has happened in the Hudson River.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: I think this is a blueprint for people all around the world, that their rivers can be saved.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That there is something that can be done.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Absolutely. People around the world have heard about this as an amazing story about a river, which was so badly misused.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: And so badly polluted, and it was one of the most polluted rivers here in America, if not the world. And then things have been turned around.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Enough so that Pugh spent 32 consecutive days in the river swimming twice a day, both early in the morning, and well into the night to align his swim times with the rivers current.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Sometimes, I`ll be swimming at night, and I`d turn onto my back and start doing backstroke and I`m looking at the heavens and you`ve got the big blue moon, and all of these stars and it was really, really comforting.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to want to get in here, it`s a lot cooler.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I decided to jump in myself.\par \par \uc2 All right, you convinced me.\par \par \uc2 On day 25 of the journey, when Pugh had just 50 miles to go, long distance from my swim team days, I eased into my stroke. But trust me, Lewis Pugh makes it look easy.\par \par \uc2 People that I told that I was going to swim with you, there are still people who said, you`re getting in the Hudson River? Where?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So, it still has some of that reputation.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: I hope we can change that reputation.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: I hope more people will swim in this river. This is such a special river.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: One that Pugh has come to know intimately. And one that drew a crowd of spectators to downtown Manhattan, to take in his final stretch within it.\par \par \uc2 But there`s one crowd Pugh hopes to rally in even larger numbers, members of the United Nations who have the chance to ratify a new treaty aimed to protect 30 percent of the world`s High Seas by 2030.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: This is the last piece of the jigsaw puzzle which we needed in order to protect the planet properly. So for me, the mission isn`t complete.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Because it`s not just the swim.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Because it`s not just a swim. I`ve got to get to the diplomats. I`ve got to get the Environment Ministers, I`ve got to get to the heads of state now and deliver the message.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Your determination as one person to make world leaders listen, to make people listen. Where does that come from?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Deep down in here. I`ve seen oceans change. And for me, this is a defining issue of our generation.\par \par \uc2 In a short period of time, we really have to protect the planet. I love being in the oceans. I love being in rivers. I am absolutely determined to spend my life doing this work.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Dana Jacobson in New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo to him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, he did it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean -- yes, he did it and he is totally right that the Hudson is a success story in the sense that it has come a long, long way from terrible, terrible pollution and the more people are in it and active with it, the more we care about it, the more we`ll keep it clean.\par \par \uc2 But just for the record, the New York state environmental officials say that the New York City portion, the very end there, still not considered to be an everyday safe swimming river.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I`m getting texts from people I work with at the "NFL Today." Shout out to Ellen. She basically is saying the same thing like there`s a reputation but I swim in this river all the time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I was surprised that she told me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: After the rainstorm --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ellen swims in there all the time?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes. She said --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The issue is the runoff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is the runoff.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the sewer system, so after a big rainstorm like we had last week --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what she said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Then you`ve got to stay out of it, and I`m sure he was timing it like that.\par \par \uc2 I`ll tell you what though? Swimming at night, something brushes you in the water, I couldn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it just give you an appreciation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For what we have and often we take for granted.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We should go for a night swim together as a show.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is one of those things that`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What you`re talking about, Willis?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A night swim?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead, retired \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend, that would be Michael Jordan joins a very exclusive list and we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not even a swim. A night swim?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here is your "Jeopardy!" clue. She is the most successful female champion in "Jeopardy!" history. The answer: Who is Amy Schneider?\par \par \uc2 She is getting ready on our set right now.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, she will tell us about her new memoir and the questions she has yet to answer.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS where I`ve always been able to control myself and will never conga.\par \par \uc2 Time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, you can`t control it any longer. Get up, Tony.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come on, baby. Shake that conga.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I would love to see it though, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is Michael Jordan, who is still setting records two decades after retiring from the \uc2 NBA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His Airness is now the first athlete to rank among the 400 wealthiest people in the US.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And most of his money was made off the court.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: After selling his majority share in the \uc2 Charlotte Hornets\uc2 , Jordan`s net worth is now calculated by Forbes magazine at around $3 billion.\par \par \uc2 He made most of his fortune close to two-and-a-half billion through partnerships with big brands like \uc2 Nike\uc2 , a lot of \uc2 Nike\uc2  money, Gatorade, McDonald`s, and Hanes. He made $93.9 million as a player at a time when players were not earning what they make right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was that movie we were talking about? It is called "Air."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Air."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Air."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is about the deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So good. I love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because that`s a large part of his fortune.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was like the first athlete to get a portion of every shoe sold in his likeness.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A different type of revenue share as an athlete and listen, the Jordan brand has transcended basketball.\par \par \uc2 You can see that logo in NASCAR --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anything, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Football, boxing, baseball.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you know what it means.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it was a hoop shoe at one point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then, his mom came in and according to "Air" came in and said: Oh no, we`re going to do things differently here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But don`t we just love him being on the list?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t we just love Michael Jordan?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, Gayle, you make a good point. That`s momma`s fortune.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because she was the one that started it all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, according to "Air," which is a very good move.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very good movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is about how the classic southern accent is apparently fading from this earth, from this country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say it ain`t so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: According to a big time study from the University of Georgia and \uc2 Georgia Tech\uc2 , which looked at like seven generations of Georgians, that deep southern drawl is falling off a cliff.\par \par \uc2 So baby boomers talk differently than the silent generation, talk differently than Gen X, talk differently than current college kids.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, it`s not just Georgia. So this is the interesting thing. According to people who study this, it is happening with the Boston accent. It`s happening with people in Detroit. It`s happening in California.\par \par \uc2 So this thing that was so interesting about America where we all talk a little bit different.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have a Maryland and Florida accent, it is kind of going away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did they say why?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s because we`re moving around more. We`re mixing more, and more of a national culture of media.\par \par \uc2 So I guess the question is, is this a bad thing or a good thing or not a thing at all?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still like the different accents.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think it is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still like the different accents.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like it, too. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I enjoy a good accent for sure, but I think it`s a good thing because oftentimes, unfortunately, stereotypes are attached to accents.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you are judged.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. So the classic southern accent comes out on words like prize and face, bet, bait and dress.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What words give you a little trouble?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well you know, Nate, that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: From Maryland and Florida.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do a lot of water.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, the water.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: W-A-R-D-E-R. I do a lot of water. Forward, no R and forward, forward.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then also I can`t -- you know, that little prefix U-N, you know, like unaware, I always say like O-N, unaware, unguard.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hear from people who write to me about it, but I`m going to stick to it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All I know is, I can still understand every word you say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What about you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think you`re good.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is an actor`s incredible journey to make it on stage on time and all the people that helped get her there.\par \par \uc2 Shout out to \uc2 Delta Airlines\uc2  on this.\par \par \uc2 Mikayla Renfrow is usually a Broadway understudy. In fact, she`s the second understudy, but she was flying back from a European vacation earlier this week -- earlier last week, when she got a text message the show needed her to go on as Jasmine in "Aladdin" that night.\par \par \uc2 Her flight was due to land at JFK airport at five o`clock, two hours before curtain time. She decided to ask for help.\par \par \uc2 So first, she went to the flight attendant. The flight attendant moved her up to first class, so she would be the first one off the plane. The pilot was able to radio ahead to change gates so that the gate was the first one next to Customs, so she could whisk through the airport, to a car, then a helicopter that the Delta crew arranged for her --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Took her from Manhattan where she took the subway and then ran to the theater. She had to take the subway after landing -- Delta set all this up -- getting into costume half an hour before the curtain went up.\par \par \uc2 And I just said, boy, Delta going above and beyond. She made it and then she invited the flight attendant who also helped her, Leicha Richardson.\par \par \uc2 So I just think all things came together to make sure that she got to the theater 30 minutes before curtain time, to get into costume and do it. I love that story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shout out to Delta.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shout out to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was a good one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you Mikayla, I hear that you killed that night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats. Congrats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, that was good, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Grammy-nominated artist, Arlo Parks is here. We`ll talk with the singer-songwriter and poet about her new book and her decision to share her poems publicly for the first time.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Pegasus.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is Arlo Parks in her hit song on Pegasus featuring Phoebe Bridgers.\par \par \uc2 The Grammy-nominated singer-songwriter and poet has been called a poet laureate of Gen Z discontent by "Vogue" Magazine. Parks is now releasing never before seen poems in her new book: "The Magic Border: Poetry Fragments From My Soft Machine."\par \par \uc2 A companion to her second album, her poems explore themes of queerness, Blackness, grief, and love. Arlo Parks joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS (Grammy-nominated singer-songwriter): Good morning. I`m good. Thank you. How are you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You seem to be good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 This collection of poetry is deeply, deeply personal and as you read it, you can feel it. Why did you decide to share this work publicly? And how does this compare to your music?\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes, so I`ve been writing poetry since I was about maybe 11 or 12, and I think as you say, it was very much my own personal refuge. It was this thing that I did for myself by myself.\par \par \uc2 And I think recently, you know, touring a lot, I was reading a lot and being so moved by what I was reading, and I just wanted to be able to do that for other people, and I think that`s what gave me the courage to finally kind of share this and create this book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You talk about, Arlo, about poetry has a chaotic magic to you, and I like the phrasing of that. But you also talk about having a paper bag of burrata and anchovies. You lost me on that one, I am afraid. Does that taste good? Does that taste good?\par \par \uc2 But talk about poetry having a chaotic magic to you.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re a very visual writer, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What you mean? Didn`t you think --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Every poem is like a scene.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very visual. Yes. Yes. I can see it.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes. Exactly. I take a lot of influence from cinema. And I think just from observing, I think that if you look out at the world, there is this real sense of magic to it. And I think my process is very chaotic. It`s very much kind of pulling fragments from the characters around me and my past and nostalgia and the things I observe.\par \par \uc2 And there`s something quite beautiful about that, to me, I think when I approached my poems, in this kind of whirlwind, very emotional --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Stream of consciousness that you`re allowing us to enjoy the ride as well.\par \par \uc2 When you write, it seems like it is therapeutic for the reader.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to say that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it has to be therapeutic for you. What is it like releasing this and how you have been feeling about yourself?\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes. It`s exactly as you say, it feels so cathartic. I think especially being able to actually speak to fans and people who have read the book, and maybe have been inspired to, you know, pick up writing themselves, or you know, like people who wanted to write for 20 years being like, okay, I will finally have the courage to do it now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: And I think that kind of gives me a sense of purpose, and just makes me feel like my thoughts and my feelings that I kind of put together in my bedroom are actually having a wider impact on the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to pick up on what Nate thought -- what Nate said, because I thought that too, that it must be healing bruises: "I wish I was bruises, almost every one that I love has been abused and I am included in that." So it seems to me this would be very healing to write about, to sing about.\par \par \uc2 What do you hope people will get out of your book?\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes. I think I want it to be -- I guess, I want it to be a relief. I want it to be something that people can read and see themselves in, and I want it to be something that people can read and be like: Oh, I should get this from my sister. I should get this for, you know, my partner. It`d be something that you know, people carry through with them on their journeys and share with people they love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you and Phoebe Bridgers, together. I like that combination.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes. Yes. No, we met a while ago, we met like maybe four years ago and we`ve been kind of crossing paths and singing together at music festivals and finally came together for my song "Pegasus," which is really special. She`s like a really special person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is your advice to those who want to start writing?\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: I guess just start.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just start.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Just trust your voice and start. I think, the hardest thing is very much you know, being like I want to write, I want to write, but maybe not quite having the courage to kind of put those first few words on the page.\par \par \uc2 So I think just start and trust your words.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you started -- I love your accent.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Started.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You started at 11.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Very Brit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Being this transparent isn`t easy. So thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At 11.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So thank you for this work. It is helpful for the reader.\par \par \uc2 All right, "The Magic Border."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats, Arlo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . is on sale wherever you buy your books. Tickets are also currently on sale for your upcoming tour. Okay, Arlo.\par \par \uc2 Up next, "Jeopardy!" record breaker, Amy Schneider is here. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jeopardy!")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is a very long time. "Jeopardy!" champion, Amy Schneider became the second longest running player in the show`s history last year, winning 40 consecutive games of "Jeopardy!"\par \par \uc2 Now Schneider is opening up about that moment and so much more in her new memoir. It`s called "In the Form of a Question: The Joys and Rewards of a Curious Life."\par \par \uc2 We are proud to say it`s published by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , which like \uc2 CBS\uc2  is owned by \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Schneider writes in the book: "Knowledge is a shield and a sword, a joy and a duty. If you have the desire, not just to know but to understand and you will grow more and more powerful every day."\par \par \uc2 And Amy Schneider joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Amy, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER ("In the Form of a Question" author): Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is so much in this book that you open up about, and I want to get to a lot of it. But I want to start with the key message here, which is just that learning can be fun, learning makes you powerful. It can be a joy.\par \par \uc2 Talk about what you want people to take away on that front?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes, you know, I had so many people after I was on "Jeopardy!" asking me like, you know, how do you know all that stuff? And when I thought it through, I realized that it was just because I was raised to love knowing stuff, and to be curious about the world.\par \par \uc2 And that, you know, I also happen to have the sort of memory that like kind of trivia facts stick in my mind. So like, I was lucky in that sense that that skill set applied to "Jeopardy!"\par \par \uc2 But even apart from that, I think I`ve gotten so much value out of just having that curiosity to openness and understanding the world better and ultimately myself. And that`s something that I think anybody can benefit from whether or not they`re going to like remember the capital of Liechtenstein or whatever.\par \par \uc2 But being open and curious, I think has brought me so many benefits.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it`s the capital of Liechtenstein?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Vaduz.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also like, the key thing is being willing to be wrong and actually being open to being wrong and liking being wrongly, you write, and this is very unique in the beginning of this book that you hope to disagree in the future with what you`re writing right now, what the reader is reading.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Talk to me about that philosophy, that approach.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Right. Yes, I mean, I think that, you know, I feel like -- you know, I feel like I should always look back on myself in the past. And think, boy, I was done back then. Because if I don`t think that, then I haven`t gotten any smarter since.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: And so, and I do -- like, I know that I`ve evolved my beliefs on a lot of things over the years, and I look back at times and things I used to believe and I don`t agree with that anymore.\par \par \uc2 And if that was the case, then it`s reasonable to think that there`s something I believe now that in the future I`m going to disagree with. So I just wanted to partly just like cover myself and you know, for --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because there`s a lot of topics in this book. Gayle, dive in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll say, but I like how you did every chapter with a question. Chapter Two: When did you know you were trans? And I`m wondering, did you have to have a whole thought process before deciding to go on "Jeopardy!" because you are exposing yourself to the whole world in a very different way?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was that something that was a tough decision?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: It was. You know, I`d wanted to be on "Jeopardy!" kind of like my whole life. I grew up watching with my parents but then yes, when I came out as trans, it definitely made me reconsider for a while. Because, you know, trans people can become targets and I`ve seen that happen to people. And you know, it was kind of scary to be exposed in that way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what did you think it was when you were growing up, Amy? What did you think?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes. I mean, you know, growing up in the 80s and 90s in Ohio, you know, trans person was not a concept that that me or anybody in my community knew about.\par \par \uc2 And so, it wasn`t something I could imagine being, and so when I was a kid, more or less, what I figured was, all of the other little boys probably wish that they were girls too, and are jealous of them and wish they could were pretty things and all of that. And we`re all just not allowed to say that, so we`re all hiding it, but probably everybody else feels the same way I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there`s nobody you can talk to really about it.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Right, because that is -- I mean, and also, you know, that is a thing. Like, it`s so strict in our society that men cannot do anything girly or talk about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s changing, I hope.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes, I think so. But that definitely -- you know, made it easy to believe that everyone else was secretly thinking those things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel like most people can`t differentiate knowing something and understanding something and those are two things that you write about. You know yourself. What do you understand about yourself now versus let`s say, 20 years ago?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: I mean, I think, you know, again, first thing that I`m a woman. It took me a long time to understand that.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, you know, once I started living kind of more authentically, I learned a lot more. I learned that I am like kind of an extrovert and social and I learned that I`m a lot more you know, once I started allowing myself to be authentic, I could connect with other people so much better.\par \par \uc2 And I learned that, you know, I`m really interested in getting to know people in a way that I was always kind of frightened of forming those connections before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are many things, but many things you didn`t appear to be was nervous when you were on "Jeopardy!" You always seem so calm, so in control. Were you feeling that?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: No, I was definitely nervous most of the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes. But, you know, I wanted -- first of all, I just like, I`m a performer. I was a theater kid my whole life. And you know, I wanted to look good on TV, and I also didn`t want my other contestants to know if I was feeling nervous.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Poker face, if you will.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes, exactly. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you think you would have that long of a run? Did you think, I got this game? Because you played it for so long.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: No. I mean, like, you know, I thought I could be good -- I like, I told all my friends, I was like, if things go my way, I could win - - I could see myself winning three or four games.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: That was, you know, legitimately about the most I expected.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But hold on, I don`t want people to think this is just an earnest book about learning and knowing. It is that, but it`s also a very frank and honest conversation about drug use, about polyamorous relationships.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you get into a lot of -- you`ve got tarot card reading.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Amy, go see us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, were there things in the book, and we only have about 30 more seconds that you were like: Ooh, I don`t want to put that in.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: No, because I felt an obligation to give an authentic representation of myself as a trans person because I know of many people I represent that community.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: And I want to take a give a full picture.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and that`s why you didn`t wrestle with, do I have a story? You have a story and that matters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have a story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have a platform as well.\par \par \uc2 Amy Schneider, thank you very much. Appreciate it. "In the Form of a Question" is on sale right now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is a good book?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. What is a good book? Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`ll do it for us. Very good, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Capital of Lichtenstein. That was impressive, I have to say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020231004eja400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 27, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Scott MacFarlane, Elise Preston, Jericka Duncan,  Meg Oliver, Elizabeth Palmer, Nikole Killion \par \uc2 3,650 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the news after those bombshell bribery charges, as Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  and his wife plead not guilty in federal court, the rules set by a judge about his ability to travel and the pricey bond they paid to avoid jail before the trial.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 The powerful New Jersey senator leaving the courthouse today as the calls for his resignation grow, now including the second-ranking Democrat in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Free iPhone!\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: In Philadelphia, this is just one of several stores still shut down after more than a hundred people stole everything from iPhones and shoes to clothes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN STANFORD (Interim Philadelphia Police Commissioner): We had tonight was a bunch of criminal opportunists make an attempt to destroy our city.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The American soldier who crossed into North Korea is on his way back to the U.S. after Kim Jong Un expels him. How China and Sweden secured Travis King`s release.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, for the first time, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs responds to Donald Trump`s suggestion that he deserves to be executed for calls to China.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried about your safety?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  seized multiple 3-D printed ghost guns from inside a home day care center. This comes just a little more than a week after a 1- year-old died in what police believe to be a fentanyl-related overdose at another day care center.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Nevada`s largest labor union has voted to authorize a strike, a decision that could impact Las Vegas casinos.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: They`re understaffing us and overworking us, while they`re making record profits.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Millions of vehicles recalled over a fire risk. What owners need to know.\par \par \uc2 Big news from the Boss. Why Bruce Springsteen is postponing his concerts for the rest of the year.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Touchdown. Rubio`s record ride comes to an end after a 371-day, 157-million-mile journey at the International Space Station.\par \par \uc2 FRANK RUBIO (NASA Astronaut): It`s good to be home.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with the scandal on Capitol Hill and a sitting United States senator in federal court for charges of bribery and corruption, Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  and his wife pleading not guilty today, accused of accepting hundreds of thousands of dollars of bribes in the form of cash, gold bars and a luxury convertible.\par \par \uc2 The senator was released on $100,000 bond, his wife`s set at $250,000. Calls for his resignation are growing, with 30 Democratic senators now saying he should step down, the latest being the Senate`s second-ranking Democrat, Illinois Senator Dick Durbin. Three New Jersey businessmen who were also charged in the bribery scheme entered pleas of not guilty.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of news to bring you tonight.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion will just start us off from outside the federal courthouse in Lower Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Nikole.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Senator Menendez was serious and respectful in court, although, at times, he did crack a few smiles and consoled his wife, while more of his Senate colleagues abandoned him politically.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): New Jersey Senator \uc2 Robert Menendez\uc2  arrived at federal court hand in hand with his wife, Nadine. The couple were in the same row as they were arraigned and pleaded not guilty to three felony charges, including conspiracy to commit bribery, fraud, and extortion.\par \par \uc2 The senator responded, "Yes, your Honor," when asked if he understood the charges against him and was ordered to surrender his personal passport.\par \par \uc2 Can you continue to serve effectively in the Senate, sir?\par \par \uc2 According to the indictment, Menendez and his wife allegedly accepted bribes in the form of nearly $500,000 in cash found in pockets of a coat bearing his name, over $100,000 in gold bars, and a $60,000 Mercedes convertible, in exchange for his efforts to direct federal aid and weapon sales to Egypt.\par \par \uc2 He`s also accused of enriching a trio of business partners who also pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROBERT MENENDEZ\uc2  (D-New Jersey): I`m innocent. What`s wrong with you guys?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: CBS News has learned the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating whether Egypt`s intelligence services tried to gain access to the senator through his wife.\par \par \uc2 JAVED ALI (Former Homeland Security Department Official): They will be looking to see how direct the ties were, if they exist, between Egyptian security officers and the senator, how high up in the Egyptian government were these decisions being made.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: More than half of the Senate Democratic Caucus has called on the New Jersey Democrat to resign, including the state`s junior Senator, \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Late today, Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  reserved judgment.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): For senators, there`s a much, much higher standard. And, clearly, when you read the indictment, Senator Menendez fell way, way below that standard.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Senator Menendez plans to address Senate Democrats at a lunch tomorrow at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, he cannot speak with congressional staff or political advisers who have knowledge of the case. His next court hearing is set for Monday -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion here in New York, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, Army Private Travis King is back in U.S. custody 71 days after crossing into North Korea. Pyongyang deported him with help from Swedish diplomats who arranged his safe return via China, where U.S. officials met him before heading to a military base in South Korea.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elizabeth Palmer has the new details from Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER (voiceover): Tonight, Private King is on his way home, in good spirits, according to one U.S. official, and looking forward to seeing his family.\par \par \uc2 King has already spoken with his mother, her spokesperson told CBS News.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN FRANKS (Spokesperson For Ms. Claudine Gates): Everybody is overjoyed. This is a large, very close-knit family. They have been desperately worried about him.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER (\uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  Spokesman): We thank Sweden and the People`s Republic of China for their assistance in facilitating that transfer. He is now on his way to the United States, and we expect him to arrive in the coming hours.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: North Korea said the 23-year-old was being deported because he had illegally intruded into the territory of the DPRK, claiming ill feeling against inhuman maltreatment and racial discrimination within the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Back in July, King, seen here in the black cap, joined a tour group at the demilitarized zone and then suddenly sprinted into North Korea. But, that day, he was supposed to be on a plane back to the U.S. to face military discipline after an assault conviction. Now King is finally making that journey. And having been declared AWOL by the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , he may face further consequences.\par \par \uc2 Why North Korea`s leader, \uc2 Kim Jong Un\uc2 , decided to deport King now is a mystery. But the U.S. says there were no strings attached.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER: We did not give them anything. We made no concessions as a part of securing his return.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: And, Norah, the fact that China played a pivotal role in Private King`s return is one more sign that it is committed to warming its frosty relationship with Washington.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elizabeth Palmer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to a night of chaos and violence in Philadelphia. Police say a large flash mob of up to 100 looters of mostly young people swarmed stores across several neighborhoods last night, stealing merchandise and vandalizing property.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver reports, dozens of arrests were made.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Free iPhones.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): They were out as quickly as they got in. Mobs of looters stormed more than 75 retail stores across Philadelphia Tuesday night, snatching anything and everything, some flaunting their stolen haul.\par \par \uc2 Police at this \uc2 Lululemon\uc2  store scuffled with thieves as they ran out, and a security guard at this smashed \uc2 Foot Locker\uc2  was assaulted.\par \par \uc2 JIM KENNEY\uc2  (D-Mayor of Philadelphia, Pennsylvania): Our officers ran around the city last night, braving danger and arresting people. So now, it`s time for them to pay a price for what they did.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: At least 52 people were arrested.\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOHNSTON (\uc2 National Retail Federation\uc2 ): Whether it be individual shoplifting, shoplifting for organized retail crime groups, or these flash mobs and smash-and-grabs, it`s getting worse.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Organized retail theft today is planned and spreads fast using social media. And the easy resale of stolen goods online often creates a scenario with high reward and low risk.\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOHNSTON: In many states, laws have been relaxed so that there are fewer consequences to the individual who commits crimes.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Nationwide, retailers lost $112 billion last year, much of it due to theft. Just this week, Target announced the closure of nine stores in four states due to shoplifting, citing "the safety of our team and guests."\par \par \uc2 Many stores also restricting access to everyday goods, keeping batteries and laundry detergent under lock and key.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Many of the stores robbed were closed today. Some had signs like this one telling customers they will only reopen when they feel safe.\par \par \uc2 Philadelphia police say they will have an increased presence throughout the city tonight -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Meg Oliver, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, another New York City day care is the focus of an investigation into dangerous criminal activity. Police say they discovered a ghost gun printing operation inside a day care in East Harlem. The bust comes less than two weeks after the death of a 1-year-old boy inside a center suspected of being a front for a fentanyl drug ring.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jericka Duncan has the new details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN (voiceover): New York City police say they arrested an 18- year-old and two minors who allegedly used a home-based day care center in this apartment building in Harlem as a ghost gun lab.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D-Mayor of New York City): You got an 18-year-old in his room, 3-D printer. He`s not making little robotic toys. He`s making guns.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Investigators seized two printed pistols, a printed assault rifle and an incomplete handgun all found in an unlocked room inside the day care.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: This is a heartbreaking scenario of thinking that you`re dropping your child off to a place of safe haven, just to find out that it was a dangerous environment.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The suspects are facing multiple charges, including illegal firearms possession and the manufacturing of an assault weapon. The 18-year-old`s mother ran the day care since 2021. It was last inspected by the city in February.\par \par \uc2 This investigation comes nearly two weeks after a 1-year-old boy died and three other children were hospitalized when they were exposed to fentanyl residue inside a day care center in the Bronx. Yesterday, the owner`s husband was picked up by police in Mexico. Officials now say they will review training practices when it comes to inspecting day cares.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: Who would have thought that we must add to our list of inspections, of do we have 3-D printers that can print guns?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: CBS News has learned the day care was licensed to have 12 children as young as 6 weeks old.\par \par \uc2 When asked if the mother of the suspect who ran that day care would face any charges, authorities would only say that the investigation is ongoing - - Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jericka Duncan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Police near Rochester, New York, are investigating a deadly crash involving a bus carrying more than 20 high school students. Police say the bus collided with a \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , killing the driver. The bus caught fire, but everyone got out safely. Look at these pictures. I mean, a student and an adult were taken to the hospital to be checked out.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, for the first time, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 , is responding to shocking comments posted online by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The former president suggested last Friday that the nation`s highest-ranking military officer deserves to be executed for communications the general had with China during the final months of the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: President Trump recently said that your dealings with China were so egregious that, in times gone by, the punishment would have been death.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2  (Chairman, Joint Chiefs of Staff): That`s right. He said that. That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: He is suggesting that you be punished by death, the former commander-in-chief to his former top military adviser.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 : I`m -- look, I`m a soldier. I have been faithful and loyal to the Constitution of the United States for 44.5 years.\par \par \uc2 And my family and I have sacrificed greatly for this country, my mother and father before them. And, you know, as -- as much as these comments are directed at me, it`s also directed at the institution of the military. And there`s -- there`s 2.1 million of us in uniform.\par \par \uc2 And the American people can take it to the bank that all of us, every single one of us, from private to general, we`re loyal to that Constitution and will never turn up back on it, no matter what, no matter what the threats, no matter what the humiliation, no matter what.\par \par \uc2 If we`re willing to die for that document, if we`re willing to deploy to combat, if we`re willing to lose an arm, a leg, an eye to protect and support and defend that document and protect the American people, then we`re willing to live for it, too.\par \par \uc2 So I`m not going to comment directly on those -- those things, but I can tell you that this military, this soldier, me, will never turn our back on that Constitution.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But, for the record, was there anything inappropriate or treasonous about the calls you made to China?\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 : Absolutely not, zero, none.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It almost seems odd to ask this question, because the former commander-in-chief seems to be calling for your execution.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried about your safety?\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 : I have got adequate safety precautions. I -- I wish those comments had not been made, but they were. And we will take appropriate measures to ensure my safety and the safety of my family.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We will have more of an upcoming "60 Minutes" episode on why General Milley thought those calls to China were not only proper, but necessary to avert a war.\par \par \uc2 The summer of strikes has rolled into fall, with Las Vegas culinary workers, bartenders and housekeepers voting overwhelmingly to authorize a strike.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston reports a potential work stoppage could hurt the city`s vital tourism industry.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Las Vegas is known for extravagance. But behind the scenes, there are tens of thousands who cook the meals, mix the drinks and clean the suites.\par \par \uc2 They filled this arena yesterday, voting to strike.\par \par \uc2 NORMA QUINONES (Hospitality Housekeeper): We`re struggling. It`s not easy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: The contract --\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Forty thousand hospitality workers are ready to walk out, claiming low pay, staffing shortages and safety issues, including Norma Quinones.\par \par \uc2 NORMA QUINONES: They don`t care about us. All they care is making their own money.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Of the three major hotel chains negotiating, \uc2 Wynn\uc2  and \uc2 MGM\uc2  released statements, \uc2 MGM\uc2  saying it has a decades-long history of bargaining successfully with the unions and calling their ongoing meetings productive.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Our (sp?) contract --\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: As yet another strike looms, one has officially ended. The Writers Guild has authorized its members to report back to work. Bill Maher announced he will return Friday.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from HBO)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Stephen Colbert and other late-night talk show hosts next week.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Late Show with Stephen Colbert)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Jimmy Kimmel Live!)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: We`re also learning what`s in the new deal, wage increases, minimum staffing, streaming residuals and protections on the use of A.I.\par \par \uc2 Back in Las Vegas, there`s both passion and deep concern.\par \par \uc2 TED PAPPAGEORGE (Secretary-Treasurer, Culinary Workers Union Local 226): Rent`s being jacked up through the roof, but the cost of gas, the cost of groceries, the cost of electricity, so folks need a raise.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Talks are scheduled for next week. And the union says, if an agreement is not met, walkouts could happen in just a matter of days.\par \par \uc2 The looming strike could bring the multibillion-dollar casino industry here to a standstill -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elise Preston, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, it`s T-minus three days and counting until disaster for the federal government. Unless members of Congress can come together, there will be a shutdown on Sunday. Late this afternoon, President Biden said a lot of vital work could be impacted.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane is on the Hill and reports that instead of finding compromise, both sides are digging deeper.\par \par \uc2 So, Scott, what are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: I have been talking to members of Congress all day, Norah, and they can reach consensus on just one thing. They`re running short on time and running short on hope.\par \par \uc2 The outlook is so perilous, the head of the major federal employees union told CBS News he`s urged members to save whatever they can in money, as paychecks could stop Sunday morning. An \uc2 IRS\uc2  worker in Chicago told us her colleagues are asking about food pantries and unemployment insurance.\par \par \uc2 If the government shuts down Sunday, some workers at the federal level stay on the job, but without paychecks, TSA officers, customs officers. But if they choose to call in sick without their paychecks, there can be big backlogs at the checkpoints. This would cause delays for those who need passports and visas, small business loans.\par \par \uc2 Social Security checks and Medicare payments continue, but for those who need federal help to afford baby formula and food, that help could get interrupted, too, unless there`s a compromise here. But, Norah, as the night began, there`s not even a hint of it.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wow, an impending nightmare.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, two major recalls involving millions of cars that could potentially catch fire. We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, an important consumer alert.\par \par \uc2 Kia\uc2  and Hyundai are recalling more than 3.3 million vehicles over potential fire risks. Federal regulators say Hyundai is recalling 13 different models from 2010 to 2015 because a brake fluid leak could spark a fire. \uc2 Kia\uc2  is recalling a dozen models from 2010 through 2019 due to a possible electrical short that can also ignite.\par \par \uc2 We have posted a full list of the impacted models at CBSNews.com.\par \par \uc2 Big news tonight in college football. Michigan State fired head coach Mel Tucker today for inappropriate conduct. Tucker was accused of sexual harassment during a phone call with a prominent rape survivor and activist that he had hired to speak to his team, the university terminating what`s left of the 10-year $95 million contract that Tucker signed less than two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Bruce Springsteen makes a major announcement about his health and concert tour. We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The Boss with a major announcement today.\par \par \uc2 Rock legend Bruce Springsteen is shutting down his tour for the rest of the year. Springsteen is struggling with peptic ulcer disease.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bruce Springsteen`s performance, February)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: In a statement, the 74-year-old thanked fans for their support, adding that he is on the mend and can`t wait to see everyone next year. No word yet on make-up dates for the postponed shows.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not too late to get your Powerball ticket, the jackpot now $850 million. The cash payout option would get you more than $397 million before taxes. No one has hit the jackpot since July 19th.\par \par \uc2 Houston, we have a \uc2 NASA\uc2  record. We will explain next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Astronaut Frank Rubio is back on Earth tonight after setting a \uc2 NASA\uc2  record for the longest trip in space, 371 days.\par \par \uc2 Rubio, along with two Russian cosmonauts, touched down in Kazakhstan at 7:17 a.m. Eastern time. The mission was scheduled to last just six months aboard the International Space Station, but the capsule that was supposed to take him home was damaged, so his stay was extended.\par \par \uc2 In all, Rubio completed nearly 6,000 orbits around the Earth and traveled more than 157 million miles. Talk about some frequent flyer miles.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230928ej9r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 North Korea to Expel US Soldier; Hollywood Writers End Strike; Judge Rules Trump Committed Fraud; Biden, Trump Visit Picket Lines; Shutdown Finger Pointing; \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Sued by FTC; Tech CEO Murdered; Child Care Funding at Risk; Water Crisis Explained; Paul Rodgers Rises Again; When It`s Your Turn to Serve; Leslie Odom, Jr. Live, "The Amazing Race" is Back; Record Breaking Success, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Elizabeth Palmer, Danya  Bacchus, Scott MacFarlane, Robert Costa, Nancy Cordes, Jo Ling Kent, Alexus  Davila, Nikole Killion; Stephanie Abrams, Vladimir Duthiers, Anthony Mason \par \uc2 14,670 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday. It`s hump day, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news: Hollywood writers are heading back to work today after union leaders approved the deal after months on the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I had to call some people out and I was like, have some fun, celebrate because we`ve got so much of what we wanted, like it`s overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden joins striking auto workers in Michigan and voices his support for their demands.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): So, let`s keep going. You deserve what you earn.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A judge rules \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has lied for years about the value of his properties.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: This case, for all intents and purposes, is decided.\par \par \uc2 MAN: North Korea has decided to "expel" \uc2 US Army\uc2  Private Travis King. He crossed into North in July.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Taylor Swift effect continues to grow. Sunday`s Chiefs-Bears game was most watched TV program of the week across all network.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Travis Kelce`s Jersey sales have skyrocketed nearly 400 percent.\par \par \uc2 MAN: How does it feel that Taylor Swift has finally put you on the map?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Bounces back toward the middle, Lincoln in the center field, a base hit, across to the plate. Not in time.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And just like that for the first time in \uc2 Phillies\uc2 ` history, the team has clinched the playoffs with a walk off win.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP singing.)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There are some very happy boys. I often wonder, Tony, when people are sliding, do you really have to slide? Wouldn`t you have still made it to the base?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t always have to slide, but it`s safe to slide. Also, it is very fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You want to have a reason to use the washing machine at the end of the game.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Nate, is it wrong that I want a Travis Kelce jersey? What`s wrong with me?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nothing wrong with that. That`s the Taylor Swift effect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, well, thank you for joining us this morning. We have some great performers on the show today, including the super talented, Leslie Odom, Jr. who hasn`t been on Broadway since "Hamilton." Now, he`s back to star in a revival that is very personal to him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Plus, Anthony Mason`s exclusive conversation with legendary rock and roll singer, Paul Rodgers.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Paul Rodgers` performance.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is Rodgers with "Free" in 1970 before his global success with Bad Company. He tells Anthony about nearly losing that voice you`re hearing and how his new solo album helped him get back on track.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We always like an exclusive with Anthony, but we`re going to begin today with breaking news. North Korea says that it plans to expel Travis King, the American soldier who fled into the country while visiting a checkpoint at the Demilitarized Zone back in July.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer is following this story from Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Liz, good morning to you. What are we hearing today?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, North Korea announced through its own news agency just a short time ago that King will be expelled, but they haven`t told us how or when.\par \par \uc2 The 23-year-old who is from Wisconsin, dashed across the border between North and South Korea while visiting the Demilitarized Zone last July.\par \par \uc2 He had actually gone to the DMZ on a bus tour after leaving the airport in Seoul where he was supposed to be boarding a plane back to the US to face probable disciplinary military action after he had been arrested in South Korea.\par \par \uc2 North Korea says King has confessed to illegally entering the country, but it remains a mystery why they chose this moment to expel him.\par \par \uc2 It may or may not be a coincidence that just this week, the country has reopened its border to diplomats and foreigners after the pandemic and perhaps they just feel they`ve got their hands full -_ Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much, Liz.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, turning now to more breaking news this morning, it happened overnight, this time from Hollywood. As of just a few hours ago, the writers` strike officially over.\par \par \uc2 Film and TV writers were told they can go back to work today after union leaders approved a deal that includes major gains on issues they`ve been fighting for.\par \par \uc2 Danya Bacchus is in Culver City, California where there are sure to be celebrations today, probably not that much work getting done.\par \par \uc2 Danya, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: You`re right, Tony. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Not just because the strike is over, but because it appears on all the major issues that the writers were fighting for -- pay, working conditions, and use of artificial intelligence, they made major gains.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "What do we want? A fair contract. When do we want it? Now.")\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: For nearly five months, they walked picket lines in solidarity.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Whose story? Our story.")\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Some 11,000 members of the Writers Guild of America on strike is they pushed for greater pay and protections in a new three-year contract.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We`re out here standing with our union, and for all unions.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: It appears to have paid off. The newly released details include minimum pay increases for every year of the contract, guaranteed staffing levels for writers` rooms, and guardrails designed to protect writers against the use of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HAYWOOD (WGA negotiating committee member): This is a phenomenal deal.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Eric Haywood was on the WGA`s negotiating committee and has been on the frontline since May.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HAYWOOD: The workers created that power.\par \par \uc2 A majority of the membership is over the moon. This deal, I think financially, works out to about three times as much as our last contract.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: A major sticking point with the studios was the potential use of artificial intelligence in writing scripts.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HAYWOOD: Every project has to have at least one or more human writers. We cannot be replaced by this technology.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Real Time With Bill Maher.")\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: At least one talk show was set to resume production this week from Bill Maher. "My writers in Real Time are back. See you Friday night."\par \par \uc2 But with the \uc2 SAG AFTRA\uc2  actors still on strike, it could be months before all Hollywood production gets back to the business of show business.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HAYWOOD: I hope that this inspires, you know the studios to really respond, to really hear what \uc2 SAG\uc2  is asking for.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Now, we`ve asked the producers alliance for its take on the new deal, but we haven`t heard back yet and we should note that the deal must still be ratified by the guild`s rank and file, but leadership expects it will be passed when members vote early next month -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just hearing the deal is done is good news to all of us. Danya, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to landmark ruling against former President Trump. A New York judge says Trump has lied for years about how much he is worth and says the former president committed fraud by sharply exaggerating the value of real estate holdings in business transactions.\par \par \uc2 The former president could eventually lose control of iconic properties like Trump Tower. Now Trump`s spokesperson said they`d appeal.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is in Washington following the money.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The ruling comes at a lawsuit brought by New York attorney general, Letitia James and ultimately it means whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  padded books won`t even be an issue at trial. It`s just a matter of the damages.\par \par \uc2 James is seeking $250 million against the man who staked his reputation on his self-proclaimed business success.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES (New York attorney general): Claiming you have money that you do not have does not amount to the art of the deal, it is the art of the steal.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: That was New York attorney general, Letitia James last September unveiling a civil lawsuit against former President Trump alleging he, his family, and his businesses misrepresented their property values by millions for years in order to get favorable loans, insurance deals, and tax benefits.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We love you, Trump.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: On Tuesday, a state judge sided with James ruling the documents for several properties clearly contained fraudulent valuations. The judge also skewered Trump`s defense team for its reliance on bogus arguments rooted in a fantasy world.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Apprentice.")\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Those buildings include the signature Trump Tower. The judge found Trump reported his residence there was 30,000 square feet, even though it`s actually around 11,000 square feet, ultimately exaggerating its worth by as much as $207 million.\par \par \uc2 Now, the judge`s ruling would cancel Trump and his associates` business certificates in New York and require them to turn over their properties to an independent receiver.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I don`t know what I did wrong.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In an August 2022 deposition, Trump called the lawsuit a witch hunt, a claim he has repeated in posts on his Truth Social platform last night.\par \par \uc2 CBS News legal analyst, Rikki Klieman.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: You can either take a bombastic claim that the system is out to get someone or you can believe your own eyes as you read the facts.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The judge also ordered sanctions against Trump`s lawyers saying they showed a blatant disregard for the law by making frivolous arguments the court had previously dismissed, ordering those lawyers to pay $7,500.00 each, and Gayle, this trial is still set to begin in New York on Monday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, voters do not care about Donald Trump`s legal problems. Why? Because the latest CBS News poll shows this. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a commanding lead among Republicans in the first two states that will decide the party`s 2024 nominee.\par \par \uc2 So in spite of all the legal issues, Trump leads the GOP field in Iowa by 30 points. Republicans in New Hampshire support him by an even larger margin, as you see on your screen.\par \par \uc2 Today, the former president will be in the swing state of Michigan just one day after President Biden visited the striking auto workers there.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa is in Wayne, Michigan with more on this part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 The fight for the support of the working voter, it continues today. When former President Trump arrives here in Michigan, he will speak at a non- union facility. These dueling appearances, they could be a preview of what`s to come with a possible rematch in the general election next year.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The middle class built the country.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The unions built the middle class.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: President Biden encouraged striking auto workers to stick with it and became the first president to join a picket line.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Let`s keep going. You deserve what you earn and you`ve earned a hell of a lot more than you get paid now.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "What do we want? Contracts. When do we want it? Now.")\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is looking for 36 percent raise over the next four years, a four-day workweek, and union protections for workers at 10 electric vehicle battery factories.\par \par \uc2 STANLEY GALBRETH (\uc2 UAW\uc2  member): We`re going to stick with it to get what we deserve, what we have been deprived, so yes, I`m sticking with it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And with the strike growing to 41 plants and facilities across 21 states, the back-to-back visits by Biden and Trump today are putting workers` rights at the forefront of the 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 Is the labor vote up for grabs?\par \par \uc2 WALTER ROBINSON (\uc2 UAW\uc2  member): I will say, you have to earn it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  president, Shawn Fain has been critical of Trump and stood with President Biden on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (\uc2 UAW\uc2  president): Thank you, Mr. President for coming.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But his union is yet to endorse anyone in the race for president.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`ll tell you what, the auto workers are being sold down the river by their leadership and their leadership should endorse Trump.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: President Biden, who likes to call himself the most pro- union president ever also stood with striking auto workers during his last presidential campaign in 2019.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: Hey, folks. I`m \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and I am \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: He is hoping, his historic support will give him a boost.\par \par \uc2 Can President Biden count on the union in 2024?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The union will be there with him?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The union will be there with him.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former President Trump won Michigan when he ran for president in 2016, then candidate Biden won it in 2020. You can be sure that both parties will be back here a lot in the coming year as they fine tune their message on the economy, on trade, and on manufacturing -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Michigan in the spotlight once again.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 To Washington now where Republican lawmakers in the House are still fighting with each other over a government shutdown now just three days away, however, in the Senate, Republicans came to an agreement with Democrats, voting overwhelmingly to move forward on a short term spending bill that would keep the government running.\par \par \uc2 The House meanwhile, focusing on a long-term budget and Speaker Kevin McCarthy still trying to win over a handful of right-wing holdouts.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): In a tight majority like this, four members can stop anything. Now, we are able to get members in a better place and be able to move forward. But the great thing is, I never give up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nancy Cordes is in Washington for us watching what feels like a food fight at times. Stay clean over there, Nancy. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It`s getting pretty messy over here, Tony.\par \par \uc2 You know, the vote we saw in the Senate last night does qualify as something of a breakthrough. It`s a deal that has big bipartisan support. It would keep the government open through mid-November. It includes funding for Ukraine and wildfire and flooding disaster relief.\par \par \uc2 But, and this is a big but, conservatives in the Senate notably \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  of Kentucky are threatening to slow down its passage when there is little time to spare before a shutdown this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And over in the House, making matters worse, there are still a few conservatives that are threatening to overthrow the speaker if he allows a vote on this spending deal instead of moving their plan to slash spending. They don`t like that Ukraine funding either.\par \par \uc2 Late last night, the House finally managed to clear one hurdle inching closer to a vote on four separate -term funding bills, but best case scenario, that approach would still cause big swaths of the government to shut down this weekend, so the food fight continues -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nancy, while I have you. There is an avalanche of this one, of lawmakers now urging New Jersey Democratic senator, \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  to resign after of course, he was charged with accepting bribes to secretly help Egypt`s government. What can you tell us on this front?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I can tell you that at this point, more than half of his Democratic Senate colleagues are calling on him to step down. That is serious.\par \par \uc2 Most notable is his fellow New Jersey senator, \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 . They all say these bribery charges are too serious. And don`t forget, it`s his second time facing a federal indictment. So they appear to be less likely to give him the benefit of the doubt this time around.\par \par \uc2 Menendez himself told reporters yesterday, he is not going anywhere because he says he is innocent. He is going to make his first court appearance in New York this morning -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Amazon\uc2  faces a landmark lawsuit this morning accusing the online giant of raising prices on and off its platform.\par \par \uc2 The antitrust action claims that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  abuses its dominant position in the marketplace acting as a monopoly. The company says the lawsuit is wrong on the facts and the law.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent looks at the issues in this case.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: For co-owner, Danny Caine, running the Raven Bookstore in Lawrence, Kansas is a labor of love and a daily battle against Goliath.\par \par \uc2 DANNY CAINE (The Raven bookstore co-owner): They have such a huge monopolizing hold on the book market that nearly every decision in every day is somehow shadowed by \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The online retail giant is now facing a landmark antitrust lawsuit by the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  and 17 state attorneys general accusing \uc2 Amazon\uc2  of operating as a monopoly power.\par \par \uc2 They allege \uc2 Amazon\uc2  stifles competition by charging sellers like small businesses higher fees and discouraging them from selling for less elsewhere, which causes online shoppers to face artificially higher prices, even when shopping somewhere other than \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 John Newman is the deputy director of the FTC`s Bureau of Competition.\par \par \uc2 JOHN NEWMAN (FTC Bureau of Competition deputy director): This case isn`t about \uc2 Amazon\uc2  being big. It`s about what \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is doing right now to tens of millions of American shoppers, and hundreds of thousands of businesses that need \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to reach those shoppers.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: \uc2 Amazon\uc2  says the allegations are wrong. Adding in a statement: "The practices the FTC is challenging have helped to spur competition and innovation across the retail industry and have produced greater selection, lower prices, and faster delivery speeds."\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Jo Ling Kent in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still ahead, the murder of a high tech CEO on the Forbes 30 under 30 lists shocks the city of Baltimore. We`re going to have the latest on the search for a suspect.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, millions of families could lose their child care if Congress doesn`t take action.\par \par \uc2 We will tell you why.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: An arrest warrant issued in Baltimore over the murder of a young tech CEO, police say 26-year-Pava LaPere found dead Monday hours after she was reported missing.\par \par \uc2 Alexus Davila from CBS News Baltimore has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BRANDON SCOTT (D) (Baltimore, Maryland): We have a young woman who had her life taken away and we have someone that we know cowardly took that life that we`re going to find.\par \par \uc2 ALEXUS DAVILA: Police say 26-year-old Pava LaPere was found with signs of blunt force trauma at this luxury apartment building in downtown Baltimore Monday.\par \par \uc2 Thirty-two-year-old Jason Dean Billingsley is wanted for first-degree murder, assault, and reckless endangerment among other charges.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD WORLEY (Baltimore acting police commissioner): This individual will kill and he will rape.\par \par \uc2 ALEXUS DAVILA: Police have not identified a motive in LaPere`s death, but many are in shock, including Baltimore`s mayor, who said he got to know the entrepreneur on a personal level.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BRANDON SCOTT: Pava was a very young, talented, devoted Baltimorean.\par \par \uc2 ALEXUS DAVILA: For the past four years, LaPere dedicated her career to founding and growing her company, EcoMap Technologies, a service devoted to making data and business educational and nonprofit sectors more accessible to communities.\par \par \uc2 Her work landed her a spot on the prestigious Forbes 30 under 30 list this year.\par \par \uc2 PAULO GREGORY (Pava LaPere`s friend and colleague): She is probably one of the most technological wizards that exists.\par \par \uc2 ALEXUS DAVILA: Paulo Gregory, who has worked in the tech space with LaPere for the past five years, says her goal was to lift others up.\par \par \uc2 PAULO GREGORY: Pava is just a powerhouse, one of the most dedicated, hardworking passionate, conscious human beings.\par \par \uc2 ALEXUS DAVILA: for CBS MORNINGS, Alexus Davila, Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, a potential crisis in child care.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Child care for millions of American families could be at risk if Congress doesn`t act soon. Billions of dollars in federal funding for childcare is set to expire this week.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me break this down for you. The cost of child care has increased 220 percent in the last three decades, far outpacing inflation. The average cost a year is now more than $10,000.00 per child. For many Americans, these emergency funds have become essential.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion shows us the potential impact on families and child care providers.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Hey, good morning to you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Some lawmakers and child care advocates warn we are teetering on the edge of a child care cliff and going over could leave millions of children in this country without care.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Ashley Prolter (sp?) has been bringing her two-year-old son, Ahmad to this Maryland day care center since he was eight months old.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PROLTER (Mother): I love the staff. My son, he loves it here. It feels like family.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And for Ashley, it is also affordable.\par \par \uc2 How much would you say you spend on child care a month?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PROLTER: On average, about maybe fifty.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: $50.00? Wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 But that`s with a need-based scholarship, which is provided through the state of Maryland. About 30 percent of the kids at this learning center receive a similar rate --\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH (Greenway Learning Center director): What do you have?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Which partially comes through federal funding included in the American Rescue Plan.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): The motion is adopted.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The law was passed in 2021 during the pandemic providing $24 billion for child care providers, the largest federal investment in child care in US history.\par \par \uc2 But come September 30th, it is due to expire.\par \par \uc2 Does this keep you up at night?\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: It does. The thought of losing one child much less, you know 30 percent of them, it just breaks your heart.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Patti Smith has been the director of this center for nearly 30 years.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: When the funding ends, unfortunately, I don`t think those parents will be able to pay our tuition, so those children will be without care.\par \par \uc2 We may have to close the classrooms or hopefully not the center as a whole.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: \uc2 The Century Foundation\uc2 , a progressive think tank estimates the looming child care cliff could result in the closure of 70,000 childcare programs, and more than 3.2 million children could lose their spot, costing families $9 billion in lost wages.\par \par \uc2 What are the chances we go off this cliff?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKIE SHERRILL (D-NJ): I don`t think we`re seeing really good signs right now.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIMMY GOMEZ (D-CA): I think there is a high likelihood.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: New Jersey congresswoman, Mikie Sherrill and California congressman, Jimmy Gomez, chair of the Dance Caucus have joined several other Democratic lawmakers to co-sponsor the Child Care Stabilization Act.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PATTY MURRAY\uc2  (D-WA): We have a child care crisis in America.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: It would continue funding at $16 billion for the next five years to keep centers open and help workers.\par \par \uc2 How personal is this issue to you?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKIE SHERRILL (D-NJ): It`s incredibly personal to me. There was a time when I was looking for child care, it was costing more than my paycheck to put my children in child care.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIMMY GOMEZ (D-CA): My wife and I, we have a 13-month-old son, we`re lucky because we have the resources. I have a good job that helps pay for it. But it is $2,600.00 a month. That is almost as much as my first mortgage of my house.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Ashley worries about how much more she`ll have to pay for Ahmad with funding set to run out.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PROLTER: Where is he going to go? I probably wouldn`t be able to afford it as a single parent. So without this, my son, he wouldn`t be here. It`s a stressful thing to think about.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: There is a bipartisan task force that is looking at other potential legislative solutions, but there is growing concern about this child care cliff and a potential government shutdown hitting at the same time, which could hurt families and these child care providers, especially hard, it is really like a double whammy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. These are two separate matters that a lot of people aren`t really paying attention to, are they?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, that`s right. I mean, you have some Democratic lawmakers that have proposed additional funding, but right now, you know, Republicans on the Hill don`t want to spend more. So it`s really unclear what the path forward here is.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of these child care centers are worried they could have to shut their doors.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Children need a place to go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Parents need a place to put those kids.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And parents to put -- yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If they want to get any work done.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or they could grocery shopping. I mean, you lose child care, your day is done.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: You bet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, coming up, the Mississippi River running so low, it`s causing trouble for hundreds of miles. Why is it happening and when will the water come back? We`ll ask our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We showed you yesterday how a drought has brought the Mississippi River to historically low levels. It threatens the water supply in and around New Orleans with saltwater contamination, but that`s just one problem for the areas that rely on this very big river.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  joins us now to talk about the potential impact of that.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, good morning. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Gayle, good to see you as well.\par \par \uc2 And I want to talk about how this is not just a Louisiana story. The Mississippi River, the second longest river in North America drains all or parts of 32 states and two Canadian provinces. And if you look at the entire watershed, we had exceptional drought, which is the worst possible level in the Midwest, the Plains, and also into the South.\par \par \uc2 There`s been a real lack of rain this year. Our deficits range from over four inches in Minneapolis to over two feet below average in New Orleans where so far, they are experiencing their second driest year on record.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are 16 river gauges along the Mississippi and on the tributaries that are sitting below average, and the forecast is for many of these to actually go even lower, potentially setting some records.\par \par \uc2 But let`s follow this down into New Orleans. Back in April, the Mighty Mississippi reached its highest level of the year at just over 14 feet, but as the drought worsened over the summer, water levels sharply decreased. And currently, the river sits around just two feet.\par \par \uc2 And it wasn`t just the lack of rain that dried everything up, we also had record setting summertime heat, and the hotter temperatures lead to more evaporation, which then leads to more drought in already parched areas.\par \par \uc2 And sadly, the forecast over the next seven days shows very little rain over areas that actually need it the most, and the long range forecast isn`t much better.\par \par \uc2 If we look at October through December, it does give us a better chance for some rain here in the South, but obviously, we`re going to need a lot more help.\par \par \uc2 For more in -depth coverage on climate news and sustainability, you can watch "Pattern" on \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 And Gayle, we could see more drastic conditions like this across the country and across the world with our changing climate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, what I know, Stephanie is we can always count on you to give it to us straight.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Vlad will be here, he`s got the stories you`re going to be talking about later today, but first, it is, 7:41, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Week of Beyonce. Vlad, yes, how are you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can never had too much Beyonce.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You can never have too much Beyonce.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s do it. Let`s work Saturday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Never Break My Soul." Never break my soul, folks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay. All right, let`s start with the latest on the puppy problem at the White House.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s two-year-old German Shepherd, Commander, bit a Secret Service agent Monday night. The agency said the officer was treated on site by medical personnel and is doing just fine.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is the 11th time Commander, not Major -- remember Major --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Major is already gone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Major was banished.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he has gone to another home.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Other home with the Biden`s. Commander has bit or attacked a Secret Service personnel in the past year, that`s 11 times.\par \par \uc2 First Lady Jill Biden`s communications director said the first family is continuing to help Commander cope with the stressful environment of the White House.\par \par \uc2 A couple of points here --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Like why is Commander still there?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why is Commander still there?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is time for Commander to go to another home.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Remember that the White House is not just the Secret Service, there are hundreds of people that work there in the White House.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that could be dangerous for them. And I have a personal pet peeve, I know it`s a cliche, but I have a pet peeve with owners who don`t train their dogs properly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I can`t stand seeing somebody in the street walking with a dog because --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re throwing the gauntlet down. You`re suggesting Commander --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or your dog barking or chasing another dog.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Chasing or being aggressive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Attacking another dog.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because dogs live to please us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we derive joy from them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If they are unhappy because we`re unhappy --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Vlad --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is because we`re not doing a good job as owners.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When your dog bites somebody 11 times.\par \par \uc2 TONY 