k Rangers said, she did exactly what she was supposed to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly the right thing to do. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Stay calm in that situation, but I love that she is just pulling Santiago close to her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: While the bear is munching around.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Can you imagine how fast her heart was beating in that moment?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pretty hard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. We would all do that though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you called it right, Mama Bear. She`s the real Mama Bear.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mama Bear.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, that was good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You go in to Mama Bear mode when you think your child is in danger.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You have to. That`s an instinct.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We get it. We get it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is an instinct. I get it.\par \par \uc2 All right, Grammy winner Megan Thee Stallion has a powerful message about mental health.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEGAN THEE STALLION (Rapper): I`ve always been told I`ve got to be strong. Thick-skinned, stiff upper lip, tough as nails.\par \par \uc2 But to be everything for everybody, it wears on you. Black don`t crack, they say, but it can. I can, we all can.\par \par \uc2 Y`all it`s okay to not be okay. Reach out to a friend if you see them going through it. No matter who you are, being vulnerable is what makes us whole.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is an incredibly powerful message.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is Megan`s new video for the mental health awareness organization, Seize the Awkward, which was created by the \uc2 Ad Council\uc2 , \uc2 The Jed Foundation\uc2 , and \uc2 American Foundation for Suicide Prevention\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The mission is to inspire young adults to be open about mental health struggles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well done, Megan.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That was powerful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well done. She looks gorgeous and I really liked the message that she is sending.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of people need to hear this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think it is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Especially coming from her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The point that she makes is that it is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: After what she has been through.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People think that you`re strong, but inside, you`re crumbling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s okay to be like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is really good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve got another story here that takes us to an Astros-Mariners game last night. Visiting Astros player hit a foul ball into the stands and then this happened.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: You bet yes. He gets to the seventh, he certainly well with Kirby. Oh, where did that ball come from?\par \par \uc2 MAN: The ball came back from the crowd at the pitcher, Kirby.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If you`re looking as flummoxed as George Kirby, let me explain what happened.\par \par \uc2 He was taken aback after getting hit by a foul ball -- getting hit by a ball thrown from the crowd.\par \par \uc2 He was not hurt. It did not take long to find the person who threw the ball. The crowd pointed him out quick. The young man wearing a Mariners` jersey was escorted out with a Park official giving him a lecture. Can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why did he do that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He thought it was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He thought it was funny?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was a foul ball hit by the Astros and he wanted to throw it back. He didn`t mean to do it. That`s what happened. Tony, you`ve got a story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can`t do that. Yes. We usually do that with a homerun, not a foul ball.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, I`ve got a quick win. Yes, shout out to my friends and family in Baltimore. The \uc2 Orioles\uc2  lost one of the greatest players of all time, team legend, Brooks Robinson.\par \par \uc2 He was one of the best third baseman in baseball history. They call him the Human Vacuum Cleaner because anything hit his direction, he was going to hoover up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a great nickname.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: MVP in 1970, also World Series MVP, led the \uc2 Orioles\uc2  to their second championship, 86 years old. It`s a shame we lose him right now when the \uc2 Orioles\uc2  is having such an amazing season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Amazing year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They might win a third World Championship this year.\par \par \uc2 What I`ll most remember about Brooks Robinson is he had the flip up shades.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, on those on those day games, he would flip them down, make the play every time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So cool.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also throwing out Willie Mays in the World Series in 1970, one of the greatest plays a third baseman has ever made. People should look it up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maybe they finish out the season in his spirit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It will give them a little edge. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We had a lot of time today, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Isn`t it fun when we have all of this time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never too much Vlad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hold on, be quiet, Vlad. I need a little bit more time.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason will be here with an exclusive interview with music great, Paul Rodgers. Stick around. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shawna said, don`t get used to it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Don`t get used to it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS, I`m Gayle King. Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, and first on CBS MORNINGS, Tony winner and Oscar nominee, Leslie Odom, Jr. is back on Broadway for the first time since "Hamilton." He`s ready.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Singer, Paul Rodgers is a legend for his work with the band, Bad Company. Well, he spoke exclusively to Anthony Mason about a health condition that nearly took his voice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. The "Amazing Race" is in its 35th season and we`ve got host, Phil Keoghan to tell us all about it and we`ve also got a little surprise for him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He likes a surprise, we should do, but first here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Breaking news: North Korea says it plans to expel the American soldier who crossed the border back in July.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: The 23-year-old dashed across the border between North and South Korea while visiting the Demilitarized Zone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hollywood writers can return to work today after union leaders approve a deal.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HAYWOOD: A majority of the membership is over the moon.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A judge rules former President Trump and his company vastly exaggerated real estate values for years.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Ultimately, it means whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  padded his books won`t even be an issue at trial. It`s just a matter of the damages.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shrek.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you say you want a fantasy getaway? How about a stay at Shrek`s swamp in the beautiful Scottish Highlands on \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shrek.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does anybody ever want to live like Shrek?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nope.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I would stay the night there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You would?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, why not? It`s a well-known movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, it would be an experience.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It would be an experience.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, kids.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin this hour with a CBS MORNINGS exclusive. I can`t tell you how much we love when that happens.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got one of the most acclaimed voices in rock music.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Can`t Get Enough.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Remember that song? Yes, you do. That`s "Bad Company" frontman, Paul Rodgers singing their hit, "Can`t Get Enough" of your love.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, he is talking about a recent major health scare that nearly took away his ability to sing. Oh no.\par \par \uc2 He and his wife Cynthia tell Anthony, as in Mason, how his new solo album helped him find his way back.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Paul, considering what you`ve been through, this is kind of a miracle.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS (Singer): It kind of is a miracle. Actually, it`s a miracle to me, too.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Paul Rodgers` new solo album, "Midnight Rose" shouldn`t come as a surprise. He`s been making music for more than five decades now.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "All Right Now.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: As frontman for the group, Free and for the band he co- founded --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bad Company.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The singer is often ranked one of rock`s greatest vocalists.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Feel Like Makin` Love.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But four years ago, Rodgers endured a massive health scare that he has kept secret, until now.\par \par \uc2 Was there a point at which you actually couldn`t sing?\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: I couldn`t do anything, to be honest. I couldn`t speak. That was a very strange thing.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`d prepare something in my mind, and I`d say it, but that isn`t what came out, and I go, "What the heck did I just say?"\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How bad was this?\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS (Paul Rodgers` wife): Eleven minor strokes and two major strokes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Paul`s wife, Cynthia Kereluk-Rodgers says the first major stroke was sometime in 2016. The second in October 2019 was the one that sent Rodgers into major surgery.\par \par \uc2 That seemed to be terrifying for you.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: It was terrifying, but I couldn`t be terrified. That`s just taking time away from his healing process.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. You were convinced he was going to heal.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: Yes. He had to. I was just praying. All I wanted to be able to do was walk and talk with him again. That`s all I asked for.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: I never say never, as Cynthia says.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Rodgers` surgeon said he needed an endarterectomy, a procedure to remove plaque, clogging a carotid artery.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: They cut the neck and he said that he was very careful because he knew I was a singer, and that when you cut the neck, it`s very close to the vocal cords.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Did you know going in what the risks were of that surgery?\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: Yes, I did. They told me. They were very clear. You may not come out of this alive. And I said, oh, well, that`s a plus, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 So I was like fingers crossed, you know?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: And when I woke up, my eyes, I thought, oh, I`m still here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: His doctor even had a playlist.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: He said while he was performing the surgery, he played Bad Company, and I thought, okay, like, which track? Please tell me it was "Seagull" or you know, something gentle. You know, tell me it wasn`t "Deal with the Preacher" or you know, something like that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Rodgers` son and daughter visited him after the surgery, but recovery was slow.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: I would bring the guitar out in its case, and I`d put the lid up. And he`d walk by and he`d put the lid down, and this went on for a few weeks.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: I don`t -- I don`t remember at all.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: I know you don`t, love.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You were trying to coax it.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: I thought that music would be his way back to him.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: And it was.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: Actually, it definitely was.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It was six months before he played the guitar again. But gradually, the music came back.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: Each thing was a step forward. Each thing that I did was an achievement. You know, oh, I can do this. I can sing.\par \par \uc2 Yes, that tempo feels really a groove, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: A year-and-a-half ago, Rodgers went into the studio in Vancouver.\par \par \uc2 How did it feel for you to hear him sing again?\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: Oh, no. Oh, sweetie.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: It was -- it was amazing.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: I didn`t -- I didn`t think that would ever happen, and I had heard him singing around the house and it wasn`t what it was. It wasn`t what it once was.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: Now, you tell me.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: But amazing to hear him sing again. Just phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: To have an album to put on a turntable.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK-RODGERS: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Rodgers saw the new vinyl for the first time with us.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: Look at that. Wow.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s on the newly resurrected Sun record label.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: Here comes the sun. And I say it`s all right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Like the label, Paul Rodgers is rising again.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, it really is kind of a miracle, you know what he`s been through. You know, one of the things Cynthia said was part of the treatment that he did was he did hyperbaric oxygen treatments, which he said were incredibly helpful.\par \par \uc2 It was -- she said, it was like watching a wilted flower come back to life, but it`s been a really long road. They didn`t want to tell anybody until he was really back. He said they didn`t want pity. I didn`t want people to be sorry for me, but it has been a remarkable recovery.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of people feel that way when they`re going through something. They don`t want people to know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, he may be the star of the show, but she to me is the secret sauce.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love when she said, "It was terrifying, but I couldn`t be terrified." And just that tender moment when he said, I don`t remember that. She goes, I know you don`t, love. I hear that Anthony, it just melts me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then his reaction to her when she teared up.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: They`ve been through a lot together. She literally slept in the hospital with him for weeks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll bet she did.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: When he was there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It just shows you what support can do for a recovery.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Oh, and he knows it, too. You can see it. He is leaning on her for a lot of things even now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now he`s ready though. He`s ready to come back.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, and sounds terrific. You know, what a voice that man has.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did Cynthia say?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You said it best, it a miracle that he would even walk and talk again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is amazing. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let alone hit the stage.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That relationship is a miracle. I was just thinking.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Anthony, you`ll be back with us tomorrow, you have another big interview.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, you know, I just -- actually, I just came in from Las Vegas at three o`clock this morning because I was with Bono and Edge.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, oh for The Sphere.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The Sphere opens on Friday. We got a little sneak peek. We`ll have that for you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to go.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is it everything that is advertised it to be?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is -- well, it is more, because you can`t even envision how incredible it is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t wait for this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were with them? Oh, wow, Anthony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s incredible. Don`t push Anthony. He`s close to the edge.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that`s right. I see what you did there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bu-bump-shh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done, Tony.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is that a dad joke? I think that it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is a dad joke, indeed. All right, I had that in my back pocket. That`s going to be great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Looking forward to that. Because The Sphere opens on?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Friday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Friday.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Friday night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, Tony winner and "Hamilton" star, Leslie Odom, Jr. will talk with us about his return to Broadway and why this new role is one of the hardest of his careers.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hamilton.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But first, it is 8:09, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, former Second Lady, \uc2 Karen Pence\uc2  is here. There she is. Hey, Karen. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE (Former Second Lady of the United States): Hey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She`s going to talk about her new memoir, you`re going to find about her family`s trip to Washington for four years during the Trump administration. Also, tonight`s Republican presidential debate. I see you`re excited.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: I`m excited. I`m catching a plane when I leave here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, very cool.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s a picture of the White House because the 2024 presidential race is heating up. There`s a debate tonight, and our next guest is giving us an inside look about what it`s like to live in Washington and be in that building very regularly.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about the Second Lady, \uc2 Karen Pence\uc2 . She`s the former wife of Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not the former wife.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, current wife of --\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: We`re still married.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, no, sorry. He`s the former vice president --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Karen was like, what?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re the current wife -- wife of former vice president -- I put the former in the wrong location is what I did there. Okay. So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We know. We know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So we`re clear. All right, the wife of \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , former vice president, current Republican presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was vice president, of course, during the Trump administration, 2017 to 2021 and Mrs. Pence is out with a new book. It`s called "When It`s Your Turn To Serve: Experiencing God`s Grace In His Calling For Your Life." It details her journey as a political spouse, which has been guided by her faith.\par \par \uc2 Karen, thank you very much for being here. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We were just talking in the greenroom about kind of a central mystery that I think a lot of people have in their minds about the ongoing support for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , particularly in the Christian community and in the conservative community.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of that support goes back to you and your husband in 2016 introducing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to that community. Your husband said, this is a good man. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a good man.\par \par \uc2 Do you still feel with all this happened that he`s a good man?\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Well, it`s interesting, because you were in Indiana then.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: You covered that. I remember.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I never judge whether someone is a good or bad person, but I feel like in 2016, and then, once they won the election, they served as conservatives, they governed as conservatives. The Trump-Pence administration was conservative.\par \par \uc2 But I think the president has stepped away from those conservative foundations, and you see it in a lot of the things that he`s been saying recently.\par \par \uc2 And so that was kind of the impetus for Mike saying, I think -- I think I need to run. I think I need to stand for those things that we did in the Trump-Pence administration, because I`m a true conservative, and he`s been a conservative forever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s interesting that you say that because we have some polling data showing that among Republican voters, they like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but you`re right, they don`t see him as a true conservative, and yet they do support him.\par \par \uc2 That is a lot of support among Christians, evangelicals, and I am -- I`m so curious, I know a lot of people are, why does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have so much support among the religious right, conservative Christians, when he himself in his public behavior doesn`t exactly live in accordance with Christ and the gospel?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And your values. He seems to be very opposite to many of the values that you talk about in the book.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: I think it`s going to be interesting to see how the race shapes up, because I think if people start listening to the things that he is saying, they`ll start to kind of question, well, wait a minute, that`s not what you said four or five years ago.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, he is out there. He`s a pretty public guy.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why aren`t people seeing it currently?\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Well, I think that`s our job then. That`s our job tonight at the debate. That`s Mike`s job to get his message out and that`s one of the reasons we`re here today and talking to you guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, your book "When It`s Your Turn To Serve: Experiencing God`s Grace And His Calling For Your Life." You start off by saying, this is not a political book. This is not a memoir. It`s something that you want and you have -- you`re very specific about what you want readers to get out of this book, and what is that?\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: You know, Gayle, I want people to be encouraged. So when the reader reads this and sees some of the fun little behind-the-scenes stories, I hope they`re inspired to serve when they feel that calling.\par \par \uc2 You know, if I had said no to a lot of the things that came my way, I would have missed out on so much. So I tell a lot of stories about the initiatives we had as Second Lady and First Lady of Indiana.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You talked about how you and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  met. He came to you and wanted to be in your guitar group. That sounds like a good pickup line to me.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Yes, I was like, the first thing he said to me was a lie. Hey, what`s that about? But no, if he could have followed it up, though, he, actually, I found out he is a guitar player.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The thing that struck me about the book because of course, many people would want to hear your take on January 6th because that picture of you, Karen, standing in the room and you`re at the curtains.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Closing the curtains.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re closing the curtains.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And your family is in there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. There was only a page-and-a-half on January 6. I`m assuming that was a deliberate choice on your part. Why?\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Well, it was intentional. Mike talks about January 6 extensively in this book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, but that`s not your perspective then.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Right, and my perspective, I`m really glad you asked that, Gayle, because from my perspective, it kind of goes along with the book. At that moment, we didn`t feel fear.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You didn`t feel fear?\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: We did not feel afraid. I felt such a sense of calm and peace. Yes, I shut the curtains because we always are aware of windows, you know, when you`re in a public position.\par \par \uc2 But at that moment, it`s like the whole team have had this sense of purpose. Like, okay, we can`t worry about everything else that`s going on. Right now, we need to get us back in session. We need to finish our job. We need to certify this election.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`d been hustled out of there like your hair was on fire.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: We were and I`ll tell you that was humbling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was terrifying watching from my kitchen table. I`m surprised to hear you say you didn`t feel fear.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: It is humbling. It`s a humbling thing to have someone protecting you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you feel -- it is a humbling thing.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you feel fear now for the country? The direction of the country? The state of the country?\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: I think most Americans do. I mean, I think we`re all kind of looking at a lot of things that are going on in our country right now and people are afraid first of all, for their -- you know, paying their bills, getting a mortgage, the border.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, this is the thing, of course you`re voting for \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . But if \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is not the nominee, do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be back in the White House knowing what you know and what you know about the man and the work that you did together?\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: I don`t think anybody who puts himself above the Constitution should ever be president of the United States. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. The cover of the book has a bee in a flower.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She can draw -- she can paint.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She can paint watercolors.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, your watercolors are -- yes, I`m glad you brought that up, Tony. They`re beautiful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are absolutely gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But this means something, the bee in the flower.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: The bee does mean something because when I was First Lady of Indiana, we started a beehive at the governor`s residence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: And the first thing I asked when I became Second Lady was, do we have bees at the vice president`s residence? So we started a beehive and everywhere we went as Second Lady around the world, we would go visit the beehives.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, well, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was not surprised to learn that you memorize poetry as a kid.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because you have a natural rhythm with language. The book is serene, it`s clear.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think it`ll give a lot of people a sense of calm in an otherwise a tumultuous period here in our politics. So Karen, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PENCE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she rides motorcycles and flies planes, just saying.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "When It`s Your Turn To Serve" available now wherever you get your books.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, we`re going to talk with Phil Keoghan. You know him, he is host of "The Amazing Race" right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  about what they have planned for the -- listen to this -- the 35th season and how the idea for the reality show actually started all with a dare. Always like a good dare.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS, your favorite morning show. Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hamilton.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you have goosebumps? Yes, you do.\par \par \uc2 That is Leslie Odom, Jr.`s Tony Award winning performance as Aaron Burr in "Hamilton."\par \par \uc2 Since then, he has starred in the "Glass Onion: A Knives Out Mystery" and "One Night in Miami," from which he was nominated for not one, but two Oscars, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now he returns to Broadway in a revival of the late Ossie Davis` play "Purlie Victorious." It`s a non-Confederate romp through the cotton patch, you could say.\par \par \uc2 So Leslie Odom plays the charismatic Reverend Purlie Victorious Judson who schemes to take back his family`s inheritance and his church in the segregated south.\par \par \uc2 In this scene, Purlie returns to his family home with his sidekick, Lutiebell.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Purlie Victorious.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Leslie Odom, Jr. is also we`re happy to say the play`s producer, but we`re really happy about this, because he joins us first on CBS MORNINGS and listen to this, guys. It`s opening night and he`s here.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR. ("Purlie Victorious" actor): That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tonight. Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, bravo. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re lucky because it`s been in preview, so we got to see it.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I love how the play opens, right before it even -- before it starts when we`re sitting in our seats, the voice of God, which sounds like Kenny Leon, the director.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: . says, "Sixty-two years of drama turned into outrageous comedy. Lean into it and you`ll get it."\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you want us to get, Leslie? Number one, you were knockout. You are knockout in this play.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Bravo.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: We want you to get all this joy. I`ll tell you. I, as an avid theater goer, I go to see so many Broadway shows and off Broadway shows, and I`ve done a handful of them. This is a very rare piece.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How so?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: It was very written --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number one, you`re stepping in Ossie Davis` shoes. Let`s just start with that for a second.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Yes. You don`t always get to do that, but it is just very rare to have a piece that is this joyful, and this meaningful, that you can have this much fun and still get your veggies. You know, that you could have this much fun and still leave thinking about something, leave having been impacted and not just entertain, but also you know -- yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I was going to say you -- you know, this is the first revival of it. It came out in 1961, but you were aware of it long before. You used to perform portions of it for auditions back in the day, right? So how did it come to you in the first place?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Yes, as a young actor, it`s just one of those pieces that somebody put in my hand like, hey, you know, you should -- if you`re going to be acting and doing monologues here, this show has some monologues in it, and I say, you know, drama, it occupies this really interesting, unique space in literature in that it is undeniably great literature when you think about Shakespeare, Moliere and you know, we read this stuff in eighth grade, wherefore, dost, thou, art, you know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: And so it`s hard to grasp the full meaning of a play when you read it. They`re meant to be seen. They are meant to be experienced.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: So when I read it as a kid, I didn`t understand what a riot display was, you know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: It really wasn`t -- you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very funny.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Yes, as we started to work on it, as we got into the room, as I started to hear my cast mates find their characters, that we discovered the romp.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. The romp, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, speaking of finding your character, I`m always curious about the parallels that a performer draws between them and the character that they`re playing. So what are some things about Purlie Victorious that you identify with?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Oh, wow. I revere him so much, you know, the character. I hope that people would say that. I am as bold. I hope that people would say that I`m as brave when needed to be that I stand for justice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: You know, he`s a good dude. He`s ridiculous. Right? But oftentimes, I mean, he is like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But aren`t we all kind of ridiculous?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: That`s what I need is, you know, when I was thinking about I was, you know, doing some clowning research you know, when you start thinking about this is an outrageous comedy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: But there`s a famous clown that says, you know, you don`t -- you can`t -- you just -- you have -- you admit that you`re a clown, you`re already a clown. You know, you`re not like going in search of a clown. It`s really just about admitting already.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is in you.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is ridiculous, but he is a lot of fun. And I wonder, you know, we opened with a clip from "Hamilton." I wonder where that sits with you? I know Lin came to see the show the other day.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does that mean to you, that role, and that the fact that you two have the connection that you have when you look at this? Lin was there. There`s Lin backstage.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Backstage when he came to see -- where does that sit with you? That`s how most of us got to know you.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Yes. Lin-Manuel -- the show opened the door to all of the wonderful things, you know, that have happened in my life and Lin- Manuel was the first one, you know, to really ask us to believe that we were capable of more than we had historically been asked to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: A room full of Black and Brown people, you know, no one had ever asked us to fly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: And Lin said, I think you can do it. Lin and Tommy. This is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But now, you`re on Broadway yourself in the lead role, Purlie Victorious. You`re a producer. Your wife, Nicolette, your lovely wife who is here, too, also one of the producers.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does that mean to you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And what does she say to you because you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think so, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Speaking about clowning, my wife sometimes think I`m a clown, right?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Yes. You are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, so she doesn`t mean it in a nice way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s not as poetic when she says that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does this mean?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Did your wife give you any notes on the performance?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: You better believe she did. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s an actress, too, we should say.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: She`s a brilliant actress.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There she is.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: She earned that producer credit, believe me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is. Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: I mean, Broadway means something to me. Broadway is the -- you know, it`s the pinnacle of -- you guys could do this show, you know, you could do it on all access. You could do it on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . I mean, this -- it means something to have this incredible studio, right out -- so, we`re right on the same street as you guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: This means something. What Broadway means is it`s the -- it`s the standard of our industry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, you`ve had two Broadways.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Two Broadways.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two epic iconic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: Come on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does it feel like two bites of the apple to you?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: It does. It -- you know, Nic and I, we were toasting a couple of nights ago, just you know, celebrating and I said to her like, it`s just very, very fair in a career that you get quite possibly two bites of the apple like this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: That you could have something that was so successful and wonderful and joyful as "Hamilton" and maybe get it one more time, you know, so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, listen, we are cheering you on opening night. I didn`t know it was opening night, and you`re here.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ODOM, JR.: That`s right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It seems like you should be home getting a little nappy-poo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: First day --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will take it. We will take it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we will take it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re one of the most versatile talents, seriously.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats and Bravo to you, Leslie Odom, Jr.\par \par \uc2 You can watch "Purlie Victorious" a non-Confederate romp through the cotton patch on Broadway at the Music Box Theater right here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, up next, in a moment, "The Amazing Race" is hitting a major milestone. Host, Phil Keoghan is in our progressive greenroom to share what we can expect from Season 35 of the hit CBS series.\par \par \uc2 First, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Amazing Race.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s Phil Keoghan on the very first episode of "The Amazing Race" back in 2001. Now, the hit CBS -- I know, uhm, is right, Phil.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN ("The Amazing Race" host): You heard I did that one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Now the competition show is returning for its 35th season.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The largest ever number of teams, 13, will set off on a nearly 24,000-mile adventure around the world competing for $1 million. That`s the price.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Amazing Race.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, they begin in Los Angeles where contestants have to walk a tightrope between two wings of the famed Biltmore Hotel. Here`s a preview of tonight`s Season 35 premiere.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Amazing Race.")\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Ready to go?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The man himself, Phil Keoghan joins us right now. Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Good morning. I`ve got to say, this place is amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well we did all of this for you.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: I haven`t been here for a while. I mean, you guys really have -- I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We rolled out the red carpet just for you Mr. Keoghan.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. Now --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not as amazing as the race.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Yes, but you know what I was just thinking? Why am I traveling around the world, when all I`ve got to do is just come to this beautiful set, nicely lit and you can put any country behind me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We can turn it --\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: I`m good to go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There you are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anything you want.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: No more jetlag.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. Now, but speaking of the show, you have been hosting "The Amazing Race" for more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What keeps bringing you back? Aside from the money.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Yes. Well, you know, I was saying to Gayle before that I think when you love what you do, you really --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: And you`re passionate about what you do, and I am passionate about people, I am passionate about places around the world. I`ve been lucky enough to travel around the world since I was three. My mum was a music teacher. My dad was a plant scientist. We got to travel extensively throughout South America and around the world with my parents.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: And I saw how they were with people, and you know, being a teacher, you`re a giver and my father working in agriculture, he is a giver.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: And sharing and so, I just fell in love with people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: And I think right now "Amazing Race" is needed more than ever. There`s so much divisiveness in the world.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: And we`re talking a lot about what makes us different. And I think with "Amazing Race," we`re actually showing more about how we are the same.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: We care about our families, we care about putting a roof over our family`s head and we --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And maybe we can work together to accomplish anything.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Yes. There`s lots of stuff that accentuates what`s wrong, and I think with our show, it`s just a little bit of an escape to say, hey, isn`t this a beautiful world, which by the way feels small.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is fascinating though, Phil, the different teams and to see them work together, and what I like is to hear their backstory.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But like the shot we just showed, they`re walking on the tightrope, some people are eating insects and stuff. What happens if you get there and you go, I don`t really want to do that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Amazing Race.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because they don`t know what`s going to happen. They do not know what the challenges are going to be.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: That`s true. But you know, they are highly motivated by a million dollar prize.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So I could eat a cricket for a million dollars, yes.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Gayle, I think you would do a lot of things for a million dollars. So, I think you would go across in your high heels for a million dollars.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: With your beautiful orange microphone. No, I think, look, the thing I always say to all the teams at the start, I say, there`s only one team that`s going to win.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Amazing Race.")\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: And this experience is worth way more than a million dollars. This is an experience that money literally cannot buy. We are going to give you and share my line at the start of "The Amazing Race" is, "The world is waiting for you."\par \par \uc2 And I mean -- I`ve meant it from the start, from Season One, when we started here in Bethesda Fountain in New York City, the world is waiting for you.\par \par \uc2 There`s so many wonderful things out there that we`re going to share with you and the audience.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`re not bored because you say you produce, you direct, you write. That`s part of the fun for you, that you get to do all of those things and all of those challenges.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Well, I think -- I think being able to do more than just host on the show has allowed me to get to be excited about turning up and going to work every day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: The hosting gig is tough, I`m not going to lie. It`s 12 shows in roughly 21 days. But yes, I`m stimulated by working as part -- with a team of really incredible people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Phil, it seems like every season, you give people the world, so we are going to give you something after the commercial break.\par \par \uc2 So you`re going to stick around?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Free cookies and coffee for you.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: I mean, where else am I going?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, good. Good.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: I`m not going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Season 35 of "The Amazing Race" premieres tonight at 9:30 PM Eastern right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you`re not here, we will just put you on the monitor.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we`re back with "Amazing Race" host Phil Keoghan whose show has won 15 Emmy Awards and that means we`ve got a surprise for you, Phil.\par \par \uc2 Chrissy Fernandez with the Guinness World Records is here with a very big announcement.\par \par \uc2 Chrissy, what have you got for us?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Looking very official, Chrissy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Uh-oh. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 CHRISSY FERNANDEZ (Guinness World Records): Today we are here to present you, Phil Keoghan with the Guinness World Records titles for the Most Primetime Emmy Award Wins For An Outstanding Reality Competition Program, and the award for Most Consecutive Primetime Emmy Award Wins For An Outstanding Reality Competition Program.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 CHRISSY FERNANDEZ: You are officially amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are officially amazing. I like that, Chrissy.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Of course, this goes to all the thousands of people who have worked on "The Amazing Race." Thousands of people around the world and of course, Bertram and Elise, who are the executive producers, who came up with this idea in the first place and launched this back in 2001.\par \par \uc2 And it is an honor to receive it from you here today. So -- and thank you for -- thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But just think though --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What a journey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right? I mean, this began as a dare, a friend of yours came up with an initial idea, it developed from there. At that beginning point, did you ever think you`d be standing next to two official Guinness World Record plaques?\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: No. No, I didn`t know. Personally, I didn`t know whether we were actually going to get through the first season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: I remember, like I said, we started in Bethesda Fountain. I was considerably younger than you -- are you all right holding those? I feel terrible. You`ve got to stand --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Can we get a chair?\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: You look strong, but I`m just saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can we get Chrissy a chair?\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Yes, so I was standing there and I remember we -- I said go, and then it was such a build up to go. We also just stood around I`m like, okay, and then we`re like, oh my goodness, we`ve got to get to the airport. Like, they`ve gone to the airport. Like, let`s go everybody. So we took off.\par \par \uc2 But I honestly -- it was such an audacious and outrageous idea that I wasn`t sure like are we going to actually make it around the world and get this thing done?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But just think about that, to go from the Bethesda Fountain to where you are in this season?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And kind of -- just think about that. I mean --\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Well, it`s an amazing -- literally, an amazing journey, and you know, I had missed out on "Survivor." I was up with Jeff Probst for that gig.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, you were.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh really? Wow. That`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Recently fresh in my mind. Yes, it was down to the two of us for that gig and I thought that was -- you know, that would be incredible.\par \par \uc2 And then I was up for some other gigs and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then you get this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Honestly, it is amazing.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Got close with him, and then this happened.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When the when the AI overlords take over, I`m going to just do "Amazing Race" competitions personally my whole life. I`m just going to be -- you`ll do it with me, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. I`ll do it with you. I want to physically do it by the way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I do. I honestly want to do it. Yes. I don`t mean to announce it. I mean, like I want to be on the zipline.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it`s funny how life works out because I would imagine you`re disappointed when you didn`t get "Survivor," and then you get this and this seems so perfect for you, Phil.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: Well, thank you. And you know, I remember I was lucky enough to be on Oprah`s show and it was before "Amazing Race" had launched.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEOGHAN: And I remember Oprah was like, what is this "Amazing Race" thing? I said, just hold on, Oprah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But now, she knows. Now, she knows.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Simply amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Chrissy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And congratulations. Thank you, Chrissy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Phil.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230927ej9r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 26, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Omar Villafranca, Nikole Killion, Jo Ling Kent,  Robert Costa, Jericka Duncan, Scott MacFarlane, Nancy Chen \par \uc2 3,790 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, we`re following a number of breaking stories. A judge finds \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed fraud for years, the former president`s business licenses rescinded in New York.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 A judge rules \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  inflated the value of his assets by billions, defrauding banks and insurers. What we`re learning.\par \par \uc2 The other breaking news in the case of that New York City day care drug ring, the owner`s husband in custody tonight in Sinaloa, Mexico, all related to the fentanyl poisoning of a 1-year-old boy.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Wall Street didn`t build the country. The middle class built the country.\par \par \uc2 (Protesters cheering and applauding)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : And unions built the middle class.\par \par \uc2 (Protesters cheering and applauding)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: President Biden joins the picket line. What impact his support could have on negotiations.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: A sitting president has never walked a picket line before. So it`s historic, just like this strike is historic.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Lives were saved.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: New information about the threats that a man was accused of making against a church in Northern Virginia. Police say the man was armed with a loaded gun and had extra ammunition when he was arrested in Prince William County.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: With just days until the deadline, what a government shutdown could mean for millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Extremely dry conditions along the Mississippi River have dropped freshwater levels and currents to historic lows, threatening water treatment facilities along the river.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: After a string of thefts, Target announces it will close a number of stores. We will tell you where.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: The \uc2 U.S. Federal Trade Commission\uc2  and 17 states are filing an antitrust lawsuit against \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . The suit alleges the company illegally inflates its prices and has -- quote -- "monopolistic control" over its competition.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The New York City Ballet is celebrating its 75th anniversary. How the role of women has changed in one of the most prestigious ballet companies in the world.\par \par \uc2 WENDY WHELAN (Associate Artistic Director, New York City Ballet): I hope to find the balance, so that everybody has found the way to belong to the world of ballet.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 And we begin with the breaking news, a judge just ruling that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , his sons, Don Jr. and Eric, and the entire \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  committed extensive and persistent fraud over a decade. The ruling says the former president inflated the value of his assets by billions of dollars and then lied about his net worth on financial documents to banks and insurance companies.\par \par \uc2 This now leads to the cancellation of the company`s New York business license, which means the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  could lose control over properties like Trump Tower in Midtown Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s decision is a major win for New York Attorney General Letitia James, who is seeking a fine of $250 million and a permanent ban on the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , including Trump himself, from doing business in New York state.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane is going to start us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Scott.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  launched his political career saying he`d mastered the art of the deal. A judge has ruled Trump inflated some of his net worth and some of those deals.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): A judge has found \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  engaged in fraud, overinflating the worth of his real estate holdings and misleading banks and insurers.\par \par \uc2 The ruling comes days before the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  is to stand trial in a civil case alleging years of fraud and misconduct brought by New York Attorney General Letitia James last year.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from September 21, 2022)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES (New York Attorney General): Claiming you have money that you do not have does not amount to the art of the deal. It`s the art of the steal.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In the ruling, the judge found Trump and top executives at the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  lied about his assets in order to secure favorable loans and lower insurance premiums, in one instance, claiming his 10,000-square-foot apartment was roughly 30,000 square feet.\par \par \uc2 The judge summarizing Trump`s approach to bookkeeping: "This is a fantasy world, not the real world."\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit from Attorney General James seeks a quarter-of-a-billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 ATTORNEY GENERAL LETITIA JAMES: And there cannot be different rules for different people in this country or in this state. And former presidents are no different.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: When Trump was deposed in the case in August of last year, he pleaded the Fifth, and then repeated it more than 400 times.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from August 10, 2022)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Same answer.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Trump has repeatedly denied wrongdoing and lashed out at James for bringing the suit.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from September 23, 2022)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: This raging maniac campaigned for office ranting and raving about her goal. Her only goal is we got to get \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We`re going to get him.\par \par \uc2 She knew nothing about me. I never heard of her.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In a statement to CBS News, a Trump spokesperson calls the ruling fundamentally flawed.\par \par \uc2 The judge has also ordered sanctions of some of Trump`s attorneys, Norah, saying some of their arguments were -- quote -- "frivolous."\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to the breaking news with the capture tonight of a fugitive wanted in the fentanyl-related death of a 1-year-old at a New York City day care center.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jericka Duncan reports, the suspect was taken into custody in Mexico by DEA agents.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN (voiceover): The husband of the Bronx day care owner was taken into custody on a bus in Sinaloa, Mexico, after a manhunt that lasted more than a week.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say Felix Herrera-Garcia was seen leaving the day care on surveillance video carrying two shopping bags after being tipped off by his wife, Grei Mendez, that police were on the way. Authorities say she called Garcia before calling 911 to report four of the children in her care were unresponsive.\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS\uc2  (U.S. Attorney, Southern District of New York): All of that happened while the children, the babies were suffering from the effects of fentanyl poisoning and in desperate need of help.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The funeral for 1-year-old Nicholas Dominici was held last week. He and three other children ages 8 months to 2 years old were poisoned by the deadly drug after coming into contact with its residue at the day care center on September 15.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D-Mayor of New York City): We had fentanyl in a day care center.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Police later revealed that bags of suspected drugs were found hidden under a secret trapdoor under the floorboards of the day care center where the children played and a kilo of fentanyl was found sitting right on top of children`s mats used for napping.\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS\uc2 : They were running a drug operation from a day care center -- a day care center, a place where children should be kept safe, not surrounded by a drug that could kill them in an instant.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: As horrifying as this story is, it could have been a lot worse.\par \par \uc2 A source tells CBS New York that those three children who were hospitalized thankfully have recovered -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Oh, that is some good news.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, the list of Democrats calling on Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  to resign is growing, fellow New Jersey Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  now among the more than 20 Democratic senators saying Menendez needs to step aside.\par \par \uc2 Menendez is back on Capitol Hill today, but is set to appear in court tomorrow on three counts of federal bribery charges. This afternoon, Menendez once again rejected calls to resign, claiming he is innocent.\par \par \uc2 Now to President Biden`s historic trip to Michigan to walk the picket line with \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  in their strike against Detroit`s Big Three automakers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa reports, the president stood in solidarity with union members and told them to stick with it on day 12 of the walkout.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): Wearing a union ball cap and armed with a bullhorn, President Biden told the striking \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  to keep fighting for higher wages.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Unions built the middle class.\par \par \uc2 Protesters: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : So let`s keep going. You deserve what you have earned, and you have earned a hell of a lot more than you`re getting paid now.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: For President Biden, who exchanged fist bumps with the workers, the historic visit to the picket line was personal and political, underscoring a long career backing labor.\par \par \uc2 TIM DRISCOLL (Union Member): To have the president of the United States walk the picket line in support of workers, that`s huge.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The president is trying to shore up his support among working voters as he seeks reelection and to reclaim a state he won by about 3 percent in 2020, after Trump`s 2016 victory in the battleground.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has yet to endorse Mr. Biden, but union president Shawn Fain cheered on his visit today.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (President, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ): Our president chose to stand up with workers in our fight for economic and social justice.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Biden`s stop came just one day ahead of former President Trump`s visit to Michigan on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 With the strike growing to 41 plants and facilities in 21 states, the back- to-back visits are putting workers` rights and economic pain at the forefront of the 2024 race.\par \par \uc2 Is the labor vote up for grabs?\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: I would say you have to earn it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Trump is skipping Wednesday`s primary debate in California and will make a prime-time address to workers at a non-union facility outside Detroit. It`s unclear if he will visit a picket line.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s coming Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Yes, he can go over to Macomb County. That`s redneck country.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You don`t want him here?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: No.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: I`m glad he`s coming. It`s going to show that he`s supporting the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and our cause.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Advisers to former President Trump and President Biden tell me that these dueling appearances could be a preview of what`s to come in the 2024 campaign, many visits to Michigan and a battle for the support of working voters -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert Costa, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Police in Virginia say they stopped a potential mass shooting at a church where 1,000 people were attending Sunday service. Investigators say a 35- year-old man was arrested at the Park Valley Church after someone flagged the threats he posted on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Police say he was sitting in the back row with a loaded handgun, an additional magazine and two knives. The Fairfax County police chief says a kill manifesto was found in his home.\par \par \uc2 Now to the sweeping antitrust case against \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , alleging it operates as an illegal monopoly. A federal lawsuit filed today accuses the e-commerce giant of strong-arming small businesses and harming consumers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent has the details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  is taking major legal action against \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , calling it a monopoly power.\par \par \uc2 The agency, along with 17 state attorneys general, filed a landmark lawsuit in Seattle federal court today, alleging the online shopping giant exploits its monopolies in ways that enrich \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , but harm its customers.\par \par \uc2 The FTC claims that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  stifles competition by charging sellers like small businesses higher fees, which causes online shoppers to face artificially higher prices, even when shopping somewhere other than \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . The government also accuses the platform of replacing search results on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  with paid ads, putting its own brands over higher-quality competitors.\par \par \uc2 Amazon`s top lawyer responded, saying: "The practices the FTC is challenging have helped to spur competition and innovation across the retail industry and have produced greater selection, lower prices and faster delivery speeds. The lawsuit filed by the FTC today is wrong on the facts and the law."\par \par \uc2 If the FTC wins against \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , how will things change for shoppers?\par \par \uc2 JOHN NEWMAN (Deputy Director, Bureau of Competition, \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 ): You`re going to see lower prices, not just lower prices, but you`re actually going to have choices. You might see a lower price on one site than on a different site, instead of right now where you see just, you know, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  acting as this dominant monopoly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: When I asked, the FTC declined to say whether it wants to break up \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , but it is seeking structural relief, which could mean the company selling off some of its parts.\par \par \uc2 But, for now, there is no court date set. And \uc2 Amazon\uc2  says it is ready to make its case in court -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Fascinating story.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, members of Congress are scrambling to avoid a government shutdown. It appears to be an uphill battle in the House, with hard right Republicans seeking to slash spending at all costs.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion reports from Capitol Hill, where time is running out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Can you guarantee that there will not be a shutdown at the end of this week?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): Look, I have worked all my time to make sure that there could -- would not be a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): House Speaker Kevin McCarthy is urging members to back a package of long-term spending bills to fund agencies from the Defense Department to Homeland Security, but with a caveat to include legislation that beefs up border security, a demand pushed by some conservatives.\par \par \uc2 Why are you making this such a sticking point to keeping the government open?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY: Because it`s so -- because it`s so easy, because the House and Senate didn`t pass a new bill. If the president wants to shut the government down because he thinks the border should continue to run like this --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But the legislation is unlikely to pass the Senate and avert a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from White House video)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): There`s no reason for us to be in this position.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: In a new video, President Biden warned of the potential consequences, including service members who could go without pay. About 800,000 federal workers could also be furloughed.\par \par \uc2 Jessica LaPointe is a single mom who works for the \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LAPOINTE (Federal Employee): We`re just waiting and almost feeling abandoned by Congress at this point, as we`re still expected to work for the American people without getting paid.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Tonight, the Senate unveiled a new bipartisan plan to fund the government through mid-November. It includes disaster relief and aid to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But that could be a nonstarter for some House Republicans, who oppose more Ukraine funding.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion on the Hill tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the state of Louisiana is facing a potential drinking water crisis. Persistent drought conditions have lowered levels of the Mississippi River, allowing saltwater to creep in from the Gulf of Mexico, where it threatens to contaminate the freshwater.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca is in New Orleans, where the city has declared an emergency.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HEATH JONES (\uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2 ): This is pretty significant.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Heath Jones is with the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2 . They`re in a race to fix a looming threat brewing underwater.\par \par \uc2 Extremely dry conditions over the past few months have led to historic low levels on the Mississippi River, allowing dense saltwater from the Gulf to creep its way upriver, which could affect the ability to treat and supply drinking water in New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 In July, these boats built a sill, or an underwater levee, to try and slow the saltwater. But, last week, the sill was overtopped. Now engineers are working to increase its height by 30 feet.\par \par \uc2 We`re about 20 miles south of New Orleans in Plaquemines Parish, and this is where the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  is adding to their sill. They`re taking silt from the bottom of the Mississippi River and then stacking it over here to form a higher barrier to try to slow down the saltwater.\par \par \uc2 If you get this barrier higher up, how much time do you think that buys you?\par \par \uc2 HEATH JONES: Ten to 15 days.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: So far, the dense saltwater has moved about 70 miles upriver. It`s predicted to hit Belle Chasse in Southern New Orleans by the middle of next month.\par \par \uc2 Solutions include using barges to ship millions of gallons of water to treatment plants, or tapping into other water resources upstream.\par \par \uc2 Donald Link co-owns several restaurants in the Big Easy. He`s already inquiring about water tanks.\par \par \uc2 DONALD LINK (Business Owner): We`re not just going to sit here and hope. What is it? Hope is not a plan. We`re going to get ready for it, and hope that it never happens.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The underwater battle is having ripple effects on boat traffic.\par \par \uc2 Parts of the Mississippi River behind me will go down to one lane for big boats, and that`s going to slow down the shipment of important goods like grains and soybean. But it`s only temporary -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Omar Villafranca, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And there`s breaking news tonight. Police reveal a possible suspect in the murder of a young tech CEO in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news tonight: Police in Baltimore just revealed a photo the man they are looking for in connection with the death of a 26- year-old tech CEO.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say the body of Pava LaPere was found in her apartment on Monday, when she appeared to have suffered blunt-force trauma. Earlier this year, she made the prestigious "Forbes" 30 Under 30 list for social impact. Late today, police issued an arrest warrant for this man, Jason Billingsley. He was convicted of attempted rape in 2011 and is considered armed and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Target announced today that it`s closing nine stores in major cities next month because of theft and organized retail crime. The company says the wave of shoplifting is threatening the safety of its workers and shoppers. Stores are closing in New York City, Seattle, San Francisco`s Bay Area, and Portland, Oregon.\par \par \uc2 There is news tonight about a historic Academy Award that went missing for decades. Well, that is straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There is big Oscar news tonight.\par \par \uc2 Hattie McDaniel`s long-lost Academy Award is finally being replaced this weekend in a special ceremony.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Hattie McDaniel, 1940)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: McDaniel was the first African-American to win an Oscar for her supporting role in the 1939`s Gone With the Wind.\par \par \uc2 When she died in 1952, her historic award was donated to \uc2 Howard University\uc2 , where it was displayed, until it disappeared in the late 1960s. This Sunday, \uc2 Howard University\uc2  will receive an official replacement Oscar in a ceremony called Hattie`s Come Home.\par \par \uc2 The New York City Ballet is still on its toes after 75 years and counting. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: If you can`t watch the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, you can listen, subscribe wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight, one of the best-known dance companies in the world is taking a bow, with the celebration of a major milestone.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Chen shows us what is old is new again at the New York City Ballet.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip courtesy of New York City Ballet)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN (voiceover): A joyful moment on stage, 75 years of excellence, included dancers from the very start.\par \par \uc2 RUTH LAWRENCE DOERING (Former Ballet Dancer): Look how much the seats were. Three dollars.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Ruth Lawrence Doering was 16 when she starred in the New York City Ballet`s first performance in 1948, and her shoe broke mid-show.\par \par \uc2 RUTH LAWRENCE DOERING: The show must go on. I mean, what, I`m going to say excuse me, please?\par \par \uc2 I have to go offstage and fix my shoe?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RUTH LAWRENCE DOERING: No, you do what you have to do.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: What was the audience reaction on opening night?\par \par \uc2 RUTH LAWRENCE DOERING: The applause was thunderous.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The performance was born out of the creative genius of renowned choreographer and company co-founder \uc2 George Balanchine\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WENDY WHELAN (Associate Artistic Director, New York City Ballet): And then just get the arm moving.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The ballet`s first female associate assistant director, Wendy Whelan, a former principal dancer, now helps decide what is performed.\par \par \uc2 WENDY WHELAN: Balanchine would commission female choreographers always, a little bit. Now we have a full-on commitment to it, but especially to diverse women.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WENDY WHELAN: That`s very, very important to me.\par \par \uc2 (Clip courtesy of New York City Ballet)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The company today is one of the world`s most prestigious.\par \par \uc2 RUTH LAWRENCE DOERING: Nobody knew it was going to be this great company. It`s -- it`s wonderful.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: A celebration 75 years in the making.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230927ej9q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 President Biden Joining UAW Picketers; Government Shutdown Standstill; Sen. Menendez Fights Back; Writers` Strike Set to End; More Daycare Fentanyl Charges; Turbulence Hospitalizes Passengers on Plane; Ukraine Strikes Russian Naval Base; Maui Residents Head Home; Remembering David McCallum; Winter COVID Surge; Drinking Water Crisis; School Building Gap; Double Chicken Please; The Color of Dance; Jeff Probst Live; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Nancy Cordes, Scott  MacFarlane, Elise Preston, Ramy Inocencio, Jonathan Vigliotti, Celine  Gounder, Omar Villafranca, Stephen Stock, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 14,811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 President Biden heads to Michigan today to show solidarity with striking auto workers, even as a possible government shutdown looms.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The \uc2 UAW\uc2  gave up an incredible amount back when the automobile industry was going under.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  rejects calls to resign amid charges he accepted bribes.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): These charges are formidable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maui residents finally return to Lahaina after last month`s devastating wildfires.\par \par \uc2 TAWNI KATAYAMA (Lahaina resident): A lot of us are sad, and a lot of us like myself, you know, we`re angry.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That oil geyser in Oklahoma City officials say, a company digging in the area hit a pipeline causing the crude to spew into the air.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In Canada, there is outrage after a Nazi veteran was invited to Parliament to see Ukraine`s president. The man was even given a standing ovation.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU (Canadian prime minister): Deeply embarrassing for the Parliament of Canada and by extension to all Canadians.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 Padres\uc2  facing the Giants.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Rapper, Flavor Flav thrown out the first pitch. Hey, that`s pretty good.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What a pitch.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Right. Straight down the middle.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: With the necklace situation as well. I feel like that`s even more impressive.\par \par \uc2 FLAVOR FLAV (Rapper): Yes. Now, that was a strike, too. I`ll let y`all know, yo, I might end up on the squad, on the top 10.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People are looking at Flavor Flav`s throw. I`m looking at those ladies with those dresses. They had on like three dresses that were saying, what are those?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It looked like he had a pretty good throw though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, not bad. It`s not easy to throw when you`ve got all that chain around your neck. But --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He floated it in there. It was a strike.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was a strike, and that`s all that matters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s all that matters.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got some important stories to share with you this morning including a possible water crisis that`s developing right now in New Orleans. How our changing climate, they say could put the entire city in danger of having no water to drink.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also, CBS News and \uc2 CBS\uc2  stations have an eye-opening investigation into our nation`s crumbling schools. Those buildings are in poor, more minority areas. They are quite literally falling apart, and the situation is even worse than you may think. We`ll show you why.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, "Survivor" host and executive producer, Jeff Probst is here with a preview of the new season that you will only see on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . He`s been doing this for 23 years and he has got a lot of great stories to share.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, but we`re going to begin with President Biden planning to make a major political statement today by walking a picket line, an unusual presidential behavior.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden will be in Michigan with striking auto workers one day before former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  plans his own visit, though not on a picket line we hear. This comes as House Speaker Kevin McCarthy struggles to overcome his GOP critics in Congress and prevent a government shutdown, which would come this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House where a lot is going on. Nancy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 This move today by the president is unusual no question, but it is not entirely surprising for a man who likes to call himself the most pro-union president ever. It will also give him a high profile opportunity to highlight his economic platform and achievements, which is something that polls show he needs to do.\par \par \uc2 So today, President Biden is going to fly to Detroit to stand with picketing \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers from Wayne County.\par \par \uc2 A reminder, among other things, they are seeking a 36 percent raise over four years, and they want to extend union protections to workers at 10 electric vehicle battery factories.\par \par \uc2 Now as the strike expands, we`re already starting to see some economic impacts. Ford announced on Monday that it would pause work on a $3.5 billion electric vehicle plant in Michigan, while some automotive suppliers in the state are already warning that layoffs may soon be necessary.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden`s trip today comes one day before former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is slated to skip the second GOP debate tomorrow to speak to auto workers in Michigan himself.\par \par \uc2 Let`s not forget Michigan is a swing state and both of these men are clearly already in general election mode. Trump weighed in on Sunday on the looming government shutdown. He said conservatives should shut it down if they don`t get everything they want. The White House called that position shameful.\par \par \uc2 That position also puts Trump at odds with House Speaker Kevin McCarthy who is still trying to coax this small group of GOP hardliners to accept a short-term fix that would keep the lights on in the government and keep federal workers from being furloughed.\par \par \uc2 Government funding runs out on Saturday, Gayle, at midnight.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all hoping they work something out. Thank you, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  is vowing to put up a fight and resist calls for his resignation. In his first public comments since being indicted on sweeping federal corruption charges, the New Jersey Democrat portrayed himself as the victim of a conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning to you. He seemed to really double down yesterday.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 And he faces quite a week ahead. He makes his first court appearance tomorrow in New York and he has vowed to return here to Washington to work. All these calls for him to resign are increasing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ (D-NJ): I recognize this will be the biggest fight yet.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A defiant Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  showed no signs of backing down Monday.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ (D-NJ): I firmly believe that when all the facts are presented, not only will I be exonerated, but I still will be the New Jersey`s senior senator.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In an indictment filed last week, prosecutors allege Menendez used his influence on the Foreign Relations Committee to secretly aid the government of Egypt and try to thwart the criminal prosecution of a friend in exchange for bribes including a Mercedes convertible, bars of gold, and more than $500,000.00 in cash, some stuffed in congressional jackets bearing his name.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the senator didn`t answer questions, but said he`s long hoarded cash.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ (D-NJ): For 30 years, I have withdrawn thousands of dollars in cash from my personal savings account, which I have kept for emergencies, and because of the history of my family facing confiscation in Cuba.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: CBS News legal analyst, Rikki Klieman.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: It`s always risky for a defendant to say something, anything that could ultimately be used against him or her. In this case, the risk however, for Senator Menendez seems to him to be greater about keeping his own seat.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Congressman Andy Kim announced he`ll run against Menendez for his Senate seat next year.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY KIM (D-NJ): He is not someone that is able to represent New Jersey at this moment, and I believe that New Jersey deserves better.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Menendez, his wife, Nadine and three New Jersey businessmen named in the indictment all face significant prison time if convicted. A trial is highly unlikely this year, but it could run right up against the November 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 Nate, Senator Menendez is up, his seat is up in that election.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we will stay tuned till then.\par \par \uc2 Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Hollywood, leaders of the Writers Guild of America are expected to approve a new contract today after months on a strike. The New Deal is still -- well, it is needed to be ratified by the union`s members, but the strike could officially end before that happens.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston has the latest in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Elise, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Nate, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of optimism among writers here. Picketing has stopped and the WGA is considering officially lifting the strike to allow writers to get back to work within the next 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 Today, the WGA board and council will vote to move the deal forward, then over the next few weeks, writers will decide whether to officially accept the new terms.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "What do we want? A fair contract. When do we want it? Now.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: After nearly five months on the picket line, writers like Alex Zaragoza are greeting the news of a new contract agreement with more than just relief.\par \par \uc2 ALEX ZARAGOZA (WGA member): I immediately just had that like sobbing and just feeling so happy.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Union members are still only getting snippets of their new contract terms, which if approved, would be locked in for three years.\par \par \uc2 ALEX ZARAGOZA: Whatever it is, I trust that it is going to be a good deal because we`ve all been there fighting.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HANDEL (Entertainment attorney): It appears that the WGA got a lot and got more than expected.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Entertainment attorney, Jonathan Handel.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HANDEL: On basic wage increases, the writers were asking for six percent. The studios were offering five. They presumably compromised somewhere in that range.\par \par \uc2 On AI, the Guild got protections, we are learning.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Even though studios own the rights to scripts purchased, writers don`t want their work to be used to train AI programs to replicate their work, which could potentially put them out of a job in the near future.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Shut it down.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Producers are now expected to meet with representatives of striking actors as soon as next week and the pair of contracts could shape entertainment labor disputes for years to come.\par \par \uc2 ALEX ZARAGOZA: This has always felt much bigger than us.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: What are your thoughts moving forward?\par \par \uc2 ALEX ZARAGOZA: Anytime you get to the level, to the point in which you have to strike you`re doing it for necessary reasons.\par \par \uc2 We have been living in a world that`s become increasingly exploitative of workers and we are going to continue to fight for each other.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, it will still take some time for Hollywood to get back up to speed. When it comes to new theatrical releases, movie goers should expect a lull at the box office at the start of 2024 that could run into early spring -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Elise, got it. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Back here in New York, a third person has now been charged in that deadly fentanyl poisoning of an infant at a daycare. Federal prosecutors allege that Renny Paredes helped sell fentanyl that was packaged and stored there and investigators say they found the drug under a trap door and also on the children`s sleeping mats.\par \par \uc2 A one-year-old boy died and three other children were hospitalized after being exposed. The daycare operator and attendant already face murder and drug charges, though they`ve claimed they didn`t know about the drugs.\par \par \uc2 Investigators also released new images of another suspect, this is the daycare operator`s husband who prosecutors or investigators say, these pictures show him fleeing the facility, possibly with evidence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s an awful story.\par \par \uc2 Severe turbulence on a \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  flight sent eight people including a crew member to the hospital yesterday. Now, this flight was heading from Ecuador to Fort Lauderdale when it hit turbulence. The plane is now being inspected and the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is investigating.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says there were 163 serious injuries from turbulence reported from 2009 to 2022, seventeen of those were just last year. Serious injuries include these: Broken bones, severe bleeding, and second or third degree burns.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning to Ukraine, the country`s Special Forces have claimed one of their biggest successes of the war so far. A missile strike Friday severely damaged a Russian naval headquarter in the Crimean peninsula, formerly part of Ukraine, and Ukraine says the admiral in charge of Russia`s Black Sea Fleet was killed.\par \par \uc2 But this morning, there are questions about the accuracy of that claim. Ramy Inocencio has more from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Ramy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Nate, yes, good morning.\par \par \uc2 And in just the past hour, we`ve learned that Russian state media, Russian state television have broadcast, Viktor Sokolov, the top commander, the top admiral of Russia`s current Black Fleet, apparently listening to a state briefing by the Ministry of Defense.\par \par \uc2 Now, this goes against what`s happened according to Ukraine`s Special Forces, saying that they actually killed him. The video that we`re talking about was posted in the Telegram channel of \uc2 Russia`s Ministry of Defense\uc2 , and appeared only briefly. It is important to note, it is unclear, though for now when that image of Sokolov was shot, but if he is indeed alive and well, then the information and disinformation war work between Ukraine and Russia is clear and is still very much happening.\par \par \uc2 Now, this also contradicts Ukraine`s Special Forces claiming he was killed in a strike last Friday. The headquarters of Russia`s Navy in Sevastopol in the annexed region of Crimea, Ukraine struck that building using British Storm Shadow missiles and apparently tried to hit a meeting of Russian naval officers that was billed as a significant strike on Crimea.\par \par \uc2 But Gayle, we now know that Ukraine`s claimed success is actually not one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Ramy Inocencio reporting from Ukraine, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to Hawaii where some residents of Maui took a very big step in their healing journey. Yesterday, a small number of Lahaina residents were allowed to return to see what`s left of their homes. Now, this comes seven weeks after those devastating wildfires.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti was there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Car after car entered what`s left of this Lahaina neighborhood, each resident returning home for the first time in weeks to no home at all, just foundations of ash.\par \par \uc2 NOREEN WALES (Lahaina resident): It is pretty bad. After so many years living here.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Like many, Noreen Wales and her granddaughter, Tawni were completely overwhelmed when they first saw the destruction.\par \par \uc2 TAWNI KATAYAMA: It`s unrecognizable. It`s hard to process.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: For many, this is a chance to come to terms with what happened.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s going to break our family apart.\par \par \uc2 I just can`t believe it`s gone. It`s heartbreaking, you know? All our memories were here.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Officials say it will take years to rebuild and there are concerns about who will direct the recovery effort.\par \par \uc2 Darryl Oliveira stepped in as interim administrator of Maui Emergency Management Agency after Herman Andaya resigned in August, but Oliveira tells me, he will be leaving the role in November.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned at all about who will take over after you leave and whether or not it will be as streamlined as it has been in recent weeks?\par \par \uc2 DARRYL OLIVEIRA (Maui Emergency Management Agency interim administrator): Well, I think that`s one of the reasons why we want to start a recruitment as soon as possible to allow for a nice transition with whoever might be coming on board.\par \par \uc2 I think as long as we provide for transition, it should be as smooth and very minimal hiccups or anything for the community.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: But many residents feel they need more from officials to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 TAWNI KATAYAMA: There`s not enough support for the people that live here. You know, for the Hawaiians that are here, and I get it. There`s a balance there. We survive on tourism. But we should be the priority. You know, we live here. We`ve been here.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: For CBS MORNINGS, Jonathan Vigliotti, Maui.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Today, all of us here at \uc2 CBS\uc2  are mourning the loss of the beloved Scottish actor, David McCallum who died yesterday here in New York.\par \par \uc2 For 20 seasons, McCallum starred as Dr. Donald Mallard, "Ducky" as he was known on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  show "NCIS."\par \par \uc2 (Clip form "NCIS.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And before that, he became something of a teen idol for his role as a mysterious Russian spy on "The Man from U.N.C.L.E."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Man from U.N.C.L.E.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Which ran for four seasons back in the 1960s.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Great Escape.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He also had memorable parts and films like "The Great Escape."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Greatest Story Ever Told.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And as Judas in "The Greatest Story Ever Told," and on top of it all, he was an accomplished musician.\par \par \uc2 David McCallum was 90 years old.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that`s a nice long life. I remember I was a little kid for "Man from U.N.C.L.E." It was my dad`s favorite show. I was not allowed to watch it because it was not children appropriate, which made me want to see it even more. But that`s how I remember him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Yes, a legacy left behind.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, why a drought hundreds of miles away from New Orleans is threatening to deprive the city of its drinking water.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, a state where city schools need desperate repairs, but most of the money to fix the buildings goes to the wealthy districts. We`ll show you what our investigation found on that.\par \par \uc2 And a thrill ride turns passengers literally upside down for nearly half an hour. Yikes.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS, your favorite morning show.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Getting later into the fall here, we might be looking at another COVID surge in the not so distant future.\par \par \uc2 The federal government is once again offering free home test kits to Americans, and for more on this, we are joined by CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder, who is also editor-at-large for public health at KFF.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 So free COVID tests once again. Why now?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So as of yesterday, COVIDTest.gov is up and running again. The government wants to make sure everybody, every household has tests on hand ahead of the holidays, because you have people of different generations who are coming together, close together over the holidays, so it`s a risk for grandma, grandpa. We just want to make sure that people know if they have COVID, it might be infectious to others.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of us have those tests stockpiled and some of them have expired. Does that still work? Sometimes with food, sometimes it`s medication even though it`s expired, you can still eat it or it is still effective.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Right. So the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has extended expiration dates on the tests. There`s actually information about this on the CBSMORNINGS.com website. You can look up your specific brand and see if yours is still valid.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so some people are having trouble with the updated COVID-19 vaccine being approved by their insurance, but what`s going on there?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So you`ve had a transition from the \uc2 US government\uc2  providing the COVID vaccine to now business as usual, which means the private sector.\par \par \uc2 And so, there have been a number of glitches with insurance billing codes, shipping of vaccines, which has led to a lot of problems. If you have private insurance, whether it`s private, Medicare or Medicaid, you should have your free vaccine, no cost to you. It`s covered by insurance since September 11th, but because of these glitches, there have been some issues.\par \par \uc2 You may want to wait until early to mid-October just for these things to get ironed out. If you do get your vaccine now, you may need to resubmit or appeal a denial, but you should get it for free.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I heard you say that I know we can look it up. But if we don`t feel like looking it up, is there a certain time, if it`s two weeks late, that the expired tests were a month late? Two months late? Do you happen to know?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, I mean, two weeks, a month later is not an issue. But you really don`t want to be using it way beyond the extended expiration dates.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. But that milk and that little bag of chips, they are still good. Go for it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is not going to hurt you. Not going to hurt you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I believe in healthy/\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Go for it. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dr. Celine Gounder, thank you very much for being here. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 For more tips on how to get the updated COVID vaccine for free and on information on COVID test expiration dates, go to our CBSMORNINGS.com website, CBSMORNINGS.com.\par \par \uc2 Up next, in just a moment, a potential water crisis in New Orleans. We`ll be back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s 7:30.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana faces of potential drinking water crisis. Millions of people there get their fresh water from the Mississippi River, but the summer drought sharply lowered the river`s water level and now that threatens to allow saltwater from the Gulf of Mexico to contaminate the drinking water supply. Not good.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca is in New Orleans where the threat is the highest.\par \par \uc2 Omar, good morning to you. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 You may be wondering why salt water contamination is such a big deal down here. Here`s the short answer.\par \par \uc2 Basically, the local facilities can`t treat water with a high salt content. It`s corrosive to the pipes and then metal starts leaching into the water and then that makes the drinking water bad.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  has been working since July to prevent that from happening.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: In the southern most stretch of the Mississippi River, trouble is brewing.\par \par \uc2 Is it panic mode yet? Or is it just preparation time?\par \par \uc2 RICKY BOYETT (\uc2 US Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  spokesperson): We`re in the preparedness and awareness, but we want to make sure everybody knows what we`re facing.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: What they`re facing is something called a saltwater wedge. Extremely dry conditions along the Mississippi River have dropped freshwater levels and currents to historic lows, threatening water treatment facilities along the river.\par \par \uc2 RICKY BOYETT: The only thing that will fix the saltwater intrusion problem is rain. We need to get more water in the river.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  says the saltwater has so far moved about 70 miles up the river from the Gulf of Mexico and is predicted to hit Belle Chasse in southern New Orleans by the middle of next month.\par \par \uc2 How is the saltwater able to push through the freshwater?\par \par \uc2 RICKY BOYETT: So the saltwater is very dense and it crawls along the bottom of the river, so when there`s not enough flow from the Mississippi River, the Mississippi River will go over it. It`ll go on the top and the saltwater will continue to climb along the bottom of the river.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: In July the Corps constructed an underwater sill or levee meant to block the flow of saltwater, but the sill was overtopped last week.\par \par \uc2 Engineers are working to increase its height by 25 feet, but that is not expected to hold back to saltwater for long.\par \par \uc2 In New Orleans, bottled water has turned into a hot commodity with some store shelves nearly empty.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I have been out since early this morning, nothing on the store shelves, don`t know when they`re going to get anything in.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Further south in Plaquemines Parish, there have been drinking water advisories in place since June.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOHN BEL EDWARDS (D-LA): There is not one thing that`s going to solve this challenge for us, it`s going to be a combination of lots of different efforts.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: That could include transporting tens of millions of gallons of fresh water daily to nearby water treatment plants to dilute the salty water.\par \par \uc2 How basic is water back in the kitchen?\par \par \uc2 DONALD LINK (Restauranteur): I would say, it is important in the kitchen - - stocks, soups. Dishwashing, I mean we use a lot of water.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: But Donald Link, who co-owns seven restaurants in New Orleans, including Cochon isn`t panicking.\par \par \uc2 He says after numerous devastating hurricanes, the BP oil spill, and COVID, people here are used to adversity.\par \par \uc2 DONALD LINK: We just go right into planning mode.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: So what is your planning mode right now?\par \par \uc2 DONALD LINK: Oh, planning mode right now is that, you know, I`ve had the conversations and it seems to me like we have time. So we start looking at sourcing if we need to buy bottled water, I`m hoping we never get to that point.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Local and state officials are also planning some just in case solutions, one of them being tapping into water farther upstream and even bringing water down the Mississippi by barge, millions of gallons by barge and they`re doing this because there is not any major rain in the forecast in the upper parts of the Mississippi River -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just in case solution sounds good to me.\par \par \uc2 Omar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, our investigation on school funding, why the money often fails to go to the districts that needed most to repair crumbling buildings.\par \par \uc2 We will have the latest. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Public education is supposed to be equal in this country, a ruling that goes all the way back to the Supreme Court in 1954, Brown versus Board of Education.\par \par \uc2 But nearly 70 years later, here we are and many schools still fall short, and most of those schools are in cities or areas where people of color make up the majority.\par \par \uc2 An investigation by CBS News and \uc2 CBS\uc2  stations found that one reason for this is well, many states give more money to wealthier majority White school districts to improve their school buildings.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  stations national investigative correspondent, Stephen Stock looks at the funding gap and one state`s effort to fix it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. RICHARD LOFTON (Johns Hopkins School of Education): But it`s cold, so how can I focus? Can you please talk to the school to fix the toilets and to fix the building.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Those are the words of children who spend long days inside Baltimore`s aging city schools.\par \par \uc2 DR. RICHARD LOFTON: They`re not fixing our school buildings, but they will fix White people`s school buildings.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Dr. Richard Lofton reads student testimonials he gathered as part of his research project documenting the impact of crumbling campuses.\par \par \uc2 DR. RICHARD LOFTON: So you`re saying the second floor is hotter than this floor?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Like Baltimore`s Franklin High.\par \par \uc2 DR. RICHARD LOFTON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 I hope that people who are making decisions about education actually come into the schools and walk around and see what we`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: So I have to tell you, when I walk down this, I feel like I`m in a prison.\par \par \uc2 CYNDI SMITH (Baltimore City Public Schools director of Facilities Design and Construction): I know, right, doesn`t it? Why would your kids want to come here when it looks like you`re in a prison?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Baltimore City Schools facilities director, Cyndi Smith gave us a tour of Franklin High.\par \par \uc2 CYNDI SMITH: No AC, you can feel it.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: So this is a floor that`s actually collapsing. Look along the wall, you can see, it has dropped two, maybe even three inches.\par \par \uc2 This folks is an actual lab for high school students. People attend lab in this classroom.\par \par \uc2 CYNDI SMITH: We have a typical classroom, tap boards that are a mess, casework that needs to be replaced.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Why can`t the school district do better?\par \par \uc2 CYNDI SMITH: Because we don`t have enough money for it.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Across the country, our \uc2 CBS\uc2  stations found deteriorating schools in high poverty districts.\par \par \uc2 In Colorado, rotting windows and damaged ceilings are part of that state`s $17 billion need for repairs. In Florida, walkways riddled with leaks, exposed rusty nails. And outside Los Angeles, a water polo team practices in a decaying 70-year-old pool.\par \par \uc2 MAN: So this is impacting your grades.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And your future.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Schools in high poverty communities have less to spend from local property taxes. Our analysis found they invested on average 40 percent less in buildings than districts with low poverty.\par \par \uc2 We also discovered many state governments make the disparity worse, putting more money towards booming districts that can be less diverse.\par \par \uc2 Federal data shows that on average, these states give majority White school districts almost four times more money from capital investments than majority Black districts.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHARFSTEIN (Johns Hopkins Public Health professor): For decades, Baltimore has been disinvested. There have not been the same levels of investment in a city that is majority black than in the rest of the state.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Johns Hopkins Public Health professor, Josh Sharfstein says the funding inequity stems from generations of racial discrimination.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHARFSTEIN: The consequences of that are physically manifest in school buildings.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Maryland recognize that. Now, it is targeting funding to schools most in need.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are investing in a more equitable system across the state of Maryland than almost any other state in the country.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Lawmakers did that by targeting $400 million for building and repairing schools in lower income and minority school districts paid for in part by taking excess property taxes from wealthier districts and giving it to poor districts, and by a sales tax on digital products like streaming and music.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Absolutely, Maryland has to do more and we will -- and we have and we will. The plan that we`ve put forward is the next 10 years.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: Their 10-year plan won`t come in time for the kids at Franklin High. You can`t even drink the water there.\par \par \uc2 Wait, wait. That just blows my mind.\par \par \uc2 CYNDI SMITH: I know, right. Many of our pipes are so deteriorated that they just -- lead flows through that water, so that water is not drinkable.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: So you can`t drink the water.\par \par \uc2 CYNDI SMITH: You cannot drink the water.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN STOCK: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m investigative correspondent, Stephen Stock, Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So he leaves us with a very jaw-dropping detail there. I mean, you`ve just got to figure out a way to do better, and when you`re in an environment that makes you feel good, I actually think you pay attention, you learn better.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it makes you angry when you see the disparity of what`s happening in the schools in this country.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re absolutely right about that. The quality of the facilities.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It makes me angry. Yes, it matters.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They do have an impact on how you feel walking in. And there`s all kinds of studies showing that if you have, you know, new uniforms help teams play better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: New school facilities can make people learn better.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And to that point, on the flip side is how you are seen by the outside world is how you end up seeing yourself. So if you`re a kid that`s grown up in these schools, elementary, middle school, high school, and you don`t know any better, the rest of your life could be --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Affected --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: By that perspective.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The whole thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Plus, it continues to hollow out public education because people look at those schools, they think, well, if they can get their kid out of it, they do and then fewer and fewer kids enroll, they become further segregated in many cases and they continue to decline. It`s a major cycle of trouble.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We have to do better, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you`ll be talking about today, but first, it is 7:44, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch" with Mr. Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How are you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bet you`ll elevate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Cuff it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Beyonce, Vlad. I love that song.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that song, too, Gayle. It`s one of my favorite Beyonce songs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mine, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, we were going to talk about Beyonce.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re teasing that song, but I have a very special story tomorrow. We`re going to share it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to start though this morning with the compelling new docuseries, "72 Seconds in Rittenhouse Square." It`s debuting today on Paramount+. It is centered on the 2018 stabbing death of a White man and the trial of the Black man charged with killing him.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Paramount+.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The case rocked a Philadelphia community already divided by racial tension. Here is a preview.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "72 Seconds in Rittenhouse Square.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Incredibly powerful documentary. You can stream "72 Seconds in Rittenhouse Square" on Paramount+, which like CBS News is a part of \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a very good tease because I`m curious. I don`t remember that story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, it`s an incredibly powerful story. A lot of nuance and dynamics in it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And this is put together by See It Now Studios, which is run by our friend, Susan Zirinsky.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Susan Zirinsky.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We know it is going to be good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We know it`s going to be good. Yes. We know it is going to be good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pretty good --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, some visitors to Canada`s Wonderland Amusement Park near Toronto found themselves in a nightmare. Their ride got stuck on a Saturday night leaving riders -- look at this video.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from TikTok.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are upside down 75 feet in the air.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gosh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. In this video, you could hear the passengers on the lumberjack, I guess, that`s the name of the ride.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Screaming, you can hear that screaming over my screaming. They are screaming for help.\par \par \uc2 It took nearly -- get this -- 30 minutes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oof.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thirty minutes that you`re upside down. It took 30 minutes for that ride to start up again, finally allowing people to get off. The park said two riders reportedly had chest pain and lost feeling in their legs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just think about the blood rushing to your head and how painful that is, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Oh man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Also --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are they all right? They`re all right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When they got it fixed, the ride had to complete it circuit, so like you`ve been upside down for 30 minutes and then like you actually have to take the ride like for another --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`re okay?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re okay?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They`re all okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Shout out to the guy working there who made sure that the locks were on all the seats because they needed them when they were up there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Remember the Buccaneer? The ship that would go like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was on that once when I was like 10 and from that day on, never again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Never again.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t go on any amusement park rides. I have enough excitement in my life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t blame you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am good to go.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk about the Annual Global Citizen Festival that was held in New York City had everybody partying in the rain over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Global Citizen Festival.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The crowd, of course, went wild for BTS star, Jungkook performing without his bandmates for the very first time.\par \par \uc2 DJ D-Nice introduced Busta Rhymes, Big Daddy Kane, and others to honor the 50th anniversary of hip-hop, but the highlight of the evening was Lauryn Hill reunited with the Fugees.\par \par \uc2 Gayle introduced Lauryn Hill.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Awesome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She was there. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Gayle King.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Gayle singing and dancing in the rain, "Doo Wop (That Thing)" from "The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill." You shared this photo with your family and the legendary, Lauryn Hill with Fugee bandmate, Pras.\par \par \uc2 I was also on stage with Grammy-winner, Kamen (sp?) along with activist, Olivier Bahemuke Ndoole and Pashtana Durrani captured this selfie from the stage.\par \par \uc2 The event bought $240 million.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To end global poverty.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I could not -- I could not believe the number of people that showed in the rain.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In the rain.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And stayed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like dancing and stayed on to the very end and actually, everything worked out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lauryn Hill made an entrance like the queen that she was.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She was amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Those were very cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Vlad -- hey, Vlad, you`re sticking around.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m sticking around, baby.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To the best cocktail bar in America.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I did. I did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, the show "Survivor" is sure living up to its name lasting 23 really exciting years on the air. So coming up, host, Jeff Probst is here in the studio. He`s got a preview of the new season.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. "The Color of Dance" is not just a book, it`s part of a movement in dance to inspire more diversity. We`ll talk to the author.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Vlad, get in here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s up?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re sticking around and taking us on a journey to the best cocktail bar in North America.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right, that`s right. I didn`t just talk to the owners about their signature drinks. Guys, I also learned how to make them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are not surprised.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 President Biden heads to Michigan to stand with striking auto workers on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: This move today by the president is unusual, no question, but it is not entirely surprising for a man who likes to call himself the most pro-union president ever.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ (D-NJ): I recognize that this will be the biggest fight yet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: New Jersey Senator Robert Menendez resists calls to step down over federal bribery charges.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY KIM (D-NJ): I believe that New Jersey deserves better.\par \par \uc2 ALEX ZARAGOZA: I immediately just had that like, sobbing and just feeling so happy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Writers react to news of a tentative deal to end the strike with a contract that could be approved by their leaders today.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HANDEL: It appears that the WGA got a lot and got more than expected.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Crews clean up in Oklahoma City after oil spills out of a damaged pipeline.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We want to make sure that we contain most of the product on site, and if it moves off site, we address those.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Cute movie, dogs everywhere. It was -- I mean, it`s perfect. Perfect day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that`s the premiere of the latest "Paw Patrol" movie, a new Guinness World Record is set for most dogs attending a film screening.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It was a bit of a nail biter. We lucked out though because there is no rain and they ended up with 219 successful dogs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So I do have to add that it`s not an easy world record to break actually for a successful doggy. The dog has to watch the movie for at least 10 minutes and that we confirmed.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so dog owners out there, could you get your pet to watch a movie for 10 minutes outside with other dogs?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With other dogs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With other dogs. Tough.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That seems like a challenge.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Difficult to judge.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that`s --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anyway, that`s my tidbit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that they tried.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly right. Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s begin this hour with a New York City bar offering cocktails unlike anything you`ve tasted before. It`s called Double Chicken Please. That`s the name of the restaurant. It was recently ranked the best bar in North America and the sixth best in the world.\par \par \uc2 It opened to mostly crickets during the pandemic. Now, it is the hottest spot in town. I met the duo behind the bar`s success and even tried my hand at making one of their signature drinks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If you order cold pizza at Double Chicken Please, don`t expect a slice. Instead, a tequila cocktail made with parmesan cheese, fresh tomato, and basil. It`s the brainchild of self-taught bartender, GN Chan, a former street magician and industrial designer from Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 How did you go from hobbyist to this?\par \par \uc2 GN CHAN (Double Chicken Please co-owner): I graduated college, started my own design studio, it wasn`t that successful. So I was broke for several months and ended up just on the street and then that`s why I went to the bar to ask for a job.\par \par \uc2 FAYE CHEN (Double Chicken Please co-owner): A little slow at the door.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He met Faye Chen at that bar about a decade ago. She trained him for that job.\par \par \uc2 Faye, do you remember the first cocktail you taught him to make?\par \par \uc2 GN CHAN: Cosmopolitan, Sex on the Beach.\par \par \uc2 FAYE CHEN: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GN CHAN: Oh, those were like very classic. That`s ten, twelve years ago.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: From Japanese cold noodles to key lime pie. The process begins in the kitchen where culinary ingredients are developed to bring out unexpected flavors like fresh corn on the cob and maple syrup infused with burnt brioche.\par \par \uc2 GN CHAN: It is all about flavors.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Chan honed his chops before launching a bar of his own in 2017, but the location fell through.\par \par \uc2 GN CHAN: I just want to clear my mind, basically. I just want to travel by myself with my car and say, oh, this is great. So I turned that into almost like a tasting menu cocktail van-ish, just my skateboard, just you in a bar and start serving.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Traveling across the country in this 1977 \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  bus, it was a test drive of what would become Double Chicken Please.\par \par \uc2 What was it when he said, I`ve got a \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  bus from 1977 and I`m making cocktails and it breaks down all the time, but you should come with me.\par \par \uc2 FAYE CHEN: I was not surprised, and I found this guy. That`s how much I understand him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Chen went along for the ride, and eventually the two opened a permanent location. They named it Double Chicken Please, as an homage to Chan`s nickname, Chicken Cutlet.\par \par \uc2 There`s a front bar with cocktails on tap, and a back bar called The Coop, offering craft cocktails. Both serve a variety of fried chicken sandwiches with a range of flavor profiles.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: This is great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We thought it`d be fun to have some special guests figure out the inspiration behind one of the most popular drinks here while Tony Dokoupil and Nancy Chen snacked on fried chicken.\par \par \uc2 Okay, show me what you just did there.\par \par \uc2 Chan taught me how to make their signature French toast cocktail.\par \par \uc2 GN CHAN: Almost like a touch more than an ounce.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GN CHAN: It`s pretty good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Hey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey. What have you got?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Then, the taste test.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, so what`s happening now?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Here`s what I want you to do. I want you to take a sip.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Then take a bite of your cookie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And then take a sip again.\par \par \uc2 What did you notice?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: This is a darker chocolate than I expected. I think I expected it to be more like an Oreo, so this balances out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What flavors are you tasting?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It feels like a cousin of a kind of Vietnamese coffee with coffee liqueur.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this is inspired by a particular dish that you might have at breakfast.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s pancakes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What do you say?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: I mean, you seem like you were I mean you were really clearing up pancakes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, you`re close. You`re close.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: French toast.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony Dokoupil. All right. There is raw egg in this. There is coconut water and maple syrup.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I put maple syrup in my coffee every morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was tasting the edge of that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You are essentially having French toast and this is meant to simulate your coffee.\par \par \uc2 What`s the future like for you? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 FAYE CHEN: This is really a good timing for us to push to do more, to say something to the world, you know, to present ourselves.\par \par \uc2 GN CHAN: We are passionate, even when we`re tired, just keep doing it because I want to be there. I want to be someone.\par \par \uc2 The main thing is the technique of making drink, the main thing is the ingredients, the main thing is the story. The main thing is the way to present a drink. We`re still pushing the envelope.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, you nailed it and I was very skeptical, even as I was making the cocktail that this drink would ultimately taste like a piece of French toast, but it did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It really -- it really did. I don`t think I nailed. You had to give me -- you led me to breakfast.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ah yes, because at first your eyes look at it and you think it`s kind of a coffee drink because of the color.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But then once you tune in those taste buds, absolutely like overwhelming French toast flavor.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And they make these incredible fried chicken sandwiches and fried chicken nuggets. There are -- you saw on the video, you can go to the front of the bar and there are cocktails on tap, which I had never seen before. There`s like cocktails that you just say oh, I`ll have one of those and they tap it for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So this is a fusion of flavors, of course.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it`s also a fusion of food, wine, liquor, and art.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s exactly what GN envisioned.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is art.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He started out as a graphic artist and part of -- everything -- look at the -- this is the inside of the bar, right, look at that the mural behind you and the alligator. There`s a design aesthetic to the bar.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But I`ve never seen really anywhere in New York City and that`s why when you get there at four o`clock in the afternoon, there`s a line around the block already. Everybody has to wait in line. It`s democratic like that, even their parents when they came to visit had to experience the line.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just for a little bit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think I might have let parents back --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just to experience.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Bravo to GN though that he figured it out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: GN and Faye.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Considering his background. I think he and his partner are fantastic.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You guys drink, I don`t. I was very fascinated by the chicken sandwiches that I want to go for that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle, they`re fantastic. I think I had one bite on camera, I had the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He ate the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am going. I am going.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is called Double Chicken Please.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is really good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The next one on their menu is Street Taco.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, GN. Bravo. Bravo. Bravo. Vlad, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a new season of "Survivor." It starts tomorrow night. The man in charge, you know him, Jeff Probst will sit down with us in just a bit. He`s on his way to the studio.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:09. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still had, the many colors of dance world. The author of a new photo book is here to talk about the importance of widening opportunities for everybody in ballet.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from ABT.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That of course is dance superstar, Misty Copeland and the iconic "Swan Lake."\par \par \uc2 In 2015, Copeland became the first African-American female principal dancer in the prestigious American Ballet Theatre`s 75-year history.\par \par \uc2 A new book aims to inspire ballerinas of all colors continuing to break barriers, and it`s called "The Color Of Dance."\par \par \uc2 Photographer, TaKiyah Wallace-McMillian traveled across the country to showcase the diverse beauty of dance and all its shapes and hues. The book builds on her popular photo project, Brown Girls Do Ballet that went on to become a nonprofit empowering young dancers.\par \par \uc2 TaKiyah Wallace-McMillian joins us first on CBS MORNINGS. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN ("The Color of Dance): I`m excited to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, very glad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are excited to have you. So when your daughter, Charlie asked you about taking ballet.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You stumbled upon what is the lack of diversity in it. How did that inspire Brown Girls Do Ballet?\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Absolutely. You know, as a new mom at the time, one of the things that stuck out to me when I was looking for classes for her was that there were very few images of little girls that look like her on our local dance websites.\par \par \uc2 And as a kind of a hobbyist freelance photographer at the time, I just decided as a personal project to take that on and try and find more dancers of color that were enrolled in ballet programs. And what started as something that was just for myself and her, so that she would be able to see more of herself in this space, turned into this whole viral moment that inspired other dancers around the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the thing about this book, though, TaKiyah, every page is so beautifully shot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`re the photographer.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, every time I would turn the page, I`m not kidding, I would gasp at the beauty of it.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because what did you want every page to represent? You were very intentional about what you wanted.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: I was. I wanted -- I wanted people to be able to see the landscape of dance itself, especially for little girls of color. So you have different ages, shapes, sizes, abilities, and skill levels because not everyone steps into a ballet class for the first time and is ready for principal. It doesn`t happen like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: So you get to see that, the buildup of the dancer in this book, and I really wanted any girl to be able to open up this book and be able to find herself in it on one of those pages.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You didn`t just take pictures, you had conversations with a lot of these folks, right?\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What did you learn from the ballerinas? From the dancers?\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: So I learned a lot actually. I always jokingly tell people now when I started this whole project, I knew nothing about ballet, but now I feel like I`m working on a master`s, maybe a PhD in the area.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ballet mama.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Yes, absolutely. Some of the things that they face that were not my reality, but are realities for them every day, things about you know how to pull my textured hair back into a proper ballet bun and the struggles associated with that. Or if I want to wear a protective style, how do I do that in time for class and in a way that my dance teacher finds acceptable?\par \par \uc2 Maybe my hips are a little bit fuller, my chest is a little bit fuller. How do I make sure that I have the right leotard so I can make sure that I am in class and presenting properly?\par \par \uc2 I`ve learned so much about how girls feel in this space, especially when they may be and often are the only one in those classes that look like them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There are so many great quotes in the book. One of them is from Grace Butler who says, "We`re often told dance like no one is watching," which most of us do. But you say brown girls should dance, why?\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: They should dance because they`re not only doing it for themselves, but they`re inspiring the next generation. If you see more of yourself in a space of more people who look like you in a space, there`s never a barrier for you, you never know a time -- this younger generation will never know a time when that was impossible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So don`t dance like no one`s watching, dance like everybody --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody watching. Everybody.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Everybody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Charlie was three. How old is she now?\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: She is 14 years old now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you said as a parent, we want to do what for our kids? Because you said if Charlie said I want go to the moon --\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: We want to give them everything. Yes, I knew as soon as I held her in my hands on August 21, 2009 that she has --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is a Leo?\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: She is a Leo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: And fiery, too. That it didn`t -- it didn`t matter what she wanted to do. If she wanted to know how to -- or she wanted to go to the moon, I was going to be looking for tickets to get her there, you know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: So something like this, with it being ballet, while it was foreign to me, I knew nothing -- I`m not that girl. I`m wearing pink today, I am not that girl.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: But it was completely foreign to me, but it was something that she wanted to do. So I was going to make it happen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: That`s what parents do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now we have works of art that`s beautifully captured and well written that can help with the inclusivity, but what more needs to be done in this space?\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: So many things, I will say that there have been great changes in the ballet world. There are more brands that are offering brown tights and brown shoes, which is amazing, and there are more artistic directors that are looking at their companies and saying it should be reflective of the communities where we perform.\par \par \uc2 But there are still changes that need to be made across the world and the parts that are given to dancers of color and the opportunities as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and you have even thought about the shoe color.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And so Misty Copeland --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right, and the tights.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Misty Copeland mentioned it too.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I never thought about the difference in the shoe color.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what a difference that makes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or the hair.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or the hair.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or the hips and the body.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All of these little things that could be overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Bravo. Bravo. Bravo. Well done.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: Because she is a school teacher, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: TaKiyah Wallace-McMillian, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is a school teacher.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: I am.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 TAKIYAH WALLACE-MCMILLIAN: I am not in my class today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you taught us today. Thank you so much. "The Color of Dance" is on sale wherever you buy your books.\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead, CBS MORNINGS Deals with a backup power station that can help you with some very special moments and it`s going to be at a special reduced price.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST ("Survivor" host): Welcome to the \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS tribal council. Gayle, please.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: It`s been a great show today. It`s been a great season overall. But it`s now time to tally the votes to see which anchor will get to lead the interview with me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pick me.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Before I read this, I just want to make sure you understand the stakes. Gayle, again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: It`s me. So, when you interview me, have a question. Think about it in advance. If I choose to respond, be prepared with a follow up. These are basic journalistic qualities we are expecting from the winner.\par \par \uc2 I will now read the vote. After careful consideration, the person who gets to interview me is Gayle. Congratulations, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jeff, I just love you, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: I look forward to talking with you after the break. We`re going to go to commercial. Gayle, congratulations. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is my honor. It is my honor.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Boo.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Sorry, fellas. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 I won. I won. I won.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Survivor.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all remember Rich. That`s an iconic moment in reality TV history right there from Season One. The finale of the hit \uc2 CBS\uc2  show "Survivor," it has aired -- listen to this -- more than 23 years ago. And guess what? It`s still going strong returning for a milestone 45th season tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 And guess who`s been at the top the whole time? Jeff Probst. He has been "Survivor`s" host since the show`s debut in May of 2000. That is hard to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You are the real survivor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Only on -- yes, you are the real survivor. You`re right, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Only on CBS MORNINGS, here`s a preview of tomorrow season premiere. This one set in Fiji. I like it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Survivor.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yessiree, the vibes are immaculate. Guess what? I love that phrase.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jeff Probst joins us now. You already saw him, but he`s back again.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, congratulations to you, number one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number one, the host the whole time. That`s hard to do. When you first heard the pitch 23 years ago, I know you couldn`t have thought it`s going to last 23 years. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Well, when I first heard it, then I wanted to be a part of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You really wanted it?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Desperately.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why? Why?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: For real. The concept still, the same, which is this really interesting experiment. You can lump all these shows say their reality shows, but all unique, and what`s unique about "Survivor" is it really is this human adventure where you`re dropped in a jungle and forced to survive with people who inspire me.\par \par \uc2 I feel the same way 23 years later. I still love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there`s something wrong with your mic. So --\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Oh, well. There we go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because we are live TV. Thank you, Patty.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There we go.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Now, I`m in my element. I like this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but you were saying, but you didn`t think, you couldn`t possibly have thought it would last 23 years, but you still love it today. But --\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you think was going to happen with the show?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Well, I will tell you, after the first season premiered the finale, after the clip you just showed with Richard Hatch, which we thought was the worst thing ever because he was the villain. We thought that`s the end of our show.\par \par \uc2 But I was certain in my wisdom, we would get at least two more seasons because it was so popular. I never dreamed it would get 10 seasons or 20, let alone 45.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve read that you feel like as host, you`ve watched a demonstration of the human spirit in each season.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: A hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How come?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do you see?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Because you go out there with the lure of the game. So we designed this game to have you adapt like this. This is what happens on "Survivor." Your mic doesn`t work, what do you do? Well, you get another mic? Some people are quicker at that, others aren`t.\par \par \uc2 When it`s over, what you realize, it wasn`t about the game it was about you and what you went through living in a jungle, conspiring against each other, playing detective. How`s your emotional intelligence? Are you socially good?\par \par \uc2 And I was saying to Gayle before we started this that at the end of every season, every player goes, I cannot believe how much I changed. I`m a different human being.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and you --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For better or worse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, for better or worse and you say that.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Almost always, but almost always for better, because you`re out there alone, no technology, no friends, stripped of everything. And you end up saying, wow, I love my wife more. I`m going to be a better parent. I`m going to quit my job, you know, whatever that thing is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you said that and I want to read it out loud because it is pretty powerful.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nobody comes home from "Survivor" the same because the game forces you into situations that reveal your character, your fortitude, your drive, your inspiration, and it connects you back to the things that make you alive as a human.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: That`s pretty good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, that`s great you said it.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you know, what is great about that is because this day and age, we lean on so many things.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Technology, our jobs, our friends, but we don`t lean on ourselves enough.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: A hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In this show, it really examines that. That`s why it`s so special.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: What you just said is it for me, which is it`s amazing what you can do if you just get up off the couch and say I`ll give it a go and that`s what "Survivor" is testing you on.\par \par \uc2 We were talking about the challenges.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: I mean you have these epic challenges that you can`t imagine. You look at it and go, I`ll never get up there.\par \par \uc2 But you`ve got people with you, they`ll help you get up there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got it. We`ve got it.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: And when you get -- we got it, and then when you do get up, there you go, I did it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Survivor.")\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: And then you go back to conspiring against the person who just helped you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you think it`s changed you, Jeff? In the 23 years?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, good question.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you think it has?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST: I wonder who I would be without it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 JEFF