ould take your Sagely pills with you in that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. How much are we talking for this?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Okay, we start this at under $85.00.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ready, Nate?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: And there you go. Look at those hands.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Look at those hands.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: You know what? He should be in the NFL or something.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You should be in the NFL. All right --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh. We`ve got a monster. Hold on. Hold on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to break things. We`re going to get in trouble.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let me --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, watch this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: And the competition is on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Of course, we partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything you saw, scan the QR code on your screen - -\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Shawna --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Or visit \uc2 CBS\uc2  deals.com and be sure to text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000 to get early access to some of our most popular deals.\par \par \uc2 We are coming back. No, we did not put --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: I didn`t --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, I didn`t do that. I didn`t do that. It wasn`t me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: It wasn`t me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bad Shawna.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bad Company`s frontman, Paul Rodgers has been dealing with some serious health scares and left him unable to sing or even play the guitar for six months.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, Rodgers and his wife, Cynthia talked to Anthony Mason about it for the first time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK (Paul Rodger`s wife): I would bring the guitar out in its case, and I`d put the lid up and he`d walk by and he put the lid down.\par \par \uc2 And this went on for a few weeks.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS (Bad Company frontman): I don`t -- I don`t remember that at all.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK: I know you don`t, love.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You were trying to coax it.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK: I thought that music would be his -- his way back to him.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: And it was.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA KERELUK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODGERS: Actually. It definitely was.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I can`t wait to see that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Can you imagine that? That`s tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. You`re a musician. Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s really rough.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230926ej9q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 End of Writers` Strike; Ophelia Lashes East Coast; Countdown to Shutdown; Sen. Menendez Bribery Allegations; Migrant Spike Strains Communities; Emotional Return to Lahaina; Historic Asteroid Mission; Views from the Top; A Lesson in Sacrifice; Turning Norms Upside Down; Usher Live; "Dork Diaries" in Paris, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Elise Preston, Errol  Barnett, Nancy Cordes, Scott MacFarlane, Jonathan Vigliotti, Mark  Strassmann, Vladimir Duthiers, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 14,129 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS as we start another week, it`s Monday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, the Writers Guild reaches a tentative deal with Hollywood studios that will end the months long strike. What we know so far.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I mean, I think this is an amazing victory for all concerned. This is a long strike. It almost broke the record.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Talks on Capitol Hill failed to slow momentum toward a crippling government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): No doubt in my mind that the country is headed for a shutdown and everyone should prepare as such.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Democratic senator, \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  rejects demands from fellow Democrats to resign after he is indicted on corruption charges.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): What is here in this indictment is quite clear.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The remnants of Tropical Storm Ophelia moving up the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Dumping heavy rain and leaving behind major flooding.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Farewell to a legend, two-time World Cup winner Megan Rapinoe played her final game for the US women`s national soccer team.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RAPINOE (Soccer player): I guess that`s it. Love you, guys.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Dolphins beat the Broncos, 70 to 20.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Everything going right for Miami.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Forget the 70 points the Dolphins put up on the Broncos. Everyone`s talking about Tai Tai.\par \par \uc2 TAILOR SWIFT (Singer): Hey.\par \par \uc2 MAN: How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s all that matters, Taylor Swift was in the house. She was Travis Kelce`s guest.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A budding romance.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, we all need to calm down.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Are we going with Tra-Lor or Tay-Vis?\par \par \uc2 MAN: The romance we all need. It feels like it`s right for America. It just feels right.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve been told we can`t get into it right now, but we`re going to.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Everybody wants this to happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know nothing, but I`m here for it. Whatever is happening, I`m here for both of them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We got some thoughts on it. We`re going to talk about it later.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are going about it. It`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a very big show for you. And it`s only Monday. Look at us.\par \par \uc2 Grammy winning superstar, Usher, talk about superstars. He`s joining us for his first live TV interview since he was named to headline the Super Bowl halftime show right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`ve also got an exclusive conversation with the CEO of \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . It`s a platform nearly everyone uses especially kids, so we talk to the company about what they are doing to keep us all safe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, in today`s lesson in "Kindness 101," it comes from a former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player who now works on a field of a different kind as a farmer, Steve Hartman will show us the importance of sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is a different kind of field.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with the breaking news. Have you heard overnight? After nearly five months on the picket line, the Writers Guild says a tentative deal has been reached to end the Hollywood strike. Oh, we all hope so.\par \par \uc2 So we don`t know all the details just yet, but there are signs that the writers may have cause to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston is in Los Angeles. She`s been following the story.\par \par \uc2 Elise, good morning to you. A lot of people were saying please, please, please let it be true. What do we know?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 This has been a long time coming, 146 days to be exact for the union`s 11,000 writers. While it still could be weeks until they get back to work, working conditions and demands like higher pay and residuals could be significantly improved.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Striking members of the Writers Guild of America were out in force over the weekend, even as marathon negotiations which at times included the top brass of four major studios indicated their work stoppage might soon be over.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hopeful. Fingers, toes, eyes like everything crossed for a resolution.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: And late Sunday, it came.\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement, the WGA and the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  said they`ve reached a tentative agreement for a new three-year contract.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELLONI (Puck founding founder): Hollywood is breathing a huge sigh of relief that --\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Matthew Belloni is a founding partner of Puck News.\par \par \uc2 While the details of the agreement are officially still under wraps while the contract is finalized, Belloni says he has learned the writers are getting significantly more than what the studios were offering before the strike.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELLONI: They made gains on AI, they got protections against their work being used in AI models. They also made gains on minimum staffing. This is the number of writers that are contractually required to be used on a TV show.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: There were joyful reactions on social media Especially from actors whose own strike negotiations have stalled. For Mindy Kaling: "Can`t wait to go back to work and see my people." LeVar Burton: "This is a huge step in the right direction." And from Quinta Bronson a simple, "We`re back, baby."\par \par \uc2 While striking actors are expected to use the WGA agreement as a template, they face some significantly different hurdles.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELLONI: There are things that are transferable. However, there`s a lot of things that are not transferable. For instance, the actors have asked for a much more significant basic wage increase.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now picketing has been suspended until the tentative contract is finalized and ratified. Late night talk shows could return early next month and Belloni says, best case scenario of actors can get back to the bargaining table, all of Hollywood could be back in full swing by November -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what I like to hear, Elise. One strike down, a couple more to go. We`ll check back in with you later. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Here in the Northeast, what`s left of Tropical Storm Ophelia is still slamming much of the region this morning. The system already brought flooding along the coast, including North Carolina, where it made landfall early Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 Errol Barnett has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: This summer, we probably had 200 yards of beach and now we have 10 yards maybe. The beach is gone.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Tropical Storm Ophelia took a bite out of this beach in Sea Isle City, New Jersey leading to erosion and dangerous flooding.\par \par \uc2 Ophelia first slammed ashore early Saturday bringing 70 mile per hour winds to parts of the East Coast. Up to five feet of storm surge swept through the town of Belhaven, North Carolina, about 60 miles from where Ophelia made landfall, tearing through homes and reaching the tops of fire hydrants.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That storm has just hit us perfectly coming in.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: \uc2 The US Coast Guard\uc2  rescued five people including three children from a 38-foot catamaran anchored on Cape Lookout as Ophelia caused swells as high as 10 feet.\par \par \uc2 In Raleigh, North Carolina, more than 40 wrecks were reported after the storm flooded roads. The most serious, when a tree hit this car sending the driver to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 PAUL KIMMEL (Monmouth County, New Jersey): Thought it was an earthquake.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Paul Kimmel (sp?) says he and his family were trapped inside their New Jersey home after a tree fell on their house.\par \par \uc2 PAUL KIMMEL: The steps were blocked, the whole roof caved in.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: They were forced to climb out a window to escape and his oldest daughter was injured.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Errol Barnett.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In less than a week, much of the federal government will shut down if Congress does not provide more money for it to operate. House Speaker Kevin McCarthy tried and failed over the weekend to make a deal with fellow Republicans who want to see some very deep spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House for us. She has been following all of this. Nancy, what`s going on? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Tony, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 The speaker was indeed at work all weekend, but if anything these holdouts appear to be even more dug in opposing any of the options to fund the government quickly.\par \par \uc2 And so with just five days to go, it`s hard to see at this point how Congress keeps the government open come Sunday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): There is no doubt in my mind that the country is headed for a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A band of far-right Republicans argued this weekend that a government shutdown might be the only way to force Congress to spend less.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM BURCHETT (R-TN): This dysfunctional Washington cannot continue.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Congress now has just five days to either pass 12 annual bills to fund the government or pass a short-term fix.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I think if we`re able to get 45 days just like the stop gap and get the rest of our business done, it would be very productive.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But that small group of conservatives is threatening Kevin McCarthy`s speakership if he tries to work with Democrats to fund the government temporarily.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): Kevin wants it in one big up or down vote, keep the government open, shut it down. I`m saying single subject spending bills. It`s the only way to break the fever and liberate ourselves from this out-of-control spending.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The funding fight is creating clear division within the GOP.\par \par \uc2 REP. GARRET GRAVES (R-LA): The arsonist that lit their house on fire. They`re whining about their house burning. They`re going to want credit for putting the fire out and then they`re going to set up a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  to get paid for what happened.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A shutdown would force many of the nation`s four million federal employees to work without pay.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): A small group of extreme Republicans don`t want to live up to the deal. So now everyone in America could be forced to pay the price.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Including thousands of food safety inspectors represented by Paula Soldner.\par \par \uc2 PAULA SOLDNER (Joint Council of Food Inspectors chairperson): They`re going to be going out inspecting the food, but they`re not going to be able to pay for the food that they need to feed their families.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: With the House in gridlock, the Senate is going to try this week to pass their own bipartisan short-term spending patch and send it to the House. But again, if McCarthy even allows that to come up for a vote, there are those on the right who say they try to oust him, Gayle, so he doesn`t have a lot of options.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Nancy, we are all watching. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  plans to say more today about the criminal charges against him and his wife. They are accused of a conspiracy to commit bribery, fraud, and extortion and the New Jersey Democrat is under big pressure to resign from the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 It seems so far, the senator is doubling way down.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Senator Menendez has temporarily stepped down as chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, but has given no indication he`ll step down from his Senate seat. In fact, he continues to deny the allegations and will face cameras this morning amid calls for him to go.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): I do believe that it is in the best interest for Senator Menendez to resign in this moment.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A growing number of fellow Democrats are calling on New Jersey Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  to step down after a federal corruption indictment alleges he took bribes from a foreign power.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): The details in this indictment are extremely serious.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The indictment unsealed Friday alleges Menendez shared highly sensitive information about the US Embassy in Cairo sought to interfere in criminal prosecutions and used his power and influence as chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee in exchange for bribes in the form of cash some found in a coat with Menendez`s name, gold bars, a Mercedes Benz, and even home mortgage payments.\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS (Southern District of New York US attorney): Senator Menendez allegedly provided sensitive, non-public \uc2 US government\uc2  information to Egyptian officials, and otherwise took steps to secretly aid the government of Egypt.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Menendez will have to appear Wednesday in federal court in New York along with his wife, Nadine, and three other co-defendants.\par \par \uc2 A defiant Menendez responded: "It is not lost on me how quickly some are rushing to judge a Latino and push him out of his seat. I`m not going anywhere."\par \par \uc2 And most Democrats in the Senate where they have a slim majority are not ready to call for him to go either.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): In terms of resignation, that`s a decision to be made by Senator Menendez and the people of New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Democratic governor, \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  is among the Democrats calling on Menendez to resign and he would appoint Menendez`s replacement if that were to happen. Also New Jersey democrat, Andy Kim has announced he`ll run for Menendez`s seat next year -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Mexico`s government says it will start deporting migrants who are waiting in its northern cities to enter the United States. The move is meant to reduce the strain on US border crossings as a number of rising migrants arrive from the south.\par \par \uc2 The mayor of El Paso, Texas says more than 2,000 people a day are asking for asylum at the border there. He says the city is at a breaking point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We turn now to Hawaii where residents of Maui are finally being allowed back to their properties in Lahaina which was of course mostly destroyed by a wildfire, you`ll recall. At least 97 people killed by those flames and smoke last month.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti shows us now how the locals are still struggling to cope with their grief.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: It`s been nearly seven weeks since the Pagdilao family has walked around their hometown of Lahaina. Their painful first steps were made here at this memorial to pay respect to their patriarch, Pablo Pagdilao III, another name added to a long row of crosses, each one a family member lost to the Lahaina fire.\par \par \uc2 The family ended their visit together in prayer.\par \par \uc2 GROUP: Amen.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Grateful for what is left.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIAN SANTOS (Lahaina fire victim`s grandson): A lot of us have not actually been able to come back. To see someone go in an unexpected way, it kind of hurts.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Pagdilao who was partially paralyzed, died while trying to flee the fire by car with his wife, Nelda. The flames blocked their exit on Front Street and Nelda couldn`t carry her husband over the seawall to safety.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIAN SANTOS: When she went over the wall, she tried to pull him down, but unfortunately, the fire got really close and my grandma did whatever she could, she did not give up, but my grandpa told her, go, just leave me. Just go find the family and go take care of yourself --\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Painful memories like this await many families who will be returning to what`s left of their homes in Lahaina this week.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP singing.)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The community gathered Sunday night to discuss recovery efforts, Kami Furtado (sp?) was among those present.\par \par \uc2 KAMI FURTADO (Lahaina resident): The sock store, the ice cream store.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Furtado is a sixth generation Lahainan whose family owned about a dozen historic buildings on Front Street.\par \par \uc2 Is it your family`s commitment that you`re going to keep this in the family instead of selling it off to some big developer?\par \par \uc2 KAMI FURTADO: We will be --that`s our intent is to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And it will be a long road to recovery and rebuilding. The roadblock behind me will be lifted later on this morning.\par \par \uc2 There are about 40 homes in this neighborhood, most of them have been destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, officials tell me, they hope that those who choose to return home to what little is left of their homes will be able to find some measure of closure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and hopefully, they will find something. That`s a very tough, emotional story.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a remarkable \uc2 NASA\uc2  mission returns home with the first ever samples taken from an asteroid. What the rocks and the dust yep, they tell a story about the origins of life right here on Earth.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:15, time check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, Taylor Swift cheers on her favorite tight end. We`re here to break down the tape.\par \par \uc2 Is she scouting for new album material or is this matchup a touchdown? Will she take it all the way?\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 NASA\uc2  celebrating the end of a historic seven-year mission to bring an asteroid sample to Earth. A capsule carrying it landed yesterday in the Utah desert. This is the first time \uc2 NASA\uc2  has ever collected a sample from an asteroid and is the largest retrieve from space since the Apollo moon rocks more than 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Today, a military jet will fly the material to an especially built lab in Houston.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann is on it and he visited the lab.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This moment seven years in the making, a \uc2 NASA\uc2  probe returning to Earth with pieces of an asteroid, a time capsule from the birth of our solar system.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It was charred, but it came in at 27,000 miles an hour. So we expected that. Otherwise, it looked perfect.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Liftoff of OSIRIS-REx.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The OSIRIS-REx mission, an engineering marvel launched in 2016. Two years later, it caught up with Bennu, a near-Earth asteroid the size of the Empire State Building. The spacecraft spent a year-and-a-half mapping Bennu searching for a safe place to collect a sample, then finally dodging boulders briefly plunged its arm into a small crater.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And we have touchdown.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Like a vacuum cleaner, the spacecraft sucked up asteroid rocks and dust about a half pounds` worth and locked it away for the 1.2 billion mile journey back to Earth.\par \par \uc2 DR. LORI GLAZE (NASA`s Science Mission Directorate`s Planetary Science Division director): This is the largest sample of extraterrestrial material that will be brought back to Earth since Apollo.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Lori Glaze is NASA`s head of planetary science.\par \par \uc2 And the challenge is to keep the sample as pristine as possible.\par \par \uc2 DR. LORI GLAZE: We`ve got to keep that sample uncontaminated by us.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: To protect the samples --\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOPHER SNEAD (NASA`s OSIRIS-REx sample collection deputy curator): This is the staging area.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 NASA\uc2  built an ultra-clean state of the art lab at the Johnson Space Center in Houston. It`s the same building that houses the Apollo moon rocks.\par \par \uc2 Deputy curator, Christopher Snead is already imagining opening the Bennu samples in this lab.\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOPHER SNEAD: It`s going to be an incredible wow moment. We`re going to learn a lot of new things. There are going to be unexpected surprises.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Bennu is rich and carbon and could contain the chemical building blocks of life. NASA`s new samples four-and-a-half billion years old could provide clues about how planets like ours formed.\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOPHER SNEAD: These samples are very small, but they can answer some of the fundamental questions about the formation of our solar system, where the ingredients from life came, and perhaps how common that might be in the universe.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Bennu has intrigued \uc2 NASA\uc2  for another reason. There`s a chance, a remote, one in 2,700 possibility the asteroid could collide with Earth in the year 2182.\par \par \uc2 It`s worth tracking.\par \par \uc2 DR. LORI GLAZE: It`s absolutely worth tracking.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But not necessarily worrying about.\par \par \uc2 DR. LORI GLAZE: I wouldn`t worry about it. I`m not losing any sleep over it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 The better odds, Bennu will tell us more about our solar system`s beginnings and how we became us.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Mark Strassman in Houston.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is so much to unpack right there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s just -- it`s amazing. I`m still getting over planes taking off and they`re sending stuff into space.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yep. Even --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am right there with you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Catching asteroids, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: 