ar \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s Neal. It is a very big job. I mean, \uc2 YouTube\uc2  is trying to be all things video, whether it`s long video, short video, official videos, professional, big legacy media videos, little independent creators. I mean, anything you`re watching on a screen, they want to be the ones serving it up on their platform.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Does he have kids in his house? Because I am curious about --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. So he`s got a 15 -year-old. He`s got a sixth grader, and a fourth grade girl. The fourth grader is still on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  Kids. And I asked him straight up like, can you assure parents that \uc2 YouTube\uc2  is safe for kids? And the proof is that his own kids are on there, all three of them.\par \par \uc2 However, he does use the parental controls which he recommends.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. So when he was making the example of a town hall, being able to allow people to speak, as you know, in regards to your question about what`s allowed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Can you lie?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I whispered out loud, oh, that`s different. But as he continued to talk, I changed my tune because a town hall in a small town that will influence those people there the same way, if you scale that out on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , you allow all of the voices to be heard, it can impact the world, and there lies the gray area.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, there is like the gray area. I mean, you know, basically bottom line, who decides what is on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  at the end of the day, it`s not a government, it all comes up to \uc2 Neal Mohan\uc2 . That`s the guy.\par \par \uc2 So it was great to meet him. I have to say, he has done a --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it seems like, it`s in good hands.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s in good hands. Yes, I was going to say. I`ve been you know -- he`s been at the job for six months. He`d been part of \uc2 Yo