c2 ROC Nation\uc2  produces the halftime show.\par \par \uc2 USHER: He said it`s time for you have that moment. I`m like, what are you talking about? He`s like, the Super Bowl. Like, oh, yes, you ready? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "You Make Me Wanna.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Usher will headline the event on February 11th in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Usher My Way: The Las Vegas Residency.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Where his residency has sold out shows.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "U Don`t Have to Call.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: While he hasn`t said if any surprise guests will be joining him on the Super Bowl stage.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Boyfriend.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He promises his 13-minute performance will be unlike anything fans have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 USHER: Just the excitement in this moment like to really be able to savor this moment.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "OMG.")\par \par \uc2 USHER: You remember the first time you`ve ever heard your voice you know, on a format or a radio, right? This is like --\par \par \uc2 MAN: One of those.\par \par \uc2 USHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "OMG.")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nice. That`s a nice way to end it. Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, we`re ready for it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are not ending it though, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Listen, Usher will join us in just a bit to talk about the Super Bowl Apple Music. Super Bowl LVIII halftime show in just a bit, so make sure you stick around all right.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:48, time to check the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, our series, "Kindness 101" takes us to the fields to learn about sacrifice. Why former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player, Jason Brown gave up his pro football career to focus on farming.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King. Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, the man of the hour, music -- multiple Grammy winning superstar, Usher. It`s his first live TV interview since the big announcement. He`s going to headline the Super Bowl halftime show.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil The authors of the best-selling "Dork Diaries" series are here. The mother and daughter team will tell us about their brand new books.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Today`s edition of "Kindness 101" is a good one. It`s a lesson about sacrifice from a former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player who gave it all up to become a farmer and help feed the hungry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like how he`s thinking.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, the Hollywood writers` strike may be coming to an end after a tentative deal is reached.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Picketing has been suspended until the tentative contract is finalized and ratified.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The storm has just hit us perfectly coming in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tropical Storm Ophelia slams into North Carolina leaving a trail of destruction along the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 PAUL KIMMEL: We couldn`t get down the steps because the steps were blocked. The whole roof caved in.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: House Speaker Kevin McCarthy fails to make headway over the weekend in efforts to prevent a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): Who`s going to get hurt the most is the American public.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): The details in this indictment are extremely serious.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: New Jersey senator, Robert Menendez resists calls to resign. Even though he is indicted on new corruption charges.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Senator Menendez has temporarily stepped down as chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, but has given no indication he`ll step down from his Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And now, the defense comes up with a big play.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And \uc2 Detroit Lions\uc2  defensive end, Aidan Hutchinson busts the move after his first two sacks of the season.\par \par \uc2 AIDAN HUTCHINSON (\uc2 Detroit Lions\uc2  defensive end): I was waiting to do the stanky leg for about three games now. So, it`s been in my head for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My God.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s just say he was ready.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. I see you, Hutch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For that moment, right guys? He was ready.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are big men, but they are still little kids.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is so true.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it when they can act like a little kid, too.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with our series, we call it "Kindness 101." It is one of our favorite things we do here.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman and his children share stories built around kindness and character -- you can never have too much of that -- and the people who`ve mastered those qualities.\par \par \uc2 So today`s lesson is sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 Steve and his kids check in with former \uc2 NFL\uc2  center, that`s Jason Brown, who sacrificed his successful football career to become wait for it -- a farmer instead.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good morning. I`m here with my daughter, Meryl with a lesson on --\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Sacrifice. And for our definition, we turn now to my brother, Emmett at the Dictionary Desk.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Hi, Meryl.\par \par \uc2 So I`m defining sacrifice as giving up something that`s important to you for the sake of someone else or to benefit them.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good. And why would anyone ever want to do that?\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Well, can I use a chest reference super quick?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: So a sacrifice in chess is like when you sacrifice a piece, but it helps you like later on. So a sacrifice isn`t always something that is just a complete loss to you, you can gain something from a sacrifice as well.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Interesting. We`ll get to that in a bit.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Sacrifice is next level kindness, Kindness 201 sort of, masters level of kindness.\par \par \uc2 This is when you give till it hurts because you know, in the end, you`ll receive something far greater.\par \par \uc2 To see what I mean, let`s take a look in our library, a tale of a man who has so much because he sacrificed so much.\par \par \uc2 At one point, number 16, Jason Brown was one of the best centers in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . At one point, he had a five-year $37 million contract with the \uc2 St. Louis Rams\uc2 . And at one point, he sacrificed all of it.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN (Former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player): My agent, you know, he told me, he said you`re making the biggest mistake of your life. And I looked right back at him and I said, no, I`m not.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So what could possibly trump the \uc2 NFL\uc2 ? You wouldn`t believe.\par \par \uc2 Jason quit football to be a farmer, even though he`d never done it a day in his life.\par \par \uc2 How did you learn even to do what you`re doing?\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Get on the internet. Watching \uc2 YouTube\uc2  videos.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Thanks to \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and some good advice from other farmers here in Louisburg, North Carolina, Jason was able to harvest a field of sweet potatoes.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: But when you see them pop up out of the ground, man, it is the most beautiful thing that you could ever see.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He says he has never felt more successful.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Not in man standards, but in God`s eyes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But God cares about the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . I see people praying to Him on the field all the time.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Yes, there is a lot of people praying out there. But when I think about a life of greatness, I think about a life of service.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Which leads us here.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Which leads us here. Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: See, his plan for this farm is to donate the bounty to food pantries.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA PAGE (Organizes food collection for the needy): It`s unusual for a grower to grow a crop just to give away.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Rebecca Page organizes food collection for the needy.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA PAGE: And that`s what Jason has done, and he is planning to do more next year.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Jason has a thousand acres here, which could go a long way toward eliminating hunger in this neck of North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Love is the most wonderful currency that you can give anyone?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You sure you played in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Because I feel like cuddling you right now.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Apparently, even on the farm, holding is still a penalty.\par \par \uc2 Farmer Brown joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Jason.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Glad to be here coming from First Fruits Farm.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: You should have tried hugging my dad. It`s not as bad as you might think.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Look, look, I know I look like a big teddy bear, but you`ve got to be warned, don`t try and hug the bears.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Are you still giving away what you grow?\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Definitely. To date, we have donated more than a million pounds of food and still growing.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: How do you afford to do that?\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: We have thousands of volunteers. They will give up a Saturday morning to come get their hands dirty, and help to harvest and grow all of this awesome food.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: What have you gained from your sacrifice? It seems illogical that sacrifice could get you something.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Oh, well guess what? All good things come with sacrifice. You`re not going to be able to get the awesome juice from the berries or the grapes, unless they`re squeezed. You`re not able to get diamonds unless they`re put under pressure, all right, and going through sacrifices, that`s what actually brings out the best in us.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Beautiful. Jason, thank you so much for what you do and for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 JASON BROWN: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You know that saying, give till it hurts. I think we need to change that to give till it hurts and then keep giving till it feels great.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Until then, stay kind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When Meryl asked the question, I was wondering, how does Jason afford to do this? But then he said, it is all about volunteers. How much should we love Jason? I love what he says, he thinks of a life of greatness. He thinks of a life of service. That`s what greatness means. I loved everything about him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s so good because as athletes, we`re taught that we have to sacrifice our bodies and our time with our loved ones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he is saying, you know I`m tired of that. I`m tired of breaking down my body. How about I break a sweat and sacrifice my time to help feed people. That is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve never heard of that story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gorgeous. I love that story so much. I love the follow up, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very nice. I do, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. What did he say, give till it hurts and then --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And keep giving until it feels good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Keep on giving until it feels great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Steve Hartman, they did it again. Love them and the family.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, we will talk with Usher about performing at the \uc2 Apple\uc2  Music Super Bowl LVIII halftime show. You know, it`s going to be right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, what will it mean for him to take the world`s biggest stage? He is going to join us in just a bit so you can find out.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:08, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, we`ll talk about advocate for millions of people around the world, people she believes are often mislabeled and overlooked.\par \par \uc2 Her new book, it is a memoir, it`s been called the must-read memoir of the fall. We will get into it.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our next guest today is Alicia Roth Weigel. She is an activist and Human Rights Commissioner in Austin, Texas and now also a first time author.\par \par \uc2 Weigel identifies as intersex, which is the I in LGBTQIA and intersex people are generally born with both male and female biological characteristics.\par \par \uc2 Here is Weigel talking about her own experience in the documentary "Every Body."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL ("Intersex Cowgirl" author): I was born intersex between the two sexes. I have physical and anatomical traits that don`t fall neatly into that male-female box that birth certificates would make you think is how the world is.\par \par \uc2 You feel so alone and isolated and like --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Weigel is now out with a new memoir. It is called "Inverse Cowgirl" and she writes: "Every human sometimes feels like a Lone Ranger, but the more we signal our weirdness to the world, the easier it`ll be to find one another and feel more at home."\par \par \uc2 Alicia Roth Weigel, good morning. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Thanks so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the book is called "Inverse Cowgirl." It`s an eye- catching cover, and that title definitely gets attention. You say, it`s anyone who turns societal norms upside down.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes, So "Inverse Cowgirl" came from the fact that I`m intersex in Texas. But more broadly, yes, it`s anyone who has felt like they don`t fit into whatever prescribed box that society wants to place you in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very early in the book, which by the way, is funny, it`s insightful. I can see why people have called it the must-read memoir or the fall, you make the point that about two percent of the world`s population falls into this category of intersex, which makes it as common as redheads.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Which I have two in my family, by the way.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what has your experience been like? And how does it fit into the sort of broader category?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes, so my experience growing up was just one of total isolation. And just thinking there was no one else like me. I was totally and utterly alone. And no one wants to feel like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: I also was pressured to never share my truth, and I think when you grow up hiding and needing to present a facade to the world, that really takes a toll on you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not even your brother, which --\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes. Never told even my own brother.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I second Tony`s emotion about this book. I think it`s a must read for people.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I have to say, I`m embarrassed. I had never even heard this term before, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: But I had never heard it until I was 27.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I had never heard it. So I want people to understand exactly who you are. You write about you are born with a vagina, but you have internal testes that were removed when you were a baby.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And because of that, what happened to your body? You couldn`t have a period.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You couldn`t have children.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have no body odor.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, and so you were also told, don`t tell anybody about this. What did you think was happening? What did you think you were? Because you said you didn`t even know what it was that it had a name until you were 27. So what did you think it was.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: They always told me I had a disorder and I was being fixed, and that`s the language that all intersex people regardless of what variation you`re born with, we receive that from doctors and from society, that we are a problem inherently because of how we are born and we are being fixed.\par \par \uc2 But when you`re told that you have a disorder, that means you`re pathologizing our existence, when there`s actually nothing wrong with us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: We are just born different. And then by trying to fix us, they often break us, like they did me. By removing my testes that forced my body into hormonal withdrawal. That then leached calcium from my bones to where I now have osteoporosis in my early 30s.\par \par \uc2 So again, by trying to fix what -- if it ain`t broke, don`t fix it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And as you think about the messages that you were sent and how you hear certain things, you start to wear it like a heavy coat.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or a cologne that reeks.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And then one day you wake up, you say, no, I don`t need to wear these titles, these tags, what they`ve been placing on me.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What message do you have for kids that are out there that might be dealing with the same issues that you dealt with?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: First and foremost, you`re not alone. Second, there`s nothing wrong with you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: You`re beautiful. You`re perfect just the way you are. And there`s a whole beautiful community around the world that is here to embrace you.\par \par \uc2 So you know, go and \uc2 Google\uc2  intersex. There are plenty of organizations that can help you move through the process of claiming who you are and owning it with pride.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said that this book has been difficult for you to write, but you also said it`s been healing.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tell us both parts of that.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes. So the way that I describe writing a memoir is kind of like doing therapy on your whole life in a truncated timeframe and knowing the whole world is about to read the transcripts.\par \par \uc2 It`s definitely a doozy, but I`m glad I did it because it forced me to face myself and I think in this world, we often kind of run from things that we don`t want to face and it makes sense.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I watch trash reality TV sometimes to numb out, too, but I do think that by facing myself, all of myself, my full self, even the demons in the closet, the shadows, it helped me process all of these things that I`ve been through and move through them and I no longer carry those burdens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because you are very much a woman and feel like a woman.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And identify as such.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes, I mean, look at me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I mean your advocacy --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Touche.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Your advocacy at the end of it, you want to in part, be a matter of policy. So we are talking about doctors just before the cameras started rolling, how has the medical community shifted its approach to treating people like yourself?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Yes. So it is a slow process. Unfortunately, it stems from a lack of information. Most people in Med School get maybe one or two lines in their whole Med School textbook.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They don`t even know. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: They don`t even know. And so, we`ve been working directly with the Biden administration, and we`ll be releasing a report later this year on health inequities faced by intersex people.\par \par \uc2 I`m hoping that we can get that into the hands of doctors and elected officials across the country, so that we can finally do better by our community.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good job.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ROTH WEIGEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, the book "Inverse Cowgirl" is on sale right now.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, "Talk of the Table." We`ll be back in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In case you missed it, we have an exclusive deal on non- slip no show socks in \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BELLMAN (Lifestyle expert): Me and my husband both love these. It is the only style thing that we agree on.\par \par \uc2 This silicone on the back of the sock is going to keep it around your heel, so it`s not going to slip down. That`s what`s going to cause the blisters.\par \par \uc2 I wear these with my sneakers. I wore these with booties the other day. My husband loves them with all of his shoes. They`re so comfortable. They wash so nicely.\par \par \uc2 Lots of different bundles, color, sizes, starting at $26.97. I`m telling you, I don`t want any other no-see-um socks. I love these socks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I worked out in the socks and they didn`t slide at all, which was shocking.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BELMAN: Right so shocking. Because so many socks let us down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My quality.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And as you just heard with our exclusive discount, you can get the Cloud sock starting at $26.97. That`s up to 31 percent off the retail price. It`s a great deal.\par \par \uc2 To start shopping now, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com, and be sure to text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000 to get early access to some of our most popular deals, and tune in tomorrow for even more deals including a weekly pill organizer to keep you organized available at 30 percent off the retail price.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, I wore the slip socks this weekend and guess