ut this is the thing, nobody doubts that you can do it including you. But as you sit here today, do you have any concerns you thought, I`ve got to make sure that this goes right -- is there any -- are you even kind of nervous?\par \par \uc2 USHER: You know what? I`m living in the flow. Obviously, something I`m doing in this time is connecting and I feel home isn`t just an actual destination, it is a comfort in myself, it is being able to be in this flow.\par \par \uc2 I`m so happy to be here in Paris and have the opening of my show last night while announcing my -- celebrating my daughter`s birthday coming back to Vegas a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 USHER: There is -- something`s happening, so I`m not worried as much. I think God got it. I think he`s definitely --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. God got it and Usher --\par \par \uc2 USHER: He is definitely --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Even the plant looks healthy behind you. Everything`s --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Everything is going --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everything is growing well over there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Usher, you`ve got it, too. You`ve got it, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are having what they call a really great week -- your daughter`s birthday, this announcement, and it`s only going to get better from here. I can`t wait to see what you do.\par \par \uc2 Please know, we are cheering you on. Always. Can`t wait. Can`t wait to see you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You got it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got it bad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you`ve got it. You`ve got it bad. We got it bad for you.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Usher.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Usher.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The \uc2 Apple\uc2  Music Super Bowl LVIII halftime show in Vegas will airs Sunday February 11 right here where? Where, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is time to check your local whether, it is 8:41.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The family behind the best-selling "Dork Diaries" series is taking readers on a journey to Europe with plenty of drama along the way.\par \par \uc2 "Dork Diaries 15: Tales From Not-So-Posh Paris Adventure" follows awkward teen, Nikki on a trip to the French capital. It`s published by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , part of our parent family company.\par \par \uc2 All right, both author Rachel Renee Russell and her daughter, Nikki Russell, who co-wrote and illustrated the book join us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. How are y`all doing?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI RUSSELL ("Dork Diaries 15: Tales From a Not-So-Posh Paris Adventure" author):: Good morning, doing well. Happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, we`re happy to have you.\par \par \uc2 Rachel, take us to the beginning. How did "Dork Diaries" come about?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL ("Dork Diaries 15: Tales From a Not-So-Posh Paris Adventure" co-writer and illustrator): Well, actually, "Dork Diaries is based on my two daughters. When they were in middle school, they got good grades, they were hard workers and teachers loved them.\par \par \uc2 But they were tall for their age, so they were teased a little bit and bullied and called dorks. And they had a really, really hard time in middle school.\par \par \uc2 So when I decided to write a book, I decided to, I wanted it to be fun and funny, but I wanted it to be inspiring and something that other kids could read and to help the situations that they`ve felt they were in, so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the message was what basically?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL: The message was to when you`re being bullied to be strong. Report to an adult and to find other kids that are like you, because there`s friends out there for everyone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nikki, what`s it like working with your mother? Because I`m curious what happens when you all disagree with an illustration? And how you all collaborate together?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI RUSSELL: Oh, it`s simple. She grounds me. No, really, because I`m older now, we have to find a civil way to handle it. So we talk it over.\par \par \uc2 I`m in my office that`s actually right in her home, so I get to collaborate by just walking across the home and stepping into her office and talking it over.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got a pretty good thing going on. I mean, it is our 15th book together, so, we`ve obviously got into the madness.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL: And usually she`s right because I`m older and she`s younger, and she`s more in tune with, you know what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, okay, Mama. All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t feel that way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Gayle`s always right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t feel that either, but I don`t get to her just because she is younger.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikki, the character is based on real-life experiences of yours.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI RUSSELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there is one in particular where she says that moms are notorious for oversharing photos. Do we have photos? And is that based on a real experience?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI RUSSELL: I think we actually do, because she is the queen of embarrassing photos.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Oversharing them? Mom?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Comments?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL: Yes. Well, when we go --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do we have photos?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL: We go on book tours at schools and there are some photos of her that I love, so I can share them, like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nikki, what is wrong with that picture?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI RUSSELL: Oh, gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s wrong?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI RUSSELL: Oh no. You`ve got me wearing braces.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Cutie pie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, as a mom, you just look at it, and you say, that`s is a great thing. One of the main lessons -- I love this -- is never give up on your dreams. And the best memories you said, because we live in the selfie age where everybody takes a picture of every damn thing, you said, the best memories are stored in your what?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL: In your heart.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL: In your heart. Exactly. Not on your phone always. Phones are wonderful. But we want to keep things in our hearts and our minds.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI RUSSELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rachel, there`s talk about "Dork Diaries" movie. Is that happening?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL: We`re still working on it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL: I`m passionate about keeping the film in theaters, so I want a theatrical release, so girls can go to the movie theaters and see themselves there, so that`s important to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And never giving up on your dreams.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL RENEE RUSSELL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nikki, talk about that, never giving up on your dreams.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI RUSSELL: So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is this a dream for the two of you?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI RUSSELL: Oh goodness, yes. This is just such a blessing. I have this little saying, failure is the prerequisite of success. So you may fall down along the way, but it prepares you for doing better.\par \par \uc2 It teaches you what you`re making mistakes on and helps you to improve, and also it humbles you for a moment like this. You can help others know that if they fail, they too will eventually succeed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, thank you so much for joining us. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right, Rachel Renee Russell and Nikki Russell, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Dork Diaries 15: Tales From A Not-So-Posh Paris Adventure" goes on sale tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, a little Usher. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re very excited about Usher, but you know what else we`re excited about? Happy birthday, 90th Birthday. This is Alicia Alford -- she is one of our top producers here. It`s not her birthday. It`s her grandmother`s birthday. And she turns 90. Happy Birthday, Mrs. Alford.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Birthday to you from us -- from us to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we are so psyched about Usher, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, we can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And thanks to Usher today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Birthday to Mrs. Alford. And Usher, can`t wait. We`ll see you --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230925ej9p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For September 23, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,106 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: With days to go before a government shutdown, Congress is paralyzed by infighting.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES (R-Louisiana): The arsonists have lit their house on fire. They`re whining about their house burning.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Fiery words from top Republicans who worked through the weekend to find a way forward on funding the government before month`s end.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Will you work with Democrats?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): Look, I believe we have a majority here and we can work together to solve this.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can Republicans unify and strike a deal that also works for the Democratic-controlled Senate? We will have the latest on negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Then:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS (U.S. Secretary of Homeland Security): Our immigration system is absolutely broken, and Congress needs to fix it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: America`s Southern border is under strain once again, with migrant crossings on track to hit record highs.\par \par \uc2 We will ask Texas Republican Tony Gonzales what he is seeing in his district and whether the issue could complicate spending negotiations on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Plus:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STRIKER: No justice!\par \par \uc2 STRIKER: No Jeeps!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The labor protest targeting America`s Big Three automakers expands, as workers in 38 locations in 20 states go on strike.\par \par \uc2 We will hear this morning from Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio- Cortez\uc2 , who`s headed to Missouri later today to join a picket line.\par \par \uc2 Arizona Democratic Senator Mark Kelly just got back from a trip to Ukraine. We will ask him what he learned.\par \par \uc2 Former Trump National Security Adviser Robert O`Brien will also join us.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, a conversation with the first lady of Ukraine, \uc2 Olena Zelenska\uc2 , about the horrors of war and the hard work of healing.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 It`s setting up to be another consequential week in Washington, with just a few days to go before a possible government shutdown unless Congress strikes an 11th-hour deal to fund it.\par \par \uc2 We`ll get to that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with New York Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio- Cortez\uc2  on the expanding autoworkers strike.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This strike now in 20 states, I know you are headed to Missouri later to strike outside a GM facility.\par \par \uc2 The president goes to Michigan Tuesday. He only announced that after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he was going to \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike. Do you think injecting this high level of presidential politics complicates getting a settlement?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Well, I think, right now, we are in such a crisis in our economy, a crisis of inequality, that it is going to take a level of political and popular support unlike that that we`ve seen in a strike situation like this in recent and modern history in order for us to get a breakthrough.\par \par \uc2 What we`ve seen at the Big Three are CEOs giving themselves as much as a 40 percent wage and -- and compensation increase, while workers have actually seen a real wage decrease over -- over the last 10 to 15 years. And in order for us to break that norm, I think it`s going to take an unprecedented level of involvement to make sure that workers get what they deserve here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: UAW`s president, when he was with us last week said, when I asked him why the union hadn`t yet endorsed President Biden, he said that has to be earned.\par \par \uc2 That sounds like he`s disappointed that party leadership is not doing as much as they could. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Well, I think it always has to be earned.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden showing up to the picket line on Tuesday is a historic, historic event. We have never seen in modern history a president show up to a picket line like this. And I think it should be earned. It needs to be earned. And I believe that President Biden is -- is working towards that, especially when he lands in Michigan on Tuesday to earn that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, one of the things that`s happening in the auto industry, as you know, is this market shift and transition to electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 You were quoted back in July saying you look forward to buying a union-made electric vehicle. But you buy -- but you currently have a non-union-made \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . \uc2 UAW\uc2  already makes some electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, why wasn`t that -- is it a problem with the quality? Is it a problem with the style? Is the market just not there?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: No, the -- our car was purchased during the pandemic, when travel -- before a vaccine had come out. So, travel between New York and Washington, the safest way that we had determined was an E.V.\par \par \uc2 But that was prior to some of the new models coming out on the market that had the range available. But we`re actually looking into trading in our car now. So, we`re looking into it. And, hopefully, we will soon.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who has majority share in \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , has said a lot of things against the unions.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of these electric vehicle battery manufacturing plants as well are not unionized. So there`s tension here.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 And it`s something that I have been speaking with President Fain about, because one thing that we`re all on the same page about is that we do not want the transition from a fossil fuel economy to an electric economy to -- to represent an erosion in the unionization and rights of workers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You were quoted in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " in August as saying: "Immigration is arguably this administration`s weakest issue."\par \par \uc2 What did you mean?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Well, I think the politics around immigration in the United States is, to no surprise to many people, one of the most contentious issues.\par \par \uc2 We saw this dramatically inflamed under President Trump, but it remains to be a very controversial and contentious issue. And that makes -- I believe that makes enacting -- enacting some of the policy changes necessary much more complicated and difficult.\par \par \uc2 Now, this week, after perhaps almost a year of pushing from both the Hispanic -- the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2  to the New York delegation in Congress, we pushed to make sure that the Biden administration extended Temporary Protective Status, otherwise known as TPS, for Venezuelans, which will allow and open the pathway for Venezuelan migrants to actually begin working and supporting themselves, which will reduce the strain on our public systems, particularly New York`s shelter system, and more.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s 500,000 Venezuelans, but only if they came here before July.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So does Congress need to do something, like the HIRE Act that one of our guests, Tony Gonzales, has put forward or others to -- to put that into law, so it`s not just a one-off?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Well, I definitely think that we need to have comprehensive immigration reform, so that we aren`t constantly doing this patchwork of policy extensions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That hasn`t happened for decades.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: That has not happened for decades.\par \par \uc2 But, additionally, I think we also need to examine the root of this problem, because if we are constantly engaging in foreign policy that drives people to our Southern border, in this specific instance, U.S. sanctions that were originally authored by \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  began and precipitated, certainly took a large part in the driving of populations to our Southern border.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after those sanctions, those broad-based sanctions...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re talking about in Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after those broad-based sanctions were enacted, we started seeing dramatic increases in these populations that were coming to our Southern border. And so we have to address the root of these population movements and the migration crisis. And we have to also have to address the domestic U.S. policy issues when it comes to immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you know the Maduro government has also been responsible for large parts of that.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you saying that you want to -- you want the Biden administration to pull back pressure on him?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: I think we need to reexamine the nature of these sanctions.\par \par \uc2 There are sanctions that are very specific, for example, the Magnitsky Act sanctions, that do actually focus on the decision makers and people who may be violating norms, practices, civil rights, but broad-based sanctions that punish the overall economy and harm everyday working people that are driving them into the economic and political destitution, that force millions of people, both not just to the United States, but even to our regional partners.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  of New Jersey, as you know, has just been indicted on bribery charges.\par \par \uc2 Should he resign? And what do you think of his statement that it has to do with him being a Latino?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Well, I think it`s -- the situation is quite unfortunate, but I do believe that it is in the best interest for Senator Menendez to resign in this moment.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, consistency matters. It shouldn`t matter whether it`s a Republican or a Democrat. The details in this indictment are extremely serious. They involve the nature of -- of not just his, but all of our seats in Congress.\par \par \uc2 And while, you know, as a Latina, there are absolutely ways in which there is systemic bias, but I think what is here in this indictment is quite clear. And -- and I believe is in the best interest to maintain the integrity of the seat.\par \par \uc2 I want to emphasize that all people are -- they must be extended the presumption of innocence until proven guilty.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Some Republican members like Matt Gaetz have threatened to try to oust Speaker McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 If he threw a motion to vacate, would you vote with him as a Democrat to get to 218 to oust Speaker McCarthy?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: I mean, I think that -- we would have to cross that bridge when we get to it.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McCarthy has been very weak. I think that he has also engaged in just absolutely terrible decision-making for the American people, from continuing to try to cement denying the right to an abortion among women, to the denial of -- to policy that denies the reality of climate change...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: ... to basic fiscal irresponsibility and recklessness.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: We are at the brink of a shutdown right now.\par \par \uc2 And so, absolutely, I think there is grounds. However, we are also in the midst of an extremely chaotic Republican Party. And we do not want chaos to reign in Washington either.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congresswoman, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ: Thank you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to Texas Republican Congressman Tony Gonzales, who joins us from San Antonio this morning.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, a lot to get to with you, but I want to start on that question of what we should brace for in the coming days in regard to a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 You had said you opposed a short-term deal, a continuing resolution, to keep the government funded in the near-term. Speaker McCarthy`s floated a 45-day short-term deal with border provisions added into it. Are you still a hard no?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES (R-Texas): I think -- yes, good morning, Margaret. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 Continuing resolutions don`t solve the problem. They just kick the can down the road. And so we have to lock ourselves in the room and solve the problem. And the way you do that is by passing House conservative appropriation bills and working with the Senate.\par \par \uc2 This is -- I called it a month ago and I said we`re on a path to a shutdown, because Speaker McCarthy wants that and President Biden wants that. Everyone feels as if this is a political problem, that they can just blame one another.\par \par \uc2 Who`s going to get hurt the most is American -- the American public. So we have to come together, and we have to pass appropriations bills. That`s where I`m at.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But in order to pass those 11 bills -- only one`s -- one of the 12 has been passed -- you need time.\par \par \uc2 And then you got to send it to the Senate. Then they have to reconcile it. Then you got to send it to the president. You don`t have that kind of time, most likely. So, without signing on to a short-term patch, are you basically saying you want to see a shutdown?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I don`t want to see a shutdown, but, in my experience, if you give Congress more time, they`re just going to take that time.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t -- time does not equal solutions, and the exact opposite. If there is a hard cliff, then they are forced to come together. And that`s what I think we need. We need to have a hard line that forces everyone to get in the room and pass these bills.\par \par \uc2 I don`t want to see a shutdown, but there is no doubt in my mind that the country is headed for a shutdown, and everyone should prepare as such.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you have confidence in Speaker McCarthy`s ability to lead?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: You know, I wouldn`t bet on Kevin McCarthy, but I also wouldn`t bet against him.\par \par \uc2 I`m in a district where I`m just trying to keep my head above water. You know, this border crisis is very real. It`s -- it`s -- Margaret, it`s very similar to when a school shooting hits your town. And, sadly, too many Americans know what that`s like, or a hurricane.\par \par \uc2 And this border crisis as -- is as if a Category 4 hurricane has hit. So I don`t have the time to -- to pontificate on political futures for other -- other people. We`re just trying to keep our head above water here in Texas.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, if there is a shutdown, are the Border Patrol agents in your district prepared to show up to work at a time of migrant spike and not get paid?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: You know, that`s the -- that`s the ugly part of a shutdown is, you`re going to have -- you`re going to have real people get hurt.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, Border Patrol agents are showing up to operate in processing centers. They`re not out in the field, so they`re not really doing their jobs. And they haven`t been doing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: And many -- many agents have told me: You know what, Tony?\par \par \uc2 Right now -- I mean, I will give you an example. In El Paso, the facility, 200 Border Patrol agents are in that soft-sided facility taking care of migrants, meaning they`re not out in the field protecting America from bad actors. So, in many cases, they might as well already be shut down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Who is preventing that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: It`s -- it`s the failed Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 You know, when we talk about the border crisis, we know what the problem is. The problem is -- is Joe Biden`s failed immigration policies. We see the images. We hear the stories, but we never hear solutions. Let`s -- let`s talk some solutions.\par \par \uc2 One, I think the House should immediately take up the Homeland appropriations bill. Republicans can`t just be the party of rhetoric. We have to be the party of solutions. And there are some solutions in this bill. One of those is, I have been pushing very hard and many of my colleagues to end catch-and-release.\par \par \uc2 I have an amendment in that Homeland bill that ends catch-and-release. Another one is, once again, the Border Patrol agents, they`re -- they`re out of the game. The -- the folks that are doing the real work are the sheriffs and the deputies. And -- and there`s a program called Stone -- Stonegarden, and there`s a $10 million upgrade for that.\par \par \uc2 This helps with manpower and equipment to help fight the border crisis. But the -- the third piece, which I would argue is most important...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: ... is repatriation flights.\par \par \uc2 And when I met with Secretary Mayorkas earlier this week, that`s what I asked him for, repatriation flights, meaning, if somebody does not qualify for asylum, you don`t bus them to New York, you don`t send them to L.A., you don`t let them go other places. You send them back to their country of origin.\par \par \uc2 If we do that, that is how the crisis ends short term. And then, long term, there needs to be some movement on immigration reform, and in Congress.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 But you can`t do that with Venezuelans, part of the problem here. Secretary Mayorkas took the president of Honduras to the border yesterday. They have sent military members to work in offices. They`ve granted half-a-million Venezuelans these work permits, potentially. Do you support any of that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: In my eyes, that`s going the wrong way.\par \par \uc2 Eight hundred -- 800 troops to the border solves nothing. They might as well be human cones if you don`t allow them to interact with migrants. You need to send immigration judges to the border and get these cases heard in days and not years, and, if they do -- do not qualify, once again, send them back.\par \par \uc2 You know, the -- the granting work visas to -- to the Venezuelans, 500,000, by the way, it only makes immigration reform more difficult in Congress, because, essentially, what you`ve done is, you -- you`ve encouraged those that have come over illegally. And these people have come over illegally into our country, and you`re giving them the access to work.\par \par \uc2 It undermines everything that we`re doing in Congress to try to come together. The real solution is finding Venezuela, working with Venezuela and other countries to take back some of their people. I would love to see Secretary Blinken -- I`m going to meet with him next week.\par \par \uc2 I would love to see Secretary Blinken spend more time in the hemisphere solving these difficult issues with some of our foreign -- foreign allies and partners.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Speaker McCarthy has said there are going to be some border-related provisions in this potential continuing resolution.\par \par \uc2 You just laid out a whole bunch of things you want. If those are put on the table, would you change your mind on a short-term deal?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: You know, Margaret, there`s -- there`s a difference between fantasy and reality.\par \par \uc2 And I live in reality. My district is hit with the realities of this border crisis. So I`m not looking for a messaging bill that says all the right things and accomplishes nothing. It`s once again why I sat down with Secretary Mayorkas this week and said...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: ... what are some real, tangible things that we can accomplish today?\par \par \uc2 So, in my eyes, a continuing resolution that just has fake things doesn`t get us any closer. What we need to do is draw a hard line in the sand and pass appropriation bills and move that over to the Senate and work with the Senate to get this ultimately signed by the president.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Congressman, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And the time clock is ticking on that.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in one minute, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to Arizona Democratic Senator Mark Kelly. He is in Austin, Texas, this morning.\par \par \uc2 Senator, it is great to have you back on the program.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY (D-Arizona): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You just -- you just came back from Ukraine, and you met as well with President Zelenskyy this week here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Can Ukraine count on that $20 billion that President Biden is asking Congress for?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Well, Margaret, thank you for having me on.\par \par \uc2 This was my second trip to Ukraine. I went in April. I had the chance to meet with commanders, U.S. military commanders, Ukrainian, Polish. The aid that we are giving them is critical. If this was the stop, they would lose, and Putin would win.\par \par \uc2 So, I am motivated to get this done in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  -- there`s bipartisan support for this -- in a C.R., providing funding for Ukraine. And so we`ve got to get this across the finish line, or this would be a disaster.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Bipartisan support in a short-term deal, you said.\par \par \uc2 Would you vote for a short-term deal if it didn`t have Ukraine aid in it? You know I`m asking you this because, the House, there`s a problem getting this through.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Hey, for -- for years, when we`ve done C.R.s, they`ve been bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 Senator McConnell feels like we should continue to support Ukraine, Senator Schumer as well. Working across the aisle in the Senate, you know, we can get this done. The dysfunction in the House is unprecedented. You know who really cares about whether or not we fund Ukraine or not and continue to provide the support? It`s the Chinese president.\par \par \uc2 This has implications across the globe. You know, I cannot -- I -- I`m -- I spent some private time with President Zelenskyy, you know, just this last week, and tried to reassure him that this is coming.\par \par \uc2 And if we do -- if we fail at this moment, there`s a chance that we -- a year from now, two years from now, we wind up in a situation where we could be in direct conflict with the Russians. We do not want that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 CBS\uc2  has confirmed that President Biden, in his meeting with President Zelenskyy, said he would provide Ukraine with ATACMS. These are these longer-range surface-to-surface missile systems that would allow them to hit behind Russian lines.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainians have been asking for months for these systems. Whether it was the F-16s or the ATACMS now, are you frustrated at how long it takes for approval to happen for these systems?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Yes, I mean, this conflict`s been going on for a year- and-a-half now.\par \par \uc2 And, in the beginning, we provided artillery, ammunition, eventually HIMARS. F-16s are not like other weapons systems. They`re complicated. You have to train pilots. You have to train maintainers. I worked with the administration on that. We`ve gotten them cluster weapons now. ATACMS is an additional capability. There were some issues we had to work through.\par \par \uc2 They`ve been, you know, stressing the need for this over -- over a period of time now. And we`re at the point that we`re going to provide them this capability. And I think it`s going to be helpful.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What restrictions should there be? Why the apprehension for so long?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Well, an artillery shell goes about 18 miles. An ATACM missile goes about 190 miles. So, there was concern, where -- where and against what targets would they use them?\par \par \uc2 We don`t want this to escalate. But we`re at the spot right now where they need an additional capability to maintain some more progress. The progress on this counteroffensive, it`s been good. You know, I talked to Ukrainian commanders.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: There are things that are, you know, starting to provide some, you know, problems, but we`re making progress there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 Very quickly, should Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  resign from his position?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Well, these are serious and shocking charges, bribery, corruption.\par \par \uc2 I have never seen anything like this. He stepped down from being chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee. That`s a serious step. I think Senator Menendez is going to have to think long and hard about the cloud that`s going to hang over his service in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, he is up for reelection. It sounds like you are drawing attention to some questions about that.\par \par \uc2 I have to take a quick...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Well, I mean, he`s -- he`s going to be in...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sorry. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: I was going to say, he`s going to be in court next week.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: And he`s -- he`s got to figure out whether he can adequately serve the people of New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have to take a quick break and continue this conversation with you on the other side of it. Senator, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you miss an episode of Face the Nation or want to see an extended interview, you can find it all on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and our Web site. Or, if you prefer to listen, you can subscribe to our podcast. Just search Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be back with Senator Mark Kelly, former National Security Adviser to Donald Trump Robert O`Brien, and our conversation with the first lady of Ukraine, \uc2 Olena Zelenska\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We continue our conversation now with Arizona Senator Mark Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Senator, the governor of your state said Friday the state`s overwhelmed. She was talking about migrants. We are on pace for a record number of border crossings.\par \par \uc2 The vice president told me recently that the administration`s policies are making progress. Do you buy that or does the administration need to change their approach here?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Margaret, here`s the thing. I mean the border in my state and others has been in a crisis for - I mean it`s generations, decades now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: And we`ve spent billions of dollars on border security, and it, you know, it`s still a problem. I`m on the phone with mayors and sheriffs and the border patrol agents frequently. I spoke to Secretary Mayorkas about this just three times last week. They`re making changes. Border security is national security. I`m really concerned about next week and the government shutting down. If there`s a government shutdown, border patrol agents are not going to get paid. This is going to - I mean if we think it`s bad today, just think about what that looks like.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, what is the real-world impact of that? Tony Gonzales, from Texas, was saying, you know, border patrol agents, he claims, are being prevented from doing their jobs in the first place right now and so it wouldn`t make that much of a difference. But why do you think practically it really would?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Well, border patrol agents are on the front line of this crisis. I mean so are - so are sheriffs. He mentioned sheriffs. But we`ve got thousands and thousands of border patrol agents on the border enforcing our laws and also processing, you know, asylum seekers. If they stay home, I mean this crisis will be compounded by an order of magnitude.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s unconscionable to think that Republicans in the House are going to allow the government to shut down under the circumstances we`re under today. I mean there`s always been bipartisan support for stop-gap spending bills. Having a government shutdown, I mean, it takes off -- you know, the growth of our economy is affected. It`s going to affect our air traffic control system. If you`re - if you`re going to travel over the holidays and there`s a government shutdown, I mean that`s going to impact you. Kids don`t get meals when there`s government shutdown. The military doesn`t get paid. So, these things -- these things are connected. We`ve got a crisis at the border and what the speaker is allowing to happen in the House of Representatives could make this worse.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator Kelly, thank you very much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 And we turn now to Robert O`Brien. He served as national security adviser under former President Trump, and he joins us this morning from Park City, Utah.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador, it`s good to have you here with us.\par \par \uc2 AMB. ROBERT O`BRIEN (Former White House National Security Adviser): Nice to be with you, Margaret. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about a number of national security matters, but I want to start on this idea of a potential government shutdown. National security wise, what do you think the message is to the world when basic matters of governance seem politically insurmountable, and the government shuts down?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: Well, what we used to say in the NSC in President Trump`s administration was, economic security is national security. And we`ve got too big of a debt and we`ve got to get the debt under control. I think Speaker McCarthy`s done a great job and negotiated a good deal and I think that it would be great if the Republicans got behind Speaker McCarthy. But I understand why there`s concern over the size of the debt and I understand the -- the frustration of some of the - the GOP members. I mean it`s understandable.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, but, I mean, Republicans have objected to even passing defense spending at this point. Aren`t we in a sort of dangerous moment?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: Well, I came out in favor of passing an NDAA and I think a lot of other conservatives, like \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  and Mike Waltz and others did. So, I think we need to get that defense bill passed immediately.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 You were the hostage envoy under President Trump before you became his national security adviser. And you did help bring three citizens home from Iran. This week we saw a number of Americans return in a very happy reunion. Siamak Namazi, Emad Sharghi, Morad Tahbaz. Why wasn`t the last administration able to get them out? Why is it so hard to bring home Americans from Iran?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: Well, we had unprecedented success in the Trump administration. As you know, Margaret, we brought home over 58 hostages from countries all over the world, North Korea, Iran, Russia. And -- but we had a ground rule we wouldn`t pay ransom. And look, I was so pleased to see Siamak and Morad and the others come home. I felt like I failed them by not getting them home. But we weren`t going to pay $6 billion, we weren`t going to pay a billion plus per hostage because that creates a market for hostages. It makes your blue passport worth a billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 And so I am -- I`m concerned that Americans traveling abroad are going to be targeted by countries like Russia and China and Iran, but also terrorist organizations who know that they can get a big ransom if - if we - if we pay these sorts of fees. And we just weren`t prepared to do it. But we had unprecedented success bringing Americans home from all over the world.\par \par \uc2 The other - the other - the other issue, Margaret, is, what is Iran going to do with that money? We saw this with the JCPOA. They took that money and they spent it on terrorism and on ballistic missiles and on nuclear programs. I`m afraid they`re going to build more drones that kill Ukrainians with the money we just gave them. So, I`m thrilled to see Siamak and Morad and the others home, but I`m - I`m very concerned about the - the look of the deal internationally.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, as you know, the current administration would argue that those funds are being held in a restricted account in Qatar, just like they were held in a restricted account in South Korea under the Trump administration, where Iran did have access, though they had difficulty getting to that money. Why is that not a sufficient level of protection?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: Well, the president from Iran came to UNGA last week and said he`ll use the money any way he pleases. It`s the government money of the people of Iran and he`ll use it however he wants. And - and we have to understand, Margaret, money is fungible.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: So, the money that they`re spending now on children`s hospitals and on food, they`ll use that for the military and for terrorism and take this money and replace it with the other programs.\par \par \uc2 So, money`s fungible. This money is going to the Iran regime. It`s a terrorist regime. It`s the largest state sponsor of terrorism. And it`s going to kill a lot of people, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Should travel be banned to Iran?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: I - I believe so. Absolutely. The problem is, is this happened after the JCPOA when we paid hundreds of millions for hostage. The price went up under the - for - the price went up from Obama to Biden. But as soon as they let a couple hostages go, Jason Rezaian and others, they just restocked the pool of hostages.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: They went and took a few more dual citizens who were visiting family in Iran and took them hostage.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: So, we`ve got to stop Americans going to Iran. But we`ve got to start thinking about Americans going to China and Russia as well, where they`re taking hostages and using this against us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about national security as it relates to the upcoming U.S. election as well.\par \par \uc2 You have been very clear that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the 2020 election. You helped the transition of power between administrations. CNN reported you considered resigning after January the 6th. Is that report true?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: That -- that`s an inaccurate report. I never considered resigning. My - my feeling as national security advisors is that, you took the job for the good days and the bad days. And the Abraham Accords --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That was a pretty bad day.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: That was a bad day, but, you know, we had - what had the Abraham Accords, we had Serbia Kosovo. We got \uc2 NATO\uc2  to spend money defending themselves, which is now helping Ukraine. So, President Trump had a really - really a successful foreign policy run.\par \par \uc2 But - but there are bad days too and that was a bad day, but I was going to stick around for the president and for the American people. And I - fortunately, I have a lot of senators and congressman who reached out and said, please stay, we`ve got foreign adversaries, China and Russia -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: Who will want to take advantage of the chaos. And so \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  and John Ratcliffe and myself, the national security team, Chris Miller over at defense, we stuck around and we sent a strong message to the Chinese, we sent a strong message to - message to the Russians, that the American people are united. That we`re -- we remain fundamentally strong. And we`re not going to let them take advantage of any domestic political discord in - in the United States. And I think that served the president well and I think it served the American people well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well -- well, given Donald Trump`s role in the events leading to that day, I wonder if you would serve in a second Trump administration?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: Look, I - I - I had great run as national security adviser and as a hostage envoy and I - I served the country well, I - I think, and -- but - but you always have to wait and see what the president of the United States asks you to do and what your family wants you to do. But I grew up in a household with a dad who was a Marine and a mom who was a big patriot. And, by the way, it`s her 80th birthday today, so happy birthday, mom.\par \par \uc2 And my - my feeling is, is the president asks you to serve, and I`ve got kids who are serving in the military, you salute and say, yes. But that`s up to President Trump if he wins, which I - I -- right now, according to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," he`s up by 10 points. So, it looks good for him. But that`s up to him and my family.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You don`t have any doubts?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN: Well, I think there`s going to be a great team. And I think we need a return. America looks weak now. We`re not weak. We`re fundamentally strong. But America looks very weak right now and we have to return to a posture of peace through strength or we`re going to have real problems with - with China, with Russia, with Iran in the future. And so I think anyone who believes in peace through strength and is asked to serve and has experience to do so should - should do so and defend America, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I know you cannot talk about the details of the two investigations brought by the special counsel Jack Smith because you were subpoenaed. So, we have to leave that part of the conversation there.\par \par \uc2 Robert O`Brien, thank you very much for joining us on FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ukrainian President Zelenskyy and his wife, Olena, visited Washington last week to press the case for more U.S. assistance in Ukraine`s fight against the Russian invasion. We spoke with the first lady during their visit with the aid of a Ukrainian government interpreter. We began by asking her about what life is like for Ukrainian children.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (First Lady Of Ukraine) (through translator): Unfortunately, only one third of Ukrainian children can now attend school because our schools have to be safe, to be located in safe areas, and we need schools with bomb shelters. And my foundation continues fundraising resources to ensure that we have comfortable bomb shelters in all Ukrainian schools.\par \par \uc2 Well, the rest of Ukrainian children, two-thirds continue going to school online because they live in - in the front-line areas. Many children left Ukraine to live abroad. When children were leaving Ukraine, sometimes they could only take their documents with them and left everything behind. That is why those children and teachers require laptops, require iPad to continue education. And we just have a lot of support, including from our American partners.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You spoke at the United Nations. And you said to a gathering of leaders there, the horrific sexual violence being conducted by the Russian military, including against children, a four-year-old child. What was it like in that room when you shared that?\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (through translator): We say that there are sexual crimes committed by Russian occupiers. And this is a war crime. Leadership of the Russian army allows Russian soldiers to do this. This is something they commit consciously. They try to threaten population in Ukraine to demonstrate that they are in charge.\par \par \uc2 When we address people from many countries and share this terrible numbers with them, indeed you see pain in the eyes of those people. You feel horror. But this is not enough. We have to unite our efforts to insure that those who committed those crimes face justice. Sometimes people are concerned this is a taboo in many cases to say that -- and acknowledge that you`ve been a victim of sexual crime. You need to be truly courageous to let others know that you`ve been -- become a victim yourself. And people will only start talking about it when they will see that those who committed those crimes have been taken to justice.\par \par \uc2 We know from witnesses, from people who saw those crimes being committed, from neighbors, that in some villages all women were raped. But not all those crimes have been documented. Because they are people who do not want to officially provide evidence about those crimes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s a powerful statement. You need to be courageous to admit you`ve been a victim.\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA: (Speaking in Foreign Language).\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, your -- your husband, President Zelenskyy, said that genocide is underway in Ukraine. He said Russians had elected their own Hitler. These are powerful statements. And I wonder, given the stakes of what you`re talking about, how you think about, in a capital like this, debate over whether to help continue Ukraine or not?\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (through translator): Well clearly it is a natural reaction of any individual who lives in a country which became a victim of genocide. You become deeply concerned that some people cannot understand what`s going on. But we hope that all Americans understand what`s going on. And we do not believe that this assistance that we now receive from Americans will stop.\par \par \uc2 Of course there are political debates and there are discussions, there are different opinions. Some people agree, they disagree. This is a democracy. But nevertheless, truth has to win because it is clear that truth is on Ukraine`s side.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Part of genocide is destroying a culture. And you`ve been working to get Ukrainian books out publicly. I saw you at the \uc2 Metropolitan Museum of Art\uc2  getting things translated.\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA: (Speaking in foreign language).\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that your way of fighting back?\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (through translator): That`s one of the ways for us to demonstrate who we really are. Culture is also the area of the battlefield. We see that the occupiers want to destroy our culture. We see hundreds of libraries which have been burned by the occupiers. Thousands of museums and cultural institutions have been destroyed. By destroying our identities, they just want to destroy our nation. And that is why we have to inform about our culture. And this is an important sector of our work.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The president also said Ukraine knows the names of tens of thousands of children and has evidence hundreds of thousands of other kids have been kidnapped by Russia. Do those children get to know their culture? What happens to them when Russia takes them? And - and how do you get them back?\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (through translator): We know about 19,500 children who were officially deported. These are the children who current on the occupied territory and we know that cases have been documented, they have been taken to Russia.\par \par \uc2 We manage to return back approximately 680 children. We have the least of children who are being looked for by their parents. There were a lot of children who started in orphanages and boarding schools, and those boarding schools were taken to Russia as an institution.\par \par \uc2 This is a true problem because these are kids. Sometimes they do not fully understand what`s going on. It is easy to manipulate them. Manipulate the emotions. Almost all children who we manage to return to Ukraine told us that in Russia they were told that no one cares about them in Ukraine, that no one is looking for them. They have been preparing them for adoption under directive (ph) of the Russian Federation.\par \par \uc2 So, they try to make them leave their roots, so to say. Made them forget that they`re Ukrainians. Of course, children who are almost reached their adulthood, they have great understanding of what`s going on, but small children can be much more susceptible to the Russian disinformation. And that is why when we spoke at the United Nations, we proposed to develop a new system of joint efforts that would make Russia return Ukrainian children to their country. We are responsible for these children. We cannot play with those children`s destinies. It is not human.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There`s a warrant out for Vladimir Putin`s arrest because of this program.\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (through translator): Well, this was a very powerful political statement, and we very much hope that one day this statement will be fully implemented.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I do want to ask you, you were a comedy writer before the war.\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (Speaking in English): Former. Former.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Former.\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (Speaking in English): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And now you have this extraordinarily life and circumstance and you`ve sacrificed a lot with your family. Do you get to spend time with your children, your husband, together as a family?\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (through translator): No, I have no complaints because when you see what`s happening to people close to him, the problems of our family cannot be compared to those things which have currently faced by other Ukrainian families. Children dying. Children are being killed.\par \par \uc2 The president lives at work. Sometimes we see each other once a week. Sometimes we see each other several times a week. But my children next to me, and I`m confident that we will overcome because we know what is the goal, what is the final goal. We have to see our victory. We have to see happy faces around us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When people in America think of Ukraine, they think of President Zelenskyy and your family. How would you feel about him running again in 2024 if elections are held?\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (through translator): Well, you know, this is a very difficult question for me. You know, even when he ran for the first time I didn`t fully endorse it. But if he runs again when he -- if he runs for the second time, if he decides that it is necessary, well, we have some experience. We`ve been there. It is not as scary as - as it was in the first time.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know whether he has made this decision or not. It will depend on the situation in our country and the situation and the possibility of organizing free and fair elections. It will also depend on whether our society would need him as a president. If he will feel that Ukrainian society will no longer wish him to be the president, he will probably not run. But I will support him, whatever decision he takes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Whatever decision.\par \par \uc2 Madam First Lady, thank you, Madam Zelenska, for your time.\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (Speaking in English): Thank you. Thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You can watch our extended conversation on facethenation.com.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230924ej9o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 22, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Holly Williams, Manuel Bojorquez, David Martin,  Errol Barnett, Kris Van Cleave, Alex Wilson, Scott MacFarlane \par \uc2 3,693 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A remarkable day here in Washington tonight, a powerful U.S. senator accused of taking lavish bribes and using his position and influence for personal gain. The new stunning details.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Robert Menendez\uc2  and his wife charged with corruption. Prosecutors say they found nearly $500,000 of cash in his home stuffed in envelopes and clothing, plus the gold bars and luxury vehicle.\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS\uc2  (U.S. Attorney, Southern District of New York): Here are three kilograms of gold together worth approximately $150,000.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We`re tracking Tropical Storm Ophelia, as it barrels up the East Coast, with strong winds and heavy rain. How it will impact your weekend plans?\par \par \uc2 More autoworkers walk out on the job and onto the picket line. What we`re learning tonight about the investigation into that deadly bus crash involving a high school marching band, as the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  arrives on the scene.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Migrant crossings at the U.S. Southern border have risen to near record levels this month.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: As thousands continue to make the dangerous journey to the U.S. border, communities on this side say their resources are stretched thin.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re hearing audio of a 911 call in South Carolina after a U.S. Marine pilot ejected from an F-35 fighter jet Sunday, leaving the plane to fly on autopilot until it crashed.\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH: I`m sorry, what happened?\par \par \uc2 PILOT: I`m the pilot. We need to get, uh, rescue rolling. I`m not sure where the airplane is.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: If you have a \uc2 Costco\uc2  mattress, listen up. About 48,000 mattresses sold at \uc2 Costco\uc2  are now under recall after customers reported mold growing on them.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And an out-of-this-world discovery, as the first U.S. sample of an asteroid heads to Earth.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 And we begin with the shocking new details in the federal indictment of a powerful U.S. senator. And, tonight, the calls for New Jersey`s \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  to resign are growing, including from the governor of his state and from his own party, who say the three-term senator needs to step down immediately.\par \par \uc2 A grand jury indicted the Democratic senator and his wife on three counts of conspiracy to commit bribery, extortion, and fraud in a case alleging he aided a foreign government and New Jersey businessmen. The 39-page indictment just unsealed today is astonishing in its scope.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors found hundreds of thousands of dollars inside the lawmaker`s house, with cash stuffed in his congressional jacket with his name on it, and then these gold bars that were wrapped in paper towels. In a statement, Menendez denies any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 This is his second indictment in 10 years, which is believed to be the first time in American history that a sitting U.S. senator has been indicted on two separate criminal cases.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill with all of the details.\par \par \uc2 Scott, I have read through this indictment. The charges here, the accusations are stunning in their detail.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And they are rocking Capitol Hill, Norah.\par \par \uc2 The senator faces prosecution and calls from inside his own party to resign, accused of leveraging his powerful seat here at the Senate to enrich himself.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): The indictment against New Jersey Democrat \uc2 Robert Menendez\uc2  and his wife alleges vast corruption, trading political favors with three New Jersey businessmen in exchange for riches.\par \par \uc2 Federal agents say, when they raided the family home last summer, they found $100,000 worth of gold bars, $480,000 in cash, some stuffed in envelopes found in a jacket bearing Menendez`s name and outside, in the driveway, a $60,000 \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  convertible allegedly given to them by one of the businessmen he helped.\par \par \uc2 The indictment says agents discovered a \uc2 Google\uc2  search by Menendez asking, "How much is one kilo of gold worth?"\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS\uc2  (U.S. Attorney, Southern District of New York): The senator and his wife accepted hundreds of thousands of dollars of bribes in exchange for Senator Menendez using his power and influence to protect and to enrich those businessmen.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Prosecutors allege Menendez wielded his Senate Foreign Relations Committee chairmanship to help one of the businessmen, American Egyptian Will Hana, secure a lucrative contract in Egypt and helped Hana get sensitive information about the U.S. Embassy in Cairo, which was ultimately shared with the Egyptian government.\par \par \uc2 They say the scheme started in 2018, just months after a mistrial was declared in a prior corruption case Menendez was involved in.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from November 16, 2017)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROBERT MENENDEZ\uc2  (D-New Jersey): I have made my share of mistakes, but my mistakes were never a crime.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The new indictment details the involvement of Menendez`s wife, Lebanese-born Nadine Arslanian, whom he married in 2020 after 10 months of dating.\par \par \uc2 The senator is seen here serenading her at India`s Taj Mahal --\par \par \uc2 (Senator \uc2 Robert Menendez\uc2  singing)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: -- before proposing.\par \par \uc2 Arslanian is accused of taking a no-show job with Hana, who allegedly had close ties to Egyptian intelligence. And, according to the indictment, Hana, in a text message to an Egyptian general, referred to Menendez as "our man."\par \par \uc2 The 69-year-old senator who will temporarily step aside from his role as committee chairman blasted the charges, saying: "The excesses of these prosecutors is apparent. They have misrepresented the normal work of a congressional office, and, on top of that, they have attacked my wife."\par \par \uc2 Congressional ethics watchdog Noah Bookbinder:\par \par \uc2 No matter the outcome of this case, no matter the party, you think this damages public trust?\par \par \uc2 NOAH BOOKBINDER (Executive Director, Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington): Corruption in any party, any part of the ideological spectrum, is damaging to the American people`s faith in government.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The indictment could complicate Democrats` attempts to hold onto their slim Senate majority next year. Menendez himself is up for reelection, hasn`t announced any plans to resign his seat.\par \par \uc2 It would be New Jersey`s Governor, \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 , who would declare his replacement, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, there`s some breaking news, as parts of the East Coast are preparing for heavy rains, strong winds and flooding, with Tropical Storm Ophelia forming in the Atlantic. Beaches in North Carolina and Virginia are already seeing dangerous surf and rip currents.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in meteorologist Alex Wilson from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WILSON (Meteorologist, \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Ophelia set to make a mess of a lot of folks` weekends. As of the latest update, we now have hurricane watches, in addition to tropical storm warnings across sections of the Carolina and Virginia coastline, expecting heavy rain as well. Some areas could see three to five inches of rainfall.\par \par \uc2 We look at the wind advisories also to account for the wind gust potential, high wind warnings closer to the coast. So falling trees will definitely be a concern, power outages possible, storm surge an issue as well, four to six feet along portions of the North Carolina coast.\par \par \uc2 Then we have got coastal flood watches, coastal flood warnings and coastal flood advisories. Because of the rainfall potential, flood watches in effect for parts of North Carolina and Virginia. And, Norah, the heaviest rain begins to advance in tonight, continues tomorrow all the way through Monday -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the unprecedented strike against Detroit`s Big Three automakers is expanding after the companies failed to make progress in negotiations. And then, late today, President Biden said he will travel to Michigan on Tuesday to join the picket line and stand in solidarity with the men and women of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave continues his reporting from Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: One day longer, one day stronger.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Across 20 states, coast to coast, approximately 5,600 more autoworkers walked off the job at 38 parts distribution centers belonging to \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , as their union expanded its weeklong strike against the Big Three.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (President, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ): Our stand-up strike strategy is designed to do one thing, win record contracts after years of record profits.\par \par \uc2 Nelter Brantley joined the strike today.\par \par \uc2 NELTER BRANTLEY (GM Production Operator): I hope it`s the last one. We`re out here for a reason, and I hope that they hear our reason and just, you know, meet us halfway.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Shutting down parts distribution means consumers may soon feel the impact of the strike as car dealers run out of parts for repairs.\par \par \uc2 MARICK MASTERS (Business Professor, \uc2 Wayne State University\uc2 ): They`re regarded as cash cows for the companies.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 Wayne State University\uc2  business Professor Marick Masters:\par \par \uc2 MARICK MASTERS: It affects customer relations, and the union wants to have an impact on the companies where it will be most keenly felt. And they know that they are dependent on their customer base.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Already on strike are nearly 13,000 union employees at three Midwest plants.\par \par \uc2 STRIKERS: Fair pay, fair benefits!\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: General Motors called the expanded walkout unnecessary and criticized a series of leaked messages obtained by "The Detroit News" apparently from a senior \uc2 UAW\uc2  official saying the union aimed to inflict operational chaos, adding: "If we can keep them wounded for months, they don`t know what to do, and they`re still helpless to stop it."\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  tells CBS News it now questions if the \uc2 UAW\uc2  ever had an interest in reaching an agreement in a timely manner.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Union President Shawn Fain dismissed those messages essentially as strategic planning.\par \par \uc2 The union did not expand its strike against Ford today, saying they had made real progress, finding agreement on things like cost of living and job protections. But the two sides remain far apart when it comes to pay -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Kris Van Cleave, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, federal investigators are at the scene of that horrific bus crash on a New York interstate. The charter bus was carrying high school kids to band camp yesterday, when it overturned and crashed 50 feet down on an embankment.\par \par \uc2 The band director and a retired volunteer were killed and several students were critically hurt.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Errol Barnett has the new details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT (voiceover): Tonight, investigators are focusing on how a potential tire blowout could have caused this coach bus to roll over down an embankment, killing two.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HUMM (\uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2 ): As a result of the crash, multiple occupants were ejected from the motor coach, and two received fatal injuries.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: It left a horrific and chaotic scene Thursday afternoon along I-84, about a two hours` drive northwest of New York City.\par \par \uc2 New York State Trooper Jason Lewis witnessed the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 JASON LEWIS (New York State Trooper): There was was mayhem. It was a very horrendous, tragic accident just by viewing it before I even got down to the scene.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: The bus involved was leading a caravan of six en route to a band camp weekend in Greeley, Pennsylvania, shuttling 40 members of the Farmingdale, Long Island, high school band and four adults.\par \par \uc2 JASON LEWIS: Numerous children scattered around, the crying, you know, asking for the parents. Just as I would do with my two children, I aided to them, I cared for them, tried to calm them down.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Killed in the crash, their 43-year-old band director, Gina Pellettiere, and volunteer chaperone 77-year-old Beatrice Ferrari.\par \par \uc2 Pellettiere was a single mother who is being remembered for her dedication to her students and music.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN KALINOWSKI (Former Student): She made school fun, she made life entertaining. And she brought a lot of joy to a lot of people, whether they were in the band or not.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Ferrari was a retired history teacher, a mother (sp?), who looked forward to this trip every year.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL ALDIERI (Son-in-Law of Beatrice Ferrari): She was a mom, a grandmother, my mother-in-law. She was like a mom to me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Now, investigators hope to learn more once they interview the bus driver. That hasn`t happened yet.\par \par \uc2 And, Norah, it`s unclear if any of the students were even able to wear seat belts.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Errol Barnett, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, for the first time, we are hearing a dramatic 911 call after that $100 million F-35 fighter jet crashed in South Carolina last weekend, the caller the pilot who ejected and the man whose backyard he landed in.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` David Martin is following the investigation from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN (voiceover): The case of the missing F-35 began in the backyard of this house in North Charleston, South Carolina, with a hard-to- believe 911 call.\par \par \uc2 911 CALLER: I guess we got a pilot at our house. And he says he got ejected. So, he ejected from the plane.\par \par \uc2 911 CALL OPERATOR: I`m sorry, what happened?\par \par \uc2 911 CALLER: We got a pilot in the house. And I guess he landed in my backyard. And we were trying to see if we could get an ambulance to the house, please.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: The pilot had no idea where his $100 million airplane had gone.\par \par \uc2 PILOT: We have a military jet crash. I`m the pilot. I`m not sure where the airplane is. It would have crashed-landed somewhere. Has there been a report of an airplane crash?\par \par \uc2 911 CALL OPERATOR: I have not seen any come up yet.\par \par \uc2 PILOT: Ma`am, I`m a pilot in a military aircraft and I ejected, so I just rode a parachute down to the ground.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Where had the plane gone? Local law enforcement didn`t have a clue.\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH OPERATOR: We do not have any crash information, and the pilot lost sight of it on its way down due to the weather.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: No crash information because the plane kept flying for about 60 miles after the pilot punched out.\par \par \uc2 911 CALL OPERATOR: How far did he fall?\par \par \uc2 PILOT: I was at 2,000 feet.\par \par \uc2 911 CALL OPERATOR: OK. And what caused the fall?\par \par \uc2 PILOT: An aircraft failure.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Retired Marine Dave Burke used to fly the F-35.\par \par \uc2 What are the types of aircraft failures that could force a pilot to punch out, but allow the plane to keep flying?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BERKE (Former F-35 Pilot): This had to be something along the lines of a massive electronic or flight control failure.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: When the 911 operator asked the pilot if he was injured, he said his back hurt, no doubt from the force of the ejection.\par \par \uc2 He is 47 years old, and the Marines say he has decades of flying experience -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That 911 operator got quite the call.\par \par \uc2 David Martin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to the crisis at the Southern border. Texas Governor Greg Abbott has ordered more buses to the cities of Eagle Pass and El Paso to send more migrants north to Democratic-run cities amid the surge in asylum seekers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez reports daily migrant crossings have nearly doubled from just a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): They arrive by any means necessary on top of trains, pulled from under barbed wire or across the Rio Grande, sometimes with deadly consequences, the strain of more than 8,000 a day.\par \par \uc2 Crossing the border is moving north to cities like San Antonio, where overflowing shelters mean some migrants sleep outside.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIO HERRERA (U.S. Customs and Border Patrol Agent): Migrants that we have encountered here in this area are trying to evade arrest.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: We rode along with Customs and Border protection agents, who say they have conducted more than 500 rescues in the El Paso sector over the last year, migrants often left in bad shape by cartels.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIO HERRERA: These migrants have been walking for a little while, so our agents` response at that time, in that moment was crucial, because they were lost.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The majority of the migrants are from Venezuela, which is a country with very limited diplomatic ties with the United States.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean? Well, it`s not as easy to deport or repatriate people. That poses other challenges, says \uc2 Ruben Garcia\uc2 , who runs an El Paso shelter.\par \par \uc2 RUBEN GARCIA (Director, Annunciation House): If they get released by Border Patrol and you ask them, do you have a family member, relative, friend in the U.S. that can receive you, many of them do not.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Desiree Pastra (sp?), Francisco Araujo (sp?), and their two girls are among the lucky ones who have a relative in Houston they will stay with. But the month-long journey has left them physically and emotionally scarred.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (Through Translator): I know that, for you, it is difficult.\par \par \uc2 (FRANCISCO ARAUJO speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: You`re feeling a lot.\par \par \uc2 (DESIREE PASTRA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: But they do not qualify for the temporary legal status being offered by the Biden administration to some Venezuelan migrants because they did not enter the country before July 31st.\par \par \uc2 Norah, tomorrow, the head of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , \uc2 Alejandro Mayorkas\uc2 , is set to visit the border.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Manny Bojorquez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the war in Ukraine and the fight over the U.S. funding of weapons. Fears over corruption have some House Republicans demanding more oversight, while some hard-liners want to cut aid entirely.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Holly Williams takes a look at how billions of dollars are being tracked inside Ukraine for this Sunday`s "60 Minutes."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (OLEKSANDRA USTINOVA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): Oleksandra Ustinova was an anti-corruption activist and is now an outspoken member of Ukraine`s Parliament.\par \par \uc2 (OLEKSANDRA USTINOVA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: She chairs a government commission that tracks all military aid coming to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDRA USTINOVA (Ukrainian Parliament Member): These Javelins provided by the United States.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: She filmed this video for us inside what she called a top secret warehouse, storing American Javelin anti-tank missiles.\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDRA USTINOVA: We have online databases with the serial numbers of every American piece of weapon that your embassy has access to. They can come, type in, let`s say, a Javelin or a HIMARS, and see in which brigade it is, and then go check it if they don`t believe.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: So the U.S. officials are going to the front line?\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDRA USTINOVA: We are letting them. Sometimes, they may be sending people. Sometimes, they are going to the logistic centers to see whether it is there or not, whether it`s available.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And you can see Holly Williams` full report on "60 Minutes" this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, officials in Florida are investigating what they believe is a deadly alligator attack. We have got those details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, officials in Largo, Florida, are investigating a suspected alligator attack.\par \par \uc2 Video from the scene shows a 10-foot alligator near a puddle of blood. Police say a body was found in the area. The victim has not been identified.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there`s important health news about a new vaccine to help expectant mothers protect their infants from a serious illness. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: In tonight`s Health Watch, breaking news: The CDC has just approved \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 `s new vaccine aimed at protecting infants from the respiratory illness RSV.\par \par \uc2 The shot now available in some places is recommended for moms who are 32 to 36 weeks pregnant. Health officials say, in the first six months after birth, the vaccine can reduce a baby`s risk of RSV hospitalization by 57 percent.\par \par \uc2 Now this consumer alert. Nearly 50,000 mattresses sold exclusively at \uc2 Costco\uc2  are being recalled because of possible mold exposure. Federal regulators say that Novaform ComfortGrande 14-inch and then the Novaform DreamAway 8-inch mattresses may be exposed to water and developed mold after being packaged.\par \par \uc2 More than 500 people have reported mold on their mattresses. They were sold between January and June of this year. Customers are advised to contact the manufacturer for a refund or replacement.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 NASA\uc2  capsule will return to Earth this weekend, along with a passenger that`s more than four billion years old.\par \par \uc2 We will explain next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight, parts of a large asteroid will crash into a Utah desert this weekend.\par \par \uc2 But don`t worry. It`s all part of a \uc2 NASA\uc2  mission. The small \uc2 NASA\uc2  spacecraft carrying about a half-a-pound of rocks in space dust from the asteroid Bennu will touch down Sunday morning around 10:55 Eastern time. It will complete the final stage of a seven-year, four-billion-mile journey, and is the largest sample of an asteroid ever collected from space.\par \par \uc2 That`s pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night, and have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230923ej9m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 21, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jonathan Vigliotti, Scott MacFarlane, Ed O`Keefe,  Jo Ling Kent, Chris Warren, Manuel Bojorquez, Ali Bauman \par \uc2 3,684 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: A bus carrying a high school marching band crashes in New York. We have the breaking new details, including the number killed and injured.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 A bus full of ninth graders on the way to band camp overturns, the scene tonight, with windows broken and a ladder to get students out.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: They have used whatever they can find here, tree branches and limbs, to try to create some sort of bridge over this water to get to the other side.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We`re on the border where nearly 9,000 migrants crossed into the United States yesterday alone, nearing record levels.\par \par \uc2 Day care horror, the new photos tonight of a trapdoor hiding even more fentanyl and other drugs inside that childcare center in New York.\par \par \uc2 Fox succession, as media mogul \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  steps down, leaving the empire to his son. What it all means.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy`s high-stakes trip to Washington, meeting with the president, Congress, and military leaders, making the case to keep funding Ukraine`s fight against Russia.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: The Writers Guild is continuing negotiations with Hollywood studios.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: The two sides are reportedly nearing a deal. This would finally end the writers strike, which has thrown Hollywood into a near standstill.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: California`s Salton Sea is drying up, exposing a toxic layer of dust. Could lithium mining help or make the air here worse?\par \par \uc2 FRANK RUBIO (NASA Astronaut): It`s an honor to represent our nation, to represent \uc2 NASA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And the new record that`s truly out of this world.\par \par \uc2 FRANK RUBIO: Keep working hard. Have big dreams. The hard work and the dedication are the key components to making those dreams come true.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with breaking news in a fatal bus crash involving a high school band in New York. Police say at least two adults are dead and dozens injured. Five students are in critical condition tonight after a charter bus went off the interstate and tumbled down an embankment about 45 miles northwest of New York City.\par \par \uc2 Images from the scene show the bus on its side with its windows smashed out. We are told 40 students and four adults were on the bus, everyone taken to the hospital, some flown to Level 3 trauma centers.\par \par \uc2 Well, the marching band, including cheerleaders, were heading to a band camp in Pennsylvania, the bus, part of a caravan who watched their classmates crash. The rest of the students, they headed back to their high school.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ali Bauman will start us off tonight from near the scene, as we are just learning the preliminary cause of the crash.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Ali. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 The students were supposed to arrive to that camp in Greeley, Pennsylvania, around 2:00 p.m., according to a schedule on their Web site. Instead, a bus some of them were traveling on, plunged off the road just 40 minutes before making it there.\par \par \uc2 And police just revealed that a preliminary cause of the investigation -- or a preliminary cause of the crash could be failure of a front tire.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN (voiceover): Debris litters the scene along this New York interstate where a bus chartered by the Farmingdale High School band landed in a ravine after rolling down an embankment just outside Wawayanda, two hours northwest of New York City.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (D-New York): But imagine the fear, the screams and the aftermath, when these high school students, many of them freshmen, were surrounded by this chaos.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: The vehicle was carrying 45 band members, along with teachers and was part of a six-bus caravan of 300 people heading to a band camp in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of first responders rushed to the scene just after 1:30 p.m., forced to use ladders to rescue victims, as the bus teetered among treetops. Some of the injured were pulled through windows. Crews also scoured the woods to find anyone who may have been ejected.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: It is likely that a faulty front tire contributed to the accident.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: Traffic was shut down for hours. And, tonight, officials say it is unclear how the bus careened out of control.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: \uc2 The National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  will send a team here to investigate. They`re expected to arrive on scene by tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 The high school tells us it`s trying to reunite students with their parents now. And some students told us earlier that this is so devastating. It`s something they never expected could have happened -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So traumatizing.\par \par \uc2 Ali Bauman, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the crisis at the Southern border, we`re now learning 140,000 migrants were apprehended in the first 20 days of September. The influx is nearing record levels, and, new today, the Biden administration says it`s sending 800 active-duty troops to help.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez is there and tells us about the dangerous journey which led to the death of two people in the last day, including a 3-year- old boy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): Near Eagle Pass, Texas, hundreds of people continue to cross the Rio Grande, trying to slip under wire fences, overwhelming city services.\par \par \uc2 But not everybody makes it. Two people, including a 3-year-old boy, have already drowned in the past two days. Still, in Juarez, Mexico, we found more are risking those final steps north, like this group from Venezuela, using tree limbs as a bridge to get to the other side.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes, they fall in. And it`s been difficult really to watch when they have to handle children, babies, and try to keep them safe, literally handing them over to somebody, who plucks them over to the other side.\par \par \uc2 The majority are from Venezuela, but will not be eligible for temporary legal status and expedited work permits announced by the Biden administration for nearly half-a-million Venezuelan migrants because they did not enter the country before July 31st.\par \par \uc2 In New York, where the change could apply to less than 10,000 people, officials worried it did not go far enough.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D-Mayor of New York City): I`m getting 10,000 a month. And so we can`t spike the ball.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Republicans have criticized the Biden administration`s move, saying it will only motivate more people to try to cross.\par \par \uc2 But back along the border, we found migrants say, what they`re fleeing, political persecution or poverty, are bigger motivators.\par \par \uc2 You didn`t know that about -- about the new status. But does -- would that change what you`re doing?\par \par \uc2 (MANUEL BOJORQUEZ speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: It doesn`t change why you`re leaving.\par \par \uc2 But U.S. Customs and Border Patrol agents say cartels have been using misinformation for months, urging migrants to head north. They found this group, parched and lost, asking for help.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD BARRAGAN (Acting Special Ops Supervisor, Border Patrol): These cartels are very good at what they do. It`s a multibillion-dollar industry. They`re focusing on migrant smuggling and the disinformation that they`re feeding migrants to fuel that profit stream.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: And he tells me, at this specific point of the border, where Texas, Mexico and New Mexico meet, not everyone is looking to turn themselves into Border Patrol agents and request asylum.\par \par \uc2 He says roughly half are trying to avoid agents all together -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manny Bojorquez, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a tropical storm warning is in effect from Maryland to North Carolina, with a potential tropical cyclone developing off the South Carolina coast.\par \par \uc2 For what areas will be impacted, let`s bring in Chris Warren from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 We are watching a developing system off the Southeast Coast, not a tropical storm yet. It is potential Tropical Cyclone 16. And it is expected to bring tropical-storm-force winds. Tropical storm warnings are posted through parts of the mid-Atlantic. And much of the Eastern Seaboard through the weekend will have those strong winds and big impacts at the beaches, with high surf, storm surge, and very heavy rain.\par \par \uc2 The timing for this, the worst of the weather happening tomorrow night through the day on Saturday, with winds gusting 40 to even 50 miles an hour, the rain, Norah, and the surge all made worse potentially at high tide.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Chris Warren, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to a changing of the guards at one of the biggest and most influential media companies in the world. \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  revealed today he is stepping down as chairman of Fox`s parent company and \uc2 News Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent has more on the man that changed both American television and politics.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): Ninety-two-year-old media magnate \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  announcing he will leave his role as chair of Fox and News Corp. in mid- November, becoming chair emeritus of both businesses.\par \par \uc2 His son Lachlan will take the reins, part of a longstanding succession plan. But this won`t be your typical retirement. In an internal memo to \uc2 Fox\uc2  employees today obtained by CBS News, \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  said: "In my new role I will be watching our broadcasts with a critical eye, reading our newspapers. You can expect to see me in the office late on a Friday afternoon."\par \par \uc2 Over the last 70 years, he amassed a vast media empire across Australia, Europe, and the United States, starting \uc2 Fox Broadcasting Company\uc2  in 1986. And 10 years later, he founded Fox News Channel, providing him an influential role in conservative politics.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Sky News, 2017)\par \par \uc2 RUPERT MURDOCH\uc2  (Chairman and CEO, \uc2 News Corporation\uc2 ): I have been publishing here for about 40 years, and I get into a lot of fights.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Murdoch`s career has also been plagued by controversies. In 2016, he ousted his longtime ally Fox News chairman and CEO \uc2 Roger Ailes\uc2  amid sexual harassment and abuse allegations.\par \par \uc2 Just this year, \uc2 Fox\uc2  paid a $787.5 million settlement in a case brought by voting machine manufacturer Dominion after Fox News spread former President Trump`s 2020 election lies.\par \par \uc2 How is Lachlan different than his father? And can he maintain power in this situation?\par \par \uc2 DAVID FOLKENFLIK (NPR): Lachlan is a bit more affable. He`s, if anything, more conservative than his father. And the real question is whether he can convince his adult siblings who help run the family trust that he can do the job.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Upon Rupert Murdoch`s death, his other children could challenge Lachlan.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, experts say that viewers and consumers of the Murdoch media empire should expect no major changes to the product the companies are putting out -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jo Ling Kent, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Talks to end the nearly-five-month strike by Hollywood writers are continuing into the night. The Writers Guild of America and the major studios remain stuck on issues, including pay and the use of artificial intelligence. There are reports of progress.\par \par \uc2 And with the autoworkers strike now in its seventh day, the clock is ticking towards a Friday-at-noon deadline. The union representing 146,000 workers at the Big Three automakers is threatening to expand its current strike if there`s no significant movement in the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy is in Washington tonight, his day packed with high-stakes meetings, including with President Biden and members of Congress, all to win support and funding for his country`s ongoing war against Russia.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  enjoyed a warm welcome at the White House, and President Biden announced plans for the latest installment in aid to Ukraine, another $325 million.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The American people are determined to see to it that all we can do to ensure the world stands with you.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But what a difference nine months makes. Last December, Zelenskyy delivered a primetime address before a joint session of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Today, House Speaker Kevin McCarthy said there was no time for such a speech and wouldn`t even be seen in the halls of power with the Ukrainian president.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy instead met behind closed doors with leaders of the House and the full Senate. That`s because, while a majority of Americans continue to support providing assistance, there`s a mostly Republican divide on Capitol Hill over how to keep funding the war.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-Texas): We need to give them everything they need.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-Georgia): We should be for a peace resolution, ending the war there, not funding it.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: There are growing concerns in the U.S. and Europe about how the aid is being used. Zelenskyy fired his defense minister and six other deputies this month amid accusations of corruption.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy also visited the Pentagon today. The U.S. has provided nearly $44 billion in military aid since the start of the war. And the White House is asking for $24 billion more. But it didn`t commit today to giving Zelenskyy long-range missiles.\par \par \uc2 Today, another reminder of why Ukraine wants them, as Russians hit six Ukrainian cities, its largest missile attack inside Ukraine in more than a month.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Lawmakers who met with Zelenskyy today said they appreciated his detailed assessment of the war.\par \par \uc2 And the Ukrainian president once again expressed gratitude for U.S. assistance, adding -- quote -- "Oversight, transparency and accountability for all the aid is absolutely important" -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ed O`Keefe at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight in Washington, there`s a race to avoid a government shutdown, with little time to make a deal. Chaos reigns in the House, as Republicans failed to advance -- advance a defense spending bill for the second time this week.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane reports on the embarrassing rebuke for Speaker Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): With a deadline looming for a painful government shutdown --\par \par \uc2 MAN: The resolution is not adopted.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: -- things continue to fall apart in the U.S. House today.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I don`t know what direction they`re coming from.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A handful of Republicans, some of whom oppose sending more aid to Ukraine --\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ELI CRANE (R-Arizona): I have been no on Ukraine the entire time.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: -- continue to jam House Speaker Kevin McCarthy, who`s been trying and failing to pass bills to keep the government open with Republican votes only.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): This is a whole new concept of individuals who just want to burn the whole House down. That doesn`t work.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Some members warn, an October 1ST federal shutdown is increasingly likely, which could rattle the stock market and Americans` 401(k)s, create backlogs for people who need veterans` benefits, visas, and passports, and shutter national parks.\par \par \uc2 Shabay Izquierdo, a TSA officer and union rep, says they expect they will have to work for no pay.\par \par \uc2 How much worry is there among your colleagues about this?\par \par \uc2 SHABAY IZQUIERDO (TSA Officer): Anxiety level is at an all-time high. Distress level is at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The 750,000-member \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2  has warned members to save up whatever money they can.\par \par \uc2 EVERETT KELLEY\uc2  (National President, \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 ): Sixty percent of Americans live paycheck to paycheck, OK? And you just imagine payday, but no paycheck.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: CBS News has learned the Biden administration Budget Office has shutdown preparation phone calls with federal officials tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 As Congress races towards an October 1st deadline, Norah, they`re recognizing it`s difficult to race when the U.S. House is stalled.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to a growing health and an environmental crisis for thousands of residents in Southern California.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jonathan Vigliotti shows us how the state`s largest lake is drying up and leading to even bigger problems on land and in the air.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA GALINDO (Pediatric Nurse, Pioneers Memorial Hospital): Say ah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): Four-year-old Damien Lopez has symptoms many who live near California`s Salton Sea have.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LOPEZ (Mother of Damien Lopez): His cough gets very wheezy. I try to control them.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Control often means visiting pediatric nurse Christina Galindo.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA GALINDO: And then out.\par \par \uc2 I can see up to 25 to 30 patients a day, and maybe half of those are dealing with respiratory issues.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: A recent study found that more than 20 percent of the region`s 178,000 people have asthma-like symptoms. This is nearly triple the national rate for asthma.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID LO (Professor of Biomedical Sciences, \uc2 University of California, Riverside\uc2 ): Why people who live close to the Salton Sea have such a high incidence of asthma.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Dr. David Lo led a study at U.C. Riverside that identified that cause, the Salton Sea itself, formed in the early 1900s after a dam break flooded the valley with Colorado River water. Today, its primary source is nearby farm runoff.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID LO: Fertilizer, heavy metals, like some arsenic, selenium.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: For decades, this dangerous mix sat on the seafloor. But without replenishment from the Colorado, the Salton is rapidly receding, exposing a dry and toxic lake bed to the wind, and also attracting a new industry looking to mine another chemical that lies below.\par \par \uc2 FRANK RUIZ (Audubon California and Lithium Valley Commission): If California wants to electrify every single vehicle by 2035, they`re going to need every piece of lithium they can get.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Frank Ruiz works for the California Audubon Society and sits on the commission overseeing the mining of lithium.\par \par \uc2 FRANK RUIZ: We don`t completely understand the impact of the lithium industry. No industry is 100 percent free of environmental impacts.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Ruiz says lithium could be liquid gold for a region facing some of the highest poverty rates in the state. For now, it`s unclear if lithium is a lifeline or a threat.\par \par \uc2 FRANK RUIZ: This is a toxic -- toxic dust.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Do you worry that the Salton Sea and the surrounding communities could be sacrificed for the greater good?\par \par \uc2 FRANK RUIZ: I hope not. Taxes and revenues can potentially provide money to continue covering this toxic playa.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Michelle Lopez hopes her family is not left in the dust.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LOPEZ: I`m concerned that, one day, they are going to be like, you have to leave your house because you can`t live in here anymore.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: For Eye on America, Jonathan Vigliotti, CBS News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Investigators make a shocking discovery inside that New York City day care that was operating as a drug ring.\par \par \uc2 That story`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A startling discovery tonight at that New York City day care, where a 1-year-old died after being exposed to fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 Police say they found a trapdoor in the play area, inside, more fentanyl and other drugs. The woman who ran the day care is in federal custody. Her husband is on the run. Three other children became seriously ill from drug exposure.\par \par \uc2 Convicted murderer Alex Murdaugh pleaded guilty today federal charges of financial fraud and money laundering. This is the first time that the disbarred South Carolina attorney admitted to his crimes. Murdaugh, serving a life sentence for murdering his wife and youngest son, is accused of stealing from clients, including the estate in his longtime maid, who died in a fall at his home.\par \par \uc2 He still faces about 100 different charges in state court.\par \par \uc2 Two passenger jets are struck by laser lights near a major U.S. city. We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Two \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  planes were zapped by green laser lights near Boston`s Logan Airport this morning. Nobody was hurt and the flights were not impacted.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 FAA\uc2  says that these laser strikes threaten air safety, with more than 9,000 reported last year. The fine for shining lasers at aircraft is up to $11,000 per violation, so don`t do it.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, an American astronaut is celebrating a special anniversary in space. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight, \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut Frank Rubio is celebrating one year in space.\par \par \uc2 That is longer than any American in history for a single spaceflight. He was only expected to be at the space station for six months, but the capsule that was supposed to take him home was damaged, extending his stay. Rubio finally returns to Earth next week on day 371. He says he`s looking forward to hugging his wife and four kids back home in Miami.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230922ej9l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Detained Americans Back in the US; Biden Speaking at United Nations; Missing F-35 Crash Site Found; Arrest Made in Deputy Murder; Hunter Biden Sues IRS; Michigan State Football Coach Fired; Assassination in Canada; Unsafe Minivans; Maui Fires Aftermath; Teachers` Fight for Paid Leave; A Lesson in Joy; Oprah`s Newest Book Club Selection; Matteo Bocelli Goes Solo; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Margaret Brennan, Nancy  Cordes, Christina Ruffini, Elise Preston, Holly Williams, Kris Van Cleave,  Vladimir Duthiers, Steve Hartman, Emmett Hartman, Meryl Hartman \par \uc2 14,331 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday, my favorite day, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking News: Five Americans freed from Iran are back on American soil, but not everyone is happy with this deal.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (R) 2024 presidential candidate): They are the biggest exporter of terror and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has given them now $6 billion to go and make mischief.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden speaks today at the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2  to rally support for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE (White House press secretary): They have been fighting so bravely in a way that has surprised the world.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Canada says India may be tied to a religious leaders assassination, causing serious fallout.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU\uc2  (Canadian Prime Minister): The killing of a Canadian citizen on Canadian soil.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Talk show host, Bill Maher, has had a change of heart and now says he won`t bring back his HBO show while the writers` strike continues.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A huge ovation as he was carted away, got hit directly on the knee.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Browns star running back, Nick Chubb going down with that horrific knee injury.\par \par \uc2 MAN: For the second straight week we`ve seen an injury on Monday football that leaves the sports world gutted.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Helicopter is five minutes out.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Doritos just attempted to pull the cheesiest stunt ever as part of a TikTok trend where you dip something and cheese and then see how much comes with it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Doritos set a world record for the longest cheese pull.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That`s 49 feet. Forty-nine feet.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Why are we doing this?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Because we can we can.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Because we can.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to get right to it. Why? Because we can.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a big show for you on this Tuesday, including the one and only Oprah. You know, there is only one, alongside the author of her latest book club pick. This is a CBS MORNINGS exclusive and we love when that happens. She`ll tell you what the book is and she says you don`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the latest edition of "Kindness 101" with Steve Hartman and his talented kids. Today`s lesson is all about joy. It turns out that what makes us happy as children can still make us very happy when we are all grown up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am thinking of vacuuming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to say --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, is that happy time? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does your happiness involve a vacuum cleaner?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Stick around and find out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will talk about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But let`s begin with the news, why don`t we? Because we`ve got breaking news.\par \par \uc2 Five Americans wrongfully detained for years in Iran are finally back in the US. In fact, their plane landed just this morning at Fort Belvoir in Virginia, that`s near Washington.\par \par \uc2 After months of secret negotiations to make this happen, the group includes two business people, an environmentalist, and a pair of others who wish to remain anonymous.\par \par \uc2 Chief foreign affairs correspondent, Margaret Brennan looks now at how this deal came together.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Just before dawn, the five newly freed Americans touched down today on US soil.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (US Secretary of State): It means that husbands and wives, fathers and children, grandparents can hug each other again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Businessman Emad Shargi and environmentalist, Morad Tahbaz have been prisoners since 2018, held on what the US considers trumped up charges.\par \par \uc2 Shargi`s family said his freedom meant it was the first time in years that they felt light in their homes.\par \par \uc2 Three other American prisoners and two family members accompanied them Monday on a \uc2 Qatar Airways\uc2  flight for Doha, where they were handed over to US custody.\par \par \uc2 For 51-year-old businessman, Siamak Namazi, that flight ended an eight-year ordeal.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And it`s, you know, a day for celebration, but tomorrow begins the rest of his life and it`s going to be a long journey towards recovery.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In exchange for the release, President Biden granted five Iranian nationals clemency. Two were turned to open stretched arms of loved ones in Tehran late Monday, two remained in the US and a fifth joined family in another country according to Iranian officials.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the regime will have access to $6 billion worth of unfrozen Iranian oil assets held in a restricted account in Qatar.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): But that`s my biggest problem. It is not the Americans who are coming home, it is that now, elements all over the world realize we can get something in exchange for Americans. Let`s take some.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Biden administration insists the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  will be able to ensure the funds are only used for humanitarian purposes like food and medicine.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 : We have the means and mechanisms to make sure that that happens.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The next stop for the freed Americans is a US military facility in the DC area for medical support to prepare them to once again be free persons.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all glad they`re home. Very happy for their families.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is here in New York today. He`s here this morning to address the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2 . Now his focus is support for Ukraine, but the visit also comes with a very tricky time for the president here at home.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes joins us in the studio. Always good to have you here, Nancy. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, at a fundraiser here in New York last night, President Biden argued it is his experience on the world stage building alliances taking on dictators that justifies a second term.\par \par \uc2 Well, this morning, he is laying out his foreign policy platform for hundreds of world leaders, even as the urgency mounts on a host of challenges here at home.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The war in Ukraine, competition with China, and climate change -- those global flashpoints are at the top of the agenda as President Biden addresses the United Nations today.\par \par \uc2 While in New York, he`s also set to meet with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2 , whose government he has slammed as extreme.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Hopefully, Bibi will continue to move toward moderation.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But back in Washington, the problems are piling up.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): September 30th is only 12 days away.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A government shutdown looks increasingly likely as Republicans waged an internal battle over federal spending.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): It is a good thing I love a challenge because every day is going to be a challenge.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (\uc2 United Auto Workers Union\uc2  president): We`re going to keep hitting the company where we need to, when we need to.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  are threatening to expand their five-day old strike if there is no progress on a deal by noon on Friday.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: Either the Big 3 get down to business and work with us to make progress and negotiations or more locals will be called on to stand up and go out on strike.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: President Biden is set to host Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  at the White House later this week, but there is still no guarantee he`ll be able to sell the GOP on another $24 billion Ukraine aid package.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: Look, if Ukraine falls, what will happen in 10 years? Just think about it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: On "60 Minutes," Zelenskyy argued the Russians will invade other countries unless they`re defeated in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: If the Russians reach Poland, what`s next? A Third World War?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: What will it take? Another $70 billion?\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: I don`t have an answer. The whole world has to decide whether we want to stop Putin or whether we want to start the beginning of a World War.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Zelenskyy is set to speak today at the \uc2 UN\uc2  as well, not long after President Biden speaks, so Zelenskyy has addressed the gathering remotely before, but this is the first time he`ll be there in person with Russian officials sitting in the hall unless they walk out, which is something that they have been known to do when they are being critiqued.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. It is going to be quite a moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Either way, it is going to be very interesting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have anything to say about President Biden who seems to be taking on the age issue head on like, okay, I`m older, now what?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Right. He is here in New York to talk foreign policy, but he is also doing some fundraisers for his re-election. He said at one of those closed-door events last night, he knows that people have questions about his age. But for him what that translates into is experience and a record, sort of trying out some lines behind closed doors that you`ll probably see on the campaign trail pretty soon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Isn`t it interesting, Nancy, how closed-door events always are not so closed? You know how they say, it`s all private. Don`t worry. Somebody is out always talking.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But there`s no video.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: We like video.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is always a Chatty Cathy in politics, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Always.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nancy, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 There are big questions this morning after the US military lost track of a Marines F-35 fighter jet after the pilot ejected.\par \par \uc2 Following a day of intense searches, the Marine Corps says debris from the F-35, similar to the one pictured here was found yesterday in Williamsburg, South Carolina. That`s about two hours northeast of Joint Base Charleston.\par \par \uc2 The Marines are pausing all flight operations for two days. Christina Ruffini has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: The Marine Corps says a mishap caused a pilot to eject from an F-35 fighter jet like this one over South Carolina Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 While the pilot was quickly and safely recovered, the almost $80 million aircraft built to evade detection did just that and went MIA.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Officials with Joint Base Charleston say they`re still looking for a fighter jet.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: At one point, Joint Base Charleston even asked the public for help, looking for any information to help recovery teams as air traffic data from flight 24 shows other planes apparently searching for the rogue F-35.\par \par \uc2 Crews eventually found the crash site Monday night, but how does one of the world`s most advanced aircrafts go missing?\par \par \uc2 ADM. \uc2 JAMES WINNEFELD\uc2  (Former vice chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff): It`s a bit of a mystery, but this is very much a one-off event.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Admiral \uc2 James Winnefeld\uc2  is a former vice chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.\par \par \uc2 ADM. \uc2 JAMES WINNEFELD\uc2 : The F-35 is a very stealthy airplane. So it`s possible that if the aircraft was doing simulated air combat against another airplane, once inside the restricted area, the pilot could have turned off that identification system that all aircraft use in controlled airspace in order to prevent his or her adversary that afternoon from finding him.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Following the F-35 mishap, the Marine Corps announced it will temporarily shut down aviation operations. Also citing two other incidents, the death of an F-18 pilot in San Diego during an August training flight and the recent deaths of three Marines in an Osprey crash in Australia.\par \par \uc2 ADM. \uc2 JAMES WINNEFELD\uc2 : After a string of incidents, you would find that the senior leadership would order a safety stand down to try to see if there`s any consistent theme behind these accidents that could be nipped in the bud.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Now a spokesperson says the F-35 pilot was taken to the hospital in stable condition on Sunday. The Marine Corps is still investigating what caused the incident and prompted the pilot to eject, but has not provided any additional details -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Christina, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A suspect is under arrest in the killing of a Los Angeles County Sheriff`s deputy who was shot inside his patrol car. That suspect, 29-year-old Kevin Salazar arrested early yesterday after an intense standoff in Palmdale, California. He is expected to be arraigned tomorrow for the shooting death of Deputy Ryan Clinkunbroomer. The reason for the killing is still unclear though as Elise Preston reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Come out with your hands up. LA County Sheriff`s Department.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: After a 36-hour manhunt, the intense search for the suspect behind the deadly shooting of Los Angeles County Sheriff`s deputy, Ryan Clinkunbroomer led officers to this Palmdale home.\par \par \uc2 The suspect had barricaded himself inside for hours until chemical agents were deployed. Twenty-nine-year-old Kevin Salazar then surrendered and was taken into custody.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF ROBERT LUNA (LA County Sheriff`s Department): Our deputies gave this suspect an opportunity to peacefully give up. That is not the right that was afforded to our deputy.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Officers have yet to discover a motive for what they`re investigating as a targeted shooting, but they are confident they have the person responsible for the deputy`s death.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF ROBERT LUNA: Our deputy left this station in uniform to serve. Ryan`s family will never see him again.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Outside the Palmdale sheriff`s station, a makeshift memorial continues to grow as deputies work to unravel the reasons behind their colleague`s violent death.\par \par \uc2 The 30-year-old was a proud third generation law enforcement officer. He had gotten engaged just days before the attack.\par \par \uc2 Can you give us more insight as to who he was?\par \par \uc2 CHIEF ROBERT LUNA: An incredible young man. He`d give anything to anybody. He was unselfish and he loved serving this community.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Elise Preston in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No reason will ever make sense. So senseless.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden is taking on the \uc2 IRS\uc2  with a new lawsuit today. The complaint filed yesterday, accuses the agency of unlawfully releasing his private tax information. It also claims that two agents targeted and sought to embarrass the president`s son. How? By revealing details about his taxes in more than 20 nationally televised interviews, including an exclusive CBS News interview with \uc2 IRS\uc2  supervisor, that`s Gary Shapley.\par \par \uc2 Shapley`s attorneys call the lawsuit frivolous, while the \uc2 IRS\uc2  has declined to comment. Biden was indicted last week on federal charges of lying on an application to buy a firearm.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to college football where Michigan State plans to fire head coach Mel Tucker over allegations of sexual misconduct. Tucker was suspended after "USA Today" reported the claims earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 The school has been investigating the allegations for months. Yesterday, MSU officials announced they had "amassed a body of undisputed evidence of misconduct."\par \par \uc2 Tucker is accused of sexually harassing a rape survivor and activist who was hired by Michigan State to speak to its football team about sexual violence. He has called her allegations completely false.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now to a major announcement from Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  in a growing international crisis. Trudeau says there is credible evidence of ties between India`s government and an assassination carried out in Canada back in June.\par \par \uc2 This is something that the government of India denies, but our Holly Williams reports now on the fallout.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Hardeep Singh Nijjar was a Canadian and a prominent Sikh leader, shot dead by two masked gunmen in British Columbia this past June.\par \par \uc2 The Sikhs are a religious minority in India and Nijjar was a supporter of a separate state for members of the group.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: His killing sparked protests by Sikhs in Canada, who blamed the Indian government for his murder.\par \par \uc2 Now the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  has responded by expelling a senior Indian diplomat.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU\uc2 : Canadian security agencies have been actively pursuing credible allegations of a potential link between agents of the government of India and the killing of a Canadian citizen, Hardeep Singh Nijjar.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: India`s government calls the allegations absurd and has retaliated by expelling a Canadian diplomat.\par \par \uc2 Assassinations carried out by foreign governments and their agents typically draw a punitive response. The US imposed sanctions when a Saudi Arabian journalist, Jamal Khashoggi was murdered inside the Saudi consulate in Istanbul, Turkey in 2018. And America also sanctioned Russia over the poisoning and attempted poisoning of the Kremlin`s enemies residing in the UK, though Moscow denies any involvement.\par \par \uc2 Canada`s Prime Minister says the country`s sovereignty is at stake.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU\uc2 : Let us remain calm and steadfast in our commitment to our democratic principles and our adherence to the rule of law. This is who we are and what we do as Canadians.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: There are also economic consequences to this. Talks have been paused on a new treaty with Canada that India hoped would boost trade by around $6 billion a year -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Holly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, newb crash tests raise concerns about potential risks for minivan passengers. What parents need to know about their children`s safety in the rear seats, but first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, a very tough choice facing many teachers across the country who may want to start a family of their own. We`ll take a look at the big problem of parental leave in this country.\par \par \uc2 Plus, golf great, that`s Phil Mickelson comes clean about his addiction to gambling. He has a very important message for others.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching your favorite morning show, CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, we have a new warning for anyone driving or thinking of getting a minivan, me included.\par \par \uc2 A new study by the \uc2 Insurance Institute for Highway Safety\uc2  focuses on 2023 models of the \uc2 Honda Odyssey\uc2 , Toyota Sienna, Chrysler Pacifica, and Kia Carnival. None of those four minivans that were tested earned an acceptable or good safety rating.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has a closer look at the crash results.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: In test after test, popular minivans earned low marks in new crash tests from the \uc2 Insurance Institute for Highway Safety\uc2  focusing on how backseat passengers fare in a head on collision.\par \par \uc2 IIHS\uc2  vice president, Jessica Jermakian.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA JERMAKIAN (\uc2 Insurance Institute for Highway Safety\uc2  vice president): So in all four of the vehicles we saw, the rear occupant was at increased risk of chest injury either because of poor belt positioning or are high belt forces.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Three of the four minivans received a marginal rating. The \uc2 Honda Odyssey\uc2  rated poor. While each did well in protecting those upfront, that was not the case for the test dummy in the back equivalent to the size of a 12-year-old.\par \par \uc2 The minivans had done well in previous versions of this test, but researchers decided to raise the bar because they felt that backseat passengers were being overlooked. Since then, every class of vehicle tested has left room for improvement.\par \par \uc2 From a number of SUVs to sedans and small pickups, most vehicles tested struggled with the new standard.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA JERMAKIAN: People in the rear seat are at increased risk of fatal injury compared with those in the front seat and it is not that the rear seat has gotten less safe over time, it is that the front seat has gotten so much safer by comparison.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Automakers say they are reviewing the findings, but safety is a top priority and their vehicles meet or exceed federal safety standards.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sounds like minivans have some more work to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Yes. I`m definitely in the minivan curious market with four kids, but that`s eye opening because you`re always told that the backseat is safer regardless of vehicle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, for kids.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And now, they`re saying actually compared to the front, the front has gotten way better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was interesting in the lead, Nate said you were interested in a minivan. Of all the cars in the world, would you have ever thought Nate Burleson wanted a minivan? I`ll go first. No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would say yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know my Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`d make a minivan look good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, Hawaii Governor Josh Green, we`re talking Maui. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s 7:30. CBS MORNINGS and exactly six weeks since the horrific deadly wildfires broke out on Maui, there are nearly a hundred people confirmed dead in those fires, 31 others reported missing, some 2,000 buildings destroyed. The vast majority of those buildings were people`s homes.\par \par \uc2 And there have been a lot of questions, lingering questions about whether at least some of this could have been prevented. To help us understand it all, let`s talk now with Hawaii Governor Josh Green. He is in town to talk to the \uc2 UN\uc2  about climate change, which he has been making the point contributed to this disaster.\par \par \uc2 Governor, good morning. Thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH GREEN (D-HI): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s start on the lingering questions of accountability and lessons learned.\par \par \uc2 So for about a decade, there had been a warning that the area of Lahaina was at extremely high risk for fire and there were funds available, but there`s no evidence that preparations were made using those funds. Was that a mistake?\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH GREEN (D-HI): In retrospect, of course, we wish everything could have been done differently.\par \par \uc2 Right now, people have been investing in renewable energy instead of some of the protections that might have saved people. These are all kind of Monday morning quarterback type issues. Right now, we`re just heartsick because we lost 97 of our loved ones.\par \par \uc2 The other issues, of course, are why did a storm like this occur in 2023, and not over the many decades before? Well, a big part of it is the changing climate.\par \par \uc2 Between 1953 and 2003, in Hawaii, we had six fire emergencies. Last month, August, we had six fire emergencies. So for anyone who doubts that climate change is a part of this, they are not being honest.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So if the climate has changed, I imagine then the response and the preparedness would have to change as well. Another point of concern from citizens on Maui is that the mayor of Maui apparently did not know the extent of the damage in Lahaina until the following morning, so maybe 12 hours after the fires swept through town.\par \par \uc2 Were they not monitoring emergency radio traffic? How could they not know for that long?\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH GREEN (D-HI): You know, obviously the mayor has to speak for himself and his team. What basically happened was a fire broke loose early in the day, they put it out. And then several other fires, three other major fires began across the island of Maui. Because of those three fires, people were scattered to the wind, then the fire seemed to reignite back over Lahaina.\par \par \uc2 By that time, we had hurricane force winds, that`s when I say there`s climate impact. We had 74 mile per hour winds that spread embers all across Lahaina.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t know why his emergency management folks were unable to communicate adequately with him. I do know that the fire was so fast and furious that it took only 17 minutes to come through Lahaina.\par \par \uc2 Again, we`re going to do all that we can to mitigate these kinds of things going forward. But I`m here in New York to tell the world, we are no longer just anticipating the impact of climate change and wildfires and how they could take our people out, we are enduring it in real time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Which is why it feels so urgent to learn these lessons of Lahaina so that if another event happens today even, you`re in a position to respond.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about the death toll. It had been above 100, then some good news, it was revised down to 97.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH GREEN (D-HI): Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When we spoke weeks ago, in fact, in the days after the fire in Maui, you were preparing, you were bracing yourself for hundreds dead.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH GREEN (D-HI): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What explains this fluctuation?\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH GREEN (D-HI): So a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 So in the first few days, and like you said, it is six weeks today, the first few days, you get reports from every possible person saying they don`t know where their loved one is, they had a friend in town, maybe someone was traveling. So we had a report list of 3,200 individuals, which then quickly got chopped down to about 1,200 and then 800, then it was 355, I believe and finally down to where we are now.\par \par \uc2 What really happened was, as we saw who had passed away, they were being conservative until they could get the Department of Defense scientists and physical anthropologists in. A lot of the casualties occurred in cars or in a couple of houses on Front Street. And so they just didn`t know for sure how many individuals were in those facilities or houses.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of it is in the early phases difficult to ascertain when you have a fire that`s like 2,000 degrees Fahrenheit. It`s just tough.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, it`s good news, no matter how you slice it, a lower number is better than a higher one in this context.\par \par \uc2 In our final 30 seconds here, your message to the rest of America, the rest of the world that is interested in revisiting, going to Maui, going to Lahaina which will reopen on October 8th, should they come?\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH GREEN (D-HI): They should. It is a difficult call. But when people come to Maui, in this case, even West Maui on October 8th or the weeks and months thereafter, you will be helping our people heal.\par \par \uc2 Everyone has to brace themselves globally for these kinds of challenges, and we have to prepare better. But part of Hawaii is, it gives kind of a sense of hope to the world because everyone has this 