s is still investigating what caused the incident and prompted the pilot to eject.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hey got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And beloved Cardinals pitcher, that`s Adam Wainwright, it is his 200th career win just two weeks before he plans to retire. Go Adam.\par \par \uc2 ADAM WAINWRIGHT (Baseball player): I love being a \uc2 St. Louis Cardinals\uc2  and I love that I got to play my whole career here.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for embracing me. I love you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go big or go home. What a nice little closing thing for him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is winding down.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People don`t spend their whole careers on a team or a company or anywhere anymore, you know, everyone is moving around.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So it`s nice to see.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, how about a little "Kindness 101" to start this hour.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman and his children share stories built around kindness and character, people who have mastered those very important qualities, and today`s lesson is a great one, joy.\par \par \uc2 So this morning, Steve and his kids dip into the archives to bring us a story from 2007 about someone who can teach us all about finding joy in the simplest and most tedious things, for him that simple thing, a vacuum cleaner.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good morning. This is Meryl.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Hi, everyone. Today`s topic is finding joy.\par \par \uc2 And for our definition, we go to my smiling brother, Emmett at the Dictionary Desk.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Good morning Meryl.\par \par \uc2 I`m defining joy as a feeling of great delight, intense ecstatic happiness.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I`ll take some of that.\par \par \uc2 Remember the other day when I was taking air out of that inner tube?\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Yes, that was fun.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: That was really fun for whatever reason. I don`t know if it was the --\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: It went like this, pfft.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Of like the air going out.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It was just taking air out of an inner tube.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Oh, no. There`s really like random things in life that bring you joy for some reason.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Or what about --\par \par \uc2 (MERYL HARTMAN and EMMETT HARTMAN popping bubble wrap.)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Are you really enjoying this?\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: It`s just really satisfying.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This is proof that if you want to find joy, especially in the little things, you need to think like a child, and no one proves that point better than the kid you`re about to meet.\par \par \uc2 This is a story from my archive about how you can find your joy, even in the most unlikely places.\par \par \uc2 Kyle Krichbaum of Adrian, Michigan is vacuuming on cloud nine.\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM (Nine years old): It`s kind of like people who like baseball better than football.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You like vacuums better than --\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: Everything.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And he`s felt that way since before he could say Hoover.\par \par \uc2 MARYLYNN KRICHBAUM: When Kyle was a baby in his little baby seat and I`d be vacuuming, he would just -- he would just be mesmerized by the vacuum and he would just -- he`d follow it everywhere.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So his mom got him his own vacuum at age one, dressed up as a Dirt Devil for Halloween at age two and as his former teacher recalls, was vacuuming during recess at age six.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And actually one day, vacuuming one side of the room, the next day, he will vacuum the other side of the room, also vacuum the principal`s office, anywhere he could vacuum.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Later, he started collecting them.\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: It is a slow starter, but it still runs perfect.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Much to the chagrin of his older sister.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE KRICHBAUM: He is constantly vacuuming, that`s all I hear. And it`s just like, why? Why? Why? Why? Why? Why? I don`t understand.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Do you remember what it was?\par \par \uc2 Kyle is so obsessed he had me call my parents to find out what vacuum I grew up with?\par \par \uc2 They had a \uc2 JCPenney\uc2  at one time.\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: A Penncrest.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Apparently, this particular vacuum is very rare. Only one other is known to exist.\par \par \uc2 What did you do with it? You threw it out?\par \par \uc2 And there went his holy grail, but that`s part of the joy for Kyle, the quest.\par \par \uc2 He repairs neighbors` vacuums and uses the money to acquire more.\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: Did it survive?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And more vacuums.\par \par \uc2 When is Enough, enough?\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: My parents say that all the time. When I go off to college, my parents say that they are going to have a big vacuum sale.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He thinks they`re joking. I`m not so sure.\par \par \uc2 It`s been 16 years since we first told that story. Is it possible? Is he still finding joy from vacuum cleaners? Let`s find out.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Kyle.\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: Hello. How`s it going?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Do you still have your collection?\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: I do. I have a shelf with like, vacuum stacks vertically and horizontally across some shelves.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Did you ever get your hands on a green Penncrest?\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: Ah. I sure did.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You did.\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: Isn`t that hilarious?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Where did it come from? It was it from Toledo, Ohio, was it?\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: It`s from Akron, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Oh, it could be the same one.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: No way.\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: I know maybe somebody picked it up.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: What has been your most joyous moment as a collector?\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: If I find something that like one of my friends is after and then I can restore and surprise them with it, that`s always fun. My restoration --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Wait, wait, wait. What kind of friends do you have?\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: Vacuum friends. Come on?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Because you surprised me with the old vacuum. I`m not sure that would bring me joy.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: How have you been able to maintain this childhood joy into adulthood?\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: I don`t know. I feel like we get so dogged nowadays with like everything that`s going on in the world that you have to find something to like and appreciate, you know?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Kyle, thank you so much for spending time with us.\par \par \uc2 KYLE KRICHBAUM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I`m glad we did that lesson, because if he can find joy in cleaning equipment, we should be able to find it in a lot of other unlikely places, all we need to do is think like a child.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Until then, stay kind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number one, Meryl, I love your haircut. I love your haircut.\par \par \uc2 But I think he`s so right, think like a child.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope we -- I hope I never lose that feeling, honestly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s a wonderment that we had and a curiosity that really fueled us as kids and we started adulting and we let it go, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But unfortunately, I can`t think like a child when I`m vacuuming up what a child put on the floor. The rice crumbs and pieces of cracker and everything. It is my night, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There are certain things that we can dive into from our childhood that brings a smile to us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re right. You`re right. You`re right. And actually, I do enjoy it because it means if I`m doing the vacuuming, I don`t have to do the bath time, which you know, it is very wet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that`s the good tradeoff.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s a tradeoff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I love the fact that he still loves vacuums. Number one, I think that was one of my favorite ones.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was really good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To goes 16 years later and to still see him and to still see that that brings him joy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know what else brings us joy? Oprah because she`s in the house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she does.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She reveals the next selection for her Book Club and the author who talks about getting the news. But first, it is 8:07, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fall on Me.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my goodness. That`s Andrea Bocelli and his son, Matteo with their hit duet, it is called "Fall on Me."\par \par \uc2 The first time I heard it, I swear to you. The hair stood up on the back of my neck. So beautiful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The dogs are howling right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, the dogs are howling, but this song is really beautiful. I`ll take it Tony. You ain`t wrong, you ain`t wrong.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Matteo Bocelli -- but I try hard -- ahead, Matteo Bocelli talks with us about stepping out alone without his dad by his side. He`s got a debut solo album and it is so good.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching your favorite morning show, that would be CBS MORNINGS. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  this morning, it`s time to hear from Oprah as in Winfrey. The author of her newest Book Club selection.\par \par \uc2 We recently sat down with both of them for the big reveal.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are here now with Oprah as in Winfrey, because there`s only one. We are so excited. Why? Because she`s here to reveal her newest Book Club selection, so drumroll please.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (Talk show host): Drum roll.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Da-da-da-da-da.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And the book is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: "Wellness."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: By who?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Nathan Hill.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hello, Nathan Hill.\par \par \uc2 So "Wellness" follows a relationship of Elizabeth and Jack over the course of their 20-year relationship from their meetings as lonely students in Chicago, back in 1993, to their struggling marriage, and they were on the struggle bus in 2014, big time.\par \par \uc2 This is Hill`s second novel. You remember following his best-selling book, "The Nix." That was way back in 2016, but it was named one of the best books at that time.\par \par \uc2 We are very excited to have best-selling author, Nathan Hill, and of course, Oprah is always welcome.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Nathan, you were just telling us before we went on there, that`s the first time you`ve seen the book with the sticker on it.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL ("Wellness" author): Yes, this morning was the first time I`ve seen this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Oprah`s Book Club sticker. It has been around since 1996.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: Doesn`t it look nice?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It looks very nice. I always like to hear the moment of when you found out. I know -- how did it work for you?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: Oprah crashed a Zoom call. I was having what I thought was just going to be a normal meeting with my publisher, and then my screen was taken over by Oprah, and the strangest thing happened.\par \par \uc2 I`m very accustomed to seeing Oprah on a screen. So my impulse was just to kind of sit back and listen and learn and be entertained.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: And it took, I think an awkward amount of time to realize that she was actually speaking to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Speaking to you.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: Like I had to speak back. It was a very surreal moment.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s your story of hearing from her, but how did you come to Nathan? How did you come to this book? Why`d you pick it?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Well, Lee Newman, who sends me books from the Oprah Daily team on a regular basis, I have stacks and stacks of them, sent this to me and we started talking about why this would work. I mean, it`s basically a love story gone wrong.\par \par \uc2 I loved it, because it was set in Wicker Park --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which you know well.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes, Chicago area. And so it felt in the beginning very familiar to me. And then Jack and Elizabeth became less and less familiar to me, but I really appreciated your ability to take language and draw us into the story in such a way -- in such a modern, contemporary, vibrant way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Nathan, this inspiration for the book is based on a short story that you wrote a long time -- decades ago.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But when you revisited it, it kind of spoke to you differently.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: It did. It did. Yes.\par \par \uc2 The first chapter is a short story that I wrote almost 20 years ago now. I had just moved to New York City, I was living in this tiny studio apartment in Queens. I had nothing but like a mattress on the floor and my window looked out to this wall of other windows into other apartment buildings.\par \par \uc2 And I imagined the scene where two lonely people were glimpsing each other through those windows and slowly falling in love, and I wrote that as a short story and I thought it was fabulously romantic.\par \par \uc2 And then fast forward, you know, fifteen, twenty years, and I`m older. I`ve been happily married for many years. And I think back to that couple and I thought, well, maybe they`re romantic, but they`re also really naive.\par \par \uc2 You know, they`re maybe kind of idiots like projecting these fantasies onto the other people without even knowing them. And it made me wonder what would have become of these people, you know, if we track them? If they got together? If they got married? If they became parents? What would happen to them?\par \par \uc2 So I started writing --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Don`t you love the novelist mind?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I just love the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I love that, because you know, when they were both -- they`re both sort of semi stalking each other, but it wasn`t a creepy way, it was very fascinating to me when they first met. The lines that -- the line that Jack said to Elizabeth was what, Oprah? Because --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Come with.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He just walked up to her and just said "come with."\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Come with.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that line so much.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: Thank you. Thank you. I wanted his first words to be completely without guile, just completely pure, just "come with" and it`s also an invitation to the reader.\par \par \uc2 This is a bit of a long book, and I`m kind of asking the reader to come with me for it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And let me say this, to all you men out there that are looking at Gayle and want to shoot your shot, just say come with.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is going to fall right into your arms.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, I`m trying to find somebody I can go up to and say "come with." I`m not kidding. I`m not kidding, because I think it`s so sweet and so genuine. That`s what I like.\par \par \uc2 Also, this book also raises a question, Oprah, about what love really is? Didn`t that resonate with you?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes. That resonated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And what resonated is that each of them trying to find their own way, and you know, even the -- you know coupling with the other couples and it`s --\par \par \uc2 You know, it is a marriage trying to find its space and I think a lot of people go through that and I think a lot of readers are going to appreciate --\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: The way you wrote Jack and Elizabeth. I mean, I think, what are they doing now? How are they doing?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 You are also prefacing with algorithms because we know what the life the world we live in today.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes, yes, yes. And I wanted to know, is all that true?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: There`s a section of the book that`s essentially told from the point of view of algorithms of like \uc2 Facebook\uc2  or social media algorithms. The book is really about the stories that come to define us. You know, how our worlds can be built by the stories that we believe in, and I had the depressing experience of like losing people to \uc2 Facebook\uc2  rabbit holes and conspiracies and what better example of losing yourself in a story than that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Losing them how, though, because they would just believe the crazy stuff --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: They just went down the rabbit hole.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: They went down the rabbit hole.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And they became different people, but --\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: From the people that I thought they were, exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: And so I read -- oh, my goodness, I read hundreds of pages of like \uc2 Facebook\uc2  patent applications, so.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You did some serious research for this.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: I did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Art, psychology.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: Brain meltingly dry documents, those things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it helped.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, it`s amazing that you have a whole bibliography section, which is not typical for fiction, but Oprah, I mean, because it is -- you can learn a lot from this book.\par \par \uc2 You recently wrote a book, last time you were on, talking about happiness.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The book is called "Wellness."\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Did you learn anything more in your search for happiness from this book?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Well, what I learned is that when you`re sitting with a good book, and you`re absorbed, and you are allowed to be transported to another way of being and other people`s experiences, that for me, that`s the best kind of relaxation and comfort.\par \par \uc2 And so spending time with Jack and Elizabeth, it was good for me, good to know them through a novel.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a little tease about what kind of people they are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what do you hope that people will take away from the book for you?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HILL: Everybody believes, you know, stories about themselves. They believe stories about the people in their lives, about the world and because, we`re fallible, some of those stories are going to need amendments, and some of those stories are going to be wrong.\par \par \uc2 And so believe what you believe, he says, but believe lightly. Believe with curiosity, believe with humility. I think that`s kind of at the heart of the book.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I predict you have a second bestseller on your hands.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : I predict so, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you say, Oprah?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I certainly say so. Nathan Hill.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Wellness" is on sale now. Check out CBSMornings.com for a reader`s guide and a free excerpt from the novel, and you can go to our \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page to find out how to get more involved with Oprah`s Book Club.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead, the top colleges that will give you the best bang for your buck. That and more in "Talk of the Table" coming right up.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I say this all the time, we meet the nicest and most talented people in our progressive greenroom. This is singer -- what`s your name?\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI (Singer): Matteo, and thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your last name?\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: Bocelli.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bocelli.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: Is it important?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not when you`re singing, it is not.\par \par \uc2 He is releasing his debut solo album -- when is your debut solo coming?\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: It`s September 22nd.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That by the way is this Friday. We`re going to talk about that after your local news. But first, will you play something as we go to break? What would you like to play?\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: So this is actually my last single.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: It`s "Fasi."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. "Fasi."\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: And it is in Italian.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: Just a few seconds.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 (MATTEO BOCELLI performing "Fasi.")\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning. We`re at a table and we are talking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And this is how we --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do it. It is Friday night. It`s Tuesday morning.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is Katy Perry, the one and only, the latest start to sell off the rights to her music catalogue and it is vast.\par \par \uc2 This deal is reportedly worth $225 million. You go, Miss Perry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It includes the singer`s stakes in master recordings and publishing rights for the five albums that she released between 2008 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 So those albums had 16 multiplatinum singles including "Firework," "Teenage Dream" and "I Kissed a Girl" and I like it.\par \par \uc2 The buyer is Litmus Music, which also owns the rights to Keith Urban`s master recordings, producer Benny Blanco`s portfolio which includes Maroon Five.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Moves Like Jagger" and Rihanna`s "Diamond." So they know what they`re doing at Litmus. The CEO over there is Hank Forsyth.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He says this about Katy: "Her songs are in a central part of the global cultural fabric." They`re so grateful to be working again with her. "She`s such a trusted partner whose integrity shines in everything she does."\par \par \uc2 I went to go see Katy in Las Vegas when she had a residency there. There`s nobody like her and --\par \par \uc2 Oh, I took my sisters and Katy was so nice, hung out and talked to us, like she enjoyed speaking after that concert. She stayed for half an hour, and she just really is a doll.\par \par \uc2 So this is a big deal for her. Very happy for her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I always think when I hear about these major sales of the master recordings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Their catalogues.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Like, all of these people at one point were little kids, just like mouthing words to songs they heard on the radio.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then they sell them for a quarter million dollars one day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you know what I think when I see that number she sold it for, how much money will be made off of her songs in the future with TV, film, and shows.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yep, and she is still making music.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And she is still making music.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is still making music.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And making money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It` not like she is retired, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am thinking, it is going to be a lot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Tony. What have you got?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, my "Talk of the Table" -- so Americans are losing faith in the value of a college education. And in fact, for more than a decade now, enrollment in four-year colleges has been falling.\par \par \uc2 So I was very curious to see that "The Wall Street Journal" has done an alternative ranking of colleges based on value.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is how much they cost you and how much you can expect to earn out of it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The results are really interesting. It`s public universities dominating the list and I`ll give you the top five here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The power ranking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s not all that you`re going to expect. All right, so at number five, we have \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Perennial grade school, typically on lists. Number four, \uc2 University of Florida\uc2  higher up on the list than you usually see it. Number three, California State University Los Angeles, better known as UCLA.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: UCLA.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Number two, \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2 . Another perennial. The number one best value college in America, \uc2 Baruch College\uc2 . A lot of people right now --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t see that coming.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Probably thinking, never even heard of it. It`s a school here in New York City. It is a public college.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think people should check out this list because if you look at the top 50, there is a lot of schools you recognize like Harvard is 16.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And a lot of schools that you probably hadn`t even thought of like \uc2 New Jersey Institute of Technology\uc2 , \uc2 Northern Kentucky University\uc2 , Colby College, Michigan Technical University -- so it`s like public colleges and technical schools seem to be giving people the bang for their buck that they`ve been missing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. And Cal State LA has called us and said we are not UCLA, we are Cal State LA. Sorry, Cal State.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my God, I missed the state. I`m so used to seeing UCLA.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, me, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Again, it proves my point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: These are not the schools you`d expect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too. Me, too. I thought it was UCLA, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony did that on purpose to drive home his point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are on the phone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To make my point even stronger.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but you`re right, though. Bang for your buck.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Check out that list.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because people are thinking about that financial commitment and the investment and possible debt of what college can put them in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People are googling \uc2 Baruch College\uc2  this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, that was a good one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so we had a conversation earlier about vacuum cleaners, so let`s keep that cleaning conversation going. My "Talk of the Table" is a new study that asked people how clean they feel their homes are, and the results may surprise you.\par \par \uc2 And so it turns out people in this poll consider their home completely clean only 11 days a year. The survey --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Out of 365?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, out of 365, Gayle, the survey of 2,000 homeowners and renters found they spend about six days a year doing that deep, deep cleaning. Sixty-eight percent of them say they devote an entire day to one room. The kitchen is given the most attention followed by the bathroom or living room, and then you know, a few people said they focus on the bed room or their office space.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Doesn`t that might surprise you? Eleven Days out of 365? That`s the only time --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But I feel like, people don`t really clean up unless they know someone`s coming over.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is coming.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do some lazy, lazy wiping of the counter every now and then. I`m like, it is so lazy. Like, I see crumbs, I am like, I`ll just move the toaster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I`ll just move them over here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just move the toaster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: By the toaster. That`s funny.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now we`ve got a new album from a singer with a last name you may or may not recognize, Matteo Bocelli frequently collaborates with his dad, that would be Andrea, including their 2018 duet, I love this song, "Fall On Me."\par \par \uc2 Now the younger Bocelli is stepping into the spotlight. He`s got a debut solo record. You go, Matteo.\par \par \uc2 It features, contributions, and it is called "Matteo" -- how about that? It features contributions from songwriters like Ed Sheeran.\par \par \uc2 Their collaboration "Chasing Stars" is an ode to a father`s unconditional love.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Chasing Stars.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve got autumn walks with your father in the song. Matteo Bocelli joins us in the studio.\par \par \uc2 It`s so good to see you again, and congratulations. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: Thank you. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like the first thing that you released, "Chasing Stars" because there`s a line in it that says: "I was a lost boy who found faith in music." Did those words resonate with you? I know you worked with Ed Sheeran on this song. Go you on that, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: Yes, very much. Actually, I received, you know, this song from Ed and his brother Matthew and honestly, the song was already kind of like written by him.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Chasing Stars.")\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: But you know, the lyrics were so authentic to myself, that I said, I must do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I must do it. But what`s interesting material about your voice because I interviewed you and your dad, when "Fall On Me" came out and your dad didn`t even know you could sing, because you didn`t really sing around the house in front of your dad, true?\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: No, let`s say he knew that I was singing but he never like this, like heard me like properly singing. So yes. Once my mom, she went to him and she was like, have you ever heard your son singing properly? And he was like, probably not.\par \par \uc2 And so I remember I was singing like "Canaria" of Paolo Tosti. That`s where let`s say, I started to have some lessons from him, took some lessons.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he heard you. And he thought what?\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: No, honestly, I mean, you know every parent is always, you know, like, making good compliments to their sons and so probably, my father as well, but he was like truly surprised. Especially he always says about the power of the voice. No? Because it is not -- it`s not so important today, but in theaters, if you sing, you know, opera, it is an element that you must have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The power of the voice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have to reach every seat.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what did you do to develop your voice from that young age when it was recognized? Your mom recognized it. Your father recognized it. What was that training process like now to your solo first album?\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: You know, like, the album is pop? No? And so the training is not very much like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Arias.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like classical training.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: Yes, exactly. Like classical training, even if I studied at the Conservatory of Music, so I have kind of both training, no? The one that you can get from school, from your conservatory, and the one that you get singing by yourself at home, because you have totally a different way of expressing yourself with the words.\par \par \uc2 But I will say obviously, the technique is not the key of success, obviously. But it`s very important because it`s good to -- you know, to preserve your voice, your vocal cords and to not try to not ruin them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. You call your father your hero and you know, it`s a dream for a father to see their kids follow in their footsteps.\par \par \uc2 What has he taught you about this business? This industry?\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: First of all, I must say that when he first listened to the song, he was like, because the song goes, "When I was younger my father was my hero," and he was like, "Hmmm. I like this song."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Those lyrics spoke to him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like these lyrics. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: So yes, he`s definitely been like a very important you know, example for me. You know, he taught me many things. He gave me lots of values and -- but also like musically speaking, he taught me many vocal techniques, secrets in how to approach, now, in general like a career.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: It has been, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you like pop, Matteo. That`s interesting. You like pop music.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what you want to do.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: Yes, this is you know, obviously that`s my toughest challenge now 