RLESON: Ash, I appreciate you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, of course, we partner with all of these companies to get these deals, to buy anything that you saw, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com to start shopping now.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (MATTEO BOCELLI performing "Fasi.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have Matteo Bocelli, he stuck around. We`re really glad about that. Matteo, my phone has been blowing off the hook with people saying, "Is he single?"\par \par \uc2 The answer to that question is, no, he is not single. Kate is here today and she is telling all of you guys, "Back back."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Back off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Matteo`s new album is out on Friday. His US tour kicks off November 22nd in Fort -- where is it, Matteo? Fort Lauderdale on the 22nd of November.\par \par \uc2 What would you like to play us out? What would you like?\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: A few -- a few seconds of -- this is the last single I released.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: It is called "Fasi."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Fasi."\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI: It is a very important song for me. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s go. All right.\par \par \uc2 (MATTEO BOCELLI performing "Fasi.")\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230919ej9j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 18, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Adam Yamaguchi, Kris Van Cleave, Jessica Moore,  Elise Preston, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 3,628 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Five Americans set to arrive back home tonight after a prisoner swap with Iran, trading $6 billion in frozen funds for their freedom. The new details we are just learning.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines. Arm in arm and hugging on the tarmac in Qatar, the gratitude tonight from their families, criticism from others, the risky deal made.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: A person of interest has been detained in the fatal shooting of an L.A. County sheriff`s deputy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: The 30-year-old deputy was killed in broad daylight while waiting at a red light.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT LUNA (Los Angeles County, California, Sheriff): Our deputy left this station, and he was murdered, ambushed by a coward. Ryan`s family will never see him again.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: More autoworkers to walk off the job, the Treasury secretary asked about the impacts on the economy, as employees walk the picket line in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: An apparent fentanyl exposure killed a 1-year-old boy at a Bronx day care. Two people are now facing murder charges.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Day care owner Grei Mendez and building tenant Carlisto Brito each face nearly a dozen charges, including murder, assault and child endangerment.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Just pulled out a 9-millimeter wrapped in plastic out of the gas tank. Mexico has incredibly strict gun laws. To get firearms, criminals have to smuggle them in.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS DEMLEIN (Former Senior ATF Special Agent): We`re talking about people that are buying guns and trafficking throughout these very complicated networks all over the United States.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #5: Military officials are looking for a state-of-the-art F-35 warplane. The F-35 was flying out of Joint Base Charleston when the pilot had to eject. There is no concrete evidence that the plane has crashed.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And the historic discovery more than 16,000 feet underwater. Our first look at a shipwreck from the battle of midway.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, and thank you for joining us as we begin a new week together.\par \par \uc2 Five American citizens tonight are on the final leg of their yearslong journey to freedom. Just hours from now, the prisoners who were wrongly detained inside Iran will arrive back in the U.S. to Fort Belvoir, not far from where we are here in the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 It took months and months of secret diplomatic talks. And here`s what we know about the high-stakes deal. The U.S. released $6 billion of frozen Iranian oil funds and let five Iranians who were facing charges for nonviolent crimes walk free.\par \par \uc2 Republicans tonight are voicing concern about the money headed to Iran, a state sponsor of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 Now, earlier today, we saw the Americans get their first taste of freedom in years. The White House said President Biden had an emotional call with their families after they left Iran.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` chief foreign affairs correspondent, Margaret Brennan, is here to start us off.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good evening to you, Norah.\par \par \uc2 And these five American families will be reunited later tonight. President Biden called the family members. And, as one said, for the first time in years, there is light in their home.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voiceover): Joy and relief after the five Americans and two family members touched down in Qatar today and were handed over to U.S. officials.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  said he had an emotional conversation with the freed Americans.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of State): It`s very good to be able to say that our fellow citizens are free after enduring something that I think it would be difficult for any of us to imagine.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For 51-year-old businessman Siamak Namazi, it is the end to an eight-year ordeal, according to his attorney, Jared Genser.\par \par \uc2 JARED GENSER (Attorney for Siamak Namazi): He has missed some of the best years of his life, eight years. He wants to be married and have kids. He now has to figure out where he`s going to live, what`s he going to do for a living, for a job, and how does he rebuild his life?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Iran detained environmentalist Morad Tahbaz in 2018. The same year, businessman Emad Shargi was first arrested and held without trial in Iran`s notorious Evin prison on what the U.S. considers trumped-up espionage charges.\par \par \uc2 In return, five Iranian nationals were granted clemency. Two will remain in the U.S. Two others returned to Iran today. Critics of the hostage diplomacy worry the regime will now, as part of the deal, have access to $6 billion worth of unfrozen Iranian oil assets held in a restricted account in Qatar.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER (R-Ohio): These monies can be used to support terrorist organizations, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and, you know, adventuresome actions of Iran.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Biden administration insists the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  can prevent that misuse and only permit humanitarian purchases like food and medicine.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: The funds were moved to another bank, where we have absolute oversight of how they -- how they`re used. And we have absolute confidence in the process and the system that`s been set up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Now, another concern is that releasing funds incentivizes hostage-taking by putting a price on the head of other Americans.\par \par \uc2 As for other diplomacy with Iran, both President Biden and Iran`s President Raisi are in New York City, but, Norah, they do not have plans to meet.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Quite a development.\par \par \uc2 Margaret Brennan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a manhunt is over in Los Angeles, as we`re learning new details about how police tracked down a man accused of ambushing a sheriff`s deputy. The 29-year-old suspect is accused of a brutal killing of an officer just five days after he got engaged.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston has the new information.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Dozens of SWAT officers surrounded this home before sunrise, determined to capture the man suspected of gunning down one of their own.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT LUNA (Los Angeles County, California, Sheriff): We believe we have the murderer of our deputy in custody.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: After barricading himself for several hours, officers deployed chemical agents, forcing 29-year-old Kevin Salazar to surrender.\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF ROBERT LUNA: Our deputies gave this suspect an opportunity to peacefully give up. That is not the right that was afforded to our deputy three days ago out here across the street.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Thirty-year-old Deputy Sheriff Ryan Clinkunbroomer was fatally shot ambush-style just yards from his Palmdale station.\par \par \uc2 Police believe this surveillance video shows the moment a dark gray \uc2 Toyota\uc2  pulls up next to his patrol car at a red light, pauses, and then drives off.\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF ROBERT LUNA: He was shot and killed. Why? We don`t know yet. He was murdered, ambushed by a coward.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The outpouring of sorrow has been immense.\par \par \uc2 Clinkunbroomer, who just got engaged, had been with the sheriff`s department for eight years. Both his father and grandfather had also served as deputies.\par \par \uc2 Can you give us more insight as to who -- who he was?\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF ROBERT LUNA: An incredible young man in this bloodline of people who served our community for decades and decades.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Clinkunbroomer`s family is asking for the public`s prayers.\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF ROBERT LUNA: Ryan`s family will never see him again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, the sheriff says tips from outraged community members helped officers capture the suspect.\par \par \uc2 Some of those arresting officers were Deputy Clinkunbroomer`s partners, who are mourning their fallen friend -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elise Preston, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there`s disturbing new information about the suspected fentanyl exposure of four young children at a day care center in New York City. Sources tell CBS New York that the day care was a drug front, police revealing tonight that the fentanyl was found under a mat where the children had been napping.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jessica Moore has the update from the Bronx.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA MOORE (voiceover): Tonight, as 1-year-old Nicholas Dominici`s parents grieve, police say day care owner Grei Mendez called her husband before calling 911 to get help for the children who were unresponsive.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D-Mayor of New York City): We`re all parents. And you cannot be a parent and not be angry at what happened to those four babies. I am.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA MOORE: Newly released video shows Mendez`s husband appearing to throw out drugs before first responders arrived at the scene. Now police are searching for him.\par \par \uc2 They say a kilo of fentanyl and drug-making equipment was found inside the day care. Mendez and her husband`s cousin, Carlisto Acevedo Brito, who rented a room at the rear of the day care, were both arrested on Saturday night and charged with murder, showing depraved indifference. In addition to four counts of endangering the welfare of a child, criminal possession of drugs and 12 counts of assault.\par \par \uc2 Mendez`s attorney said she didn`t know about the drugs.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES ARANDA (Attorney For Grei Mendez): She rented a room to somebody, and she didn`t know what was going on.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA MOORE: Tonight, all three of the hospitalized children, two 2-year- olds and the 8-month-old sister of one of them, are listed in stable condition.\par \par \uc2 Dominici had only been attending Divino Nino day care for a week. His parents say he would have turned 2 in November.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN (Through Translator): We can`t -- what are we going to do? Not even in a daycare can children be safe.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA MOORE: The feds are now investigating this case.\par \par \uc2 This day care was licensed by the city and even recently passed a surprise inspection by the Office of Children and Family Services. When we reached out to them, they called the incident horrific, but declined further comment -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How did they pass that inspection?\par \par \uc2 Jessica Moore, that`s a key question. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well now to the historic \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike against Detroit`s Big Three automakers where little progress is being made in the negotiations. With the walkout now in its fourth day, both sides remain far apart when it comes to getting a new deal.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave reports again from Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thank you!\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Tonight, as striking workers continue to man the picket line, the autoworkers union is poised to expand the walkout as soon as this week if there`s no deal with the Big Three automakers.\par \par \uc2 Union President Shawn Fain:\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (President, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ): Our members are fed up. Our members deserve more. And if they continue down this path, then we will continue to amp up our strategy as far as striking goes.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 UAW\uc2  is currently seeking up to a 40 percent raise over four years, as well as job and cost of living protections. The union rejected offers from Ford, General, Motors and Chrysler parent, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , centered around a 20 percent raise.\par \par \uc2 KYLE O`SHEA (Strike Captain, Stellantis Autoworker): We have been under the thumb of these billion-dollar industries for a long time, and we`re fighting back.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: So far, just three plants have been targeted, limiting the economic impact.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary): I think it`s premature to be making forecasts about what it means for the economy. It would depend very much on how long the strike lasts.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Already affected is the Northland Chrysler dealership in Oak Park, Michigan; 70 percent of their customers are \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers.\par \par \uc2 You may not have inventory, but you also may not have buyers.\par \par \uc2 KEN THOMAS (Operating Partner, Northland Chrysler Dodge Jeep Ram): Correct. Not having a paycheck for a week is an impact on the buyers themselves. So they`re not going to be able to come in and do that basic service. They can`t buy a car if they`re not actually working.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Now, the automakers are signaling, while they are talking with the union, they`re yet to make new offers.\par \par \uc2 And across the border in Canada, about 5,000 Ford workers could walk off the job at midnight, when their labor agreement expires. Those negotiations are continuing -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Kris Van Cleave, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Hunter Biden`s legal team is going on the offensive, filing a lawsuit against the \uc2 IRS\uc2  for the alleged unlawful release of his private tax details. Attorneys claim two agents violated Biden`s privacy rights when they disclosed his tax information during media interviews, including with CBS News.\par \par \uc2 Biden was indicted last week on federal charges related to his purchase of a firearm in October 2018 while he was a drug user. The \uc2 IRS\uc2  said it won`t comment due to pending litigation.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to an in-depth investigation into the arming of Mexican drug cartels by American gun smugglers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adam Yamaguchi reports on how up to a million guns are making it across the Southern border every year.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI (voiceover): We are about to see how easy it is for American weapons to get into the hands of the Mexican cartel.\par \par \uc2 Oh wow. Just pulled out a 9-millimeter wrapped in plastic out of the gas tank.\par \par \uc2 Within the last couple of hours, this smuggler drove this car across the border into Mexico. Mexico has incredibly strict gun laws, making it next to impossible for civilians to buy firearms. Only one gun store exists for the entire country, and it`s on a military base. So, to get firearms, criminals have to smuggle them in, often in cars like this.\par \par \uc2 It`s got this AR-15 that was hidden in this rear seat. How much do you make when you do this?\par \par \uc2 MAN #1 (through translator): Five thousand dollars. It`s very easy to buy guns in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: U.S. Intelligence estimates up to one million American guns are smuggled into Mexico each year. Those weapons empower cartels to protect a deadly drug trade, fueling an epidemic.\par \par \uc2 Last year alone, the DEA seized 379 million doses of illicit fentanyl in the U.S., enough to kill every American. A growing chorus of political leaders have been sounding the alarm, pushing to designate cartels to terrorists and deploy the U.S. military against them.\par \par \uc2 But, if that happened, odds are the cartels would shoot back using weapons and ammo that were made in America. We traveled to a cartel stronghold along the Southwest border to find out how they`d react if the U.S. attacked.\par \par \uc2 This man said he was a lieutenant in the Sinaloa cartel.\par \par \uc2 Would the cartels respond if the U.S. military struck?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2 (Through Translator): If another person comes and wants to take property, one is going to fight.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER LANDAU (Former U.S. Ambassador to Mexico): I was the U.S. ambassador to Mexico, and so I`m very aware of this issue. We are going to get caught in a quicksand if we go into Mexico that will make Vietnam or Iraq or Afghanistan look like a tea party.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: The Mexican cartels are among the deadliest groups in the world, but a CBS News investigation found the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has known the narcos have been smuggling military-grade weapons out of the U.S. into Mexico for years.\par \par \uc2 So, we are arming the cartels?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS DEMLEIN (Former Senior ATF Special Agent): A hundred percent. No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Former ATF agent Chris Demlein led a law enforcement team that discovered the scope of the problem.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS DEMLEIN: I had an intel analyst come to me one day. It was instantly apparent that the cartels had large-scale covert weapons trafficking networks all across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 I said: "Who`s doing something about this?"\par \par \uc2 And he said: "Nobody."\par \par \uc2 I went: "Well, you want to do something about it?"\par \par \uc2 And he was like: "Yeah."\par \par \uc2 I`m like: "Well, (expletive deleted). Let`s do something about it then."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: And what they did was start Project Thor, a multiagency task force that discovered highly sophisticated gun-running networks across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Now, the full extent of Project Thor has not been reported on. And we will have that story for you tomorrow night -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Extraordinary reporting.\par \par \uc2 Adam Yamaguchi, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas now, Ukrainian troops have breached Russian lines and recaptured key territory in the eastern part of the country near the bombed-out city of Bakhmut. Ukraine`s president is in the U.S. for a speech at the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  tomorrow in New York.\par \par \uc2 And then he will travel right here to Washington to meet with President Biden and congressional leaders to ask for more aid.\par \par \uc2 And one person who has gotten to know President Zelenskyy well since the Russian invasion began is an Academy Award winner Sean Penn. His new documentary follows a comedian-turned-commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 We sat down with the filmmaker just after coming back from his eighth trip to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL (voiceover): With rare access, Sean Penn turns the camera on the people of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Superpower, Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN (Actor/Activist): Because you can.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Because I can.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: "Superpower" on Paramount+ chronicles Penn`s trip to the war-torn country and his time with its president, beginning with an interview as the first Russian bombs fell.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Superpower, Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And what was your sense of Zelenskyy at that moment, this comedian-turned-politician-turned-war-president? You saw it on day one.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: You know, overnight, he became a wartime president, and against a nuclear superpower. It appeared he was born for it, and it was an overwhelming sense of the courage that`s now been talked about and demonstrated by him.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Superpower, Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: It wasn`t anything that I`d ever seen, you know, with that kind of scope in -- with almost all of the Ukrainians that we saw in that moment.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Superpower, Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now the debate in Washington continues over aid to Ukraine a year-and-a-half since the war began.\par \par \uc2 Is \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  just waiting out the American public and congressional support?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: I think yes is one of the answers. And it`s really time that the American people -- we, I, all of us, in our whatever way do our part to partner with and support and demand that leadership does its.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Do you think that Ukraine will ultimately win this war?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: There`s not a cell in my body that sees anything other than that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Our extended interview streams Thursday on Person to Person, including what it was like for Penn to meet \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  back in 2001.\par \par \uc2 There`s some breaking news just coming in, new developments in the search for a missing F-35, one of the world`s most advanced fighter jets.\par \par \uc2 We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking tonight, a search team just discovering a debris field two hours northeast of Joint Base Charleston. It is believed to be the wreckage of an F-35 fighter jet that disappeared on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The $100 million stealth jet crashed after the pilot safely ejected over Charleston, South Carolina. Today, the Marines grounded all aviation crews for a two-day safety stand-down. This is the third incident involving Marine aircraft in recent weeks, including a fatal Osprey crash.\par \par \uc2 There was a bear scare today at Disney World -- those details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A bear sighting today at Disney World forced about half of the Magic Kingdom to temporarily shut down.\par \par \uc2 The female black bear was spotted in a tree. Florida Wildlife officials say it was safely captured and will be relocated to an area near the Ocala National Forest. Those are the bear necessities.\par \par \uc2 All right, an underwater survey takes a look at historic Battle of Midway. We have got the amazing new images. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight, an extraordinary view from the ocean floor giving us a new look at the warships lost during the Battle of Midway 81 years ago during World War II.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this, the first images of the USS Yorktown since it was located some 25 years ago. We can see the flight deck and the anti-aircraft guns. Also revealed is the first up-close look at the Japanese aircraft carrier Akagi sunk in June of 1942 that marked a turning point in the Pacific war.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230919ej9i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For September 17, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Kris Van Cleave, Anthony Salvanto, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,188 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: A historic strike hits one of America`s major industries.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: What are we?\par \par \uc2 UNITED AUTO WORKERS MEMBERS: \uc2 UAW\uc2 !\par \par \uc2 MAN: One more time.\par \par \uc2 UNITED AUTO WORKERS MEMBERS: \uc2 UAW\uc2 !\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden`s economic agenda is in the crosshairs, as the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  go on strike against Detroit`s Big Three automakers, demanding higher pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Autoworkers help create America`s middle class.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How close is a deal that would end the strike? And will economic anxiety erode the president`s standing in key battleground states?\par \par \uc2 We will get the latest from \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain and Michigan Democratic Congresswoman Debbie Dingell.\par \par \uc2 And more bad news for Biden, as his son Hunter is indicted on gun charges and the House GOP launches an impeachment inquiry alleging corruption by the president and his family.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think he could do better. I think he`s trying, but he`s not -- he`s not strong enough.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Plus, we will have new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll data to gauge how voters are thinking about the president`s age and performance.\par \par \uc2 Then: A prisoner swap with Iran is expected in the coming days. We will get the latest from Congressional Intelligence Chairs Republican Congressman \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2  and Democratic Senator Mark Warner.\par \par \uc2 Finally, a conversation with actor and director Sean Penn about his new documentary from the front lines of the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We begin this morning with \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ` simultaneous strike against Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . Roughly 8 percent of the union`s workers stopped work at plants in Ohio, Michigan and Missouri after their contracts expired Thursday night. The union says, if they don`t get a deal, they will walk out of additional plants.\par \par \uc2 Talks continued throughout the weekend and will resume with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Senior transportation correspondent Kris Van Cleave is in Toledo, Ohio, with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice-over): Hitting automakers in their bottom line.\par \par \uc2 For a third day, nearly 13,000 \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  have pumped the brakes at plants in Michigan. Ohio and Missouri, striking after their union contract with the nation`s Big Three automakers ran out.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: I`m here for the long haul.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: The long haul. And if I have to be out here rain, sleet, snow, thunderstorm, tornado, striking, I`m here.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: I don`t think they`re honking for the CEOs.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And they`re getting the backing of high-profile Democrats. Pennsylvania Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  joined the picket line, while Vermont`s Bernie Sanders headlined a huge rally just feet from the Detroit Auto Show.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BERNIE SANDERS (I-Vermont): It is totally reasonable for autoworkers to finally receive a fair share of the record-breaking profits that their labor has produced.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: About an hour south of Detroit, there are 10,000 autoworkers here in Toledo.\par \par \uc2 More than half of them are now on strike at this Jeep plant. But that likely has more to do with what`s built here, the popular and lucrative Jeep Wrangler and Jeep Gladiator, vehicles that could soon be in short supply on dealer lots.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT VASQUEZ (\uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  Member): Most of us can`t even afford the vehicles that we`re building.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Robert Vasquez (sp?) has worked at the Toledo plant since 1997.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT VASQUEZ: We`re in it to the end. You know, we -- we want our fair share.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The union is seeking a 36 percent raise, with cost of living increases to counter inflation, the unwinding of concessions made during the Great Recession, and worker protection, as the companies transition to less labor-intensive electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 They also want a four-day workweek and a return of pensions. Both are nonstarters for the automakers. The Big Three are offering about a 20 percent raise.\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 ): It`s a record from a gross wage increase perspective in our 115-year history.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Can GM be successful if you met all their demands?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA\uc2 : Actually, no. The life of the contract, the initial demands were over $100 billion. We`re -- we still have a waste to go.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: GM now expects to idle a plant in Kansas, and Ford announced 600 temporary layoffs blamed on ripple effects from the strike.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That was Kris Van Cleave reporting from Toledo.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  President Shawn Fain.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, sir.\par \par \uc2 You have said that you had reasonably productive conversations with Ford yesterday. Does that mean they`re going to put a more generous offer on the table?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (President, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ): Good morning. Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 And that`s up to them. That`s -- you know, the reason we`re in this situation right now is because all three of the Big Three companies chose to wait. They chose not to negotiate for the eight weeks we had. We started this back in July. And we told them then, don`t wait until the last minute, or you`re going to find yourself in a bad position.\par \par \uc2 And, unfortunately, they chose to wait until the last week to get down and start talking, to get serious about this. And that`s where we are now. And if we don`t get better offers and we don`t get down and take care of the members` needs, then we`re going to amp this thing up even more.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you said progress is slow. Will you order strikes at additional plants this week? Are you preparing for that?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: We`re prepared to do whatever we have to do. So the membership is ready. The membership is fed up. We`re fed up with falling behind.\par \par \uc2 It`s been decades of falling behind, and especially this past decade, in the most -- wealthiest times in the history of these companies. There is no excuse. These companies have made a quarter-of-a-trillion dollars in the last 10 years, $21 billion in the last six months alone. And our workers` wages and conditions have went backwards.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re asking for a 36 percent pay raise, as our reporter just laid out there.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said they have offered 21 percent. What are you expecting in tomorrow`s negotiation with them? That seems forward movement.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: We have asked for 40 percent pay increases.\par \par \uc2 And the reason we asked for 40 percent pay increases is because, in the last four years alone, the CEO pay went up 40 percent. They`re already millionaires.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: It`s shameful that one of the -- one of the leaders of the corp -- one of the corporations is sitting in his second home in Acapulco while we`re bargaining, rather than being at the bargaining table.\par \par \uc2 And so our demands are just. We`re asking for our fair share in this economy and the fruits of our labor.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, 21 percent is a no-go for you?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: It`s definitely a no-go, and we have made that very clear to the companies.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ford`s CEO said last November that electric vehicles are going to require 40 percent less labor to produce than combustion vehicles.\par \par \uc2 I know it may not be the intention, but I wonder how you think this transition to electric vehicles may be eating away at your union strength.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: Well, I don`t believe it`s eating away at our union strength.\par \par \uc2 It is the way it is right now. Unfortunately, this is -- this is what`s wrong with our economy and this is what`s wrong with America right now. The billionaire class keeps taking more and more, and the working class keeps getting left behind.\par \par \uc2 And the unfortunate part in this transition right now, like always, go back to the -- go back to the Great Recession. The banks got bailed out by our taxpayer dollars, and they just kept on doing what they do, while working- class people`s homes got foreclosed on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Automakers got bailed out too.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: You go back to the, you know -- yes, automakers got bailed out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And taxpayers lost money on that.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: And, again, the workers were unfairly -- the workers were unfairly blamed for everything that was wrong with those companies. It was bad -- bad decisions on the parts of the companies that put us in that position.\par \par \uc2 And the sad reality is, you know, the workers paid the price for that. We made all the -- all the -- all the sacrifices, and, after a decade of massive profits, the workers have went backwards.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: Our wages have went backwards. Our benefits have went backwards. The majority of our members have zero retirement security now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: And, meanwhile...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: ... it`s insulting that a CEO gets on air this last -- in the last few days and says that her $29 million salary is justified by her performance.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: No, it`s not. It`s justified by the performance of the worker, on the backs of the workers, and by paying them poverty wages.\par \par \uc2 And that`s unacceptable in this country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I know. You have also said, though, it`s a shell game to talk about CEOs handing back part of their money.\par \par \uc2 You`re talking about something that`s more fundamental to the structure of this entire sector of the economy. And that`s why I`m asking you about the transition, because many of the factories in this country that make batteries for those electric vehicles are not unionized.\par \par \uc2 And that is where the White House is pushing the industry to go, more towards those electric vehicles. Is it -- is that -- isn`t that part of this challenge for you leverage-wise?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: So, the challenge is where we`re going to go as a country.\par \par \uc2 Again, I get back to this point. Our tax dollars are financing a massive portion of this transition to E.V. We believe in a green economy. We have to have clean water. We have to have clean air. Anyone that doesn`t believe global warming is happening isn`t -- isn`t paying attention.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: But this transition has to be a just transition.\par \par \uc2 And a just transition means, if our tax dollars are going to finance this transition, then labor can`t be left behind. And, as it stands right now, the workers are being left behind. The companies want to talk about being competitive.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: It`s not about being competitive. Competitive is -- is a code word for race to the bottom.\par \par \uc2 What they want is, they want to pay us poverty wages, so they can keep on making billions more in profits and they can keep enriching the shareholders and the CEOs and the corporate executives, while the workers pay the -- pay the -- pay the price for it...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: ... and get left behind.\par \par \uc2 That`s got to stop in this country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ford has said that your demands would more than double the labor costs, which are already significantly higher than the labor costs at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , at \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , and other foreign-owned automakers, who don`t use union labor.\par \par \uc2 So, how do you make the case that these automakers need to keep investing in more expensive union shops, rather than move to these right-to-work states?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: First off, labor costs are about 5 percent of the cost of the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 They could double our wages and not raise the price of the vehicles and still make billions in profits. It`s a choice. And the fact that they want to compare it to how -- how pitiful \uc2 Tesla\uc2  pays their workers and other companies pay their workers, that`s what this whole argument`s about.\par \par \uc2 Workers in this country got to decide if they want a better life for themselves, instead of scraping to get by paycheck to paycheck, while everybody else walks away with the loot.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: And when we bargain good contracts, going back to the founding of this union, people join the \uc2 UAW\uc2  because we set the standard.\par \par \uc2 People join unions because they -- it`s a better way of life. And that`s what we got to do. We have to bargain a good contract. And then we`re going to go organize these places...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: ... and bring these workers in, so they get their fair share of the economy that they get nothing of right now.\par \par \uc2 Most of these workers in those companies are scraping to get by so that greedy CEOs and greedy people like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  can build more rocket ships and shoot theirself in outer space. And that`s unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden says he`s the most pro-union president in American history, but you haven`t endorsed him.\par \par \uc2 What is it going to take for you to do that?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: Our endorsements are going to be earned. We`ve been very clear about that, no matter what politician it is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How does he earn it?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: We expect action. We expect action, not words.\par \par \uc2 And this -- this fight we`re in right now. I mean, obviously, you know, people are talking about them trying to interject themselves into our -- into our negotiations. You know, this -- this negotiating -- our negotiators are fighting hard. Our leadership is fighting hard.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to be won at the negotiating table with our negotiating teams, with our members manning the picket lines and our allies out there. Who the president is now...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: ... who the former president was or the presidents before them isn`t going to win this fight.\par \par \uc2 This fight is all about one thing. It`s about workers winning their fair share of economic justice...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: ... instead of being left behind, as they have been in the last decades.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be watching closely.\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 And we did ask all of the Big Three automakers to come on the show this morning, and they declined.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Democratic Congresswoman Debbie Dingell, who has longtime ties to GM and the auto industry. She represents Detroit and is in Southfield, Michigan, this morning.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, you have said these are the most important negotiations you have witnessed in your lifetime. You have longstanding ties to these automakers. Is it really more significant than when the Bush and Obama administrations bailed out the automakers?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE DEBBIE DINGELL (D-Michigan): Yes, it`s far more significant. And I will tell you why.\par \par \uc2 Those days were -- these companies were facing bankruptcy. And, quite frankly, it was because of management decisions. And the autoworkers were scared for their jobs, and they stepped up and they gave away their cost of living increases to help. They gave in 2008 and 2009.\par \par \uc2 Now the companies are back in a strong position. But, really, where the rubber hits the road, Margaret, we are in a transition of this industry. We`re competing in a world marketplace, that we are -- some of the countries in Europe, you will see, in the last quarter, the electric vehicle sales are more than 50 percent of the sales.\par \par \uc2 That`s what we`re competing in. And we have to make sure the worker is part of this transition. It should not be either/or. It needs to be both. We have got to make sure the worker can afford to buy that electric vehicle...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE DEBBIE DINGELL: ... that they`re going to have the support that they do. And we have got to make sure we`re paying a fair and decent wage.\par \par \uc2 And all workers, everybody in this country benefits when workers are paid well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But those electric vehicle battery manufacturing plants, so many of them are joint ventures, that they are partially foreign-owned. They are not unionized. That`s a choice.\par \par \uc2 How do you reverse that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 DEBBIE DINGELL\uc2 : The -- well, I mean, that is something that`s very real. And on the table right now is, really, there`s probably only one battery plant that may be under the master agreement.\par \par \uc2 We have got to -- it`s not an easy question. It`s not easy to deal with. And I also want to say something else that everybody doesn`t understand. They`re -- yes, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  does have a huge discrepancy in what they`re paying their employees. And most people in this country can`t afford a \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE DEBBIE DINGELL: Even a lot of executives can`t afford to buy a \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is that most -- at a \uc2 Toyota\uc2  -- I have looked at the studies. Almost all workers at auto plants benefit from where these negotiations go. This is where the rubber hits the road. We have got to figure out how we`re going to do this transition, how we`re going to go from the transition of an internal combustion engine, which -- and pay people who are making that battery a decent wage, similar to what they`re making for ICE.\par \par \uc2 And there are going to be new jobs and different jobs that are going to come from this transition. But it`s not a talking point moment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE DEBBIE DINGELL: This is a real, intentional, hard moment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden said he was sending his staff to Detroit. They`re not there right now. You just heard Shawn Fain say something about the White House injecting themselves into negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the president and White House should intervene?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 DEBBIE DINGELL\uc2 : First of all, I do not believe that the president should intervene or be at the negotiating table. I have said that from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE DEBBIE DINGELL: But, you know, if anything that the pandemic has just taught us is that, like, who`s in an office and who`s working.\par \par \uc2 I talk to Gene Sperling multiple times a day and have all summer. So, there are -- I don`t think they have got a role at the negotiating table. I think every one of us that are policymakers and other stakeholders need to understand what these issues are, what we can do to support those discussions at the table, and what we need to do coming out of these to help make a strong, viable, competitive industry that`s employing American jobs.\par \par \uc2 And I`m not going to let these electric vehicles be built in China, even though someone else says they want to build 100 percent in China. I`m fighting to make sure they`re here with good-paying American jobs.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Michigan -- I don`t have to tell you this, that it is a competitive state, and we`re going into an election year.\par \par \uc2 I just asked Shawn Fain what it would take for the union to endorse President Biden, who says he`s the most pro-union president ever. And he said that has to be earned. That`s a pretty big statement.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 DEBBIE DINGELL\uc2 : You know, I`m -- well, first of all, he`s also said that Donald Trump`s -- would be a disaster, and I could go into that at length.\par \par \uc2 But I really have to tell you that I think that we have got to keep these two issues totally separate. I`m really worried about what`s happening at the table, and that it`s going to set -- it`s going to -- it is going to determine the future of the auto industry in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 I want to keep presidential politics out of this and do what`s right from a policy perspective. Then we can talk about the presidential election. Michigan`s a competitive state. I keep telling you all it`s a purple state. It`s not a blue state.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE DEBBIE DINGELL: But when we get a good agreement that keeps America strong, keeps our workers strong, then I think they will know who they`re going to support.\par \par \uc2 And they`re going to support someone that supports the American worker.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure. But Democrats love to say that they are the...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE DEBBIE DINGELL: And that`s somebody, by the way, that doesn`t care about...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, go ahead. Sorry?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 DEBBIE DINGELL\uc2 : Well, no, I mean, everybody`s like, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he`s going to swoop in and do a pickup.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he doesn`t care about their pay raises. He doesn`t care about COLA. He doesn`t care about retirement. He doesn`t care about pensions. He doesn`t care. I mean, last time he campaigned in Michigan, he told the companies they should move to other states where they pay people less money.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that we all, as policymakers, need to understand the issues. How do we support this transition...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 DEBBIE DINGELL\uc2 : ... so that we are staying competitive in this country?\par \par \uc2 But I want to do presidential politics after this is done at the table, and we are keeping a strong, viable industry...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 DEBBIE DINGELL\uc2 : ... which is, make the workers part of the success.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Debbie Dingell, Congresswoman, I appreciate your insight.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in a minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our latest CBS News poll shows President Biden in a precarious position as he seeks reelection, down one point, within the margin of error, in a hypothetical matchup with the Republican front- runner, former President Trump, whom he beat by seven million votes in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Questions about Mr. Biden`s age seem to be taking the toll. Only one-third of voters think he would definitely finish a second term if reelected. As for Trump, just over half are sure he`d finish his term if returned to the White House.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to discuss it is our elections and surveys director, Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 The vice president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , told me last week \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to be just fine. It doesn`t seem like voters are convinced.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: They`re not entirely convinced.\par \par \uc2 And there is concern. And that speaks to uncertainty, in this way. When people don`t think that a candidate is going to finish a second term, they`re less likely to vote for that candidate. And that`s certainly happening in the case of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It`s also happening with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, there`s also a component here when people are asked, do you think these candidates are physically fit enough, are mentally, cognitively fit enough? And neither one of them inspires overwhelming confidence here.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not accruing well for the president right now. You see a substantial number of voters who aren`t sure that he is, and that is helping \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , those voters switching. And even in the case of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you don`t have an overwhelming number of voters who think he is physically fit or cognitively fit.\par \par \uc2 The reason that all speaks to uncertainty is that, well, people don`t like uncertainty, but when they`re making a presidential vote, you`re thinking about, well, if this person isn`t going to vote to finish their second term, then what am I going to get for my vote? And that creates a new wrinkle in the decision process.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So what else do voters say they want from a president?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, this is important, because you start with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Voters have always told us, they think he`s calm. They think he`s predictable. Those qualities really met the moment in 2020, during the pandemic, when he was running against then-President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Well, today, they want more. They want someone that they think is tough. They want someone that they think is energetic. And those are qualities they do not ascribe to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does do better on those qualities. And that`s important.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s this, Donald Trump`s voters overwhelmingly say one reason they`re voting for him is, they think things were better under him. And that relates to the economy. It relates to people`s finances. I mentioned the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 We look at whether people say they`re doing better or worse now than they were before the pandemic. And people say that they are not. By 2-1, they say that they are still worse off than better. And those people are voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That`s kind of classic. If you`re not better off, you vote for the out-party.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s interesting people are focused on those first years of the Trump presidency, rather than the pandemic years of the Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 But we`re really early in this, Anthony. Why are you polling when we don`t officially have a Republican nominee a head-to-head race?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes, it is very early.\par \par \uc2 But, number one, you have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  so far out in front in the Republican primaries. The indictments haven`t hurt him. They have only really helped him. So you have this presumption that we`re going to get a rematch. And that is unusual. And, look, any time you have something that`s unusual, you want to understand it as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Another point for context here, right? Democrats are going to look at this. That is a shift, as you said, from Biden`s fairly comfortable 2020 win to where it is now.\par \par \uc2 In the summer of 1995, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was down to \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 . In the summer of 2011, Barack Obama`s disapproval rating was hitting all-time highs. Both of them turned it around and got reelected. OK, maybe that gives Democrats hope. But this certainly is a shift from what we saw in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So what is it that people think is at stake?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Ah.\par \par \uc2 Well, I should also say, we`re polling on a race people don`t seem to want.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Which is to say, when we ask people how they feel about getting this rematch, they said that they think that means politics in the U.S. is broken.\par \par \uc2 And -- yes, but, look, having said that, they also see really high stakes here. And they say that not only is democracy and rule of law potentially at stake, but, maybe in a sign of the times, it`s only going to be safe if their guy wins.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Of course.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Salvanto, thank you for sharing your insights.\par \par \uc2 We will be back with a lot more Face the Nation. So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We are joined now by the Republican chairman of the House Intelligence Committee, \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE TURNER (R-OH), (Chairman, House Intelligence Committee): Good morning. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A lot to get to with you, but I want to start on what`s happening within the next eight days. We may be facing a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Back in June it took Democratic votes to push through that debt ceiling deal. The speaker announced an impeachment inquiry. Isn`t this going to make it harder for you to work across the aisle and to do something like avoid a government shutdown?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Not really. There are a number of things, obviously, where there`s, you know, bipartisan consensus, one of which, as you`ve indicated, was the budget debt deal. Kevin led in that. The American public wanted a change from the Biden policies of spending, working on closing the border, addressing the issues of China, and he delivered. And, you know, would the American public have given the House with a small margin of Republicans in - in control, the Senate, Democrats in control, small margin, they expect bipartisan solutions. Kevin has led in delivering those. This is certainly one where everyone wants the government to be functioning. We want to adhere to the deal. Move the country from -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`ve got eight days.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, what do we do here? I mean is this a continuing resolution situation? Because it doesn`t seem like House Republicans are on the same page.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Well, I -- you know, as you just said, it is probably going to result, in the end, in a bipartisan solution. The -- we`re going to - we`re going to pass a spending bill. That`s what is going to be required, and we`re going to do it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it will require a Democrat. So, you don`t think the impeachment inquiry will cost you?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Well, again, there - there are things that are important to the country and that for which people will come together. And I think this is one of those, that the country came together when -- on the - that debt budget deal. And at that time investigations were already ongoing into the - the Biden family businesses. And I think that`s certainly something that`s expected that will continue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: One of the issues for some members of your caucus is the continued support for Ukraine. And we know President Zelenskyy is going to be here this week.\par \par \uc2 Can you meet with him and tell him he`s going to get the $13 billion that the White House is asking for? Can you get your caucus to support it?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Well, the House certainly - and certainly the Republican caucus overwhelmingly supports aid for Ukraine. There will be issues over what the administration has asked for and what Congress ultimately gives. Speaker McCarthy has made it clear that the White House should have come to us and worked on what the package was and not just sent it to the - to Capitol Hill for - for dispensation.\par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, you know, Zelenskyy`s a great spokesperson. He really makes the case better than anyone that this is a fight for democracy and that Putin`s goals are well beyond Ukraine into Eastern Europe and into the Baltics. Having him here is going to be very, very persuasive.\par \par \uc2 The last time we had votes on the House floor on the issue of aid for Ukraine, nearly 300 members voted in affirmative out of 435. A majority of the Republicans voted in the affirmative. And I think that will continue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But this is not going to complicate the vote to keep the government funded and open? You - you don`t think (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Well, again, this is another -- this is another essential item that we have to do.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: And this -- this was - this was not included in the budget to - a debt limit deal -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: With an understanding, though, because everyone knew. I mean this is on our TV sets every day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Everyone knows this is ongoing and this is going to require U.S. support.\par \par \uc2 You know, Zelenskyy has a higher approval rating in the United States than any nationally elected official. Him coming here I think will be very persuasive.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Even to the more conservative members of your caucus?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Well, not everyone has to vote yes. If you get 300 out of 435, that`s certainly overwhelming. I think we`re going to be back there again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, I hear - do I hear you saying no government shutdown?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: I didn`t say that. I said we`re going to pass a spending bill. We`ll just have to see when.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, we`ll be -- OK.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The White House is going to announce additional capabilities. They`ve said that. Do they get the long-range missiles to Ukraine that they`re asking for, the ATACMS.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: I hope they do. I mean the administration has consistently said no to everything Ukraine has said multiple times publicly and then ultimately recanted and provided them.\par \par \uc2 What we know from this, you know, era of the conflict, Ukraine is still on the offensive, which was the goal of the offensive. Russia is on the defensive. There are a number of impediments that are making it difficult for that offensive to push Russia out. At the same time Ukraine has to get additional ground and/or longer range weapons to put Crimea at risk, which is where some of the assaults are coming from that - that, you know, are killing Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: So, it`s incredibly important that we provide them this capability.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Your committee was briefed by the White House within the past week about this expected prisoner swap with Iran. Their diplomatic mission shared with us the list of Iranians they expect President Biden to pardon. Are you comfortable with this swap?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Well, I mean, when - when we received our briefing at the committee, obviously, we made known our - our concerns. I mean whenever you put a price on American heads, you get an incentive for people to take more hostages. And these are -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Releasing the $6 billion in frozen oil revenue (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: These are, you know, billions and billions of dollars and so that`s a concern.\par \par \uc2 The administration says we`re really only giving them their own money -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: But it`s still money that they didn`t have.\par \par \uc2 The other aspect is, is that these moneys can be used to support terrorist organization, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and, you know, adventuresome actions of Iran. The administration says this is limited to humanitarian aid, but they also acknowledge that funds are fungible, which means they can use - move them around and will aid them in being able to do other things. So, people are - are very concerned as to what this is as a pattern. The administration`s answer is, people shouldn`t go to Iran. I certainly want to echo that also. People should not be going to Iran.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re not moving on a ban, a travel ban, though?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: No, not at this time, but I think that people -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: In seeing this should know that - that they`re at risk and they shouldn`t go to Iran.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you, because you`ve made clear in the past you`ve been disappointed by the level to which the intelligence community has shared with you, their assessment of the classified documents investigations into -\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Both the current and former presidents. Do you have any updates on that? Are you any more satisfied now?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: What they have done, they`ve shown us, probably an equal amount of both Biden classified documents and Trump classified documents. And what you can see from looking at those is both have egregious items in them. None of these documents should have been out of a controlled environment. Both the Biden documents and the Trump documents.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know the status of the Biden special counsel, although as you know the Trump matter is - is currently moving in court. It`s certainly curious that we don`t know what`s going on in the Biden matter. They have limited the amount of documents that we have seen to those that - that they would suggest are - are not subject to executive privilege or the type of documents that we would normally see. We want to see them all. There are already documents that are listed in the Trump court pleadings that are described in detail that we have not yet seen, and we are pushing to make certain that they provide this to Congress.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you have no reason to believe that they are in any way on equal footing in terms of the level of classification of documents or do you have any indication -\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: I can tell you from the ones I`ve seen there are equally egregious and equal classification issues that both Biden documents and Trump documents have the equal concern and threat and equal classification.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have so much more to ask you about, but I have to leave it there right now to go to this commercial break.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to Virginia Democrat Mark Warner. He is the chairman of the Senate Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have to pick up where your Republican colleague just left off. Are the Trump and Biden classified documents that were in their personal possession, and not in controlled areas, equally egregious?\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: Well, Margaret, three things quickly. One, the administration took way too long to get us these documents. Two, while Mike and I have a great working relationship, I believe, based on the documents I`ve seen, that there is a difference in terms of the potential abuse that came from the Trump documents. And, third, it`s one of the reasons why I`ve got bipartisan legislation that would reform the whole classification process. We way overclassify. We, frankly, should have a process in place so that no president or vice president ever takes documents after they leave office. That is kind of the lowest common fruit.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: We ought to get that passed. We`ve got part of that in the intel authorization bill and I hope becomes the law of the land so we can prevent this from happening going forward.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`ve said based on documents you`ve seen, but you want to see more documents?\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: We have actually -- I`m about at 98 percent satisfaction at this point.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, 98 percent satisfaction.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot more on the national security front that we`re tracking right now, including this potential prisoner swap with Iran to bring five Americans home. Are you comfortable with the trade?\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: I`ve not gotten the brief. The Senate Intel Committee has not gotten the brief. We will be getting it shortly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Wasn`t the staff briefed?\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: Well, I can tell you, I have not been personally briefed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You weren`t? OK.\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: I think we need to start with the premise, it`s always the policy of our country to try to bring back Americans, who are held hostage. That was not only under Biden, it was Trump, it was Obama, Bush. I want to hear what kind of constraints are being put on in this exchange in terms of what has been reported of the $6 billion that was South Korean payments to Iran that would be released. I want to hear that and get those details before I weigh in further.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because you have concern that money is fungible and there could be abuse?\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: I - you know, there is obviously -- money is fungible. The administration has said there are guardrails. I want to get a better description of those guardrails first.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have been very active on artificial intelligence. And we talked about this back in January.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  just announced a few days ago that China has a new capability to automatically generate images for use in influence operations to mimic American voters across the political spectrum and create controversy along racial, economic and ideological lines. How much of a risk is this to our upcoming elections?\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: It`s an enormous risk. And artificial intelligence, I`ve spent as much time on this I think as any member of the Senate, and I never spent something where you -- the more time I spend, in certain ways the more confused I get. The whole economics around these large language models, which used to be, you know, who had the most data, who had the most compute power would win. That fundamentally changed after \uc2 Facebook\uc2  released its so- called llama model into the wild in the spring.\par \par \uc2 We just had a major session, Leader Schumer put together, had the kind of the who`s who in the room. And what it - what I`m concerned about is even the AI leaders who say they want rules, guardrails, I`m concerned that when you actually put words on paper will those major tech companies support that? Because you`ve seen, we in social media have done zero.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: Now, in terms of China, China is a major player in AI. And where I think we ought to start, where AI tools, whether it comes from China or domestically, could have the most immediate effect would be the public (INAUDIBLE) in our elections -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. And (INAUDIBLE) legislative reaction.\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: Which \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  just cited. And hear -- hear me - hear me out - hear me out on this. But the other area beyond elections is faith in our public markets. These same tools could completely disrupt the confidence in our public markets by using these same deep fake tools.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: So, I believe we ought to start. If we can put together an alliance between the capitalists and the small d democrats, we might at least get guardrails coming in the next year with the elections and the concerns about our markets.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you`re concerned not just about spooking, you know, the stock market. We`re talking about misleading people going into an election. Congress isn`t going to legislate ahead of the election, are they? I mean Leader Schumer said this is the most difficult thing we`ve ever undertaken.\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: I think this is - this is why the notion of trying to solve it all, the bias questions, the whole questions around deep fakes -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: The questions around what`s called hallucination, where you get answers that have no relationship to what the question was asked. But we ought to at least start with some guardrails around trust in our public election, trust in our public markets. There I think we can move before our elections. I think it will be bipartisan. Let`s start on that framing point. I think we can all agree there could be huge disruption in both of those areas. And that`s where I`m focusing my time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You may have heard our \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling there at the top of the program. And one of the data points I want to show you here. It says, when people compare their finances now to how they were before the pandemic, by two to one they say they`re worse, not better. And when they feel worse, they tell us they`re voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How can President Biden win over those voters?\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: Well, I think we`ve seen from President Biden`s actual record, record amounts of job growth coming again after Covid. We`ve seen major legislation. There are now laws in infrastructure, in the so-called CHIPS bill, and transition in our energy economy, and most of that has only been about 10 cents on every dollar spent out. So, I think the positive effects of that will really continue to penetrate this coming year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do the people in Virginia feel that, that you talk to?\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: I - listen, I think there is a general feeling, oh, my gosh, everybody seems to be at each other`s throats here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: You know, the notion that we`re going to potentially go into a government shutdown. \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2  and I work very closely together.\par \par \uc2 But I do think - I wish the House leadership would be spending a little more time on what would happen with a government shutdown, which makes us look bad around the world, and, frankly, in a state like mine, in Virginia, where we have so many government workers, government contractors, it will be a disaster. And yet the attention coming out of the House leadership is on impeachment and putting forward things they know will not ever pass the Senate in any kind of bipartisan fashion. And I think that is part of the underlying unease that voters feel.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you believe we are headed for a government shutdown?\par \par \uc2 MARK WARNER: I would like to say no, but we`re eight or nine days away and we`ve not even been able to see the House pass the most basic defense appropriations bills. I hope and pray that Speaker McCarthy will say, hey, I`m going to throw over the far right, and I`m going to put together a bipartisan effort with the Democrats and mainstream Republicans to keep the government funded. I think that would get, again, 350, 400 votes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator, good to have you here in person.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Actor and director Sean Penn is the co-director of a documentary called "Superpower." It`s about President Zelenskyy and the war in Ukraine. Zelenskyy agreed to meet with Penn in person for the first time on camera on the same day Russia launched its full-scale invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (President of Ukraine): Great, great, anyway. It`s very important now, all the support. And I think your - your voice, your voice (INAUDIBLE), I think Americans has to hear. So, I think you see that we are just ordinary people who want to live in my - live in our country, yes, and how are you here? It`s - I mean it`s so dangerous.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN (Co-Director, `Superpower": To be here at this time, in this country with you, with your countrymen, I mean it`s such - there`s - there`s so much inspiration to be had here.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We spoke with Penn on Friday about his new film.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In one of the clips, Andre Yermak, one of the top advisors to President Zelenskyy, says to you, you know, the U.S. position should be stronger. If the United States and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn`t do something now essentially he says America is over. That followed with a pretty robust financial investment by the United States, more than $50 billion. Pledges of weapons. But from what I`ve heard you say, you think the United States isn`t doing enough?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN (Co-Director, "Superpower"): It is my absolute feeling that the caution with which the United States has pledged support, which seemed, in my reading of - of - of the February 2022, was a -- like a lean on in the fear of nuclear conflict. Something I think all of us should look very carefully at and understand that, of course, it is possible. And that`s to be concerning.\par \par \uc2 The likelihood is extremely low. And as one of our witnesses in the film says, you know, are we going to let a gangster with nuclear weapons dictate the way we live? So, I think that -- and that Ukrainians won`t let him do that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In the documentary you spoke with a Ukrainian fighter pilot named "Juice," who I understand was killed later in a training incident. Was your personal connection to him and his -- the case he made to you part of why you are pushing this point of F-16s so strongly?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: I was pushing it when I was with Juice and he was alive. He dreamt of flying Ff-16s. And I think that he was a man who was born into a time where he had to do this extreme thing, and he did it with poise and - and skill and focus and - and compassion. And so he had come to Washington to lobby for the F-16s and also was buying helmets for his helicopter pilot friends on \uc2 eBay\uc2  here to bring back.\par \par \uc2 So, it was like that. And then I suppose now what -- was it July that it was announced that there would now be an F-16 program. Those resources were coming.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Eventually, yes.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: Yes, over a long period of time. And what a couple of weeks later I got the message that he had been killed. I - I - I think not only would Juice be alive today if we had been as bold as we like to claim to be historically as a country with our principles, with our Republicans and our Democrats, with our leadership, the citizenry too. While we`re putting all those Ukrainian flags out, we should have been as -- demand decisiveness in this case because at some point caution becomes cowardice. But there`s still an opportunity for us to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Fifty-eight percent of Americans polled by \uc2 CBS\uc2  disapprove of the way \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is handling the situation with Russia and Ukraine. And I`m getting the sense from you, you`re disappointed too.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: Yes. I respect President Biden very much. There have been a couple of things that I think have been disasters up to this point. There`s a -- there should be an implicit understanding between private citizens and leaders in government that, you know, there are things that I don`t know about things that should be -- need to remain classified.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: So, every day, even when I was with Juice, I was privately thinking, yes, it`s our job to fight this fight, but privately I was thinking, but maybe they are doing it behind closed doors and tomorrow we`re going to wake up to that squadron. Enough time has passed I think it`s been today the tragic mistake and I hope and encourage this president that he deserves the legacy of doing this properly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Polling also shows big majorities of Americans continue to support economic sanctions on Russia. Sixty-one percent of Republicans, though, say the U.S. should not send weapons to Ukraine. Fifty percent say the U.S. should not send aid and supplies to Ukraine. That is a big shift from where the Republican Party was in terms of -- in the past being very strong on Russia. But some of it in terms of the rhetoric reflects this sense that America needs to fix itself at home.\par \par \uc2 How do you respond to - to that thinking?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: I think there`s more than a compelling argument that would change those minds. And I understand why they`re confused. I mean I`m hoping, in its little way, that -- that this film can help context. I would be confused if I hadn`t had the opportunity to do this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Better communication of the why and the justification of the billions of dollars that have already been spent.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: Yes, I think about - yes, if the current leadership would just do one thing now, it would be the president saying to his cabinet, we are not spinning the story on Ukraine anymore. So, if it`s about what are they capable of, we`re going to let our commanders in Fresno at the Cal National Guard that`s being doing joint military exercises with them for 30 years tell us what their capability is. And we`re going to say it unfiltered to the American people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You end the documentary talking about this feeling of unity you had when you were in Ukraine and you compare it to what you see here at home. And you actually end on the images of \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio- Cortez\uc2 , the congresswoman, and \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  of Georgia, and you say, we`re going backwards. Why do you think they symbolize that?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: I think that we`ve come to a point where we, as the voters, we as the citizens, have to look at our politicians and say, look, you`re very smart. We agreed with your policy. Why would you want to call it socialist? Why - why would you want to put up a middle finger to people who have a reaction to that? You`re a leader. Just get the policy across. I`m not interested in these people`s self-celebrating or grandstanding. Jerks like me do enough of that. Leaders can`t do that anymore.\par \par \uc2 And so, on the right, on the left, we have to demand that people actually are accountable for the -- accountable and ourselves for the division that we have and break it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you, Sean Penn, for sharing your work with us.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And "Superpower" debuts on Paramount Plus on Monday.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 Tune in to "60 Minutes" tonight for its season premier for an interview with Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy right after football.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230917ej9h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For September 15, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Janet Shamlian, Nancy Cordes, Charlie D`Agata, Elaine Quijano,  Chris Warren, Kris Van Cleave, Steve Hartman, Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 3,605 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the historic strike of autoworkers. Employees of America`s Big Three car manufacturers want a big raise and better benefits. How long could this last and how will it impact the entire economy? And what the president is encouraging the automakers to do.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We are the union!\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN WARWICK (Autoworker): It`s not what we want to do, but it`s what we have to do to get our fair share.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nearly 13,000 employees at key Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  factories trading the production line for the picket line.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Can GM be successful if you met all their demands?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 ): Actually, no.\par \par \uc2 KELLY CASS: Hurricane Lee may have been downgraded to a Category 1, but we`re talking about big impacts, because it`s a very big storm.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news: The special counsel asks for a gag order for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , plus the White House on whether President Biden will pardon his son on federal gun-related charges.\par \par \uc2 The high seas drug bust. A wild boat chase ends with the seizure of three tons of cocaine.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In Libya, more than 11,000 people are dead in the flood-stricken city of Derna. Authorities have now blocked civilians from entering the city, as search teams scour through the mud and destroyed buildings.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: After a summer of extreme weather, how developers are coming up with innovative ways to combat Mother Nature.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: This Louisiana home is hurricane-proof because of how it`s built.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What a new study says about snacking and your health -- the new details.\par \par \uc2 And it`s Friday, so we go On the Road with a reminder it`s never too late to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN JONES (Former Brunswick High School Student): "There were many days that I wanted to strike up a conversation with you."\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Did you get a sense that he was trying to open a door?\par \par \uc2 PAUL FLESHOOD (Former Classmate of Marvin Jones): Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And when you saw that, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 PAUL FLESHOOD: I thought, well, I`m going to go through that door.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin with the historic strike against all of America`s Big Three automakers. Tonight, the impact already felt, with Ford revealing it will temporarily lay off 600 non-striking workers at its Michigan assembly plant. \uc2 General Motors\uc2  expects to shut down its Fairfax plant in Kansas City as early as next week.\par \par \uc2 Officials say there is movement in the negotiations, but both sides remain far apart. President Biden, who ran as a pro-union candidate, is sending two of his top aides to Detroit to meet with both sides. It has been 18 hours since about 13,000 began their strike at assembly plants in Missouri, Michigan, and Ohio, impacting \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent company of \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Chrysler.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave will start us off tonight from outside the Ford plant in Wayne, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, Norah, the union says there were no negotiations today. Both sides expect them to resume tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  did make a counteroffer to the Big Three late last night, but the companies say the asks were still far too great, and then time ran out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Outside idled auto plants in three states and in downtown Detroit just feet from the annual Auto Show, striking \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  making their voices heard.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY HARRIS (Autoworker): I think we deserve more pay. I have been here 24 years and ain`t had a raise in eight years.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: At the stroke of midnight, 12,700 \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers walked off the production line and joined the picket line.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: No justice!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: No Jeeps!\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Halting work at factories in Michigan, Missouri, and Ohio belonging to the nation`s Big Three automakers, an unprecedented move to strike Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  simultaneously.\par \par \uc2 Union president Shawn Fain rallied strikers.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (President, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ): The billionaire class has been taking everything, and the working class has been left scraping paycheck to paycheck just trying to survive.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The union is strategically striking plants that make popular and lucrative SUVs and trucks, like the Ford Bronco, the Jeep Wrangler, and the Chevy Colorado.\par \par \uc2 It`s a warning to automakers that more production disruption could be on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN WARWICK (Autoworker): I don`t think anybody wants to do this, but this is what we have to do, so this is what we`re going to do.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The union is seeking up to a 40 percent raise, job protections as automakers switch to less labor-intensive E.V.s, and an unwinding of concessions made during the Great Recession.\par \par \uc2 The Big Three are negotiating on many of the demands, but, so far, their proposed raise is about half.\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 ): We have a historic offer on the table.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: GM CEO \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why not just give them what they`re asking for?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA: We have to make sure the company is going to succeed for the next 115 years, and that means we need to invest in the products you see around me.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): No one wants a strike.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The strike is a political tightrope for President Biden, balancing his pro-union views with economic concerns leading into an election year.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The bottom line is that auto workers help create America`s middle class. They deserve a contract that sustains them in the middle class.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Part supplier Global Technology sells wheels to several automakers. CEO Dale Hadel says they will feel the impact immediately and will be forced to consider cutting staff if the strike drags on.\par \par \uc2 DALE HADEL (CEO, Global Technology, Inc.): Fortunately, there will be other orders from other customers that are non-Detroit Three, but not enough to carry all your costs and everything. So there will be some furloughs.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  says they are yet to see any ripple effects in their operation from the strike at their -- at their Toledo plant.\par \par \uc2 However, that may not be the case for businesses surrounding some of these facilities. They have told us they`re very worried that, as workers go on strike, they won`t be eating out and they won`t be spending money at their businesses, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, so many people affected.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the weather, with Hurricane Lee targeting New England and Southeastern Canada with strong winds and coastal flooding. The Category 1 storm battered Bermuda overnight with heavy rains and wind gusts of up to 62 miles per hour. A state of emergency has been declared in Massachusetts and Maine, with residents on edge and utility companies staging crews in preparation for mass power outages.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist Chris Warren from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking the storm`s progress.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Just a few hours away now from when Lee will really make its presence felt across the Northeast. Tropical alerts up from Massachusetts, all the way up through Maine and into Canada, some of the worst of the winds, the strongest winds expected in the morning along the coastal areas, really from that 7:00 in the morning to 7:00 in the evening time frame midday, winds still gusting 40, 50, even 60 miles an hour or stronger.\par \par \uc2 Winds will slowly be dying down overnight on Saturday. Rain arrives first, though, tonight, lasting through a good portion of the day. And Norah, by Sunday, conditions will be dramatically improved.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Some good news.\par \par \uc2 Chris Warren, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to a horrific incident at a New York City day care. A 1-year-old boy is dead and several other children are in the hospital after an incident in the Bronx.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elaine Quijano joins us now.\par \par \uc2 And, Elaine, what have you learned?\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Well, details are still emerging, Norah.\par \par \uc2 But three children have been hospitalized, among them, a 7-month-old girl and a 2-year-old boy. We`re told that both are now in stable condition. They were rushed to the hospital around 2:40 this afternoon after a report of cardiac arrest. Another 2-year-old was taken to the hospital via private means, and a 1-year-old was declared dead at the scene.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I went to see what was happening. And she started saying that the kids were dying. And then I saw the -- one of the kids laying down, and he wasn`t responding. But it was really, like, traumatizing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Now, police and fire department officials are still on the scene investigating.\par \par \uc2 CBS News New York reports that the day care, which is being operated out of a large apartment building, was just inspected by the state of New York last week. No violations were found. And, Norah, fire officials say that a preliminary investigation shows that there was no indication of elevated levels of carbon monoxide.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Elaine Quijano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to some more breaking news. Special counsel Jack Smith wants a judge to issue a limited gag order against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in that federal trial over his alleged efforts to overturn the 2020 presidential election. Smith cited the former president`s harassment of prosecutors, potential witnesses and the citizens of D.C. Prosecutors want to prevent Trump from undermining the integrity of the case and intimidating the jury pool.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas now to the aftermath of this week`s devastating floods in Libya. Officials fear up to 20,000 people could be dead in the city of Derna on the Mediterranean.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): Even in the desperate search for survivors, a chaotic race to recover the dead.\par \par \uc2 Waterborne diseases from the sheer number of bodies pose a deadly threat to those who survived Monday`s catastrophic flooding, now struggling to survive its aftermath. A wall of water and mud several stories high swept away whole neighborhoods, a quarter of the coastal city of Derna.\par \par \uc2 Downed buildings and destroyed cars show the force of the floodwaters unleashed after the collapse of two poorly maintained dams following torrential rains.\par \par \uc2 (SAAD speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "It`s not easy for a man to go through this," said Saad, who lost his home. "No one helps you, not even the government."\par \par \uc2 The devastation has been compounded by Libya`s civil war, a country torn in two. The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . says countless lives would have been saved if a functioning alarm system and capable evacuation teams were in place and better care of critical infrastructure like those failed dams.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Awad Alshalwy, TikTok)\par \par \uc2 AWAD ALSHALWY: We buried 25 people without knowing who they are or taking any samples. And the families of those people will never know who they are, will never know what happened -- what happened to their -- to their children, to their sons, to their daughters and their wives.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And those who have already accepted the very worst.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata, CBS News, London.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Say a prayer for those poor people.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, President Biden is facing a political and legal storm with the indictment of his son \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . The White House says the president has no plans to pardon his son.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes reports from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We have climbed out of our great economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): This is what the president had hoped to focus on this week, a surging economy under his watch. But then his son`s indictment took center stage.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Should Hunter get a pardon, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: With Hunter Biden facing felony charges, it is now a near- certainty that the case will vie for headlines through Election Day. So will the impeachment inquiry launched by House Republicans this week.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): These allegations paint a picture of a culture of corruption.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Democrats, sensing a new phase, have stepped up their defense.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2  (D-New York): There is not a shred of evidence that President Biden committed an impeachable offense.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But those aren`t the only headwinds he`s facing. A new report this week showed inflation inching back up. And polls consistently suggest Americans have concerns about Mr. Biden`s age.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-Florida) (Presidential Candidate): Absolutely legitimate concern. The presidency is not a job for someone that`s 80 years old.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It`s all catnip for his opponents.\par \par \uc2 How does the president plan to convince the American people over the next year that 80 is not too old for someone who`s running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): Eighty is the new 40, didn`t you hear?\par \par \uc2 Look, what I can speak to is what this president has done, right? I can speak to his experience. I can speak to the wisdom that he has. I can speak to his record.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: One person who is not fazed by Mr. Biden`s age is his rival, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who said in an interview this week that 80 is not too old to be president. That could be because, at 77, Trump himself would turn 80 while in office if he returns to the White House -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 After a summer of extreme weather, heat, wildfires and hurricanes dominated the headlines, we get more now about what people are doing in tonight`s "In Depth."\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian shows us how some people are making changes in how they live to ensure the next natural disaster won`t leave them without a home.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): Max Begue loves almost everything about living in coastal Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 MAX BEGUE (Louisiana Resident): Every time I come in, I love the way it looks.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: But hurricane season brings back memories of Katrina in 2005, when his home and neighborhood were washed away, almost as if they never existed.\par \par \uc2 Did you think about leaving at all after Katrina?\par \par \uc2 MAX BEGUE: We all did, and a lot of people left, but -- but I -- I chose to stay.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: He also chose a geodesic dome for his new house, made of close to 300 interwoven triangles.\par \par \uc2 MAX BEGUE: I built the dome because I didn`t want to go through the process of losing another house.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The dome home is able to withstand winds topping 200 miles per hour, making it essentially hurricane-proof.\par \par \uc2 When you told people you were building a dome home, what did they say?\par \par \uc2 MAX BEGUE: They thought I was a kook.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Not anymore. The spherical home is also energy-efficient, because surface area is minimized. His electric bills are usually less than $100 a month, a third of what his neighbors pay.\par \par \uc2 Domes have long been built for their resiliency, from the world`s first dome stadium, the Astrodome, to the majestic iron dome of the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 ABEER SAHA (Curator, \uc2 National Museum of American History\uc2 ): We really want to be able to show how geodesic domes are not just stable and resilient, but they`re also imminently efficient and portable and practical.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The Smithsonian recently reassembled this dome, the first built in North America, after six decades in storage, part of a focus on extreme weather.\par \par \uc2 ABEER SAHA: We`re absolutely not thinking enough about the role of housing and structures in climate change.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Dome-shaped buildings made of concrete can withstand wildfires, floods and earthquakes. Their shape also allows them to disperse tremendous weight without collapsing.\par \par \uc2 Construction costs are generally higher, but so is the chance of survival.\par \par \uc2 How long will you stay here?\par \par \uc2 MAX BEGUE: Well, how long do I have?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A dome of self-defense for those living on the front lines of Mother Nature`s beauty and her fury.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, CBS News, St. Bernard, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A surprising verdict for three men accused of plotting to abduct Michigan`s governor. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There were gasps of shock in a Michigan courtroom today, where three men were found not guilty of their alleged roles in the 2020 abduction plot against Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . Nine other anti- government extremists were previously found guilty.\par \par \uc2 Now a major drug bust on the high seas. Colombia`s navy says, after a high- speed chase on the Caribbean Sea this week, it seized three tons of cocaine from a boat valued at $100 million. Four people were arrested.\par \par \uc2 Is snacking good for your health? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s Health Watch. A new study underscores the importance of smart snacking.\par \par \uc2 Researchers found that more than one in four people said they eat healthy meals, but then they snack on highly processed foods and sugary treats, especially those who snack after 9:00 p.m. Researchers say poor snacking choices are linked with higher body fat and cardiovascular disease.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman goes On the Road with an important lesson about the past and the healing power of time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Never mind the limousine, marching band, and red carpet. What amazes 75-year-old Marvin Jones the most --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Hi.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: -- is that he is back at his old high school, period.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN JONES (Former Brunswick High School Student): Because, when I left Brunswick High School in 1966, I said I would never return.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of protests)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It was a different time. Schools across the South were desegregated, including Brunswick High in Lawrenceville, Virginia, where it fell to Marvin and these 14 other kids to take that first, painful step.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN JONES: On the bus, the students would bring KKK flyers. And when I would come down the hall, they would close their nose, say, "Here comes a skunk," OK?\par \par \uc2 I felt as if I had leprosy.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Even decades later, those memories haunted.\par \par \uc2 So, to heal, Marvin decided to put pen to paper, writing letters to the very students who tormented him.\par \par \uc2 What did you say in the letters?\par \par \uc2 MARVIN JONES: I would tell what each person had done to me.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Marvin wrote about 90 such letters to former classmates, pouring out his pain, whether people wanted to hear it or not, and most didn`t.\par \par \uc2 But one of the letters he mailed struck a different tone, and that letter was very well-received.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN JONES: That is he.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: The recipient was Paul Fleshood. Marvin says Paul was one of the few students who never bullied him or said an unkind word.\par \par \uc2 PAUL FLESHOOD (Former Classmate of Marvin Jones): Really touched me.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Marvin wrote:\par \par \uc2 MARVIN JONES: "There were many days that I wanted to strike up a conversation with you, in that I perceived you as one of the students I could have been friends."\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Did you get a sense that he was trying to open a door?\par \par \uc2 PAUL FLESHOOD: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And when you saw that, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 PAUL FLESHOOD: I thought, well, I`m going to go through that door.\par \par \uc2 Hey, buddy.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Marvin and Paul became close friends.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN JONES: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 PAUL FLESHOOD: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And that friendship eventually led to this.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: We acknowledge their sacrifice. We celebrate their legacy.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Last week, Paul and other leaders in the community hosted a ceremony honoring the Brunswick 15, those 15 brave children who were once treated like untouchables, now embraced with open arms.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN JONES: It means a lot. It means that we have overcome a lot.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Marvin used to say he never had one good day at Brunswick High School, but, almost 60 years later, it looks like maybe he finally has.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman, On the Road in Lawrenceville, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Kindness can help right those wrongs.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night, and have a great weekend. And for those who celebrate L`Shana Tovah, happy new year.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230916ej9f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 14, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mark Strassmann, Ramy Inocencio, Elise Preston,  Chris Warren, Kris Van Cleave, Catherine Herridge \par \uc2 3,674 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the breaking news: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  indicted on three felony charges, all tied to his alleged possession of a gun while using narcotics, the new information, as the president`s son faces possible jail time.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 Our new reporting as \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  becomes the first child of a sitting president to be indicted by a federal grand jury.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The talk of the auto show should be the latest models and newest technology. Instead, it is the giant elephant in the room, the looming strike.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (Pre sident, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ): We are not asking to be millionaires. We are just asking for our fair share so we can survive.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Hurricane Lee is creeping closer to New England.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: We now have a hurricane watch for parts of Maine, the first in 15 years, and a tropical storm watch that stretches down to Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Cyberattacks hit Las Vegas, the new details, as we learn Caesars reportedly paid a $15 million ransom. Who may be responsible?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: The \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  has announced a voluntary recall of a popular children`s toy. The agency says they pose a risk of injury or even death.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Eye on America: how an indigo farmer in South Carolina is reclaiming the crop to honor her ancestors.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: To you, it is not just anybody`s history.\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS (Genotype): Right.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This is --\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS: My family history.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Look how big it is!\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Take a look at this, the scary moment 14-foot gator charges a group of Girl Scouts. The heroic actions of their brave troop leader.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: The American rescued from deep inside a Turkish cave is sharing his incredible survival story.\par \par \uc2 MARK DICKEY (Caver): You can get to amazing places.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And back.\par \par \uc2 MARK DICKEY: And back.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this busy Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with breaking news, a federal grand jury in Delaware indicting \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  on three gun charges. The president`s youngest son could face up to 25 years in prison if he is found guilty of the felony counts, all stemming from his purchase of a firearm at a time when he was using drugs.\par \par \uc2 Special counsel David Weiss alleges Biden lied about his addiction on paperwork and illegally obtained a gun. Per the ATF, it is against the law for a user of certain controlled substances, including cocaine, to possess a firearm.\par \par \uc2 Today`s news comes less than two months after a plea agreement between \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and federal prosecutors fell apart after the judge refused to sign off on it.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of new details to bring you tonight.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Catherine Herridge is here to start us off.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Catherine.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 This is an extraordinary situation for the attorney general, whose department is prosecuting the leading candidate for the GOP nomination, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the president`s son, at the same time, who is facing new legal jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE (voiceover): The three felony counts \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  faces stem from his possession of a handgun in October 2018 identified in court records as a Colt Cobra .38 Special revolver.\par \par \uc2 The president`s son is accused of making a false and fictitious statement about his drug use on a federal gun form and to a firearms dealer. The third count alleges he did knowingly possess a firearm while on drugs.\par \par \uc2 Tom Dupree is a former senior Justice Department official.\par \par \uc2 How serious are the charges?\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE (Former Justice Department Official): These are all charges that carry fairly significant potential jail terms.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The president`s son had previously reached an agreement on the charge of gun possession that avoided prosecution if he had abided by the terms for two years.\par \par \uc2 But that deal, which included misdemeanor tax charges, collapsed. His lawyer insisted today the gun agreement remains valid, saying: "The evidence has not changed, but the law has, and so has MAGA Republicans` improper and partisan interference in this process."\par \par \uc2 A senior House Republican who is at the center of the newly announced impeachment inquiry into the president said the charges fell short.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMES COMER (R-Kentucky): This is the one crime he committed that you can`t tie \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  into.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has openly admitted his past struggle with addiction, as he told "CBS Sunday Morning."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from April 4, 2021)\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN (Son of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ): I went one time for 13 days without sleeping, and smoking crack and drinking vodka exclusively throughout that entire time.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Mr. President!\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: And while the president had no comment about his son today, he has long defended him.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The 11th Hour, May 5, MSNBC)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): First of all, my son has done nothing wrong. I trust him. I have faith in him.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: But \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  could still face more charges for alleged tax violations.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE: Today`s news about this indictment being filed, unfortunately, for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , it`s probably the first chapter in what could be a long book.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Based on the court records, those tax charges could be filed here in Washington, D.C., or California, where \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  lives.\par \par \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblowers first told CBS News that they believe the evidence supports serious felony tax violations -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Catherine Herridge, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, switching gears now to what could be the first ever strike against Detroit`s Big Three automakers all at the same time. Nearly 150,000 \uc2 UAW\uc2  members are threatening to walk off the job just hours from now without a new contract.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave reports from the Motor City that a work stoppage would have a negative effect across the entire economy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Countdown to a shutdown. Without a deal, autoworkers will go on strike at Ford, GM, and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plants at midnight. Negotiations appear to be stalled.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (President, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ): The Big Three can afford to immediately give us our fair share. If they choose not to, then they`re choosing to strike themselves.\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2  (CEO, \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2 ): I think they`re preparing for a historic strike with all three companies.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Ford CEO \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2 : What their initial offer was is to pay our hourly workers about $300,000 each and to work four days. That would basically put our company out of business.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Do you feel this has been a good-faith negotiation?\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2 : It`s hard to say what word, because we have never really seen this before.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is seeking up to a 40 percent raise. The automakers are offering about half that. Also on the wish list, a 32-hour workweek and the return of pensions, nonstarters for the companies, also phasing out concessions made in 2008, when the car makers teetered on the verge of collapse.\par \par \uc2 The Big Three are on a roll of big profits over the last decade, more than $20 billion so far this year alone.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: Corporate greed is the problem, but our membership is fed up and fired up.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The fight is playing out as the auto industry is going through a major shift to electric vehicles. E.V.s require less labor to manufacture, and workers tend not to be unionized, meaning labor costs for competitors are significantly less.\par \par \uc2 ZACH VAN VLEET (Autoworker): We`re falling behind further and further.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: A Friday strike would leave third-generation autoworker Zach Van Vleet (sp?) living off $500 a week in strike pay, which doesn`t go far for a family of four.\par \par \uc2 How long until you start to really feel that pinch?\par \par \uc2 ZACH VAN VLEET: Honestly, I don`t think it would take long at all. If we do go out, how long is it going to take to get a deal?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 UAW\uc2  says, without a deal, it will strike key plants belonging to all three automakers and then expand, if necessary.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK ANDERSON (Economist): It`s a dangerous thing to say you`re going to strike just some plants, but not others, because that`s not how the industry can operate. And you will see more than just those plants shut down.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Economists say even a relatively short 10-day strike against all three automakers could cost $5.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we`re learning President Biden has spoken with union leaders and executives at the Big Three, trying to find out how to unstall these stalled negotiations -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Kris Van Cleave, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the weather and Hurricane Lee, as it moves across the Atlantic and closes in on New England. The Category 1 storm is currently located west of Bermuda, lashing the island with strong winds and pounding waves.\par \par \uc2 In Maine, residents are not taking any chances. They`re pulling their boats out of the water to protect them from getting damaged or destroyed, with waves up to 20 feet expected to slam the coast.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist Chris Warren from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is following the storm`s latest track.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Tropical storm conditions are expected up and down the New England coast. Even though much of the U.S. is out of the cone, the storm will be getting bigger. Also have to monitor the potential for some flooding with some of the rain with this system.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a look at those tropical alerts from southern Massachusetts all the way up through coastal Maine. Even inland from down East Maine could have some strong tropical-storm-force winds. As far as the worst of the weather, really look for things to go downhill tomorrow night. High tide could lead to some coastal flooding on Saturday, with winds peaking Friday night, Norah, into Saturday afternoon, with dramatic improvements at least on land by Sunday.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Chris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is investigating the rapid descent of a \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  jet overnight. The airline says the flight from Newark, New Jersey, to Rome lost cabin pressure about 50 minutes into the flight and had to circle back to the airport for an emergency landing.\par \par \uc2 Now, the flight map shows the plane dropped about 30,000 feet in altitude in just seven minutes before safely landing. No injuries were reported.\par \par \uc2 Now to the cyberattack causing havoc at \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2  nationwide, Hackers have crippled the company`s computer systems, shutting down casino slot machines, ATMs, and even guest room doors in hotels from Vegas to Atlantic City.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston reports from Las Vegas, where the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating this multimillion-dollar hack.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2  is not only one of the biggest players on the Las Vegas Strip; it`s Nevada`s largest employer.\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, it`s paralyzed, casinos filled with nonworking slot machines, computers and phone lines are down, and frustration is growing.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: You don`t know what you`re dealing with, right? You don`t know how much is compromised and how much is not compromised.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I don`t even want to pay with my card right now. I`m scared that they`re going to hack all of our information.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: A group that reports on cyberattacks claims that all hackers did to compromise \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2  was hop on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 , find an employee, then call the help desk.\par \par \uc2 How was this even carried out?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, it`s still not clear.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: CBS News cybersecurity expert Chris Krebs says several recent cyberattacks may have been homegrown.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: We have built up this kind of mythology around ransomware operators being Eastern Europeans or Russians hiding under the skirt of Putin and the Kremlin. It may be that there were also Americans involved in these most recent attacks.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: According to \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 , a few weeks ago, \uc2 Caesars Entertainment\uc2  paid about $15 million ransom to restore its systems. The company confirms that attackers breached loyalty customer data, including driver`s license and Social Security numbers, but has taken steps to ensure that the stolen data is deleted by the unauthorized actor.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Even if you are able to get some of these folks, another`s going to pop up right behind them, because they have been able to demonstrate that it is a very lucrative profession, that cyber crime pays, and it pays very, very well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Krebs says you can increase your own cybersecurity. Keep your phones up to date with the latest protective software.\par \par \uc2 When conducting business online, make sure you`re using legitimate Web sites. And, if shopping online, use credit cards, not debit cards -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good information.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, new details about that dangerous fugitive who was captured in -- outside of Philadelphia on Wednesday after a two-week manhunt. Danelo Cavalcante was discovered by thermal imaging and then subdued by a police dog named Yoda. The \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  says Cavalcante told investigators he was planning to carjack someone and was hoping to travel to Canada and then possibly Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 The convicted killer also telling officers they nearly stepped on him multiple times during the search while he was hiding under leaves and brush, and that he survived on watermelons and stream water. Well, he`s now being held in a maximum security prison, awaiting new charges from his time on the run.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, an American cave researcher who became trapped more than 3,000 feet underground in Turkey is finally sharing his incredible story of survival.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ramy Inocencio spoke with the New York resident about his life- threatening ordeal.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK DICKEY (Caver): So, I`m doing great, not like 100 percent yet. On the outside, I look pretty good.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voiceover): But on the inside, doctors are still not sure what caused U.S. caver Mark Dickey to suddenly fall ill during a mapping expedition more than 3,000 feet under a Turkish mountain, struck with severe internal bleeding and vomiting.\par \par \uc2 MARK DICKEY: At some point, I just was in such bad shape that I couldn`t even check my own pulse. I was close to the edge.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Over the next 10 days, nearly 200 rescuers from Europe and Turkey answered the call for help, navigating his stretcher up vertical ascents, through tight passages, past waterfalls, and in near freezing cold, until --\par \par \uc2 This is the moment we have been waiting and hoping for.\par \par \uc2 His journey from the depths of the earth finally came to its end.\par \par \uc2 To what extent will this ordeal stop you from going back into the earth?\par \par \uc2 MARK DICKEY: No extent whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: I expected that answer.\par \par \uc2 To that end, why do you love doing this so much?\par \par \uc2 MARK DICKEY: This is one of the last frontiers of exploration. These places that I`m getting to are so challenging, so difficult, it`s like climbing Mount Everest. You can get to amazing places.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And back.\par \par \uc2 MARK DICKEY: And back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And Mark told me he will be staying here until next week for more scans. But next month is already on his mind, Norah, and his next caving adventure.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Just incredible.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a staggering new death toll from Monday`s catastrophic floods along Libya`s Mediterranean coast. The Libyan Red Crescent aid organization says more than 11,000 are dead in the city of Derna, with another 10,000 still unaccounted for.\par \par \uc2 New video shows the devastation. Some survivors say the only warning they received to evacuate was the explosion of dams bursting, unleashing those waves of floodwater.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, what`s old is new again in South Carolina`s Lowcountry. Indigo, a largely forgotten crop, once was one of the South`s most profitable exports, and it`s making a comeback.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann reports how indigo is bringing together the country`s past and present.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): Sheena Myers makes her indigo soap knowing nothing can scrub away South Carolina`s past.\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS (Genotype): There`s a whole history behind what I`m doing.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It`s deep.\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS: Yes, it`s real deep.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Indigo dyes beautiful color, comes shrubbed and shrouded by an ugly history. In the mid-1700s, wealthy South Carolina planters called it blue gold, a labor-intensive cash crop produced by the sweat of enslaved people.\par \par \uc2 For Myers, it`s personal.\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS: Very personal.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But, to you, it`s not just anybody`s history.\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This is --\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS: My family history.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Among those enslaved indigo workers, her great-great- grandmother.\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS: Because they were humiliated, now I`m being honored. And me being honored is like I`m honoring them as well. I don`t think they would ever have thought in a million years that they would have a -- a descendant creating things like this.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Her company, Genotype, sells indigo-based skin care and medicinal products for psoriasis, peptic ulcers, bronchitis. Annual sales top $1 million.\par \par \uc2 Immerse it?\par \par \uc2 PRECIOUS JENNINGS (Executive Director, Align the Fibers): Immerse it down in.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Down the road, Precious Jennings grows indigo to process its natural dye powder. Think of it as farm to fabric, digging for healing in the dirt of a former plantation.\par \par \uc2 PRECIOUS JENNINGS: Every day I come onto this land, I honor and think about and give gratitude to the people that were here and enslaved on this land.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Myers wants to pass her business and family history to her three sons.\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS: If they keep this business alive, it won`t disappear.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Keep growing the indigo --\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: -- the history stays alive.\par \par \uc2 SHEENA MYERS: It will continue. It will continue.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Growing a new indigo legacy rich in humility.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Mark Strassmann, Charleston, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There is new information tonight about that luxury cruise ship that ran aground with more than 200 people on board.\par \par \uc2 We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A luxury cruise ship that was stuck in the mud after running aground in a remote area of Greenland on Monday night was finally pulled loose today by another boat.\par \par \uc2 The 206 passengers and crew on board will not be taken to a port, where they can fly home. The tour companies says a small number of people also tested positive for COVID.\par \par \uc2 Near Chicago, officials are investigating what caused a giant tent to collapse today, injuring 26 people, five seriously. It happened at an employee appreciation event at \uc2 Cintas\uc2 , which makes work uniforms and other clothes. The company says it`s now cooperating with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 A terrifying encounter between Girl Scouts and an alligator during a camping trip they won`t soon forget. We have got some of the scary details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now an important consumer alert about a popular activity for kids.\par \par \uc2 More than 50,000 Chuckle & Roar Ultimate Water Beads activity kits are being recalled after a 10-month-old child died from swallowing the beads and a 9-month-old was seriously injured. Water beads are crystals that grow when you put them in water. The recalled kits made by Buffalo Games of New York were sold exclusively at Target from March through November of last year.\par \par \uc2 Customers are advised to contact the company for a refund.\par \par \uc2 A group of Girl Scouts survived a way-too-close encounter with an alligator estimated to be 14-feet-long. Have a look at this video. The gator appears to be stalking the girls, swimming towards them at a lake in Texas` Huntsville State Park last weekend.\par \par \uc2 The Scouts scrambled out of the water, while their brave troop leader got between them and the gator. I`m thinking they should get a new badge for that one. Wow.\par \par \uc2 All right, an iconic piece of clothing worn by the people`s princess goes on the auction block. We have the record winning bid next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight, perhaps one of the most famous sweaters in the world went up for auction today at Sotheby`s and sold for a price tag fit for a princess.\par \par \uc2 Princess Diana`s iconic black sheep sweater fetched a record $1.1 million. That is more than 14 times the original estimate, and the most ever for an item of clothing worn by the late British royal. Lady Diana Spencer first wore the sweater to a polo match in 1981 during her courtship with then- \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2 . And the rest, they say, is history.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching. Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230915ej9e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Idalia`s Path of Destruction; Idalia`s Florida Impact; Idalia`s Path; Deadly Johannesburg Fire; Senator McConnell Health Concerns; Giuliani Liable for Defamation; Student Loan Payment Resuming; Tough Questions in Maui; Improving School Safety; Tricks of the Trade; School Supply Sticker Shock; 10-Minute Mocktail; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Jarred Hill, Omar  Villafranca, Jim Cantore, Scott MacFarlane, Weijia Jiang, Jonathan  Vigliotti, Meg Oliver, Jamie Yuccas, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 15,004 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 August 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS. It`s Thursday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Holy [bleep]. Holy [bleep]. That car is flying. What the [bleep]. What the [bleep].\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Idalia tears across the southeast destroying homes and businesses. We are in North Carolina and Florida where recovery has just begun.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP HENLEY (Crystal River, Florida resident): I`m telling, I`m an eighth generation Floridian. I`ve been here 62 years. This is as bad as I`ve seen any.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: New questions about Republican Senator Mitch McConnell`s health after he freezes during a news conference for a second time.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Did you hear the question, Senator? Running for re-election in 2026.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We asked tough questions of officials on Maui about what they did not do during the wildfires.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RICHARD BISSEN (Maui, Hawaii): I can`t speak to what or whose responsibility it was to communicate the --\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: But you`re the boss of this island, so yes, you can speak to it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Dozens of people are dead after a fire broke out in a five-story building in Johannesburg.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Authorities are now conducting recovery efforts.\par \par \uc2 MAN: US Open sixth seed Coco Gauff knocked off Mirra Andreeva of Russia in straight sets and advances to the third round.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Philly slugger, Bryce Harper joins elite company with a milestone homerun.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Red crows going back toward the wall. It`s done. It is number 300.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Ninety-two thousand people Pat Memorial Stadium in Nebraska to watch the Huskers volleyball team, the New World Record for the most people to ever attend to a women`s sporting event in any sport.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Huskers won in a three set sweep.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, that was not a Taylor Swift concert or Beyonce. That was the girls volleyball team -- the women`s volleyball team.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to go back and play volleyball there. That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s fantastic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love the school showing up for their women`s program like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Big time. Big time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a big show for you today, including a look at the very big questions surrounding the response to the wildfires in Maui. Jonathan Vigliotti pressed the Maui mayor and a key state official about why they appeared to do so little even as wildfires were exploding.\par \par \uc2 We also have a big announcement, something to help the fire victims today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, also in our "School Matters" series, you`ve heard the phrase trade secrets. Well, it turns out they are only secrets if you never get around to learning them.\par \par \uc2 Well, I visited a high school in Pittsburgh where job training is the focus for professions that actually have jobs waiting for students. I also spoke to some college kids who say there is more to education than learning a trade.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking forward to that, but we begin with Idalia, the hurricane turn tropical storm that has cut a path of destruction across the southeast.\par \par \uc2 Strong winds high tides and blinding rain made roads impassable from Florida to the Carolinas while also making it dangerous in some places to stay put.\par \par \uc2 The system is now over the North Carolina coast. A lot of low-lying towns they are vulnerable to storm surge, Jarred Hill joins us from one of those communities, Morehead City.\par \par \uc2 Jarred, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: Hey, good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 The just started picking up here in Morehead City, but the real thing that officials are watching for is the water. Where I`m standing right here just about 30 minutes ago was completely covered, but the good news is that the pumps here in the town are working. The bad news is that the high tide is still coming and that could be a concern for the next couple of hours.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: As Tropical Storm Idalia churned up the coast, it lashed North Carolina with pounding rain and maximum sustained winds of 60 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That car is flying. What the [bleep].\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: Intense winds created a brief tornado in Goose Creek lifting this car straight off the ground. Miraculously, the local police chief said two people sustained only minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 Several inches of rain and the fifth highest tide on record flooded streets in nearby Charleston. Howling winds and piercing rays descended on Georgia early Wednesday. Downed power lines and trees now block the streets in Burke County.\par \par \uc2 Back here in the low lying port of Morehead City in North Carolina, the assistant fire chief says the storm surge plus an exceptionally high tide known as a king tide will create a dangerous mix.\par \par \uc2 DYKEMAN BAILY (Morehead City in North Carolina, the assistant fire chief): King tide is going to add about a foot over normal. If you were to have an emergency, we may or may not be able to reach you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: Now, the other thing, talking about emergencies here is that you don`t need a lot of wind, you don`t need a lot of water to make it difficult for those emergency responders to get to you if you need help.\par \par \uc2 In fact, when I was talking to the assistant fire chief there, he said that just about 35-mile-per-hour sustained winds would stop them because of safety from being able to get to anybody who might need help.\par \par \uc2 So again, they`re telling folks stay-at-home if you don`t need to be outside.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, back to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Jarred, thank you.\par \par \uc2 No place felt the wrath of Idalia more than the Big Bend area of Florida where today there are countless stories of loss. In Cedar Key, the floodwaters overtook a hotel which experienced catastrophic storm surge. That hotel is called -- listen to this -- the Far Away Inn -- and Omar Villafranca is there for us right now.\par \par \uc2 Omar, good morning to you. I bet it kind of feels very far away this morning.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: It does, and good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 This is what a nine to 10-foot storm surge can do during a hurricane. This is one of the small cottages that was here at that inn. It is no more. You`re looking at the debris behind me. I`m standing here in what used to be the kitchen. This tile is still on the floor, but all the appliances are spread around here.\par \par \uc2 Now Cedar Key is a population of about 800 people, but the good news is, most of them left before the storm hit.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Hurricane Idalia made landfall along Florida`s gulf coast Wednesday morning, packing 125-mile-per-hour winds, slamming communities with heavy rains and storm surge.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The storm tore through buildings like this canopy at a gas station in Perry, Florida.\par \par \uc2 In some places, water covered everything in sight. The storm downed trees and power lines at one point leaving roughly a quarter of a million Floridians without electricity.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We`re going to continue to work through this over these next days, weeks, and a hurricane of this magnitude. You`re talking about months.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Even Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  wasn`t spared. His wife posted this picture of a fallen 100-year-old oak tree in front of the Governor`s Mansion in Tallahassee.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP HENLEY: I`m telling I`m an eighth generation Floridian. I`ve been here 62 years. This is as bad as I`ve seen it.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Crystal River resident, Phillip Henley (sp?) told us he was among the last to leave his neighborhood as the floodwaters rose.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP HENLEY: One guy said that there was swimming out the window, so that tells me, it is up over windows in there.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: So your house may be fully flooded.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP HENLEY: Fully flooded.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: These rapids are actually in the middle of a parking lot, and this has residents concerned because they say this water is spilling over from the river and draining into the canals where many homes are located, and that is starting to flood the houses.\par \par \uc2 That includes homes like Michelle Mitchell`s (sp?).\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MITCHELL (Resident): Oh, it sits on -- it`s about water in there.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: We were with her as she learned via a community \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page that her property was underwater.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MITCHELL: It floods when it rains, but nothing like this. This is almost catastrophic to me.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: And Mitchell is far from alone. Several fan boat rescues were underway Wednesday, and we spoke to some people whose homes were so inundated with water that had to be saved by local officials in high-water rescue vehicles.\par \par \uc2 When you went to bed, where was the water?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: No water. No water. No water at all. And the whole road, there was water.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: And then you -- they knock on your door and wake you up.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, because my car was getting underwater, so I just wake up and I start freaking out. So.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Were able to save anything?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Nope. I lose everything including my car.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Cleanup continues. We also talked to several residents who say they haven`t been able to go and check on their home and some of them don`t even know if they have a home to go back to.\par \par \uc2 Now as for this cleanup, obviously, it will take months is what we`re hearing from residents. Search and rescue teams are still going to be around the area, also looking for people who may need help -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Omar, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more now, we`re going to go to meteorologist, Jim Cantore from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 . He is also in the hard-hit area of Cedar Key and he is watching where this storm is headed now.\par \par \uc2 Jim, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Hey, good morning. Yes. You know, there was a little talk earlier that this storm may come out and then do a loop back toward Florida. That`s not a concern right now.\par \par \uc2 But let`s show you what`s going on. We still have some heavy rain and some flood warnings out for eastern North Carolina. It has been raining in Charleston. It has been raining in Savannah. That`s coming to an end now, but we actually had a push of water like we had here at Cedar Key where we saw some of that water rise pretty high, but right now it`s about the rain. That rain should taper off today and we will watch Idalia move out to see, kind of transform a little bit and then wait to get a push.\par \par \uc2 The good news is that push is going to be out to sea versus back toward the United States and that`s what we see going into a Labor Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 Oftentimes, you know, Labor Day weekend is one of those times during the peak of the season, you wonder: Hey, are we going to get a tropical system? We actually look like we`re going to be pretty clear this weekend, in terms of any kind of tropical system, so that`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is good news, Jim. And as Idalia heads out to sea, I`ve got to ask you, you`ve been through without exaggeration, dozens of these hurricanes. Is there anything that stood out about this one?\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: Yes. I think we dodged the bullet, frankly. As bad as it is and all the damage that we`re showing you, it is all storm surge. This is not wind. We only had 177-mile-hour gusts. But here`s the height of it yesterday and you can see what we went through here.\par \par \uc2 The water was just coming up big time from five o`clock in the morning. You can see it bouncing all over the place. But if you look at the graph of the tidal gauge, notice we were going into low tide at the time the water was coming up over 10 feet. So that saved us about three feet of surge here, and frankly, probably saved a lot of homes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow, very good luck there. Jim Cantore, the veteran from Cedar Key. Jim, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: Thanks, man. You bet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning now to South Africa, where at least 73 people have died in a devastating fire that ripped through a five-story building overnight in Johannesburg.\par \par \uc2 Officials say, at least one child is among the dead and that death toll is expected to rise. At least 52 others were injured, with many being treated for smoke inhalation. The building was known as an informal settlement for homeless people.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters have extinguished the flames and the search and rescue teams are now looking for anyone still trapped inside. There is no word yet on what actually caused the fire.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And this morning, there are new questions about the health of Senate Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . Yesterday, he suddenly froze up and went silent before reporters for the second time in about a month. Now, later in the day, one of his colleagues say, McConnell, who is 81 years old was back sounding like his usual self.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: As he took questions after a visit with a local Chamber of Commerce in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): What are my thoughts about what?\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Running for reelection in 2026.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Oh, I`d say --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Senate Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  began to answer, trailed off and then appeared to freeze up for several seconds.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Did you hear the question, Senator? Running for re-election in 2026?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: An aide briefly intervened as McConnell stared straight ahead, expressionless.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: All right, I`m sorry, y`all. We`re going to need a minute.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Before resuming.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Okay.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: His staff said McConnell became momentarily lightheaded and planned to see a doctor before his next event. It`s the second such incident this summer.\par \par \uc2 In July, at the Capitol, McConnell suddenly went silent.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): Okay, Mitch. Is there anything else you want to say or should we just go back to your office? Do you want to say anything else to the press?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: His staff also blamed that incident on lightheadedness.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I`m fine.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: You`re fine? You`re fully able to do your job?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: McConnell was hospitalized with a concussion in March after suffering a fall at a DC hotel and he is missing votes. McConnell is in the top five for the most absent member of a very narrowly divided Senate.\par \par \uc2 Number one is the oldest, 90-year-old California Democrat, \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 . She missed weeks of Senate proceedings this spring while hospitalized for shingles.\par \par \uc2 Still, Florida GOP senator, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  told CBS News, he is confident in McConnell as the party`s leader.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): My belief is he is going to come back, he is going to work hard.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Senator McConnell attended a fundraiser in Louisville after the incident yesterday. We don`t know if he went to a doctor. Any absence of any senator for an extended period is potentially pivotal here in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  that has seen dozens of tiebreaker votes in just the past few years -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: His office did say he would be consulting with a doctor. Scott, thank you very much -- we hope so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We hope so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For that report, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right now, to a resounding big time defeat in court for \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . The former President Trump`s one-time lawyer, he is being sued. He is facing defamation lawsuit by two Georgia election workers, Ruby Freeman and her daughter Shaye Moss.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the judge entered a default judgment in their favor after the former New York Mayor failed to comply with subpoenas, and as the judge put it, turned over blobs of indecipherable data.\par \par \uc2 Freeman and Moss told the House Committee -- the January 6 Committee that Giuliani made false statements about them related to unproven claims of election fraud.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RUBY FREEMAN (Georgia election worker): I`ve lost my reputation. I`ve lost my sense of security, all because a group of people starting with number 45 and his ally, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  decided to scapegoat me and my daughter.\par \par \uc2 SHAYE MOSS (Georgia election worker): It has turned my life upside down.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Giuliani will have to pay more than $130,000.00 in plaintiffs` legal fees ahead of a trial to determine other damages.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, still ahead, one couple with a heavy student loan burden talks about the high personal and financial cost of resuming payments, while the Biden administration works on a new plan to lighten the load.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:15, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, Jonathan Vigliotti asked the mayor of Maui County why he did not call on the state for backup during or immediately after the deadly wildfires.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: You are the highest ranking official here on the island. If the buck stops with your office. How is that possible?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RICHARD BISSEN: Yes, I can`t speak to what or whose responsibility it was to communicate --\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: But you`re the boss of this island, so yes, you can speak to it.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RICHARD BISSEN: I`m saying, I can`t say who was responsible for communicating with General Hara.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now Jonathan also as Major General Kenneth Hara, who is in charge of the state`s emergency response, why he was not aware of the scale of the crisis right away.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS, your favorite morning show. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Starting in October, the bill is due again for federal student loan borrowers. Interest will be charged starting tomorrow after it was stopped in March of 2020 to help people during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang looks at the White House and how they`re trying to help.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERIN HEFFERN (Student loan borrower): Come on. This way.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: In Indianapolis, Erin Heffern and her husband are dreading the return of student loan bills.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HEFFERN: We lose sleep over it. It is like, it is just -- it takes so much out of us.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Together, they owe $116,000.00 which breaks down to payments of $1,500.00 a month. During the pandemic, they got some relief.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HEFFERN: It`s really hard to even have a strategy because it`s such a large portion of what we make.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: For 39-year-old Heffern, the financial strain amounts to more than struggling to pay bills. It has impacted her goal to start a family.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HEFFERN: Not having children has probably been the biggest, biggest choice that we`ve made because of our student loan debt. We can`t live paycheck-to-paycheck and bring children into this world.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The couple is among 45 million Americans who have student loan debt totaling $1.7 trillion. The Biden administration tried to deliver up to $20,000.00 of relief to millions of borrowers, but in June, the Supreme Court blocked the program with a six-three decision.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has vowed to keep fighting, feeling pressure from progressives in his party.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Under my new plan, reducing that payment to just five percent of your disposable income.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Under the president`s Save Plan, which will be fully implemented by next summer, interests will not accumulate as long as borrowers make their monthly payments. Those will be based on income and family size.\par \par \uc2 More than one million low-income borrowers will qualify for a zero dollar monthly payment.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE SINGLETARY ("The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " personal finance columnist): It`s a game changer for a lot of people who are in student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Michelle Singletary is a personal finance columnist for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE SINGLETARY: People look at how much they owe. They get discouraged and they just get pounced. If you are not sure you have enough money, call your loan servicer. Figure out the right payment plan for you and take action.\par \par \uc2 I`m telling you, this debt will haunt you for decades. So call and see what is possible.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HEFFERN: This way, bud.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Heffern was holding out hope for loan forgiveness. Now, she is grappling with reality.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HEFFERN: I just -- I don`t think -- I don`t -- yes, I don`t think that we`ll ever get any relief from it. I think we`ll take these loans to our graves, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: There is a so-called on-ramp program for the next year that allows people to be spared the worst consequences of missing payments on their loans. They won`t be considered default or reported to credit bureaus, but if they miss their payments, interest will add up and Singletary stresses that collectors will get that money in the end, so try to pay -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would listen to Michelle Singletary. She knows her stuff. Weijia, thank you very much for that. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, in our "School Matters" series, we are covering a lot of ground today. We`re going to show you a new high-tech system designed to help keep kids safe.\par \par \uc2 Plus, career education, no debt here, makes a comeback. Students learning practical skills that lead to good jobs right away.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Three weeks after Maui`s deadly wildfires, there are growing calls for top officials involved in the emergency response to resign.\par \par \uc2 Now the current death toll is 115 and more than a hundred people -- more than 300 people are still missing it this hour. Those local and state officials are being criticized for what seems to be a lack of accountability in the face of some very tough questions from reporters, including our own Jonathan Vigliotti. He has been covering the disaster on Maui since it happened.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, you asked very important questions yesterday. Not sure you got the answers. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Good morning to you Gayle.\par \par \uc2 This morning, still many questions, not enough answers. The calls for resignation come after a nearly two-minute exchange I had with top leaders, both here in the state and on the island and they would not explain where they were and what they were doing at the time of this deadly fire.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: As the flames barreled down on Lahaina --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh man. That house is burning.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And social media showed dozens clinging to life in the ocean to escape the inferno, CBS News has learned Maui County mayor, Richard Bissen did not call on the state for backup, either during or in the hours after the firestorm.\par \par \uc2 Social media showed the fire, showed people running for their lives, and yet you`re telling me your office was not in communication.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RICHARD BISSEN: You`re talking about social media, sir. To answer you, sir, I am not going to speak to social media. I wasn`t on social media. We didn`t have time for that.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Bissen did not explain what he was doing at this time.\par \par \uc2 You are the highest ranking official here on the island. If the buck stops with your office, how is that possible?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RICHARD BISSEN: Yes, I can`t speak to what or whose responsibility it was to communicate there at that time.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: But you`re the boss of this island.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RICHARD BISSEN: I am.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: So yes, you can speak to it.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RICHARD BISSEN: I am.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Let him answer.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RICHARD BISSEN: I can`t say who was responsible for communicating with General Hara.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Major General Kenneth Hara, director of the state`s emergency response said in an earlier interview, he was initially unaware of the scale of the crisis.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. KENNETH HARA (Hawaii Emergency Management Agency director): But I thought everyone had gotten out safely. And it wasn`t until probably the next day that started hearing about some fatalities.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: So my question is for now, General Hara. I would like some clarification --\par \par \uc2 We pressed Hara on this point.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. KENNETH HARA: But because of the winds, we couldn`t start our helicopters, and we couldn`t launch them to commitment. We were ready. We were ready to go. So the narrative you`re pushing is not totaling up.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Don`t you think -- it`s not a narrative. Don`t you think it was important for your agency to know about the deaths?\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. KENNETH HARA: I don`t think I could have done anything about that.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Hara and his local subordinate, Herman Andaya were both at an emergency response conference hosted by FEMA off the island at the time of the fire.\par \par \uc2 HERMAN ANDAYA (Former Chief of Maui Emergency Management Agency): I do not.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Andaya resigned after telling CBS News he didn`t regret his decision to not sound emergency sirens.\par \par \uc2 Hara did not clarify what his state and local teams were doing during and after the fire.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been looking at the timeline --\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. KENNETH HARA: What I`m trying to say is that --\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And it is getting murky.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. KENNETH HARA: That fire was so rapid and by the time everyone had situational awareness, it was too late, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Situational awareness and statements released overnight, Hara said he would not step down unless directed to do so.\par \par \uc2 The mayor now saying people are rightfully seeking and deserve answers, though he would not provide more. And the governor is now weighing in saying now is not the time to judge, though this island and its leaders will be tested again this morning. There are red flag warnings, in effect, Nate, throughout the day for the first time since this fire hit more than three weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Jonathan, when it comes to situational awareness, you`ve been there from very near the beginning. Certainly, the aftermath. You`ve covered it extensively. You`ve talked to a lot of people there, not only the officials. Hearing those answers, which you were uniquely able to get, how do you think they`re going to sound to the people of Hawaii? To the people of Maui?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Well, we already know here on the island, so many people are frustrated. The common narrative that we`re hearing from leaders is that this fire was a bomb and just exploded. But we know through our reporting and the timeline, there was a very long fuse.\par \par \uc2 Early in the morning, there were red flag warnings. The wind was so dangerous, schools were canceled, and yet so many residents are frustrated with the lack of a response to protect the people.\par \par \uc2 The power, it`s still a question, if it was turned off. There were no evacuations. Obviously, the sirens were not sounded. And again, those questions, where were these top leaders as the fire barreled down, and they were seemingly unaware of the true extent of this crisis -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, they are clearly very defensive, and as we now sit here, three weeks out, and we`ve seen so much more information --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It just makes me wonder, what did they know in real time? In real time, it seems like there was a lot of information that they could have used to make different decisions.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Yes, it is a great question and it is worth noting, we have put out dozens of phone calls and text messages to the county and to the governor`s office over the past three weeks. We have requested proper sit down interviews and said we`ve had to do these piecemeal interviews and these press conferences.\par \par \uc2 It is worth noting that invitation for both the mayor and also for the governor still stands.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Jonathan, with so much pain and sadness still in the air, it is tough to ask those questions. So thank you for doing that, because the people of Hawaii need answers and also help.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, once again, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of help, later, in "What to Watch," we`ll share a big announcement from some of our favorite people on a new way to help fire survivors on Maui.\par \par \uc2 Up next, though, our latest "School Matters" story, how new technology is helping keep students safe in many schools.\par \par \uc2 You are watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: School is of course a busy time for parents, for students, for teachers alike, and those responsible for keeping people safe on campus, those folks, too, a major concern for everyone.\par \par \uc2 Last year, there were more than 50 shootings in schools across these United States. Forty people killed, a hundred injured and we`ve already seen 25 school shootings in this year, all before summer break.\par \par \uc2 As part of our "School Matters" series, Meg Oliver is here to show us an innovative program that is helping keep our kids safe. I wish we didn`t need these programs, but we do.\par \par \uc2 Meg, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 Tony, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 As a parent, one of our greatest worries is how safe our kids are at school. It seems it`s no longer a matter of if, but when another tragedy happens. That`s where a new high-tech safety solution comes in, a potential game changer in school safety.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: When an emergency happens in a school, response time is critical.\par \par \uc2 How much time are you saving here?\par \par \uc2 IVO ALLEN (911inform founder and CEO): Minutes. We`re about a 60 percent reduction in response time --\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Ivo Allen is the founder of 911inform, a security software designed to connect school staff, dispatchers, and first responders simultaneously for anything from a fight to a health issue to gun violence. It`s now in more than 1,700 schools across the country.\par \par \uc2 IVO ALLEN: We basically connect into the phones in the building, the camera system, the HVAC, all the technology that`s in the building. We give them instantaneous access to everything that`s in this building.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: That instant access can be life-saving. In Parkland, Florida in 2018, it took officers only five minutes to respond, but 11 minutes to enter the school. By then, the shooter was gone and 17 people were dead.\par \par \uc2 911inform works by notifying school staff before the phone even rings at 911 Dispatch.\par \par \uc2 There are no students in the school right now, but you`re going to give us an example. So, you dial the 911.\par \par \uc2 IVO ALLEN: Right, so I`m going to dial 911. So our system instantaneously picked up notified exactly where I called. So, if you look here --\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Can you see inside that classroom?\par \par \uc2 IVO ALLEN: I can click on that classroom, I can view, and there`s a 360.\par \par \uc2 Within one touch, I can lockdown that building.\par \par \uc2 AUTOMATED VOICE: Lockdown. Lockdown.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Police departments receive the system for free when a district signs up. Depending on the size of the school, installation can cost up to $25,000.00 and maintenance runs about $5,000.00 a year. 911inform allows users to instantly see the closest door, lock and unlock it, check live security cameras and communicate silently.\par \par \uc2 What kind of pressure do you feel in terms of keeping students safe?\par \par \uc2 KRIS SANDMAN (School resource officer): It`s pretty much what I think about all day long.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: In 2019, school resource officer, Kris Sandman responded to a credible active shooter threat as students were arriving at Morris County Vocational School in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 KRIS SANDMAN: And we decided we needed to lockdown the school for the safety of the students. Our staff that was not on campus yet had no idea that we were in a lockdown because they didn`t receive any kind of notification.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: No one was injured, but the chaos prompted the school to install 911inform.\par \par \uc2 Can this security software save lives?\par \par \uc2 KRIS SANDMAN: Absolutely. There is no doubt in my mind that this system will save lives.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: That smart system is not just for active threats, but also medical or mental health incidents. 911inform told us the system sees the most activations for those with upwards of 200 per day across the country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I like Kris Sandman`s answer, it is all I think about all the time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We never used to have to think about anything like this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Never. Never.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, as a parent with a young child or a school age kid of any age.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very, very scary to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that does seem like a lifesaver, but you know, you also long for a fix that would happen before the threat is there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We caught up last year with something that "The Daily Tar Heel," that is a student newspaper of the University of North Carolina published. This was on Wednesday. These are text messages from students on campus following not a shooting, but a believed or a risk of a shooting. There was a report, they had a lockdown, and that bolt of fear shoots through the campus and these are the responses from people.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got: I`m in class. I`m losing it. I`m literally shaking. What is happening? I wish these never happened.\par \par \uc2 We know that parents are worried. You`re worried when you put your kid on a bus and they go to school.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The kids are also very worried. There is an incalculable mental health toll here for all involved, particularly the young people who are trying to learn and you know, so yes, these camera is awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There was a shooting at the UNC.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where the student went and apparently shot his teacher.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not -- at \uc2 UNC Chapel Hill\uc2 , not at this campus.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Listen there was a time where we would go to school and do fire drills, earthquake drills knowing that most likely it wouldn`t happen. Now these kids, they do these shooter drills, knowing that the likelihood is something and it increases year after year because of so many school shootings in America.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Well, if it is in a high school or \uc2 UNC Chapel Hill\uc2 , if you can pinpoint exactly where it`s coming from and it is in the back of the school, those responders can go in the back door instead of the front door, saves so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Know where the shooter is.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because of the cameras. I say every school should have this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like the cameras. I like the cameras in the classroom where you can see instantly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That seem to work.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Meg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, up next, we`ll have the stories that you`ll be talking about today. But first, it is 7:45, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (GAYLE KING and JAMIE YUCCAS singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: She is not having a cruel summer at all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, she is not.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What pitch was that? I couldn`t quite hear that. All right, time now for "What to Watch." Jamie Yuccas filling in for Vlad Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What are you trying to say, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m saying, I`m a dog. I can`t hear that level of high pitch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He doesn`t appreciate my singing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That level there at that height.\par \par \uc2 (GAYLE KING and JAMIE YUCCAS singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Let`s go do karaoke, and we will sort this out, okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. Okay.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: We begin with some big names teaming up to help Hawaii`s wildfire victims. \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  and Dwayne Johnson revealed this morning, they are joining forces. They created The People`s Fund of Maui, and the goal is to give cash directly to the people who need it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (Talk show host): The greatest sense of empowerment is being able to have some control when everything seems so out-of-control, and we`re calling it The People`s Fund of Maui because every dollar that you send is going to go into an account that goes directly to the people.\par \par \uc2 DWAYNE JOHNSON (Actor): Just knowing everything that took place and knowing the trauma that has taken place and knowing that it`s going to take a long time to rebuild, it probably will get a little worse before it gets a little better, but we`re here doing what we could do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And we are happy they are doing it. Every adult resident who was displaced on Maui is eligible for -- get this -- $1,200.00 per month during the recovery.\par \par \uc2 Oprah and The Rock are giving $10 million to get the fund started and they`re calling for others to make donations. Winfrey, of course, owns property on Maui. Johnson, who is of Samoan descent lived in Hawaii during part of his childhood.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. They`ve been working on this for quite some time and the whole thing was, how do you get the money to the people immediately?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How do you decide? So they didn`t want to set up a fund where somebody takes 10 percent to manage it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they wanted to give something to every adult. Because first, it started with, we will give something to every household. Well, how many people are in a household?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So then you have to go and find out how many adults over the age of 18 are in every household. It`s been quite a process.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well, they lost homes and businesses.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I think they`ve come up with something. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And they really do need the financial assistance.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, so they are soliciting donations and the goal is to give it to everybody for as long as they can.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That`s fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well now, the latest results from the US Open. It was a tough day for Chris Eubanks, a friend of this broadcast.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Right? Eubanks lost to France`s Benjamin Bonzi in four sets. Then there`s the battle of the teenagers though, 19-year-old Coco Gauff beat 16-year-old Russian, Mirra Andreeva.\par \par \uc2 After the match, Gauff told reporters that after taking a little bit of a break, she is having more fun on the court.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 COCO GAUFF (Tennis player): I have a lot of joy in me, but I just seem to ball it up when I play, and now I`m really just having fun and laughing and smiling and I think it`s making tennis more enjoyable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And we`ve seen this before, right? You know Simone Biles took a step back. Now, she is having more fun, doing really well. It`s fantastic.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is fun to watch. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony and I are going tonight.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are going tonight.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, we also sent Tony back to school.\par \par \uc2 Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in his eight o`clock hour, the latest on Idalia`s path of destruction through the southeast. We`ll have an update from North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. For a lot of high school students, college is the ultimate destination, but are schools ignoring the trades that could lead to lucrative careers? For our "School Matters" series, I go back to school to find out what other jobs I maybe should have learned about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson. It is also back to school shopping season and we`re all dealing with higher prices. We`ll share some tips on how you can get it all done and save a little.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody likes to save a little money, but first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 MAN: Holy [bleep]. That car is flying. What the [bleep]?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Idalia pummels Florida, Georgia, and the Carolinas with wind rain and floods.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MITCHELL: This is almost catastrophic to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The hurricane leaves devastation in its wake along Florida`s gulf coast.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The top of the roof just caked, slapped in on me and my three kids and my grandson.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A massive fire leaves dozens dead in Johannesburg, South Africa.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: They believe that anyone that is found at this stage is unlikely to be found alive.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): What I felt about what?\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Running for re-election in 2026.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Another very concerning moment for Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who appears to freeze again while speaking to reporters.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Did you hear the question, Senator? Running for re-election in 2026?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from the US Open.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And during the doubles match at the US Open, Coco Gauff forget she`s still one game short of victory.\par \par \uc2 COCO GAUFF: I thought the match was over. I had like a great return, and I like went for a hug and Jess was like going away from me. And I was like: Damn, like what did I do? And then I realized that the match wasn`t over. She`s like: What`s the score? And I was like: Oh, there was no game set match.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Every time you see her, you go, "We love you Coco."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is so enthusiastic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s great. Her partner in that moment though was like: Well hold on. Game face. Keep it on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Too soon. Too soon.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Not yet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here we go. We`re going to begin this hour with Tropical Storm Idalia, which is still causing damage after slamming into Florida`s gulf coast yesterday as a Category 3 hurricane. Now, it`s now over North Carolina after generating dangerously high winds across the southeast.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, the storm surge left towns partly underwater, and today, more than 300,000 people throughout the southeast still have no power at this hour.\par \par \uc2 Jarred Hill is in Morehead City, North Carolina, that`s right along the coast there.\par \par \uc2 Jarred, good morning to you. It seems like the winds have kicked up a little bit since we last saw you.\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: Yes, Gayle. The winds have definitely kicked up a bit here in Morehead City, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 While this storm might not be the hurricane that it was when it hit Florida, as you can see and hear, it still has got some juice.\par \par \uc2 Overnight though, we saw some torrential winds and rains really blowing through this area from this storm as it was coming through. The sustained maximum gusts of winds there about 60 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 Now yesterday, the storm crossed into Georgia with strong winds and heavy rain as well. In Charleston, water levels rushed over the battery wall which is a popular landmark there, bringing the fifth highest tide on record to the city.\par \par \uc2 But then check this out, there is some stunning video here, a tornado in Goose Creek, South Carolina sent this car flying into the air. Now, the local police chief says the two people only sustained minor injuries from that.\par \par \uc2 Now meanwhile in Florida, we are getting a better picture of all the damage Idalia left behind. The storm with maximum sustained winds of 125 miles per hour completely submerged entire communities. Roads like these and Crystal River are now impassable after the system unleashed life-threatening storm surge.\par \par \uc2 Another example of Idalia`s power, this surveillance video, check it out, shows a house in the Horseshoe Beach as washed away and completely really torn apart by the storm.\par \par \uc2 Now as far as casualties, we know that there have been two so far in Florida, two individuals were killed while they were driving in that storm there.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we are continuing to watch the conditions here in North Carolina as this king tide continues to swirl off the coast here and that potentially could bring an intense amount of flooding to some of these communities on top of what they`ve already seen.\par \par \uc2 Back to you -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Jarred Hill for us. Jarred, thank you very much. Appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 All right, turning now to our "School Matters" series. It is a week-long series. We`ve got more of it.\par \par \uc2 We`re highlighting stories and trends in education and finding out what matters most to students to teachers and parents, too. And this morning, we`re looking at where the jobs are and how we are preparing our kids for them, because I have to say it is not going so well.\par \par \uc2 There is a "massive shortage" of skilled workers according to the \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2 . Companies are looking for plumbers, for electricians, for carpenters, for auto mechanics, and I`m just naming a few here, and it is really no surprise because for decades, enrollment in high school vocational arts programs, we used to call them Shop Class, they`ve been in decline, but now that is changing.\par \par \uc2 We recently visited a high school where kids can learn a trade.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The problem with school, as many kids will tell you --\par \par \uc2 BOY: What`s unlevel?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is there`s not always a clear connection between the work and how you`ll use it in the real world.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What`s our total span if the run is four-foot-three.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But that`s not a question at a school like this.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MIKE (Career and technical education at Pittsburgh Public Schools): The data says that the new jobs are in the technical fields.\par \par \uc2 And I hear you`re going to put up the walls.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Angela Mike runs the career and technical education program here at Carrick High and other schools in Pittsburgh, preparing students for jobs they know will be waiting for them at graduation.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MIKE: It`s phenomenal, because we have a student who may be wondering, why am I learning this in math? And then they go into a class like carpentry, and then they put it to use and now they can go back in their academic class, and now, it all makes sense.\par \par \uc2 So learning through relevant things really helps the light bulb come on, and I see that across all our programs with students.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Those programs include carpentry, automotive repair, health care, and engineering technology. And since taking over in 2010, Mike has overseen a 15-fold increase in the number of kids graduating with these industry certifications, a pretty solid answer to the question of what`s next?\par \par \uc2 What percentage of the students who graduate have a job if they want one, when they leave?\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MIKE: Every single student can have a job if they would like one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But after decades of well-meaning parents and political leaders pushing a message of college for all, there are more trade jobs than graduating seniors to fill them.\par \par \uc2 Is there still a little bit of a stigma attached to technical or vocational -- to use the old-term -- education?\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MIKE: There definitely is. It`s not a hundred percent where everyone is on board, but we`re working on that.\par \par \uc2 It has a lock and handle on it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And when her students go to class, and it looks like this, they to know that other students may scoff.\par \par \uc2 What do they think of this kind of work?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK SALERNO (High school student): They think it`s kind of stupid when I`m trying to get into a trade school. They want to get into college, but they don`t have a backup plan if it fails.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Of course, for other families, including just about everyone we met on moving day at \uc2 Montclair State University\uc2  in New Jersey, the college plan is still the only one.\par \par \uc2 Why college? Why did you want that path for yourself?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN NEWPAUER (\uc2 Montclair State University\uc2  student): Well, my parents both went to college. So it just -- it`s kind of just like a natural consequence. Like they went, so I am going to go, too.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CIRONE (\uc2 Montclair State University\uc2  student): I thought that I would give myself a little bit of stability.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you think there`s more stability in a college degree than a trade path?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CIRONE: At least for me.\par \par \uc2 JADEN CIPRINO-GOINES (\uc2 Montclair State University\uc2  student): I feel like if I do college, I`d get more chances to have better jobs, better opportunities, and I`d be one of the first college students in my family.\par \par \uc2 ABUBACAR KEITA (\uc2 Montclair State University\uc2  student): I don`t know, I just sort of just knew that like, you know, just coming here was just the best - -\par \par \uc2 MAN: College is the best way.\par \par \uc2 ABUBACAR KEITA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You think college is the best way?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I would think so.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON (HVAC technician): How`s it going?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON: Josh. Nice to meet you, sir.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yet, Josh Henson (sp?) is hoping more kids realize there actually is another option and it does come with perks.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON: When you get to a job and something has failed, but you have to figure out why it failed. That`s the -- you know, that`s the big ticket right there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s like a little murder mystery. It`s like --\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s like what killed this AC?\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON: Yes. Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Henson is a 25-year-old HVAC technician in Bucks County, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON: Hello, good, sir. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And when we joined him recently on a call, I realized pretty quickly some of the things they don`t teach in college.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON: This goes on here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: While some may think of trade school as a Plan B, for Henson, it was a preference.\par \par \uc2 You dropped out of a four-year college to go to a vocational school.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So if anything, you wish you made this decision earlier.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON: A hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Can you make a six-figure income in a career in this profession?\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON: Absolutely. Absolutely. We have guys at our job that are master mechanics, been doing this for 10 years, 15 years that are making 55 to 60 an hour.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Henson says his company and many others like it are right now looking for that next generation.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HENSON: HVAC, plumbing, electrical -- all of the trades are very, very understaffed. We definitely need as much as we can get.\par \par \uc2 MAN: All right, get the wrong one, all right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a need that could be met by students like these if only more high schools would teach them.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MIKE: My hope for the future is that more students take advantage of the opportunity for a career in technical education they don`t end up down a path or a career choice that doesn`t have jobs at the end for them waiting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So just to be clear, the pitch is you`re going to have a job right away.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MIKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`re going to be doing something you love.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MIKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Am I missing anything?\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MIKE: No. You said it perfectly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`re going to be paid well, too. I don`t think people realize how lucrative those jobs can be.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you something, when your air conditioning or your heating goes out when you really need it, I don`t think about a college degree. I just want somebody, please help me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Please.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: ASAP. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Please come and help.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And guess what? When we as a country don`t have enough people who have those skills --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It costs more to hire them. And also, there are bigger delays, and so people are out of their AC for longer. And by the way, we always talked about climate change, and like turning the system over to like electric vehicles. What do you think is going to put in all those charging stations and weld all that stuff?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And lay the road to pull off to the side? It`s people who have those skills. So as a country, we need to invest in them. And this message of the last 30 years of college first is not for everybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we need to move some of that respect from college to these other trades, which we need so badly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. Think about when we were locked down and how many people were still working because they were essential to this country moving forward.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And those are the people that knew these trades.\par \par \uc2 And listen, there are people that go to college, get a degree, and get the job they want. But then there are people that get that degree and they don`t go into the space that they desire.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When you go to trade school, you can get a job almost right away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You get a job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or people go to college and then they don`t graduate and then they got the debt and they don`t have a degree and they`re in big trouble. That`s what that fella was talking about a backup plan.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you know --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was good, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, you know, I like it. If I could go back-to-school, I think I`d do carpentry. You know, my father and my stepfather are both good carpenters. They were drug dealers also, but they were carpenters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, there is that. But I like how Miss Mike angled that though when she said, it is skills. It is skills.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, so it is good, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am glad you didn`t follow the family business when it came time for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, they had to hide the money somewhere.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, they had to hide it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They laundered it through building houses, but you know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, that was -- now, all jokes aside, that was really good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really for the next generation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it was.\par \par \uc2 Never mind. And our "School Matters" series takes us shopping for back-to- school supplies. Didn`t you love going back-to-school shopping? Yes, we did.\par \par \uc2 We will have some tips on how to save money. We like that, too. But first, it`s 8:12, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, we have a very special guest that will join us for our "Talk of the Table" so stick around.\par \par \uc2 And also our new series, "The Dish: Recipe" serves up a refreshing drink. We`ll find about how to make an agua fresca perfect for Labor Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s happy music to get you going. We like that.\par \par \uc2 As we continue our "School Matters" series, families across the country are dealing with the rising prices in their back-to-school shopping. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 Spending on school supplies is expected to increase 20 percent from last year to $7.1 billion, but families plan to spend less on things like clothing and technology. Janet Shamlian joined one Texas mother on her shopping trip and found some ways to save on school supplies.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Nausheen Asif has been studying where to find the best deals so she can ace this back-to-school shopping trip for her daughters, Mary in the eighth grade, and Misha in fifth.\par \par \uc2 The girls have a list of class required items to cross off from folders to glue sticks and markers.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s her list. Asif is a special education teacher and usually spends hundreds of dollars of her own money buying supplies for the classroom.\par \par \uc2 Do you have to factor supplies for your classroom into your family budget?\par \par \uc2 NAUSHEEN ASIF (Mother and Special Education teacher): I do because my husband will definitely like: Is this for the kids or is this for your classroom?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: That`s the reality of being a teacher.\par \par \uc2 NAUSHEEN ASIF: It is.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Another harsh reality, the price of school supplies has skyrocketed, up almost 24 percent in just two years, so Asif is making sure her girls know the price has to be right.\par \par \uc2 NAUSHEEN ASIF: How much is that one?\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Go check.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NAUSHEEN ASIF: Yes, yes. You`ve got to check.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: You`ve got to check the price.\par \par \uc2 Asif planned her shopping during a tax free weekend in Texas, one of 17 states holding back-to-school tax holidays this year. She is also trying to do most of their shopping at just one store, factoring in the cost of gas and their time.\par \par \uc2 NAUSHEEN ASIF: I know there`s a sale at another store for like Kleenex and some composition notebooks, but I prefer here because we can also get their clothes.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: But Consumer Reports deals editor, Samantha Gordon says taking your time shopping could save you more.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA GORDON (Consumer Reports deals editor): You don`t necessarily need to buy everything all at once. A lot of times what will happen is you buy more supplies than your kids actually end up using. So just get what they need right now.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: She says consider waiting for Labor Day sales for big ticket items like laptops and electronics.\par \par \uc2 NAUSHEEN ASIF: These are $12.99.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The Asifs are also shopping for clothing. After the girls outgrew almost everything they wore last year. With the price of apparel at more than three percent, Gordon says that`s another opportunity to save.\par \par \uc2 Check platforms like Facebook Marketplace and \uc2 Poshmark\uc2  for gently used clothes and don`t rush to buy winter clothes now. Fall discounts are coming.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA GORDON: You`ll start to see sales for the holidays as early as October and that will include apparel, so you can find savings if you wait a little bit longer.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: With state sales tax waived on most of her purchases, and a one-time teacher discount, Asif was able to save about $100.00 on this shopping trip.\par \par \uc2 NAUSHEEN ASIF: We`ve got clothes school supplies. We`ve got the water bottles lunchboxes, so we got most everything we need.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And another tip, save your receipt and continue to monitor prices.\par \par \uc2 Consumer Report says you could get a price adjustment if an item is further discounted after your purchase.\par \par \uc2 Overall, Asif spent close to a thousand dollars on back to school shopping, about $300.00 more than last year.\par \par \uc2 NAUSHEEN ASIF: It just so happened this is the year that we have to replenish.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The anticipation of heading back to school comes this year with a higher price tag.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Janet Shamlian in Katy, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t think anybody minds waiting for a deal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If it means waiting a month so you can get a better deal, but they lucked out pretty well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Looking at the haul. It looked really good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Gave me flashbacks to shopping with my mom for back- to-school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She was a school teacher. We didn`t have a ton of money and she goes like: Do you really need this? You know, there`s a lot of taking stuff back. But you know, as a kid, you don`t understand how long that list is of supplies to buy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And how long that receipt is and how big the number is at the bottom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like, mom, I promise I`ll make it worth it. I`m going to get the best grades this year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Everybody has got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As a kid, you have no concept of money, and I`m sure everybody has heard the phrase, money doesn`t grow on trees.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, Gayle, and that`s why people should stick around for CBS MORNINGS deals, including a device designed to backup all of your photos on your phone and you can get it for 50 percent off the retail price.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, a new voice for "Talk of the Table" and she is here early like a pro, student journalist, Payton Lee is ready to