it and run away. The wonderful thing about this is like my best friend saw me talking about this on the show and she said, I bought two for our girl, Caitlin (sp?), her daughter, who just went to \uc2 UC Berkeley\uc2 . She wanted her to have them as that extra sense of safety.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is true for college students.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: And so you put this in a care package.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a good idea.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: You go hiking by yourself. You run by yourself. Women say it gives them that sense of I`ve done something to protect myself.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And kids walking to and from the bus now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s the price on that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, absolutely. You can hang that on backpacks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even for little kids.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Even for the little kids. You may have this for $19.99.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: So don`t buy one, buy two.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So right here I can imagine people at home saying you`ve got candles next to flowers, you`re in trouble. These are not real flames, although they look like them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Right. This is the Luminara flameless candle and people who have them, they love them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: These are great. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Because they flicker and they look so realistic. What I -- I`ve got to tell you something, when I opened the box and I put it up at home, it was during the daytime so I was waiting for night and everything. Then I forgot it was there. And then at two o`clock I got up in the morning for a little drink of water and I had a dread feeling that I left the candle on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I left the candle on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: And then I realized that looks like a real candle and it didn`t burn the house down so if the baby knocks it over, if a flower gets on it, if the dog`s tail wax it, boom, you`re okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right. And they look good, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: They do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They`ve got different designs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes. They have the embossed turtle. They have ivory and these --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One that looks melted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, and these are made with paraffin wax, so they`re going to have that real look and we have them for up to 54 percent off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes, look at that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: So they start at $21.00. You`re going to want that for the holidays coming up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that is real. Yes, that`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the significance of the embossed turtle is?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The terpenes. That`s from your alma mater.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There it is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Way to bring it home, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Gayle Bass, thank you, and of course, we partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything that you saw, scan the QR code on your screen or head over to CBSDeals.com to start shopping now. I see you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you know, we`ve been featuring different back to school stories this week. It`s all part of our "School Matters" series.\par \par \uc2 So tomorrow, I get to introduce you to a very inspiring teacher in my life.\par \par \uc2 When I was in high school my teacher, Roger Heathcote is his name. There`s Roger and his wife, Peggy, let me live with them during my senior year, so I didn`t have to move across the country because my family was moving. And they have some stories to tell.\par \par \uc2 We caught up with him the other day.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, Gayle, she oftentimes jokes how people say she could talk to a tree.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Was she always into news or breaking news or reading the paper?\par \par \uc2 ROGER HEATHCOTE (Gayle King`s high school teacher): She was into music and to basketball players.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wait a minute, Roger, not basketball but basketball players?\par \par \uc2 ROGER HEATHCOTE: The basketball players.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY HEATHCOTE (Roger`s wife): She was into reading the daily newspaper that came the day after the basketball game. She was the statistician for the basketball team and didn`t miss a game.\par \par \uc2 But she`d wait for the newspaper to come so she could open the sports page to see what they had written about her Menlo Atherton Bears and she`d cut all those newspaper articles out.\par \par \uc2 And as I remember, you made a scrapbook at the end of the year, didn`t you, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I did. I made a scrapbook and I gave it to the basketball coach. I was very into basketball. I still love that sport.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a great sport. It`s a great sport.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is my favorite sport. It is my favorite, favorite sport.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Did you happen to date a basketball player though? Because I believe what Roger said was you like basketball players.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I heard him. I heard him. I heard him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You went to prom with a basketball player?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did. I did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now we`re trying to track him down.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a whole series.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s see what he`s up to.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People from Gayle`s past.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, I know a few single \uc2 NBA\uc2  players if you want me to hook you up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My number is nine one seven -- that`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS,. your favorite morning show. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230831ej8v00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for August 8, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Carter Evans, Ed O`Keefe, Mark Strassmann, Alice  Gainer, Chris Warren, Kris Van Cleave, Meg Oliver, Omar Villafranca \par \uc2 3,582 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Tonight: the assault charges after a massive brawl breaks out at a dock in Alabama. What police are saying tonight about whether or not the fight was racially motivated.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 The violent altercation caught on video. What sparked the fight and why more charges are expected.\par \par \uc2 DARRYL ALBERT (Montgomery, Alabama, Police Chief): This is not indicative of who we are as a city. The city of Montgomery is much better than that.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We won`t take it anymore!\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Nearly 11,000 city workers are walking off the job in Los Angeles today, demanding higher pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Members of the union representing these employees are accusing the city of refusing to honor previous agreements and engaging in unfair labor practices.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Deadly storms sweep through the East Coast, causing mayhem on the roads and in the sky.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Here in Maryland, this tree sliced through this home, trapping the homeowner inside under a pile of debris.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The nation`s airlines are in recovery mode after the storm canceled more than 1,700 flights and delayed nearly 8,000 more.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Breaking news: A divided Supreme Court allows President Biden to regulate ghost guns. What it means for the country.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): These guns are weapons of choice for many criminals.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Rockaway Beach remains closed after a woman was attacked by a shark.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Bitten so badly there, she was rushed to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A record jackpot. Tonight`s Mega Millions prize surpasses $1.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Cha Cha Slide music video)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And remembering the deejay who brought us the Cha Cha Slide.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Cha Cha Slide music video)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good evening, everyone. Thank you for joining us. I`m Major Garrett, in for Norah.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with new details on that violent riverfront dock brawl in Montgomery, Alabama, that went viral and therefore has drawn nationwide attention.\par \par \uc2 Police say arrest warrants have been issued for three individuals on assault charges, this after a simple disagreement turned into a massive fight involving more than a dozen people. Videos of this incident blew up on social media after a black boat captain was assaulted by a group of white men.\par \par \uc2 Many social media commentators noted the racial makeup of the alleged attackers, but police said today, the fight did not fit the criteria of a hate crime.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca will start us off tonight from Montgomery.\par \par \uc2 Omar, good evening.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 This is the boat that was trying to dock, but was blocked for more than 40 minutes, which led to what many people are calling the melee in Montgomery.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): A viral boatside brawl that was all hands on deck.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Video taken by eyewitnesses show Damien Pickett, co- captain of a large riverboat, asking a group to move their pontoon, so the riverboat could dock in its assigned space.\par \par \uc2 They refused and attacked Pickett, wrestling him to the ground, while punching and kicking him. Pickett`s co-workers and others rushed to his aid, including 16-year-old Aaren Rudolph, who jumped into the water and swam to help.\par \par \uc2 Once the riverboat docked, the fight escalated.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Woo-hoo!\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: One woman was pushed into the river.\par \par \uc2 This man uses a folding chair as a weapon and hits this woman. Multiple people recorded the brawl, including Christa Owen.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTA OWEN (Riverboat Passenger): I just wanted to make sure that the police knew, when it came to the end, who threw the first punches.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Today, authorities have charged three white men with third-degree assault and are looking to question the man who was wielding the chair.\par \par \uc2 DARRYL ALBERT (Montgomery, Alabama, Police Chief): We did examine if there was enough to file hate crimes charges on this case. And we have also looked at what it takes to elevate this to a riot. We have worked with our local district attorney, and it didn`t fit the criteria for that.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Montgomery`s first black mayor, \uc2 Steven Reed\uc2 , says his city shouldn`t be marred by this latest incident.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN REED\uc2  (D-Mayor of Montgomery, Alabama): This is not indicative of who we are. So I think it`s important for us to address this as an isolated incident, one that was avoidable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Police say this investigation is active and still ongoing and that more charges are expected -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, thousands across the mid-Atlantic are picking up the pieces and assessing damage following a series of powerful storms overnight. At least two people were killed, one in Alabama and one in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver is in White Hall, Maryland, where she`s seen some of the damage up close.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): Powerful storms continued to pummel the Northeast today, with a tornado knocking down trees as it swept through Mattapoisett, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 Two days of raging storms caused flooding across the state, making some roads impassable. On Monday, from New York to Tennessee, the force of Mother Nature was evident.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: This had to be a tornado.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The storms brought down trees and knocked out power for thousands around Knoxville, Tennessee, eye-opening scenes everywhere.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Aaron (sp?).\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Frightened drivers in Westminster, Maryland, were caught in a dangerous thunderstorm Monday, as winds up to 80 miles per hour knocked down power lines like dominoes. Nearly 50 people were rescued from their cars.\par \par \uc2 The wild weather brought airports across the East Coast to a standstill.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave is at Washington`s Reagan National Airport.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: What a difference a day makes, only a handful of delays up on the board and just one cancellation here in Washington, as the nation`s airlines recover from the storm that canceled more than 1,700 flights and delayed about 8,000 more.\par \par \uc2 From New York to Atlanta, travelers still found themselves camping out at airports, stranded by the storm, waiting for word on when they can finally get a flight home.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: It was hell.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Yes. So, let me just say we`re very tired.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Here in White Hall, Maryland, a giant oak tree sliced open this house, trapping Todd Sexton`s 76-year-old mother.\par \par \uc2 What was she buried under?\par \par \uc2 TODD SEXTON (Son of Survivor): Bricks from the chimney, drywall, tree limbs.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Firefighters quickly rescued her from the debris.\par \par \uc2 Does it feel like a miracle to you?\par \par \uc2 TODD SEXTON: It`s -- there is -- there is definitely a guardian angel inside this residence.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Today, crews removed that big tree that split the house in two and boarded up the roof.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, power outages in the mid-Atlantic and South have seen a dramatic improvement, with less than 200,000 still without electricity -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Meg Oliver, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on the storms and the brutal heat, let`s bring in meteorologist Chris Warren from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chris, good evening.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Major.\par \par \uc2 As Meg showed us, trees are extremely dangerous when there are storms around, whether you`re outside or even in your home. And there was a lot of strong winds, creating a lot of damage, more than 600 storm reports widespread across the Southeast, all the way up to New England and the mid- Atlantic, heat still a big concern across the Southern Plains and continuing to expand as well across the Gulf Coast.\par \par \uc2 And around the Gulf Coast, you have a lot more access to humidity. So, temperatures, while we`re going to see readings into the triple digits, you get close to the Gulf and Florida, some areas, it`s going to feel, Major, like it`s 110-plus.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Chris Warren, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 Swimmers were kept out of the water today at a popular New York City beach after what`s believed to have been an extremely rare shark attack. A 65- year-old woman was severely injured.\par \par \uc2 We get the latest now from Alice Gainer of our \uc2 CBS\uc2  New York station.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALICE GAINER (voiceover): Officials say the 65-year-old woman was about 10 feet into the water at Rockaway Beach in Queens yesterday evening, when she felt a pain in her left leg.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: This is crazy. The -- I can`t believe someone got attacked.\par \par \uc2 ALICE GAINER: Lifeguards rushed in to help, carrying her to shore, applying a tourniquet, and keeping her conscious, before EMTs brought her to a local hospital.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Just finding out about a shark bite just blew my mind.\par \par \uc2 ALICE GAINER: The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  quickly launched surveillance drones, but were unable to spot any sharks.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: It`s concerning, but, at the end of the day, that`s where sharks do live.\par \par \uc2 ALICE GAINER: Today, another popular beach nearby was closed after a shark sighting. Monday`s apparent attack, the latest in a busy season for New York beachgoers, five bite incidents already this summer, all in just 36 hours over the Fourth of July weekend.\par \par \uc2 LYUDMILA EMAG (Shark Attack Survivor): I felt like I was in a trap. It was holding on to me, and I had to -- I didn`t know it was a shark.\par \par \uc2 ALICE GAINER: Scientists say, while still extremely rare, cleaner and warmer waters could be contributing.\par \par \uc2 GAVIN NAYLOR (Director, Florida Program for Shark Research, \uc2 University of Florida\uc2 ): We`re seeing a lot more bony fishes close to shore than we`re used to seeing. And with that come the predators that feed on them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ALICE GAINER: Rockaway Beach remains closed to the public, as officials investigate yesterday`s incident.\par \par \uc2 If confirmed, this would mark the first shark bite here since 1953 -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Alice Gainer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the city of stars is now a city on strike, with thousands of Los Angeles workers joining an already crowded set of picket lines. Government employees, traffic cops and airport workers hope their one-day disruption of city services will draw attention to their stalled contract negotiations. And it may be working.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #1: We love the union!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): L.A. is a city counting strikes like a bowling alley.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #2: If we don`t get it, shut it down!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Eleven thousand city employees, all union service workers, are staging a 24-hour walkout.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #3: I want respect!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: They`re indignant. Contract talks with the city have sputtered.\par \par \uc2 Think of all this as a warning shot, a 24-hour reminder of what disruption feels like.\par \par \uc2 PHYLLIS STRINGER (Custodian): Here in L.A., it`s the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Custodian Phyllis Stringer (sp?) echoes the frustrations of hundreds rallying outside City Hall, double trouble, low wages, high inflation.\par \par \uc2 PHYLLIS STRINGER: We need some help.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You`re working too hard and making too little?\par \par \uc2 PHYLLIS STRINGER: Yes. Yes. You know, we need a lot of more employees to come out to work with the city.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: No contract!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: No actors!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: More walkouts. Two Hollywood unions have struck the set. Without these more than 75,000 actors and writers, all studio production has stopped.\par \par \uc2 What`s scarce here, insight into an end in sight, L.A.`s labor actions just the latest in America`s summer of strikes, widespread walkouts, from nurses to baristas, nearly 340,000 union strikers this year, more than 10 times the number just two years ago.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Shut it down!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: One unusual demand here, fill existing job openings to ease workloads.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is a one-day strike. If you had to strike for real, would you do it?\par \par \uc2 PHYLLIS STRINGER: If I had to, yes. I will sacrifice, just like I`m -- sacrificed today.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: L.A. Mayor Karen Bass is in a tough spot. She`s pro- union, but very much in the crosshairs of these strikers. She has said they deserve a fair contract.\par \par \uc2 But defining fairness is always the great divide between management and workers -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Damage from the mayhem in New York City`s Union Square last Friday is estimated at $200,000, according to \uc2 CBS\uc2  New York. Chaos broke out when thousands of teenagers showed up for a giveaway promoted on social media. The crowd ripped down construction barriers, damaged police cars, private vehicles and the new entrance to a subway station.\par \par \uc2 Influencer Kai Cenat has been charged with inciting a riot. Police are scrubbing video of the incident and intend to make more arrests.\par \par \uc2 A divided Supreme Court today sided with the Biden administration`s crackdown on so-called ghost guns, or firearms without serial numbers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe reports on the significance of this ruling, as the use of these untraceable guns rises.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Police nationwide say ghost guns are a big reason why violent crime has spiked in major cities in recent years, and they have been used in school shootings in California, Maryland, and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Ghost guns can be bought online, some made with a 3-D printer, and most don`t carry serial numbers, making them harder to track. Today, the Supreme Court, with conservative Justices \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  joining the three liberals in the majority, allowed the regulations to stay in place while legal challenges continue.\par \par \uc2 The new rules were first introduced last summer in response to the growing wave of violent crime and seek to treat ghost guns like other firearms.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS CHITTUM (Former ATF Acting Deputy Director): It simply treats them as firearms under the Gun Control Act, where a person has to appear in person, present identification, and pass a background check.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Thomas Chittum is the former number two at the ATF and helped draft the regulations.\par \par \uc2 FORMER ACTING DEPUTY DIRECTOR THOMAS CHITTUM: Increasingly, what we have seen is that criminals turn to the Internet, order these kits from the comfort of their home, assemble them, and have a firearm that is not easily detected by law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: ATF figures show the number of ghost guns recovered more than doubled from 2020 to 2021.\par \par \uc2 Police say it`s led to violent crime, like this incident in New York last month, when a 25-year-old man on a scooter allegedly shot up six locations across the city using a ghost gun.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Opponents of the new regulations argue in federal court ATF doesn`t have the legal authority to change gun laws on its own.\par \par \uc2 The case could drag on for years. The White House says it`ll continue to use every tool available to stop gun violence in our country -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Ed O`Keefe -- Ed O`Keefe, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 There`s been another big campaign shakeup in the 2024 race for president. Republican candidate and Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has demoted his campaign manager and put the chief of staff of his governor`s office in charge of his 2024 bid. This move comes just weeks after DeSantis let go of about a third of his campaign workers. DeSantis has been struggling to gain traction against the front-runner for the GOP nomination, former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Now to Mega Millions mania. The third largest -- largest jackpot in history is up for grabs tonight, with an estimated $1.58 billion the line, or a $783 million lump sum payout. Now, the odds of winning, well, they`re not in your favor.\par \par \uc2 But, as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans reports, all it takes is $2 and a dream.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): Across the country, people are lining up to pay up --\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: It`s going to be a winner.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: -- for a minuscule chance of becoming a billionaire.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: If you don`t play, you can`t win.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The lottery is the most popular form of gambling in the U.S., where people spent upwards of $100 on billion tickets in 2021.\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, the line to buy them is out the door at Blue Bird Liquor, considered one of the luckiest stores in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: There you go.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Fernando Leone (sp?) drove almost two hours to buy a ticket here.\par \par \uc2 So, what are you going to do with the money?\par \par \uc2 FERNANDO LEONE (Lottery Player): You know, first thing I`m going to do, I`m going to change -- I`m going to change my phone number.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Two tickets worth more than a billion dollars were recently sold in the Los Angeles area --\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: Get my name (sp?) --\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: Can I have a ticket, man?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: -- one at this store in July just blocks from Skid Row downtown.\par \par \uc2 And while there is nothing you can do to increase your odds, there is a strategy to avoid having to share your winnings, according to this Harvard statistician.\par \par \uc2 MARK GLICKMAN (\uc2 Harvard University\uc2 ): You should just completely have patternless, random numbers, no birthdays that are -- that have special meaning to you, because other people are likely to make those same picks.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Carter Evans joins us now from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 So, Carter, come on, what are the odds?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Well, you`re truly paying for an opportunity to dream, because the odds are not good at all, one in 300 million.\par \par \uc2 That means you`re more likely to get hit by falling space debris -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So you`re telling me I got a chance.\par \par \uc2 The popular drug Wegovy may have additional health benefits far beyond diabetes treatment and weight loss. We will explain all that next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: In tonight`s Health Watch, a new study by the maker of the weight loss and diabetes drug Wegovy finds the medication may also benefit heart health.\par \par \uc2 The pharmaceutical company \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  says a trial involving more than 17,000 overweight and obese people with cardiovascular disease showed Wegovy can cut the risk of serious heart problems by 20 percent. These results, if independently verified, could put pressure on insurance companies to cover the popular drug.\par \par \uc2 Federal regulators are looking into a potential power steering problem in more than one million Ram 1500 pickup trucks. The investigation was opened after nearly 400 complaints were filed about the issue in model years 2013 through 2016. Officials say the problem has led to three crashes, but no injuries. The truck maker says it is cooperating with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Criminal charges are pending after a car goes flying into a house. We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Cars might fly one day, but we hope not like this.\par \par \uc2 A home in Central Pennsylvania was badly damaged when a car crashed into the second floor. You can see here how it got wedged upstairs. Police say the driver intentionally launched the car into the house and, not surprisingly, will face criminal charges. No one inside was injured.\par \par \uc2 Dangerous currents were no match for the impressive teamwork displayed by a group of lifeguards in Flagler Beach, Florida. Drone video shot last weekend shows the lifeguards forming a perfectly choreographed human chain, connecting themselves with ropes and buoys to rescue a man on a boogie board who apparently ended up too far from shore. They got him back on dry land in no time.\par \par \uc2 We remember the man behind the popular dance craze the Cha Cha Slide.\par \par \uc2 That is next.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: If you can`t watch the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, you can listen, subscribe wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Finally, we learned today that musician D.J. Casper has died following a seven-year battle with cancer.\par \par \uc2 Now, you might not know his name, but you definitely followed his orders on the dance floor.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Cha Cha Slide music video)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The Chicago native is best known as the creator of the world-famous Cha Cha Slide.\par \par \uc2 The hit song released in 1998 initially caught on at fitness centers around Chicago, before exploding on the radio -- yes, the radio -- two years later. Cha Cha Slide became a staple at weddings, flash mobs and sporting events. The infectious song even spent weeks on the U.S. and British music charts.\par \par \uc2 D.J. Casper, born William Perry Jr., was 58 years old.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. For Norah O`Donnell, I`m Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230809ej8800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for August 4, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 John Dickerson, Jonathan Vigliotti, Shanelle Kaul, Manuel  Bojorquez, Lana Zak, Chris Warren, Omar Villafranca, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,362 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Tonight, torrential downpours lead to dangerous flash flooding across parts of the South. Plus, sweltering temperatures still blanket much of the South and West.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 Water rescues underway in Tennessee, after more than 10 inches of rain falls in under 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Literally, everything in our yard floated away.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Mass chaos in New York`s Union Square after crowds gather for a giveaway of gaming consoles and computers.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: We have seen at least a dozen people being arrested.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : School shooting reenactment. Investigators fire dozens of rounds as part of a trial against the Parkland, Florida, resource officer, who never entered the school.\par \par \uc2 DR. TONY MONTALTO (President, Stand With Parkland): He is still guilty of running away from our children.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: The victim in the decades-long Gilgo Beach murders investigation has been identified. Authorities say Jane Doe No. 7 was Karen Vergata.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND TIERNEY (Suffolk County, New York, District Attorney): I think it`s important that we remember and honor not only Ms. Vergata, but all of the victims on Gilgo Beach.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : As the writers strike nears 100 days, the widespread effect it`s having, as a multibillion-dollar industry sits at a standstill.\par \par \uc2 ALEX UCEDA (CEO, Alex Catering Corporation): We were, like, working, like, 10, 11 jobs every day. It drops to maybe one or two jobs.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Hospitals and emergency rooms across multiple states shut down after a cyberattack.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Their wedding was like a fairy tale.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: House on fire.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And that was the end of that. But, unbeknownst to the bride and groom, angels were swooping in.\par \par \uc2 JAKE LANDUYT (Groom): To have them pick up a reception, like, out of ashes made the wedding better than we ever could have imagined.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Friday night. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 , in for Norah.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with chaotic weather across much of the nation, from scorching heat to thunderstorms and heavy rain. Millions of Americans are under flood alerts tonight. And flash flood emergencies were declared in parts of Kentucky and Tennessee this morning after torrential downpours dropped nearly a foot of rain in some areas.\par \par \uc2 Entire neighborhoods are underwater in Union City, Tennessee, where residents were rescued from their homes and evacuated. In the rest of the South, it`s the heat making things dangerous for 65 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 It`s been that way almost every day this summer in Arizona and Texas, where \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca starts us off with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Tonight, severe weather slamming parts of the South.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I want you to see how deep this is.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Nearly a foot of rain falling over 24 hours in Northwest Tennessee, the flash floods forcing emergency evacuations, this trailer park community under waist-deep water, some two dozen people rescued onto dry land.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Look outside. Literally, everything in our yard, it floated away. I had to get my cat and literally, like, the clothes on my back.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Almost up to our porch now.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: A frightening scene in Union City, Tennessee, video taken from a front porch showing the widespread high water levels.\par \par \uc2 Parts of Kentucky and Alabama soaked, too, leaving this roadway washed out. This comes as the record-breaking heat makes a comeback across the South and West, millions of people from Texas to Arizona come under triple-digit excessive heat warnings, as folks brace for the return of oppressive temperatures.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The agency that manages Texas` power grid has issued a watch for next week, when we are expecting more temperatures in the triple digits and demand to be up.\par \par \uc2 Now, they say, so far, the power grid has held up even after setting seven new peak demand records just this summer -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The threat of heat and severe storms is far from being over, so let`s bring in meteorologist Chris Warren from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, John.\par \par \uc2 We are tracking a dangerous, severe weather threat for the Northeast, but, as Omar was showing us, this very heavy rain setting up overnight across parts of Tennessee leading to some widespread flooding. That has since dissipated. Our focus here turning on the Northeast, with more strong storms, golf ball-size hail possible, with some of these storms 60-to-65- mile-an-hour winds also a possibility.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, dangerous heat continues across a lot of the Southwest and Southern Plains right into early next week, and, once again, John, temperatures will be well into the triple digits.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Chris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Mayhem broke out in New York City this afternoon, when thousands of young people flooded the streets after a popular social media streamer promoted a giveaway.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lana Zak reports, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  deployed more than 1,000 officers to control the chaos and made multiple arrests.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK (voiceover): Chaos erupted in Manhattan`s Union Square Park, as a large, rowdy crowd gathered for a free giveaway by a social media influence.\par \par \uc2 Thousands descended on the park after popular Twitch star Kai Cenat, who has millions of followers online, promised free PlayStations to his fans. The crowd grew agitated, forcing Cenat`s security detail to shield him and escort him to safety.\par \par \uc2 Some in the crowd started throwing objects at the police from a nearby construction site, forcing the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  to call in its highest level of mobilization.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This assembly is unlawful. You are ordered to disperse.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: More than 1,000 responded. We were there as police cleared the crowd.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is starting to physically move in, in formation. They`re clearing this area.\par \par \uc2 There are reports that several officers were injured as some nearby businesses had to evacuate their stores.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY MADDREY (New York City Police Chief): At this time, we have successfully dispersed the park. We still have crowds mulling around Manhattan. And we have officers following them, preventing them from destroying property.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Cenat was later seen getting into the back of a police SUV. It is unclear where he is right now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: The situation here was extremely tense at times.\par \par \uc2 We understand that Cenat did not have a permit for the event and is currently being questioned by authorities -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lana Zak, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, one of the deadliest shootings in American history was reenacted at a school in Parkland, Florida. Nearly 140 live rounds were fired inside \uc2 Marjory Stoneman Douglas High School\uc2  as part of a civil lawsuit against the officer who was supposed to protect the school that day.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez spoke to some of the victims` families on why they wanted this day to happen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): Ballistics experts spent hours inside the school`s 1200 building shooting live rounds that would mimic the sounds heard during the 2018 mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 Eerie, painful, but necessary, says Tony Montalto, whose daughter Gina was killed.\par \par \uc2 Why a reenactment?\par \par \uc2 TONY MONTALTO (President, Stand With Parkland): The reenactment is an attempt to disprove the falsehoods that were mentioned during the trial of the failed deputy, Scot Peterson.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Details of the reenactment included setting off a fire alarm that blared during the shooting, retracing the shooter`s footsteps along the second and third floors, and firing up to 139 rounds, the number fired that day, all to see what it may have sounded like outside, where surveillance cameras captured former school resource officer Scot Peterson a golf cart and later taking cover outside the building on his police radio for more than 40 minutes.\par \par \uc2 He did not go inside to try to stop the shooter, who killed 17 people.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The defendant is not guilty.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: A jury in June found him not guilty of several charges stemming from his response to the shooting in a separate criminal trial. His attorneys argued he could not tell where the shots were coming from due to echoes.\par \par \uc2 TONY MONTALTO: He is still guilty of running away from our children. He is still guilty from running away from the students and teachers who depended on him for protection.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: It is unclear at this point whether the judge will allow the reenactment to be used as evidence during the upcoming civil trial. But it had to be done now, because the building is set to be demolished -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Manuel Bojorquez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There`s an update on the federal case concerning former President Donald Trump`s handling of classified documents. Three additional criminal charges were filed last week. And, today, Trump submitted a not guilty plea and does not plan to appear in person next week for his arraignment on those charges, which include trying to destroy surveillance video.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, Trump pleaded not guilty to the most recent indictment, charges stemming from his efforts to overturn the 2020 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Police in the Gilgo Beach investigation have identified a possible seventh victim using DNA technology.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Shanelle Kaul reports, investigators have not confirmed a link between the victim and suspect Rex Heuermann, who has been charged with the killing of three other women.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL (voiceover): For 27 years, she was known only as Fire Island Jane Doe. Today, her true name.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND TIERNEY (Suffolk County, New York, District Attorney): We were able to identify Fire Island Jane Doe as Karen Vergata, who was 34 years old at the time of her disappearance.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Suffolk County district attorney Ray Tierney says Vergata lived in New York City and worked as an escort before disappearing on Valentine`s Day in 1996.\par \par \uc2 Her remains were found in two locations near Gilgo Beach. Last year, police used DNA testing to confirm her identity.\par \par \uc2 DISTRICT ATTORNEY RAYMOND TIERNEY: Using a buccal swab from a relative.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: The identification comes after Rex Heuermann was arrested last month and charged with the murders of three victims. He`s pleaded not guilty. He`s also suspected of murdering a fourth woman, but authorities won`t say today if he`s linked to Vergata.\par \par \uc2 DISTRICT ATTORNEY RAYMOND TIERNEY: It`s important to note that there are no charges at this time.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Vergata is among 11 people found dead near Gilgo Beach. Most were escorts.\par \par \uc2 Criminal defense attorney Joe Tamburino says, there could be a connection.\par \par \uc2 JOE TAMBURINO (Attorney and Legal Analyst): And this could be a pattern, and possibly his first one. Ms. Vergata is the same type of victim that we see in the other cases.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: And, this week, prosecutors asked for a court-ordered DNA sample from Rex Heuermann, hoping to determine whether or not he`s connected to any of the other bodies found here near Gilgo Beach -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Shanelle Kaul, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating a cyberattack targeting hospitals and clinics in at least four states Thursday, forcing several emergency rooms to shut down. \uc2 Prospect Medical Holdings\uc2  says it took its computer systems offline after learning it had been breached by hackers. The problem also caused urgent care and elective surgery centers to close and ambulances to be diverted.\par \par \uc2 The systems are not expected to be online until next week.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department reports the U.S. added 187,000 jobs in the month of July, which was less than expected. One possible reason for the slowdown was the entertainment industry, with thousands of writers and actors out of work and on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jonathan Vigliotti reports on the wider economic impact, as the WGA strike nears day 100.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Union!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Power!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Union!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Power!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Union!\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): Hollywood writers and actors hit the pavement yet again, as the writers union met with studio executives for the very first time since their strike began.\par \par \uc2 CHAD COE (\uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  Member): I think it`s hopeful, because it`s been -- it`s been crickets, it`s been silenced for a long time.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: In California, film and TV production accounts for more than 700,000 jobs and nearly $70 billion in wages.\par \par \uc2 KONSTANTINE ANTHONY (D-Mayor of Burbank, California): We are really fighting for the rights of the people who are working and living in the city.\par \par \uc2 Yeah!\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Konstantine Anthony wears many hats. He`s an actor and the mayor of Burbank, home to several major studios.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR KONSTANTINE ANTHONY: If people aren`t coming to work, if people are on strike, they`re not spending money at their local grocery store. All of those secondary industries are greatly affected by the loss of that income.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: That includes Alex Uceda`s catering company, typically feeding Hollywood`s film and TV crews.\par \par \uc2 ALEX UCEDA (CEO, Alex Catering Corporation): At the end of last year, we were, like, working, like, 10, 11 jobs every day. It drops to maybe one or two jobs.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: He says he`s had to lay off nearly half of his 50 employees.\par \par \uc2 Are you able to give me a sense, percentage-wise, how much business you have lost?\par \par \uc2 ALEX UCEDA: I have to -- can we say at least 70 percent.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Corporate greed has got to go!\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Picket lines grew last month as the actors began to strike as well. Top stars have donated $1 million each to help others pay their bills.\par \par \uc2 For those who depend on Hollywood to make a living, today`s talks bring a glimmer of hope.\par \par \uc2 ALEX UCEDA: I beg all the people from the studio, please, please make it happen, you know, for the good of everyone.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And many fall television shows and films are already delayed.\par \par \uc2 Even if a deal is reached, it could take several weeks, if not longer, to ramp up production, John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jonathan Vigliotti.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 Turning to the invasion of Ukraine, a Russian navy ship was apparently damaged overnight in what Ukraine says was a drone strike. Ukraine says this video shows a sea drone approaching the Russian ship at a port in the Black Sea. The video drops out on impact. Another unverified video shows the ship listing to its side as it`s being towed away.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of baby monitors are being recalled over the potential risk of burns. What parents need to know -- next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now an important consumer alert about baby monitors.\par \par \uc2 Philips Avent is recalling nearly 13,000 digital video baby monitors because the lithium-ion batteries can overheat, posing a risk of burns or property damage. About two dozen have overheated, with several causing minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 The recall impacts models SCD630 and SCD843 made between March 2016 and December 2019. Customers should contact Philips Avent for a free replacement.\par \par \uc2 Taylor Swift shares a special moment with the 6-year-old daughter of the late basketball legend Kobe Bryant. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taylor Swift`s concert)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: If you can`t watch the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, you can listen, subscribe wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We want to share a heartwarming moment from Taylor Swift`s concert in Los Angeles from last night.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taylor Swift`s concert, from Vanessa Bryant via \uc2 Instagram\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Swift gave her -- the hat off her head and a big hug to 6- year-old Bianka Bryant, the daughter of the late basketball legend Kobe Bryant, who, along with his daughter Gianna and seven others, died in a helicopter crash in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Bryant`s widow, Vanessa, posted an image of a jacket featuring a special moment between Swift and Kobe Bryant on stage back when he tipped his hat to her for a record 16 sold-out shows at the Staples Center.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next, with a wedding day for the ages and the community that saved the day.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Finally, tonight, from the ashes of ruin comes a story of true love.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from A New Odyssey)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Bride and groom Elizabeth and Jake Landuyt say their wedding was like a fairy tale.\par \par \uc2 PRIEST: You may kiss your bride.\par \par \uc2 JAKE LANDUYT (Groom): Everything we could have dreamed of until --\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH LANDUYT (Bride): My dad was doing his father of the bride speech, and, just a minute in, he was interrupted by some of our guests.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from A New Odyssey)\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: House on fire.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And that --\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Which house?\par \par \uc2 was the end of that.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: What`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: As we first reported a couple years ago, the cottage right next to their wedding venue on Mackinac Island, Michigan, caught fire, and everyone had to evacuate the area.\par \par \uc2 This is a picture of the newly-fleds abandoning their reception.\par \par \uc2 JAKE LANDUYT: I just figured we had to walk away from that.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH LANDUYT: So, we just started heading towards the church.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: The church where they had just been married. This time, they prayed for everyone`s safety. And in the end, no one was hurt, and even the building was saved.\par \par \uc2 It seemed like the only thing that couldn`t be salvaged was their wedding day. But, unbeknownst to the bride and groom, while they were in that church praying, angels were swooping in from all over town.\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: We needed to step up and do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: First, the chef at the venue took all 120 meals to a restaurant next door.\par \par \uc2 MAN #6: We just cooked it, sauced it, and off and down the street it went.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Down the street to a resort that had an event space available.\par \par \uc2 MAN #7: And we started just pulling everything that we had.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And what they didn`t have, yet another restaurant provided.\par \par \uc2 MAN #8: So, we got it on a cart and pushed it down Main Street.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And because of everyone`s efforts --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s going to be great.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: -- in less than an hour, the bride was back to blushing.\par \par \uc2 And what did you charge for this help?\par \par \uc2 MAN #8: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 MAN #6: I didn`t charge them anything.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 MAN #7: No.\par \par \uc2 JAKE LANDUYT: To have them pick up a reception, like, out of ashes in a very literal sense made the wedding better than we ever could have imagined.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Two years later, they are now one person more. Last month, Elizabeth, Jake, and baby Owen returned to Mackinac Island to celebrate their anniversary, this time without the dramatics, but with some perspective.\par \par \uc2 Dare I say you are glad it worked out the way it did?\par \par \uc2 I caught up with the family over Zoom.\par \par \uc2 JAKE LANDUYT: I don`t know if I would -- if I would quite use the word glad.\par \par \uc2 But that was one day, one part of our life journey, and now we have a much more important part now.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Adorable evidence that, even when life turns on you, it often turns back.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman, CBS News, On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. For Norah O`Donnell, I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good night, and have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230805ej8400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for July 20, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Kris Van Cleave, Jamie Yuccas, Mark Strassmann,  Charlie D`Agata, Mike Bettes, Carter Evans, Lilia Luciano \par \uc2 3,686 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, our new reporting on the suspected Gilgo Beach serial killer, as the search for more victims expands to at least four states. Plus, CBS News has learned police are looking into whether at least one woman at least one woman killed inside the suspect`s Long Island home.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 The suspect`s wife files for divorce, as investigators say she inadvertently helped with his arrest.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND TIERNEY (Suffolk County, New York, District Attorney): We didn`t get a whole lot of sleep, because we were worried that our investigation was going to come to light.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Hospitals face an influx of patients, as the heat wave expands. Tonight, how to keep your home cool.\par \par \uc2 The breaking news, a driver allegedly hits pedestrians here in Washington, D.C. We have the details.\par \par \uc2 Strike averted on Broadway, but actors and writers remain on strike. How it`s hurting the livelihoods of people beyond Tinseltown.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HARRIS (Production Sound Mixer): All the work that I used to do is gone, because none of that can exist without \uc2 SAG\uc2  actors and WGA scripts.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: One lucky Southern California Powerball player is a billion dollars richer.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: The winning ticket was sold at a convenience store in downtown Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN BECKER (Spokesperson, California Lottery): We`re standing here today wondering if the person who bought the ticket even realizes it yet.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Two baby Mexican wolves flew in from New York, they`re a highly endangered species. The Wildlife Service has 24 hours to get them into a den here in the forest, where they can start life in the wild.\par \par \uc2 It`s a hard life?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DICKS (\uc2 U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service\uc2 ): It`s a very hard life.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: She`s America`s most experienced astronaut. We go Person to Person with Peggy Whitson to talk about shooting for the stars.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY WHITSON (NASA Astronaut): I might be a little bit addicted to space.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: I think you`re a space junkie.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY WHITSON: I think maybe that`s true.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin tonight with stunning new details in the Gilgo Beach murders on New York`s Long Island. Law enforcement sources tell CBS News that the investigation has spread to at least four states, including New York, New Jersey, South Carolina, and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Police are trying to determine if the suspect, 59-year-old Rex Heuermann, is connected to any additional unsolved murders, including a wave of killings in Atlantic City in 2006. Well, now that investigators have his DNA in a statewide database, other law enforcement agencies can run his information against open cases.\par \par \uc2 Last Friday, Heuermann was charged in the murders of three women and is the prime suspect in the death of a fourth. He pleaded not guilty to all charges. Investigators are combing through his Massapequa Park home inch by inch for evidence. And police in New York are now in possession of an SUV from South Carolina that may be connected to at least one of the murders.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lilia Luciano is going to start us off tonight from outside the suspect`s home.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Rex\uc2 , did you do it?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voiceover): Tonight, the investigation into the Gilgo Beach murders spreads nationwide.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned police are looking into whether one or more of the three murders Rex Heuermann is charged with may have taken place inside the Long Island home he shared with his wife and children. His family was apparently away at the time of those murders.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND TIERNEY (Suffolk County, New York, District Attorney): There is absolutely no evidence that -- that anyone acted with -- with the defendant, much less his family.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Law enforcement officials also tell CBS News investigators are looking to see if Heuermann is connected to other unsolved murders throughout the county and the country.\par \par \uc2 Suffolk County officials have executed several warrants, including in South Carolina and Las Vegas, where the alleged killer had ties. Police in South Carolina are looking into whether a 2014 missing person case could be tied to Heuermann as well; 18-year-old Aaliyah Bell went missing about 20 miles from where Heuermann owned land, but, so far, police have found no connection.\par \par \uc2 Investigators are searching the property for evidence. And it`s where they found a Chevy Avalanche, which may have been used in at least one of the three killings.\par \par \uc2 KIM TOLAND (Waiter, Gene`s Restaurant): It makes you stop and think about the seriousness of what it -- goes on in the world today.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Long Island investigators are actively working with Atlantic City police to determine if Heuermann is involved in a string of unsolved killings of young women.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND TIERNEY: As it stands right now, these charges are just allegations. But we look very much forward to proving them in court.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Heuermann`s wife of almost 30 years has filed for divorce.\par \par \uc2 Her attorney tells CBS News she and their adult children are all in shock, that they were told to leave the home where they lived with the clothes on their back and haven`t been allowed back since, as the search continues more than a week later -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Lilia Luciano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn now to the unrelenting heat dome engulfing much of the nation that shows no signs of letting up. More than 125 million Americans are under these heat alerts tonight in more than a dozen states.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): As the unrelenting heat wave enters its 40th consecutive day, the temperature is expected to reach a record-setting 119 in Palm Springs, where hiking trails are closed, but road crews are still on the job.\par \par \uc2 The pavement they`re working on is 142 degrees. Jose Sanchez (sp?) figures he will need to drink at least two gallons of water to stay hydrated.\par \par \uc2 JOSE SANCHEZ (Construction Worker): For the first four hours, you`re OK, but then, the last four hours, you got to take a lot of breaks.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: No kids on the playground today, and you can understand why. The temperature here on the slide is 171 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Further north, in Death Valley, a 71-year-old hiker died after collapsing during his visit in the 121-degree heat. Meanwhile, in Phoenix, temperatures are staying in the triple digits, flirting with a new daily record. And heat-related E.R. visits are on the rise in the Southwest, up 134 percent from last week.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, the heat`s killed 18 people in Arizona`s Maricopa County, the heat straining the power grid. Energy bills are predicted to jump almost 12 percent this summer.\par \par \uc2 Just outside Dallas, A.C. repairman Jonathan Momreal (sp?) can only answer about four to six calls a day. Today, he`s fixing a unit in a 111-degree attic.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MOMREAL (A.C. Repairman): Sometimes, you do kind of pat yourself on the shoulder and say, that`s a good job.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And nobody`s dining outside today here in downtown Palm Springs. They`re trying to stay inside to stay cool.\par \par \uc2 And to keep the temperatures down in your home, of course, close the shades, turn on the ceiling fan, circulate that air, and also get rid of those old incandescent light bulbs. They throw off a lot of heat -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s good information.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Carter.\par \par \uc2 For the forecast and an explanation what`s behind this record-breaking heat, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Norah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 The record-setting heat wave we`re experiencing across the South only intensifies and expands now through the weekend. Dozens of cities from Florida all the way to California will experience record-setting high and low temperatures. So, now the question is, why? Why is it so hot in so many places?\par \par \uc2 Well, you can blame this right here, high-pressure heat dome that`s been parked over the Southwest for the better part of a month now. And it really doesn`t move over the course of the weekend and next week. The jet stream has gone well to the north. In fact, that high pressure only re-intensifies next week.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at the outlook through the end of the month, most of the country remains well above average for temperatures. For a place like Phoenix, the takeaway is this forecast, temperatures 110 or higher through July. Norah, what that tells us is, Phoenix is likely to set its record for the warmest month in city history.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Chris (sic), thank you.\par \par \uc2 There`s some breaking news just coming in, explosive news about now former Washington Commander`s owner Dan Snyder. The \uc2 NFL\uc2  says that Snyder has agreed to pay a $60 million fine after an independent investigation found he sexually harassed Tiffani Johnson. That`s a former cheerleader and team marketing manager.\par \par \uc2 Investigators found Snyder oversaw executives who deliberately withheld at least $11 million in revenue from other teams. The news broke this evening minutes after \uc2 NFL\uc2  owners unanimously approved the sale of the team to a group led by businessman Josh Harris which includes \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend Magic Johnson.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the war in Ukraine, Russia is continuing its assault on Southern Ukraine and targeting grain facilities, threatening food supplies in countries that are facing hunger.\par \par \uc2 We get more from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): Russian cruise missiles and drones have turned Ukraine`s Black Sea ports into a battlefield, engulfing whole neighborhoods in a raging inferno of twisted metal and rubble, the third night in a row of some of the heaviest bombardment the cities of Odesa and Mykolaiv have seen since the start of the invasion.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin calls it retaliation for the attack on the bridge to Crimea, but timed to coincide with Russia`s pullout of an agreement allowing safe passage of Ukrainian grain from these very ports. Ukraine says strikes on a grain terminal destroyed 60,000 tons of grain, enough to feed 300,000 people.\par \par \uc2 Moscow now threatening that any ship caught passing through those waters will be considered a legitimate military target, and, in another emerging development, the apparent appearance of Wagner chief \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  in neighboring Belarus addressing his forces, trash-talking Russian front-line troops as a disgrace, and hinting that, when the time comes --\par \par \uc2 (YEVGENY PRIGOZHIN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: -- he and his fighters will return to the battlefields of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (YEVGENY PRIGOZHIN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And, tonight, Norah, U.S. officials confirm that Ukrainian forces have begun using those controversial U.S.-supplied cluster munitions for the first time.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There was a scary scene here in D.C. today. Police are now investigating a deadly crash in a parking garage at \uc2 MedStar Georgetown University Hospital\uc2 . One woman was killed and five other people were injured when a car hit a group of pedestrians. The injured are expected to recover. One official said there is no indication that the crash was intentional.\par \par \uc2 The lights of Broadway will continue to shine after a last-minute deal between producers and the backstage workers union. The agreement avoids another major strike, with Hollywood actors and writers continuing to walk the picket line.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann reports, the shutdown of the industry, which supports nearly 2.5 million jobs, is already being felt beyond the screen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: No contracts!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: No actors!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): In Hollywood, striking the set has a whole new meaning, with repercussions rippling 2,200 miles away in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 This is decades of stuff, right?\par \par \uc2 BILLY BIGGAR (Biggar Antiques): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Billy Biggar`s family-owned prop house services the Hollywood of the South. It`s jam-packed with movie-making esoterica for rent, millions of items.\par \par \uc2 BILLY BIGGAR: Lots of fake food.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Gathering dust, unneeded, with quiet on every set here.\par \par \uc2 BILLY BIGGAR: It`s hard to keep upbeat.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Is business as bad as it can be?\par \par \uc2 BILLY BIGGAR: Yeah. When your clients are shut down, you`re shut down.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Avengers: Infinity War, \uc2 Walt Disney Studios\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Of Hollywood`s six highest domestic grossing movies, four were shot in Georgia, "Black Panther."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Black Panther: Wakanda Forever, \uc2 Walt Disney Studios\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Two Avengers movies.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Avengers: End Game, \uc2 Walt Disney Studios\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And "Spider-Man: No Way Home."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Spider-Man: No Way Home, \uc2 Sony Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: All featured this peach in their closing credits, $3.5 billion in annual wages and more than 46,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just Georgia, billions in wages for workers in states like North Carolina, Florida and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Chris Harris is an Atlanta production sound mixer. His union`s not on strike, but he`s not working either.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HARRIS (Production Sound Mixer): It`s just that all the work that I used to do is gone, because none of that can exist without \uc2 SAG\uc2  actors and WGA scripts.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Billy Biggar`s prop house also can`t exist without them.\par \par \uc2 BILLY BIGGAR: If it lasts until December, it`s going to get hairy for a lot of people, including myself.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And define hairy.\par \par \uc2 BILLY BIGGAR: Financially.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: So, from prop houses to major local studios like Tyler Perry`s behind me, Georgia`s moviemaking business is feeling it.\par \par \uc2 In Biggar`s case, he says the family business is on the line with these two Hollywood strikes -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Quite a ripple effect.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, from the Hollywood strike to a Hollywood ending for one lucky Powerball player. The only winning ticket worth more than $1 billion was sold in the City of Dreams, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jamie Yuccas spoke to the owner of the store where the odds were in one person`s favor.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS (voiceover): Once word got out that this downtown Los Angeles mini market sold the ticket, the excitement couldn`t be contained.\par \par \uc2 ANGELICA MENJIVAR (Sold Winning Lottery Ticket): Wow. It`s unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: The store`s owners, a mother and daughter, claimed their million-dollar check and immediately turned around and started selling more lottery tickets.\par \par \uc2 Is this life-changing?\par \par \uc2 ANGELICA MENJIVAR: No, it`s same thing. For us, no change. Life just keep going. We are like a normal, any other person, so we keep going. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 GLENDA FLORES (Business Owner): From now on, every day --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLENDA FLORES: -- I`m going to go and buy there.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: As for the winner, they have a year to come forward. And many wait once learning their names are made public.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Inside Edition)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: However, that didn`t stop one woman from showing up in front of all those cameras, claiming to have hit the jackpot.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Oh, God bless you! Thank you!\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Another called the store also claiming to be the winner.\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN BECKER (Spokesperson, California Lottery): It would take us a while before we were able to say, yes, that person is indeed the winner. We won`t know for a while.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: In an only-in-America moment, the block this business sits on is just steps from the infamous Skid Row.\par \par \uc2 A number of homeless people frequent the store and many now wonder if one of them could be the winner -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Yuccas, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the extraordinary efforts being undertaken to bring back the once nearly extinct Mexican gray wolf to America`s Southwest. But it`s not without controversy, as ranchers fear their livelihoods are being threatened with more of their cattle being killed.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, CBS` Kris Van Cleave looks at the tensions that come with the growing wolf population.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): The race to save an endangered species has five newborn Mexican wolf pups on a nearly 2,500-mile journey.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Precious cargo first.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: From captivity in New York to New Mexico and the wild.\par \par \uc2 Veterinarian Susan Dicks:\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DICKS (\uc2 U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service\uc2 ): Time is trauma, and the very best place for a wolf pup to be is with a mother.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The Mexican wolf, or lobo, was plentiful in the Southwest, until it was hunted nearly to extinction. By the mid-1970s, there were just seven in existence.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DICKS: They are doing better and improving, but that`s a fine line. Disease comes through, something happens, they could be lost.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: There are now about 250 back in the wild, but a lack of genetic diversity makes rehoming pups from captivity necessary.\par \par \uc2 Is this wolf country?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MARKS (Rancher): Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: At Barbara Marks` family ranch in Blue, Arizona, wolves were a threat back in 1891. And she says they`re targeting her calves again now.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MARKS: The numbers have increased dramatically, so they have become more of an issue and more of a year-round issue.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Wildlife officials estimate about 100 cattle a year are lost to Mexican wolves. Marks opposed releasing them into the nearby national forest, but also knows her new neighbors are here to stay.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a baby Mexican wolf. He`s about 10 days old and very soon is going to meet his new mom in the wild.\par \par \uc2 But to get there required hiking through miles of difficult and prickly terrain to reach the wolf den. The wild pups are pulled out, given a health screening, and introduced to their new siblings.\par \par \uc2 BRADY MCGEE (\uc2 U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service\uc2 ): We have got them all mixed together, all the puppies smelling the same. And we put microchips in both of them and put them back in the den. When we walk away from it, the mom will come back.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: It would seem like to me that if you just sort of increased the size of a litter, that the wolf would notice.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DICKS: We don`t think they can count, but they will care for pups whether or not they`re theirs.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: An endangered symbol of the Old West seeking a new life.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Kris Van Cleave, Reserve, New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Could tornado damage at a major \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  plant make America`s drug shortage worse?\par \par \uc2 That story`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is assessing the damage from a tornado that ripped through its pharmaceutical plant in Rocky Mount, North Carolina, on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The EF-3 tornado, with 150-mile-an-hour winds, tore open and collapsed the roof, scattering debris. There were no serious injuries, but there is concern that it could make America`s drug shortage worse. The plant, which employs more than 2,000 workers, produces drugs that are injected through an I.V., including anesthesia and other medications for surgery.\par \par \uc2 A deadly explosion rocks a major international city. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Officials in South Africa are investigating a tremendous, deadly explosion that rattled the city of Johannesburg Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 You can see that it tossed heavy vehicles into the air and blasted a hole in the road, sending people running. One man was killed and nearly 50 people were injured. Officials suspect it was a gas explosion.\par \par \uc2 Our interview with America`s most experienced astronaut next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Today marks the 54th anniversary of the first moon landing, a moment that Peggy Whitson says inspired her to follow in their historic footsteps.\par \par \uc2 Now she is America`s most experienced astronaut.\par \par \uc2 In our new episode of Person to Person, we sat down with Whitson to discuss her record-breaking career and what inspired her dream of working among the stars.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PEGGY WHITSON (NASA Astronaut): I was 9 years old when the first astronauts walked on the moon, and, at the time, I thought, cool job. I want to do this, too.\par \par \uc2 I was a farm kid in an Iowa, and I was -- I never really told anybody about it because it seemed so unreal to me. And, coincidentally, when I graduated high school was the first year \uc2 NASA\uc2  picked the female astronauts.\par \par \uc2 I`m like, maybe this is possible for me to become an astronaut. And I try and tell young people, it`s so important to take advantage of the opportunities you are given along your path, because getting there isn`t always a straight line.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: She was rejected four times from \uc2 NASA\uc2 . Look at her now.\par \par \uc2 You can see more of our Person to Person interview tonight streaming on the CBS News App at 9:30 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230725ej7k000p2\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For July 16, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Paul Goodloe, Chris Livesay, Danya Bacchus, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,041 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 July 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Millions around the world experience firsthand the effects of a warming planet. And most of Hollywood goes on strike, yet another blow to our economy.\par \par \uc2 A brutal and persistent heat wave has nearly a third of Americans living under a heat alert, many of them confronting temperatures that are downright dangerous. We will examine the impact on the U.S. and the rest of the world also suffering from the devastating temperatures.\par \par \uc2 Mesa, Arizona, has been particularly hard-hit. Mayor John Giles will tell us how his city is coping.\par \par \uc2 Then: President Biden is back from a trip to Eastern Europe, where he rallied America`s \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies to stand together against \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We will not waver. We will not waver.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But he`s back home and faced with new developments from other foreign adversaries, another provocative missile test in North Korea, new tensions with Iran, and more cyberattacks out of China.\par \par \uc2 We will sort through those challenges with White House National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan and check in with the chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee, Michael McCaul.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, one of America`s marquee industries, entertainment, is at a virtual standstill, as actors join writers on strike, hitting the picket line together for the first time in 60 years to demand higher pay and increased job security at a time of profound technological and social change.\par \par \uc2 We will ask media executive \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2  about the ripple effects on the broader economy. And we will hear from tech reporter Kara Swisher on the upheaval in the entertainment world, the tech industry and beyond.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot to get to this morning, but we want to begin in with some of the extreme weather we`re seeing at home and abroad. There are climate shocks across the country with flooding in some parts and a record-breaking heat wave in others.\par \par \uc2 CBS News correspondent Danya Bacchus is in Los Angeles with more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS (voice-over): In the Southwest, a heat dome is intensifying.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s like I`m sitting in a baker, and I feel like I`m going to get toasted.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Phoenix, which has been above 110 degrees for 16 days, hit a record high Saturday of 118. Las Vegas could see 117 today. And Death Valley, one of the hottest places on Earth, is on track to hit 130, the blistering heat attracting visitors.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I have never experienced this kind of heat.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: The extreme heat and dry conditions sparked multiple fires in Southern California, burning thousands of acres and prompting evacuation orders.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, beachgoers are finding it hard to cool off. Water temperatures there have been reaching above 90 degrees. Wildfires in Canada are causing smoke to once again blanket portions of the Midwest, bringing air quality alerts to Minnesota and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 On the East Coast, Vermont is in a state of emergency following flooding. Flash flooding also took the lives of three people in Bucks County, Pennsylvania, Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Southern California fire crews worked throughout the night.\par \par \uc2 And, Margaret, they remain on high alert, as the peak of this Southwest heat wave is expected to hit today.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Danya Bacchus for us from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 For a look at how the rest of the world is coping, we turn to CBS News foreign correspondent Chris Livesay, who filed this report from Rome.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Well, the heat wave is named after the three-headed dog that guards the underworld in Greek mythology. And with temperatures set to rise even further, it has its jaws firmly locked on Southern Europe.\par \par \uc2 (voice-over): People have been collapsing under the weight of the heat wave Cerberus.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: For two days, Greek officials were forced to close the ancient Acropolis during the most scorching hours to prevent further injuries.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We put on 60 SPF, so we`re good. We have our water. No complaints.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: It`s really hard. I have got asthma, so this heat is terrible for it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: In Italy, 16 cities have been placed under a red alert for the extreme heat. Some regions in the South may even exceed 120 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Surface temperatures are sizzling even hotter. In Spain, thermal imaging resembles the sun, as ground temperatures reach a blistering 140 degrees. Forest fires ripped through the Spanish island of La Palma, destroying homes and displacing hundreds, all this while East Asia is being inundated. Mudslides and torrential rain in South Korea have left dozens dead and a climbing death toll.\par \par \uc2 In one incident, a freeway tunnel was flooded, leaving many drivers trapped.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Indeed, temperatures have been rising steadily for years.\par \par \uc2 The highest temperature ever recorded in Europe was right here in Italy, just a hair under 120 degrees, only two years ago. The fear is that this year and years to come could be even worse -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Chris Livesay from Rome.\par \par \uc2 We now want to go to \uc2 Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist Paul Goodloe from Atlanta, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Paul, are we near the end of this heat streak?\par \par \uc2 PAUL GOODLOE (Meteorologist, \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ): Not at all, not even in Europe, also not in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this afternoon, temperatures in Europe already upper 90s, even some temperatures above 100 degrees, and then, in the U.S., the Southeast sultry, the East sultry, stormy in the Northeast. The Southwest is broiling here, and the heat building in the Pacific Northwest, the interior as well.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s Phoenix, yet another day at or above 110 degrees. This streak started way back in June, but it really continues in earnest this month. In fact, our streak is at 16 days. The old record is 18 days. We`re at least going to have 21 days or more. And in terms of the end, it`s nowhere in sight, the forecast from this month into September likely above average, well above average in the Southwest for -- yes, at least for the rest of this summer -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re seeing reports of some kind of unusual things, like, in New England today, a warning of a tornado watch. I grew up there. I don`t remember that ever happening really before.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing the federal government and \uc2 NOAA\uc2  say that June was a record heat level, with sea temperatures the highest for any month on record. What is the global impact of all these changes?\par \par \uc2 PAUL GOODLOE: Well, it`s all about the extra energy we`re having here and changing and impacting our weather, which also is impacting our climate.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned temperatures. The waters around Florida, the sea surface waters, we`re talking not only bathwater, but hot tub water-type temperatures here. You don`t really get much relief in the water, it is so warm. And that plays into what happens in our atmosphere. The water is warming, so it`s adjusting the temperature above that water, adding more humidity.\par \par \uc2 For every one degree temperature increase, we can see 4 percent more water vapor, heavier snow events, but also heavier rain events. It doesn`t mean we still can`t have long, persistent droughts. It`s the long-term adding energy or heat in our atmosphere which is fueling wilder extremes.\par \par \uc2 And we`re seeing this also with rainfall, not only today in the Northeast, but also last week. A one in 100-year flood events doesn`t mean you only see one in 100 years. It`s the probability of any given year of 1 percent seeing that type of rainfall. But guess what? We`re seeing more of that.\par \par \uc2 In fact, more of -- days seeing two inches or more of rain have steadily increased since 1950. We`re seeing that right now with flooding in the Northeast. And, by the way, it`s not just here. Every region of the country has seen an increase in our heavy rain events, leading to more wilder flood events.\par \par \uc2 We have the concern for severe weather, including flash flooding going on, widespread flood alerts going on across the entire region, because their energy is just that supercharged. And, by the way, the two things we talked about this morning, heat and flooding, those are the two biggest weather killers.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I know things like tornadoes, even hurricanes, they have a lot of headlines, but the day-to-day occurrences of heavy rain and now the super long heat events we`re dealing with across the country and the globe are killing a lot more people -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Paul, wow. That is sobering. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And we turn now to John Giles. He`s the Republican mayor of Mesa, Arizona, a city about 20 miles east of Phoenix at the epicenter of this heat wave.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Giles, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 I imagine it`s always challenging to manage a city in the middle of a desert. But with this record streak of heat, what -- what is the impact on people`s health and the community?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GILES (R-Mayor of Mesa, Arizona): Well, good -- good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 We absolutely are used to high temperatures in the Phoenix area and in Mesa, but -- but this is unusual. Due to some of the, I guess, global conditions that you just talked about earlier, we aren`t experiencing the normal rainstorms that we usually see this time of July in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re used to several days over 110, even -- even days in the high teens. But this is unusual because of the weeks-long duration of it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And so what is the short-term or future impact that you are planning for?\par \par \uc2 You mentioned rain.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What does that mean for your water supply?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: Well, water is actually always an issue in the desert, obviously.\par \par \uc2 And then so we -- Arizona, believe it or not, is -- really leads the nation when it comes to water conservation. This is the only place in the country where you have to prove that you have a 100-year guaranteed water supply before you can build anything. So that is always on our minds.\par \par \uc2 And we are continuing to see challenges with the Colorado River. Thankfully, we`ve got other water sources that we rely on. So we feel good about our water future, although that`s something that we`re very -- still working very hard on.\par \par \uc2 Temperatures, we do have a long-term plan for that as well. We have short- term plans. We have a lot of cooling stations. Every year, we do a big hydration donation campaign in Mesa, where we collect about several hundred thousand of these, so that we`re prepared for helping those who are outside, don`t have the resources to be inside during the hot temperatures.\par \par \uc2 And then, long term, we, of course, have climate action plans, but any climate action plan in a place like Mesa has got to take into account heat mitigation to be something that is taken seriously. So, for example, in Mesa, we have launched a campaign earlier this year to plant a million new trees in our community...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: ... to create more shade. And, also, we`re investing in transportation infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you going to have to slow development -- I mean, do you know at this point -- in terms of more construction because of the strain on infrastructure and resources? And how is the electrical grid faring?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: Electricity is good.\par \par \uc2 We have two major electric companies in the Phoenix area, \uc2 Salt River Project\uc2 , \uc2 Arizona Public Service\uc2 , Tucson Power down in Tucson. And we -- so far, we have not seen any brownouts or any hint that -- that the grid is going -- is going to be an issue or the wholesale electricity production is a problem.\par \par \uc2 And, believe it or not, the irony here is that the Phoenix area is really in a -- in a -- in an economic boom right now. Places like Mesa, we`ve got \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2  all coming in making multibillion-dollar investments, bringing a lot of great jobs. So the heat and the -- the weather, frankly, is the reason these companies are coming to Mesa and Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 Hurricanes, blizzards, tornadoes, mudslides, we just don`t do that here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: So you do have to tough out, you know, some -- some -- some high temperatures in the middle of July. But, other than that, the weather is actually a bonus for us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, when you were here in Washington with us last on Face the Nation, we talked a bit about your concerns related to migration.\par \par \uc2 And you said at the time that -- that the city does get overwhelmed. You`re not right on the border, but you deal with the impact. I know Border Patrol says there has been a -- that the number of apprehensions has gone down in the past few months. Are you seeing that? And how is the extreme heat impacting people`s movement?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: Well, the heat absolutely is an issue for people who are attempting to cross the border, particularly those that are migrating from Central America, so that is something we`re very concerned about.\par \par \uc2 And the Border Patrol and humanitarian groups along the border are constantly, every day, rescuing people that are in life-threatening situations. So -- so that`s very concerning. The last time I talked with you, we were very nervous about the impacts of Title 42 and what would happen with that transition.\par \par \uc2 In the past, we`ve -- we`ve been -- every time there`s a surge at the border, communities like Mesa are heavily impacted, because our resources are called upon when the federal government`s resources are not enough. We have been so far so good, though, when it comes to the Title 42 situation. But, again, we remain very concerned and are looking for a long-term solution there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Mr. Mayor, good luck to you faring in this heat. Thanks for sharing your insights.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in a minute, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The gears of America`s entertainment industry ground to a near-complete halt last week when the union that represents actors, \uc2 SAG- AFTRA\uc2 , joined writers on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 A disclosure: Some CBS News staff are \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  members, but work under a different contract than the actors and are not affected by this strike.\par \par \uc2 But to understand who is impacted here, we are joined by \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2 , a former movie studio head who`s currently the chairman of \uc2 IAC\uc2  and \uc2 Expedia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2  (Chairman and Senior Executive, \uc2 IAC\uc2  and \uc2 Expedia Group\uc2 ): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, we were trying to gauge the economic impact of this.\par \par \uc2 And, according to the \uc2 Milken Institute\uc2 , it could cause $4 billion in economic damage. What do you think the impact will be? And how long will these strikes last?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Well, the problem with this particular -- all strikes get settled. The issue for this one is -- is when, because you have almost a perfect storm here, which is, you had COVID, which sent people home to watch streaming and television and killed theaters.\par \par \uc2 You -- you`ve had the results of huge investments in streaming, which have produced all these losses for all these companies who are now kind of retrenching. So, at this moment, this kind of perfect storm, it`s OK if it gets settled in the next month.\par \par \uc2 But I will posit what happens if it doesn`t. And there doesn`t seem to be enough trust and energy to get it settled soon. What will happen is, if in fact, it doesn`t get settled until Christmas or so, then, next year, there`s not going to be many programs for anybody to watch. So, you`re going to see subscriptions get pulled, which is going to reduce the revenue of all these movie companies, television companies, the result of which is that there will be no programs.\par \par \uc2 And at just the time, strike is settled, that you want to get back up, there won`t be enough money. So this actually will have devastating effects, if it is not settled soon. And the problem with settlement in this case is, there`s no trust between the parties. There are existential issues, obviously, A.I., which...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : ... I think is just overhyped to death in terms of the worries that actors and writers are going to be replaced, rather than assisted, which is what I think will happen.\par \par \uc2 So -- but there`s no trust. You have the actors union, saying, how dare these 10 people who run these companies earn all this money...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : ... and won`t pay us, while, if you look at it on the other side, the top 10 actors get paid more than the top 10 executives.\par \par \uc2 I`m not saying either is right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Actually, everybody`s probably overpaid at the top end.\par \par \uc2 The one idea I had is to say, as a good-faith measure, both the executives and the most-paid actors should take a 25 percent pay cut to try and narrow, narrow the difference between those who get highly paid and those that don`t.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, let...\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : But I...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to talk about what you just said in terms of...\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : No, no, the other...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : The only other thing I would do, I would call for a September 1 deadline.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : There`s a strike deadline. I think there should be a settlement deadline, because unless it happens by September 1, the actions -- and, you know, of course, who cares about Hollywood? Who cares about it?\par \par \uc2 But the truth is, this is a huge business...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : ... both domestically and for world ex -- for world export.\par \par \uc2 And if it -- if these conditions...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : I`m -- like, don`t sound like I`m crying to the skies, but these conditions will potentially produce an absolute collapse of an entire industry.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, and, also, they`re all the people who are paid by the hour who work on these sets who aren`t receiving pay when things are shut down. It`s not just people in front of the camera here.\par \par \uc2 But on your point about A.I. and existential threats here, Fran Drescher, the head of the union, said: We`re all going to be in jeopardy of being replaced by machines.\par \par \uc2 That`s not the only industry worried about that. If you were running a studio right now, what restrictions would you put on generative A.I.?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Right now, overly hyped, as all revolutions that are at the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 You know, the downward consequences of the beginning of a revolution, you know, it can be the Romanovs getting shot and Marie Antoinette losing her head. But, in this case, I think the one-to-three-year period, not much is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 But post that, there are, of course, all these issues. Now, by the way, these issues are not, I believe -- relative to some of the worries about replacement, I do not think you`re going to replace -- A.I.-generated actors. I don`t think you`re going to replace writers. Yes, you can ingest all this stuff and spit out something that sounds like Shakespeare, but guess what? It is not original Shakespeare.\par \par \uc2 And writers will get assisted, not replaced. Most of these actual performing crafts, I don`t think in danger -- are in danger of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the union wants some say in, I guess, crafting some guidelines on this.\par \par \uc2 I know A.I. impacts the publishing industry, because you have said that published content should be -- and somehow compensated for if it`s plugged into A.I. to program it. Are you going to seek to sue?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Yes. Well, actually, we are. A group of us, I think, are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : And it`s not that -- a group of us are.\par \par \uc2 It`s not that either \uc2 Google\uc2  or \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  that are the two real leaders of this, in terms of -- certainly, \uc2 Google\uc2  with having a monopoly on advertising. They too want to find a solution for publishers. The problem is, they also say that the fair use doctrine of the copyright law allows them to suck up all this stuff.\par \par \uc2 We, in the publishing side, do not agree with that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : It is -- it will be, long term, catastrophic if there is not a business model that allows people professionally to produce content.\par \par \uc2 That would be, I think everybody agrees, a catastrophe. The only way to get there is either legislation or litigation. I think litigation is not going to end up in some courtroom. But I think litigation will hopefully lead to sensible legislation here.\par \par \uc2 What has to happen is, copyright has to be -- copy -- unless you protect copyright, all is lost.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 So are you thinking of what -- like, the AP just came to an agreement with ChatGPT where they have to license AP`s archives of new stories in order to plug them into their model. Is that the future for publishers?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Yes. Yes, it`s probably -- yes, but that`s probably meaningless.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that is not going to solve anything. And, of course, it`s -- I understand AP and Chat -- look, ChatGPI -- what do -- GPT -- what do they want? They want to continue to suck all this stuff up. They want to, of course, say, we`re open to commercial agreements.\par \par \uc2 But, on the side of those people who are dependent upon advertising, \uc2 Google\uc2 , for instance, they say, yes, we`ll give you a revenue share. Right now, the revenue share is zero. So what percent of zero would you like today? I mean, that`s rational, but it`s not the point. The only way you get to the point is protect fair use, in other words, protect the copyright.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 Are you going to launch litigation soon? And who`s this group you referenced you`re with?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Well, it`s not fair for me to actually specify the group, other than -- other than it`s the leading -- it`s leading publishers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : And -- and, yes, we have to.\par \par \uc2 It -- it -- it`s not antagonistic. It`s to stake a firm place in the ground to say that you cannot ingest our material...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : ... without figuring out a business model for the future.\par \par \uc2 I will just give you one quick reference point. Twenty years ago, when the Internet began, the Internet was deemed free.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : And so everybody, all publishers said, OK, let`s put it all up for free. Everything`s great. Take it.\par \par \uc2 OK, it almost killed publishers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have got to wrap you here.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : All right? It took 15 -- it took 15 years to get back paywalls that protected publishers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, we`re...\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : I don`t think that same thing is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2 , I love having you.\par \par \uc2 Hope to have you back soon. Got to go.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 And we are continuing a theme with podcast host and tech watcher Kara Swisher of talking about how technology is really changing so many industries.\par \par \uc2 And you, of course, you`ve watched this.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER (Host, "On With Kara Swisher" Podcast And Co-Host, "Pivot" Podcast): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you`ve also interviewed \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2  before.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, many times.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Many, many times.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Dozens of times, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I was reading an interview you did with him back in 2019 and it almost forecast some of this -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because he said Hollywood`s irrelevant.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: He did. He said -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the rise of tech companies.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, I think he said, if their - if their children had teeth - their children wouldn`t have teeth because they`re so inbred and they don`t understand what`s happening. He had some sort of colorful \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2  metaphor about that.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, he was -- he`s been very prescient (ph) and honest. And that`s the reason I`ve known him for so long because he was one of the first people to contact me from Hollywood, and one of the only. There were just two, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2  and him, about the internet back in the early days. And everyone else thought it would go away. And I kept saying, it`s not going away and it`s going to keep changing. And that`s what`s happening now. And it continues. And so Hollywood continues to struggle with the implications of technology, even at this advanced stage.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that`s the streaming and digital ways of --\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: That`s the latest.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Using the pipes to get it out to consumers. But it`s also the content makers.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Right. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s - it`s that \uc2 Apple\uc2  makes content and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: That`s right. They`re competing with people with enormous amounts of resources and money that - and that Hollywood doesn`t compare to.\par \par \uc2 Last year I interviewed \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , and he even said \uc2 Disney\uc2  was too small. Think about that. Think about the idea that \uc2 Disney\uc2 , with its parks and its other - its characters, it`s IP, has - has a hard time competing with these tech companies that are getting better and better. Whether it`s \uc2 Apple\uc2  TV or its \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . You know, obviously, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  is really, you know, really plowed the field here and it`s now back again in a very strong position, as a - as a standalone company versus a Hollywood company.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The CEO of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , got some criticism certainly from the union -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: He did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: About his comments that this was just sort of the worst possible time to have this kind of negotiation -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because all of the strain on the industry.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, I think he`s right, but they don`t like that message, right? I think he`s probably right. At the same time he earns an enormous salary, so it`s easy to point to it and say, what a greedy man, that kind of thing. And I get that. And I think it`s important for them to talk about financials. But it almost has nothing to do with what`s going on in Hollywood right now, which is the streaming - the shift to streaming, which is necessary and important, is expensive. The economics aren`t worked out. They`ve overspent. There`s enormous competition between and among all the different companies. And nobody`s figured out how to pay for people.\par \par \uc2 Now, the actors are correct, is, they should get a piece of this. And figuring out whose value -- who values and who`s valuable is going to be very hard.\par \par \uc2 And -- but there - there is a real strain on these - on these companies at this moment in time. It doesn`t mean it`s not going to change, but it certainly is one of those sort of \uc2 Rubicon\uc2  moments that Hollywood faces from time to time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I`m - and I`m guessing that the -- giving everyone a 25 percent pay cut is not something that - that proposal from \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2  is not going to go anywhere?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: No. No. No, I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: But they`re right in like, what`s their value? Whose value is what and who contributes to what? And what happens over time, when you could use AI and all kinds of things to replicate things rather easily, including writers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what I want to ask you about is AI because this isn`t just the entertainment industry, this is every industry.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: No, everything.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: People are saying, am I going to be replaced by a robot?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Right. Right. Well, not a robot.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you know what I`m saying.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Maybe a robot.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Maybe a robot - well, anyway.\par \par \uc2 But on that point, how is the tech industry thinking about this? Because when you hear the calls for legislation, things are not moving swiftly in Congress. They are just now really starting classified briefings on what AI is.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Right. Right. Well, they haven`t legislated the old internet yet.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Not -- not in 25 years, there`s not been one piece of legislation, not a privacy, not antitrust, not algorithmic transparency. So, I imagine they`ll get to this sometime in 2060, 2070. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 You know, they`re more - they find this more interesting and they`re actually moving a little faster because it`s a global issue. It`s a global issue and a competitive issue with China, for example, and so they`re thinking about it because of the implications of AI are much more profound. They`re more like the shift -- farming shift, the manufacturing shift, the internet shift.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: So, this is a big moment, actually. I don`t - I know Barry said it was under sung. No, it`s not. I don`t - I thought crypto --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He said that about Hollywood but then he didn`t when he was talking about publishing.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, publishing. Of course. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 I think he`s right, they`re not going to suddenly make, you know, an AI, I don`t know, Brad Pitt or something like that. He`ll - he`ll be around until his career is over essentially and be fine.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that - that there`s implications on writers and everybody. I mean someone that -- in the - in the green room just said, is AI going to kill us? And I`m like, what do you do? You know, he`s a lawyer. And I`m like, oh, yes, kind of.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Maybe.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Like a lot of what you do. But it`s going to change what you do versus say you`re a lawyer writing a contract. It can write a contract and then you can check it and it can write it faster. If you`re a PR person, it will write a press release better and quicker. If you`re in journalism, headlines faster and better and then you can pick it.\par \par \uc2 It`s a lot like using a spreadsheet.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: It`s like, you don`t use a calculator anymore, you use a spreadsheet. And you don`t think that that`s put all these accountants out of business. It just changed their work.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, what does the entry of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  announcing he has a new firm and also wants China to be -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What did he say, on team humanity to shape artificial intelligence?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Sure, China`s always really good that way. No, it`s a --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s a surveillance state.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: It`s a surveillance state. You know I think that.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think it has to be a global solution for a lot of issues because when you think about things like killer robots, yes, maybe we should all come that AI robots you need to be thinking about that and there should be global decisions made as a - as a - as a group of countries discussing what should and shouldn`t be used.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, every country is competing and Elon`s was early to AI. I`ve interviewed him many times about it a decade ago. But he was late because he - \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and he parted ways for a variety of reasons. And then they, of course, launched ChatGPT, et cetera, and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  made the investment. So, Elon was ahead but then was behind. And so now he`s trying to be ahead again after calling for a pause.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, I remember that, a six month pause.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Oh. I know. I know. It`s so funny that he calls for a pause and then started a company.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Then - yes, and -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: And then one would imagine that was hypocritical if one was of that mind, but -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, there was no pause, right?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: There was no pause.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What is his entry - Elon Musk`s entry in -- excuse me, he`s already entered the social media space through \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but what is the entry of Mark Zuckerberg`s new product Threads mean for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Very bad.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Very bad for Musk?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Very bad.\par \par \uc2 First real - I mean there`s been a lot of really interesting efforts. I like them all. But this is someone who has an immediate social network that you can tap into, which is \uc2 Instagram\uc2  a very smart way to do it.\par \par \uc2 It`s a good product. They`re slowly rolling it out. It seems safer. It seems more civil. It`s a real -- Musk made - gave Zuckerberg an opening. And if they were, say, in a cage match, he would have had the stuffing knocked out of him at this moment.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see if he can recover. But I don`t - I don`t think it can because advertising is way off. He`s created a haven for white supremacists. There`s no safety on that platform. And so \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  can come in and say, oh, look, we have safety. Oh, look, we have a network. And it was really smart of Mark. And he`s - he`s sort of redefining his narrative. And hopefully he can get it right this time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Versus \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and all the criticism there.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Last time, yes. Yes, exactly. Yes, I think it`s not going to be a huge business. It`s, you know, maybe $8 billion to $10 billion for him if he turns on the advertising and - and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s gone from $5 billion to maybe $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A 50 percent drop in revenue.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: At least.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: If not more.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Kara Swisher, it`s always great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Any time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Particularly in person -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To help us make sense of these - these shifts that change our economy -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Change our politics, change our society.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re joined now by the chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee, Texas Republican Representative Mike McCaul.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE MCCAUL (R-TX): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have a lot to get to with you, but I want to start on the National Defense Authorization Act, which passed the Republican- controlled House and it was -- it drew a lot of attention this week because of the social policy issues attached to it. So, this is a bill that pays military personnel. It does things that are necessary for national security.\par \par \uc2 Once this goes to the Senate, they`re going to chop out all those things. You know that.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It comes back to you in the House. Are you confident that Republicans can get this necessary piece of legislation through without having to turn to Democrats to help you get it over the finish line?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: You know, we saw this actually when the Democrats had the majority, they passed a very partisan, you know, NDAA bill. It went over to the Senate. You know, I`m a - we did the conference committee. And, traditionally, the more, you know, was - the more partisan minutes get stripped out.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day this always ends up it is as a bipartisan bill. You know, but there were some -- certain policies, like, for instance, the Hyde amendment since 1980, not to fund taxpayer abortions that, you know, our members felt was very important to put in there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that`s still -\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: I think that`s one that will survive.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, are you -- you`re talking about the provision that would restrict funding to allow service members to travel -\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: And pay expenses, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it doesn`t, in any way, the Pentagon policy, fund abortions? It`s the --\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, you know, I wish they hadn`t --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Or fertility services.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, they actually kind of started this argument. And, look, this is a process. You know we had a lot of amendments. Our members needed that vote. And I do think, at the end of the day, we come together as a conference. And it will be a bipartisan bill. I think there`s nothing more important than our national defense and our military. We give the largest pay raise in 20 years. We upgrade our triad system, our nuclear capabilities, hyper sonics, a lot to counter China, and particularly in Taiwan. So, it`s vitally important we not politicize this bill at the end of the day. And I feel very confident we`ll have a bipartisan bill coming out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Because you did vote to eliminate the diversity offices at the Pentagon, to deny transgender troops coverage for hormone therapy, for this restrictions on funding for people to get reproductive services, including fertility treatments and abortions to travel. None of that, you think, ends up in the final bill --\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, because that goes against the -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because none of those things will get Democratic votes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, it does against -- since 1980 we haven`t funded anything that goes towards taxpayer, you know, abortions. I think some of the policies on culture that the - that the Defense Department has instituted has caused problems within our own military. Recruitment is at an all-time low now. After Afghanistan, and then to watch these videos that these - these trained, you know, say SEALs have to watch, you know, injecting their own social, moral policies, let`s make it about readiness and our ability to fight a war.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Well, yes. And that`s why the defense secretary said, what, one in five troops are now female. And that what he put this policy in to be able to do is for them to travel to get things that aren`t covered in the states they`re living. So, should - shouldn`t all troops, regardless of where they`re stationed, get the same treatment? Like, why penalize them for living in Texas (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, they`re free - they`re free to travel to another state to have an --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But they`d be penalized. They`d have to take time off.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: To have an abortion. Just not at taxpayer expense.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They`d have to take the time off and the like. And so that would impact their ability to do their jobs, arguably, right, if they have to - to go on leave.\par \par \uc2 But, anyway, I want to also ask you about one of the things that was in there -- not in there, but many conservative members of your caucus wanted it to, and those were restrictions on funding for Ukraine. What does that indicate about what Republicans will get over the finish line in the fall in terms of an actual supplemental to help Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: All right. Well, you know my position on Ukraine. We should have, a year ago, been putting in the weapons we`re putting in just now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: For victory, not to - just to survive.\par \par \uc2 There were several Ukraine amendments. They - they all failed. And I would say the majority of Republicans voted to support Ukraine. At the end of the day, you know, the Reagan Institute did a great - a great poll that showed that over 70 percent of Republicans support Ukraine. And I think that was reflected in our vote. Yes, we had about 70 members that voted against it, but I do think, when it comes back, you`re going to see a more bipartisan support for things like our efforts in Ukraine, particularly as we`re in the counteroffensive.\par \par \uc2 To me, it`s very dangerous to have these amendments when Ukraine is in the crossfire trying to push the aggression of Russia back in the counteroffensive.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re talking about amendments like what Marjorie Taylor Greene was trying to attach.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Correct. Correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And Republicans did oppose this thing that they --\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: We -- we did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The conservative (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: And it failed. And I think that`s good news.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`ve been pushing for longer range missile systems, like ATACMS. Do you have enough funding in this current allotment or what you can put together in the fall to continue the pace of weapons to go to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Sure. It was already appropriated in the supplemental last year. You know, the $90 billion. I mean, it`s a draw down authority.\par \par \uc2 We have ATACMS. So I have great sources on the ground and they`re telling me right - right now, because of the mines and the fortifications, that what they need -- the cluster munitions are going to help with, you know, killing Russians in the field. However, they need the longer range artilleries to hit the depots, the energy, the - the logistical supply lines. They don`t have that. And they don`t have air cover. And that`s -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: That`s really important here because the F-16s were held back so long by the administration, and the pilot training, that they don`t have what they need to win in this counteroffensive. And it`s really sad.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you quickly about Iran. You`ve been vocal in asking the administration for a briefing on the presidential envoy, Rob Malley, and why he is suspended. Do you have any promise that you`ll get it -- that you`ll get information?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, we sent -- I sent a letter. We were rebuked. We have been given no answer about his status. Remember, this is a top negotiator to Iran on one of the most, you know, on nuclear weapons programs --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Our highest classified secrets. So, we are giving a deadline of July 25th to have, you know, the diplomatic security and management secretary come in and brief us in a classified space.\par \par \uc2 Margaret, I can`t tell you how important this is because if he somehow, you know, worst case scenario, if he transferred intelligence and secrets to our foreign nation adversaries --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right, but there`s no proof of that at this point?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: There is no proof of that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: But if he did, that would be treason, in my view.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Unfortunately, I have to leave it there because we have a hard out here. But more to talk to you about, always.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Thanks, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to President Biden`s national security adviser, Jake Sullivan, who we spoke with earlier about the latest Chinese cyber hack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN (National Security Adviser): This was an intrusion actually into \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , into \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 `s cloud system. And they went in through that to get into the unclassified e-mail system of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  agencies. It was actually the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  that discovered the intrusion, alerted \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , shut it down. And we`re taking steps to make sure that that`s not a vulnerability going forward.\par \par \uc2 We have seen this kind of thing before many times over many administrations, and we take steps to try to hold the relevant actors responsible. And we`ll do so in this case.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They`re China-based actors according to \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . Do you have any reason to dispute that?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: No, I have no reason to dispute what \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  is saying.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Treasury secretary told us that China`s decision to cut off ingredients for computer chips starting August 1st may be retaliatory for some of the actions the U.S. has taken to restrict tech sales to China. Do you see this as tit-for-tat moves here? Are we in a period of escalation despite your diplomatic outreach?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: Look, I can`t get inside the heads of the Chinese decision makers, so I`m not sure what was motivating them. What I do know is that I -- I think it`s a self-defeating move because I believe that it will only reinforce the determination of many other countries in the world to de- risk, to find ways to reduce dependencies and increase the resilience of their own supply chains, including for the kinds of critical minerals that are at issue in this particular decision.\par \par \uc2 So, from our perspective, we are being clear and transparent about the steps we`re taking. We`re not looking to end all trade with China. What we`re looking to do is have a small yard of restrictions on technology with national security implications and a high fence around that yard. That`s what we`re going to continue to do. And China, of course, will have to make its own decisions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Staying in Asia, we saw this week that North Korea appears to have taken a significant step towards an intercontinental ballistic missile that could put the U.S. within range of a nuclear weapon, potentially. Are you concerned that they will carry out another nuclear test in the coming weeks?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: I have been concerned for some time that North Korea would conduct what would be its seventh nuclear test going back multiple administrations. And I remain concerned about that. I don`t see any immediate indications that that`s going to happen, but it would not come as a surprise if North Korea moved forward with another nuclear test.\par \par \uc2 With respect to its intercontinental ballistic missile capability, this is a capability they began testing several years ago. They have continued to test it. We watch all of those tests very closely to see how it is developing. And we coordinate extremely closely with our allies, with Japan and Korea, to make sure that we are responding in lockstep to this threat.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Have you made any new diplomatic overtures since this test in order to negotiate with Pyongyang or talk to them at all?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: Not since this test, but over the course of the Biden administration we have indicated to North Korea that we`re prepared to sit down and talk without preconditions about their nuclear program. And we`ve also made clear to China that it is the United States who is ready for diplomacy and North Korea who is not.\par \par \uc2 So, from our perspective, China has a role to play here, too, given its relationship with North Korea, to indicate to the North Koreans that its continued testing is destabilizing and, frankly, is, in fact, only creating circumstances in which the United States, our allies and partners, have to step up our activities and posture to respond to the threat.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You are just back from Europe, where the president was at this \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit. On the diplomatic front, though, there are other issues as well, including the expiration of what`s known as this Black Sea initiative to allow for ships carrying food leaving Ukraine to safely pass without Russia attacking them. Is there any sign from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that he is willing to extend this?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: Look, I can`t predict what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will do. He has been all over the map with respect to this initiative over the course of the past many months. It is possible that Russia pulls out of it. It is possible they continue.\par \par \uc2 If, in fact, they pull out of it, the rest of the world will take a look at that and say that Russia has turned its back on ensuring that the countries of the global south, in Africa and Latin America and Asia can get the food they need at affordable prices. And I think that will come at an enormous diplomatic cost to Russia going forward.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a choice \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is going to have to make. We are prepared for any scenario and we`re working closely with the Ukrainians on that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Because it`s set to expire at midnight tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the promises made at \uc2 NATO\uc2 , there was this general pledge to potentially allow Ukraine to join in the future. Are you concerned that that will shape a negotiation, potentially, with Russia to end the conflict where they are incentivized to just drag this out?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: The Ukrainians are currently, as we speak, bravely and courageously pushing against the Russian lines in the south and in the east. They are inflicting enormous damage on the Russian forces. The west is working to continue to tighten the squeeze of our sanctions, hollowing out Russia`s defense industrial base, weakening its capacity to produce advanced technology. We will continue to put economic pressure on Russia and the Ukrainians will continue to put military pressure on Russia. So, I think, in the end, if Russia chooses to continue fighting in this war it will come at a grave cost to Russia and Ukraine will continue to make progress on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we are going to make sure that Ukraine has the support it needs for as long as it takes, and that is a message that came out of the NATO summit. And, finally, yes, we said at \uc2 NATO\uc2 , very simply, Ukraine`s future is in \uc2 NATO\uc2 . We meant it. That`s not up for negotiation. That`s something that now all 31 allies have committed to.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Not up for negotiation. OK.\par \par \uc2 On Iran, before I let you go, you`ve said that the administration is trying to put Iran`s nuclear program back in the box, ultimately through some diplomatic effort. Are you close to any type of understanding on that front and any type of understanding that would allow for the four Americans to be released?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: We have tried very hard to secure the release of the four unjustly detained Americans in Iran. We have done so since the day that President Biden took office. We have had indirect contacts with Iran on this in an effort to try to get a deal that could get them released. We have not arrived at an understanding that would get them out at this point. We are continuing to work at it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And by understanding, in terms of the nuclear program, this would not be any kind of written agreement? We are not close to any kind of actual deal?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: With respect to the nuclear program, we`re not close to any kind of a deal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: May I quickly ask you about Rob Malley, the president`s envoy. Is he coming back to the administration? We understand he`s being suspended as his security clearance is being reviewed.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: Rob Malley has served multiple administrations faithfully and well. He is a public servant. He is a diplomat. He is engaged in high- level, high stakes diplomacy for a long time. And he`s someone who a lot of us, including myself, have deep respect for.\par \par \uc2 I can`t speak to the current circumstances. I have to refer you to the State Department on that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Jake Sullivan, thank you for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back with a lot more FACE THE NATION. So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230716ej7g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hollywood on Strike; Dangerous Heat Wave; Brutal Heat Wave; Birth Control Breakthrough; Iowa`s Abortion Ban; Secret Service Closes on White House Cocaine Investigation; Beaches Closed Due to Bacteria Concern; Aspartame Health Questions; Tiffany Chen`s Struggle; What is Bell`s Palsy; R&B Star, Chxrry 22; Simon Pegg`s Blockbuster Return; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Errol Barnett, Danya  Bacchus, Stephanie Abrams, Janet Shamlian, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Elaine  Quijano, Roxana Saberi, Nancy Chen \par \uc2 14,235 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  mornings. It`s Friday. You made it. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER (\uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  president): They plead poverty when giving hundreds of millions of dollars to their CEOs. It is disgusting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hollywood shuts down as actors join writers on strike for the first time in more than 60 years.\par \par \uc2 BOB IGER (\uc2 The Walt Disney Company\uc2  CEO): There`s a level of expectation that they have that is just not realistic. it will have a very, very damaging effect on the whole business.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Extreme heat could cause more wildfires in the west and there`s no relief in sight.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Southwest, record heat in the summer is dangerous heat.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And in a historic move, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  approves the nation`s first over-the-counter birth control pill.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: How big of a deal is this?\par \par \uc2 DR. PAMELA BERENS (UT Health Houston professor and OB-GYN): I think it`s a really big deal.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Secret Service has closed its investigation into the discovery of cocaine at the White House. Republicans are crying foul.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Marketa Vondrousova became the first unseated finalist in Wimbledon since Billie Jean King back in 1963.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s one of Steph Curry`s favorite times of the year, the American Century Championship Golf Tournament in Lake Tahoe.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: First try. Does that count as a hole-in-one, guys? I mean, technically right?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Beyonce kicked off the US leg of her world tour in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And the anchors in Philadelphia, they really got into the spirit.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s Fox 29`s Alex Holley, her backup dancers worker fellow co- hosts.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know who they are, but they look like a fun group to me. I like that. Local anchors.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I applaud any public dancing, all right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because I don`t do much of it myself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, local anchors.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What about you? You don`t want to get into your Beyonce bod?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nope, nope, nope.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You don`t want do it for the G hive?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know that it is -- very cute, Nate. I know it seems hard to believe, but I try to keep public humiliation to a minimum.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But that was good. That was good.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS on this Friday. I don`t know about you, but it seemed like a very long week. So excited to be here today.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a big, big show for you today, including an exclusive conversation with martial artist, her name is Tiffany Chen -- she is Robert De Niro`s partner -- about a health issue she faced after giving birth to their adorable little girl. Now, it is something many women deal with.\par \par \uc2 We`re also going to meet the baby, Gia, that`s her name, and this will be her first appearance on national TV.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ll share our interview with actor, Simon Pegg in our final day of "Mission Impossible" week. This is his fifth movie playing field agent, Benji Dunn. We asked him about the most intense moments he has had on set, and I`ll tell you what, there have been a few of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll bet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There sure have been, and of course, that`s an interview that happened before what we`re about to talk about, the big news today.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood on strike, a historic shutdown. Just hours ago, around 160,000 performers went on strike, walked off the job over issues that have major implications for the future of entertainment.\par \par \uc2 This is the first time that the writers and the actors have been on strike at the same time since -- in 63 years. \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was president of the Screen Actors Guild the last time this happened, not president of America, of the Guild, and it comes after a breakdown in negotiations between the actors union, that`s \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , and the group representing the studios.\par \par \uc2 Errol Barnett is in New York, where of course a lot of this work gets done and a lot of that work is not happening as of this morning.\par \par \uc2 Errol, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Hey there, Tony. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 You`re right, tens of thousands of television and movie actors are expected to join rioters already on strike. This likely includes some of the favorite people that you watch on a regular basis.\par \par \uc2 We`re outside \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2 . That`s just one of the big studios. You can see the fencing already up for the upcoming picketers.\par \par \uc2 The central issue here, the same as it is in other parts of the economy -- compensation, especially when it comes to a rapidly changing technology like artificial intelligence, and streaming platforms.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: With screenwriters and actors now both on strike.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Hollywood is at a standstill.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER: We are the victims here.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  president, Fran Drescher says studios are ignoring enormous shifts in the industry and in the economy.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER: You cannot change the business model as much as it has changed and not expect the contract to change, too. What are we doing moving around furniture on the Titanic? It`s crazy.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: The union`s main sticking points include limiting how artificial intelligence can be used, along with increased pay and residuals from streaming services, where \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  claims its members are missing out on millions.\par \par \uc2 MATT DAMON (Actor): We have to protect the people who are kind of on the margins.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: But the studios say streaming has affected their bottom line as well.\par \par \uc2 In the first quarter of 2023, \uc2 Disney\uc2  streaming services, which include \uc2 Disney\uc2 +, \uc2 Hulu\uc2 , and ESPN+ lost the company more than $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 BOB IGER: There`s a level of expectation that they have, that is just not realistic, and they are adding to a set of challenges that this business is already facing.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: The \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , which bargains for Hollywood companies says in a statement that the strike: "Will lead to financial hardship for countless thousands of people who depend on the industry."\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER: They plead poverty, that they`re losing money left and right, when giving hundreds of millions of dollars to their CEOs. It is disgusting.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: And one other note about why this fight over compensation is so crucial for so many in these unions, you have to earn a certain baseline of income, Gayle, to qualify for health care coverage. That`s true under the \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  benefits; and for many middle class actors, this is about their livelihoods.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, this affects so many people and so many actors are living paycheck to paycheck. Thank you very much, Errol.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the relentless heatwave, and relentless is a word here, engulfing much of the nation. Have you ever seen the weather maps look orange and red and yellow and purple I saw the other day, and magenta, there you go, I`ve never seen anything like that.\par \par \uc2 This is a major health concern with more than 111 million people under extreme heat alerts. Parts of Arizona and Texas have had triple digit temperatures every day this month with no end in sight. Another reason this is actually dangerous, it can lead to more wildfires, especially in California.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters there on high alert as Danya Bacchus shows us.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Californians are being warned. This weekend will bring the hottest weather of the year with desert areas expected to reach a scorching 120 degrees.\par \par \uc2 This sweltering forecast also comes with this serious threat of catastrophic wildfires that thrive in these conditions.\par \par \uc2 TANNER RIGGS (California firefighter): This weekend`s heatwave is something that`s on all firemen`s minds right now. We can have brush fires any day just because it is so hot.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Tanner Riggs (sp?) has been fighting fires in California for more than a decade. He says this year, there`s even more fuel for the flames because all the vegetation that grew after and especially wet winter is turning into hot dry brush.\par \par \uc2 TANNER RIGGS: We have so much grass, it dries extremely fast and it carries fire extremely well.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Firefighters say that along with the unprecedented temps and stronger winds mean the fires are more aggressive than ever before.\par \par \uc2 2020 saw the worst wildfires in the state`s modern history. More than 4.2 million acres burned.\par \par \uc2 TANNER RIGGS: Fires are getting larger, quicker and that`s typical for a heatwave like this. I think we`re going to have more acreage burned this year.\par \par \uc2 It`s concerning to the entire county and the entire state.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: And the recent rise in temperature has caused many dry patches like this one throughout the state. Officials do their best for brush clearance, but the amount currently drying out is more than usual and difficult to maintain -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Danya, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking the heat for us.\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 If you think it`s been hot, just wait. Things really turn up this weekend in the Southwest. Saturday, records start falling in New Mexico and Arizona. Sunday, we will be close to tying our all-time record high in Vegas at 117 degrees. Death Valley is going to approach 130 with our lows around 100 degrees.\par \par \uc2 And Nate, the thing is, this type of heat is going to continue at least through next week.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Steph, quick question.\par \par \uc2 Last week we talked about it being the hottest day ever recorded. Are we still breaking records for this planet?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: Yes. Preliminary daily data shows that we passed the hottest average global temperature on July 3rd, and have been above that value every day since setting a new record on July 6th.\par \par \uc2 Now we`ve been seeing this warming for months. Yesterday, \uc2 NOAA\uc2  released their June Global Climate Report and overall, the temperatures across the US this past June, they were near average, but the planet, we experienced our hottest June on record with nearly nine percent of Earth setting record high temperatures for the month.\par \par \uc2 One of the factors influencing all of this heat, the ocean temperatures. June marked the third consecutive month of record ocean waters, and not only are we seeing the warmth in the Pacific with the building of El Nino, but also over the North Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 And get this, the water temperatures near Miami are in the mid-90s, so that is going to keep the humidity up and it also could cause a significant coral bleaching event.\par \par \uc2 Now the temperatures are expected to stay above average for the next several months, and all of this human-induced warming contributes not only to more heat waves, but also more extreme weather events around the globe.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage on climate news and sustainability, you can watch Pattern on \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices. And Nate, 2023 could rank among the warmest on record continuing a trend we`ve seen over the past decade.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know the rising heat is a serious topic, but I just keep thinking about Nelly, "It`s Getting Hot In Here," Steph. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s getting hot in here, but we`re going to leave our clothes on, we promise.\par \par \uc2 Now we turn to an historic decision by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  approving the first over-the-counter sale of a birth control pill. Anyone of reproductive age will be able to get it without a prescription or parental approval. You`ll be able to buy it in pharmacies, grocery stores, and even online.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian has more on this change, this huge change in reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The daily pill called Opill which has been available in this country by prescription for 50 years could be on store shelves without a prescription by early next year.\par \par \uc2 How big of a deal is this?\par \par \uc2 DR. PAMELA BERENS: I think it`s a really big deal. Yes.\par \par \uc2 Young women --\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Dr. Pamela Berens is an OB-GYN and professor at UT Health Houston.\par \par \uc2 DR. PAMELA BERENS: I think having access to birth control is really important for women and this is another way to get easier access.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: More than three-quarters of US women under age 50 in a recent survey said they wanted birth control pills available without a prescription if deemed safe and effective. They are already available over-the-counter in more than 100 countries. The move was opposed by the Catholic Medical Association.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The medication should not be over-the-counter. I think a doctor needs to be counseling and discussing this with patients if that`s what the patient is choosing.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The approval comes amid increased focus on birth control since last year`s overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  reports, of the more than six million pregnancies in the US each year, almost half are unintended.\par \par \uc2 Opill contains the hormone progestin and must be taken at the same time every day to be effective. Common side effects are irregular bleeding, headaches, dizziness, and nausea as with many birth control pills.\par \par \uc2 DR. PAMELA BERENS: This is a very safe option. It may not be the perfect option for many people.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: French drugmaker, Perrigo hasn`t announced a price for the pills, which are sold in 28 day packs, but says, it will make them affordable and provide Opill at no cost to those who can`t pay.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Janet Shamlian in Houston.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In Iowa, the governor plans to sign a new abortion law today banning the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy. She will do it at an evangelical forum that several Republican presidential hopefuls plan to attend, a sign that the abortion issue will play an important role in the campaign.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Des Moines.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Hey, Nate. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Good morning from Iowa where six Republican presidential hopefuls will make their case to over 2,000 evangelical voters who play a key role in those Iowa caucuses. But one contender will be notably absent. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is skipping the event.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Protests broke out at the Iowa State Capitol this week after lawmakers passed a six-week abortion ban. The legislation includes exceptions for rape, incest, life of the mother, and fatal fetal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KIM REYNOLDS (R-IO): Good day.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Republican governor, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  is set to sign it into law Friday at the Annual Family Leadership Summit.\par \par \uc2 Many of the Republican presidential hopefuls will also be there to woo evangelical voters and are heaping praise on the popular governor.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC) (2024 presidential candidate): Well, Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  is one of the most remarkable individuals in the country, and she is one of the strongest governors her state and any state has ever seen.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL) (2024 presidential candidate): I want to just let everyone up there know how much I respect what your governor and the legislature have done with passing the heartbeat bill. That was a big victory for life.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But the current frontrunner, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not. Instead, he recently lashed out at the governor for not endorsing him posting: "I opened up the governor position for \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  and when she fell behind, I endorsed her. Now, she wants to remain neutral."\par \par \uc2 Trump is also choosing to opt out of an Iowa tradition by skipping Friday`s GOP candidate forum.\par \par \uc2 BOB VANDER PLAATS (The Family Leader COP and president): I believe he is making a mistake.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Bob Vander Plaats is an influential Iowa evangelical leader whose organization is hosting the event.\par \par \uc2 BOB VANDER PLAATS: You can thank the former president for all the good things he did in his administration when he was elected to the presidency in 2016, but 2024 is not 2016 and they want someone who can win, but that`d be a fresh vision, fresh generation.\par \par \uc2 I think he`s our biggest risk though, to not win back the White House.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And also joining today`s forum, former Fox News host, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 . He will be questioning that candidates on stage and many of those candidates have kind of struggled to navigate the issue of abortion on the national stage since the fall of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But here in Iowa, there have already been legal challenges filed against that new ban to try to block it from being enforced -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It should be interesting. Caitlin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of interesting that cocaine found at the White House, the Secret Service says it`s got no idea who left a small baggie with the stuff inside the White House and that the investigation is now close to the surprise and disbelief of many.\par \par \uc2 The bag was found, as you may recall inside a guest entryway in the West Wing or to the West Wing rather, two weekends ago about 10 days or so in a cubby where visitors leave their cell phones.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it looked for traces of DNA and fingerprints, but found no reliable results. No physical evidence. And the Secret Service said that the surveillance cameras apparently did not provide any additional leads.\par \par \uc2 Republican House Speaker Kevin McCarthy is now questioning how the White House, which of course has around the clock security could not identify who left the cocaine and I have to say it`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I liked how you used the word "disbelief" when you were reading that story. Here`s a White House, supposedly one of the most secure places in the world, something as significant as that and people say well, sorry.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No answers for you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It doesn`t make a lot of sense to a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is surprising.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead, a health warning for beach goers in the Northeast this summer. There is danger in the water and we are not talking about the sharks.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:17, that means it`s time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, come back plans for an Olympic hero. Why gymnastics gold medalist, Gabby Douglas wants another shot of glory 12 years on.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Some beach goers in the Northeast may have to make other plans after high levels of bacteria were found in the water. At least five Northeast states have been posting at beaches, lakes and ponds because of water quality.\par \par \uc2 Elaine Quijano is in Boston. She`s got the latest warning from the health officials.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: The water is a red flag. Please swim at your own risk.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: At Constitution Beach in Boston, swimmers were given this advisory after elevated levels of bacteria were detected in the water.\par \par \uc2 In Massachusetts, there were 71 beach closures or advisories on Thursday alone after water tests showed high levels of fecal matter and colonies of bacteria that could make swimmers sick.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE GUILFOY (Beachgoer): I have, you know health issues that would be -- I would be vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Experts say recent heavy rainfall along the East Coast overflowed combined sewer systems in the state pushing human waste into the rivers and to beaches.\par \par \uc2 What`s the danger if people go swimming in this water?\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBBIE GOLDSTEIN (Appointed Commissioner of the \uc2 Massachusetts Department of Public Health\uc2 ): People could get sick and many could have a mild illness, something like nausea or vomiting. Some people can have skin reactions, can even get an eye infection or an ear infection.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Nationwide, more than 1,400 beaches had at least one swim advisory or closing last year.\par \par \uc2 The head of \uc2 Massachusetts Public Health Department\uc2  says more beach closures could be the new normal.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBBIE GOLDSTEIN: As we see increasing rainfall and increasing severe weather patterns mostly associated with climate change, we are seeing increases in the number of beach waters that need to be closed down because of contamination.\par \par \uc2 DAN DOHERTY (Kelly`s Roast Beef director of operations): I want to make a sandwich out of that.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Dan Doherty the head of operations for Kelly`s Roast Beef on Revere Beach, says the 10 beach closures so far there this summer have affected their bottom line.\par \par \uc2 DAN DOHERTY: We are off about 40 percent from last year with the closures. People aren`t coming down here to wait in line for you know, a roast beef sandwich.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: As for a long-term solution, experts say separating Massachusetts combined sewage systems have sewage is in one pipe and storm water in another would be a permanent fix -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ************************************************************************ ***** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER: The entire business model has been changed by streaming, digital, AI.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment of history that is a moment of truth. If we don`t stand tall right now, we are all going to be in trouble.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We hope you like the news because there`s going to be a lot more news in your media diet because just about everything else is on strike. Those words there from Fran Drescher. She`s an actor, of course, president of the \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  union and she is talking about why actors are now joining writers on the picket lines forcing much of Hollywood to shut down production.\par \par \uc2 Other stars like Jane Fonda and Wanda Sykes, Lin-Manuel Miranda had already been protesting along with the writers in support of industry colleagues who they say are struggling just to make a living.\par \par \uc2 In response to the actors now striking, the studio representatives that`s the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2 , said in part: ". presented a deal that offered historic pay and residual increases, the union has regrettably chosen a path that will lead to financial hardship for countless thousands of people who depend on the industry."\par \par \uc2 So with the actors disputing that characterization, of course, what is really going on here, we`re joined by "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " media reporter, Nicole Sperling, who has been covering this extensively.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING ("The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " media reporter): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what`s the immediate impact of both the actors and the writers on strike now?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Well, the industry was pretty much 80 percent shut down with just the writers. A lot of productions have stopped, and surprisingly, a lot of writers were able to shut down productions by picketing very aggressively and forcing studios to not want to go forward with productions.\par \par \uc2 But now, we`re done, like everything shut down. Like there is no -- and there is no promotion of projects, too, which is a really big deal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Big deal for you guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are still recovering from COVID. I mean, it`s a big deal for us now, in terms of guests. You know, I, I worry about the day that we`re just sitting here doing jazz hands because there is nobody here to talk to.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But weren`t we already behind the eighth ball because of COVID?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Yes. I mean, theater chains are still coming back from COVID. They`re still 25 percent down what they were in 2019 before the pandemic. You know, studios had ramped up aggressively so with all of their content with the streaming wars that were happening when everyone was fighting for eyeballs and subscribers, and then that shut down -- a lot of production shut down during COVID. I mean, we saw it happen.\par \par \uc2 Those all came back, but the industry is still struggling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is still not -- what do you make of Fran Drescher? You know, because you could certainly feel her passion, her commitment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was an incredible speech.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought so, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So many quotable lines there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is worth looking at everything that she said, and her anger. Was she effective, do you think?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Oh, I think she was so effective. I mean, I think she rallied those flagging writers who`ve been stuck on the picket line for the last 70 days like this may -- she may be fighting her own battle, but she really rallied troops all around the country, and you`ve got 160,000 actors that she really got going and I think she was -- she was incredible.\par \par \uc2 And she was off the cuff. There was no teleprompter she was reading from.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I saw it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And also, the writers were already out, Nicole. There has to be strength and numbers with the actors.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it true?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Yes, absolutely. I mean, now we have 170,000 people, essentially who are going up against the studios. That`s a big number, and you`re going to see a lot of really recognizable faces on the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: And you know, that`s -- that may be a two-edged sword because you have this perception of actors make a lot of money. What are they out fighting for? But you know, there`s a ton of working actors out there who are living paycheck to --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And have second jobs at grocery stores and --\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And taking side jobs,.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There are recognizable faces. And on the other side, there`s recognizable names and figures in the industry responding, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , being one of those, CEO of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , which is one of the biggest producers of film and movies. He said that their demands are not realistic.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What do you make of that take? And then also, do the studios have the money?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Well, they could cut the salaries of the CEOs and I think that`s the big argument and having him on the day he gets his contract extended for two more years going out and saying their objectives are unrealistic, doesn`t really play well, with all of those people fighting for contracts because of how much money he is being paid and how much money all of these executives -- these top CEOs in Hollywood have been paid for years.\par \par \uc2 So the income inequality that this whole country is dealing with is kind of being -- the spotlight is being shined on the entertainment industry as this microcosm for what`s happening everywhere.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Fran Drescher is right when she says the whole business model, it has exploded, right?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And while these media and film companies are still making money as a top line, the streaming divisions are very often in almost every case, losing lots and lots of money.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And so they`re not wrong about that either.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: They are not.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So how can that -- what is a fair compensation potential model here? What are people even like theorizing could work?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: I mean, it`s still so up in the air. You know, they want gains.\par \par \uc2 What the actors and the writers want is to share in success. That`s how it used to be. You know, Fran Drescher was on "The Nanny," it`s not the world of "The Nanny" anymore. You`re not going to get 30 episodes. It is going to be syndicated and, you know, go on and have these multiple lives.\par \par \uc2 You`re in a streaming show. It runs for eight episodes, and it doesn`t go anywhere else. So it doesn`t sell to another, you know, DVD or sell to anywhere else where they can get residuals based on that.\par \par \uc2 So that model, they kind of killed their own model because you had a linear television model that then went to cable, that then went to DVD, and then you had a cable model -- like there were many bites at the apple, so everybody was sharing in the wealth and that`s not happening anymore.\par \par \uc2 And I think what surprised all of the studios was just how fast cable revenue went down and how fast linear television revenue went down, like people just stopped watching and they cut their cords. They went straight to streaming.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: And that I think has been devastating, so they are not wrong that the business model has definitely changed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which side has the upper hand here before you go?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Well at this moment, right today, I think the hand is on the side of the actors and writers because you know the energy is there, and I think people can relate to their struggle at the moment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And there`s power in solidarity.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Meanwhile, with the news, fresh episodes daily.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, fresh episodes, yes, live and in color.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Yes. There you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There we go. It`s a great promo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Nicholas Sperling, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, why you may want to think twice before reaching for your next diet soda. We sort through New research from the World Health officials on the widely used artificial sweetener in it. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is a new warning about a popular artificial sweetener found in thousands of products from diet drinks to your gum. A group linked to the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  calls it a possible carcinogen. We are talking about aspartame. I know you`ve heard of it. You may have seen it on many ingredient labels.\par \par \uc2 But another study released in the same report says aspartame is okay when taken in limited quantities.\par \par \uc2 The Food and Drug Administration calls it safe if you don`t exceed recommended levels, and that`s why people are so confused.\par \par \uc2 So Roxana Saberi is going to sort through it all.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Two hundred times sweeter than sugar, aspartame is found in roughly 6,000 products worldwide, from diet sodas to sugar free Jell- O to chewing gum. It`s been in wide circulation since the 1980s and regulatory agencies across North America, including the \uc2 FDA\uc2  have repeatedly called it safe.\par \par \uc2 But the WHO`s cancer agency now says aspartame is possibly carcinogenic, citing limited evidence for a type of liver cancer in humans.\par \par \uc2 But according to its own hazard classification, it says the confidence level in the assessment was low, admitting more research is needed.\par \par \uc2 And a second \uc2 WHO\uc2  group, its Food Additive Committee concluded there was not enough data to suggest the current recommended daily intake of aspartame is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN KEANE (\uc2 American Beverage Association\uc2 ): That is a powerful statement and the WHO`s confidence in aspartame safety.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Kevin Keane, the interim head of the \uc2 American Beverage Association\uc2 , a lobbying group says the \uc2 WHO\uc2  report shouldn`t give consumers pause.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN KEANE: It`s unfortunate some are creating confusion about it, but the \uc2 WHO\uc2  was very clear, its Food Safety Agency was very clear that they find aspartame to be safe.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The \uc2 WHO\uc2  still says daily consumption of up to 40 milligrams for just over two pounds of a person`s body weight is acceptable.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALLISON SYLVETSKY (Nutritionist): No matter how much you weigh, you could have 20, 30, 40 diet sodas per day before you hit that amount.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: But nutritionist, Allison Sylvetsky says that doesn`t mean you should.\par \par \uc2 What should people who are trying to limit their sugar intake do?\par \par \uc2 DR. ALLISON SYLVETSKY There`s a lot of context we need to think about and so rather than thinking that choosing that diet soda is going to somehow make you healthier, right, we can make broader dietary changes to have a healthier dietary pattern.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Roxana Saberi.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Up next, the stories that you`ll be talking about today, but first it is 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is time now for "What to Watch," Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay. Nancy is here.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Happy Friday. Good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How are you?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Good to wrap up the week with you guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In for Vlad.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: I am.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to start though with some sad news.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The official cause of death for Lisa Marie Presley, an autopsy report released yesterday says Elvis` only child died from complications of a small bowel obstruction. It was caused by scar tissue from bariatric weight loss surgery years ago.\par \par \uc2 The toxicology report found therapeutic levels of oxycodone and other medicine in her blood, but they were not considered contributing factors to her death. Presley was 54 when she died in January, after complaining of severe abdominal pain. She was found unresponsive in her California home.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a surgery hundreds of thousands of Americans get, but there are risks. It`s gotten a lot safer, but unfortunately you do see situations like this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So take that pain seriously if you have it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right away get some help.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: All right, now turning to news out of the sports world.\par \par \uc2 A trailblazing Olympic gymnast says she is making a comeback.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: In 2012, Gabby Douglas .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay, Gabby.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: . became the first Black woman to win the all-around title. The last time she competed was in the Rio Olympics. That was seven years ago.\par \par \uc2 But she wrote on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  yesterday: "I wanted to find the joy again for the sport that I absolutely love doing. I know I have a huge task ahead of me and I am beyond grateful and excited to get back out on the floor."\par \par \uc2 Douglas will be 28 years old when the Summer Olympics in Paris begin next year. She joins Simone Biles, the 2016, all-around champion and signaling a return to elite competition.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is going to be something to watch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am excited about this. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was mesmerizing to watch back in the day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just looking at her legs, the power in the shape of her legs was just gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the grace in which she formed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. I`m excited about it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: It`ll be fun to watch. I mean 28 is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And 28 is still young, I say.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: I was going to say --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, young in life, I mean, young in gymnastics is a different question.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: You know, that being said, a 30-year-old from Italy won Olympic silver back in 2021 in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gabby for the gold.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: So you never know. We are cheering for her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We like that. All right.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And Super Bowl MVP, Patrick Mahomes is featured in a Netflix docuseries, "Quarterback." It premiered this week and Nate, you got a sit-down with the \uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2  superstar. You spoke about the series as well as football, fatherhood, and being the bad guy for other team`s fans. We have a preview of your conversation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s a better feeling playing the hero at home or the villain on the road?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MAHOMES (\uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2 ): Oh man, I like to be the villain a little bit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Me, too.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MAHOMES: No, it`s a -- obviously, it`s cool and arrowhead to be the hero.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MAHOMES: And win the games and be able to hoist the AFC Championship trophy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MAHOMES: But it`s just as fun, no offense to any my division opponents to go to their places and win those games and so it`s definitely -- both are great, but even though I had the smile, I like being the villain sometimes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: You say that like -- you asked that question like someone who`s also been the villain on the road.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And you enjoyed it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought that was --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. I enjoyed being the villain occasionally. You have to be.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because those fans are harsh, but you know, I love the fact that Patrick Mahomes opened up.\par \par \uc2 It is rare in this business where you have somebody as good as him. Patrick Mahomes is in -- he is a Hall of Famer already. He is only 27 years old.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two-time Super Bowl champion, two-time Super Bowl MVP and he was able to open up like never before, so we kind of got some unprecedented access into the mind of a truly, truly great player.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A champion.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The documentary is out now. It looks so good. Katy and I got into bed last night to watch a different show, and when I opened up Netflix, the preview came on and I press play. She got up out of the bed. I was like wait, wait just a couple more minutes, a couple -- it`s that good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is good, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t want to see it because of the way you were talking about it. I had no desire.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Man, it is really good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, I want to see it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Usually, when I watch something about football, I turn into a football player and I`m very judgmental. I watched the entire "Quarterback" series from start to finish, and it took me into the place where I just felt like a fan watching the game and they give us footage that we have never seen before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never seen before. Oh, I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is really good. Really good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Really looking forward to that. It`s also nice to know the man behind the myth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: All of this and get to know what drives him. So I`m looking forward to that. That airs on Monday here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And also his wife.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And his wife --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: His joined us and sat down. They talked about the ups and downs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And other quarterbacks are in series as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always like to see the wife.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, her cousin is Marcus Mariota.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always like to see the wife.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Gayle`s exclusive interview with Tiffany Chen about an unexpected medical crisis after she had a baby with Robert De Niro.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, an exclusive conversation with Tiffany Chen. That`s Robert De Niro`s partner. She introduces us to their gorgeous little girl, talks about her struggle with a condition known as Bell`s palsy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Tony Dokoupil. Tiffany Chen is one of thousands of Americans who experienced Bell`s Palsy. We`re going to talk to an expert about what you need to know and why it`s more common among pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. We`re also wrapping up "Mission Impossible" week with actor, Simon Pegg aka field agent, Benji Dunn. He has some amazing behind the scenes stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The whole movie is amazing. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: An historic Hollywood shutdown as both writers and actors strike at the same time.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER: We are being victimized by a very greedy entity.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A punishing heatwave could lead to more wildfires in California as millions face temperatures in the triple digits.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: Things really turn up this weekend in the Southwest. Saturday, records start falling in New Mexico and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  approves the first over-the-counter birth control pill.\par \par \uc2 DR. PAMELA BERENS: I think having access to birth control is really important for women.\par \par \uc2 BONNY REYNOLDS (65 years married): We come here every week.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD REYNOLDS (65 years married): Just the two of us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And a couple celebrates their 65th wedding anniversary at their weekly date night spot, \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BONNY REYNOLDS: We`ve gotten older, but we`ve grown together\par \par \uc2 LLOYD REYNOLDS: I am still in love with her. She is a blessing in my life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the good life right there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. they are living well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that really is what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Honesty.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Going to your favorite place.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Whatever your favorite place is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whatever it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sixty-five years of marriage. That is a success story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And having a partner that just makes you feel better and you feel better about them. I love that. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 We begin this hour with our exclusive interview with martial artists Tiffany Chen, who is also Robert De Niro`s partner.\par \par \uc2 Back in April, the couple welcomed their first child together. Her name is Gia Virginia Chen De Niro. Soon afterward, Tiffany Chen lost all movement in her face.\par \par \uc2 Now an MRI revealed that she has Bell`s palsy. That`s a neurological disorder that causes severe muscle weakness or facial paralysis.\par \par \uc2 Only on CBS MORNINGS, Tiffany Chen told us what it`s been like to lose her smile while under very intense public scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Intern.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before Tiffany Chen and Robert De Niro started dating, Chen, a world champion martial artist played his tai chi instructor in De Niro`s 2015 movie, "The Intern."\par \par \uc2 How did he do?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN (Martial artist): I`ve had better students, but he is okay. He had a lot of other stuff to do in the movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve had better students, but he was okay.\par \par \uc2 So how do you guys -- how do you go from I`m the tai chi instructor teaching on the movie to now we`re a couple.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: It`s just -- that`s how it evolved. When we were working, it was just work, and then a couple years later, we got back in touch. And I said, oh, I guess we get along and I guess we really get along.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On April 6th, the couple welcomed their first child together. A girl named Gia Virginia Chen De Niro.\par \par \uc2 What was your pregnancy like?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: It was really easy. Really, really easy. I mean, I would joke around like during the pregnancy, you just keep getting bigger, bigger and bigger. But I was very active.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. So you`re an athlete, so you`re in very good shape.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: And I was just very in touch with my body until I had this crazy new experience where I didn`t know what was going on, but I knew something was wrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She describes the first sign.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Right after delivering, I noticed I was getting a sharp pain behind my left ear like I was in the room, just after having my C- section.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did the doctors tell you it was when you said the pain behind your ear.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: They kind of just looked at me blank. They just kept saying it`s probably because of the fluid buildup after giving birth. And then when I finally could shower I remember looking at myself in the mirror and saying something definitely is looking odder, but it was all in the eyes. And I said well maybe I`m just off because I have so many meds pumped into me.\par \par \uc2 When I got home, it was like, everything was starting to just fall down on itself, like my face was melting on itself and then a week after giving birth, that was when it all hit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Chen called her doctor and was re-admitted immediately.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: I lost all facial function the minute I got into the hospital, and the one thing that threw everybody off was that it was perfectly symmetrical how everything had fallen, and how everything just wasn`t working.\par \par \uc2 One of the doctors, a couple of times, I heard the doctor say, do you think it can be Bell`s? And they said, no, Bell`s is only on one side. She is perfectly symmetrical.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you are drooping on both sides.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes. Everything fell, and the craziest thing is, you have no idea how much your face does without doing anything. I got to the point where my lower lip would fall, and even if I relaxed my face now, my look can`t fall that way. I got to a point where it was just hanging, and I`d have to hold it up. And to talk, I`d have to hold my chin and I`d have to manipulate my lower lip to make certain sounds.\par \par \uc2 I couldn`t say Bob. So it was like having a speech with my partner --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You couldn`t even say his name?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: No, I couldn`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was he saying to you during this time about what was happening? Because this was not his first baby, so he has seen pregnancy and the baby --\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was he saying about what was happening?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: He was very sweet. He tried to say that he didn`t see any difference. He didn`t see any changes, like no, he`s like -- he`s like, you look fine. He`s like, maybe you look a little stern. And I`m like, really? My whole face has melted on itself, but he was very strong, very supportive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Treatment for Bell`s palsy often includes steroids and antiviral medicine. Chen says she tried steroids, but has mostly relied on acupuncture.\par \par \uc2 Are you self-conscious about your face and --\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: The only -- it is -- you know, I am and then I`m not. I`ve never really cared too much about what I look like, but what bothered me is that I couldn`t smile at the baby at the beginning and I couldn`t -- I couldn`t give her kisses. That`s what made me self- conscious.\par \par \uc2 And then we decided, yes, we`re going to live our life and be social.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know how this goes. Bob De Niro, one of the most talented actors of our generation has a baby at 79. That`s news.\par \par \uc2 And so the first time people see you out in public, that generates a lot of conversation. How did you take that? What was your reaction to the headlines?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Some of the things they said, it takes a life -- I have pretty thick skin, so I was kind of I would see a lot of "Oh, they`re so tired," or they`re this or they`re that or they look somber, or they`re not happy, and I mean, they said those things. It was a little frustrating to see sometimes.\par \par \uc2 And then every time we would go to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, criticized you, too tired to smile, like you said that you don`t look happy. It just shows you don`t know what`s going on in people`s lives is my point.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes. Like mind your own business a bit and if you want to say something, don`t always attach what you think in your mind is the story. You don`t know us. You don`t know what happened in somebody`s life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why did you want to sit and tell your story with us today?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Because I think that this is something that is private to us all in a certain way, especially if you`ve experienced it, but it shouldn`t be kept so private, that people lock themselves in their homes and women should not feel hurt by it, ashamed by it or just scarred by it.\par \par \uc2 The thing I did worry about was, what if it didn`t get better? Was my kid going to get made fun of for having the weird-looking mom? That`s the only time like vanity comes into play. It`s how it affects the child.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people were saying all sorts of nasty things about, you know, my appearance and me being angry, and I`m just kind of like, this is like the happiest time of my life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but how did you handle the nastiness? You said it didn`t bother you. I`m surprised.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes, I was just sort of like, well, you just see how many nasty people are out there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. There is a lot.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: And then when you see how nasty people are, you just look at that, and you`re like, well, how could I be better? What can I do to be better to educate people more? And to also help people who would normally be scarred by these comments not be scarred? How can I be an ally to the people who need the ally?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And how has Gia changed your lives?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: She`s made it more fun.\par \par \uc2 It`s okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at her hair, Tiffany.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes, she is a sun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I pick her up?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I pick her up? Hi. Oh my God. Look over there. You`re making your TV debut on CBS MORNINGS. Yay.\par \par \uc2 She`s looking right at you. Here`s mommy over here. Here she is.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Hi, honey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There she is. Oh, she smiles. That was a smile.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: She is talking to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was a smile. That was not gassy. That`s was a smile.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: It was legit. Yes. That`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gia, what`s your favorite Robert De Niro movie? Which one do you like?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: The one in the delivery room.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The one in the delivery room.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Where I`m the star.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You`re the star. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Okay this my last question. You`re the only one in the room who couldn`t answer this at this particular moment The best thing about Bob De Niro is --\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: How much he loves his family.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s nice.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: That might make me a little teary-eyed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that`s nice.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tiffany says that Bob De Niro has been very supportive of her during this time and she has also been supporting him after the death of his grandson earlier this month. The De Niro family certainly have been going through it.\par \par \uc2 He was at the interview to cheer her off camera, and of course, he was invited to take part in the interview, but he made it clear, I took a picture of him looking down at us -- he made it clear that this was her story to tell. He wanted to be there to support her, but he didn`t want to take anything away from what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say bravo to Tiffany Chen because, you know, she is not a mouse burger girl who`s like, oh, woe is me, people are talking about me, this hurts my feelings.\par \par \uc2 She said, I really want to help other women, just to not be ashamed, that you have no control over this, and that you have to go on and live your life despite how nasty people have been to her, and I was very touched by that.\par \par \uc2 She is wearing sunglasses by the way, not because she`s vain because she said, you know, I came deliberately with no makeup. But because of this condition, it hurts her eyes. The lights hurt her eyes, so she wears these -- she wears her sunglasses.\par \par \uc2 But I think it`s -- listen, I never knew that Bell`s palsy had a connection to pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, me neither.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did not know that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she said a lot of women do, but a lot of women don`t know. And you know, I`m very appreciative that she decided to sit down with us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bravo to her, but also bravo to you and the \uc2 CBS\uc2  team. That was a little bit of an emotional roller coaster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re talking about this disorder, so right out the gate, you grab our attention, and then you go from marriage to their relationship and of course you pull out a baby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, you`re really pulling at our heartstrings there but, bravo, that was well done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is a great piece.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, and she is a martial arts champion. She`s won many medals, gold medals. You know, she`s very accomplished.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And she is also funny. She is also funny/\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is hilarious.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, hilarious.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was your favorite -- how was Bob as a student? Not my best, but he did okay. I think that`s funny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is really terrific.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Of course, we`ve really just scratched the surface here about the condition, Bell`s palsy. There`s a lot more to learn, so what we`re going to do next is talk to a top expert who is the founder of the Facial Paralysis Institute, that is coming up in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Doctor.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:11, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, actor, Simon Pegg was full of great stories about the "Mission Impossible" series when we spoke to him.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`ll tell us what it was like working on the set of the new film and working with legendary, Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: You don`t realize how much your lips do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: You have absolutely no idea. You just think it`s your teeth.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I feel like that after I have Novocain at the dentist.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: That`s kind of -- that`s exactly what this feeling was like you have. You`re biting, but your lips actually pull the food into your mouth and I couldn`t pull that way I could just bite.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We just heard Tiffany Chen, a martial artist and partner of Robert De Niro discussing her ongoing struggle with Bell`s palsy.\par \par \uc2 Now it started after their daughter was born back in April. Bell`s palsy is a rare condition affecting about 40,000 people in this country every year, but pregnant women appear to be at three times greater risk for Bell`s, especially in their third trimester or right after giving birth, which is what happened to Tiffany.\par \par \uc2 The cause is unknown.\par \par \uc2 With us now is Dr. Babak Azizzadeh. He`s founder and director of the facial paralysis Institute and joins us at the table.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much because I know this is your specialty. You got on a redeye from California and came here this morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH (Facial Paralysis Institute founder and director): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, for Tiffany, this was a complete surprise to her that this could happen after pregnancy. But it`s more common than most people know, isn`t it? I applaud her for speaking out.\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: Yes, it happens all the time. And patients, because they recover most of the time, not all the time, you know, they`re treated and they go on, but it`s a devastating process until the recovery takes place and not everyone recovers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not everyone recovers?\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: No --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because 