ot only in the franchise, but one of the biggest stunts in movie history, you know? And so I thought I better capture this for posterity, and --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And in case the other cameras fail, we have one shot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We got it. We`re good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You got it on the iPhone.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Don`t worry, guys. I got it. I got it on the phone. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is great. Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 So you were -- you came into the "Mission Impossible" franchise at MI4 or --\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: MI3.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: MI3.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s six, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. That`s a long time ago.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes, yes. It was.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible III.")\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: And he was a kind of lab technician at the time, and JJ Abrams had called me and I thought it was a nice little cameo, and I was going to have a little -- you know, I`d go in Hollywood and then I`d go home to my dogs and everything will go back to normal, but here we are five films later.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now, I know you`re working with a script. But then you have these scenes, like there`s one where you`re dismantling a bomb while like tossing off one liners to your two buddies.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: High stakes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: High stakes.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How much of that is predetermined and you`re just delivering the line? And how much room is there for you to just have fun? Because you know these people at this point.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Oh, yes. Well, Chris McQuarrie is always really keen for us to invest ourselves in the characters and to throw stuff in. And he`s an Oscar-winning writer, so the script is amazing. But there`s always room in the moment to kind of chuck in little asides.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I mean, you never know what you`d be doing, what you`d be saying when you`re diffusing an artificially intelligent nuclear bomb. So in the moment, things come up, you know that you throw in.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I want to ask you about the villain because it`s a little unusual villain in this movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How would you describe?\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Well, when I was told about the idea of this entity, this sort of self-aware kind of computer program that the Esai Morales, our brilliant, sort of like human villain is working at the behest of, I thought that sounds very "Mission Impossible." It`s a bit sci-fi, mission has always been about new technology.\par \par \uc2 But in the time, since we started shooting to now, the notion of AI has become more and more prominent in our sort of social discussions.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: And it feels incredibly prescient now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Speaking of existential threats, potential "Mission Impossibles." You`re on strike right now with the writers.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s a possibility that the actors may go on strike.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In not too distant future. These things need to be worked out or there is no more Hollywood, there is no more entertainment.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What are your thoughts in general on the strikes?\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Well, things need to be fair, you know? Things need to be equitable and the infrastructure needs to be adapted to accommodate the new ways we watch film and TV, streaming.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, the tech companies need to understand that for things to continue, everyone needs to be treated fairly. And as soon as that gets sorted out, and it will do hopefully, things will be okay.\par \par \uc2 But we can`t face another content drought next year, you know? We`ve only just got back on our feet where everyone is only just back in the cinema. If everyone next year is ready to go and there`s nothing to watch, that`s going to be a terrible thing.\par \par \uc2 So we need to just talk sensibly and get it all sorted out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m curious to get your opinion on movie making, filmmaking and also artificial intelligence. You know, when we keep it in the back of our mind what`s going on with the writers.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: The only thing AI can do is create mediocrity, because all it can do is aggregate what`s out there. So yes, it can write a script, but it will be rubbish, do you know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 AI has had no childhood trauma. AI has never had a boyfriend or a girlfriend, never got heartbroken.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re spot on.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: It`s never been through anything the impetus to create art. When it has its own personality and its own experiences, it can write a film about what it`s like to have no body, but at the moment, it can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 And so to rely on it would be to just make everything mediocre and we have to fight mediocrity in order to create great art. I know that sounds lofty.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, no, no that might be one of the best answers I`ve ever heard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the idea of AI. Hey, Simon Pegg, we appreciate you joining us.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Pleasure, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re one of the most versatile talents in the business.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good luck moving forward.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Lovely to be here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One" is in theaters now.\par \par \uc2 Go see it. Run, don`t walk. We`ll be right back with one more surprise. Stay right there.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before we go we always like it when babies stop by the studio especially if there are babies. Ramy Inocencio, introduce your family.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Gayle, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I swear they were quiet two seconds ago. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They were comfortably wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: So, this is Ian and Ethan here, in from London.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Can we help? Come on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here now Uncle Nate and Uncle Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got these --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You didn`t introduce your husband.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on. It is okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Anyways.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How old are they?\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: A year-and-a-half, twins. We did fertility and surrogacy, December 24, 2021.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, what did you do?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, no. Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: So great to see you guys, I think.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ramy, how do you look so sharp? You`re looking dapper.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: You`re going to have papa later on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jon Tower just said, we can go here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can do better. Give me another chance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome, welcome. It is really good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s cut that again, right? This is not live, is it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good to see you in person, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. We will see you Monday on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 This was a great idea 30 seconds ago.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s why I got a vasectomy. No more babies for me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before we go, let`s take a look at all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend. See you Monday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Bye.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You just can`t handle it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The extreme weather all across the country, nearly 90 million Americans under heat advisories with triple digit temperatures all the way from the Pacific to the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: There is not going to be any break for folks here in the Southwest. It is expected to be hotter this weekend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Millions from Pennsylvania to New Hampshire we`re left in the path of dangerous flooding.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: This is what`s left of Route 218. Behind me, you can still hear the sound of the sound of the water rushing down this cliff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a dream run at Wimbledon for upstart, American tennis star, Christopher Eubanks.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER EUBANKS (Tennis player): The coolest thing about is when I checked my ESPN alerts for the past week, a lot of times my name was on it.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: People are still throwing things at concerts. This time, Harry Styles got hit in the eye on Sunday at his show in Vienna, Austria.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What happened to the good old days when people were throwing garments on stage?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I think that still happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: By garments, you mean panties and bras.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I didn`t say it, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Yes, what happened to underwear --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You threw your panties on stage before? You didn`t answer the question, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I refuse to answer on the grounds of potentially incriminating --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But she did agree to answer some questions because --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, hey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, hey. I`ve been waiting for it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARVEY ("Family Feud" host): Good or bad, give me any L word you called your man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lamb chop.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARVEY: Lamb chop.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Well, you will always do my lamb chop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it was great fun. It was great fun.\par \par \uc2 BEN PLATT (Actor): It is a very tiny indie film with 19 days to shoot and children were shooting upstate and there`s goose poop everywhere, so like it`s the fact that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is what? What? Goose --\par \par \uc2 MOLLY GORDON (Actor): Goose poop.\par \par \uc2 BEN PLATT: There was a lot of goose poop.\par \par \uc2 NOAH GALVIN (Actor): There was a lot of goose poop. There was like a lot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it. Got it.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY GORDON: I am sure "Mission Impossible" have someone to clean up the goose poop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Duh-duh-duh-duh --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s back and this is "Mission Impossible" week. Are you a good guy or a bad guy?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How would you describe yourself in this movie?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA KIRBY (Actor): A bit of both.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 VANESSA KIRBY: I mean, I was preferring the bad guy. He presented me this beautiful box of chocolate like out of nowhere.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So Tom Cruise is doing these stunts with chocolate in his pocket?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where it`s your heart and mind at that point?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER MCQUARRIE ("Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One" director): It is very much an out of body experience. It`s life and death.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: And Gayle, you also --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he appreciates the fans.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: You do your own stunts here. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, walking from here to there, it is really tough for me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When it comes to thick, is it okay for guys to call the woman thick, like If a guy says you know she`s thicker than a Snicker, or she is thicker than a pot of cold oatmeal.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE BUTEAU (Actor): What in the Family Feud is going on?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is thicker than a first grade pencil. You know what I am saying like --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE BUTEAU: Because big women are beautiful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. That`s right. Damn right.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE BUTEAU: You didn`t agree. I am just kidding.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m still in the thicker than a first grade pencil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There it is. There it is. There it is. You`re learning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got to end on a positive note.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hold on. Hold on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come on out here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Get her in here. If we get you off to a good start in the morning, it is only because she gets us off to a good start in the morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we heard you like sweet potato pie.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: I love sweet potato pie. You know what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Make a wish.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Birthday, Shawna.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may  not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or  broadcast without the prior written permission of VIQ Media  Transcription, Inc. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright  or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230714ej7e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for July 5, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Carter Evans, Meg Oliver, Charlie D`Agata, Lilia  Luciano, Chris Warren, Scott MacFarlane, Weijia Jiang, Kris Van Cleave \par \uc2 3,702 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight: new details about the cocaine found in the White House, as questions mount about how it got into the West Wing.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service investigation, as we learn the illegal substance was found near cubbies used by visitors to store their cell phones.\par \par \uc2 A.T. SMITH (Former Deputy Director, \uc2 U.S. Secret Service\uc2 ): The White House is the most iconic building in the world. It is embarrassing, to a certain point.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Shocking allegations. Prosecutors suggest the man arrested near President Obama`s home with weapons and ammunition got the address from President Trump`s social media platform.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: Storms will be popping off all day and even into the overnight. We`re talking flooding, large hail, damaging winds. In the Northeast, we`re going to be five to 10 degrees above average.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The suspect in Philadelphia`s July 4th mass shooting in court today, as the country reels from a wave of gun violence.\par \par \uc2 TABATHA TAYLOR (Shreveport, Louisiana, Councilwoman): You will not get away with this. I want whoever you are to pay.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A growing number of shark attacks as some of the country`s most popular beach destinations is putting swimmers on high alert. And, in New York, five shark bites were reported in just two days off Long Island beaches.\par \par \uc2 HADLEY BOGGS (Employee): It`s nice to have some financial freedom.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Help wanted from teens whose paychecks are up nearly 12 percent for those summer jobs.\par \par \uc2 HUGO MALDONADO (Regional Operations Manager, Los Angeles County Parks and Recreation Department): We`re now competing with supermarkets. We`re now competing with fast food restaurants.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: I`m Kris Van Cleave in Chicago. A program here is giving new life to old cars and creating new opportunities for young people.\par \par \uc2 JEFF BATTLES (Shooting Victim): I refuse to be a stereotype. I won`t let anything that I have been through stop me from excelling, succeeding.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening, and thank you for joining us this Wednesday night. I`m Jericka Duncan, in for Norah.\par \par \uc2 We begin with the Secret Service leading an investigation into who brought cocaine into the White House. The surprising discovery of a bag of suspicious white powder initially caused a panic, leading to the temporary closure of the building on Sunday. The illegal drug was found near a visitor`s entrance for those taking a tour of the West Wing. Today, lab tests came back positive for cocaine.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and the first family were at Camp David at the time.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Weijia Jiang starts us off tonight from the White House with more.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, good evening.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the White House says it is very important to President Biden to get to the bottom of who brought that illegal drug inside, but officials caution, this could be a challenging investigation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): Tonight, the leading theory is that someone on a White House tour may have left a small bag of cocaine in the West Wing, according to senior law enforcement officials.\par \par \uc2 The discovery Sunday night put the White House on temporary lockdown until the substance was deemed not a threat. The bag was discovered on the ground floor in a common area near the entry to the West Wing, not far from the Situation Room. It was close to a set of storage cubbies where visitors leave their cell phones during a tour.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service is leading the investigation, looking at visitor logs and camera surveillance.\par \par \uc2 A.T. SMITH (Former Deputy Director, \uc2 U.S. Secret Service\uc2 ): There are camera systems that they will go back as a part of this investigation and research. Not every camera is trained on every body at every minute. So there`s always going to be a possibility that this could go unanswered.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: White House visitors undergo background checks and go through magnetometers on site. But the Secret Service does not have canine units that target illegal drugs. Instead, the dogs search for explosives, biohazardous materials and weapons.\par \par \uc2 This episode shines a light on the fact that you can bring in illegal substances into the White House. So what`s preventing a visitor from bringing in anthrax or something that`s not magnetic into the White House?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): I totally understand the question. But it is under investigation. So we`re going to let the Secret Service do their job. I`m just not going to get ahead of if or whens or changes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Senator Tom Cotton, a Republican from Arkansas, sent a letter to the director of the Secret Service to say that Congress needs to know if the White House complex is not secure.\par \par \uc2 And he asked for a plan to correct any security flaws -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Weijia Jiang at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there`s new information on the January 6 defendant arrested near the home of former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  here in D.C. New court filings reveal how the suspect may have gotten the former president`s address.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane has more on what federal prosecutors have learned.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): After federal agents spotted and chased Taylor Taranto outside the D.C. home of former President Obama, they say they found these guns, hundreds of rounds of ammunition, and a machete in Toronto`s van parked nearby.\par \par \uc2 The feds had already been trying to arrest Taranto for allegedly being part of the mob that attacked the Capitol January 6, for which he faces four misdemeanor charges. Late today, federal prosecutors told a judge Taranto could have learned Obama`s address from a social media post by former President Trump. They allege Trump posted what he claimed was Obama`s address on his TRUTH Social platform the same day Taranto was arrested here the home.\par \par \uc2 Taranto is also accused of targeting Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  of Maryland, who led Trump`s second impeachment. They say Taranto posted a video on June 18th inside an elementary school near Raskin`s home.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Prosecutors today argued Taranto also made a threat to the office of House Speaker Kevin McCarthy late last month.\par \par \uc2 In the current case, Taranto has not yet entered a plea, but he`s back in court tomorrow morning because prosecutors want him jailed until trial -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, those details very, very concerning.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to some breaking news. The U.S. is accusing Russia of harassing U.S. military drones in Syria. This is the view from the Reaper drones. Officials say they were conducting a mission against ISIS, when Russian fighter jets dropped parachute flares in front of them, forcing the U.S. drones to veer off course. One Russian plane flew in front of the drone and engaged in an afterburner.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. calls these actions reckless and unprofessional.\par \par \uc2 Well, turning now to the weather. More than 39 million Americans are under heat alerts tonight, and nearly 16 million of you are in the path of severe weather from Colorado to Illinois.\par \par \uc2 For a look ahead, let`s bring in meteorologist Chris Warren from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chris, good evening.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 More summertime thunderstorms with multiple threats, damaging winds, large hail. Could see some tornadoes and more flash flooding. These are the areas of concern through this evening. This includes parts of the Midwest, portions of Texas, into Colorado, Oklahoma, and Kansas. And some of these storms will be going overnight into the morning.\par \par \uc2 And then, tomorrow, that severe weather threat picks back up, not just possible, but likely to see some of these strong storms, heat building again across parts of the Northeast, upper 80s and even some lower 90s. And about the heat, the Earth has been very warm. When you average all the temperatures, the surface temperatures around the world, the 3rd was the hottest day on record, until July 4 becoming, Jericka, now the hottest day on record across the Earth.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Wow. Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 Well, we turn now to the latest wave of deadly gun violence in the United States. Nationwide, 21 people have been killed and 124 injured in mass shootings in just the first five days of this month. Well, today, the suspect accused of killing five people and injuring two children in Philadelphia on Monday was arraigned on murder charges.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lilia Luciano reports from the outside of the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voiceover): Tonight, officials in Philadelphia say Kimbrady Carriker posted disturbing messages on social media and left a will at his house before going on a rampage Monday evening.\par \par \uc2 Surveillance video shows the moment the suspect appears to open fire.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KRASNER\uc2  (Philadelphia District Attorney): So far as we know, this defendant did not know any of the victims. There was no dispute between him and any of the victims.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Carriker was charged today with five counts of first-degree murder and held without bail after police say he carried an AR-15-style rifle and a 9-millimeter ghost gun to the Kingsessing neighborhood and opened fire at random, killing five people.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, surveillance footage released today appears to show the moment a gunman opened fire on a crowd in Fort Worth, Texas, late Monday night; 11 people were shot, three killed. Two days later, police still have no motive and no suspects.\par \par \uc2 KADESHA WEATHERLY (Mother of Paul Willis): My family is heartbroken. This shouldn`t have happened.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: These are just two of about 20 mass shootings since the beginning of July. Just overnight, across the country, there were at least six mass shootings, killing at least five people and injuring 41.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPHINE WAMAH (Sister of Joseph Wamah): I`m going to miss that beautiful smile.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Back in Philly, family members and community leaders gathered today to mourn; 31-year-old Joseph Wamah loved art.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPHINE WAMAH: I really love him. And the fact that you did this to us, like, for your own agenda, for your own reason, it`s just really pissing me off.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Today, the city of Philadelphia filed a lawsuit against some of the largest suppliers of ghost guns and ghost gun kits, which describes that type of untraceable weapon that the shooter was carrying Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Lilia Luciano in Philadelphia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, swimmers and local officials are on high alert following a recent uptick in shark attacks. At least five shark bites have been reported in recent days off New York`s Long Island.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver reports on what`s behind the increase.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): Beachgoers continue to flock to Long Island for sun and surf, despite reports of at least five people bitten by sharks since Monday.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I`m not going to go in the water today.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Today, state officials returned to the shoreline, patrolling from above with drones.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOODMAN (New York State Park Police): That gives us the best opportunity to see sharks or other dangerous marine life before it actually becomes a problem.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Two teenagers suffered suspected shark bites at separate Long Island beaches Monday. And, yesterday, three adults were injured. None of them were serious.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, there has been at least 23 reported shark attacks across the country, compared to 51 in all of last year. One person died.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG O`CONNELL (Executive Director, O`Seas Conservation Foundation): If you go in the water right now in Long Island, you are swimming with sharks.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Craig O`Connell is a shark biologist. He says conservation efforts have increased the shark population, especially among sand tiger sharks. After nearly 50 were spotted on Tuesday, a Long Island beach was closed for more than an hour.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG O`CONNELL: Sand tiger sharks are relatively calm, docile sharks. You see them in the aquariums quite often. They have these menacing jaws, absolutely massive teeth, but to be the culprit for these attacks, to me, is incredibly rare.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: So rare, the risk of swimmers being killed by a shark is about one in four million.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Get out of the water!\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: O`Connell says sharks are more interested in fish than people.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG O`CONNELL: If we were on their menu, we would absolutely not stand a chance.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: O`Connell says, if you want to enjoy the water and stay safe, always stay in a life-guarded area, never swim alone or at dawn or dusk.\par \par \uc2 As far as those drones patrolling overhead, so far today, they haven`t detected a single shark along any Long Island beach -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Definitely some good news. Thank you, Meg.\par \par \uc2 Well, turning overseas, the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  says it stopped Iranian warships from seizing two oil tankers today. It happened in the Gulf of Oman near the Strait of Hormuz. The Navy released video of one of the tankers taking gunfire from the Iranians. No one was hurt, but bullets hit close to crew living spaces. Officials say the Iranian ships took off when confronted with the Navy destroyer the USS McFaul.\par \par \uc2 Well, the area around Europe`s largest nuclear plant is on edge tonight, with Ukraine and Russia accusing each other of planning to attack the facility. It remains under Russian control.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata was in the city of cap earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): The specter of a nuclear disaster looms again tonight, both sides warning of a catastrophic attack.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: President Zelenskyy accuses Russian troops of planting objects resembling explosives on top of reactor units. That follows Russian claims Ukraine is plotting to strike it with missiles packed with nuclear material gathered from other facilities.\par \par \uc2 Neither side providing any evidence, and the nuclear watchdog the \uc2 IAEA\uc2  said there`s no visible indications of mines or explosives. Residents in Zaporizhzhia have become used to living in the shadow of the threat. Drills are routinely held in the region for civilians.\par \par \uc2 But, this time, it feels different, says chairman of the regional council Olena Zhuk (sp?).\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZHUK (Chairman, Zaporizhzhia Regional Council): It`s obvious. For me, it`s completely obvious that they will try to do something.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Russia already stands accused of recent sabotage, blamed for blowing up the Nova Kakhovka dam just last month, wiping out dozens of towns and villages.\par \par \uc2 Young mother Irina Korniyenko (sp?) has withstood the worst of the war so far.\par \par \uc2 (IRINA KORNIYENKO speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "We`re ready for everything," she says. "So much has already happened here that I think nothing can scare us anymore."\par \par \uc2 Listening closely, 11-year-old Alena (sp?) struggles to match her mother`s courage.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: We were told that residents would be alerted by mobile phone what to do in case of an emergency, like get inside and close the doors in windows.\par \par \uc2 But Ukraine`s Health Ministry has said people should be prepared to evacuate at a moment`s notice -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Such a stressful situation.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 Well, the summer job market is heating up for America`s teenagers.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Money Watch, CBS` Carter Evans explains why businesses are turning to younger employees and higher wages.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): Once a coveted summer job, lifeguards are hard to come by this year, forcing some pools in Los Angeles to shut down.\par \par \uc2 HUGO MALDONADO (Regional Operations Manager, Los Angeles County Parks and Recreation Department): We`re short about 200 lifeguards. I have never seen anything like it.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And you can`t even attract them at close to 20 bucks an hour?\par \par \uc2 HUGO MALDONADO: No, we can`t. We`re now competing with supermarkets. We`re now competing with fast food restaurants. All of those sectors have increased their wages.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: on average hourly wages for workers 16 to 24 years old were up nearly 12 percent from last summer.\par \par \uc2 NICK BUNKER\uc2  (Research Director, Indeed Hiring Lab): Now, if you`re a prospective job seeker, you`re looking around and you realize, wait, that job makes how much now? And you`re starting to reconsider jobs you hadn`t before.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: This seems like a really good time to be a teenager looking for a job.\par \par \uc2 NICK BUNKER\uc2 : This is probably one of the advantageous times. You know, strike now, while the iron is hot.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Mashti Malone`s ice cream shops struggled to scoop up seasonal employees last year, but not this summer.\par \par \uc2 MEHDI SHIRVANI (Co-Owner, Mashti Malone`s): I was very overwhelmed with all the applicants.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Co-owner Mehdi Shirvani now has to turn them away.\par \par \uc2 MEHDI SHIRVANI: To be honest with you, we`re further overstaffed right now.\par \par \uc2 MAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 MEHDI SHIRVANI: I offer $17 an hour to start.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: For scooping ice cream?\par \par \uc2 MEHDI SHIRVANI: Yes. They make an average $22 to $23 per hour, including tip.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Not a bad wage for Hadley Boggs` first ever summer job.\par \par \uc2 HADLEY BOGGS (Employee): I was shocked. It`s nice to have some financial freedom.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The 17-year-old turned down a job at a grocery store that paid less.\par \par \uc2 HADLEY BOGGS: I hope to save for college and also have some fun money on the side that I can spend my senior year.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Just one of many who will head back to school with pockets full of cash.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, CBS News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, major delivery disruptions could be looming, as union negotiations break down at UPS.\par \par \uc2 That`s straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The largest labor strike in the U.S. since the 1950s could be weeks away.\par \par \uc2 Talks between shipping giant UPS and the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  broke down this morning. Now both sides are accusing the other of walking away from negotiations. Right now, roughly 340,000 UPS workers who deliver about 19 million packages a day in the U.S. could go on strike by August 1.\par \par \uc2 Well, there are some concerns tonight about plans to update the U.S. citizenship test. The proposed changes include a new English-speaking section where test-takers would describe photos and a new series of multiple-choice questions in the civics section. Some immigration advocates fear that the changes will make it harder for those who are less proficient in English.\par \par \uc2 Well, weight loss company Jenny Craig is back from the brink. We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You have heard of Jenny Craig.\par \par \uc2 It`s the weight loss company that recently said it was shutting down after more than four decades. Well, it is back, but only online. Wellful, which owns the rival weight loss system Nutrisystem, has acquired the Jenny Craig brand.\par \par \uc2 It`s planning a relaunch this fall. Weight loss companies have struggled in recent years with the rise of drugs that often contribute to weight loss.\par \par \uc2 Well, a lesson on fixing cars and how it`s changing lives. We will explain next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Finally tonight: A nonprofit is helping people turn a corner with classic cars.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave shows us the Chicago auto mechanic who is giving young men and women direction.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Jeff Battles is finding a new direction through his love of old cars after the 19-year-old was shot last year.\par \par \uc2 JEFF BATTLES (Shooting Victim): Wrong place, wrong time, the wrong people. It hit me in my right shoulder and came through my neck right here.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: He says that was a wake-up call.\par \par \uc2 JEFF BATTLES: I almost, like, lost my life, man. I got to change. I got to do better.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Doing better brought to Chicago teen to the Automotive Mentoring Group and its founder, Alex Levesque.\par \par \uc2 ALEX LEVESQUE (Founder, Automotive Mentoring Group): The only way that you can change the behavior of a person is if you change the way they think.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Fixing up old cars to teach new skills and find good- paying jobs for people many have written off.\par \par \uc2 You bring in a rough crowd.\par \par \uc2 ALEX LEVESQUE: That`s what I like.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Why?\par \par \uc2 ALEX LEVESQUE: Because nobody else wants to deal with those guys. So I want to deal with those guys, because those are the guys that I see are the real problem.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: About 1,500 have passed through since 2007. Levesque says 85 percent have turned their lives around.\par \par \uc2 ALEX LEVESQUE: I don`t necessarily think that this is the answer to all of it. I just know it`s a damn good answer. And it`s what I know how to do.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And a lesson Jeff Battles is learning.\par \par \uc2 JEFF BATTLES: I refuse to be a stereotype. I`m starting from the foundation, and I`m going to work my way up.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Restoring cars and lives by building a future.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, CBS News, Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Mentoring makes a difference.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. For Norah O`Donnell and all of us here, I`m Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230725ej75000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Hot and Rainy July 4th Forecast; Airlines Work to Catch-Up After Delays, Cancellations; Philadelphia Mass Shooting; West Bank Raids; California Hotel Strike; China Crackdown on U.S. Businesses; Finding the Best Sunscreen; Presidential Politics; Blockbuster Summer; Meeting his Goals; A Grand Old Flag; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed O`Keefe, Anne-Marie Green, Christina Ruffini, Stephanie Abrams,  Kris Van Cleave, Wakisha Bailey, Ramy Inocencio, Jonathan Vigliotti,  Margaret Brennan, Nikki Battiste, Shanelle Kaul, Anthony Mason \par \uc2 14,514 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 July 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Happy Fourth of July and welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Anne-Marie Green.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I`m Ed O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And I`m Christina Ruffini.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Gayle, Tony, and Nate are all off, but we`re here for you today. Here`s today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 MAN: For Independence Day, all of these areas that are highlighted in this pink color could see some storms in the afternoon; for cookouts, could see them in the evening for firework shows.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Millions of Americans brace for severe weather with a busy travel season in full swing for the holiday.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Another deadly mass shooting, this time in Philadelphia. Police captured the suspect allegedly armed with an AR-style rifle.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE OUTLAW (Philadelphia Police Department commissioner): Thank God, our officers were here on scene. I can`t even describe the level of bravery and courage that was shown.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A $28,000.00 reward is now being offered in the search for suspects who opened fire during a block party in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: A massive Israeli raid in the occupied West Bank enters a second day.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re not here to conquer or hold the ground. When it`s finished, it will be over.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thousands of Southern California hotel workers are out on strike.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Workers say they deserve better pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Opening Day at Wimbledon.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Novak Djokovic brings into the second round.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Djokovic remains perfect.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Joey Chestnut.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The official weigh-ins for Nathan`s Famous Fourth of July Hot Dog Eating Contest, Joey Chestnut hopes to retain his crown.\par \par \uc2 MAN: When it comes to competitive eating, he is the GOAT.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Shooting for his 16th win.\par \par \uc2 JOEY CHESTNUT (Nathan`s Famous Fourth of July Hot Dog Eating Contest competitor): It is not going to be pretty, but I`m going to do whatever it takes to get those dogs down.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  offensive tackle Jordan Mailata singing "Isn`t She Lovely" by Stevie Wonder)\par \par \uc2 MAN (voice over): Oh, the pipes on the big fella. That`s \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  tackle Jordan Mailata singing "Isn`t She Lovely" by Stevie Wonder at his wedding.\par \par \uc2 MAN (voice over): She`s dancing with her dad, and he is singing to her.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN (voice over): It is a beautiful wedding. I mean, he has a beautiful voice.\par \par \uc2 MAN (voice over): He sure does.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: That`s obviously a beautiful song and he nails it.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: He keeps going --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: And he nails it. He can hold on to a note the way he can hold on to a football.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Get you a man who can sing. Let me tell you.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Yes, yes. just -- you know, don`t leave. We like your singing, but we need you back on the field, too, you know, later on.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I don`t know (sp?).\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: No, you don`t. I am a Giants fan. Please go sing.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Sorry. We`re not ganging up (sp?).\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: All right, so welcome, everyone to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: We`ve got a -- look, before you go out to the parades or the cookouts, we have plenty to share with you this morning. It`s summer blockbuster season and your mission, should you choose to accept it, could be to see the new "Mission: Impossible" movie or maybe you want to think pink with "Barbie."\par \par \uc2 Either way, Erik Davis from Fandango will be here to tell you what to see and what you might want to skip.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Plus, we got a chance to visit the actual Star-Spangled Banner, the actual flag, which is on display in Washington.\par \par \uc2 On this Fourth of July, we`re going to take a look at how the stars and stripes became a powerful symbol of resistance after the British burned down the White House more than 200 years ago.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: But we begin with bad weather likely to get in the way of your barbecues and your fireworks across the country. Storms could make things rather unpleasant and even dangerous in the upper Midwest and here in New York City. It is also hot in much of the country with temperatures far above average in some places.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: For more on how this is affecting holiday plans. We`ve got Kris Van Cleave on the travel beat in Phoenix, but we`ll start with \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This isn`t exactly what people want to hear.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): No, Ed. Good morning and happy Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 Our big picture shows a lot of heat and storms across the country, but we want to break it down by region. We`re going to start in the Northeast. We have a few showers this morning, but Mother Nature`s fireworks will kick off again this afternoon with more thunderstorms. Highs mainly in the 80s with Boston and Portland a touch cooler than average.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of cool, water temperatures only in the 60s and 70s. If you`re brave enough to get in the water, swim near a lifeguard as we have a rip current risk. Our water temperatures in the South, much warmer. We`re in the 80s. But afternoon storms could cut your day short at both the beach and the lake.\par \par \uc2 Now in the Midwest, storms could turn dangerous. Hail and gusty winds are a main threat. Behind this, by the way, much cooler air spreading south and east. And the thing is, we need that rain. Some cities have issued burn or firework bans as the drought continues to expand and worsen.\par \par \uc2 And of course, Ed, all of these storms that we`re following could cause more delays at the airports.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It could, but \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , we thank you.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And all this nasty weather is not making things easier for holiday travelers and it wouldn`t be a holiday without Kris Van Cleave in an airport.\par \par \uc2 Kris is currently in Phoenix with more on what to expect.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Good morning, Christina.\par \par \uc2 You know, airlines had been hoping for some independence from all of this dismal flying weather they`ve been going through. It doesn`t sound like that`s going to happen just yet.\par \par \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , hardest hit last week with all of the delays and cancellations. It is offering many flyers that had their trips disrupted, up to 30,000 frequent flyer miles as an apology, and as the trip home is now starting to loom in front of many travelers, the weather remains a giant question mark.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice over): This morning, the nearly 51 million Americans expected to travel for the Fourth will be watching the weather.\par \par \uc2 Be it on the roads or at packed airports that set an all-time record on Friday with nearly 2.9 million passing through TSA checkpoints. Some are still trying to get to their holiday destinations.\par \par \uc2 MONIQUE PERRUSSOT (Newark Liberty International Airport passenger): I am really, like, overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice over): Monique Perrussot is in New Jersey but is supposed to be in California.\par \par \uc2 MONIQUE PERRUSSOT: Well, I`m hoping that they put me on the next flight, so I don`t have to wait till six in the morning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice over): Airlines took advantage of a relatively calm Monday to catch up after seemingly endless storms have made holiday travel a huge challenge, delaying more than 66,000 flights and canceling over 9,000 more in the last 10 days alone.\par \par \uc2 HENRY HARTEVELDT\uc2  (Atmosphere Research Group travel industry analyst): When there`s a disruption, there is no flexibility left, no wiggle room in the system to accommodate the displaced passengers.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice over): Still compared to last year, U.S. airlines are canceling significantly fewer flights, but delays are up nearly 12 percent compared to this time in 2022.\par \par \uc2 AAA expects most Americans will drive to their destination and are finding gas prices are about four cents lower compared to last week and about $1.30 less than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The difference between the flight and then the car it was only going to be like a $200.00 difference.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice over): At LAX in Los Angeles, Quincy Jennings (sp?) feels lucky his flight landed on time.\par \par \uc2 QUINCY JENNINGS (SP?) (Traveler): I know that a lot of people over the past weekend flights had got delayed, so I was hoping that mine wasn`t. And it wasn`t, which was a great thing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: TSA expects busy, but not record-setting crowds at airports through next weekend as people stretch out this holiday. On the roads, AAA expects midday today and tomorrow late afternoon into rush hour to be the busiest times to drive. Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Chris, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Turning now to two more mass shootings in America over this holiday period, we start in Philadelphia with an attack across four city blocks in the southwest part of the city, at least five people were killed, two other people were injured, both of them children.\par \par \uc2 Police say the gunman had an AR-style rifle and a handgun and he is now in custody.\par \par \uc2 Wakisha Bailey from our CBS News Philadelphia station has the latest.\par \par \uc2 Wakisha, good morning\par \par \uc2 WAKISHA BAILEY: Good morning, Anne-Marie.\par \par \uc2 Well, the youngest victim hurt by gun violence is only two years old.\par \par \uc2 Now, Philadelphia`s mayor says that he is horrified by this shooting. And he also says that his heart is with the loved ones and the families involved, but there is still one question that`s not answered. That`s why.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 COMMISSIONER DANIELLE OUTLAW: This person decided to leave their home and to target individuals.\par \par \uc2 WAKISHA BAILEY (voice over): On the streets of Southwest Philadelphia Monday night, police see a 40-year-old man opened fire hitting at least seven people.\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA POLICE DEPARTMENT (voice over): Multiple shots fired.\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA POLICE DEPARTMENT (voice over): Units use caution. We`re getting multiple calls still coming out. Anybody coming in 56 and Chester (sp?) use caution. They`re still out there shooting.\par \par \uc2 WAKISHA BAILEY (voice over): When police arrived at the scene, they could hear the gunshots ringing out.\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA POLICE DEPARTMENT (voice over): All units use caution. We got somebody with a long gun.\par \par \uc2 WAKISHA BAILEY (voice over): Officers track down the alleged gunman and cornered him in an alley. He was taken into custody.\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA POLICE DEPARTMENT (voice over): Male is wearing a bulletproof vest with extra magazines in there as well.\par \par \uc2 WAKISHA BAILEY (voice over): Police say he was wearing a bulletproof vest with multiple rounds of ammunition in it, armed with an AR-style rifle and handgun.\par \par \uc2 Officers arrested another person, but their connection to the alleged shooter is unclear.\par \par \uc2 COMMISSIONER DANIELLE OUTLAW: As victims were being shot, we have another person that we believe acquired a gun and returned fire in the direction of the shooter that we have in custody.\par \par \uc2 WAKISHA BAILEY (voice over): The ages of the four victims killed range from 20 to 59. Police believe a fifth victim who was discovered dead in his home just after midnight is related to the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Two children were injured, age two and 13 years old. They were taken to a hospital.\par \par \uc2 COMMISSIONER DANIELLE OUTLAW: We`re canvassing the area to get as much as we can to identify witnesses and do everything that we can to figure out the why behind this happening.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WAKISHA BAILEY: And police have not identified the victims or at least a possible motive. Meanwhile, as of Sunday, we are told by city records that there have been at least 212 homicides here in Philadelphia and that`s a 19 percent decrease as the same period as last year. Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Wakisha Bailey, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Gun violence also turned deadly overnight in Fort Worth, Texas. Three people were killed and eight others wounded when shots were fired in the Como neighborhood hours after a local festival. The motive for that shooting remains unclear and police have not arrested anyone in connection with the attack.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Turning overseas, as Israel continues its huge raid in the occupied West Bank, we are learning about a suspected car ramming and stabbing attack in the Israeli city of Tel Aviv. Local media say about five people were hurt and the attacker was shot.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in the West Bank city of Jenin, Israeli forces are continuing their deadly assault targeting what they say are terrorist hideouts.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voice over): Terrified from Israel`s biggest incursion in nearly 20 years, more than 3,000 Palestinians have now fled a refugee camp in the northern West Bank town of Jenin.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voice over): "Today is a black day," said this woman. "They invaded."\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voice over): "I felt the world caught on fire."\par \par \uc2 An Israeli Army spokesman says they have not come to conquer, but to dismantle terrorist infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Israel`s assault kicked off just after midnight Monday. Authorities say they`ve neutralized an underground shaft for explosives, confiscated weapons, and broken up two situation rooms in an attempt to root out Iranian-backed Palestinian militants.\par \par \uc2 Israel arrested about 100 people, some still in custody, others released.\par \par \uc2 It is well known that Fatah, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and Islamic Jihad have an armed troop presence in Jenin.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Israeli prime minister): Jenin has become a safe haven for terrorists.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voice over): Israel`s Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  pressed Washington for support.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : I have no doubt that as Israel exercises its inherent right of self-defense, the United States will stand firmly by our side.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voice over): He said his country`s anti-terror operations will last as long as required with more Palestinian anger and revenge near doubtless in its wake.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Ramy Inocencio, London.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Well, in this country, it is day three of a major hotel workers` strike in Southern California, right now in the middle of the busy holiday weekend. Fifteen thousand hospitality workers including housekeepers, cooks, and bellhops walked off the job Sunday to demand better pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is in Los Angeles with what`s at stake as hotels scramble to accommodate guests.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (GROUP protesting.)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice over): Hospitality workers in Downtown L.A. say striking was no longer a choice, but a necessity.\par \par \uc2 ALETA MANZAO (Protester): It`s really expensive.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice over): Aleta Manzao (sp?) says even working 40 hours a week cleaning hotel rooms isn`t enough to keep her and her three kids afloat.\par \par \uc2 How hard is it to make ends meet these days?\par \par \uc2 ALETA MANZAO (SP?): It is really hard paying the rent, paying bills.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice over): She is one of thousands taking to the streets of Los Angeles and Orange Counties demanding a raise to be able to live closer to the cities where they work where housing costs are some of the highest in the country.\par \par \uc2 MARIA HERNANDEZ (\uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11): The workers have told them what they need to actually be able to live here and survive here.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice over): The union wants an immediate bump of $5.00 an hour. The hotels are offering $2.50. For an employee working 40 hours a week, that`s a difference of over $5,000.00 a year.\par \par \uc2 The union also wants better health benefits, higher pension contributions, and less strenuous workloads.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There isn`t any -- enough workers to cover my shift.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice over): During the pandemic, hotels cut jobs, but as the tourism industry bounced back, workers say they`re still being asked to do more with less.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the bargaining group representing most of the hotels involved said: "The union has shown no desire to engage in productive good faith negotiations. The hotels want to continue to provide strong wages, affordable quality family healthcare, and a pension."\par \par \uc2 The hotels say they plan to continue serving guests in the short term, while the workers are asking for guests` support by taking their business elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jonathan Vigliotti.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: This morning, Americans are being urged to reconsider travel to China over fears of possible wrongful detention. It comes as Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is set to visit the country this week.\par \par \uc2 "Face the Nation" moderator and chief foreign affairs correspondent, Margaret Brennan recently traveled to Beijing where there is a concern a new Chinese counter-espionage law could put foreign companies at risk.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice over): Chinese state TV broadcast these images of police raiding the Shanghai offices of U.S. firm, Capvision.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice over): Chinese authorities claimed it was an ordinary law enforcement act.\par \par \uc2 NICK BURNS\uc2  (U.S. Ambassador to China): Has happened to four or five of our firms over the last couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice over): U.S. Ambassador to China \uc2 Nick Burns\uc2  told Chinese officials the U.S. is deeply concerned by this intimidation.\par \par \uc2 NICK BURNS\uc2 : These are punitive actions, and they`re unwarranted, and they are often, you know, the Chinese take these actions, they raid an American firm, they fine them, they jail the employees.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice over): Bain and Mintz, other U.S. consulting firms have also been targeted. All of these companies conduct market research for Western firms to invest in China.\par \par \uc2 It is part of President Xi Jinping`s expanding control of the state and business, which may complicate his attempts to court American investment.\par \par \uc2 Burn says the issue is a broad new espionage law that cracks down on data collection, which opens up Americans to criminal prosecution for simply conducting what would be a normal business activity in any other country.\par \par \uc2 Roberta Lipson\uc2  runs a private hospital group in China where she has lived since the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTA LIPSON\uc2  (Chindex International CEO and president): American companies are concerned because of the ambiguity of the regulations and not really understanding what they`re about.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice over): She used to urge for the government to regulate. Now, she worries new restrictions will hurt medical research, and argues it would be disastrous for both the U.S. and China if businesses pulled out.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTA LIPSON\uc2 : Being here makes us better. Being here makes us want to be better than all of the other competition.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice over): For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Margaret Brennan, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`ve taken you to Phoenix, Philly, the Middle East, and China. And still ahead, important tips on how to protect your skin this summer.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:16 and change, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Still to come, another scary moment involving a roller coaster, this time riders including kids left hanging upside down.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Well, from SPF to UVA, choosing the right sunscreen can seem a little daunting, especially when there are so many choices. Here are some tips to keep in mind though, very simple.\par \par \uc2 No matter what you buy, apply sunscreen 30 minutes before going out into the sun, and then be sure to reapply every two hours. Those are the basics.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste takes a look at some of the different factors to consider when picking a sunscreen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voice over): With countless sunscreens on the market, trying to choose which is the most safe and effective can be overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH HALE (Dermatologist): I tell people to protect their skin every single day with a broad-spectrum sunscreen, SPF 30 or higher.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voice over): Dr. Elizabeth Hale says broad-spectrum protects from both UVA and UVB rays.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What`s the difference between UVA and UVB?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH HALE: That SPF number, that refers to a product`s ability to block UVB, which are shorter wavelength, and those are the rays that cause sunburn. But every single day ultraviolet A rays or UVA, those are longer wavelength, those penetrate every day, year-round, even through windows, through clouds, even in the middle of winter. They can contribute to skin cancer and otherwise aging of the skin.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voice over): There are two basic types of sunscreen -- chemical and mineral. Mineral sunscreens are physical. They work like a shield sitting on the surface of your skin and deflecting the sun`s rays. The main ingredients are titanium dioxide and zinc oxide, minerals that are classified as safe and effective by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If you plan to swim or sweat, you may want a chemical sunscreen. It works like a sponge absorbing the sun`s rays. Although those chemicals can be detected in the blood weeks after they were used, some doctors say that does not mean they are dangerous. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  says more studies need to be done.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH HALE: We know that skin cancer, which is the most common cancer diagnosed in the United States is also the most preventable.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voice over): For CBS MORNINGS, Nikki Battiste, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: And as you see right there, Black folks, too, all of that melanin is not enough to protect you alone. You`ve got to make sure that you wear sunscreen.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: You know what? I was really bad about doing it. So I got one of those little sticks, it looks like deodorant, and I put it next to my keys. So every time I leave the house now, I try to remember at least get it on my face.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Smart.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: All right, ahead, what to expect in the 2024 presidential campaign over the summer and beyond. We`ll talk to experts on both side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Doesn`t the White House look lovely this morning?\par \par \uc2 The race for the 2024 race and whoever might live in that house is heating up with at least 12 candidates, including former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the Republican field is crowded already. And on the Democratic side, Vice President Biden is facing two primary challengers, at least at this point.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to talk about the latest in the 2024 race are Republican strategist Leslie Sanchez and Democratic strategist Joel Payne.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you both.\par \par \uc2 Joel, I want to start with you. Because obviously, you know, the country is still absorbing what the Supreme Court has decided in terms of affirmative action, gay rights, the student loan forgiveness plan, not great if you`re somebody on your side of the aisle with these decisions, at least legally, societally, but politically, how might Democrats be able to respond and move forward based on what the Supreme Court has decided?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE (Democratic strategist): Well, Ed, I think you`re right to center first on how Democrats are feeling, which is not great in terms of the impact of these decisions. This is progress that has been pretty hard fought.\par \par \uc2 Politically, though, you`re right, and if you think about the three groups who were impacted by these decisions last week, or really four groups, voters of color, African Americans, and Hispanic American voters, young voters and LGBTQIA Americans -- they were all impacted. They were all in the crosshairs of these decisions by the Supreme Court. Those are key parts of the Biden coalition.\par \par \uc2 So if you`re thinking about how does this impact 2024? Those are going to be groups that are going to be buoyed, that are going to be more enthused to show up in the turnout in 2024 because of those decisions, and I think that is a consideration and I`m sure the folks in the White House are thinking about.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Leslie, I know, just about every Republican candidate is pretty pleased with what the Supreme Court decided last week. But this has been, at least in recent years, sort of the slowest build of a primary campaign like -- it didn`t seem like they`ve been out as much. Maybe they`re still waiting to see about the Trump indictment and whether things will go differently.\par \par \uc2 But you had a good indicator of what to watch for in the next few days because they`ve been spending so much time behind the scenes worrying about money.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ (GOP strategist): It`s always about money, Ed. You know, traditionally this time of summer before the presidential campaign is the invisible primary. It is when you have a lot of would-be candidates on the phone trying to see if they can bundle some of those big donors together.\par \par \uc2 A couple of things have happened. One, you`ve seen large powerful conservative organizations, like the Koch brothers put together their resources to the tune of 70-million-plus dollars to support candidates that are not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That was unheard of in 2016. It`s certainly not in 2020 to the extent that it is now.\par \par \uc2 And you have a lot more conversations behind the scenes of people waiting to see if they can really be that alternative.\par \par \uc2 So if we follow the money, or if you remember, in 2016, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn`t need that money. He said he was going to self-fund his campaign like the others did. Now that you`re seeing this coalescing, I think it`s going to change the dynamic later in the fall, and certainly leading into the winter.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Leslie, I wanted to follow up a little bit and ask you your take on essentially the culture wars that we`re seeing in the Republican primaries. And I am wondering, you know, we`re seeing some of those groups that Joel mentioned at the top, especially LGBTQIA groups being kind of in the crosshairs of some of the platforms of a lot of these candidates.\par \par \uc2 And I`m wondering, do you think that`s an effective strategy? Is that something voters really care about? And then, once you get into the general, is there a way to bring that back to the middle? How does that translate to the general population when they go to the polls?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: You know, there has been a long argument within the Republican Party about how you sound and look inclusive if you have candidates who are not inclusive.\par \par \uc2 So over the last two decades, there has been an increase in the diversity of, you would certainly say, of people that are running to leave the Republican Party. When issues like this come up, if Republicans do not stand strongly against things that dehumanize any aspect of our very broad coalition, I think politically, they pay a very high price.\par \par \uc2 They may not see it early on and think that they are going to get a quick point, but we have a long history showing when they`ve done this against the LGBTQ community or against Hispanics, against immigrants. I mean, you can go -- there is a long list. It is not a winning strategy, and I think that`s the cautionary tale, certainly for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Joel, I just want to ask you quickly about alternative candidates, if you will, you know, Robert F. Kennedy, \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2 , these are people who you would have considered sort of fringe and perhaps, they are still fringe now, but even fringe candidates can shift the conversation for the lead candidate.\par \par \uc2 Is there a concern within the Republican Party especially when you have somebody like a -- rather the Democratic Party, especially when you have somebody like John F. Kennedy, you know, who has some sort of, people could say wild conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I know we`re short on time. No, in a word and there is no concern.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think that the vote that you`re seeing in some of the early polls for an RFK, Jr. for \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2 , those are stand-in votes for Democrats who are probably a little anxious about having a candidate in advanced age that we really haven`t had before in Joe Biden being an octogenarian, and at a time where the country is really tense.\par \par \uc2 I think when it matters, I think Democrats will circle behind Joe Biden. You`ve already seen some big interest groups, big labor unions, big progressive groups already get behind President Biden, and I think you`ll continue to see that coalescing.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: All right, Leslie Sanchez and Joel Payne, thank you so much. Great seeing you. Happy Fourth of July by the way.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: You, too.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: Happy Fourth.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: All right ahead, some important holiday travel tips from Brian Kelly, The Points Guy. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: If it seems like a lot of people, you know, are traveling this holiday weekend other than us, of course, the TSA`s numbers back that up.\par \par \uc2 More than 10 million passengers have passed through the airports since Thursday, including 2.8 million on Friday and that is a new record.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now with some summer travel tips is Brian Kelly, the founder and CEO of The Points Guy.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Brian.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KELLY (The Points Guy founder and CEO): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Of course. All right. So if you`ve already traveled, you`re already in this. You can`t take it back now. You`ve got to come back.\par \par \uc2 So all of these travelers who are out there, when they are coming back to the airports, what should they expect? How should they navigate canceled flights? And how bad do you think it`s going to be?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KELLY: Well, you know, my biggest tip is make sure you get TSA PreCheck, which will drastically reduce the amount of time you spend in security lines. And also pack your patience.\par \par \uc2 You know, the air traffic system is based heavily on -- you know, delays are based on weather. So, so far, this Fourth of July week, we`re seeing good weather. So hopefully we`re seeing smooth sailing.\par \par \uc2 A week ago, we saw thousands of delays a day and that has smoothed out. So I always recommend download your airline`s app. Also download FlightAware. It will actually tell you where your flight is coming from, if there are delays, and you really just have to be proactive these days.\par \par \uc2 But we`re looking, hopefully, to a much smoother Fourth of July holiday week.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: All right, pack your patience. That`s my one bag that always seems to get lost, but I will work on it.\par \par \uc2 All right, if you`re driving, especially up and down the Eastern Corridor, which gets really crowded. What are the best times to hit the road, or the worst?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KELLY: You know, it really just depends on the city, but we recommend leaving -- and this is the same for flights as well -- as early in the morning as possible.\par \par \uc2 A ton of people are hitting the roads this year. We`re seeing gas prices down 30 percent from last summer when gas prices were through the roof. But you know, I always like using apps like GasBuddy, because gas prices vary dramatically state-to-state. It will actually help you plan and fill up at the cheapest gas stations.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: If you`re looking to go far like Europe this summer. We`ve already talked about this week how the prices are really through the roof. We talked yesterday about some ways to get around it.\par \par \uc2 But is this a good time to use those points? Is that an effective way to try to defray some of those costs?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KELLY: A hundred percent. I`ve used frequent flyer miles this summer to go to Europe. \uc2 Virgin Atlantic\uc2  has a really good fare sale going on right now, \uc2 Air France\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 My biggest tip is the best frequent flyer programs are not the \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 . They are the foreign frequent flyer programs. In fact, if you have the \uc2 American Express\uc2  points, they just launched a transfer bonus to Iberia. So don`t just look at the U.S. frequent flyer programs, look at foreign ones. And we`re seeing $600.00 air fares this summer in economy to Spain, and I`m a big fan of Portugal, and not just are the air fares cheap, but also once you get there, restaurants and everything else are much cheaper compared to other parts of Europe.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I mean, that sounds pretty good right now.\par \par \uc2 Real quickly, before we go, for the parents here at the table. If you`re trying to get your kids on vacation and not break the bank, you got any good tips for places to go?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KELLY: Well, the good thing is domestic airfares are about even compared to last year. So I recommend going domestically.\par \par \uc2 Our national parks are amazing like Lake Adirondack. We`re seeing cheaper hotel fares. So looking domestically, driving can save you dramatically.\par \par \uc2 But if you are looking to go internationally and take the kids, we`re seeing great airfares to London as far as -- as low as $400.00 on Norse Airlines. So yes, I recommend using Google Flights to find the cheapest flights.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: All right, this Colorado girl loves a national park.\par \par \uc2 Brian Kelly, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And later in \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, we`ll show you something that could help make your summer trips easier. We`re talking about luggage. See what`s available with a discount of more than 70 percent.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the stories you`ll be talking about today, but first, it`s 7:44 and changes as Ed O`Keefe would say, it is time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Countdown with Giselle.\par \par \uc2 Time now for "What to Watch." Shanelle Kaul filling in for Vlad, who is on Vlad to Dad leave.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Happy Fourth. We all got the blue memo.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Shades of blue this morning.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: You have a little bit of red.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I`m trying.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: One of us should have worn red. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: You got it in there. I figured out now why Vlad is always the only one dancing because you guys are all sitting.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Because he`s standing there, yeah.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: We dance, we fall out of the chair.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: All right, speaking of close calls, let`s talk about this first story because we told you yesterday about this story that happened, there was a crack in a roller coaster support beam at a park in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Jeremy Wagner/Storyful.)\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Remember this video?\par \par \uc2 Today, we have another scary amusement park story to kick off "What to Watch." So take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of rollercoaster riders stuck upside down from Scott Brass/\uc2 Facebook\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: This new video comes from a festival in Wisconsin -- where get this -- eight people, they got stuck upside down on the fireball ride on Sunday. This is just terrifying.\par \par \uc2 Seven riders were actually children and according to one of the witnesses there, they were stuck there for more than four hours.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: What?\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: That`s so sad.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Before first responders could get there and get them down. It was a huge mission there.\par \par \uc2 They were briefly checked out at a hospital before being sent home. A fire official blamed this on a mechanical failure and said the ride was recently inspected by the state.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Well, that is making me feel better.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Well, why these rides are always recently inspected?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Which is why I am sticking to the teacups.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Something is going wrong.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Yes, that`s the safest --\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Yes, carousel.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: I`ve had some bad experiences too on the teacups. Yes, I have.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Yes, I have. You don`t want to eat before you go on --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Anne-Marie --\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: That`s rule number one. No, this is really scary. And some of the kids that were there, they felt sick afterwards, they were really scared, they were shaken and it`s just one of those things where you think you`re going on this joy ride. Obviously, there is a little bit of thrill there, but you don`t think you`re actually in danger, right?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Four hours. Goodness gracious.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: That was a long time.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Anyways, everyone was safe after all, so that`s good to know.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about what`s happening in Hollywood today. The day of reckoning, almost here.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission: Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: There it is. There it is.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: There it is.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: There is the stunt.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: It is.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Oh, look at that.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: No problem for him, right? "Mission: Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One." Starring Tom Cruise officially hits theaters next week and it is expected to take moviegoers on the most dangerous mission yet.\par \par \uc2 So last night of course, the big Australian premiere and cruise was there. He took some selfies with some fans. He signed some autographs. Obviously, these people were just so excited he was there and by the way, it was his birthday.\par \par \uc2 After mingling with the crowd, he got a little bit of a surprise.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Jabba`s Movies/7 Network Australia.)\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: All right, and yesterday was his birthday, get this, 61st birthday.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Goodness gracious.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: And still going at it. Still jumping off cliffs.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: I know.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: He takes the AARP card and throws it in the --\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: That`s how you stay young.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I`m not done yet.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Shanel