od cast. It`s the viral movie of the year.\par \par \uc2 And so I think a lot of people are going to go see it.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But is it for kids?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: No, it`s not necessarily for young, young kids. There are 43 other Barbie movies that you could show your really young kids.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: I thought you were going to say there are 43-year-old kids, but this is going more --\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: But you know, they`re billing this as like a comic adventure.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Greta Gerwig It is directed by Greta Gerwig, who also co-wrote it. So it`s going to have some deeper themes as well. But yes, I think the young, young kids may not get it as much, but they`ll like how colorful it looks.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Now, are you supposed to do "\uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 " and then "Barbie" or "Barbie" and then "\uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 "? Because my brother is in it. He is going to do the seven hours. He is going, but can`t decide on the order.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Yes, they call it Barbenheimer I would do "\uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 " --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Or is it Oppenbarbie (sp?)?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Oh, yes. It could be either one.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: But I would do "\uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 " because it`s probably the drama.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "\uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 .")\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS (voice over): It`s a long drama. It`s based on J. Robert Oppenheimer, who is credited with creating the atomic bomb. It is a Christopher Nolan movie.\par \par \uc2 I would see that first and then the lighter movie second, so you`re walking out with a smile on.\par \par \uc2 I say, "Barbie" is the movie you see with your friends, "\uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 " is the movie you see on the biggest screen you can.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN (voice over): But "\uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 " is not your sort of frothy summer movie.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice over): Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN (voice over): Were you going to say that? I mean, it is not typical summer fair.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: No, and it`s not typical from Christopher Nolan necessarily either. He`s known for his like big sci-fi genre kind of head-spinning kind of movies, and this is more of a drama and a biopic.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But you actually say that the movie of the summer potentially is the new "Mission: Impossible" movie brought to you by \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Yes. I do.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes, I`m just pointing (sp?) -- well, whatever. It`s Tom Cruise. It`s the seventh "Mission: Impossible" movie though, right?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Yes, but --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But why does it have the potential to be the movie the summer?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Well, I mean, look, this is a franchise that gets better with each installment.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission: Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS (voice over): And I think it is the movie of the summer because it very much is that sort of quintessential action masterpiece that you want to watch right smack in the middle of summer. Things to know about this one, it is a part one of a part two. So "Dead Reckoning Part Two" --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice over): There will be another.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS (voice over): -- comes out next summer.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice over): They know how to -- but this is like the "Top Gun" of 2023?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS (voice over): I think so, you know, and I think this film is going to do even better because of "Top Gun: Maverick" last year. And, you know, this is Tom Cruise driving a motorbike off a cliff.\par \par \uc2 He is really doing these things.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice over): Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN (voice over): Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS (voice over): Putting his life on the line. I think audiences appreciate that about him, and I think that`s why they show up to see his movies.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: So what about another sequel, "Indiana Jones" that we just showed there? I mean, I`m reading that it is the best of the series.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Well, it is Indiana Jones punching Nazis for a couple of hours.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Indiana Jones and the Dial of Destiny.")\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS (voice over): You know, it`s America`s birthday, like that`s what I want to go see.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voice over): That`s very `Murica (sp?).\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS (voice over): You know, I will be literally taking my family to see this later this afternoon, and I thought it was delightful. It`s great to see Indiana Jones at retirement age, but counterbalanced with Phoebe Waller-Bridge, who plays his goddaughter --\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN (voice over): Right.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS (voice over): -- who is just wheeling and dealing and scheming the whole movie. The two of them are just really great.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And is there something worth taking the kids to see? Back to the kids.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Yes, well --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: They can`t see "Barbie." What should we go see?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Well, there is a "Haunted Mansion" movie that`s based on the classic ride from Disneyworld and Disneyland.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Haunted Mansion.")\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS (voice over): It`s not connected to the Eddie Murphy movie of 2003, but it is very much inspired by the ride you see in the parks. It has got a great cast. A little spooky, a little funny, a little exciting.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s good to kind of switch up -- change up summer a little bit at the end of July.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: All right, Erik Davis, trying to get us to the movies. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, how a symbol of American defiance during the War of 1812 became the inspiration for our national anthem.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to members of the team preserving this historic flag for future generations. But first, it`s 8:07, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Up next, Emmy-nominated "Ted Lasso" star Toheeb Jimoh takes Anthony Mason back to his former school in London.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH (Actor): It felt bigger when I was here, but I guess I`m probably just little, you know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This was your stage.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: This was my stage. I did all of my rehearsals here. I did all of my performances here. Like, I lived in this room. This was like the birthplace of, like, me wanting to be a performer, me wanting to be an actor. This was my safe haven.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: After the break, Jimoh shows Anthony his soccer skills and the stage where he fell in love with acting.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You know that song. We are revisiting some of our favorite Anthony Mason stories from this year, and this morning, we are featuring his conversation with Toheeb Jimoh, star of the hit series, "Ted Lasso" that recently wrapped up its third season.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice over): That`s a beautiful rendition as you know. The Emmy-nominated Jimoh won fans over as the charismatic soccer player turned restaurant owner, Sam Obisanya.\par \par \uc2 Before we know the fate of the Richmond Greyhounds, Anthony met up with the 25-year-old in his hometown of London.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is this where you used to play?\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes, man. This is where we used to do football tryouts here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): Toheeb Jimoh took us back to his middle school field at the Norwood School in the Brixton neighborhood of London.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: I`m going to flop now because I`m on camera.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): It`s here he learned the moves that he shows off playing soccer star, Sam Obisanya on "Ted Lasso."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You came to the part with some real experience though.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes. Like, I grew up playing football my entire time through school. Football is the main sport around here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): Jimoh never played professionally, but he did win a local school tournament.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: I remember I went crazy and I used to wear my medal to school every single day for like the next two weeks.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): From Norwood, Jimoh went on to drama school. And a year after graduating in 2019 landed his first film role.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The French Dispatch.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): A small part in Wes Anderson`s "The French Dispatch."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The French Dispatch.")\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: And then in pretty quick succession in that same summer, I got "Ted Lasso" and "The Power."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Crazy.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes, it has been a bit of a whirlwind.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Power.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): In the new Prime Video series, "The Power." Jimoh plays Tunde, a Nigerian journalist who discovers that a group of young women has developed a mysterious power to generate electricity through their hands.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Power.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: On the first episode, you literally get a shock.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Power.")\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH (voice over): It is a really mind-boggling thing. Like, it poses really dumb questions about power and, you know, who has it, who doesn`t?\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Yes, it posed really interesting questions about the power dynamics between men and women.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Has it changed much?\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: No. It`s exactly the same.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): Jimoh discovered his love of acting here at Norwood.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: It felt bigger when I was here, but I guess, I was probably just little, you know?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This was your stage.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: This was my stage. I did all of my rehearsals here. I did all of my performances here. Like I lived in this room. This was like the birthplace of, like, me wanting to be a performer, of me wanting to be an actor. This was my safe haven.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): His courage to pursue it professionally was inspired by his instructors.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: This is one of my teachers --\par \par \uc2 MARTIN BLAKE LOCKE (Teacher): Hello.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Martin Blake Locke (sp?). This is the man I used to force -- I used to, like, sit you down over here and just like yell Shakespeare at you.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN BLAKE LOCKE: I remember you did, up there --\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN BLAKE LOCKE: -- you did Julius Caesar.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Julius Caesar, Mark Anthony.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN BLAKE LOCKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 Friends, Romans, countrymen, lend me your ears.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN BLAKE LOCKE: That was one of the moments, I thought, okay, we`ve got a chance here. A chance.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): Jimoh also introduced us to Marcus Condron, Deputy Head of Performing Arts at the school.\par \par \uc2 He says you changed his life.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CONDRON (Deputy Head of Performing Arts): That`s very nice. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Do you remember that time that you pulled me into your office and, like, you sat me down and you`re like, I think you can like -- I think you can be an actor.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CONDRON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Like, that meant a lot to me, you know. My God, I always cry when I`m on this stage.\par \par \uc2 Genuinely, it had a really, like, profound impact on me. And like, I owe so much of what I`m doing now to you. And I`m like, just -- I can`t thank you enough because you didn`t have to, but you went above and beyond for me.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CONDRON: You`re worth it.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes. I appreciate you. I want to stop crying now, but --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What did you see in him?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CONDRON: You know what? It is easy, and it`s hard to say at the same time. I think you see the talent and then it`s -- we always say to them, then the hard work comes, and he had plenty of that as well.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CONDRON: So, you know, not -- not always the best behaved at all points throughout his school journey.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): But he`s a school hero now for his starring role in "Ted Lasso."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): In the last season, Sam`s story took an unexpected turn when he began dating an older woman.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Did you know the romance was coming in Season 2?\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: No.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You didn`t?\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: No, no. Nobody told me about that. I was reading the script, and I was like, "I see."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): That older woman was team owner, Rebecca Welton, played by the Emmy-winning Hannah Waddingham.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH (voice over): Fans used to message me and be like, is there anything romantic? Or is it just platonic? And I was like, oh, no, it is platonic.\par \par \uc2 (on screen): But like, I think what they hit on was that there is a connection between those two characters.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: And yes, I thought it was really, really special.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And you two are friends off screen as well.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Oh, me and Hannah. Yes, yes. She is dope. She`s a rock star, that woman. And I think that`s why I also felt really comfortable by it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): When Sam faces a critical decision in Season 2, he looks in his locker at a photograph of his parents.\par \par \uc2 Those actually are your parents.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH (voice over): My real-life parents. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH (voice over): Sam and Toheeb were like one in that moment.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): Jimoh`s father and mother are Nigerian immigrants.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: My whole life has kind of been about, like where I fit in. I kind of felt like I fell in between being a British kid and being a Nigerian kid and never really felt like a part of either club.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (voice over): Born in Britain, Jimoh spent his early childhood in Nigeria, returning to London around age seven.\par \par \uc2 (on screen): So your first memories are in Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes. My first accent was a Nigerian accent.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: And so yes, again, like it`s really dope, because I get to play two Nigerian characters in Sam and Tunde in what is actually my like, first voice, you know. So I always find it really weird how now my career has taken me back to the thing that I kind of like got rid of to fit in, you know?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Must have made your parents proud though.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes, yes. I think so. My dad is like one of the most stoic people ever, so like, I could do anything. I could win 14 Oscars tomorrow, and he`d just be like, "Good." I mean, "It`s good."\par \par \uc2 Yes, my mom always had an idea that I was going to be the U.K.`s first Black prime minister. Maybe I`ll play him one day. You know what I mean? But that`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: He is a good guy.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: It`s great. You know, I -- that moment where he is talking to his teacher, I just thought -- you know, the importance of devoted dedicated teachers.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: That one conversation he probably barely remembers gave him the boost to pursue something like this. And you know, I`m the product of immigrants too and I know immigrants, they want you to be a lawyer. They want you to be a doctor. It`s hard to pursue creative -- you know, creative --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: There is another clip with an actual Premier League Soccer player who is from West London. And he meets his teacher, who changed his life years later, and turns into a little kid and starts crying and hugging him and saying, Mr. Pigeon (sp?). Teachers are so important and do not get enough credit.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Look, Anthony Mason always gets rave reviews, but apparently, Toheeb Jimoh is getting rave reviews for playing Romeo.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Oh, I`d watch that.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: At the Almeida Theatre in London. So if you`re in London, try to score tickets to that. We will see.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: I love it.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Coming up, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  might be seeing their biggest challenger yet, plus meet the robot that conducts a human orchestra, but has one major weakness.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: On this