lmost not worth it because I don`t know who`s saying what and I don`t know if it`s true or not.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: And I was never sort of a robust \uc2 Twitter\uc2  user and it is because I find it toxic. This is what you know, many people have sort of complained about before.\par \par \uc2 I like funny \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I want someone to create only funny \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and let`s like get rid of all the toxicity and no one has been able to do that yet.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: All right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Maybe that`ll be Threads.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: My "Talk of the Table" is robots. Robots.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Specifically a robot leading South Korea`s National Orchestra.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in that country, a humanoid robot guided the musicians through three numbers, including one jointly with the orchestra`s human conductor. I don`t know how I feel about this. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip with robot conductor leading South Korea`s National Orchestra.)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voice over): Now the human conductor says he was impressed with the robot`s detailed moves, but pointed out one major weakness, it can`t actually listen to the orchestra.\par \par \uc2 So you know, it`s --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice over): Then what`s the point?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I guess, it is like a very fancy metronome, right? It can keep the tempo, but if they are off tempo --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It`s like C-3PO was given the baton to conduct the death march.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: It looks like what I do when I`m pretending to be orchestrating --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Right, so if somebody is getting too loud or somebody is off, you can`t have --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: If you`re not enamored by the music, you can at least watch the conductor who is entertaining, right?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Fair.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So you know, they`ve got big hair, they`ve got big swoop or something.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: If it is just the robot doing this, who wants to watch?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I don`t, Ed. I don`t.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: But I would watch you doing that.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Just doing this?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Yes, let`s not give Ed anymore power. It didn`t end well for --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: My "Talk of the Table" is holiday themed. It is after all, the Fourth of July, the Nathan`s Hot Dog Eating Contest in New York City. It is Coney Island, a little later this morning.\par \par \uc2 Part of me actually wants to go in-person to see this because I`ve always been somewhat intrigued by these contestants, including Women`s Champ, Miki Sudo, and the world`s top competitive eater and 15-time Nathan`s Men`s Champ, Joey Chestnut were weighed in yesterday, both vowing to break their records today.\par \par \uc2 Sudo, looking to beat her personal best of 48 hot dogs and chess not his personal best of -- get this -- 76 hotdogs in 10 minutes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOEY CHESTNUT: The crowd has affected me. I come out there hungry. They`re expecting me to come out there eating fast, and I`m going to deliver. And they want to see a record and I`m going to do everything I can to deliver that, too.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I know you have beef with this, Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: It`s a fun tradition. I don`t want to be Debbie Downer, but I have issues with competitive eating. I don`t like it. I think there`s a lot of people who need food and eating it for sport seems --\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: It`s a tradition.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: All right, okay.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Well, I don`t understand. Why the weigh in? Why the weigh in? I mean, you`re not being judged by the pound.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Because the before and after.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: They are athletes, Anne-Marie. They`ve got to do the weigh in.\par \par \uc2 Ed, you`re way too into this story.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I am. It`s like -- there`s Groundhog Day and there`s the Eden, there`s the parade. It`s like every box is checked for, okay, has Joey Chestnut won? Good. Let`s go to the parade.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I will give you for better or worse, it is very American.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It is.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: It is.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: It is.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And speaking of, we`re going to turn now to a bit of history on this Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 We recently got a chance to see the actual Star-Spangled Banner. It`s the flag that inspired Francis Scott Key to write the words to our national anthem.\par \par \uc2 Not only that it`s on display at the \uc2 National Museum of American History\uc2  in Washington. We talked to the people behind the exhibit and about the flag`s legacy as an enduring symbol of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voice over): In the murky waters of Baltimore Harbor, between the Chesapeake Bay and a decommissioned fort, a red, white, and blue buoy marks the spot.\par \par \uc2 PETTY OFFICER RYAN FRAZIER (\uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2 ): Behind us is our Francis Scott Key buoy. What it is, it is a marker for where Francis Scott Key observed the British bombarding Fort McHenry for a 24-hour period.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voice over): With the War of 1812 raging, the British had already marched on Washington and set fire to the White House when they set their sights and ammunition on the last offense of a young nation`s industrial port.\par \par \uc2 American, Francis Scott Key was aboard to a British ship at this spot in the harbor. And as the sun began to break over the water, he squinted through the smoke to see who had won.\par \par \uc2 PETTY OFFICER RYAN FRAZIER (voice over): The morning after, they raised a 30 by 42 foot American flag, and he saw that and he wrote a poem.\par \par \uc2 (on screen): And that poem became the national anthem.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voice over): This is the actual flag Key saw, the Star- Spangled Banner, now housed in a climate-controlled light protected chamber at the Smithsonian`s \uc2 National Museum of American History\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JONES (National Museum of American History Military history curator): They know that the Star-Spangled Banner is here.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voice over): Jennifer Jones is a military history curator and part of the team tasked with preserving this O.G. piece of Americana.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JONES: The museum receives about 4 million visitors a year and I think this is probably one of the things people say, oh, we have to see this.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: What is it about a flag?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JONES: I think it embodies our values. And everybody`s values are different, and I think that people bring their own ideals to this object, not just this flag, but any American flag.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voice over): After the war, the flag, and the words that inspired became a sensation.\par \par \uc2 Key`s poem was quickly set to a popular and ironically British tune and soon rebranded as "The Star-Spangled Banner."\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Whitney Houston singing "The Star-Spangled Banner" at the 1991 Super Bowl is shown.)\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Chris Stapleton singing "The Star-Spangled Banner" at the 2023 Super Bowl is shown.)\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Jimi Hendrix performing "The Star-Spangled Banner" at Woodstock 1969 is shown.)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voice over): In 1931, it finally became America`s official national anthem.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JONES: Those words were inspirational to a nation, fighting to become independent, and to create a more perfect union.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voice over): For more than 200 years, these tattered threads and the stanzas they inspired have meant different things to different people. Triumph and tragedy, discovery --\par \par \uc2 MAN: They`ve got the flag up now.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voice over): And disappointment, injustice, and salvation.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JONES: We hope that people come and think about what it means to them.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: It`s a bit of an American Rorschach test where they can --\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JONES: Sure. It`s -- I mean the fragility, I mean, if you look at how fragile the flag is, that`s really synonymous with our democracy.\par \par \uc2 You know, we have to be participants, we have to be thinking about it. We have to protect it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Whitney Houston singing "The Star-Spangled Banner" at the 1991 Super Bowl is shown.)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Nicely done, Ms. Ruffini.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Thank you. Got to love that Whitney Houston rendition.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: It is much, much larger than I imagined it would be.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: It`s huge and they designed it. It was so big, the women who sewed it had a ticket to the brewery next door to have enough space to roll it out and they were paid about $200.00, which at that time was the equivalent of a year`s salary.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Which, I mean for context is bigger than this screen.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Yes, it is. It`s similar. The screen is not as long, but it is wider, kind of.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And what`s with the holes in it?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: So I asked that. I said is this from the battle? And they said no. After the war, the flag toured the country, the days before you could take a selfie with it, and they would cut off little pieces of it to give to people as souvenirs.\par \par \uc2 So over the years, a lot of the pieces have returned to the Smithsonian, the one that`s still missing is the star.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: The star. Did they not think maybe they were going to want to keep it and use it again, or something along those lines?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: They think the mother of the woman of the guy who had it in his possession gave it to someone and it`s in a grave somewhere because they think, at this point, it would have come back, it would have resurfaced and the fact they don`t have it, they think someone was buried with it.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: You know, when I was thinking about the fight and I thought to myself, other than the Union Jack, I can`t think of another flag that has its own sort of nickname.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And it`s so iconic. I asked if it was something about the graphic design of the flag, and the woman said no, she thinks it`s about what the flag represents about American power, American culture and its reach around the world.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And it`s right there at the Smithsonian in D.C.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: It is right as you walk in. It is the first exhibit. Go see it.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Great.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Right. Then go see the First Lady gowns or something upstairs.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I do like a good dress.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Thank you. Great story, Christina.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. Lifestyle host, Gayle Bass is here in the progressive greenroom -- there she is -- with a device designed to soothe your sore muscles. Look how soothed she looks.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Very soothing.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Yes. She is going to tell us how you can get an exclusive discount, but first, a check of your local weather.\par