ng and you`re like a spider trying to get that. You put this on the door of your wall. Check out the video right there. So you put that on your door, you clamp it in, 26 to 40 inches and then you target the area where you`re sore.\par \par \uc2 So see those hard reach areas under the arm that guy is getting. Right there, that neck, hard to reach. This makes it easier. That ball targets specific spots. Right here, my lower back, that ball is fantastic.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: All you need is a good doorframe and $69.99 to make it happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, absolutely. You are right. It is $69.99.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Thirty percent discount. Look my dad went to Wharton, I`m finally selling something.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: We are going to switch places again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: All right, one more thing.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I am taking my thing with me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: This is a demonstration. You`re going to have to put that down because I`m going to use your hand for a demonstration.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: This is the IronMatik. You do not need to have an ironing board. This is a ironing mat right here. I`m going to iron this. This iron is warm.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: I`m going to do this. Now, I`m going to put my hand under right there, ironing mat. It`s hot, but my hand isn`t burning.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to do this on the table, touch the table and tell me if that table is hot.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It is not hot, folks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: It is not hot, this has seven layers in it to protect you and to protect your table, this ironing mat.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So you could do it on the kitchen counter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: And we have it in multiple colors. You want to do this on the kitchen counter, on furniture. You are safe to do it on just a wide table like this because not everybody wants to walk up to the third floor where they have their ironing room. This makes it convenient for you and you can travel with it. It fit in my carry-on.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Also, I have a tiny apartment. I don`t want to store a giant ironing board.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I would much rather just use my stupid kitchen table. That --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: -- would be great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yeah, this is something that provides a solution where if you don`t want to set up the ironing board, you just use the mat where you want it --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: -- at your convenience. And they have a mitt that goes with it.\par \par \uc2 So if you have a shirt and you`re trying to iron a shirt like that weird angle, look at that.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Forty-seven percent off on that one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Gayle Bass --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: $49.99.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything you saw today, scan that QR code right there, right there or head over to CBSDeals.com to start shopping now.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "After Hours.")\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: That`s "After Hours" by sisters Ali & AJ from their latest album. It is their 20th year as a band. Tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, Anthony Mason shares his conversation with them who say that they always have been vocal about what they want.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You both seem sort of remarkably savvy, though. I mean, even back then.\par \par \uc2 AJ MICHALKA (Ali &AJ): I don`t always know where that came from.\par \par \uc2 ALY MICHALKA (Ali & AJ): We were always still brave enough to just raise our hand and continue to like, "Can we do this? Can we do this?" And I think, I`m sure, at some point it drove people nuts.\par \par \uc2 But I think that at the end of the day, though, people knew that we really cared and they`re like, these two sisters, man. They`re really persistent. You know, they really don`t even --\par \par \uc2 AJ MICHALKA: They don`t stop. They don`t shut up about what they want.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Potential Breakup Song.")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN (voice over): Aly & AJ also discuss revamping one of their biggest hits, their new album, and getting a second shot at their music career.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: All right. That`ll be fun. That`s tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You guys had fun?\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Always.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: This was a joy.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: It was great.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: I love working with you guys.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Enjoy the parades.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Happy Fourth.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: And the hot dog eating.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230704ej7400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For June 30, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 John Dickerson, Janet Shamlian, Jonathan Vigliotti, Elaine Cobbe,  Mike Bettes, Kris Van Cleave, Major Garrett, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,516 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Tonight: major rulings. The Supreme Court shuts down President Biden`s student debt forgiveness plan and sides with a Web developer who refuses to provide services promoting gay marriage, in a blow to LGBTQ rights.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s top headlines.\par \par \uc2 A restriction to the power of the Biden administration, as the president condemned today`s ruling.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I think the court misinterpreted the Constitution. Today`s decision has closed one path. Now we`re going to pursue another.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: A new record for the July 4 holiday, and AAA is predicting that nearly 51 million of us in this country will be on the move.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: TSA and airlines, they expect airports to stay busy all the way through next week.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The storms and the heat will continue into our holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Actors poised to strike. The current contract for the Screen Actors Guild nears expiration, while the Writers Guild hits day 60 of their strike.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANE FONDA (Actress): We`re here as actors today standing in solidarity with writers.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Riots erupted across France for a third straight night following the deadly police shooting of a 17-year-old during a traffic stop.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: And the Oscar goes to Alan Arkin in Little Miss Sunshine.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Actor and Brooklyn native Alan Arkin has died. He`s well-known for his Oscar-winning performance in Little Miss Sunshine. Alan Arkin was 89 years old.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : On the Road with a street performer collecting small bills to make big change.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Every donation goes toward finding a cure, every dollar a fulfillment of Larry`s newfound purpose.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening and thank you for joining us on this Friday night. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 , in for Norah.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with the Supreme Court rulings on LGBTQ rights and President Biden`s student loan forgiveness program.\par \par \uc2 In a 6-3 decision, the justices threw out the administration`s plan to cancel $430 billion in student loan debt for up to 43 million Americans. The six conservative justices said the plan exceeded the president`s authority to grant such relief without authorization from Congress.\par \par \uc2 The nation`s highest court also voted 6-3 in favor of a Christian graphic artist who objects to designing Web sites to promote same-sex marriages, saying her right to free speech outweighs anti-discrimination laws. President Biden said the court misinterpreted the Constitution and announced new efforts to help students deal with their debt.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Major Garrett has all the details and is here to start us off.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good evening, John. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 As the court term came to an end, its conservative majority again asserted its dominance, both on the tension between the First Amendment and state anti-discrimination laws and on the repayment of student loans. And in that case, the High Court dealt the Biden White House a significant setback.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT (voice-over): Writing for the majority, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said the Biden administration overstepped its legal authority to justify its sweeping student loan cancellation plan.\par \par \uc2 A 2003 law allows the administration to -- quote -- "waive or modify statutes pertaining to student loans during national emergencies." Roberts wrote: "It does not permit basic and fundamental changes in the scheme designed by Congress."\par \par \uc2 The administration sought to use the COVID emergency to cancel some $400 billion in student loans, one of the most expensive executive actions in U.S. history. At the White House, President Biden said he would seek other ways to fulfill a campaign promise to lower student debt burdens.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I didn`t give borrowers false hope. But the Republicans snatched away the hope that they were given. And it`s real, real hope.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Around 40 million Americans would have qualified for some debt forgiveness, including \uc2 Howard University\uc2  graduate Evan Quaintance, who owes about $45,000.\par \par \uc2 Your reaction to the court`s ruling today?\par \par \uc2 EVAN QUAINTANCE (Student Borrower): Disappointed, but not surprised.\par \par \uc2 I didn`t come up in a neighborhood or in a family that had the preexisting conditions to give me the toolkit to get into school. The only way me and my siblings knew how to get there was to pay for it. And the easiest way for us to do that was for student loans.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The plan would have forgiven up to $20,000 in loan debt to some borrowers. Six Republican-led state sued to block the program, including Iowa, where Brenna Bird is attorney general.\par \par \uc2 BRENNA BIRD (R-Iowa Attorney General): I don`t think it is right that people who chose not to go to college have to pay other people`s loans.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The Supreme Court`s conservative majority also prevailed on a case pitting free speech against gay rights. Lorie Smith, a Web designer from Colorado, wanted to create specialized Web pages for weddings, but not for same-sex couples, arguing doing so would violate her First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 LORIE SMITH (Owner and Founder, 303 Creative): I`m a Christian. I believe in marriage is between a man and a woman, and I want to create messages that are consistent with that faith.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: In a 6-3 opinion, the court sided with Smith, ruling that her free speech rights superseded state anti-discrimination laws.\par \par \uc2 Writing for the majority, Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  said: "Colorado seeks to force an individual to speak in ways that align with its views but defy her conscience about a matter of major significance."\par \par \uc2 LORIE SMITH: Nobody should be forced to create a message that goes against the core of who they are and what they believe.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  wrote in a dissent: "Today, the court for the first time in its history grants a business open to the public a constitutional right to refuse to serve members of a protected class."\par \par \uc2 SARAH KATE ELLIS\uc2  (President, \uc2 GLAAD\uc2 ): It sets precedent moving forward that it is OK to discriminate against LGBTQ people in America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Back to the student loan decision, it is worth noting the Trump and Biden administrations temporarily lifted payment requirements during the pandemic for all student debt holders and waived all penalties.\par \par \uc2 Quite separate from what the court decided today, John, those repayments are due to start again in October.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Thank you, Major.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, tens of millions of Americans are on the road and in the airports trying to get to their holiday weekend destinations.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave reports that severe weather is also on the move, along with brutal heat and smoky skies.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice-over): Tonight, America the mobile, millions on the move for the long Fourth of July holiday, even as travel troubles linger during a seemingly unending period of severe summer weather, nearly 8,500 flights canceled since Saturday, and approaching 50,000 delays.\par \par \uc2 VIOLET BRAITHWAITE (Air Traveler): Four cancellations in, what, a two-day span, no luggage now.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Violet Braithwaite lost two days of vacation and missed a graduation ceremony.\par \par \uc2 VIOLET BRAITHWAITE: I was actually flying into New York earlier, work maybe a day or two. Graduation is today, spend time with family and friends. Down the drain.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The TSA and airlines expect today will be the busiest at airports, just as delays and cancellations are finally improving.\par \par \uc2 ERIN SWARDENSKI (Air Traveler): I do feel really lucky that, so far, everything`s good. And I keep checking my phone. I check like every 20 minutes, but, so far, so good.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And AAA says it`s also the busiest on the roads, with a record Fourth of July weekend ahead.\par \par \uc2 Dustin Frieder is driving from Texas to Colorado.\par \par \uc2 DUSTIN FRIEDER (Driver): You know, I looked at the prices. It would have cost me over $2,000 to fly four people out there.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Coast to coast, dangerous weather could threaten holiday fun. Denver has been slammed by hail and flooding.\par \par \uc2 A derecho windstorm race through the Midwest Thursday, leaving tens of thousands without power, as extreme heat scorches the South, and smoke from Canadian wildfires blankets the Northeast with hazy skies and unhealthy air.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It definitely looks nastier outside today, for sure.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And while temperatures are rising here in the Southwest, gas prices are falling.\par \par \uc2 Arizona saw the steepest decline in the last seven days, down 16 cents a gallon -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Kris.\par \par \uc2 For what to expect this Independence Day weekend, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): John, good evening.\par \par \uc2 You have mentioned how poor the air quality is from the Canadian wildfires. It looks like the smoke will peak in the Northeast tomorrow. So, places like D.C., Philadelphia, New York and Boston, Saturday is the day where we will be under air quality alerts, gradual improvements, though, as we go through Sunday, much better for Monday, as well as the holiday on Tuesday itself.\par \par \uc2 Expanding heat across the South because of the high-pressure dome here, a lot of storms that are riding along the periphery of that. Saturday could be a very violent day for storms, especially across the Midwest. Winds here could gust 75 miles an hour or greater.\par \par \uc2 In the West, it`s all about the heat, a lot of heat warnings, heat advisories. Places like Phoenix and Vegas could see temperatures 110 to 115. And on Fourth of July, especially in the Eastern U.S., John, watch for scattered thunderstorms for the holiday.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Thank you, Mike.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas now, tonight, French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  urged parents to keep their children off the streets before a fourth night of protests, sparked by deadly police -- a deadly police shooting.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elaine Cobbe is in Paris.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE (voice-over): Fireworks turned into weapons, as protesters launched them at riot police and at buildings across France.\par \par \uc2 Cars were torched and firefighters responded to some 6,000 fires over the last two nights; 45,000 police officers are on the streets. The government has deployed armored vehicles to quell riots. Many local authorities have limited public transport services and imposed curfews.\par \par \uc2 The protesters accuse the police of racism and excessive force after 17- year-old Nahel of Algerian Moroccan descent was shot dead at close range during a traffic stop Tuesday morning. The officer who fired the fatal shot has been placed under formal investigation for murder.\par \par \uc2 A third of the 900 people arrested last night were under 18, some as young as 12.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT EMMANUEL MACRON SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE: "It`s the responsibility of the parents to keep their children at home," said President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nahel`s mother, who led a march in his memory Thursday, will bury her son tomorrow, amid fears of renewed violence then and through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Elaine Cobbe, CBS News, Paris.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Back here in Washington, the man arrested yesterday after allegedly threatening to blow up former President Barack Obama`s home will remain in jail until his detention hearing next Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The government says 37-year-old Taylor Taranto had two guns, 400 rounds of ammunition, and a machete in his van. The Washington state resident is also charged with four counts stemming from his alleged involvement in the January 6 attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood actors have supported writers on their two-month-long strike, but, soon, the actors may walk their own picket line. Members of the Screen Actors Guild are threatening to strike unless an agreement is reached with major studios by midnight tonight.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jonathan Vigliotti reports on the showdown that could lead to a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Big-name stars have been a big boost to writers on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 JANE FONDA (Actress): Your fight is our fight!\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: But they could be next.\par \par \uc2 In what`s become a Hollywood cliffhanger, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , the union that represents actors, is hammering out a new contract with the studio producers.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DERGARABEDIAN (Senior Media Analyst, \uc2 Comscore\uc2 ): This is a huge deal right now for Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Its impact could also reach viewers, affecting everything you see on television and at the movies.\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL L. JACKSON (Actor): This war is one I have to fight.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: One sticking point is streaming. The major platforms have raked in billions, while actors were paid a lower rate.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DERGARABEDIAN: In the past, the monies that were earned by actors and writers was pretty direct. But now, with streaming, it`s a different model. And now I think everyone`s scrambling to deal with these new financial and technological advancements.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Artificial intelligence is another big issue.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN CRABTREE-IRELAND (Chief Negotiator, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 ): There`s potential to use that technology to create performances that never existed or even to create performers that never existed. And that`s -- that`s something that - - that could be a real -- a real threat.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Union president Fran Drescher addressed members last week.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER (President, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 ): We are having an extremely productive negotiation.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And hundreds of actors have signed a letter asking for a transformative deal.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DERGARABEDIAN: I think the whole industry is on pins and needles right now, but we will just have to wait and see.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And if actors join writers in striking, it would be historymaking.\par \par \uc2 The last time both groups striked at the same time was in 1960. And, back then, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was the president of the Actors Guild, John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jonathan Vigliotti.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 Now to a major fire that broke out this afternoon on the \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 . studio lot in Burbank, California. Thick black smoke could be seen for miles as it rose over the city. The chief of the Burbank Fire Department said the transformer fire is now out, and he added that there were no injuries to civilians or to firefighters.\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, Hollywood is remembering actor Alan Arkin.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian takes a look back at the life of the Academy Award winner who died on Thursday at age 89.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voice-over): Alan Arkin could teach a master class in comedic timing.\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN (Actor): Don`t kill anybody for a few days. See what it feels like.\par \par \uc2 JOHN CUSACK (Actor): I`ll give it a shot.\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN: No, no, don`t give it a shot. Don`t shoot anything.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A skill honed at Chicago`s Second City improv club led to a six-decade stage and screen career in both comedy and drama.\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN: Back off, Italian boy~! Keep away from this one. It`s too big for you!\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: From Inspector Clouseau...\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: ... to Argo...\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN: If I`m doing a fake movie, it`s going to be a fake hit.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: ... and The Kominsky Method.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL DOUGLAS (Actor): Please, don`t sugarcoat it. I`m a grown man.\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN: I`m not sugarcoating. I`m -- I`m lying.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: More than 100 theater roles, television shows and movies, winning a Tony in his 20s.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Alan Arkin in Little Miss Sunshine.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In his 70s, he took on the Academy Award for his role as the cranky grandpa in Little Miss Sunshine.\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN: Now, you`re trying, right?\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL BRESLIN (Actress): Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN: Well, then you`re not a loser.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A part he almost didn`t get, as he told \uc2 CBS\uc2  Sunday Morning.\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN: They thought I was too virile for the part, which was the best turndown I have ever gotten in my life.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Deadline film critic Pete Hammond:\par \par \uc2 PETE HAMMOND (Chief Film Critic, Deadline): Oh, he could play almost anything, any range, and that gave him that ability to do all kinds of roles.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: But even with accolades and a star on the Walk of Fame, the most important opinion was Arkin`s own.\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN: If I base my life on what critics would say, my life would be a disaster.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A giant of the acting world...\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN: With all due respect.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: ... gone at 89.\par \par \uc2 ALAN ARKIN: You`re a very funny man. We will miss you here.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Janet Shamlian, CBS News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A private jet with a popular comedian on board skids off the runway.\par \par \uc2 We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Comedian \uc2 Gabriel Iglesias\uc2  has quite a story to share with his audience tonight as he takes the stage in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 His private jet slid about 200 yards off the runway into a field when he landed this afternoon at Western Carolina Regional Airport. Iglesias posted online that everybody on board is OK, but shaken up, adding he`s happy to be alive.\par \par \uc2 FOX News has agreed to pay $12 million to former \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  producer Abby Grossberg to settle claims that she faced a hostile and discriminatory workplace.\par \par \uc2 In a statement Grossberg said: "I`m heartened that FOX News has taken me in my legal claims seriously."\par \par \uc2 FOX News said: "We are pleased that we have been able to resolve this matter without further litigation."\par \par \uc2 Mexican officials make a major drug bust at sea. What they say was inside the submersible -- that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Mexico`s navy made a major drug bust this week, seizing a submersible packed with what`s believed to be 3.5 tons of cocaine.\par \par \uc2 Officials say the 85-foot-long sub was spotted more than 600 miles out in the Pacific. Five people were arrested.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next with a man looking to make a difference by hitting all the right notes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Finally, tonight, Steve Hartman goes On the Road with a man using the power of music to honor his past and provide hope for the future.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voice-over): What would compel a man, a retired businessman, to become a street performer, playing for bills in a bucket at the age of 83?\par \par \uc2 Is it love?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Loss? Purpose?\par \par \uc2 LARRY KINGSLEY (Musician): Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LARRY KINGSLEY: On our honeymoon.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Larry Kingsley says the love part is Georgeanne, his wife of 23 years.\par \par \uc2 Did she always put her head right there?\par \par \uc2 LARRY KINGSLEY: Usually. I liked it too.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Unfortunately, she is also the loss part. Four years ago, Georgeanne was diagnosed with Alzheimer`s disease.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KINGSLEY: And the doctor says: "You know, it`s going to be difficult."\par \par \uc2 I said: "I know, but I`m married to her, so I`m going to be with her."\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And with her, he was. When Larry got out the old trumpet he bought back in his Air Force days and started playing three times a week on a sidewalk in Cary, North Carolina, Larry says Georgeanne loved it, although she was confused.\par \par \uc2 In fact, she used to yell at him. "Why don`t you get a real job?" she`d say, assuming he was out here panhandling. And Larry laughed it off, knowing that, in a way, this had become his job, his mission.\par \par \uc2 Every donation goes toward finding a cure, every dollar a fulfillment of Larry`s newfound purpose.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KINGSLEY: The day that she died, I played that night.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KINGSLEY: But, in my mind, it`s -- I just say, the show goes on.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: After Georgeanne died last year, Larry started playing six nights a week. He has now raised more than $15,000 and has vowed to keep it up until Alzheimer`s is just a memory.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Steve Hartman, On the Road, in Cary, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And the show goes on.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s CBS Evening News. For Norah O`Donnell, I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good night. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230725ej6u000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 UPS Union Workers Threaten To Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Carter Evans \par \uc2 497 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, let`s turn now to what could turn into the largest work stoppage in the U.S. in more than 60 years; 97 percent of \uc2 UPS\uc2  union workers voted to authorize a strike this summer if a deal can`t be reached on a new contract.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans reports, a walkout would likely devastate the nation`s fragile supply chain.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GIL GUZMAN (\uc2 UPS\uc2  Driver): \uc2 UPS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voice-over): Gil Guzman has been driving for \uc2 UPS\uc2  for 23 years.\par \par \uc2 Did you vote to authorize a strike?\par \par \uc2 GIL GUZMAN: I did. We are the best in the business. And it`s time to get paid like it.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: He is one of 330,000 employees who want wage increases and more full-time jobs.\par \par \uc2 GIL GUZMAN: We work hard. We work hard for our job. We work hard for this company. We move this country around.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: \uc2 UPS\uc2  is the biggest shipping company in the U.S. Around 6 percent of the nation`s gross domestic product passes through its brown bands. Nationwide, drivers deliver nearly 19 million packages every single day. And a halt in operations would cause major supply chain disruptions.\par \par \uc2 NICK VYAS (Founding Executive Director, USC Global Supply Chain Institute): The reason we call it a chain is that it`s so integrated. So, any strike, especially the long-lasting strike, could have a devastating effect, both for the summer, back-to-schools, and upcoming holidays.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The boom in online shopping during the pandemic is still going strong, resulting in record profits for the company.\par \par \uc2 GIL GUZMAN: So why not share with your employees?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Drivers already won a big concession this week when \uc2 UPS\uc2  agreed to install air conditioning in its trucks.\par \par \uc2 If you could say one thing to the people who are in the negotiating room, what would you want them to know from you?\par \par \uc2 GIL GUZMAN: Just don`t mess it up. You got 330,000 strong UPSers that give their blood, sweat, and tears for this company. And both sides, let`s just work it out and make it happen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, a strike is not a guarantee at this point in the negotiation, and \uc2 UPS\uc2  says it is aligned with its workers on many key issues, and it is committed to reaching a deal before this contract expires at the end of July -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: This will be watched closely.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230620ej6g0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For May 27, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Christopher Krebs, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,620 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Breaking overnight, the White House and congressional Republicans reach a deal in principle on the debt limit. But will they find the votes to pass it in time to head off a default?\par \par \uc2 Finally, a breakthrough in Washington. House Speaker Kevin McCarthy told reporters late last night that he and President Biden had agreed on a two- year post-2024 election extension of the nation`s debt limit and that both sides had made concessions.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): Yes, historic reductions in spending, consequential reforms that will lift people out of poverty into the work force, rein in government overreach.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the plan has a potential for a resounding round of no`s on both the far right and the far left in the House, exactly what President Biden feared earlier in the week.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We have to be in a position where we can sell it to our constituencies. We have a pretty well-divided House, almost down the middle. And it`s not too different in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will talk to the top Democrat in the House, Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , and to an ally of McCarthy`s, Congressman French Hill.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Chicago Fed\uc2  President Austan Goolsbee will weigh in on the economic impact.\par \par \uc2 Then: a closer look at the growing concerns over A.I. with the president of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Brad Smith\uc2  and analysis from CBS News cybersecurity expert Chris Krebs.\par \par \uc2 And a tribute to the class of 2023, a generation of graduates who faced down some unprecedented challenges.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JIM RYAN (President, \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 ): You can do hard things because you have already done hard things.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 With just eight days to go before Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  says the U.S. will be forced to default on its spending obligations, there is a deal in principle to head off the crisis. The bill is still being written, and we are short on details at this point, but it will include a temporary cap on spending in areas other than defense, veterans or entitlements.\par \par \uc2 The plan would expand work requirements for able-bodied adults who receive some government benefits, including food stamps, but not for Medicaid recipients. And it calls for clawing back some unspent money from COVID relief funds.\par \par \uc2 Leaders in both the House and Senate will now have to convince their members to vote for the bill. And we are seeing signs just in the last hour that it could be a tough challenge for both sides.\par \par \uc2 House Democratic Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  joins us now from his district in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get straight to it. Can you deliver a majority of Democrats?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2  (D-New York): Well, good morning Margaret.\par \par \uc2 I do look forward to the White House briefing that`s going to take place later on this afternoon with the House Democratic Caucus. We`ll be able to have a robust discussion.\par \par \uc2 But let me say this. President Biden has delivered a result that avoids a catastrophic default, that prevents us from our economy crashing, and stops the extreme MAGA Republicans from triggering a job-killing recession, which, as we`ve seen over the last week or two, increasingly seemed to have been a position that they were taking for political reasons.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, well, we`ll only avoid a default if you can get the votes to get it through.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McCarthy predicted this morning he can get a majority of Republicans, he says. Some Democrats, he thinks, will vote for it. But he also quoted something he says you told him. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY: I think there`s going to be a lot of Democrats who vote for it too. Right now, the Democrats are very upset. But one thing Hakeem told me, there`s nothing in the bill for them. There`s not one thing in the bill for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Did you say that? And how do you convince Democrats?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : I have no idea what he`s talking about, particularly because I have not been able to review the actual legislative text. All that we`ve reached is an agreement in principle.\par \par \uc2 Now, what I have consistently said both privately and...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Did you even talk to him?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : I talked to him yesterday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : But I haven`t talked to him since that point in time.\par \par \uc2 What I have consistently said, however, privately and publicly, was that the extreme MAGA Republican negotiating position, and that the extreme bill that they passed on April 26, the default on America act, contained nothing that was consistent with Democratic values or American values.\par \par \uc2 And it was unreasonable to think that that negotiating position was going to be able to result in a resolution...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : ... that would make sense for the American people, when he understood and everyone understood that a bipartisan resolution was the only way forward to avoid a catastrophic default.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. OK. And that`s a bill that is going nowhere.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the one that was or is being written as we speak, the head of the Progressive Caucus, \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , said on CNN this morning, you need to worry about the left flank of the party. She has 102 members in that caucus. How many Democratic defections do you expect?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : Well, I have had several conversations with \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2  over the last several days, and will continue to do so. And I expect that she`ll be part of the caucus briefing that takes place later on today.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what I can say is that the agreement that was reached in principle by President Biden does several important things. In addition to avoiding a devastating default that would hurt everyday Americans, it protects Social Security.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : It protects Medicare. It protects Medicaid. It protects veterans. It protects the American people from the type of devastating spending cuts that they were...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: She`s concerned about environmental -- environmental policy changes and food stamps.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : Hold on. It protects -- hold -- hold on. I will get to that.\par \par \uc2 It protects the American people from the types of devastating spending cuts that were proposed by Republicans in their default on America act. Those are incredibly significant steps forward.\par \par \uc2 Now, in terms of the permitting issue, I haven`t had the opportunity, nor have any of us had the opportunity to review what the proposed language may ultimately be in terms of the permitting situation.\par \par \uc2 In terms of SNAP, as I understand it -- and, again, we`ll have the opportunity to review that language in a few hours, when it is released, but, as I understand it, it will actually result in an expansion of eligibility for people like veterans and the homeless because of changes made to exemptions as a result of what will be in this agreement, in terms of the original 1996 law that was passed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, according to the White House, it is going to relax work requirements for veterans and homeless people, but it still pushes up the age to 54 from 49.\par \par \uc2 Are you implying -- do you mean to say here that the White House hasn`t kept you fully briefed on the details?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : The White House has, of course, kept all of us fully briefed every step of the way.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : However, I do think that, as the White House has indicated, this is only an agreement in principle.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE HAKEEM JEFFRIES: There will not be a final agreement until we all are able to review the actual legislative text.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 The White House is still calling this a budget deal and a separate debt limit increase, but McCarthy says it`s a 150-page bill and not a clean debt ceiling increase. Is this one vote, one bill?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : Well, that remains to be seen. And, ultimately, that`s a decision that House Republicans have as their prerogative to make.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : I do hope and expect to see a significant number of House Republicans voting for this agreement.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : It`s my understanding that they are committed to producing at least 150 votes, if not more.\par \par \uc2 They were the ones who negotiated this agreement with the White House. And I expect that they will provide a significant number of votes to get it over the finish line.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, so 150 Republicans. You need something in the range of 70 Democrats. Do you think you have that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : Well, I do expect that there will be Democratic support once we have the ability to actually be fully briefed by the White House.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE HAKEEM JEFFRIES: But I`m not going to predict what those numbers may ultimately look like.\par \par \uc2 We have to go through a process consistent with respecting every single member...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : ... of the House of Representatives and their ability to fully understand the resolution that has been reached.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2  of Connecticut, not a progressive, said this morning: "If this vote fails, I think we`re going to see the kind of market reaction that none of us want to see."\par \par \uc2 Can you deliver a vote? Can you deliver someone like him? Can you deliver a vote that wins on the first try? He`s talking about a market crash.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : Well, we have to, of course, avoid a market crash. We have to avoid tanking the economy. We have to avoid a default.\par \par \uc2 The reason why we`re in this situation from the very beginning is that extreme MAGA Republicans made the determination...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : ... that they were going to use the possibility of default to hold the economy and everyday Americans hostage, period, full stop.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. Understood. But that`s -- that`s where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : And that`s why -- that`s why...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that negotiation happened.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You negotiated with them. So here we are.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : And that`s why I`m -- and that`s -- well, that`s why I`m thankful that President Biden has reached a resolution.\par \par \uc2 And that`s also why I`m thankful that, notwithstanding the fact that they were trying to jam 10-year spending caps...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : ... down the throats of the American people, we were able to apparently match up a freeze in spending consistent with 2023 levels...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : ... 2023 levels, not the 2022 levels that what they were trying to extract...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, there`s some disagreement.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : ... and make sure that we avoided a catastrophic default.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There`s some disagreement on those, but we don`t have the text. So we will just have to wait on that.\par \par \uc2 But I want to ask about the backup plan; 213 Democrats voted for this discharge petition, this idea of being able to vote to lift the debt ceiling, even if Republicans -- if the Republican leader doesn`t do it. You`d need five Republicans. Can you do that? Can you guarantee people that we will not see a default?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , glad to have you with us. Hopefully, we see you here in person next time.\par \par \uc2 We got a lot of work ahead in Congress and also on the Democratic side of - - Republican side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 And that`s where we`re turning now, to Arkansas Republican Congressman French Hill, who joins us from Little Rock.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL (R-Arkansas): Margaret, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let`s pick up where we left off. The leader says 150 Republican votes can be delivered by the GOP. Do you have those votes for this deal?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: I feel confident that we`ll have those votes after people review the text, talk with their colleagues, compare it to our goals.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McCarthy is the only person who`s demonstrated urgency on this point, starting when he was sworn in, and starting with this first meeting with President Biden to get to a sensible and responsible increase to the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: He had two red lines, Margaret...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: ... no tax increase and not a clean debt ceiling. And I think he`s achieved that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. So it will be just one bill, one vote?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: That`s my view.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. That decision has been made, it sounds like, based on what you`re indicating here.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In terms of the votes, though, you still have to be able to deliver those 150 Republicans to vote this through.\par \par \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  says he can get the Democrats in line. We`ll see. Can you guarantee that this vote will happen and will succeed on first try?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: I believe it will.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you why. We had our plan, which we passed, as you noted, on April 26, with the full support of the American...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: ... people to stop the avalanche of spending. And each component we had of limiting the rate of growth limiting the amount of spending...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that -- but that`s past tense. I don`t want to talk about the past tense. That bill`s dead.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: No, no, no, it`s not past -- no, no, no, it`s not...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re talking about the deal that just was brokered.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: No, no, no, it`s not past tense.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to talk about whether you can actually deliver that on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: We can. We can.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This Wednesday is when the speaker says it will happen on this tentative agreement.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: OK, well, my point is that it`s not past tense.\par \par \uc2 Each of the components we had in the bill on the 26th is reflected in this negotiated deal and principle that the speaker achieved with President Biden. And that`s why I believe that we`ll have those votes on Wednesday, because we limit the rate of growth, we cut spending, we claw back unneeded spending and recision, we stop unnecessary spending, and we get our economy growing with regulatory relief and by encouraging more people back to work.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, Chip Roy and others from the Freedom Caucus said they don`t even want to see the vote happen, and they`re going to try to block it.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: OK. Well, they need to read the text and visit with their colleagues.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Have you read it? Because we haven`t.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: This is a -- not a -- no, I`m going to read it this afternoon...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: ... when it`s posted on the House Web site.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 So, in terms of what is being drafted, there`s differences here in terms of spending freezes, and which year we`re comparing it to. The spin for the Republicans and Democrats is very different on this. What is actually going into the text?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: Well, we`re going to limit the rate of spending growth for nondefense, nonveteran spending. It will be at FY22 levels. Defense and Veterans will be at FY23 levels, and then we capped the growth right and spending 1 percent a year for six years.\par \par \uc2 That was a key component in the April 26 bill when we offered 1 percent of growth for 10 years. We also are going to go to a Massie-Emmer approach on appropriations bills, which finally opens up and gives strength to the appropriations process, that if all 12 bills are not passed by September 30, then we go to a continuing resolution at 99 percent of current year spending levels.\par \par \uc2 That`s an immense incentive for the Congress to do its work and pass all 12 bills in the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: And that`s the spending issue. And then we -- we claw back, as you noted, COVID relief money, other recisions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: We stop the first year of the 10-year increase in the \uc2 IRS\uc2  budget. That`s nearly $2 billion of spending stopped of about $80 billion that was to be spent over 10 years for hiring \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, we will wait to see that text as well.\par \par \uc2 I want to get to what you were just talking about in terms of freezes. I mean, every American knows that -- that your dollar doesn`t go as far as it used to because of inflation. This may be a problem for you in the Senate, because we already have Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  criticizing this deal and the defense increase, as you referred to it, because it doesn`t keep up with inflation.\par \par \uc2 How do you sell that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: Well, inflation is...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because he just -- he`s -- he`s said...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... that national security threats are increasing, and this is a disaster for the Navy and a win for China and Putin. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: Well, first, I think that`s why Speaker McCarthy and President Biden agreed to the $888 billion spending level, which was the president`s level, plus 3 percent inflation, was exactly that point contained in this negotiated deal.\par \par \uc2 I also would say, look, we`ve had a gross overreaction in fiscal and monetary policy since the pandemic. And that`s why House Republicans are trying to slow the growth in spending and get back to normal before the pandemic, and all areas are going to have to sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: But, look, the speaker and the president agree. We want to preserve defense, preserve -- preserve veterans, and take Social Security and Medicare off the table.\par \par \uc2 And those are big chunks of the federal budget.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, this has to make it through the House and then through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, it sounds like there`s still a lot of convincing you need to do on those points you just laid out.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried that the scope of this isn`t big enough for you to then go back and sell to some of the skeptical members of your caucus who wanted to see bigger reductions?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: Well, I`m one of those people that wanted bigger reductions. But I also recognize we control only the House of Representatives. We`ve got to get it through the Senate, as you note, and the Biden administration controls the central government here.\par \par \uc2 If they were so concerned about the debt ceiling, they could have negotiated with McCarthy a lot earlier, or they could have even raised the debt ceiling when they controlled both branches of the House and Senate and the federal government in December. But they didn`t.\par \par \uc2 So this is the world we have. It`s not the spending cuts I would prefer. But when you look at pay-go on regulatory costs, that`s a big change. When you look at the Massie-Emmer note...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: ... on 99 percent C.R. on appropriations, that`s a big change.\par \par \uc2 So I think we`re in the absolute right direction. And it absolutely follows the goals of House Republicans as laid out on April 26.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 All right, Congressman, thank you very much for coming on, giving us a glimpse into what Republicans are putting on paper right now.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in a minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to Austan Goolsbee, who is the current president of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2  and a former White House economic adviser in the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to talk to you.\par \par \uc2 I know you were chairman of President Obama`s Council of Economic Advisers when we came to the brink back in -- in 2011. Markets are closed tomorrow, but we are still very close to that default deadline. How dangerous is the territory we are in, even with this tentative deal?\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE (President, \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2 ): Well, it`s definitely a little dangerous.\par \par \uc2 As -- as Chair Powell has stated from the beginning, we must raise the debt ceiling. Now, the fiscal decisions, of course, are between Congress and the president. And however they sort it out is -- is good by us.\par \par \uc2 But if you -- if you did not do that, the consequences for the financial system and for the broader economy would be extremely negative.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How important is it that this vote succeeds on the first try?\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: I know -- like I say, this is a fiscal decision left to Congress and the president. So, I -- I -- it`s -- it wouldn`t be the place...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: ... for anybody from the Fed to be saying what -- what they should pass or -- or how they should vote.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: But I -- I liken it to there`s a legitimate argument, if you`re trying to lose weight what, what can you eat and how much exercise.\par \par \uc2 Everybody should be able to agree that the first strategy should not be cutting off your toe.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: Because that doesn`t save much weight, and it`s really painful.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: And that`s -- that`s kind of where the debt ceiling is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Treasury Secretary Yellen said it`s -- it`s already kind of painful, because she`s already seeing borrowing costs and that -- go up and that there is a cost to being in this place of brinksmanship.\par \par \uc2 What is -- can you in any way quantify what the impact is to the economy being in the place we are?\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: Yes, look, Margaret, you raise a great point that even the anticipation of these problems does have consequences on the economy and does have consequences on financial markets.\par \par \uc2 In a way, this couldn`t be happening at a worse time. So, I`m -- I`m definitely heartened that you saw both parties there on the program expressing confidence that they`re going to be able to raise the debt ceiling, and -- and -- because if you -- if you just look at what`s happening to the rates, you already see that -- that there`s fear and uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 But there are multiple steps that can get worse. So,if you have banks already on edge because of the financial and -- and banking stresses that we`ve seen over the last couple of months, taking the safest asset on anyone`s balance sheet, which is U.S. Treasuries, and kind of calling it into question is not good for the banking system, is not good for lending, is not good for the real economy.\par \par \uc2 And you`ll start to get into other problems. Like, if the rating agencies downgrade...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: ... U.S. Treasuries again, then that could raise the interest rates we have to pay even more.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: And you get into secondary problems, like there are insurance companies that aren`t allowed to hold things that -- whose rating isn`t high enough. So let`s just avoid it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: Let`s just raise the debt ceiling and -- and get on to the next thing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 And -- and I understand that you`re in a very different role now at -- at the Fed than you were back then, but this is a complicated economic environment we are in. Can you say at this point -- I know you haven`t seen the text -- no one has -- what this will do to the fight against inflation and some of the choices you will have to make at the Fed?\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: Well, look, as I say, raising the debt ceiling and decisions about the budget have -- that -- that`s none of the Fed`s business.\par \par \uc2 The law gives the Fed two jobs, maximize employment, stabilize prices. We`ve done very well on the employment side. We`re improving on the inflation side, but we have not succeeded. Inflation`s still well higher than where we want it to be.\par \par \uc2 So, at -- at a moment of banking crisis, it will be a great relief if we raise the debt ceiling. We can go back to dealing with the -- with the matters at hand, which are the real economy side of -- of employment and inflation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you have a sense yet of whether you personally want to raise interest rates again at this next June meeting?\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: Well, as a voting member of the FOMC, I have tried not -- to make it a point not to prejudge and make decisions when you`re still weeks out from the meeting.\par \par \uc2 And we are going to get a lot of important data between now and then. I think the part that makes this job difficult is, you got two simple goals, but the actions that the Fed takes take months or even years to work their way through the system. So the Fed has raised the interest rate by 5 full percentage points over the last year.\par \par \uc2 That`s the fastest increase in decades, rivaling ever. And some part of that still has to work its way through the system.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: So, there`s no doubt inflation is too high still. It has come down. And we`re just trying to manage, can we get inflation down...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: ... without starting a recession?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE: There are people who say we cannot. But I think we -- that -- that we can. And that`s for sure the goal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, President Goolsbee, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On this Memorial Day weekend, we want to pause and pay tribute to those who lost their lives protecting our country.\par \par \uc2 We would not be here today without them. And we thank them for their service.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Artificial intelligence is raising concerns on a number of fronts, even among the companies who have devoted considerable resources towards its development.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  president \uc2 Brad Smith\uc2  late last week.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said: "A.I. offers perhaps even more potential for the good of humanity than any invention that has preceded it."\par \par \uc2 I mean, that`s an incredible statement.\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH (Vice Chair and President, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 ): It`s fundamentally an invention that can help us all do research, learn more, communicate more, sift through data better.\par \par \uc2 And its uses are almost ubiquitous, in medicine, in drug discovery, in diagnosing diseases, in scrambling the resources of, say, the Red Cross or others in a disaster, to find those who are most vulnerable where buildings have collapsed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Data crunching, essentially?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: That`s one part of it.\par \par \uc2 It identifies patterns in data that may be difficult for humans to access. But, in a sense, it`s going to impact all of our lives in a multiple of different ways. So, think about it as the next step in our ability to learn, communicate, express ourselves.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But what should American consumers know about artificial intelligence?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: It`s a co-pilot, if you will, to help us do things.\par \par \uc2 I think one good thing for everyone to know is, it`s already part of our lives. If you have a Roomba at home, it finds its way around your kitchen using artificial intelligence to learn what to bump into and how to get around it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: So it isn`t necessarily as mysterious as we sometimes think.\par \par \uc2 And yet, at the same time, it is getting more powerful. It can do much more to help us, and I think the other thing that all of us should think about as Americans is, like any powerful technology, we need to keep it under human control. We need to keep -- keep it safe. And that will require the work of companies that create it, that use it. It will require, I think, a level of law and regulation as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You just made a big jump from a Roomba...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: Yes, I did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... to the machine takeover here.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when you say that you have to make sure humanity has control -- in control here, is there really a risk that it won`t be?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: Whenever you have something that fundamentally can do good, but could also go and do harm, you put a braking mechanism in place. You put a safety brake, an emergency brake.\par \par \uc2 We should think about A.I. the same way.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What`s the most promising concept you`ve seen?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: Well, I do love these examples where A.I. can detect a disease, a form of cancer, before the human eye or other human doctors might.\par \par \uc2 You take something like pancreatic cancer. You know, it is so small when it begins that typically it`s undetectable to the human eyes of doctors. And yet A.I. is very good at sifting through patterns and detecting things and flagging them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So what`s the next interface that people should expect?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: Think of the ability to, in effect, tell a computer what you want it to do. You don`t need to learn to code. You can simply say, can you go find information about whether this restaurant is open on Monday nights, and, if so, does it take reservations and how do I make one?\par \par \uc2 You can write that in one sentence and get all of the information back. You no longer have to spend your time clicking on links and finding answers. Take that example and generalize further. You want help. You have writer`s block. You`ve got to write a memo. You need to sift through your e-mail. You want to create a PowerPoint slide.\par \par \uc2 You can tell a computer what you want it to do. As I say, we`re -- we create what we call a co-pilot. You don`t have to know how to do everything. You just have to know what you want done and how to ask for it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you know the -- the information is accurate?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: I do think that it`s, in part, based on using your brain.\par \par \uc2 I have often found, in the world of technology, if something doesn`t sound right, you should double-check. That will still be true.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On the concerning side of the ledger, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  predicted A.I.`s ascendance will disrupt 300 million jobs here in the U.S. and in Europe. How fast is this going to happen?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: It will be years, not decades, although things will progress over decades as well.\par \par \uc2 There will be some new jobs that will be created. There are jobs that exist today that didn`t exist a year ago in this field. And there will be some jobs that are displaced. There always are.\par \par \uc2 But I think, for most of us, the way we work will change. This will be a new skill set we`ll need to, frankly, develop and acquire.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have a very deep concern here about deepfakes.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is content that looks realistic, but is completely computer- generated. On Monday, there was a photo that actually moved the markets. It was a fake photo, it looked real, of an explosion near the Pentagon. And it was potentially partially created by A.I.\par \par \uc2 The market sold off quickly. It was fact-checked.\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that image was put out there from an account that looked legitimate as well.\par \par \uc2 So how do you stop something like this from happening?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: We`ll need a system that we and so many others have been working to develop that protects content, that puts a watermark on it so that, if somebody alters it, if somebody removes the watermark, if they do that to try to deceive or defraud someone, first of all, they`re doing something that the law makes unlawful.\par \par \uc2 We may need some new law to do that. But, second, we can then use the power of A.I. to detect when that happens.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So that means a news organization like \uc2 CBS\uc2  would have video that somehow could be identified, besides our little eye icon, something embedded in there that your computers would see...\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... to say this is real?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: Yes, that is exactly where this should go.\par \par \uc2 And I would guess and hope that \uc2 CBS\uc2  will be absolutely at the forefront of this. You embed what we call metadata. It`s part of the file. If it`s removed, we`re able to detect it. If there`s an altered version, we, in effect, create a hash. Think of it like the fingerprint of something. And then we can look for that fingerprint across the Internet.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask about another topic here that`s related.\par \par \uc2 The RNC -- politics -- they put out an attack ad using A.I., and I know we have video of it. It was meant to mimic a news report from the future, from 2024. It said, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the election, and then it shows this dystopia.\par \par \uc2 And in teeny, tiny little script in the upper left hand corner, it says, generated by A.I. Is that sufficient?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: I do think that there is some real virtue in telling the public when they are seeing content that has been generated by A.I., instead of a human being, especially if it is designed to look like a human being, a human face or voice, so that people know, no, that`s not the real person.\par \par \uc2 We, I think, will need some new standards in that space.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Who sets that?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: This, I think, is one of the issues that we`re going to need to discuss together and find a path through.\par \par \uc2 Now, we do need to balance that. We live in a country that, I think quite rightly, prides itself on free expression.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re on the cusp of a presidential election year. How much of a factor is this going to be, these deepfakes and misleading ads?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: Well, I think there is an opportunity to take real steps in 2023, so that we have guardrails in place for 2024, so that we are identifying, in my view, especially when we`re seeing foreign cyber influence operations from a Russia, a China or Iran, that is pumping out information that they know is false and is designed to deceive, including using artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 And that will require the tech sector coming together with government, and it really will require more than one government. This needs to be an international initiative. But we`ve done that in recent years in other spaces. We can do it again, and I think we should.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2 , the CEO of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , which made ChatGPT, testified recently before Congress and recommended an entirely new federal agency be set up to oversee A.I.\par \par \uc2 You like this idea.\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it`s also implying that the federal government is not currently up to the task.\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: We do need more than we have.\par \par \uc2 We need our existing laws to apply. They need to be enforced. But, especially when it comes to these most powerful models, when it comes to the protection of the nation`s security, I do think we would benefit from a new agency, a new licensing system, something that would ensure not only that these models are developed safely, but they`re deployed in, say, large data centers where they can be protected from cybersecurity, physical security and national security threats.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you convince people that this isn`t the big bad tech giant of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  setting the rules of the road and running the little guys off of it?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: We`re not suggesting that any single company or the entire industry together should be the one to set the rules.\par \par \uc2 We should have the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , elected by the American people, setting the rules of the road, and we should all be obliged to follow them. Look, we need rules, we need laws, we need responsibility, and we need it quickly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There were a number of tech leaders, including \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , and one of the co-founders of -- of \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Steve Wozniak\uc2 , who called publicly for a six-month pause in A.I. systems that are more powerful than GPT-4 or have governments institute a kind of moratorium until there are safety protocols in place.\par \par \uc2 Is there something to that? Do we need to tap the brakes a bit here?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: I`m not inclined to think that that`s the answer. First of all, it will take 12 months to get the government to debate whether to decide whether to have a pause that will last for six months.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: But I think the more important question is, look, what`s going to happen in six months that`s different from today?\par \par \uc2 How would we use the six months to put in place the guardrails that would protect safety and the like? Well, let`s go do that. Rather than slow down the pace of technology, which I think is extraordinarily difficult -- I don`t think China`s going to jump on that bandwagon -- let`s use six months to go faster.\par \par \uc2 Let`s adopt an executive order here for the federal government, where the government itself says it`s only going to buy A.I. services in certain categories, say, from companies that are implementing A.I. safety protocols and the like. You know, let`s start to get some legislation moving.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you think this will happen, some -- some regulation or some legislation in the year ahead?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: I do.\par \par \uc2 The world is moving forward. Let`s make sure that the United States at least keeps pace with the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You can see our full conversation with \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  president \uc2 Brad Smith\uc2  on our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We want to turn now to Chris Krebs. He was the director of the \uc2 Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\uc2  in the Trump administration and is now a CBS News cybersecurity expert and analyst.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to start on some of the other news from \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  within the past days, which is that they, alongside U.S. intelligence, revealed the discovery of malware from a state-sponsored Chinese actor embedded in U.S. critical infrastructure that was meant to shut down communications between the U.S. and Asia in the event of a conflict.\par \par \uc2 That sounds a lot like planning for a potential invasion of Taiwan to a lot of analysts. How significant a breach was this?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, I think it`s significant, in the fact that it`s an escalation or evolution of Chinese capabilities.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s no surprise that China is in the cyber-offensive operations game. Ten years ago, \uc2 Mandiant\uc2 , a cybersecurity company, released a report called APT1. They talked about their espionage capabilities. That same year, there`s evidence that they were in U.S. gas and oil systems and stealing network schematics.\par \par \uc2 So, from a pure cyber play perspective, China is quite capable. Even -- even this year, Director Wray of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  said that the Chinese have 50 hackers to every one \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent. So they are quite capable.\par \par \uc2 But, to your point, what`s different about this report is, it shows operational preparation of the battlefield. It shows that they`re getting in place, that, if tensions continue to escalate with Taiwan, they are in a position to cut off lines of communication, logistics and the ability of the United States to support and defend Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, this was in Guam, which is a -- there`s a U.S. military installation there in the Pacific, that would be very key in the event that you just laid out there.\par \par \uc2 But, broadly speaking, how vulnerable is U.S. infrastructure to these kinds of attacks?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, I mean, I think, just as \uc2 Brad Smith\uc2  pointed out in the previous segment, is that we are using technology in virtually every aspect of our lives, from day-to-day to operational capabilities and critical infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 So we are highly dependent upon technologies. And we don`t always implement them in the proper way. And there are vulnerabilities. And there are misconfigurations that we have seen bad guys from criminals to state actors take advantage of.\par \par \uc2 But I think we have seen an improvement in resilience. And I think the Ukrainians` performance in the face of a Russian cyber onslaught last year shows that you can be resilient, you can prepare, given the right information and the right time and investments to secure and harden and improve systems.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  is proposing an entirely new branch agency, government agency, be set up to regulate A.I. Do you think we`re at that point?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: I called for the creation of a digital agency last year in a keynote I gave at the Black Hat conference in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 I think we are well past the time that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  needs to rethink how it engages and creates market interventions on technology, cyber disinformation and beyond. And A.I. is probably that kind of forcing function that will push us there.\par \par \uc2 And there is precedent for reshaping government to meet emerging risks, 1939, the Federal Reorganization Act. The government was reorganized, 2001 in the wake of 9/11, we reorganized. I think we`re on the cusp of that. And I think the issue is that government is not keeping pace with technological development...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: .. and the harms that we`re seeing in society.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the U.S., the Biden administration is looking to restrict the sale of certain technologies, export controls, to adversaries like China, for example.\par \par \uc2 But the CEO of some -- some of these companies, they don`t -- they don`t like it. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , which is an A.I. chipmaker, they have pushed back, saying the Chinese will just build their own chips.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of this solution of trying to export-control?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, they -- China is a significant market for many American companies, whether it`s on Wall Street or technology and chip companies.\par \par \uc2 I think the current policy to restrict the more mature and advanced chips to the Chinese market, I think it`s a smart policy decision. I think it maintains European and American geopolitical competition -- competitive posture. I think we need to think, though, how does this apply in an A.I. model perspective?\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s, in part, what Brad was getting at with his policy agenda. And that is going to take quite some time from a legal and regulatory framework to really scope these things out and figure out, at what points of the economy do we make those interventions?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right,so not a quick timeline to turn that around.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I mean, hearing a tech giant ask to be regulated is unusual, to say the least. What are we to make of that?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, if you kind of sit back and look at it, the tech industry is one of the least directly regulated.\par \par \uc2 There are secondary regulations that come in through the \uc2 New York State Department of Financial Services\uc2 , for instance, and other California laws. But I think, when you see this sort of push for regulation, in part, it says that they`re concerned, they`re worried, but they`re also looking for, I think, a little bit of protection.\par \par \uc2 And I think, if you step back and look at kind of the range of risks that you may encounter across A.I., there are very mundane risks that do not require additional regulation. And Brad talked about that. And that`s criminals using A.I. to build the better mousetrap. It`s students using A.I. to cheat.\par \par \uc2 But as you get further up the risk hierarchy, there is disinformation. There`s autonomous weapons systems that will have A.I. built in. And then you kind of get to that point of, as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has talked about, that super intelligence turning into the Terminator. There are areas, particularly, I think, on the models, not necessarily implementations in the apps, but the actual learning large language models that are driving a lot of the innovation right now, that`s an opportunity to look at licensing.\par \par \uc2 And, in part, I think what they`re trying to achieve is some indemnification that, hey, we -- we`re playing by the rules. You have put in place guardrails. You have reviewed our models. And, if it`s abused by someone else outside of our control, then that`s not -- that`s not on us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Protect themselves as well.\par \par \uc2 Chris Krebs, always good to get your analysis.\par \par \uc2 We will be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This Memorial Day weekend, we`d also like to pay tribute to the hundreds of thousands of college graduates this year who may be better prepared for real life than those before them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This spring, on college campuses from coast to coast, reminders of the unprecedented challenges faced by the class of 2023.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): A once-in-a-century global pandemic took millions of lives and disrupted life for billions more. America ended our longest war, and Russia launched the first major ground war in Europe since World War II.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Some faced more adversity than others. Graduates at the University of Idaho spent an agonizing six weeks without an arrest in the stabbing deaths of four of their fellow students last November.\par \par \uc2 LOUIS FREEH (Former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director): You have gone through a fiscal crisis, a pandemic, and a horrific tragedy that could shatter any community, but did not here because of the strength and the work and the love that were shown to you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Tulane students had been looking forward to some normalcy in the fall of 2021 after a long year of COVID restrictions.\par \par \uc2 What they didn`t expect was an evacuation ahead of what ended up being a Category 4 hurricane.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL FITTS (President, Tulane University): This is a class that came back together after being displaced by Hurricane Ida, only to defy expectations, defeat the odds, and question the status quo.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  community lived through the horror of one of its own allegedly murdering three and shooting two others just hours after what would become the school`s last football game of the season.\par \par \uc2 JIM RYAN (President, \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 ): The deaths of three students, Devin Chandler, Lavel Davis Jr., and D`Sean Perry, were devastating. The depth of the loss of these talented and beloved teammates, classmates, and friends is incalculable.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The tragedy, UVA`s President Ryan said, on top of COVID brought more life lessons than could be taught in any classroom.\par \par \uc2 JIM RYAN: You rose to those challenges with grace and courage. You masked when it mattered and even when it didn`t, because you cared about this community above all else. And when tragedy struck last November, you organized and attended a silent vigil that brought this community together in profound and powerful ways.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: UVA`s athletic director, Carla Williams, was invited to deliver the school`s commencement address.\par \par \uc2 CARLA WILLIAMS (Athletic Director, \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 ): I had decided to politely decline, because I just did not know if I had enough left in the tank to give you guys my best. But, as fate would have it, I received a text from D`Sean Perry`s mom, Ms. Happy Perry, asking me if I thought the university would consider allowing her to stand in D`Sean`s place today.\par \par \uc2 I said: "It`s permissible, but are you sure you can do it?"\par \par \uc2 She paused and said: "Yes. He would be very proud of me, and I will power through to do it for him."\par \par \uc2 It was in that moment that I knew I would be speaking today.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We thank Carla Williams for her perseverance and for offering this sentiment we wish to pass on to all graduates this year:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARLA WILLIAMS: You are bright and shining examples of the best we have to offer. We need your courageous spirit. We need your innovation. We need your creativity. We need your stubbornness. We need your toughness, your brilliance, your grit.\par \par \uc2 And we need your compassion. We need each of you, and we need all of you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for Face the Nation, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230528ej5s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 College Graduates Face Uncertain Job Market\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Jacobson, Nancy Chen, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 1,913 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: With college graduation season here, we are taking a closer look at the job market for the class of 2023.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 75 percent of new grads are worried that the current economy will affect their job prospects according to monster.com.\par \par \uc2 As Nancy Chen shows us, they face hiring freezes and layoffs in some industries, and maybe wondering, what`s next?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: For the Class of 2023, it is hard not to get caught up in the excitement and thrill of graduation day.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s very much on, I don`t think it`s hit me that I graduated.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But ask these grads what`s next, and you`ll likely hear a range of responses.\par \par \uc2 What`s next for you guys?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Taking a nice long vacation and then starting work at a bank.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m looking for jobs right now. I know it`s like peak season for graduates.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I am doing civil engineering. There`s a lot of demand.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The job market is really hard right now.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: That`s because this year`s class is entering a bumpy labor market after starting college in fall 2019, just months before the pandemic hit.\par \par \uc2 According to LinkedIn, hiring overall is down 29 percent since April 2022, while hiring for entry level jobs requiring a college degree has dropped nearly 32 percent.\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why is it so tough right now?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, the labor market is really in transition. We had explosive job growth for sort of the first half of the pandemic era, and things started to turn and when the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  started raising interest rates and growth started to slow down, we saw job growth start to decelerate.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Which means even more challenges to navigate, especially with recent layoffs in the tech and finance industries, and the rise in AI technology.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DOBROWSKI (\uc2 Indeed.com\uc2  VP of global corporate communications): If there`s one class that is such an interesting case study, it is this Class of 2023 more than any other class.\par \par \uc2 Scott Dobrowski is Indeed`s career trends expert.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DOBROWSKI: What we are seeing in how they search for jobs and find jobs, it is working to the advantage of the Class of 2023.\par \par \uc2 They are trained in uncertainty and finding the right path that works for them, because they had to do it over the past four years.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Are there still opportunities for graduates right now?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DOBROWSKI: What this year`s class is stumbling upon is that the industries that many of them thought they would be going into, that is not the case.\par \par \uc2 So for instance, many people who thought they`d be going into tech, which has been riddled with layoffs are actually finding opportunities in retail, travel, and tourism.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Along with being versatile, Dobrowski also recommends focusing on building skills and experience rather than getting a certain job title right off the bat, as well as remembering to negotiate even in an uncertain market because it could impact pay for years to come.\par \par \uc2 Also, this graduation season, there will be plenty of pomp as usual, even if the circumstances are changing once again.\par \par \uc2 Overall, are you optimistic?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I am. I`m optimistic, I think. I hope.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: She hopes.\par \par \uc2 Now despite that uncertain labor market, these graduates are still pretty optimistic. According to monster.com, 88 percent of graduates who are job hunting are confident they will get an offer shortly after graduation, which is an even higher percentage than last year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, they`re confident. I hope they are right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love the confidence even though they`re looking at numbers that are pretty stark, down 32 percent for jobs requiring a college degree education level.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: The world will smack it down.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. If I were a high school grad right now, I`d be saying look carpentry, plumbing, electricity. They will never be unemployed.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Lots of bulbs.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s what I said.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are the soccer generation, what do we know?\par \par \uc2 Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, I will have the stories you`ll be talking about today, but first it is 7:45. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." our musical appreciation, working on the night.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I appreciate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Boogie Wonderland."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Of course.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, working on the night.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to hop right into it because we`ve got a lot to cover this morning. Let`s begin with the ongoing controversy over book banning in schools, this time involving Amanda Gorman, the first national youth poet laureate.\par \par \uc2 A grade school in South Florida restricted access to Gorman`s acclaimed poem, "The Hill We Climb" after one parent filed the complaint.\par \par \uc2 Gorman wrote that work for President Biden`s inauguration in 2021. Let me play a sample for you.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA GORMAN (First national youth poet laureate): If we`re to live up to our own time, then victory won`t lie in the blade, but in all of the bridges we`ve made, that is the promise to glade.\par \par \uc2 The hill we climb, if only we dare, it is because being American is more than a pride we inherit, it`s the past we step into and how we repair it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: According to documents shared by the Florida Freedom to Read Project, the parent said Gorman`s poem, which turned into a best- selling book was, "not educational," and indirectly included, "hate messages."\par \par \uc2 The parent also said -- get this -- Oprah Winfrey wrote the book, of course, she did not.\par \par \uc2 Gorman responded saying she was "gutted," and that she wrote the poem so young people could see themselves in a historical movement.\par \par \uc2 The school district says, no literature has been banned or removed, but it determined that "The Hill We Climb" is better suited for middle school students and was shelved in the middle school section of the media center.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, I`d say a couple of things about this. First, it feels almost like a non-story because it is a decent compromise to put it in the middle school section. I`m sure if you`re a really advanced elementary school, or you can walk over there.\par \par \uc2 Second thing and speaking as a parent of four, if you`ve got a fifth grader or a fourth grader who is sophisticated enough to be going through libraries and grabbing books off shelves, that they can handle the content of a book like that, or really any book. I mean, that`s advanced.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: As long as the availability is there for students. Obviously, banning of books, far larger issue, but availability has to be there of this material.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. And Amanda Gorman has been tweeting about it. She has been posting about it on social media. So, we`ll see. We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 But this is just the power of one parent.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They believe, it is.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: One voice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  is making it clear that the days of sharing your account with all of your friends and your family, well, those days are over. The streaming platform announced yesterday, it has extended its password sharing crack down to the United States.\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  will begin charging an extra $7.99 a month for add-on users who do not live in the customer`s household. That is $2.00 less than the cost of a basic plan.\par \par \uc2 The company says it will start blocking access to devices that try to log on from different addresses without paying the fee.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m in favor of it because like every \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  I`ve ever stayed in, someone like continues to log into my account. Hopefully, this will end that.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Good luck monitoring that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s really funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead in our Road to the Tonys, Anthony Mason takes us backstage at the acclaimed Broadway musical "Kimberly Akimbo."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 And coming up in this hour, our countdown to the Tonys continues with Anthony Mason who talks with the stars of "Kimberly Akimbo," one of the year`s most powerful Broadway musicals.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`m Dana Jacobson. For our "Changing the Game" series, meet two Vietnamese-American sisters who started their own food company to bring delicious Asian flavors into your home and I`m hearing we might get a taste.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. Only on CBS MORNINGS, The Points Guy reveals his 2023 rankings of America`s best airlines. See if you agree.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is hard to argue with, but we`ll try.\par \par \uc2 First here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  will announce his 2024 White House bid tonight on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with billionaire, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Our latest polling shows Governor DeSantis trailing former President Donald Trump by more than 30 points.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I think everybody needs to relax.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There is still no deal to raise the debt ceiling and avoid a potentially disastrous default.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE: We pay our bills. We have never defaulted in our history and we will never.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The voices of women as we have seen are absolutely needed.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: South Carolina`s Senate passes a six-week abortion ban sending it to the governor`s desk to be signed into law.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: This comes after a month of wrangling and failed attempts.\par \par \uc2 ED SHEERAN (Singer-songwriter): I`ve got tickets for all you guys to come.\par \par \uc2 (Cheering.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And this was the moment \uc2 Ed Sheeran\uc2  surprised a group of Florida high schoolers with concert tickets and their own special performance.\par \par \uc2 (ED SHEERAN singing "Perfect.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If that walked into my music class, that kind of talent, I would quit guitar. I am like, I`m out of here.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I was just thinking, he is dressed like a high school.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, he does look like a high school.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Looks like he belongs.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Can you imagine if you were sitting there in that class?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I cannot. I`ve got to C in high school guitar.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I am sorry to hear that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Vlad will work with you later.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Lessons after the show, Tony.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230525ej5o000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Looming Real Estate Threat\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Carter Evans \par \uc2 2,370 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In cities all across the country, maybe where you live, there is a lot of empty office space and that is because many workers are still working from home more than three years after the pandemic began. And truth is, a large number are unlikely ever to return, at least not full time.\par \par \uc2 In New York alone, get this, one real estate firm estimates that the city`s empty office space is the equivalent of more than 26 empty Empire State Buildings.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER RISINGS (Rising Reality Partners co-founder and CEO): I mean, when you look at it, you say this is where \uc2 Google\uc2  should be, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  should be.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Chris Risings commercial real estate company spent $35 million remodeling this historic Los Angeles building into an epic office space, and it is nearly empty.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER RISINGS: The building is about 300,000 feet and leased two floors out of the 11.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Nationwide, there`s an epidemic of empty office buildings in Los Angeles, New York, and San Francisco, less than half the office space is occupied, and much of it is financed with short-term bank loans.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER RISINGS: I see a tsunami of loans coming due that can`t be can`t be refinanced.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: About one-and-a-half trillion due by the end of 2025.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK CARROLL (Carroll founder and CEO): I think you`re going to start seeing a lot of defaults on office buildings.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Patrick Carroll manages a real estate portfolio worth more than $7 billion.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK CARROLL: Unfortunately, it is really the perfect storm. You know, these buildings are worth less than they were when they were originally financed and interest rates being up, valuations down, they`re not going to be able to refinance these buildings.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Companies like \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  are now requiring employees to come in three days a week. Same with \uc2 Credit Karma\uc2 , where it`s not business as usual.\par \par \uc2 Aren`t you supposed to be at work?\par \par \uc2 LUPE ROMO (\uc2 Credit Karma\uc2  senior manager): We are at work.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Senior manager, Lupe Romo is working on his pool game.\par \par \uc2 How is this not a waste of time?\par \par \uc2 LUPE ROMO: These are partners of mine. This is an investment in people.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Exactly what CEO, \uc2 Ken Lin\uc2  wants to see after three years working from home.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH LIN (\uc2 Credit Karma\uc2  founder and CEO): We think there is more energy, there`s more creativity, people work better together.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: To encourage collaboration, there is a game room, a music studio, and yoga all free, including the coffee bar.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got a long line here.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH LINN: Well, it is by design, right? We actually want interaction.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: But nationwide efforts to get employees back to the office are meeting resistance.\par \par \uc2 A recent Pew survey suggests that about a third of workers in the US who can work from home now do so all the time.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the commercial real estate market and what is happening now could cause more banks to fail?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK CARROLL: I mean, absolutely. You can see a run on all small regional banks and that could put us right back to where we were with a financial crisis of `08.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER RISING: It`s a beautiful building, tough investment.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Carter Evans in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Carter, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad Duthiers has got the stories you`ll be talking about today. Isn`t that right, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve got them right here, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But first, it is --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s the goods.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But first, it is 7:43 -- he is cramming. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is only one person.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean. You know who we are talking about, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Beyonce, right? That`s Beyonce.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is Beyonce, Tony D. You nailed it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you see? He said it confidence.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He did. He did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Beyonce.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He didn`t look around for a little bit of validation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right? Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Making sure I got it right.\par \par \uc2 So we are going to talk about Beyonce. But let`s start with the defending \uc2 NBA\uc2  champions, living to play another game.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Golden State Warriors\uc2  defeated the \uc2 LA Lakers\uc2  last night to avoid playoff elimination. Steph Curry led the warriors with 27 points.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Of course, he did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A game high, eight assists, I mean, Steph, go Steph.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Golden State is trying to rally from a three to one playoff deficit for only the second time in team history. The Lakers may have trouble going into Game 6 tomorrow. Anthony Davis left the game in the fourth quarter after Golden States` Kevon Looney hit him in the head. Ouch.\par \par \uc2 Davis left the arena at the end of the night --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that hurt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not intentional.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not intentional, but he did leave after doctors checked him out. According to \uc2 ESPN\uc2 , there was no mention of a concussion, so that is good. The Warriors still have to win the next game in LA to force a winner take all finale at home, so it ain`t over yet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`d like to see them all go. I`d like to see this game go all seven games.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you have to talk about the \uc2 Knicks\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 New York Knicks\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They won last night.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They pulled out yesterday. Yes, Jalen Brunson, Quentin Grimes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They clutched in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, so they were down three-one, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To bring home that victory. Yes, so go \uc2 New York Knickerbockers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are still alive.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are still alive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can sing the Bee Gee`s song, staying alive, uh-uh-uh -- I know you know it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s the move at the end, right? Put your hand on it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He puts the hand on -- he puts the hand and then he does the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know you can, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel like, are you the anonymous bidder on all the Travolta clothes?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Going to. I think Gene Siskel was the one who owned the original one.\par \par \uc2 And then you pointed --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Of course, you know exactly who.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gene Siskel. He was a standup guy, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, Tony.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Remember Siskel and Ever?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Anyway, all right, now to football at a slime filled Christmas classic, the \uc2 Las Vegas Raiders\uc2  will play the Super Bowl champion \uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2  in this year`s Nickelodeon NFL Nick-mas game, a Christmas Day telecast tailored for kids and families. It will air alongside regular game coverage which you can watch on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and stream on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 This is the third straight year Nickelodeon and CBS Sports are teaming up for this special event which promises plenty of slime. Nickelodeon`s weekly series, "NFL Slime Time" returns in September. Of course, it is hosted by our very own, Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know Nate is going to be involved in this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh yes. And Nick star, Young Dylan.\par \par \uc2 Nickelodeon and \uc2 CBS\uc2  are part of \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 . The full 2023 NFL schedule will be revealed tonight on the NFL Network and it all leads to Super Bowl 58. Have you heard? It is right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shouldn`t we go, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think, we should.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think we should go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Should we go?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If we book now, we can probably get good deals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s take our table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Shawna?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pick our table and go to Vegas.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everybody, should we all go?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We vote yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am getting confirmation, we are going. Yes, we go ahead.\par \par \uc2 No, I do not have confirmation. I do not, but I am trying to will it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shawna said we could go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is like, sure, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love you, Shawna. Thank you very much. We will remember that.\par \par \uc2 All right, finally, a superstar has returned to the concert stage and of course, we are all talking about Beyonce in the Beehive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @WentToJarrett/\uc2 Twitter\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Beyonce opened the Renaissance Tour in Stockholm, Sweden last night. The vibe, a futuristic disco extravaganza.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @BeyonceGarden/\uc2 Twitter\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @JustBeingAshhh/\uc2 Twitter\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Beyonce even performed some robot moves. It`s her first solo tour in nearly seven years.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @BeyonceGeraden/\uc2 Twitter\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Get this -- the setlist, 37 songs features favorites, like "Dangerously in Love." And she sang every single track from the Renaissance album.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, it is called the Renaissance Tour.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But still.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Every single song. She will return to North America in July.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Folks, I know people that are like getting multiple -- they`re going to like Stockholm --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They want -- all over the world and hoping to see the concert in LA which will be around her birthday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. September 4th.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. September 4th, and people think that it could be a humdinger.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m already trying to get --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Humdinger.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A humdinger.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Tyler Perry was in the audience last night standing next to Jay-Z and Blue. I mean, I know some people who were there that said it was insane.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Insane.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You had some -- you posted something on your \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because Joseph who is my former --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, yes, right, right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is now a big muckety-muck over at -- yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He went?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. He was there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be there soon, I`m sure of it.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am going, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Actor, Danielle Pinnock of the hit CBS comedy, "Ghosts," one of my favorite shows, she will be here to tell us about the Season 2 finale.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, a man who served 28 years for murder he did not commit is here in the studio today. Lamar Johnson is his name. He will tell us his remarkable story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Danielle Pinnock from the hit CBS series, "Ghosts" talks with us about tonight`s second season finale and her character`s surprise killer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. Now that I`m a Papa, we`re back with our series "Vlad to Dad." This morning, we are looking at the healthiest foods for babies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we`re going to show another cute baby today. It`s an exclusive, but first here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP: I have no idea who this woman -- this is a fake story, made up story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Former President Trump is questioned for the first time since a jury found him liable for the sexual abuse and defamation of writer E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 KAITLAN COLLINS: You did not go to the trial and actually testify.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 KAITLAN COLLINS: Do you wish that you would testify?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP: No, it wouldn`t have made a difference. This is a rigged deal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Migrants cross the southern border in record numbers with many more expected after Title 42 expires tonight.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Today, the House is scheduled to vote on a GOP scripted bill that would revive some Trump-era border policies.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congressman George Santos denies charges of fraud, money laundering, theft, and lying to Congress.\par \par \uc2 REP. GEORGE SANTOS (R-NY): I`m going to fight the witch hunt. I`m going to take care of clearing my name and I look forward to doing that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: An FDA panel recommend selling a popular birth control pill over-the-counter letting women take the pill without a prescription for the very first time.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This really provides women with a lot more autonomy and control over their contraception.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my God, he ate the cake.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the California family`s birthday cake disappears and a bear is caught in the act with frosting on its face.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: He is finishing all the cake.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: He ate the cake. Go bear. Go. He ate the cake.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who knew bears like cake?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, I knew. Go bear go. He is not going to cut it either. That`s pretty --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He ate the cake. I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very good.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230512ej5b000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Virginia Library System Is Helping Working Moms Stay Connected To Their Kids, And It`s Catching On Around The World\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 432 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: While remote work has been a blessing for millions, it comes with its own unique challenges.\par \par \uc2 Nobody understands that more than working moms, who often find themselves doing double duty, both their jobs and childcare.\par \par \uc2 In our continuing series Moms in Focus, CBS` Jan Crawford finds, necessity is truly the mother of invention.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voiceover): For keeping your concentration, it is hard to beat the library.\par \par \uc2 T`KEIAH WOODSON (Parent): Hello.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Especially when you have to keep your eye on your little one.\par \par \uc2 T`KEIAH WOODSON: At home, it`s impossible. Here, I can just get things done.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Here at the Fairfield Area Public Library, near Richmond, Virginia, there is a special place for children and their parents, like kindergarten teacher T`keiah Woodson.\par \par \uc2 T`KEIAH WOODSON: With the creative center, it is much, much easier, and they`re close enough where I can just reach over, but I can still do what I need to do on the computer.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It`s called Work and Play Station, designed and created by two working moms.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA WEEDMAN (Director, Henrico County Public Library): I would see moms and parents and caregivers struggling, and I thought, there has got to be a better way to do this.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: When the library opened four years ago, photos quickly went viral.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON WRAY (Principal Interior Designer, Quinn Evans): All of the public libraries I`m working on right now, every librarian is asking to have one.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And this started with you thinking, there has to be a better way.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA WEEDMAN: It touches me. It really does. It visibly shows people that we care.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And, as working moms, they knew there was a better way.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford, CBS News, Henrico County, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Always a better way.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s tonight`s CBS EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell, looking forward to Mother`s Day this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230511ej590005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for May 9, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Manuel Bojorquez, Nicole Sganga, Nancy Cordes,  Nikki Battiste, Omar Villafranca, Major Garrett, Elaine Quijano, Jan  Crawford \par \uc2 3,696 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  becomes the first former president in history to be found liable of sexual abuse and defamation. The breaking news, after a federal jury awards former columnist E. Jean Carroll $5 million in damages.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 A jury in New York City deliberates for less than three hours in the civil trial against the former president.\par \par \uc2 JOE TACOPINA (Attorney For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ): Part of me was obviously very happy that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not branded a rapist.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news, embattled George Santos charged by the Justice Department. What we are learning.\par \par \uc2 New details about the Texas mall shooter`s disturbing online history and the hero police officer who saved lives.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN HARVEY (Allen, Texas, Police Chief): He immediately addressed an attacker that was injuring and murdering innocent victims.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The debt ceiling standoff shifts to the White House, where President Biden holds a high-level meeting with congressional leaders.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): We have now just two weeks to go.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Today, there are new guidelines on breast cancer screening. How often should we get a mammogram?\par \par \uc2 So you think every other year is not sufficient?\par \par \uc2 DR. MAXINE S. JOCHELSON (Memorial \uc2 Sloan Kettering Cancer Center\uc2 ): Correct.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON WRAY (Principal Interior Designer, Quinn Evans): Every librarian is asking to have one in their space.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Our series, Moms in Focus: What happens when a mother sees a problem? She comes up with an innovative way to help other working parents.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Lonnie Walker gets up the first shot on the court.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Lonnie Walker, great effort.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Walker, pull-up. Walker hits another one! It`s the Lonnie Walker game!\par \par \uc2 LEBRON JAMES (American Professional Basketball Player): We don`t win the game without Lonnie Walker tonight. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 We began with breaking news, verdicts in the civil rape and defamation trial of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . A federal jury in New York found \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  liable for sexually abusing advice columnist E. Jean Carroll nearly 30 years ago. The jury also found the former president liable for defaming Carroll when he denied the allegations and said she was lying to get publicity for her book.\par \par \uc2 The former writer for "Elle" magazine was awarded $5 million in damages. The trial saw Carroll testify in graphic detail about the assault inside a luxury department store dressing room in the 1990s. The jury was also shown a videotaped deposition of Trump being questioned under oath about the allegations.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elaine Quijano has been following the trial and is going to start us off tonight outside the federal courthouse in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 E. Jean Carroll appeared to nod as the jury`s decision was read. It was an emotional nine-day trial. And, in a statement, Carroll called the verdict a victory, not just for her, but for democracy and sexual assault survivors everywhere.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): Writer E. Jean Carroll walked out of court saying very little, but the jury`s verdict spoke volumes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`ve been here the whole time.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: After just under three hours of deliberations, the jury of six men and three women found Trump liable for one count of battery and one count of defamation.\par \par \uc2 It awarded Carroll about $2 million for abuse and about $3 million for defamation.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: This verdict is the first time that a former president of the United States has actually been found liable for sexual abuse, as well as defamation.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Carroll had accused Trump of raping her in a New York department dressing store dressing room in the mid-`90s. She first went public with her claim in 2019 memoir.\par \par \uc2 Trump denied the accusation, calling it a hoax.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I was falsely accused by this woman. I have no idea who she is. It`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: During the trial, Carroll gave graphic testimony. She told jurors: "I`m here because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  raped me, and, when I wrote about it, he said it didn`t happen. He shattered my reputation."\par \par \uc2 Trump waived his right to testify, and the defense called no witnesses. Outside the courthouse, his lawyer, Joe Tacopina, said the deck was stacked against his client.\par \par \uc2 JOE TACOPINA (Trump Defense Lawyer): Part of me was obviously very happy that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not branded a rapist.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: But, during the trial, jurors heard parts of Trump`s deposition in the case.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And she`s not my type.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: He misidentified Carroll in a photograph as his second wife, Marla Maples.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: It`s Marla.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: And was asked about that infamous "Access Hollywood" recording.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTA KAPLAN (Attorney For E. Jean Carroll): It`s true with stars that they can grab women by the (EXPLETIVE DELETED)?\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Well, that`s what -- if you look over the last million years, I guess that`s been largely true, not always, but largely true, unfortunately or fortunately.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: His deposition could be said to be a disaster in this case. The worst witness against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Trump`s lawyers say they plan to appeal. The former president is facing a host of other legal challenges, including criminal probes in New York, Washington and Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 The former president has denied any wrongdoing -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elaine Quijano, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in chief Washington correspondent Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 Good evening.\par \par \uc2 All right, what`s the political impact of this? Because he`s running for president.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And he`s the current Republican front-runner, not just a former president. But this was, in all terms, Norah, a character sketch of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  then and now. And how -- and Senate Republicans are not rallying to his defense.\par \par \uc2 Let`s just talk about the case for a second. The defense for former President Trump was, he`s rude, he`s crude, but he wouldn`t sexually batter this woman and then defame her. Well, in a contested process, where everyone had every opportunity to put evidence and witnesses before a jury, a jury said no, in fact, that`s precisely what the former president did.\par \par \uc2 Now, those who rally to the former president`s defense say, well, there was a character debate in 2016 he passed and was elected or this is all politically timed. But Senate Republican leaders today, Norah, on Capitol Hill, in the aftermath of this verdict, said, there`s too much drama. Voters will evaluate that. And, yes, Trump could get the nomination, but he can`t be elected in a general election.\par \par \uc2 That`s Senate Republicans, their verdict on this verdict.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That suggests the tide may be starting --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Possibly.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- to turn.\par \par \uc2 Major Garrett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to some more breaking news. CBS News has just learned that the Justice Department filed charges against embattled New York Republican Congressman George Santos. He is expected to surrender in New York tomorrow, when the nature of the charges will be revealed.\par \par \uc2 CBS News previously reported that federal prosecutors were looking into the finances and financial disclosures of the freshman congressman after he admitted to lying about parts of his resume and background. A spokesman for Santos has declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Now to the investigation into the mass shooting at an outlet mall in the Dallas suburb of Allen, Texas. Eight people were killed, including three children, and seven others injured.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca reports, the gunman had been researching the location for weeks. But police say his victims appeared to be chosen at random.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Tonight, investigators are running digital forensics on the gunman`s cell phone and computers, trying to learn more about a possible motive.\par \par \uc2 Troopers confirmed the shooter had embraced white supremacist and neo-Nazi ideology in online posts he made on a Russian social media site.\par \par \uc2 HANK SIBLEY (Regional Director, \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 ): We do know that he had neo-Nazi ideation. He had patches. He had tattoos. Even his signature verified that.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Investigators today admitted they still don`t know why the gunman opened fire outside the Allen Premium Outlets.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR HANK SIBLEY: The big question that we`re dealing with right now is, what`s his motive? Why did he do this? Well, the big question is, we don`t know.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: But authorities say the attack didn`t last long, thanks to the quick response of an Allen police officer who stopped the shooter.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR HANK SIBLEY: I think, within three or four minutes, the Allen P.D. officer had neutralized a suspect.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: We understand that he worked some private security gigs before. Did he ever work at the Allen Premium Outlets?\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR HANK SIBLEY: Not to my knowledge.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Investigators also said they believe the shooter targeted the location, but shot people at random.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR HANK SIBLEY: He was very random in the people he killed. It didn`t matter the age, same race or sex. He just shot people, and which is horrific in itself.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Among the eight people killed was 26-year-old engineer Aishwarya Thatikonda. For friends and co-workers, the shock of the shooting is still hard to fathom.\par \par \uc2 SRINIVAS CHALUVADI (President & CEO, Perfect General Contractors LLC): She had big dreams. She wanted to make a living in this country, to become a permanent resident, buy a home, get married, make a family, build a family.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Troopers say the shooter had three guns on him at the time of the attack and had five more in the car, along with dozens of rounds of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 All were legally purchased. And investigators aren`t releasing the name of the Allen police officer who stopped the shooter. But many are calling him a hero. And, through his attorney, he says he`s doing well. He`s just trying to process this tragedy -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to an important story. An increase in breast cancer diagnoses has led to new guidelines for when women should get their first screenings. The U.S. Preventive Services Task force now says women should begin regular mammograms starting at the age of 40.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, breast cancer accounts for more than 43,000 deaths every year, and it is the second leading cause of cancer deaths among women.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikki Battiste reports, it`s a -- it`s a disease that one in eight women will develop in her lifetime.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voiceover): The new guidelines, mammograms every other year beginning at 40, are a dramatic shift from the task force`s previous recommendation that women begin biannual screenings at age 50.\par \par \uc2 DR. MAXINE S. JOCHELSON (Memorial \uc2 Sloan Kettering Cancer Center\uc2 ): I`m happy they moved to 40, but every other year is not good enough.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: In fact, the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  recommends screenings every year, but beginning at age 45.\par \par \uc2 It`s also confusing. What should women do?\par \par \uc2 DR. MAXINE S. JOCHELSON: I think it`s very important for women to be proactive and really promote themselves for having what is good screening.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: According to the \uc2 Radiological Society of North America\uc2 , the rate of metastatic breast cancer among women between 25 to 39 is up 32 percent since 2009.\par \par \uc2 SARA GIGLIO (Breast Cancer Survivor): I had a double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Despite having no family history, Sara Giglio chose to get annual mammograms before she was 40. She was just 42 with a newborn baby when she was diagnosed.\par \par \uc2 SARA GIGLIO: I did find out after the surgery that the cancer did spread.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Like nearly half of women 40 and older in the U.S., Giglio has dense breasts, which increase the risk of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jules Cohen is her oncologist.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULES COHEN (Medical Oncologist, Stony Brook Cancer Center): If you`re worried that you`re going to be diagnosed with an incurable disease, and that keeps you away from screening, I would say that`s -- that`s not something you need to worry about.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The new guidelines do not apply to people with a personal history or high risk of breast cancer. They should continue to consult doctors on exam frequency.\par \par \uc2 Women with dense breasts can opt for additional screening, like an ultrasound or MRI, but they`re often not covered by insurance -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikki Battiste with that important information, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, President Biden sat down with congressional leaders in the Oval Office this afternoon in hopes of finding common ground on raising the debt limit and avoiding an economic disaster.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 All right, Nancy, did any agreements come from this, come out of this high- stakes meeting?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Unfortunately, Norah, if Wall Street and Main Street were looking for reassurance coming out of this meeting, they did not get it.\par \par \uc2 Instead, President Biden and House Speaker Kevin McCarthy stuck to their positions and agreed to meet again on Friday. But, at that point, the U.S. could be less than three weeks away from defaulting on its debt, unless Congress acts.\par \par \uc2 And here`s why this is so serious. Even a brief default could cause a downgrade of U.S. credit and delay the distribution of food stamps and Social Security checks and so much more, while a prolonged crisis could drive up interest rates and put more than eight million Americans out of work.\par \par \uc2 Now, here`s the impasse. President Biden says Congress needs to raise the nation`s borrowing limit, no strings attached. But Speaker McCarthy and Republicans say the debt limit hike needs to be paired with major domestic spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Did the president tell you that there were any spending cuts that he would be open to?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): Well, I asked him numerous times, are there some places we could find savings? He wouldn`t give me any.\par \par \uc2 So I`m hopeful that we will be able to find them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Top aides from both sides are going to keep talking, but there`s not much time left to work this out. Congress only has 12 working days between now and June 1, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Quite a stalemate.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, and another deadline facing the Biden administration. Border cities have declared states of emergency, as thousands of migrants await the ending of the pandemic-era rule known as Title 42.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez reports, President Biden spoke with Mexico`s president by phone today to try to work on some possible solutions to this growing crisis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): Hundreds of migrants continue to arrive in El Paso daily, seeking shelter at facilities that are already filled to capacity, among them Johana Yovera, who is from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 She crossed the border five days ago with her 11- and 13-year-old daughters. They were one of the lucky ones to get a bed.\par \par \uc2 (JOHANA YOVERA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: She wants a better life for her daughters, she says. The economy and the education in Venezuela are not good.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Today, border officials handed out these flyers, warning migrants to go through proper immigration channels or face arrest or deportation. You can see long lines of migrants waiting to surrender.\par \par \uc2 With days to go before Title 42 expires, city and state officials are bracing for a sudden increase in migrant crossings. Republican Texas Governor Greg Abbott sent nearly 600 more National Guard troops to beef up the border.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (R-Texas): President Biden is laying down a welcome mat to people across the entire world, saying that the United States` border is wide open.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: But the Biden administration announced new regulations. One policy would banish migrants from the U.S. for five years if they go outside the asylum process or are deemed ineligible for protection.\par \par \uc2 Still, in Brownsville, Texas, city officials are also dealing with the migration influx.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nicole Sganga is there.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Here in Brownsville, about 2,000 migrants arriving daily, immigration officials using Fort Brown, a former military outpost, as a pop-up processing center to help expedite asylum claims.\par \par \uc2 They`re hopeful this contingency measure meets the demand. But the question now, for how long?\par \par \uc2 TREY MENDEZ (Mayor of Brownsville, Texas): The city of Brownsville`s process has always been something that could process about 1,000 people a day. We have been doing it about 16 hours a day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Back here in El Paso, authorities said they would limit traffic along the main port of entry as part of the operation to reduce illegal crossings.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, the first of 1,500 military personnel are set to arrive at the border, not to interact with migrants, but support Border Patrol operations -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manny Bojorquez, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Also in Brownsville, a vigil is planned tonight for the eight victims killed by an SUV driver who slammed into a group of migrants outside a shelter on Sunday. The driver has been charged with eight counts of manslaughter and 10 counts of aggravated assault with a deadly weapon. Investigators are awaiting toxicology results and are trying to determine if the crash was intentional.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas, Russian leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  presented his twisted take on his invasion of Ukraine today, saying the West has unleashed a real war against Russia. Putin spoke at a World War II Victory Day parade, which was scaled down to only a single tank because much of Russia`s weaponry is either in Ukraine or has been destroyed in the war. Overnight, Russia attacked Ukraine with dozens of missiles.\par \par \uc2 An investigation is under way after a small plane crash in the suburb of Tacoma, Washington.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Two people were critically injured today when a small sea plane crashed in a suburb of Tacoma, Washington.\par \par \uc2 You can see the mangled wreckage in the yard of a home. No houses were hit and no one else was injured. The cause of the wreck now under investigation.\par \par \uc2 An 8-year-old is lost in the wilderness for two days. We will have his remarkable story. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to a remarkable story of survival from Michigan`s Upper Peninsula.\par \par \uc2 Police say an 8-year-old boy camping with his family got lost while gathering firewood on Saturday. For two days, more than 150 people searched for him. On Monday, he was finally found safe and sound about two miles from his campsite. He had sheltered under a log with nothing to eat or drink, but came through it appearing to be just fine.\par \par \uc2 Very lucky, indeed.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up: A Virginia library system is helping working moms stay connected to their kids, and it`s catching on around the world.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: While remote work has been a blessing for millions, it comes with its own unique challenges.\par \par \uc2 Nobody understands that more than working moms, who often find themselves doing double duty, both their jobs and childcare.\par \par \uc2 In our continuing series Moms in Focus, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford finds, necessity is truly the mother of invention.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voiceover): For keeping your concentration, it is hard to beat the library.\par \par \uc2 T`KEIAH WOODSON (Parent): Hello.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Especially when you have to keep your eye on your little one.\par \par \uc2 T`KEIAH WOODSON: At home, it`s impossible. Here, I can just get things done.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Here at the Fairfield Area Public Library, near Richmond, Virginia, there is a special place for children and their parents, like kindergarten teacher T`keiah Woodson.\par \par \uc2 T`KEIAH WOODSON: With the creative center, it is much, much easier, and they`re close enough where I can just reach over, but I can still do what I need to do on the computer.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It`s called Work and Play Station, designed and created by two working moms.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA WEEDMAN (Director, Henrico County Public Library): I would see moms and parents and caregivers struggling, and I thought, there has got to be a better way to do this.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: When the library opened four years ago, photos quickly went viral.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON WRAY (Principal Interior Designer, Quinn Evans): All of the public libraries I`m working on right now, every librarian is asking to have one.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And this started with you thinking, there has to be a better way.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA WEEDMAN: It touches me. It really does. It visibly shows people that we care.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And, as working moms, they knew there was a better way.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford, CBS News, Henrico County, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Always a better way.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell, looking forward to Mother`s Day this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230510ej59000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Writers Hit Picket Line\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Elise Preston, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 452 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is Day 2 now of the strike by TV and film writers demanding higher wages and they are picketing outside some prominent studios across the country, including our parent company, that would be Paramount.\par \par \uc2 The stakes are big -- huge for the future of entertainment and many shows have already been knocked off the air.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston has more now from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What do we want?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Contracts.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Outside production studios across the country.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Corporate greed has got to go.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Thousands of writers have stepped away from their keyboard to form picket lines.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Better pay, better residuals. Larger writers` rooms.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Here in Los Angeles, WGA president, \uc2 Meredith Stiehm\uc2  joined protesters outside of Netflix Studios.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH STIEHM (WGAW president): They need to fix the model that they`ve broken and until they do, we are going to be here and we are going to be on strike.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Late night has taken the first hit.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Talk.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Daytime shows like \uc2 CBS\uc2  "The Talk" have also gone dark.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Bold and the Beautiful.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: And it is the same story for soaps.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Bold and the Beautiful.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Among popular series, Showtime`s hit, "Yellowjackets."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Yellowjackets.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Which is part of Paramount has stopped working on its third season.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "House of the Dragon.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Meanwhile, HBO`s "House of the Dragon" will continue production on Season 2 since they tell "Variety" that scripting is already complete.\par \par \uc2 Cynthia Littleton is the co-editor-in-chief of "Variety" and author of "TV on Strike." She says a prolonged strike would mean writers lose their jobs as studios shift production to unscripted content.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA LITTLETON ("Variety" co-editor-in-chief): The studios are going to lose a lot of money on having to scramble. They are going to have to shut down shows that they thought they were going to produce. There is going to be pain all around.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: For CBS MORNINGS, Elise Preston, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To all the writers out there, on behalf of CBS MORNINGS, we say thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230504ej530008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Writers Strike Begins; Pilots Authorize Strike; Deadly Illinois Dust Storm; Michigan Sets May Snow Record; Gruesome Oklahoma Discovery; New Details in Texas Manhunt; Violent Protests in France; Treasury Issues Debt Limit Warning; American Killed in Ukraine; Health Risks of Forever Chemicals; Fashion`s Biggest Night; Oprah`s Book Club Pick; 2023 Tony Nominations; Live, Learn Love Well, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Elise Preston, Charlie  De Mar, Stephanie Abrams, Omar Villafranca, Nancy Cordes, Charlie D`Agata,  Roxana Saberi, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 14,199 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday, the second day of May already. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight: Entertainment writers are on strike. Now this could have a huge impact on your favorite movies and TV shows. We`ll have the latest support.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON ("The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon" host): I support my writers. I need my writers. I need them real bad.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They need to write your piece or else.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON: Yes, I`ve got no show without my writers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Multiple people are dead after a dust storm in Illinois caused a massive pileup.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It was like somebody put a brown blanket in front of your windshield. You can see nothing.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In Oklahoma, police said, they found the bodies of seven people on a rural property during a search for two missing girls.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And some of fashion`s biggest stars channeled cats for last night`s Met Gala in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (DOJA CAT meowing.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Serena Williams announced before the Gala that she is pregnant with her second child.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Congratulations, Serena.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Alex Verdugo with his third walk off of the season.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Mr. Walk Off strikes again.\par \par \uc2 MAN: James Harden channeling his inner Cookie Monster vibe pregame and the beard was a monster in the game, including the dagger with eight seconds to go.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Harden has got 45.\par \par \uc2 MAN: For the first time in 15 years, television and movie writers are going on strike.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Which means that writers might have to do something totally against their nature. Go outside.\par \par \uc2 These are our writers, these people, these are our writers, and I`ll stick myself in there because I`m WGA, too, and they are so important to our show.\par \par \uc2 They write the monologue, the meanwhile, the cold open, and without these people -- without these people -- this show would be called "The Late Show" for the guy rambling about "The Lord of the Rings" and boats for an hour.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, I sure hope this is resolved soon.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because everything starts with the written word -- everything.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it certainly have a point it would seem, I hope everybody can work this out sooner rather than later. The last strike was a hundred days. We don`t want that again.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got some big reveals on today`s show only on CBS MORNINGS. Very tough to get her, I had to grovel. Oprah will be here in person to tell us about her latest --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Say what?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are you sure?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Please, Oprah. Please, please come and she said okay. She is coming to talk about her latest Book Club pick.\par \par \uc2 We`re also going to speak with the author. Oprah says, this book is epic and breathtaking. So, we hope you`ll stick around for this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, we will have this year`s Tony nominees in six major categories first on CBS MORNINGS. Two of Broadway`s brightest stars, Tony winner, Myles Frost from "MJ" and Lea Michele who`s in "Funny Girl" will be here to make the announcements.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right, from the writers of books to the writers of plays to the writers themselves, we`re going to begin with that breaking news from Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 Entertainment writers now on strike. What that means is production is halted on many of your favorite shows as Writers Guild members push for better pay on streaming shows and better working conditions.\par \par \uc2 It is an action taken by more than 11,000 writers all across the country. And the last time this happened back in 2007, Hollywood lost more than $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston has the latest now from \uc2 Sony Pictures Studios\uc2  in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Elise, they say this is the strike that streaming brought. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Picket lines will form outside of studios on both coasts later this afternoon. Shows and production will come to a halt.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned that last strike was 15 years ago, it lasted more than three months. Insiders warn appointment TV viewing could be delayed even longer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Writers might have to do something totally against their nature, go outside.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jimmy Kimmel Live.")\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Stephen Colbert is one of several late night hosts whose shows could go dark tonight, as more than 11,000 Hollywood writers prepare to stop working today and start picketing.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Succession.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: At the forefront of the strike says the union are dwindling earnings for writers in the age of streaming.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Trek: Picard.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: While companies like \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and our parent company Paramount have flourished --\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELONI (Puck News): The writers in a nutshell feel that the streaming revolution has really left them behind.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Matthew Beloni is a Hollywood insider and founding partner of Puck News.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELONI: They don`t get as much money when shows play on streaming services than they did when shows are played on traditional linear television.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Young Sheldon.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Roughly 85 percent of households are subscribed to at least one streaming service, but the writers claim they aren`t sharing in that success.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Mandalorian.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: And with shows producing fewer episodes each season, writers receive less pay.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Mandalorian.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The WGA says almost half of its TV writers work at the minimum salary up from 33 percent from a decade ago, but studio producers say their budgets are already maxed out.\par \par \uc2 Last time, writers took to the picket line was 2007, which helped give rise to reality TV.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Keeping Up with the Kardashians.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: That strike lasted 100 days and its impact cost the city of Los Angeles more than $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it could last six to eight months?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELONI: This could last a year. We are in a perfect storm here because the studios are all under financial pressure right now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, the alliance representing studio producers says it presented a comprehensive proposal to the Writers Guild last night. The Guild says it is insufficient.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, there is added pressure for a deal as both the Screen Actors and Directors Guild seek new contracts next month -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, let`s hope it doesn`t last a year. Thank you, Elise.\par \par \uc2 Pilots for American Airlines voted overwhelmingly to authorize a strike for their members putting pressure on the airline big time to increase pay ahead of this summer travel season.\par \par \uc2 Pilots picketed for two hours yesterday outside the airports around the country, but this does not mean that pilots are walking off the job just yet. It could happen though if there is no deal soon.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just American Airlines by the way, \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  pilots also began voting yesterday to potentially authorize a strike of their own.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now we move to Central Illinois where a freak dust storm is blamed for a string of accidents that killed at least six people.\par \par \uc2 Witnesses say drivers on Interstate 55 south of Springfield couldn`t see when the dust kicked up yesterday. More than 30 other people were hurt and some of those injuries are described as life threatening.\par \par \uc2 Charlie De Mar has more on the people who made it out alive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What is happening?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Terrified drivers wove through the chaos on I-55 with almost no visibility yesterday.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: At least six people were killed and more than 30 injured in a series of crashes after dust engulfed the highway.\par \par \uc2 DISPATCHER: There are multiple injuries, multiple injuries, multiple vehicles.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Illinois State Police said 30 commercial vehicles and 40 to 60 passenger vehicles were involved in the accidents, which took place over a two-mile stretch of road.\par \par \uc2 They also say, two semis caught fire.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Nothing in my 24 year career that I can recall having something as severe as this.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Authorities blame the dust storm on excessive winds blowing up loose dirt from nearby farms.\par \par \uc2 DISPATCHER: Traffic is at a standstill at the 76 and building up.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Both the northbound and southbound sides of the road had been shut down to all traffic.\par \par \uc2 Nathan, you`re covered in dust right now?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN CORMIER (Truck driver): Yes. I am sure my lungs are, too.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Nathan Cormier was one of the dozens of cars that got stuck on the interstate.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN CORMIER: I went on this side of the trailer of the truck, and I`m glad I did because there are two red cars. They careened that back of that truck. So if I had moved, I would have been careened myself.\par \par \uc2 And then I pulled up further and got out, I just went ahead and went from vehicle to vehicle to try to find people.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: The injured victims around him ranged in age from two years old to 80 years old.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Charlie De Mar.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As you can see, they are having a tough time in Illinois. Let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 . She is with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 . She`s got more information on the conditions that led to the crash in Central Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 There were several factors at play.\par \par \uc2 We had an intense low pressure in the east, high pressure to the west, this sets up what we call a pressure gradient, which makes for strong winds.\par \par \uc2 In Central Illinois, those winds were gusting 35 to over 40 miles per hour over that dry topsoil that farmers had been tilling, and there is no windbreak, meaning no trees or anything to slow the flow over I-55, which runs north-south, so those northwest to west winds were blowing all that dust right over the road.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ll still see breezy conditions today. Gusts up to 40 miles an hour, so we could see more blowing dust and difficult driving conditions. It does start to ease into our Wednesday, but this system will also bring rain and more snow into the upper peninsula of Michigan, we could see a foot of snow and we`re already in some places five feet above average.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 And Gayle, this system is a slow mover. It will take the next couple of days to fully clear the coast.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not the news they want to hear out there. Thank you very much, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 . Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now to an absolutely horrific story out of Oklahoma. Seven bodies discovered there about an hour south of Tulsa. We don`t have many more details than that, but we do know there is heartache here.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 EDDY RICE (Okmulgee County sheriff): We`ve searched the property and we`ve located seven bodies.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Oklahoma law enforcement found those bodies in Henrietta, Monday, but would not discuss any additional facts of the case.\par \par \uc2 GERALD DAVIDSON (Oklahoma State Bureau of Investigation Interim supervisor): There is no suspect at large that we are looking for right at this moment.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: While neither a motive nor the name of a suspect has been released, the father of 16-year-old Brittany Brewer confirmed his daughter was among the dead.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN BREWER (Brittany Brewer`s father): She always played with her little sister and I don`t know how I`m going to tell her because she thought Brittany`s lost, Brittney is missing. Britney is missing.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve got to tell a four-year-old she`s gone, and I don`t even know how to do that.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Earlier in the day, Oklahoma Highway Patrol reported Brewer was endangered and missing, along with her friend, 14-year-old, Ivy Webster. They were believed to be traveling with 39-year-old, Jesse McFadden.\par \par \uc2 Brewer`s father told Tulsa station, KOTV that his daughter was friends with McFadden`s children, and had spent the weekend with the McFadden family and was supposed to come home Sunday night, but never arrived.\par \par \uc2 Records from the state`s Department of Corrections showed that McFadden was convicted on first-degree rape charges in November of 2003 and released from prison in October 2020.\par \par \uc2 He was slated to stand trial Monday on charges of soliciting sexual conduct with a minor and possession of child pornography.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Omar Villafranca.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: An awful story and we`re learning new details about the suspect in a shooting that left five people dead in Texas, Friday, as law enforcement continues a desperate search to find him.\par \par \uc2 Officials tells CBS News that 38-year-old Francisco Oropesa is a Mexican national who has illegally entered this country and been deported several times going back to 2009.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is an $80,000.00 reward for information that could lead to Oropesa`s capture.\par \par \uc2 According to a witness whose wife and son were killed, police were called five times before the shooting took place, but help did not arrive in time. The local sheriff says deputies got there as fast as they could.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In France, what had been peaceful protests on May Day, that`s the International Workers Day turned violent last night as protesters threw Molotov cocktails and fireworks at riot police in Paris. One of those gasoline bombs hit a police officer and exploded.\par \par \uc2 And workers -- the conditions here, they`re angry about the French government`s move to raise the national retirement age to 64. They are also demanding higher wages because of inflation.\par \par \uc2 Cars and businesses were set on fire in the cities of Leon and Nantes and police responded in those cities with teargas and water cannons.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  is warning Congress that the government could run out of cash to pay its bills in less than a month if lawmakers don`t raise the debt limit. This deadline means more uncertainty for our economy that has had recent bank failures and continuing inflation.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 We are now starting to see a little bit of movement. President Biden called all four congressional leaders on Monday and invited them to a meeting next Tuesday here at the White House to discuss the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 This new alert from the Treasury Department is only adding to the pressure to reach a resolution quickly.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): For over 200 years, America has never ever, ever failed to pay its debt.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That record could be tested in just four weeks, according to Treasury secretary, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the letter to congressional leaders, Monday, she warned that the government won`t be able to meet its financial obligations potentially as early as June 1st unless the debt limit is raised or suspended by Congress.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We pay our bills and we should do so without reckless hostage taking from some of the MAGA Republicans in Congress.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The bill is passed.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: House Republicans passed a bill last week that would raise the debt limit, but the bill included a range of steep spending cuts targeting many of President Biden`s priorities.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats say that bill is dead on arrival.\par \par \uc2 On his first foreign trip as House Speaker, Kevin McCarthy spoke about the debt ceiling from Israel, saying the bill should be a starting point for negotiations.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): We will not pass the debt ceiling that just raises it without doing something about our debt.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: While the US has never defaulted on its debt, it did come close during a similar fight in 2011, which rocked the market and led to a downgrade of US credit.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): The President of the United States needs to needs to see the reality, see the writing on the wall and do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: \uc2 Greg McBride\uc2 , chief financial analyst at \uc2 Bankrate\uc2  says a failure to act could have even more serious consequences this time around.\par \par \uc2 GREG MCBRIDE (Bankrate chief financial analyst): If the debt ceiling doesn`t get raised, all bets are off, we will have a recession and it won`t be a mild one.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: This added uncertainty comes as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is still trying to deal with the impact of high inflation.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Fed begins a two-day meeting to decide whether to increase interest rates again, and by how much. All of this, Gayle, is high stakes for the White House. The state of the economy, of course will be pivotal to the president`s re-election prospects.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, we`ll all be watching. Thank you, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, the most amazing looks from last night`s Met Gala. There were some doozies you could say, including a series of tributes to legendary designer and his cat. Let`s just say people got very creative, but first it is -- that`s Doja Cat right there -- but first, it is 7:15. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, the latest on Ukraine`s plans to strike back with a spring offensive against Russian soldiers. Plus, the hidden threat from so-called forever chemicals, how the potentially dangerous substances are showing up in farms and fish.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The family of a one-time US Marine says he has been killed in the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 His mother says 26-year-old Cooper Harris Andrews from Cleveland was hit by a mortar shell in the eastern city of Bakhmut. Russian troops have destroyed much of that city in recent months trying to take control.\par \par \uc2 A Ukrainian counteroffensive is said to be coming soon.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in Ukraine where Russian missiles are hitting civilian targets away from the frontlines.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: A huge fireball lit up the skies in a series of strikes, whatever hit it or whatever it hit was big.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian authorities would only say it was an industrial complex.\par \par \uc2 Not all the missiles hit their mark. We have now learned that two people were killed and 40 were injured in this missile strike and Pavlohrad, around 70 miles from the frontline.\par \par \uc2 Residents describe air raid sirens blaring all night.\par \par \uc2 Olga and Serhiy Litvinenko (sp?) took shelter in a garage here until around 2:30 in the morning when he decided to go inside. Less than two hours later, the garage took a direct hit.\par \par \uc2 Have you had time to realize how lucky you are to be alive?\par \par \uc2 (SERHIY LITVINENKO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "Yes, I got lucky," Serhiy told us. "I was extremely lucky. I`m still trying to process exactly what happened. In my mind, it feels like I`m somewhere else."\par \par \uc2 Explosions echoed above the skies of Kyiv in the second heavy barrage in three nights. Soldiers and civilians alike brace for more preemptive strikes ahead of Ukraine`s much anticipated spring counteroffensive.\par \par \uc2 A senior Ukrainian defense official told CBS News, preparations are nearly complete. Recent rainy weather may have stalled the start of it.\par \par \uc2 But he said when it begins, the whole world will know.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Charlie D`Agata, Pavlohrad, eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "The whole world will know," and we will be watching.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up in a moment, Ed Sheeran, he makes music on the witness stand as he defends himself against the claim that he copied one of Marvin Gaye`s greatest hits, why he says he is "done" if he loses this case.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope he`s not done.\par \par \uc2 And only on CBS MORNINGS, Oprah will be here to reveal her newest Book Club pick. We`ll talk with her and the author in the studio today.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS where we are learning more about a hidden threat to our waterways and our health. So-called forever chemicals have been widely used for decades in product like cookware, cosmetics, and food packaging, and they have since leeched into our water and can be found in the blood of most Americans.\par \par \uc2 These chemicals have already been linked to a range of health problems including some forms of cancer. Roxana Saberi looks now at what`s being done in an effort to protect us.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: On a hilltop in Maine, Songbird Farm sits silently, a shell of what it was, and could have been.\par \par \uc2 ADAM NORDELL (Songbird Farm): Welcome to the ghost town of our dreams.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Adam Nordell and his wife, Johanna bought the 44 acres in 2014 to raise organic produce and a family.\par \par \uc2 Seven years later, they learned their land was riddled with toxic chemicals called PFAS.\par \par \uc2 Did you know what PFAS were?\par \par \uc2 ADAM NORDELL: No, no, no, no. I didn`t know what PFAS chemicals were.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: He soon found out.\par \par \uc2 They are a family of thousands of compounds that last so long in the environment, they are known as forever chemicals.\par \par \uc2 For decades, they`ve been widely used to put the nonstick in cookware and to make stain and waterproof fabrics, firefighting foam, food packaging, even cosmetics.\par \par \uc2 The toxins at Songbird Farm were traced to sludge, the solids left after wastewater is treated spread there as fertilizer in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 ADAM NORDELL: It contained a whole host of industrial chemicals that were washing out of consumer products in people`s homes or that were entering the wastewater treatment district from industrial facilities.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Adam says tests found their water and some crops had dangerously high levels, so did his family`s blood. He says it was 250 times higher than average.\par \par \uc2 ADAM NORDELL: Living with PFAS fast exposure is terrifying. I feel like I have a poorly wired time bomb inside of me.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: That`s because PFAS had been linked to health problems like low birth weight, liver damage, and some kinds of cancer.\par \par \uc2 While Maine is leading the way in identifying farmland contaminated with PFAS, a recent study estimates that sludge like what was applied here has also been spread on around five percent of all farmland in this country.\par \par \uc2 Well, not all sludge contains toxins, cattle that ate feed grown in tainted soil have been destroyed in at least three states. The chemicals also leech into lakes, rivers, and groundwater from factories and landfills.\par \par \uc2 DAN BROWN (Climatologist): They`ve really contaminated fish everywhere.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Climatologist, Dan Brown showed us a stretch of a river near Detroit, where the fish are too contaminated to eat.\par \par \uc2 DAN BROWN: I just can`t imagine that there is any place that`s going to be truly safe from PFAS at this point.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: A recent analysis of freshwater fish samples collected by the \uc2 EPA\uc2  found PFAS are an issue in almost every state. They are so pervasive, studies have shown, more than 95 percent of Americans now have detectable levels in their blood.\par \par \uc2 And now for the first time, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  is proposing strict regulations limiting PFAS in drinking water, more than 90 percent lower than previous recommended levels.\par \par \uc2 ELSIE SUNDERLAND (Environmental scientist): I was very pleased to see this.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Elsie Sunderland studies PFAS pollution at her \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  lab.\par \par \uc2 How would you characterize the Federal government`s approach toward PFAS?\par \par \uc2 ELSIE SUNDERLAND: I would call it ambitious, yet fragmented. You know if we`re really concerned about PFAS and our everyday exposure, we should be proactive and we should be banning these chemicals from non-essential uses.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: In an e-mail to \uc2 CBS\uc2  news, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  says it does products in the US` general food supply, which is among the safest in the world.\par \par \uc2 The agency says very few of those products have detectable levels of PFAS, and those that do are low.\par \par \uc2 ADAM NORDELL: In this greenhouse, we grow tomatoes in the summer and spinach and head lettuce in the winter.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Back in Maine, Adam has turned from working on his farm to pushing for new laws to help other farmers with dreams spoiled by forever chemicals.\par \par \uc2 ADAM NORDELL: If I can help protect other people from what happened to me, that motivates me to get up and go to work every day.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: For CBS MORNINGS, Roxana Saberi, Unity, Maine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Up next, all the best looks from fashions biggest night, the Met Gala, why some celebrities dressed up as cats and got real frisky.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Met Gala is one of fashion`s biggest nights. Here are some of the spectacular looks from last night. That`s Rihanna and A$AP from the event last night at the \uc2 Metropolitan Museum of Art\uc2  right here in New York City. There is Idris Elba and his lovely wife, Sabrina. It is also a fundraiser, Pedro Pascal -- with a different theme every year. Oh look at Salma Hayek and Jared Leto. I`m trying to read and look at the pictures at the same time -- Lil Nas X.\par \par \uc2 For 2023, the organizers chose to honor designer who died four years ago, a fashion industry legend.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On the first Monday of May, New York`s East Side becomes a center of high fashion and for this year`s Met, Gala guests were instructed to dress in honor of Karl -- Karl Lagerfeld, that is.\par \par \uc2 Some guests paid homage to his signature style with looks from the fashion houses where he spent decades working, while others walked that not so red carpet dressed in bedazzled costumes or t-shirts and bucket hats.\par \par \uc2 "Vogues" editor-in-chief and Lagerfeld`s close friend, Anna Wintour came in a simple tribute, a vintage Chanel. She has been running the Met Gala since 1995.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR\uc2  ("Vogue" editor-in-chief): There`s a lot of media attention around the evening and we are very, very grateful to that because it also puts a spotlight on the exhibition, which is also the primary -- another primary reason that we do the evening is we want to bring visitors into the museum.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before the fundraiser, which annually brings in millions. We sat down with Wintour and the curator in charge of the Met Museum`s C