 GAYLE KING: But Ed is saying he didn`t --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "My Sweet Lord."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Ed is saying that he didn`t even really hear that song ahead of time before he wrote it and he also said this though --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nate, Nate. Leave Ed Sheeran alone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He hadn`t even heard that song. Everybody has heard that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nobody hasn`t heard that, but he was saying, I haven`t -- whatever age he was, but he says if he loses he`s going to give up music.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because he is so upset by this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will see.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is so insulting to him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, I want to do something I`ve always wanted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope he doesn`t lose.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Guess who`s in the house? Oprah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oprah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oprah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oprah.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In the greenroom to reveal her latest Book Club selection. What`s up, Oprah? How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oprah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, you`ve always wanted to do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve always wanted to do that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dream come true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in his eight o`clock hour, Oprah is here only on CBS MORNINGS, we love when that happens, along with the author of her latest Book Club pick. It`s big and it is a good one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Millions of people know Peloton instructor, Emma Lovewell. Well, well, she`s here to tell us about her new book that highlights progress, not perfection.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 We will also be announcing the Tony nominations first on CBS MORNINGS with some help from some of Broadway`s biggest young stars, Lea Michele and Myles Frost.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know a thing or two about Broadway, but first here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELONI: These writers are going in guns blazing saying we`ve been left behind.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Breaking overnight, the Writers Guild of America is on strike, how your favorite shows and movies could be affected.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Shows and production will come to a halt.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my God. Oh my God. Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A dust storm causes a series of deadly crashes in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Nothing in my 24-year career that I can recall having something as severe as this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A man accused of killing five of his neighbors in Texas is still on the run.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): We urge the public to heed all guidance from law enforcement officials and stay safe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Rappers Lil Nas X and Doja Cat are inspired by Karl Lagerfeld`s famous feline at the Met Gala and get very, very into character.\par \par \uc2 (DOJA CAT meowing.)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: So what are you excited to see in there today?\par \par \uc2 (DOJA CAT meowing.)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Is it your first Met?\par \par \uc2 (DOJA CAT meowing.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s just say, it just works on the carpet at the Met Gala. You can show up as a cat and nobody blinks an eye.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I thought that was a result of the writers` strike.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. That, too.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with Oprah she is here. She is revealing her newest Book Club selection, only on CBS MORNINGS and we love when that happens.\par \par \uc2 Oprah, drumroll, please. What did you select?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (Book Club): Drum roll. It is the long awaited "The Covenant of Water" by Abraham Verghese and it is one of the most --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is big.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: It is 715 pages.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: But I felt transported, I felt like I lived in a different land for a while. I felt expanded. I came away humbled that someone could have a mastery of words the way Dr. Verghese does, and I am just -- I think this is an offering to the world.\par \par \uc2 I just -- I know that it`s a big thick book and you were complaining about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. No. And this is what would happen. Well, I`m going to wait till we introduce the author of "The Covenant of Water."\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: "The Covenant of Water."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a great story of a South Indian family suffering from a very mysterious curse. During the search for answers, family secrets are revealed that bond them together, but also threaten to tear them apart.\par \par \uc2 Abraham Verghese, last "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " best-selling "Cutting for Stone," sold more than one-and-a-half million copies in this country alone, so we are very excited to have you here, Abraham Verghese and we welcome you.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE ("The Covenant of Water" author): It`s such a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, when I heard that that book was 700 pages, I said to Oprah, people have lives. This is not fair to do to people.\par \par \uc2 People have jobs. They have children, they have work. And so when I was reading it, I would call her and I said, it is so long. She goes, is it good? I go, it`s beyond good. She said, well, keep reading and hung up the phone.\par \par \uc2 She is not hearing it. She said -- and she said, I make no apologies for the length. So my challenge here today is not to give anything away because it`s pitch perfect. It takes twists and turns that you never ever expect.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It grabs you right from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right from the beginning, all the way to the very last page.\par \par \uc2 So what was your process? How did you come up with his story?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: Well, I wish I could say that I knew the whole story from the beginning, but I had a general idea, three generations. I had the figure of a Big Ammachi, a strong woman. I think a lot of women in fiction are portrayed as evil or you know, malevolent. But I just wanted a good mother and have her sort of carry the story.\par \par \uc2 I knew very little else. I mapped it all out, but it kept changing every time --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As you were writing. So, were you changing as you went along?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: I was. I think, there is a saying in fiction that character is determined by decisions taken under pressure, and the moment I put my character under pressure, they would tell me, they were going a different way and so I had to take off and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have to say, guys, I`m under strict orders from Oprah do not reveal any of the plots, do not really discuss the story --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Because here`s the thing --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just keep it -- but can I share this?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Okay, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On Page 24, can I share this? This gives nothing away, that rubbing ginger on a flaccid penis works. Is that a good tease, Oprah?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: It does not work.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Okay, well, that`s a very good tease, but listen, this is what I loved about the book. I thought this expressed on Page 15, the very beginning of the story. The grandmother is certain of a few things. A tale that leaves its imprint on a listener, tells the truth about how the world lives.\par \par \uc2 And so unavoidably it is about families, their victories and wounds and their departed, including the ghosts who linger. It must offer instructions for living in God`s realm where joy never spares one from sorrow.\par \par \uc2 That`s all there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That sums it up beautifully, too.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: That is a sentence.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Plus, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is sweet. That is grandeur.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Verghese, you are a doctor and a writer. How do these two professions, these two callings interact?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: You know, I always resist the temptation -- the definition of two hats because I see myself as just all physician, and even the writing comes out of the great privilege of physicianhood and being attentive to details and trying to gather them together in one unifying package.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I realize that`s a bit disingenuous now I have a best-selling book that I`ve written before this and I`m on Oprah, so I must be a writer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: But my identity is so wrapped up in the great privilege of being a physician.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, it was touching to me is your eyes teared up. You and Oprah met for the first time today. Your eyes teared up when you were speaking to her.\par \par \uc2 What was it like when she called you? I`m wondering where the emotion came from for you about her?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: Well, I gave her some grief, I think because I didn`t believe this. This was two days before April 1st. So I didn`t really believe this was Oprah, but once I knew, I stood up, that was my first reaction.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You stood up.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: I stood up. I`m in awe of this lady who, you know, have brought so many readers to books in general, and that`s what writers want.\par \par \uc2 I shared with her one of my favorite quotes that the difference between people who don`t read and who can`t read is no difference. And she brought readers -- this is Mark Twain`s quote, not mine, she brought readers these books and I just love this lady for that.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : He teared up and I teared up. We both were crying in the call. That`s never happened.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ever.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Because I just feel like what it takes to have a day job, and then really, you`re doing this in your spare time writing, but to have the gift of being able to use words, to put people in a different place in their mind and in their spirit is an incredible gift.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Seeing this connection in this conversation between the two is beautiful, and this isn`t going to stop here. There`s some exciting news about a podcast.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Exciting news because --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tell us all about it.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: This is the first time I`ve taken a fiction book and turned it into a series of podcasts, because the story is epic. It is broad in scope and I know that already, Dr. Verghese has so many fans out there from "Cutting for Stone," which I hadn`t read until I read this book, then I went and read "Cutting for Stone."\par \par \uc2 So, so many fans who wants to delve in a more intimate way with the story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And so we`re doing it a series of podcasts.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A series of podcasts.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : On the book, yes, on the book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not only that Dr. Verghese, Abraham, this is the first time I`ve ever seen Oprah pick a book from an author and then go back and read all of their other books. I`ve never seen you do that before.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: It has never happened before.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: I mean, her enthusiasm when she called me for the book, she was just going on about it. Other than my editor and my publisher and my partner, no one has ever read the book in that -- and being that engaged. I was just blown away.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I got to my knees when we were done with the call.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, Dr. Verghese, you mentioned earlier that you mapped it out and you mean that literally. You mapped it out on a whiteboard three generations of families. Can you explain your writing process for a while.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that your whiteboard?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. We have a picture. Look at that.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: I tend to think visually and so --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Wow.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: I really wanted to have a blueprint before I launched into this, but the trouble is, as I said the blueprint kept changing, and so there are many, many iterations of my whiteboard.\par \par \uc2 So it kept catching up with the novel. I wish I could say I knew it all, but that was my attempt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you know how this was going to end when you started it?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wondered about that because the ending I think is pitch perfect. Did you, in the back of your mind think that?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: It occurred to me towards the end and I`ll tell you something else, I had the privilege of recording the audio book and as I was reading it for two-and-a-half weeks, I was seeing connections I swear to God I never planned to put in there, so I think the muse, God, the subconscious mind does mysterious things and you just have to be open to it and willing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I like what you say about secrets. You say, every family has secrets, but not all secrets are meant to deceive. What defines a family is not blood, but the secrets they share.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that resonates with many, many, many people.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I want to just say, I`ve been reading since I was three years old and I have over the years -- you know, what is prized in my house are my --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Books.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : My first edition novels, and I have a whole room full of them, and I would say this goes on my list of top three books of all times and that includes Miss Toni Morrison.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : All right, so I have never felt this way about a book. I`m trying to get the rights to the book. I want to -- I said to Abraham Verghese, I don`t know why they are calling you "Doctor" or Abraham.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: Abraham.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : I said to Abraham, that, you know, I believe that this should be a series, a multi-part series. I have never, you know, attempted in all of my years of selecting books to do a series after it shows up, but I said, I want to be the steward of this. However, this comes to light on the screen and whatever screen it is, I feel that I should be the steward for this to make sure that this story gets what it deserves in the world.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You feel that when you start reading it?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes, and it is one of the top three books I have ever read.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Abraham, this is what was waiting for you after more than 10 years of work with that whiteboard and having the story pass through you after that age.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How does it feel hearing that, Abraham?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM VERGHESE: It is just an awesome feeling. I mean, you know, I think you write in such solitude, but you`re always visualizing a listener, and I never in my wildest dreams imagined Miss Winfrey would be the listener and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Something tells me, Oprah knows people, so this is going to happen. I think in her mind, she is already casting for the book.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The movie. Aren`t you? You`ve got somebody --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I`ve got some people in mind. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oprah And Abraham, thank you, thank you. It`s a pleasure. I was very excited about today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pleasure to meet you.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: You know what we are doing? So normally, I would choose another book around July. We`re giving people time to read this book because, listen, this is going to take you on an adventure. It`s going to take you into another way of being with yourself, and so, I won`t be back here for a while.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So people get two months to read it.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes. Two months to read.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that`s very important because, Oprah, people got lives. They`ve got jobs.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: People have got lives.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They`ve got stuff to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They`ve got things they need to do.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: "The Covenant of Water."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo. Bravo. Thank you both. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 "The Covenant of Water" is on sale right now. Check out CBSMornings.com for a reader`s guide and a free excerpt from the novel.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead, head on over to our \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page to find out how you can get more involved with Oprah`s Book Club. All right, I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just ahead, "Talk of the Table" Don`t adjust your TV set. We`re coming at you a little bit earlier than usual.\par \par \uc2 It is 8:12. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up first on CBS MORNINGS, we are announcing Tony nominations in some of the biggest categories. Broadway star, Lea Michele; and Tony winner, Myles Frost will join us in studio for the announcement.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for a very special early edition of "Talk of the Table" I repeat early edition. You are not late for work. You are not late for school. Your morning is not off. Our morning is off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that does throw people because they set their clocks to this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It does throw people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we are here early. Glad to do so.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is quite the weekend in Barcelona, Spain. If we hadn`t been in DC for the White House Correspondents` Dinner, I would have wanted to be in Barcelona.\par \par \uc2 Bruce Springsteen and the E Street Band gave a concert on Friday night and they had a couple of very special backup singers join them on stage. Here is a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bruce Springsteen and E Street Band performance.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That of course is \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  rocking out to "Glory Days." She`s not the only celebrity. That`s Kate Capshaw, Mrs. Steven Spielberg on the other side with the short blonde hair, also rocking out.\par \par \uc2 I love that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @PointBlankSpain/\uc2 Twitter\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you had the Springsteens and the Spielbergs on Saturday night, but Sunday night, look who is in Barcelona -- Tom Hanks and his wife, Rita Wilson in the audience, also rocking out singing and dancing along taking selfies. This was two days before these two celebrated their 35th wedding anniversary.\par \par \uc2 Then on Saturday night, Springsteen joined former President Barack Obama and Steven Spielberg at Barcelona`s restaurant. Next time, you`re in Barcelona, this is where you want to go, Amar, the Amar restaurant where they took this picture with the staff there. I`m thinking that was a fun weekend in Barcelona.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Man.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Doesn`t that look good?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I thought DC was the place to be.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. This is Barcelona.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re turn, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, my "Talk of the Table" is proof that you`re never too old to start a new adventure.\par \par \uc2 Harold Hooper is 91 and he became -- recently became a new owner of a bait and tackle shop in Muskogee, Oklahoma. His family says he`s always full of energy, and just always doing something, but they didn`t see this coming.\par \par \uc2 Hooper loves to fish, but he says the real reason he bought the shop is to help his family.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HAROLD HOOPER (91 years old): I wanted to make sure that she had a way through school and didn`t have a dad hanging over half her life.\par \par \uc2 My granddaughter, it took her about 12 years to pay for her student debt and I didn`t want that for my great granddaughter.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that? First of all, it`s a blessing that he is there for his great granddaughter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But we did a little something called "Never Too Late," so that`s just proof right there. If you`ve got something on your mind, go out there and do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go out and do it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I agree. It`s a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so for my "Talk of the Table" loyal viewers will recall that not too long ago, I ran a video from western North Carolina of David Oppenheimer having a run in with the bear. He`s in his carport here.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from David Oppenheimer/Storyful.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I remember this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is relaxed and after work and the bear is looking around, oh, what are you doing here? They look at each other, moment of surprise, confusion.\par \par \uc2 Well, continuing our coverage of surprise encounters between humans and bears. I have a new video. This next one, there is no sound on. It is a security video.\par \par \uc2 That is a principal -- that is a principal in Central West Virginia who is actually trying to lock the dumpster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, look at the lady that opens the door.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then she goes running back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like that the bear runs the other direction.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I bet you, he hasn`t run that fast in years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was some high knees right there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you imagine the kids looking, hey, there is Principal Burleson. But nobody was hurt. That is good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nobody was hurt. The bear is okay. The principal is okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my goodness. All right, so coming up. We will talk with popular Peloton instructor, Emma Lovewell about her new book and her mantra, "Progress Not Perfection."\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead, Broadway stars Myles Frost and Lea Michele are in our progressive greenroom to reveal nominees, some of the biggest categories of this year`s what guys -- what Awards?\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE (Actor): Tony Awards.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Tony Awards.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST (Actor): Tony Awards.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, we are so excited to announce nominees in six major categories for what? The 76th Annual Tony Awards.\par \par \uc2 And not only that, we`re joined by two of Broadway`s hottest stars. Hot star number one, Myles Frost; hot star number two in no particular order, Lea Michele.\par \par \uc2 Myles won a Tony last year for his performance as Michael Jackson in "MJ: The Musical." Congratulations to you as well. Lea is currently in the middle of a critically acclaimed run playing her dream role, Fanny Brice in "Funny Girl" on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 Lea and Myles, welcome.\par \par \uc2 Lea, I just have to say you killed on the red carpet last night.\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You killed it at the time when you sang, "Don`t Rain on My Parade." You were fantastic.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: That was beautiful.\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Myles, start us off because you remember what it`s like. You wake up the morning, the nominations are announced. What`s going through their minds both of you. Myles, start us off. What are they thinking?\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Oh my gosh. First of all, am I up there?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: I`ve got to call my mom if I am.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: You know, my mom had put together this whole Zoom call with all of my family, because I couldn`t be with them physically and we all waited in anticipation and when my name came up on that screen, we just -- we screamed. We all screamed and cried.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: It was a beautiful moment, you know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Lea, you said at the Met Gala last night, there were Broadway people and they were saying, please call my name tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: Yes. I know so many people from the Broadway community were at the Met Ball last night and I knew I was looking, I was like, okay, I have a feeling I`m going to be saying some of your names tomorrow morning. So I`m just so excited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Let`s see if Lea`s feelings are going to come true.\par \par \uc2 Myles, you start us off. You`ve got the nominees for Best Performance by an Actor in a Leading Role in a play are --\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Yes, yes. The nominees for Best Performance by an Actor in a Leading Role in a Play Are --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Topdog/Underdog.")\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Yahya Abdul-Mateen II, Suzan-Lori Parks` "Topdog/Underdog."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Topdog/Underdog.")\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Corey Hawkins, Suzan-Lori Parks` "Topdog/Underdog."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Good Night, Oscar.")\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Sean Hayes, "Good Night, Oscar."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Between Riverside and Crazy.")\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Stephen McKinley Henderson, "Between Riverside and Crazy."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Death of a Salesman.")\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: And Wendell Pierce, Arthur Miller`s "Death of a Salesman."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good category. Okay.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very cool. All right, Lea, up to you now. What have you got for us?\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: The nominees for Best Performance by an Actress in a Leading Role in a Play are --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "A Doll`s House.")\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: Jessica Chasteen, "A Doll`s House."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Prima Facie.")\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: Jodie Comer, "Prima Facie."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Summer, 1976.")\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: Jessica Hecht, "Summer, 1976.")\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ohio State Murders.")\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: And Audra Mc