YLES FROST: Most definitely.\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: Having a little champagne and just --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Or a lot, I mean, like a lot. I did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Broadway knows how to have fun. Broadway has some good --\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: Well, some people have to work -- most of them have to work. It`s Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Right.\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: In shows, I mean.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: I let that be Tuesday night`s problem.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like the way you think.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Responsibly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you both for coming. It was so nice to have.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: . stars of your stature and to get up early. We know hair and makeup, all of that stuff can be a pain. We get it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So thank you so much. This is a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 LEA MICHELE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: No problem. Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We really appreciate it. Lovely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love all things Broadway and lots of nominees to cheer on this year, as always.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: There is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Myles Frost and Lea Michele, we thank you again.\par \par \uc2 You can watch the 76th Annual Tony Awards on Sunday, June 11th at eight o`clock Eastern Time right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 Myles and Lea will live stream the rest of the nominations from the Sofitel. That`s a hotel, starting at nine o`clock on the official Tony Awards \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 You guys got to go.\par \par \uc2 MYLES FROST: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Peloton instructor, Emma Lovewell teaches fitness classes to millions of people and she is in our progressive greenroom right now to share her inspiring new book, "Live Learn Love Well." But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL (Live Learn Love Well" author): You will have doubts, questions, judgments, acknowledge them, but don`t let them in. It never really gets easier. You just learn how to handle it and how to believe in yourself.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is Peloton instructor Emma Lovewell.\par \par \uc2 Emma teaches cycling, Pilates, dance cardio, and core strengthening -- that`s just to name a few -- to millions who subscribe to her popular fitness programs.\par \par \uc2 Now, she is out with a new book called, "Live Learn Love Well: Lessons From A Life Of Progress Not Perfection," part memoir and part advice. It`s filled with personal stories and life lessons.\par \par \uc2 Emma Lovewell joins us first on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 How are you doing this morning?\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: I am great. Thank you, guys, so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just rode you yesterday.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your R&B ride is really great.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: I love --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Starting with "Vision of Love," yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are fearless.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are working, but you say writing this book scared you.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How so?\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: I`m totally stepping out of my comfort zone. I think I`ve always wanted to write a book but I didn`t know when it would happen. And when the opportunity presented itself, I took it. I was like, you know do it while afraid. I`m just going to do it.\par \par \uc2 And here we are. It`s been an amazing experience. Two years of working on this and I`m so happy to announce that it`s out today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so it`s been amazing but it`s also been healing for you.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Yes, I think it was kind of like going to therapy. It`s really like going back and looking at all my life stories, opening up old journals and diaries from when I was a kid, calling my mom and dad, my brother and being like, do you remember this story? And he`s like, that`s not how it happened. It happened like this. You know, kind of rehashing all of this family trauma and also, you know, some beautiful moments and getting to relive them and then write them down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Family trauma and a little bit of drama, Emma, because you write in the book, it was very personal for you.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: It is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your mom is Chinese, your dad is White, as you point out. You grew up mixed race, and just how that affected your life, how you fit in some communities and then not in others. Could you talk about that a little bit?\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Yes. I think that I grew up in a community that was mostly White and so I often, you know, looked at my mom and thought, why are you different? Why are we so different? Why is our household so different?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why are we eating that kind of food?\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Why are we eating this kind of food, the other kids don`t do this, and I didn`t understand her until I got older. And now as an adult, and I`ve traveled so much, I really now see how much my mom has gone through.\par \par \uc2 She created a home in another country, learned a new language and I look at my mom now and I think she`s an absolute rockstar.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. I totally get that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We should explain that this philosophy of progress, not perfection, which I think we all agree is a great motto.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I like that, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tell me about it.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Yes. I`m definitely a perfectionist, I have been. I go through, you know, times -- seasons of my life where I`ve been really focused on being the best, being the best.\par \par \uc2 And even with writing this book, it was -- is that journey too, where I`m like, no, I need to make more edits. I can make more -- I can make it better, I can make it better.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I really think just the -- there is no such thing as perfection, right? So if we get rid of that idea and just enjoy the process, like we`re showing up for ourselves every single day, that`s what we want. That`s what we want to see for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You talk about your voice is your power, and there was a time when you didn`t understand that, but you wouldn`t even be at Peloton if you hadn`t used your voice, because you reached out to Peloton directly and said, hey --\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m very interested.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Yes. I asked --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To \uc2 John Foley\uc2  at the time.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: I sent an e-mail to \uc2 John Foley\uc2 , I had met him before. My amazing partner, Dave, encouraged me. He`s like, just e-mail the CEO, and I was like, who do you think I am, honestly?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And who e-mails a CEO?\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Yes, who e-mails a CEO, right? But I took his advice, which was great advice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good advice, Dave. Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Totally. I slammed my laptop and like, did a lap around the apartment because I was like, ah, you know, it`s off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Twenty minutes later, he responded. He said, I`d love to chat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Twenty minutes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Twenty minutes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: He said, let`s get you on a call and let`s get you in for an audition.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there you are. You put a lot of time and effort into your playlists.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your playlists, I think are some of the most fun things that you do.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tell us about your process because you`re a great lip-syncer. So much so that you give advice on how to do a good lip sync.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Yes, I grew up always entering the lip-sync contest, which was you know, part of the reason why I think I love performing and so yes, in my classes. I pick music that inspires me and hope that it will inspire you, too.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Peloton Interactive\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: And I feel like if you see me being happy and having joy and enjoying the process, you`re going to be happy, too. You`re going to experience my joy. And so if I`m motivated, I hope that you are motivated, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Every week you have a standing date with yourself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that, too, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Explain exactly what that is and what you have learned from that.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Yes, so at Peloton, I would teach an evening class and instead of getting on the road and heading back home, I would decide after class I`d take myself out to dinner.\par \par \uc2 So I have an ongoing list of all these restaurants I want to try in the city.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Man, I love that.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: And I go and I sit down at the bar, table for one. It`s usually some like noodles. I love ramen noodles or some Asian dish.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: And I just -- yes, at first it was a little awkward, but I was like this is for me. I`m dating myself. I love myself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you a good date? Are you a good date, Emma Lovewell?\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: I had to put my phone away. That was the hardest thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is --\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: It is hard to just -- you want to sit there and just scroll, but.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did that once. I`m very uncomfortable going out by myself because I fear people are looking at you. She doesn`t have any friends dah- dah-dah.\par \par \uc2 One time sitting at a restaurant by myself, I`m reading my book and the waiter says, excuse me, ma`am your book is upside down.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a true -- that didn`t go so well when I tried to date myself.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Gayle, I was going to say, other people could go out with that book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And just be by themselves.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Of self-care.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And how about the title, Tony? "Live Learn Love Well," and the author is --\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Emma Lovewell.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Thank you, guys, so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, thank you. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats, Emma.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LOVEWELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Live Learn Love Well" is out today wherever you like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, we`ll show you a product designed to help you impress your friends on the links, KlockItGolf is a training tool that helps golfers practice at home.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to our exclusive discount, you can get it for 30 percent off the retail price. To start shopping now, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It says practice at home, but they were all outside.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was a pretty setting. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We all need practice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maybe they have some big lawns.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Maybe they do. You`re right. That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230502ej5200105\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Writers Strike Appears To Be Written In The Stars, As A Midnight Deadline Fast Approaches For The WGA To Reach A New Deal With Hollywood Studios\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Kevin Frazier \par \uc2 665 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A writers strike appears to be written in the stars, as a midnight deadline fast approaches for the WGA to reach a new deal with Hollywood studios.\par \par \uc2 Entertainment Tonight co-host Kevin Frazier reports, more than 11,000 writers of your favorite films and TV shows could walk off the job and onto the picket line as soon as tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER (voiceover): It is a true Hollywood cliffhanger.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Action!\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: And the ending is still being written.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Late Night with Seth Meyers/NBC, April 28)\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS (Host, Late Night With Seth Meyers): Incredibly grateful that there are negotiators for both sides, incredibly hopeful that they can come to an agreement.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: The first impact would be late-night TV.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Tonight Show)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Jimmy Kimmel Live)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: All these shows could be off by tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Saturday Night Live/NBC)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Plus the remaining episodes of Saturday Night Live.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Saturday Night Live/NBC)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: This week`s scheduled host is Pete Davidson.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon/NBC, April 28)\par \par \uc2 PETE DAVIDSON (Actor): As you know, the writers strike might happen.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON (Host, The Tonight Show): Oh, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 PETE DAVIDSON: So, I have been working on this for, like, two, three months. And they`re like: "Yes, well, we will know Monday."\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: More than 11,000 writers are set to walk the picket line if no deal is reached with Hollywood producers.\par \par \uc2 The main issue is streaming; 78 percent of households now subscribe to at least one streaming service. But the writers say those series pay less and have fewer episodes; 10 years ago, the WGA says a third of its TV writers worked at the minimum salary. It`s now half.\par \par \uc2 But studio producers say their budgets are already stretched and they can`t make a profit by paying more when revenues are falling. Also playing a role in the labor talks, A.I. The writers want assurances that studios won`t use artificial intelligence like ChatGPT to generate new scripts.\par \par \uc2 The last writers strike in 2007 lasted 100 days and cost California more than $2 billion. Back then, Stephen Colbert was on \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Colbert Report, \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 , 2007)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT (Host, The Late Show With Stephen Colbert): Where are my words?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: We have no script, Stephen.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Why not?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: The writers are on strike.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Now, a long strike could push back the fall TV season.\par \par \uc2 And even if there is an agreement, consider that, next month, contracts expire for the unions representing both Hollywood`s directors and actors -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Kevin Frazier, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration estimates that more than 20,000 Russian troops, many of them convicts thrown into combat, have been killed in the battle for Bakhmut over the last five months. This bodycam video from the Ukrainian side shows the intense fighting in a battle that`s become known as the Meat Grinder.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. estimates another 80,000 Russians have been wounded. The fighting rages on. This video shows a Russian missile strike in Eastern Ukraine today that killed two people and wounded 40 others.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230502ej51000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Video Shows Fire Coming From The Engine Of An American Airlines Plane\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 477 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Turning now to a scary incident in the sky, an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  flight was forced to return to an Ohio airport after its engine caught fire following a bird strike on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian spoke with one of the frightened passengers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): On a plane, it doesn`t get much more frightening than this, flames shooting from the engine and an ominous warning.\par \par \uc2 OVERHEAD ANNOUNCEMENT: This is a good time to review that safety briefing card in the seat-back pocket in front of you.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: This was American 19-58 from Columbus to Phoenix early Sunday, a bird strike shortly after takeoff sparking a fire, forcing pilots to make an emergency landing on one engine.\par \par \uc2 PILOT: Mayday, mayday, mayday, American 19-58. We had a bird strike and an engine failure.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: When a bird strike impacts a plane and takes out an engine, how does it impact the flying?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT\uc2 : Well, the airplanes are designed to be flown on one engine. So that`s the safety redundancy that is built in.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Pilots reporting more than 17,000 wildlife strikes in the U.S. last year, a fraction of the more than 36 million flights monitored by the \uc2 FAA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Marni Kallestad was on Sunday`s flight.\par \par \uc2 MARNI KALLESTAD (American Airlines Passenger): I was extremely concerned when I saw the flames. They were shooting out pretty large at a certain point. And I started to think that we might not make this.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That engine is on fire.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: More frightening moments aboard another \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  flight last Thursday in North Carolina, an engine fire shortly before takeoff from the Charlotte Airport. The plane returned to the gate.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For its part, \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  attributed that fire in North Carolina to a mechanical incident and says no one was hurt in either incident -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Janet Shamlian, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, officials in Philadelphia are investigating the cause of a four- alarm fire at a warehouse. One person was injured. Witnesses say a series of explosions rock the neighborhood. At least 140 firefighters raced to the scene, as thick black smoke could be seen for miles. The company that owns the facility describes it as an appliance and scrap metal recycling center.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230426ej4o0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Bus Drivers, Custodians, And Special Needs Assistants Go On Strike At The Nation`s Second Largest School System\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Carter Evans \par \uc2 744 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 March 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: More than 400,000 students are out of the classroom in Los Angeles and will be for the next two days. School employees are on strike at the second largest school district in the country.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Carter Evans tells us tonight what they are asking for.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): Walking the picket lines in the pouring rain, L.A. school support staff are flanked by teachers in solidarity.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are supporting our sister union. It`s a crisis.\par \par \uc2 REBEKAH ANDERSON (Special Education Assistant): Without bus drivers, without cafeteria workers, without custodians, the classroom doesn`t run.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Rebekah Anderson (sp?) is a special education assistant. Her union says low wages are pushing workers into poverty. They are demanding a 30 percent raise.\par \par \uc2 REBEKAH ANDERSON: Because we don`t get a raise every year.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: After 22 years, she now makes $25 an hour.\par \par \uc2 REBEKAH ANDERSON: Inflation, everything has gone up, rent, gas, food. Everything is just terrible right now.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Most of the union`s members work fewer than six hours a day, bringing the average pay to nearly $32,000 a year. The average price for a one-bedroom apartment in the city of L.A. tops that at $2,800 a month.\par \par \uc2 Emmanuel Morales (sp?) is a custodian making $18 an hour, just above minimum wage here. He still lives with his parents.\par \par \uc2 Could you afford your own apartment?\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MORALES (Custodian): No, not -- if I could -- if I wanted to, I couldn`t, not with the type of money.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The district is offering a 23 percent raise with a 3 percent bonus, and negotiations are still stalled.\par \par \uc2 ALBERTO CARVALHO (Superintendent, \uc2 Los Angeles Unified School District\uc2 ): I believe this strike could have been avoided, but it cannot be avoided without parties at the table.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: No matter the outcome, nearly half-a-million children will lose three days of schools, increasing the burden on parents.\par \par \uc2 MAYRA PEREZ (Parent): She is autistic. With her not being in school three days, she could have a delay back with her program that she is currently in.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, 80 percent of the students in this district are considered low-income, and they depend on schools like this to provide daily meals.\par \par \uc2 Well, workers prepared three days worth of food for them, and, today, they are handing them out in parking lots across the city -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Still thinking of those kids.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, California is being slammed by the 12th atmospheric river of the season. Heavy rain, strong winds and flooding are already causing problems across Southern California.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hey there, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Norah, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 It is nonstop, persistent rain, snow and wind across California from Northern California to Southern California, big low pressure system rolling through the San Francisco Bay Area. But showers go all the way down to San Diego, where we have had severe thunderstorm warnings today, and then a lot of mountain snow that goes from California all the way to the \uc2 Colorado Rockies\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here`s the footprint of all that. In many areas, around an inch of rain is still to go, but also some high winds, which now brings us a concern for some power outages, as trees may come down. And then, in the highest elevations, two to three more feet of snow, and, eventually, that comes into the Intermountain West as well.\par \par \uc2 Then, as the energy rolls into the South, three potential days of severe weather, tomorrow, mainly Missouri and Illinois, and then it`s Oklahoma and Texas going into Thursday. And then, by Friday, Norah, it`s across the Lower Mississippi River Valley.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230322ej3l000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Security Concerns over Possible Trump Indictment; Members of Oath Keepers Convicted; Video of In-Custody Death Released; Student Charged with Capital Murder; Los Angeles School Workers Go on Strike; Powerful Cross- Country Storms; \uc2 First Republic Bank\uc2  Shares Plummet 47 Percent; Putin and Xi Praise No Limit Friendship; Man Speaks Out After Wrongful Conviction; Meteorologist on Lesson from Live Health Scare; "Ted Lasso`s" Rising Star; The Reality of Egg Freezing; "The Night Agent;" \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Robert Costa, Carter  Evans, Stephanie Abrams, Jill Schlesinger, Jeff Pegues, Elizabeth Palmer,  Lilia Luciano, Anthony Mason, Jon LaPook, Alissa Carlton, Nikki Battiste \par \uc2 14,522 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 March 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday, my favorite day of the week. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today, but we`re still here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Police are on alert over Donald Trump`s possible indictment, while \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  seizes the moment to take a swipe at the former President.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Paying hush money to a porn star to secure silence over some type of alleged affair, I just -- I can`t speak to that.\par \par \uc2 MAN: "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " has published a surveillance video showing the final moments of Irvo Otieno who died in police custody.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another major storm is bearing down on California threatening the State with more flooding and mudslides.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSON (D-CA): Twelfth atmospheric room. We`ve gone from extreme to just weather whiplash.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: China`s President continues talks today with his "Dear Friend," \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN (US Secretary of State): It suggests that China feels no responsibility to hold the Kremlin accountable for the atrocities committed in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Nine thousand \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees will be out of a job within the next few weeks. This is now the second largest round of layoffs in the company`s history.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The cast of super-hit "Ted Lasso" visited the White House. They are teaming up with President Biden to raise awareness about mental health.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: . stepped through.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In the women`s basketball tourney, top seed Indiana gets upset by Miami 70 to 68.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Destiny Harden fulfills her destiny with the game-winning shot.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made the shocking announcement that he expects to be arrested on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL\uc2  ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): Some Trump supporters online have been talking about creating what they are calling a patriot moat to surround him and prevent the police from taking him.\par \par \uc2 AL FRANKEN (Comedian and former US Senator): Nurse, you have to find someone else to finish the brain surgery. The President has summoned me to Mar-a-Lago to be a human moat for him.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Al Franken on "The Late Show."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He has got the deadpan delivery again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to late night, although apparently --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to late night.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The reporters outnumber the protesters so the Patriot moat is yet to come into force.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, let`s just hope it`s a quiet day in the country, please.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. We are very excited to tell you we`ve got a big show for you today including a new installment of our series, we call it Facing Fertility. Nikki Battiste speaks to a woman who decided to freeze her eggs at the age of 33. It is something -- have you heard -- that more and more women are choosing to do and Nikki takes us inside the very emotional journey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, we`ve got Anthony Mason`s conversation with Toheeb Jimoh, who you may know for his Emmy-nominated performance in "Ted Lasso." They went back to Jimoh`s old school in London.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH ("Ted Lasso" actor): It felt bigger when I was here, but I guess, I was probably just little, you know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. This was your stage.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: This was my stage. I did all of my rehearsals here, I did all of my performances here. Like I lived in this room.\par \par \uc2 This was like the birthplace of like me wanting to be a performer. Me wanting to be an actor. This was my safe haven.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If you`re a "Ted Lasso" fan, you know, Jimoh is one of the best parts of the show and you`re going to love him even more after you to see this piece.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love him already.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this show so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I do love "Ted Lasso." I wish I could live within that show sometimes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I do, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because back in this world, we are talking about what everyone has been talking about for days now and it is law enforcement on alert nationwide after former President Trump called for his supporters to protest today. Trump`s language, of course, echoes his comments in the run up to the January 6 riots.\par \par \uc2 And as a result, extra barricades are now in position around the Criminal Court in Lower Manhattan where Trump could be indicted in connection to a hush money payment to an adult film star. Security has also been increased at the US Capitol with new barricades and extra patrols.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa is outside the Court in New York here.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s Republican allies are now going after the District Attorney leading this investigation, all as the protection around this Court is on display.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Steel barricades were brought into Lower Manhattan after former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  predicted his arrest and called for protests.\par \par \uc2 Inside the Courthouse, the grand jury continued its work on Monday, hearing from the potential final witness.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTELLO (Attorney): He is totally unreliable.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Attorney Bob Costello, a longtime Trump ally was brought in to testify about Cohen`s credibility. Cohen was Trump`s former fixer and a key witness in this investigation.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTELLO: If they want to go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and they have solid evidence, so be it. But \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  is far from solid evidence.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It was Cohen who made the alleged hush money payments to Stormy Daniels in the final days of the 2016 campaign. He later pleaded guilty to felony charges related to that payout.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN (Former US Attorney): This has many similarities to a mob case for the main witness to have problems, blemishes, even convictions, and it will fall to the prosecution to say, sure, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has some issues, but here is why you can believe him.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Many House Republicans are defending the former President and are calling on New York District Attorney Alvin Bragg to submit testimony on what they call a politically motivated prosecutorial decision.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): Some bookkeeping error from seven years ago is misdemeanor is now what they are going after, so it makes absolutely no sense.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a potential 2024 rival to Trump criticized the DA, but also had this to say.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): I don`t know what goes into paying hush money to a porn star to secure silence over some type of alleged affair. I just -- I can`t speak to that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: CBS News has learned that an indictment of Trump is not expected today, but could come sometime later this week.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Trump`s campaign has been issuing a flurry of fundraising appeals to his supporters -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bob, thank you. Very interesting comments from \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and he said it with a straight face. It provided better humor yesterday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Bob Costa.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, six people affiliated with the far-right group, the Oath Keepers were convicted Monday for their role in the January 6th attack. Four were found guilty of conspiracy and obstruction among other offenses.\par \par \uc2 Now, they face up to 20 years in prison. Two others were acquitted on some charges, but convicted of misdemeanor trespassing. This is the third trial involving members of the Oath Keepers. Other members of the group have already been convicted of seditious conspiracy against the government.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There is now surveillance video in the case of a mentally ill man who suffocated to death while in police custody. A warning it is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 This is the man who lost his life, 28-year-old Irvo Otieno. The video shows officers and staff pressing their way on Otieno as he was checked into a mental hospital. Defense attorneys tried to stop any release of the footage, which was obtained by "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." Ten people have now been charged with murder.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Pegues has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: The video which is silent shows Irvo Otieno handcuffed and in leg irons being brought into the Virginia Hospital`s admission room by Henrico County Sheriff`s Deputies on March 6th. They walk him a chair. About eight minutes later, at least two officers are seen holding Otieno on the ground. They seem to react when he moves.\par \par \uc2 The officers then appear to lose their grip on Otieno and they roll him across the floor. Four minutes later, additional officers and hospital staff joined to restrain them.\par \par \uc2 Soon thereafter, at least nine people are seeing piling on top of Otieno to hold them down with other hospital staff observing what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 Otieno`s mother was showing the video last week.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE OUKO (Irvo Otieno`s mother): My son was treated like a dog, worse than a dog.\par \par \uc2 My son was tortured.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: According to the video, Otieno was held down for about 11 minutes.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP (Civil Rights attorney): You see him being restrained so brutally with knee on his neck.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Twenty minutes after entering the Admissions Room, Otieno appears to be a mobile. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " says resuscitation efforts lasted less than an hour. He died at the scene.\par \par \uc2 Seven Sheriff`s Deputies and three hospital workers have been charged with second-degree murder and the prosecutor says additional charges and arrest are pending.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jeff Pegues.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right, Vlad, that video is very tough to watch and it`s interesting to see the hospital people standing around.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People with medical training.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wish we could hear audio to see, did anybody say, hey, hey, hey. Please stop.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and he is in leg and arm irons.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shackles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I don`t know why the huge amount of force there. All of those individuals on top of him. I really do wish we had audio and there`s going to be a lot of questions. We`ll learn more definitely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That story is not over.\par \par \uc2 In other news, classes are canceled today at a high school in Arlington, Texas. That was the scene of this country`s latest school shooting. A male student died after being shot outside Lamar High School yesterday before classes began. A female student was grazed by shrapnel, but she is expected to survive.\par \par \uc2 The suspected shooter was arrested at the scene and has been charged with capital murder. Police say he never entered the school building. There is no word yet on a motive.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say the victims and the suspect are under the age of 17.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And today hundreds of thousands of students are unable to attend classes because of a three-day strike in the second largest school district in the country. Support staff are leading this strike in Los Angeles, 35,000 teachers joining them in solidarity.\par \par \uc2 The workers say that their salaries are so low that many are at risk of living on the streets. Carter Evans is covering this for us in Los Angeles\par \par \uc2 Carter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 This is a school bus yard behind me and as you can see, picketers are out in force here and they`re planning to be out in force around Los Angeles today. Thirty thousand workers for the union representing support staff like teachers` aides, bus drivers, custodians. They`re planning to strike for three days.\par \par \uc2 Also, teachers from the school district are planning to join in, in solidarity. So that means hundreds of thousands of kids will not be going to school for the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 Now, the news comes after failed negotiations between the district and the union earlier on Monday. The union wants a 30 percent raise. Now they say average salaries for supports have total about $25,000.00 a year and that puts many of them below the poverty line especially when you factor in inflation and the rising cost of housing.\par \par \uc2 The school district has countered with a 23 percent raise offer and a cash bonus and says the door is open to continue negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Now Los Angeles is the second largest school district in the country. So this is going to impact about a half million students.\par \par \uc2 And in preparation for all of this, about a thousand schools are going to be open today, but with no educational staff, so there is not going to be any instruction, just some supervision.\par \par \uc2 Also here in Los Angeles, many families depend on the school district for meals for their children. So there will be food distribution sites around the county for the next three days.\par \par \uc2 And of course, you can see all this rain here. We`ve got another one of those atmospheric rivers coming through, so it is going to be a very wet day on the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Carter, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s take a look at the weather, shall we?\par \par \uc2 This morning, California is getting slammed by the twelfth atmospheric river of the season. Yes, that`s a thing. The massive storm system is bringing the threat of flash flooding, dangerous wind gusts, and heavy mountain snow.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking it all.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, good morning. Sounds like a mess. What do you see?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Ugh. Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 It is the first full day of spring, but somebody forgot to tell the West. Let`s start with the snow that you mentioned. We`ll get feet of it from California on into Colorado. There is also the threat of flash flooding, especially in to Southern California and strong winds could send loose objects flying.\par \par \uc2 Now, there will be widespread gusts of 50 to 60 miles an hour at higher elevations up to 75 miles an hour. So those high profile vehicles, flights and power could all be affected.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be a Tuesday-Wednesday event for us in the West, and like past storms, this energy will cross the entire country. So, if you live in the Midwest, the South or the Northeast, this is headed your way.\par \par \uc2 Now anywhere from LA down into San Diego, we could get half a month to a month`s worth of rainfall. And in the Sierra, along with the feet of snow, wind chills as low as 20 below zero.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices, and Gayle, after this weather clears, we stay a little bit unsettled, but thankfully, we get a break from these big monster storms at least through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Looking forward to that, Stephanie. The weather sometimes is always a mess, but you never are. You`re always on point.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 : Oh, Gayle. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There is new concern in the banking sector this morning following the failure of multiple banks this month. \uc2 First Republic\uc2  until recently, the 14th largest bank saw its shares plunge yesterday despite a $30 billion lifeline from 11 other banks.\par \par \uc2 The news comes as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is considering another interest rate hike.\par \par \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  joins us now to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 Jill, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So front page of "The Wall Street Journal" Dimon, as in Jamie, pursues more aid for \uc2 First Republic\uc2 . It is on the brink of collapse. Should people be worried?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, I think we have to all be worried about this. This is not something we encounter where medium sized banks that have a couple hundred billion dollars in assets are having runs on their asset base.\par \par \uc2 But here is what I think is important.\par \par \uc2 First Republic\uc2 , like \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  had this large percentage of uninsured deposits, meaning a lot of people had accounts with more than The $250,000.00 \uc2 FDIC\uc2  insurance limit. Now, as other banks were failing, remember that the Fed came in and Treasury Department said we`re going to insure everybody, everyone more than two fifty, but they haven`t made that similar announcement for other banks.\par \par \uc2 That means that despite 11 banks putting $30 billion into First Republic, $70 billion has left that bank this, month and I think that is what is a cause for concern.\par \par \uc2 The idea that maybe these 11 banks which put $30 billion in may try to do something to help the bank out. That`s actually good. And also we have comments that were just released where \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is going to deliver a speech later today, and there is sort of an implicit comment that she put out that said, hey, if other banks that are systemically important run into a problem, we`re probably going to backstop those folks over two fifty.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s talk about the Fed, which is meeting today to decide whether or not to raise interest rates again. Will they? What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, the idea here is that inflation still remains high, okay, we know that. And the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is very clear, they need to fight inflation. The inflation rate six percent year-over-year.\par \par \uc2 Now the problem is, with the instability in the banking system, a lot of people are saying, "Hey, go on pause. Just wait. Chill out for a meeting." The problem is, I think that if the Fed were to do that, it sends a signal that they are spooked about what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 And if we`re really believing that this is mostly about confidence, and we need to shore up confidence, I hear that the most likely path for this two- day meeting is a quarter of a percentage point increase. Whether or not you think that`s good or bad, I think that`s what`s most likely. That way the Fed can sort of say, "Hey, we`re dealing with inflation, but we`re not raising rates by so much that`s going to further crimp the banking system."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Markets seem to agree with you. It was up big yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: As always, great to have you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Turning overseas, meetings between the leaders of China and Russia are entering a second day in Moscow ahead of a big State Dinner there.\par \par \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  held more than four hours of talks yesterday. It was an opportunity, it would appear for them to flaunt their so called no limits friendship, all in defiance to the West and in defiance of global criticism of Russia`s invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Liz Palmer has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , one of Moscow`s few political allies arrived to an enthusiastic welcome and a warm greeting in the Kremlin from President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , who called him a Dear Friend.\par \par \uc2 (VLADIMIR PUTIN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Last February at the Beijing Winter Olympics, the two men first declared that friendship was without limits.\par \par \uc2 It since being tested by Russia`s invasion of Ukraine, and by the International Criminal Courts arrest warrant for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as a suspected war criminal.\par \par \uc2 China has recently published a ceasefire plan for Ukraine, but its terms which don`t call for a Russian withdrawal have been dismissed by the White House.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN: Calling for a ceasefire that does not include the removal of Russian forces from Ukrainian territory, would effectively be supporting the ratification of Russian conquest.\par \par \uc2 It would recognize Russia`s attempts to seize the sovereign neighbor`s territory by force.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Today, President Xi and Putin are likely to discuss Ukraine. A breakthrough isn`t likely though it is expected that Xi will soon speak with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , and Western officials don`t believe China has committed to supplying Russia with weapons.\par \par \uc2 High on today`s meeting agenda will be trade. Under heavy Western sanctions, Russia depends more than ever on China for goods and services and to buy its oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 President Xi is well-placed to drive a hard bargain with a man who may not be the ideal friend, but who he can at least count on to stand with China against its main rival -- the United States.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Elizabeth Palmer in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, a powerful story of freedom and forgiveness. Lilia Luciano sits down with a man who was wrongfully convicted of murder.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, the cast of "Ted Lasso" visited the White House yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Anthony Mason spoke to one of the show`s Emmy-nominated stars, Toheeb Jimoh.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our colleague KCAL meteorologist, Alissa Carlton, we`re happy to tell you this okay this morning after fainting live on the air during Saturday`s broadcast. Very scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Carlton will join us after the break with our own Dr. Jon LaPook to tell us what caused her sudden collapse and her story may help you.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS where we have got a story for you.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, 35-year-old Sheldon Thomas walked out of a Brooklyn, New York Courtroom free after almost two decades behind bars.\par \par \uc2 Thomas was arrested back in 2004 for murder after an eyewitness pointed to a picture of a guy named Sheldon Thomas in a police lineup of photos, but the man in the image was actually a different person entirely, happened have the same name, and as we step through the circles of absurdity here, that guy also had no link to the case.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, prosecutors continued to focus on the Sheldon Thomas they had in custody, which eventually led to his wrongful conviction.\par \par \uc2 Thomas talked to our Lilia Luciano just days after his release in his very first TV interview.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHELDON THOMAS (Wrongfully convicted of murder): I have been waiting for this my whole life.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Sheldon Thomas was 16 when police took down the door of his home and locked him up. Now, 19 years later, he has been effectively exonerated.\par \par \uc2 What`s been hard about just being out and about and so little time --\par \par \uc2 SHELDON THOMAS: Just like what you said, being out and about, but just in being around people.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Thomas was convicted for second-degree murder in the December 2004 death of a 14-year-old boy in Brooklyn. Attorneys William Kasten and Lesley Risinger have worked with Thomas for more than a decade. They say the case should have never even made it to trial.\par \par \uc2 How much of his case was based on lies?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM KASTEN (Attorney): A hundred percent. From the police interaction to him, every party failed him in the system.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In a pretrial hearing, a detective lied, then admitted to lying on the stand and the case still moved forward.\par \par \uc2 He is arrested based on a photo that is not his.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM KASTEN: The people involved in decision making refused to accept the fact that a mistake was made. How can a jury reach a fair verdict? It was rigged from the start.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The unit that reviewed his case after years of failed appeals concluded that Thomas was denied due process at every stage and found one of the detectives involved had been harassing Thomas after a prior arrest and lied about knowing him.\par \par \uc2 Did you ever feel like they were targeting you specifically?\par \par \uc2 SHELDON THOMAS: Most definitely.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: It should have been the other way around. You`re innocent until proven guilty.\par \par \uc2 SHELDON THOMAS: From my lens, the way I see life is always like that for people like me. For people that look like me, it`s always like that, you`re guilty until you actually prove that you`re innocent.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Nearly two decades later, he finally stepped into what still feels like a mirage of freedom.\par \par \uc2 SHELDON THOMAS: I haven`t even cried yet since I`ve been home, like I haven`t even got to the point where it actually hit me. I still have dreams about being in prison, scared to go to sleep.\par \par \uc2 As the days go by, it dawns on me a little bit more, a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE MATTHEW D`EMIC (New York Supreme Court): Mr. Thomas` motion to vacate his judgment of conviction is granted.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: At a hearing this month, Thomas shared how his hope was sustained for so long by releasing resentment.\par \par \uc2 SHELDON THOMAS: When I was sentenced, Del Giudice (Sentencing Judge) said I deserved the rest of my life in prison. He was wrong. I didn`t deserve what happened, and I forgive him.\par \par \uc2 I know I have lost 20 years already, but ultimately, I prevailed. I don`t like losing, so I have to let go because I don`t want that to be what defines me as a human, as a person. I want love to define me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Sheldon says he can`t sleep because he is afraid that he`s going to wake up and it was all a dream. He can`t sleep because of the other inmates he met up in Attica, who he believes that they are innocent.\par \par \uc2 We`ve reached out to the detective that we mentioned in this case who was involved in the original arrest and he said he did not want to comment.\par \par \uc2 You know, here is one thing that the attorney told me. When you commit a crime and you serve your sentence, when you walk out, there is resources. There is assistance.\par \par \uc2 For him, they just opened the door and you`re on your own. So he is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, he has no assistance whatsoever?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Nothing right now. He is relying on his elderly mother who is working a second shift to support him and on crowdfunding that his attorney set up.\par \par \uc2 And eventually perhaps, he may sue and maybe he`ll win, but he is not ready for that. Right now, he is just focusing --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what is it like for him to be around people, do you know?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Well, that`s what he was saying --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Around people.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: He said, we were walking around Central Park and he said, when you`re in prison, the only time you get outside, you`re in the yard and if somebody gets close to you --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is normally not a good thing.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: It is not a good thing. You`re going to get attacked.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: So he feels like that self-preservation means isolation. And even with his family, he says, I feel so bad because they reach out to me and I just need the solitude.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I hope he will allow himself a good cry. He certainly deserves and needs that. But I marvel that he can sit here today after the injustice that happened to him and say, I forgive them. I would still need help on that, I mean --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I want love --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I want love to define me.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yes, he is going to need a lot of, you know, therapy, and we talked so much. I wish I could share everything, but he did --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: I asked him what got you through it and he said Viktor Frankl`s book, "Man`s Search for Meaning."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Man`s Search for Meaning." One of the greatest books ever written.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: He had read it over and over and he read about this man in a concentration camp and he said, well, at least that`s not this and if he can get through it, and all of those lessons got him through.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That book has got incredible power.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a powerful story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is an amazing human being.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Incredible grace under pressure. Lilia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The most inspired person I`ve met in my life.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s saying a lot because you`ve met a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Lilia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, ahead, a scary moment during a live newscast. Meteorologist, Alissa Carlton is standing by to tell us what made her faint on air. Hey, Alissa.\par \par \uc2 She`ll share her message to help other people.\par \par \uc2 We are coming right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our colleagues at CBS Los Angeles and their viewers witnessed a very scary moment over the weekend. It was on the air.\par \par \uc2 KCAL News meteorologist, Alissa Carlton fainted live on air Saturday, just as she was about to deliver a forecast. A warning some of this video you`re going to watch is difficult to see.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from KCAL News.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ay-yay-yay. Alissa was checked out in the hospital. The good news, we are very happy to tell you, she is okay. We can see that with our own eyes. She joins us now from Bakersfield, California.\par \par \uc2 Here at the table is CBS News chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jon LaPook who is here to help us all better understand exactly what happened to you, Alissa.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. We are so glad to see you.\par \par \uc2 Can I just say you gave us such a scare, the people who love you, the people that don`t even know you were thinking "Is she going to be okay?"\par \par \uc2 So, take us to that moment. Did you feel anything amiss that Saturday morning as you were delivering the news?\par \par \uc2 ALISSA CARLTON: Well, thank you, Gayle and everyone there at the table.\par \par \uc2 You know, in the morning, I felt fine. It wasn`t really up until about 15 minutes prior to the incident that I started to feel a little nausea, and at that point, I went to the restroom and thought, "Okay, I will be fine. I`ll get through the hit and I`ll get something to eat," because I hadn`t had breakfast that morning.\par \par \uc2 I also had been drinking a lot of coffee and so most likely, I was maybe a little dehydrated. So at that point, I thought, I`m just going to power through, I`ll be fine as I usually am. Well, you all saw what happened.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s what we always do, Alissa. We power through.\par \par \uc2 What did your doctor say ultimately caused this?\par \par \uc2 ALISSA CARLTON: Well, you know, in the past, I have been diagnosed with a leaky heart valve. So initially, when this happened, I think a lot of people including myself thought I was having a heart attack on the air. That was not the case, thankfully, and heart test proved that I`m fine. My heart is great.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a thing called vasovagal syncope, and that is the condition that I have. I do have a history of fainting. It`s just when you`re on a public platform, sometimes it happens and people now see this.\par \par \uc2 So it hadn`t happened in a while and I`m now going to have to learn how to really recognize the symptoms of this condition.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, well, we`re glad you`re doing okay, Alissa.\par \par \uc2 Dr. LaPook, she mentioned vasovagal syncope, if I have it right, it`s quite a mouthful. What is it?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: All right, syncope is blacking out, vaso is blood vessel, and vagal refers to the vagus nerve and if the vagus nerve is overstimulated or inappropriately stimulated, the pulse rate can go down and the blood pressure goes down and none of the blood goes up to the head and you can faint.\par \par \uc2 And we saw with Alissa, she was sort of propped up so she couldn`t go flat and then eventually she went down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It looked like she -- Alissa, it looked like you tried to steady yourself and then, you just fell flat over. You just totally passed out.\par \par \uc2 So it seems like you felt something coming, right?\par \par \uc2 ALISSA CARLTON: I could feel that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISSA CARLTON: I did. I could feel the tunnel vision coming and just starting to get black and so I tried to hit, you know, brace myself on that desk, but it was too late.\par \par \uc2 And in all honesty, what I probably should have done was just not been at that wall for the hit. When I started to feel something come on, maybe just go and sit in the chair and just let them go to me and take me with nothing there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That leads me to the question. Do you have symptoms?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Yes, very often there`s warning. You may get sweaty palms, you may feel that tunnel vision, that graying out, you`re dizzy. And people like especially in this situation, you`re embarrassed, right? I`m going to just -- I`m going to sit up and look normal. That`s the worst thing you can do.\par \par \uc2 You want to get flat, so your heart is at the same level as your head and it is pumping the blood vessels.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Flat like?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Flat like on your back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lay down on the floor?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Maybe your legs up if you can get it. Your head to the left in case you vomit and that`s something that people tend to, especially if you`re about to go on the air, that`s the last thing you want to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Alissa, you`re also a health coach. Given what Dr. LaPook said about recognizing the signs. What would you recommend to your clients, the people that come and listen to you going forward?\par \par \uc2 ALISSA CARLTON: Well, I would definitely say stay hydrated, which I always do tell my clients and sometimes, as a coach, we pour into a lot of other people and we tend to leave ourselves last. So I recognize that.\par \par \uc2 I`ve had this passion for health for many years and I continue to strive for ultimate health and I also -- I would tell them, one, get enough rest. Make sure you`re hydrated and take care of yourself.\par \par \uc2 As I was talking to the camera person this morning, I said sometimes we put ourselves last. We go, go, go until something happens and then, it is too late.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Alissa --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s what happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Alissa, have you looked at the tape yourself? Have you seen it?\par \par \uc2 ALISSA CARLTON: I have many times. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, please be careful next time.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, we say do not power through.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Don`t power through. It`s very, very frightening and make sure that you do get evaluated because there are other causes of fainting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes. And I`m picking up my mug because I`m going to stay hydrated.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Stay hydrated. Wake up in the morning, you`re dehydrated in general.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 ALISSA CARLTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Alissa Carlton, we are so glad you`re doing okay today. And Dr. LaPook, thank you very much for explaining it all to us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Mr. Duthiers will take center stage. We will have the stories you`ll be talking about today. But first, it is 7:45. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: . another upset over number one seed with this possession. Harden step through. She gets it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Yes. Let`s talk about the women`s game.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Big upset.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Major March Madness upset for the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  women`s tournament. The ninth seeded Miami Hurricanes knocked out the top seeded Indiana -- I mean, in what --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hoosiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. That`s big.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hoosiers. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s big.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the thing about March Madness. I feel like when you get in that tournament, it like starts all over again.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It seems to be anybody`s game.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you know what the other thing is about March Madness?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, we`re watching them on TV. You almost think they`re professionals. I talked to the Princeton head coach yesterday --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I like that guy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Guess what the kids do after their games?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go get something to eat.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, they are back in class.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Back in class.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are still in school.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are still in school studying for exam.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They go get something to eat.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. They go back there. Like so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re on television. They are celebrities in our eyes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are big-time stars.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they`re all going back to campus and they`re going to class.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are kids.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just shout out to the Miami Hurricanes` first appearance in the Sweet 16 since 1992.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I love that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Women`s game is exciting.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk about the stars of "Ted Lasso."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They visited the White House yesterday to discuss one of the show`s central themes, mental health.\par \par \uc2 Actor, Jason Sudeikis was joined the White House briefing room by his castmates, Hannah Waddingham, Brett Goldstein, Brendan Hunt, and Toheeb Jimoh. He stressed the importance of checking in with each other every now and again.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON SUDEIKIS ("Ted Lasso" actor): We all know someone who has or had been that someone ourselves actually that`s struggled, that`s felt isolated, that`s felt anxious, that has felt alone. So please, you know, we encourage everyone and as a big theme of the show, is like to check in with your -- you know, your neighbor, your co-worker, your friends, your family, and ask how they are doing and listen, sincerely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So I love how a show like this is also addressing real- life issues. Love that.\par \par \uc2 And I got a cool picture. Look at Hannah Waddingham posted this on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . She wanted to share with everybody. It is so cool. She says: "Never in my wildest dreams that I think I would stand under this picture. Humbled beyond belief." Isn`t that a great shot?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It`s so great they were there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Love this show. Love Jason.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Anthony Mason in conversation with "Ted Lasso" star, Toheeb Jimoh.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, we are continuing our series that we call Facing Fertility with a woman who takes Nikki Battiste through her decision to freeze her eggs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. You may recognize Emmy-nominated actor, Toheeb Jimoh from "Ted Lasso." Well, he is taking our Anthony Mason back to his old school where he had an emotional reunion.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers. Actor D.B. Woodside is excellent as a member of the Secret Service in the new show, "The Night Agent." He will be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Excellent is the word. He is so good in it.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement is on alert after former President Trump calls for protests if he is arrested over allegations he was involved in a hush money scheme.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: CBS News has learned that an indictment of Trump is not expected today, but could come sometime later this week.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " releases surveillance video showing officers and hospital staff restraining a man who later died.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We got several calls of shots fired outside the school.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A shooting on a high school campus in Texas leaves one student dead and another injured.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  could announce another interest rate hike this week in the fight against inflation as concerns remain about the fallout from two big bank failures.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZANDI (Chief economist of Moody`s Analytics): They`re trying to thread the needle here, balance 