oles yet.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is this where you used to play?\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes, man. This is where we used to do football tryouts here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Toheeb Jimoh took us back to his middle school field at the Norwood School in the Brixton neighborhood of London.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: I`m going to flop now because I`m (on camera).\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s here he learned the moves that he shows off playing soccer star, Sam Obisanya on "Ted Lasso."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You came to the part with some real experience though.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes. Like I grew up playing football my entire time through school. Football is the main sport around here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Jimoh never played professionally, but he did win a local school tournament.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: I remember I went crazy and I used to wear my medal to school every single day for like the next two weeks.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: From Norwood, Jimoh went on to drama school. And a year after graduating in 2019 landed his first film role.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The French Dispatch.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: A small part in Wes Anderson`s "The French Dispatch."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The French Dispatch.")\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: And then in pretty quick succession in that same summer, I got "Ted Lasso" so and "The Power."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Crazy.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes, it has been a bit of a whirlwind.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Power.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In the new Prime Video series, "The Power." Jimoh plays Tunde, a Nigerian journalist who discovers that a group of young women has developed a mysterious power to generate electricity through their hands.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Power.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: On the first episode, you literally get a shock.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Power.")\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: It is a really mind-boggling thing. Like it poses really dumb questions about power and you know who has it, who doesn`t?\par \par \uc2 Yes, it posed really interesting questions about the power dynamics between men and women.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Has it changed much?\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: No. It`s exactly the same.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Jimoh discovered his love of acting here at Norwood.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: It felt bigger when I was here, but I guess, I was probably just little, you know?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. This was my stage.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: This was my stage. I did all of my rehearsals here. I did all of my performances here. Like I lived in this room. This was like the birthplace of like me wanting to be a performer, of me wanting to be an actor. This was my safe haven.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: His courage to pursue it professionally was inspired by his instructors.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: This is one of my teachers --\par \par \uc2 MARTIN BLAKE LOCKE (Teacher): Hello.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Martin Blake Locke (sp?). This is the man I used to force -- I used to like sit you down over here and just like yell Shakespeare at you.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN BLAKE LOCKE: I remember you did up there.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN BLAKE LOCKE: You did Julius Caesar.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Julius Caesar, Mark Anthony.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN BLAKE LOCKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 Friends, Romans, countrymen, lend me your ears.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN BLAKE LOCKE: That was one of the moments, I thought, okay, we`ve got a chance here. A chance.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Jimoh also introduced us to Marcus Condron, Deputy Head of Performing Arts at the school.\par \par \uc2 He says you changed his life.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CONDRON (Deputy Head of Performing Arts): That`s very nice. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Do you remember that time that you pulled me into your office and like you sat me down and you`re like, I think you can like -- I think you can be an actor.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CONDRON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Like, that meant a lot to me, you know. My God, I always cry when I`m on this stage.\par \par \uc2 Genuinely, it had a really like profound impact on me. And like, I owe so much of what I`m doing now to you. And I`m like, just -- I can`t thank you enough. Because you didn`t have to, but you went above and beyond for me.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CONDRON: You`re worth it.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes. I appreciate you. I want to stop crying now, but --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What did you see in him?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CONDRON: You know what? It is easy, and it`s hard to say at the same time. I think you see the talent and then it`s -- we always say to them, then the hard work comes and he had plenty of that as well.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CONDRON: So, you know, not -- not always the best behaved at all points throughout his school journey.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But he`s a school hero now for his starring role in "Ted Lasso."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In the last season, Sam`s story took an unexpected turn when he began dating an older woman.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Did you know the romance was coming in Season 2?\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: No.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You didn`t?\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: No, no. Nobody told me about that. I was reading the script, and I was like, "I see."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That older woman was team owner, Rebecca Welton, played by the Emmy-winning Hannah Waddingham.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Fans used to message me and be like, is there anything romantic? Or is it just platonic? And I was like, oh, no, it is platonic. But like, I think what they hit on was that there is a connection between those two characters.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: And yes, I thought it was really, really special.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And you two are friends off screen as well.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Oh, me and Hannah. Yes, yes. She is dope. She`s a rock star, that woman. And I think that`s why I also felt really comfortable by it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ted Lasso.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: When Sam faces a critical decision in Season 2, he looks in his locker at a photograph of his parents.\par \par \uc2 Those actually are your parents.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: My real-life parents. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Sam and Toheeb were like one in that moment.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Jimoh`s father and mother are Nigerian immigrants.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: My whole life has kind of been about, like where I fit in. I kind of felt like I fell in between being a British kid and being a Nigerian kid and never really felt like a part of either club.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Born in Britain, Jimoh spent his early childhood in Nigeria, returning to London around age seven.\par \par \uc2 So your first memories are in Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes. My first accent was a Nigerian accent.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: And so yes, again, like it`s really dope, because I get to play two Nigerian characters in Sam and Tunde in what is actually my like, first voice, you know. So I always find it really weird how now my career has taken me back to the thing that I kind of like got rid of to fit in, you know?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. Must have made your parents proud though.\par \par \uc2 TOHEEB JIMOH: Yes, yes. I think so. My dad is like one of the most stoic people ever, so like, I could do anything. I could win 14 Oscars tomorrow, and he`d just be like, "Good." I mean, "It`s good."\par \par \uc2 Yes, my mom always had an idea that I was going to be the UK`s first Black Prime Minister. Maybe I`ll play him one day. You know what I mean? But that`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The next episode of "Ted Lasso" drops tomorrow on Apple TV+ and "The Power" premieres on Prime Video, March 31st. I saw the first episode, it looks really promising. It`s an exciting show.\par \par \uc2 You know, the other thing that`s really interesting about Toheeb is, he was one of the few Black kids from Brixton to get into drama school. That was a huge achievement in itself, and the school celebrated him for that, but as you see, he has made the most of it. It`s that high-wattage smile of his, first of all, that just lights up the screen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number one, he seems like such a nice guy and as your pieces often do, Anthony, it just touches on all the heart points. I love when he was with his coach.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love the storyline with him and Rebecca, of, course.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With the older woman and the younger man. I love that storyline.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why do you love that storyline, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just do. Hush, Vlad, hush.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, I noticed you were very focused on that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But to give us some insight in to him who came from and what his background is, it is so nice.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s so interesting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this show.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: . the circling back to being Nigerian because that was really tough for him being born in Britain. He thought himself as British. But you know, he couldn`t be seen as either one in effect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is he as nice as he seems? I know, he is.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Absolutely. And you know, that moment with his coach, he literally took us to him. He wanted -- he wanted to introduce us to his coaches.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have already seen the first episode of "Ted Lasso." I can`t wait for the new one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I am looking forward to it also.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t wait. Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am also looking forward to "The Power." I hear it is pretty good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, "The Power."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, I really like it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anthony, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is 8:10. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, our series, Facing Fertility, looks at the pros and the cons of freezing eggs. Meet one woman who shares her journey and the lessons other women can learn.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For Part 2 of our series, Facing Fertility, we`re going to take a look at a procedure that is growing in popularity: Egg freezing.\par \par \uc2 Research shows that a woman`s age when she freezes her eggs and how many she actually freezes makes a big difference in whether she will have a baby.\par \par \uc2 A study from NYU Langone -- and we`re going to have a lot of numbers here, so listen with both ears -- shows the average age of a woman who freezes her eggs is around 38. Her chance of a live birth from those eggs is 39 percent. The odds improve for women who freeze more eggs at a younger age.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste shows us one Chicago woman`s experience with the procedure.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY (Egg freezing patient): I know that I want kids one day, but I don`t know how it`s going to happen or when.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Erin Hanley decided to freeze her eggs in the fall of 2020. She was 33 years old.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: I always wanted to have options so that I can make a choice later.\par \par \uc2 I`m really excited to have this be over.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Hanley took us on her journey with her.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: What if I`m doing it wrong? I`m not, but like, what if I was?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Through videos she recorded during the process.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: It`s a huge needle.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Egg freezing involves about two weeks of hormone shots to stimulate the follicles in a woman`s ovaries. Each follicle typically releases one egg. The shots are usually done at home.\par \par \uc2 That first night you come home, you have to give yourself shots. How were you feeling?\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: Very nervous. You kind of mix your own solutions to in your shots, and so I laid them all out, had them on my counter and then I had a friend here and I was like, "Why don`t you fill me?"\par \par \uc2 One-two.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: All the way, push it, push it, push it, push it, push it, push it, push it.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: Heading in to my appointment on Friday.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Throughout, Hanley went to the doctor for bloodwork and ultrasounds to monitor her progress.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: It has gotten bigger since yesterday.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: They have. So this one`s about 18.5 millimeters, so you`ve still got some time.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: You`re feeling okay?\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: The first couple days I felt fine. I was getting really tired by Day 4 probably. And then also really bloated. You get very bloated.\par \par \uc2 It`s like this weird belly.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Once the eggs are big enough, a procedure is done to retrieve them. Typically around 80 percent of the eggs are mature enough to be frozen.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: They retrieved 20 and then they ended up freezing 14.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The eggs can later be thawed and fertilized in an effort to make embryos. On average, only 30 percent become viable embryos, but not all lead to pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 DR. RACHEL MCCONNELL (Columbia Fertility Center reproductive endocrinologist): Whenever you`re freezing eggs, you always have to remember that 14 eggs will not equate to 14 babies.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr Rachel McConnell is a reproductive endocrinologist at Columbia Fertility Center in New York City. She is not Hanley`s doctor, but was given permission to review her medical records.\par \par \uc2 DR. RACHEL MCCONNELL: With her being 33 years of age and freezing at least 14 eggs that are mature, she has probably about an 80 percent chance of at least having one live birth from that group of eggs.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: For women of all ages, the live birth efficiency from frozen eggs is four percent. In general, this means 25 frozen eggs will yield one live birth. That`s according to an NYU study published last year.\par \par \uc2 Dr. McConnell says women should start thinking about their reproductive health at an early age, and doctors should start educating patients at age 25.\par \par \uc2 DR. RACHEL MCCONNELL: There are techniques and there are tests that they can even do to be able to have some idea what their egg reserve is like.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Did your OBGYN ever talk to you about the option of egg freezing or about family planning at all?\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: No, actually. It has always been interesting to me that they don`t talk to you about it. In fact, they send you to a specialist.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Did you have any reservations about the process?\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: Oh, yes. I think there`s a lot of reservations about it. First, the cost. It`s a lot, especially if you don`t have insurance to pay for things.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Egg freezing can cost anywhere from five to ten thousand dollars a cycle, but despite the high cost, more women are choosing to make this expensive and at times, very uncomfortable investment.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, more than 12,000 women froze their eggs, nearly double from 2016.\par \par \uc2 How much hope were you given that freezing your eggs would lead to someday having a baby?\par \par \uc2 ERIN HANLEY: I think it was definitely hope because it is better than not having it at all. I do think you don`t know until you go in there either how your eggs are going to be. Are they viable? I don`t know. So that was a concern for me that I would actually go through this whole thing and then nothing happened after.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The purpose of the hormone shots we saw Erin give herself was to stimulate as many follicles in her ovaries as possible. The follicles then produce the eggs for retrieval.\par \par \uc2 While Erin got 14 eggs, some women might not produce any and others may get 20 or 30. It depends on the woman`s body and how it responds to the shots.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so good you`re even having this conversation because I don`t recall it even being an option back in the day, so I think it`s good that you`re talking about it that women can know. If you haven`t met the right one, you can take precaution, you can take steps.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Plan.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And starting earlier is better.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Yes, always earlier. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fourteen eggs does not mean 14 babies. That`s also great to --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Vlad, if you have to, you would be able to wield that needle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he can.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It is not that bad, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nikki, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow in our Facing Fertility series, a candid conversation on menopause. We talk with our friend, Drew Barrymore about our experiences with menopause and some of the biggest misconceptions.\par \par \uc2 It`s a really interesting conversation.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is crazy, it is 8:30 in the morning, and welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning and eeny, meeny, miny moe, Vlad is going to go first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve got pole position.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is this year`s World Happiness Report and it is a of the happiest countries. Now I want to break down what the key factors are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: . that researchers are looking at when it comes to deciding which country ranks high -- healthy life expectancy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Average income.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Social support.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Important.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Low corruption.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Well, that could be frustrating.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We don`t like corruption.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, we don`t.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We like having low corruption.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we don`t like corruption.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly. And this one I love -- communities where people have the freedom to make life decisions and look after each other, and where people look after each other. I want to stress that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Friendly is right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So where do we need to go?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And America is ranked number one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wonk-wonk. No, America is not ranked number one. For the sixth year in a row, Finland is the happiest country in the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have never been to Finland.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, Finland, and more Nordic countries take up the top spots. Denmark and Iceland in second and third place, if you`re wondering. Do you want to know where we rank?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do want to know. Are we in the top 10?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tell us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are not in the top 10, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: America came in fifteenth. I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have some work to do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We have a little bit of work to do. I mean, if you look at the countries that are in the top 10, they include Israel. We know Israel very well. I`ve lived there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s -- I question that one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony, Israel is a wonderful place to live.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are like in week nine of like hundreds of thousands of people fighting for the future of the country. It`s a pretty -- okay, but basically, they seem happy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They seem happy. Netherlands, Sweden, Norway, Switzerland, Luxembourg. I`ve lived in Luxembourg. It is a very happy place, a wonderful country to live, and New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I want to go there, too. All of your Israeli friends are calling you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. The Israeli friends are -- yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is not --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re right. There are issues, but --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It doesn`t strike me. It`s a warzone half the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are in the top 10 though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, yes, okay. Good for them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we cover a lot of important issues on this show and I`m very proud of that work, but sometimes, the algorithm -- that is me --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, which is great. Your deadpan delivery always gets me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know. It`s a country I know. Anyway, so where was I?\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" -- sometimes the algorithm that has me serves up just something that is a nice break in your morning, just the perfect best bear scratching its back video that I`ve actually ever seen on the internet.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s just roll it and I`ll give you some background.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Debra Howell/Storyful.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at the hip action.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, there`s nothing to be said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, what more can you say?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, my. What --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The bear is a better dancer than I am.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: My favorite is the head because the head is sort of like you know, back and forth. Look at his head.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is some animal pleasure right there and it is just fur --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He`s got his hand. His little paw.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is some kind of pleasure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, God.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I need to find that tree. Okay. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, God.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad, come back. We`re not done here. We are not done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are not done. We are not done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, what have you got, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" -- speaking of happiest countries, I think Jordan should have been on the list. Why? Because Oprah and I just went there last week.\par \par \uc2 These are some of the pictures. Normally, you know, people share their vacation pictures and they`re boring. I think these are very interesting.\par \par \uc2 There were camels. We went to Petra. Petra, guys is an amazing place. That`s where -- you probably know this, but where "Indiana Jones" --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that Aqaba? No. No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was in Petra.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Petra, Petra.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, in Petra. It is run don`t walk to go see Petra. We went to Wadi Rum. We went to the place where Jesus was baptized. And then we were in the Dead Sea taking -- you can`t go to the Dead Sea without getting a mud bath.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A mud bath.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So of course, had to do that. I`ve got video.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: How does it feel, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It feels great. It does feel great. I do wish it wasn`t raining, but it feels great.\par \par \uc2 We are in the Dead Sea, people. You have to do it.\par \par \uc2 Could you please get a shot of Oprah`s Dead Sea experience.\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (Talk show host): Hi.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How are you feeling, Oprah?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I decided to do it. I have a facial at 2:30. I am going to be moseying on back. I`m getting my purse wet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How cold is it?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is amazing though, guys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s amazing. You really cannot drown in the Dead Sea.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you loved it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The people of Jordan -- yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because you float.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were so amazing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you`ve never been -- you`ve never even -- you weren`t familiar with Petra.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. I wasn`t.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know you probably know about Petra. I didn`t.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is one of the Seven Wonders of the World.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle, Oprah`s Dead Sea experience reminds me of your Nickelodeon slime time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh that`s right, with the umbrella.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where everybody is into it, except for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. It was -- you know, people on the internet are so mean. People said, well you know the reason why Oprah didn`t get in the Dead Sea, she probably has hemorrhoids and hemorrhoids aren`t good in the Dead Sea. I can tell you --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She does not have hemorrhoids.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is very salty, but it does burn, I will say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. She does not have hemorrhoids.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am not a doctor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But she had planned to go, but then it started pouring rain. The rest of us -- Derick and Giselle and I, we all said we`re going and it was great. It was great. It is salty, and it does burn. You`re right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right about -- Jon Tower just said "Gayle, please. We need to keep going now."\par \par \uc2 Turning now to our next guest, I`ll bet he can`t wait to come out to the table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He wants to come out here, at this table.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: D.B. Woodside has left the building. No, he is still here.\par \par \uc2 He is known for his roles in the hit TV shows, "Lucifer," "Suits," and "24." In his TV show, it is called "The Night Agent." It is so good. He plays Secret Service agent, Eric Monks who is returning to the job after a long hiatus.\par \par \uc2 In one scene, he tells his team leader the truth about why he was away for so long after taking a bullet for the President.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Night Agent.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t really think he is an addict. D.B. Woodside joins us now at the table.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to see you, David Brian Woodside, because I bet your mom is watching since she lives in New York.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE ("The Night Agent" actor): My mom is probably watch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are so very glad to have you at the table.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Yes. She does not like the name D.B.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: You know, that`s a little problem.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, D.B. You know, listen, I plan to watch a couple of episodes, two or three. It is 10 episodes. I watched all 10 episodes because this show, this series is that good and you are knockout in it. So bravo.\par \par \uc2 It takes many twists and turns. That`s what I like about it.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you say of all the characters you played and you`ve played many, this one is closest to you, how?\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: It`s -- well, the role itself is just really kind of gritty and it is about someone who is battling this drug addiction after doing something really great.\par \par \uc2 When I was much younger I -- you know, explored that area and I think a lot of addicts sometimes, use to kind of numb the pain of things that they`re going through. But he has an amazing comeback backstory.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you did as well. You did as well.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: When I was much younger, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you were much younger.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you say that you`re an introvert.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Listen, how can you have this job and be an introvert?\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Play the roles you play.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: That surprises a lot of people, but it`s a wiring of the brain, you know? Yes, there are a lot of actors that are extroverts, but there are quite a few of us, that are introverts as well, which means we like to go to work, do our job and then when the job is over, we need to go home to recharge before we can go back.\par \par \uc2 So you`re not going to see us out. You don`t really see me out going places.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are not going to see you the parties.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: You`re not going to see me at the parties, no. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s basically turning on the light switch, right?\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When you go back to work, so you flick it on, you`re on and you do your job and people interact with you and you interact with people who think he is so great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you go home and you`re like lights out.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Exhausted.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Exhausted.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If you want to reach me, text.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Yes. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So to prepare for this role, you put out a call -- you play a Secret Service agent. You put out a call on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , I think it was.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . or on social media to meet with any past Secret Service agents. Did you actually meet with any? And what did you learn?\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: I talked to -- I mostly talked to two people that had served. I talked to one ex-Secret Service agent. I talked to a guy that was in the Rangers, Noah Pandia (sp?) and I talked to my friend, Aimee, who was on "Lucifer." Her boyfriend served as well.\par \par \uc2 And all three of those incredible men really taught me a lot and shared stories with me, and it really helps to get into the mindset of this guy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s fantastic.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Did you learn more about why, you know, to be a Secret Service agent, you have to take a bullet potentially for the person you`re protecting, that is the job description.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Yes. It`s actually not the person -- you`re not taking a bullet for the person, you`re taking a bullet for the institution.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: And when you start thinking about it that way, it`s very different.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hadn`t thought of it that way.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: So, you are there to protect the institution, not the individual.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`re still taking a bullet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: You are taking a bullet, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You still could end up dead like D-E-A-D, dead.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to go back to you being an introvert because I`m so fascinated by you, in terms of your idea of a perfect night in and how you view your job, and how much you still like your job. What`s your idea of a perfect night in for you?\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Oh my gosh. A perfect night in --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Asking for a friend.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Is having a glass of wine with a really good book or a great show that I haven`t had the chance to see, to kind of binge watch that and, you know, I`m fortunate enough to have a little gym at home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A gym at home.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Yes, I work out. Maybe have one friend over and have dinner. Go for a walk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I need to change chairs with Tony. One friend over.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle is basically, she`s got a little check book. She is like, "uh-ha."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Does it have a treadmill?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I got that. I got that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And an elliptical and a rowing machine?\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: It definitely has a treadmill.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, good.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Not a rowing machine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: And not an elliptical.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, well, we can work on that. And what else would we be doing? Just pretending here. You can use your acting skills at this moment.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: All right. Do you like walks?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I`ve got some tennis shoes.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: I like walks through neighborhoods because I like to look at different -- the different architecture of homes. I think it says a lot about an area.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you like dogs?\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: I love dogs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: Absolutely love dogs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too. Me, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you cook?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: I try.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Going to be ordering in. Okay.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: I try, but I want to learn.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re going to be ordering in. Yes.\par \par \uc2 D.B. WOODSIDE: . how to get better.\par \par \uc2 TONY DO