For parents.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So fun.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And I love, so the original one here. This was on Shark Tank by the way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: The original one looked like this, a little egg. Now, they have the bunny. They have the dinosaur.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. I love it.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So lots of really fun options. Take the stink and the mess out of decorating.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the price?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Which I love. Yes. So this is $17.99. It is 36 percent off and look at what we`ve done with these eggs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are very pretty. I love it.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: They`re gorgeous. Yes. It is so -- a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, what have got here at the end?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: All right, so this is such an important safety device. It`s called invisaWear.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: This is from a woman-owned business. We`re so excited. This is Rajia Abdelaziz and she came up with this device after she herself was followed by men in a van who were yelling at her.\par \par \uc2 This actually has on the back, a button. You push the button twice and it`s going to send an alert to five of your friends or family members.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you`re in trouble.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: If you`re in trouble.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got help.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You can -- whether you`re in trouble, whether you have a medical condition. So my daughter, my son, and I all have a medical condition where we faint and we feel it coming on. We push this twice, it alerts people and it also tells them where they are. It`s the GPS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is very handy.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very handy. Great products today.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: I love scrunchie, arm band.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: $174.99.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Twenty nine percent.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes, 28 percent off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a deal.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Elizabeth Werner, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We packed a lot in there and of course, we do partner with all of these companies to get these incredible deals. If you want to buy something you saw today, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com. Don`t even have to get up.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us actually. No, we`re going. Bye. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230321ej3l000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Protests Erupt In France\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Elaine Cobbe, Gayle King \par \uc2 2,448 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Parts of France came to a standstill as workers staged their biggest nationwide strike of the year so far, a year that is far from over.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, more than a million people took part in demonstrations against a proposed government plan to raise the retirement age from 62 to 64. The protests were largely peaceful, but there were clashes with riot police in some cities.\par \par \uc2 Elaine Cobbe is in Paris for us.\par \par \uc2 Elaine, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The controversy raging around Macron`s pension reforms continues in the Senate behind me, and on the streets where French workers were out in force yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE shouting in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE: More than a million people marched in towns and cities across France to say no to President Macron`s reforms that will see them working longer and retiring later.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE: This public servant says she has been working since she was 17 and was first due to retire at 60, then 62; now, it will be 64. She says it`s not fair to change the age in their career.\par \par \uc2 The protests drew workers from all walks of life, a reminder how the changes will affect everyone who isn`t already retired or about to retire this year.\par \par \uc2 The atmosphere at the Paris March ranged from lighthearted to aggressive.\par \par \uc2 There were some violent clashes in Paris on the sidelines of the marches with what police called radical elements that had nothing to do with the protests.\par \par \uc2 The unions say they are pleased with the turnout at the marches across the country and with the numbers who went on strike in sectors from transport to education to energy.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday`s nationwide strikes hit railroad and air transport hard, causing widespread delays and cancellations. It led to blockades of ports and oil refineries and closed power plants, a concern to people already hit by an ongoing energy crisis.\par \par \uc2 The unions say there are other ways to ensure there will be money to pay for today`s young people when they retire.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE: But many, like this woman say President Macron`s government doesn`t listen. "They talk down to us," she complained.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE: French women say they will lose more than most and protested again here today at the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The debate continues inside as outside, the unions promise to keep protesting until the reforms are withdrawn -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Elaine Cobbe reporting from Paris, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Vlad has the stories we think you`re going to be talking about today, but first it is 7:42, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." A little Harry Styles.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Loving this song. This is such a great song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. "Golden."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Golden," Harry Styles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s begin with a golden celebration. Joyful, but also tearful for the \uc2 Los Angeles Lakers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The team retired the jersey of one of its longtime stars, Paul Gasol in a halftime ceremony. His number, 16, will hang next to longtime teammate and close friend, Kobe Bryant`s number 24. Gasol and Bryant reached three \uc2 NBA\uc2  Finals together with the Lakers. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PAUL GASOL (Former \uc2 NBA\uc2  player): I can`t go on without talking about the person and the face that I don`t see. The brother that elevated me, inspired me, challenged me to be a better player, just to be a better man overall.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Kobe.")\par \par \uc2 PAUL GASOL: I miss him -- I miss him so much like many of us do. I love him. I wish he was here with Gigi. I really do.\par \par \uc2 But I think he`ll be proud and he was looking forward to this moment. So, I love you, brother.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Paul Gasol, ladies and gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 Kobe Bryant`s widow, Vanessa attended the ceremony in a rare public appearance. She hasn`t been seen at the Lakers Stadium since 2020.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Must be very hard for her to go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Incredibly difficult and she shared a message from her late husband. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VANESSA BRYANT (Kobe Bryant`s wife): Pablo, Kobe predicted you and he would be together in the rafters. Here`s a little video I want you to watch.\par \par \uc2 KOBE BRYANT (Former \uc2 NBA\uc2  player): There is no debate when how -- when he retires, he will have his number in the rafters next to mine. The reality is, I don`t win those championships without Paul. \uc2 NCAA\uc2  doesn`t have those two championships without Paul.\par \par \uc2 So, we know that, everybody knows that and I really look forward to the day when he is there giving his speech at center court in front of all the fans who have supported him over the years.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad, goosebumps. How lovely of her to come and how really generous of her to share that tape at this particular time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is so beautiful, but to know that Kobe wanted to be there this moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Of course.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We know he is there along with Gigi.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at that shot. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And those others who perished, but you know that Paul also named his daughter after Gianna. He named her Elisabet Gianna, after Kobe`s daughter, Gianna.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it wasn`t the ceremony he was thinking about when he was imagining that future, but it was still a beautiful moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It really was.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A memory.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Beautiful moment. Beautiful moment.\par \par \uc2 All right now to a proud moment at Paris Fashion Week, Zaya Wade made her debut yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Thomas Cristo/\uc2 Instagram\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The 15-year-old hit the catwalk for the New You Show, and you can guess who was in the front row?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Who can we guess was in the front row?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mom and dad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Her parents, Gabrielle Union and Dwyane Wade beaming with pride and looking very chic themselves in all black matching outfits.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She looks good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She looks great. Cheering her on.\par \par \uc2 One month ago, a Los Angeles Judge granted Zaya a legal name change and assignment of gender.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Zaya has that model expression down along with the walk.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She really kind of does, doesn`t she, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I always ask models, why do you guys do that? And they said because they don`t want to distract from the clothes. I go, but I`m so busy looking at your expression. No, no, because we want the focus to be on the clothes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, it`s a thing. I didn`t know it was a thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, it is a definite thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, she`s got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve got a movie called "Zoolander" for you. You should check that out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s our look.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s our documentary about the modeling world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The blue steel.\par \par \uc2 All right, this is a great story, too.\par \par \uc2 So we all know there`s been a winner of the record breaking Powerball lottery and get this, he seems to be living his best life ever. That`s my Robin Leach "Lifestyles of the Rich and Famous." You get that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did get that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You get that. Okay, good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wasn`t sure. Patti, did you get it? Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You guys didn`t get it?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Patti and I weren`t sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Come on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It sounded like it was from a cooking show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anybody in the studio -- Giselle didn`t get it. Klaus, did you get it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Giselle is too young.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ladyline, did you get it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Klaus, you got it, baby?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, he got it.\par \par \uc2 So, let me explain to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Edwin Castro from California claimed a $2 billion jackpot last month. He hasn`t appeared in public and we don`t know much about him.\par \par \uc2 Tony, before we did the story was like, I want to know about his life before he won this prize.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How old is he?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How old he is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got to know his age.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m like, who cares? He`s a billionaire now. It doesn`t matter.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to know what he is doing for a living like months ago.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I do, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So let me just show you what we found. Records show --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, that Edwin Castro bought this palatial $25.5 million mansion in one of the highest profile neighborhood in the Hollywood Hills.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go, Mr. Castro. I love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The home has, get this, more than 13,000 square feet of living space.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fantastic.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Five bedrooms, a pool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wine cellar, movie theater.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Spectacular views of LA and also the neighborhood, his neighbors, oh, Ariana Grande, Dakota Johnson, Jimmy Kimmel, as one does.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I say go Mr. Castro.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love this story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know if you gets that much money and you say you know I`m going to buy the house of my dreams.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, I`m all for it as well. I think Jimmy Kimmel is probably going to come by and knock on the door.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I envision he`s like the Count of Monte Cristo. He just appears and people are like, who is this dashing stranger who`s a billionaire now living in our neighbor?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know. He`s not quite a billionaire because he you know, you do have to pay taxes on lottery winnings, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. He actually got something like nine hundred plus million dollars. He took the lump sum.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He took the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He took the lump sum, right, he`s still going to have to pay about 25 percent at least to the IRS, so that`s $250 million. It`s going to be a sad day when that happens.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Downer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s still doing better than everybody in the room. So we say, go, Mr. Castro.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you. All of us combined.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly, right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is great. This is great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony is like, I`m still just thinking about the repercussions of all that money and the government.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a nice problem to have, right, okay?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still ahead, David Begnaud has got a story that is going to make you feel good today about the world around you, also feel a little bit hungry.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, David Begnaud has a story about using the power of social media for good. How about that? How one daughter inspired a community to help her family`s struggling business.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. A major league baseball team is making history with two women at the helm. You`re going to meet the \uc2 Miami Marlins\uc2 ` President and General Manager who are truly in a league of their own.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. In our series "Changing the Game," we talk with a woman who couldn`t find the right makeup so she started her own company in college.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like how she thinks.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE: Fast forward to ensure that justice is done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Two of the four Americans kidnapped in Mexico are dead. We spoke to the wife of one of the survivors.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: What did he tell you?\par \par \uc2 MICHELE WILLIAMS: He is just glad to be alive he is safe because two other people lost their lives.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A collision between two small planes in Florida kills four.\par \par \uc2 STEVE LESTER (Polk County Chief): There was an in-air collision, and both planes immediately went down -- went to water.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A lawsuit challenges a new abortion law in Texas claiming the exemption for medical emergencies is unclear.\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN: At this point in my life, it is just really terrifying to think about getting pregnant in Texas again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A new vaccine protects honeybees helping farmers who depend on them.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANNETTE KLEISER: Just like magic. You vaccinate the Queen and she passes it on to the millions of offspring that make the colony.\par \par \uc2 MAN: ... spins, needs help. It is an incredible shot.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the College of Charleston beats UNC Wilmington to claim a ticket to March Madness.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There it is. Charleston is going to the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  tournament. The Cougars will be dancing in 2023.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, Vlad, I think you`re right. It`s like nothing but underdogs --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. I`m loving it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We always like the Cinderella story. I like that queen story when she said you vaccinate the queen and she passes it on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is the queen, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are still talking about that Ben Tracy story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we like that.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230309ej380005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Extreme Weather Across the Country; Winter Storm Moves East; China Considering Sending Weapons to Russia; Lab Leak COVID Theory Gains Support; Deadly Migrant Shipwreck; Protecting East Palestine Residents; Alex Murdaugh`s Defense; Vision Loss Breakthrough; Debating the Four-Day Workweek; Star Power at \uc2 NAACP\uc2  Image Awards; Big Winners at the \uc2 SAG\uc2  Awards; One Year in Ukraine; Power of Acceptance; How to Protect Your Home; Baking a Change; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Omar Villafranca,  Stephanie Abrams, Nancy Cordes, Catherine Herridge, Nikki Battiste, Seth  Doane, Jon LaPook, Vladimir Duthiers, Charlie D`Agata, Scott MacFarlane \par \uc2 14,140 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Another round of storms threatens California, as powerful tornadoes carve a path of destruction to the Midwest. We will have the latest on that.\par \par \uc2 MAN: So like, it like felt like a bomb hit outside or something, the whole house kind of shifted and it got moved, and the next I know, it was gone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The CIA Director tells CBS News he is confident China`s government is considering sending weapons to Russia to use in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BURNS (CIA Director): That would be very risky and unwise bet.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Dozens of migrants are dead after their overloaded boat sank in rough seas off the southern coast of Italy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: New information from the Department of Energy suggests COVID-19 most likely originated in a lab.\par \par \uc2 REP. SETH MOULTON (D-MA): The Chinese have mishandled COVID Every step of the way."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Michelle Yeoh made history as the first Asian woman to win Best Actress in a movie category at the \uc2 SAG\uc2  Award.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Kyle Busch grabs his fifth win at Fontana in a record 19 consecutive cup seasons breaking a tie with Richard Petty.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Clark she fires and goes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A thrilling weekend of buzzer beating finishes in the world of basketball.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s good. Matthew Cleveland stuns Miami.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Arizona State down to the number seven Arizona, three quarter court.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Desmond Cambridge. Oh, he got it. He won it. Arizona State has done it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: My goodness.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Desmond Cambridge, Jr. The Sun Devils have taken down number seven.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How did he do that? Is that skill or is that a lucky shot?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think it is a little bit of both.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a lot of skill. He shot like it was a free throw.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All net.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Flawless.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Against your rival on their court, game winner.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love a good buzzer beater. It never gets old to me see people happy like that.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. We`re happy, too. We`ve got some great news to kick off this week.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to show you the first drug ever approved to treat one of the major causes of vision loss in this country. This is a very big deal. Dr. Jon LaPook is here to tell us how it works and whose lives it could make better.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Plus, from the moment you get home you start to get ads and promotions for products designed to keep your home safe. Well, we`re going to break down what actually works with the deputy editor of Consumer Reports.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But we began this morning with nasty weather all across the country. We`re talking about snow in California, a dust bowl in Texas and a record breaking tornado outbreak in Oklahoma that has left tens of thousands without power. That`s where Omar Villafranca is for us.\par \par \uc2 Omar, good morning. What are you seeing there?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 We`re getting a firsthand look at the power of this storm and we happen to find a little vein here, a little path of destruction. Check this out behind me. You`ve got a red \uc2 Jeep\uc2  that was tossed onto another car, it is right in front of a house. It`s missing most of its roof.\par \par \uc2 Now this storm last night, it was loud, it was moving fast and it was all part of a wild night of weather here on the South Plains.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Everybody okay in there?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Powerful tornadoes ripped across Oklahoma overnight, tearing roofs off homes and flipping over trucks.\par \par \uc2 MAN: All of a sudden like, it like felt like a bomb hit outside or something. The whole house kind of shifted.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: At least eight tornadoes touched down in the State, the highest ever recorded in the month of February.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And it got moved and the next thing I know, it was gone. It was done like three seconds flat.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: In the Oklahoma City area alone, several twisters toppled chimneys, crumpled siding and sent debris flying into the air.\par \par \uc2 Chelsea Riley said the wind shattered the window right next to her 10-year- old daughter`s bed.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA RILEY (Norman, Oklahoma resident): She threw her blanket over her head and she waited for me to come get her. Her brother came screaming to her rescue and I pushed them in the bathroom.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: She said she left them in the bathroom as she raced to find her two other children ages five and six. When the wind calmed down, she saw just how much damage there was.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA RILEY: There were shingles everywhere fence panels broken, we jumped in the car and tried to leave and as I`m trying to get out of my driveway, things are broken everywhere.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What in the world is that?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The dangerous system also slammed Kansas. At least two tornadoes were confirmed in the State and it brought golf ball sized hail to Texas.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You shouldn`t be on this weather.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: And spun up dust storms in the Panhandle turning the sky yellow.\par \par \uc2 It`s the same storm system that dumped historic rain and snow on the West Coast over the weekend. Hundreds of drivers were stranded on the icy and snowy roads as freeways came to a standstill.\par \par \uc2 In the Sierra, drivers are being warned to stay off the roads with near whiteout conditions expected over the next two days.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`ve already shoveled this multiple times.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: No one was killed here in Norman, but there are reports of several people that were injured. Right now, about 14,000 customers are without power here in Oklahoma, but once the sun comes up, we`ll get a better idea of the extent of this damage -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Omar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on where this weather is headed, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 We have more unsettled weather on both coasts. The eastern storm is actually the same system that made a mess out of California at the end of last week and the severe weather in Oklahoma. In the West, we can`t seem to catch a break. There are several more rounds of rain and snow as we head over the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start in the Great Lakes. Today, ice can coat trees, power lines, and roads and we actually could see some spotty power outages. Then on top of that, we could get up to five to eight inches of snow and it`s not just the Midwest. We will also see some much needed snow in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 Now almost everyone is below average and by a lot. Both New York City and Boston are down by about two feet. And by the way, this storm, it has it all. We could see gust up to 55 miles an hour. There`s also the threat for a few tornadoes from Illinois over into Ohio and it will take through the day today in the Great Lakes and then tomorrow the entire day, it will be into the Northeast, a messy morning commute and also pretty messy as we are heading back from work.\par \par \uc2 But then we look west, several waves of energy will keep us unsettled through Wednesday. Already Blizzard warnings into the Sierra gusts to 75 miles an hour feet of snow on top of the feet we just got and then of course rain at the lower elevations.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices. And Nate, it doesn`t end in the West with the stormy weather. What you see there will end up in the East by the end of this week, so it`s definitely something to keep an eye on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Stephanie, always with the information we need before we head out the door and hit the roads. We appreciate you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The head of the CIA tells CBS News that he is "confident" China is considering sending weapons to Russia for the Ukraine war.\par \par \uc2 William Burns spoke exclusively to "Face the Nation" about the latest Intelligence as President Biden warns of the potential major consequences if China takes that step.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House with more on this part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning. This could be a game changer not in a good way.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That`s right, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 The CIA Director told CBS News that the agency has begun to collect Intelligence that indicates that China is considering giving weapons to Russia.\par \par \uc2 Now, he didn`t share the source of that Intelligence, but he said that US officials are going public with it now in the hopes of deterring Chinese officials from doing it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BURNS: We remain seriously concerned should China provide lethal equipment to Russia.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: CIA Director, William Burns told "Face the Nation" moderator, Margaret Brennan that the US has not seen evidence that China has actually shipped any weapons to Russia yet.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BURNS: I think the Chinese are also trying to weigh the consequences.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: President Biden made it clear last week what would happen if the Chinese crossed that line.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We imposed severe sanctions on anyone who has done it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: As the war grinds on past the one year mark with no end in sight, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  and some allies are pushing the President to send new weapons including F-16 fighter jets.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN (White House National Security Adviser): The question of F- 16s is really a question for another day.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: White House officials from the President on down insist Ukraine doesn`t need F-16s.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: What they need right now are tanks and armored personnel carriers and infantry fighting vehicles, artillery and air defense systems.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Burns said the US does have to help the Ukrainians regain the upper hand because otherwise Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will drag out the war as long as he can.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BURNS: Putin`s view of Americans, of us, has been that we have attention deficit disorder and we`ll move on to some other issue eventually. And so Putin in many ways, I think believes today that he cannot win for a while, but he can`t afford to lose.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: While questions and concerns loom about China`s intentions, another world power is offering assistance to Ukraine. Saudi Arabia has agreed to provide $400 million worth of aid. This comes after the Kingdom`s Foreign Minister made a surprise trip to Kyiv yesterday -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And we have new information this morning on how the COVID-19 pandemic may have started. The Department of Energy says it has data that supports the theory that the COVID outbreak began with a laboratory leak in Wuhan, China.\par \par \uc2 Senior investigative correspondent, Catherine Herridge has been covering this story for us for years. Catherine, good morning. Other parts of the Intel Community have different conclusions. What are your sources telling you?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Well, good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Two sources familiar with the \uc2 US government\uc2 `s COVID-19 origins investigation tell CBS News, there is new reporting from the Department of Energy that supports the lab leak theory as plausible.\par \par \uc2 The sources confirmed the Department of Energy has low confidence in the finding first reported by "The Wall Street Journal," though it is not clear whether that reflects weak data or a limited amount of information.\par \par \uc2 While the new reporting is classified, the sources say they believe it may be separate from an earlier analysis by the Department of Energy`s Lawrence Livermore Labs, which is home to some of the government`s most qualified biologists. That assessment also pointed to a potential accidental lab leak in Wuhan, China.\par \par \uc2 While there is no consensus among US Intelligence officials about how the pandemic started, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and now the Energy Department had both found an accidental lab leak increasingly plausible.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US government\uc2  ultimately wants to nail down answers to not only how COVID-19 killed over a million Americans, but also how to prevent it from happening in the future -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and Catherine, there are huge political and diplomatic implications as well, which raises the question of why it is just so hard to know exactly where this pandemic started.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Well, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  has met several roadblocks because of the Chinese government`s persistent lack of cooperation. Nearly two years ago, a CBS News investigation spoke with current and former government officials, who said there was significant circumstantial evidence that supported the lab leak theory. There remains little evidence they said that the virus infected humans through animals -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So where does the investigation go from here?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Ohio Congressman Mike Turner told CBS News in December that Republicans on the House Intelligence Committee will issue subpoenas to the Intelligence Community to gather information about the origins of the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Still three years since the pandemic began, critics say no meaningful policy changes have been made to address potential future outbreaks -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. Got it for now. Catherine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to Italy in the latest on a deadly shipwreck at sea. The boat carrying up to 200 migrants broke apart yesterday near the tip of Italy. More than 60 children and adults were killed, at least 80 others were rescued. At least one person is charged with migrant trafficking.\par \par \uc2 Seth Doane has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: Wooden debris buffeted by rough seas washed up and was strewn across this beach in Southern Italy, evidence of the tragic end to this journey.\par \par \uc2 Following the shipwreck, some huddled in blankets, as first responders searched for survivors and collected the dead.\par \par \uc2 Migrants believed to be mostly from Afghanistan, Pakistan, and Iran set out from Turkey last week in a wooden boat said to be operated by traffickers. They were spotted by the \uc2 European Union\uc2 `s border agency over the weekend, but a patrol dispatched to intercept them turned back due to rough weather.\par \par \uc2 (POPE FRANCIS speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: While Pope Francis offered prayers Sunday, thanking rescuers and those who "give welcome."\par \par \uc2 The incident also whipped up political debate. Italy`s Prime Minister \uc2 Giorgia Meloni\uc2  expressed sorrow for lives