osing arguments in this dramatic trial on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: During nearly six hours of testimony on Friday, Alex Murdaugh denied murdering his wife and son.\par \par \uc2 ALEX MURDAUGH (Alleged to have killed wife and son): I can promise you, I would hurt myself before I would hurt one of them.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: But he did concede a history of lying about millions of dollars in financial fraud, his addiction to opioids, and even his whereabouts on the night of the murders, admitting he`d been at the crime scene before they happened, despite telling law enforcement otherwise.\par \par \uc2 ALEX MURDAUGH: Everything about me not going to the kennel was a lie.\par \par \uc2 CREIGHTON WATERS (Prosecutor): And you`re able to just do that so easily and so convincingly and so naturally, don`t you?\par \par \uc2 ALEX MURDAUGH: That`s not for me to judge.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: CBS News legal analyst, Rikki Klieman says Murdaugh`s decades of experience as a lawyer served him well on the witness stand going far beyond yes or no answers to prosecutor`s questions.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: He was about as experienced a witness, as I`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 CREIGHTON WATERS: That you hurt the ones you love the most, didn`t you?\par \par \uc2 ALEX MURDAUGH: And didn`t say that. You`re implying to this jury and to me, that was me talking about hurting Maggie and Paul and that`s not what that was.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The prosecution says Murdaugh`s alleged motive to kill was to distract from his financial crimes. That defense has portrayed him as a family man who would never do such a thing.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: The defense just has to poke holes in the case in order to raise reasonable doubt and all they need is one juror.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Klieman told us that in criminal cases, the prosecution does not have to prove motive, but she says in this case, the prosecution`s theory may be difficult for the jurors to believe. The defense attorneys say they will call four witnesses today before resting their case -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nikki, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is such a fascinating case, Nate. However this turns out, I really want to hear what the jurors are thinking, what they believed, because they said during his testimony that some of the jurors were crying.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They were actually crying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were actually crying. That`s never a good sign for prosecutors...\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: ... is what I`ve been told. It will be interesting to see what they believe and who they believe in this particular case.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The defense only needs one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right about that.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a new treatment for a type of blindness that affects at least one million of us. But first, it is 7:17. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up: There is new interests in the four-day workweek. Remember how people here showed us how much they liked the idea. I don`t know anybody that doesn`t like the idea.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: New interest.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We can`t do it --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: New interest.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A whole new interest. Please let it happen. Plus -- we can`t do it in the news business though.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a movie that`s become an Oscar favorite after a big haul at the Screen Actors Guild Awards last night.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is two minutes 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 MAN: LB on countdown one. Hold, hold, hold. We are standing down to do a T-tap ground issue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Standing down inside three minutes of launch, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and \uc2 NASA\uc2  scrubbed a rocket launch to the International Space Station overnight.\par \par \uc2 The Launch Director stopped the countdown as you just heard to investigate a possible issue with the ignition of the Falcon 9 rocket engines. Onboard two Americans, one Russian, and an astronaut from the United Arab Emirates. They were ready to start a six-month mission.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  now says the next attempt will be early Thursday morning if everything checks out. Meanwhile, there are four other astronauts waiting on the space station for their ride home.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are going to be a little delayed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I`d be totally freaked out, Tony. You`re ready to go. Just psychologically you`re ready.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then then it`s -- stop.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in our game changing series, a bakery owner talks with us about being Black in the world of fine dining.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It`s a perfect song for this segment.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  recently approved the first drug to treat a leading cause of vision loss affecting at least one million older Americans.\par \par \uc2 CBS News chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jon LaPook is here to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Jon, this is very exciting news.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: It certainly is, Gayle and good morning.\par \par \uc2 If you or someone you know is getting older, you`ll want to pay attention. The condition is called macular degeneration and it affects a person`s central vision.\par \par \uc2 You can go from this where there`s clearly a spot forming in the middle of the visual field to this, where this progressive blurriness involving the entire field. Now there can be total loss of vision and it can happen starting around age 50.\par \par \uc2 The new drug, an injection called Syfovre, doesn`t reverse that loss of vision, but it can slow it down.\par \par \uc2 Seven years ago, Bob Norman started having trouble reading. Tests revealed he had the advanced form of this problem in both eyes. He joined the clinical trial to test the drug on his right eye. We spoke with him and an eye doctor who is a retinal specialist who helped oversee this study.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BOB NORMAN (Macular degeneration patient): The doctor basically said, there is nothing we can do, you`re going to go blind, eventually. I`ve had to give up tennis, I`ve had to give up bowling. It has killed my retirement plans.\par \par \uc2 The right eye, yes, it is stable at the point where I can get down pretty far on that eye chart. So you know, the left eye has got to be treated, or that`s going to go blind.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: What do you think of this treatment? And how big of a deal is this?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s a huge deal. This would be the first available treatment to slow disease progression for this area of huge unmet clinical need. It would represent a game changer, a beginning of a new treatment era for these patients with geographic atrophy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Doc, so how do you know whether the blurry vision could become worse?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: You`ve got blurry vision, you go to an eye doctor. I mean, that`s pure and simple. People should be getting eye doctor examinations routinely anyway. And increasingly, as you get older, certainly by 60 or 65, you should be going once or twice a year, that`s what the societies recommend, but they`ll do that special exam, a dilated exam where the pupil goes open and you have to wear the sunglasses afterwards. They can really get a great look at the retina, because it`s not something that you just got to know intuitively.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So a dumb question, the eye doctor. Is that the person where you get your prescription? Or is it an ophthalmologist? Big fancy word?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Yes, it is an ophthalmologist and if you --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are one and the same?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: An optician is the person who makes, you know -- who may - - can look at your eyes or he or she can look at your eyes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So I go to \uc2 Warby Parker\uc2 , they do eye exams there. Is that the same?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Right. Well, you know, it needs to be a dilated eye exam. I mean, lots of different people say they can do it right. What you ultimately want to get to is a retinal specialist, because this is not something where just anybody can do it. There are special tests.\par \par \uc2 You`re looking to say, ah, there`s that geographic atrophy, that advanced form. And it affects, you know, more than a million Americans.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Explain, Jon, why this is such a big deal and who is eligible? Because this has never happened before.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: It`s never happened before. People have heard the term macular degeneration.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: There is the wet form and the dry form. This is -- we`re talking about the dry form. The wet form, which is about 15 percent of the people, there`s a good treatment for it for many, many years. This dry form, there`s been nothing.\par \par \uc2 So this is a big advance in terms of --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re saying it`s a game changer that --\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Yes, finally, I think -- you know, and we`re optimistic, right? You`re slowing the vision loss, you`re not reversing it. And I spoke to people -- a couple of eye doctors who are very excited about it.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to Dr. Steven Schwartz, who is a retinal specialist at the Doris Stein Institute at UCLA and 10 years ago, I did a study with him. He is using stem cells to try to not just slow the loss, but actually reverse the vision loss and have people regain, restore vision.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is still an experimental stage, but I think people are looking forward, not only with this drug to be able to use it in people with the advanced form. Also maybe over time, when I spoke to Dr. Ladd (sp?) about it, she thought, well, maybe the next step would be to have people who have intermediate loss to see if it helps them, and then really just early loss of to see if that helps. You want to try to prevent the loss.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s your definition of older American because mine keeps changing? Used to be 50?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: It`s 10 years older than whatever age I am.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is this it really in this particular --\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Yes, So I think, you know, I looked at the curve. You know, it starts like even in the 40s, the curve starts going up, 50 starts going up more and then by the time you get to 60 and 70, it is really going up.\par \par \uc2 So I think, when you hit 40, you should get a baseline exam. You hit 50, certainly, you should be getting a few in between there, but I think by 50, you should really be getting them regularly.\par \par \uc2 And I looked it up last night the societies say by sixty, sixty-five, you should be getting it every year or two. Don`t wait to get the symptoms because you may not have the symptoms.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Get the good exam.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, let`s remain optimistic. Just for the --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Jon LaPook, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re headed out the door, you don`t want to miss the show, catch the top interviews and pieces from this morning on the podcast. Just search for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS On-The-Go on your favorite podcast app or scan the QR code on your screen right now, which means we can go wherever you go.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a new push that might make it easier for you to take a four-day workweek. How about that?\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS. Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll go. I`d like to be a four-day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, you`re not going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that what they have right there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I`d like to go four-day workweeks.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Working less for a living. Maybe.\par \par \uc2 We told you last week about how a pilot program in the UK testing a four- day workweek was so successful, that nine out of 10 companies involved said they would make it permanent.\par \par \uc2 So it raises an obvious question here in these United States: Could it work here?\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane reports now on the growing interest in State Legislatures and even Congress to give employers the chance to find out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: One fewer day each week of the alarm clock and the morning commute, John Byrne says once his software company in Baltimore instituted a 32-hour work week, the productivity of his 37 employees increased, and so did profits.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BYRNE (\uc2 Tricerat, Inc\uc2 . President and CEO): We`ve asked the employees to ruthlessly look at their work, get rid of extraneous meetings, extraneous phone calls, paperwork, things of this nature and reduce down the amount of wasted work.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The COVID stay-at-home orders gave millions of workers their first taste of flexible work arrangements and some employers discovered less can be more.\par \par \uc2 Are you drawing people like you and me? Or are you drawing younger employees?\par \par \uc2 JOHN BYRNE: Definitely younger employees. We`ve had a lot of folks coming right out of college that are like this is where we want to work.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A CBS News Review finds at least a half dozen States to varying degrees are now considering legislation to make four-day workweeks more common, including Maryland where a new legislation would offer companies that institute shorter workweeks a tax break.\par \par \uc2 Maryland delegate, Vaughn Stewart is one of the bill`s sponsors.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN STEWART (Maryland delegate): We`re expecting that workers can be at least as productive in a 32-hour week as they are in a 40-hour week.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: It`s a pretty provocative argument.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN STEWART: Sure, but we`re thinking that with more hours of rest, workers are going to be able to function better.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Employees in four-day a week studies have reported less stress and less burnout and better physical health. But advocates like Boston College Professor, Juliet Schor say the idea might require prodding from government.\par \par \uc2 JULIET SCHOR (Boston College professor): Historically, work time reduction has always involve government. We put in or at least you know, in the last 200 years or so, we put in a 40-hour week in the United States in the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: New legislation in New York, California, and in the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  would require companies that work employees more than 32 hours a week to pay overtime.\par \par \uc2 Similar proposals have failed in the past. California`s Chamber of Commerce called it a job killer not suited for all employers. Even supporters of the four-day workweek acknowledge it is not for everyone.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN STEWART: Absolutely. We don`t think this is something that every single industry and every single business can do, but that`s what we want to study.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: I think it`s important to note, Nate, this might not apply to people in television. But the supporters of that Maryland bill say they want it to be a range of employees impacted -- white collar, blue collar -- to reflect a broad spectrum of employees.\par \par \uc2 But Nate, we`ve got to cut you back from the seven-day week you`re working right now.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Well, Scott, you are just as much as me, but you know what, we love working seven days a week here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 , right, Scott?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: If no other reason we get to see Gayle more, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is true.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And speaking of Gayle, how do you feel about it?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know anybody that wants to work seven days, but when you look at the benefits of the four-day week, I don`t see why more people wouldn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, but parents with young children are like, does it mean I have to take care of my two-year-old and four-year-old another day of the week? No. I mean, I don`t want it then. I want them in school. I want the sitter working five days.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: True. No doubt about it. We`ll see though. We`ll see what happens from State to State.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it. I like it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you`ll be talking about today. But first, it`s 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." An all Awards "What to Watch," Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is an all Awards "What to Watch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Big night last night.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There is a lot to talk about what 