e Angela Bassett at the end. She looked - - I mean, she just keeps knocking it out of the park.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is so regal. Now the Oscars are coming up. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody is cheering Angela Bassett.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Can`t contain myself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, me, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s talk about the \uc2 SAG\uc2  Awards.\par \par \uc2 An emotional night at the Screen Actors Guild Award in Los Angeles. The big winner, "Everything, Everywhere All at Once."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Netflix\uc2 /\uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It got the top award for outstanding cast. The star of the movie, the incredible iconic, Michelle Yeoh was chosen as Best Actress, making her the first Asian woman to win a leading film acting award from \uc2 SAG\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE YEOH (Actor): Thank you for giving me a seat at the table because so many of us need this. We want to be seen, we you want to be heard. And tonight, you have shown us that it is possible.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is so wonderful. Also making history, Ke Huy Quan, who became the first Asian to win Best Male Supporting Actor in a film at the \uc2 SAG\uc2  Awards.\par \par \uc2 Have you -- did you see Tracy Smith`s profile of Ke Huy Quan on Sunday morning?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes, I did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was so wonderful and his wife telling him if you can see it, if you can dream it, it can happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It can happen.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And it has happened.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He`s been -- I mean, since he was a little kid, I`ve loved this actor, and one of the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was in "Goonies" right? He was in "Goonies"?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was in the "Goonies," but he was also in "Indiana Jones: Temple of Doom." Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was his breakout.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Breakout role. Such a wonderful night for him. And one of the big surprises of the night was cast mate, Jamie Lee Curtis who beat out Angela Bassett for Best Supporting Female Actor.\par \par \uc2 Another big winner, Brendan Fraser got Best Male Actor for "The Whale." Everybody should see that movie. It`s a wonderful film and acting legend, Sally Field received the Lifetime Achievement Award.\par \par \uc2 Guys, Sally Field has been in our lives. I used to come home from school and watch "The Flying Nun" and "Gidget." And then like all the movies that she`s done, and she`s still a big icon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And still doing great, Sally Field.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wonderful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wonderful. Congratulations, everyone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A night of awards. Appreciate you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *********************************(08:00AM,EDT)***************************** ****\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, one year on the ground in Ukraine. Hear the emotional stories from Charlie D`Agata and his team, including the harrowing first night of the war.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`m Tony Dokoupil. All kinds of technology will claim that they`ll keep your home safe, but which of them actually works. We`re going to get advice from one of my favorites, Consumer Reports.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. For today`s "Changing the Game," we talk to the founder of a bakery that specializes in blondies, and Gayle, guess what? She bought some samples.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, my hips say no, but everything else says yes, please. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 MAN: Everybody okay in there?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Severe weather hammers Oklahoma as a massive storm sweeps across the country.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA RILEY: My daughter covered her head and her back window busted out behind her head.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BURNS: I think the Chinese are also trying to weigh the consequences.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The CIA Director tells CBS News, the US believes China is considering providing weapons to Russia for its war on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: New information from the Department of Energy adds to the debate over the origins of COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: Right now, there is not a definitive answer that has emerged from the Intelligence Community on this question.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Everything, Everywhere All at Once.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And actor Ke Huy Quan gives a very emotional speech after becoming the first Asian man to win a SAG Award as Best Supporting Actor in a film.\par \par \uc2 KE HUY QUAN (Actor): To all those at home who are watching who are struggling and waiting to be seen, please keep on going because the spotlight will one day find you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s so beautifully said, and the spotlight has certainly found him. Everybody is pulling for him Oscar night, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Motivation Monday for all of us with that speech.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is such a great storyline.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with one more look back at Russia`s invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It was just over a year ago, if you can believe that, one year ago, and it comes from the \uc2 CBS\uc2  news crew that was in Ukraine`s capital on that very first night of the war. We had senior foreign correspondent, Charlie D`Agata; our producer, Justine Redman, and camera operator Abdi Cadani. They show us how that first night unfolded.\par \par \uc2 Now, Vladimir Putin`s decision to invade has changed their lives and the lives of millions of others.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ABDI CADANI (Camera): We didn`t believe that can happen. I didn`t believe it was going to happen. I didn`t think he would start a war.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The night before the invasion, I just had a hunch, and I strolled down to the cocktail bar at the hotel we were staying and there were a bunch of journalists talking to one another saying, "Is it going to happen? Isn`t it going to happen?" And there was literally a piano player playing.\par \par \uc2 And I told Justine to come down and join me in a drink because I have a pretty good feeling that this might be the last time we have a drink at this hotel bar for a long time.\par \par \uc2 JUSTINE REDMAN (Producer): And at this point, it`s about 2:00 AM and we knew that this could be our last chance to get any sleep for who knows how long. So, everyone went to bed. And about half an hour later, my phone rings.\par \par \uc2 So I had to go and wake everyone up again. I go, "All right, come on. Everyone up. Everyone ready."\par \par \uc2 ABDI CADANI: And I was like, "Oh my God, this could actually happen." And to be honest with you, it was terrifying.\par \par \uc2 JUSTINE REDMAN: Way off in the distance, I heard a series of booms, and I`ve been in enough conflict zones to know what that sound is, saying, "Okay, that`s it. Here we go."\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: What we didn`t realize and really nobody did was the scale of the invasion. Was it going to be an attack and assault from the east? Was it going to be massive airstrikes? Is it going to be cruise missiles?\par \par \uc2 And as it turned out, it was all of those things.\par \par \uc2 JUSTINE REDMAN: Was I scared? There was one night when we were just finishing up our live shots. And Charlie is standing in front of the camera.\par \par \uc2 What happened?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: What the hell was that?\par \par \uc2 JUSTINE REDMAN: And suddenly, there is this massive flash of light like nothing we`ve ever seen before and it was silent, or at least it was until about a good, I don`t know, 10 seconds later or something when there was this boom.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Now we have training when something like that happens, you get away from the windows and balcony you get low and you get away from whatever that was.\par \par \uc2 None of that training kicked in. So I decided to turn around and get a closer look at what had just happened.\par \par \uc2 JUSTINE REDMAN: Trying to see if there was something else going on that would make sense of this.\par \par \uc2 And I said, it is probably nuclear. And we sort of laughed. I was like, but actually, I`m not even kidding.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: It was scary at the time and we can laugh about it now, because nobody was killed.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 JUSTINE REDMAN: On the day the war started, Zelenskyy made a statement that the military was going to start issuing small arms to all veterans and all volunteers. And we saw hundreds, hundreds of people, mostly men, streaming into the center, and walking out with guns, and they didn`t have any training.\par \par \uc2 It`s not like they had marching orders, they were just getting guns. And we looked at each other and like, "This is about to turn into the Wild West."\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: At the same time, we`re keeping our eye on the emergency exit, because if this is going to get really bad, we need to find out where we can get to, and when we`re going to jump. And we had those conversations.\par \par \uc2 ABDI CADANI: How is it different? The consequences of this war, I felt like they could go much further, you know. It could go as far as Europe. It could go beyond, there could be a nuclear war. And so I really felt like we were kind of on the cusp of something huge that could reshape the world.\par \par \uc2 JUSTINE REDMAN: I don`t think it`s changed me as such, but it`s been another ongoing reminder of how precious life is. We`ve seen so many people with absolutely tragic stories of loss.\par \par \uc2 You know, it is hard to see young children go through these things and not, as a parent think of your own children and just how heart wrenching it is.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: When you witness that repeatedly, it does have an impact on your life. It has an impact on your family, it has an impact on everything.\par \par \uc2 War is a terrible, terrible thing.\par \par \uc2 ABDI CADANI: You come away from this always feeling like you`ve come this close to death, and so you kind of come away feeling more grateful to be alive.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I think it`s that kind of coming to terms with your own mortality again, and again, and again, and feeling like you`ve got away with it that, you know, is a hallmark of covering stories like this.\par \par \uc2 And then you know, we walk away when the people -- you know, the victims come.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so interesting to hear their perspective. I think, Charlie sums it up nicely when he says war is a terrible, terrible, terrible thing, and it affects so many people on so many different levels. It`s interesting to hear their perspective as it is beginning that very first night.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. It`s also just a reminder of the number of teams we have in place all over the world in difficult situations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who are in danger.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And also, you know, I thank our viewers for paying the attention to this story that we`ve wanted to give it because only because of their interests that we`re able to tell these stories.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s exactly what I was thinking. That`s spot on.\par \par \uc2 You know, we have and we`ll always be willing to leave the comfort of the studio to cover a story. You guys have done it over the course of your careers, for a long time. But this is where we find ourselves on a daily basis. Those teams we have all around the world do a job that is not easy, and oftentimes dangerous.\par \par \uc2 So I`d like to speak on behalf of all of us, if I can, and just say thank you to those teams that give us the stories that we need to hear on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And a year later, it is still a story with no end in sight, although we heard President Zelenskyy say it will end in 2023. I hope he is right about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We can only hope.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, powerful words from famous parents of a transgender teen. We will talk to an activist about their call to protect a community that`s been threatened too many times.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:09. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up in just a moment, new ideas for home security, whether you`re renting or you own. Consumer Reports will show us how to keep intruders out, find the best locks for your doors, and get the best deals on insurance just in case something goes wrong.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In a powerful acceptance speech at Saturday`s NAACP Image Awards, Gabrielle Union-Wade and her husband \uc2 Dwyane Wade\uc2  called for support for the Black LGBTQ community.\par \par \uc2 The actor and retired \uc2 NBA\uc2  star shared their message while receiving the President`s Award for Social Justice Work.\par \par \uc2 Wade dedicated the award to their 15-year-old daughter, Zaya, who came out as transgender in 2020. Just days ago, Zaya was granted the legal right to change her name and gender.\par \par \uc2 Union pushed for a new era of activism to protect that Black LGBTQ community, particularly transgender people.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION-WADE: Will we fight for some or we fight for all of our people? Black trans people are being targeted, terrorized, and hunted in this country, every day, everywhere.\par \par \uc2 DWYANE WADE: Zaya, as your father, all I`ve wanted to do was get it right. I`ve sat back and watched how gracefully you`ve taken on the public scrutiny, and even though it`s not easy, I watched you walk out of that house every morning as yourself.\par \par \uc2 I admire how you`ve handled the ignorance in our world. I admire you.\par \par \uc2 You`ve made me a better human just simply by being who you were born to be.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With us now is Raquel Willis, an author and activist dedicated to black transgender advocacy.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, how are you doing?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS (Author and activist): Good morning. Great. Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. We`re so happy you`re here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what did you make of Gabrielle and Dwyane`s speech and the messages behind it?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: I felt very touched. It felt like something I`ve been waiting to hear for a long time and I think a lot of Black trans people have been waiting to see, you know, our leaders step up and share the importance of accepting us as who we are and allowing us to be the drivers of our own destinies.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gabrielle called it a new era of activism. So where were we before and where are we going?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Well, you know, I think it`s interesting. I don`t quite know that it is a new era in that way. I think, you know, Black trans people have always been here. We go all the way back to the Stonewall Riots of 1969. That was such a seminal moment in our community.\par \par \uc2 Marsha P. Johnson, so many folks from there, so it is not new in terms of people being trans. We`ve always been here. I think what`s new is the visibility is new and there are more discussions than ever before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are talking about it. Share a little bit of your backstory, Raquel? When did you know as a little person growing up that you felt different or believed that you were different?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: I knew I was different very early, but I would say around nine is when I started kind of praying to God, because I grew up in a very Catholic home, that I would just wake up and be a girl and it would all make sense.\par \par \uc2 And so there was all of that happening and bullying happening along the way, but the only language I had was that I was gay. And so there wasn`t this kind of distinction between gender and sexual orientation, which I now know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did your parents tried to get counseling for you? What did they do?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Well, when I came out to my mom, as gay, initially, she did try and find a counselor, but the counselor wasn`t equipped. And I think a lot of parents and adults in general are not equipped. You know, there are stats that say that there are only about 30 or so percent of Black Americans in particular, who say they know a trans person.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: So I think that even in the Black community, we have to think about that. But in general, you know, only about around 40 percent of the general public know of a trans person.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So let`s think about what Gabrielle and Dwyane are doing, how important it is that they`re speaking the way they`re speaking. What is the message that people need to hear? What do people need to know?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Well, I think what people need to hear is that we have to be humble in our approach to guiding folks and protecting and defending young people. We have to understand that we don`t have all the answers. I don`t even have all the answers for the younger trans folks who are coming up today.\par \par \uc2 But I think we are open and we listen actively and we love with our whole hearts and our whole souls and I think that`s what the Wade-Unions are trying to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and they are a very public, popular, famous couple and they are very supportive. It seems like they`re very intentional with their support. So what is your message for those who might not have that support, kids that are searching for not only their own identity, but for someone to help validate who they are?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Well, I want young folks who feel misunderstood or isolated to hold on. I know that it is difficult. I know you feel like the world is against you and some time, that is actually true. But there are so many people out here who want you to have the fullest life possible.\par \par \uc2 And I think especially for young trans youth, know that whatever hate you hear, there is so much love that washes over that and people are fighting for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`ve also said that -- I`ve heard you say that this work is hard, because even if supporters speak up, then they get criticized. True?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Absolutely. I think we -- and we see it every day. We`re seeing at least 300 or more pieces of legislation moving around the country targeting LGBTQ+ folks. We`re seeing these curriculum bans and so much more and it is a really difficult time and we need folks who know what`s right and know to show up for people and love and support to speak up and that`s what the Wade-Unions were doing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you feel like things are getting better? Or because of the politicization of all this, it is getting worse?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: I think in some ways, things are getting better. It is a very nuanced answer for me. I think, it is better in terms of people have more access to education and understanding themselves than ever before.\par \par \uc2 We have more and more Black trans led organizations around the country moving and fighting on so many different fronts. But it is difficult, and it`s a time where we`re seeing the rise of fascism, of course, the rise of disinformation and so much more against so many communities on the margins and trans folks are in that group of marginalized folks.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Raquel Willis, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are out here speaking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are out here speaking and spreading the message.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, the pastry master who left the fine dining world and made her mark making blondies. She is part of a series we`re calling "Changing the game." Those are blondies by the way.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS deals, we will show you something that might help you keep track of things in your car. The Drop Stop creates a barrier that prevents things from falling between the front seats of your car and the center console -- your phone, your keys, you name it.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to our exclusive offer, it is available at more than 35 percent off the retail price.\par \par \uc2 If you`d like to start shopping for this and other products, you can scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll never lose another fry again, Tony.\par \par \uc2 All right coming up, new gadgets to protect your home or just keep an eye on visitors.\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk with Brian Vines who is in our progressive greenroom with Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There he is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be right back after your local news.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We are already talking about Nate`s "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is "Talk of the Table" or around the table and we`re talking and Nate, we`re going to put you on pause. Gayle, you`re going to go first.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. My "Talk of the Table" is Olivia Newton-John being remembered in her beloved Australia. The singer and actor died in August after a long battle with cancer. Fans and family and friends gathered for a state memorial service in Melbourne yesterday. Her husband, John and her daughter, Chloe fought back tears is a remembered the "Grease" and "Xanadu" superstar.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN EASTERLING\uc2  (Olivia Newton-John`s husband): Every day with Olivia with supernatural. Every day with Olivia was a bit of magic.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE ROSE LATTANZI (Olivia Newton-John`s daughter): I know she`s here standing beside me and within me. I can hear her voice, "You`re safe, my darling. Don`t be afraid. You are so much stronger than you think. Speak from your heart. That`s all you have to do."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The service also included musical performances and video tributes from many famous friends. It was streamed live, it`s available until Sunday.\par \par \uc2 I remember you know she died back in August. She was 73. She had been battling cancer on and off for 30 years.\par \par \uc2 I love when her husband described it as every day with her was magical. What a nice way for people to remember you to think every day.\par \par \uc2 Her daughter also said this, "I love how she loved so fully and completely."\par \par \uc2 And even though you knew it was a possibility, it was still a surprise when you heard that Olivia Newton-John had passed away. She`s such a sweet, sweet, sweet person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sweet person. An icon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so my "Talk of the Table" as Tony mentioned earlier are the top 10 words that Americans can`t pronounce.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Word wizards at the website unscrambled words, analyzed 12 months of \uc2 Google\uc2  pronunciation searches. So I want to throw up a couple of words and see if two of the smartest people I know can knock it out. So Tony, you want to go first?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What have we got here? Let`s pop something up for Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Word number one. Let`s see what we`ve got. I cannot do that one. The one at the bottom uh-uh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Acai.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Acai.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s the top. I can do --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What about the second one. That`s the --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to gyro.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have heard gyro and gyro.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gyro, okay. All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Omicron is an easy word.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Omicron, we know that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This next --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A lot of Black folks, go, omarion, which is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Omicron, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What about --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Charcuterie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Gayle. And then this is a Vietnamese last name.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nguyen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nguyen. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. So those are just some of the words and then --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gnocchi.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Worcestershire.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re a professional talker on TV.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What about the cryptocurrency?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, that`s Dogecoin or something.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dogecoin.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s sauce. I can`t -- Worchester?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Worcestershire.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Worcestershire.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And then the last one was pho, right? Even though it looks like pho.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I always say pho.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I always pho.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So the lesson is, if you don`t say something, just \uc2 Google\uc2  it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, the lesson is don`t say it however I`m saying it because I`m always saying it wrong.\par \par \uc2 All right, may "Talk of the Table" is a man who fought off boredom by going to Disneyland. It started back in 2012 and all of those trips, they added up eight years and change, and it landed him in the Guinness Book of World Records.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What`s the number?\par \par \uc2 JEFF REITZ (Huntington Beach resident): Today is the final day on day 2,195.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He went every day for eight years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s wrong with him, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Eight years, three months, and 13 days. He was only stopped in fact by the park shutting down because of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And now you have to get reservation, so it`s unlikely anyone will ever surpass this 2,900-plus day visit, five days short of 3000 which was his initial goal. What happened was -- this is Jeff Reitz. He was unemployed in 2012.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Somebody had given him a season pass. He was trying to lift his own spirits, his friends were trying to keep him up and he started going, started loving it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And started loving IT.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Started getting recognized for it and kept it moving to the point where when he finally did get a job, sometimes he took days off of work just to make it to the park.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just to go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Make it to the park.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, \uc2 Disney\uc2  will lift your spirits.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I will agree with that. But eight years every day at \uc2 Disney\uc2  seems like a lot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Did he get a Golden Pass?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, what does he have?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So he, for a long time, he had a season pass. He no longer does. But I think at this point --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Disney\uc2  have got to do something.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I think --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do the right thing, \uc2 Disney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I think so. Yes, I think so. He kept --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He should get something for life. He actually should.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He needs a statue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He clearly is a kid at heart, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Right there in the front, just a golden statue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. Moving on for now, whether you own your home or rent your home, you may be looking for better ways to protect it.\par \par \uc2 A list of potential threats is long extreme weather, break-ins, even porch pirates who steal packages and they`re not only dangerous, they can cost you a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 Consumer Reports has put out a new list of ideas, ideas that they hope will help you out on all of this. Testers have identified the very best protection devices, security systems, video, doorbells, indoor security cameras, and even sturdier and more secure door locks.\par \par \uc2 If you don`t want all that technology around, you want to do it the old- fashioned way. Consumer Reports deputy editor, Brian Vines is here.\par \par \uc2 Brian, this is a well-timed segment because just last night at 11:30, we heard a noise in the house. I got the elbow in bed. I had to get up, baseball bat over the shoulders. Tighty whities on below, walking through to every room, got to look everywhere. Give the report where to get back home.\par \par \uc2 If we had a better security system, maybe I wouldn`t have to get out of bed.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES (Consumer Reports deputy editor): Welcome to Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you have a security system.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We do, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You saying "Welcome to Brooklyn."\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: Yes, that`s old school. Listen, I sleep with a bat under the bed, not for nothing, but it`s the thing. But security systems can be your eyes and ears. So that 11:30 nudge can resolve with a quick scroll of the smartphone. But also, you know, just being better aware.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So where do people begin?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And how hard is it?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: It is not very hard. One of the things that I suggest is beginning with your most risk averse friends, if you have that sort of, I would say, "Oh, this is potential problem. This is a potential problem."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: But we ranked many security systems and for those who have a DIY spirit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: And want to avoid that about a dollar per square foot that it costs to install a professional system.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: There are some very good do-it-yourself systems and we ranked them. The security system from \uc2 SimpliSafe\uc2  for about $165.00, the essentials kit offers a great level of protection. And these things typically have that big brain console that is the sort of mothership of the whole thing, and comes with some sensors for your windows as well as motion detectors and a camera, so you can set it up yourself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you think it`s easy to install, Brian?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: I personally own this system, and it was easy to set up. There`s some trial and error, I like to say it`s choose your own adventure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: You can put it in this corner, make sure that the motion sensor has a very wide field. So corners are great to get the widest fields. Also camera set up, when you`re setting up the camera, put it into place and test it throughout the day, when the sun comes in, is it going to wash out a part that you would like to see secure, your visual.\par \par \uc2 So live with your system and make it custom. It`s a very democratized way of looking at security now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So as far as where to put the camera, you`re saying just play around with it a little bit before you have a permanent location.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: Absolutely. And that`s the great thing because nothing is settled until you settle it so you get the base system, the keypad, but the sensors for motion can go anywhere. They are portable.\par \par \uc2 So you can put them, we recommend near a point of entry, your front door.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, front door, of course.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: The stairs, in a room where you keep your treasures, so where your valuables are, but it is a system that lives with you and it can adapt to your needs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what you`re describing sounds portable. I`m from the door opens, it talks to me. I do the keypad, wired to something. I want to make it very difficult for anybody who is trying to get in. You`re saying that the portable ones, the DIY ones are just as effective.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: They can be just as effective.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you can carry it around with you.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: And you know, we`re very much a mobile people on the move right now and if you want something that`s portable, you can take it with you instead of investing that $1.00 per square foot in your 2,000 square foot house, 2,000 bucks for a system that you leave behind when you move.\par \par \uc2 So you can take $462.00 for the \uc2 SimpliSafe\uc2  system.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That makes sense.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: You can take it with you and work it in an apartment or in a dorm or wherever you may be. That is an advantage.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It can also be costly to lose packages that get delivered when they get left outside your door. So you might want to doorbell camera to at least see who is coming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Those video doorbells. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, what do you what do you recommend on that front?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: We have three that are recommended and we based it on the image quality on how quickly the system alerts your smartphone when someone is there, and also their privacy ratings.\par \par \uc2 So the top three that we`re recommending are represented here from RING`s video three doorbell to the \uc2 SimpliSafe\uc2  to the TP-Link.\par \par \uc2 Now one thing that I love is this price spread, right? The $199.00 RING doorbell happens to be Amazon`s number one seller.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: And it`s also battery powered. So once again that portability factor. You don`t have to hardwire it. You can hardwire it, but it`s not necessary. It has a battery pack and that`s $199.00 but available right now at Target for 150 bucks. So that`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right now?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: Some news you can use.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And those are the ones you can talk through, right?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: You can absolutely --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can speak to whoever is on your porch.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: Yes, what are you doing here?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do you have any specific set of words somebody is to say if you are trespassing? Back up?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: Why are you here?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why are you here?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: We have dogs inside.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I guess we`re going to have to put the low tech lock solutions on our website because we`re running low on time. Also home insurance as well, and Gayle, just to close things out, yes, I have a professionally installed security system. I just want to stop Katy from being like, "Go check that out." And then, you know, bat on the shoulder.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: Always.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Maya Angela has a great story. She didn`t have a security system but she did have a weapon. She heard somebody coming up the stairs, and she said: "Come in. Is this a day you are choosing to die? Please come in."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then she heard the footsteps going away.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am going to say that, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am a gangster. Brian Vines, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, something tasty for our series, "Changing the Game." Bakery CEO, Auzerais Bellamy became a big name selling blondies, and she is in our progressive rain room with some free samples.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There she is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, you ready?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m ready. I am ready.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. But first, here is a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our series, "Changing the Game," highlights extraordinary women making a difference now and for future generations, you could say. During Black History Month we`re focusing on Black entrepreneurs and creators.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`ve got to introduce you to Auzerais Bellamy. She`s the founder and CEO of Blondery. That`s a bakery that specializes in blondies, which are like brownies except they`re lighter and Auzerais says, and better when she is doing it.\par \par \uc2 Bellamy is a trained pastry chef who worked in some of the top kitchens around the world. She found it hard to grow her career in the fine dining industry. So she said, you know what? I`m going to start my own.\par \par \uc2 Bellamy started selling her baked goods online in 2017. Customers can now buy them at her bakery in Peekskill, New York, and around the world on her website.\par \par \uc2 Auzerais Bellamy joins us. Look at -- I love your storefront. That is nice.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY (Blondery founder and CEO): Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, why did you decide -- you were working with some of the top chefs in the country? Thomas Keller, Per Se, French Laundry. I know all of those places.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you said I want to do this on my own. Why?\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Well, I hit a glass ceiling. I think that fine dining on a whole is really good. It`s kind of like the military, you learn like the skills, the techniques, and then you kind of want to be creative and I wasn`t able to be creative there, so I branched out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you started in your home kitchen in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Yes. No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No?\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: In California.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In California.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Yes. I am from the Bay Area.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We got it wrong here, so...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it does say, you started selling blondies online back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You went from making $2,000.00.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In 2018 to $200,000.00.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: In two years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. That`s a big jump.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s big.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is big.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Thank you. And now, over half a million and yes, like we --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did what over -- say again?\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: We did over half a million in sales last year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Other than they taste really good, I will try them in a second. What do you think the secret is to that growth?\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: A good product.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A good product.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talk about it.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: A good product.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what do you think makes yours a good product? What is different from the --\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: We use the best ingredients. European butter. We use Guittard Chocolate, Valrhona Chocolate, King Arthur flour and we have a beautiful space as well. I think that that matters. My mom always says you cook in a nasty kitchen, you`re nasty cook.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did she say that?\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The kitchen does matter.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, you couldn`t wait for us, man?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Presentation does matter.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We don`t have a lot of time in this segment. We have a lot of food here.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: The is the pecan and salted caramel. That`s what started the business.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is delicious.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, let me do it. I`m going to jump in on this pecan.\par \par \uc2 So, what is your advice to someone who is sitting at home that might be working under an umbrella and thinking about taking that leap -- that leap of faith that most people are scared to do. What would you tell them?\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Yes. If you want to take the island you have to burn the boats, so I think that that is important, like burn all the bridges you can so you can`t go back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If you want to take the island, you have to burn the boats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am feeling that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: I think it is important. Like because you don`t -- the Plan B is what`s going to mess you up for your future.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: If you don`t have a Plan B --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is not a 10-layer cake. That is an 11-layer cake.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Eleven-ish. There is probably more.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Eleven-ish. Okay. All right. I mean, what goes in -- what thought process goes into creating something like that?\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: I just wanted to create something super decadent, and I did it during the pandemic on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  Live and people loved it. We raffled it off, and it raffled off for like $500.00. So I was like, oh, I could probably sell this for maybe half the price.\par \par \uc2 So it is $250.00 and it`s devil`s food cake, chocolate butter cream, vanilla butter cream, salted caramel ganache, toasted pecans, and then we have one without nuts as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are there any Black pastry chefs who do what you do? I mean, just in the business in general?\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: I mean, there`s tons of Black pastry chefs, but not at the scale I would say that I`m doing it and I hope that there becomes more, so I have a \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel where I`m sharing, like how to ship perishables that has over 40,000 views.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How did you learn how to do this? Was your mom a good cook?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And how long was the process?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And how long was the process?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How did it get this good? How long did it take?\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: It took me 10 years to refine the pecan and salted caramel blondie recipe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ten years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: But you could do it by mastery. You do it over and over and over and over until you get it right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle --\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: So, it took me over 10 years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle, she got it right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got to try this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She got it right. You should take it --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is amazing.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: That`s a smaller version of the 11-layer cake.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is orgasmic.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love when you when we said it`s in Peekskill. You go, "It`s just an hour away."\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Yes. It is just an hour away from Grand Central.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because that does seem -- that seems so far to me.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh no.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: It`s closer than Beacon and it is a community of Brown and Black people. That`s what I love about Peekskill the most, because when I first got there, I was like, oh my gosh, this is a community of people who look like me, and then the Mayor is a Black woman, Mayor Vivian, and before that, it was a Black man.\par \par \uc2 And I just was like, so supported. The community is so supportive in Peekskill. I thought I was just going to do retail just to see what it`s like but -- and then like, shut it down, but we can`t do that. They wanted us there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is clearly a passion for you. Clearly.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Yes. And my team, I have a team, too and they all are very diligent at their work and I appreciate that the most.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Auzerais, I apologize for the lack of professionalism.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because I lost focus after eating this. This is amazing.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 AUZERAIS BELLAMY: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 This right here, oh my.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This right here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because sometimes you taste something that is sweet and it is overwhelming --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate was telling us he was going to fast today, but that has changed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, it is not working out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is a wrap now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it was delicious, so...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So that`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230227ej2r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Surprising Jobs Report, The Lowest Unemployment Rate In Over 50 Years\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Carter Evans \par \uc2 967 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Back here at home now to the economy and the surprising monthly jobs report. U.S. employers added 517,000 jobs in January, and the unemployment rate fell to 3.4 percent. That is the lowest it`s been in more than half-a-century.\par \par \uc2 Stocks, however, fell over concerns that the strong labor report will mean the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will keep raising those interest rates.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: OK, a cheeseburger.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voice-over): Business is back at the Tallyrand Restaurant in Burbank, California.\par \par \uc2 Owner Karen Ross says she just made five new hires.\par \par \uc2 How have things changed?\par \par \uc2 KAREN ROSS (Owner, Tallyrand Restaurant): I have had in the last two, three months more applications that I have seen on my desk in four years, an abundance of people looking for work.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Jobs in hospitality and leisure got the biggest boost, with restaurants and bars accounting for one out of every five hires in January. In all, more than a half-million jobs were created, nearly triple expectations.\par \par \uc2 And, today, President Biden took a victory lap.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We have created more jobs in two years than any presidential term.\par \par \uc2 NICK BUNKER\uc2  (Economic Research Director, \uc2 Indeed.com\uc2 ): You look at all the data from the labor market right now, and there`s no sign that we`re about to tip into a recession.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: But economist \uc2 Nick Bunker\uc2  with the job posting site \uc2 Indeed.com\uc2  says there are still caution signs.\par \par \uc2 NICK BUNKER\uc2 : The cautious part of my optimism comes from how this data is interpreted by policymakers, particularly at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: With the Fed raising interest rates at the fastest clip in 40 years, some business leaders have been preparing for a recession.\par \par \uc2 The tech industry cut almost 100,000 jobs in 2022. And the layoffs are accelerating. Just last month, almost 42,000 tech jobs were lost.\par \par \uc2 NICK BUNKER\uc2 : The tech sector is actually a very small percent of the overall labor market. What would concern me is if we start hearing these sorts of announcements from companies like \uc2 Walmart\uc2  or large fast-food chains.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Are you still hiring?\par \par \uc2 KAREN ROSS: Still hiring.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: But Karen Ross is still struggling with inflation.\par \par \uc2 KAREN ROSS: The profit margin is very, very tight. We can only raise our prices so much.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, inflation has been declining in recent months, but it`s still stubbornly high.\par \par \uc2 And after today`s blockbuster jobs report, many Fed watchers think the Central Bank is almost sure to raise rates above 5 percent in May -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Carter Evans, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, the economy will certainly be a focus of next week`s State of the Union address. CBS News` prime-time coverage of President Biden`s second State of the Union and the Republican response begins Tuesday at 9:00 p.m. Eastern/6:00 p.m. Pacific right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . And we hope that you can join us.\par \par \uc2 Police say that they have caught the suspect behind a string of mysterious incidents at the Dallas Zoo, including the theft of those two monkeys.\par \par \uc2 We have got details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We have some breaking news.\par \par \uc2 A sixth Memphis police officer has been fired following the violent death of Tyre Nichols. The Memphis Police Department said Preston Hemphill was fired for violations of personal conduct, truthfulness, and compliance with regulations when it comes to the use of his Taser. Five other officers were fired and criminally charged.\par \par \uc2 To Texas now, where police say they have arrested a man who stole two monkeys this week from the Dallas Zoo. The 24-year-old suspect is charged with multiple counts of animal cruelty and burglary in connection with other incidents at the zoo where animal enclosures were cut open.\par \par \uc2 Police say he was arrested Thursday after visiting the Dallas Aquarium. The stolen monkeys appeared to be unharmed when they were found inside an abandoned house.\par \par \uc2 Big changes aimed at keeping kids healthy could be coming now to school meals. We are going to dig into the details.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: News today that the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is investigating what happened at Newark Liberty International Airport in New Jersey when two \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  jets clipped wings today. A plane parked at a gate was clipped by an empty plane that was parking at the next gate. No one was hurt, but passengers on the jet that was hit had to switch to another plane.\par \par \uc2 Well, for millions of kids, big changes could be coming to the school cafeteria menu. The Agriculture Department is proposing new nutrition standards for school meals vehicle. For the first time ever, officials are brought recommending limits on added sugars in sweetened foods, including cereals, yogurt, flavored milk, and breakfast pastries.\par \par \uc2 The plan also calls for a gradual 30 percent cut in salt by 2029. A 60-day public comment period on the plan opens next week. It sounds like a good idea.\par \par \uc2 All right, an Alabama man`s generosity is exposed, and it is starting a movement to help those in need.\par \par \uc2 That`s On the Road. It`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230217ej230005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  On Advice For Workers Facing A Changing Career\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Gayle King, David Begnaud \par \uc2 846 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, this morning, we`re taking a look at how to navigate a very uncertain job market right now. And in fact, according to a new \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  survey, almost 40 percent of people out there are worried they`re going to be laid off this year, and 20 percent of people, fully 20 percent actually expect their jobs to be gone in the next year.\par \par \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  career expert, Catherine Fisher is here to help us with some advice.\par \par \uc2 All right, Catherine, you got laid off. What now?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (\uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  career expert): Well, first of all, go into the job market really confident. Understand that you were not laid off because of you personally, it was because of business performance.\par \par \uc2 Then you need to lean into that network. You`ve been building your context all throughout your career, ask for help. We`re seeing a lot of people on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  posting that they`ve been laid off, and where you really see the comments coming in to help is when people are specific about what they need.\par \par \uc2 Do they want to learn a new skill? Do they need an introduction? How do you help them? So, it`s really about being prepared for that job search.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, it is interesting you say that people -- there is no stigma associated with it. The survey says that most people think there is a stigma. You say not to take it personal. It`s hard not to take it personal.\par \par \uc2 And you say there is no stigma, what do you mean -- about being laid off?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, So what happened is during COVID, when people you know, companies were laying people off, shedding jobs, other industries were hiring, all of a sudden, there was this kind of reckoning in terms of like, you know, stuff happens. Businesses go up and down, and it`s not about you personally. And you also sometimes need to take time off, so that you can take care of a family member.\par \par \uc2 And so this concept of like, oh, you know, you have this mark on you is gone. And you know, really you`re seeing this with all these posts on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  about people asking for help. We`re starting to see a trend with people becoming more comfortable.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: When people are having success using \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  to find a job, what are they doing right?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: They are leaning into their network. They are using the open-to-work on their profiles so that signals to recruiters that they`re available.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: They are brushing up on their skills. This is really important is that, you can get a job in an industry that you`re not used to working in if you have the right skills.\par \par \uc2 Take an inventory of what skills you have, take a look at the jobs that you`re interested in, do a match of where those gaps are, get those skills right away and start storytelling of how you`ve applied the skills to your job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You mentioned the career cushioning of idea that you prepare for this by making sure things are in order.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do you mean by that exactly?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: You know, it`s like you don`t want to clean out your closet once a year because that is overwhelming, at least -- you know sometimes I do that, but it is about always making sure you`re ready to go.\par \par \uc2 You know, your resume is up to date. You`ve been keeping your context warm. You know exactly what you want in your career. You`re up-to-date on those skills.\par \par \uc2 And you know, career cushioning is good in this layoff situation. It`s also good if you`re confident in your job because a new job opportunity could come your way, and you want to be ready to do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is just good to be ready.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is good to be ready.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Open all possibilities. Thank you, Catherine Fisher, always good to have here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Much appreciated. Very important information.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`ll do it for us. Time flies when you`re having fun.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you right here tomorrow at the table on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it also.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy Almost Friday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Almost Friday is right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230216ej2200232\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Ordering A Return To Work\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Scott MacFarlane, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 2,074 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 February 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Millions of Americans have been working from home during this pandemic, but now the COVID restrictions are disappearing so many large employers are saying to their employees, "Come back to the office."\par \par \uc2 Later today, Congress will consider offering many Federal employees to return as well. Scott MacFarlane is in Bethesda, Maryland with more on that.\par \par \uc2 Scott, I wonder how employees are feeling about that order.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: We`re about to find out, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Three years into the pandemic, a review of Federal records by CBS News finds more than a million Federal workers are working at least part of the week at home. Republicans want to bring that number back to pre-pandemic levels and bring many of those workers back to the office.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is telework Monday, not so bad across the metro area with overall lower travel volumes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: When COVID arrived, a mass migration to telework followed, including for Federal workers.\par \par \uc2 VALENCIA MCINTYRE-DUBOSE (\uc2 US Department of Veteran Affairs\uc2  employee): I love it.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Valencia McIntyre-DuBose, who for 23 years has processed benefits for the \uc2 US Department of Veterans Affairs\uc2  and helped the employee union says she`s never been more efficient now that she is working from home.\par \par \uc2 You think it`s helping to retain more Federal workers being able to telework?\par \par \uc2 VALENCIA MCINTYRE-DUBOSE: Yes, it`s easier. You can get the job done, and it seems to me that by me doing telework, I get my work done faster and easier.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The number of \uc2 US government\uc2  employees telecommuting has more than doubled since 2018, but in a vote later today, the US House now under Republican control is pushing a plan to bring most Federal workers back as the threat of COVID is receding.\par \par \uc2 REP. BOB GOOD (R-VA): Everybody should come back to the office and do what the American people have to do. Certainly, if you`re a Federal employee, the standard ought to be higher.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Many private businesses are already pushing workers to get back to the office most of the time, including \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 , and \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  explained to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS` Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 TIM COOK (\uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO): You collaborate with one another because we believe that one plus one equals three. And so that takes the serendipity of running into people.\par \par \uc2 That doesn`t mean that we`re going to be in here five days, we`re not.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Republican Chair of the House Oversight Committee argues backlogs at the \uc2 IRS\uc2  during tax season and slowdowns in processing Social Security checks and veterans benefits could be reduced if employees got back to the office.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): As the rest of America went back to work in person, many Federal workers did not, and the American people have suffered as a result.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Also weighing in are Democratic Mayors of some major cities including New York, San Francisco, Houston, and Washington where telework has squeezed some local businesses and emptied downtown buildings.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR MURIEL BOWSER (D) (Washington, DC): America wins when the place where people come to join and change the world is buzzing.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But some Democrats in Congress are pushing back against the current bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): We cannot enter a time machine and simply disappear the utility of telework in recruiting and retaining new generations of Federal workers.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Advocates for the roughly one million teleworking Federal employees say an end to telework could trigger a wave of retirements, resignations, and further backlogs.\par \par \uc2 MAX STIER (\uc2 Partnership for Public Service\uc2  founding President and CEO): We need an effective government to solve some of our biggest problems and effective government means great talent. The greatest benefit of telework is that it helps us attract and keep the best people possible to serve the public.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: This US House plan to reduce telework will likely pass along party lines today. The \uc2 US Senate\uc2  won`t take it up, so it`ll stall for a while, but Tony, House aides tell CBS News they could make this part of the negotiations later this year to prevent a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot to think about there, Scott. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will have the stories you`ll be talking about today, but first it is 7:44, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, time now for "What to Watch." Mr. Begnaud is in.\par \par \uc2 What is going on?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is this song?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: "Who Stole My Monkey?" Look, it is Zachary Richard, the great from Louisiana recorded this song when I was growing up, "Who Stole My Monkey?" So I said that should be the soundtrack for this story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, David, there you go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there you have it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ask and you shall receive.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So, it`s okay if I segue, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We start with an update to those missing monkeys of the Dallas Zoo. Police said two Emperor tamarin monkeys were stolen on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Well, yesterday, officers found the monkeys inside a closet at a vacant house, just south of the zoo. They apparently got a tip, they were there. The animals were safe. They were returned to the zoo for a veterinary checkup.\par \par \uc2 There have been no arrests thus far. And by the way, when they got off the set yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Folks in my hometown e-mailed me and said, "David, there were 12 monkeys stolen from the zoo in your hometown."\par \par \uc2 What is going on with monkeys?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is going on with the monkeys, David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then they just abandoned them for them to be found.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I don`t know. I guess there is a black market for monkeys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There must be. That`s kind of sad. And what makes this a particularly sad story is that the vulture actually died, right? There`s a $10,000.00 reward for information.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The Dallas --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Dallas Zoo.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Dallas Zoo yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The Dallas Zoo.\par \par \uc2 All right, so little change of pace here. Tom Brady just made a leap from the grid iron to glam.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Last night, the \uc2 NFL\uc2  superstar walked the red carpet for the LA premiere of the movie "80s for Brady." He produced the film and appeared in it himself.\par \par \uc2 Also there last night, his co-stars: Jane Fonda, Lily Tomlin, Sally Field, Rita Moreno, and Billy Porter. Tomlin called Brady a wonderful actor. "80 for Brady" is distributed by \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 , which is a division of \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` parent company, \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to see this.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It actually seems like a fun movie and all of these women admit that they`re so smitten with Tom Brady.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It seems everybody that meets him says, what a great guy he is. Of course, he`s great to look at. We all know that. But they say that he`s got -- he is a great actor.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is very loving and very collaborative on this particular movie.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Those ladies are the group.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, I think it is fun.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I just -- that seems like a hoot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we like them.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Oh, a hundred percent like them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I like it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: All right, so we end with the controversy over a Best Actress nominee at the Oscars. A lot of people were surprised when Andrea Riseborough got a nod last month for her starring role in the indie film "To Leslie." Watch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "To Leslie.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: All right, so here`s the deal. The nomination led to claims that there was an aggressive campaign by some A-list celebrities for Riseborough, that may have broken Oscar rules.\par \par \uc2 Well, yesterday the \uc2 Motion Picture Academy\uc2  says it conducted a review and decided not to revoke Riseborough`s nomination. The Academy did say it discovered social media and outreach campaign tactics that caused concern and the tactics are being addressed with the responsible parties.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But doesn`t everybody campaign for these Oscars?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everyone lobbies, right? They do big interviews with the media.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They have parties. They have meet and greets. They send letters, e-mails.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I`m so glad that they decided not to -- that she was not at fault and that nothing will happen because number one, you could -- I`ve never heard of "To Leslie." I`d never heard of Andrea, actually, be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I watched it on the plane and she is really very, very good.\par \par \uc2 And when you see her performance, you can see why she was nominated, and I`m a layperson. You can see why she was nominated.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We just live in a day and age now where because of social media --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The exposure is different to get your name out there in front of these judges.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But David, she didn`t start the campaign. Other people started on her behalf.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I get it. I get it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also, what do you consider an aggressive campaign. It sounds like these A-listers, George Clooney is knocking on the door at 4:00 AM.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Forcing them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Gayle`s exclusive conversation with tennis legend, Serena Williams. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil, and that`s David Begnaud.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, an exclusive visit with Serena Williams. We`re going to go to her Florida farm where she talks about life after tennis, her family, and as the kids say today, "She`s booked and busy."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we`re also going to have a first look at a course in African-American Studies the Florida Department of Education has banned. The College Board CEO and the course creator will show us what`s actually in it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And in our "Road to the Grammys," Nate talks with hip-hop star Tobe Nwigwe, nominated for Best New Artist about his fast-rising career in music, TV, and movies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We begin this hour with breaking news.\par \par \uc2 Vlad Duthiers he sent me a text, he said I can share with the audience. The baby is here. Her name is Celine Mari Wang Duthiers. Celine, it`s a little girl.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Round of applause.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And we`ve seen a picture. She`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know the control room is freaking out because they said, we said you can`t say, but he just texted me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We got the note.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It literally just came. He just texted me and said, okay, you can say it. And he says we`ll have pictures tomorrow. I`m so excited.\par \par \uc2 So happy for Vlad. So happy for Marian and --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The whole family.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Baby Celine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, you`ve become Dad, and we wish you all the best. We will talk about it more tomorrow, and pictures to come.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am so psyched.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230216ej21001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Winter Weather Travel Mess; Freezing Forecast; Memphis in Mourning; Manhunt Ends for Oregon Kidnapping Suspect; Lilia Luciano; Target Store Shooting; Biden`s Classified Document Controversy; New Details in Baldwin "Rust" Charges; Santos Steps Down from Committees; Trump Deposition Tapes Revealed; Ordering a Return to Work; Serena`s Next Act; African-American Course Controversy; Hip-Hop Renaissance Man; Foot Fashion Forward; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, David Begnaud, Omar Villafranca,  Stephanie Abrams, Elise Preston, Adriana Diaz, Kris Van Cleave, Caitlin  Huey-Burns, Ed O`Keefe; Scott MacFarlane; Nate Burleson \par \uc2 14,014 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 February 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day, the first of February. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And I`m David Begnaud.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today, but we`re still here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Winter storms across the South and the Midwest, slowing down travel and knocking out power for tens of thousands.\par \par \uc2 MAN: More ice doubling the amounts we`ve already seen in some areas so we can expect more power outages, dangerous travel.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A heartbroken community bids a final farewell to Tyre Nichols this morning even as the investigation continues.\par \par \uc2 RODNEY WELLS (Tyre Nichols` stepfather): Justice for Tyre.\par \par \uc2 GROUP: Justice for Tyre.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A new development in the Biden classified documents controversy: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched his office back in November.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Former President Trump questioned in a New York lawsuit pleads the fifth more than 400 times.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Anyone in my position, not taking the fifth amendment would be a fool.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Alec Baldwin and the armorer on the sets of "Rust" have been officially charged with involuntary manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Davidson students section is getting creative with their free throw distraction.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You see the swim team. I hope it is the swim team, if it`s not, you`ve got another story, and he throws it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Embattled Republican Congressman George Santos is stepping down from the \uc2 House Small Business Committee\uc2  and the Science, Space and Technology Committee.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): That`s a shame because they won`t benefit from his expertise. I mean, after all, Santos was the first dog in space.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): And a six- year-old boy in Michigan ended up opening his father`s \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  app and ordered $1,000.00 worth of food. According to his father, the doorbell just kept ringing and cars just kept coming.\par \par \uc2 This is true. Among other items, the kid ordered $1,000.00 worth of jumbo shrimp, salads, shawarma, chicken pita sandwiches, ice cream, and chili cheese fries. There`s no denying it, this kid is going to have the best Super Bowl party on the block.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: My kind of party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Six years old -- yes, I was going to say, six years old, he knows how to use \uc2 Grubhub\uc2 . You can`t even be mad at it. It is so cute.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, you can`t. Man, I want an invite.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is so cute.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A little entrepreneur on the way.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Really excited about today`s show. Why? Because we have Serena Williams. We visited her Florida farm for an exclusive conversation with Serena and her husband, \uc2 Alexis Ohanian\uc2  did a little drive by talking about their new life. You`ll be glad to know she is doing really fine without tennis. In fact, you`re going to see her during the Super Bowl.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And Nate is going to talk with hip-hop star, Tobe Nwigwe in our "Road to the Grammys" series. He discusses giving up his football dreams, finding new meaning in music, and developing the unique sound that made him a Grammy nominee as Best New Artist.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got a lot of news to get to though, beginning with that deadly winter ice storm gripping parts of the American South this morning and accidents on slick roads have killed at least two people as of yesterday and tens of thousands of people are without power this morning.\par \par \uc2 Texas got more snow than New York City has seen all winter and 38 million Americans from Texas to Virginia are under weather warnings and advisories this morning.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca is in hard-hit Memphis which on top of everything else has got this weather coming through.\par \par \uc2 Omar, good morning.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Temperatures are still below freezing here in Memphis and after several hours of sleet and freezing rain, this is what everything looks like. You have trees and roads encrusted in ice, turning this whole region into a travel nightmare.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Here in Memphis, icy conditions lead to a 12-car pileup on Tuesday. Police said five drivers were hospitalized all with non-life threatening injuries.\par \par \uc2 MAN: People are just taking it for granted that they can just stop and you can`t. These roads are slick.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from NewsNation.)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: In St. Francis County, Arkansas, a similar scene. Ice sent three semitrucks sliding off the road, slowing traffic on Interstate 40 to a crawl. It was so cold, even some of the Transportation Department`s cameras froze over.\par \par \uc2 There were hundreds of collisions across Texas as well, including a pileup in Austin the left one person dead early Tuesday morning.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Dallas Fort Worth International Airport\uc2 , the winter blast was a factor as more than 60 percent of flights were cancelled, part of the nearly 2,000 cancellations nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The cold forced Dallas resident, Andrew Ventura to move some of his belongings into his truck, using it to keep warm and charge his phone after his apartment lost electricity.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW VENTURA (Dallas, Texas resident): Power outages have been pretty bad around here. It started last night and then it lasted throughout the night, until about 4:00 or 5:00 AM. The power kicked back on and then went back off a few hours later.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Despite thousands of outages across Texas, Governor Greg Abbott said there is not a threat to the State`s power grid.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): Local outages are not a reason to say that there is a problem with the power grid. The power grid is functioning just fine as we speak.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Dozens of schools across the region are closed today because of the weather and Memphis is under an ice storm warning, until at least tomorrow morning -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Omar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partner