ANE: Many private businesses are already pushing workers to get back to the office most of the time, including \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 , and \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  explained to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS` Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 TIM COOK (\uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO): You collaborate with one another because we believe that one plus one equals three. And so that takes the serendipity of running into people.\par \par \uc2 That doesn`t mean that we`re going to be in here five days, we`re not.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Republican Chair of the House Oversight Committee argues backlogs at the \uc2 IRS\uc2  during tax season and slowdowns in processing Social Security checks and veterans benefits could be reduced if employees got back to the office.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): As the rest of America went back to work in person, many Federal workers did not, and the American people have suffered as a result.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Also weighing in are Democratic Mayors of some major cities including New York, San Francisco, Houston, and Washington where telework has squeezed some local businesses and emptied downtown buildings.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR MURIEL BOWSER (D) (Washington, DC): America wins when the place where people come to join and change the world is buzzing.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But some Democrats in Congress are pushing back against the current bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): We cannot enter a time machine and simply disappear the utility of telework in recruiting and retaining new generations of Federal workers.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Advocates for the roughly one million teleworking Federal employees say an end to telework could trigger a wave of retirements, resignations, and further backlogs.\par \par \uc2 MAX STIER (\uc2 Partnership for Public Service\uc2  founding President and CEO): We need an effective government to solve some of our biggest problems and effective government means great talent. The greatest benefit of telework is that it helps us attract and keep the best people possible to serve the public.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: This US House plan to reduce telework will likely pass along party lines today. The \uc2 US Senate\uc2  won`t take it up, so it`ll stall for a while, but Tony, House aides tell CBS News they could make this part of the negotiations later this year to prevent a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot to think about there, Scott. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will have the stories you`ll be talking about today, but first it is 7:44, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, time now for "What to Watch." Mr. Begnaud is in.\par \par \uc2 What is going on?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is this song?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: "Who Stole My Monkey?" Look, it is Zachary Richard, the great from Louisiana recorded this song when I was growing up, "Who Stole My Monkey?" So I said that should be the soundtrack for this story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, David, there you go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there you have it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ask and you shall receive.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So, it`s okay if I segue, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We start with an update to those missing monkeys of the Dallas Zoo. Police said two Emperor tamarin monkeys were stolen on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Well, yesterday, officers found the monkeys inside a closet at a vacant house, just south of the zoo. They apparently got a tip, they were there. The animals were safe. They were returned to the zoo for a veterinary checkup.\par \par \uc2 There have been no arrests thus far. And by the way, when they got off the set yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Folks in my hometown e-mailed me and said, "David, there were 12 monkeys stolen from the zoo in your hometown."\par \par \uc2 What is going on with monkeys?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is going on with the monkeys, David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then they just abandoned them for them to be found.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I don`t know. I guess there is a black market for monkeys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There must be. That`s kind of sad. And what makes this a particularly sad story is that the vulture actually died, right? There`s a $10,000.00 reward for information.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The Dallas --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Dallas Zoo.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Dallas Zoo yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The Dallas Zoo.\par \par \uc2 All right, so little change of pace here. Tom Brady just made a leap from the grid iron to glam.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Last night, the \uc2 NFL\uc2  superstar walked the red carpet for the LA premiere of the movie "80s for Brady." He produced the film and appeared in it himself.\par \par \uc2 Also there last night, his co-stars: Jane Fonda, Lily Tomlin, Sally Field, Rita Moreno, and Billy Porter. Tomlin called Brady a wonderful actor. "80 for Brady" is distributed by \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 , which is a division of \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` parent company, \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to see this.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It actually seems like a fun movie and all of these women admit that they`re so smitten with Tom Brady.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It seems everybody that meets him says, what a great guy he is. Of course, he`s great to look at. We all know 