nd she is really very, very good.\par \par \uc2 And when you see her performance, you can see why she was nominated, and I`m a layperson. You can see why she was nominated.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We just live in a day and age now where because of social media --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The exposure is different to get your name out there in front of these judges.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But David, she didn`t start the campaign. Other people started on her behalf.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I get it. I get it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also, what do you consider an aggressive campaign. It sounds like these A-listers, George Clooney is knocking on the door at 4:00 AM.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Forcing them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Gayle`s exclusive conversation with tennis legend, Serena Williams. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil, and that`s David Begnaud.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, an exclusive visit with Serena Williams. We`re going to go to her Florida farm where she talks about life after tennis, her family, and as the kids say today, "She`s booked and busy."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we`re also going to have a first look at a course in African-American Studies the Florida Department of Education has banned. The College Board CEO and the course creator will show us what`s actually in it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And in our "Road to the Grammys," Nate talks with hip-hop star Tobe Nwigwe, nominated for Best New Artist about his fast-rising career in music, TV, and movies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We begin this hour with breaking news.\par \par \uc2 Vlad Duthiers he sent me a text, he said I can share with the audience. The baby is here. Her name is Celine Mari Wang Duthiers. Celine, it`s a little girl.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Round of applause.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And we`ve seen a picture. She`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know the control room is freaking out because they said, we said you can`t say, but he just texted me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We got the note.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It literally just came. He just texted me and said, okay, you can say it. And he says we`ll have pictures tomorrow. I`m so excited.\par \par \uc2 So happy for Vlad. So happy for Marian and --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The whole family.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Baby Celine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, you`ve become Dad, and we wish you all the best. We will talk about it more tomorrow, and pictures to come.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am so psyched.\par \par \uc2 Okay, now we`re going to begin this hour with another exclusive interview with one of the greatest athletes of all time: Serena Williams.\par \par \uc2 The tennis legend is showing off a new talent, golf, in this year`s Michelob ULTRA Super Bowl commercial. Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip form Michelob ULTRA.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Only on CBS MORNINGS, we sat down with Serena Williams for a wide-ranging conversation about tennis and her family, what is she doing these days and we caught up with her and her husband, \uc2 Alexis Ohanian\uc2  on their farm in Florida for a glimpse of their lives, or her life, specifically after tennis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Michelob ULTRA.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does it take to get Serena Williams to do a Super Bowl commercial? What does it take?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS (Former professional tennis player): Well, it`s not -- so first of all, I have to have a genuine like for the product, right? Second of all, like, what are you doing outside of that? Is there something special? And that`s what I love about Michelob ULTRA. They really are really adamant about making sure there`s gender equality when it comes -- equity, I should say when it comes to male and female; in particular, women athletes and men athletes.\par \par \uc2 So this is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s reflected in that commercial.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: It is reflected in that commercial and this is the second year in a row that they have the same number of female athletes or women athletes as they do male athletes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When I looked at the commercial, Serena, and the way you swung the golf club, and I remember the "Vogue" letter, and you said you`re not retiring, you`re transitioning and evolving. Is this your way of telling us you are evolving into golf because you certainly have the outfit ready.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I am 100 percent not evolving into golf.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from August 2022 US Open/\uc2 ESPN\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I last met up with Serena Williams at her final US Open.\par \par \uc2 She has changed the game in so many ways.\par \par \uc2 You know, the day after the US Open and you wake up and you`re done with tennis, as we know it in that form, what was that morning like? Because I woke up that morning thinking, "I wonder what Serena is doing? I wonder how Serena is feeling?"\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: That`s a good question. That`s a really a question. I`ve never -- no one has asked me that yet.\par \par \uc2 I was actually not very happy at all, not -- just because of the result, I was there to keep going and to keep winning. And yes, it wasn`t -- it was more or less like I can`t believe I let that match go more than anything, you know.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, it was a great moment and a great memory and I`ve never felt -- I don`t feel like I`ve left anything, but I just felt like, what could I have done better that day?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When Serena revealed her decision to step away from tennis in "Vogue" last year, she wrote this: "If I have to choose between building my tennis resume and building my family, I choose the latter."\par \par \uc2 She also shared that her daughter, Olympia, wants to be a big sister.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: To this day, she is like, now she prays.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Prays for a --\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Now, she prays with -- she is like -- and the other day, she`s like, "Everyone has a brother or sister, but me." and I`m like, "Olympia, I`m working on it. Mommy worked a little too long in tennis, stayed a little longer than expected." But yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And so now that you`ve -- it`s been a minute since you`ve been off the tennis court, you said you still like to win. You`re still a competitor.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what does winning look like to you these days?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Winning to me, just picking out the best deals to invest in, you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the tennis champion`s venture capital firm, it is called Serena Ventures is focused on funding founders with diverse backgrounds.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: We need people like me, we need women writing the checks because I really am a big believer, and like attracts like.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Investing is something Serena and her husband, \uc2 Alexis Ohanian\uc2 , founder of venture capital firm, 776, and social site, \uc2 Reddit\uc2 , have in common.\par \par \uc2 These days, the couple spends their time here at their South Florida farm, where they grow fruits and vegetables and raise chickens.\par \par \uc2 Serena, do you want to pick one up?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I`ve never touched them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A mini horse that their daughter, Olympia, named Daisy and a steer named --\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Antonio Enrique Cortez Leche.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Serena?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS OHANIAN\uc2  (Serena Williams` husband): There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Serena, that`s the name of the camel.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I missed a couple of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Antonio who?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Antonio for Antonio Banderas.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Enrique for Enrique Iglesias. Cortez because it was a spy -- we are watching the "Spy Kids" movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: And that was the last name in the "Spy Kids" movie.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS OHANIAN\uc2 : So I let the kids do the naming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you just called it Leche.\par \par \uc2 Alexis, I don`t know if you know this, the first time when I heard about you, Serena said -- I said, "Oh, how is it going? Who are you dating?" She goes --\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: What did I say?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Oh, I am dating this tech nerd. You probably never heard of him." That`s what --\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS OHANIAN\uc2 : Au contraire.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you remember saying that? She said, "I`m dating this tech nerd. You probably never heard of him." I said, "Well, what`s his name?" She said your name? I go, "I know him. He`s great. He is like, he one of the good guys."\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS OHANIAN\uc2 : I appreciate -- I appreciate you vouching for me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What makes this work, Alexis, for you; and Serena, what makes this work for you?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: He is able to handle me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you allowed to handle?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS OHANIAN\uc2 : Yes, and no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does she allow --\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: No, I`m very easy and I`m very relaxed and chill, but I`m somehow super intense and I like things really perfect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why does it work for you? This relationship?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS OHANIAN\uc2 : Because I knew I had met someone that I was never going to outgrow, and more importantly, I knew I had met someone who was always going to explicitly -- believe me, sometimes -- or implicitly challenge me to be better.\par \par \uc2 She`s a ride or die, and you don`t see a lot of it because thankfully, I don`t do my work in public. But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is a ride or die.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS OHANIAN: Oh, a thousand percent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS OHANIAN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so lovely to sit here at the place you call The Farm. The tennis court is right there.\par \par \uc2 What do you think when you look at a tennis court now? I was wondering.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Yes, in this particular tennis court, I see me out there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You do.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I see me training. I see me grinding.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you can look at it, Serena, and not feel a pang of "Ugh."\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I feel relief when I look at the court sometimes, because --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Relief? How?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I feel like I`m so happy, I don`t have to train for two hours today. I feel more pain just like looking at old pictures than I --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Of yourself?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Yes, looking at older pictures or championships or something like that. I`m like, "Oh, I miss that." Or that`s when I feel like different.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does the door open just a sliver?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: It`s hard to say, I would say no. For now, no, because it`s just -- I`ve literally given my whole life to tennis, and it is time for me to give my life to something else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: And then my dad is like, "Serena, you should play one more -- you should play like two more Grand Slams or." and I`m like, "Dad, like stop."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stop.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: This is not --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you know, I came across some old video I`m going to run in this piece. It`s just sweetest piece of video.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD WILLIAMS (Serena Williams` father): Don`t hit that racket on the ground.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I was smashing my racket even back then.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD WILLIAMS: You can play tomorrow, next year and the year after that. What`s important? What`s important to your home?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Family.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD WILLIAMS: Family is important. Because the family is important in your home. You shouldn`t be out here getting disgusted because of a tennis ball. Right?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: That`s so sweet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your little girl voice, and that`s why you wanted people to see "King Richard" because we did get to see him in another light.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Yes. We totally -- this is literally the light that -- that was the truth. "King Richard" was absolutely true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, and then "King Richard" had that moment at the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 What did you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I thought it was such an incredible film and I feel that there was an incredible film after that with Questlove that kind of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was overshadowed.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: It was overshadowed, but I also feel that I`ve been in a position where I`ve been under a lot of pressure and I`ve made a tremendous amount of mistakes. And I`m the kind of person that`s like, "I`ve been there. I`ve made a mistake. It`s not the end of the world."\par \par \uc2 We`re all imperfect and we`re all human and let`s just be kind to each other, and I think that`s -- so that`s often forgotten a lot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You tweeted recently allowing yourself to relax was harder than you ever imagined.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s hard to relax for you?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: It`s so hard for me to relax. I don`t know how to relax.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I literally don`t. I`m like -- I have to schedule it in my schedule.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I am not kidding. I have to schedule relax or I won`t do it, and I haven`t had that in my schedule in a while, so I need to put it back in there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, we were totally relaxed hanging out with Serena and her family that day. She also says, if she had been a man and was in the health that she`s in, she`d probably still be playing tennis because she loves it so much, but family is also a very top priority.\par \par \uc2 But I love that moment with her dad. I think it showed a different side of him that many people didn`t really understand Richard Williams. She is totally in love with her father, and he is still calling and giving her tips about what she should do and how she should do it.\par \par \uc2 But she is very happy. They`re very happy together.\par \par \uc2 And I loved Alexis` answer, too. He finally met someone that he couldn`t outgrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Isn`t that nice?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am going to text that to Katy later today. It is a very sweet sentiment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you look more in a partner, I thought it was sweet.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: They seem to have a nice rhythm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They do, and they`re working on -- she says we`re working on it. We`re working on it, and when it`s time, she will share that, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like the name she came up with for the cow as well. Very creative.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With the Spanish accent, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not bad, not bad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicely done, Serena.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead and first on CBS MORNINGS, the creators of a new AP chorus on African-American History reveals what`s in the course after Florida already decided to ban it.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:11. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Been Broke.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That is Grammy-nominated artist, Tobe Nwigwe. Ahead for our "Road to the Grammys" series, he talks with Nate about finding success on his terms.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. Come on back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As we mark the start of Black History Month, the College Board is introducing a first of its kind course on African-American Studies. It is the first for many reasons in part because high school students for the first time will combine History along with Art and Culture in this course.\par \par \uc2 It has also become controversial. Florida`s Education Department under Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has already announced the course will not be allowed in Florida high schools, at least based on the pilot, even though the actual has not been released. The framework for the class has not been available until now.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, College Board CEO David Coleman, and AP African- American Studies Director and Program Manager, Brandi Waters join us to unveil what`s actually in this course that has got everybody talking.\par \par \uc2 So Brandi, I want to go to you first. As the creator of this course, what`s in it? What can students expect?\par \par \uc2 BRANDI WATERS (AP African-American Studies Director and Program Manager): This course is so exciting. It`s an amazing course.\par \par \uc2 It is one where students have the opportunity to delve into the depths of African-American experiences. They see the diversity of these communities and the broader connections between the US and the African diaspora.\par \par \uc2 It is a course that allows students as you mentioned, to explore resources in the Arts and the Sciences and Politics, and really come away understanding their current situation much more deeply.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How much did it change from the pilot to what`s being revealed today?\par \par \uc2 BRANDI WATERS: So we were constantly iterating. We put out a number of different sources for the first pilot and immediately started talking with students and teachers where the course was living, and just asked them, which sources are interesting, which sources are engaging, which sources make your creativity come alive -- and started tweaking from there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: David, what did you think about the controversy, especially in Florida, where people were criticizing the course who really hadn`t seen it and didn`t know anything about it?\par \par \uc2 DAVID COLEMAN (College Board CEO): You know, we at the college board don`t really look to the statements of politicians, but we do look to the record of history.\par \par \uc2 So when we revised the course, there are only two things we went to. We went to what Brandi described, which is feedback from teachers and students as well as 300 Professors who have been involved in building the course, and we went back to principles that have guided AP for a long time and served us well.\par \par \uc2 And what you`ll find when you read it, as I hope you will, is you`ll find a unflinching encounter with the facts and evidence of African-American History and Culture that gives young people the chance to think for themselves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And isn`t African-American History American history as well? I think people sometimes make the distinction, don`t you?\par \par \uc2 DAVID COLEMAN: I couldn`t agree with you more. You know, we all know today is the first day of Black History Month.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID COLEMAN: And I know, you know that the great black educator, Carter Woodson is the one who inspired Black History Month. And he said in 1922, excuse me, 1922, just about a hundred years before today, he said, "Soon, we`ll be able to tell our story. And only then, can we be recognized as people."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But this raises the question and Brandi, maybe you speak to this, if you know, of course, African-American History is American History, why the separate course? Why the need for it?\par \par \uc2 BRANDI WATERS: Well, what`s really exciting about this course is that it`s not an African-American History course, it is an African-American Studies course. So we`re giving students the opportunity to enter this professional discipline that normally they wouldn`t access until college, but now they can get it in high school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And all students.\par \par \uc2 BRANDI WATERS: All students.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All students.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Brandi, the phrase, "critical race theory" has become so politically charged, and it`s almost used to denigrate some in the lexicon. When you hear the phrase and the way it is misused, what do you tell people who say, "Oh, it`s critical race theory," that`s what it --what do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 BRANDI WATERS: Well, I think as a scholar who has read many of these works, the first question I have to ask them is what they mean by critical race theory. I`m sure you`ve had this question before, too.\par \par \uc2 DAVID COLEMAN: Absolutely. And I`ll tell you just our point of view in the Advanced Placement Program.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Please.\par \par \uc2 DAVID COLEMAN: . about theories like this. It`s not about picking this theory or that. No AP course, whether it`s our course in Japanese Culture and Language, or Spanish Culture and Language -- require students to study a specified theory, trains them in an interpretation. Instead, it immerses them directly in the facts and evidence, and lets them think for themselves.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The facts and evidence.\par \par \uc2 DAVID COLEMAN: Instead, what they do is they have a research project at the end of the course where they get to pick something, and then they do compare what they`re learning about other interpretations. So of course -- yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, one of the criticisms was it significantly lacks educational value.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Again, based on the pilot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, based -- yes. What do you both think when you hear that?\par \par \uc2 DAVID COLEMAN: I`ve got to tell you, working with Brandi over these past few years has been a glory to me. And, and I just want to give her a chance to describe the so many surprises everything is in this course.\par \par \uc2 The Black inventors and artists who we have just recently been seeing, the Black women and men and gay Americans who are pivotal in the Civil Rights Movement.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID COLEMAN: The people of faith from all backgrounds who contributed so much to the anti-slavery cause and the role of faith in the Black community. This course hides from nothing, it sees everything. But Brandi, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRANDI WATERS: Sure. I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In less than 30 seconds, Brandi.\par \par \uc2 BRANDI WATERS: That critique has been around for a while, but I know in my life, this field has changed the way that I think about my communities, how I relate to others and students will have that same experience.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can adults take the class? I`m thinking this sounds good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Probably buy the textbook.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: About a hundred dollars though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can adults take the class? I`m serious.\par \par \uc2 BRANDI WATERS: They should follow along with students.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 BRANDI WATERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID COLEMAN: We`re hearing about families who are doing the readings at their dinner table. This is deep work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Hard walls. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo. Congrats.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We`re better for listening to you both.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Starting the day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID COLEMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, Tom Brady makes a big announcement about his future.\par \par \uc2 We`ll tell you what it is.\par \par \uc2 Local news, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" and we`ve got a real talker.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is a good talker.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Here we go. Somebody went to the beach this morning and sat down on the beach where it looks like this morning and they recorded this video. Tom Brady says he is -- well, just watch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM BRADY (Professional \uc2 NFL\uc2  player): Good morning, guys. I`ll get to the point right away. I`m retiring, for good.\par \par \uc2 I know the process was a pretty big deal last time. So when I woke up this morning, I figured I just pressed record and let you guys know first.\par \par \uc2 So it won`t be long-winded. You only get one super emotional retirement essay, and I used mine up last year, so really, thank you guys so much to every single one of you for supporting me -- my family, my friends, teammates, my competitors.\par \par \uc2 I could go on forever, there`s too many.\par \par \uc2 Thank you guys for allowing me to live my absolute dream. I wouldn`t change a thing.\par \par \uc2 Love you all.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: A lot of sincerity there. This is the second time Tom Brady has announced his retirement, but this time, he says it`s for real.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and you could see that that was tough for him. I love the simplicity of it. I`m going to get right to the point. I`m retiring.\par \par \uc2 He has a lot of fans, a lot of people who care very much about him, and everybody just wants him to be okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is going through stuff, we all know that, too. But I`m glad he has reached a decision that he seems okay with despite the emotion there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: A lot of people saying go into broadcasting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I think he could do that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It seems inevitable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think he that, too. What Tom Brady want to do next?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think he has got a bright future.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I think so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whatever he wants to. Congratulations to Tom Brady. People saying thank you .\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: . for all the years that you`ve given us.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" -- it is a big announcement, too. This is from \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 . It`s her latest project and you`re hearing it here first on CBS MORNINGS. We like when that happens.\par \par \uc2 The former First Lady is launching "\uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 : The Light Podcast." This is the first of eight weekly episodes. It will be available on Audible on Tuesday, March 7th, so that`s coming up soon.\par \par \uc2 The podcast really came out of Mrs. Obama`s book tour for her second bestseller. It`s called "The Light We Carry: Overcoming in Uncertain Times." That book is still flying off the shelves. Look at that picture of \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 . Could you put that picture back just a second?\par \par \uc2 During that six-city tour -- look at her -- during that six-city tour, she sat down with friends like Ellen DeGeneres, Tyler Perry, Oprah, David Letterman, Tracee Ellis Ross --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wasn`t going to say me, but I ran into her in Philadelphia, it was a great conversation, we had a great time. Sold out everywhere she went. Everybody shared personal stories about topics like relationships, issues connected to race, gender, and visibility, adapting to change, and the importance of lighting up for others.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s there for everyone to hear on "\uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 : The Light Podcast."\par \par \uc2 I love the title of the book, too, "The Light We Carry" because she believes, Mrs. Obama believes that all of us carry a light even though you may not know it, everybody carries a light.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Her husband, of course, was the man in the forefront. But when you sit down and you listen to her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: . I saw her in conversation with Oprah in Brooklyn, remember?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I do remember.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The woman exudes a light about her, and there is a wisdom to her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That if you sit and listen, I`m telling you, it is something to hear.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: David Begnaud, you ain`t wrong.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Just my opinion.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s why every four years, there`s a "Draft Michelle" movement for President.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Oh, yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She says, no, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she is very firm. No, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: No, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is booked and busy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Tony D.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is a kind of dispiriting dispatch about Generation Z and money. So unlike our generation, David, and you as well, Gayle, for us to find out just how broke we were when we were 18 to 25, you had to go to an ATM or to a bank teller, and you got the little slip, and it was bad news, right?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s so true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s so true. Now 18 to 25-year-olds, they have perpetual access to their finances on their phone, they`ve got all kinds of apps. They can talk about money in all kinds of new ways. They don`t want to do it at all.\par \par \uc2 In fact, they would rather talk about anything than money. They`d rather talk about politics, they`d rather talk about sex, they`d rather talk about infertility. That`s how afraid of the topic they are. And it is kind a worrying trend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are they afraid of? Not having it or --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They don`t have it?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How to handle it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, they don`t have it. It`s a generation that`s struggling more than others when it comes to accumulating it. But also it`s kind of part of this soft -- they call it soft savings, where you want to live for the now and not worry so much about the future.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you know, the future does come.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So I think it`s concerning.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: More about sex and less about finances.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Okay. Got it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: All right. So, we turn now to wire "Road to the Grammys" series counting down to music`s biggest night -- can`t wait for it.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we have Nate`s conversation with Grammy nominee, Tobe Nwigwe.\par \par \uc2 The hip-hop recording artist and actor is having quite the year. Not only is he up for Best New Artist, this summer, he will also be featured in the new "Transformers" movie, and he currently stars in the highly rated \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series, "Mo."\par \par \uc2 Nate recently set out with Nwigwe at one of his favorite spots in Houston, Texas to talk about his unique sound, his laid back vibe, and newfound celebrity\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I wanted to give you a congratulations on your nomination: Best New Artist.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE (Grammy nominee): Come on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Grammy nomination for you and the squad.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Come on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What did you feel when you heard that news?\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: I felt like, "Impossible."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Day Ones.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If you know hip-hop, then you know Tobe Nwigwe. If you don`t, you will soon.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fye Fye.")\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: There`s a video of this African runner who won a race or something like that, and at the end of the video, they asked him about like, how he felt about it? I feel exactly like he did. He said, "I never expected it." I never expected it, but God did it for me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Born to immigrant parents from Nigeria, the 35-year-old Houston native first found success on the football field as a standout linebacker at North Texas University with a shot at the \uc2 NFL\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But after a career-ending ankle injury, Nwigwe was thrust into a reality he wasn`t ready to come to grips with.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: It was the very first time that I ever had to face who am I outside of Tobe, the football player. I call it the best worst injury of my life, because my only dream ever in life was to make it to the leagues.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There was a moment where you talked about your crutches being out of reach.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You hitting the floor --\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: I had to do some soul searching in all aspects of my life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from #getTWISTEDsundays.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nwigwe used music as an outlet for all that unspent athletic energy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from #getTWISTEDsundays.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Harnessing it into a popular \uc2 YouTube\uc2  series, " #getTWISTEDsundays."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from #getTWISTEDsundays.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Where he`d freestyle over a beat. That`s his wife and collaborator, Ivory Fat Nwigwe working on his hair.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: I said, "All right, cool. I`m going to do something every Sunday."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: And I`m going to do the absolute best I can within them six, seven days, I`m not going to make no excuses, I`m going to be consistent. I`m going to be disciplined.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to persevere through whatever is happening in life and do it, and I did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You did.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from #getTWISTEDsundays.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve been a person, an artist.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That sort has been very transparent about your culture.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: About your love, your relationship with your wife and family. Why did you make that conscious decision?\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: I just felt way more comfortable being 100 percent myself in all aspects of my life. So I`m going to draw from my real experience in my real life, my real situations, and I`m going to put that into music, so that when you see me out, or when you see me wherever, it`s not like, oh, I`ve got to become Tobe, the rapper. I`ve got to be something Tobe, the artist.\par \par \uc2 So when I --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is no code switch switching.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: There`s no code switch. Oh, man. And there is a freedom for me.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Need You To.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You said I put my real life in my music.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There were some current events that happened and one of them being the death of Breonna Taylor. You were doing something authentic.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It went viral. Why did you decide to touch on that topic?\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Because I really felt God was like, yes, you have to say something about this and put it to, whatever kind of beat you needed to put it to, but just say it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: It was the first song I ever put together where I have no wrong words and nothing. It was literally just the state --\par \par \uc2 (Clip of "I Need You To.")\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: "Arrest the cops who killed Breonna Taylor." Everybody who thinks that you need more evidence, yes, you`re really goofed.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Been Broke.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: His authenticity is resonating with both fans and critics. The father of three has built a following largely through social media without much radio play and without the support of a major label.\par \par \uc2 I`ve seen Pharrell vibing with you.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Yes, that`s my friend.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You were kickin` it with Chance the Rapper. I saw you kickin` it with Dave Chappelle. I mean, the list goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Yes, and all of these relationships are truly genuine. People have reached out to me, and I`ve just reciprocated the love that they`ve shown. People just respect the art that we put out, the type of people that we are, and they pull with us. They really rock with us.\par \par \uc2 For me, it`s essential just because it`s who I am.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We spent the day with Nwigwe and his wife, Fat, walking through the grounds at Bayou Bend Collection and Gardens.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Catfish Blackened w/ Grits.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is where they shot the bulk of the visuals for his latest album, "MoMINTs." And it`s on that record where you can hear some of his musical influences.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Lauryn Hill, Biggie, almost every rapper from Houston.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Slim Thug.")\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: In the late 90s early 2000s --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How would you describe your sound?\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: You`re going to hear some Nigerian influence. You`re going to hear some African influence. You`re going to hear some Houston influence.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Lord Forgive Me.")\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: You`re going to hear some street trap influence. You`re going to hear some --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gospel.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Yes. You`re going to hear spirit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Live bands.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Yes, you`re going to hear some soul.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Instruments?\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: Yes, instrumentation. It is just a fusion of my life experiences.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nwigwe built his reputation behind the mic.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: I`m in the new "Transformers" movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, he is bolstering that rep in front of the camera.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Transformers: Rise of the Beasts.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He was cast in the new "Transformers" film due out this summer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mo.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And \uc2 Netflix\uc2  renewed his hit TV series, "Mo" for a second season.\par \par \uc2 But despite going a little Hollywood, rap`s newest Renaissance Man will always be Houston.\par \par \uc2 TOBE NWIGWE: I know exactly who I was before I started doing any of this, and my only advice would be to like, hey, before you start, get active, figure out specifically who you are and what you stand for, what you stand on. Once you have that down packed, go full-fledge and stay consistent, stay disciplined, stay focused, but have a vision and just push forward.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I love his message and that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is still pushing forward.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That is the takeaway. Every one we feature on this show who is doing something great will always say, when I found myself --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And I started living that loudly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: My career soared.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Everybody. Can I just say, icons of men style there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What a power, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They were looking great.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: No kidding.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On the grounds of wherever that place was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cheering for Tobe. I like what Nate said, he will always be Houston. Everybody from Houston says that, too, by the way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Tobe.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Renaissance Man.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Thank you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lot to listen to, lot to look at, too.\par \par \uc2 All right, tomorrow on our "Road to the Grammys" series, we will talk about who will win this year`s music educator Grammy Award. Who is it going to be? We will reveal the name only on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 And of course you can watch the 65th Annual Grammy Awards this Sunday, 8:00 PM Eastern right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and also on Paramount+ -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Guess what? We`ve got a sneaker pioneer, Tony, here in our progressive greenroom. Hello, Jennifer Ford.\par \par \uc2 She is breaking barriers in the industry and making history with \uc2 Nike\uc2  in the process. Love when that happens.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:42. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our series, "Changing the Game" highlights rather extraordinary women and for Black History Month, we are highlighting Black women making a difference in their fields. Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 And now for our future generations, sneakers are a multibillion dollar industry -- I bet you know that -- and Jennifer Ford, remember this name, is the only Black female founder of a sneaker retailer in the US. How is that possible? She owns Premium Goods in Houston.\par \par \uc2 After 20 years in the business, Jennifer was recently invited to design two new Air Force One`s for \uc2 Nike\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is a big deal. The collaboration marks the first time \uc2 Nike\uc2  has collaborated with a woman`s retailer on new designs, and we are very proud to say Jennifer Ford is in the house with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 Welcome and Bravo. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD (Premium Goods founder): Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sneakers are -- to me seem to be having a moment. You can wear them with ballgowns, you can wear them with jeans, you can wear them with dresses, wear them to work. Where did your love start and how?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD: Well, my love for sneakers probably started when I was in junior high in high school when like Jordans came out, but it really made sense to me more so after college when I can afford to buy them.\par \par \uc2 So I lived in New York at the time in the late 90s, early 2000s when sneaker boutiques were starting to open here and you would see them on the West Coast a little bit, in Chicago, but we didn`t have anything in my city at the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I know, but a lot of women, myself included would go to work in sneakers and then change when you get to work.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Put on your real work shoes. Did you do that?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD: Oh, totally. That was my thing. It`s like walking the streets of New York. You don`t want to walk around in heels, and I worked for a company that required you to wear pantyhose and heels to work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD: Very uncomfortable, not the practical thing in the subway, so that`s totally my thing, to take them into work and then change after.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The thing about sneakers now is you can wear them in all of those different settings, but you can also not wear them. They are like works of art, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, people collect them. They put them on a shelf. It looks like a store in their house. Nate has got a whole like display.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he has a whole room.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So talk us about what you`ve designed here. Your two sneakers, your two Air Force Ones.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD: I designed two Air Force Ones. I was given the opportunity to partner with \uc2 Nike\uc2  to do a collaboration and I designed two of them. Based off my career at Lord & Taylor, I used to be a jewelry buyer and it just made sense to me.\par \par \uc2 I love jewelry and accessories, and I wanted to create something that was beautiful, but that you could wear. I believe people should wear their sneakers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD: They are comfortable. There are tons of them. If you`re buying it, enjoy it, feel good in them. So I wanted to create something that I could wear and that would mean something to me and as they developed, they just made -- they both made sense to name them after my daughter and my niece. Just --\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I`ve got 30 seconds for this question.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Your inspiration to girls watching who see you and want to be you.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD: Oh, so it`s -- being the only Black female in the industry and being one of the few that are in the industry, it seems like it`s not an easy career to get into, but it has been a great journey, everyone has been super amazing through it.\par \par \uc2 And I just want girls watching and women watching to believe in themselves and to follow their dreams.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But did you think this could be a business for you? A successful business? Did you think that?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD: I was 26 when I opened my boutique and I thought I could take on the world. I had just left New York and I was going home to be close to my family and do something I love. So honestly, I didn`t think that far ahead into it. I just kept doing something I loved and I`ve developed a great team in the process, and friendships and family through it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Jennifer, guess what? You are still taking on the world.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and I love your advice, buy those sneakers and wear those sneakers. Don`t just put them in the box and keep them fresh and pristine. They are for wearing.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jennifer, I wear a size 10. Thank you so much for coming. We are glad to have you here. Bravo. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FORD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cheering you on.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on your favorite morning show, CBS MORNINGS. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Those are really great.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230201ej2100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Deal Keeping Trains On The Tracks\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Scott MacFarlane \par \uc2 884 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: nationwide rail crisis averted. What it means, as there are new signs the U.S. economy is bouncing back.\par \par \uc2 The deal keeping trains on the tracks, we have the new details, as inflation slows and gas prices plunge. How low could they go before you fill up for your Christmas road trip?\par \par \uc2 Breaking news: middle school overdose scare. At least 10 students treated after reports they ingested edible marijuana products.\par \par \uc2 New details in the quadruple college murder. A grieving father reveals his daughter was killed in the same bed as her best friend, as there are new questions about whether the gruesome stabbings were targeted.\par \par \uc2 Royal drama. Just as the prince and princess of Wales visit Boston, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  drops the trailer for the Harry & Meghan series.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN MARKLE\uc2  (Duchess of Sussex): Doesn`t it make more sense to hear our story from us?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And tonight`s Eye on America. After the nation`s math scores` largest drop ever, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver visits one school that may have the solution.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Do you think this could be a model for schools nationwide?\par \par \uc2 AMALIA CALAFIORE (Teacher): Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the CBS Evening News with Norah O`Donnell, reporting from the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening and thank you for joining us on this busy Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 There`s new information tonight in that mysterious quadruple murder of those college students in Idaho. And we`re going to get to those middle schoolers taken to the hospital in a suspected mass drug overdose.\par \par \uc2 But, first, here in Washington, lawmakers from both parties coming together tonight to avoid a railroad strike that could have had devastating consequences to the U.S. economy. Tonight, the bipartisan bill is awaiting President Biden`s signature. The legislation will raise wages, but the agreement stopped short of giving employees seven days of paid sick leave.\par \par \uc2 The move comes amid some good economic news. Today, the Commerce Department reporting a key inflation rate slowed more than expected, and gas prices keep on falling. A tank of gas is at its lowest since February, with Americans paying, on average, $3.47 a gallon. And GasBuddy is predicting prices could dip below $3 by Christmas. What a gift that would be.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane is here with all the latest details.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Scott.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 This is an economic crisis avoided in the 11th hour. Congress averted the strike late today, but not everyone is pleased Congress intervened at all or with the end result.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voice-over): One week and one day before the deadline, Congress warded off a gut punch to the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (D-Delaware): President Biden`s clear call too us to ensure that we don`t derail Christmas is going to carry the day.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The deal prevents a walk-off by 115,000 railway workers that could have cost the economy $2 billion a day, snarled holiday shipping traffic, and spiked inflation further.\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., hardware store manager Charles Hawkins was preparing for the worst.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HAWKINS (Hardware Store Manager): If you`re a profitable business or just a business in general, once things start to sell, you need to replenish. So it`s very, very important to actually be able to get things delivered\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: President Biden, who built a record of supporting labor unions, defied some of them by asking Congress to intervene.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We`re going to avoid the rail strike, keep the rails running, keep things moving.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Many Republicans argued Congress shouldn`t be meddling in a labor negotiation.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-Missouri): I`m not going to vote to impose an agreement that the workers have explicitly voted on and explicitly rejected.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The deal gives rail workers a 24 percent raise and $5,000 in bonuses over five years, but not seven days of paid sick leave, for which unions had been fighting.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BERNIE SANDERS (I-Vermont): They are one of the few industries in America today that have zero sick paid leave.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A separate vote to add the sick leave failed, a major disappointment for the unions.\par \par \uc2 TONY CARDWELL (President, Brotherhood of Maintenance of Way Employees): It`s unfortunate that this happened, and we`re going to continue to fight the sick leave issue outside a contract.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: President Biden says he will sign the bill as soon as it reaches his desk.\par \par \uc2 Acknowledging labor unions aren`t happy about this, he says Washington has averted what he called a Christmas catastrophe -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020221211eic100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 House Votes to Halt Rail Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Scott MacFarlane, Gayle King \par \uc2 625 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to Washington where it could be up to the Senate to avert a national rail strike that may have devastating consequences for the economy. The House voted yesterday to push through a deal, which was rejected by some unions leaving workers wondering if they will get paid sick leave which they say is essential.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: For Charles Hawkins in the hardware store he manages in Washington, this is crunch time. Holiday sales keep them profitable.\par \par \uc2 This feels like a sensitive time of year for you all for your business.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HAWKINS (Hardware store manager): Absolutely. Absolutely. We pretty much wait the entire year for this.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Even the smallest interruption or inventory shortage can rattle the business, much less a large supply chain disruption from a railway labor strike.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HAWKINS: Once things start to sell, you need to replenish. So it`s very, very important to actually be able to get things delivered.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A nearly century old law gives Congress the power to resolve labor disputes involving the nation`s railroads and President Biden asked congressional leaders to do so, as the impasse between some rail unions and the industry is now perilously close to a December 9th deadline.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The joint resolution has passed.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The House voted on a bill to force through the latest offer to rail workers.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Let me be clear in that a nationwide rail shutdown would be catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: That deal includes 24 percent raises over five years and some bonus payments, but does not include the seven days of paid sick leave for which some unions have been fighting.\par \par \uc2 GREG REGAN (\uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  Transportation, Trades Department President): Our members told us that sick leave is the number one concern, that people are working outdoors, they are dangerous jobs in many cases. So I think, you know, sick leave is an important aspect to it.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The House passed a separate provision Wednesday to provide the sick days, but the Senate would have to do the same and some Republicans say they shouldn`t be involved in the first place.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): This administration is asking the Federal government to use the force of Federal law to impose contract terms that the workers have voted against. So, I`m not for that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: US Labor Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  and Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg meet with Senate Democrats today with averting this rail strike at the top of the agenda. But Gayle, there is still no decision on when the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  will vote.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the clock is ticking on this. Thank you very much Scott.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in the House have made history with their new leadership. Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  of Brooklyn has become the first Black leader of either party in Congress. He will take over from Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , who is you know is remaining in the House after stepping down from the top job.\par \par \uc2 Starting in the New Year, Congresswoman Katherine Clark, she is from Massachusetts will be the second highest ranking Democrat, House Minority Whip; Congressman \uc2 Pete Aguilar\uc2  of California will be Democratic Caucus Chairman.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221211eic10002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Aftermath of Deadly Southern Storms; House Votes to Halt Rail Strike; Inside China`s Crackdown; Mauna Loa`s Creeping Destruction; Christine McVie Remembered; Deadly Force Robots Approved; Former FTX CEO Speaks Out; A Royal Visit to Boston; Netflix Drops Trailer for \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  and Meghan; The Root`s Most Influential African-Americans; The Hardest Part; Peloton`s New Fitness Challenge; Trailblazing Business Mogul; Best Places to Travel in 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Manuel Bojorquez. Scott  MacFarlane, Holly Williams, Jonathan Vigliotti, Anthony Mason, Jeff Pegues,  Vladimir Duthiers, Ben Tracy, Michelle Miller \par \uc2 13,616 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS and welcome to December. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is. We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Deadly storms slam the South. Tornadoes destroy homes and other buildings across multiple states. We will take you to Alabama with the latest.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It was just a loud and tremendous roar. You can even hear the house turn hard. It was so loud.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The house advances a controversial build to block of rail workers strike, now it`s up to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): A nationwide rail shutdown would be catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 MAN: US House is making history. New York Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  will be the first Black lawmaker to lead a major party.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: New controversy for the Royals as William and Kate visit the US for the first time in years.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`ve never actually seen any Royal in person. This the first time. I had to go to Boston.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Music fans everywhere are mourning the loss of Fleetwood Mac`s singer-songwriter, Christine McVie.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This truck smashed into an overpass in Indianapolis and then exploded in flames. Somehow the driver was not hurt.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh. Dramatic video showing the moments a base jumper hit the side of a cliff in Utah.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my God. Is he just hanging there?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Two days after a Lakers fan makes a shot worth $75,000.00, another fan goes home $25,000.00 richer.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`ve got 25,000 reasons why you love that shot.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: San Francisco Police will now have the ability to deploy potentially lethal remote-controlled robots.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They are not going to be roaming around with guns, just explosives.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): This is such a bad idea. Do you know how often robots make mistakes? Can you imagine if Siri had a bomb? It is like, "Hey Siri. Play 21 Savage. Now killing your family. No. No. Wait, which members of my family, but still, no, but let`s talk about it."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trevor can find the funny and anything, but I do worry about a robot that just can kill a man.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ve seen the movies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re going to get into it later in the show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lots to say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. We`re going to have more on that story that is stunning the music world. Tributes, they are pouring in for one of the great voices in the rock world. That is Fleetwood Mac`s, Christine McVie, who has died at the age of 79 very suddenly yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason will share part of a conversation that he had with McVie about her life and career, and we`ll hear how she is being honored by her former bandmates.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, from a legend to someone who`s just getting started, 22-year-old Noah Cyrus, the younger sister of Miley and a Grammy nominee for best new artist. She speaks openly with Vlad about her struggles, life inside one of music`s most famous families and her new album.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Could be a legend in the making.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to be begin with the news though and the brutal weather as we began yesterday, hitting much of the country on this first day of December, a winter storm blanketing large parts of the West as parts of the south clean up from a deadly swarm of tornadoes stretching across at least three States. Two people killed in Montgomery County, Alabama, and that is where we find our Manuel Bojorquez today.\par \par \uc2 Manny, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 We saw the pictures yesterday. What are you seeing today?\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This was the Community Center for this neighborhood, and you can see that it is destroyed. Still it illustrates why forecasters always urge people to seek shelter in the innermost room of a structure. You see those inner walls back there, they are still standing, increasing the chances of survival.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh my God, I`m in it.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: After a night of torrential rain and ferocious tornadoes across large portions of the South.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, Lord.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Daylight illuminated the full scale of the destruction.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And you see the total devastation that comes through with this storm. That`s something, I mean it takes your breath away.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Alabama`s Montgomery County was hit by an EF2 tornado with 115-mile-per-hour wind. The Sheriff there says a mother and her eight- year-old son died in bed when a tree fell on their mobile home Wednesday morning.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Before they could even get the warning that tornado was up upon them. And so, we are praying for the family.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The National Weather Service says a total of 73 tornado warnings were issued throughout the region along with 120 severe thunderstorm warnings.\par \par \uc2 In Utah, Alabama, an EF1 tornado blew the roof off this apartment complex and heavily damaged the Ezekiel Baptist Church.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN LAWS (National Weather Service Birmingham scientist): So we could have a dangerous situation in November as easily as we could March, just as easily we could in January.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Kevin Laws is Chief Scientist at the National Weather Service in Birmingham, Alabama. He says a warming Gulf of Mexico has made the tornado season longer.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN LAWS: It is allowing that rich Gulf air to come north and all it takes is just one strong cold front to come through, and unfortunately, this is the results from it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The father of the child who died in this neighborhood when the tree fell on their home, he survived and is in the hospital. Deputies tell me, despite all the damage here, everyone else in this community is safe and accounted for -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Manny, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to Washington where it could be up to the Senate to avert a national rail strike that may have devastating consequences for the economy. The House voted yesterday to push through a deal, which was rejected by some unions leaving workers wondering if they will get paid sick leave which they say is essential.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: For Charles Hawkins in the hardware store he manages in Washington, this is crunch time. Holiday sales keep them profitable.\par \par \uc2 This feels like a sensitive time of year for you all for your business.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HAWKINS (Hardware store manager): Absolutely. Absolutely. We pretty much wait the entire year for this.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Even the smallest interruption or inventory shortage can rattle the business, much less a large supply chain disruption from a railway labor strike.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HAWKINS: Once things start to sell, you need to replenish. So it`s very, very important to actually be able to get things delivered.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A nearly century old law gives Congress the power to resolve labor disputes involving the nation`s railroads and President Biden asked congressional leaders to do so, as the impasse between some rail unions and the industry is now perilously close to a December 9th deadline.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The joint resolution has passed.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The House voted on a bill to force through the latest offer to rail workers.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Let me be clear in that a nationwide rail shutdown would be catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: That deal includes 24 percent raises over five years and some bonus payments, but does not include the seven days of paid sick leave for which some unions have been fighting.\par \par \uc2 GREG REGAN (\uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  Transportation, Trades Department President): Our members told us that sick leave is the number one concern, that people are working outdoors, they are dangerous jobs in many cases. So I think, you know, sick leave is an important aspect to it.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The House passed a separate provision Wednesday to provide the sick days, but the Senate would have to do the same and some Republicans say they shouldn`t be involved in the first place.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): This administration is asking the Federal government to use the force of Federal law to impose contract terms that the workers have voted against. So, I`m not for that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: US Labor Secretary Marty Walsh and Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg meet with Senate Democrats today with averting this rail strike at the top of the agenda. But Gayle, there is still no decision on when the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  will vote.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the clock is ticking on this. Thank you very much Scott.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in the House have made history with their new leadership. Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  of Brooklyn has become the first Black leader of either party in Congress. He will take over from Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , who is you know is remaining in the House after stepping down from the top job.\par \par \uc2 Starting in the New Year, Congresswoman Katherine Clark, she is from Massachusetts will be the second highest ranking Democrat, House Minority Whip; Congressman Pete Aguilar of California will be Democratic Caucus Chairman.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning some of China`s largest cities are easing COVID restrictions while police are also watching for any new protests. You saw them over the weekend, thousands of people blasting the government`s COVID policies in China`s largest public demonstrations in decades.\par \par \uc2 Now, the government may be trying to defuse public anger and prevent a larger outbreak of protests.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams lived and worked in China for more than a decade and she shows us now how the newest protests bring back memories of a much more turbulent time 33 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "Down with the Communist Party," they shouted on the streets of Shanghai, an eruption of anger against a one-party state in power for over 70 years.\par \par \uc2 ZHANG LIJIA (Journalist and author): It takes courage to go out to the street to protest.\par \par \uc2 (HOLLY WILLIAMS speaking in foreign language.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Zhang Li Jha is a Chinese journalist based in London.\par \par \uc2 She says the last time China saw anything like this was in 1989 when university students led demonstrations in Beijing`s Tiananmen Square.\par \par \uc2 This is the first time since 1989 we`ve seen protesters explicitly calling for the downfall of the communists.\par \par \uc2 ZHANG LIJIA: This is the first time.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: So this is huge.\par \par \uc2 ZHANG LIJIA: This is huge. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Jiang led a pro-democracy protest herself in 1989 in the Southern City of Nanjing and says she was interrogated by the police.\par \par \uc2 And how dangerous is it to hold a protest like that in China calling for the downfall of the Communist Party?\par \par \uc2 ZHANG LIJIA: Very likely, those leaders who lead these chants will ended up in prison or maybe even worse.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The Tiananmen protests ended with a ruthless crackdown. A bloody massacre carried out by China`s military. But Zhang says the upheaval in 1989 forced China`s leaders to give their people more personal and economic freedom. It`s been a recipe for success until recently, with heavy handed COVID restrictions forcing entire neighborhoods into quarantine.\par \par \uc2 ZHANG LIJIA: Chinese people have a reputation being gentle or even docile, but people are not stupid.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: In 1989, did you learn something about the ruthlessness of the Communist Party?\par \par \uc2 ZHANG LIJIA: Certainly, yes. Until the last minute, we did not believe that the government really opened fire to some people and the lesson is, they would do anything to preserve their power.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Zhang Lijia told us if China`s government is smart, it`ll ease COVID restrictions and there are signs that may already be happening. If it doesn`t, she says there will be more protests -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It does seem like we were in COVID lockdowns here just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Hawaii`s erupting Manau Loa volcano may be now threatening a main road. The world`s largest active volcano continues to blast lava hundreds of feet into the air on the State`s Big Island and is showing no signs of stopping.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is near Manau Loa with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Good morning to you. The pace of this lava is slowing down quite a bit as it reaches flatter terrain. Geologists though say they have detected what they are calling a large number of tremors around the eruption sites, which means at least for now, this lava will continue to flow.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Manau Loa continues her fury. Two active vents are shooting fountains of lava into the air and feeding glowing rivers of molten rock down the mountainside.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`ve waited my whole life to see this.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The rare event caused thousands of spectators to jam pack the DKI Highway, which became an impromptu viewing point.\par \par \uc2 What`s going on in your head as you`re watching this glow?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Just new birth, new beginnings.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: But it`s also creating a worrisome scenario for officials.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Thousands of residents and visitors, they are flocking to the DKI and the eruption site to view this current Manau Loa eruption is creating a safety hazard.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Although the flow a slow moving, it is still persistent and emergency managers are ramping up their planning as it threatens to cross over this same highway.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s very high probability that this level of flow if it continues, will definitely reach the road. So, we`re looking at about two days out.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: But despite the risks Manau Loa opposes, it`s a unique chance to see how nature creates new land.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And it`s still unclear how much longer this lava will continue to flow. Scientists say it could be a few days, even a few weeks. For now, this once in a generation event is more of an attraction than it is a threat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It sure is beautiful, but it is also very scary. Thank you very much, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 Now to some very sad news from the music world. Members of Fleetwood Mac and other artists are mourning the loss of Christine McVie. The group`s longtime keyboard player died yesterday after a short illness. That`s according to her family. She was 79 years old.\par \par \uc2 In a tribute, Fleetwood Mac`s lead singer, that`s Stevie Nicks called her "My best friend in the whole world." I love that.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason spokes McVie back in 2017 about her life in one of the most popular rock bands of the 1970s.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Everywhere.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Christine McVie was part of Fleetwood Mac`s classic lineup, the group that sold more than 40 million albums with "Rumors."\par \par \uc2 Do you remember the first time you sang together?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE MCVIE (Fleetwood Mac): I was playing "Say You Love Me." And you and Stevie chirped in with these fantastic background vocals. I mean, I sat there with goosebumps, I could not believe it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Say You Love Me.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: As singer-songwriter and keyboardist McVie, helped to make that magic, but she was working as a window dresser when she got her break in music.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE MCVIE: Somebody walked past the window while I was dressing a dummy and it was a friend of mine who said, "Do you want to join a band?" So, I said, "Well anything beats this."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The band called Chicken Shack had a modest hit in Britain in 1969.\par \par \uc2 There weren`t a lot of female blues singers in the late 1960s.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE MCVIE: We were living in a man`s world for sure back then. But I just loved it. I loved the blues and that was the thing that drove us on. Fleetwood Mac were my idol when I was in Chicken Shack.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The next year after marrying Fleetwood Mac, bassist John McVie, she joined the band and would write some of its biggest hits.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Don`t Stop.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Including "Don`t Stop" and "You Make Loving Fun."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "You Make Loving Fun.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But in 1998, tired of the travel and the feuding, McVie, quit and move to the English countryside, where she had stay for 16 years, until Mick Fleetwood coaxed her back.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE MCVIE: He said, "Why don`t you just come on and do "Don`t Stop" with us. That`s in the end what I did.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fleetwood Mac performance.)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE MCVIE: I am looking around at these other four individuals, it was just a mind blowing experience.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Did you know right away, you wanted to come back after that?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE MCVIE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And she did. She was with the band on its last tour in 2019. In a note on social media, the band said she was the best musician anyone could have in their band, and the best friend anyone could have in their life.\par \par \uc2 Stevie Nicks said in the note that she put on social media that she only learned that she was ill this past weekend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. That`s how sudden it was.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, and I`m told the band did not expect this and obviously, they are just devastated. That`s Lindsey Buckingham who we saw in the interview with her, of course, he is formerly from Fleetwood Mac.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Fans shocked as well. I mean, just what a legacy.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: An extraordinary blues singer, which is, as I said in the conversation with her, there weren`t a lot of blues singers in England in the late 60s, early 70s. She is a phenomenal singer, and what`s interesting is because she is kind of understated, she didn`t get as much attention, as say Stevie Nicks did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But she was such an important part of that band and a phenomenal songwriter as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you hear that music, though and it brings back memories about what was going on in your life at that time.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: There was a lot going on in that band, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was going on in your life at that time, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there was. Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s on your mind, Gayle? What was his name?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re here -- Kenneth.\par \par \uc2 We`re here to talk about Christine McVie. I was very sorry to hear the news. It`s always surprising when it comes, so shocking, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Listen, she is 79. I know it was a good life. It`s still, it still is a shock.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is definitely unexpected.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What a legacy.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, another controversy hits the Royals as Prince William and Kate travel to Boston for a good cause.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up: A controversial crime fighting decision in San Francisco, city leaders giving police the go ahead to deploy robots that can use deadly force. We`ll show you what`s behind it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the CEO of FTX makes his first public comments since the multibillion dollar collapse of his cryptocurrency exchange. Could his statements land him in even more legal trouble? We`ll get into it.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead for you, Vlad`s conversation with musician, Noah Cyrus. She talks about what is it like growing up in a family of famous artists with sisters with a name like Miley Cyrus and a dad with a name like Billy Ray Cyrus.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS (Singer): You know, whenever your dad or whatever is the basketball coach in your high school and everybody is like, "Oh, you`re going to grow up and be just like your dad. Oh wait, your older brother plays basketball, too. You guys are going to be just like him."\par \par \uc2 And it`s like, what`s the competition? It`s not there with people you`re not related to. So it was.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Noah. Hear her emotional words about overcoming past struggles and the key piece of advice that she got from her dad.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, we also call it an invitation, because you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on the go podcast. What do you get? Today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. Look at the three of us there. Cute, right?\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. San Francisco has taken a controversial new step in the use of military style tactics by police departments, and it sounds like something out of science fiction.\par \par \uc2 City supervisors there gave law enforcement their approval to use robots. That`s right, robots, capable of using deadly force in emergencies. It comes at a time of rising crime in the city. But as Jeff Pegues reports, critics say the move could accelerate a troubling trend in American policing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: San Francisco Police are assuring the public that robots armed with explosives would be used sparingly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LAZAR (San Francisco Assistant Police Chief): We weigh out do we want to risk lives in the public by getting ourselves exposed to the suspect? Or can we send a robot in to deal with it?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: The City`s Board of Supervisors voted overwhelmingly in favor of the idea Monday night.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It seems like it would do more harm than good.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Those against it that will raise ethical concerns and cited the impact on people of color.\par \par \uc2 DEAN PRESTON (San Francisco Board of Director): And there is serious potential for misuse and abuse of this military grade technology and zero showing of necessity.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: There are currently more than 1,000 robots and unmanned vehicles in use by police departments primarily by bomb squads.\par \par \uc2 The first time a robot was used to deliver explosives in the US was in 2016 when Dallas Police deployed one against a man who had gunned down five police officers. The man was killed when officers detonated the explosive that the robot carried.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, San Francisco Police acknowledge that it does not have a specific plan in place on when to