st?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I see the list? I think it feels good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Can you see the list? Here you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I haven`t seen the list.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You haven`t seen the list.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at these names.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at all of these names.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And thank you to "The Root."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is very thick.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you to "The Root."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now, you`ve got to go to the party.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad, we appreciate you. And you also have a great conversation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Obama is on the list.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . with musician, Noah Cyrus.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: Grammy-nominee, Noah Cyrus in a candid conversation with Vlad. How she is forging her own path while remaining very close to her very talented showbiz family\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`m Tony Dokoupil. If you know Peloton, you know the name Robin Arzon, that`s their head instructor. Well will first on CBS MORNINGS, she is going to tell us about her new fitness challenge, a challenge for everyone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. BET co-founder Sheila Johnson became the first Black female billionaire in America. She speaks to Michelle Miller about the meaning of success.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She certainly knows. But first, here is today`s Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh my God, I`m in it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The South cleans up after destructive tornadoes tear across multiple States.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The house is gone. Everything else is gone. But we`re still alive and that`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congress intervenes in an effort to prevent rail workers from going on a nationwide strike.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HAWKINS: Once things start to sell, you need to replenish. So it`s very, very important to actually be able to get things delivered.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hawaii`s Mauna Loa volcano threatens new disruption.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: It`s still unclear how much longer this lava will continue to flow. Scientists say it could be a few days, even a few weeks.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fleetwood Mac performance.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Christine McVie of the legendary band, Fleetwood Mac has died at the age of 79.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Spotify Wrapped Day. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Spotify`s personalized yearend wrap-ups give listeners an interesting portrait of themselves.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Thank God Tinder doesn`t do a yearend wrap-up. Just to remind you of all the terrible decisions you made on desperate nights. It`s like, wow, I did a lot of people with pet snakes this year.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I want Tinder to do it. That would be amazing. Please. That would be great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s pretty good. Trevor, I think you`re giving people ideas.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with the evolution of musical artist, Noah Cyrus.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Just Want a Lover.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That song, "I Just Want a Lover" is one of the hit songs from her debut album, "The Hardest Part."\par \par \uc2 So if the Cyrus name sounds familiar to you, and I bet it is, it`s because she is the daughter of country singer, that`s Billy Ray Cyrus and she is the younger sister of popstar, Miley Cyrus, you know them both.\par \par \uc2 But 22-year-old Noah Cyrus is a success story in her own right, thank you very much, earning a Grammy nomination for her Best New Artist in 2021, in that category.\par \par \uc2 So Vlad sat down with her the other day and learned the inspiration behind "The Hardest Part."\par \par \uc2 Vlad, good morning. Vlad, you`ve been in the show three times.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Work hard for money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 So there`s an expanded deluxe version of Noah Cyrus` new album which is set for release tomorrow. It deals with heavy subjects from contemplating suicide to recovering from drug addiction. So, we asked Cyrus about overcoming her darkest struggles, and what it has been like to share that journey through her music.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The album is called "The Hardest Part." Does that mean that the hardest part is behind you?\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: I don`t think so. I think there`s a lot of parts to life and I think a lot of them are pretty [bleep] hard.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To understand what Noah Cyrus has been going through, look no further than the opening lyrics of her debut album.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Noah (Stand Still).")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Take me back to Noah at age 20.\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: Sorry. Noah at age 20, I couldn`t look in a mirror anymore. I didn`t have any love for myself. I was struggling with drug abuse every day. I was terrified and I was just trying to push that all down.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Cyrus talks openly about her recovery from Xanax addiction and told us she was hooked on the anxiety medication for years after it was first introduced to her recreationally as a teen.\par \par \uc2 Her album`s lead track titled "Noah (Stand Still)" marks a turning point in overcoming her struggles, driven in part by advice from her father, Billy Ray Cyrus.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Noah (Stand Still).")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Billy Ray dueting with his daughter on a rerecorded version of the song.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Noah (Stand Still).")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know the advice that people usually give when things are tough, "Just keep going."\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: Just keep going. Just keep going.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just keep going.\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When he told you to just stand still, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: I think a lot of my life and my family`s lives has been go, go, go. You have to let that storm settle and give yourself time to process and time to think.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from of "Mr. Percocet.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Though Cyrus has taken time to stand still, she has also been on the move. The singer-songwriter cross the country to tour her album this fall.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Noah Cyrus performance.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we met her before an intimate acoustic set hosted by the Grammy Museum at the National Sawdust Performance Space in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Noah Cyrus performance.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She told us she wrote that song called "Unfinished" when she was just 15 years old about a love that could have been.\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: As I kept making music, "Unfinished" was still so special to me and it felt like it deserved a bigger moment. It`s an old one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, and you --\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: That is vintage Noah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Vintage Noah.\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Just Want a Lover.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: As she`s honed that vintage Noah sound, Cyrus has shied away from the inevitable comparisons her last name tends to invoke, her preferred analogy, basketball.\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: You know, whenever your dad or whatever is the basketball coach in your high school and everybody is like, "Oh, you`re going to grow up and be just like your dad. Oh, wait, your older brother plays basketball, too. You guys are going to be just like him." And it`s like, what`s the competition? It`s not there with people you`re not related to.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Noah Cyrus performance.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Cyrus` growing pains are prevalent theme in her music, old and new.\par \par \uc2 I know you`re 22 years old, but I don`t get that you`re 22 years old from your music.\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: No, I`m like 50.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Did you enjoy being a kid?\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: I don`t think I let myself enjoy being a kid at all. No. What I`m trying to do is get back to who that person was and doing the things that made that younger Noah happy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Those things Cyrus told us include horseback riding. She was a competitive equestrian athlete growing up, and the passion is a recurring motif in her music videos.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Burned LA Down.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She said, she has used the same horse in several videos as a tribute to her childhood horse, Constantine.\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: I`m always riding out of this trauma or this pain with Constantine.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re so transparent in your music about that trauma and pain. Do you get that being who you are is perhaps what draws people to you and to your music?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Noah Cyrus performance.)\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: And I have a hard time connecting with that because I`ve had such a hard time connecting with myself in the past. I don`t know what it is. Like I have a hard time accepting that love from other people.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You think that you`re worthy of it?\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: I do. I think everybody is.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Noah Cyrus performance.)\par \par \uc2 NOAH CYRUS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What I was driving out there is because she is 22 years old, but she`s an old soul.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, wiser -- she seems wiser and more mature and certainly older than her years. I like her voice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She has an incredible voice, the music -- and again, when I came to the story, I didn`t know a whole lot about her. But as I started to research her and I listened to all of her music, I was hooked. I was drawn into this haunting voice that she has on most of her recordings and that song "Stand Still," I urge everybody to go and listen to it especially the rerecord one with Billy Ray.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I liked a picture of her and her dad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Her and her dad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How would you describe her singing style compared to Miley`s? It`s different.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s different and I think she is charting her own path. It`s a -- to me, her music is introspective. It`s very transparent. I think she is one of those artists that when you listen to her music, you get a little bit of an understanding of who she is as an individual.\par \par \uc2 A lot of other musicians, what they project in their music is often not who they are right there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? Especially in rock and roll, you`re always projecting to be cooler.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sure. Rockstar.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: . and more amazing than you are. But in her music, I feel like when I listened to it, I get a little inkling into her struggles, her triumphs and just who she is deep down in her heart.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, but doesn`t want to be compared to Miley. You know, it reminds me of Beyonce and Solange. They are both very great singers there in their own right now. But they are sick of being compared to --\par \par \u