r \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like bread will make you spread.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, okay. All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What were you thinking, Tony? Like bread puts weight on you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes, okay, all right. Yes. Okay, never mind.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, are you okay?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m good. You know, I am --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got to have tissue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, Tony. Tony, everybody in the room got it. Everybody in the room got it. Tony is like, "What?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You all right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re thinking about something else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, moving on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, my "Talk of the Table" is "Friends" star, Courtney Cox - - you know her -- surprising some of her fans on their tour of their Warner Brothers Studios in Burbank, California. That`s where "Friends" was filmed. Here`s the setup.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Just sit around on the couch if you can. I am going to ask a few questions, take a few photos of you.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MAN: So I am going to start with a quick photo, and then we will get into some questions. Sorry, my phone is not working. Can I use your phone for a second? All right, ready? Three, two, one. Three, two, one. Three, two, one. Great.\par \par \uc2 All right, first, tell me if you guys like it because I want to make sure that you guys actually like the photo.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: There is somebody.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I am going to cry right now.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Can you tell me if that`s good?\par \par \uc2 Is that a good photo?\par \par \uc2 MAN: No. You`re playing with me.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s Monica.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Courtney and her photographer. Those of course are tears of joy from the fans who were thrilled and very surprised to say the least. So Cox says she was filming on the lot and she just decided, hey, I`m going to go over and surprise some of the people.\par \par \uc2 I love that she did this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tears of joy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, tears of joy and I love their reaction. It`s such a good photo bomb because you have no idea that it is coming. Nicely done, Courtney Cox.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is that iconic couch right there, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is also so sofa related.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A new study shows if you want to be where the action is, forget being a fly on the wall, you want to be a couch. Here is what I mean.\par \par \uc2 There is a study that was commissioned by a sofa company surveying 2,000 people in Britain. It found that the average couch stays in a home for about eight years. That seems about right.\par \par \uc2 During those years, the couch will endure 316 drink spills, 401 naps, and more than 8,500 hours of TV. All right, that seems about right there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, the researchers say there will be an average couch that will witness 311 arguments, then that couch will host 286 episodes of couples making up, if you know what I mean.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was wondering how you are going to do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, what Tony was thinking earlier.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: About the spread. But yes, so a lot of cuddling on the couch as we like to say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you`re right though --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When you think about couches, that is where all the action is, usually in people`s homes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes, it does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes, I would not want to be a couch cushion.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Tony, get your mind out the gutter. We`ve got 30 minutes left.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know what you do on your couch when you`re sitting there alone in the room, okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is true. This is true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. No. That`s not what I`m talking about. I`m not talking about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. I`m going to go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am talking about if you have a nice bowl of beans, okay?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay. I`m going to go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Beans?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Turning now to pioneering business -- I`m going, I`m going.\par \par \uc2 Shawna goes, "Move on now." I am going.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Turning now -- I didn`t do that -- turning now to pioneering businesswoman and pro sports team owner that`s Sheila Johnson. She is part owner of the NHL`s \uc2 Washington Capitals\uc2 , the NBA`s \uc2 Washington Wizards\uc2 , and has a stake in the Washington Mystics of the WNBA. You go, Sheila.\par \par \uc2 Johnson, who cofounded BET was recently named CEO of the Year by "Washington Business Journal" and she owns five star resorts around the world. She is a badass.\par \par \uc2 "CBS Saturday Morning" cohost, that`s Michelle Miller spoke with Johnson about how she became the first Black female billionaire and the personal challenges she overcame along the way.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON (Businesswoman): Go Caps.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: When it comes to the \uc2 Washington Capitals\uc2 , Sheila Johnson is what you call a proud vocal partner.\par \par \uc2 Did you ever see yourself as a professional sports mogul?\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: No. I wouldn`t call myself a mogul.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Still, the 2018 Stanley Cup Champ scored an ultimate trifecta for her.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: I don`t know when things are going to work and when they`re not going to work. I really trust my instinct.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Making Johnson the first and only Black woman with an ownership stake in three professional sports teams.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: I have the wonderful WNBA national champions 2019, Washington Mystics.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Drop the mic.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: I have the Wizards which we`re still working on, for a title, and then we have the 2018 NHL Capitals.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And how does that fit in?\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: It`s my side gig. It`s my side gig.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Her story begins in Maywood, Illinois. Her mother, an accountant; her father, one of only 11 Black neurosurgeons in the country.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: You talk about learning resilience, we had to move 13 times because my father could not practice in White hospitals, and everyone asked me, you know, moving that much, what was your childhood like? I thought it was an adventure.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Her first act in life was as a concert violinist, but it was in 1980 that Johnson took the first step in building an empire when she cofounded BET, along with her ex-husband.\par \par \uc2 Talk about those early days, what was your vision?\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: The whole purpose of starting BET was to give the African- Americans a voice. It was the birth of cable, and everything that was being pumped out there from CNN to Nickelodeon, you name it, nobody talked about the importance of the African-American voice.\par \par \uc2 If I could do it all over again, my voice could be listened to. I think the network would have looked a little different.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: More news, less entertainment. But by 2001, the network sold to \uc2 Viacom\uc2  and cemented Johnson in history as the first Black woman billionaire.\par \par \uc2 You don`t like that title.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: I don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Why?\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: Money is not everything. It`s how you use what you have to make things work and make your life work.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Her life took a turn when she divorced in 2002, a pivotal moment where she realized she lost herself in her marriage.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: You did kind of withdraw into the shadows and take on their identity and one of the biggest lessons I`ve learned in life was that shouldn`t have happened. I had lost sight of who I really was.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You emerged after the divorce.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: It took me two years, but I figured out finally who I was. It was like a rebirth.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Shortly after, she remarried, and that rebirth became the jumping off point for her hospitality empire that now includes everything from a PGA Golf Course to resorts around the world, including the Salamander in Middleburg, Virginia, a place she now calls home.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: It`s buzzing.\par \par \uc2 BETSY DAVIS (Middleburg, Virginia resident): She had this idea --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Betsy Davis has lived in Middleburg her whole life and says since the opening of the resort in 2005, this area has seen a growth in business by almost 70 percent.\par \par \uc2 BETSY DAVIS: There`s a difference in thriving and surviving and we will always survive, but this energy that has helped us to really thrive.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: A stone`s throw from horse country is the nation`s capital, and a cause close to the longtime equestrian creating a better life for police horses.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: The living conditions of these horses was unbelievable. We saw rats in the green areas where you know they get their food. You can see the desperation in their eyes and they had been in those weeds since 1976 with the purpose that they were going to get a new home, but it never happened.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And she did.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: The here is where the --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: By helping to raise over two $22 million to build these new stables.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: People don`t realize, yes, you`ve got the nation`s living room here, but this living room has to be protected.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Using her empire to give back is at the core of Johnson`s work, a far cry from the little girl whose childhood dream was to take the classical music scene by storm.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: My mother always told me to never say can`t, she hated that word that you can`t do something, and I think especially for young women, they need to find out who they are first.\par \par \uc2 And this is a very important path.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Johnson says she is now in her third act, and is the happiest she`s ever been.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: I don`t regret anything that has happened to me personally or anything because I wouldn`t be sitting here talking to you. Everything that happened to me has made me who I am now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And it`s been a big year for Johnson. She acquired the prestigious Mandarin Oriental Hotel here in Washington and she might be adding another sports team or two to her roster. Both the \uc2 Washington Nationals\uc2 , the baseball team and the Commanders, the football team could be up for sale by the end of this year, and she and her partners, they`re high up on the list of potential buyers -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Another -- how about that, Michelle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Michelle, you captured her beautifully. Listen, I have such admiration for her. She is such a badass, and she is such a class act. So we know it seems the divorce certainly was very difficult for her. But she came back with a vengeance.\par \par \uc2 She found new love. She is now a huge, huge force in the business industry. You really did her justice. So nicely done.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Thank you. I mean, she`s very -- she is transparent and humble.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You know, you don`t find many humble multimillionaires out there, but she is one of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, man. She`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, well done, Michelle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, nice piece.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, if you`re thinking about taking a trip in the New Year, we`ve gotten you covered.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`ll reveal "Conde Nast Traveler`s" new list of best places to go in 2023.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With December already here, many people are dreaming about where to travel in the New Year, and a recent survey found that 58 percent of American travelers have a leisure trip planned in the New Year and 51 percent have a trip plan to visit friends and family.\par \par \uc2 So if you`re looking for inspiration, "Conde Nast Traveler" is out with their new list, the 23 best places to go in 2023. It is featured in their December issue.\par \par \uc2 Only on CBS MORNINGS, "Conde Nast Traveler`s" Jesse Ashlock joins us to reveal their travel destinations for the New Year. Jesse, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK ("Conde Nast Traveler" Deputy Global Editorial Director): Great to be back with you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So there`s kind of the aspirational and the attainable, and the aspirational, there are some big overseas trips here. You`ve got Western Japan, British Columbia, and Queensland, Australia. Why are they on the list?\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK: Well, Western Japan and Queensland, Australia are both places you couldn`t visit the last time we did this. So a big thing is, borders reopening on this list, which is super exciting, and we`ve also got Auckland, New Zealand. We`ve also got Taiwan. But I`d say Japan is the place that our readers are most excited about.\par \par \uc2 But Japan is usually an urban experience and everybody discovered nature again during the pandemic. So, we`ve showcased Japan`s nature with the Seto Inland Sea where there are all these new Onsen Ryokan where you can relax in a bath and look at the snow-covered mountains and the rice terraces.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve always wanted to go there. You`ve also got old classics with a new look. What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK: Well, number one on that list would be Egypt where finally at last, the Grand Egyptian Museum is open next year right in the shadow of the Giza Pyramids. And perhaps fittingly, it`s the 100th Anniversary of the discovery of Tutankhamun`s tomb.\par \par \uc2 So everything from Tutankhamun`s tomb is in that museum, as is 16 artifacts that The Met returned to Egypt this year and a whole bunch of other stuff. It`s going to be the largest archaeological museum in the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anything in this country?\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK: So I should add that we did a list -- a second list that`s all this country, so for readers who are budget conscious or don`t want to travel too far from home, you have tons and tons of options.\par \par \uc2 One of my favorites because I was just there is the Hudson Valley just Upstate New York. And super beautiful bucolic locations where the Hudson River School painted and so perhaps, it is appropriate now that it is becoming a prime wellness destination.\par \par \uc2 Auberge Resorts has a new place where I stayed, it is beautiful, called Wildflower Farms. The famous Mohonk Mountain House is just sweet on their spa and I am sure you`re familiar with Ranch Malibu.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK: Yes, so hello to the Ranch Hudson Valley. It`s coming next year.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really? Okay. What about Memphis, Tennessee?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Central California.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Central California.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We hear about Northern and Southern, but this is new.\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK: Central California. So, I`m a California boy and I`m not really that familiar with this part of California, and so, it`s a place I want to go. You know, Paso Robles has been kind of the wine lovers that you have on the files radar, but it`s becoming a more well-rounded travel destination and the Santa Ynez Valley has these cowboy towns.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to plug Auberge again because they have a property called The Inn at Mattei`s Tavern. And it`s this cool historic site that used to be a stagecoach stop in the 1800s.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that.\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK: So that`s a prohibition era hangout, and now you can stay there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Macon, Georgia?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I haven`t heard anyone say, you know what, honey, we need to go to Macon, Georgia. And I am not mocking Macon, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK: Our America list is full of places like that that I`m learning from and I`m very excited about, so Macon, Georgia, birthplace of Little Richard, birthplace of Otis Redding, and now the home of Mercer Music at Capricorn, which is a music center and museum dedicated to the heritage of this place, the musical heritage of this place.\par \par \uc2 Just outside of town, Ocmulgee Mounds historic site is a place where these earthworks that are thousands of years old likely to be our next national park.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good variety.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jesse Ashlock, thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK: Pleasure to be with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you bring extra magazines?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 JESSE ASHLOCK: Oh, yes. I`ve got one for you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. I really want one.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: we are still talking about where do we want to go? You can go your own way. Listen to Jesse`s suggestions. I definitely want to go to Japan and Egypt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are still remembering Christine McVie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Macon, Georgia is on my list now. I mean, it was under the radar, but under the radar no more.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I`ve never been to Australia. Have you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a long flight.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Long flight.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve never been.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it`s worth the trip. And play your Christine Fleetwood Mac songs today, a way to remember her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, today is the day for it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow. Idina Menzel will be here.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221201eic100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Severe Storms Slam the South; Powerful Storms on the Move; Oath Keepers Leader Found Guilty; Biden Asks Congress to Halt Railway Strike; Senate Passes Landmark Marriage Act; New Evidence in \uc2 Delphi\uc2  Killings; Team USA Advances at World Cup; Trump Dinner Backlash; Tracking Hawaii`s Mauna Loa; Twitter Rolls back COVID Misinformation Policy; Hero Dog Saves Owner`s Life; Life-Changing Discovery; Gabrielle Union Live; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Manuel Bojorquez, Jim  Cantore, Scott MacFarlane, Weijia Jiang, Nikole Killion, Roxana Saberi,  John Dickerson, Jonathan Vigliotti, Vladimir Duthiers, David Begnaud, Drew  Barrymore, Ross Mathews \par \uc2 14,954 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King. It`s the last day of November.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight: An outbreak of severe weather brings heavy snow, rain, and high winds, including destructive tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Stormy skies and some of those storms could become severe. Large hail, high winds, a tornado risk as well that`s quite significant.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For the first time in decades, two people are convicted of conspiring to overthrow the government in a plot ending with the January 6th attack.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): It is a very serious charge.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A landmark vote from the Senate as lawmakers pass a bill to protect same sex and interracial marriages.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Rail workers unions slam the President as congressional leaders signal they will support a bill to block a looming strike.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Not an easy call, but I think we have to do it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: New images showing Hawaii`s Mauna Loa volcano roaring to life. Fountains of lava as high as 60 meters.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: During a Lakers game, a woman is shown on the big screen, took off her prosthetic leg, and she chugged some beer out of it.\par \par \uc2 ADAM SANDLER (Actor): They were like, "Can we write your speech, daddy?"\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Gotham Awards in New York City, Adam Sandler Lifetime Achievement Award, his teenage daughters wrote his acceptance speech.\par \par \uc2 ADAM SANDLER: Most of the awards on his trophy shelf are shaped like popcorn buckets, blimps, or fake mini Oscars that say "Father of the Year."\par \par \uc2 MAN: Making a big run, it is meant for him. Dest snuck in behind. Dest and a little bit of Pulisic -- scores.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): The US played Iran in the World Cup. It was win or go home and we won, baby.\par \par \uc2 Sorry, sorry. It`s soccer. I forgot. Hold on one second.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even if you don`t pay attention to soccer in this country, people yesterday we`re going "We won. We won."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Losing their minds.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was very exciting to watch.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Big, big win.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Survive and advance. Survive and advance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s huge. All right, we`ve got a big show for you.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 A lot to get to today, including the story from our David Begnaud, a story about determination, a story about love, and it begins with a woman`s search for her biological parents, but it is not about what she discovered. This is a story about what she did next, and you`re going to want to stick around.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plus, friend of the show and friend in general, that`s talk show host, Drew Barrymore, always has got a lot on her mind. She`ll join us today along with Ross Mathews. We`ve got a special edition of "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We also have Gabrielle Union who will be joining us and she is getting serious awards buzz for her new role in her new movie, "The Inspection." And the film has an important message that she wants to share.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a very good movie. She is very strong and I am looking forward to that conversation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I don`t even recognize her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we`re going to begin with the news as we always do.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news overnight: Severe storms wreaking havoc across multiple States in the South. Take a look at these pictures coming in the aftermath of a confirmed tornado strike in Mississippi. All of it part of a dangerous system forcing many to shelter in place.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking Louisiana, Alabama, and Tennessee all in the danger zone overnight, and Manuel Bojorquez has made his way to Utah, Alabama for us.\par \par \uc2 Manny, good morning.\par \par \uc2 What are you seeing where you are?\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 We are at an apartment complex here in Utah and in the background, we can make out where one of the buildings appears to have the roof shorn off by the system, the storm system that came through this area. The reason we can`t get you any closer right now, all of these power lines that are down in this driveway area here, but also as far as the eye can see on this block.\par \par \uc2 The National Weather Service so far has confirmed more than 20 tornadoes across the region. It was a long night of sirens and warnings across parts of the South.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Overnight, a ferocious downpour over Starkville, Mississippi. Police and first responders blocked off a rain-soaked street in the eastern part of the State where county officials say several shoppers were temporarily trapped inside a damaged grocery store.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Even in the midst of tragedy, God`s worked.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: And in Steens, a late night tornado ripped trees from their roots and left these homes in tatters.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I mean, literally that.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: And in Lowndes County, this Fire Department wasn`t spared. It took a hit with its roof being torn off.\par \par \uc2 That tornado just one of many reported and part of a dangerous system that whipped up continuous heavy winds, pounding rain and quarter-sized hail fell across parts of the South.\par \par \uc2 In Memphis, police and crews were busy all night. Intense rain caused enough accidents to litter highways and streets. Several of those crashes caused by slick roads and hydroplaning.\par \par \uc2 In Caldwell Parish, Louisiana, at least two people were injured in a reported tornado strike that destroyed several homes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: So far, there are no immediate reports of deaths. The National Weather Service will have teams out in the area assessing damage like this to try to determine the strength of those tornadoes -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Manny, we thank you. Not looking good in Alabama right now.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, Jim Cantore from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking the storm and finding out what`s next.\par \par \uc2 Jim, good morning.\par \par \uc2 You saw the pictures. What are you thinking?\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Yes, Gayle, another destructive tornadoes -- Louisiana, Mississippi, Alabama. We`ve already got more than two dozen reports of tornadoes in through here and that number is going to go up unfortunately, even reports this morning of fatalities in Alabama -- reports of fatalities in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 We`re at the tail end of this thing now, but we`re not done with the system itself. So, let`s talk about that as we watch a lot of wind and rain energy head into the Northeast today.\par \par \uc2 And if you remember the day of Sunday, which was a big travel day after Thanksgiving, think, okay, here we go again with a big wind and the delays and of course, the rain that will be moving in, and it`s not just the southerly winds, which will be the dominant story today, but those winds will turn around from the West, it would be very, very cold tonight. And we will have heavy lake effect snow not in Buffalo, to the south of Buffalo and up into the Tug Hill Plateau where it could accumulate to a foot.\par \par \uc2 Our focus quickly shifts to the West, all right. Several weather systems coming in. This is just the first one and look at the snow in the Sierra all the way up through the Cascades, even Seattle could see accumulating snow on the ground, albeit it will be less than an inch.\par \par \uc2 Busy weather pattern these days, Gayle. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jim, I know you like the weather, but sometimes the weather just is not your friend and we just all have to deal.\par \par \uc2 We thank you.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: No, it`s not. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, the leader of the Oath Keepers, a far-right political group is facing many years in prison. Stewart Rhodes and other members of the group were convicted of multiple charges by a Washington, DC jury yesterday. They were accused of plotting the assault on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is outside the Federal Courthouse in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning. I hear that rain in the background.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Yes, Nate. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two years after the attack, a jury has convicted five members of the far-right Oath Keepers on Federal charges, including the founder, Stewart Rhodes on a Civil War era charge of seditious conspiracy of plotting to block the peaceful transfer of power in America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: It was a marathon two-month long trial involving Stewart Rhoades, founder of the far-right Oath Keepers, the most high profile figure to date to face charges in connection with the deadly January 6th riot.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors showed messages and images of the Oath Keepers hatching a plan to equip themselves with tactical gear and to stage guns outside the Washington, DC city limits to mobilize if then President Trump invoked the Insurrection Act.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN (Former US Attorney): He was responding to things that the former President was saying that makes it a titanic event that will be surely in the history books, this conviction for seditious conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Prosecutors also argued Rhodes talked of Civil War and was the architect of a plan that included some members of the Oath Keepers breaching the capitol in a military stack formation. It was all laid out in the audio presented at trial.\par \par \uc2 STEWART RHODES (Oath Keepers): Stay fully armed and prepared to go in on armed if we have to.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Rhodes took the stand in his own defense, arguing the group brought gear because they feared being targeted by left-wing agitators, but he didn`t sway the Federal jury that convicted both Rhodes and a top deputy, co-defendant Kelly Meggs on multiple counts, including seditious conspiracy. Each man facing up to 20 years in prison on that charge alone.\par \par \uc2 The jurors has found three other Oath Keeper co-defendants not guilty of seditious conspiracy, but convicted them on other Federal charges.\par \par \uc2 Defense attorneys told CBS News they plan to appeal.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It goes without question we`re disappointed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A conviction for seditious conspiracy isn`t just rare. The Department of Justice hasn`t won one in nearly 30 years and several other accused far-right group members face trial of that same charge in the coming weeks, and Gayle, one of those trials will likely be underway here January 6, 2023.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all remember that date. That verdict says a lot. Thank you very much, Scott.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is facing backlash from some progressive allies for his efforts to avoid a national rail strike. Rail workers are threatening to walk off the job. They are asking for increased pay and paid time paid time off. But President Biden is asking Congress to intervene to stop this possible strike in order to protect the economy during this very busy Holiday shipping season.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang at the White House with more on the story.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, good morning. Things are getting very tense in this situation.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning, Gayle. Good morning to everyone.\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, bipartisanship is such a rarity these days that when there is some, it is a big deal and Democratic and Republican leaders agree that a rail strike would be devastating to the US economy and that is why they are scrambling to prevent one.\par \par \uc2 President Biden sat down with the top four congressional leaders for the first time since the Midterm Elections. He told them it was not an easy call, but he thinks they have to halt collective bargaining to protect the economy.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said the House would vote today to adopt a tentative agreement that the administration brokered back in September. Four unions have voted against it.\par \par \uc2 The contract includes a 24 percent raise over five years, but workers want paid sick leave which progressives support. Senator Bernie Sanders tweeted that he intends to block the bill saying of the paid sick leave: "Please don`t tell me that the rail industry can`t afford it. Rail companies spent $25.5 billion on stock buybacks and dividends this year."\par \par \uc2 If rail workers walk off the job, that could mean even more financial pain for the average American already dealing with high inflation. It could wreak havoc on the supply chain and delay getting goods to store shelves, which might lead to prices going up on just about everything right before Christmas.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said that he is working to get enough votes to pass the legislation. He is not sure if he`s there yet. If Congress does not intervene, the strike could start next Friday -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Weijia, we will be watching. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Another measure in Washington getting bipartisan support these days, a historic step to protect marriage equality. Twelve Republicans joined with every single Democratic senator to approve legislation cementing Federal protections for same sex and interracial couples.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: The bill as amended, has passed.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: As the Senate approved the Respect for Marriage Act.\par \par \uc2 TIM LEKKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: A thumbs up from Justin (sp?) Kettler and Tim Lekker (sp?), who have been watching the legislative process play out for weeks from Dallas.\par \par \uc2 TIM LEKKER: We are moving in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The couple runs a home design business and has been married since 2017, two years after celebrating the Supreme Court`s landmark ruling legalizing same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN KETTLER: And that added security and protection and rights that are inconsequential to so many people, except for us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TAMMY BALDWIN (D-WI): What a great day.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Wisconsin Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  is the first openly gay senator in US history and sponsored the legislation to codify marriage protections into Federal law.\par \par \uc2 What does this legislation mean to you?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TAMMY BALDWIN (D-WI): I have in my public life been fighting for equality, fighting for freedoms and progress, and we want to just make sure we protect that moving forward.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The bill does not legalize same sex marriage nationwide, but does require the Federal government and States to recognize legal same sex and interracial marriages performed in other States. It also includes religious liberty protections, but Republicans were split.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE LEE (R-UT): This legislation will put the weighty thumb of government on the scale against religious organizations and individuals.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROB PORTMAN (R-OH): LGBTQ rights can coexist with religious freedom protections.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The vote was prompted after the overturning of Roe vs. Wade sparking fears same sex marriage rights could be next.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN KETTLER: It shouldn`t be this difficult or this complicated. It is two adults who love each other fighting for the same rights.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The House has already passed a similar measure and will take up the Senate version as early as next week. There`s been a real push by Democrats to try to get this done now during the lame duck session before Republicans take over the House Majority next year which could make it tougher to pass -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nikole, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now an update on the former cold case of two teenage girls. Thirteen-year- old Abigail Williams and 14-year-old Liberty German were murdered in \uc2 Delphi\uc2 , Indiana five years ago.\par \par \uc2 Last month, prosecutors charged Richard Allen of \uc2 Delphi\uc2  with killing the girls. Now, a Judge has ordered the release of some of the evidence against him. Those documents show investigators found an unused bullet near the girl`s body and connected it to a gun found in Allen`s home. Officials say Allen couldn`t explain why the bullet was found there. He also said he never let anyone use the gun or borrow it. Officials won`t say how the girls died.\par \par \uc2 Allen has pleaded not guilty. His lawyers filed a motion yesterday to move the trial to a new location.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At the World Cup in Qatar, Team USA is still alive. Did you see this game? But it was not easy.\par \par \uc2 Its star player, that`s Christian Pulisic scored the only goal in a tense one to nothing win -- they call it zil over there -- one to zil over Iran yesterday, sending the US into -- oh, nil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nil.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I was debating whether to step in behind that one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s so funny, Tony because I saw you on the corner my eye and I said, "Did I say something wrong?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m hardly a soccer expert. But I don`t think anyone says zil.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, the way I was reading it. I`m sorry. I had to go whoop.\par \par \uc2 Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, zero and nil.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Zero -- yes, okay -- sending us into the second round of the tournament. My bad. I just got excited, I screwed it up.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi is in Doha, Qatar`s capital city. Roxana, listen, as you see, I know very little about soccer, but I was watching the game yesterday. And boy, it was nerve racking.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: It was, Gayle. I was inside the stadium here in Doha last night to witness what was pegged to be the most politically charged soccer match in decades.\par \par \uc2 The mood inside was tense, but also electric.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: And we`re underway.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Even before the first whistle.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Pulisic got there with the header.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The young US team knew --\par \par \uc2 MAN: He has to drive it cross --\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The snatch was all or nothing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Making a big run. It`s meant for him. Dest snuck in. Dest and a little bit of Pulisic -- scores.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Christian Pulisic opted for everything giving fans in Doha and across the US a one to zero victory, but colliding with the Iran`s goalkeeper, the 24-year-old star forward paid a price tweeting this photo from a hospital after the game.\par \par \uc2 His team saying he`s been diagnosed with a pelvic contusion or bruised pelvis. But he made it back to his hotel to celebrate. Iran`s wants players now head home to the turmoil that had followed them here that played out on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 With pro-regime supporters seemingly out in full force, social media videos appeared to show them harassing anti-regime protesters.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They attacked us. They attacked this child.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: They attacked me.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Some Iranian-Americans rooted for the US.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We feel like if we support Iran here, we are supporting that regime and that`s not something we want to do.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD cheering.)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: But many fans here said that for 90 minutes, soccer swept politics aside.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s important for us to just enjoy this beautiful sport and bring both cultures together and really just -- people were all here to do it, right?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The US now advances to the next round. It`ll play the Netherlands on Saturday in a knockout match -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Roxana Saberi, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to watch on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What a game. You`re going to watch, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually am. I actually am. As long as they`re in it, I`m going to be watching as long as they`re in it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We talked about it yesterday. We needed a point. We needed to win. We needed to score.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We couldn`t tie and USA got it done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They did not do that. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got soccer fever and the temperature is rising.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that. All right, coming up, potential danger from the eruption in Mauna Loa, we`ll go to Hawaii for the latest on the possible threat to the surrounding island.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s just about 7:18. It`s time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead in just a moment, Republicans are reacting to former President Trump`s dinner with White nationalist, Nick Fuentes and rapper, Kanye West now known as Ye. Of course, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  is going to be here to talk about the fallout from that meeting between the candidate and two quite public antisemites.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead, David Begnaud shares the inspiring story of one woman on a mission to find her biological father. After decades of searching, she finally located him.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES (Adopted as an infant): My heart was beating out of my chest. I was like, what is this going to be like? And is he going to accept me?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Right.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: Is he going to want to see me? Is he going to want to talk to me at all? Is he going to say you can`t be my daughter? You know, I didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What happened next, guys, was so special that David jumped on a plane to Florida so he could tell this story himself. You`re going to like this one.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back now at 7:30 and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is defending himself against a Republican backlash over his recent dinner with two known antisemites.\par \par \uc2 As you may know by now, Trump hosted White nationalist and Holocaust denier, Nick Fuentes at Mar-a-Lago last week along with the rapper, Ye, formerly known as Kanye West.\par \par \uc2 And then yesterday, House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy and Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , publicly condemned the meeting.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I don`t think anybody should be spending any time with Nick Fuentes. He has no place in this Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): There is no room in the Republican Party for antisemitism or White supremacy, and anyone meeting with people advocating that point of view, in my judgment, are highly unlikely to ever be elected President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: CBS News chief political analyst, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  is here.\par \par \uc2 John, exactly the person to talk to on a morning like this. You know, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  did not say Donald Trump`s name, which is noteworthy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What else stands out to you in what might have been a familiar kind of episode from the realm of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the last five years?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well, that`s what stands out is how familiar it is, that even when you come up against antisemitism, there is this collective silence at first about taking him on either because Republicans are afraid of taking on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because he has littered the roadside with Republicans who have tried to take him on, on moral issues and he has won.\par \par \uc2 There are no moral slam dunks with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his party, in part because of his supporters who defend him and because we`re in a time where Republicans` the worst sin is giving aid and comfort to the other party. So, if by criticizing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on no matter what the issue, it looks like, you`re giving aid and comfort to Democrats or the media, then they rally around him.\par \par \uc2 And there is no one Republican whose responsibility is to uphold these standards. You might say, well, on antisemitism or racism, it`s everybody`s job. But that`s not the political world we`re in right now in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But can we just look at the history for just a second of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He won for President. He won the presidency by saying racist things. That didn`t seem to stop anybody. What does it say?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well, that`s why I said to Tony, this seems very familiar. I mean, think about it. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ran for President saying that \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was not born in America. So, that`s just a straight up racist play.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Then as a candidate before he is elected, he said that a Judge of Mexican heritage couldn`t judge him fairly, which Paul Ryan at the time said was a textbook statement of racism. Then \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was ambivalent about the KKK and David Duke, and that that was reprehensible. All of those things were said and he was still elected.\par \par \uc2 After Charlottesville, this was -- he also -- the President said something that \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  said you are making it morally equivalent between people carrying Nazi flags, and the people protesting those flags. None of that hurt \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, is the thinking for the Republicans, well, I`m not going to say anything, because if you do, you say it at your own peril.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : You say it to your own peril, and also, what they know is that when they criticize \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he doesn`t say what -- doesn`t do what previous politicians would have done in an instance like this, which is recognizing, oh, I did something bad I am going to shrink away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m sorry, I was wrong.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Right. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  runs in the middle, grabs the lightning rod and stays there. No Republican wants \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the middle of the conversation, who worries about the national reputation of the party.\par \par \uc2 And they saw the downside of that in the 2022 elections, where Donald Trump`s candidates and his persona hurt the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I worry about this behavior, though, John being normalized that after a while, we`ll just think, well, it really isn`t that big a deal when it still really is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it still really is wrong.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But what the former President is saying is, you know, oh, I didn`t know which is another familiar kind of talking point when he has been accused of playing footsie with White nationalists before.\par \