y, January 6, they said was legitimate political discourse.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , there has been this thing where he crosses a line, and then his supporters basically just lower the line or change the line. They`ve changed the standard, which is what normalizing is.\par \par \uc2 So does this get normalized or do Republicans say because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seemed to have been somewhat toxic in the last election, this is -- it`s kind of a combination. It`s both a moral failing on Donald Trump`s part, but also because he is no longer a winner, then they can speak out and it`s more powerful for them to do so. Or it`s like every other instance in which there`s a lot of chatter, and then it goes away and he still retains the supremacy in the middle of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: John, you`re the best. We appreciate you. You can make the most complicated topics very digestible for us and the viewers.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well, thank you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, Hawaii`s tallest volcano is red hot this morning. We`re in Hawaii with a potential danger from Manau Loa`s first eruption in decades.\par \par \uc2 And here`s a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. You know what you get? Today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That`s a great deal.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The world`s largest active volcano, Hawaii`s Mauna Loa is attracting a new rush of tourists after its latest eruption. Look at those pictures. That`s real stuff.\par \par \uc2 So far, this spectacular display is not threatening any populated areas. That`s good, but the Governor has declared an emergency.\par \par \uc2 Mauna Loa`s neighbors are preparing now for any possible impact.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti has the latest from Ha