ermination that led to a life-changing discovery. Deanna Shrodes who was adopted as a baby was building a family in Florida, while at the same time trying to learn about her own origins.\par \par \uc2 So after years of searching, Shrodes met her biological mom who died before revealing the identity of her biological father.\par \par \uc2 Our lead national correspondent, that`s David Begnaud spoke with Shrodes about getting answers and answering the stranger`s prayers in the process.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The life story of Deanna Shrodes was a story without a beginning. At least she didn`t know the beginning, but she was determined to find out.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: I have to know where I started, and I have to know who else out there is a part of me. That desire never, never, never left.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Shrodes was adopted as an infant. She was 27 years old when she found her biological mom.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: She was not open to meeting me, and then I went and met her personally. I just showed up knocked on the door.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So you meet her, and you guys end up having a relationship for how many years?\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: Twenty years until she died.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You had one question for your mother, which you kept asking.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And it was?\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: Who is my father?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And her answer was?\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: I`m going to go to the grave with the name. I had no idea she was sick, had no idea that there was cancer brewing. Weeks later, she was diagnosed and a couple of months later, she was dead.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: She took it to the grave.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: She took it right to the grave.\par \par \uc2 Just give me a call.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Now, the story almost ended right there. But her mom did tell her two important things about her dad. He was Greek and from Richmond, Virginia. So, she set out creating a private \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group Finding Mr. Greek. She gathered a group of friends and volunteers who would help search for him and she signed up for multiple DNA registries, hoping that science would lead her to her father.\par \par \uc2 How many years do they try to find Mr. Greek?\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: Well, it`s been 10 years. I got maybe five matches on my father`s side. So I`m like, "This is terrible." Like we don`t have anything to work with here on the DNA side.\par \par \uc2 Just open yourself up for just a new level of healing and restoration in your life.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: She is an ordained minister. So, she prayed a lot.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: I told my husband I told my best friend, Laura (sp?), I said, "Listen, guys, you might think I`m crazy, but I was in prayer. God spoke this to me. Your father`s name is Gus."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Well, sure enough, in May of this year, after nearly a lifetime of wondering, her prayers were answered. There was a match to a cousin she never knew she had.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: I reached out to this cousin and I said we`ve just matched on \uc2 23andme\uc2 , and he said I think you`re my Uncle Gus` daughter and I said, I think that, too.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: His name is Gus.\par \par \uc2 Gus Nicholas, 92 years old. He lived in Richmond all his life. A bachelor who was never married. He was a retired ballroom dance instructor.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: I prepared myself to find a grave and I now found a person and it was just absolutely mind blowing. I couldn`t believe that I had found a person.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Shrodes called him the very next day.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: My heart was beating out of my chest. I was like, what is this going to be like? And is he going to accept me? Is he going to want to see me?\par \par \uc2 When I found him, he was so excited to be found. He said, "I woke up this morning, and I was alone. Now this afternoon, I have a daughter. I have a son-in-law. I have three grandchildren. I have great grandchildren." He said, "I`m not alone in the world anymore." And I said, "No, you`re not. You`re not."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Within a week, she was at Gus` bedside.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: Well, I`m here.\par \par \uc2 GUS NICHOLAS (Deanna Shrodes` biological father): I`m glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: I`m glad I`m here, too.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: As it turns out, just four months before they met, Gus had fallen at home. He was found lying on the floor. He could no longer care for himself. So the State placed him in a nursing home and that`s where they first met.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: Gus would say to me, "Please, don`t let me die in here. Don`t let me die in here." And I said, "You know what? I`m going to make sure."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes, they are father and daughter, but still strangers, which makes what happened next absolutely remarkable.\par \par \uc2 Deanna said to Gus, "Would you like to come and live with me?" To which Gus responded: "How soon can we go?"\par \par \uc2 And so 56 years after she was adopted, and 75 short days after she first called him, Shrodes and her husband, Pastor Larry Shrodes took Nicholas out of that nursing home and drove him to their home, right outside Tampa, Florida.\par \par \uc2 GUS NICHOLAS: Okay, you got the orange juice?\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: They now provide for him full time. She cooks his meals, Greek he always prefers and she feeds them to him.\par \par \uc2 Her guest room is now his bedroom. There are the mementos that he asked her to bring from the Richmond home. His trophies, dancing shoes, favorite hat, and a picture from the good old days.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: I love you so much.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Ninety-two years of memories are packed into this 80 square foot room. It is just big enough for his hospital bed, but intimate enough for the conversations they have.\par \par \uc2 GUS NICHOLAS: I knew your mama for six to seven years, I knew her.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Hi, Gus.\par \par \uc2 I even got a chance to say "hello."\par \par \uc2 How does it feel to have your daughter find you?\par \par \uc2 GUS NICHOLAS: It feels good.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUS NICHOLAS: I didn`t my daughter, my daughter found me.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: She found you.\par \par \uc2 GUS NICHOLAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: To the man who gave her life, she is prepared to give back to him until the end of his. She`s asked just one thing of him, "Will you tell me all about you?"\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: It is the most worthwhile thing I`ve ever done in my life. It`s the hardest thing. It`s the most worthwhile thing. It`s the most incredible miracle I`ve ever had the privilege to live out.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I`m living the dream.\par \par \uc2 GUS NICHOLAS: Isn`t that wonderful?\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHRODES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: David, what a story. I mean, she took such a risk just knocking on the mother`s door, saying, here I am. And then to find Gus.\par \par \uc2 I believe that stuff happens where you hear a voice and it tells you something. And it turns out to be the real thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it`s Gus. It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I have to tell you, guys. I heard this story on a Saturday morning from a viewer who reached out to me and said, David, you`ve got to hear this story. I called her right away and two hours later, I said, if I can convince my bosses to let me go do the story, we`ll be there right away. And sure enough, we did.\par \par \uc2 It is remarkable. The journey, the determination she had and to find that man and then say, let me take care of you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take you. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I was just so moved by it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love him laying in the bed saying, "Did you have the orange juice?" Already, they have such a connection, and I can`t help but notice that sign above the bed, too that says, "Give it to God and go to." I know you noticed it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Give it to God and go to sleep."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: David, I`m curious. Did Deanna ever find out why her mom wanted to go to the grave with Gus` name?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not tell.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Oh, I knew somebody would ask that. Good question. She doesn`t know for sure. She thinks it`s because her mom, God rest her soul, didn`t really want to share her with Gus. There may have been some resentment between the two of them dating back to when they first met, and she felt that`s why her mom wanted to keep it a secret.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did Gus even know that she existed? Did he know?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He did. My understanding is he did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m glad they found each other and certainly the way they found each other.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right on time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We love it when viewers send you story ideas and we really love it when the bosses sign off on it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Keep them coming.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Keep them coming. David reads his DMs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We love the happy endings, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we love a happy ending.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: David Begnaud, thank you very much, my friend. See you again soon.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, after Gabrielle Union winning critical praise for her new film, it is called "The Inspection." Ahead, she`ll join us to talk about the complicated role and the movie`s powerful message.\par \par \uc2 But first, 8:10. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up: Drew Barrymore will join us for a special edition of "Talk of the Table" and we`ll find out what makes her TV show so successful. I know a few things and a few reasons why.\par \par \uc2 And Sarah Gelman from Amazon Books is hanging out with Vlad and Gayle. She has some great ideas on parenting books for our "Vlad to Dad" series.\par \par \uc2 I can`t wait for Vlad to become a dad. We would say, "Who is your Vladie? Who is your Vladie?"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, I`m going to do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know you are going to do that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am totally going do it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, you know those graphics. They are among my favorites because we are continuing our new series of "Vladie to Daddy" where we help --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You have a little spin on it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s still "Vlad to Dad," but I am going to say --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Vlad to Dad." I love it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Vladie to Daddy." We`re going to help our own Vlad Duthiers prepare for fatherhood. It`s right around the corner. And today, we are highlighting books for people, actually who have children of all ages.\par \par \uc2 Whether you are an expecting parent or couple or whether you`ve got teens going off to college, we`ve got something for you.\par \par \uc2 And a couple of the books we`re about to talk about are published by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , which like CBS News is a division of \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 , but a lot of these books are not as well.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Amazon\uc2  books editorial director, Sarah Gelman joins us now for more on all of it.\par \par \uc2 Vlad is here as well to soak in the knowledge.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hello. Yes. I`m ready for it.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN (\uc2 Amazon\uc2  Books editorial director): I feel a lot of responsibility right now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The expectations are very high.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Oh my gosh, I hope I don`t mess up your child. You learn, you cannot mess up your child.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I believe that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will get to that in a bit.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will get to that.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes. We will get to that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, okay, so Sarah, book one is called "Good Inside" and it`s by kind of an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  famous parenting guru.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Right. So this is Dr. Becky Kennedy or Dr. Becky as her followers call her, and she really rose to fame during the pandemic. She has 1.5 million followers on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh wow.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: And she believes that parents and children are good inside, and it is really this look at how she wants us to stop correcting our kids and instead really connect with them. And she takes very specific examples, things like problem behaviors like sleep or whining and sibling rivalry and gives examples for those.\par \par \uc2 I will say that I started reading this book, I use some of the tips with my kids and they worked. So, that gets my stamp of approval.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But like when you say not correcting children, I mean, it`s good manners to keep your elbows off the table, right? It is good manners to stand up when a lady walks in the room.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: But there is a different way to deal with it. You don`t like flick the elbow anymore. It`s a different way. I think our parents were maybe a little more focused on --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, go for the shoe.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Take the shoe of and --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad has a dark childhood. Sarah, just to prepare you for that fact.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re talking about that little pinch where you`re like "I told you to."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Don`t embarrass me in the store."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That actually hurts. I had a thing the other day with favorite daughter Kirby and her son is 14 months. And I said "No, Luca. No." And she said, we don`t say no.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I do that to my mom, too. It drives her crazy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You tell him what he should do. I go, oh --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How did you feel when you heard her?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, what did you --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Annoyed. We can`t say no, don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But anyway.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: So, I actually feel like "Good Inside" is a book that I would maybe give to my family, to my parents because it seems very gentle and you want people to understand what you`re doing. There`s actually a reason behind it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The other book, we can`t actually even show you the cover of the title because it`s got some profanity in it.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And so we can only say the title.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It is "You Can`t Eff Up Your Kids" by Lindsay Powers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: And Lindsey is actually --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you like this why?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I love this book. So Lindsay is actually an editor on my team.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That title.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, it`s very provocative.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: But before she joined my team, she wrote this book and she was the guru behind this No Shame Parenting Revolution. And she is -- you have that friend who you want to take on vacation because they do all the research for you and you just show up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Lindsay is like that friend, but for parenting.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: She believes that we overstress way too much as parents and that kids are resilient, and if you love them, they`re going to end up okay. And she takes on some pretty controversial topics like drinking during pregnancy, she looks at screen time, she looks at the effects of divorce on kids.\par \par \uc2 And I just feel like this book gave me so much help. It`s a great book to give to someone who is pregnant. She has some very liberating advice on pregnancy and childbirth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I believe she is right about that. And by the way, guys, the reason we can`t say the title, that`s the Federal Communications` --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: FCC anyway.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you`re a mom of two boys.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I am.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This next book is for parents of two to seven-year-olds.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Exactly. So this is "How To Talk So Little Kids Will Listen." And it is actually --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this title.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It is based on the bestseller "How To Talk So Kids Will Listen and Listen So Kids Will Talk" and one of the authors of this book, Joanna Faber is the daughter of the original author of the best-selling book.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes. So this is a book -- it just gave me a lot of hope as a parent that I could be more patient and just a better parent with my kids. It takes specific situations like going to the grocery store with your little kids, which can be very challenging. It tells you how to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 And my favorite thing about this book is that it has an entire chapter devoted to neurodivergent children who have different needs than non- neurodivergent children.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this other book is focused on raising independent kids and you say it reminds you of 80`s style parenting. We were talking about, 70`s style parenting, grew up watching "Gilligan`s Island" and "Star Trek" and reruns because we were at home.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I was a latch key kid.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gen X with the latch key like generation.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, and you stayed out and played.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And look at me now.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Until the lights came on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean like.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at us, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at all of us. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: So, Lenore Skenazy is the author of this book and she actually became infamous when she wrote in her "New York Sun" column that she let her then nine-year-old son ride the New York Subway all by himself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At what age? What age?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nine?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: He was nine. A mature nine and yes --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony, how do you feel?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a little early. I mean, a mature nine. I don`t know. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: She was called the world`s worst mom.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I remember these articles. There was a lot of chatter in Brooklyn about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I think the times are different now. I don`t think you could do that.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I don`t think that was that long ago.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: But she believes that we just protect our kids too much and that we should give them freedom to roam and that that`s better for them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s true.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, she says this thing that she thinks that instead of baby proofing our world, we should world proof our baby and I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When we were young, we used to go on a real-do adventures, all day long.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Our parents really didn`t care.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They didn`t know where you were. They had limited questions when we got back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We were out in the woods like.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are like, look, he came back. He made it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You were gone. I didn`t even notice you were out of the house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sarah Gelman, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up. Gayle`s got a story that will make your blood boil about a porch pirate who was caught on camera.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got to see you. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hey, look who is in our progressive greenroom. It`s Drew Barrymore and "Drew`s News" co-host Ross Mathews, also known As Rossi Ross.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Hi. Rossy Ross.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that she calls you that.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: I know, every day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are going to join us for a special segment of "Talk of the Table" -- you guys have something picked out? Yes or no?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: We have our stories.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: We`ve got stories. Did our homework.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got stories, too.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I love them, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We love that they`re here.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: It works. Look at that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, graphics.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know how it goes. We are very excited because we`ve got a very Special Edition of "Talk of the Table" today.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined by Drew Barrymore, host of "The Drew Barrymore Show." You know that, and her "Drew`s News" co-host, that`s Ross Mathews.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Like our "Talk of the Table" and "Drew`s News," Drew and Ross talk about their favorite stories of the day.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Drew Barrymore Show.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s one of my favorite segments of the show. So we`re joining forces to bring you some of the stories that we are all talking about this morning.\par \par \uc2 And Drew, you`re in pole position. I love that you looked at the graphics and went, graphics.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I do think they matter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They do.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I always notice them and I`m going to kick it off today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What have you got?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: All right, finding love can be tough in any town or city, but where you live might play a bigger role than you think.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is a new study from WalletHub on the best and worst cities in America to be single. The study ranked more than 180 cities on factors like the percentage of population that is single, the average price for a two-person meal, and the number of attractions.\par \par \uc2 And at the bottom of the list are Fort Smith, Arkansas; Hialeah, Florida; Brownsville, Texas; and Glendale, California.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to go the best cities. I don`t want to go there, Drew. What are the best cities.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Well, you might need a warning on some of the worst. Apparently, Warwick, Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 This study found that the top five best cities for dating are Portland, Oregon; San Francisco; Denver; Madison, Wisconsin. And at number one, Seattle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Seattle.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: If you`re wondering about New York City, it was ranked number 151 out of 182. Gayle, that might explain a few things to us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a lot of concrete hearts in the concrete jungle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It sounds like it.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: One thing they said that I really liked is pick a city that has things that you`re interested in. If you`re a hiker and you like nature, like go to that city; if you`re a city person, move to that city.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Makes sense.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: So that was a good tip, Rossie.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Right. But are there any cities that it`s great to be single in? No, I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Because you love, love.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: I love, love. I can`t help it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Sorry not sorry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ross, how about Seattle though?\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Hey, that`s where I`m from. Go Sea Hawks. I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s where I am from.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: I know. We were just talking about it at the break.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" is a story about how it`s never too late in life to reinvent yourself.\par \par \uc2 Meet Van Gogh, a homeless senior dog who like his famous namesake only has one ear. And his shelter says he was rescued from a dogfighting ring. They say he is very friendly and always happy, but no one wanted to adopt him.\par \par \uc2 So they had to get creative. You`ve got to market these dogs and they had Van Gogh, start painting. Look at that.\par \par \uc2 They put some globs of paint on canvas and covered it in plastic wrap and then coated that with peanut butter and Van Gogh went wild. And while he was snacking on the peanut butter, he was actually painting his own version of "Starry Night," and the shelter held an online auction for Van Gogh`s painting. And they sold out.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Look at it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at the dog`s work.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: The dog did that with a peanut butter and best of all, Van Gogh was adopted on the last day of the auction. This is so special.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a happy ending.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: I work with the Palm Springs animal shelter. I live in Palm Springs when I`m not here with Drew in New York and there are so many dogs out there who need homes, and .\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Well, that`s a great way to get creative and make a story. I mean Van Gogh is my hero.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so glad that you explained that there was plastic because I thought they`re letting the dog eat the paint? I was confused.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I was thinking that at first, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So I`m glad you explained that Ross. That`s a good one.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I thought the same thing.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes, like it cannot be --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is somebody that we all know around here, MSNBC`s Stephanie Ruhle.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Love her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Love you, Stephanie. I know, I love her, too.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Beyond.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Calling out the thief -- this is so good -- who stole some packages outside her home here in New York. Security cameras show the person opening the gate -- look at this guy -- walking towards the door. You can see the Holiday Grinch lean in. I am sorry we blurred out his face because -- go online, you can see his face.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can see the full face, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I want people to see this guy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Somebody knows the guy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Somebody knows him. Please turn him in. to on Stephanie`s Twitter -- pick up the boxes before leaving packages in tow.\par \par \uc2 So Stephanie wasn`t at home, she wasn`t at home obviously. She`s not going to let it slide without a little retaliation. So, she tweeted this. This is my favorite part: "To whomever stole the packages in front of my door, I hope you have good use for my daughter`s Christmas show costume. It`s too late to get another and she will be absolutely heartbroken. And if you get really sweaty in the sports clothes that you stole, that`s not sweat, it`s kids tears."\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I think it is the time of year where people are going on people`s property, taking stuff.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: There`s a lot of stories about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am so glad, Stephanie that you called it out, and that we put it up and please go to her \uc2 Twitter\uc2  so you can see with this because I think somebody needs to turn him in.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I want to help her get her costume now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s what I was thinking. I was hoping another parent maybe got two or something.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes. Maybe someone can sew. We`ve got wardrobe departments. We can help.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Like, when is the show?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Let`s get Project Runway on the phone.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . on the world. All right, so my "Talk of the Table" which I teased earlier in the show is about my yearend Spotify list.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Artists, genres, and songs I listen to the most. Now, a lot of people are thinking, you`re a 40-something dad. You probably listen to country music. I`m not really interested in your list.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you would right based on the genre, top five genres. Do we have that chart? It says country is number one, contemporary country is also there, number four.\par \par \uc2 So you`re bored. But to my surprise, despite this genre list. It might give you a certain idea about me. Here are my top five songs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What have you got here?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: They were very cool.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: I love this. You`re sharing this.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: You`re very cool, Tony.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nothing but Harry Styles. And not only --\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not only are those my top five songs, but Spotify informs me that I am in the top one percent of Harry Styles listeners worldwide.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I am so proud of you.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Gayle and I went to Harry Styles` concert together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We did. So we approve of this list, because that album is so good.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: And the concert was like transformative.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it was so good.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: It really was. It was more than a concert.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at Tony, Drew in that cool dad --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at Tony.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, look at him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, Tony, you did a profile on him years ago before he really blew up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Before he blew up, we had a lot of time with him. He`s a lovely man and he is a hell of an artist.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: And human sunshine.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes, he is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Personified, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Nate, you have. favorite story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I have a good story for you.\par \par \uc2 So we like to do stories about Florida men, because we have one here at the table. But my talk that tape was a lawsuit over how long it takes to make a certain mac and cheese.\par \par \uc2 A woman in Florida has filed a proposed $5 million class action suit against Kraft-Heinz the maker of the Velveeta cheese and shells -- shells and cheese that is. She accuses the company of false advertising and fraud.\par \par \uc2 So, here`s the thing. On the label, the box says, "Ready in three-and-a- half minutes," but the lawsuit claims that`s not true at all. The 15-page - -\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fifteen pages.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Fifteen-page document points out that you have to remove the lid in the cheese sauce pouch, add the water to the cup, stir then microwave for three-and-a-half minutes, and you`re still not done. You still have to add the contents of the cheese sauce pouch.\par \par \uc2 The woman claim she was duped.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: and all of this takes much longer than proposed. CNN reports that Kraft-Heinz calls the lawsuit frivolous.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Just a little.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just to wait and sit down and take several seats, that`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, if you look in the box.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it is supposed to be three-and-a-half minutes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`re a parent and you`ve got kids, they`re hungry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Or you`ve got to be somewhere.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I mean, by the way, I just learned something I didn`t know Kraft owned -- well, that makes sense, Velveeta -- or Heinz, well, Kraft and Heinz, that`s a monopoly and I just love Kraft macaroni and cheese.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: It`s her favorite thing.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I do love the Velveeta shells and cheese though.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Sure.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: If I`m going to --\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: You`re human.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes, but I didn`t know it was all in the same family.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: No. But I like how she takes it seriously. It`s like if my pizza is not here in 30 minutes or less, I am seeing you in Court.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I will see you in Court.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Gayle, you`re the master of the microwave. Come on, now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s frivolous.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope that the lawyer that wrote that 15-page complaint is giving a little talk at a Law School. That would be a packed auditorium.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Well, we are talking about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Maybe she has got a point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not a -- joke.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What are you working on lately? That`s a big case.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to have you guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Can`t really get into it.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: We love you guys. Thank you so much for having us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Great to have you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Drew Barrymore, Ross Mathews, lovely to have you here. Appreciate it, guys.\par \par \uc2 All right, "The Drew Barrymore Show" airs weekdays on stations all around the country. Check your local listings. You guys are laughing at each other.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: We are excited we are here with you.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Up next, we`re excited about our next guest as well. Actor, producer, and best-selling author, Gabrielle Union. She`s in our progressive greenroom talking to Vlad and talking about her new movie with us, "The Inspection." It`s good and she is great in it.\par \par \uc2 But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Being Mary Jane.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I so miss that show. That`s Gabrielle Union. She won the NAACP Image Award for Outstanding Actress for her portrayal of Mary Jane Paul on the hit series "Being Mary Jane." It was so good.\par \par \uc2 Over her three-decade career, Union became a powerful force on screen and movies like "Bring It On," "Bad Boys 2," and the reboot of "Cheaper by the Dozen."\par \par \uc2 Now, she is receiving rave reviews -- with a capital R -- for her new film. It`s called "The Inspection."\par \par \uc2 It`s based on the true story, the film`s director and writer. His name is elegance Bratton. So the movie focuses on Ellis French, a young gay man who joins the Marines during the military`s "Don`t Ask Don`t Tell" era. Union portrays French`s homophobic mother, Inez.\par \par \uc2 In this preview, Inez confronts her son after he asked for his birth certificate for his military application. Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip form "The Inspection.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that movie was tough. Gabrielle Union is also executive producer of the film. We welcome you.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION ("The Inspection" actor): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re so glad to have you in studio, Gabrielle Union.\par \par \uc2 Now, Elegance came to you because he wanted you to be the executive producer, and then he asked you to star in it and you balked at first. He clearly saw something in you that you didn`t even see in yourself.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was it?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: He said -- he`s like, it absolutely has to be you. We`ve known you could do this the whole time. And immediately I knew what he was talking about. He`s like in the Black community, we know you have range. Don`t get it messed up if they don`t see it. I see it and I know you can do this. He`s like, but it has to be you.\par \par \uc2 And I didn`t know what that meant at the time, but he had such a huge level of confidence in me that it became contagious and I was like, I can get through it. Yes, I can.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you say this. You said it came at a time when you had low self-esteem about the industry, low self-esteem about who you are as an actor, as a human, and being a mom.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you mean, Gabrielle?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Yes. I think, you know, when you`re constantly being barraged by images of perfection, everyone`s perfection, right, you start feeling like, I`m not adding -- it`s not adding up. I`m not keeping up in any capacity in my life. I just feel like I`m underwater in every area of my life and I`m drowning.\par \par \uc2 So for a man like Elegance to have that kind of confidence in me was really my needed boost, and not just in Hollywood, but in my life. It was -- it started me on the on the path of trying to figure out what kind of woman I want to be for the next 50 to 60 years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, thanks for that transparency. A lot of people can identify with that. It does take somebody telling us, you know how to swim for you to figure it out. You say that you had to find the humanity in Inez character? How did you do that? And was it difficult?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Yes. I had to do a lot of research. I had to do just a huge deep dive, and not just into who this woman was. It`s a real person. That`s a daunting task in and of itself.\par \par \uc2 But I had to go inside myself to find her humanity and try to figure out at what point do we intersect? What can I relate to about this woman that I, generally speaking, don`t tend to find a lot of positive, out of folks that behave like this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You couldn`t be more different, right? I mean, in your real life, you`re an LGBTQ advocate.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Polar opposites.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As a mother, as a person in the world and this is a mother who is so staunchly against the LGBT community that she is rejecting her son.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Her own child. Well, I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was so hateful, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Oh, gosh, it`s awful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So opposite of who you are and what you believe.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Yes. And when you`re so focused on the world, thinking you`re good, and a good mother, you`ll be willing to barter with anything just to be thought of as good, worthy, deserving.\par \par \uc2 You get on that assimilation training. You don`t know when to get off. And the next thing you know, you`ve hit a brick wall, and life is over and you`ve been shaped shifting and tap dancing your whole life, and you know, for people who wouldn`t spit in your direction if you were on fire. But that`s what I had in common with her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Gabrielle -- in common with her.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: I wanted that. I wanted that kind of deep validation from the world.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: And that was how I got into the character, that kind of sameness, that darkness does live within most of us. And I found that darkness within myself as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wouldn`t spit on you if you are on fire. Yes, that -- they really don`t like you when they`re behaving that way.\par \par \uc2 But let`s talk about the rave reviews that you`re getting in this role. Honestly, they are saying --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well-deserved.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well-deserved, star turning, Oscar worthy. Do you see yourself differently after playing this character? Has it changed how you feel and see yourself as an actress?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How so?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Once I was able to deliver, I was like, you know, I mean, like I`m an athlete.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re competitive.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: I come from an athletic background.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: So it`s either you do it or you don`t?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: You know what I mean? Either you won or you lost? I`m very black and white about that. So by the last scene, because we said we kind of shot it sort of in order. The last scene was, you know, I`m never giving up on you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: And when that ended, and I got back to my room. "Uh."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I nailed that.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: That`s how it`s done.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Damn right.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: But I gave myself permission to dream of other roles. I always kind of play things that are closer to me, because it`s easier, it`s safer. My good friend, Sanaa Lathan, she said, 20 years ago, baby, if it doesn`t scare you, it`s not worth doing. I thought, well, that`s a terrible route. I`m like, I want to do things that make me comfortable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t you think people see you differently too?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Immediately.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Immediately.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Immediately. As soon as the movie ended in Toronto at the Toronto Film Festival, the calls coming into my team were different. Like I`m not even up to audition for roles like this. It`s not even like I had the opportunity to try and fail.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: You`re on certain lists. I am just not on the serious actress lists. I am -- you know. Well, I wasn`t just thinking of myself as a producer on this. My list of people who would play, Inez. I wasn`t on that list. I didn`t see it in myself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I`m glad Elegance convinced you, I`m so glad because you knocked it out of the park.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You really did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I can`t wait when the Oscar nominations come out to see what you`re going to wear. That`s what I say.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to put it out there in the universe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talk about it.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Claim it. Claiming it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats. Congrats. And our best to \uc2 Dwyane Wade\uc2  who tattooed your initials on his wrist --\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Finally. I was like okay, like, okay, I get Martin -- I get that I came after Martin Luther King. But every number that you`ve ever worn in life like including like AAU and Pop Warner like, can I be like the 17th tattoo and he got a tiny little one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there it is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So where is tattoo at?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Oh, his? Oh, on me? Never.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, okay. I was just asking.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: It`s right here. This is it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. All right.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: This is it. I couldn`t even get a second ear pierced, I was like, that`s too risque.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations to you and our best to Mr. Wade.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gabrielle Union`s movie is called "The Inspection." Jeremy Pope also was here, knockout in this. It is in theaters now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wherever you like to go.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221130eibu000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Will Republicans And Democrats Come Together To Avoid A Work Stoppage Just Days Before Christmas?\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Ed O`Keefe \par \uc2 585 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, here in Washington, members of both parties in Congress are vowing to avoid a nationwide rail strike that could potentially derail the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 We get the latest now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): In his first post-election meeting with top congressional leaders, President Biden today said lawmakers need to put the brakes on a potential railroad strike.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s not an easy call, but I think we have to do it. The economy`s at risk.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: A strike could start next Friday and cost the U.S. economy at least $2 billion a day, the White House saying nearly 800,000 workers could lose their jobs within two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Among the many potential supply chain disruptions, shipments of coal, lumber and grain and some holiday gifts. Gas station owners say it could hard to keep fuel in stock. A tentative deal was reached in September brokered by the White House.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : This agreement is a big win for America.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But, in recent weeks, workers in four unions voted to reject that deal, saying it didn`t include enough paid sick leave. With talks at an impasse. Mr. Biden said he was forced to call on Congress to step in.\par \par \uc2 Peter Kennedy, who represents one of the rail workers unions, said he and his members are disappointed in the president, who`s traditionally stood with organized labor.\par \par \uc2 PETER KENNEDY (Rail Union Negotiator): I respectfully disagree with him and how he`s going about doing this, because what he`s doing is taking away the members` right to strike.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The current deal, which includes a 24 percent pay increase over five years and an additional paid day off, will now go before the House tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI (D-California): We must avoid a strike. Jobs will be lost. Even union jobs will be lost. Water will not be safe. Product will not be going to market.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: By law, Congress can vote to block rail worker strikes, and they have done so 18 times in the past. But this deal still has to go to the Senate, where, tonight, Bernie Sanders is vowing to block it unless he gets a vote on an amendment that would give rail workers seven days of paid leave -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ed O`Keefe with that new information.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 There`s also breaking news tonight. The Senate has just taken an historic step, passing legislation that would enshrine same-sex and interracial marriage rights into law, giving them federal protection. The vote to pass the Respect for Marriage Act was bipartisan, 61-36, with several Republicans joining every Democrat in improving -- in approving the bill. It now heads to the House, where it`s expected to pass as early as next week.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020221203eibt0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Allegations Of Human Rights Abuses Become Part Of The Story Surrounding The World Cup\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Holly Williams \par \uc2 661 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Allegations of human rights abuses are also part of the story surrounding this multibillion-dollar sporting event. Labor advocates say migrant workers who built the massive soccer stadiums endured unsanitary conditions in extreme heat. Injuries and deaths have been reported.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Holly Williams spoke to a widow whose husband died at a construction site.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voice-over): Qatar won the right to host the soccer World Cup 12 years ago. A Middle Eastern nation, roughly the size of Connecticut, Qatar is one of the wealthiest countries in the world, and it`s poured more than $200 billion into infrastructure for the competition.\par \par \uc2 But, to build it, Qatar has relied on an army of migrant workers, mostly from South Asia and Africa, toiling in temperatures of up to 120 degrees and living in crowded, squalid camps like this one.\par \par \uc2 MUSTAFA QADRI (Chief Executive Officer, Equidem): Migrant workers are cheap because they`re being exploited.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Mustafa Qadri founded Equidem, an organization that has investigated alleged labor abuses in Qatar, including workers going unpaid for months at a time and unsafe conditions, with deadly results.\par \par \uc2 MUSTAFA QADRI: It`s very hard to give an exact figure. I would suspect it`s actually in the hundreds. I think hundreds of workers have died to make this World Cup possible.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Budhan Pandit left his home in Nepal to build roads in Qatar, sending money back to his family, before he was killed in an accident last year.\par \par \uc2 (URMILA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: His widow, Urmila, told us her family received no compensation, just her husband`s body.\par \par \uc2 Labor rights groups want Qatar to set up a fund of around half-a-billion dollars to compensate injured and unpaid workers and the families of those killed.\par \par \uc2 MUSTAFA QADRI: It`s really conflicting, and that, I think, is -- also makes you very upset, knowing that we`re going to watch our teams that we love play and, at the same time, this is all made possible because of this incredible exploitation.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Qatar claims that it is paying back unpaid wages, and it has made some important changes, including raising the minimum wage and allowing migrant workers to change jobs without their employer`s permission.\par \par \uc2 But human rights groups say those changes don`t go far enough and, in many cases, are not being enforced.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A passenger jet caught fire today after a deadly collision on the runway.\par \par \uc2 That story when we return.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: As we mentioned, a passenger jet caught fire today on a runway at Lima, Peru`s International Airport.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say the plane, as it was attempting to take off, collided with a fire truck. Two firefighters were killed. Officials say all passengers and crew aboard the plane are safe.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the founder of the failed blood-testing company \uc2 Theranos\uc2  has been sentenced to more than 11 years in prison. \uc2 Elizabeth Holmes\uc2  was convicted of fraud and conspiracy earlier this year for misleading investors and endangering patients with a bogus blood-testing technology.\par \par \uc2 Holmes, now 38 and expecting her second child, had requested home confinement.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman is On -- is On the Road, showing us the true meaning of kindness from two 12-year-olds. And we will go On the Road when we return.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020221122eibi000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Historic Storm to Slam Six-Plus Million; Tracking the Storm and Frigid Weather; \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  Steps Down; Idaho Murder Manhunt Intensifies; North Korea Fires Long-Range Missile; US Grants Saudi Crown Prince Immunity; Mass \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Exodus; Qatar World Cup Controversy; Taylor Swift Fans Seeing Red; A Father`s Eternal Love; An Unbreakable Bond; Last Seen in Breckenridge; Inside the Stirring Drama, "She Said;" World Cup Controversies, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, David Begnaud, Tanya Rivero, Kelly  Cass, Nikole Killion, Christina Ruffini, Jonathan Vigliotti, Holly  Williams, Vladimir Duthiers, Natalie Morales \par \uc2 13,061 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Friday. Happy Friyay to you. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And I`m David Begnaud.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re glad David`s here. Tony is off today. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Millions are in the path of a potentially historic snowstorm that could dump up to six feet of snow.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): A major, major storm, an extreme weather event. That means it`s dangerous. It also means it`s life threatening.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s the end of an era as \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the first female Speaker of the House announces she is stepping aside.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): The hour has come for generation to lead the Democratic caucus.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Building access blocked for every \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employee as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  gives workers an ultimatum committing to hard work or leaving.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And 10 million people are without power in Ukraine this morning as Russia hammers that country. What it means as the snow begins to fall.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Taylor Swift fans fuming. \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  is canceling the public sale tickets due to extraordinary high demand.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Look at this rhino, the big fella and the dog is taking a nap and he wakes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: "The Late Show`s" Stephen Colbert hosting a celebrity pickleball tournament.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): This is Not The First Place Trophy and you guys can go. I`ve got to give the big trophy to the people who won.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): On Saturday, President Biden`s granddaughter, Naomi, is getting married at the White House, and that`s not the only celebration for the Biden family this weekend. Sunday will be President Biden`s 80th birthday.\par \par \uc2 Yes. I remember. I remember when my grandmother turned 80. I said "Do you know what you should do? You should run for President."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m guessing his grandma said, "No. I think I`ll pass I`ll stay in the UK."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I`ll pass.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very funny.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Happy Birthday, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We`re going to be sharing stories about courage today, including a father and son team that are defying expectations on the Ironman circuit.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Agar is the dead and he provides most of the muscle, but it is Johnny who had the dream despite being born with cerebral palsy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR (Born with cerebral palsy): I look at other athletes, I say to myself, you know, if they see themselves pushing themselves, you know? What can`t I do?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Now, if you don`t know this is a time triathlon -- running, biking, swimming. It`s not easy for anyone. But they`ve got something big on their side. It is the power of love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And love is very powerful.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the new film, it`s called "She Said" recounts the true story of "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " investigation into \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2 . Our CBS News contributor, that`s Jody Kantor was the reporter on that team and actors Zoe Kahan plays her in the movie. They`re both going to be here today to talk about honoring the women who spoke out for justice. It`s a good movie.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes, can`t wait to see it.\par \par \uc2 But we are going to begin with a pretty dangerous storm dumping several feet of snow in Western New York and around the Great Lakes. So, this is what it looks like right now in the Buffalo, New York area where you can see driving conditions are deteriorating quickly and there is actually a travel ban on the roads right now.\par \par \uc2 It is a situation that could be life threatening for millions of Americans. In some places, it is coming down at a rate of up to four inches every hour.\par \par \uc2 Tanya Rivero reports from Buffalo, which is currently under a State of Emergency.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TANYA RIVERO: Across Western New York, winter is here early and packing a punch.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, a relentless snowfall rained down on Buffalo as its skies lit up. This wintry preview developed when Midwest moisture was fueled over Lake Erie, accumulation could be historic.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): This is considered an extreme event, an extreme weather event.\par \par \uc2 TANYA RIVERO: Buffalo and its surrounding areas could get up to five feet of snow by Sunday.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): That means it`s dangerous. It also means it`s life threatening.\par \par \uc2 TANYA RIVERO: It`s the type of blast the area hasn`t seen in almost a decade and has alarmed officials enough to take certain precautions early.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`d much rather implement a driving ban earlier than waiting for the worst case scenario and then having it too late, and that`s what happened in 2014.\par \par \uc2 TANYA RIVERO: Eight years ago, Buffalo sustained a three-day historic snowstorm that resulted in 13 deaths and left thousands of drivers stranded.\par \par \uc2 Now just a few hours away, this storm caused chaos on the roads in some areas of Pennsylvania along Lake Erie.\par \par \uc2 The brutal winter storm is pummeling the Midwest. Snow plows are out in force. Some areas of West Michigan already have a foot of snow. This is their first storm of the season, but it is nothing new to them.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It could hit anytime in November and then can snow in April. You know, it`s just the Michigan weather.\par \par \uc2 TANYA RIVERO: Back in Buffalo, they may be used to it as well, but officials are pleading with residents to just stay home.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Don`t put at risk all these other people who have to go out and bitter cold weather.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TANYA RIVERO: As you may be able to see the snowfall is just picking up here again and Metro Buffalo. It is affecting not only the roads where that travel ban is still in place, but it is impacting almost all travel in and out of Buffalo. Almost every flight in and out of Buffalo`s main airport has been canceled -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tanya, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on where that weather system is headed, let`s bring in \uc2 Kelly Cass\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kelly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KELLY CASS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 It`s been quite a 24-hour snow record and we are not even done yet. This is great news for kids who may have a snow day, maybe off from school today to get out there and play in the snow. Not so good for the grownups though who have to travel through it.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got travel advisories. In fact, it is just not a good idea to travel when you`ve got this very heavy lake effect snow and we`re going to continue to pile it up today and into the weekend. Another two to three feet of snow on the way especially for areas just south of Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 If you`re going to get out there and dig out from the snow, say in Michigan, wear all the layers. We`ve got those wind chill values in the single digits, at times dipping below freezing or dipping below zero. And you can see that cold air is stuck with us all throughout the weekend.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, this is not just below average cold, we are going to be flirting with records, in many cases temperatures only reaching about 20 degrees in Des Moines, which will be a new record high, a new record cold high.\par \par \uc2 And Nate, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, it looks like we need to bundle up. That`s for sure. \uc2 Kelly Cass\uc2 , at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And on Capitol Hill, we are witnessing the end of an era. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the first and only woman to hold the job announced she will not seek a leadership position in the next Congress. So she says, she will continue to represent her district.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is on Capitol Hill with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 The Speaker is winning rare praise from both sides of the aisle, and I understand there were a lot of tears in the room yesterday.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, a lot of tears and a lot of hugs, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 And you know, listen, it`s no secret that Speaker Pelosi and Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  didn`t always see eye-to-eye, but in his statement last night, he called her impact consequential and said her path breaking career is one that will long endure.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): I will not seek reelection to Democratic leadership in the next Congress.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: With that announcement Thursday, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  ended speculation about her political future.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): For me, the hours come for a new generation to lead the Democratic caucus that I so deeply respect.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And reflected on her legacy as the first and only female Speaker.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Never would I have thought that someday I would go from homemaker to House Speaker. In fact, I never --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Thanking her husband, Paul, who was recently attacked, the 82-year-old mother of five was applauded by lawmakers on the left and some on the right, embraced by colleagues in the well of the House like Congresswoman Barbara Lee, who serves with Pelosi in the California delegation.\par \par \uc2 REP. BARBARA LEE (D-CA): She`s been such a great leader who has led us through many, many, as I said transformational policies, the Affordable Care Act, the Infrastructure bill, the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: First elected in 1987, Pelosi earned the Speaker`s gavel in 2007.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , I extend to you this gavel.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And again in 2019.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): The power this Speaker is awesome. Awesome.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: She worked with at least four Presidents, some she got along with, others she did not. Her exit comes as Majority Leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  also announced plans to step down from leadership clearing the path for a potential new crop of leaders, including Democratic Caucus Chair \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , who is expected to announce his bid for Minority Leader today.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): I think we all hope in whatever capacity we find ourselves in to continue to benefit from the wisdom, guidance, and advice of Speaker \uc2 Nancy D`Alesandro Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Now, if elected, Jeffries would become the first African- American to lead a party in Congress and he would primarily be tasked with taking on GOP leader, Kevin McCarthy who was recently nominated as Speaker after Republicans were projected to flip the House.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy notably was not present during Pelosi`s speech telling reporters, he was in meetings -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Nikole, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to Idaho in the desperate search for evidence in the murder of those four college students. There was a vigil held last night in Boise, the State capital about 300 miles from the scene of the crime, but there is still no suspect, not even a murder weapon.\par \par \uc2 But there is now an official cause of death for the four friends who were killed in their home early Sunday for reasons police still don`t understand.\par \par \uc2 Christina Ruffini has more from Moscow, Idaho.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: While authorities in Idaho hunt for a mass murderer, a college community remains on edge.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It was nerve wracking to hear them say that we are no longer like totally safe.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Autopsies of the four young victims have been completed with hopes of finding DNA evidence of their killer on their bodies.\par \par \uc2 CATHY MABBUTT (Latah County Coroner): I was shocked when I got a call for four homicides.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Latah County Coroner, Cathy Mabbutt declared the victims` manner of death, stabbing, likely from a large knife. She told us the case is one of the worst she`s seen.\par \par \uc2 CATHY MABBUT: They were pretty involved in the community. So, yes, it feels very personal.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: The victims are seniors Madison Mogen and Kaylee Goncalves, friends from separate sororities, who lived and worked together for several years at a restaurant downtown. Ethan Chapin, a freshman and triplet who loved playing basketball and his girlfriend, Xana Kernodle, a junior who majored in marketing.\par \par \uc2 Her father Jeffrey, who lives in Arizona told our Phoenix affiliate that Xana had bruises on her arms possibly from fighting off her attacker.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY KERNODLE (Victim`s father): She a tough kid. Whatever she wanted to do, she could do it.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Investigators are trying to piece together a timeline using live stream video of Mogen and Goncalves at a food truck before they headed back to their off campus house.\par \par \uc2 Police revealed they are working with the two surviving roommates who were home during the attack, but are not considered suspects.\par \par \uc2 University officials say they`ve increased campus patrols and are excusing all absences.\par \par \uc2 BLAINE ECKLES (University of Idaho Dean of Students): If they want to leave and go home now they absolutely can. We`re going to support them in doing so.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Nate, we spoke to a neighbor who was here during the incident and I said, "Did you hear any shouting or screaming?" And he said no. But this is a college campus. There`s a frat house across the street, hearing yelling on a Saturday night isn`t out of the unusual. He didn`t realize something had happened until he woke up and saw the police cars at the house behind me.\par \par \uc2 Now, the FBI Behavioral Analysis Unit is assisting local police and we are still waiting on that toxicology report. They will tell us the victim`s state at the time of their death.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still so many questions. Christina, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Okay, we turn to breaking news overseas.\par \par \uc2 South Korea and Japan are reporting that North Korea fired an intercontinental ballistic missile this morning that had the potential of reaching all of the US Mainland. The missile reportedly landed near Japanese waters. This is North Korea`s second major weapons test this month.\par \par \uc2 The US quickly condemned the launch. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who was attending an economic summit in Thailand called it a brazen violation of multiple UN Security resolutions adding that it unnecessarily raises tensions.\par \par \uc2 The White House is reporting that the US and South Korea responded with the launch of a joint Air Force drill?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Breaking overnight, the Biden administration has determined that Saudi Arabia`s Crown Prince is immune from a lawsuit regarding murdered "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist, Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 Prince Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  approved a mission to kill or capture Khashoggi back in 2018. Now, that`s according to US Intelligence officials. But in the filing yesterday, the \uc2 US State Department\uc2  said that since Mohammed is Saudi Arabia`s sitting head of government, he is immune from this suit under international law.\par \par \uc2 The State Department says it takes no view on the merits of the President`s suit and it reiterates its unequivocal condemnation of the heinous murder of Jamal Khashoggi. The civil lawsuit was filed by Khashoggi`s fiancee, who is seeking damages.\par \par \uc2 And after the State Department`s filing, she tweeted this: "Jamal died again today." A Judge will ultimately decide whether to grant the Prince immunity.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we are going to turn now to the chaotic situation at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  after the site`s new owner, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , gave employees an ultimatum, work extra hard or quit. Many chose to walk away.\par \par \uc2 Now sources tell Reuters that the company`s offices are closed until Monday.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  appears to be taking a gallows humor approach to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s latest troubles Thursday night, posting an emoji of a skull and crossbones, followed by an image of a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  tombstone.\par \par \uc2 He also tweets: "How do you make a small fortune in social media? Start out with a large one." But for many employees, it is no laughing matter. This cellphone video appears to show a scroll of insults aimed at \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  projected against \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s San Francisco headquarters.\par \par \uc2 ZOE SCHIFFER ("Platformer" managing editor): \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is an unstable company right now.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Zoe Schiffer is managing editor at the investigative tech newsletter, "Platformer." She says earlier this week, employees were given an ultimatum, either pledge to high intensity "hardcore" work with long hours by five Thursday afternoon or resign with severance pay.\par \par \uc2 Schiffer says many more workers than expected decided to leave.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Within 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Some even going up mine to count down to the deadline --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Four, three, two, one.\par \par \uc2 Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 ZOE SCHIFFER: Hundreds of people have said they`re out and a lot of those people were working on core infrastructure projects to keep \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s site reliable, to keep it secure, and to keep user data private.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Schiffer says so many employees left, the company didn`t have enough staff to figure out who was still working and needed to shut down its offices and cut badge access until the dust settled.\par \par \uc2 ZOE SCHIFFER: They are very scared that employees are going to sabotage the service on their way out the door.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And despite the trending #RIPTwitter, Schiffer says \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is probably not facing an imminent collapse, but she says when there is high traffic and fewer workers to fix those problems, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  could become glitchy or difficult to log into and those frustrations she says could be all it takes to lose lots of users -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jonathan, thank you very much. This is sort of one of those #HotMesses. This can`t be going according to his plan. And the employees now are very angry, it seems.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wait, wait, how do we know that that isn`t his plan? We have no idea why he chose to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and what his intentions were.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It is interesting you say that because he was tweeting this morning, hey, we`ve had more engagement and more traffic in the last 24 hours than before, you know. So look, I think it`s important to note, there is a lot of conversation even though not everybody in America is on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Most people in America are not on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Correct, but politicians use it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: News people use it. A lot of conversation starts on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and then pops up on television and other places.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And when journalists -- I think about this, like how long can we keep using it as a legitimate source if it continues to devolve into the chaotic situation that it is? What`s next?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. No doubt about it. Well said. Well said.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just can`t believe that`s the plan, but very you raise a very interesting point. You know, Elon does not run away from controversy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He leans into it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sometimes he stares it up.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He leans into it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, new dramatic twist in the outcry over \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2 `s disastrous presales for a Taylor Swift concert that left millions of fans outraged and ticketless, and I`m telling you, these Swifties are upset.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are mad.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: They are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, but first, it is 7:17. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, a major controversy over the World Cup in Qatar and how migrant workers were used to build the facilities. Holly Williams is here to share that story just two days before the competition kicks off. YOU`RE watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Cold, wintry morning.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, we`re headed to Michigan to meet an athlete who does not let cerebral palsy stop him from crossing some of the toughest finish lines in the world, thanks to a lot of help from this dad.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Walking in races was my way of knowing that, okay, I`m not just going to say thank you anymore, I`m going to actually put my words in the action.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You know, I get emotional not because I pity you, I get emotional because it just gives me a feeling of inspiration to listen to you.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Oh, thank you. Thank you. You know to be able to inspire someone else given my challenges purpose, I think.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It gives my challenges purpose. Well, that`s powerful.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR (Johnny Agar`s father): Do you feel okay?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I can`t wait to share this story with you.\par \par \uc2 For our series "Pushing the Limits," we will show you their biggest accomplishment yet.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. Gayle calls it a deal. It really is.\par \par \uc2 Your local news this next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We are just two days away from the kickoff of this year`s World Cup in Qatar. And on the surface, the stadiums are highly impressive. The country, one of the richest in the world has spared no expense in creating state-of- the-art facilities to host the competition, except it seems when it comes to the workers who actually built them, many migrants are forced to live in squalor.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams spoke to some of those workers and she is here with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Hey, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Well, this World Cup has always been controversial. Firstly, the weather is so hot in Qatar, they`ve had to switch the competition from the summer to the winter. Recently, the former President of FIFA, that soccer`s governing body said it was a mistake to give the World Cup to Qatar.\par \par \uc2 But for many people, the biggest issue is Qatar`s human rights record.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: They went wild in Qatar 12 years ago --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Qatar.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: When they heard their country had won the right to host the World Cup.\par \par \uc2 A Sheik from the country`s ruling family promised it would be a success.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Thank you for giving Qatar a chance, and we will not let you down. You will be proud of us.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: A Middle Eastern nation roughly the size of Connecticut, Qatar is one of the wealthiest countries in the world. It has poured more than $200 billion into eight state-of-the art, air conditioned soccer stadiums and all the other infrastructure to host over a million spectators.\par \par \uc2 But to build it, Qatar has relied on an army of migrant workers, mostly from South Asia and Africa. toiling in temperatures of up to 120 degrees and living in crowded squalid camps like this one.\par \par \uc2 MUSTAFA QADRI (Equidem founder): They`re like anyone else in the world. You want to have a better life than your parents. You want your children to go to college to have a better life than you, so you`re desperate for any opportunity.\par \par \uc2 So you have a World Cup dependent on migrant workers because they`re cheap. And migrant workers are cheap, because they`re being exploited.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Mustafa Qadri founded Equidem, an organization that investigates labor abuses. He says he was arrested in Qatar while researching conditions there, including forced labor, workers going unpaid for months at a time, and unsafe work sites with deadly results.\par \par \uc2 MUSTAFA QADRI: It`s very hard to give an exact figure. I would suspect it is actually in the hundreds. I think hundreds of workers have died to make this World Cup possible.\par \par \uc2 EMRAN KHAN (Migrant worker): Sometimes --\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Emran Khan comes from Bangladesh and claims he worked shifts up to 48 hours straight on buildings including Lusail Stadium, where the World Cup Finals will be held.\par \par \uc2 EMRAN KHAN: I have no choices. Workers had no choice. No rights. No choice.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: He told us he was paid about $350.00 a month, half of what he was originally promised.\par \par \uc2 EMRAN KHAN: If I am making complaint against company, they just say go back, pack your clothes and go back.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Go back to Bangladesh.\par \par \uc2 EMRAN KHAN: Yes. Just leave.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Budan Pandit (sp?) left his home in Nepal to build roads in Qatar sending money back to his family before he was killed in an accident last year.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: His widow, Ermila (sp?) told us her family received no compensation, just her husband`s body. They`ve now fallen deeper into poverty she says, and sometimes can`t afford food.\par \par \uc2 Labor rights groups want Qatar to set up a fund of around half a billion dollars to compensate injured and unpaid workers and the families of those killed.\par \par \uc2 This month Qatar rejected that suggestion as a publicity stunt.\par \par \uc2 MUSTAFA QADRI: It`s really conflicting, and that I think just also makes you very upset knowing that we`re going to watch our teams that we love play, and at the same time, this is all made possible because of this incredible exploitation.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know when you think about that exploitation, I think is a word there. Is Qatar doing anything to address any of these claims?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Yes, also Gayle, Qatar says that it is paying back unpaid wages and it has actually made some pretty significant changes, including raising the minimum wage and allowing migrant workers to switch jobs without their employer`s permission.\par \par \uc2 But human rights groups say those changes just don`t go far enough, and actually, in many cases aren`t even being enforced.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Holly, would a report like this even be shown in Qatar? Like would they block this? Would the censor stop it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: That`s -- yes, that`s such a good question. I don`t know the answer to it. I guess when you go to Qatar, what really strikes you is just the kind of imbalance in power between a really tiny country --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Incredibly wealthy, just a few hundred thousand citizens and then these hundreds of thousands or even millions of migrant workers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that gap between the extremely wealthy and those who are struggling financially.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there are so many questions about how they even got it to begin with, and that raises -- that`s a whole another issue.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And you`ve got another report coming up on Monday.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: We do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Holly, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, in our next hour, we`ll talk with soccer analyst Roger Bennett about all of the controversy surrounding the World Cup.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, more bad news for the already heartbroken Taylor Swift fans. How \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2 `s latest announcement shatters any hope they had of seeing their idol. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is growing outrage aimed at \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  after the company cancelled today`s highly anticipated general ticket sale for Taylor Swift`s new tour.\par \par \uc2 Now \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  says this, extraordinarily high demand and insufficient inventory are to blame. This news follows widespread reports of outages during the tour`s presale this week that prevented many preapproved fans from securing tickets and they are hot.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Duthiers has more on that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Anti-Hero.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Taylor Swift`s fans are seeing red.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I literally feel like I`m about to burst into tears right now.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Ah.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2 , better count its days.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Millions of Swifties were ready to snag whatever tickets they could today, but in a shocking announcement, \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  canceled its general sale for her new tour, citing high demand and low inventory.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taylor Swift performance.)\par \par \uc2 AMBER GARRIS (Taylor Swift fan): I never could have imagined how insane this whole process has been.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Amber Garris (sp?) is a self-proclaimed Swifty. She was unable to purchase tickets during the presale, so she had been anxiously awaiting today`s shot to see her favorite artist with her best friend.\par \par \uc2 AMBER GARRIS: It obviously wasn`t meant to be if we didn`t get tickets, but we`re upset.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bad Blood.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The "Bad Blood" between Taylor Swift fans and \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  has been brewing all week.\par \par \uc2 During Tuesday`s presale, 15 percent of customers experienced a glitch on the site. \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  says bot attacks and fans without invite codes to the presale drove unprecedented traffic to the site, four times their previous peak.\par \par \uc2 GREG MAFFEI (Liberty media CEO): The site was supposed to be opened up for 1.5 million verified Taylor Swift fans. We had 14 million people hit the site including bots.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The technical issues are the latest development in an ongoing decade-long fight between Congress, fans, and the ticket behemoth.\par \par \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  and its parent company, \uc2 Live Nation\uc2  have faced antitrust scrutiny since their merger in 2010. It combined tickets, promotion, concerts, and management under a single umbrella and became the only major player in the concert game.\par \par \uc2 This week, lawmakers including Tennessee`s Attorney General renewed their calls for the company to break up.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SKRMETTI (Tennessee Attorney General): We`re seeing a situation where people are trying to use the service and aren`t getting the product that they paid for and the product that they were promised and that could be an indicator that there`s not enough competition in the market.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In a letter to Live Nation`s CEO, Senator Amy Klobuchar said the company abuses its market positions and that its power insulates it from the competitive pressures that typically push companies to innovate and improve their services, that can result in dramatic service failures where consumers are the ones that pay the price.\par \par \uc2 Paying the price has taken on a double meeting, as the cost of concert tickets has also skyrocketed up nearly 18 percent since 2019. \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  says it`s not conducting resales for the Taylor Swift shows, but others are, with tickets on some websites reaching tens of thousands of dollars.\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY WALLACE (Taylor Swift fan): I was really looking forward to going to see Taylor Swift because I`ve gone to every concert since "Speak Now."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sydney Wallace knew all too well that tickets would be hard to get. After several technical glitches, she was hit with an $11,000.00 bill on the ticket app SeatGeek.\par \par \uc2 Now her credit cards are frozen and she doesn`t know if she`ll go to the tour at all.\par \par \uc2 SIDNEY WALLACE: They knew the demand ahead of time, they knew how much traffic was going to be on the site and they created this environment where no one really had a chance. I don`t even know if I`d get another chance to even get in to view the tickets.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bejeweled.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, Wallace did get a little bit of good news, she told us that SeatGeek will refund her money in three to five days -- we hope so.\par \par \uc2 We reached out to Live Nation and \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  about the lawmakers` claims of it being a monopoly, but they did not get back to us yet. And to give an example of demand for this tour, listen to this \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  says based on the volume of traffic to the site on the first day alone, Taylor would need to perform more than 900 Stadium shows.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does that translate to? A stadium show every single night for the next two and a half years. People love Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But what`s interesting, a lot of people are directing their anger directly at her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At Taylor?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: At Taylor, like this was not her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is not her fault. She is popular. People want to see her. Oh man. How about that?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: This \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  is massive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There has to be a better way to get the tickets.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But Gayle --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have heard of people waiting online though on the phone for eight hours to get a ticket.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but you know what --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know what you`re going to say.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m going to say this, it might be controversial. Swifties don`t come at me, but if you come from the old school era where you used to sit out in tents overnight and wait for tickets.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It used to be a lot worse, I`m just saying for those that cover the 70s, 80s, and 90s. That`s going to be all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you`ve camped out in a tent before?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Back in the day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who did you camp out for? I`m curious. I`ve never done that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: MC Hammer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You know, I am like saying I`m old school like back in the day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right, back in the day.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, David Begnaud brings us a Special Edition of "What to Watch." You know it`s going to be good. It`s David.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But first, it is 7:45. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, it`s time for "What to Watch" with David who is filling in for Vlad. David, what do you have for us?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You`re all going to love this.\par \par \uc2 To honor Vlad who is about to become a girl dad soon. I`ve got a tender story about a girl dad. It`s about the strength of a bond between the father and his daughters, and proof that father`s love is forever.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Gregory Holmes was a veteran, a church Deacon, and above all, a dad to three daughters.\par \par \uc2 Nichelle was his Little Lady, Nia he called Baby Doll, and Nicole, she was Big Stuff. Holmes taught them to work hard and be smart, and being smart meant buying their first home. Nichelle did just that, and the mail soon brought a letter from daddy.\par \par \uc2 NICHELLE HOLMES (Little Lady): Congratulations, Little Lady. You`re now the queen of your castle. Congratulations on home ownership. I`m so proud of you. Love, Daddy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: When Nia bought a home, she, too got the letter addressed to Baby Doll and signed, Daddy. Then it was Nicole`s turn.\par \par \uc2 NICHELLE HOLMES: Nicole closes on her home. Nicole didn`t get a letter.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You see Gregory Holmes died last year in a motorcycle accident. It left a void in Nicole`s home and in her heart until just a few weeks ago when her mom, Teresa was going through some papers and there it was.\par \par \uc2 NICHELLE HOLMES: And I`m blown away. I`m crying. It was a letter to Nicole.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It turns out daddy hadn`t forgotten about her. You might say he`s watching over her. As the Deacon preached many times, love never dies.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, so he had written a letter already.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He wrote it, stashed away and his wife found it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that, Little Lady, Baby Doll, and Big Stuff.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: They all got their letters.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So he wrote it before he passed.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes. Isn`t that beautiful?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And it was in his spot, like he had a little man cave in the basement and his wife was in there just going through papers. And she was like, whoa. And she wrote to me, on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . She said, "David, there`s a miracle that happened in our family. This is a story you`ve just got to hear."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What a lovely man who clearly loved his daughters. I love the tradition that he started with them, too.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes, ain`t that great?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know what, I`m going to top that. You know, I have an 18-year-old, 16-year-old, and my daughter is 12.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am going to do that. Start writing them letters when they accomplish something big in their life and continue it when they`re out the house because they`re always going to be your babies, and Gayle knows.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s a really good idea.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Always.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Always.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s all so true. Always your babies.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: David, that was awesome.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know you have something coming up with a bit.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`s got another touching story about Ironman athletes who are pushing each other to cross new finish lines in life.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s Friday, I`m Gayle King. That`s Nate Burleson. That`s David Begnaud. Tony is off today.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, women in search of justice. The new film, "She Said" is about the \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2  investigation at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ." We`ll talk to Jodi Kantor and actress Zoe Kazan, who plays her in the movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, two women disappeared near a Colorado ski town in 1982.\par \par \uc2 "48 Hours" has the story of how their killer was eventually found.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And we are sharing a story of love and endurance. It`s about a father and son team unlike any other on the Ironman circuit as they push through challenges to live out a dream.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We begin this hour with a powerful story about defying expectations. For our series "Pushing the Limits," we will follow a father and son`s journey to some of the toughest triathlon courses in the world.\par \par \uc2 Jeff and Johnny Hagar aren`t your typical Ironman athletes, but with more than 200 races under their belts, they have got the same grit and determination as all of the other competitors.\par \par \uc2 They talk with us about overcoming the odds and redefining what it means to be a champion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Well before sunrise, Jeff Agar is already 20 miles into his day. At 59 years old, he is training for another triathlon. It is a goal fueled by his greatest motivation who is on his own ride up the stairs.\par \par \uc2 This is Jeff`s 28-year-old son, Johnny.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: Oh, you`re a good little boy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: When he was just three weeks old, Johnny was diagnosed with cerebral palsy.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: Johnny. There you go, attaboy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It is a muscle disorder that makes it challenging for Johnny to walk and talk.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Look at you. There you go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Jeff and his wife, Becky were devastated.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: It took a couple seconds to figure out this is a totally different world for us. This is not going to be the typical type of thing.\par \par \uc2 BECKY AGAR (Johnny`s mother): I remember asking him one day, I said, you know what if he can`t play catch with you? You know what does that mean to you? And he said -- you know, I just love him. It doesn`t matter what he can and can`t do. You know it was at that point, I felt, gosh, I got the right guy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The right guy.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: Mom`s cooking when we get home.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Who pushes through countless long runs, early morning swims in Michigan`s frigid lakes and bike rides four hours or more around the neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: I believe I can fly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Triathlon is an individual sport that Jeff is taking on for two people, totaling 400 pounds with their equipment.\par \par \uc2 When he swims, he pulls Johnny.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: When he bikes, he hauls him.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: Good morning. Thank you. Good to be out here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And when he runs, he pushes him.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: I mean, I`m not a fan of swimming, biking, or running. So which is perfect for drama. So, this probably would be in the top hundred things I would select to do, I`m not doing it because I love it. It wasn`t like a dream of mine. This is Johnny`s dream. I`m just giving him the legs and the power to do it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Are you trying to prove something to Johnny?\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: That`s a really good question. I think we`re trying to show him that you just can`t let obstacles stand in your way. He`s on the inside now. He is not one of these people externally thinking what`s it like? He is athlete, he is on the inside of this game.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Sad biker, dad?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Together, the Agar`s have competed more than 200 races across nine States over the past 14 years.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: I look at other athletes, I say to myself, you know, if I see themselves pushing themselves, you know, what can`t I do?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: How do you feel about what your dad does for you? Because really, it`s all for you.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Yes, I think, you know, for me, it`s a tremendous blessing. You know, I`m able to express my desires through him and he understands that. You know, I saw Dad pushing himself to get me across the line and I said to myself, this is ridiculous. I am capable of doing more.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Ironman.)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So two months ago, they went after a goal they`ve attempted five times before.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Let`s go, Dad. Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Finishing a full Ironman race. That is the ultimate endurance event. It happened in Maryland where the father and son duo had to complete the 2.4 mile swim, 112 mile bike ride, and 26.2 mile run, that`s 140 miles in under 17 hours, and for the very first time, they did it with just five minutes to spare.\par \par \uc2 And look who crossed the finish line for the team.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Johnny Agar. You are an Ironman.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Walking and racing was my way of telling that, okay, I`m not just going to say thank you anymore. I`m going to actually put my words in to action.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You know, I get emotional not because I pity you. I get emotional because it just gives me a feeling of inspiration to listen to you.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Oh, thank you. Thank you. You know, to be able to inspire someone else, it gives my challenges purpose, I think.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It gives my challenges purpose. Well, that`s powerful.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Home stretch, Daddy.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: That`s right, buddy.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: And doing good.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Johnny is the heart. Jeff is his motor.\par \par \uc2 If I were married to him, I would be over the moon and love with him watching him do what he does for my son.\par \par \uc2 BECKY AGAR: You nailed it. Yes. Even though he got him the 140 miles he will always be hiding behind Johnny. He doesn`t want to be announced as an Ironman. It`s Johnny`s moment and it`s the most beautiful thing to me.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Nobody helps you and Johnny get across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: Right. Yes. You don`t get any extra time. You get no extra accommodations. That`s the tough part of it. But the beauty of it is, if you finish, when they say you`re an Ironman, you are an Ironman. You are the same thing as everybody else.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What made you emotional there?\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: Just the fact that he would see it as he`d rather not succeed, then be treated like he is different.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Okay, just relax, breathe.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So what`s next? Team Agar is doubling down.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Good.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: They want to secure a spot at the Ironman World Championship. That`s happening next year in Kona, Hawaii.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: It is considered the most brutal one day endurance event on the planet.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: How long does it take?\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: You get 17 hours.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Seventeen hours.\par \par \uc2 JEFF AGAR: And we would probably use every minute of it, for certain.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Now, they competed in the World Championship back in 2016, but they failed to finish the bike portion in the time allowed, so they were eliminated.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Sometimes you win, sometimes you learn, so.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Sometimes you win, sometimes you learn. What a priceless lesson, embraced by his teammate and hero.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: It`s not about the medal for me. It`s not about the fame. It`s about doing it as a team.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Is there a better man than your dad?\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: I have yet to find one.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Father and son, their bond as strong as iron, tempered by love.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is good. That is good.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I mean, what else can you say?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I need a blanket and a pillow. You just want to go to bed and just think about what you just saw. That`s amazing, David. That Team Agar, the way he said that sometimes you win -- sometimes you lose and sometimes you learn.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You learn.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He also said it gives my challenges purpose.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Purpose.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that? The perspective.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He wakes up at like four in the morning to go bike and run and swim and he doesn`t even enjoy that.\par \par \uc2 Gay Yes, that`s the -- I thought that was interesting, too.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He does it because his boy has a dream.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And that`s why he does with it. I just -- I`m telling you, it gets me every time. I cried watching the edit. I cried during the interview.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I just hope that every child describes their parents like that. You ask --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought that, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Have you found a better man?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He said, I haven`t found it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I have yet to find one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, what else fascinates me that despite doing all that, they`re now ready to take on something. I hear it is something -- it is more brutal and more challenging. They go yes, let`s go do that. I`m surprised. I thought that would be the end of the story.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t think that they would want to do that again.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: They want to go back to the World Championship one more time. One more time. They`ve got to be invited. So, they`re hoping to get a slot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, they`re going to be invited. Where do we send your piece to? They`re going to be invited.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They`re going to be invited.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, David. Thank you, Agar family. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, the new movie, "She Said" tells a story behind the investigative journalism that helped fuel the #MeToo Movement. We`ll talk with Jodi Kantor of "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " and actor Zoe Kazan, who plays Jodi on the big screen.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:09. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: I`m Natalie Morales, "48 Hours."\par \par \uc2 Two women murdered near a lux ski town. That same night, a man is rescued on a mountain pass. Decades later, police conclude he was their killer.\par \par \uc2 That`s coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "48 Hours" is investigating to cold case murders near a popular Colorado ski resort Annette Schnee and Bobbie Jo Oberholtzer were both killed on the same night in 1982 just outside Breckenridge.\par \par \uc2 Now, their cases remained a mystery for almost four decades. In her first investigation for "48 Hours," CBS News correspondent, Natalie Morales reports on what led investigators to the killer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: On the night of January 6 1982, an airline passenger flying above this mountain paths outside of Breckenridge, Colorado noticed a series of flashing lights coming from below that looked like an SOS, a call for help. It came from the driver of a truck stranded in a raging snowstorm.\par \par \uc2 Dave Montoya, a local Fire Chief at the time went to the location after the pilot radioed down the coordinates.\par \par \uc2 So what were you thinking as you`re driving?\par \par \uc2 DAVE MONTOYA (Former local Fire Chief): Well, who does that? I thought it was a tourist.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: What Montoya didn`t know then was that he was about to rescue a man who had just murdered these two young women, Bobby Jo Oberholtzer and Annette Schnee. It would take investigators nearly 40 years to make that connection.\par \par \uc2 JIM HARDTKE (former Colorado Bureau of Investigation agent): We muse about whether or not we were going to die before we solved this case.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Back in January 1982, all investigators knew was that Annette Schnee was last seen around 4:45 in the afternoon, Bobby Jo disappeared hours later says then Colorado Bureau of Investigation agent, Jim Hardtke.\par \par \uc2 JIM HARDTKE: Bobby Jo Oberholtzer was last seen in Breckenridge at about 7:30 in the evening.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: The following morning, Bobby Jo`s body was found, a bloody glove lay in the snow near her belongings. Annette`s remains were discovered six months later about 10 miles away.\par \par \uc2 But in a strange twist, it would be these orange booty socks that linked the cases. One of the socks was found on Annette Schnee`s left foot and the other was recovered near Bobby Jo Oberholtzer`s body.\par \par \uc2 JIM HARDTKE: This is amazing. This ties the cases together.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: But it wasn`t until nearly four decades after Dave Montoya saved that stranded motorist that DNA revealed the identity of the killer as Alan Lee Phillips.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MONTOYA: Everything catches up. You don`t get away with anything forever.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t get away with anything forever. That`s true. Natalie Morales joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Natalie.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of little twists in this because the night of the murders, we`re going to learn that local Fire Chief unknowingly helped the killer and then it turns out that they knew each other. Explain.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: They did. Well, they in fact, Dave Montoya went up there thinking as he said, I thought I was going to rescue a tourist. He gets up there. He realizes, huh, this guy is the guy I work with in the mines, Alan Phillips.\par \par \uc2 So he -- Alan Phillips that day said he had been drinking too much, wanted to avoid the police on the highway. So, he decided to take the mountain pass and that`s where he got stuck.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Because he knew, I mean, said, I know Alan. He is okay.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes, and he did report though what happened and you know, rescuing him and that actually made local news there. So they had a record of him being saved and rescued on that very night that these two young women disappeared.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: DNA evidence was crucial. Where`d they get it from?\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes, the DNA was found on the belongings of Bobby Jo. It was near her belongings, a glove and a bloody tissue. Some of the DNA was hers, of course, but the other DNA they didn`t have a match and the database didn`t exist back then. CODIS was not around at the time until later in the 90s.\par \par \uc2 They ran it through the CODIS database. That`s the criminal DNA database. There were no matches there. It took only in the last two years where they`ve created this Ancestry DNA type testing, genetic genealogy that they were able to run the DNA and they came back with two names, and one of the guys was still living there in Breckenridge or not far from Breckenridge, Colorado. So that`s where they got him.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Ancestry. Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Natalie Morales, good to see you and have you in studio. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Thank you. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can see Natalie`s report, "Last Seen in Breckenridge" on "48 Hours" tomorrow night at 10/9 Central right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a lot of great guests coming up, so we`re doing "Talk of the Table" a little bit earlier than usual and David is going to kick it off for us. What have you got?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: All right, here we go. I want to circle back on the Ironman story we just did on Jeff and Johnny Agar.\par \par \uc2 Before the shoot, we found out that Johnny was a big \uc2 Detroit Lions\uc2  fan. So we asked our boy, Nate, to record a quick little video for him, and Nate hasn`t seen what happened when we showed Johnny the video.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, nice.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, Johnny, what`s up? This is Nate Burleson, former \uc2 Detroit Lions\uc2  wide receiver now TV host.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Oh my.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You and your dad are incredible. So, I wanted to tell you that you are awesome and inspiring.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY AGAR: Oh my goodness. He was always somebody that I strived to be like, and so that was something really, really special.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh come on, man.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: All you, brother.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t do that to me.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Lit him up, man. Lit him up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is so nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m speechless. That was awesome.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Lit him up, because that`s the kind of dude you are. Look if people don`t know, Nate`s producer went to him and said, hey, there`s this guy who loves you. You know, do a video.\par \par \uc2 And you did the kindest, nicest most inspiring video for him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that`s so nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Johnny, I meant every word. You and your father are inspiring many people in different walks of life, including me. But for the athletes out there, the touchdowns, the records, all of that will fade. It is the people that love you and support you like that family right there. Those type of memories will last a lifetime. That was awesome, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well because you did that, Nate. We have something special for you, a Popeyes chicken sandwich.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, what have we got?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Popeyes. This is -- whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is my "Talk of the Table".\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We get no food? Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because it`s well-known how much I love the Popeyes fried chicken sandwich. This is the new Popeyes chicken sandwich -- thank you very much, Christopher. It`s the blackened one. It`s the same brioche bun, pickles, Mayo, spicy version is available. The difference is the chicken patty is naked as they call it. It`s not breaded, or nearly so.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s a Cajun spice blend.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: David, you like it?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You took the top off? Top bun off? You`ve got a convertible.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Nate, what`s that commercial? Love that chicken from Popeyes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I just say --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s that Louisiana --\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Now, why are you letting it sing it by myself?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because I wanted to hear your voice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I just say, I don`t own any Popeyes stock.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am not a spokesperson. I just love the Popeyes chicken --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good. We`ve got that Popeyes orange zone today though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a coincidence.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It has been five years since a bombshell investigation uncovered decades of abuse and harassment allegations against media mogul, \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The reporting fueled the #MeToo Movement and earned "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " journalists Jodi Kantor and Megan Twohey a Pulitzer Prize.\par \par \uc2 Jodi joined us in the studio just after the breaking story. We all remember this. Weinstein is now serving a 23-year sentence for rape and sex assault in New York, is on trial right now in Los Angeles on additional sex crime charges.\par \par \uc2 Now, the new film "She Said" is out today. You must go see this movie. It tells the story behind the investigation.\par \par \uc2 In this clip, one of Weinstein`s former production assistants tells Jodi Kantor`s character about the agreement she made with the \uc2 Weinstein Company\uc2  to keep her allegations quiet.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "She Said.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jodi Kantor is also a CBS News contributor, she joins us now with actor, Zoe Kazan, who I think you nailed it. She played Jodi in the movie.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 ZOE KAZAN ("She Said" actor): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: First, can I say guys, Bravo, Bravo, Bravo.\par \par \uc2 Jodi, I have to start with you because we all know this story. You`ve been here many times talking about it and I felt I know the story. I am trying to explain why it was so emotional watching it on the screen. And I think because you really took us behind the scenes, you and Megan took us behind the scenes about the work that you put in and what it took to get the story.\par \par \uc2 Why was it such an emotional time for those of us who have seen this movie?\par \par \uc2 JODI KANTOR: Well, I`m so grateful to Zoe and the rest of the team that made this film because what they`ve done is they`ve taken frankly, the inspiration that Megan and I feel in our work and have put it on the big screen.\par \par \uc2 I think you`ve seen the bravery of these sources. The women are not shown as pure victims. They`re shown as these adult women with a difficult choice to make about you know, are they going to cooperate with this investigation? Why should they? There is enormous risk for them.\par \par \uc2 But then, what I think you also see is the power of the truth and the feeling that, you know, we really can confront a bully. We just have to do it together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was it emotional for you, Zoe? And I`m wondering how you nailed Jodi so well.\par \par \uc2 First I want to know, was it emotional in how you captured her?\par \par \uc2 ZOE KAZAN: Yes, I mean, I cried reading the book when the women decided to go on the record. I felt the same way reading the script. I sort of cried every time I read it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "She Said.")\par \par \uc2 ZOE KAZAN: I think it`s just very emotional to see women stand in their truth and their power and to see an institution like "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " stand behind them and put heft behind those individual voices. You know, watching women be believed I think, is very powerful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you study Jodi though? Did you meet with her? Did you watch --\par \par \uc2 ZOE KAZAN: Right. I think I sound like a stalker. I`m like, I tried to absorb her essence. We got to spend time together in a very chill way. We`re both Brooklyn moms. We like spend time with our kids. We went to dinner together. Had a glass of wine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you two didn`t know each other.\par \par \uc2 ZOE KAZAN: No, we`ve never met, but when I first met Jodi, I felt like, oh, I could have known her already. Like there was a real sense of familiarity. And she and her husband, Ron, are so inspiring and such impressive people but getting to know them felt like very easy, like they just were so welcoming and generous. And let me kind of peek into their lives and ask all sorts of nosy questions.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There was a decision.\par \par \uc2 JODI KANTOR: Zoe was very probing. There were times when you asked me things where I was like, I could tell you the truth, but it might end up on screen.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There was a decision made not to show Weinstein`s face. How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 JODI KANTOR: So the movie is based on a book we wrote.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JODI KANTOR: And the book is called "She Said." It is called "She Said" for a reason, right? Because in those two little words are so many multiple layers of meaning and man, I just think this film unpacks that so beautifully and what -- you know, it is a story about -- there are some men who helped in a really powerful way, but at the end of the day, this is a book and a film about female narration.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the power of using your voice.\par \par \uc2 Jodi, you mentioned how this film accurately depicts not just how hard it was for these women to come forward, but then also go on the record, which are two completely different things.\par \par \uc2 How difficult was it in real time when that was happening?\par \par \uc2 JODI KANTOR: So difficult because you`re asking people to take on real risk and you can`t disguise that. You know, one of the scenes in the film that`s the truest to life for me, is when Ashley Judd calls me to -- calls my character --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who plays herself in the movie.\par \par \uc2 JODI KANTOR: Ashley plays herself in the movie, and she is -- you know, I think what Ashley and Zoe are doing in that scene is sharing with the world something very special that happened in real life, which is that this woman put her career and her reputation .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: On the line.\par \par \uc2 JODI KANTOR: . on the line for the sake of other women, and I try to be very professional in the workplace and in my conduct at "The Times." But I`ve got to tell you, I mean, I broke down crying because for me, it was the first time I really knew the investigation was going to work.\par \par \uc2 And I think you can -- like I think you did such a beautiful job of showing the layers of emotional reaction kind of beneath the professional exterior.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought we saw it in real time. Have you seen Zoe change changes in Hollywood since the article came out?\par \par \uc2 ZOE KAZAN: We`ve had some really concrete changes, like having an intimacy coordinator who`s sort of like a stunt coordinator, but for any intimate contact on screen, having Codes of Conduct that we agree upon before we start filming, having anti-harassment workshops with other cast and crew.\par \par \uc2 But I think you have only to open a newspaper to see how far as a society we have to go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ZOE KAZAN: And it seems that one of the messages of the film is that collective action can have an impact, that -- I don`t know -- that there`s a power in standing together and that like she said, to take down a bully, you don`t need to be a powerful person. Just stay tough --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Beautiful, beautiful job and I think, Jodi --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jodi, you talk about it so coherently and just you know almost distantly, but what you went through, you and Megan, you had death threats. Your daughter asked you what is rape. I mean, there`s so many things that we saw on your personal life. I`m in awe. I`m in awe and Bravo. Bravo to you both.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Bravo.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Public service --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well done. Well done.\par \par \uc2 Jodi Kantor and Zoe`s Kazan, we thank you. "She Said," as I said, is out in theaters today. Please, run don`t walk to see this movie.\par \par \uc2 The World Cup in Qatar is one of the most controversial games in soccer history. Soccer expert, that`s Roger Bennett is here. Where`s Roger? There he is in our progressive greenroom to tell us why the host country faces so much criticism.\par \par \uc2 They are getting hammered and it seems rightly so based on what Roger is going to tell you.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:37. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The 2022 World Cup kicks off on Sunday with Team USA playing its first game Monday. But for many soccer fans, this year`s tournament comes with a lot of controversy.\par \par \uc2 The host nation, Qatar, faces criticism over his treatment of migrant workers as well as for its human rights record including laws criminalizing homosexuality, yet more than one million people are expected to attend the competition in person and five million will be tuning in worldwide.\par \par \uc2 In his podcast, "World Corrupt," Roger Bennett delves into the controversy surrounding the World Cup. The "Men in Blazer" host is also a coauthor of the book, "Gods of Soccer: The Pantheon of the 100 Greatest Soccer Players."\par \par \uc2 Roger, good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT (Soccer analyst): Nate, when I`m in the presence of the one true royalty that I respect, Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here we are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what I`m talking about?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here we are, Roger.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I say that every morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We say that about you, Roger.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now Roger, World Cups have been held in countries accused of human rights violations before, 1934;s Mussolini`s Italy comes to mind. But why is this different?\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: This is like for American audiences, this is like lifting up March Madness and saying we`re going to play it in Saudi Arabia. It is almost the perfect storm of authoritarian grotesqueness.\par \par \uc2 You have corruption as to why it was awarded there in the first place. In our podcast, DOJ spokesman, Matthew Miller who was there when it was awarded to Qatar, called it the most corrupt thing he`d ever seen in his lifetime. And then they added, and I came through in New Jersey politics.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: You have a country that`s not fit for purpose, smaller than Connecticut. It`s going to be like the fire festival of World Cups, layer in all the human rights abuse -- women`s rights, LGBTQ+ rights abuse, press freedom, and then you have the fact that "The Guardian" reported six and a half thousand foreign workers died making the country ready for our entertainment.\par \par \uc2 This is truly going to be a World Cup soaked in blood.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For a country that`s basically the size of Connecticut. I mean, Roger, you`re very graphic. You say when Qatar was awarded it, you practically soiled your pants. That`s how upset you were. That`s how upset you were.\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: It is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have loved this game since you were a little boy, that you know they said the process you could literally see money changing hands, literally. And so they`re awarded this. Why are people supporting it then? Why aren`t teams saying, well, we don`t want to play in a place like that.\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: The World Cup has been compared to a global eclipse that hits the entire planet for 30 straight days instantaneously.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So true.\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: You guys know, Nate, as well as anybody that sport is just an anesthetizer. We look at it rationally and we understand this brazen corruption. But the second the whistle blows --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All is forgiven?\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: And Lionel Messi dribble through five defenders, slaps them, a kinetic transcendent rainbow of wonder, like, Oh, my God, and that is why Qatar wanted the World Cup in the first place.\par \par \uc2 There`s a human rights abuse. There`s the deaths of the foreign workers. There`s geopolitical conflict with Saudi Arabia and they want to say, we are here. Forget that stuff. Look at Lionel Messi doing the goal thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: And it`s sports washing and it`s almost using this jewel, which is the spine of my life and millions of people around the world the World Cup gives us the biography to our life and using it for brazen geopolitical purpose.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Are we at a time in our world now Roger where this organization will stop awarding this prestigious event to companies like Qatar?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good question.\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: You`re talking about this organization? FIFA?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: I mean, if you`ve watched Bond movies and you know what Specter is? It`s like saying, will Specter, just of its own accord, stop criming? I mean, the reality is that FIFA is meant to safeguard football, grow it, but it does its own checks and balances. And it also knows we don`t watch sports.\par \par \uc2 I watch sports to be whisked away, to get away from the human darkness, and again, they know that they can pretty well do anything because when Neymar, when Ambato, please, God when Christian Pulisic does something wonderful, we are just completely mesmerized, so they get away with -- and they continue to get away.\par \par \uc2 The reform of FIFA, ultimately, the only thing that will stop this because Saudi Arabia is sniffing around the notion of a 2030 World Cup bid, is this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: Being the most strident effect of World Cups where we both watch the football and marvel it, but watch it in split screen, as families and the players make a protest so it doesn`t have --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Despite as you say you are disgusted by it, you said, we will be watching. That is what I am --\par \par \uc2 ROGER BENNETT: I went to Russia in 2018 and I regret it. Retrospectively, I was doing Putin`s bid for propaganda, reporting how great it was in Moscow and St. Petersburg. I will not be going to Qatar.\par \par \uc2 We`re doing a live tour. We`re going across America to help Americans make memories. We are just like politics. You don`t back off when politics gets start. You marvel and fight, you marvel and fight.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re going to keep talking during the commercial break.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Roger just had the best line. He said Jodi needs to take on FIFA. Agree.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a look back at all that mattered this week. Have a great weekend. See you Monday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Gods of Soccer."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: An alarming situation in Poland, our \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally, where a missile landed in the village killing two people.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: The President of Poland and the head of \uc2 NATO\uc2  are saying it was likely because of a Ukrainian response, not a Russian attack.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: However, Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy saying this is not our missile and not our missile strike.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The former President is now officially ready for 2024. He announced he is running to get back into the White House.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: He`s decided the best way to possibly avoid indictment is to get back on center stage.\par \par \uc2 TIM COOK (\uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO): We`re a company that wants to enrich people`s lives with our products. To have the ability to contact in the event of an emergency is huge.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It really is.\par \par \uc2 I`ve heard more than a hundred times about Gayle King falling over and her watch talking to her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He gives you anecdotes and then it seems like you have to do a lot of homework to research to nail it just right.\par \par \uc2 STEVE MARTIN (Actor): I`ll jump in because it`s not easy to remember these stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is one of your own compositions?\par \par \uc2 STEVE MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What shall we call it? "Gayle?" Doesn`t that have a ring to it?\par \par \uc2 STEVE MARTIN: I am going to call it "Barbara Walters."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just a touch closer to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I had to do the whole "Three Amigos" thing, the hips and the thrust.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s really close.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where did the camera go?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: (Barking.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Meow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Things are getting real frisky right here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We have gone to the dog.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Special Happy Birthday to Clark.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On camera number five.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Clarke is in the house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Clarke is in the house. Look at Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Speaking of looking good, the bald beauty himself --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bald beauty.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Chapeau to "Wakanda Forever." Chapeau means --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hat.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I didn`t know that at all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember from French class.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You used to have a French boyfriend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Je m`appelle Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People who went on vacation with their significant other after the spark was gone, the spark actually came back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You spend so much time together.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Practicing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sure, yes. Practicing at love is what you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s what I mean, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is that for me?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS (Lifestyle host): Oh, yes, this is for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, you surprised me with that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have actually not seen "The Christmas Story" from beginning to end.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve seen only clips, you know, walking in and out of the living room.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony. Sit your butt down and watch it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I haven`t seen it either.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not all the way through. Not all the way through.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am out. I can`t take this type of energy.\par \par \uc2 NATASHA LEGGERO (Comedian): As a woman, I would have to have five drinks and be an amputee for my parenting style to be equal to that of a sober father. Like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Greatest dad ever. I did it.\par \par \uc2 Can you fix this tear that I have?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Give me the story here --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was getting dressed this morning, and I think I`ve been eaten too much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The panther attack in there? Oh. What is happening?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How does this look?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s wrong with you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel like Terry Crews right now.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221118eibi00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Biden`s High-Stakes Meeting with China`s Xi; President Zelenskyy Visits Liberated Kherson; Inside Kherson after Russia Withdrawal; Democrats Retain Control of the Senate; Suspect at Large after Deadly UVA Shooting; Six People Killed at Dallas Airshow; Thanksgiving Price Spike; Bobby Bostic`s Second Change; \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  Exclusive; Wakanda Forever Massive Opening; Yellowstone Premier Break Records; Dolly Parton`s Monumental Award; One-on-One with Patti Smith; A Conversation about Antisemitism; A Lesson on Positivity; Misty Copeland Live, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Nancy Cordes, Chris  Livesay, Nikole Killion, Dan Schutte, Omar Villafranca, Nancy Chen, Erin  Moriarty, Vladimir Duthiers, Anthony Mason, Carter Evans, Steve Hartman,  Meryl Hartman, Emmett Hartman \par \uc2 13,263 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday. I`m Gayle King\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We are here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Democrats take a victory lap after winning key races in Nevada and Arizona giving them control of the Senate for the next two years.\par \par \uc2 GOV. LARRY HOGAN (R-MD): It`s actually the third election in a row that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has cost us the race and it`s like three strikes, you`re out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden holds a high-stakes meeting with China`s President Xi this morning ahead of the G20 Summit in Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We need to figure out where the red lines are.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Three people are dead after a shooting at the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A search is now underway for a suspect.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And investigators try to learn what caused the midair collision that killed six people at an airshow in Dallas.\par \par \uc2 KIYA HALL (Witness): I just see like flames and everything go flying.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A suspect is now in custody after an explosion killed six people in Istanbul. It is considered to be a terror attack.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Dolly Parton receives $100 million to give to the charity of her choice. She can thank \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live.")\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Comedian Dave Chappelle hosted "SNL" after reports that the writers of the show were boycotting his appearance.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live.")\par \par \uc2 MAN: Late in the fourth, Vikings down four with the ball, fourth and 18.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, my goodness. Justin Jefferson put it in. The touch of his life.\par \par \uc2 MAN: During one of the most incredible games you`ll ever see, one of the most incredible crabs you`ll ever see.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There is no way.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And we head to over time.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Ducks up and throws. An interception. The Vikings with the knockout ball. The Vikings win the game of the year.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shoutout to my former team, the \uc2 Minnesota Vikings\uc2 . What a game?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That catch by Justin Jefferson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Have you ever seen anything like that, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Never.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It might be the greatest catch I`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is it the glove or is it something more?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s the man wearing the glove.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So we can`t call that a lucky catch.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was all skill.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was skill.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A little bit of luck.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You go, Mr. Jefferson.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS today. It is a very busy Monday for us to start the week. We`ve got a pack show. We love when that happens actually.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: Anthony Mason talks with multitalented rocker, that`s Patti Smith. She has given us music. She has given us art. She has given us poetry and now, newest project, she is giving us a book of photos, one for every single day of the year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Plus in our series "Kindness 101," Steve Hartman follows a little boy who shows us the true meaning of positivity.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And ballerina, Misty Copeland is here. She overcame many obstacles to become a star and now, she is telling the story of her mentor, a dancer who was held back by racism more than half a century ago.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And most of us had never heard of this dancer, so looking forward to Misty being here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to start with the news as we always do, and the news today is a landmark meeting between two of the world`s most powerful leaders, President Biden and China`s President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 . They are holding talks in Indonesia this morning ahead of the upcoming G20 Summit.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden, for his part is hoping he can press the Chinese leader on a range of issues everything from Taiwan`s future to Russia war in Ukraine, also North Korea`s nuclear ambitions. And we find our Nancy Cordes in Bali this morning traveling with the President.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 And the two men clearly have a lot to discuss here in Bali because they had been meeting for nearly two hours now. President Biden says they are looking to set down some red lines after trading warnings, accusations, and threats for two years.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Mr. President, do you trust President Xi, sir?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The leaders of the world`s two largest economies sat at long tables facing each other and acknowledged the frosty relationship.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We share a responsibility in my view to show that China and the United States can manage our differences.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: President Biden has described China as the biggest challenge to international order. The two nations at odds over trade technology, Chinese spying, human rights and China`s growing aggression towards Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, China has flown military planes near the self-governed island firing missiles into the surrounding waters. When House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  traveled to Taiwan this summer, the Chinese retaliated by cutting off communication with US officials.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KENNEDY (Center for Strategic and International Studies China expert): I think both sides realize that if we keep going down the path that we`re headed on, that will be conflict.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: China expert, Scott Kennedy says Russia`s invasion of Ukraine is also on the agenda. The Chinese initially stood by Russia, but that was before Ukraine began taking back territory.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KENNEDY: They know where their economic bread is buttered, with the West, not with Russia.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Today, President Xi argued they can both do better.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT XI JINPING speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: The world expects that China and the United States will properly handle the relationship.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Russia is also a member of the G20, but President Biden will not have an opportunity to confront Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  at this Summit in Bali, and that is because Putin has decided not to attend in the face of his faltering invasion of Ukraine -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Faltering" is the word for it.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes for us in Bali. Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, Ukraine`s President made a surprise visit to a key city just free from Russian troops. \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  arrived in Kherson this morning and spoke to soldiers and civilians calling the liberation the beginning of the end of the war.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay spoke to Zelenskyy where he found a city full of joy in the middle of war.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: So this is the road to Kherson. We`ve been given a military escort and we have to drive very, very carefully because the road is just littered with vehicle after vehicle that`s been blown to pieces, not to mention craters leftover from explosives and other debris from what has been nearly nine months of an effort to reclaim this area and the city.\par \par \uc2 But the children of Kherson can`t wait any longer to come outside, like Kirol (sp?) and his friends.\par \par \uc2 (KIROL speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "We`re going to play hide and seek in those trenches once we`re allowed to," he says.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy is wasting no time either.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy is this the beginning of the end of the war?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (Ukrainian President): We`re ready for peace, but our peace for our country is all our country, all our territory, and that`s why we are fighting against Russian aggression.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: For the people of Kherson, victory and freedom have never tasted so sweet. Once they could be killed for singing the Ukrainian national anthem, now, they can shout it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Russians promise that they`re here forever. They`re lying. They`re not here anymore and they`re not forever.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: But the jubilation belies unspeakable trauma for survivors like Oleksandr (sp?). He takes us to his mother`s house where he says 40 Russian soldiers lived for months.\par \par \uc2 You can see his mother`s clothing all over the floor.\par \par \uc2 As we poke through the mess, he tells me he couldn`t stand it anymore, "So we asked them to leave." They answered by pointing their rifles at him and giving him a shovel.\par \par \uc2 They made you dig your own grave?\par \par \uc2 (OLEKSANDR speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Only at the last minute after two nights of digging, he says, did they change their minds and let him live. There goes one of the collaborators, he says, pointing to a neighbor he claims helped the Russians occupy Kherson.\par \par \uc2 How can you go on living with them?\par \par \uc2 (OLEKSANDR speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "I don`t know," he says, "And I don`t know how they should live with themselves."\par \par \uc2 And now the rebuilding can begin. President Zelenskyy also told me how grateful he was to President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Congress for the American weapons that have been critical and retaking this city.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Chris Livesay, Kherson, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and as Chris is tagging out, you can still hear the gunfire and the shelling there. Happy to say he`s okay. Still a very dangerous situation.\par \par \uc2 Now, the latest on last week`s Midterms. We now know who will control the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  for the next two years.\par \par \uc2 After a very lengthy vote count, CBS News projects that Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  was reelected in Nevada. So, that gives Democrats and their Independent allies at least -- at least 50 seats, enough to keep control with a potential tie breaking vote from Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Control of the House remains still unclear today with fewer than a dozen seats still undecided.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is on Capitol Hill with the latest on the story.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Nikole.\par \par \uc2 So what are you hearing about what will happen now?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, Gayle, CBS News estimates that Republicans are favored to win a narrow majority in the House, but Democrats still believe they have a slim path to victory.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Cortez Masto.")\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: In a rousing rally, Nevada Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  celebrated after clinching a second term.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO (D-NV): When the national pundits said I couldn`t win, I knew Nevada would prove them wrong.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Her victory over Republican, Adam Laxalt gave Democrats a one-seat edge to claim the majority in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , hoping to build on the party`s agenda.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): We can do more that benefit the American people that have helped the average family.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Democrats now have 50 seats in the chamber compared to 49 held by Republicans with one key outstanding race in Georgia that goes to a runoff next month.\par \par \uc2 In the House, both parties remain shy of the 218 seat threshold to gain control.\par \par \uc2 Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said she would wait until after the election to announce if she`ll run again for leadership and criticized GOP leader, Kevin McCarthy`s presumptive bid to take over the gavel.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): No, I don`t think he has it, but that`s up to his own people.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: New members arriving for orientation in Washington were filled with suspense.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are all just refreshing our phones constantly to see who might have a slim edge.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The finger pointing only intensifying after a red wave failed to materialize.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Those who are most closely aligned with the former President did -- underperformed. Those who are talking about the future who have managed their States well, they overperformed. The American people want ideas and they want a future.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: This is happening as former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  sits down for a series of interviews about his new book that in part grapples with former President Trump`s role in the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (Former Vice President of the United States): The President`s words were reckless. It was clear he decided to be part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: As the Republican Party continues to debate the former President`s impact on the Midterms, he appears to be moving full steam ahead for 2024. Trump advisers tell CBS News, he intends to launch his campaign with an announcement from his Mar-a-Lago home Tuesday -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be following that as well. Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We have breaking news from the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  where officials say a gunman who killed three people is still on the loose. The shooting happened late last night on the campus in Charlottesville, Virginia and police are searching for a student who authorities believe pulled the trigger.\par \par \uc2 Dan Schutte of our Charlottesville affiliate, WCAV is at the UVA campus where classes today are canceled.\par \par \uc2 Dan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAN SCHUTTE: Multiple law enforcement agencies are looking for a suspect they`ve identified as Chris Darnell Jones, Jr. Campus Police warned students of a shooting through their emergency network last night that happened at around 10:45 PM. They received reports of shots fired at an on campus parking garage.\par \par \uc2 Minutes later, authorities ordered to shelter-in-place at the grounds of the university and the surrounding community, and so a massive search with multiple agencies was underway after identifying the suspect as Jones.\par \par \uc2 Police released this picture of Jones from the University`s Athletics website. It lists him as a member of the school`s football team in 2018 when he was a freshman. In an e-mail to the campus community, the school`s President said: "I am holding the victims, their families, and all members of the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  community in my heart today, and we will make plans to come together as a community to grieve as soon as the suspect is apprehended."\par \par \uc2 The President said in an e-mail that those who are injured are receiving medical treatment. Classes are canceled today.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS on the grounds of the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 , I`m Dan Schutte --Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dan Schutte, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Investigators in Texas are trying to find out what caused two vintage military aircraft to collide midair and plunge to the ground at an airshow. Six people on board those planes were killed Saturday, no one on the ground was hurt.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca has the latest reaction to the crash at Dallas Executive Airport, and we must warn you that some of the video in his report is very difficult to watch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Thousands of spectators were at the Wings Over Dallas Airshow to see more than 40 rare World War Two era planes take to the skies, Saturday. Here is a P-63 King Cobra fighter plane slowly taxiing in front of spectators before flying over the crowd, and this is the B-17 Flying Fortress bomber taking off.\par \par \uc2 Moments later, the crowd below watched in horror as the two planes crashed into one another and plummeted to the ground.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: First Responders rushed to the crash site, but all six people on board were killed. No one on the ground was injured.\par \par \uc2 KIYA HALL: I just see like flames and everything go flying and you know, just fire everywhere.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The two aircraft involved in the incident are considered extremely rare. Airshows must get special waivers to use them and pilots must demonstrate an ability to fly low and carry out airshow maneuvers.\par \par \uc2 Neither of the planes were equipped with black boxes, so investigators said witness photos and videos will be key to determining the cause of the collision.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GRAHAM (\uc2 NTSB\uc2 ): I want to express my deepest sympathies to the families who lost loved ones in this terrible tragedy.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Among those killed, Terry Barker, a retired \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  pilot of 36 years, and a former two-term City Council member in nearby Keller, Texas.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Omar Villafranca, Dallas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hundreds of millions of Americans are getting ready for Thanksgiving, it`s now just 10 days away, if we can believe that. Whether we like it or not, inflation is making everything so much more expensive. So the cost of a turkey is up 17 percent and grocery prices overall are much higher than that.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen found the reason for some of the price hikes and she has got some ideas on how you can save some money.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: An hour outside of Pittsburgh, the Jones Turkey Farm has been in the family for three generations.\par \par \uc2 A lot of work goes into raising these?\par \par \uc2 DAVE JONES\uc2  (Jones Turkey Farm): Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But \uc2 Dave Jones\uc2  has never been challenged like this.\par \par \uc2 It seems like this year has been much more difficult than ever.\par \par \uc2 DAVE JONES\uc2 : It tops everything. Everything doubled this spring.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: From the feed to the fertilizer, the cost to run the farm has gone up, which means the cost for customers has, too.\par \par \uc2 How much did you have to raise the prices of your turkeys?\par \par \uc2 DAVE JONES\uc2 : Fifty cents a pound this year, and I should have raised it a dollar a pound, but at that point, I figured I`d lose too much business I`d have turkeys leftover.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: All this as farmers battle one of the worst outbreaks of avian flu, which has wiped out at least six million turkeys, hiking prices even further.\par \par \uc2 And household items used to make some Thanksgiving staples are also gobbling up more money. The price of eggs is up 43 percent, butter nearly 27 percent, and flour about 25 percent.\par \par \uc2 Is this the costliest Thanksgiving that you`ve seen?\par \par \uc2 EMILY WEINSTEIN ("The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  food and cooking editor): Oh, without a doubt.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Emily Weinstein is the food and cooking editor of "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ." Her tips on curbing cost start with the centerpiece itself, turkey.\par \par \uc2 How do we save money on these guys?\par \par \uc2 EMILY WEINSTEIN: Just buy exactly the size turkey you need. Don`t buy a giant one hoping for leftovers.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: She says to highlight vegetables sides over meat and use frozen ones instead of fresh.\par \par \uc2 Is there a way to spread the cost here?\par \par \uc2 EMILY WEINSTEIN: If you`ve ever considered doing a Thanksgiving potluck, this is the year to do it. Ask your loved ones to bring dishes. Everyone will be happy to be involved.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And start shopping for nonperishables right now. Retailers like \uc2 Walmart\uc2  are pricing Thanksgiving staples the same as last year and \uc2 ALDI\uc2  is taking them back to 2019.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: At the end of the day, though, I mean choose what means the most to you and you can cut corners elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So a turkey at $60.00 is no problem.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes, it`s a lot of money, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And eggs up 43 percent. That`s giant.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, my mind is still blown that Thanksgiving is how many days away?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ten days.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ten days.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Are you ready?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, I am not ready. You might as well just start calling it 2023.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Get your check pen ready.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, the inspiring story of the Missouri inmate who turned his life around in prison and is now free. Erin Moriarty talks with him and the Judge who sentenced them and then helped secure his release. Telling. You`ve got to stick around for this one.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:18. Time to check the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, Nate talks with \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  who tells us how the tech giant is weathering the hiring downturn in Silicon Valley.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a big payout for Dolly Parton. We`ll tell you why she is getting $100 million from \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead: Anthony Mason speaks with punk rock pioneer that is the one and only, Patti Smith, about her longtime love of photography. Who knew? An unexpected \uc2 Instagram\uc2  popularity.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Your \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page really takes off.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH (Musician): It did. I didn`t -- my daughter was my first --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Follower.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Follower, and now I have over a million. It`s like everything else, I don`t know what they see in me, but I`m -- I`m very grateful to have all of these people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is always nice when your daughter is the first one to follow you. Go, Patti.\par \par \uc2 Why she is now sharing some of her favorite photos in a new book.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, an invitation actually, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. You get today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That`s the deal, people.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We are sharing a big update on the story that we`ve been following here for years -- actually, Erin has been following it for years, we appreciate that, Erin -- about a prison inmate in Missouri, who shows the power of transformation and second chances.\par \par \uc2 His name is Bobby Bostic. He was serving a 24- year sentence -- you heard that right -- 241 years for a series of robberies he committed when he was just 16. Bostic is now 43. He changed his life in prison. How? He went to school. He read and wrote books, even though he had no hope of getting out.\par \par \uc2 But that all changed, thanks to a very unlikely ally.\par \par \uc2 "48 Hours" correspondent, Erin Moriarty as we said, she has been covering Bostic`s case for a very long time. This is a very exciting update.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: But we have all been following this long.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Following you following it.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Yes, we have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s true.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Well, what Bostic did in prison was extraordinary. He got an education, he wrote 15 books, so we had to be there last week when Bobby Bostic was released after more than 27 years, thanks to the Judge who first put him behind bars.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Forty-three-year-old Bobby Bostic was making more than a fashion statement when he walked away from a Missouri Prison last week in a brand new suit.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY BOSTIC (Former Missouri prisoner): The new me, the suit represent the new me and it`s a business suit and I can now home go home with the new business in life, so that`s what the suit represents.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Bostic`s six first hug, Evelyn Baker.\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER (Former Judge): God, I am so happy.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY BOSTIC: I am happy, too.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: The now retired Judge who sent Bostic to prison, but spent the last four years fighting to get him out.\par \par \uc2 You were emotional when he walked out towards you.\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER: Yes. He made me cry.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY BOSTIC: I wasn`t trying to.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: This day, once seemed inconceivable. Bostic was incarcerated in 1995 at age 16, after he and a friend committed a series of armed robberies in St. Louis. One victim was grazed by a bullet.\par \par \uc2 Did you intend to hurt him? To kill him?\par \par \uc2 BOBBY BOSTIC: No, if I intended to hurt or kill him, I would have kept shooting.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Convinced Bostic was a lost cause, Judge Baker showed no mercy. After he was convicted on 17 counts, the Judge ordered his sentences to run consecutively for a total of 241 years.\par \par \uc2 We visited Bostic in prison again two weeks before his release.\par \par \uc2 Were you angry with her?\par \par \uc2 BOBBY BOSTIC: No. It motivated me to say, "One day, if I ever do get, out I will see her and she will realize the mistake she made when she sees the person I became."\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: That didn`t seem very likely. Bostic wouldn`t be eligible for parole until he was 112 years old. Many people would have just given up, but Bostic found drive even when he had nowhere to go.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY BOSTIC: Once you make so many mistakes, you get tired, you want to do something different. So, I read, that`s how I found myself, in books and that`s the most peace I ever get in the world. It is a natural high basically.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Bostic obtained an Associate`s Degree and is working now on his Bachelor`s in Business. He wrote poetry and prose, 15 books in all, including a biography of his mother.\par \par \uc2 His stunning turnaround convinced Judge Baker, she made a mistake.\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER: Two hundred and forty one years is insanity, when I think back on it, and I`ll say right now, it`s insanity. He was a kid, he was a little boy.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: But you`re the one who sentenced him, Judge?\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER: Yes, as he evolved, I have evolved.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: I mean, is it fair to say that the case of Bobby Bostic has haunted you?\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER: That would be a very true statement.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Judge Baker began advocating for Bostic. In the summer of 2021, the Missouri Legislature passed the Bobby Bostic law, which allowed him and other inmates incarcerated as juveniles to apply for parole.\par \par \uc2 How unusual is that for the Trial Judge who sentenced an inmate to then appear at the parole hearing asking for his release?\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER: I don`t know if it ever happened before, but it was something I wanted to do.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Why?\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER: Because it was time for Bobby to come home and be with his family. He wasn`t the kid I sentenced.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: And late last year, the Parole Board gave Bostic a date when he could finally go home.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY BOSTIC: And then when I see my name on the paper this morning that said "released." Bobby Bostic released. I`m like, it is official.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: So you two have a very a strange unique bond?\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: You put him in prison and you helped him get out.\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER: The Bobby Bostic I put in prison is not the Bobby Bostic who got out. Bobby did what many people can`t do. He created himself. He took the good, the bad, and the ugly and he turned it into something that`s quite beautiful.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Feel good about today?\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER: I`m ecstatic. It`s better than Christmas, Easter, Fourth of July, Thanksgiving rolled into one.\par \par \uc2 MARQUISE BOSTIC (Bobby Bostic`s sister): Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: His family thoughts so, too.\par \par \uc2 There will be some people who won`t think you and other men who`ve been convicted as juveniles deserve a second chance. What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 BOBBY BOSTIC: Oh, well, some people, they`ve got a right to feel that way, it basically is a right. It is up to us, the juveniles who get out and get a second chance to do some different to prove to those people like I`m just like you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: In case you`re wondering, none of Bostic`s victims objected to his release and one even wrote a letter supporting him.\par \par \uc2 Nationally, more and more inmates arrested as juveniles are getting second chances. In Missouri, none of those released in this past year have reoffended nor have any returned to prison. I should point out and you probably realize this, he has a lot to be thankful for, and you could probably guess where he and his family are spending Thanksgiving -- with the Judge.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are they?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. I wouldn`t have guessed that.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I thought with the family. That was a surprise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who was the lady hugging him in the car?\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: That`s his sister, Marquise, and that`s who he is living with right now, and she owns a cleaning company and he is going to work there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t see that coming with the Judge. I didn`t see that.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Yes, I think it`s going to be hard for him. He has not made any decisions for himself for the last 27 years. Harder than what I think he realized.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The judge has evolved, Erin, but I do wonder what took so long?\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Well, I don`t think she realized how much he had changed. I was very impressed to learn how much lawyers who worked for the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  in Missouri, have worked on this case, and they reached out to her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s good.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: And they`re the ones who really lobbied for this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Great job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Great story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Erin Moriarty, we appreciate you. That was awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Great story.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, up next, \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  talks exclusively with CBS MORNINGS about whether he is hiring workers at his company amid a wave of layoffs in Silicon Valley.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  is telling CBS MORNINGS that the world`s most valuable company has slowed some hiring. This follows news that \uc2 Facebook\uc2 `s parent company, \uc2 Meta\uc2  slashed more than 11,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 We were on Apple`s campus in Cupertino, California for look at some new technology for a story that you`ll see tomorrow only on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 In our exclusive interview, we asked Cook how economic conditions are affecting his company.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There have been reports that \uc2 Apple\uc2  has frozen hiring in certain areas.\par \par \uc2 TIM COOK (\uc2 Apple\uc2  Chief Executive Officer): Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Where do you see \uc2 Apple\uc2  as it tries to navigate this unpredictable economic future?\par \par \uc2 TIM COOK: So, what we are doing as a consequence of being in this period is we`re being very deliberate on our hiring. That means we`re continuing to hire, but not everywhere in the company are we hiring.\par \par \uc2 But we believe strongly in investing for the long term and we don`t believe you can save your way to prosperity. We think you invest your way to it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As we were talking off camera, you said it`s a beautiful day, people are at work.\par \par \uc2 TIM COOK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There were people working from home. Now, you have people returning to work. What did you do to incentivize people that worked at \uc2 Apple\uc2  to show up again?\par \par \uc2 TIM COOK: Well, for us, you know, we make product.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TIM COOK: And you have to hold product.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TIM COOK: You collaborate with one another because we believe that one plus one equals three, and so that takes the serendipity of running into people and bouncing ideas off and caring enough to advance your idea through somebody else, because you know that will make it a bigger idea.\par \par \uc2 That doesn`t mean that we are going to be in here five days, we`re not. If you were here on a Friday, it would be a ghost town.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can see our full story tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, including some of the new technology in the iPhone 14 that is absolutely mind blowing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Agree, agree with \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2 . I think you work better when you can see your people face-to-face -- your colleagues, your bosses, everybody. I prefer that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Those conversations in the hallway, over a desk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, you don`t want them to happen on a Friday. I know everyone wants to go out and enjoy their life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I get that. I get that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But it does -- I think it does increase company`s bottom lines and he is saying that, "We make a thing."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We need to talk about it. We need to hold it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It builds that chemistry, you know, and I mentioned the features, they are absolutely mind blowing. Life changing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And then also, there is a very unique approach that \uc2 Apple\uc2  has about working there in the building and then giving people extended time on the weekend. You would want to stick around for this interview. It`s going to be great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, don`t mind Ghost Town Fridays.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, well stick around now because Vlad Duthiers will be here in just a moment with the stories you`ll be talking about today. But first, it is 7:44. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, a little "Black Panther" tune there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Music.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "What to Watch" Vlad. Half the table has seen it, half the table has not, but you`re talking about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did it a good weekend?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It had an amazing weekend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But, have you seen it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I haven`t seen it yet because my wife was on assignment this weekend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You want to see it with her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve seen the numbers though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I want to see it with her, but let me just fill in our viewers on what`s going on. It definitely lived up to the box office hype.\par \par \uc2 "Black Panther: Wakanda" superhero sequel had the second best opening of the year with $180 million in ticket sales --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Say that number again.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In North America, $180 million.\par \par \uc2 That was the best November opening ever, beating out the previous record holder, "Hunger Games: Catching Fire" in 2013.\par \par \uc2 It`s huge. I mean, let`s just say right now. It`s huge. Everybody is talking about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I don`t know anybody that has seen it that walks out and go "Eh. Just okay."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Who would do that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really? You don`t anybody?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I thought it was good. I thought it was good. I compared it to the first one. The first one was amazing, Tony.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So I thought the first one was better, but this one was a good movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Security.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A good movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I said the first one is one of my favorite movies of all time though. So, it is hard to measure up to that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Security.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s still a good movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A good movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why you all beefing with me?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is an excellent movie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. I do -- I did here and this is not a spoiler because people have been talking about this that there`s a little reference to Haiti and the Haitian Revolution.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which I`m excited to see.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. True.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, yes, I had heard that.\par \par \uc2 All right, so chapeau to "Wakanda Forever."\par \par \uc2 Now to another highly anticipated -- Chapeau means --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hat.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I didn`t know that at all.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can give me 10 guesses, I wouldn`t have gotten it all.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I remember from French class.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You used to have a French boyfriend?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Je m`appelle Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Jon Tower, who is our broadcast producer is in my ear, "Move on, Vlad. Move on."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now, to another highly anticipated premiere Season Five of "Yellowstone," the most watched show on cable TV last season. So in the two-part premiere, John Dutton, played by Kevin Costner returns as the family patriarch. He won the race for Governor of 