et as much done as we can to continue to fulfill the agenda.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thanksgiving dinner will cost Americans a lot more this year because of inflation.\par \par \uc2 DAVE JONES\uc2 : It tops everything. Everything doubled this spring.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In 1995, a Judge sentenced a 16-year-old boy to 241 years in prison. We were there when the same Judge celebrated his release.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: You`re the one who sentenced him, Judge?\par \par \uc2 EVELYN BAKER: As he evolved, I have evolved.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Lion King" Musical.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And "The Lion King" celebrates 25 years on Broadway -- yay -- with its cast performers.\par \par \uc2 Boy, I`ve seen "Lion King" nine times and to anybody who hasn`t seen it, run don`t walk to the theater and you can`t be late.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t be late. Yes, we got that one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve haven`t seen that as well, but you were talking about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I haven`t it seen either.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh man, you`ve got to do it. Run don`t be late.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nine times is a pretty big endorsement.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you`ve got to see it. It is so fantastic what they do on that stage.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin though this hour with punk rock pioneer, her name is Patti Smith. She made her name with her music and her songs. She found new fans with her national book award winning memoir called "Just Kids."\par \par \uc2 Now, the Rock and Roll Hall of Famer has reached another audience with her photography.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, Smith has shared personal photos like these on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page, and has become very popular.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, Patti Smith spoke with Anthony, as in Mason, about compiling some of her favorite photographs. It`s called "A Book of Days." It includes a photo for each day of the year -- I like that -- including a leap day.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I love this garden.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love it, too.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I`ve always loved it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The Elizabeth Street Garden in Lower Manhattan is a little paradise to Patti Smith.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: For a person like me that loves the city, but loves statues and sculpture.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I feel like I`m in another world here. Look at his face.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: So beautiful.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In her eyes, the inanimate.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Hello? How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Can seem alive.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Sorry. I just -- I just love it here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Suddenly, she scurries over to take a picture of a statue.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 I love greyhounds.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Intimate images like these fill her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page, which she only started at her daughter`s urging in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Your \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page really takes off.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: It did. I didn`t -- and my daughter was my first --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Follower.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Follower, and now I have over a million. It`s like everything else, I don`t know what they see in me, but I`m very grateful to have all of these people.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Now, she has collected many of her favorite photos in "A Book of Days."\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: February 1st, Jesse with roses in the snow.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The day her daughter brought her flowers in a snowstorm.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Yes. That`s the benevolence of a daughter.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: July 22nd at a cafe in the Czech Republic,\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: The caption is "Sometimes the world seems nuts." And so it does.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Patti is often in cafes.\par \par \uc2 Very iconic, those coffee images.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Oh, like, I`ve done a lot of them. In fact, I just looked up "coffee cup" on my phone, and I was shocked to see there was like maybe 120 different from all over the world.\par \par \uc2 I still love in the mornings to take a picture of my coffee and my notebook and I just do it for myself.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: November 1st.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: This is also a Polaroid. This is my thinking chair. I sit and let it take me where it will, as if it were a small wooden ship. Yes. I mean, these things are -- they are like my friends.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love how you turn some of these things into almost like religious objects.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I`ve been like that since I was a little kid.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: September 14th, the birthday of her late husband, Fred "Sonic" Smith. In grief, Patti turned to photography.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Early `95 after my husband died, I went through a serious creative crisis. I really couldn`t write, I didn`t have much energy for anything except to take care of my children. But the need to create was very strong. And my husband`s old Polaroid camera was just sitting on the bookcase, and I had a pair of Nureyev`s slippers, and I just took a picture. Just one photograph. It made me feel that I had done something of worth creatively that day, and then I got hooked.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You needed it in a way.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I did.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Patti uses pictures to mark the birthdays or passing days of people she loves, or of artists she admires.\par \par \uc2 July 27th.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: On his passing day, Sam Shepard Stetson. I look at this hat, and I see Sam.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She often visits their grave sites in her travels.\par \par \uc2 Have you ever been here before?\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: No.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Down under Mulberry Street --\par \par \uc2 We`re in the catacombs.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Catacombs.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Beneath the original St. Patrick`s Cathedral.\par \par \uc2 We visited the historic basilica of Old St. Patrick`s Cathedral. The only active Catholic cemetery in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I`ve been to catacombs that have felt frightening, really.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Usually cobwebs.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I love coming into churches and lighting a candle and thinking of people that we love.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: "Memory," she writes, "Is music."\par \par \uc2 When Polaroid stopped making film for her camera, Patti was forced to switch to her cell phone.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I found that I made friends with it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s nice.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I always tell my Polaroid camera, "I still love you."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I asked Patti about her entry for January 29th.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: This -- I`m sorry, this is just --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No, no.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: This is for me like one of the most emotional pages in the book.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Of photo by a friend of Patti deep in thought.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: And when I looked at it, I saw my mother when she was negotiating a lot of difficult things and that is when I would say, "Mommy, what is it? What are you thinking? What is it?" "Oh, nothing."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: "Now I know what nothing is," she writes.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: You would pick the one that always makes me cry. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The last picture in the book, December 30th marks her birthday.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Every birthday, my mother would call me at 6:01 in the morning and leave this message "Wake up Patricia, you are born.\par \par \uc2 Her photographs have given Patti Smith love from a whole new audience.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I`ll be 76 at the end of the year, and it`s like, to be at this time in my life and feel all new people, new audiences, new generations interested in the work that I`m doing is, I mean, it`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s invigorating.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: Yes, it`s inspiring.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In the Elizabeth Street Garden, she had one last photograph to take.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: I took a picture of two lions.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The other lion, our cameraman, Kenton.\par \par \uc2 That`s fabulous.\par \par \uc2 KENTON (Cameraman): I`m a Leo.\par \par \uc2 PATTI SMITH: There you go. Thank you for bringing us here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I`m so happy we could come.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very nice shout out to Kenton because I also think he is a lion.\par \par \uc2 I didn`t know this side of Patti Smith -- Anthony.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No, this has been a side of Patti actually for quite a while, as she`d mentioned back into the 90s. But it`s not one a lot of people are familiar with. But I`ll tell you, this book of photographs is absolutely wonderful. It`s really lovely, because she`s a fabulous photographer, but it`s also very personal and very intimate.\par \par \uc2 It`s a great book just to sort of pick up in a moment or to actually spend quite a bit of time with because she has some wonderful caption. There`s a reason she picked every one of these pictures.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll bet it`s going give people ideas, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anthony, don`t you think people are looking at that and said, just for my own self that I would like to take a picture every day and see how that ends up.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think -- I think it`s really beautifully, beautifully done.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A journal of photography.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Capturing the moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what was it like for you to be able to take Patti Smith someplace she hadn`t been before? I thought that was also very cool.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I know. Well, neither one of us had been there, Gayle. We both had been to the Elizabeth Street Garden and that was just -- it was a delight to be with Patti in there, but the catacombs, I didn`t even know they were down there.\par \par \uc2 We were going to go to the garden and suddenly my producer, Rebecca Castagna said, "We should go to the catacombs." And there we were.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a good producer. I love that piece, Anthony and I also love what Patti Smith said about her iPhone camera. She made friends with it. A lot of people can relate to that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anthony, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead: Steve Hartman tells us about the power of positivity with some help from a little boy named Quinn. All for our series "Kindness 101."\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:10. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, we look at the recent rise in antisemitism in the country and how it is affecting Jewish teenagers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: With a show of hands, how many of you have seen swastikas in school? So most of you. Okay.\par \par \uc2 How many of you had been subjected to antisemitic language in school?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They tell Carter Evans about the sting of antisemitism and why social media is making it worse.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: At its very best, social media has the power to bring people together, but it has also made it easier to spread lies and to attack people for who they are, and in that latter category, that includes antisemitism now more prevalent by many measures and it has been in decades, that`s according to the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One synagogue in Los Angeles is giving Jewish teenagers now the space to talk about antisemitism as a group.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans spoke with some of those teens to find out how hatred is affecting them personally.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: At Shomrei Torah Synagogue outside Los Angeles --\par \par \uc2 AVRA MILNER (Rabbinic intern): What are the things that you have had to deal with in terms of antisemitism in your own lives?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Rabbinic intern, Avra Milner (sp?) has an honest talk with Jewish teens who speak openly.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Swastikas were being spray painted in my locker area.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: They would say "Hail Hitler."\par \par \uc2 BOY: I was just heartbroken when I heard what Kanye said because truthfully, it`s not just Kanye that is saying all of this. It`s a catalyst for so much more.\par \par \uc2 AVRA MILNER: How did you deal with it? What did you do to get through that moment?\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I took off like any jewelry that indicated that I was Jewish, and I didn`t deny, but I kind of erased it from like my personality publicly a lot of the time because I was just afraid.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: I think what struck me about this conversation is how easily all of you were able to come up with multiple instances where you were the subject of antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 So maybe with a show of hands, how many of you have seen swastikas in school? So most of you. Okay. How many of you had been subjected to antisemitic language in school?\par \par \uc2 The first time you realized what antisemitism was? The first time you experienced it, maybe saw it? How old were you?\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I was online. I think I was seven or eight and it was like a super popular like game, and there were these two trolls, I guess, and they said "Hail Hitler. Gas the Jews." And I still remember those exact words like so many years later, because they just kind of hit really deep and it was kind of like a shaping experience for me.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: What about social media? Do you see a lot of antisemitic language on social media?\par \par \uc2 GIRLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOYS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: That I believe is where I got my -- I had my first encounter with an antisemite. He ended up texting me one time in all caps of "oven, oven, oven." Which --\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Not the same text.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yes. I did report his account but like there is no way of knowing if anything actually happened.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Do you feel that it is easier for people to be antisemitic online?\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOY: The cover of the --\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOY: It is like a blanket of protection over them. They could go online as any other name, and like, say, extremely extremist things. And then if, oh, no, that account gets banned, I`ll make another one.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I think that social media and pop culture has had some positive effects on marginalized communities, but we`ve also just seen the amplification of stereotypes and harmful conspiracies.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: When someone like Kanye West amplifies those types of messages with his platform, does that give those words more power?\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Unfortunately, it does. Because he has a huge fan base. His followers on some social media account are double the amount of Jews in the world, which is crazy to think about. I know, at least some of them might be antisemites and that`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: It goes from people, you know, saying he is not really like that, he is -- whatever -- going through a tough time. And then the next second, there are Neo Nazis holding up signs on the 405 Freeway on an overpass saying "Kanye was right about the Jews."\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Which brings me to another question. Why should everybody else care about antisemitism?\par \par \uc2 BOY: It`s not just Jews, this can happen to anybody. Antisemitism, right now, it is talking about Jews, but there are so many ways that it could be flipped against a different minority and maybe even some time majority groups.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Any harm being done to human beings is unacceptable and no matter who you are, you should care not because we`re Jewish, but just because we`re other humans.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right and --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is another awful conversation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very powerful, and they`re so young.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To get this lesson so early. It`s just another really, really strong example of how your words have a lot of power. And I liked what the young man said, you know, Kanye`s words are catalysts for others.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who may think differently and then act out some of the craziest things that they`ve heard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And teenagers already have enough to deal with.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Other teenagers are acting -- I mean, they don`t -- you don`t know anything at that age.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that`s not who you want to be.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You need to learn something.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, coming up, Misty Copeland will tell us about a dancing inspiration, a ballerina whose career was nearly ended by racism.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead in our series "Kindness 101," Steve Hartman and his kids give us a lesson in positivity -- we all need a little positivity these days -- you`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We appreciate you for that.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring some of the stories that we call "Talk of the Table" for obvious reasons.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where did the camera go?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The camera hit the TV.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Over there. We`re still here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just a little collision in the studio, but all good, all good. Everybody is good.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so we call it "Talk of the Table" because this is a table and we`re talking. Who is in pole position?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m going first.\par \par \uc2 So this weekend while Teddy was watching a movie and Eloise was napping, I was staring at my phone, I looked at Apple News.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They have the most popular stories on there and the first one was about brain boosting foods.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There were blueberries on there, so I ate some blueberries.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Boosted the brain.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I boosted the brain up and then I read the second most part of their story on Apple News, which was a paper from scientists at \uc2 NASA\uc2  at the Jet Propulsion Laboratory.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Proposing that the reason why we have not seen intelligent life out there in the universe is because of something called the Great Filter.\par \par \uc2 The idea is that there is some kind of inherent dysfunction in all intelligent life that makes that life eradicate itself before it gets to the point where it can go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 So the paper is about how we avoid becoming victims of the Great Filter, so we can explore the whole universe. It encourages a moment of introspection here on planet Earth about how far we`ve come and what it will take to go even further.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And in the meantime, pass a blueberry. I do like Apple News, too. I like how they write stories. I do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love it. The rankings are always great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you get it very quickly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So were those regular blueberries or were those blueberry gummies? Because your mind was working.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: CPD only here in New York State.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a good question. What kind of blueberries was it, Tony Dokoupil?\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is Craig Daniel, Craig leaving his Bond days behind him if you will, and using a new commercial to show off a new image. So pay attention to how he starts and how he ends.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Belvedere Vodka advertisement.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at Daniel Craig with the groove. That was a very casual Daniel Craig dancing through a hotel in Paris. It`s a vodka commercial, directed by Oscar winner, Taika Waititi. What I think is so fun, you have to go online, look at the whole thing because he starts out very Bond, James Bond, gets in that car and you see him doing very loosey-goosey movements and it says, in the commercial his face tells one story but his body tells another and the commercial is telling about a dialogue between the two.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Craig is showing us a different side, a different image. I love this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re doing this, it is never too late series where I learn to dance. I have to give you a little bit of a preview. I don`t dance quite as well as that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s because you didn`t have any Belvedere.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I guess not. That could be the answer, although maybe that`s it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like a sip or something. You`d be dancing a lot different.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And also, don`t you think it helps when you have a grill in your teeth, too? You need a TD.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Attitude. Attitude.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A little internal inspiration. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is a young girl`s reaction to seeing a photo of herself, which she has been waiting for a long time. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PATRICE LANDER (Jahzara`s mother): Almost there. We are almost there.\par \par \uc2 JAHZARA LANDER (Child model): That`s my diaper?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE LANDER: Is that your diaper? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 JAHZARA LANDER: Look.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE LANDER: I don`t know. Is it?\par \par \uc2 JAHZARA LANDER: Yes. Look, this one.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE LANDER: Oh, my gosh. Who is that?\par \par \uc2 JAHZARA LANDER: Me.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE LANDER: Is that you?\par \par \uc2 JAHZARA LANDER: Me. That`s me.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE LANDER: That`s you.\par \par \uc2 JAHZARA LANDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is little Jahzara rocking the Afro puffs. She`s finally seeing her picture of herself on a box of Pampers. Her mother says Jahzara posed for that picture two years ago, and it was their first booking as a child model. The mom says they`ve been looking ever since to see the Jahzara`s picture on the store shelves, it finally happened last month when they went to \uc2 Walmart\uc2  in Ontario, Canada and that`s what happened.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, don`t you love her little voice, "That`s me."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s incredible.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that`s me. Look at me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so important to see yourself, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, shoutout to Jahzara.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, it is important what that means to that little girl.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, that was her job. She was the child model.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She`s the model. She is like, "When am I going to see myself?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to turn now to our series "Kindness 101" with Steve Hartman. In it, we share stories built around kindness and character and the people who have mastered those qualities.\par \par \uc2 Today`s lesson: Positivity.\par \par \uc2 Steve and his kids show us how one family`s positive outlook in tough times opened a window to a world they could never have imagined.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good morning. This is Meryl, my soon-to-be nine-year-old daughter here to help me teach a lesson on --\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Positivity.\par \par \uc2 Emmett at the Dictionary Desk, can you define this word?\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Yes, I can, Meryl.\par \par \uc2 Positivity is the practice of extracting a sunny takeaway from a not so good situation.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Yes, positivity isn`t being happy because you won the lottery. That`s easy.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: It`s not too easy.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: No, not winning the lottery, but being happy if you win the lottery.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Yes. Winning the lottery is going to be something that everyone is excited about. Positivity is really like you have to take a bad situation and look at it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Yes. Positivity is finding joy in the negative, strange as that sounds.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And this is a very important tool to have in your tool belt.\par \par \uc2 So let`s look now in our library of life lessons and pull down a tale that illustrates the power of positivity.\par \par \uc2 Aside from immediate family, no one was allowed in the house to see three- year-old Quinn Waters of Weymouth, Massachusetts, and more importantly, Quinn wasn`t allowed out.\par \par \uc2 JARLATH WATERS (Quinn`s father): We basically keep him in a bubble just as a precaution.\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS (Quinn`s mother): Even a cold, a common cold, could be something that will bring him back into the hospital.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Parents, Jarlath and Tara say Quinn`s natural immunity was temporarily wiped out after he got a stem cell transplant to treat his brain cancer.\par \par \uc2 QUINN WATERS (Cancer patient): We are the Champions --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Fortunately, the kid is a fighter and he kept a mostly positive attitude.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Do you want a drink or anything, dude?\par \par \uc2 QUINN WATERS: No, thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But it still stunk.\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS: He sees all of this happening and he knows he`s stuck inside and there would be days --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Days when Quinn was literally pounding to get out.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, staring out a window is a poor substitute for walking out a door. Quinn`s connection to the outside world has been limited to whoever passes by, which hasn`t been all that limiting, actually.\par \par \uc2 JARLATH WATERS: It started out off family members like coming to the window.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Then the neighbors started showing up to entertain -- the police caught wind, and pretty soon topnotch performers were just showing up on Quinn`s front lawn.\par \par \uc2 It is turning into like a vaudeville stage out there.\par \par \uc2 JARLATH WATERS: Yes, the window kind of became his window through the world, you know?\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It got so you never knew what might happen by. One minute it could be a dog parade --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Look at that dog.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: The next, a team of Irish step dancers -- everyone brought together by word of mouth and a will to help Quinn get better.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Which his parents say did start happening.\par \par \uc2 JARLATH WATERS: It`s the positive energy from all of these people that we believe has gotten him through his sickness, you know. You can never repay, you know -- just maybe pay it forward, you know.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Being indebted never felt so fortunate.\par \par \uc2 (MAN singing.)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Quinn`s parents, Tara and Jarlath joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Hello.\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS: Hello.\par \par \uc2 JARLATH WATERS: Hi.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: First of all, you`ve got to tell us how Quinn is doing.\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS: Oh, Quinn is doing really well. He relapsed in 2021, but since that time, he received radiation and he`s been stable.\par \par \uc2 JARLATH WATERS: Playing T ball know with his friends in school.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He looks great -- hair, I`ve never seen hair on the boy.\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JARLATH WATERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS: He`s got a great flow going. He`s got the hockey hair we call it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: What struck me most after our first visit was the fact that you seemed to think that positivity would actually make him better.\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS: I don`t think it can all be just modern medicine. I think you have to have a positive outlook on things.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: So you think that thoughts can change reality?\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS: One hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 JARLATH WATERS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS: Yes. They`ll change the outcome, and I think it`ll change your outlook as you go on anything.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And has the grass grown back on your yard?\par \par \uc2 JARLATH WATERS: Grass will never be the same.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Well, thank you so much for joining us. We`ve been positively thrilled to have you.\par \par \uc2 TARA WATERS: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 JARLATH WATERS: Thank you. Bye.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: A really good lesson there, and a reminder that often when one door closes another window opens.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Until then, stay kind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will do exactly that, Meryl. It`s so great to see him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know. It is so good to see him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Up and about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And doing well. I just love these pieces. The family dynamic between --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Hartman family.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Hartman family is just so good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. They are a lesson in all things goodness. All of them. It`s great. Thank you, Steve and Meryl, and of course, Emmett.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at who is in our progressive greenroom, it is ballerina, Misty Copeland.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Misty Copeland. She is here to share the incredible story of her mentor who faced down racism with a whole lot of grace while trying to perform. She knows a little something about that, too, I bet.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:39, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ballerina, Misty Copeland dazzles audiences like here in the ballet, "Giselle." She became the first Black woman to get promoted to principal dancer at American Ballet Theatre in 2015. Her new book is called "The Wind at My Back: Resilience, Grace and the Other Gifts from my Mentor, Raven Wilkinson." Copeland writes about her own path and lessons she learned from Wilkinson, a Black ballerina in the 1950s and `60s whose career was derailed by racism.\par \par \uc2 Misty Copeland joins us now. Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND (Ballerina and "The Wind at My Back" author): Good morning. I`m so excited to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are so glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, you didn`t learn about Raven Wilkinson until you were well into your career. How did you learn about her?\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: I was being a bunhead, that`s what we call them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A bunhead?\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: A bunhead. You know, I was I was doing my research in history just wanting to learn more about the world that I was a part of.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because ballerinas wear the bun.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: Yes. And you know, kind of like being a nerd I guess.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: And I was watching a documentary called "The Ballet Russe to Monte Carlo" about the company. And, you know more than halfway into this documentary, this Black elegant woman comes onto the screen and it just stopped me in my tracks because I had no idea that a Black woman existed in that company. She was the first and only Black woman to dance with the company. I didn`t know anything about her.\par \par \uc2 And my initial reaction was recognition. I could see myself in her and the power of representation, it is so important. And then I was angry, because I didn`t -- how could I not know about her?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: Especially being the only Black woman ABT, you know, for the first 10 years of my career, you know, it just made me frustrated that our history is not documented, and it`s often erased as Black people, especially in America.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then Misty, you get to meet her and you two become incredibly close.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was that like for you getting to know her and I wonder what it was like the first time she saw you dance? I would be so intimidated by that. It would be like cooking to me for Julia Child. You two became very close.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: Well, my manager, Gilda Squire, she actually did the -- she went and found out where he was living.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She found her.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: And we found out she was living a block away from me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: In the West Sie, it was stunning. And then I found out that she had been attending all of my performances since I joined the company when I was 17 years old.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: So, I didn`t have that moment of "Oh, my gosh. Raven is coming to watch me for the first time," she`d already been watching me, which was incredible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But when you knew she was in the audience, that`s when you knew.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: But I knew she was in the audience. It was this amazing support. And, you know, getting to know her, she just gave me a different sense of belonging and purpose than I had before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you said she supported you not only as a dancer, but as a woman.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: Yes. To have -- to watch her journey and see that so much hasn`t changed or hadn`t changed in 50 years, between our careers made me want to push even harder to be able to continue on and go into spaces and do roles that she never got an opportunity to dance and not just her, so many Black ballerinas throughout history.\par \par \uc2 And Raven just became, as she said, the wind at my back, and that`s what she would say to me before every performance, and to this day, I still have those voicemails saved, where she would say whenever you go onto the stage, let me be the wind at your back. I`m going to be there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you can hear her voice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. So meeting Raven must have been a life-changing experience. Let`s go to another life-changing experience, becoming a parent, my mother. Your son is now seven months old.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We can call you Jackson`s mom.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: Oh, I love that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How did that change you?\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: I feel like it`s made me step back from myself. You know, as a dancer, as an artist, as an athlete, as a performer, so much of our time is focused on us and putting the work into our bodies and recovery, whatever it is, it`s so focused on you.\par \par \uc2 And to have a step back and worry about this other little being has completely just changed -- I don`t know -- the way that I look at the time and you know, yes, there is time that I have to give to myself and focus, but maybe not so much.\par \par \uc2 A balance, it`s been all about finding a balance, but I can`t wait to go back on stage with this new perspective of having this incredible love.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is that what is next?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When do you go back on stage? Yes.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: That`s so -- we`re looking at the winter of 2023.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, all right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, that`s about a year from now.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, Misty Copeland, thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MISTY COPELAND: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congratulations on everything, and the new book. "The Wind at My Back" goes on sale tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tomorrow on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, we will show you something that could make a great gift for anyone on your Holiday list, the Kelvin 23 Urban Ultra mini tool. It`s a compact device that gives you 23, yes, 23 tools in the palm of your hand.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to our exclusive offer, it is available at 40 percent off retail price, that`s a good price and you can start shopping for that and other products that might be essentials in your everyday life and make your life a little bit easier.\par \par \uc2 Scan the QR code on your screen right now or you can visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A little MacGyver like.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I`m a big fan of that. You know, I`ve been trying to add little skill sets at home. I told you I did the caulk behind the sink.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In the kitchen. Life changing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got a lot going on. You`ve got a little flame in the backyard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I got the flame -- well, I had to shrink down the flame. I got too big of a flame. And I want to do the grout in the shower, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve got a question for you. Can you fix this tear that I have?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Give me the story here?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was getting dressed this morning and I think I`ve been eating too much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The panther attack in there.\par \par \uc2 Oh, what is happening?\par \par \uc2 Well, they told us we have 45 seconds to fill. I didn`t think that was going to be how it got filled.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How does this look?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s wrong with you? What`s wrong with you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, we`ll see you tomorrow, all right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a good look. Pioneering.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel like Terry Crews right now.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221114eibe00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 North Korea Fires 20-plus Missiles Near South Korea; Obama and Biden Make Midterm Push; Close Race in Virginia; New Details in Pelosi Home Attack; Another Interest Rate Hike; CVS Agrees to $5 Billion Opioid Settlement; Iran Sentences Protesters to Death; Abortion Providers Go Mobile; Rapper Takeoff Murdered; Remembering Julie Powell; Lena Horne Makes Broadway History; Living with Tourette Syndrome; Flipping Workplace Norms; "The Good Fight" Final Season; One Smart Cookie, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Elizabeth Palmer, Robert  Costa, Scott MacFarlane, Nancy Chen, Imtiaz Tyab, David Begnaud, Adriana  Diaz, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 14,044 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We are all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): You heard me say it before, we`re at an inflection point in history.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R) (Pennsylvania Senate candidate): Here`s the question: Are you happy with where America is headed?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With the Midterms just six days away, both parties are in a fight to the finish over who will control Congress.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Air raid sirens sounded on a South Korean island one.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: One North Korean missile landed close to the South Korean sea border.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  meets today to talk about interest rates. What it could mean for the US economy.\par \par \uc2 STACY ESSER (New Jersey realtor): I would describe the housing market as sort of a tug of war between buyer and seller.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the music world is remembering Migos rapper Takeoff who was tragically gunned down.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN I fell to my knees and I started crying because something like this wasn`t supposed to happen.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The sentencing hearing has begun for the shooter found guilty of killing 17 people at a high school in Parkland, Florida back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 Phillies\uc2  win Game Three the World Series and take it to one lead against the Astros.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Monstrous blast.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : You get one of these.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN (First Lady of the United States): Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): The President and the First Lady greeting trick or treaters at the White House for the first time.\par \par \uc2 They had a great time giving out candy to all the trick or treaters. Knowing Biden, I`m guessing the kids got licorice and \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  schedules.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): Designers have created a very limited edition $134,000.00 iPhone with a diamond studded gold Rolex on the back. Here it is here. Look at that.\par \par \uc2 Yes, so now when you drop your phone, you can crack two expensive screens. An iPhone with a built-in Rolex. Can you imagine that?\par \par \uc2 Imagine a phone -- imagine a phone that also tells the time.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Imagine somebody buying that and they are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Imagine that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they will.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Too much money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and they will.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS today.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got some powerful stories to share with you, including a very personal one from our David Begnaud. He is talking to people who have Tourette Syndrome. It`s a disorder that can lead to isolation and stigma as well as physical suffering.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is very personal because as David has discussed with us before, he himself has a mild form of Tourette`s. He will tell us about the myths, the medications, and most importantly the hope available for those really suffering.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the excellent Paramount+ show "The Good Fight" is coming to an end after six seasons. It`s a good excuse to have the beloved star, Christine Baranski here in studio.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with an alarming sign of rising tensions between North and South Korea. Developments over the past 12 hours, the North has fired nearly two dozen missiles into the sea around the Korean Peninsula.\par \par \uc2 According to officials in South Korea and Japan, one of those missiles splashdown in an area that is supposed to be and was until now a no-fire zone.\par \par \uc2 South Korea responded with its own missile test while it continued joint military exercises with the US.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer has more now from Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen an unprecedented number of North Korean missile launches this year, but today`s was unusually aggressive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: South Koreans watch the news that one more time their neighbor was firing weapons in their direction and this time, far too close for comfort. One missile flew south and actually crossed the informal maritime border. It landed in the sea just 37 miles from the South Korean City of Sokcho. The other flew near the island of Ulleungdo, near enough to activate air raid sirens --\par \par \uc2 (MAN and WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: And warn people to seek shelter.\par \par \uc2 All week, US and South Korean war planes have been flying joint drills, the latest in a series of military exercises that have infuriated North Korean leader Kim Jong-un.\par \par \uc2 Today he retaliated with multiple missile launches, and in turn, South Korea fired three air-to-ground missiles of its own northward in a dangerous tit-for-tat.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Analysts and my diplomatic sources say that there is no reason to think tension on the Korean Peninsula is going to diminish. anytime soon, in fact just the opposite. They expect that North Korea may escalate with an underground nuclear weapons test sometime quite soon -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Elizabeth, reporting from Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Here in the United States, the midterms have you know, are just six days away -- six -- and some big names, they are out on the campaign trail right now for Democrats and Republicans, too.\par \par \uc2 Former President Obama spoke in Nevada and President Biden spoke in Florida, both urgently pressing their message that democracy is on the line next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa joins us now from Pennsylvania, one of five States with extremely close races for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Big win for the \uc2 Philadelphia Phillies\uc2  last night in the World Series has a lot of people in this region excited, but voters in the Philadelphia suburbs, they`re paying attention to more than sports -- the economy, crime, abortion rights, those issues, also the talk of the town.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States): Hello, Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Democrats came out swinging Tuesday night hoping to fend off a potential red wave.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA: Just about every Republican politician seems obsessed with two things, owning the libs and getting Donald Trump`s approval. They`re not interested in actually solving problems.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was in Las Vegas where the Senate race in Nevada is razor thin.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Democracy is on the ballot this year.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in Florida where Democrats are in an uphill battle against GOP incumbents.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The economy works for everybody.\par \par \uc2 Compared to the MAGA -- mega MAGA, I call them, trickle-down economics that the Republicans offer these days.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Control of Congress might come down to five crucial States, all with candidates neck and neck in the polls. Bucks County, Pennsylvania northeast of Philadelphia is where Republican candidate, Mehmet Oz kicked off his final push for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ: Are you happy with where America is headed?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: As Democratic opponent, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , Tuesday reiterated that he is ready to serve as he recovers from a stroke.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D) (Pennsylvania Senate candidate): This campaign is all about anyone that`s ever been knocked down, that has had to get back up.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But it`s the economy that has independent, Chelsea Mitchell concerned. She owns this vintage vinyl store where inflation has become a broken record.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MITCHELL (Business owner): Well, I can tell you that a copy of Abbey Road on vinyl used to cost like $24.99 and has jumped $5.00. I`m voting for whoever is -- has the small town in mind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Later today, First Lady Jill Biden will be in Philadelphia. She and President Biden have been busy campaigning in Pennsylvania for Democrats. She will attend Game Four of the World Series -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it`s a good World Series, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Control of the House is also up for grabs on Tuesday. Our latest battleground tracker poll shows Republicans favored to win a majority, but there are a lot of close races including one in Virginia involving a member of the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane spent time traveling in that State and now he is here with us.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And Nate, good to see you as well.\par \par \uc2 This is a race against Republican candidate, Jen Kiggans and incumbent Democrat, \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 , and Congresswoman Luria has something you need to talk about, what she calls her effort to prevent another attempted insurrection and attack on democracy.\par \par \uc2 It is just unclear if that`s going to sway enough votes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: At a Farmers Market in Virginia Beach, there`s one thing on voters` minds.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Food prices are bad. I mean, it`s gone skyrocketed.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Prices of everything, you know, gasoline, groceries. It`s unreal.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Like in so many congressional races, inflation and abortion rights are dominating the debate between Democratic incumbent Congresswoman \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  and Republican challenger Jen Kiggans. both \uc2 US Navy\uc2  veterans.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): I think a woman should have a right to choice.\par \par \uc2 JEN KIGGANS (R) (Virginia Congressional candidate): We are suffering this year because of the failed policies of the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  administration.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Except, this is a race in a campaign season like no other.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  maliciously repeated this nonsense.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Because Luria isn`t a typical incumbent. She is one of nine Members of Congress who became national figures leading the House January 6 Select Committee.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): Our hearings have shown the many ways in which President Trump tried to stop the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Her opponent slams Luria for focusing too much on the investigation.\par \par \uc2 JEN KIGGANS: So, I think it shows that she is out of touch with this district.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Being on the Committee is out of touch?\par \par \uc2 JEN KIGGANS: So focusing on January 6th, making that a priority and using her celebrity on that Committee to do things like fundraise, and I just think it`s out of touch with what the voters of the second district are really caring about right now.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Luria strikes back criticizing Kiggans for questioning Virginia`s 2020 election result, a State President Biden won by 10 points.\par \par \uc2 Is there any political peril to being a face of the January 6 Committee in one of these tossup evenly split districts?\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): Honestly, I don`t care. The truth about it is, is that the work that the Committee is doing is so important. Being on the right side of history, getting to the facts.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The three of the other nine members of this panel are going to be gone at the end of the year. Two are retiring. Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , as you recall, lost her primary in Wyoming. This was a wedge issue, her service on the committee, and last night in Michigan, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who says she is going to defeat the election deniers endorses a Democrat in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. It is a little jarring to see that picture, but she really is standing up for what she believes in. But it`s interesting when you talk to voters, emotions are so intense on both sides. Everybody feels very strongly about the selection.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: There we are in Virginia Beach where they have strong thoughts as you say, but they bring up inflation. They bring up the grocery prices. They bring up the gas prices.\par \par \uc2 I mentioned the January 6 Committee, they`re all familiar with it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s not top of mind for them.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Not top of mind.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One thing affects you today, the other thing may affect you down the line.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: It`s immediate versus long term.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scott, it is good to have you in studio. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, the Midterms are less than one week away and we will have full coverage for you here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  starting at 8:00 PM Eastern on November 8th.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: CBS News has confirmed new information about the attack on House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s husband, Paul. Sources say the \uc2 US Capitol Police\uc2  have cameras outside the Pelosi home, which captured images of the break-in, but officers only noticed a problem after local police had responded. This was originally reported in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The man accused in the attack, his name is David DePape appeared in court yesterday in San Francisco. He has pled not guilty to multiple charges.\par \par \uc2 According to a Court filing, DePape told police that he was "on a suicide mission." He had a list of politicians he was targeting after allegedly breaking in and asking Paul Pelosi "Where`s Nancy" before attacking him with a hammer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we have breaking news this morning on the opioid epidemic: \uc2 CVS Health\uc2  has announced a $5 billion settlement of lawsuits tied to the company`s handling of prescription opioids, which of course are addictive.\par \par \uc2 The settlement, if accepted would be one of the largest payouts from the crisis and the first nationwide agreement with a major drugstore chain.\par \par \uc2 Others including \uc2 Rite-Aid\uc2 , \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  have made deals with individual States. CVS, for its part says it did not admit liability and nonfinancial terms have yet to be resolved.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is expected to raise interest rates by another three-quarters of a percentage point today. Regulators are trying to bring down the worst inflation we`ve seen in 40 years. But that policy change has pushed mortgage rates above seven percent.\par \par \uc2 As Nancy Chen shows us, the rising cost of borrowing is hurting home buyers and sellers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TILL (Homeowner would be home seller): Want one more treat?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: for about a year, Andrew Till tried to sell his New Jersey home in search of more space.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TILL: So we`ve had a lot of activity and a lot of offers on the house when we had it on the market. It is like every weekend, our broker calling and saying, "Can you get out?"\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But even after 10 lucrative offers, he and his family took it off the market. He says there are a few options for them to buy a new house, especially after refinancing their mortgage in March to below three percent.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been looking to sell, why haven`t you?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TILL: Our interest rate right now that we have locked in is very low. So our borrowing costs would more than double.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: With today`s rate, a monthly mortgage payment on a $400,000.00 home would cost you about $750.00 more than the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 How would you describe the housing market right now?\par \par \uc2 STACY ESSER: So I would describe the housing market as sort of a tug of war between buyer and seller, and that tug of war is almost creating like a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Stacy Esser is a realtor in the New Jersey area.\par \par \uc2 STACY ESSER: It is not that we`ve never seen a seven percent interest rate, it is that we`ve never seen interest rates in basically one year go from two and a half percent to seven percent.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Esser says, this kind of volatility is causing deals to fall through.\par \par \uc2 STACY ESSER: A buyer might have a pre-approval at a certain rate, get an accepted bid and then the interest rate shift, they didn`t lock in and all of a sudden, they can`t afford that same house.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: About 60,000 deals have been cancelled so far this year, the highest share on record since the start of the pandemic, and new listings are down about 19 percent from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 What is the end game to all this?\par \par \uc2 STACY ESSER: I think we`re going to see way less transactions in 2023. I think people are going to be sitting on the sidelines until things settle down.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Till says, he will wait until then.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TILL: We just wanted to see if we could strike while the iron was hot, it didn`t happen and we`re happy here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The housing market is a leading indicator of whether the nation is heading into a recession, but here is the thing, it could also contribute to one.\par \par \uc2 Fewer homes being purchased also means less construction, as well as people buying fewer items like appliances and all of this could have huge ripple effects throughout the economy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I haven`t played tug of war since I was a kid. It`ll be interesting to see who wins in this one, the buyer or the seller?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Or we could all potentially end up losing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember that, too. Thank you, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Turning to an escalating situation in Iran. A Court has ordered seven anti-government protesters to be executed. It`s all part of a crackdown against demonstrations that have spread across the country over the past six weeks after a young woman died in police custody.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab reports on this latest Court case.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In a trial lasting just 10 minutes with no defense lawyers present, these men, one only 22 years old were sentenced to death for protesting.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In a video posted online, the Mother of Govagub Mohammad (sp?) pleads for his life saying, "What kind of Court orders an execution and the first appearance?"\par \par \uc2 Iran`s crackdown on the nationwide protests have been brutal. According to the group, Human Rights Activists in Iran, thousands more face similar hearings.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Demonstrations, which were initially led by young women, outraged at the death of 22-year-old Mahsa Amini, who in September died while in the custody of Iran`s so-called Morality Police for are apparently wearing her mandatory headscarf improperly.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Filling the streets of the capital, Tehran chanting "Women, Life, \uc2 Liberty\uc2 ," and even burning their hijabs.\par \par \uc2 In the deeply conservative coastal city of Bandar Abbas, women at a university tore down a cafeteria wall that separates men and women in defiance of the regime`s other strict gender segregation laws.\par \par \uc2 Now, Iranians of all ages are demanding the fall of the theocratic regime, but some are paying the ultimate price for rising up. This video posted online, which CBS News cannot independently verify apparently shows a group of policemen beating a man on the ground before the sound of a gunshot is heard.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Now, early reports from inside Iran suggests that police are investigating that case, but make no mistake, these protests are the biggest challenge Iran`s all powerful clerics have ever faced.\par \par \uc2 Now, they are blaming "foreign agents" for the unrest, but Iranians tell us all they want is to be free -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Imtiaz Tyab, thank you. Just disheartening video.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, we have problems in this country, we obviously do, but the rights and freedoms we have protected that kind of terrible, terrible stuff from happening. I mean, killing protesters on day one of a trial. Think about that. That`s not justice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up: A look back at the career of a rapper called Takeoff from the group, Migos, who was just 28 years old when he was gunned down yesterday, and how it is part of a bigger story about gun violence across America.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up: A CBS MORNINGS broadcast exclusive, we`re going to take you to a new mobile abortion clinic in Illinois set up after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Why it will be parked near the border with other States.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So let`s talk about Tourette Syndrome. Do you know anyone who has it? Chances are you do, but you don`t know.\par \par \uc2 There are several famous people diagnosed with Tourette`s like singer, Billie Eilish and US soccer star, Tim Howard, and our own David Begnaud who is here to introduce us to three people who are living with TS.\par \par \uc2 So I was saying, David, if you hadn`t told me I wouldn`t have known you had it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Really? You`ve never seen me tic?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I honestly haven`t.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You thought I had seen it?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes. You just were kind enough not to say, "Hey, what`s that?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I would have asked you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes. Knowing you, you would have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would have just said, "Are you okay?"\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: This morning, we`re going to let you hear from a cop, a local TV reporter, and a CPA -- they all have Tourette`s, but their cases are really so different.\par \par \uc2 And so this morning, we`re going to raise awareness about having Tourette`s and having this conversation to sort of dispel some myths, talk about some medications, and also just let you see how this man for example, had one of the most severe cases I`ve ever seen in my life and is functioning as close to normal today as possible.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking at the big issues for voters in the Midterms including access to abortion. A new study by an abortion rights advocacy group found that abortions declined by six percent nationwide in the months after Roe v. Wade was overturned, but the number of procedures has gone up in the States where abortion is legal, especially in places like Illinois, which border States with more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz traveled to the southern part of that State, home to a new mobile Planned Parenthood clinic, complete with two exam rooms, a waiting area and a lab.\par \par \uc2 Her report is a CBS MORNINGS broadcast exclusive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN cheering.)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This may look like your standard long haul RV, but step on board and it`s a fully operational medical facility, or as these Planned Parenthood employees call it, an act of activism.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (Planned Parenthood of St. Louis Chief Medical Officer): It looks very much like a regular brick and mortar Health Center for us.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But you can like feel the fact that we`re not on --\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We`re not in a building.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: It is a little more bouncy.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Dr. Colleen McNicholas is the Chief Medical Officer at Planned Parenthood of St. Louis. She says the mobile clinic will be parked in Illinois near the border with Missouri to make abortions easier to access from out-of-state.\par \par \uc2 They plan to provide medical abortions by pill here in the mobile unit by the end of the year and surgical procedures next year.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Hands Off Our Body.")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It`s part of Planned Parenthood`s answer to the Supreme Court overturning Roe vs. Wade in June. Since then, they`ve seen a 370 percent increase at their Southern Illinois clinic from patients traveling from beyond Illinois and Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Was the influx what you anticipated?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We saw it a lot more quickly than we expected it to come.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It came fast and furious.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: It came fast and furious.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We are in Illinois where abortion is legal, but is providing abortion to women in Missouri where it is illegal, undermining Missouri law?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: You know, our priority is making sure that the constituents who have been left behind in those States have access to basic healthcare.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Pro-Life, Pro-Woman.")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Missouri only had one abortion clinic left before Roe fell due in part to lobbyists like Sam Lee of Campaign Life Missouri, an antiabortion rights group.\par \par \uc2 SAM LEE (Campaign Life Missouri): We think it`s very irresponsible for Planned Parenthood trying to solicit Missouri women and then when a woman has a complication, where can she go back to, to get help when that abortion van is gone?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Instead, Lee wants to see more resources go to alternatives for pregnant women here in Missouri, like pregnancy centers and housing help.\par \par \uc2 SAM LEE: It always comes down to for women who are unsure about their decision who are being coerced into their decision, being given true choices.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Roe v. Wade has got to go.")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: With so many raw emotions being expressed on both sides of this issue --\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We were really thoughtful about the security and safety of the unit.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The RV is equipped with cameras and will initially have a security guard.\par \par \uc2 Still, Dr. McNicholas says this is the time to push boundaries.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We`re really at a critical moment, a public health crisis where we need folks to be thinking differently, to be working together, to be acting boldly and to be taking risks.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Adriana Diaz in Southern Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, coming up, the latest on a shooting that killed a prominent rap artist and why many see this as a part of a wider picture of violence in these United States.\par \par \uc2 Also a reminder for you this morning, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Get the day`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That`s a deal.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, the all too familiar scourge of gun violence in America has taken the life of another well-known musician. The rapper known as Takeoff was part of the Grammy-nominated hip-hop trio, Migos.\par \par \uc2 Police say he was shot dead early yesterday outside a private party in Houston. Two others were injured and the gunman got away. Authorities say Takeoff was an innocent bystander. He was just 28 years old.\par \par \uc2 There has been an outpouring of support for Takeoff, along with the anger at gun violence in America that has cut down a young talent.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Video obtained by TMZ appears to show the moments leading up to the shooting that killed Takeoff. In it, you can see fellow Migos` member, Quavo who was Takeoff`s uncle in a verbal dispute.\par \par \uc2 Takeoff is seen standing on the outskirts before shots ring out.\par \par \uc2 (GUNSHOTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Moments later, Takeoff his on the ground. Police say he died on the scene, outside a third flood bowling alley in downtown Houston.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We have no reason to believe that he was involved in anything criminal at the time, just as people described him, a very peaceful, loving, great entertainer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Migos performance.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: At just 28 years old, Takeoff was part of one of hip-hop`s juggernaut, Migos.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Migos performance.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The group has claimed two number one albums on the Billboard 200.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In 2018, they appeared on "The Late, Late Show" with James Corden to showcase their rap skills and their sense of fashion.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Following Takeoff`s death, tributes poured in from across the hip hop community.\par \par \uc2 Chance the Rapper tweeted that he was "brokenhearted and confused," calling Takeoff a "one of a kind friend."\par \par \uc2 In an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  post, Drake reminisced about seeing the world with Takeoff and "bringing light to every city we touch."\par \par \uc2 And the artist known as Desiigner announced he was done with rap, following Takeoff`s death.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, there have been several high profile rappers who were shot and killed, including the Notorious B.I.G. and Tupac in the 90s, Nipsey Hussle in 2019, Pop Smoke in 2020, and PnB Rock in September of this year.\par \par \uc2 But experts say those isolated incidents are part of a much bigger issue.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE RICHARDSON (\uc2 Ohio State University\uc2  Professor of Literacy Studies): It`s not just a Black thing, it is not just a hip-hop thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Elaine Richardson is a Professor of Literacy Studies at \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2  with a focus on hip hop in African-American culture.\par \par \uc2 This is a larger problem that we all need to be trying to figure out how to stop the killing.\par \par \uc2 FAT JOE (American rapper): I mean, it`s a terrible time not just for rappers in America. Like, it is so violent in America right now. It is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is scary.\par \par \uc2 FAT JOE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We spoke to hip hop elder, statesman, Fat Joe last month before Takeoff`s death.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Migos performance.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like Fat Joe, some of Migos` music falls into the gangster rap genre, which can have violent themes. But as Fat Joe told us, not all of the stories artists tell indicate who they are privately.\par \par \uc2 FAT JOE: Fat Joe was primarily a gangster rapper his whole career. I`ve been with my wife for 29 years. I`ve put my kids through college. My kids go to private school.\par \par \uc2 You met Fat Joe off the record. He is the nicest guy in the world. But image and music, tough guy. This is creativity. This is music. This is art.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, the hip-hop community, now, the music-at-large community is mourning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because it is another senseless death.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there is outrage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Senseless is a word.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is outrage, because you know, there is gun violence in America. But listen, I have to be honest here. As a Black community, we find outrage when there`s violence against our race against other people, and I`m not saying these two things have to do with each other. But we have to have the same type of outrage when there`s Black on Black violence.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I just get tired of the thoughts and prayers. There are too many of them, whether it`s a mass shooting or something like this, where a young life is taken from us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I`ve met Takeoff actually a couple of times because I know the Migos group.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was really one of the nicest guys and you know, people get lumped up and make assumptions that are often incorrect, but this is another senseless example.\par \par \uc2 They`re playing a game, the game gets out of hand, and people pull out a gun and start shooting. It makes -- I think Chance said it best "brokenhearted and confused" because this is so unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 And it does put another stain on rap music.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: . and rap artists.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s -- yes, and that`s not right either.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, all right, some rappers recently saying being a rapper is one of the most dangerous jobs in America, which is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you`ll be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 A little fun with some sad news off the top.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, we`ve got some sad news. This is really heartbreaking when I saw this pop yesterday, I knew I wanted to share it with everybody.\par \par \uc2 We are remembering Julie Powell, the creator of a popular blog that inspired the movie "Julie and Julia." Powell`s husband says she died last week after a cardiac arrest. She was just 49 years old.\par \par \uc2 In 2002, you will remember Powell who was an untrained cook famously blogged about her attempt to spend the year making every recipe in Julia Child`s "Mastering the Art of French Cooking."\par \par \uc2 Well, her blog was turned into a best-selling book and a movie. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Julie and Julia.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That of course is Amy Adams as Powell, cooking in her tiny kitchen. In 2012, Powell joined our table and explained how her cooking led to wisdom outside of the kitchen, too, take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JULIE POWELL (August 11, 2012): There is a great moment that I think is - - that she writes in the middle of the page that I think is sort of a great life lesson which is, it will look like a total mess, you will not know how you`re ever going to come out the other side. But just keep doing and all will come out as it should and I think that`s a good life lesson.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you know, her story was so fascinating. In 2002, she was working as an administrator. She was an aspiring writer and she was knocking her head up against things.\par \par \uc2 She knew she had her art inside of her, didn`t know how to get it out. She started blogging and then thousands and thousands of people waited on every week on her every word, every week for her blog and you know, the rest is history.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`re a movie and Amy Adams is playing you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Her age was what so shocking to me, that at 49, she lost her life.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Forty-nine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Forty-nine years old.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charming book, a reminder for women in particular cardiac arrest is real, it`s a risk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You should checked out, talk to your doctor.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She will be missed. She will be missed.\par \par \uc2 All right now to a moment of history in New York theater district. This happened yesterday. The Brooks Atkinson Theatre was renamed the Lena Horne Theater.\par \par \uc2 Horne is the first Black woman to have a Broadway theater named in honor. You know, Horne who died in 2010 was one of the few Black movie stars during the 1940s.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Lena Thorne performance.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She got her start at the legendary Cotton Club in Harlem when she was only 16 years old. She started movies and on Broadway, and importantly, was very active in the Civil Rights Movement.\par \par \uc2 I asked Horne`s granddaughter, actress and producer, Jenny Lumet, what does this historic moment means.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JENNY LUMET (Actress and producer): This is a celebration of the contributions of Black women to theater history, I think.\par \par \uc2 I think it is a celebration of all people and theater history. But Black women, as we know, tend to get the short end of the stick and this, I feel like, it`s not about Grandma. I say, I feel like it`s about a "thank you."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Grandma was something else.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I got to meet her once.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I did. When Oprah had her Legends Ball, Lena Horne couldn`t come. So, Oprah wanted to deliver the gift to her personally because she got all the legends diamond earrings, that`s what Oprah does, and went to take it to her in person. I got to come.\par \par \uc2 So we`re sitting in the bedroom with Lena Horne, talking to her, we walked out of there like, "Oh, my God, I can`t believe."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s Lena Horne.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s Lena Horne.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was. I`ll never forget it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She`s a legend. When I was stuck at home and COVID, I watched "Stormy Weather." I encourage everybody to go out and watch this movie, "Stormy Weather." It`s one of the most incredible movies ever and she is the main star, but of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she is so beautiful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Of course with Cab Callaway and Bojangles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, we`re now two days after Halloween, but we`re talking Halloween costumes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are talking Halloween costumes. Supermodel, Heidi Klum, she never disappoints when it comes to her creative Halloween costumes.\par \par \uc2 This year, she was thinking outside of the box, the tackle box. Let me show you what I mean.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Villainous.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Klum dressed up as a worm alongside her husband, who was dressed as a fisherman. She posted this video and several pictures of her just warming around during her annual Heidiween Party.\par \par \uc2 Here she was interviewed, laying on the red carpet. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: What is it like you`ve done so many spectacular costumes, how did this one come about?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI KLUM (Model): I don`t know. I just wanted it to be something random I wanted to think outside of the box, and you know I thought, a rain worm would be kind of fun.\par \par \uc2 We all know worms, but I don`t think I`ve ever seen anyone being a rain worm before.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, no, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is a reason for that. She`s very clever.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is of course, the Queen of Halloween.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very clever. Very creative, but it`s so crazy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. It`s creepy and this is her thing. She does this every year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I hope, one year, I can get to go to this party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never disappoint.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really awesome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: A personal look at a misunderstood disorder. David Begnaud explores his own diagnosis with Tourette syndrome and talks about some treatments he says can help.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. The hit show "The Good Fight" now in its final season on Paramount+, star, Christine Baranski is here and don`t worry, folks, no spoilers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. C is for cookie, but it`s also for Christina. Milk Bar`s Christina Tosi is here with her new cookbook devoted entirely to that delicious treat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She says it`s her safe place. We want to go there.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Tensions increase on the Korean Peninsula after the North fires a record number of missiles in a single day.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: My diplomatic sources say that there is no reason to think tension on the Korean Peninsula is going to diminish anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Democracy is on the ballot this year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is all hands on deck for Democrats and Republicans in key Midterm races.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER (R) (Georgia Senate candidate): They`re coming after me with everything right now.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA): Character matters.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : This campaign is all about anyone that has ever been knocked down, that has had to get back up.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ: Are you happy with where America is headed?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No.\par \par \uc2 JEN KIGGANS: I think it shows that she`s out of touch with this district.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A close House race in Virginia hinges on how voters feel about the investigation of the January 6th attack.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): The truth about it is, is that the work that the Committee is doing is so important.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Today, the Fed considers raising interest rates again to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The housing market is a leading indicator of whether the nation is heading into a recession, but here is the thing, it could also contribute to one.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the entertainment world is paying tribute to Migos rapper, Takeoff after he was gunned down in Houston.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: They called in Takeoff because he`d record his verses off of just a single take, and he was so proud of what Migos had achieved and for what they achieved.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: James Corden always says it`s such a great time in the car with whoever is with him, but last night, he was talking about the three of them being in the car and they`re all singing "Sweet Caroline." Neil Diamond, that was a very funny video.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so hard to believe. People are still processing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The loss today.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour though with a look at a nervous system disorder that is personal to us, because it affects one of our own.\par \par \uc2 Tourette Syndrome causes sudden repetitive movements known as tics that are very hard to control sometimes. An estimated 350,000 to 450,000 kids and adults in this country are living with Tourette`s, but many of the cases go undiagnosed.\par \par \uc2 You might be surprised but some of the people who live and we have to say thrive with it in their lives. For instance, actor Dan Ackroyd, and singer Billie Eilish have both talked about growing up with Tourette`s.\par \par \uc2 And our own lead national correspondent, David Begnaud is raising awareness by sharing his own experiences and insights in this report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN (Milaca, Minnesota Police officer): It is kind of hard to explain, but --\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: In this New York City exam room, the patient, Craig Elgin is here because he has Tourette Syndrome.\par \par \uc2 Watch what happens when he talks.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: Other than -- having to tell people I have Tourette Syndrome.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: His condition is evident by those uncontrollable interruptions called tics.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: I`ve got to toe tics. I`ve got legs stretching tic, hand stretching, wrists, arm stretch. I got the huff, the sniff, the laughing tic, hard blinking, throat clearing, face grimace and the new ones come and go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Like if you tried right now to stop what you`re doing, could you?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: I really have to focus.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Deuce Bigelow: Male Gigolo.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Tourette syndrome has long been an easy target, disrespected in pop culture. That just leads to misconceptions, says Joohi Jimenez-Shahed, the neurologist who is treating Greg Elgin at Mount Sinai Health System.\par \par \uc2 What are some of the most common misconceptions about Tourette`s?\par \par \uc2 DR. JOOHI JIMENEZ-SHAHED (Mount Sinai Health System neurologist): You know things like why can`t you hold it down? So, it must be something that you`re doing on purpose. The misperception that it`s all about swearing and you know, doing inappropriate behaviors, which may affect some individuals with tics and Tourette`s but not everybody.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Even with persistent symptoms, Craig Elgin at 37 years old functions pretty normally. He is a police officer with the Milaca Minnesota Police Department.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: It doesn`t inhibit anything that I do. I mean there are times where you know like everyone you get a little self-conscious about it and you`re like, "No, I shouldn`t do that just because of my tics."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It looks tiring.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: You get used to it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Really?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: Yes, like online that`s like one of the top three questions I get asked the most.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Off duty and online, Elgin has brought together more than 150,000 people on social media. That`s where he`s known as Tourette`s cop. That`s how I found him.\par \par \uc2 I was diagnosed with Tourette`s at the age of six.\par \par \uc2 I feel like we know each other I know.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: I know. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I`ll give you a hug.\par \par \uc2 I introduced Craig to Kelsey Christiansen who also reports on television. She, like me has a mild case of Tourette`s.\par \par \uc2 Watching her on the air, you really wouldn`t notice anything.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CHRISTIANSEN (KSTP 5 Eyewitness News reporter): . are still awaiting their sentencing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Off camera though, that continuous eye blinking you see is the giveaway.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I have nose tics, a tongue tic, neck tics, stress often induces them. So does just talking about Tourette`s.\par \par \uc2 People are like "What are your secrets?" I tell everybody my secrets, because what I say is, if you try my secrets, you ain`t going to do me like I do me.\par \par \uc2 Meeting other people who have Tourette syndrome is invaluable to better understanding this complex neurological disorder.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: Like I kind of use social media as like, my therapy. Like I`m able to talk about it. I`m able to open up about it like yes, you know, it`s completely okay to have Tourette syndrome. You can still be normal and make noises.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What causes Tourette`s?\par \par \uc2 DR. JOOHI JIMENEZ-SHAHED: Hard question to answer. We know that there is probably a strong genetic component. There`s some new research coming out that relates to environmental exposures. So, things maybe that happened with the individual`s mother perhaps during pregnancy, other psychosocial features that might influence the manifestation. So it`s a very complex disorder in that sense.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There are extreme cases and this is how that can look.\par \par \uc2 Alex Brown was 15 years old when doctors at Baylor University Medical Center in Texas took that video.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN (Tourette syndrome patient): I had to take a medical leave from school.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You couldn`t even go to school.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: For about five or six years, we didn`t do anything. We didn`t go out to eat. Didn`t go to church. We were homebound. We were completely shut off.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: At the height of it all, what were your tics?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: Anything you can think of. I was opening car doors. When someone would cook at my house, I would walk by and just touch the hot stove. I would scream really, really loud to the point to where now I can`t sing because my vocal cords have been stretched so much.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He lived with his advanced condition called coprolalia for more than seven years Before Dr. Joohi Jimenez-Shahed recommended that he have stimulators implanted deep into his brain.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: It`s right on this side, right in the middle of my head.\par \par \uc2 DR. JOOHI JIMENEZ-SHAHED : So deep brain stimulation is kind of like a pacemaker. So you have a battery, it is placed in the chest, but rather than the battery being connected to wires that go to your heart, the wires actually go into the deep brain structures that are involved in that circuitry that produce the tics.\par \par \uc2 And for some individuals who have these really kind of otherwise difficult to control tics, this may be an effective way to get some relief.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The procedure, which is often used in Parkinson`s patients is increasingly becoming successful in controlling severe Tourette`s.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: I woke up from the surgery and it had already started working. They had not even turned the stimulator on and I could already tell a difference and that was so liberating. It was magical.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What could you tell?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: It was very soothing. It felt good not having the urge to do tics.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Five years after he received the implants, doctors at \uc2 Baylor\uc2  made this recording.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: Graduate from high school. I am at \uc2 Abilene Christian University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What is now possible because of this?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: I passed the CPA exam, I`m working a job. I can do whatever I want, that any other person can do.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But for many others, like Kelsey Christiansen, just getting a diagnosis and finding the right corrective treatment for them can be difficult.\par \par \uc2 What does the diagnosis do for you?\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CHRISTIANSEN: It`s clarifying. It`s comforting, because I know why I`m doing what I`m doing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What led you to go in and see the doctor?\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CHRISTIANSEN: You know, not to sound cheesy, but your story. You`re the only other reporter/journalist on air that I know that has Tourette`s.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I don`t think it`s a big deal. Do you?\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CHRISTIANSEN: No, I don`t at all.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Road Within.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And the media has helped, too, create in conversations, raising awareness about Tourette`s, like in this moment between \uc2 David Letterman\uc2  and musician, Billie Eilish where she is ticking.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LETTERMAN\uc2  (Talk show host): Then the awards -- what`s going on? A fly?\par \par \uc2 BILLIE EILISH (Singer): No, I`m ticking.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LETTERMAN\uc2 : Really?\par \par \uc2 BILLIE EILISH: Yes. If you film me for long enough, you`re going to see lots of tics.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But for people like Craig Elgin, even without the stigma, there is still suffering.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the day after we interviewed and while our team was still with him, he left the interview and drove to an emergency room. In the days before we met him, he was sleeping less than an hour a night. It had become too much to bear and so we watched as he walked himself into a hospital asking for help.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How is he?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How is he? Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He is better. So here`s what happened. I introduced him to Dr. Shahed here in New York City. She saw him, she put him on medication. He hadn`t been on meds before. The meds helped, they reduced the tics. But the meds for Tourette`s often have bad side effects. So they cause really bad nightmares.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And restlessness. But they dialed back the medication. He`s still on it. But you know what he said? He said, "David, I want that deep brain stimulation device," and so he is coming back to New York in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And he and Dr. Shahed are going to talk about that as a real option for him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But when you have it that way, and you see the man you were talking to and you see the difference that it makes, as scary as that deep brain stimulation looks and sounds, you think if that`s going to help me, I want to do it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It was life changing for him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I can see that. I think most people when they think of Tourette`s, they think like the clip we showed with the movie where you just scream and say something wildly inappropriate.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We`re often the butt of a joke.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is so much more than -- yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You know, we`re often just the butt of a joke. And I thought, did you guys know I had Tourette`s? Did you see me ticking?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve talked about it early on. We chatted about it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You saw me?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I knew because you told. If you hadn`t told me -- and now when I see in the piece, you do the thing with your neck, I`ve seen you do that. But I didn`t think it was Tourette`s. I just thought you were stretching your neck.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then also, you know, it seems like there`s -- when you have it sometimes just before you`ll do a tag or just before you do a tease, I`ll see it and then also see your ability to turn it off.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Control it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then this profound serenity comes over you, which is what makes you connect with viewers. There`s an ability for you to be so in the moment that I think is somehow a counter strength that comes out of this condition that I`m jealous of to be honest with you. It`s an amazing thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but not everyone with Tourette`s, to your point has that type of control.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: No. Look if you ask somebody to control their tics, it`s like me saying, "Hey, don`t sneeze. Hold it." Right? or "Don`t blink." At some point, you just erupt.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you don`t know when you`re going to tic.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you can go days and not have a tic, right?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Listening to that piece, listening to that was making me start to tic. You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Stress induces it, but when the red lights sometimes comes on, on the camera, I do stop. But in the last couple of years, I`ve gotten more tics, you know, and like my eyes dart all the time. It can be really humiliating.\par \par \uc2 But the whole point of this story was I wanted to create a conversation because I want little boys and girls watching to know that if David Begnaud be where he is as the national correspondent of CBS News with Tourette`s, you can do whatever the heck you want.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Mission accomplished on that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mission accomplished. Thank you, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Thank you all listening.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great story. Great piece. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right ahead, the author of a new book will tell us how companies can break down barriers in the workplace and improve diversity and inclusion.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:12, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up after more than 10 years, actor, Christine Baranski is stepping away from the iconic character, Diane Lockhart. She`ll tell us about the final season of "The Good Fight" and the role she would like to play now.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We hear a lot these days about the push for diversity and inclusion in the workplace. But what exactly does that look like?\par \par \uc2 Well, there`s new research showing that more diverse, more equitable, and inclusive leadership actually helps organizations make better decisions, in fact, nearly 90 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 And now, there`s a new roadmap to achieving that. Author and executive coach, Patrice Gordon is just out with a new book. It`s called "Reverse Mentoring: Removing Barriers and Building Belonging in the Workplace." She says that everybody benefits when leaders and members of underrepresented groups come together, tell stories, share information, and Patrice joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Patrice, good morning.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON ("Reverse Mentoring" author): Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much for being here. The title, "Reverse Mentoring." I think it`s almost self-explanatory, but not quite.\par \par \uc2 So tell us what reverse mentoring is.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: So, reverse mentoring actually started with Jack Welch in \uc2 GE Capital\uc2  in the 90s. He recognized that the leaders in the organization was so far removed from what was actually happening with the Internet. So the younger individuals and generations that came in, he got them to mentor the senior leaders so they had a really good understanding of what is this thing coming and how do we use it and how do we make the most of it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So rather than older workers pairing off with younger, newer employees, it`s the opposite.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Absolutely opposite. And what I have done in my research, and you know, since you know the social change that`s happened, it`s really use the difference that we have within organizations to make sure that leaders understand what it is like to be an underrepresented individual.\par \par \uc2 So in the work that I`ve done and the research I`ve done, I`ve extended that age, to gender, to ethnicity to neurodiversity, to veteran status, for example. So those underrepresented individuals within the organization are really trying to make sure they have a voice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you speak of what you know, because you did this. Your boss -- you ended up mentoring your boss.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who is this harder for? Is it harder for the mentor or the mentee?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: It depends. And I was quite a confident mentor. So, I was quite confident in my story. But I definitely think through my research and speaking to lots of leaders, the mentees definitely have a hard time, right?\par \par \uc2 As a senior leader, you are expected to have all of the answers, you`re expected to know the direction of the business, you`re not necessarily expected to be vulnerable and necessarily courageous in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 And being a reverse mentor means that you need to put yourself in that position of a novice and really listen to the underrepresented.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So those in position of leadership, it`s hard for them to understand how to be vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because they are told not to and they feel like it`s a sign of weakness in front of their employees. What advice do you give mentors?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Mentors or mentees --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mentees.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: The mentee, the senior leader --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The older ones, okay.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: The older ones, the senior ones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Really understanding why you want to get involved. So you have power, how do you want to use it within the organization? Are you willing to share your power? And if you are, you need to put yourself in that room and put yourself in that vulnerable position to be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you have to have buy in from the top. You know, after the death of George Floyd, many companies said, listen, we need to change, we need to be more diverse, we need to be more inclusive, and that seems to have petered off a bit.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You say there is a difference between diverse -- don`t say diversity, you say inclusion.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Absolutely. Absolutely. So Verna Maya, she says, you know, diversity is being invited to the party, but inclusion is being asked to dance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: I`m really in organizations, what we`re trying to do is, yes, we can bring as many diverse people in as possible, but actually, are they going to enjoy working in the environment? Are you going to get value from them because they feel included? And that`s the key that I`m trying to get to here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`ve got to you got to make sure it`s spread throughout the company. It doesn`t matter if the leader says this is what we want to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If it is not passed on to middle management. You can have these training sessions and coaching sessions. What difference do they really make if there`s not buy in from everybody?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Absolutely. One of the key things I`m really invested in encouraging companies to do is making sure that we set some targets. So, as we have financial targets or sustainability targets, you know, everyone`s really clear that we can get to net zero by 2050. But what about diversity, equity and inclusion targets?\par \par \uc2 I think we need to get braver, we need to kind of put a stake in the ground, and we need to hold everyone to account and the way that we do that is through hitting them in the pocket, making sure that their financial package is linked to diversity, equity and inclusion goals.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And another way we do that is getting the book.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Yes, get the book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is getting the book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Patrice Gordon, thank you so much. "Reverse Mentoring."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I could listen to you all day. I love your accent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is on sale now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It`s time to bring you some of the stories that we like to call around her "Talk of the Table" -- why? Because this is a table and we be talking and Tony is in pole position.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a table, we be talking. I`m Tony.\par \par \uc2 I don`t want to talk about me, myself, and I. I want to talk about Ross Chastain who is a NASCAR driver who did something really remarkable at the Xfinity 500.\par \par \uc2 Now to set it up and for Gayle King to understand in particular. We`ve got to show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: First, what typical turns look like in NASCAR.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You slow down on the turn, because if you don`t slow down, you will spin out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually know Formula One. Excuse you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, but this is NASCAR, a little different. I don`t know anything about anything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually know Formula One.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, I`m sorry. You think the World Series though has a halftime.\par \par \uc2 I`m just --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is not true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am just covering my bases, okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was money talk.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. All right. So anyways --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is not true, Nate. Go ahead, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we got a slow turn. That`s what they usually do. Ross Chastain needed to make up multiple spots to qualify for the championship series. So coming around the final turn at the Xfinity 500, he opted not to slow down and instead did this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Take a look at what he did.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I have never seen anything.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He dropped it in the fifth gear, accelerated through the turn and hugged the wall. The best part of this is his explanation for why he did it. Do we have the sound?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROSS CHASTAIN (Motorsports racing driver): I played a lot of NASCAR 2005 on the GameCube with Chad growing up, and you could get away with it. And I never knew if it would actually work and I mean, I`ve done -- I did that when I was eight years old.\par \par \uc2 I just made the choice, I grabbed fifth gear down the back and full committed. Once I got against the wall, I basically let go of the wheel and just hoped I didn`t catch the turn for an access gate or something crazy, but I was willing to do it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did he say in the beginning? What did he --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He pulled a move in a real NASCAR race that he had pulled on a Nintendo GameCube in 2005. He had done a video game move in real life, and it swung him -- and set a track record and qualified for the Championship Series.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that`s not common.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No one had ever done that before, that moment, and when he was all casually like, I hope it didn`t catch a turn for a gate. Oh yes, I hope I didn`t die in a ball of fire, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was risky, but it worked.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I wonder if other drivers will do that now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope not. That looks very dangerous, Tony.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is -- drumroll please. Oprah`s Favorite Things List, just in, just in time for your holiday shopping. It is one of the favorite things that we do over at Oprah Daily.\par \par \uc2 So I was with her when she unveiled some of this year`s favorites. It is a whole process.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Family run, husband-wife duo`s.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is nice. I want to get one of these myself.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Local makers.\par \par \uc2 Truff. Truff. Truff. Truff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The pineapple is good.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Brands started by friends and work.\par \par \uc2 Great for first apartments. Love, love, love.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: When you shop from these folks, you`re investing in their dream.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Investing in a dream. That`s what Oprah likes to do. We`ve got an exclusive clip.\par \par \uc2 You`re seeing first here on CBS MORNINGS when she surprised one of the small business owners who made the list for the first time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: That`s why I`m choosing it as a favorite thing.\par \par \uc2 JAINABA JENG (Kitchens of Africa founder): Oh, my God. I can`t even believe this. I am going to tell my family. They`re going to go crazy. You`ve been teaching me forever.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I`ve been teaching you forever and now I`m eating your sauces. That`s a full circle moment.\par \par \uc2 JAINABA JENG: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is Jainaba Jeng. She`s the founder of Kitchens of Africa, a company that introduces African taste to your kitchen. It`s based by the way in Raleigh, North Carolina and its roots extend all the wake back to Gambia, a tiny country in the Western part of Africa, where Jainaba Jeng was born and raised.\par \par \uc2 But it`s fun because Adam Glassman who is a creative director and Oprah and I were all on a Zoom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So we`re just talking to them about "What`s special about your thing? Why do you think you should be on?" And then Oprah pops up on the Zoom. So, you saw the moment where Oprah just popped up on the Zoom and people just lose it every single time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Every time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that never gets old.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man. All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Favorite Things List" is out, shopping for everyone on your list.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Appreciate it. Thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is a new list of the best places to retire in the US. Move over Florida. Sorry, Tony. Pennsylvania is number one in this year`s US News and World Report Survey. Lancaster to be exact, just 80 miles west of Philadelphia ranked as the most desirable city for retirees.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania took five of the top 10 spots. Florida took four though you know still a decent place to go. The survey looked at six major factors -- housing affordability, happiness, healthcare, quality, retiree taxes, desirability, and job market rate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t see you shoveling the driveway on that list, but that`s what you`re doing in Pennsylvania in the winter.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, don`t be a hater, okay?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was surprised though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was surprised.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was surprised about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is something fishy about that. I love Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There will be an investigation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, what have you got, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We were very excited for our next guest. What have we got, Nate? Christine Baranski, a legend in the house today.\par \par \uc2 For more than a decade, this Emmy, Tony Award winner has graced your TV screens as Attorney Diane Lockhart, first on the hit CBS series "The Good Wife," and then on its spin off. "The Good Fight." That`s on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 Now, she is saying goodbye to this beloved character in the show`s final season. Here`s a taste.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Good Fight.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, she still got power. Christine Baranski joins us in the studio.\par \par \uc2 We felt that the minute you walked in the room, power coming in, power coming.\par \par \uc2 So Christine, I don`t know if we`re ready. I don`t know if we`re ready to let you go. Are you ready? This is it?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI ("The Good Fight" actor): No, don`t think I am. I`ve been -- I`ve played this character for 13 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: And not only the character, but the people with whom I work, the whole atmosphere. It took me through so many times of my life, marriage and losing my husband and getting three grandchildren and it`s just part of the fabric of my life and the character, too, you know, articulate, intelligent, passionate, liberal feminist. I liked walking in her shoes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But there is no -- and you walk in her shoes very well. She wears very nice shoes and very nice dresses. But you know, there is no other show like this on the air on any platform, because you guys tell stories in real time.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sometimes shockingly, so.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so real.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: And it wasn`t even intended that way, but we were shooting the pilot for "The Good Fight," six years ago and it was election night and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  became, you know, was claimed the winner. And suddenly the show took a pivot and became about what it is like -- what it has been like for --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you use the real names. That`s the thing. Sometimes shows will take real headlines, but they don`t use the real names.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You all used real names and tell the people.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: No, we are pretty brave and also, the show is -- you know, address topics that are utterly prevalent. In fact, the final season takes us up practically to Election Night, which is uncanny. Our season finale will just abut that Election Night with all of the anxiety and, you know, the precipitous moment that that we`re living through the show takes us through that.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I`m very proud of the show for that reason, and people will look back and think, wow, that captured this crazy dystopian time in our country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I`m so proud and happy for you. Last time, you were here. You said I`m working harder than I`ve ever been. I look better than I`ve ever been.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Did I say that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: That`s a little arrogant.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, it wasn`t arrogant. You didn`t say it in an arrogant way. You didn`t. You didn`t.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Well, I feel better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You did not say in an arrogant way. And you said I`m making more money than I ever have.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. I am glad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now you`re 70 years old and you claim that very proudly. Do you still feel all of those things?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I do. You know, I just finished "The Gilded Age" last Thursday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People love that show by the way.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: And so I was number one on two call sheets and doing you know, finishing one show and a day later, just going right into the next show. And you know what it`s like, getting up at 4:30 in the morning to, you know, get in the van and, and spend 12 to 14-hour day, and I thought, "Damn, I`m still doing this." And I am still having fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And dam good doing it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I would hope that I`m an inspiration because I think life can get better and better especially for women. We raise our children, we hold things together. Why do we think suddenly that it is ending, just when in fact so many aspects of your life can open up?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I agree.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: You know, travel, learning. I took several courses at Oxford in a summer program. I`ve got grandchildren, I`ve got family, I`ve got friends.\par \par \uc2 Life is you know, where`s the wood -- life is good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is good. So, what is next for you? Speaking of just getting started.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I am going on a little vacation. I`m going --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well deserved.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I am going -- tomorrow, I am going on a vacation, but --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Broadway?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Oh, I don`t -- you know, I think if we are lucky, "Gilded Age" will come back for a third season because it`s so popular and it`s just a fantastic show and that would take me into yet another cycle, and.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: You know that that`s eight to 10 episodes that takes at least six to nine months.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: But, you know that`s life in a corset and a wig, but you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People say it has the best dialogue and that you have the best lines in the show.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: It is up in my contract, the best creators of the lines, otherwise, they don`t show up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The stuff that you get to say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But are there roles that you are dying to play that are out there that you would like?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: You know, I have Juilliard training and we trained to do classical plays, and I love language. And I would love honestly, to do Shakespeare or to do a play like Tom Stoppard that really demands, you know, the skills that I attained in drama school. And honestly, "The Gilded Age" makes a lot of use of that because of the formality of the dialogue and the setting, that old New York and she`s such an old New York character.\par \par \uc2 So yes, I would love to just, you know, have well-written monologues and just get to sink my teeth into something with the language.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, Christine, you know people and I think if there is anything you want to do, your life really is yours to design. They don`t call you a legend for nothing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When they come up with a name for whatever age we`re living through, you can be in that show. That would be terrific.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Thank you so much. It`s such a pleasure to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. Congratulations. Congrats. And your grandmother name is what?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Mini.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mini. I like it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mini.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s yours, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I`m working on it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the child is how old? Seven now?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s either going to be Gaya or Gan-Gan. Whatever.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, we`ve got figure this out. The time is ticking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is almost in high school.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Luca is writing full sentences.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s going to be Mini. Thank you, Christine.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Good Fight" is now streaming now on Paramount+. Oh, Nate got jokes. New episodes out on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, coming up next, we`re going to talk to a Chef and Milk Bar owner, Christina Tosi about her new book all about cookies. It is "All About Cookies." She`ll tell us how she finds inspiration for her new recipes. But first, look at those cookies, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Our next guest, Dessert Empire is getting a little sweeter. Award-winning chef Christina Tosi is the owner and founder of the famous Milk Bar. The national bakery chain is known for treats like the compost cookie and their layered birthday cakes.\par \par \uc2 Her new book is all about cookies, a Milk Bar baking book, shares more than 100 new cookie recipes.\par \par \uc2 Christina Tosi joins us now.\par \par \uc2 How are you doing, Christina? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI (Milk Bar owner and founder): Hi, Nate. Good morning. How are you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s good to have you. My mouth is salivating because we have all these treats in front of us. We`ll get to that in a second. Now, you said your love for baking all started with cookies. How so?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI: Cookies were my entry into the kitchen. I was raised by these fierce matriarchs and we baked, that`s what we did in our free time. Everything from like the classic dense fudgy oatmeal cookie to a bar cookie.\par \par \uc2 Cookies were also like our way out to create community and support people and show love and make people feel something, feel seen, to speak to their soul.\par \par \uc2 Cookies is where it all became and cookies is like what Milk Bar is all about. And of course what this cookbook is all about, which is like a homecoming for me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know you say cookies are your safe space. They are your secret to living life on your own terms, and a cookie is not just a cookie. So what do you mean when you say a cookie is not just a cookie? It seems like you have life lessons even when it comes to cookies.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI: They are so -- I mean, dessert outright has its own life lesson, right? Dessert is a totally opt in course. It`s a way for us to speak to people`s souls. It`s a way to make people sort of feel something to indulge et cetera.\par \par \uc2 But for me, the cookie is the underdog of the pastry world. It has this humility. It is portable, it`s transportable, it`s shareable. It`s this low barriered entry. There is a cookie that exists in nearly every culture in this world. And yet a cookie in my opinion has infinite possibility to break the rules and breed creativity and really speak to someone`s tastebuds.\par \par \uc2 So for me, the cookie -- the cookie needs more love and that`s what this cookbook is all about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love the cookie, Christina. The cookie is my number one dessert. It`s not optional, by the way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Breakfast too, Tony. I see you in the morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is not even kidding.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am not lying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel if we did pumps right now, I`m getting a little bigger every day, but I love those cookies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And my mom used to say life is unpredictable, eat dessert first and I live by those words. So, tell us about the cookies on the table in front of us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That you dropped off. These are great.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI: Okay, so first off, I brought you a slew of like the classic Milk Bar cookies dense and fudgy. You can get the better bakeries nationwide. You can get them online at MilkBarScore.com. Those are like the hallmark of Milk Bar.\par \par \uc2 But then I also wanted to bring you some of my favorites. This babe right here is the Jelly Donut Sammie. It`s a dough that`s made using fryer oil. You put your favorite jam or jelly inside and then you can decorate it like the top of your favorite donut.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve got to go for it.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI: Do it. Go for it. There are these what I call cookies that like to party, those giant cookies. That`s what we call an XXL Cookie or Cookie Cake. You can swing by our New York City flagship and create your own cookie, but it`s a cookie that you can slice like a slice of cake that you can share.\par \par \uc2 There`s a peanut butter chocolate caramel one, which is a love letter to my favorite candy bar the Take5 candy bar. There is a chocolate one with pretzels and potato chips.\par \par \uc2 I mean cookies really are about being seen, bringing your tastebuds to life and just giving you the possibility to like shake you from sleepwalking, see life in color, and so I figured I`d send you a whole array.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we thank you. Your cookbook is beautifully done. I could lick the page. You`ve got a de leche cookie, a peachy shortcake cookie. Do you have a favorite cookie if you had to choose and then we`ll let you go?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI: I mean, that`s like asking someone to choose their favorite like grandkid on your terms, but I think it has to be the fluffernutter. It is the Sandy Cookie made with peanut butter. You swirl some marshmallow in, it`s actually on the back of the cookbook. It is this baby right here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Christina, we`ve got to get out of here. We love you. Thank you for the cookies.\par \par \uc2 "All About Cookies" on sale now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wherever you buy your books.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221102eib200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Completes Twitter Takeover; Midterms and the Economy; Capitol Rioter Sentenced to Seven Years; Outrage in Uvalde; Putin`s War of Words; Russia`s Renewed Offensive; Army Announces Troop Childcare Changes; Mississippi River Water Crisis; Danny Elfman`s Musical Mind; The Right Skill for the Job; "From Scratch," \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Jonathan Vigliotti,  Nancy Cordes, Janet Shamlian, Holly Williams, Catherine Herridge, Ben  Tracy, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 13,969 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Friday. Happy Fri-yay to you. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  takes over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . How his plans for the platform could have a major impact on hate speech and on global politics.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I don`t know if it`ll be immediate, but it`s pretty clear. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will probably be invited back on the platform.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got good news on the economy, but fears of a recession remain. What it means for the upcoming midterms.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): GDP report: Things are looking good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: New outrage from the family members of Uvalde`s shooting victims. Why the top cop in Texas still refuses to resign.\par \par \uc2 STEVE MCCRAW\uc2  (Texas DPS Director): DPS as an institution, it did not fail the community.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  claimed that he had no plans to use nuclear weapons in Ukraine, despite previous threats to do so.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : If he has no intention, why does he keep talking about it?\par \par \uc2 MAN: The January 6 rioter who attacked a DC police officer during the insurrection has been sentenced to seven and a half years in Federal Prison.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Terrifying moments on board a plane when the nose of the aircraft cracked.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Brady can`t find anybody open and down he goes again.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Ravens take this game 27 to 22 and Tom Brady drops two games below 500 for the first time.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Brady three-game losing streak, first in two decades, sets the all- time Shaq record tonight.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Overnight, Rihanna released her first new solo music in six years.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): And on top of that, her song is going to be part of the new "Black Panther" movie. Are you kidding me? It`s going to be so great. I mean, it`s going to be great for us. It won`t be great for the Wakandans because it`s going to be hard to concentrate on fighting the bad guys when a Rihanna song starts playing.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s time to defend our whole -- oh, Shuri, are you hearing this right now? Are you getting this?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am so going to miss Trevor Noah. I`m so glad he`s going to be here a little bit longer, but he`s perfectly right about that song. It is just released. You can hear a little bit of it. It`s so good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How about that movie? You`ve seen it?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is. I can`t wait for the world to see this movie. I cannot wait.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS this morning.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, as in Mason, has another very fascinating profile. Who is he sitting down with? Danny Elfman. You might not know his name, but you sure know his stuff. He`s one of the most popular composers in TV and film.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beetlejuice.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You recognize his theme song it`s from "Batman."\par \par \uc2 He`s also known for "The Nightmare Before Christmas," "The Simpsons," and many other hits.\par \par \uc2 He tells Anthony the secret behind his iconic music.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talk about a strong resume.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "From Scratch.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And actor and writer Tembi Locke is here, along with her sister, Attica to talk about a very personal new project. It`s a \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series based on her book about her real life love story with her late husband.\par \par \uc2 We loves stories about love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is going to be a great conversation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We really do, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We love stories about love.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, we are suckers for love over here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look forward to that, but we are going to begin with one of the nation`s most politically influential social media platforms now under new ownership that could have big implications for our democracy.\par \par \uc2 According to multiple reports, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is now officially in charge of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  after a multibillion dollar deal that he actually tried to back out of at one point.\par \par \uc2 There are also reports that he has already fired the CEO and other top executives. Changes come in there.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, musk tweeted: "The bird is freed."\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti has made his way to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  headquarters in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, yesterday Mr. Musk walked in with a sink, Apparently executives are walking out with little boxes of their stuff. Change is coming.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 And the question this morning, how will Musk balance out freedom of speech, while at the same time discouraging and reining in misinformation. The stakes are incredibly high for millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 I want you to keep in mind, US prosecutors determined in 2016, a Russian agency used \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and other social media sites in an attempt to sway the election. Even \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s founder said misinformation about false election claims led to the attack on the Capitol last year.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ANDERSON (TED Talk Head): You made an offer to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Billionaire entrepreneur, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has been talking and tweeting about it since April.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (\uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO): Well, I think it`s very important for there to be an inclusive arena for free speech.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: After roaring onto the roads with \uc2 Tesla\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK: Well, welcome.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And soaring into the galaxy with \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , Musk has now apparently taken over the Twitterverse in a deal valued at $44 billion.\par \par \uc2 BRENT PHIL (Jefferies technology analyst): I think he`s clear that it`s going to -- he is going to take it in a different direction, which.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Brent Phil is a technology analyst at Jefferies. He says the immediate firings of top executives as reported by \uc2 The Associated Press\uc2  would be part of those changes.\par \par \uc2 BRENT PHIL: He was clear that he didn`t align with the Board or the senior management team and that he believed the company was poorly run.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Added to that, Musk has been vocal about the role he wants to see the social media giant play in global communications. He tweeted a message to advertisers Thursday saying in part, "The reason I acquired \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is because it is important to the future of civilization to have a common digital Town Square," voicing concern that "Traditional media has fueled and catered to polarize extremes. And in doing so the opportunity for dialogue is lost."\par \par \uc2 He added, "\uc2 Twitter\uc2  obviously cannot become a free for all hellscape where anything could be said with no consequences." But Phil wonders if Musk`s business model where even controversial voices are welcome, will work.\par \par \uc2 BRENT PHIL: In the short term, the only way the financial health continues is advertising, and I can tell you, every advertiser I speak to is running from this platform, not flocking to the platform.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And when we talk about the threat of misinformation, there will be a critical test in the weeks to come when millions of American voters head to the polls for the midterms -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, people will be watching out for misinformation and the like, but to the earlier point, you reported that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says he doesn`t want a "free for all hellscape," his words where anything can be said no consequences on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think the question on a lot of people`s minds is what does that mean for folks who have been banned or suspended people like, I don`t know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Kanye West?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Yes, a great question, Tony.\par \par \uc2 At this point, we can only speculate on what Musk himself has said publicly. Back in May, he said he would reverse a ban on Trump, his quote say, "morally, wrong flat out stupid to have such a ban."\par \par \uc2 At this hour, we can say right now, Trump is still suspended. Of course, all of that could change and rapidly. We`ve seen a lot of rapid decisions in just a few hours once \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was taken over by him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s the Chief Twit now, as he puts it.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Some people say "Just get your popcorn and let`s go."\par \par \uc2 President Biden and other Democrats are applauding a new report showing the economy grew more than expected over the summer. Now, they hope that this is a sign that we are not going into a recession, in spite of those rising prices and interest rates, which is a key issue for the voters.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House was more on that story.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Here at the White House, they made a big deal about those GDP numbers. They argued it is another sign that the economy is continuing to power forward. But the question is whether many Americans really feel that improvement in the face of rising prices.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Great economic report today.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: President Biden hailed the GDP rebound, Thursday, a healthy 2.6 percent annual spike after two straight quarters of decline.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Although it may not feel like for everyone, people`s incomes went up last quarter, more than inflation went up.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But economists aren`t applauding yet. They say high inflation and a decline in consumer spending are warning signs of what is to come.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GAPEN (\uc2 Bank of America\uc2  Head of US Economics): We do think a recession in 2023 is more likely than not. It could start as early as the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Federal Reserve has tried to drive down prices by hiking interest rates, but groceries are still 13 percent more expensive than last year, and the Fed`s moves have pushed mortgage rates to a 20-year high, over seven percent for the average 30-year fixed rate.\par \par \uc2 With 11 days to go before the midterm elections, Republicans are hammering the president on the economy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Every single thing they touch turns to garbage. Inflation out of control.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In Syracuse, President Biden argued the GOP has no plan for the economy.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : My Republican friends in Congress seem to be hoping for a recession, many of them.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  was overheard Thursday warning President Biden about a critical battle in the Georgia Senate race.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): The State where we`re going downhill is Georgia. It`s hard to believe that they will go for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But our vote, our early turnout in Georgia is huge, huge.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: During that tarmac conversation, Leader Schumer also told President Biden that it appears that the debate in Pennsylvania didn`t hurt the party that much. He was referring to the race between Democrat, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican, Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and the Vice President are going to be traveling to the Keystone State today to hold a dinner supporting \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  tonight -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is a lot hanging in the balance with Midterm Elections approaching. Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A man who dragged a police officer into a mob of January 6 rioters has been sentenced to seven and a half years in prison. Albuquerque Head pleaded guilty to pushing through barricades at the Capitol after listening to then President Trump`s speech.\par \par \uc2 During the riot, Head wrapped his arm around officer Michael Fanone`s neck and dragged him shouting, "I got one." The crowd responded yelling, "Kill him."\par \par \uc2 Fanone testified that he was injured and traumatized by the attack. The 90- month sentence is the second longest for a January 6 rioter so far. The Judge said Head is responsible for "some of the darkest acts committed on one of our nation`s darkest days."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was certainly that.\par \par \uc2 There was outrage at a hearing in Uvalde, Texas about last May`s school shooting. Parents and relatives of victims are demanding that the head of the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  resign for what happened that day.\par \par \uc2 But yesterday, he defended his agency`s response to the attack that killed 19 children and two teachers. Janet Shamlian has that story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BRETT CROSS (Legal guardian of Uziyah Garcia): If you`re a man of your word, you`ll resign. We`re not waiting any longer.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Angry families of Uvalde victims demanding the resignation of Texas DPS Director, \uc2 Steve McCraw\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 McCraw once vowed to step down at his department had any culpability in the botched response.\par \par \uc2 One Trooper, Juan Maldonado was fired last week for his inaction and others are under investigation. Maldonado can be seen in this bodycam video obtained by a San Antonio news station.\par \par \uc2 JUAN MALDONADO (Fired Trooper): Wow. This is so sad, dude. He shot kids, bro.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Other unidentified officers can be heard contemplating their own safety before entering the school with the gunman inside/\par \par \uc2 OFFICER #1: Want to jump the [bleep] gate or what?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER #2: What`s the safest way to do this? I`m not trying to get clapped.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In his first public comments since June, McCraw on Thursday, was defiant.\par \par \uc2 STEVE MCCRAW\uc2  (\uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 ): DPS is an institution, okay. Right now, it did not fail the community. Plain and simple.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: He refused to resign or provide details on DPS` internal review of the Uvalde response.\par \par \uc2 JESSE RIZO (Uncle of Jackie Cazares): Our town is divided.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Jesse Rizo, the uncle of Jackie Cazares, blame McCraw for what he called continued misinformation.\par \par \uc2 JESSE RIZO: Your officers either lied to you or you painted a picture that was favorable to your department, sir.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: McCraw blamed local police in the days after the massacre. It was later revealed, of the 376 law enforcement officers who responded, 91 were DPS Troopers.\par \par \uc2 Brett Cross was the legal guardian of 10-year-old Uziyah Garcia.\par \par \uc2 BRETT CROSS: My son is dead. Eighteen of his [bleep] classmates are dead, two teachers are dead. There are many more wounded and everything, but he is going to make this -- they`re all going to do this politically.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: McCraw said the DPS investigation will be complete by the end of the year, but the parents I talked to are not hopeful that they`ll have any more information at that time. It comes as the Governor just announced $15 million for the construction of a new elementary school in Uvalde. That`s because Robb Elementary will be demolished -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY D