r \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, the Secretary of the Army responds to our reporting on the military childcare crisis. How she says her own experience as a working mom are driving her effort to find a solution.\par \par \uc2 But first it is 7:15, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, Ben Tracy shows the effect of severe drought along one of America`s most important waterways.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: There is a crisis on the Mississippi River, water levels hitting historic lows due to a lack of rain. We will show you what is being done to try to keep commerce moving for one of the most critical corridors in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re looking at Seaside Heights, New Jersey, one of the coastal towns that was heavily damaged by Superstorm Sandy.\par \par \uc2 The powerful storm that made landfall 10 years ago tomorrow had a lasting impact on the region around New York City.\par \par \uc2 In a new documentary "Sandy," CBS News, New York looks at some of the hardest hit communities and how they have changed in the past decade.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TEXT: Breezy Point, Queens.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: One of the things that I treasure the most was when my husband died in the hospital, I had his clothes and I don`t have anything of him now. I lost everything from him.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: What are you hoping to find?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Something I could take with me.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I think I found something.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I saw the plate. It was the only thing that I could see that was still there. I can see everything else was in rubbish.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The storm is not the disaster. The disaster is after the storm.\par \par \uc2 TEXT: Long Beach, Long Island.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And the scammers. They would come up to you, walk up to your house and say, "Let me help you. Let me help you." And you`d have fork over, you know your checkbook, hand it to the person and you never see the person again.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The person that did my house walked away with about $200,000.00 and I`m one of thousands and nothing happens to them. Absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 TEXT: Staten Island.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And this is where my house was right here. This is where I walked in, this is where I came out. Over there was my driveway. This is where I almost lost my life, right here where I`m standing. It was some night.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can watch "Sandy" on the New York section of our CBS News app on CBS News New York`s Pluto TV channel or by scanning the QR code on your screen right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s amazing that it has been 10 years. I know -- to me, it doesn`t seem like 10 years, I`ll bet to them, it does. But it is interesting how you can see something that looks like garbage, but you see how she took that little piece of plate and held it to her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because to her, it represents what she used to have.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I remember Breezy Point. I remember Lower Manhattan plunged into darkness. The Jersey Shore, the big Ferris Wheel plunged in the ocean.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is still damage along the Jersey Shore, at least and some of the scummiest scum on the planet, I`ll tell you what, those scammers were taking money that should be going to people who were recovering.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s going to be a great piece.\par \par \uc2 Okay, coming up, ahead, composer, Danny Elfman has created some of the most memorable scores in TV and film. I`m talking about "The Simpsons" theme, which he wrote in his car.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Who doesn`t recognize that? Who doesn`t?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody knows.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN (Composer): I actually was hearing it in my head while I was watching it, and as soon as I got in the car, it clarified and I heard all the parts.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You also sang on it, briefly.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: That was in hindsight, the smartest thing I ever did.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Because?\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Because my health insurance is still now from singing "The Simpsons."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is so good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Living in my head, Nate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anthony Mason talks to him about his other favorite projects, it is going to be a great conversation. He is working with Tim Burton.\par \par \uc2 Local news, though coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We have an update on our CBS News investigation into a childcare crisis in the Armed Services that could affect the military`s readiness.\par \par \uc2 Now, our reporting revealed some Army Special Forces soldiers in Florida are driving up to three hours round trip every day to take their children to childcare.\par \par \uc2 Now, in an interview that you`ll see only on CBS Mornings, the Secretary of the Army says, help is on the way. She spoke with senior investigative correspondent, Catherine Herridge.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, can`t wait to hear what she has to say.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 For more than a decade, the Seventh Special Forces Group, that`s the Army`s elite Green Berets have struggled to get access to affordable and really childcare that`s near where they work, but that has changed recently, now that the Secretaries of the Air Force and the Army are directly involved.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Why is childcare so important to the Army?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE WORMUTH (United States Secretary of the Army): Well, it`s really a readiness issue.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: And now, Christine Wormuth, the first woman to serve as Secretary of the Army is taking it on.\par \par \uc2 As a working mother, is childcare personal?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE WORMUTH: It`s absolutely personal. I mean, I have personally had that experience of racing to drop my kids off at the daycare center and get into work on time, or racing to get out to pick them up and sort of being the last parent to pick them up and getting the stink guy from the workers.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Three hours in the car every day just to get to childcare.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA (Former Special Forces Soldier): Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Chelsea, a former Special Forces soldier who asked us not to use her last name for security reasons show me part of the drive that drove her out of the Army.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA: It just wasn`t a sustainable solution.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: She and her husband still in Special Forces juggled up to three hours of commuting each day for childcare, driving from their home in Crestview, Florida, past their training base at Camp Bull Simon`s 20 miles away to where the Army offered childcare spots, another 20 miles to the south on Eglin Air Force Base.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA: It`s unaffordable for military families to live closer to Eglin.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Was it impactful to hear from soldiers like Chelsea who had to quit the service?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE WORMUTH: Absolutely. I mean, you know, I never want to lose a great soldier and family because they can`t get childcare.\par \par \uc2 Working closely with the Air Force --\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Earlier this month, Wormuth announced plans for a new Childcare Center on the Army Special Forces base.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE WORMUTH: We need to have our parents have childcare that`s not too far away. So, having it be on Camp Bull Simons, I think really is the optimal solution.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The Army plan goes beyond the Florida base with greater stipends for daycare, and at least 20 new childcare facilities in the next decade.\par \par \uc2 But with over 11,000 kids in military families waiting for childcare, The Pentagon still faces the same staffing shortages as civilian daycares.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE WORMUTH: Part of what we`re doing is trying to make sure we`re recruiting the best staff. We`re in a war for talent, frankly, so we`re trying to raise our pay.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: And with the war in Ukraine and China flexing its military power, Wormuth says childcare is central to the mission.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE WORMUTH: We don`t want a situation where someone is distracted from, you know, the main thing which is doing their job, because they`re worried about their kids or we don`t want to lose a good soldier because they weren`t able to make the childcare work.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA: Good job.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: CBS News has confirmed that the Army and the Air Force recently signed a formal Memo of Agreement that provides for some short term fixes, but the plan is to build a new child development center on Camp Bull Simons -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, you know, if the home front is not working, it is hard to do the job. It really is a readiness issue, as the Secretary said.\par \par \uc2 Kudos to you and your team for bringing -- shedding light on this issue and then to the army for hearing it and saying, "Yes, we`re going to fix it."\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s going to change things.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, up next, we`ll show you how an extended drought is draining the once Mighty Mississippi and what it could mean for the prices you pay in stores.\par \par \uc2 Also, a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast for the day`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A water crisis on the country`s most famous river threatens a crucial shipping corridor and could drive prices even higher for consumers.\par \par \uc2 Water in parts of the Mississippi River is sinking to record low levels because of a months` long drought in the Midwest that is causing bottlenecks for barges that move vital cargoes like agricultural products.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent, Ben Tracy shows us what is being done to keep the river flowing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: This used to be an island in the middle of the Mississippi River, reachable only by boat. Now, you can walk to what`s known as Tower Rock.\par \par \uc2 Months of below normal rainfall have sent the river to record low levels, creating chaos for barge traffic.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODY (Represents river shipping and towing industry): We haven`t seen these levels for about three decades.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Paul Rody (sp?) represents the river shipping and towing industry. Barges are being slowed and stranded at the worst possible time just as farmers are sending their harvest downriver.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODY: Sixty percent of our Ag exports travel right down here the Mississippi River down in New Orleans and out for export, so if we don`t have barges available that`s a real problem, not just for America`s farmers, but for the world frankly.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: It is also a critical supply chain for everything from coal and petroleum, to fertilizer, and road salt.\par \par \uc2 How important is this river to moving goods around this country?\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODY: The Mississippi River is a vital transportation artery. It`s absolutely a water superhighway. Annually, we will move 500 million tons, give or take.\par \par \uc2 If you wanted to put that on trucks, you could circle the Earth 13 times with trucks bumper to bumper.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Billions of dollars in economic losses and higher prices for consumers are expected as the drought drags on.\par \par \uc2 What`s your biggest concern?\par \par \uc2 LOU DELL`ORCO (St Louis District Corps of Engineers): No clouds, no rain.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And you need more than a little rain.\par \par \uc2 LOU DELL`ORCO: A lot more.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Lou Dell`Orco is Chief of Operations for the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  in St. Louis. The city is now the gateway to growing problems downriver.\par \par \uc2 The Army Corps has been dredging nonstop for three months desperately trying to keep a nine foot deep shipping channel open by sucking up enough silt and sand to fill an Olympic sized swimming pool every hour.\par \par \uc2 If this drought continues, can you dredge your way out of this?\par \par \uc2 LOU DELL`ORCO: We can dredge it to a certain point and then Mother Nature wins.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: In Louisiana, salt water from the Gulf of Mexico is creeping up the river, threatening the drinking water supply.\par \par \uc2 Baton Rouge has a new tourist attraction, a sunken ferry boat from the early 1900s resurfaced as the water receded.\par \par \uc2 This punishing drought comes as climate change is making rainfall patterns all over the country more extreme.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT CRISS (Washington University Professor): It is drought or flood.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Washington University Professor Robert Criss has studied the Mississippi`s flow for decades.\par \par \uc2 Was your sense that this is not going to be as reliable as we thought it once was?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT CRISS: It clearly is not reliable. When the water is too high, they can`t run the barges; when the water is too low, they can`t run the barges.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And yet the barges have to keep running on a not so Mighty Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RODY: Unfortunately, there`s no silver bullet. This is irreplaceable. We`ve got to keep commerce moving.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ben Tracy in St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you will be talking about today, but first, it is 7:42. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is Albert.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got a Rihanna Return. Now, we`ve got a Vlad Return.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, we just learned that Vlad`s middle name is Albert.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Albert.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Albert.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Albert.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vladimir Albert Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Vladimir Albert Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, Vlad. But Albert is -- Big Al is what I got called in college because I know -- I know. Look at my driver`s license.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, hey, hey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was waiting. I was waiting. I was waiting. All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you want to talk about Rihanna?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s talk about Rihanna.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, here we go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s focus on Rihanna. And all of her fans, including all of us --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Have been waiting for this moment. She dropped her new single "Lift Me Up" ove