 \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Positive news on the economy, an issue front and center in the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Every single thing they touch turns to garbage. Inflation, out of control.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Families of Uvalde victims demand the resignation of the top cop in Texas over the botched response to the school shoot.\par \par \uc2 BRETT CROSS: If you`re a man of your word, you`ll resign. We are not waiting any longer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Secretary of the Army speaks with CBS News about our investigation showing a childcare crisis for many military families.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE WORMUTH: I never want to lose a great soldier and family because they can`t get childcare.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Trevor Noah asks an important question where did \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  get that sink in the video he shared of himself at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  headquarters?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Is this the one he ripped out of the wall when the Judge told him he had to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ? Aaargh.\par \par \uc2 If Jeff Bezos walked into \uc2 Amazon\uc2  with a sink, his employees would be like, "Oh wow, we`re finally getting a restroom." He`s like, "No, no. Go away."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have to say, Tony called this yesterday saying, how did he get the sink? And I go, what difference does it make where he got the sink?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Have you found some clarity, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have no clarity at all. It takes a lot of planning to get a sink, not just sitting around. You`ve got to go to a hardware store.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But now that it is official, it has finally sank in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is sinking in. Yes, put that in a nice glass box in the lobby. It`s not part of the story of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, you`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 All right. Hallo-weekend is here and we have just the story to get you in the spooky spirit.\par \par \uc2 Oscar nominated Emmy and Grammy winning film composer, Danny Elfman has scored some classic creepy films in his long career.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beetlejuice.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It sure is showtime.\par \par \uc2 Elfman may be best known for his longtime creative partnership with Tim Burton on films like "Beetlejuice," "The Corpse Bride," "Alice in Wonderland," and "Batman." Classic, classic, classic, classic.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason visited him in his California recording compound. Anthony, what`s up man?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: A lot of classics, right?\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Danny Elfman is performing his first ever career spanning headlining show tonight and tomorrow at the Hollywood Bowl, and what a career it is.\par \par \uc2 The prolific composer has scored some of the most iconic movies of all time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: When the score of "The Nightmare Before Christmas" was played live at Bank of California Stadium last year, Danny Elfman walked out to thunderous applause.\par \par \uc2 Elfman wrote the music for Tim Burton`s animated classic.\par \par \uc2 Is this one of the puppets used in the film?\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: This is the puppet used in the film. Well, there are several.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He has an original Jack Skellington puppet in his studio.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: All his joints move, his fingers move.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Elfman himself was the voice of the skeleton in his songs.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Danny Elfman performance.)\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Tim would come over and he would bring these beautiful sketches of the characters and he`d tell me the story, a little bit of time, and as he told me the story, "I`d go. Okay, that`s a song."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Nightmare Before Christmas.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is there a secret to writing a score?\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: You lose yourself in the film. I mean, this is something that every film composer has in common. We`re wired in a way that if you play scenes in front of us, we`ll hear --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Music.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Music.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Edward Scissorhands.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Elfman scored more than 100 films from "Edward Scissorhands," to --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from (Spider-Man.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: To "Spider-Man."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Men in Black.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: "Men in Black."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: To "Mission Impossible."\par \par \uc2 You basically have to hear the sounds inside the director`s head.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: You have to figure out how do you express yourself for their vision, and that`s hard. That`s a trick.\par \par \uc2 So sometimes, you know, you equally have to be a musician and a psychologist.\par \par \uc2 This is headquarters.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Mission Control.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: When the industry went silent during the pandemic, the 69- year-old composer started making music on his own, surrounded by antique dolls, heads under glass, and his biggest obsession, hands.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: I dreamt my whole life of hands, you know, coming after me, and what can I say?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It led to an album he called "Big Mess."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Big Mess.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Was this basically the first music you`d written for yourself in decades?\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Oh, yes, definitely.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Since he`d fronted a band called Oingo Boingo back in the 80s, and 90s.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Coachella.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Then at Coachella this past April.\par \par \uc2 He performed a career spanning concert.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: A crazy show where I mix up old stuff, new stuff, film stuff, wrap it all together in this ridiculous way.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I know you were nervous about that performance.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: I was beyond nervous, but I decided I`ve got to lock into this and I took my shirt off, which was how I performed in the 90s and it brought me back a quarter century and feeling like sweaty and exposed and raw, which is how I loved feeling on stage.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Your whole film career was an accident, really.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Everything in my life has been an accident. I`d never intended to get into film scoring. Tim Burton and Paul Rubens approached me to do "Pee-Wee`s Big Adventure."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You tried to say no to that?\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: I did.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Pee-Wee`s Big Adventure.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It was 1985 and Burton, a fan of Oingo Boingo persisted.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: And I wrote this piece of music for him. I put it on a cassette and sent it to him and I said, "I`ll never hear from him again." My manager called me a week later and he goes, "Danny, you got the gig."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Pee-Wee`s Big Adventure.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It would be the first of 16 films he scored for the director.\par \par \uc2 Why were you and Tim so compatible artistically?\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: We both grew up on horror films.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: And when I met him, he idolized Vincent Price and idolized Peter Lorre. That kind of defined to us for the next 35 years.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Alta Vista Production movie.)\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Vincent Price was often the tormentor of Peter Lorre.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Alta Vista Production movie.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In 1989, Elfman wrote two of his best known scores. The first was for a new TV show.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Simpsons.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You wrote this driving home in a car.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: I wrote it in the car on the way home. I actually was hearing it in my head while I was watching it, and as soon as I got in the car, it clarified and I heard all the parts.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You also sang on it briefly.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: That was, in hindsight, the smartest thing I ever did.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Because?\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Because my health insurance is still now from singing "The Simpsons."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You said that you think that "The Simpsons" was probably the easiest thing you wrote, and that "Batman" was the hardest.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Yes. Well, it was the hardest score I`d ever worked on.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Batman.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: They didn`t want you.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: They didn`t want me. And the producer didn`t want me, only Tim wanted me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So, Tim Burton had him audition for producer, Jon Peters.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Tim says, "Play the march. Play the march." The march means the titles.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Batman.")\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: And Jon Peter starts conducting and then he stands up and Tim looks at me, he goes --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How do you typically approach this? You watch the film multiple times? So what do you have to do?\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Yes, I watch it a lot and you know I live in it. Also, sometimes you`re working on a film and you`re committed and you like the director, but it`s not a very good film.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: You still have to give it the same amount of energy, even if you know that your music is not going to save the movie.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Does that make it harder at that point?\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What do you do?\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: You just do the best you can. It`s called being professional.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: It`s like, it`s the same for all of us in the film.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What a fascinating process.\par \par \uc2 DANNY ELFMAN: It is a fascinating process.\par \par \uc2 When a really good movie happens, it is always a little bit of a miracle. You know, it`s like nobody sets out to make a bad movie.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right? He has made so many. He`s been part of so many great ones. Two new projects. He`s got "White Noise" with Noah Baumbach. Also, "Wednesday Adams" with Tim Burton, yet again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And he`s actually going to be doing -- he is going to be doing "Nightmare Before Christmas" in London and Phoebe Bridgers is playing the part of Sally when he does that again.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He calls it a trick and it really is amazing. So, he watches something with no sound at all. No music.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, can you imagine that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then puts it in. I can`t imagine it. Once he puts it in, you can`t imagine it any other way.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s a different kind of dude, Anthony, but he is clearly a genius.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: A genius.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is fascinating to see how he does.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He says there`s one kind of movie he doesn`t like to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is the romantic comedy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Romantic comedy.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Because he said -- I said -- he said, he made an exception with "Silver Linings Playbook" because it was just weird enough for him. I said, "Why won`t you do them?" He said, "Because I`m hopeless. I just -- it just doesn`t come to me."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. Interesting. I see a scene and that`s all I see is a scene and he sees -- he hears music.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Can you imagine?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fascinating. Yes, that was great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was well done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love it. Thank you, Anthony Mason.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, why people who are looking to switch careers may be in luck. We`ve got some good news for you. We will tell you about the top skills that employers are searching for right now.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:11. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "From Scratch.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Something great it was. That`s a scene from the new \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series, it is called "From Scratch," inspired by a real-life romance.\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk with the two sisters who created it about what it was like to turn your own experiences into a series about food, family, and the best part, love.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We always thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our next guest can help you find a new job if you`re looking to change careers.\par \par \uc2 LinkedIn has released a list of the top 20 skills that employers are looking for when making new hires. Those skills include an ability with social media, an ability to manage time, leadership, problem solving. Also, financial skills.\par \par \uc2 LinkedIn career expert, Catherine Fisher joins us now with more on this.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (LinkedIn career expert): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, people are looking less at where you went to school and instead asking: Can you do the job? And that means soft skills?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes. So when we looked at some of the top skills in a lot of the job listings on LinkedIn, those soft skills are really coming through. So like you said, problem solving, communication, leadership.\par \par \uc2 Companies want to know that you can do -- like they assume that you can do the job and they can test you with this in interviews, like with writing skills, et cetera. But how are you with those problem solving, especially as companies are really trying to figure out this new way of working.\par \par \uc2 You know, a lot of companies we are seeing -- are we doing flexible work? Are we not? As we`re approaching these headwinds with the economy, can you help us grow and scale the business? So those skills are really important.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So how did it used to be, Catherine. Companies would look at what and now they`re looking at what?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: You know, they used to look at your degree, where you went to school, but now they`re looking at what skills you`ve acquired.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whether you have a degree or not.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: It depends on the role.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re saying that doesn`t matter?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: But what it really depends is those skills that you`ve acquired through your career and so now people used to look at their job as I`m going to get this job and then I`m going to go to the next, and it was the ladder, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Now, it is about a collection of skills and what is good about this is that really crosses any industry or job and you can acquire those skills in so many different ways.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you say it`s important to sell yourself.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: It is and so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Toot your own horn?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Absolutely, you should always be selling yourself and whether you`re looking for a job or getting -- you know, even if you`re happy in your job, you should be always looking at what are the most in- demand skills?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So how do you do that? How do you -- for example, you can say --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a good question, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I`m very good at -- you know, working with people or --\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where am I supposed to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because there`s a fine line between confidence and cockiness.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I come from a sports background, and they say we`d rather you be cocky and go out there and believe in yourself as long as you can back it up.\par \par \uc2 How do you feel like people should talk about their own skills?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: You have to be able to story-tell. You need to have concrete examples of where you`ve applied this skill.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: So for example, I was actually just talking to a woman I know who her first job in PR, she got it because she had bartending on her resume and her hiring manager and she thought maybe I shouldn`t include that on my resume and the hiring manager said no, because you have those great skills.\par \par \uc2 But then also, as you go in to sell it, you need to be able to show, hey, you know, so I had this problem. Let`s say you were working for a food bank.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: And they were having issues with the food going bad overnight, when people would make donations. You did a whole campaign, you got a refrigerator, and then you were able to increase the donations and fewer -- less food went bad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: So, it`s really showing those specific examples of how it sells.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Skills, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There are fewer hires being made across the economy, but there`s one sector in particular that`s adding jobs. What is that?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, well think of that as we`re heading into the holiday season. It`s shipping, its logistics, its supply. So it`s when you`re purchasing an item, how you get it from the shipping container to the warehouse, you wrap it up, you ship it, you take it to your home.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All of those jobs.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Oh, those jobs. Those are --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: From beginning to end.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They need employees.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: And it`s great for seasonal work.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. Very cool. Maybe Tom Brady would make a LinkedIn profile. Looking for a job. He`s got soft skills.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, he does.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He does.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He got lots of skills.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I bet he`s gone soft with age.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Catherine. It is really good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, we`re going to talk with two sisters who created the new \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series. It`s called "From Scratch." It`s a real life story about love lost that inspired this show. How did they do that? What`s it about? We`ll tell you when we come back.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In case you missed it on this week`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, we showed you items that might help you save time and money in the kitchen and more.\par \par \uc2 The Lotus trolley bags are reusable shopping bags. The set of four includes one cooler bag for frozen and refrigerated foods. They are designed to be used hundreds of times. You can get them for 40 percent off the retail price with our exclusive offer.\par \par \uc2 To buy the Lotus bags and the other items that we have for you that might become essentials in your everyday life, scan the QR code on your screen right now or head over to CBSDeals.com to start shopping now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, coming up in just a moment, people who hate the idea of Halloween on Monday. Why they say it`s bad for just about, well, everyone.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got to agree with that one.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming out you next.\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk about it. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel you on that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Time now to bring some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 Who is up first?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m up first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is in pole position.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I have something for you. Everyday superhero story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My "Talk of the Table" is an amazing story about how one man`s wrong turn saved the lives of four people in Southwestern Iowa.\par \par \uc2 So let me set this up, Brendan Birch (sp?) says he turned into a street by accident and then he saw a house on fire. How crazy is that? He got out of his car and then he started banging on the windows trying to wake up everybody inside. And then this happened.\par \par \uc2 A Ring doorbell actually caught the video. It shows three children running out of the door in through the burning porch to safety.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looks like something out of a movie. Seconds later. They`re 22-year-old brother is the last one to make it out. So how about that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But just turning on the wrong street he saw and did something.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And did something.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. That`s incredible.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Most people would have --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Most people that you didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think there`s a couple of options, they keep driving or they call 9-1-1.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would call 9-1-1.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think most people would call.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They would call, yes, first.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They wouldn`t get --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re not going to try to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You wouldn`t get out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. I would not get out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that was wise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much, Brendan. You saved some lives there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is all about Halloween this year. The holiday falls on a Monday, which has some people upset because they say it`s tough on children with school the next day and adults who have to go to work. I think that`s fair.\par \par \uc2 So there is an online petition. It already has close to 3,000 signatures to change Halloween to the last Saturday in October. I`m here for that.\par \par \uc2 The argument is that Thanksgiving is always on a Thursday. Mother`s Day and Father`s Day are always on a Sunday. So, why can`t we have Halloween on the last Saturday in October?\par \par \uc2 You know when you`re a kid you don`t care when Halloween is. You just --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Monday, Tuesday Wednesday -- but when you get older and especially when you`re a parent, you think why can`t it be on a Saturday? So I like this. I hope this picks up.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s something about marketing research that says how do people celebrate Halloween this year? Well, here you go. Here are some numbers: 67 percent of those asked say they will hand out candy, 51 percent will decorate their home or yard, 47 percent plan to dress up, and 44 percent will carve a pumpkin, 28 percent will throw or attend a party, Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And 20 percent will dress up their pet.\par \par \uc2 Now, when favorite daughter, Kirby and favorite son, Will --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Will dress up just up their pet or dress up as their pet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dress up their pet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I probably said it wrong.\par \par \uc2 When favorite daughter, Kirby and favorite son, Will were younger, I was all into the thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When they left the house, my lights were dark on Halloween. Please, don`t ring my doorbell.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle. Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, ain`t that bad?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s terrible.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Terrible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am like, Nope. Nope. Nope.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re over there peeking out the blinds saying, you better not knock on this door. Don`t. Get, kids. Get.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I know you don`t do that. I know your house is all decorated. I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But what is Baby Luca going to be for Halloween?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, good question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have a couple of options, but I`m not allowed to share at this particular time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was given very strict instructions, actually.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t want to give out --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very hard to be muzzled, Tony. It is very hard.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Especially about your grandbaby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is very hard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Eloise is going to be Animal from "The Muppets" because she is wild. She`s a year and a half. We were going to make her a Ghostbuster because Teddy is going to be a ghost.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they can be like a pair and then she just threw one of these tantrums in the kitchen. We were like, "That`s Animal from `The Muppets.`"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is wild.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is wild.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is going to be the Animal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is fun. I do think Halloween is what -- what are you doing for your -- are we allowed to say?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are we allowed to say you`re having a Halloween party?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or is that a secret?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am having a Halloween party. If you know me, pull up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you know me. If you know me is the key.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If you don`t know me, don`t pull up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We disregard this message. Okay.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is about lotto, lotto fever, the Powerball jackpot. It`s worth an estimated $800 million now, and there happens to be a new book out about lotto that caught my attention. It`s called "For a Dollar and a Dream: State Lotteries in Modern America."\par \par \uc2 And get this: These two data points really hit me. Americans spend more on lottery tickets every year than they spend on cigarettes, than they spend on coffee, than they spend on smartphones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I believe that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s surprising.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also, also, more money than video streaming, concerts, books, and movie tickets combined. Smoosh it all together. Think about that. $91 billion.\par \par \uc2 Here`s data point number two, though, which gets me thinking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I know what you`re going to say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, the rich people don`t buy lottery tickets anywhere near the rate that poor people buy lottery tickets, okay.\par \par \uc2 Financial experts at Bankrate say that players making more than 50 G`s a year spend about one percent of their income on the lottery.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People making less than 30 spend 13 percent, that`s thousands of dollars. So, the question is should -- and this strikes me -- should our State governments, which are supposed to be promoting the general welfare, be selling the thing where your poorest residents are buying the most, and guess what? They`re not winning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They are selling hope though, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The two always -- why do you always -- I got the hiccups.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I agree with you tough.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You always have a chance, don`t you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You always have a chance, but you have less of a chance of making the middle class to spend 13 percent of your income on lottery tickets.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But to Tony`s point, if you redistribute that money elsewhere instead of a lottery ticket you might be able to build some type of wealth for your family.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m just saying it always struck me as weird that the government that gives you like, you know, it can give you low rate healthcare, can help you with a job, can help you with housing.\par \par \uc2 It is also being like, oh, by the way, you want to gamble a little bit on the side?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is weird.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, Debbie Downer. The jackpot --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why is that Debbie Downer?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Keeping it real.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: $800 million. People are going to buy something.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, you still didn`t say what you`re going to be for Halloween, but that`s okay.\par \par \uc2 Sisters behind the -- I know you all noticed that -- the sisters behind the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series "From Scratch," they are in our progressive greenroom. We`re going to talk with Attica, there she is on the left and Tembi is on the right in the magenta. about bringing Tembi`s real life love story to the screen. It`s good, too.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:35, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are getting an inside look at a new \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series based on a real life love story. You know we love that around here.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "From Scratch.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "From Scratch" is inspired by the true story of an actor and writer. Her name is Tembi Locke and her late husband, a Sicilian chef named Saro. They met and fell in love -- I love this song. I love this song.\par \par \uc2 They met and fell in love -- Bocelli -- while Tembi was studying abroad in Italy and it is based on her memoir published by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , which by now you know is like \uc2 CBS\uc2  is part of \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So in the memoir, Tembi writes about their big and beautiful love, adopting a daughter, and the obstacles that they faced including cancer. We are sad to say that Saro died back in 2012.\par \par \uc2 Now Tembi and her sister, that`s Attica, are bringing their love story to the screen on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , and while the names of the characters have been changed, the challenges they face and their love is real. For real, for real. Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "From Scratch.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, Tembi and Attica join us in the studio. It`s good to see you both. I`m telling you, people need to have a box of tissues when they`re watching this.\par \par \uc2 But listen, you`re 20 years old, Tembi. You`re in Italy. You`re studying -- you`re studying abroad and you literally bump into Saro we will call him in the movie.\par \par \uc2 You literally just bump in -- Lino is what he is called in the movie. You literally just bump into him in the street. Did that really happen that way? I found that so hard to believe.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE ("From Scratch" Author, co-creator): I know, so did I and I lived it.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I bumped into him on the street. I was walking with a friend and we rounded a corner and we -- you know, just the way you would, a stranger, you just brush shoulders. You bump into each other.\par \par \uc2 She -- my friend happened to know him, and so they began a conversation. I was standing there. I didn`t speak Italian really? And so I just thought, "He`s cute." Little did I know that meeting would change the course of my life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It really did. He is a chef.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you all go through -- it seemed to be difficulty on both families that had trouble accepting this relationship. True?\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 My in-laws were Sicilian farmers from a very small mountain rural town who the idea of their son marrying an American first of all was beyond anything they could conceive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: I wasn`t -- I`m not Catholic, right? African American --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And your dad`s like, "He does what? He`s a chef? Is that a job?"\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: Yes. Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A lot of obstacles it sounds like.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: A lot of obstacles. And we show in the series the way parents sometimes in their well-intended, you know, sort of love for a child sometimes can get in the way of what really was love.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What the heart wants.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: Yes, what the heart wants.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Attica, why did you think that Tembi should share this story with the world?\par \par \uc2 ATTICA LOCKE (Author and executive producer "From Scratch"): I had a front row seat to one of the most interesting stories I`ve ever seen. The quality of love that they have for each other, the way in which they chose to be fully present in life, even through life`s biggest challenges, even with cancer, they were so fully alive.\par \par \uc2 And I thought this story about these East Texans and these rural Sicilians becoming over decades, a family beyond the language and borders and culture was too riveting to not write down.\par \par \uc2 I just really wanted her to write a book. And I wanted to read the book because I wanted to read the book.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Attica, let me just get a fact check on one thing in particular here, so she writes the book and you wanted to read it, and then you pitched it to Reese Witherspoon`s production company without clearing with her first.\par \par \uc2 ATTICA LOCKE: Yes. Without her.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s true?\par \par \uc2 ATTICA LOCKE: I did. It is absolutely true. I got in their offices one day, and they were talking about interesting books and I was like, "Well, you know, I know an interesting book," and I just pitched my sister`s entire book, her life story. And for a second, they were like "Your sister? Wait a minute. I don`t know about our list." But they said yes.\par \par \uc2 They were curious and they said yes, and Reese read it, and everyone in the office has read it. And they said within like a week we were sitting there trying to figure out how do we make this into a show? And I called her from the valet --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How did she respond when you called her?\par \par \uc2 ATTICA LOCKE: I called her from the valet and I was like, "Don`t be mad. I may have pitched your entire book."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never a good start when somebody says, "Don`t be mad."\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: And I knew, I was like, "What has happened?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Some of the scenes were so personal. I kept wondering, did that really happen? Like you guys had a fight? You and Lino had a fight and it is pouring rain and you`re up in your apartment and he comes, he is walking in the rain and you come running down and you run to the rain and you hug each other and you jump in his arms.\par \par \uc2 Did that really happen?\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: He stood in the rain --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you were wrong, by the way in the fight.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle is like, you was out of pocket, girl.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: I was out of pocket. Listen, I had a lot of growing to do. He inducted me into a love that was bigger than what I could see at the moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did that really happen though?\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: So yes, he stood and waited in the rain for me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: He waited in the rain for me and I didn`t run out and hugged in the street, I was like "Get out of the rain. Come upstairs."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t want to get my hair wet.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: I don`t want to get my hair wet. But yes, that moment happened and it was one of the moments that I write about in the book and it was a life-changing moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is diagnosed with cancer and you actually end up -- you`re together for 10 years. You were a caretaker. I am glad to say that you have found love again and you are remarried.\par \par \uc2 And I was wondering, we were talking before you came because your husband - - current husband says, "I think this is good. You`re telling the story because it gives me a template of your heart."\par \par \uc2 Nate and Tony were like, wow, no, how I would feel about --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, no, I was being honest, and I thought you know, does your ex love still own a little bit of real estate of your heart and your mind?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And might that be hard for your current partner?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And how does your current husband feel about that? Just an honest answer from a couple of men that are curious. Married men by way.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: No. Absolutely. And to re-partner with someone --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 TEMBI LOCKE: No, it`s a beautiful question -- and to re-partner with someone after loss means to own all of who they are and that includes their past. And my love for my late husband does not diminish because I love my husband now, and the heart can hold both.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. The heart gets bigger.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, girls. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Attica and Tembi Locke, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Beautiful story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "From Scratch" is available now on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You had not one, but two loves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was beautifully handled.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You guys are awesome. Great answers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was a great answer.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so Gayle, I heard your Halloween costume is Simon Spoon from "Bridgerton."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, it`s Sexy Red Riding Hood.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, Sexy Red Riding Hood.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, who is the Big Bad Wolf then?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. Let`s take a look back at all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 See you Monday.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Russia is claiming that Ukraine could detonate a radioactive device on its own territory.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ukraine says only Russia will consider launching such an attack.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: If called upon, these fighters say, they are ready to strike.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rishi Sunak is Britain`s new Prime Minister.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is the first person of color ever to lead the government.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: But you know, this famous black door at Number 10 Downing Street has proven that it is a revolving door as well.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE JORDAN (Actor): Hello fellow hunker downers.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This was not the way we planned to tell Leslie Jordan`s story.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE JORDAN: I couldn`t bear that. Just live quietly. So here I am.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: A warm, lovely human being.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was walking joy and spreading joy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What are you hoping to do next?\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH GARZA (TikTok influencer): My goal is ultimately to become one of the greatest philanthropists of my generation.\par \par \uc2 You guys are getting a home.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You all should have seen Gayle in the piece, she said, "David, what happened? How does he get paid?"\par \par \uc2 I said, it is in the tag.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This one over here said, I don`t want to go to a movie with you. You talk through the whole movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Am I right? Or am I wrong?\par \par \uc2 I think we`re missing somebody right now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Beep, beep, beep.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at you, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Thank you very much. This is how I shop every day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s how you shop?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I actually don`t fold any clothes. I don`t think it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You just crumple it up and throw them in --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I just crumple it up and I take it from the dryer and I throw it in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So now you know that Tony wears wrinkled underwear.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel very strongly about this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do with that what you will, America.\par \par \uc2 BILLY PORTER (Singer): They were like, well, what do you think about the people who say all your -- you`re only doing this for attention? I was like, "That`s my job."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the point.\par \par \uc2 BILLY PORTER: I will not be shamed for being best at my job.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s literally what the red carpet is.\par \par \uc2 BILLY PORTER: That`s what it is for.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS (Actor): I want my kids to grow up, have safe, healthy relationships with their partners and I want them to enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Have fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel you on that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is called pleasure.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: I hate to tell you, but I had sex before I was married and I am like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You tramp.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What to do in the event of a zombie apocalypse.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, look at how he is saying this with a straight face. Zombie apocalypse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is saying it. If zombies took over the world, where would you go, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where would you go? Where would you hide?\par \par \uc2 Oprah`s house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good. I feel very safe --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony all thinking the same thing.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, so your favorite scene is the spoon thing?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I like that spoon.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hide the spoons.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re not the only one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am not going to say nothing else about it. You want it good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That does it for us. Put that spoon away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Nate got joke.\par \par \uc2 Tony is here because Nate said we need some vanilla in this picture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ERICA LILES (Hiker): We were the only group that had someone walking beside us carrying our backpack.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How many porters did you have?\par \par \uc2 ERICA LIKES: Seventy two.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow. We need a bigger studio, including porters.\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE (Hiker): But we made it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, were they carrying the bags or carrying you all.\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK (Hiker): Both. Sometimes both.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate goes, "What`s the point?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of hiking? Climbing mountains?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the point of hiking?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, what happened when you went hiking?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Pull it up later in the show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t say I was good at it. I didn`t say I was good at it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221028eias00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hiring For The Holidays\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Nancy Chen \par \uc2 1,030 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`re fast approaching the season of giving and that means a season of hiring for retail stores. More than half a million Americans typically look for extra work at this time of year. Someone who knows all about putting in work, Nancy Chen, is here to tell us all about it.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Hey, good morning to you, Nate. Love that introduction.\par \par \uc2 The labor market may be cooling slightly, but it is still a great one for people looking to boost their holiday budgets, especially with those rising costs we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 Inflation makes this holiday buying and hiring season an unusual one, but there are plenty of opportunities to be found.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: It`s already the most wonderful time of year -- that is, to find work for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 JASON MABEL (Splendid East Coast operations head): We`re finding that people want some extra money especially during the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: After struggling to fill shifts the past two years, Jason Mabel who heads East Coast operations for clothing retailers, Splendid, kicked off holiday hiring earlier than ever -- in September.\par \par \uc2 JASON MABEL: This is a time where maybe a teacher might have off during a few weeks during the holiday season and they want to come in and pick up some shifts.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And with holiday costs expected to increase this year by six percent, people are looking for ways to keep up.\par \par \uc2 Given inflation and a looming possible recession, are there more people who are coming off the sidelines to seek those seasonal jobs?\par \par \uc2 JULIA POLLAK\uc2  (\uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2  chief economist): It`s definitely a driver. People are looking for cash and so those bonuses and higher starting pay points are very, very attractive to them.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Julia Pollack is the chief economist for \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 , and says even as the labor market begins to cool, it is still sizzling. Companies are coming out with big incentives, including higher wages.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  just announced sign-on bonuses going up to $3,000.00 with potential opportunities for full time jobs.\par \par \uc2 This is a good time to be a job seeker.\par \par \uc2 JULIA POLLAK\uc2 : This is the best job seeker`s market of all times. Employers are still having to make very attractive offers and job seekers is still in the driver`s seat.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But as consumers jumpstart holiday shopping earlier to stretch their budgets and plan to spend less than usual, major retailers are divided on how they are hiring.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and Target are aiming for roughly the same holiday hires as 2021, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , the nation`s largest retailer, announced it is bringing on only about a quarter of last year`s figure.\par \par \uc2 Macy`s is also pulling back taking on about half as many seasonal workers as last year.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE DE VITA (\uc2 Urban Edge Properties\uc2  EVP of Development): You can even see around us right now, all the people were seeing higher foot traffic, people are getting out there earlier and they`re also looking for jobs.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Danielle De Vita oversees about 80 shopping centers nationwide. We met her in Paramus, New Jersey, which seems the most retail sales in the country, but predicting hiring targets can still be difficult.\par \par \uc2 You over hire, and then that cuts into your profit margin; you under hire, and you don`t have enough people and people walk away. How difficult is it to get that fine line just right.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE DE VITA: This year, it scales up based on the fact that the expectation is there may be more people in store. So, that is the relative uptick.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of people out there who are looking and returning to the workforce, and that is something that we`re working to facilitate.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The hiring blitz last year meant some businesses started this season with a higher headcount than usual, since a lot of temp workers ended up staying on.\par \par \uc2 Still industry analysts say if you`re looking for a seasonal job, now is the time since it`ll look a lot different even a month from now. I mean, a lot of these hiring sprees are happening as we speak.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and if you`re looking for that job, get ready because the holiday rush is for real.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We always wonder if Americans will spend money during the holidays and every single year we seem to spend even more.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s got to be hard to just try to figure out what`s the right thing to do. Nobody really -- are people going to go back to the stores?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: They are. They are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know. Are they?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: A lot of people are spending the holidays with their families in person this year, so that also changes the way that people buy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, no, I`m already noticing a difference. I like the shopping in person, not just --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, new rules for hearing aids that should lower their costs for millions of Americans. Dr. David Agus will tell us what you need to know if you or a loved one has hearing loss.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast to get the day`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Love this song.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221021eiai000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Iranian Athlete Missing after Hijab Protest; Russia`s Ruthless Offensive; Bone-Chilling Cold; Raging Washington Fires; Candidates Face-Off in Ohio Senate Race; \uc2 Trump Hotels\uc2  Controversy; \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  Contempt Sentencing; Hiring for the Holidays; New Access to Hearing Aids; Inside the World of Harry Potter; Oprah`s Book Club Selection; John Grisham Live; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Elizabeth Palmer, Holly  Williams, Jordan Steele, Robert Costa, Scott MacFarlane, Nancy Chen, David  Agus, Vladimir Duthiers, Anthony Mason \par \uc2 13,838 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight more deadly strikes in Ukraine as police there try to shoot down Russian drones before they cause any damage.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET) (Coordinator for Strategic Communications at the National Security Council): It`s an effort to terrorize the Ukrainian people, to make them fearful, to push them to capitulate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tempers flare at a high-stakes debate for the Senate in Ohio where there is an extremely close race.\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R) (Ohio Senatorial candidate): When you insult families, you strike a nerve with normal people.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): I didn`t talk about you. I would never talk about your family, J.D. I wasn`t raised that way.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The DOJ is recommending a six-month jail sentence for \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  for refusing to testify before the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Properties owned by former President Trump vastly overcharged Secret Service agents according to a new report,\par \par \uc2 REP. GERRY CONNOLLY (D-VA): He is nickeling and diming the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In Florida, a lucky Mega Millions winner in the wake of Hurricane Ian, one of two winning tickets was sold in hard-hit Fort Myers.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Heart-stopping video of a bear attack in Japan is now going viral.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Chargers hosted the Broncos, tied at 16 and overtime.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s going to kick -- is good. And the Chargers win it in overtime.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Broncos country. Let`s ride.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): The superstar K-pop band, BTS, has announced that its members will soon begin reporting for mandatory service in the South Korean military.\par \par \uc2 Yes, which is great news for South Korea, because their army is basically unstoppable now. There`s no army in the world that is going to take a shot at these guys. Are you kidding me? Who`s going to be stupid enough to take a shot? Everyone`s a fan. Enemy soldiers are going to be like, "I just got stabbed by Jin. This is the last best day of my life."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trevor Noah nailed it again. He ain`t wrong about that because they are beloved.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are beloved.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are beloved there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wait a minute, is that BTS over there?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I will be curious what their job is going to be. Are they going to be part of a music group or are they going to be dropped in the DMZ. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A music group like keeping the morale up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s going to be interesting.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday. Guess what? Lucky you, we`ve got your fall reading covered on today`s show. Why? Because only on CBS MORNINGS, Oprah is going to reveal her next Book Club Pick. We can`t tell you what it is yet, but Oprah says it`s good and we`ll talk to the author, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. Plus, you may recognize Tom Felton as Draco Malfoy from eight, yes, eight "Harry Potter" films. His new book, "Beyond the Wand " -- love that title -- details his journey and his struggles off screen. He opens up to Anthony Mason all about it.\par \par \uc2 And we have another great day of CBS MORNINGS deals. You can get a head start on those \uc2 CBS\uc2  deals at CBSdeals.com.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I saw them in the lobby getting ready, looks pretty good.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin though with a growing international concern -- big concern over the fate of an Iranian athlete who is missing after defying her government. She is a rock climber. Her name is Elnaz Rekabi. She has not been heard from since competing without hijab at a competition in Seoul, South Korea.\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes at a time of historic unrest in Iran after the death of a woman in the custody of the so-called Morality Police.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer has more on this story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: All eyes were on Elnaz Rekabi earlier this week as she scaled the climbing wall in Seoul, South Korea without her headscarf. But once the Asian climbing championships ended, friends say she vanished.\par \par \uc2 Her decision to climb without her scarves broke the Islamic Republic`s rules for women in sport, but colleagues say it was deliberate, and especially pointed in the context of thousands of Iranian women who have been protesting for the past month against compulsory hijab.\par \par \uc2 The protests follow the death in police custody of 24-year-old Mahsa Amini for her allegedly un-Islamic dress.\par \par \uc2 Human rights group say run-ins with the Security Forces have left more than 200 protesters dead. Once video of Rekabi bareheaded went viral, \uc2 CBS\uc2  sources say she was invited to the Iranian Embassy in Seoul on a ruse, and once there, stripped of her passport and phone and put on a plane for Tehran.\par \par \uc2 The Iranian embassy in Seoul tweeted a picture of her today, head covered and said she had left Korea this morning, but reports that she`d gone missing were false news.\par \par \uc2 Friends and contacts say she hasn`t been seen in Iran yet though, and they fear she may have been taken directly from the airport to prison.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: The international Iranian opposition has been working hard to publicize Rekabi`s apparent disappearance, hoping that the uproar will keep her out of jail and that she may instead be forced to, for example, record a TV confession, saying that she had forgotten her hijab. But at the moment, there is no solid news on her whereabouts -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will keep waiting and hoping for the best.\par \par \uc2 Liz, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to Ukraine where yet another round of suicide drone attacks has brought terror to the people of Kyiv and other cities in Ukraine. It is the latest Russian escalation of a war, which has not been going well on the battlefield for the man who started it. That would be of course, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has our story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: There were Russian strikes across the country this morning and they seem to be targeting the energy and water supply, including a power plant in Kyiv ahead of Ukraine`s harsh winter.\par \par \uc2 It follows yesterday`s attack on Ukraine`s capital by a swarm of so-called suicide drones, kamikaze weapons packed with deadly explosives that detonate on impact.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine says four people were killed in Kyiv. One of them was Victoria Zamchenko (sp?), who was reportedly six months pregnant with her first child. Kyiv`s Mayor, \uc2 Vitali Klitschko\uc2  called Russia a terrorist state.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR VITALI KLITSCHKO (Kyiv, Ukraine): A lot of people, civilians, unguilty people, and that`s why these are terrorist attacks.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Ukraine claims that Russia has ordered nearly two and a half thousand suicide drones from Iran. They reportedly cost around $20,000.00, a fraction of the price tag for a guided missile. Iran denies that it is arming Russia in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "The world must stop this terror," said Ukraine`s President last night. For months, his country has been asking the West for better air defense systems.\par \par \uc2 In Russia last night, just over the border from Ukraine, a fighter jet plowed into a residential building. The fiery aftermath killed 13 people according to state media, Russian authorities say they are investigating.\par \par \uc2 The only good news is a prisoner swap. The two sides exchanged over 200 detainees yesterday, including 108 Ukrainian women, mostly soldiers, who are now free.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Many of Ukraine`s cities including Kyiv had been relatively calm for months, but Russia`s use of suicide drones, small and cheap, could have a significant impact on the course of this war -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Holly Williams for us in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s already feeling like winter in much of the country. You`d never know it`s October from this video taken in Syracuse, Indiana, where a snowy mix knocked out power. In fact, 235 million Americans are seeing the low average temperatures this week.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in meteorologist, Jordan Steele from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jordan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN STEELE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Good morning, Nate. Yes, there are so many winter-like headlines we could run with this.\par \par \uc2 The cold, the snow -- we`re going to start off with the cold. Actually, we`re going to start with the snow because the snowfall totals have been very impressive. I mean, we`re talking about a half a foot to a foot even a foot and a half in some of our bullseyed areas, all of this really taking place up in the Great Lakes, but we even saw some down in Central Indiana and the cold air really starting to sag south.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re talking ten, twenty degrees below average for the eastern half of the country and some of that cold air going all the way down into South Central Georgia where we could be looking at record cold max temperatures only in the 50s there.\par \par \uc2 So, you can imagine, tonight`s going to be even a colder night. We can be looking at temperatures dropping in the 20s in Kansas, we will be down to the upper 30s and low 40s for Dallas, you`ll be teasing some records, and even down into Florida, we could be looking at record cold maxes as well with temperatures in the 60s and 70s. Seems comfortable until you look at the rest of the country, right?\par \par \uc2 Now, you guys stay tuned to \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Nate, you`ve got to stay bundled up for the next few days. We`ve seen some viewer send in some video of the snow and they`re just like "Ugh, already?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN STEELE: So is it a sign of what Mother Nature is going to bring this winter? We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It might be time to bring out those winter coats. Jordan Steele, appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not ready for the cold just yet.\par \par \uc2 Now to a major wildfire that`s burning in Washington State.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Clark County, Washington.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pictures don`t lie. That`s the Nakia Creek Fire. It has been burning outside Vancouver, Washington for more than a week, forcing nearly 3,000 people from their homes.\par \par \uc2 Officials believe this fire may have been started by human activity and they have released this very grainy video taken the day the fire started showing a white or light-colored \uc2 Subaru\uc2  that was in the area.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Turning now to Ohio in a hot-tempered debate last night in a tight \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race between Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and Republican J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 According to an average of recent polls, this is how close the campaign is. Vance has a small edge, which is three weeks left until Election Day.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got our Robert Costa in Ohio, where he has been talking to voters who are concerned about the economy.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Ohio has long been a crucial battleground and this year is no exception. But the Senate race is much, much closer than strategists expected. The big question is whether Congressman Tim Ryan`s working class message will be enough.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE: This just shows how desperate this guy is for political power.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): I think I struck a nerve with this guy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It was a showdown last night in one of the nation`s closest Senate races with tense exchanges on abortion.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): This is not a guy who is ready to protect the rights of women.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE: My basic view here is that we need to protect life in this country.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And on immigration.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE: You cannot pretend to be a defender of border security when you voted against border wall funding multiple times.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): This guy has invested into dozens of companies that use foreign workers.\par \par \uc2 And it has been Democrats and it has been Republicans and --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Throughout the race, Ryan has cast himself as an independent voice keeping President Biden who lost Ohio by eight percentage points in 2020 at bay.\par \par \uc2 Do you want President Biden to come to Ohio?\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): No, I don`t. You know, I want to be the face of the campaign. I want to be the guy who gives the message, delivers the speeches.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It`s an uphill climb, but Ryan insists he can run his own race, galvanizing voters who are alarmed about the future of abortion rights and winning over working class voters who went for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people in Ohio are feeling economic pain. What`s your message to them in this closing stretch?\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): I get it. I see it. You know, I understand what you`re going through. Like I think workers need a tax cut immediately. The only way to help people early on -- right now in the short term is put some more money in their pocket.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: CBS News` latest polling shows that nationally, views of the economy have gotten worse amid continuing inflation and voters are feeling it personally. More now say their own financial situation is bad.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And I`m just tired of the direction the country is going, and it is time for a change. Things were going so great under the previous President. I don`t understand how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Trump`s endorsement might be enough for the author of "Hillbilly Elegy" says County GOP Chair, \uc2 Jim Renacci\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JIM RENACCI (County GOP Chairman): In Ohio, the Trump endorsement will definitely help and it`s going to be a big key. J.D. has just got to put that message out there that he is not a career politician. He is somebody who is from the outside and that`s what Ohioans want to see.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But Democrats say some Trump voters remain in play.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Trump supporters in the past, just were looking for new leadership that didn`t quite work out very well for the country at all.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Vance, who was unavailable for an interview will rally later this week with Senators Tim Scott and Tom Cotton, two conservative Trump allies, underscoring how Vance wants that Trump voter to make sure they come out in November -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, the clock is ticking. Thank you very much, Bob.\par \par \uc2 The Trump Organization\uc2  is rejecting a new claim of profiteering during former President Trump`s time in the office. The House Oversight Committee says Trump`s owned hotels charged excessive rates, sometimes more than $1,000.00 a night for a room for Secret Service personnel protecting the Trump family.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 So good morning to you, Scott. The question I have is, how much is excessive?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: According to the records newly released by Congress, Gayle, the Trump hotels and properties charged the Secret Service as much as five times the government rate for some accommodations.\par \par \uc2 Over the course of four years that amounted to more than $1.4 million in tax money that went to the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . In one case, the Secret Service was charged more than $1,100.00 for a room at the Trump International Hotel here in Washington for agents protecting Trump`s son Eric in March 2017. That`s well above the $242.00 allowed under the government rate.\par \par \uc2 Committee documents also reference agent visits to Trump`s Bedminster Golf Club in Mar-a-Lago while citing a statement from \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  in 2019 that the Secret Service effectively stays at Trump properties for free and saves the government money.\par \par \uc2 The Committee says there may also be other expenses it hasn`t seen yet. They`ve asked the Secret Service for a complete list of taxpayer funded expenses at Trump properties and the agency tells CBS News it will respond to the panel.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to CBS News, \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  stands by his claim that the arrangement was for at cost discounted and free stays and says the organizations would have made more money by selling services to full paying guests -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow. Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Government, prosecutors are asking a Judge to give \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  six months in prison for defying a subpoena from the House Select Committee investigating the January 6 attack. Bannon would be the first person to be locked up for contempt of Congress in more than half a century.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department also wants former President Trump`s one-time political strategist to pay a $200,000.00 fine. Bannon`s lawyers are asking for probation.\par \par \uc2 He was convicted of two misdemeanors in July refusing to sit for a deposition and refusing to provide documents. The Judge plans to sentence him on Friday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll be watching.\par \par \uc2 But not have that chance for up to $20,000.00 in student loan relief for people with Federal debt. Tens of millions of Americans may qualify. And here`s how it works. If you made less than $125,000.00 a year in 2020, or 2021, you almost certainly are eligible. The same is true for households making less than $250,000.00 in those same years. We`re talking about up to $10,000.00 in debt forgiveness. And if you`ve got a Pell Grant, you could see another $10,000.00 on top of that.\par \par \uc2 The application is available at studentaid.gov, that`s studentaid.gov. And the White House says the whole process should take less than five minutes.\par \par \uc2 According to President Biden over the weekend, a trial run has already handled more than eight million applications without issues. But one quick note from the administration. It`s a warning: This site, studentaid.gov is the only way to get this aid. Anything else like a phone call could be fraud, so watch out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because sometimes those phone calls are very believable.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very, very believable indeed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Be careful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right on that.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, on something we covered yesterday, new rules should lower the cost of hearing aids for millions of Americans. We`ll talk with Dr. David Agus about why this step is important to overall health.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:17, time to check the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, do you need an extra job for the holidays? Well, we`re here to tell you it`s not too early to look. We`ll show you why now is the best time for the opportunities -- in the month of October.\par \par \uc2 Plus NASCAR Bubba Wallace, he is apologizing after an encounter with another driver during Sunday`s race.\par \par \uc2 Oh, you`re watching -- let`s find out what that`s all about.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That looks great.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead today, for 10 years, actor, Tom Felton brought Harry Potter`s iconic nemesis, Draco Malfoy to life. He tells Anthony Mason about the emotional final days on set. Did I say the name wrong?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Draco.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The last day was hard, wasn`t it?\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON (Actor): Yes. It was a very bittersweet goodbye. I can`t -- I can`t drive past these studios without --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You can`t.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: No. I can barely talk about it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Really?\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: Yes. It was our second home.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my, Draco Malfoy. And I went to Harry Potter World in April.\par \par \uc2 Felton also tells Anthony about a "incredibly awkward" but chilling moment with Voldemort in the final Harry Potter film, plus a top of that, I love that music.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t we all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In this week`s CBS MORNINGS Deals, we will show you a device designed to help you find your keys if you lose him. Does that ever happen? It sure does.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will tell you all about it and how you can get it at a 40 percent off retail price discount. All of that and more, stay with us, in just a moment, local news coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`re fast approaching the season of giving and that means a season of hiring for retail stores. More than half a million Americans typically look for extra work at this time of year. Someone who knows all about putting in work, Nancy Chen, is here to tell us all about it.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Hey, good morning to you, Nate. Love that introduction.\par \par \uc2 The labor market may be cooling slightly, but it is still a great one for people looking to boost their holiday budgets, especially with those rising costs we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 Inflation makes this holiday buying and hiring season an unusual one, but there are plenty of opportunities to be found.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: It`s already the most wonderful time of year -- that is, to find work for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 JASON MABEL (Splendid East Coast operations head): We`re finding that people want some extra money especially during the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: After struggling to fill shifts the past two years, Jason Mabel who heads East Coast operations for clothing retailers, Splendid, kicked off holiday hiring earlier than ever -- in September.\par \par \uc2 JASON MABEL: This is a time where maybe a teacher might have off during a few weeks during the holiday season and they want to come in and pick up some shifts.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And with holiday costs expected to increase this year by six percent, people are looking for ways to keep up.\par \par \uc2 Given inflation and a looming possible recession, are there more people who are coming off the sidelines to seek those seasonal jobs?\par \par \uc2 JULIA POLLAK (\uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2  chief economist): It`s definitely a driver. People are looking for cash and so those bonuses and higher starting pay points are very, very attractive to them.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Julia Pollack is the chief economist for \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 , and says even as the labor market begins to cool, it is still sizzling. Companies are coming out with big incentives, including higher wages.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  just announced sign-on bonuses going up to $3,000.00 with potential opportunities for full time jobs.\par \par \uc2 This is a good time to be a job seeker.\par \par \uc2 JULIA POLLAK: This is the best job seeker`s market of all times. Employers are still having to make very attractive offers and job seekers is still in the driver`s seat.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But as consumers jumpstart holiday shopping earlier to stretch their budgets and plan to spend less than usual, major retailers are divided on how they are hiring.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and Target are aiming for roughly the same holiday hires as 2021, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , the nation`s largest retailer, announced it is bringing on only about a quarter of last year`s figure.\par \par \uc2 Macy`s is also pulling back taking on about half as many seasonal workers as last year.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE DE VITA (\uc2 Urban Edge Properties\uc2  EVP of Development): You can even see around us right now, all the people were seeing higher foot traffic, people are getting out there earlier and they`re also looking for jobs.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Danielle De Vita oversees about 80 shopping centers nationwide. We met her in Paramus, New Jersey, which seems the most retail sales in the country, but predicting hiring targets can still be difficult.\par \par \uc2 You over hire, and then that cuts into your profit margin; you under hire, and you don`t have enough people and people walk away. How difficult is it to get that fine line just right.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE DE VITA: This year, it scales up based on the fact that the expectation is there may be more people in store. So, that is the relative uptick.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of people out there who are looking and returning to the workforce, and that is something that we`re working to facilitate.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The hiring blitz last year meant some businesses started this season with a higher headcount than usual, since a lot of temp workers ended up staying on.\par \par \uc2 Still industry analysts say if you`re looking for a seasonal job, now is the time since it`ll look a lot different even a month from now. I mean, a lot of these hiring sprees are happening as we speak.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and if you`re looking for that job, get ready because the holiday rush is for real.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We always wonder if Americans will spend money during the holidays and every single year we seem to spend even more.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s got to be hard to just try to figure out what`s the right thing to do. Nobody really -- are people going to go back to the stores?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: They are. They are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know. Are they?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: A lot of people are spending the holidays with their families in person this year, so that also changes the way that people buy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, no, I`m already noticing a difference. I like the shopping in person, not just --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, new rules for hearing aids that should lower their costs for millions of Americans. Dr. David Agus will tell us what you need to know if you or a loved one has hearing loss.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast to get the day`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Love this song.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This morning, we are taking a closer look at a new policy that allows stores to sell hearing aids over the counter.\par \par \uc2 The government estimates almost 29 million Americans have hearing loss that could be helped by hearing aids, but only one in five actually used them. Why? The big issue is cost. They`re quite expensive, and over-the-counter hearing aids are much cheaper.\par \par \uc2 CBS News medical contributor, Dr. David Agus joins us now to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Agus, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Like you, this is a big, big deal.\par \par \uc2 So take us inside, where do you go to get it? And big question: Is it covered by insurance?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: So, most insurance companies don`t cover hearing aids and you know, it`s very expensive, thousands of dollars and you have to get an audiologist tests et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Well, now you can get the tests. You can do -- there are apps that you can download and look at your hearing and then go to right now many large stores carry these over-the-counter hearing aids and you get them and you can adjust them with your phone and make them personal for you.\par \par \uc2 As we just said, 80 percent of people with significant hearing loss are not getting these hearing aids. So obviously, something was wrong and this is hopefully, will fix it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, what`s the difference between getting it over the counter and the more -- when you say thousands of dollars, what does that mean exactly? And how much does it cost getting it over the counter?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: You`re always about the details, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 You know, right now over-the-counters cost anywhere from $200.00 to $1,000.00.\par \par \uc2 With the estimates from the government, you`ll save about $3,000.00 By getting these over the counter versus getting them the ones that go classically prescription, because the prescription also include audiologists, fitting, adjusting all of those functions had to be included, and so it was very expensive.\par \par \uc2 And what`s exciting is you`re getting new companies come into the space. \uc2 Sony\uc2  just announced that they`re having one, Boss is having them. So, you`re really going to get innovation in this space, and hopefully destigmatize it so that people that say, "I don`t want to do it, I will look weak, if I were a hearing aid," hopefully you`ll look cool if you were a hearing aid, and we can get it, because there are major medical issues if your hearing goes down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, and the quality is good, too. The quality is just as good, we should emphasize that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, Dr. Agus, why is it happening now? Why the innovation, and also why this push for the lower cost option over the counter?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Well, in 2017, the Act was passed, and then COVID got in the way. And so the FDA announced it over the summer, and now it has been enacted, which is great to lower costs for the consumer.\par \par \uc2 There was a lot of pushback from people saying, "Listen, I can`t afford hearing aids." And this is a way to lower the costs and hopefully spur innovation.\par \par \uc2 You know, people with hearing loss who don`t have hearing aids are significantly with more depression, isolation, and medical issues, and we can give them a much improved quality of life if we can improve their hearing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Agus, we know that hearing loss comes with age, but teens also struggle with hearing loss as well. How do you know that you might need to go get checked or go see a doctor?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: You know, I mean, nowadays teens are putting in their Air Pods and they`re listening to loud concerts and noises and you know, up to one of five teens have hearing issues. And so if you`re in a crowded place, and it is very difficult to understand conversations and others can, obviously get that test. And again nowadays, you can download an app, and so it has been democratized.\par \par \uc2 Everyone has the ability now to do a hearing test and see if they need it, and I think that`s very powerful to enable better social interactions and hopefully, long term and better quality of life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I didn`t know there was an app.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think we`re all good here. No one has started to scream to talk to us yet. So far, so good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So far so good.\par \par \uc2 Dr. David Agus, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you will be talking about today, but first it is 7:42, time to check your local weather. Hi everyone, happy Tues\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle, you were off yesterday. Vlad is back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I heard.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Lovely day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am so glad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are reunited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So glad you`re back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are reunited. It`s a good thing. It`s a wonderful thing.\par \par \uc2 But let`s start with some bad news this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If you are a seafood lover, because for the very first time Alaska has canceled the winter harvest of the snow crab saying billions of them have disappeared from the B