y grandfather.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: His grandfather had a cameo as a wizard in the first film, he was also Felton`s chaperone.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: So, he sort of developed this idea in the mirror with me one day, sort of always, always constantly looking down your nose at everything, slightly, and then the tiny hint of a flared nostril, and then a tiny hint of a smile.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Despite their on screen animosity, Felton was close with his castmates, especially Emma Watson.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: We`ve always had some strange love together, not in the romantic way that some people would like to think.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How would you describe it then?\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: Well, best friends.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: People that speak the same language, similar sense of humor. She`s got a wicked sense of humor.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The legendary older actors in the films, however, Felton often found intimidating.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ralph Fiennes who played the demonic Voldemort had an otherness Felton says that set him apart from everyone else on set.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: It is also when in full Volde mode, if you will, he is incredibly fluid and flowing and you never quite know where he`s going to go next, which is quite frightening, to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In the climactic scene of the last film, Draco had to walk towards Voldemort.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And then he hugged you.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: That`s when the hug happened. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You didn`t he was going to do that.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: No idea. In my head, I`m going, "What is he doing?" Yes, incredibly awkward, but also chilling.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: To Felton, Jason Isaacs, who played Draco`s father could be almost as frightening.\par \par \uc2 He could actually switch into character like that.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: He was smiling and lovely, and being Jason, and the moment they said the word "action," he just turned and gripped me for the first time as Lucius and I was terrified immediately.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You still call Jason Isaacs, "dad."\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: Occasionally, I do. In fact, I`m very excited. He is coming to see the play tonight.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is he? Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: Yes, he`s actually flown back from filming just this year. He`s a very proud father.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He posted their reunion on social media.\par \par \uc2 As he was wrapping up 17 weeks on stage, Felton reflected on his toughest farewell, the end of "Harry Potter."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 The last day was hard, wasn`t it?\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: Yes. It was a very bittersweet goodbye. I can`t -- I can`t drive past these studios without --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You can`t --\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: No. I can barely talk about it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Really?\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: Yes. It was our second home.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: After a decade of playing Draco, Felton moved to Los Angeles in 2012. He loved auditioning for new roles.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: Don`t get me wrong, you go into a panic spiral every time, but you prove to yourself that "I can do this."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rise of the Planet of the Apes.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He landed parts in "Rise of the Planet of the Apes."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "From the Rough.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And "From the Rough."\par \par \uc2 But to seduced by the LA lifestyle, he began drinking heavily.\par \par \uc2 "I was hiding from something," he writes in "Beyond the Wand." "Myself, perhaps."\par \par \uc2 Friends finally staged an intervention.\par \par \uc2 You were angry at that moment?\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: Yes, I was furious at the time. My brother said sometimes you need to have a breakdown before a breakthrough happens. So, something -- the wheels needed to come off somehow, a little bit.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Did you feel like the wheels have come off?\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: In that moment, definitely, because I couldn`t see really what they were talking about.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But his lawyers words would get through to him.\par \par \uc2 What did he say?\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: He said something along the lines of, Tom, I think this is the 12th intervention that I`ve been to and six of those are dead. Don`t be the seventh.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: That kind of hit me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Felton agreed to go to rehab. But then one night, he broke out.\par \par \uc2 You end up just walking down the beach.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: Yes, a lot clicked when I managed to get down to the water.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What clicked?\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: That I needed -- that I needed help, and I was willing to accept it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Tom Felton eventually returned to London, where he has been thriving. And "Harry Potter" is still a big part of his life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Harry Potter.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You`re amazed that this is actually bigger now than it was then.\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: It`s incredible. Most fans who say hello to me or shout "Draco" or whatever, they weren`t born when the first films were being made.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is it important for you to keep saying "thank you."\par \par \uc2 TOM FELTON: Yes. The Potter fan flame is not going anywhere anytime soon and I`m the first one to celebrate that and be very grateful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anthony, you do it again, just like the "Harry Potter" films, this sit down was an emotional roller coaster.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, well, it`s interesting. He is -- that part, you know, where he talks about, you know, the intervention at the end, it comes at the end of the book, and it kind of comes out of nowhere, I have to say. I certainly wasn`t expecting it, and I didn`t know that about him.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that was also -- you walk into Piccadilly Circus in London, and you hear like, all of a sudden, you know, everybody sees him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They go crazy.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And recognizes him, and we just got him inside the theater in time before he was like mobbed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And this is what he has to deal with. I didn`t realize this, but he actually auditioned for Harry, and for Ron Weasley on camera a couple of times he told me. Hardcore Potter fans probably know that, but that was news to me.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s obviously -- it is still a huge part of his life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you can see -- you can still see the emotion on his face. I love when, he said, "Jason`s coming to see the way the play."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The way that his face was so happy about that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is like a family member coming to watch.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. He was really touched because he came -- I think, he flew in literally just to see him, which was really sweet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can see that. And when he was talking about realizing that he was in trouble that he needed help, you know, it`s almost like it was hard for him to say it, but then he says that.\par \par