 Barbara Kingsolver.\par \par \uc2 I`m so glad that she`s here with you today to share more about how she crafted this beautiful, incredible story.\par \par \uc2 So get your copy today and join us online at Oprah`s Book Club to share your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 Happy reading, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Barbara Kingsolver.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Reading is a word there.\par \par \uc2 Barbara Kingsolver is the author of 11 best-selling books.\par \par \uc2 Barbara, look at them all lined up, it looks pretty, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER (Best-selling author): Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Eleven best-selling books and former President Bill Clinton awarded her The National Humanities Medal back in 2000 and we are very proud to say, very happy to welcome her here to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Barbara Kingsolver. It is really good to have you in the studio.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Hi. Oh, this is so exciting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have done it again. You have done it again. Bravo to you.\par \par \uc2 When I first heard about the book, this is how Oprah said it to me. She said, "I found a book that even you`re going to like." I don`t know if I should be insulted or flattered by that because I don`t always like all of them, but she was so right.\par \par \uc2 From the moment that -- from the very first sentence, you drew me in, but you said you use this book, you compare this books to Dickens to tell this story. Explain what you mean.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Well, this is -- I come from Southern Appalachia.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you know this area and these people very well.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: I know -- this is my home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: And I love it. And Appalachia also has its challenges, and one of them in recent decades is the opioid epidemic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: And the worst of it is that we have a generation of kids who`ve been orphaned by opioid use disorder. And you know, as sort of the attention of the world moves on, I really wanted to tell the story of these kids because it`s not over for them and they live among us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: I know, you know, we all know these kids, but it`s a dark story and it`s a hard story to tell.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: I wasn`t sure readers would want to go follow me into that dark place. And so I spent, actually a couple of years trying to get - - trying to find my way into that house. You know how to tell the story. As it happened --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because "Demon Copperhead" is a person.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should say that.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Yes, Demon --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: His real name is Damon.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: His nickname is Demon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: His nickname is Demon. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: And he is called Copperhead because of his red hair. That`s his nickname. Where I come from, everybody has nicknames, it is a thing.\par \par \uc2 So I was really frustrated, and just by chance, at the end of a UK book tour, I had a weekend free and I booked myself into this, just completely on a whim. This in that is in Broadstairs. It`s the former home of Charles Dickens, and it`s where he wrote "David Copperfield."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: And so my husband and I, you know, booked ourselves in and we had -- it was creaky old mansion on the ocean and we had it all to ourselves. And they said, "Sure, sure." You know, "Make use of the place. Sit wherever you want," including Dickens` study.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: And so I sat there, I mean, Steven went to bed, you know, and I sat at Dickens` desk looking out the window at the view that you know, the roiling ocean that was in his eyes, and I just started feeling his anger. Not at me, but sort of anger he channeled in his books because orphans, kids thrown away by society. That`s his whole wheelhouse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are inspired.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Well, it was really like, he charged me with this mission. He said, "You`ve got to do this."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It seems like everything that could happen to Demon does happen to him\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Well, what he told me, which was the key to get into the house, was you let the child tell the story. Nobody doubts the child. It`s just that`s the way you tug on the reader`s heart.\par \par \uc2 And that was "David Copperfield" starting with chapter one. "I am born," I said, "Okay, Charles, thank you." I sat at that desk and I started this book that night. And I decided --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, let me tell you something --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Well, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, thank you because you take us on quite a ride. I`m not going to tell you how it ends, but it is something.\par \par \uc2 Barbara, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 "Demon Copperhead" is on sale now wherever you like to buy books.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congratulations. Second time --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you some of the stories that we like to call "Talk of the Table" this morning. And guess what? Tony`s in pole position.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very good. So for my "Talk of the Table." I`ve got a new book. It`s called "Ejaculate Responsibly."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A brand new way to think about abortion. That is the title, it caught my attention. I think it probably caught yours.\par \par \uc2 The author is a self-described religious mother of six, Gabrielle Blair, there she is. And she says that we should not be focusing on women in this conversation, we should be focusing on men.\par \par \uc2 "Ninety-nine percent of abortions are the result of unwanted pregnancies and men cause all unwanted pregnancy," she writes. Don`t believe her? Well, the book is 28 sharp little arguments that make the case that men are the key ingredient here, the thing that men do, and she points out and she is right about this, it is voluntary, it`s easy to control, it`s cheap to control. It`s all preventable.\par \par \uc2 It`s not asking a lot. She concludes for men to avoid this. So.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually liked his point. I actually do. Listen, I think the title is very provocative.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It draws you in, but I think she`s making a very good point. What do you think, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I found this --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, she is talking about like protection.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . using contraceptives, condoms -- what -- or just like, there`s a method that some guys lean on, which is --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, she actually gets into all of that, but her point is that the key ingredient in all unwanted pregnancies, which are the main cause and driver of abortions, which no one wants, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whether you think it`s a problem to be solved or right to be fought for, no one is rooting for it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if you shift your focus, I just found the idea of shifting it off of women who have to get, you know, all kinds of -- take all kinds of invasive actions to prevent pregnancy and put it on men, it totally flipped the whole conversation on its head for me, and I did not realize the extent to which culturally, I had been thinking about it one particular way until she took it in this little book and just completely reversed it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What her name? What`s her name?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Her name is Gabrielle Blair.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go, Gabrielle Blair.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It makes sense though. It makes sense.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And there`s a lot of "methods" that guys try to use and not all of them are, you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It gets -- it`s just that word "ejaculate" on morning TV. You go, "Huh?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know. I know. But you know what? I will be handing this book out around Christmas time. You know, you can stuff this in your stocking for Santa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So people will say, "Thanks, Tony."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I prefer a tie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She does have a point. This is going to be a hot book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is going to be a hot book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I agree.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because you can talk -- whatever your position on the issue of abortion, you can talk about this, and you will prevent the issue from being one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes. There are certain guys that use certain methods. You`re not -- Tony, you can`t even pull out of the driveway, so you might as well use methods that works.\par \par \uc2 So my "Talk of the Table" is the \uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2 , and how they`re tipping off the season in the grand fashion, and they`re honoring the life and legacy of the legendary Bill Russell with various celebrations including new uniforms.\par \par \uc2 As you can see here, when we show you the actual uniform, it features 11 Gold diamond icons for the 11 championships Russell won with the Celtics. That`s the most \uc2 NBA\uc2  titles of any player. It also features Russell`s retired number six on the front of the jersey in the belt that they used to wear to buckle up the shorts back in the day.\par \par \uc2 Bill Russell died earlier this year in July. I actually had a chance to sit down with him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . in December with one of his last -- it might have been his last interview. But I`m just glad that they were able to honor the legend in the way that they did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Agree, agree.\par \par \uc2 My talk of the table is Kane Brown. I love me Kane Brown.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this guy. The country music star was recently named the Champion of Youth by the Boys and Girls Club. He is a big, big advocate of the organization and even dedicated one of his songs to raise money for them.\par \par \uc2 So in his speech, Brown spoke about the struggles that he faced as a child.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN (Singer): I went through child abuse, I went through racism. My family -- because I`m biracial -- my wife saw me and accepted me when I was first born. I lived in a car for my home in a little while. I just want to let you know that I am not going to get emotional.\par \par \uc2 Everything you go through in life makes you tougher, it makes you stronger from every career and training that you want to do. Like I said, I used to live in a car, and now look at me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Kane Brown. This was in Los Angeles last week. Other big names to win the champion of Youth Award include LeBron James, Jennifer Lopez, Colin Powell.\par \par \uc2 So tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, we`ve got a great conversation -- well, I think it`s great -- we had a great time with him, with Kane Brown.\par \par \uc2 We went to Nashville to see Kane, his wife, Katelyn, and their two adorable little girls at their Nashville home. It`s an exclusive interview, the first time that they`ve sat down together, we talked to them about recording their first ever duet. Yes, Mrs. Brown can sing.\par \par \uc2 We also played some basketball and I use that word loosely played some basketball. Who knew?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that was not an edit. Then we hopped inside Kane`s truck.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, I missed it. We`ve got to play that back or I`ll get it later.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ll get to see it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You keep going.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to put it in slow mode tomorrow -- pickup truck to sing one of his hit songs. That`s all coming up tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We showed up at his house, very strong, and they were very hospitable. Really nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Awesome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 All right, our next guest, best-selling author, John Grisham is giving readers an inside look at one of the most notorious mobs in America, it happens to be in the South. It`s a group of criminals known as the Dixie Mafia, and they ran rampant across the Gulf Coast and the South starting in the late 1950s. One official at the time, called them more brutal than the Sicilian Mafia.\par \par \uc2 Grisham`s latest fictional thriller "The Boys from Biloxi" is inspired by this real-life mob and it tells the story of two sons of immigrant families who grow up as friends, but ultimately find themselves on opposite sides of the law.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Grisham joins us now, opposite sides of this camera lens. Hello. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 This is a fantastic book and it`s a classic story of two boys who grow up best friends, but go in very different directions. And you know, a showdown is coming in a Courtroom at the end.\par \par \uc2 What made you personally want to write about this particular group of criminals from way back when?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GRISHAM ("The Boys from Biloxi" author): Well, the setting, Biloxi, has always fascinated me because it`s on the Gulf Coast and you know, a State that`s very conservative in all aspects. It was a wide open place for many decades and I grew up at the only estate outside of Memphis in North Mississippi and we were always sort of fascinated by the legend and lore of Biloxi in the Gulf Coast and the vice and the crime and the corruption and it`s just a very colorful setting for a novel.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One of the young boys, Keith, his father is a lawyer or a prosecutor and then he goes to Law School and the best friend there on the criminal side of things. You are a lawyer in Mississippi, were you aware at the time of this kind of Dixie Mafia?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GRISHAM: Yes. I mean, by the time I got to college and Law School and started meeting -- we call them kids from the coast -- the kids from the coast were always, a lot of them are friends of mine. But I knew them pretty well, and I`d been there. I`d seen some of Biloxi by the end, and I was very much aware of the legend and also the reality of the organized crime and the setting and the uncontrolled vice that was rampant on the coast, back in the 50s 60s and 70s, it kind of played out in the late 1980s.\par \par \uc2 And then when casino gambling was legalized in 1990 in Mississippi and the big casinos came in to the coast, that was pretty much the end of the controlled vice Biloxi.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to start this by saying, you know, I adore you, John Grisham. I`ve read all your books, big fan, but I was a little worried about how this one started. It said "A hundred years ago, Biloxi was a bustling resort and fishing community on the Gulf Coast." Then it goes on to talk about immigrants coming in. I yawned a little bit, then it went to chapter two. And it starts, "The rivalry began as a friendship between two boys with much in common."\par \par \uc2 The great thing that I always find about your books, John, is that you draw us in for the first line. I wasn`t drawn in with this little history lesson, the way you started it. Were you worried that your readers would feel that? I`m asking -- and I am not insulting you. I`m just asking, were you worried?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GRISHAM: I was very worried because, as you say, I usually work hard to hook the reader, if not my page one, certainly my page two. And it was very much a part of creating immediate suspense and getting the reader hooked in the story.\par \par \uc2 I was very much worried about this book, because the first chapter is all history. It`s all accurate. But you know, nobody wants to read about the seafood industry or the immigrants who built it. And so I was -- I got through it as fast as I could to get to some real action, but this is a different type of story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you got to some action, page 51, "Ginger: She was a heavyset woman of about 50 with a dress too tight cut too low in front. Her breasts were pushed up close to her chin and look somewhat uncomfortable." I know this lady, do you know her?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GRISHAM: Sure, yes. We all know this lady. I didn`t want to talk about sex this morning when I came on the show. I saw you on the set a while ago before my COVID test came back and I tested positive. So now, I`m off site, so I got kicked out of \uc2 CBS\uc2  this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 JOHN GRISHAM: Just in the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We were trying to protect your privacy John Grisham, but since you want to tell everybody, are you feeling okay?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GRISHAM: Yes, I am triple boosted. I`ve had COVID. I am tough -- I feel great. No symptoms and I tested positive for the first time in several months. So yes, I can`t be there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I hear you on that. Real quick, just like the characters in the book, you were a baseball kid growing up. So any thoughts on the World Series? What have you got?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GRISHAM: You know, it`s really weird when you have the \uc2 Padres\uc2  and \uc2 Phillies\uc2  playing for the National League title. I`m a Cardinal fan. So, that`s very unexpected. So yes, it`ll be fun, but it`s not what I wanted. And you know, if the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  make it, that`s another -- I`m not a Yankee fan.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Nate, your one line of the day, I`m going to use this in a novel. "The guy can`t pull out the driveway." That`s really quite the line.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 JOHN GRISHAM: I didn`t know you can talk about ejaculation this morning. But it is really good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We all have got that friend. We all have got that friend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: John, can I just say this book is a great, great, great story. I mean, the way you tell the tale, it talks about the death penalty. It talks about addiction. It`s so well done. Bravo to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What she is trying to say is, "stick with it."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt, John Grisham, we appreciate you, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is really, really good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: First few pages.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just like in baseball, you knocked it out of the park again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "The Boys from Biloxi" is on sale now.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next, \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. Lifestyle host, Gayle Bass is here to tell us all about them, including one, portable speakers with a battery life designed to last all day long.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, it is time for CBS MORNINGS Deals where we show you some items that might help you save some time and some money, all at exclusive low prices, and they just might become essential in your everyday life.\par \par \uc2 To start shopping now, scan that QR code on your screen. You can also go to CBSdeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we have the other Gayle in the building.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS (Lifestyle host): Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lifestyle host, Gayle Bass. She joins us, so, Gayle, we all hate that feeling when you realize --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man, I am just -- no, the other Gayle. Watch out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t find my keys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, I go through that all the time, and research shows that Americans spend 10 hours a year looking for those keys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: That research is based on me alone, okay, so not scientific. It`s like always looking for the keys, looking for everything. But I love the KeySmart Pro. I actually have one. It is a great device because you could store up to 14 keys on this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: It`s Bluetooth enabled so you can -- it has the Tile app, so if you lose your keys, you can use your phone app to track them down. Or if you lose your phone, it could call your phone even if it`s on silent, your phone will ring. So this is a great device.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even if it is on silent, your phone will ring.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Even if it is on silent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It doesn`t seem like the airplanes would let you on with this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: They will let you on with that because all that is, is just like a little bottle opener.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a little bottle opener.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, they will let you on with this?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can open a bottle with it on a plane, but can`t fly with it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Hey, I carried mine on the plane. They didn`t stop me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How much are we talking about here?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: We are talking $29.99. This is great, all of these products, because they`re all $30.00 or less today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is light, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`ll carry --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How many? Did you say 48 keys?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Up to 14 keys, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, 14 keys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Fourteen keys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know somebody carrying 14 keys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, this is -- like if you`ve got a lot of keys in your pocket.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: You`re sneaking up on the kids to see if they clean the room, they won`t hear you coming. That`s a great thing. That`s a great thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, this is something that allows you to charge a lot of devices all at once.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: This is the Power Pro and it is a five device charger and what`s great about it is it has two outlets. It has two USB ports and a USB C port. A lot of stuff is moving to -- transitioning to the USBC. What`s great about this is that you plug it into the wall, but it could charge your laptop, a couple of phones, even your speaker all at the same time and it`s pretty powerful, and it also has surge protection.\par \par \uc2 What is great is, at the holidays, you`re going to grandma`s house. There is not enough plugs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Girls weekend, same hotel room, not enough plugs. Like grandma`s table everybody could fit around it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was thinking in the hotel, I was in one recently and had to put stuff in the bathroom and then some by the bed because stuff is plugged into the hotel things. This is good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How much?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: It is great. This is 58 percent off. We have it for $24.99. A great gift. A great -- you know, it is really great for the person who has everything. They probably don`t have this or if you know somebody who has an \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 , give them that because that helps put -- it makes it convenient for anybody coming to your house as a guest.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You mentioned tunes. We`ve got tunes here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: I love this. Let me just play this for you. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, my favorite song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: This is --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, what? Keep it going. Let it get it out. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You want to hear some more? Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. There we go. Go ahead. Go ahead and tell us what it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: These are paired. This is great. You can have it as a single, but you could also pair them, this is the Raycon Everyday Speaker. What`s great is this was created by somebody who knows music, Ray J, Brandy`s brother. That family knows music.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Ray J created these?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, that`s his company, Raycon, and what`s great is that not only can you pair them. These are magnetic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is little and cute. I like this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, they are very lightweight. You can travel with them, you could take up to the park, you could them to the beach.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How much?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: These are $24.99.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, that`s a great price.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: You can stick them on the fridge. I was listening to Travis Tritt making some biscuits in the kitchen. There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at Ray J looking out for us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ray J looking out for us, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And of course, we partner with all of these companies to get these incredible deals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To buy anything you saw, scan the QR code on your screen right now or head over to CBSdeals.com to start shopping now for all of these items and more.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s see it. We`ve got Gail on the basketball court.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you play it again in slow mo, please?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Swish. That was nothing but net, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to say. Next time, Shanta, could we put it in slow mo?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, you can play basketball.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because that was my -- no, you know, I didn`t. That was my first time shooting with him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was beautiful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That looked good, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am not even joking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He will be there tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Splash.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me and Kane hanging out. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221018eiai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 America`s Exhausted Workers Flex Their Muscle\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Tonight, America`s freight and commuter trains are running as scheduled after an 11th hour tentative settlement with their unions. But with 11 million U.S. jobs open right now, American workers feel empowered.\par \par \uc2 From Los Angeles, here is Mark Strassmann.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: We are the tip of the spear.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Fifteen thousand nurses were sick of it.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Nurses.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Power.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: A three-day walkout in Minnesota, a protest over salaries and staffing.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Stand up for the working people of America. More\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Three, four.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We won`t take it anymore.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: More strikes this month, teachers in Seattle and nursing home workers in Pennsylvania. A nationwide railroad strike averted at the last minute would have thrown the economy off track.\par \par \uc2 Railroad unions forced concessions, other unions noticed.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR WHEATON (\uc2 Cornell University\uc2 , Director of Labor Studies): I think it empowers people to have that risk or the option of a strike. Yes, I think there will continue to be more potential work stoppages.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: American workers keep flexing their new leverage. Already this year, 15 major work stoppages involving at least 1,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  study tracks 271 strikes of all sizes that started this year, almost double last year`s number for the same period.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD cheering)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: More American workers are voting to unionize, from Amazon warehouse workers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , you can`t hide.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: To \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas, unions have won more than 600 elections in 2022, the most in nearly two decades.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR WHEATON: But it`s the threat of a union on organizing drive, which has changed the behavior of employers to not be quite as bad.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The state with more strikes this year than any other, here in California. Often the issue isn`t money, it is working conditions. The message from workers, "Treat me better or else."\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, CBS News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, Chicago`s outlaws, the once underground activist group still fighting for a woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 And breaking protocols, have Queen Elizabeth embraced Americans?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020220920ei9h0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Scott Pelley`s Interview With President Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Scott Pelley \par \uc2 791 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So for this Sunday season premiere of "60 Minutes," President Biden sat down with correspondent, Scott Pelley to talk about a wide range of issues, including some good news, the deal to avoid a potentially disastrous strike by America`s railway workers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTTY PELLEY: Mr. President, you have just averted a nationwide railroad strike that would have been crippling to the economy. How did you do that? And what were those last hours like in the negotiations?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Well, look, we brought business and labor together. One of the things that happens in negotiations, particularly if they`ve been a long game, like these have is people say and do things where their pride gets engaged as well. It is also hard to back off of some of these things.\par \par \uc2 So, what we did was just say, look, let`s take a look. Let`s take a look at what`s happening. You have a good deal being made for labor and for their income is going to go up 24 percent over the next five years. They`ve worked out the healthcare piece, they worked out days off, and they both sat down in my view and they were in the office today, saying, well, we finally figured out this is fair on both sides, and took that time to focus.\par \par \uc2 And, and the alternative was just not thinkable.\par \par \uc2 SCOTTY PELLEY: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : If in fact, they had gone on strike, the supply chains in this country would come to a screeching halt. We would have seen a real economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then this is the first time the President has sat down "60 Minutes" since he was elected.\par \par \uc2 You can see more of Scott Pelley`s interview with President Biden this Sunday on the premiere of the 35th season of "60 Minutes." Fifty-five years. That`s a long time, but still going strong, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tropical Storm watches are up across Puerto Rico and the US Virgin Islands this morning. Tropical Storm Fiona is moving toward Leeward Islands with strong winds and heavy rains.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking the storm.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, good morning. What are the biggest impacts we`re expecting from Fiona?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): I think the biggest impacts will be the rain. And one of the reasons, Nate, is because Tropical Storm Fiona is getting sheared. As we look at where it is located right now, you have that center of circulation, but notice all the strong convection is just off to the west.\par \par \uc2 You need that convection around the center of the system in order to develop, so that`s why we think it will be slow to gain strength when it comes to the winds.\par \par \uc2 How about our watches and warnings? A tropical storm warning means tropical storm force winds within 36 hours. A watch means tropical storm force winds within 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 So, we could see some power outages with some of those winds. But again, it`s the rain. We`re talking four to six inches. Locally, we could see some 10 to 15 inches of rain, and then of course where does it go from here? It is going to be making a turn to the north and I have to say, I cannot rule out impacts in the lower 48 just yet from this; so, we`re going to have to keep a close eye on this as we head into the middle of next week.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we will be updating Fiona`s track all day and all weekend and we will have live reports as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you and looking forward to have you in studio right here next week.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 : Yes, I`m excited about that, Nate, because usually people aren`t happy when they come to -- when we come to their city because they`re like, "Oh no, there`s going to be a big storm." I promise I`m coming with nice weather and I`m excited to see you guys, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re going to bring the sunshine.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220917ei9g000gr\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Nationwide Railroad Strike Averted: President Biden Announces A Tentative Deal Between Unions And The Rail Companies\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nancy Cordes \par \uc2 940 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: a nationwide railroad strike averted. President Biden announces a tentative deal between unions and the rail companies, avoiding what could have cost the U.S. economy $2 billion a day.\par \par \uc2 The president calls it a win for America. CBS` Nancy Cordes on the marathon 20-hour negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Migrants sent to Martha`s Vineyard. Florida`s Republican governor accused of playing politics after flying nearly 50 asylum seekers to the liberal enclave.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE DYLAN FERNANDES (D-Massachusetts): \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is a coward. Only a coward uses women and children for their own political gain.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Plus, the governor of Texas buses 100 migrants to the vice president`s D.C. residence.\par \par \uc2 Indiana`s abortion ban. The strict new law outlawing the procedure goes into effect today. CBS` Adriana Diaz is at one clinic which just saw its last patient.\par \par \uc2 The queen`s funeral. What we`re learning about the new roles for William and Harry at the final goodbye. Plus, the prince and princess of Wales greet well-wishers.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Charlie D`Agata:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: From here, they have been told that the journey will take more than eight hours.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And the unlikely friendship between Her Majesty and an American cowboy.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the CBS Evening News With Norah O`Donnell, reporting from the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this busy Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Tonight: the labor deal that prevented a catastrophic railroad shutdown. Rail companies and union leaders reached a tentative agreement less than 24 hours before workers were set to walk off the job. President Biden announced the deal after an all-night negotiating session.\par \par \uc2 The deal avoids a shutdown of the nation`s freight trains and passenger traffic that could have devastated the already fragile economy less than two months before the midterm elections. \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  is scrambling to restore service after the company canceled all long-distance routes ahead of a possible work stoppage.\par \par \uc2 Well, we have a lot of news to get to tonight.\par \par \uc2 And CBS` Nancy Cordes will start us off from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 So, how did this deal come together?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Lots of coffee, Norah, some late-night nudging by the president, and a meeting between union leaders and rail owners that was not allowed to end until they shook hands.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Yes, I am very pleased to announce a tentative labor agreement\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): Standing in the Rose Garden, President Biden hailed a deal to head off what would have been the first rail strike since 1992.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : This is a win of tens of thousands of rail workers and for their dignity.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The agreement was the product of marathon 20-hour talks led by Labor Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of Labor): Well, they weren`t getting out.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY MARTY WALSH: I can tell you that. They weren`t getting -- I told them: "You`re not getting out of this room."\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Walsh says a strike would have cost the U.S. economy $2 billion a day.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY MARTY WALSH: We have food and grain and corn waiting to be transported across the country that -- so, that wouldn`t have gotten there. That would have rotted. We have other supplies that go on trains. So, it would have been devastating.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Twelve unions secured the largest wage increases for railroad workers in more than four decades, a raise of 24 percent over five years. They also secured one more day of paid leave and, for the first time, the right to take unpaid leave to attend medical appointments.\par \par \uc2 DENNIS PIERCE\uc2  (National President, \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 ): The railroads were not particularly keen on giving us more time away from work, because that means you need more employees to run your operation. So, I think it`s a victory.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That victory so close to the deadline did come at a price. \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  had already canceled some long-distance trains, and GM, anticipating auto parts shortages, had been prepping to close some plants.\par \par \uc2 Why did it get to this point, the eve of a strike? Couldn`t that have been averted?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY MARTY WALSH: What I asked at the end of the conversation last night, when everything was done, to the companies and to the union, let`s not repeat this. Let`s get to the table earlier next year.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: This standoff was a big test for a man who has frequently billed himself as -- quote -- "the most pro-union president ever."\par \par \uc2 And the test isn`t quite over yet, Norah. This deal still has to be voted on by more than 100,000 rail workers, a series of votes that will take place over the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: They are so critical to our economy.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220917ei9f000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Shutdown That Could Hammer America`s Economy, As \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  Cancels Routes\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nancy Cordes \par \uc2 465 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, the Biden administration is working to avoid a rail strike that could derail an already fragile economy.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Nancy Cordes spoke exclusively with Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  a day after a government report showed inflation has not eased.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary): Inflation remains a problem and obviously is of tremendous concern to Americans.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  cited rising costs for rent and grocery as two of the biggest concerns.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY JANET YELLEN: We hope to see substantial improvement over the coming year.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Do you think inflation is close to peaking?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY JANET YELLEN: I don`t want to get involved in month-by-month forecasts. I do believe it will come down over time, due to the actions the Fed is taking.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: As she spoke, her colleagues in the Cabinet worked to head off a strike by freight rail workers. With the deadline now two days away, some chemical shipments have already been halted, and \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  announced it`s canceling all long-distance trains starting tomorrow, because some of them use freight rail tracks.\par \par \uc2 What impact would a rail strike have on already high inflation in this country?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY JANET YELLEN: Well, it would be a significant impediment to shipping goods. It`s certainly something we don`t want to see happen.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Secretary Yellen recently worked with European allies to impose a new price cap on Russian oil. But, already, energy bills in some parts of Europe are five times higher than usual.\par \par \uc2 What would a recession in Europe mean for the U.S. economy?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY JANET YELLEN: Well, a recession in Europe would have some spillovers to us. I think we shouldn`t exaggerate how significant they would be, but I do think this winter is going to be tough and challenging for our European neighbors.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Rail union leaders have been negotiating at the Labor Department all day long. And, in our interview, the Treasury secretary urged all the parties involved to reach an agreement by tomorrow night, because nearly 30 percent of all freight in this country, Norah, travels by rail.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Such a big story.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes with that exclusive, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220917ei9e0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Another Blow To The Country`s Fragile Supply Chain Is Looming\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Kris Van Cleave \par \uc2 683 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, a looming nationwide railway worker strike could derail the economy`s fragile supply chain.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has personally called the unions and the railroad companies to try and reach a deal before the key Friday deadline.\par \par \uc2 Well, as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave shows us, that could cost the U.S. billions of dollars per day.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Tonight, as much as 40 percent of all U.S. freight shipments could be halted by Friday, if the nation`s railroads can`t make a deal with unions representing nearly 60,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations continue, as at least three Cabinet secretaries and President Biden work to end the stalemate.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): The shutdown is not acceptable.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The two sides are at odds over sick time and attendance policies unions says penalize workers. The looming strike would idle 7,000 trains and 140,000 miles of track across 49 states, prompting \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  to cancel some long-haul service and warnings for commuter rail riders to make backup plans before Friday.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I have been commuting from the suburbs to Chicago now for over 30 years. I can never remember this happening.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Already, freight railroads are refusing some cargo.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK (Chief Economist, \uc2 KPMG\uc2 ): There`s just no question that it would be extremely devastating. This is sort of one of those things that is a main artery for the entire U.S. economy. And if you cut off that artery, you get a stroke. And you can`t afford that.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Nearly half-a-million extra big rigs would be needed to replace the idle trains. The first rail strike in 30 years could cost the economy an estimated $2 billion a day, disrupting agriculture, automotive, energy and retail businesses.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The railroads have agreed to substantial raises and bonuses. The holdup really is that sick time. The union representing the engineers tells CBS News it is willing to fly to Washington as soon as tonight to try to make a deal to continue those negotiations with the Cabinet secretaries and the railroads to avoid a strike -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Such a huge story. Kris Van Cleave, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next: Could a federal abortion ban be coming?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2 , a former federal judge who had roles in two presidential impeachments, died today of complications from a surgery at a Houston hospital.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Starr`s investigation into Whitewater then led to the discovery of Bill Clinton`s affair with \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2 , and then to the former president`s impeachment in the House in 1998. In 2016, Starr was forced out at \uc2 Baylor University\uc2  over his handling of sexual assault complaints at the school. More recently, he joined former President Donald Trump`s legal team for his first impeachment trial.\par \par \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2  was 76.\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  sent shockwaves through both parties today, introducing a nationwide abortion ban. Graham`s bill would ban the procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy, with rare exceptions. It has virtually no chance of becoming law in the Democratic-held Congress.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next: why an \uc2 NBA\uc2  team owner has been suspended for a year and fined $10 million. We will tell you what an investigation says he did.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Health officials have confirmed the nation`s first death from monkeypox. The patient from Los Angeles County was severely immunocompromised. Another person who had been diagnosed with monkeypox died last month in Texas, but it`s unclear what role the virus played in that death.\par \par \uc2 Big story. The owner of the \uc2 Phoenix Suns\uc2  and Phoenix Mercury was suspended from the \uc2 NBA\uc2  and \uc2 WNBA\uc2  today for one year and hit with a $10 million fine. An independent investigation found that Robert Sarver had a history of racist, misogynistic and hostile behavior.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220915ei9d000ji\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Massive Nurses Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Pauleen Le \par \uc2 458 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Moving on to the largest private sector nurses strike in American history and it has now entered its second day. Fifteen thousand nurses from more than a dozen hospitals in Minnesota walked off the job yesterday after months of failed contract negotiations. They say, they are overworked and underpaid and they want that fix.\par \par \uc2 Now, the strike is expected last three days with no talk scheduled at this time.\par \par \uc2 Pauleen Le of our Minneapolis station, WCCO has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PAULEEN LE: Nurses across parts of Minnesota, Monday, sounded off in force.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are out here literally to save our profession.\par \par \uc2 PAULEEN LE: Taking to the picket line in Minneapolis, St. Paul, and Duluth to protest what they say is substandard pay and severe staffing shortages brought on by COVID.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Today is historic. Fifteen thousand nurses out in the largest nurses` strike in American history.\par \par \uc2 PAULEEN LE: Negotiations with executives across multiple hospital systems have been ongoing since March, but they have reached a standstill in recent days. The nurses` union is asking for a more than 30 percent pay raise across three years, while hospitals are countering with 10 to 12 percent increases in the same period.\par \par \uc2 PAUL OMODT (Twin Cities Hospital Group spokesperson): What they`re asking for, their wage increases are not something that the hospitals can afford.\par \par \uc2 PAULEEN LE: But the nurses on strike are quick to point out that it`s not just about the money. They say COVID-related burnout has led to staffing shortages and hospitals, which has reduced the quality of care, and if more nurses aren`t hired, they say it could impact patients the most.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Imagine yourself in a hospital bed waiting too long for pain medication to the point that you are now in tears.\par \par \uc2 PAULEEN LE: It`s a problem being felt nationwide.\par \par \uc2 A recent report from McKinsey estimates the United States could face a shortage of 200,000 to 450,000 nurses within the next three years, and although the Minnesota nurses say their strike will only last until Thursday morning, it is forcing area hospitals to improvise in the short term, bussing in replacement nurses from around the country in order to maintain their patients` services.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Pauline Le, Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220915ei9d000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Honoring the Queen; Back to Buckingham; Ukraine`s Counteroffensive; Trump Investigations Escalate; Television`s Biggest Night; Massive Nurses Strike; Ryan Reynolds Leads from Behind; Importance of Colonoscopy Screening; Oprah`s Book Club Pick; Camilla`s Comeback; Like A Rolling Stone; Rosie O`Donnell Live; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Elizabeth Palmer, Holly  Williams, Debra Patta, Scott MacFarlane, Pauleen Le, Jon LaPook, Nancy  Chen, Mark Strassmann, David Begnaud, Roxana Saberi \par \uc2 13,783 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 As mourners pay respects to Queen Elizabeth, \uc2 King Charles\uc2  visits Northern Ireland. We`ll look at the future of the British Monarchy globally.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Do you expect that more countries will drop \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  as head of state?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Ultimately, it`s their decision.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A stunning turnaround in Ukraine where Russian forces appear to be retreating from several key areas.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (US Secretary of State): Ukraine is fighting for its homeland, for its future.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The DOJ has agreed to one of former President Trump`s Special Master candidates to review those documents that were seized at Mar-a-Lago by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A major setback for \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  after the latest uncrewed mission of his \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2  spacecraft went in flames.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: But a crew capsule was able to escape successfully.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: More than 100,000 railroad workers are threatening to go on strike.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It could cost the economy $2 billion per day.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Seahawk fans welcome Russell Wilson back home with a flurry of boos.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Geno Smith is the guy who stole the show. Look at Geno.\par \par \uc2 GENO SMITH (\uc2 Seattle Seahawks\uc2  quarterback): They wrote me off. I didn`t write back though.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The new book says former aides to President Trump claimed he threatened not to leave the White House after losing the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): I hope he did it like Jennifer Hudson in "Dreamgirls." He was like, "No, no, no way. No, no, no, no."\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (Talk show host): You know what it is? It`s an Emmy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Television`s biggest night, the 74th Annual Emmy Awards in LA.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER COOLIDGE (Actress): Wait. Wait. Wait.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Actress, Jennifer Coolidge decided to have a little fun when they played a rap music.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER COOLIDGE: And Tiffany Kuzon at Mosaic and my sister is here tonight and wait, hold on, one thing. Wait, hold on. Wait. Hold on. Okay.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You go, Jennifer, because the more she said "Wait, wait, wait, hold on." They turn the music up louder.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Louder and louder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she goes, "I`m just going to go with it."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s one way to handle it. I`ve never seen that before. It works.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I say, "Well done."\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday. You know what, we love Tuesday around here because today is no exception. It`s always a good day when Oprah, as in Winfrey is on the show.\par \par \uc2 Only on CBS MORNINGS. We`re going to discuss her latest book club pick. It`s a very unusual one for her that has David Begnaud talking to an author on death row, and of course, you see he is talking to Oprah, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s an only. We also have a first on CBS MORNINGS with Ryan Reynolds. He`s on the show with a very personal reveal. You are not going to want to miss it, trust me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And how does it feel to be on your own at 21 years old and the co-founder of "\uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2 ?" We will ask \uc2 Jann Wenner\uc2  about that and his revealing new memoir about his life among legends.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s a legend, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is a legend, and he`d be talking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`s got some stories to tell, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s got lots of stories. I can`t wait for him to get here.\par \par \uc2 But first, we`re going to have the latest on the mourning for Queen Elizabeth, of course, as the scene is about to shift from Scotland over to London.\par \par \uc2 People as you might expect are still lining up to view the Queen`s coffin in Edinburgh before it is taken to Britain`s capital city tonight.\par \par \uc2 At one point, \uc2 King Charles\uc2  and his brothers and his sister stood guard around the coffin that fulfills a Royal tradition.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin our coverage with Elizabeth Palmer in Edinburgh with more on that.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth it was a very touching scene to see all the siblings surrounding their mother`s coffin yesterday.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Deeply touching, very somber. That St. Giles Cathedral you can see behind me. That`s where they were and this morning, the Queen`s body is still there, lying at rest and we have not seen a single gap in the line of people filing in to say a last goodbye.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Last night, \uc2 King Charles\uc2 , his brothers the Princes Andrew and Edward, and his sister Princess Anne gathered around their mother`s remains, and stood heads bowed in a gesture that is once ceremonial and deeply personal.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, Queen Elizabeth`s body had left Holyroodhouse, her home in Edinburgh for the last time.\par \par \uc2 Silent crowds watched the hearse travel through the heart of the city along the historic Royal Mile.\par \par \uc2 For the Service of Thanksgiving for Queen Elizabeth`s life, the oldest crown in the kingdom, the Crown of Scotland was laid on her coffin.\par \par \uc2 Outside Scots and visitors from around the world waited patiently to file through St. Giles` to pay their respects. Four thousand people an hour were expected to file past the coffin, and even so, the lines stretched out of sight.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Very surreal and more emotional than I could ever remember. It was actually one of the most emotional moments in my life.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Night fell, and still they came, determined to pay tribute.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We know it is real, but when you see a coffin in there, it is -- I am sad. It is very real, very sad.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: King Charles, though officially in mourning was also hard work, monarch in waiting for so many years, this was the real thing.\par \par \uc2 At Holyroodhouse with his wife, Camilla, he accepted condolences from well- wishers, his new subjects.\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES III\uc2 : Thank you so much for your kind thoughts and prayers.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: He also met Scottish lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES III\uc2 : . king thoughts and prayers on these awful occasions, which we have to face.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I know, you`re -- you know, you`re dealing with so many great personal loss.\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES III\uc2 : Here we are. It is called duty.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Duty, once his mother`s deepest commitment will now rule the rest of Charles` life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Now, there are just a few hours left of public viewing because the Queen`s coffin will be loaded early this evening onto a \uc2 Royal Air Force\uc2  plane and taken to London where the next chapter of this extraordinary ceremony will unfold -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Elizabeth Palmer in Scotland, thank you.\par \par \uc2 As you heard, the next stage in mourning for the Queen begins in a few hours. Her coffin will remain at Buckingham Palace overnight. Tomorrow, a procession will bring the Queen to Westminster Hall to lie in state until her funeral.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is outside Buckingham Palace where a crush of mourners is causing some problems.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Since the Queen died on Thursday, crowds have been flocking here to Buckingham Palace, bringing with them flowers, cards, drawings made by their children and other tributes. Some people have been in tears, all here to remember their late beloved Queen.\par \par \uc2 The authorities have actually asked people to stop leaving soft toys and other non-biodegradable items, including Paddington Bears, those are being piled up because of the video the Queen famously made earlier this year for her Jubilee celebration with Paddington.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PADDINGTON BEAR: Happy Jubilee, Ma`am. And thank you for everything.\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II: That`s very kind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: You can say that the police have set up these barricades to funnel people past the Palace and avoid overcrowding. But this is nothing compared to what we`ll see beginning tomorrow when the Queen`s coffin will move down this road through Central London and to Westminster Hall.\par \par \uc2 And we actually saw a rehearsal for that earlier this morning. The Queen will lie in state for up to four days and around 750,000 members of the public are expected to line up with waits of up to 30 hours to pay their last respects.\par \par \uc2 They`ll have around 10,000 police officers on the street every day. Police say it will be a massive challenge, but they`ve been preparing for this for years -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Preparing, even practicing there. Amazing footage of the rehearsal.\par \par \uc2 Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We move on to other news this morning and the dramatic advance of Ukraine`s Army causing something we rarely see in Russia, public displays of anger.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian troops -- here is why -- have recaptured an estimated 2,000 square miles of territory in recent days, and the country`s President says that counterattack is going to continue.\par \par \uc2 Back in Russia, meanwhile, Putin is now facing rare criticism at home.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta has the latest now from Kharkiv in the northeastern part of the country where Russian troops have effectively been forced out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Seizing the momentum, Ukrainian troops continue their remarkable advance, yellow and blue pretty much the only colors flying high across this region today as they win the admiration of liberated towns and villages.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "Our troops were so smart," said this woman. "The Russian soldiers ran away so fast, they planned this really well."\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  declared war on Ukraine nearly seven months ago, his troops seized massive chunks of Kharkiv, but in just a matter of days, Ukrainian forces have blitzed through Russia`s gains, snatching back huge swathes of land.\par \par \uc2 It`s a crushing defeat and a decisive strike against Moscow`s ability to resupply their forces. Now, they can no longer use liberated Izyum as a strategic hub.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "They left all their explosives and ammunition here," said this Ukrainian soldier.\par \par \uc2 As the Russian frontline collapsed, the extent of a war declared largely on civilians is becoming clear. Hospitals and schools blown up, people killed in their own backyards.\par \par \uc2 Away from the battlefield, hard questions for Putin on state-run television, which usually only pushes Moscow`s lines in a so-called Special Military Operation.\par \par \uc2 (UNIDENTIFIED MALES speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "It`s either full scale war or we get out," said former Parliamentarian, Boris Nadishtin (sp?), and policy expert Viktor Olivich (sp?) exclaimed in disbelief, "You say everything is going according to plan. You really think six months ago we planned on leaving and repelling a counteroffensive?"\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Ukrainians want to believe that the tide is turning in a war that`s been weighted in Russia`s favor, but Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  warned that while the counteroffensive was well planned, and had clearly benefited from Western military aid, it was too early to predict an outcome -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right. That`s one way to look at it. Thank you very much, Debora Patta reporting from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Here in the US, there appears to be a major escalation in the Federal investigation into former President Trump`s actions after the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has more on this story from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 More than 30 people associated with the former President have received Federal grand jury subpoenas, some of them in the past week and at least two of them had their phones seized as well.\par \par \uc2 Multiple knowledgeable sources tell CBS News the investigation branches into at least three areas, the alleged attempt to submit fake electors to overturn the 2020 election. Also, the money raised and are spent to overturn the election by Trump`s Super PAC, Save America PAC as well. And finally into Trump`s Stop the Steal rally, January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there is a new development in the Federal investigation of those records seized from Mar-a-Lago last month.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department and the Trump legal team agreeing yesterday that Federal Judge Raymond Dearie will be an acceptable selection to be the independent Special Master in that case, which is important because yesterday, the Trump legal team again asked a Federal Judge to freeze the criminal investigation in to those records until the Special Master concludes his or her work -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Scott MacFarlane staying on top of it for us. Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Moving on to the largest private sector nurses strike in American history and it has now entered its second day. Fifteen thousand nurses from more than a dozen hospitals in Minnesota walked off the job yesterday after months of failed contract negotiations. They say, they are overworked and underpaid and they want that fix.\par \par \uc2 Now, the strike is expected last three days with no talk scheduled at this time.\par \par \uc2 Pauleen Le of our Minneapolis station, WCCO has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PAULEEN LE: Nurses across parts of Minnesota, Monday, sounded off in force.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are out here literally to save our profession.\par \par \uc2 PAULEEN LE: Taking to the picket line in Minneapolis, St. Paul, and Duluth to protest what they say is substandard pay and severe staffing shortages brought on by COVID.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Today is historic. Fifteen thousand nurses out in the largest nurses` strike in American history.\par \par \uc2 PAULEEN LE: Negotiations with executives across multiple hospital systems have been ongoing since March, but they have reached a standstill in recent days. The nurses` union is asking for a more than 30 percent pay raise across three years, while hospitals are countering with 10 to 12 percent increases in the same period.\par \par \uc2 PAUL OMODT (Twin Cities Hospital Group spokesperson): What they`re asking for, their wage increases are not something that the hospitals can afford.\par \par \uc2 PAULEEN LE: But the nurses on strike are quick to point out that it`s not just about the money. They say COVID-related burnout has led to staffing shortages and hospitals, which has reduced the quality of care, and if more nurses aren`t hired, they say it could impact patients the most.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Imagine yourself in a hospital bed waiting too long for pain medication to the point that you are now in tears.\par \par \uc2 PAULEEN LE: It`s a problem being felt nationwide.\par \par \uc2 A recent report from McKinsey estimates the United States could face a shortage of 200,000 to 450,000 nurses within the next three years, and although the Minnesota nurses say their strike will only last until Thursday morning, it is forcing area hospitals to improvise in the short term, bussing in replacement nurses from around the country in order to maintain their patients` services.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Pauline Le, Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was a big bold night of entertainment at the 74th Emmy Awards last night. HBO`s "Succession" was a big winner taking Best Drama Series. \uc2 Apple\uc2  TV`s "Ted Lasso" -- we all love that show -- won Best Comedy Series. "The White Lotus" won five Emmys last night including best limited series adding to five earlier wins.\par \par \uc2 And it was also an evening of amazing speeches and some history made as well.\par \par \uc2 "ET" co-host Kevin Frazier is in Los Angeles to tell us what went down.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, what`s up, baby? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER ("ET" co-host): Nate, how are you man?\par \par \uc2 You know host, Kenan Thompson kept his promise to keep everything tame and under control, but the night certainly had its fireworks with several first time winners, hoisting an Emmy, and big wins for some of the new shows that are on the block.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHERYL LEE RALPH (Actress): I am an endangered species.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Stunned at winning her first Emmy for Best Supporting Actress in the ABC comedy, "Abbott Elementary," Sheryl Lee Ralph, the second Black woman to ever win the award stunned the audience with a show- stopping speech.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL LEE RALPH: I am here to tell you that this is what believing looks like.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: It was a big night for first timers and people of color after the Television Academy faced scrutiny a year ago for lack of diversity in winners.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Quinta Brunson.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: "Abbott Elementary" creator, Quinta Brunson would also score an Emmy for writing the series.\par \par \uc2 Among the victorious newcomers, recording artist, Lizzo taking home her first Emmy for her series, "Lizzo`s Watch Out for the Big Girls."\par \par \uc2 LIZZO (Singer): When I was a little girl, all I wanted to see wasn`t me in the media. Someone fat like me, Black like me, beautiful like me.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Squid Game.")\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Star, Lee Jung-jae becoming the first person from a foreign language show to win Best Actor in a Drama upsetting favorite, Jeremy Strong of "Succession."\par \par \uc2 And "Squid Games" director came out on top, too.\par \par \uc2 HWANG DONG-HYUK ("Squid Game" director): And I truly hope "Squid Game" won`t be the last non-English series to be here at the Emmys.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: And more wins could be on the way.\par \par \uc2 HWANG DONG-HYUK: I`m in season two. I`m working on it. Now. I`m working on the script and I believe I have better script than season one.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Eupohoria.")\par \par \uc2 KELLY CLARKSON (Talk show host): Zendaya.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: And for her role as "Rue," a high schooler struggling with addiction in HBO`s "Euphoria," actress, Zendaya made history, too, becoming the youngest two-time Emmy winner at 26. She took home the top prize in a drama.\par \par \uc2 ZENDAYA (Actress): Anyone who has loved a Rue or feels like they are Rue, I want you to know that I`m so grateful for your stories and I carry them with me and I carry them with her.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: That young lady is a superstar.\par \par \uc2 "The White Lotus" had the biggest night with a total of 10 awards and that helped \uc2 HBO\uc2  and \uc2 HBO\uc2  Max get back on top after ceding its awards haul to \uc2 Netflix\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 "White Lotus" actress, Jennifer Coolidge added to the win column by picking up her first Emmy and another first time winner, Michael Keaton, by the way, who took home his Emmy for Best Actor in a Drama and he got that Emmy from \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes, she was a surprise appearance at the show.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Yes, she was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Nate, I say it with no bias. She looked friggin` fantastic.\par \par \uc2 But let`s talk about Sheryl Lee Ralph. I loved her moment, Kevin because she was clearly so overwhelmed when her name was called. It`s like she couldn`t even get out of her seat in that moment.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Television Academy and NBC.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was so happy for her and everybody on that show.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: The show was fantastic. And Gayle, there were tears everywhere. When we talked to her on the red carpet, I said, "There will be tears in the back and cheers if you win." This has been a long time coming. We know that and she is a fantastic actress.\par \par \uc2 If you go all the way back to the beginning. When she started on Broadway with "Dream Girls."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Moesha."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: And "Moesha."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And "Moesha."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Dream Girls." Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, Kevin. Kevin, before we let you go, Congratulations to you and the "Entertainment Tonight" team on the premiere of your 42nd season.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats. Congrats, Kevin. Congrats.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Thank you guys so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got 41 to go over here.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: And congrats you guys on your first year together because you just hit a milestone, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: First year, 42. Same thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. Same thing.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Kevin, we appreciate you, man.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: You know, just a little gap. A little gap.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much. Talk to you soon.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, first on CBS MORNINGS, a new role for Ryan Reynolds, the superstar actor in his "Welcome the Wrexham" soccer co-star, \uc2 Rob McElhenney\uc2 , make a very personal regimen to stay in shape.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up only on CBS MORNINGS, and we like when we can say that, David Begnaud shares Oprah`s latest Book Club Pick. It`s unlike anything she`s ever made before.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are back now at 7:30 with a spectacular failure to launch, a setback for \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 ` Blue Origin Space Program.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The New Shepard rocket, the very same type that has carried celebrities to the edge of space and back crashed to Earth after its launch yesterday in West Texas. That`s the capsule on top. No one was on board and the capsule did parachute down safely.\par \par \uc2 However, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  has grounded Blue Origin`s rockets until an investigation is completed. The company says that the safety system worked as designed, focused on the positive. They are not at this time saying what went wrong. Mark Strassmann has more now on the failed launch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It was the ninth flight of this New Shepard rocket carrying three dozen science experiments to the edge of space, but about a minute into its climb, a malfunction.\par \par \uc2 The onboard computer sensing trouble jettisoned the capsule up and away from the failing booster.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It appears to have experienced an anomaly with today`s flight.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It`s not yet clear why the booster failed and fell back to Earth crashing out of camera view. No people were inside the capsule.\par \par \uc2 It descended under parachutes and appeared to land normally. Call it a successful failure made possible by a key safety feature installed in the center of the capsule, the escape system.\par \par \uc2 ARIANE CORNELL (\uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 ): Come on into the capsule.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Blue Origin`s Ariane Cornell showed us last year.\par \par \uc2 ARIANE CORNELL: If there is any issue detected with the rocket, we`ve got the rocket, the capsule onboard, on top, we will fire this escape mower to get the capsule far and fast away from the booster.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: New Shepard is the workhorse rocket of Blue Origin`s space tourism business. Since last summer, it has successfully flown 31 people on six flights.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is heaven.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Those human flights use a different new Shepherd booster and capsule than the ones involved in Monday`s mishap.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But for now, all flights are on hold while the \uc2 FAA\uc2  investigates.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Mark Strassman, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, we`ve got a story, we are thinking, you`re not going to miss this. Why? Tony says, "For sure." Actor, Ryan Reynolds is doing something he has never ever, ever done before. We`ll tell you what that is.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, a reminder and I like to think of it as an invitation because you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast and you`re asking, "What do I get for that, Gayle?" Well, you get today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. Doesn`t that sounds like a deal? Yes, it does.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: First on CBS MORNINGS, Ryan Reynolds is making a very personal milestone public and asking you to follow his example.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RYAN REYNOLDS (Actor): Rob and I, both, we turned 45 this year and part of being this age is getting a colonoscopy. It`s a simple step that could literally and I mean literally save your life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Reynolds and fellow actor, \uc2 Rob McElhenney\uc2  allowed video cameras to record their colonoscopies. The co-owners of the Wrexham Soccer Club in Wales want to raise awareness about the rising rates of colon cancer in people younger than 50.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Reynolds went first arriving bright and early to his appointment with our favorite gastroenterologist, Dr. Jon LaPook.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RYAN REYNOLDS: I would never normally have any medical procedure put on camera in that chair --\par \par \uc2 It`s not every day that you can raise awareness about something that will most definitely save lives.\par \par \uc2 That`s enough motivation for me to let you in on a camera being shoved in my [bleep].\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Ryan. It is the first time you`ve had a colonoscopy?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REYNOLDS: Yes and [bleep] filming it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: You`re filming it.\par \par \uc2 RYAN REYNOLDS: Which is like also just as awful.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: The procedure itself doesn`t take long. We are talking, you know, 30 minutes, something like that. It`s stunningly effective.\par \par \uc2 RYAN REYNOLDS: I ate a Graham cracker.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: You did such a good prep that I was able to find an extremely subtle polyp that was on the right side of your colon. This was potentially life-saving for you.\par \par \uc2 I`m not kidding. I`m not being overly dramatic. I mean, this is exactly why you do this, okay. You had no symptoms.\par \par \uc2 RYAN REYNOLDS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: All right, man.\par \par \uc2 RYAN REYNOLDS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: I`m thrilled for you.\par \par \uc2 RYAN REYNOLDS: Thank you so much for this. Seriously, thank you for pushing me to do this.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: This is the polyp. I just cut it off with a snare and it`s gone.\par \par \uc2 You are interrupting the natural history of a disease of something of a process that could have ended up, you know, developing in to cancer and causing all sorts of problems.\par \par \uc2 Instead, you`re not only diagnosing the polyp, you`re taking it out.\par \par \uc2 ROB MCELHENNEY\uc2  (Actor): I figured, I can`t go wrong in terms of comparing myself to Ryan. They either find nothing and that means my colon was clean in the past, or they find a polyp --\par \par \uc2 All set.\par \par \uc2 And it is either bigger than his, which is awesome or it is smaller than his, which means I had less of an opportunity to have cancer, either way I went.\par \par \uc2 Oh.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hi. You`re all done. You`re in recovery now.\par \par \uc2 ROB MCELHENNEY: So easy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Good. How do you feel?\par \par \uc2 ROB MCELHENNEY\uc2 : I feel great.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Good.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Today, we found three polyps. They were small. They were not a big deal, but certainly a good thing that we found them early and removed them.\par \par \uc2 ROB MCELHENNEY\uc2 : Thank you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`ll see you -- I`ll see you in a couple of years.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Jon LaPook joins us right now. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, how did all of this come about?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Ryan came to me. You know, I`m very protective of my patients` privacy, but he reached the age of screening, which is now 45.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It used to be 50.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: It used to be 50, and it dropped down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why? Why did it drop?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: It dropped down because we`re seeing colon cancer in younger people for reasons that are not clear, but he wanted to raise awareness. And so he said, let`s do this PSA.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So his idea, not your idea.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Not my idea.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you went in there, you did your job, you found what you described as a subtle polyp. Is that typical, and also Rob had a polyp as well, right?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: I know. Each one of them had a polyp, which potentially -- it had the potential to over time turn into cancer. Totally benign right now, giving no symptoms, you find it, you pluck it out, and instead of potentially getting cancer, you don`t get cancer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony and I are the exact same age and I can tell when you were watching that you were thinking what I was thinking. It`s going to be us soon, so I know the age is 45. Is there any other signs that someone should go in sooner?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you guys are both 41.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are forty-one. I have got some time to prepare. I`ll watch that video a few times, get used to it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: I`m so glad that you brought that up, Nate, because these recommendations now down to 45 for people at average risk, right? If you have symptoms, if you have a change in your stool, and you have to get specific. If you notice bleeding, if you notice narrowing in the stool, you have abdominal pain.\par \par \uc2 If you`ve been told of iron deficiency anemia, you`re tired because of that, it could be because of invisible blood loss from a polyp or an early cancer that you don`t even know about.\par \par \uc2 If you have symptoms, go to your health care provider and get it evaluated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I know you are discreet because I`m one of your patients. I`ve been to Dr. LaPook several times and he told me that I have a long and winding colon.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, just sit with that for a second.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that unusual?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. How was Ryan`s colon?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: It was perfect, but I have to tell you --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It looked great on screen.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: On a serious note, you know, we talked about that prep. I know people don`t love to prep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hate the prep. Is there nothing else to do? And it is so important to do.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: There are. There are different types of prep. I know people are scared of it. You can have now a combination of having fluid and pills, you`ll figure out a way to clean it out, but the bottom line is that because they were so -- they both did such a good job, we were able to see these subtle polyps and you know, normally you think about a polyp on a stalk and it`s easy, how can you miss it?\par \par \uc2 But now we`re realizing there are more and more flat polyps very subtle and when you do a good job, as Ryan did, it was a perfect job. I was able to find it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we should also say, Jon, that it really is nothing to be afraid of, because it really is a piece of cake. I know the imagery is what freaks people out, but if you do the prep and that`s just drinking the stuff that makes you clean out everything. You really are out and you feel nothing. You honestly feel nothing.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Yes, and it is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s not something that should scare people.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: It shouldn`t. We have to raise awareness and there is a myth that you know, about men and women -- women and men get colon cancer just about equally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: So --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like you said, it could be life-saving.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. It could be life-saving, three and a half years and counting, four years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re there.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: See you soon.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re going together.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re going together.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll bring the cameras.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Oh boy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: It`ll be your idea, not mine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My polyp will be bigger.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man. Dr. Jon LaPook, thank you. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 To see the entire Ryan Reynolds and Rob McElhanney video, visit our \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2  or \uc2 Instagram\uc2  pages. Also visit leadfrombehind.org for more information.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And thanks to Ryan and Rob for doing that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that was great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s huge because I know he`s a big personality, but he`s actually -- he and Blake Lively, they are private.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Very, very private.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In their lives. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And I think it was a big deal for him to do this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, and coming up, we will have the stories that we`ll be talking about today. But first it is 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the Emmy for Best Supporting husband goes to Vlad Duthiers. More on that later in this block. Nancy Chen in for Vlad once more. What have you got, Nancy?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes, good to see you guys.\par \par \uc2 We do begin with a story you will see only on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Former President Barack Obama`s charitable foundation is unveiling its first class of Voyager Scholarship recipients. A hundred college students from across the country will receive up to $50,000.00 each in financial aid along with a $10,000.00 travel stipend and free \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  housing to pursue public service.\par \par \uc2 Now, the recipients also got something else.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States): Hey everybody, now I can see everybody. Can you guys hear me? Yes?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes, we can, Obama. And \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2  surprised the students in a Zoom meeting yesterday and let me tell you, we saw some jaws drop.\par \par \uc2 They all took a Zoom class photo before he spoke with two of the recipients. Wabash College student, Chase Breaux who is passionate about criminal justice reform and \uc2 Howard University\uc2  student, Jheannelle Johnson, who