Looking real good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I loved Marian`s dress.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Well, it`s beautiful and Vlad said he helped pick it out. So Marian is a news producer at HBO`s "Last Week Tonight with John Oliver." And the show won its seventh consecutive Emmy Award for Outstanding Variety Talk Series last night.\par \par \uc2 And that was not the only pleasant surprise.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARIAN WANG (Vladimir Duthier`s wife): And Vlad, Vlad. I only discovered now, pockets.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What?\par \par \uc2 MARIAN WANG: Pockets.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You didn`t know you had pockets?\par \par \uc2 MARIAN WANG: No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pockets and an Emmy?\par \par \uc2 MARIAN WANG: For my dry bar.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations, love.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Pockets.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that. Congratulations, love. But you know what that`s like, Nancy, when you have a fancy, dancy dress that has pockets? That`s always a key.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes. You`re able to put a couple of hideaways in there. I liked that. She walked off stage and that was her first reaction is "I found out I had pockets."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations, love. That`s nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We do have some more awards news to get to, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: We do. Speaking of awards. Every year \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2  gives out a prestigious honor in memory of legendary CBS News anchor, Walter Cronkite. Now, you may recognize this year`s honoree. We can announce this morning, it is none other than our very own, Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I got the word. I got the word. It`s going to be in February and word is that I actually get to go to Arizona and they`re going to give me a day off to accept it. So, thank you Walter Cronkite.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Finally, a day-off, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, thank you, Nancy. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re talking book club.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, only on CBS MORNINGS, and we love when that happens, Oprah talks to David Begnaud about her latest Book Club Pick and the author`s remarkable backstory.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. "\uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2 " co-founder, Jann Wenner`s new memoir is full of big reveals and he is going to be here to share what it was like to be at a crossroads of history.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. The great Rosie O`Donnell will be here, too. She plays a detective in the new series "American Gigolo," and she`ll tell us all about getting her mindset for that character.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Rosie is on the case. But first here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: When you see a coffin in there, I am sad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mourners line up to honor Queen Elizabeth before her coffin is moved to London, as \uc2 King Charles\uc2  visits Northern Ireland.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: The Queen`s body is due in early evening to be loaded onto a \uc2 Royal Air Force\uc2  plane to be flown to London.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Against the odds, Ukrainian forces have Russian forces on the run in a key part of the country.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Ukrainians want to believe that the tide is turning in a war that`s been weighted in Russia`s favor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thousands of nurses have walked off the job in Minnesota to protest overwork and understaffing.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We`re serious about what we need to keep doing our jobs.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A hand to Williams, ball comes out, recovered by Seattle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Russell Wilson returns to Seattle as a Denver Bronco to face his former team. But it was the \uc2 Seahawks\uc2  who took the win.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Missed it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Not what Russell had in mind and what Geno Smith, I`m sure was dreaming about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Nate, take us there. Imagine how Russell felt going in and they booed him. Weren`t you surprised?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was surprised. I thought they would give him a standing ovation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But all is fair in love and sports.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So congratulations to Seattle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. It`s not complicated. He`s not their guy anymore.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was your guy. I mean, I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is the best quarterback the franchise has ever had. But you know what, Seattle wanted to win.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, and they did, and they did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Moving on. We always love it when we can say "only on CBS MORNINGS," especially when it comes to revealing the latest selection in Oprah`s Book Club.\par \par \uc2 Her new pick is called "That Bird has My Wings," the autobiography of an innocent man on death row. It`s written by Jarvis Jay Masters -- is his name, and more than 20 years ago, Masters was sentenced to death by lethal injection for a crime he was never accused of committing. You heard me right. Never accused of committing.\par \par \uc2 Our lead national correspondent, David Begnaud spoke with Oprah about why she picked this book, and then he also interviewed Masters from outside California`s San Quentin State Prison.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The man who is now the centerpiece pick of Oprah`s Book Club lives deep inside of San Quentin, California`s oldest prison.\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS ("That Bird has My Wings" author): My name is Jarvis Masters. I`ve been in San Quentin for 35 years.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: This video of Jarvis Jay Masters from 2016 provides a rare look at the place that holds the largest population of death row inmates in the country.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Step back away from the cell, please.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There is no way to show you more because it is against California law to bring cameras into State Prisons to interview a specific prisoner. But inmates may call out.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You have a prepaid call from --\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: Jarvis Masters.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: An inmate at the California State Prison, San Quentin.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Hello Jarvis. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: Hey, is this, David? How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I am well. How are you feeling this morning?\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: Well, you know I`m nervous for sure.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Nervous. Why?\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: Because I`m talking to \uc2 CBS\uc2 . I mean, who does that, you know?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Jarvis Masters arrived at San Quentin in 1981 from Long Beach, California and it was there at the age of six, he was first picked up by police because he had run away. He was abandoned by his mother and father.\par \par \uc2 Tell me about this armed robbery that you committed?\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: Which ones?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: How many were there?\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: There were a bunch of them. I got convicted of maybe 14 or 15.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: I had ran away from a youth authority. I just needed the money.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You`ve written two books, "Finding Freedom," "That Bird Has My Wings."\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: "That Bird Has My Wings" is the reason we are talking today. It was picked for Oprah`s Book Club. What was your intention for writing that book?\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: My intention for writing that book was to speak to the kids, people who are at risk. I saw all those faces in that book when I wrote it. More and more I began feeling like I was telling their story as well as mine.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This call and your telephone number will be monitored and recorded.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And it`s interesting, Oprah, because when you interview an inmate, you only have about six minutes and then the phone cuts off and they have to call back.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: You able to talk to him and actually hear his voice?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Wow.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Jarvis Masters. How did you first hear the name?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : I was interviewing Pema Chodron, the Buddhist monk spiritual teacher.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Pema Chodron wrote the introduction.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And she suggested that I read the book, and that was, I think, around 2014, over eight years ago.\par \par \uc2 I read the book. Loved the book. Wanted to choose the book as a Book Club then, or at least get an interview with him then. I called the then Governor of California. I appealed to the entire prison system.\par \par \uc2 My team called. We could not get an interview.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Jarvis Masters, now a Buddhist, wrote about his journey from a hardened street kid whose life took an even more terrible turn four years into his 20-year armed robbery sentence.\par \par \uc2 That`s when prison guard, Howell Dean Burchfield was killed by inmates in a single stab wound to the heart, and though Jarvis Masters was never accused of committing the murder, he was accused of making the device to commit the crime.\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: I was charged with it, I never understood why I was charged for it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Did you have anything to do with making the tool, the instrument that was used to kill him?\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: Absolutely not. No.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The record shows at the 1990 Court sentencing that the Judge said: "He was born into hell. He was born for reasons, it is almost impossible for me to comprehend."\par \par \uc2 "If people don`t want children, they shouldn`t have them. Apparently, his mother didn`t know how to not have them."\par \par \uc2 And he`s now in his 60s, and that statement still sticks with him today.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Wouldn`t it stick for somebody saying not only somebody, but somebody who is sentencing you to death, saying you should have never even been born?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I said to him on the call, "Jarvis, what happened to you?"\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Wow.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And his voice changed. And he said, "Oh, man," and he took us back to Long Beach. "At 12, I was arrested for committing the first crime." And he said, "I was a young boy when I walked into the house and I heard that my latest foster mom and stepdad were telling the social worker, they were seriously ill and that the boy would have to leave."\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: And that just turned my world upside down, even before I got into these other foster homes. I felt betrayed.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: One of the things that sparked the fire for me, he talks about the thing that carried him through life was holding on to the love that people had shared with him, you know, in spite of, you know, his mother being a heroin addict, in spite of all the crazy violent things that were happening in their house and in that moment, he believed that no matter, what my mother loves me.\par \par \uc2 No matter how many times you`ve been abandoned, it`s the love that carries you through.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So, some people will hear this and say, "A death row inmate? Oprah`s Book Club, what?" What`s the intention behind the pick?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : You know, that`s my favorite word.\par \par \uc2 Number one, my intention is to expose the story. Number two, my intention is to let people know that there are a lot of people on death row and a lot of people in prison for whom there has been a miscarriage of justice.\par \par \uc2 I do not believe that the crime that he is on death row for, that he was involved in or committed --\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  has gone so far as to help find a team of defense lawyers, that is now led by Michael Williams, an attorney with Kirkland & Ellis, the world`s largest law firm.\par \par \uc2 MAN: At the time that Jarvis was convicted, the State of California had in their possession, a confession by another individual saying that he had committed the crime, not Jarvis.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: These are three people critically involved in the case.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And it was their testimony that was used to convict Jarvis Masters.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes. When you have an innocent man on death row, it`s not just one person who said something that was wrong.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The people who are now saying that Jarvis didn`t do it are the key crucial characters who were actually convicted of doing it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, four pleaded to doing it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So, the case was built on the words of these people.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And now their words are changing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Exactly. Now, their words have long since changed.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The Jarvis I`m speaking to now sounds like a soft reflective man. The Jarvis you`re telling me about seems scary and intimidating. What changed you?\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: I think what changed me is becoming 23 and now becoming 60, and everything in between that, yes, Buddhism helped me it really, really exposed the suffering that we all live outside us.\par \par \uc2 Growing up inside the violence here, knowing what violence does to people, it changes you.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You have 60 seconds remaining.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SHEFF (Author): Part of the reason that his story is so extraordinary is because he has been living in hell for 40 years. You know, people who -- on death row, people go crazy. They kill themselves. They become really cynical and bitter and angry, and he did the opposite.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: David Sheff, an award-winning author who wrote the book, "The Buddhist on Death Row," says he found evidence that Jarvis Masters has helped people while in prison.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SHEFF: So Jarvis embraced this new path of committing himself to trying to alleviate suffering in every way that he could.\par \par \uc2 He stopped at least two people from being stabbed in the yard.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You think he`s innocent?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SHEFF: I`m a hundred percent convinced he is innocent. I`ve read it all. I believe that if he had had real representation, that he never would have been convicted.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Given all the pain you endured as a child, given the decades that you`ve spent in prison, and on death row. Has your life been worth it?\par \par \uc2 JARVIS MASTERS: That`s a good question. I`m glad that my mother gave me a chance. I`m glad she gave me a chance.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Jarvis Masters` next step to arguing for his exoneration is going to take place in a hearing in Federal Court on October 27th.\par \par \uc2 The State of California`s Attorney General`s Office will have a chance to then counter, but in response to our request for an interview, the State wrote that it refused to answer our questions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know Oprah has been talking about this story for years. I still don`t understand why he is on death row, still. Do you?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Well, it is fascinating, Gayle, in that he is the guy who`s on death row, but he was never accused of actually committing the murder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And the people who actually were convicted of committing the crime are not on death row.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That for starters is like, "Wait, what?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s very strange, and it is a reminder that, you know, we`ve always talked about guilty without a shadow of a doubt. I mean, the shadow over the criminal justice system in general is pretty large at this point. It`s troubling.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Look, and he has been trying. He`s got a powerhouse defense team now. They`re going to go to the Federal Court system and basically say the State erred.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day, if the Federal Court system decides that his constitutional rights were violated, Jarvis Masters has already served his sentence for armed robbery. So, if his conviction is thrown out, he essentially --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is a free man?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Could be released.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow. That`s --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, let`s see what happens. No one wants criminals to go free, but at the same time, you don`t want innocent people to be serving beyond what they should.\par \par \uc2 All right, David, thank you very much. That`s a great piece and a great selection for the new Oprah Book Club Pick.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, activist and actor, Rosie O`Donnell will be here in studio to talk about landing her new role as a detective in the Showtime series, "American Gigolo."\par \par \uc2 But first, 8:12, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, we`ll look at the evolution of Camilla, the Queen Consort. How she transformed her public image and what role she might play in the monarchy.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 King Charles\uc2  traveled to northern Ireland this morning with Camilla, the new Queen Consort, that`s the official term for her.\par \par \uc2 She has been by his side throughout the mourning period for Queen Elizabeth, playing a role that many people in that country would have denied her in the past.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi is at Windsor Guildhall for us, outside London with more on the public acceptance of the Queen Consort.\par \par \uc2 Roxana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 For years, Camilla was one of the most controversial figures surrounding the Royal family, but since she and Charles got married here back in 2005, her image has gradually improved. And now, for many Brits, that controversy has been laid to rest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Long Live the King.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The United Kingdom is getting more than just a new King, it is also getting a new Queen, Queen Consort that is.\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES III\uc2 : I know she will bring to the demands of her new role the steadfast devotion to duty on which I have come to rely so much.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Julian Payne was the couple`s communication secretary for six years. What role do you think Camilla will play for the King?\par \par \uc2 JULIAN PAYNE (King Charles III former Communications Secretary): She is a balancing act. What that means is that she`s got a real sense of what normality is and how to translate that and bring that into this quite rarefied atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: It`s a remarkable rise for a woman who was once one of the most derided figures in Britain.\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH: She was public enemy number one here. Incredibly unpopular media coverage she had here.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Twenty-seven years ago, Camilla Parker Bowles was considered the other woman Princess Diana blamed for the breakup of her marriage.\par \par \uc2 PRINCESS DIANA (Princess of Wales): There were three of us in this marriage, so it was a bit crowded.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The BBC later apologized for the way that now infamous 1995 interview was obtained, but Diana`s words still stung.\par \par \uc2 Charles was said to have been smitten as soon as he met Camilla in the 1970s. They each married others, but remained close.\par \par \uc2 Later, both couples famously divorced, and with Diana`s death in 1997, Camilla`s popularity plummeted even more.\par \par \uc2 Gradually, Charles and Camilla stepped back out in public.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Did he get down on one knee to propose?\par \par \uc2 CAMILLA (Queen Consort): Of course.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: By the time they married in 2005, thousands of Royal fans lined the streets of Windsor.\par \par \uc2 In pop culture, and too much of the public, Camilla`s image as the maligned mistress remained.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The \uc2 Crown\uc2 .")\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: But recent polls suggest, she is slowly warming hearts.\par \par \uc2 JULIAN PAYNE: People have started to see the person behind the headlines. She was firmly of the view that you should meet me and you should judge me on how you find me and on the work that I do.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Camilla has championed her own causes, like domestic abuse.\par \par \uc2 QUEEN CONSORT CAMILLA: How many more women must be harassed, raped, or murdered.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: It also helped that earlier this year, Queen Elizabeth announced that the Duchess of Cornwall should one day take the title of Queen Consort.\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH: It was an unbelievably brilliant move by the Queen to do that, just tidying up all the loose ends before she went.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: And so this weekend, outside Buckingham Palace, some people told us their views on the Queen Consort are evolving.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think she`ll be amazing. She will be his rock, like Prince Philip was the Queen`s rock.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: In a "Vogue UK" article earlier this year, Camilla said the decades of public scrutiny have not been easy, but that she has tried to get on with life.\par \par \uc2 The 75-year-old will now get on as Queen Consort, her main role to support the husband, the King.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Roxana Saberi, Windsor.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, Emmy Award winner, Rosie O`Donnell will join us to talk about her new role in this series, it is called "American Gigolo." She plays a cop.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s just say, when a legend enters a building, we all come to the greenroom. It`s Jann Wenner telling very personal stories about yourself and others. You worried about hurting people`s feelings? Yes or No?\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER ("Rolling Stone" founder): No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Papa was a Rolling Stone. We`ve got one of those right here.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to the CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 More than 50 years ago, just 21 years old. What were you doing at 21? \uc2 Jann Wenner\uc2  co-founded a small music publication in San Francisco. It was called "Rolling Stone." You heard of it? Yes, you have.\par \par \uc2 The magazine quickly became one of the most respected and widely read in America. It is published more than -- look at these covers -- published more than 1,500 issues covering and shaping music, politics and pop culture.\par \par \uc2 Now, with his new memoir, it`s called "Like a \uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2 ." Where did you get that title, \uc2 Jann Wenner\uc2 ? He is sharing a very intimate look at his own life, and its many ups and downs.\par \par \uc2 He writes about his private and family life and close relationships and friendships with the likes of The Beatles, Mick Jagger, Bob Dylan, Yoko Ono, Bette Midler, just to name a few.\par \par \uc2 Jann Wenner\uc2  joins us now here at the table. I`m so glad to see you.\par \par \uc2 Listen, the book is close to 600 pages. What were you going to say?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say, it reads so much like a movie that I thought we`re going to throw to a clip of it already, like has anyone started casting this thing?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, it`s so true. Because Jann, I want to go back because you opened the book with that last day and you`re leaving the "Rolling Stone" offices, and I want to talk about your feelings there.\par \par \uc2 But your first editor`s letter, you said this: "We have something here for the artists in the industry, for every person who believes in magic that can set you free."\par \par \uc2 So, take us back to \uc2 Jann Wenner\uc2  at 21, because you were fearless. You were quite bold. You were quite tough some people say and a tad arrogant.\par \par \uc2 What was your thinking back then?\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: A tad arrogant?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were very confident.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: I fell in love with rock `n` roll music at a point when I was in college. I couldn`t play it, but the idea of it so suffused me. I mean, I felt that thing, which was the spoonful said, "The magic that can set you free." There is something about that rhythm and that freedom and the joy of it that I just had to be part of rock `n` roll.\par \par \uc2 And so this is the way I was going to do it. I could write about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: I couldn`t play it very well, saying it a lot. But that`s what the magazine was devoted towards, bringing that message of freedom and vitality and within that human justice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you want to be controversial? Because you had a lot of controversial things in your publication?\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you thinking that?\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: No. I didn`t set out to be controversial? I mean, it just happens. And you discover quickly that, you know, well, that`s great if you`re in the news business, that`s what you want.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: And it was a love -- the country`s really great journalism that brought us along and galloped our success by kind of breaking story after story. You know, and it was the good journalism.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, really fantastic. I mean, Tom Wolfe was one of the early writers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hunter Thompson.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hunter Thompson. Annie Leibovitz came by as a photographer. I think she was an art student.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was an art student in 2021.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then there`s the performance themselves, the singers, the songwriters, they`re also flocking to the magazine. They`re writing letters of complaint when they don`t think the reviews are adequate. I mean, were you finding these people? Or were they finding you?\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: A combination of both. People and musicians and the music was shocked to see this publication. It was covering rock `n` roll seriously. Taking it, you know, certainly joyfully, but taking it like an adult.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Genuine respectable art form it was, and when we came along, it was just teen magazines, you know, covering --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Music beat --\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: "The Tiger Beats," "Sixteen" they were like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Well, the trades which were kind of, you know, irrelevant, really. So people saw it and wanted to be monitored and make it succeed, want to help it get along, want to be a part of it and our success is based in great part on The Beatles, on John Lennon particularly wanting to send his stuff in the "\uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2 " and wanting to be in the periodical.\par \par \uc2 And it was with John that we rose -- he helped us rise to success.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, you had --\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: A very concrete role.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where you had that cover where they were both nude and they didn`t want to put it on the album cover. And you said, we will put it on the cover of "\uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Then you sat down with John Lennon and he got upset with you. You did have a little bit of a falling out a little.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: A bit of falling out. I did -- when he was -- when I was 25 and he was 28, I think during that -- we did the interview where he talked for the first time ever about what it was like to be in The Beatles, inside The Beatles.\par \par \uc2 Before, they`d been kind of prepackaged, the Fab Four. So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He let loose.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: And he let loose and it was brilliant. That got a lot -- it created a lot of controversy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: But I think that over the years, he just decided that he shouldn`t have -- he started calling, and said Lennon remembers, he called it Lennon regrets.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: And anyway, he objected my publishing it as a book. I wanted to publish a book. And I think it turns out that that book, that interview is a key piece of history. I mean, a really historic --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And how do you tow that line between telling the truth, telling your truth, the magazine`s truth, and also balancing the relationship.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good question, yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Very mildly, I`m really good friends with a bunch of the people that we cover.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As the pictures show, I mean how many boats and how many places? Mick Jagger?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Fishing with Mick. And I think the thing is, you have to understand two things. One is that these are people who are not making faulty cars or putting out cigarettes, or they`re not politicians. They are doing good. They`re making music and art. They`re genuinely really happy.\par \par \uc2 You know, they`re making the world a better place. So, they don`t need that kind of inspection, that kind of -- those rules that apply to public figures.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is that, in the end, it`s just really -- nobody ever asked for me to change a record review, another star, whatever. That just went without saying. But in the end, you`ve got to go with, as a journalist, the truth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: As a publisher, and I think that was the fundamental value of "\uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2 " to its readers, to me, and to the artists and the artists thought they had something really special here. I think that should be protected.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: And the integrity is one of those things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can we talk about Gus Wenner, your son, 28, who is now running --\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Thirty-two.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thirty-two. Thirty-two. Who is now running things. He certainly has that confidence and swagger that you had. You made it clear that you would sort of like to hang on -- not hang on, but you would like to remain and do things and you said, Gus, listened impatiently, but respectfully.\par \par \uc2 What`s it like when you still want to be involved and your son is like basically "No, Dad, I got this."\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: You got it -- Gus is handling it with such TLC and it was like an old bull in a China shop. And I so much wanted to be still stuck in the closet giving advice and Gus eased me out real gently. I mean, really thoughtful.\par \par \uc2 And obviously, it was going to be like "Tommy Boy" in that movie. And he is more than ready to take over. He has done a great job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is killing it, too.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: He is killing it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You talk about your health issues. Good Lord, Jann, what you`ve been through. I mean, you`re throwing a ball with your son, Noah. You said, unasked to do so, you break your femur That leads, you have a heart attack.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Spinal fusion.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A spinal fusion. A torn retina --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking back to the fact that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talking about some parts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, you had a lot of stuff and you`re close with Bruce Springsteen and his wife, Patti Scialfa. I love all the Bruce stories.\par \par \uc2 But you just mentioned to Bruce casually, you know, they say doctors perform better if they`re listening to music in the operating room.\par \par \uc2 Bruce and Patti show up at the hospital. They have a mixtape to be played during your six-hour surgery.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. Do you remember?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I thought that was so cool. Do you remember any of the songs that were on it?\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Yes. Well, I remember first of all. This is going to give me good treatment from this doctor. This doctor is going to really take care of me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Yes, I remember this. Well, there was Jackson Browne on it, and Van Morrison, my favorites. A bunch of country stuff. This beautiful song and it`s called "Night Shift" by the Commodores, which --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Is a little -- it was about death. I mean, that was very --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But just the fact that he took the time to do that for you, Jann.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: That was -- it is a knock out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jann, real quick, best concert you`ve ever been to.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Best was the one I produced honestly, modestly called the "The Rock `n` Roll Hall of Fame 25th Anniversary Concert" at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  starring Bruce, Bono, Mick Jagger, Stevie Wonder, Aretha Franklin.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, oh man.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Crosby, Stills, Nash, Paul Simon, and Simon and Garfunkel.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: I mean, incredible. Two nights of four hours a night. It was just amazing. Both of those nights were --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know you enjoyed going back in time. The spirit of the times that you were able to capture, people reading this book will get that same feeling, that rush of happiness, that giddiness. It`s there on the page.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is. I know we`ve got to go. You say you`re a 75-year-old man, but you feel so young at heart. I do, too. Six kids. You`ve had two spouses. One great spouse -- both your spouses have been fantastic. Shout out to Matt now.\par \par \uc2 Good weather, sun sparkles on the ocean. The dog sleeps at your feet. Life is pretty good for you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Life is good. Yes. You`re on your own Buster Brown.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That`s what your mom used to say.\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I am reading. Thank you, \uc2 Jann Wenner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The book is called "Like a \uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2 ." It`s on sale now wherever you like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`ve already got a reader in our greenroom. It looks like Rosie O`Donnell is in there. She has been waiting a long time. She is on Page 201 already. Hey, Rosie.\par \par \uc2 How do you like it?\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL ("American Gigolo" actor): I`m loving it. It`s a great read.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There you go. Put it on the covers.\par \par \uc2 She is playing a detective, Sunday, that`s her name on the show "American Gigolo." She`s going to talk about it today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay, Rosie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: First, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s funny, Rosie --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Only on CBS MORNINGS, the great Rosie O`Donnell is here. Her wide-ranging career speaks for itself, and now, she stars as Detective Sunday in the new Showtime series, "American Gigolo."\par \par \uc2 It`s based off the film of the same name about a male escort named Julian Kaye, who is framed for a murder. In the series, Detective Sunday is on a mission to find out who set up Julian.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this clip where she tries to interview Olga, Julian`s former boss.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "American Gigolo.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, this is good. Like \uc2 CBS\uc2 , \uc2 Showtime\uc2  is part of the \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2  family.\par \par \uc2 Rosie O`Donnell is here. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Thank you for having me. Good to see all of you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Rosie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want them to let clip keep rolling.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Take me through.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Now, how did this come about? Because we hear that you didn`t audition for the role of the detective.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: It was the first time I got just an offer. You know, a lot of times you have to interview with the scenes and sometimes you just have to meet the director. But this time, I didn`t have to do either.\par \par \uc2 They called my agent and said we want to offer her this role that was originated by Hector Elizondo in the movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, in the movie.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Yes, so they --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you put it out there that you were interested in doing something like this, or you just get a random call?\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Well, you know, I`ve been doing a lot of acting roles in the last couple of years, and my agents are sort of focusing on that, which is great for me. I love the challenge of that.\par \par \uc2 And when they told me I was going to take over for him, I was a little overwhelmed. I`m like, "Are they going to change the script or anything?" No, it`s just going to be the same script, but you`re going to be playing the male character, one of the first detectives in the LAPD.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So how did you prepare? Did you talk to female detectives?\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you look at -- you did?\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Yes. A bunch of female detectives. I wanted to see what it was like for the women who are my age, who are 60 and about to age out and have to retire.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "American Gigolo.")\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: And what was their life like when they started, all those years ago when my character was one of the first detectives in the LAPD. So, I had some great stories of amazing women I spoke to, all of them battle-worn, so many years as a detective, you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t we all feel battle-worn?\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Well, I kind of do. I wake up in the morning, "I go, oh, that`s my knee. Nice to meet you."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: You know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get that. I get that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The first episode of the show explores why Julian, played by Jonathan Bernthal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who is fantastic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is great.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Wonderful actor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is radiant on the screen. How he got into -- his character got into sex work.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why do you it was important to get all of that backstory in?\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: I think no one ever really wonders what makes someone become a sex worker. I mean, we do somewhat for women, young girls. We think, "Oh, they`re trafficked in this way." And this society is set up to keep women marginalized.\par \par \uc2 But what about a boy? How do boys get groomed for sex work in an older life? And that was an important part of why the show was made to sort of look at that, but it`s a hard subject to deal with, because, you know, it`s very painful sometimes to watch to see what this man who was once a boy, what he had to go through to get to where he is to survive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It took me a minute when you said, "I`m 60." I went, "Rosie, when did that happen?" So at 60, you`re a TikTok star, you`re on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How did that happen?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, how did that happen?\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Well, my 19-year-old daughter for three years was like, "Mom, you`re going to love this. You`re going to love this." And I was like, I thought it was dancing, that you had to do the hip dancer. So, she showed me and then I sort of got addicted.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And people love you.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: It`s fun. You know, you just get to be yourself. And like, "Hi, I`m really bored." Or "Can you believe the Queen died?" And you know, like, you could just say whatever you want.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you believe the Queen died? Have you been following all the coverage?\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: I think she was an amazing woman and dedicated her whole life to the country. And I have nothing, but respect for her.\par \par \uc2 But I have some British friends who called me and said, "We really weren`t prepared. We weren`t prepared." I am like, "You weren`t prepared? She was 96. What did you expect? To have a 200-year-old Queen?"\par \par \uc2 And imagine this life, you have this amazing life? Then you see the new Prime Minister in, and you die in your sleep two days later. It`s like "Check, please. Could I take that? Is that it --"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did think of a sort of a beautiful way to go if you`re going to go at 96 and you`re in a place that you love. But Rosie, we`ve been playing clips from your talk show from back in the day.\par \par \uc2 When you look at that, what do you think? I love the show so much. I was a watcher. Every single day, you seemed to have so much fun.\par \par \uc2 When you look back on those days. What do you think? And do you miss it as you sit here today?\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: I don`t really miss it because there was some level of authenticity that you need in order to have it be real. By the time I was done, you know, I had all these children that I wanted to raise and be at their little league games and do all of that as more of a normal type mother rather than the celebrity mother who it is a big deal when I show up at school and I gave that to my kids.\par \par \uc2 And I knew I was going to do that because my mom died at 39, and when I took the chance --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At 10, yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Yes, when I took the job, I said, I`m going to do six years and I`m out, and no one believed me. They were like, "No, you`re not. There`s a lot of money. You`re going to stay."\par \par \uc2 I`m like, "Actually, I have a lot of money. I`m going to go." You know? They`re like, "Why?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you did.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: And I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you still crafting?\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: I am crafting all the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you were always a big crafter.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: I love it. My mother did that, too. She did like scrapbooks and redid furniture from the flea market, and so I think that`s in my genes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You still were the celebrity mom at school, though, were you not? I mean, you show up, it is still Rosie O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Yes, but my kids went to Waldorf schools where you`re not allowed to watch television. So, the difference between the first year when I picked them up at the public school to the second year when I went to the Waldorf program was night and day.\par \par \uc2 It was like for me going to Europe where my show never played. I can go to Europe and no one knew me. I was walking around, it was the greatest thing. And I have that now at 60 with this new haircut.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, Rosie I say this with all respect, not to age you, you have been doing this for decades.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have been entertaining the masses at a high level. You are one of the most versatile in the business. Thank you so much for everything you`ve contributed.\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL: Thank you. That`s so sweet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Rosie O`Donnell, you`re best.\par \par \uc2 And you can watch new episodes of "American Gigolo" on Fridays on Showtime.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Rosie O`Donnell has left the building. I said "Rosie, life is going so great. You`ve got four kids and didn`t you remarry?" She goes, "I`m never doing that again, Gayle. I`ve had enough divorce proceedings to last me for a lifetime."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What about you, Gayle? You getting remarried?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You got anybody?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think they`re both --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t play with me, Nate. Don`t play with me, Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am looking, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t play with me.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us.\par \par \uc2 We will see tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mama was a rolling stone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wherever she laid her hat was her home.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220913ei9d000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Seattle Teacher Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Elle Thomas, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 472 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The state of public education is under scrutiny once again as another major teachers strike gets underway.\par \par \uc2 Seattle`s first day of school is canceled with teachers hitting the picket line today. They want more pay. They also want better conditions for their students.\par \par \uc2 Elle Thomas of our Seattle station, KIRO has more on this story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I`m kind of sad because I was excited for my first day of school.\par \par \uc2 I understand why they`re doing it. I mean, teachers don`t get paid that much.\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: Better pay, smaller class sizes, and mental health resources are all issues on the bargaining table for Seattle public school teachers.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MATTER (Seattle Teachers Union President): SPS has given us no choice.\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: Jennifer Matter is the Union President.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MATTER: We can`t continue that same status quo. It`s unsustainable. It`s leading to educator burnout, where students` needs are not being met.\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: It`s a sentiment shared nationwide. A recent survey of public school teachers found that 90 percent said burnout is a serious problem. And there are at least 280,000 fewer public school teachers working now than before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 LIZ RUIZ-PUYANA (Seattle Public Schools consulting teacher): This is unprecedented, the level of trauma that we`re seeing in our populations, and just the amount of work that we have to do now after coming back from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 BRENT JONES (Seattle Public Schools Superintendent): We take this moment seriously.\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: The District Superintendent said negotiators are working hard to come to a resolution.\par \par \uc2 BRENT JONES: Seeking agreement should not result in extra stress for our students, families, staff, and community.\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: Teachers agree, but argue the strike is a necessary step toward putting the community first.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 SARA WILLIAMS (Seattle Public School teacher): We understand the impact that that has on families. I wouldn`t be here if what the district was proposing was not unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: The district did propose percentage-based wage hikes for teachers who called those increases significantly smaller than their demands.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson said the district was disappointed with the decision to strike. It added that it was still committed to negotiating on a new contract -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Elle Thomas, great reporting. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220908ei970002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 California Power Emergency; Deadly California Wildfires; Nuclear Documents at Mar-a-Lago; Russia`s War on Energy; \uc2 Juul\uc2  Reaches $40 Million Settlement; Seattle Teacher Strike; New Omicron Boosters; Online Mental Health Concerns; Memphis Jogging Murder; Celebrating One Year Together; Investing in Change; Targeting Change; Vogue World; Jon Pardi Live, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Carter Evans,  Catherine Herridge, Debora Patta, Elle Thomas, Nate Burleson, Celine  Gounder, Anna Werner, Elise Preston, Jamie Yuccas \par \uc2 13,994 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS. It`s a special day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know why it`s a special day? Because it`s our one-year anniversary at CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hurray. Nate Burleson is standing by. He is going to join us. Hey Nate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s up, Nate?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But first, let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 California on the brink. Triple-digit heat leads to a warning about blackouts as wildfires rage all over the State.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The fire comes out. It just gusted and knocked me a hundred feet. I am a 200-pound man, just knocked me down.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: According to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," a document discussing the nuclear capabilities and military defense of a foreign government was found in the search of former President Donald Trump`s home.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It really raises serious, serious concerns.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A brand new COVID booster is on the way to pharmacies, what we know about the latest shot.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Another teachers` strike this time in Seattle. They`re demanding better conditions for learning.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I`m kind of sad because I was excited for my first day of school.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: E-cigarette maker, \uc2 Juul\uc2 , will pay nearly $440 million to dozens of States after a two-year probe.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A tantrum on the court, Australian, Nick Kyrgios was not happy that he lost. He took out his frustration on two of his own rackets.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): A Federal Judge has granted Donald Trump`s request for a Special Master.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): I`m not going to lie when I first heard it, it sounded pretty cool. It said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is getting a Special Master. I was like, he is about to learn kung-fun?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Biden went to Wisconsin to deliver his anti-MAGA warning, and he noticed a heckler in the audience.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN (President of the United States): Hey, look. Everybody is entitled to be an idiot. No, no.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Let him go. Everyone`s entitled to be an idiot. Little ding dong damned dingler. Ray of chicken fine, gingersnap. Real, no Taffy glue, excuse my French. Never -- it is entitled to meet me behind the bleachers after this speech so I can introduce him to my two friends. No joke. And I mean it, folks.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have to say that is a pretty good line. "Everyone is entitled to be an idiot."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And one year of CBS MORNINGS means one year of "Eye Openers" with Stephen Colbert as the -- and we`re going to be on tonight.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. We are going to Stephen tonight. We`re hoping it`s not going to be "Your idiots here."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Never.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I say. Never. Never.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am free to make a fool of myself tonight.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a great show coming up for you. Nate is going to be joining us later from home, actually, throughout the show and I want to say a couple of weeks ago, we got to go to Nate`s House to celebrate our one-year anniversary.\par \par \uc2 There we are in the big old kitchen. That is not New York City folks. We had to throw some food on the grill. We had a great conversation, talked about what makes CBS MORNINGS so special for us, but also for everyone out there that matters -- our viewers.\par \par \uc2 It`d be just me in front of the bathroom mirror about you guys, so we will be sharing all --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nothing wrong with that either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s what it was before I got this job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we`re glad you got a job and that you`re here with us.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of great tastes, how about this? Anna Wintour -- there she is. She is going to join us to mark the 130 years of "Vogue." They`ve got an anniversary, too. So, they`re not letting it pass without a party. We`re going to talk about that, Fashion Week, and Anna Wintour`s signature look. She`s got one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She sure does.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ve also got live music in the studio today, courtesy of country star, Jon Pardi. He`s got a new hit single to share and we are sure you are going to enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jon Pardi has great energy, even at five o`clock in the morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Even at this hour.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He can sing. It`s really nice. But we`re going to begin with what could be -- could be a crisis in the making in California, where scorching heat and scorching is a word. It is pushing the state`s power grid all the way to the limits.\par \par \uc2 Record temperatures mean record demand for electricity. No surprise there. And that has forced officials to issue an alert. Blackouts could be on the way.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from OC Hawk.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, this all comes as more than a dozen large scale wildfires -- oh no continue to burn across the state. At least four people have been killed there.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans is at a power receiving station in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Carter, good morning to you. I hope you`ve got a little bit of good news this morning.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Well, the good news is it is kind of cool right now and this is the best part of the morning, and for the morning. But temperatures started soaring last week. They`re expected to continue through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 That`s putting a strain on power equipment like this and just pretty much everyone in the State.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: With temperatures soaring across California, schools without air conditioning in one northern city are canceling afternoon classes for the rest of the week.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The classrooms are seeing about 45 degrees.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The need to conserve electricity is vital says California Governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSON (D-CA): We`re heading into the worst part of this heatwave and the risk for outages is real and it is immediate.\par \par \uc2 At one point, Tuesday, the State`s power grid was pushed to the limit, with demand soaring to a record of more than 52,000 megawatts. California`s maximum is 56,000 megawatts.\par \par \uc2 According to an expert we spoke with, California just doesn`t have enough alternative energy like solar or wind resources to cover the increased demand and the State`s drought is impacting the power supply as well.\par \par \uc2 FRANK WOLAK (\uc2 Stanford University\uc2  Program on Energy and Sustainable Development Director): The reservoirs being as low as they are, you know, hit historical lows means that you just can`t run as much water through the dams as you would like to.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from OC Hawk.)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: With no letup in the heat, wildfires are causing death and destruction across the State with four dead and thousands of structures threatened. Officials confirmed the two people who died near Hemet, Monday, were attempting to escape the flames.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD CORDOVA (\uc2 Riverside County\uc2  Fire Captain): People think they have time to escape, but the way we see fires burn at the critical rates of spread, they don`t have time to escape anymore.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: \uc2 Riverside County\uc2  Fire Captain, Richard Cordova says wildfires are spreading faster than ever fueled by heat, low moisture levels and thousands of acres of dry brush.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD CORDOVA: One of the issues we`re also dealing with is the steep terrain and a rocky terrain also where we can`t get our bulldozers. Now, we`ve got to get crews in there with hand crews and do it all by hand.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And with this heat continuing, California faces a double threat for days to come -- wildfire and power outages. Energy officials say there were some power outages last night, but none of those so called rolling blackouts, yet -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yet, that is the key word. Carter, thank you very much. Appreciate it. We`ll keep following it.\par \par \uc2 Moving on for now, though.\par \par \uc2 We are getting some new details this morning about what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  may have found at Mar-a-Lago. A file discovered at the country club home of the former President, that would be President Trump had information about the nuclear capabilities of a foreign government. That according to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Senior investigative correspondent, Catherine Herridge is in Washington for us with more.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, it is an uncomfortable new detail. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Well, good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 National security and nuclear experts told CBS News if the Intelligence was compromised, it has the potential to damage collection methods, even burn sources.\par \par \uc2 The analysts who do not have firsthand knowledge of the Mar-a-Lago record said, Intelligence describing a foreign government`s military defenses, including its nuclear capabilities can reveal fragile collection methods such as human sources, electronic surveillance, and spy satellite technology.\par \par \uc2 In the Intelligence world, this highly classified information is called Compartmented Intelligence, because the distribution is limited access, highly restricted, and on a need-to-know basis, such as the President, a limited number of Cabinet Secretaries and those directly running an operation.\par \par \uc2 The experts said it will be important to know if the Military Defense Intelligence is about an adversary or a friendly nation because gathering on our partners is done, but considered extremely sensitive.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " cites people familiar with the matter which CBS News has been unable to independently verify. The former President`s spokesperson accused "The Post" of coordinating with the Biden administration in "never-ending leaks" -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Catherine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine, the UN`s nuclear agency says a safety zone must immediately be set up at a nuclear power plant to prevent a disaster.\par \par \uc2 Inspectors who visited the plant say its security is being undermined by constant shelling and Russian military equipment on the site. The plant remains cut off from Ukraine`s electric grid. Large areas of Ukraine have been left without any power sources amid all the fighting.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta is in Irpin, Ukraine, near Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Debora, it`s a disturbing development. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Indeed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This was one of the worst-hit places by Russian forces. Just look at these buildings. These are people`s homes, and despite the devastation, many residents actually still live here as they have nowhere else to go.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Winter is coming, an ominous icy threat.\par \par \uc2 (LARISSA DUBROVA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "I`m terrified," says Larissa Dubrova (sp?). She remembers the bone-chilling cold of February when Russia declared war on Ukrainian civilians aiming their missiles on her neighborhood, Irpin.\par \par \uc2 (LARISSA DUBROVA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "After that, my life was divided into before and after," she recalls. Trapped inside her home as Russian forces seized Irpin, with temperatures plummeting almost below zero.\par \par \uc2 (LARISSA DUBROVA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "It was a kind of freezing that blinds you. You can`t even think. Even my flowers were frozen."\par \par \uc2 In towns like this across the country, volunteers are helping repair shattered buildings so that people who have nowhere else to live can at least go home for winter.\par \par \uc2 You still don`t have gas? You have no heating and you can`t cook? What are you going to do?\par \par \uc2 (LARISSA DUBROVA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "I don`t know," she says, gulping back sobs. It`s a fear shared by Europe, where an energy crisis could lead to rolling blackouts, shuttered factories, and a deep recession.\par \par \uc2 Russia has choked off supplies from the Nord Stream 1 pipeline that supplied 40 percent of this continent`s gas. Cynically, the Kremlin says it had no choice, blaming sanctions as global prices soar.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine is worried that its energy blackmail, aimed at dividing its Western allies and their support of them against Russia`s invasion.\par \par \uc2 Gas is now a weapon.\par \par \uc2 (LARISSA DUBROVA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "It seems so. I hope Western leaders are wise enough to make the right decision," she says, "Because we are all hostages of this war."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Dubrova empathizes with the West facing rising energy bills, but points out that compared to Ukraine, it could be a lot worse -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Debora Patta for us once again in Ukraine. Debora, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The e-cigarette company, \uc2 Juul\uc2 , has agreed to spend nearly $440 million to settle accusations that intentionally targeted teams. Those in a two-year investigation by 33 states and Puerto Rico. Again, the allegation that the company intentionally targeted teenage users.\par \par \uc2 The tentative deal now restricts how and where \uc2 Juul\uc2  can market its vaping products. We should point out that \uc2 Juul\uc2  does not acknowledge any wrongdoing in the deal.\par \par \uc2 And in a statement, the company said it remains focused on ". transitioning adult smokers away from cigarettes and combating underage use."\par \par \uc2 In addition to this settlement, \uc2 Juul\uc2  still faces lawsuits from nine other States and many individuals along with a possible \uc2 FDA\uc2  ban on its products.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s the latest blow against the company that started as a graduate school project of two smokers, who having met them, I truly believe were trying to create a safer cigarette. Five, six years later, we`re at a situation where the potential public health benefits are completely overshadowed by these very clear public health detriments and that`s a tragedy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still remember that interview you did with the CEO at the time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of us were like, "Huh?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, the one that stays with me though is with \uc2 James Monsees\uc2 , one of the co-founders.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, what their intentions were as young people who smoked and wanted a safer alternative for themselves, and what has become of the company that they founded is quite a story. And I think I`m told still is what the potential benefit could be to the half a million people a year who die of smoking-related disease.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get your point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The state of public education is under scrutiny once again as another major teachers strike gets underway.\par \par \uc2 Seattle`s first day of school is canceled with teachers hitting the picket line today. They want more pay. They also want better conditions for their students.\par \par \uc2 Elle Thomas of our Seattle station, KIRO has more on this story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I`m kind of sad because I was excited for my first day of school.\par \par \uc2 I understand why they`re doing it. I mean, teachers don`t get paid that much.\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: Better pay, smaller class sizes, and mental health resources are all issues on the bargaining table for Seattle public school teachers.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MATTER (Seattle Teachers Union President): SPS has given us no choice.\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: Jennifer Matter is the Union President.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MATTER: We can`t continue that same status quo. It`s unsustainable. It`s leading to educator burnout, where students` needs are not being met.\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: It`s a sentiment shared nationwide. A recent survey of public school teachers found that 90 percent said burnout is a serious problem. And there are at least 280,000 fewer public school teachers working now than before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 LIZ RUIZ-PUYANA (Seattle Public Schools consulting teacher): This is unprecedented, the level of trauma that we`re seeing in our populations, and just the amount of work that we have to do now after coming back from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 BRENT JONES (Seattle Public Schools Superintendent): We take this moment seriously.\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: The District Superintendent said negotiators are working hard to come to a resolution.\par \par \uc2 BRENT JONES: Seeking agreement should not result in extra stress for our students, families, staff, and community.\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: Teachers agree, but argue the strike is a necessary step toward putting the community first.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 SARA WILLIAMS (Seattle Public School teacher): We understand the impact that that has on families. I wouldn`t be here if what the district was proposing was not unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELLE THOMAS: The district did propose percentage-based wage hikes for teachers who called those increases significantly smaller than their demands.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson said the district was disappointed with the decision to strike. It added that it was still committed to negotiating on a new contract -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Elle Thomas, great reporting. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 There is a new COVID booster headed to pharmacies around the country. It is designed to fight the omicron variant responsible for the most COVID cases in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Here to break it down. Dr. Celine Gounder, editor-at-large for Kaiser Health News and a CBS News contributor. Dr. Gounder, good morning. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Nate is back with us because he has some questions for you, Dr. Gounder.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I do, Dr. Gounder. It is good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Now for people whose last COVID shot was a while ago, maybe even last year, is this the booster update that they had been waiting for?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Big picture. If you are 12 and up and you have received your primary series, you might have even been boosted. If you`re 12 and up, you`re eligible for an updated omicron vaccine.\par \par \uc2 I would make that advice a little bit more nuanced for people who want a slightly more tailored approach. So if you are 50 and up, if you are immunocompromised, you should get that omicron booster as soon as possible. If you`re under 50, if you`re healthier, the real benefit of getting that omicron booster is not so much protecting yourself or keeping yourself out of the hospital, but rather to protect other people.\par \par \uc2 And so if you are planning to see perhaps older family members, other people with medical conditions over the holidays, you might want to delay getting that booster until two to four weeks prior to the holidays. But again, everybody 12 and up is eligible for an omicron booster right now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so speaking of delaying getting the booster. I recently had COVID. I`m okay now, for everybody who is wondering, but I`m vaccinated, I`m boosted. So, should I wait and how long should I wait if I`m going to get the omicron booster? Or should I get it at all?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: The way the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and the CDC have recommended have authorized the omicron booster, they are saying two months delay between either a vaccination or an infection. But if you actually look at the immunological basic science studies, it`s really three months or more.\par \par \uc2 So, the advice I would be giving my patients in the hospital is wait three months from your last case of COVID, your last infection or three months from your last COVID vaccination before getting the omicron booster. You want that time so that the booster actually takes and works for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Should you stick with the same brand? Like if you`ve been getting \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  all the time, should you switch to \uc2 Moderna\uc2  to get this booster and vice versa?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It really doesn`t matter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It doesn`t matter.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Just think of these as interchangeable. They`re both mRNA vaccines for COVID. They work very similarly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, and can you get the flu and COVID booster at the same time because it`s flu season. I`m debating, should I get the flu shot, too?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: You can. You can get both at the same time. You might be more likely to have a fever, chills. You can take some Tylenol to deal with those side effects. But if you can wait a little bit to get that flu shot, it may be better to wait until October or so because flu season doesn`t hit it a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. All right. Good advice. Always good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 And of course, we`ll check back with Nate, in case you`re wondering, somebody said, "What`s the deal with Nate in the van? Is that a hostage situation?" No, as he said, he has corona, but he is really fine. Really, really fine. But we have rules you can`t come back after X amount of days until you test.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We can`t. We won`t let him out of the van until he pays up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Believe me, we wanted him here today.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the case of a Memphis kindergarten teacher who was kidnapped while jogging takes a terrible, terrible turn. We`ve been following the story. We`re going to have the latest on the suspect who is now charged with her murder.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18, time you check with your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, a CBS News investigation raises more questions about a popular mental health app. A woman tells Anna Werner she nearly died after she says the company mishandled her case.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, you may have heard, we have a big celebration for our one-year anniversary. Ahead, join us as Gayle, Tony, Vlad, and I -- we share a meal together at my house.\par \par \uc2 We talk about some of our favorite moments from our first year.\par \par \uc2 Plus, "Vogue" legend Anna Wintour tells Gayle about a special anniversary issue and shares behind the scenes details about collaborating with Serena Williams.\par \par \uc2 And country music star, Jon Pardi has a special performance you don`t want to miss.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are back here on CBS MORNINGS at 7:30 with some new research out today showing a big increase in the percentage of Americans receiving mental health treatment during the pandemic, which actually I think is good news.\par \par \uc2 The CDC study shows that more than 23 percent of adults 18 to 44 years old received treatment last year and that is up from 18.5 percent in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic also saw an explosion of mental health apps and online services, which people find convenient, but there can be drawbacks.\par \par \uc2 Our consumer investigative correspondent, Anna Werner has been watching the industry closely and has more of her investigation into one of those companies.\par \par \uc2 Anna, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Yes, earlier this year we told you about Cerebral. It is a company that offers affordable prescriptions and therapy for conditions ranging from depression to bipolar disorder.\par \par \uc2 Since then, we`ve heard from more patients and employees who are concerned about the way Cerebral diagnoses and prescribes medications, including one woman who says Cerebral mishandled her case with nearly fatal consequences.\par \par \uc2 A warning: The story discusses suicide.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: The woman we will call Yvette says she has long suffered severe depression, a result of sexual trauma in her youth.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE (Patient): I was constantly depressed. It was just like this sadness, a sorrow that would just take over.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: She says she struggled to afford therapy, but then she saw ads like these.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My poor mental health was pushing everyone in my life away until I started using Cerebral.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: The ads from mental health provider, Cerebral, promised affordable prescriptions and therapy all online.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE: I thought I was honestly signing up for something that was going to change my life in a good way.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So, she joined. First going through an assessment like this one. Then the system matched her with a prescriber.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE: And the first time I talked to the prescriber, it`s only about 10 to 15 minutes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Yvette says in that first roughly 15 minute appointment, she was prescribed three medications, and in a second appointment equally brief, she was given two more. Five drugs in all.\par \par \uc2 But she says, her symptoms got worse, and she contacted her Cerebral prescriber to tell her she was having nightmares about hanging herself.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE: And I was scared, and when I reached out to her and I -- and I was just crying to her because I didn`t know what to do. She said, "I don`t have enough time. I have to go to my other appointment. I`ve already spent enough time with you."\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: The next day, a family member found her hanging from a dog leash in her bathroom.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE: I thought I was dreaming. I thought I was dreaming but I was hanging and, I didn`t remember anything.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Would you as a psychiatrist, be comfortable diagnosing someone in 15 minutes and prescribing medications?\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY SULKOWICZ (Clinical Professor of Psychiatry at \uc2 NYU\uc2  and President of the \uc2 American Psychoanalytic Association\uc2 ): Generally no.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Because?\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY SULKOWICZ: Because it takes longer than 15 minutes to get to understand someone`s life story. Diagnoses have to be made in context.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Dr. Kerry Sulkowicz is a Clinical Professor of Psychiatry at \uc2 NYU\uc2  and President of the \uc2 American Psychoanalytic Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY SULKOWICZ: Psychological diagnoses are almost by definition far more complex and it requires obtaining both a medical and a psychological and a social history. Just having that kind of conversation takes more than 15 minutes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: But the company`s CEO, Dr. David Mou maintain Cerebral`s overall outcomes are positive as he told us in June.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID MOU (Cerebral CEO): I would hope that -- so I refer my close friends, my family members to use Cerebral, and I would say that it`s important to also see the other side of the story, which is the vast majority of our cases where we are changing lives. We`re reducing suicidal thinking.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Yet, a CBS News review of Cerebral`s more than 1,500 prescribers listed on its website found only five are board certified psychiatrists. Most of the rest, some three-quarters of Cerebral`s prescribers are nurse practitioners from specialties outside of mental health.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY SULKOWICZ: I find that rather alarming. Diagnosing a mental illness is a complicated endeavor. There`s a reason why it takes years of training.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Not only that, this internal log obtained by CBS News shows Cerebral`s own staffers flagged at least 280 instances where one patient was prescribed five-plus medications.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY SULKOWICZ: These are serious medications that we`re talking about. These things are not to be prescribed or taken lightly.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: After she attempted to take her life, Yvette was hospitalized and so she underwent a lengthy evaluation with a new doctor who changed her medications.\par \par \uc2 Did your psychiatrist now tell you that you -- when you were with Cerebral, that you were on the wrong medications?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE: Oh, yes. She -- whenever she got the list of meds that I was on, she`s like, "I don`t understand why they would put you on this."\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: But now she says, she is feeling better.\par \par \uc2 You know, other people won`t see your face, but I can see the relief in your face. I could see it.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: And I can -- and the smile.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE: Yes. Even though things happened the way that they did, I`m grateful to get the help that I am getting now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Well, Cerebral declined to answer our specific questions about Yvette`s case.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the company said that its clinical standards meet and exceed those of many conventional brick and mortar facilities and that a highly qualified credentialing team oversees its hiring and professional development of patient-facing clinicians.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was a really nice interview on a very delicate story, and I`m struck by the data that you were able to produce. They`ve got 1,500 names listed on their website, but only five percent of them are actually board certified psychiatrists and 75 percent are nurse practitioners with other specialties. That`s concerning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Outside of mental health.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, outside of mental health. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Right. And the company would say to you look, you know, if these people have questions, they can ask those questions of the psychiatrists. You know, we have a Slack channel. If there are any issues that come up, I think that Dr. Sulkowicz`s point is, you know, it`s great to have access to these online mental health services. There`s such a need in this country right now.\par \par \uc2 But the devil is in the details that is a question of how you handle it, and I think these are some of the questions that are coming up. What we`re hearing from some of these patients, and some of these employees and former employees is that they have concerns about the way some of these individual cases are playing out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is scary to me that she tried to take her own life, apparently didn`t realize she was doing it, but she was on many medications. How do we know that Cerebral might have played a part? There were many things there that she was taking.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: There is a question there of what drugs she was on and whether they were the appropriate medications. Clearly, her new doctor, according to her believes they were not the correct medication.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The overall mission of making mental healthcare more affordable, more accessible, more convenient is a good one. But it does have to be done right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And it is not even five percent, it is five board certified psychiatrists.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Correct, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 If you or someone you know is experiencing a mental health crisis, please do call the National Suicide Prevention Lifeline. They are always standing by. You can reach them at 9-8-8. Again, that is 9-8-8.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, an abduction in Memphis becomes a murder case. The latest on the death of a mother of two and the suspect who already served 20 years for another kidnapping.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The suspect in the kidnapping and killing of a Memphis kindergarten teacher is due to return to Court today to face more charges including first-degree murder.\par \par \uc2 Memphis Police say that they found Eliza Fletcher`s body four days after she was abducted during an early morning jog. Some critics say the suspect, a convicted kidnapper should have still been in prison. There are a lot of questions here.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston has the latest on this investigation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Police discovered the body of Eliza Fletcher on Monday in a grassy lot behind a vacant duplex roughly seven miles from where the kindergarten teacher and mother of two was last seen.\par \par \uc2 According to an affidavit, tire tracks and an odor of decay led officers to the gruesome discovery, also recovered a trash bag containing purple shorts like the ones Fletcher was last seen wearing.\par \par \uc2 CJ DAVIS (Memphis Police Department Chief): While the outcome of this investigation is not what we hoped for. We are nonetheless pleased to remove this dangerous predator off the streets of Memphis.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You are charged with especially aggravated kidnapping.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Investigators say 38-year-old Cleotha Abston violently forced Fletcher into his SUV while she was out jogging at about 4:00 AM last Friday.\par \par \uc2 Fletcher`s cell phone was found nearby along with a pair of sandals that had traces of the suspect`s DNA.\par \par \uc2 Abston previously kidnapped a prominent Memphis attorney when he was 16 years old. Abston served nearly 20 years in prison for that crime, but had been sentenced to 24.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Caswell is the Shelby County Commissioner.\par \par \uc2 You have fought against recidivism here in Memphis for a very long time. Did the system fail in this situation?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE CASWELL (Shelby County Commissioner): I don`t know all the details on it, but it looked that way. Then, it must have.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: But Shelby County District Attorney, Steve Mulroy noted Abston served 85 percent of his sentence.\par \par \uc2 STEVE MULROY (Shelby County District Attorney): I think it`s easy to take one particular case and try to extrapolate from that. I think what we just need to do right now is just focus on going forward and making sure that justice is done in this particular case.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, the District Attorney tells us he has no reason to believe Fletcher was targeted, calling this an isolated attack by a stranger. Abston has yet to enter a plea.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Fletcher`s family called her a joy to so many, saying they are heartbroken and devastated by this senseless loss -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senseless is the word for it, Elise. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was a bottomless tragedy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We didn`t know this woman and I feel heartbroken because it`s so random, that apparently she jogged all the time and that he didn`t know her. She didn`t know him.\par \par \uc2 And to hear that details about somebody that clearly should not have been out on the streets just makes you angry in addition to all the sadness.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She leaves behind a family, two little ones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and a husband. It is awful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very sad.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, Vlad Duthiers will have the stories you`ll be talking about today, but first it is 7:44, almost 7:45. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know you do. Favorite song, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are walking on sunshine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Walking on sunshine. I like that song.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Walking on sunshine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Walking on sunshine. Yellow. I mean, it`s just -- they asked me the other day, "What song do you want to play when you come on?" I said "Walking on Sunshine."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because it`s a happy song. It`s yellow. It just makes you smile.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are feeling good. We are feeling good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, you also make us smile.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy anniversary.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy anniversary.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Happy anniversary to us. It`s been a wonderful, incredible year.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a little bit of "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We start with some sports first. Emotions running high at the US Open last night. Sadly, for Coco Gauff fans. Now, that`s you, Gayle. That`s all of us. We are all Coco Gauff fans.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I was bummed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She lost her quarterfinal match to France`s Caroline Garcia in front of a sold-out crowd at Arthur Ashe Stadium. Garcia ended Gauff`s scopes of her first Grand Slam singles title just days after the 18-year-old`s idol, Serena Williams was also knocked out of the tournament.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Garcia is exciting. She is an exciting player.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When she wins, she leaps.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I cheer all the players, but yes, I`m especially sad that, you know -- but guys, she has got a career ahead of her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I cheer all the players when they`re not playing Coco and Serena. Then I cheer for them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Then you`re cheering for them. That`s lovely.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the men`s quarterfinals. Nick Kyrgios lost in five sets to Russian, Karen Khachanov. Did I say that right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He doesn`t care. He won.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Khachanov.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Call him the winner.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just one day after beating world number one, Daniil Medvedev. The 27-year-old Wimbledon finalist took out his frustrations on a pair of rackets though, after the match.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Oh. And you k