NG: Oh, you`re thinking Natalie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Natalie Tonina.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tonina.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we`ve got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`re going to talk with the CEO of one of America`s largest retailers, that would be Target`s, \uc2 Brian Cornell\uc2 . We`re going to talk about exclusive partnerships to increase representation.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:11. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This morning, we`re getting a look at how one of the nation`s biggest retailers, \uc2 Target\uc2 , has been navigating challenges around the pandemic and economic uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Chairman and CEO, \uc2 Brian Cornell\uc2  sat down with Jamie Yuccas to talk about company initiatives promoting equality and diversity. That includes a partnership with one Black entrepreneur, Houston White.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: In North Minneapolis, Houston White`s old barber shop has evolved into a place to grab coffee or buy clothes. But at its core, it`s a place for the community to come together.\par \par \uc2 HOUSTON WHITE (Fashion designer and entrepreneur): Black professionals specifically need a sense of connection and tribe in place, and there is nothing realer in the Black community than the barber shop. It`s that place that you just feel like you can breathe and be yourself.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: So, in early 2020 when \uc2 Brian Cornell\uc2 , CEO of Minneapolis based, \uc2 Target\uc2 , heard a group of Black employees wanted him to hear their thoughts --\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2  (Target CEO): I said, "Make it happen. Let`s pick a spot."\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: The spot was White`s shop, and it was there that the two men both with the determination to help other dreamers became friends.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2 : And he had this vision to bring energy and vitality to this area, and it wasn`t about him, it was what he could do for Minneapolis and for other entrepreneurs.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Three months after White and Cornell met, a Minneapolis Police Officer murdered George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2 : I remember gathering my Black officers right after George Floyd`s murder. Those team members had tears in their eyes, and they were telling me how it affected them, their families.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Since then, \uc2 Target\uc2  has promised to invest $2 billion in Black owned businesses.\par \par \uc2 It`s preppy and it`s cool.\par \par \uc2 One of those businesses --\par \par \uc2 HOUSTON WHITE: You know, I`m a preppy street kid.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: A new fashion line designed by a hometown barber with a passion for clothes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SANDO (Target Chief Merchandising Officer): I kind of want you to close your eyes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: We were there when Target`s Chief Merchandising Officer, \uc2 Jill Sando\uc2  showed Houston his display for the first time at a Target that was rebuilt after being ransacked by looters in 2020.\par \par \uc2 JILL SANDO\uc2 : The city has been through so much the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Are you hoping that this somewhat changes the story of what people think of Minneapolis?\par \par \uc2 HOUSTON WHITE: I think it will. We are in the Lake Street store. This is hope. This is joy. This is what`s possible. And this is how we`re going to bring this community and Minneapolis forward.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: This is the hustler of the future.\par \par \uc2 HOUSTON WHITE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: For CBS MORNINGS, Jamie Yuccas, Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Jamie. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Target Chairman and CEO \uc2 Brian Cornell\uc2  is with us now to discuss his diversity goals and how the company is responding to high inflation and consumer fears of recession.\par \par \uc2 Brian, always good to have you in the studio and bravo to you, bravo to Houston White number one, for bringing it to your attention. Bravo to you for sitting down and listening.\par \par \uc2 Many CEOs give it lip service, but you certainly seem to put your money where your mouth is. This was important to you. Why?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2 : It was. I`ve got to start with Happy Anniversary. You`re having a lot of fun this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2 : I want to have this much fun every morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We kind of do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we do. We do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Much to the grin of our control room.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We do.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2 : Well, it is great to be here to celebrate Houston White, and we are launching his collection as we speak across the country.\par \par \uc2 But it`s a great human connection story, Gayle, and I met him back in 2019. Houston had a barber shop in Minneapolis, and I actually had a meeting there with about 40 of our Black team members from Minneapolis, and I spent some time just talking to them about business, about \uc2 Target\uc2 , about life, about sports.\par \par \uc2 And then I had a chance to spend time with Houston afterwards and he wanted to show me some of the plans he had in place. He had opened up this barber shop. He had plans to open up a coffee shop there, developed some housing adjacent to his barber shop, and then he started to show me his design plans for apparel.\par \par \uc2 And what was really unique for me was, I saw someone who had a dream, a plan, but was focused on execution. And coming out of the pandemic, we wanted it to come to life and it`s just great to see someone like Houston kind of put together such amazing plans and partnered with a company like \uc2 Target\uc2  to bring his dreams to life.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know one of the things that you -- that Jamie mentioned in the piece and you said is you met with your Black employees, Black officers at Target in the wake of George Floyd`s murder. What was that like for you?\par \par \uc2 Because I think as Gayle pointed out, a lot of CEOs say that they want to increase diversity in their companies, but they very rarely ever interact with anybody who is not like them. Because we all sometimes live in our bubble. What was that like for you?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2 : Well, I`ll step back. So early on in 2019, I actually spent time with a group of my Black officers, we went to Atlanta together, and I`ve been doing these what I call immersion trips for five or six years now, bring eight or 10 leaders together, I travel with them for a day, introduce them to other CEOs, business leaders in a community, and we get to know each other.\par \par \uc2 And in January of 2019, I went to Atlanta with a group of Black officers, and when we finished up the session, they said, "Brian, there are team members in Minneapolis that would love this experience." I said, "Well, let`s set it up." And they said, "Would you go to this barber shop in Minneapolis, and we`ll meet with about 40 team members there."\par \par \uc2 So, it was Houston`s barber shop, and I had a chance to just spend time with my local Black team members, and just get to know them, understand some of the challenges they were facing, things that were on their mind, but just spend that time connecting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WHITE: And for me, it actually prepared me for George Floyd`s murder, because the first thing I did coming out of that was recognize I had to connect with my Black leaders, and I will always remember, Gayle, having a Zoom call right after George Floyd`s murder, and seeing a screen after screen after screen on the Zoom call of Black leaders with tears in their eyes, talking about the challenges they have been facing, and the tough conversations they had had with family members, their kids, it was a very painful conversation.\par \par \uc2 But they helped inspire all of us to say, we better stand up and do more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, there`s nothing like a black barbershop to go and hear the truth.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And speaking of hearing the truth. I want to turn attention to the economy. There`s a lot of fear of recession out there. You see indications of that, or you would anyway, as a retailer. People go to Target for low prices.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are you seeing indications that these fears of recession are not just fears, but we`re going to get one?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2 : I wish I could give you the answer, and I really can`t right now. And I think we`re sitting here in a very unique period of time.\par \par \uc2 I go back to the last time we were together, which was three years ago. And since then, we faced a global pandemic. The US economy was locked down.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2 : Many of us learned how to work from home and educate family from home. As we started to move through the pandemic, there was trillions of dollars of stimulus pumped into the economy and child care credits.\par \par \uc2 And then earlier this year, as we`re getting back to normal, starting to go out again, war in Europe, rising fuel prices, 40 years high for inflation. So right now, I think it`s really hard to gauge the consumer.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you how we`re approaching it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, you`ll get three years because Target just announced you`re going to be CEO for another three years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`ll go ahead and announce I`ll continue to be a customer for the next three years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CORNELL\uc2 : I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Brian Cornell\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got to Target stores in my neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. This is one of the most exciting times of the year for us because it`s Fashion Week here in New York City, and our next guest is one of the industry`s, you could say, most influential icon and icon is the word.\par \par \uc2 Anna Wintour has been the top editor of "Vogue" in the US for more than three decades. You go, Anna Wintour. She is also Chief Content Officer for "Conde Nast" and "Vogue`s" global editorial director.\par \par \uc2 "Vogue" Magazine is marking its 130th birthday this year, with a first of its kind event it`s called Vogue World.\par \par \uc2 Only on CBS MORNINGS, Anna Wintour is ready to tell us all about it.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Madam Wintour. It`s so good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR ("Vogue" editor): Hello, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to see you, Anna.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, we`re going to talk about Vogue World in just a second, I promise, but this cover -- this cover just took my breath away.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: Thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let us talk about Serena for just a second. Number one, I love her body. I love the photography. And what I know about magazine world that many people don`t know is that all this stuff is worked out at least three months ahead of time.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: This was way more than three months.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, I was wondering how long did it take for you --\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: Yes, you know, Serena and I had actually been talking about this story, and you know, when she was thinking about stepping away from tennis for well over a year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Over a year? Really?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: And you know, obviously, it`s a very difficult decision for somebody in her position with her incredible achievements to come to that decision, and we would talk about it.\par \par \uc2 And I felt that we didn`t really need to revisit her career because it`s so known, so recognized, and we felt it would be much better if it was in her own voice. She worked with one of our writers that she knows to really talk about how she came to that decision, how she wanted to have another child. You know how she had such an extraordinary business ventures in her future. But at the same time, what a tough decision it was for her to make.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it was clearly very tough, but when she came to you, did she come to you and say I`m thinking about it. How did that happen?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: She came to "Vogue," I think because we`ve worked together for so many years, I think she felt it was the right platform, the right place for her to tell her story.\par \par \uc2 And originally, we thought maybe it could be more of a straight profile, but the more we discussed it, the more it became clear to both of us that this should be something that was in her own voice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So let`s talk about Vogue World. It`s your 130th anniversary. How do you feel about anniversaries? Because Anna, it`s our year anniversary.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So we only have 129 more to keep up with you?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: Well, I always like to look at anniversaries as a celebration not only of the past, but also the future, and when we discuss what we could do, we were so anxious to celebrate New York Fashion Week and also the city itself. And we thought, "What can we do that will be meaningful to our audiences and to the city as a whole?"\par \par \uc2 So we came together and we --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you come up with?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: We decided to do a big street fair meets fashion show, over 200 people on the runway. Five hundred, six hundred people in the audience, a live stream.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bardia Zenali/Vogue.)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: I think this will be a real symbol about how New York Fashion is back, how New York City is back. And anything that we at "Vogue" and "Conde Nast" can do to support that is so important to us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am curious, too, Anna about you. In terms of what you`ve done at "Vogue," it`s 34 years and I`m not talking about the R-word of retirement. I`m talking about the R-word of rumors.\par \par \uc2 I said I`ve been hearing about Anna leaving "Vogue" for five years, and she`s not leaving. And I`m thinking why would you leave when you still clearly seem to be enjoying it? Do you see a life for yourself after Vogue or that`s not even something you even entertain?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: I am very much focused on the present, whether it`s Vogue World or what we will be announcing shortly for the next Met Gala or what the next cover is going to be, what we can do globally for "Vogue" and "Conde Nast" around the world because I have such an amazing platform, Gayle, and it`s so important to me that I use that platform to help as many people as I possibly can.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you aware, Anna, of the impact that you have when you walk in a room? I always say, I know people change their clothes, myself included.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: You look great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I was trying to say, I don`t like this up so I had tucked in like this.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: That`s a great color for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And hair and makeup said, "No, no, no. That looks ridiculous. That looks bad." And I said, "But Anna is coming."\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: The necklace, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thanks. But when you walk in a room, are you clocking what people are wearing? Because people so hold on to your word, hold on to your thought. Are you clocking people yourself or not really?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: Not at all. I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not at all, really?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: I like individual style. I like it when people dress slight themselves and surprising or obviously having fun with fashion, but mostly I`m interested in them and what they have to say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before you go, I`m always fascinated -- when I was looking at tennis opening night. There you were in Serena`s box, and my eyesight isn`t that good from that far away, but I can see your signature bob and your sunglasses.\par \par \uc2 When you started that look, did you intend for it to become a trademark or it just did?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: No, it just happened and, you know, I`m -- someone asked me about my style the other day and I said, "Well, it`s really safe verging on boring." And I think it would be hard for me to change at this point, but also the glasses are very useful at times.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, God, yes. Amen to that.\par \par \uc2 Anna Wintour, we thank you. I know you probably can`t pick a favorite cover, but this might be mine.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: Well, certainly one of my favorites.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One of your favorites.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: Thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Keep soaring.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WINTOUR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Gayle. Look who is in our greenroom? It is country star, Jon Pardi in our progressive greenroom.\par \par \uc2 The Times Square cowboy, but he`s got his clothes on.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI (Country music singer): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We appreciate that. No naked cowboy today. He`s going to have a special performance for us.\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk to him about his new hit album, "Mr. Saturday Night." Is that you? Are you Mr. Saturday Night?\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Well, what is it? Wednesday? Is it Tuesday? I don`t know what date it is. It is Saturday night. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not on Sunday morning, I guess. All right, but first, a time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, "Head Over Boots," one of my all-time favorite country songs. I`ve danced with my wife around the kitchen with that song more than once.\par \par \uc2 Country music star, Jon Pardi is behind that hit and he scored his fifth number one hit on the Country Charts with the song "Last Night Lonely," which he is going to perform here live in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 It`s the first single off of his new album. That album was called "Mr. Saturday Night" and the critics not so critical, rave reviews are rolling in.\par \par \uc2 Pardi is an award-winning artist. He has got nine top 10 country singles and was named Country Music`s Best New Artist back in 2017. He is currently on the road for his "Ain`t Always the Cowboy" tour and he joins us now, Jon.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Hey --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s up.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the Country Music Awards back in 2017, they named you Best New Artist, but it`s a what have you done for me lately kind of industry and I am happy to say that you have been nominated as of today, for --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Minutes ago --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Musical Event of the Year and Music Video of the Year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: And congrats to Midland and my boys. I love those guys. We had so much fun. That video if you watch it, you know we`re having fun, and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jon, you always seem to be having fun. You`re --\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: I`m having fun right now. I am having fun right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We feel the same about you.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: You guys are great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We feel the same about you.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: And I wouldn`t want to share this moment with anybody else.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a good way to butter ourselves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, keep talking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Talk to us about this new album, and the song , "Mr. Saturday Night." What`s the inspiration behind it?\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Well, "Mr. Saturday Night" is -- it`s a fun-going guy and he is out there, you know dealing with his emotions, but the best part about "Mr. Saturday Night," the song is -- the hook is, they don`t know how much I missed her Saturday night, which I thought was genius.\par \par \uc2 A friend, Benji (sp?) wrote that song and right after I got done with heartache medication in 2019, they send me "Mr. Saturday Night." And I was like, "Man, I just finished a record. I don`t want to put this on hold," and so I let it go back into the world and hopefully, you know, some other artist would cut and as we went to get our last song list for "Mr. Saturday Night," the album, my friend Nicolette texted me, she goes, "Hey, don`t forget about this song."\par \par \uc2 And then, I played it. I was like, I`ve always loved it, and it just kind of -- it`s one of those moments where it was that one song that you didn`t know you need and then it came right in and boom, and it was album title and everybody is loving it.\par \par \uc2 So, I always say that music works in mysterious ways.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: And it`s a fun mysterious, it`s like, it just shows up. It`s like, bam.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you know, it`s not so mysterious, "Last Night Lonely" already off the charts great. People really love it.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think that resonates? Because what`s that about? I find the lyric so relatable, writing a number on a napkin.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: No, it`s got a lot of good scenery and lyrics and images and it`s a great pickup line.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Last Night Lonely.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A pickup line. How?\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Because it could be last night lonely.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. I can see that. I can see that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take me, Jon. Take me, Jon. We should say Jon is already married. You got married during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: I am just thinking --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: I bet there is somebody out there, dropping that line somewhere.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, right, right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But you didn`t drop it when you met your wife.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: It wasn`t around yet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, but I love there`s a line in "Santa Cruz." I love this song. You said "wishing summer would never end." Summer is your wife`s name.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you talking about the season or were you talking about Summer?\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: That`s for the listener to read.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to say it`s about your wife and you proposed on the Ryman Auditorium, so you don`t do anything small.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: No --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You must have known she was going to say "Yes, Jon. Yes."\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Well, she didn`t want to get on stage. I had to drag her out. She`s very -- she likes to be in her space, and you know, doesn`t want to go be in center stage, but I wanted to propose her at something that is a part of my life and a part of our life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: And I wanted everybody to see it like, because this is my life and welcome -- welcome to the circus.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, our other co-host, Nate Burleson, he has got the `rona, but he`s fine.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Nate, I know you want to talk to Jon Pardi. You are missed today, of all days, Nate Burleson. Hello. Meet Jon Pardi.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know. I know. This isn`t a good day to miss. I had the `rona, past tense, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 But Jon, I need to confirm something before I ask you my question.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You were a lifeguard, right?\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: At one point, yes. That was a day job for me when I first moved to Nashville.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Certified.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Certified.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: But, I mainly worked on my tanning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Certified. So my question for you is, what is your advice to these young artists that are just trying to stay afloat in the music industry? Because sometimes they jump in and they drown.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Afloat. I got you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I see what you did there.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: That`s why you know -- I guess, if you get down, there`s always going to be time to the new artist, you get down, but you have to -- you have to imagine that we`ve all been through something. For everybody that`s on TV or on, doing better than you are has been in that one spot and you`ve got to get over that and you`ve got to just get back on, you know, to the grind and always look for something positive.\par \par \uc2 And I know it`s hard to do sometimes, but that`s -- you know, you might wake up the next night with a great song title and write a song, and then it changes your whole career.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Keep changing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to see what you write about CBS MORNINGS in your next lyric. Let`s see how you fit that in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are an inspiration. We`ve got to save time for him to play the entire "Last Night Lonely." Stay with us, Jon Pardi.\par \par \uc2 coming up, he is going to perform that number one single, "Last Night Lonely." We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know when something is so good, you just don`t want it to end. That`s how we feel about Jon Pardi. He is back with a special treat.\par \par \uc2 As promised, Jon Pardi is performing his number one hit, you`ve heard the single "Last Night --"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Lonely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is from his new album, "Mr. Saturday Night." We apologize, Jon for Vlad. Now, it is your turn.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And there are the guys to do it.\par \par \uc2 (Jon Pardi performs "Last Night Lonely.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes, yes. Yes. Awesome. That was awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Now, that`s how you start the day, Jon Pardi. That`s how you start the day.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Terry (sp?). Thank you, Kevin (sp?).\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Billy Max (sp?).\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I won`t hold it against you. Thank you, Matt. Thank you, Lee. That was great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s us doing the pantomime of the Lincoln sign. Best line in the song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy anniversary to us. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Hey, Happy Anniversary.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you for helping us celebrate. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you come back tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s make it a regular thing. You good with these hours?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And if you can`t --\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: . just hours, you know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can see in your eyes, you like getting up early.\par \par \uc2 JON PARDI: Yes, I love getting up early.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, we like having you here.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. Unfortunately, we`ve got to go. All good things come to an end.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220907ei97000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Schools Offering Things Like A Four-Day Workweek To Get Educators Back In The Classroom\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 988 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, we have some news tonight about teachers on strike and not in the classroom. We told you about Columbus, Ohio, the largest school district in that state. Well, kids there will return to the classroom on Monday after teachers reached an agreement with the county, breaking an end to the three-day strike.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of miles away, in Kent, Washington, a suburb of Seattle, teachers were on the picket line today, impacting 25,000 students. The teachers union said the fight is over class sizes, mental health staff and higher pay.\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest problems that unions and schools can agree on is the growing crisis of teacher shortages. School districts across the country are coming up with unique incentives, in hopes of retaining teachers and attracting new ones.\par \par \uc2 In Missouri, that includes a four-day workweek and hefty signing bonuses.\par \par \uc2 Here CBS` Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ (voice-over): Teachers nationwide cramming...\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Give them 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: ... ahead of back to school, but not Ali Veatch (sp?) from Columbia, Missouri. She left teaching in April.\par \par \uc2 You taught what grade?\par \par \uc2 ALI VEATCH (Former Teacher): Mainly ninth.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What was the mental age of the kids in your class?\par \par \uc2 ALI VEATCH: Most of them hadn`t had a normal year of school since sixth grade, so probably 11, 12, for some, not all. We were all kind of in survival mode. And I just couldn`t last any longer in that -- in that mental state.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Nearly 300,000 public school educators have left the profession since the start of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 In Fort Zumwalt, Missouri, Superintendent Bernard DuBray says that`s due to burnout, pay and Baby Boomers retiring.\par \par \uc2 Are you competing with other school districts for applicants?\par \par \uc2 BERNARD DUBRAY: Absolutely, we are, just selling your school district. Our teachers very often sing our praises.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Superintendent, are you selling your school right now to any potential teachers watching this interview?\par \par \uc2 BERNARD DUBRAY: Oh, yeah, always.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Missouri starts teachers at $25,000 a year. To help recruit, the state passed a temporary pay bump. It also increased pay for substitute teachers and lowered their requirements and allowed districts to have four- day school weeks.\par \par \uc2 GWEN IMHOFF (Teacher): The four-day school week was highly attractive to me.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: That was a deciding factor for Gwen Imhoff (sp?), who got six job offers. She`s also pregnant and has to find her own sub during a substitute teacher shortage.\par \par \uc2 GWEN IMHOFF: Not a lot of people to even call for a maternity leave sub.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: For Veatch to come back, she says the whole system would need an overhaul.\par \par \uc2 ALI VEATCH: If teachers are given the resources they need to meet the needs of their students, I would go back. If students were given what they need to be successful, I would go back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: In this job market, teachers are finding more options outside of the classroom, given the rise of remote work and low unemployment.\par \par \uc2 As for Ali Veatch, who we interviewed, she`s one of several teachers from her area that joined a company to help create their internal training materials -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: This feels like a national emergency.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, a major decision that will impact millions of drivers looking to buy a new car in California.\par \par \uc2 We have got that story in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There`s big news tonight for the more than 25 million drivers in California.\par \par \uc2 State regulators approved Governor Gavin Newsom`s plan to ban the sale of gas-powered vehicles by 2035. The governor is seeking to cut carbon emissions from cars in half by the year 2040. But the policy will still need federal approval.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a new study finds \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 `s COVID pill, Paxlovid, appears to provide little or no benefit for younger adults. Israeli researchers looked at more than 100,000 patients and found the drug significantly reduced hospitalizations and deaths among seniors, but younger adults didn`t show any measurable benefit.\par \par \uc2 Here in the U.S., Paxlovid is recommended only for people at high risk of getting severely ill from COVID.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, two people from Florida have pleaded guilty to stealing and selling the private diary of President Biden`s daughter Ashley. Biden`s daughter was moving from a friend`s home in 2020, and she left behind some personal items, including her diary. They were found by a woman who, along with a friend, sold them to the conservative activist group Project Veritas.\par \par \uc2 They have been ordered to forfeit the $40,000 they were paid, and they now face five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 All right, with the U.S. Open set to begin on Monday, Novak Djokovic announced today that he will not play in the tournament because he`s not vaccinated against COVID, and he`s not allowed to travel to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And, as Serena Williams gears up for what`s expected to be her final tournament, we learned today her first opponent will be Danka Kovinic. It will be Williams` 21st appearance at the U.S. Open.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next: Eye on America with a New Jersey man working to keep kids safe, as gun violence surges across America.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220826ei8p000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Thousands Of Columbus Teachers Are Out On Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Elise Preston \par \uc2 405 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Back here at home, 47,000 students in Ohio`s largest school district are remote-learning on their first day of school because their teachers are walking the picket line. Thousands of Columbus teachers are out on strike.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Whose kids?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Our kids!\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Tonight, as negotiations between the school board and teachers union continue, drivers in Columbus cheered on picketers striking for a third day.\par \par \uc2 (Car Horns Honking)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: What do we want?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: A contract!\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: When do we want it?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Now!\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Ohio`s largest school district is now online only, after the nearly 4,500-member teachers union rejected the school board`s latest offer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER ADAIR (Columbus City School Board President): Our children are our priority. And we will do what we need to do to ensure there`s a resolution that`s in their best interest.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The union wants smaller classes and says teachers are working in dilapidated schools, where water pipes leak, lead paint peels from walls, and air conditioning and heat are nonexistent.\par \par \uc2 REGINA FUENTES (CEA Spokeswoman): And we will continue fighting until we have safe, properly maintained, and fully resourced schools in every neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The walkout comes on the heels of a new report that found average weekly teachers` wages grew in the last 25 years by just $29, when adjusted for inflation.\par \par \uc2 FAITH JOBES (Mother): They need to do what needs to be done to get the kids back in school.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Today, frustrated parents say virtual learning is a waste of valuable time.\par \par \uc2 How are you all impacted?\par \par \uc2 FAITH JOBES: Basically, it`s as if there`s no education system at all right now.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Tiffany Cammon had to scramble for her new kindergartner.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CAMMON (Parent): It was really stressful for me. I was up all late last night trying to figure out what to do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, today marked the 23rd time both sides were at the bargaining table since this spring. And we`re told today`s session is lasting longer than expected.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the district says substitute teachers will continue to run remote classes until there`s a deal -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elise Preston, thank you very much. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220826ei8o0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Columbus Teachers` Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Elise Preston, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 886 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sticking with education, more students across the country are returning to school this week, and that is highlighting a growing crisis with teachers, there are simply not enough of them right now.\par \par \uc2 Government figures show that about 300,000 teachers left the education workforce, left the profession since February 2020. That would be when the COVID pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 And in states like Mississippi, Florida, and Nevada, they`ve been hit especially hard by these shortages.\par \par \uc2 Columbus, Ohio, meanwhile, the state`s biggest school district there is opening the year this morning with the teachers on strike. You see the pictures there.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston has made her way to East High School, that`s in Columbus.\par \par \uc2 Elise, good morning. Why is this happening there now?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Tony, good morning to you. The School Board and Teachers Union have been negotiating for months without success. This ongoing strike means no one will be in class here today. Students will begin the school year online with many taught by substitutes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Teachers in Columbus have been on strike since Monday.\par \par \uc2 (TEACHERS protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: After the vast majority of the union`s roughly 4,500 members voted to reject the latest contract offer from the city`s Board of Education.\par \par \uc2 (TEACHERS protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: It marked yet another failed negotiation after 22 meetings since March. The Union claims Columbus schools are in a state of disrepair from missing ceiling tiles to lead paint to water leaks.\par \par \uc2 REGINA FUENTES (Columbus Education Association spokesperson): We will continue fighting until we have safe, properly maintained, and fully resourced schools in every neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The School Board says it is committed to addressing those issues, but insists they can only be solved with more taxpayer dollars, not a contract with teachers.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What goes in the contract doesn`t actually solve that problem.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: As the unresolved negotiations force a virtual start to the school year, the Teachers Union is calling on families to honor the picket line by boycotting remote class.\par \par \uc2 ENIYAH BROOM (Columbia City Schools student): We are starting off how we did with COVID.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The conflict has put students like 16-year-old Eniyah Broom now your broom, a senior at East Moore Academy High School in a tough position.\par \par \uc2 She told us she`d rather be in person, but supports the strike.\par \par \uc2 ENIYAH BROOM: We started off school with no air, no heat. It`s embarrassing compared to these other school districts.\par \par \uc2 Our school had been the same for like 50 years.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: You`re laughing?\par \par \uc2 ENIYAH BROOM: Because my grandma was in school and she said the same thing like, "Why does it look exactly the same?"\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Her mother agrees, but worries about how the disruption and more remote class could take a toll on some students.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I stand by the teachers, but this being the beginning of the school year I think was just terrible for the kids.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: And you get here a lot of supporters honking for this ongoing strike. As it continues, the district has set up 25 running signs for students to pick up free meals. Meanwhile, the Teachers Union and School Board head back to the bargaining table this afternoon-- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let`s give the teachers what they want and what they need.\par \par \uc2 Elise, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, a Louisville police officer has been found criminally responsible in the killing of Breonna Taylor. Former detective Kelly Goodlett pleaded guilty to a charge a conspiracy yesterday in Federal Court.\par \par \uc2 She admitted conspiring with another officer to falsify the search warrant used in the raid that led to Taylor`s death. The 26-year-old EMT was shot in March of 2020 after police burst into the apartment where she was sleeping.\par \par \uc2 Goodlett resigned earlier this month after the Justice Department announced new charges against her and three other officers. The others are accused of Civil Rights violations, conspiracy, obstruction, and use of excessive force.\par \par \uc2 Goodlett will be sentenced in November and could face up to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Two men have been convicted for their role in conspiring to kidnap Michigan Governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The Court heard how Adam Fox and Barry Croft, Jr. plan to take the Governor from her vacation home and use explosive devices to slow the police response. They could face up to life in prison. Two others have already been convicted for the plot which was intended to start a Civil War. It all unraveled due to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  sting operation.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Governor Whitmer thanked prosecutors and said that the "... verdicts prove that violence and threats have no place in our politics, and those who seek to divide us will be held accountable."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220826ei8o0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Pivotal Primary Results; Historic Student Loan Action; Columbus Teachers` Strike; Charges in Killing of Breonna Taylor; Kidnapping Conspiracy Convictions; Six Months of War in Ukraine; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s Whistleblower; Serena and Meghan Get Candid; Reading for our Lives; Costly Ambulance Rides; Reaping what He Sews; Facing His Fears; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Jamie Yuccas, Scott MacFarlane,  Nancy Cordes, Elise Preston, Debora Patta, David Begnaud \par \uc2 13,760 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, August 24th. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And I`m Jamie Yuccas.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is off, but Nate is back and we`re here. Here`s today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight: Dramatic results in key Democratic primaries in New York. And in Florida, there is a new challenger for the Republican Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Florida is the State where woke goes to die.\par \par \uc2 MAN: President Biden is expected to make an announcement today on Federal loan relief.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It includes forgiving up to $10,000.00 in Federal student loans.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The US prepares billions more in aid to Ukraine, as the nation marks its Independence Day and six months of war.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET) (Coordinator for Strategic Communications at the National Security Council): The United States is going to continue to support Ukraine for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: A disaster is declared for 23 Texas counties following historic and deadly flooding.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): Needless to say, the effects of this storm has been dramatic.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s husband pleads guilty to a DUI charge. Paul Pelosi was sentenced to five days in jail.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Elton John is giving fans an earlier than expected treat.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He offered a sneak peek of his new track with Britney Spears --\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Elton John performance.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: To the Yankee game, a fan, is going to poke a hole through a hotdog and using it as a straw to drink his beer.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is amazing. This man is a legend.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I think we need to try this.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is a no for me, dog.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Tastes a lot like hotdog though.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s former head of security has now come forward as a whistleblower, claiming that the company has major security problems that pose a threat to user`s personal information, national security, and even democracy.\par \par \uc2 Who is shocked by this? No one should be. Whenever I download an app to my phone, I just immediately assume somebody somewhere is violating my privacy.\par \par \uc2 Am I right? Yes. Sure. \uc2 Instacart\uc2 . Have fun watching me in the shower.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, pretty much I think everyone agrees there. Honestly, if they were like download this app, we`ll watch you in the shower, but your burrito will get here to 15 minutes, people will be like yes, okay.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Okay, sure. I`ll sign up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No problem. Tell me where to sign.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`ll discuss that in a little bit here on the show.\par \par \uc2 But welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We are sharing some remarkable conversations today, including one between two icons. Serena Williams is the first guest on the new podcast hosted by Meghan -- \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2 , that is -- the Duchess of Sussex.\par \par \uc2 It`s Williams` first in-depth interview since announcing her retirement and they talk about how emotional that decision was, and also something they have in common, which is motherhood.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It`s a great conversation. I listened to the whole thing, couldn`t stop listening.\par \par \uc2 Plus, for our series, "How Are You Now?" David Begnaud checks in with an agoraphobic man he spoke to earlier this year. Back then, he had just decided to share his anxiety about being in public spaces.\par \par \uc2 So, how is he doing now? He has got an amazing story you are sure to love.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m looking forward to that, but we are going to begin with a big story in the world of politics.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news overnight, in some key primary races for the Democratic Party. We begin in Florida. The former Governor \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  won the chance to challenge the current GOP Governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Of course, DeSantis may become a 2024 presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 And in New York, two powerhouse Members of Congress were forced to face each other due to a quirk of redistricting. Now, one of them out of a job.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has more on all of it on a very big primary night.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2  (R) (Florida Gubernatorial candidate): We can make history and we will shock the world.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Democrats are putting their bets on \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , a man who served as a Republican Governor before switching sides a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2 : Governor DeSantis only cares about the White House, he doesn`t care about your house.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: They`re hoping he can take on Florida GOP Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who has been on the national stage taking on public school agendas and going up against \uc2 Disney\uc2 , leading up to a potential 2024 presidential run.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Florida will be the vanguard of freedom in this country.\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL): We have to get out and exercise our right to vote.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Meantime, a former Orlando Police Chief appears set for a fight for the Florida US Senate seat now occupied by Republican \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL): The President was --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Congresswoman Val Demings, who was part of the team leading the impeachment proceedings against former President Trump has won that Democratic nomination.\par \par \uc2 REP. JARED NADLER (D-NY): When it comes to unpacking our Supreme Court, I am going to stand up and fight.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In New York, two major institutions of Congress were forced to face off.\par \par \uc2 Judiciary Chair Jerry Nadler defeated Oversight Committee Chair \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2 , which the party says is a major loss of a powerhouse Democrat due to a newly redrawn congressional district.\par \par \uc2 And two hours north, in New York`s Hudson Valley, a special election there may set the roadmap for midterm strategies come fall.\par \par \uc2 Do you see outside interests coming in here trying to fire up the voting base?\par \par \uc2 MARC MOLINARO (R) (New York Congressional Candidate): Yes, we`ve seen it, without question, obviously, everyone has a degree of interest.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Democrat Pat Ryan won last night against Republican Marc Molinaro in the first competitive House election since the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe versus Wade setting the stage for what could be the last national test case on whether abortion will drive voters in November.\par \par \uc2 REP. PAT RYAN (D-NY): The foundations of our democracy were and remain under direct threat.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Because that was a special election, Ryan will only serve in the seat for a few months and have to run again in November.\par \par \uc2 With the primary season largely complete now, we have roughly two months until we find out if Democrats have momentum to preserve majorities in Congress, or if Republicans are poised for victory.\par \par \uc2 We are in this now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are in this now. Historically speaking, it would appear as though Democrats are poised for big losses based on everything you know about politics. However, there are different issues at play here.\par \par \uc2 What`s going to motivate voters in November?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Yes. Abortion rights, women`s reproductive health rights -- that`s going to be a pivotal issue, but the question is, will it be the pivotal issue for voters.\par \par \uc2 That race last night, Pat Ryan in New York`s 19th District, both candidates acknowledged Ryan ran on an abortion rights first political platform. Question is Nate, Jamie, and Tony, will that transcend the kitchen table, grocery price, gas price issue in every district across America?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And most people vote with their wallet. They`re not looking at the other issues a lot of the time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You know, when you look at the biggest takeaways from Florida and New York, I think it is interesting that we`re talking about party establishment. Not usually the biggest takeaway, but it seems like that`s what happened here.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: I think Democrats are waking up optimistic thinking there may be some wiggle room, some opportunity here if they nominate moderate mainstream, some experienced candidates against Trump loyalists in some districts across America.\par \par \uc2 The thing is, there are a lot of variables and not a lot of competitive races. Not a lot of space.\par \par \uc2 We`ll find out over the next 76 days.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will. Good to see in person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, good to have you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Likewise.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hudson Valley native.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to a historic announcement on student loans expected today. President Biden will likely announce a plan to forgive up to $10,000.00 in student loan debt for millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 Student loan payments will also be deferred until January.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes joins us from the White House. And Nancy, this is a little politically risky.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It is, Jamie. What it means in the short term is that tens of millions of Americans will see their student loan debt cut in half or eliminated altogether.\par \par \uc2 This is a move that some are going to hail as historic, others will slam as an inflation increaser and that is the reason it took this President so long to make it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Months of uncertainty are over. President Biden is expected to announce today that the government will forgive up to $10,000.00 in Federal student loan debt for Americans who make less than about $125,000.00 a year.\par \par \uc2 Americans like Dara Zucker.\par \par \uc2 DARA ZUCKER (Student loan borrower): I`d like to see the $10,000.00 go away.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Dara borrowed $40,000.00 to get her degree. She has been making payments since she graduated in 2016, but with all that interest, she`s barely made a dent.\par \par \uc2 DARA ZUCKER: I think it is predatory to go after kids who colleges and schools to go after kids who may not have ever had any understanding of how finances work.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The President has been feeling the political pinch from both sides of his own party, with some progressives pushing to wipe out student debt altogether.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Right now, student loan debt is holding back people who are trying to buy homes, it is holding back people who are trying to start small businesses. It is even holding back people who want to start families.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But some Democrats in battleground states and many Republicans warn of unintended consequences.\par \par \uc2 SEN. PATRICK LEAHY (D-VT): I worry that we say to colleges, charge whatever you want, we`re going to forgive the loan.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN THUNE (R-SD): Canceling student debt would also be grossly unfair to the Americans who worked hard for years to pay off their loans.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Today`s moves won`t apply to students who are about to take out loans.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BUCHANAN (Student Loan Servicing Alliance executive director): That`s the challenge here is that you potentially might spend $360 billion of taxpayer money. At the end of the day, you haven`t altered the trajectory of college costs for millions of American families that are going to be going to college in the future.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That is a tougher problem to solve, but progressives argue at least for now, this move helps to level the playing field.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is also expected to announce today he is extending the pause on Federal student loan payments for another few months, that`s the pause that has been in place since the start of the pandemic -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we`ll keep following it.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Sticking with education, more students across the country are returning to school this week, and that is highlighting a growing crisis with teachers, there are simply not enough of them right now.\par \par \uc2 Government figures show that about 300,000 teachers left the education workforce, left the profession since February 2020. That would be when the COVID pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 And in states like Mississippi, Florida, and Nevada, they`ve been hit especially hard by these shortages.\par \par \uc2 Columbus, Ohio, meanwhile, the state`s biggest school district there is opening the year this morning with the teachers on strike. You see the pictures there.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston has made her way to East High School, that`s in Columbus.\par \par \uc2 Elise, good morning. Why is this happening there now?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Tony, good morning to you. The School Board and Teachers Union have been negotiating for months without success. This ongoing strike means no one will be in class here today. Students will begin the school year online with many taught by substitutes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Teachers in Columbus have been on strike since Monday.\par \par \uc2 (TEACHERS protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: After the vast majority of the union`s roughly 4,500 members voted to reject the latest contract offer from the city`s Board of Education.\par \par \uc2 (TEACHERS protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: It marked yet another failed negotiation after 22 meetings since March. The Union claims Columbus schools are in a state of disrepair from missing ceiling tiles to lead paint to water leaks.\par \par \uc2 REGINA FUENTES (Columbus Education Association spokesperson): We will continue fighting until we have safe, properly maintained, and fully resourced schools in every neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The School Board says it is committed to addressing those issues, but insists they can only be solved with more taxpayer dollars, not a contract with teachers.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What goes in the contract doesn`t actually solve that problem.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: As the unresolved negotiations force a virtual start to the school year, the Teachers Union is calling on families to honor the picket line by boycotting remote class.\par \par \uc2 ENIYAH BROOM (Columbia City Schools student): We are starting off how we did with COVID.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The conflict has put students like 16-year-old Eniyah Broom now your broom, a senior at East Moore Academy High School in a tough position.\par \par \uc2 She told us she`d rather be in person, but supports the strike.\par \par \uc2 ENIYAH BROOM: We started off school with no air, no heat. It`s embarrassing compared to these other school districts.\par \par \uc2 Our school had been the same for like 50 years.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: You`re laughing?\par \par \uc2 ENIYAH BROOM: Because my grandma was in school and she said the same thing like, "Why does it look exactly the same?"\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Her mother agrees, but worries about how the disruption and more remote class could take a toll on some students.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I stand by the teachers, but this being the beginning of the school year I think was just terrible for the kids.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: And you get here a lot of supporters honking for this ongoing strike. As it continues, the district has set up 25 running signs for students to pick up free meals. Meanwhile, the Teachers Union and School Board head back to the bargaining table this afternoon-- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let`s give the teachers what they want and what they need.\par \par \uc2 Elise, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, a Louisville police officer has been found criminally responsible in the killing of Breonna Taylor. Former detective Kelly Goodlett pleaded guilty to a charge a conspiracy yesterday in Federal Court.\par \par \uc2 She admitted conspiring with another officer to falsify the search warrant used in the raid that led to Taylor`s death. The 26-year-old EMT was shot in March of 2020 after police burst into the apartment where she was sleeping.\par \par \uc2 Goodlett resigned earlier this month after the Justice Department announced new charges against her and three other officers. The others are accused of Civil Rights violations, conspiracy, obstruction, and use of excessive force.\par \par \uc2 Goodlett will be sentenced in November and could face up to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Two men have been convicted for their role in conspiring to kidnap Michigan Governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The Court heard how Adam Fox and Barry Croft, Jr. plan to take the Governor from her vacation home and use explosive devices to slow the police response. They could face up to life in prison. Two others have already been convicted for the plot which was intended to start a Civil War. It all unraveled due to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  sting operation.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Governor Whitmer thanked prosecutors and said that the ". verdicts prove that violence and threats have no place in our politics, and those who seek to divide us will be held accountable."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The US is preparing to send another $3 billion -- with a B -- in military aid to Ukraine as that country marks six months, they call an anniversary of the Russian invasion of that country. It`s also Ukraine`s Independence Day right now and they are marking it.\par \par \uc2 Officials there are worried however, that Russia may launch new attacks acts to disrupt the occasion.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta is in Kyiv, that`s Ukraine`s capital.\par \par \uc2 Debora, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, Kyiv is celebrating its independence with this display of wrecked Russian tanks that you see behind me. It was six months ago that the unthinkable became reality when \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  invaded Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And I should warn you that some of the visuals you`re about to see are disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: It is Ukraine`s signature brand -- defiance, not fear -- which is how they`ve chosen to face this uneasy day.\par \par \uc2 Very different from last year`s celebrations, the country`s wartime President has long swapped his suit for military fatigues. He set the tone when he raised the familiar yellow and blue flag, boldly proclaiming it would once again fly over the whole country, including Crimea.\par \par \uc2 But it is a day fraught with danger, amid fears Russia will spoil the party by ramping up attacks.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "Repugnant Russian provocations are possible," warned President Zelenskyy, "Respect curfew and act on air raid sirens."\par \par \uc2 Economist, Andriy Dligach says half a year of fighting has taught Ukraine that standing up to Russia is the only way to deal with a bully.\par \par \uc2 ANDRIY DLIGACH (Center for Economic Recovery of Ukraine co-founder): We shouldn`t be afraid of Putin or war. We should be as brave as possible to be open and to be ready for the new world.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: But it`s come at a terrible price.\par \par \uc2 ANDRIY DLIGACH: We are trying to find a way how to not only survive, but to win quickly in order to save Ukraine and the infrastructure; save Ukraine and economics, and first of all, save our lives.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Kyiv is the defining symbol of that Ukrainian bravery; Putin`s hopes of a swift victory came to nothing as he was prevented from seizing the capital.\par \par \uc2 Instead, civilians who fled to the countryside of Bucha and Irpin bore the brunt of a month of terror unleashed by Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 By now, Ukrainians are used to constant air raid sirens, which are once again sounding the alarm in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 And in Russia where this is not even referred to as a war, but a special military operation, questions are surfacing.\par \par \uc2 (OLGA GRAVNIEVA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "I really don`t understand who needs this war," said Russian cemetery assistant, Olga Gravnieva (sp?). "The mothers too keep asking who needs this war?"\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Six months and here we are, with these destroyed Russian tanks. A reminder that Putin`s hopes of a swift victory parade down this very road have been halted by a nation that is fighting to hold on to its freedom -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Debora Patta for us in Ukraine. Debora, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, one of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s former top security officials filed a whistleblower complaint claiming the platform has serious security flaws. How Congress is now getting involved and the big issues that could affect you online.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, Prince Harry`s wife, Meghan reveals a close call for their son, Archie in a revealing conversation with Serena Williams.\par \par \uc2 We`ll hear what they said in the first episode of Meghan`s new podcast.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Ahead for our series, "How Are You Now?" David Begnaud checks back in with a Houston man on his journey to overcome agoraphobia, which he described as like living in a prison.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What kind of response did you get from strangers after the story aired?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON (Suffers from agoraphobia): I have heard from people around the world -- around the world -- that have supported me, that continue to check in on me, people that have said "I`ve struggled with this, too, but because you`ve shared your story, I know someone else out there is dealing with the same thing."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Wow. In our next hour, find out how Cecil Jackson is doing now.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Congress is investigating allegations of serious security failures and deceptive practices at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  in response to a whistleblower complaint to Federal authorities.\par \par \uc2 That complaint by Peter Zatko better known as Mudge, he was security lead on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s senior executive team from November 2020 until January of 2022. And last month, he filed an 84-page complaint alleging "extreme egregious deficiencies" in \uc2 Twitter\uc2  security, and that it makes false misleading statements to the public and Federal regulators. It being \uc2 Twitter\uc2  itself.\par \par \uc2 Zatko`s Attorney, John Tye spoke to our Nikole Killion about the allegations.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TYE (Attorney): What he found inside this company was unlike anything he`d seen elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Does he still believe that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  users are still at risk?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TYE: Absolutely, and that`s why he reluctantly has decided to become a whistleblower.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In a statement, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  said that Zatko`s complaint is "a false narrative that is riddled with inconsistencies and inaccuracies." The statement goes on to say, "Security and privacy have long been companywide priorities and will continue to be."\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Thompson is CEO of "The Atlantic" and the former editor-in-chief of "Wired," and he is here. You`re also quoted, as I discovered this morning in the complaint filed by the whistleblower.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON ("The Atlantic" CEO): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, what do you make of the -- I think most serious allegation for the general user that the security on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is lax?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Yes, so there is a long and detailed section of the report of the whistleblower complaint that says \uc2 Twitter\uc2  gives too many people access to user information, that it doesn`t treat data properly and that is in violation of the decree with the FTC that dates back, you know, 10 years. So that`s the most concerning allegation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The national security concern that is alleged by some lawmakers, what is that concern? And are you concerned about it?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Yes. So remember, back in the summer of 2020, when the 17-year-old got access to all of these prominent \uc2 Twitter\uc2  accounts. He got Elon Musk`s account, Bill Gates` and started tweeting out crypto scams.\par \par \uc2 Now, that was weird. It was -- it caused, it actually led to Mudge getting hired to fix the problems. But now, imagine that it`s election 