ESON: President Biden is expected to announce up to $10,000.00 in student loan forgiveness for millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 DARA ZUCKER: It is predatory to go after kids who may not have ever had any understanding of how finances work.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Teachers in Columbus, Ohio are on strike calling in part for better conditions in classrooms.\par \par \uc2 REGINA FUENTES: We will continue fighting until we have safe, properly maintained, and fully resourced schools.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s former head of security accuses a company of making false statements during a Federal investigation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And a California man really brought the heat. He now holds the record for eating 17 ghost peppers in a minute.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY FOSTER (Record holder): Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 MAN: How do you feel?\par \par \uc2 GREGORY FOSTER: I am on fire.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Should I stop it?\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: No, don`t stop it now. Keep the video running. I`m not bothered about seeing him eat the peppers. I want to see the seven minutes after he eats.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I hope he was able to drink a lot of milk.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. You asked if I`m a spice guy. I`m not like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not like that. You`re not feeling that at all?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s not me.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I`d do it for charity.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Would you?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, we`re got to set that up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`d make a phone call, look forward to the video.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we begin this hour with an important issue affecting our country -- literacy.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Education says about half of US adults ages 16 to 74, that`s about 130 million Americans, read below a sixth-grade level.\par \par \uc2 A new book called "Reading for Our Lives: A Literacy Action Plan from Birth to Six" tackles the issue head on. It says our current approach to reading is failing and ensuring literacy success must begin at infancy.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, the book`s author, Maya Payne Smart joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART ("Reading for Our Lives" author): Good morning. Thanks so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Now, you said that you wish you had this guide when your daughter was an infant. Why do you think it`s important to start so early?\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: I think it`s important to start so early for a couple of reasons. One, so that we can take advantage of this amazing brain growth and development that takes place for kids when they are at birth to three. And also, it makes you a more knowledgeable parent as your child approaches school age, so you can make better decisions about school and advocate for curriculum and other things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, when we think about infants, we think about bedtime stories. But you say it`s so much more than just reading books at night.\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: Absolutely. So, bedtime stories are really important for building oral language and vocabulary, but with the little ones, we can also do a lot of brain building through conversation.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s really about that responsiveness, the back-and-forth exchange with little ones.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: How often should parents be reading to their kids? Because I`m thinking, you know, you`ve got a new baby, you`re just trying to get the kid to go to sleep a lot of the time and stay asleep. I mean, how often should you be doing this?\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: Reading, establishing a daily reading habit is wonderful, but I like people to think beyond bedtime because that`s not the best time for a lot of families. And also, I want people to recognize that particularly with the infants, it really is about those back-and-forth exchanges and even treating their coos and babbles as if they were a conversation. That`s really asking questions.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Responding to it.\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: Absolutely. So, really thinking of it as a dialogue from day one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I really like that part of it because for a lot of parents, like reading a book to their kid at night, the parents are out of their mind tired, they`ve got another kid who is screaming for something. The kitchen still needs to be cleaned. It`s just --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Are you speaking from experience?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am speaking from absolute experience.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is that biographical, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Eloise does not get a bedtime story and I struggle to get through Teddy bedtime stories, right? But, what you`re saying is actually that for parents who have those limitations as all parents do, they shouldn`t beat themselves up. The conversation can actually be a big part of it.\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: Absolutely. The conversation is critically important. All of those words that you speak to a child, all the things that you`re labeling and pointing to, all of that oral vocabulary is important for them when one day they need to recognize it in print.\par \par \uc2 It is important as they get older, let`s say three, four, to start bringing their attention to print. So, dragging your finger across the page as you`re reading, so they understand text moves from left to right.\par \par \uc2 You forget that little ones sometimes think we`re reading the pictures.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: But they aren`t even tuned into the print.\par \par \uc2 But I also like parents to know that you can take advantage of print on cereal boxes, or a graphic at-shirt or a street sign. Wherever there is print, you can talk about it.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And that`s super smart.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Tony mentioned some of those things that causes fatigue. As a parent, you`re just dealing with life. So you`re tired at the end of the day. What advice do you give to parents that struggle to find time?\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: Parents definitely feel stressed and fatigued and distracted. So, the key thing is to remember that you`re already spending a ton of time with your child and you can build these simple literacy practices into everyday life -- mealtimes, diaper changing times -- these are all opportunities to sing nursery rhymes, and again, have this back- and-forth conversation.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I feel like you also have to get other people involved, like I`m going to visit my new nephew this weekend. And I`m thinking, okay, Aunt Jamie needs to maybe sit down and read a book.\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: Absolutely. Lean into your community.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s huge. That`s huge. To what extent is technology kind of the reason for declines in literacy, if there are declines in literacy?\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: I don`t think that technology is necessarily a reason for the decline in literacy, but it does distract us from doing some of the things we need to do to build it.\par \par \uc2 So, parents on their own devices takes away time from reading the stories and having those --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then they`re not talking.\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You`re just scrolling.\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, what are some of the biggest takeaways from your book?\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART: I think the biggest takeaways are start early because there is so much brain growth and development that happens in those early years with your child. And again, it makes you a savvy or more informed, knowledgeable parent as they approach school age.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maya Payne Smart, thank you so much for joining us. "Reading For Our Lives" is on sale now. Make sure you pick it up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Start those conversations today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Start it early.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can do it right now. There is Maya, she is on TV.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much. That`s great.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, in our "Bill of the Month" series, we`ll hear from one woman facing an ambulance bill almost three times higher than her sister`s bill for care after the very same car accident.\par \par \uc2 What you need to know about ambulances before you have an emergency.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:07, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Ahead, a lot more from our David Begnaud. Hey, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Hey, you.\par \par \uc2 Here it toes. "How Are You Now?" We`ve got the third installment of our series where we go back and revisit some of the most memorable people we`ve introduced you to.\par \par \uc2 Today, the man in Houston who wrote to us and said "I`ve got agoraphobia and I want to overcome it." How is he doing now?\par \par \uc2 Plus, it`s a birthday surprise in today`s edition of the "Uplift."\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. Come on back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning in our continuing "Bill of the Month" partnership with Kaiser Health News and NPR, we are talking about surprise ambulance bills.\par \par \uc2 Last September, an Illinois woman was driving with her brother and her sister when their car collided with a pickup truck. Well, the second shock was when they all received very different bills for identical rides to the hospital in an ambulance.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PEGGY DULA (Received high ambulance bill): You think you might have some control over the car, but you don`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: After a car crash, Peggy Dula and her two siblings were transported to the same hospital in three separate ambulances.\par \par \uc2 Peggy`s injury, a bruised sternum was the least severe.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY DULA: And my ambulance bill was $3,606.00, which I thought was really high.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was nearly triple her sister`s bill.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY DULA: I couldn`t even get anyone to offer an explanation as to the different pricing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Each ambulance was from a different taxpayer funded Illinois Fire Protection District and charged a different rate for medical services and mileage driven.\par \par \uc2 Peggy was charged more than double the rate of her siblings` ambulances for medical services. And $30.00 per mile for transportation, while the other to charged $10.00 per mile.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY DULA: So, I called the Paramedic Billing Service. They basically told me there was little they could do, that the rate was set by the Fire District.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Peggy says she has repeatedly tried to contact the Pingree Grove and Countryside Fire District, but the Fire Chief has not returned her calls.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY DULA: I felt really powerless, actually, to do anything, to change anything.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Peggy`s medical insurance covered 60 percent of what they deemed to be the reasonable and customary rate for the services she received according to her plan. She was left, that means, with a remaining balance of over $2,700.00.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY DULA: I don`t have a tremendous amount of money. That`s a sizable amount for me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are happy to say that all three siblings have recovered from their physical injuries. Their financial injuries though, that`s another matter.\par \par \uc2 Peggy Dula says she reached out to Paramedic Billing Services multiple times and tried to negotiate her bill. She also made a $40.00 payment in good faith. However, the bill was sent to collections. Paramedic Billing Services Vice President, that`s Michael Tillman says: "Patients must dispute charges directly with the ambulance service." All right.\par \par \uc2 The Pingree Grove and Countryside Fire Chief did not respond to our request for comment, but told Kaiser Health News the charges are in keeping with the Federal Ground Emergency Medical Transportation Program, which you probably never heard of until now.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Elisabeth Rosenthal is editor-in-chief of Kaiser Health News, and she joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Okay, wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL (Kaiser Health News editor-in-chief): Yes, mind blowing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is all mind blowing, because you don`t know these costs ahead of time, you get the bill after the fact. So, how is it that a company, an ambulance service can provide the same service as another, but charge a completely different price?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: Well, because the ambulance services can charge whatever they want and they can join whichever insurance networks they want.\par \par \uc2 And you know what? These days most -- many, if not most ambulance services decide to join none.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, what are people supposed to do? I mean, stand -- hop on one foot on the corner and wait for like the cheap ambulance to come by?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: Right \uc2 Google\uc2 , "Are you in my network?" You`re kind of a sitting duck, and that`s really the problem that Peggy faced. I mean, she was the least injured and she got the biggest bill.\par \par \uc2 And you know, in retrospect, she should have let her daughter take her to a doctor or called a neighbor.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, when you call 9-1-1, you`re not able to ask for the price of the ambulance. That`s not part of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: You`re not even able to ask for which ambulance. I mean, basically 9-1-1 goes out and ambulances as in this case, just arrive, and you don`t know where they`re from, whether they`re in your network, what they`re going to charge you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When you`re actually in the ambulance, on the stretcher there, can you raise your hand if capable and say, "Please take me to an in-network hospital."\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: You can ask, but they are unlikely to do so, they take you where they want.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve always heard when an ambulance shows up, you have to get in. Is there any truth to that?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: No. I mean, I always tell people, you can say no. Now, the ambulance worker is always probably going to say better to get checked out, but you might think "better to get checked out, but I feel okay." Of course, if you have a head injury --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: Or you feel really bad, you should just get in and not worry about the cost. But you know, it is really so unfair to patients, and this is the one thing that was left out of that No Surprises Act. I mean, you know, out-of-network anesthesiologist, out-of-network air ambulances are in, but ground ambulances. Why?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, it was left out of the No Surprises Act. Is that an oversight? Or did someone ask for that?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: Well, there is some speculation that ground ambulance companies are revenue generators, right, for many local fire departments. So --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That makes sense, literally.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: It makes sense, literally, but it`s not great for patients.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course not.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Is there any way that a combination of auto insurance with medical insurance can help deviate some of these costs?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: Well, you know, 12 states require people to have personal injury protection insurance with their car insurance. In that case, the car insurance health protections kick in first.\par \par \uc2 But for Peggy, that might have helped, but also we`ve run a "Bill of the Month" where auto insurance personal insurance protection is very limited, $250,000.00. If you have a big accident, you can be really out of luck that way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: So, you know, you`re really out of luck one way or the other. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Fantastic news.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: Sorry for the bad news this morning, but --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: But it is realistic.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: It is really a hole in our legislation, and it really should be plugged, I think.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh boy.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Thanks for bringing attention to it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope it is. Thank you very much, Dr. Elisabeth Rosenthal.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up in our "Uplift at Eight," a birthday surprise for a nine-year-old who received thousands of messages of support.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Aaron Gouveia/@DaddyFiles.)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is time for the "Uplift at Eight" where we share stories that bring some joy to your day.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got Jamie Yuccas laughing in the commercial break. We`ve got David Begnaud back for us. What have we got today?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I always say, if only you could hear what we say --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Life goes on. I love the "Uplift."\par \par \uc2 Today`s story is about a little boy in Massachusetts getting a very special gift.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SAM: It is a sewing machine. Wait, what kind? It`s a Singer.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You`ve got your own sewing machine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That is nine-year-old, Sam, getting his very own sewing machine for his birthday. So, the video went viral on TikTok hitting more than two-and-a-half million views.\par \par \uc2 Sam loves to sew and last month, he actually went to a sewing camp where he was the only boy. So far, he has made dresses, bags and even hair scrunchies.\par \par \uc2 His parents, Aaron and MJ say that Sam has been bullied because he likes to sew and paint his nails.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: His dad said that reading the supportive comments on the video with Sam has been miraculous for him.\par \par \uc2 Sam says he is most proud of a dress that he made for his cousin at sewing camp. And Sam, I`ve got something for you my man, nails are in.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes. I love it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There you go, Sam. Paint them, buddy.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: My fiance`s son wants to do it, too. He`s got little rock star nail like star on there. You know? I mean, just, who cares?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And here is a fun fact about me. I learned to sew in middle school and I`ve been doing it ever since.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Look at you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can make stuff and sold it at school.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, Sam, keep doing your thing.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes, just be you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He makes scrunchies? I would think a scrunchie is pretty high-level thing to sew.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I didn`t know you could sew a scrunchie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. With all like all the curves on it and everything.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shout out to Sam.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I love when guys talk about --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got some friends right here at the table, Sam.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Paint yourself some good nails, Sam. It`s all in. Keep it bright.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, speaking of kids, coming up, one more reason your kids need to eat their breakfast. Yes, we`ll look at a new study showing what they eat and where can be just as important.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I can`t believe it`s already 8:30.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Already?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Boom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Time is flying by.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It is time for our "Talk of the Table" -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Time for "Talk of the Table." I`ll jump right in.\par \par \uc2 So, my "Talk of the Table" is something that applies to kids, but also could apply to adults. It is importance of breakfast for kids and how it may affect their behavior.\par \par \uc2 A new study published in the journal "Frontiers in Nutrition" found children who skip breakfast or eat breakfast outside their home are more likely to have behavioral problems.\par \par \uc2 The study from Spain also found nutrition plays a factor in behavior. Researchers say milk, yogurt, bread, and cereals promote wellbeing and better behavior, while eggs, processed meats like bacon and sausage and cheese brings on bad attitudes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Why though?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m just --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What`s the reasoning?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got to get a little more specific than that, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve got you. Because I mean --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Cereal, I mean, there are some cereals that are not bringing about good behavior. They are bringing about like bouncing off the wall or running around the kitchen island.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Cocoa Krispies. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was raised on eggs and bacon.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I mean, I don`t eat as much bacon as I used to. It`s just like an ex-girlfriend. We don`t see each other as much as we do, but we had some good times. We had some real good times back in the day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you miss her sometimes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But I miss her sometimes, because bacon is delicious.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But I do understand eating at home, if you`re a kid before you go off to school, if you have the option to, to eat with your mom or your parent or your guardian. I think that kind of -- it puts you in a different mood.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I agree. The sugary cereal though, I don`t agree with. I mean, the eggs and bacon, you do take a nap for like the first three periods of the day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No good.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I don`t know about your "Talk of the Table" today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What have you got?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Creeps me out a little.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is a guy -- this is our continuing progress toward becoming cyborgs for real.\par \par \uc2 There is a gentleman here in New York, I believe his name is Brandon -- I don`t know how to say his last name to be honest with you -- Dalaly -- Dali.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who has a \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and who was implanted a chip into his hand that can open the \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say what?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 /Brandon Dalaly.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s got a chip in his hand. There`s the chip, thank you, and it is underneath the Band-Aid I guess, and he can open his \uc2 Tesla\uc2  with it.\par \par \uc2 He is part of a trial of about a hundred people who are doing this and the underlying company, apparently, can put all kinds of chips in your hand associated with all sorts of apps.\par \par \uc2 So, this could be the future maybe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll pass.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We could do it.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll pass.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I absolutely will not.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You never lose your keys again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Nope.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: No. I just -- I think it opens the door to too many other things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think the bad guy in a future sci-fi movie is going to be chopping off hands and taking vehicles.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Exactly. None of this sounds good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t want any of that tech inside my body.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: We`re going to go warm and fuzzy now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Okay. Because my "Talk of the Table" is about how your dog really feels about you when you come home.\par \par \uc2 Have you ever wondered this? They have real emotion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They like you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, they do.\par \par \uc2 A new study published in "Current Biology" found dogs can produce tears of joy when they are reunited with their humans. Their tears don`t fall down their faces like ours do. One reason for the tears is apparently a hormone, oxytocin, better known as the love hormone.\par \par \uc2 Researchers say hormone levels go up in both dogs and humans when they interact. See, dogs are the best.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We have -- we have some dog owners in here and --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who do we have here?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It is just dogs, it`s when you come home. I mean, look at the joy, pure joy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dogs are amazing. They are. They`ve been counting on us for like millennia now. They used to be out in the cold. And then at some point, we had a nice warm fire going and the dogs came in and here we are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: David Begnaud is on the swing back there saying "Aw." So, he agrees with that. And I agree too, and that`s what I`m trying to convince my wife to get a dog.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: To get a dog? Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What kind?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is not going to let me. I asked and I did not get a dog.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Maybe your old girlfriend, eggs and bacon will get you that dog.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was good.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead in our series, "How Are You Now?" David Begnaud catches up with a man who lived in fear of public spaces for years and now, he is trying to live his life to the fullest.\par \par \uc2 You don`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All this week, our David Begnaud has been checking in with people whose stories he shared with you in recent years, and he has been asking them a simple but profound question, How are you now?\par \par \uc2 You may remember Cecil Jackson of Houston, Texas. He was battling agoraphobia. It is a crippling anxiety that forced him to avoid public spaces. But Jackson was inspired to make a change after years of living in fear.\par \par \uc2 Back in April, David followed him as he visited places and tried new tasks as illness had prevented him from doing. And now, we are seeing just how far he`s come.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: I`m a 35-year-old Houstonian and I suffer from agoraphobia.\par \par \uc2 I seemingly lead a normal life as a local store manager, but no one knows that I haven`t traveled beyond a one-mile radius from my home in over 10 years.\par \par \uc2 My childhood was extremely limited due to my mother`s suffering with agoraphobia, and my adult years have been no different since my diagnosis at the age of 19.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You just read that on national television. How does that feel?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: It feels freeing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Finish the sentence for me. For me, agoraphobia is --\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: For me, agoraphobia is prison.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You work across the street from your house.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: I do.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You eat across the street?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: I do.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Tell me some of the things you`ve missed?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: All of my sisters` graduations, my niece`s graduation and tons of birthday celebrations, two grandmothers` funerals and my sister`s wedding.\par \par \uc2 She sends me an e-mail, right, as she is about to walk down the aisle. In the e-mail, she said, "I know that you would be here." I`m sorry, I haven`t talked about this.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It`s okay. It`s okay.\par \par \uc2 Cecil found an online lecture from Dr. Karen Cassidy, the owner of the Chicago based, Anxiety Treatment Center. This year, he e-mailed her.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: While I have never met you, it became clear that you understand some of the challenges that many of us face. I want to reclaim my life.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Dr. Cassidy arranged for a Houston therapist to treat Cecil with online sessions, but he was not prepared for what we had up our sleeve, meeting Dr. Cassidy for the first time in person.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY (Anxiety Treatment Center owner): You are the bravest I man I know right now.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been so excited to see you.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: Oh my --\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY: I`m going to be your exposure coach today.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: She flew to Houston at our invitation.\par \par \uc2 Tell her what you`d like to do.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: I`d like to ride an escalator.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: I`d like to go to the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Part of Dr. Cassidy`s therapy would be to provoke Cecil`s anxiety by intentionally hyperventilating, but this is done to get him used to the sensation of panic and learn that he can handle it and teach his body to not respond with panic attacks.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY: So, my goal isn`t to make this easy.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY: It`s to step into the heat.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY: And to discover you can take it, okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So, where is the first place, we`re going, Cecil?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: So, the first place we`re going to go is to the mall. We`re going to ride an escalator.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He was last on an escalator four years ago.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY: So, let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He`d rely on exercises to induce dizziness in order to push through.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY: Fantastic you are acing this.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What did you feel?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: The sweaty palms, the dizziness, the sensitivity to light. I think I stood there for like three minutes talking to her -- two minutes talking to you.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: And it just subsided.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Next up was the grocery store. Now, Cecil hadn`t been in one of those in three years, until now.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY: He is doing so --\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: It is amazing. It`s amazing to be here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Lines overwhelmed him, even the smallest purchase was a big accomplishment.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN CASSIDY: Yes, yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: I think that sometimes when you suffer, you feel like nobody sees you. When you deal with agoraphobia, people write you off and you`re often hidden in the shadows.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We see you, Cecil. We see you.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That was almost five months ago.\par \par \uc2 How are you now?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What`s changed.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: One of the things I remember distinctly discussing with you or writing in the letter was about, you know, those obligatory trips to the grocery store or Target, you know, those simple things. And now, that`s just a part of my life.\par \par \uc2 I`m learning to go towards fear, and I think that honestly, the biggest thing for me has been really accepting the freedom that was always there. I just didn`t know how to operate in it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There`s something about the way you`re sitting and holding yourself that is even more confident.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: Yes. Absolutely. And when I initially met with you, my issue was just agoraphobia and that if I only solved that, then that will be the answer to everything. Little did I know, that opened the door to a self-discovery.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What else have you discovered?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: Who I am? What I like? What my hobbies are? The ability to say no. The freedom in saying and the power in being able to finally say yes. To know that feelings aren`t facts. To know that feelings aren`t my ultimate authority.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Tick tock through where you`ve been, that you never would have gone to before you started on this journey.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: To give a shortlist, I`ve been on the freeway. I`ve been to a preserve. I`ve been to a number of parks. I`ve been to visit friends. That list could go on and on. It has been absolutely amazing.\par \par \uc2 And many of these things are things that just happened. They weren`t planned. I just woke up and said: "Today, I`m doing what I want to do today."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But it seems to me you found the way to go inward and find that you have the answers to unlocking the issues you needed to unlock anyway.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: You know, it`s crazy. I always thought that I needed something, that there was something that was going to come and rescue me, whether that was a person or a thing. And I thought that that would be the magical thing that did it. And I waited for a long time for that thing to come.\par \par \uc2 And what I discovered in this process is that the missing part was me. I needed me. And I hadn`t connected that link. And when I realized that, I understood that everything that I needed was already inside of me. It had been there all along.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Is there a struggle or a boundary that you haven`t been able to cross yet?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: Absolutely. I want to be clear in that. It`s still a process. You know, there are still things that I struggle with. Of course, there`s still places that I haven`t gone.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Such as? What are you still struggling with?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: I haven`t been on a plane. There are still places here that I have yet to go, but I think that for me, the difference now is that, I`m okay with being afraid and still doing it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I know it took a lot of courage to sit down and do the interview with us, and to write to me in the first place and tell me your story.\par \par \uc2 You feel like it was worth it?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 There was something so transformative about even that experience. For a long time, I thought that I could heal from this and then come back out and say, "Okay, look at what I went through and I never told a soul."\par \par \uc2 I understood for me that I couldn`t heal and hide simultaneously, and it`s been absolutely amazing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: My job is usually asking people. Sometimes many times, to tell their story. You came to me saying, "I`m ready to tell it." And that was such a unique experience, even for me to witness. I thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: I thank you with everything in me. I thank you. I think that this will be -- I know that this will be a moment that I will never forget. I know that my life has not been the same, it will never be the same and I`m excited.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: By the way, do you want to get on a plane?\par \par \uc2 CECIL JACKSON: Absolutely. First person I am coming to see is you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I see that coming in the future.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s cool.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I said to him, how has your relationship changed with your family, right? And he said, rather than them calling me and saying, how are you? They call and they say, where are you now.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Keeps going. Right?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That`s huge. I know, to think he didn`t make his sister`s wedding, but now can go into a grocery store or Target or whatever the case may be, that`s a huge leap.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, the moment of change for him was that initial consultation with the psychologist.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It was a moment of change, but I want to be clear, Tony. He has spent the last seven months in some of the most intensive therapy I`ve ever heard of. He has seven different therapists.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Three days a week. So, it may look wonderfully packaged and he may look good. It`s his part time job just going to therapy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is still a struggle.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is important to point out because people see that and they think, "How come I can`t do it? It seems so easy."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What I love about stories like this is while somebody else is going through their own journey, you can pull things out of it. He said, "Feelings aren`t facts."\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I love that, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And then he also said, "You can`t heal and hide at the same time."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You pulled out my favorite quotes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s some heavy stuff right there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Boom.\par \par \uc2 All right, here we go. Tomorrow, we`re going to speak again with Miss Claudette Colvin, I love this story.\par \par \uc2 Before Rosa Parks challenged segregation, there was a 15-year-old girl in Alabama, Claudette Colvin, who refused to give up her seat to a White lady on an Alabama bus.\par \par \uc2 Her struggle for Civil Rights has long been overshadowed, but now the 82- year-old is gaining overdue recognition including a movie that is being made about her ordeal.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, we`ll show you a surprise that we lined up for and share more exciting news in our update to come.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One of our favorite stories. Love us some Claudette.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In case you missed it yesterday on "\uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals," we showed you three items that could help keep you safe, all at exclusive low prices.\par \par \uc2 One of them, the She`s Birdie personal safety device that activates an alarm and flashing lights and it is available now that 40 percent off. Forty percent off.\par \par \uc2 To start shopping for the She`s Birdie and many other items that could easily become essentials in your daily life, scan the QR code on your screen right now. Go ahead and do it.\par \par \uc2 You can also visit CBSdeals.com.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well, before we take off here, videos like these have gotten tens of millions of views on TikTok. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: I can`t not bend.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes, they are showing men trying on a period pain simulator and not enjoying that experience.\par \par \uc2 But for Canadian entrepreneur, Lex Perry, it is much more than just laughs, it is about making people aware of a painful disorder called endometriosis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LEX PERRY (Canadian entrepreneur): It impacted my ability to attend school, my ability to attend jobs, hold a job.\par \par \uc2 Endometriosis is regularly mistaken for period pain, which is just so absolutely absurd.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well, tomorrow, we`re going to hear from Perry and we`ll have a gynecologist in-studio to talk about how to treat that disorder. It`s a really interesting conversation.\par \par \uc2 And guys, another interesting conversation we`d been having today is your doppelgangers. So, let`s go ahead and show people.\par \par \uc2 Nate, you said you look like Usher.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I think so.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You think so?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maybe. Maybe not. I don`t know. Check this out. See?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What do we think?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A little bit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A little action, yes. Yes, he could be my cousin.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I actually prefer, Nate, people say Usher looks like Nate Burleson. That`s how I --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There we go, Tony.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, there you go. Okay, how about Doctor McDreamy?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This one is not going to go well.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Patrick Dempsey. Let`s go ahead and take a look.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is not going to work.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, look at that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You give me a little scruff on that chin.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It might -- better hair as well. That`s really a landslide win for McDreamy there, unfortunately. Thanks. Wow.\par \par \uc2 Well, I`m going to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jamie, I have a question. Will you have those simulators in tomorrow? Can me and Tony trial?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well, we actually did test them on my producer. Wait until you -- I don`t know if you want to do it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, are you in?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I see that video on TikTok. I`ll try. I`ll do longer than you, it would be like riding the bull.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220824ei8o000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Finding Success In The Workplace\b0\par\par\uc2 Jamie Yuccas, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,540 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO (News producer and reporter): Being undocumented is more than a lack of status. It`s a feeling and it is always there with you.\par \par \uc2 Am I good enough? Am I worthy? Will I ever belong?\par \par \uc2 And you know most of us feel that way at some point in life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That was news producer and reporter, Daniela Pierre-Bravo in her TED Talk "From Undocumented to Unstoppable."\par \par \uc2 Now she`s on a mission to help other women of color follow in her footsteps and find success in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 As an immigrant from Chile, Pierre-Bravo spent many years undocumented and in the shadows. She worked odd jobs to pay her way through school before finding an opening in the media industry, and eventually becoming a reporter on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 In her new book "The Other: How to Own Your Power at Work as a Woman of Color," she writes about her journey and the lessons she`s learned along the way.\par \par \uc2 Daniela Pierre-Bravo, good morning to you. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Good morning. Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You know, what I love about the book is that you talk about as an immigrant, that there are really great things about entering the workplace of hustling and having this work ethic. But then you talk about how that can change and it is not so great anymore.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes, well, the immigrant ethos is a beautiful thing, right? My mom and my grandma always taught me to put my head down and do the work, right, be grateful for everything. It`s the energy that got me from undocumented to be able to go to college, to getting on an 18-hour bus ride, because I lied on my resume and said that I lived in New York, just so that I could get an opportunity to get an unpaid internship.\par \par \uc2 But you know, by the same token, when we advance in our career, especially for women of color, when we`re constantly leading with that overly grateful mentality to the point where we fear asking for more, because we don`t want to be ungrateful, it can kind of not work in our favor.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, if you put your head down and do the work in corporate America, if you don`t let people know what you`re doing, no one is going to notice.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes, you`ve got to brag about yourself.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s right. Don`t be humble.\par \par \uc2 The book is being not being humble, I`ll not be humble for you. You`ve got a great voice, you`ve got a delicate touch, it`s going to help a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 And you describe a moment in the book is sort of part career advice and then part memoir. And in the memoir part, you talk about a breaking point for yourself, when you started thinking about change. Can you talk about that breaking point?\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes, I think for a lot of women of color, we experience this breaking point of burnout. And you know, for a lot of our communities, we have been in constant survival mode, that fight or flight is very familiar to us. So we tend to find and recreate those moments in our career, to the point where we feel like our worth is predicated on our productivity, and it is hard for us to shift that mindset.\par \par \uc2 And we know that as women go on in their careers, we need to play by different rules. We can`t be constantly in that customer service mentality and it is hard because that means that we need to say no to some things that we advise.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: How do we do that?\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: And it is hard, right? And especially for women of color, because we don`t want to cause that cognitive dissonance. We don`t want to put people at dis-ease, but it`s so important as we move along in our career.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You know, I really try to cheerlead other women, but I even find it hard to cheerlead myself. What are some tips for people that you would, if you`re speaking to other women that you give them?\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes, I mean, I think that, you know, we`re so lucky now, especially Gen Z, as we -- you know, as they come up in the workplace. Gen Z is going to be the most diverse generation out there.\par \par \uc2 And when I think about as a woman of color, who is sometimes the one and only in a room, it`s not just about me, right? It`s about the advocating that I do for myself, but the more room that I leave for other women behind us, and I think that`s important that we`re not just cheerleading ourselves, and it is not just about us, but it`s about the door that we leave even wider for the women after us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Pay matters. Pay negotiation.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What tips you have on that front, and you write about why even if you get a no at first, you shouldn`t stop at no?\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes, well, there`s a lot of great tips on negotiation in the book, but that`s exactly right. It`s so important to not leave that negotiation and just assume the no is forever.\par \par \uc2 So what you want to do is you want to make sure to talk to your manager and say, okay, what are the metrics that I missed? And what are the qualifications and the metrics that you`re looking for, so that I meet them? And make sure that you don`t leave there without putting a time on their calendar, whether it`s three or six months to have this conversation to check in, because you don`t want to wait a full year for the next performance review.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You really have to know your worth though.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Sometimes that can be really hard.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes. Well, that`s why it`s so important to talk about it and talk about it often. You know, in this remote work kind of life that we`re going through, it`s even more important. It`s more than okay, it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 And you know, it`s hard to be a manager at this time. There`s a lot of things that they`re juggling, so help them help you. And one way by doing that is to make sure that you`re having consistent conversations, whether it be weekly or biweekly about what are the wins that you`re bringing to the table.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I like that a lot. Daniela Pierre-Bravo, thank you so much for being here. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: "The Other: How to Own Your Power at Work as a Woman of Color" goes on sale tomorrow. You can pick it up.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. Easy-peasy.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Pull the trigger. Pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: Ah.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`ve been hit.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Okay, oh my God.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I really did not want to take control of that airplane.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The pilot behind you, he`s got a big smile on his face as you`re going "ah."\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well, he`s done it a time or two.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, the questions you got were, did you throw up?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Which was no. How many G`s did you pull?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Seven and a half almost eight.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Seven and a half. Is Jay Ellis as handsome in person as he appears to be on TV?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: He is even better looking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Even better looking.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh my gosh. He is so handsome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. You know --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: His wife is beautiful too. They`ve got a great marriage.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m sure they do.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: He is not in the market, ladies.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That does it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220824ei8m000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Flooding Emergency; Severe Soaking Storms; Daughter of Putin Ally Killed; Arkansas Deputies Suspended; Trump`s Legal Troubles; Testimony on Hold; The \uc2 Taliban\uc2 `s Enemy at Home; A Roundabout Solution; The Monkees Music Lives On; The Need for Speed; Bonded by Name; Finding Success in the Workplace; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Jamie Yuccas, Omar Villafranca,  Charlie D`Agata, Nikki Battiste, Scott MacFarlane, Robert Costa; Imtiaz  Tyab, Anthony Mason, David Begnaud, Kris Van Cleave \par \uc2 13,239 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 doTONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday, August 22nd. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And I`m Jamie Yuccas.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle and Nate are off, but we are good hands and here`s today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Monsoon rains flood parts of the south and west bringing chaos to areas already suffering from historic drought conditions.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We`re expecting more flooding in the same areas through Tuesday morning where heavy rain is still falling.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In Arkansas, three law enforcement officers are under investigation after they were filmed brutally beating a suspect.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The daughter of a right-wing ally of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is killed by a car bomb and Russia vows violent retaliation.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): I certainly hope it wasn`t something emanating from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And new developments in the investigation into classified materials that may have been improperly taken by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . How his legal team is fighting back.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2  (R-OH): Every former President has access to their documents. It is how they write their memoirs.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Actor, Gary Busey was charged with criminal sexual contact after appearing at a horror film convention in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Basketball Hall of Famer, Dennis Rodman claims he is headed to Russia to help free \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Is there a remote-control ball there?\par \par \uc2 MAN: At the PGA Championship, someone gets a remote-controlled ball onto the green and Rory McIlroy isn`t amused.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, it`s going to be hard to control it from the water.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The winner of the BMW Championship was Patrick Cantlay.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And a tap in for fore.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s back-to-back BMW Championships.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And Cantlay is the first one to win back-to-back playoff events in the 16-year history of the \uc2 FedEx\uc2  Cup Playoffs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I mean, big congratulations to Mr. Cantlay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Looking good. Yes. Big win.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Nicknamed, Patty Ice because he ices everybody out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay. I want to know more about the remote-control golf ball that was on the green.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to be talking about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh we are.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: On "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very apt. Well, we have got a lot of great stories, it turns out, coming up in the show. Good storytelling as well.\par \par \uc2 The last surviving member of the Monkees, Micky Dolenz talks to Anthony Mason, about why he is still -- what`s the line, Vlad? A daydream away.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay. Can`t even believe it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on. Come on. The vacant look in your eyes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was like, "Where is he going with this?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Years after the band`s debut, this is a must-see conversation.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: We`re also kicking off a week-long series of what David Begnaud called, "How are you now?" Where he has updates on some of his most memorable stories. Today, he will check in with two families linked by fate and faith.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, Jamie, you`ve got a story of your own.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jamie takes part in some simulated dog fighting with "Top Gun" actor, Jay Ellis. Jamie, you look pretty -- I don`t know. I know, he says you looked pretty calm up there, but you kind of aren`t doing those barrel rolls.\par \par \uc2 I want to know if you got a call sign.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I did and I have it with me. We will reveal it in a little bit.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jamie Fox?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jamie is crying?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, I was after that ride, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And yes, it is a bad name --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am going to interrupt the streak you`re on, Vlad, because we`ve got some drastic changes in the weather in the southwest.\par \par \uc2 Dangerous conditions for millions of Americans, a monsoon storm system has hammered the drought-stricken West including in Utah where crews search for a missing hiker who may have been swept away by the rising water.\par \par \uc2 At least 13 million people between Arizona and Louisiana are on alert now for flooding, as we start this back-to-work Monday and the system is now moving east.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca has more from hard-hit Dallas.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Torrential rain poured across North Texas overnight, flooding streets like this one in Fort Worth.\par \par \uc2 In Utah, search and rescue efforts are underway for at least one hiker who may have been swept into a flooded river.\par \par \uc2 Monsoon rains pounded the region. Rescue crews worry, 29-year-old Jetal Agnihotri of Tucson, Arizona may have drowned. Park Police received reports of multiple hikers being swept off their feet by floodwaters.\par \par \uc2 This video shows an unidentified person clinging to a log as the floodwaters rushed past a group of hikers.\par \par \uc2 In Eastern Utah, floodwaters rushed through the streets and forced people to carry children out of washed-out businesses.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is nuts --\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: As patrons pushed their way out of knee-deep water in partially submerged restaurants.\par \par \uc2 Others drove their vehicles through flooded streets.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters are leaning on the rain to help combat wildfires brought on by the historic drought, but those dry conditions have hardened the soil creating a cement-like foundation for floodwaters to rise.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The flash flooding prompted some water rescues overnight in the Dallas, Fort Worth area. A few people were caught in their cars on Interstate 30 in Downtown Dallas as the water was rising quickly around them. The water soaked and submerged a few cars and even stopped traffic in the Downtown Mixmaster.\par \par \uc2 Good news though. Crews are starting to clear this area right now. You can see plenty of police vehicles are here, but there is more rain on the way - - Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Omar, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Jim Cantore from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking this system for us. It`s a big one.\par \par \uc2 Jim, good morning to you. You`ve got the rain behind you, I see. Where could we see flooding next?\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): We`re going to see it move right along I-20, Tony. Thanks to our virtual view technology, I am standing at Dallas near the Trinity River, which by the way, the gauge is going straight up. Why? Because we had more than six-and-a-half inches of rain in 24 hours, three inches in just one hour, which was the most rain we`ve ever seen in a one-hour period in Dallas, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Imagine that. Those records go back to 1953.\par \par \uc2 There`s that flooding moving through Shreveport, possibly Jackson this afternoon. Look at the rainfall here, five to eight inches. Do you remember just a couple of weeks ago on this very show? We`re saying "Hey guys, this is Day 66 in Dallas with no rainfall." We had our hottest summer on record, and now the tables have turned big time.\par \par \uc2 As a matter of fact, this is probably the wettest August on record for Dallas, given the fact that we`ve had over six and a half inches of rain in the Metroplex this morning. All of his heading southward College Station, Austin, San Antonio, through Shreveport more of that to come tonight.\par \par \uc2 So we will watch this flood threat continue through today through tomorrow and into tomorrow night. We absolutely have to watch this. Very, very heavy rain continues to head off toward the East as we go on in time.\par \par \uc2 Tony, for the latest coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Boy man, this atmosphere just turned over in a big hurry. Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know, but you`re staying dry in that studio even though the graphics are very wet behind you. Three inches of rain in an hour. Think about that.\par \par \uc2 Jim Cantore, thank you very much\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: It`s crazy. Crazy.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Definitely crazy.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas now, a deadly attack outside the Russian capital has many people wondering about a possible link to the war in Ukraine. A bomb destroyed a car owned by a prominent adviser to Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The adviser`s daughter, TV commentator, Darya Dugina was killed in the explosion. Charlie D`Agata has more from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Cell phone video set to show the fireball from the car bomb on the outskirts of Moscow. Dead at the scene, Darya Dugina, daughter of influential ultra-nationalist, Alexandra Dugin, an outspoken supporter for the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Kyiv quickly denied responsibility. The President`s Office saying "Unlike Russia, we are not a terrorist state."\par \par \uc2 The target is believed to have been Dugin himself, a man often described as Putin`s brain.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: It does sound as if Mr. Putin is following your blueprint.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: He spoke with "60 Minutes," Lesley Stahl in 2017 about his book promoting Russian expansionism, including the annexation of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER DUGIN (Russian political analyst): . a little bit disappoints us because it takes too long. So --\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: But it did the first steps.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER DUGIN: First step in the 17 years.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The attack has escalated tensions in the run up to Ukraine`s Independence Day on Wednesday, and six months since stunned Ukrainians heard this sound for the first time.\par \par \uc2 And there go the air raid sirens, it`s just another indication that although the city seems normal, nobody is running to the shelters, this city, this country is still in a state of war.\par \par \uc2 The Central Square in Kyiv has become a trophy case of Russian tanks and armored vehicles captured or destroyed in combat now climbing frames for kids, and backdrops for selfies.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: President Zelenskyy has warned that Russia might be planning something particularly nasty to spoil Independence Day celebrations in revenge.\par \par \uc2 Six months after trying to capture the Capitol itself, probably not the Victory Day Parade President Putin had hoped for.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Charlie D`Agata, Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Back here in the United States, Arkansas State Police are investigating a violent arrest caught on video. We should warn you; this footage is extremely graphic and disturbing.\par \par \uc2 It appears to show a man being violently attacked by officers as he is pinned to the ground. Nikki Battiste is here with more on what happened.\par \par \uc2 Nikki, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Vlad, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This happened Sunday in Crawford County, Arkansas near the Oklahoma border. One officer is on leave and two Deputies have been suspended pending the State Police investigation as the authorities share their version of what happened.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Disturbing cellphone video appears to show two Crawford County Deputies and one Mulberry Police officer pinning Randall Worcester to the ground and repeatedly punching him in the head and kneeing him in the legs.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is bad.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Then one officer lifts Worcester`s head up and smashes it into the ground.\par \par \uc2 The video does not show what happened before the arrest.\par \par \uc2 The Crawford County Sheriff told our \uc2 CBS\uc2  affiliate, KFSM, it started around 11 miles away in Alma, Arkansas. He said Alma Police responded to a call about a man making terroristic threats against a gas station employee.\par \par \uc2 The man allegedly spit on the employee and threatened to cut off their face. He then biked to Mulberry where the officer and Deputies in the video met with him.\par \par \uc2 The Sheriff said the conversation started calmly, but escalated when Worcester allegedly pushed one of the Deputies to the ground and punched him in the back of the head. It is unclear if Worchester was armed.\par \par \uc2 KEITH TAYLOR (Former police officer): The banging of the head against the ground. No, that is not appropriate.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Former Police Officer, Keith Taylor is an adjunct Assistant Professor at the John Jay College of Criminal Justice. He says he sees little justification for the officer`s actions.\par \par \uc2 KEITH TAYLOR: The punching in the face was unrelenting. I can understand if you`re in a battle with the person, but you`ve got one person weighing on and the other person is kicking the heck out of his legs or lower body. Those are serious uses of force.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: State Police say Worcester was taken to a local hospital for treatment and then later jailed. He faces numerous charges including terroristic threatening, resisting arrest, second-degree assault, and second-degree battery.\par \par \uc2 The big question I have is whether or not there was any body camera video and we just don`t know yet.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well, certainly once they saw that people were shooting on cell phone cameras --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: You see them pointing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is hard to watch without wincing.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the question is not whether he was a bad dude or not. The question is whether that was objectively reasonable. That`s the standard.\par \par \uc2 Nikki, thank you very much. We`ll keep following it.\par \par \uc2 Now to some disturbing allegations against actor, Gary Busey, who has been charged with multiple counts of sexual misconduct, including two counts of criminal sexual contact after a complaint from attendees at the Monster Mania Convention in Cherry Hill, New Jersey. That`s a suburb of Ph