rned police officers whose job now is to serve and protect. Most have traded in the traditional clothing they once were on the battlefield for these new fitted uniforms and caps, but it`s going to take a lot more than uniforms to turn former \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighters into officers of the law.\par \par \uc2 (IMTIAZ TYAB speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: We went on a ride-along with these recruits who confessed they aren`t finding their new jobs easy.\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  tries to remake its fighters into a professional police force, it is facing its own challenge from the Afghan affiliate of ISIS, known as ISIS-K.\par \par \uc2 According to the UN`s mission in Afghanistan, this past month is the deadliest the country has seen since the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 `s takeover one year ago.\par \par \uc2 Just last week, the suicide bombing at this mosque in Kabul killed 21 people, including the Imam. Although no group has yet claimed responsibility, it follows a similar pattern, including an attack a week earlier claimed by ISIS-K, which killed another high-profile cleric who had close ties to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ISIS-K first emerged in 2015. It is linked to the original Islamic State branch in Iraq and Syria that is made up mainly of defectors from the Afghan and \uc2 Pakistani Taliban\uc2  who don`t see those groups as extreme enough.\par \par \uc2 Last year in the final days of the US` chaotic withdrawal from Afghanistan, a lone suicide bomber detonated a device that killed 13 US servicemembers and at least 170 Afghans at Kabul Airport, an attack claimed by ISIS-K.\par \par \uc2 Today, the now infamous Abbey Gates where the bomb went off is quiet, but signs of the horrors that happened here still remain.\par \par \uc2 It`s hard to imagine one year ago, this was the scene of such desperation as Afghans tried to flee the country and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . And all we really have left to remind us of their desperation to 