s and cutting its carbon footprint. Kris Van Cleave has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Indianapolis maybe the Circle City, but 30 minutes north, a community is really sending drivers for a loop.\par \par \uc2 Round and round we go.\par \par \uc2 JAMES BRAINARD (Carmel, Indiana Mayor): Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Carmel, Indiana is home to 142 roundabouts and counting, more than any other city in the US, something Mayor James Brainard says makes the city safer and greener.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JAMES BRAINARD: Does it bug you that we`re stopped at a light?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JAMES BRAINARD: Two cars we`re going through this intersection, a roundabout what have moved 30 cars during the same amount of time.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: By 2025, this community of more than 100,000 will be a one stoplight town.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JAMES BRAINARD: We don`t have to pave over paradise. We can keep our roads more narrow and that`s better for the environment, it`s better for pedestrians.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The circular intersections typically have one or two lanes; while they slow vehicles down, the roundabouts actually flow more traffic, allowing Carmel to remove vehicle lanes even as its population more than quadrupled.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JAMES BRAINARD: I call it a road diet. We`re able to add a bike lane, add a beautiful median with trees and flowers down the middle. But we`re moving eight percent more traffic per hour.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 Insurance Institute for Highway Safety\uc2  found the switch to roundabouts cut injury crashes by nearly half and on busy intersections like coming off a highway, injury crashes dropped 84 percent, while total crashes plummeted 63 percent.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CICCHINO (\uc2 Insurance Institute for Highway Safety\uc2  Vice President, Research): As traffic fatalities continue to rise in the US, we really need to be using all of the tools in our toolbox and roundabouts are one of those tools that we`ve seen to be effective.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: They also create green spaces, allowing for civic art displays. This one was named International Roundabout of the Year in 2016.\par \par \uc2 And by eliminating idling traffic lights, Carmel`s roundabouts removed the equivalent of 5,000 cars worth of carbon dioxide, while saving drivers an estimated $14 million a year in gas says former City Engineer, Michael McBride.\par \par \uc2 If roundabouts work so well, why are they not everywhere in the US?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCBRIDE (Former city engineer): That`s a really great question. I still get asked why roundabouts? You know, is that some kind of Carmel gimmick?\par \par \uc2 People still really don`t understand that safety benefit.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Nationally, there are about 7,900 roundabouts. New York and Virginia now require they be considered as an alternative.\par \par \uc2 There is a bit of a learning curve to the roundabout. As you approach the circle, slow down. Remember to look left, don`t forget to yield. And when you see an opening, just go.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Around here, folks say that`s roundabout right.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Carmel, Indiana.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Very smart.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead this morning, David Begnaud is here to tell us all about his new week-long series, "How Are You Now?"\par \par \uc2 How are you, David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Good morning, my friend. I`m well. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 All this week, we`re going back to check in and visit on some of the most memorable people we have introduced you to.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we start with a story that is both personal and deeply private, for two families who didn`t know each other, but met at a time of both joy and grief.\par \par \uc2 There is the happiest of endings to this one. We`re 