c2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: As we should all be able to do.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is a very painful story. Good to know that Finland has a broken politics and media as much as we do here in this country and that is an actual major national story that the leader the country has to respond to.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just to blow off some steam.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And take a drug test.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But get this, there are women who are posting videos all across social media in solidarity.\par \par \uc2 (Video clip montage plays.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at this, like this is so great. Women are posting videos like this saying that they stand with Sanna, because they`re just sticking up for each other and saying "It`s okay."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at this. There may have been drugs involve with this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But who cares? It`s wonderful. It is women who are showing that solidarity and saying there is nothing wrong with dancing a little. We all do it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is a non-scandal. I don`t -- I don`t do it actually.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You don`t, not at all?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You should do it.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Just a little?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Your little move, Tony?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I had a friend recently say it`s the worst time to be a reasonable person.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is -- I identify strongly with that quote.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like that.\par \par \uc2 All right, last story. A great grandmother literally got a sweet surprise when a great granddaughter brought her the fruit that she has been missing from her backyard in Jamaica. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It is this one? Oh my God. You are the best great-granddaughter I`ve got in the whole wide world. Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re the beat great-granddaughter in the whole world, my whole life. Ninety-year-old Enid Wilson`s (sp?) home is being renovated and she has been staying with her family in England.\par \par \uc2 She has been getting homesick lately. So her great granddaughter, Kalia (sp?) decided to give Wilson that taste of home known as ginup or Spanish lime. That is a very slight sort of tangy little fruit that she was missing from her backyard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, apparently, sweeter than a lime and with a little bit of a tamarind flavor, like a tang to it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I could go for some.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I honestly -- make a cocktail. Travel\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes, right. Let`s make that up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Does it travel well? Why don`t we have more of them?\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, The Monkees` Micky Dolenz shares memories of the pre- fab four in our "Morning`s Mixtape." Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, The Monkees, one of the most successful bands of all time and for our "Morning`s Mixtape" series, Mickey Dolenz, the last surviving band member tells Anthony about keeping the band`s spirit alive.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I`m Jamie Yuccas, with "Top Gun: Maverick" soaring at the box office, I`ve been doing some aerial maneuvers of my own. Actor, Jay Ellis callsign "Payback" takes me inside the danger zone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. David Begnaud revisits two families with a very special bond, which David`s own mom helped create.\par \par \uc2 He has got a new series that is called "How Are You Now?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like the title. I like the sound of it. He`ll be here all week and so, we`ll we.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 A system that brought extreme flooding to the west could now endanger the Eastern US.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: We will watch this flood threat continue through today through tomorrow and into tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Arkansas Police are investigating a violent arrest where officers are seen on video punching and slamming a man who is pinned to the ground\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: One officer is on leave and two Deputies have been suspended.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: CBS News rides along as the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  tries to establish a police force while battling a deadly threat.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: It is facing its own challenge from the Afghan affiliate of ISIS known as ISIS-K.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And it`s the latest uplifting thing getting attention on the internet, a video of a father and a daughter dancing through the decades.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Those are some moves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are some moves.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They have got the move.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They like their music and we do as well, and we`re going to begin actually this hour by popping in the cassette for the latest installment of "Morning`s Mixtape," taking a look at the artist behind some of those iconic songs, songs you can`t get out of your head and wouldn`t want to anyway.\par \par \uc2 And today, we are catching up with Micky Dolenz, the last living member of The Monkees.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Last Train to Clarksville.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Fifty-six years ago as we sing along, this month marks the 56-year mark since The Monkees` debut single "Last Train to Clarksville" launched them on their way to super stardom.\par \par \uc2 Dolenz, Davy Jones, Peter Tork, and Mike Nesmith became household names as the quirky band -- made-for-TV band -- Anthony Mason, I am laughing at my own lack of knowledge. Not a household name to me, however they did to everyone else.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everybody else, except for me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anthony Mason caught up with the 77-year-old Dolenz on his latest tour to talk about how the band`s legacy lives on.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How are you doing, first of all?\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: I am above ground.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s a joke, but it`s not.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: It is not a joke.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Monkees performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Mickey Dolenz is the last of The Monkees, that madcap quartet who took pop culture by storm in the 60s.\par \par \uc2 A made for TV band, The Monkees had four albums hit number one in 1967, a feat not even a real band has ever equaled.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I`m a Believer.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Looking at the setlist, Micky, there are some songs you have to play every night, right?\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: They are the can`t-not-do`s.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Now, Dolenz alone is trying to keep the music alive.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Ladies and gentlemen, Micky Dolenz.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What`s it like being on tour by yourself?\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: Whoa. I haven`t quite processed it. After Davy passed, which was a big shocker. I remember then thinking, "What are we going to do?"\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Davy Jones died a decade ago. In 2016, the three surviving members -- Dolenz, Mike Nesmith, and Peter Tork celebrated The Monkees` 50th Anniversary.\par \par \uc2 Does the big five-oh mean anything really to you guys?\par \par \uc2 Tork told me then on "CBS Sunday Morning," --\par \par \uc2 PETER TORK (The Monkees): We will tour until one of us drops then the other will go on as The Monkee.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: When Mike passed, I couldn`t help but think of that.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: You and me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And here you are.\par \par \uc2 Tork died of cancer in 2019.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of The Monkees` performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Dolan`s and Nesmith had been in the midst of a farewell tour last December when Nesmith died of heart failure, three years after recovering from a quadruple bypass.\par \par \uc2 But he wanted to do that tour.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: Oh, he insisted on doing it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Why do you think he was so insistent?\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: Well, he always talked about the last of these Monkee experiences being kind of a swan song.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Over the years, Nesmith had skipped most of the reunion tours.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: He, at times had a bit of a problem with the Monkee thing. It`s like he said, "Oh, I get it now. It`s meant so much to so many people for so long."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I guess there was a doubt in him at some level.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: Always. Always had a doubt. But we all do. I`m thinking why is someone like you this huge, incredibly famous, brilliant journalist talking to me?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Because the first record I ever bought was a Monkees` record.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: I got nothing for that record. Do you mind?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Monkees` performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Back in 1966, they were just young actors playing a band.\par \par \uc2 You were probably on salary, right?\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: $400.00 a week.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What?\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: Including the concerts.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Seriously.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: For ten and twenty thousand people. Am I bitter? No. I am not bitter. No, no, I`m not. I mean, it`s been a blessing. Incredible blessing, given me an amazing life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Monkees performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The Quartet had answered this ad in "Variety." "Looking for four insane boys."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Monkees audition.)\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: Davy had a very ballad/Broadway kind of feel, a beautiful voice.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Monkees audition.)\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: Peter was down home, folky.\par \par \uc2 Nes of course was flat out country to start with.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Micky would sing lead on most of the band`s big hits.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: I was the only one who could go -- sorry, sound guy.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, we know you still can do it.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: Well, yes.\par \par \uc2 Donnie Kirshner and the producers would call me in at the studio at the end of shooting the television show, 10 hours a day. Okay, you`re going to sing this. What`s it called? "Last Train to Somewhere."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The Monkees ran for just two seasons. Fifty-eight episodes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Monkees.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But reruns kept reintroducing them to new audiences.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Monkees.")\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: The whole becomes greater than the sum of its parts and you don`t know why. It was us. It was the music. It was the show. It was the writers, the directors. Everything.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Dolenz has tried to keep his mates with him on stage by playing old home movies like these.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: And so I made it like a celebration.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How did that feel for you?\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: Very mixed emotions. Oh, boy. I can`t watch some of the video to this day.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Monkees" performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Do you consider it a responsibility to keep this going or it`s something --\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You do.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: And I love singing those songs. How can you not love singing? "I`m a Believer." Just such great terms.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We get all of the songs though.\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I`m a Believer.)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s an amazing piece, Anthony. You guys really got along you and Micky as well.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, I love Micky. He`s got -- listen, Micky is one of the great rock singers in my view, always has been, terrific voice. That`s part of why they were so successful.\par \par \uc2 By the way, "I`m a Believer," written by Neil Diamond. A lot of people still don`t know that. It is kind of what put him on the map as a songwriter.\par \par \uc2 The Monkees had the best songwriters in the business. The producers went out to Carole King, Neil Sedaka. They all wrote for The Monkees.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, it is so wild to me because as a listener, I even have a personal connection to a lot of those songs, but The Monkees themselves singing them did not originally, they became associated with them, but they didn`t write them they didn`t shut up or like --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That was part of the problem that like Mike Nesmith had. It was because he was a real musician. He actually was a songwriter. He took a song to the producers and said, hey, and they said, "No, this is not a Monkees song."\par \par \uc2 So he gave it away to a young 21-year-old singer who made it her first ever hit. It was Linda Ronstadt.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: "Different Drum" with the Stone Poneys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Mike Nesmith wrote that. That`s --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that they recognize that they give -- they have given so much joy, even though I became aware of them after the TV show was over. But the records, Davy Jones was on "The Brady Bunch."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was all --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, \uc2 MTV\uc2  started rerunning "The Monkees" back in the 80s and that gave them a whole new life with a whole new generation.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That`s where I saw them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If it comes to Paramount+, I will check it out.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. Oh, no, this is Jamie.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That`s a surprise. Take it away. It is all yours.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: "Top Gun: Maverick" hits new heights and we go dogfighting with one of the action sequel`s real live actor pilots. But first, where`s the time? Oh 8:10. Let`s check --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You have contacts in.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, as part of our week-long series, "How Are You Now?" David Begnaud pays a visit to an adorable little boy who was the focus of a story he first reported on more than four years ago. Can`t Wait for that.\par \par \uc2 This is \uc2 CBS\uc2  -- oh look at that face -- this is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: If you need any more proof that the big screen blockbuster experience is back, look no further than "Top Gun: Maverick."\par \par \uc2 Released by \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 , a division of \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` parent company. The airborne epic is still going strong, and starting tomorrow, you can download it and watch it at home.\par \par \uc2 For a victory lap, one of the film`s actors turned pilots invited us down to San Diego to fly through skies that weren`t always so friendly.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS (Actor): I am never scared.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Actor Jay Ellis is no stranger to an airfield like this. Thanks to his role in a certain summer blockbuster, he knows his way around an airplane. But usually, he is going by another name.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got the call name "Payback."\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: I did.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It was given to you.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: It was. I was curious, if you were to choose one, would it be the North Star or would it be a Gopher?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It would probably be the North Star. I`m from Minnesota and gopher is just some kind of silly.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: Yes. Well, then there you go. That`s how it actually works.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I know you know I am Gopher, so I get it.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: The one that you like the least is the one you get.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Ah.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: So you`re Gopher.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: So there we go. How did I end up here? Wearing a flight suit with a rodent`s name on it? Somehow, it is all Tom Cruise`s fault.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Gun: Maverick.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: This past weekend, his "Top Gun: Maverick" became the sixth highest grossing film in domestic box office history, knocking off the likes of "Titanic" and "Avengers: Infinity War." And starting tomorrow, it is available for digital download at home.\par \par \uc2 There`s no going back, right?\par \par \uc2 To celebrate, the film`s co-star invited me along to a friendly competition. A little something aviators call a dogfight.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Any message for Gopher?\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: Yes, it`s payback time.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I`ve never been to another airplane. How do you feel about these mountain ranges?\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: I love them. They`re beautiful. I think we should get a little closer to them.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I`m good.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: I remember the first time Tom told us that we were going to get in a jet and he turns and looks at us and tells us like you`re going to be in there every single day. You`re going to fly hours a day and you`re going to be a pilot and everyone was like, "Man, this dude is crazy. I`m not doing that."\par \par \uc2 And then you get up there and you fall in love with it. You truly fall in love with it.\par \par \uc2 Are you ready for some dogfight, Jamie?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I guess.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Payback versus Gopher. Who`s going to win?\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: You looking scared up there.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Come and get me.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: Don`t worry, I`m coming. I see you over there.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I don`t see you. That`s the trouble.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Fight is on. Fight on. Look to the left.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: Jamie, I`m coming for you.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Three, two, one. You`ve got the controls. Put up -- you just roll it upside down to the left.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Upside down and pull back?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Like that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh my gosh. Oh, this feels so unnatural.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There you go. Pull the trigger. Pull the trigger. Oh, Payback with the first kill.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I`ll give you that one.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: All right, are you ready for round two?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Let`s go get him.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, which way? Which way?\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re going to the right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: Come here, Jamie.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There they are.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: Come here, Jamie.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, did I get him. Did I get him?\par \par \uc2 MAN: There he is. She`s getting on her, fake right, fake right. Now, roll it back to the right. And spin the trigger.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I got him. I got him. I got him.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We got him.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Two has been hit.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Got you. Going down, sucker.\par \par \uc2 Is this how you were trained, Payback?\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: Yes, so this is basically how we started out. We learned like the basics of flight and then once we got comfortable with that, we did a lot of balmy runs, a lot of low-level runs and then pulling G`s to get out of there.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: When did you stop being nervous?\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: About 30 seconds ago.\par \par \uc2 MAN: All right, it is time for the tie breaker. Who wants to lose?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I think I can only do one more.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: That`s all I need.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Let`s let him jump easy. Two cats going at it. Head-to-head.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You guys are all crazy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Three, two, one. You`ve got controls. Go get her.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: Come here.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Uh-oh. Where is he?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, he`s on our six.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Eastward. Bank right. Missile lock. My three o`clock. Get flares.\par \par \uc2 MAN: All right, now pull the trigger. Pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`ve been hit.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Okay. Oh God.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: I told you, I only needed one more.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: As we came in for a landing, it was time for one final tradition that Jay pulled straight out of the film.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Gun: Maverick.")\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: It looks like we`ve got to do 200 pushups now.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I`m all about following tradition. But Tom`s right, that is a lot of pushups.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Sixty-four.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I really did about five.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Earned your wings.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I want to give a special shout out to my amazing pilot, Christoph Narco Webber down at Sky Combat Ace in San Diego. I was scared the entire time, you guys, and he kept me calm, cool, collected.\par \par \uc2 I got on the ground. People keep asking me, "Did I barf?" No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nice.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: How many G`s did I pull? Seven and a half, almost eight.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of G`s. That`s a delightful piece.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You earned your wings.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Got the trash talk down, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the "Talk of the Table." We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Coming for you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Love that.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Up next, we`ve all seen the articles asking where are they now? While we`re asking a more important question of some of the loveliest folks we`ve met over the last several years.\par \par \uc2 "How are you now?" Today`s story is all about family, the family you`re born into, the family you can find with a bit of faith, in my own family believe it or not. That`s how we found out about the story.\par \par \uc2 I`ll show you what we mean, next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning. Vlad, doing the week in the gun.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I had to move down. A little BTS. Got to do it.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: All right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What have you got, Jamie?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You butter believe this one. You like that?\par \par \uc2 May "Talk of the Table" is and surprise engagement in front of a famous Iowa attraction.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Paige Reed at Joseph`s Jewelers.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: The bride-to-be said "yes" in front of the famous butter cow sculpture at the Iowa State Fair. I love a butter sculpture.\par \par \uc2 The groom planned the whole thing even getting the artist involved in it. Needless to say, it all went down like butter.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They got into the refrigerator, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was going to say because I`d be looking at thing.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well, so the artist, that`s how we got the artist involved is that the artist came out and said "Oh, would you want to come in and see?"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And it`s 600 pounds of butter.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Vlad, your "Talk of the Table" is about how you lick butter?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love butter. But for my -- I actually switched up my "Talk of the Table" just a few minutes ago because I wanted to bring attention to a video I saw on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . Tony just as early as this morning said "I don`t dance, dude. I don`t know how to dance."\par \par \uc2 But wait, what`s this?\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Tony Dokoupil dancing.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You`re right. You don`t know how to dance.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Maybe, maybe I finally believe you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wait a minute, is \uc2 Instagram\uc2  public?\par \par \uc2 No, so the -- I am attempting to learn to dance.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We don`t want to give away.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is a strong encouragement of this very show.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is the tease, because everybody in America is going to see Tony`s moves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Also my upper thigh apparently.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love them little, little short, shorts.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do like the short shorts. This is the jazzercise class. I`m going to be an aerobics instructor on --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh great. Okay, great. Yes, I`ll sign up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is my great privilege, actually, not only move on, but to move on to a story I am very excited about.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" time is dedicated to a new series that we are debuting and featuring all this week. Our lead national correspondent, David Begnaud is going to be checking in with people he has introduced us to over the past few years and the goal is to answer one simple question: How are you now? Not where are you, how are you?\par \par \uc2 Today, we are revisiting Lafayette, Louisiana, David`s hometown to catch up with two families brought together by chance through a little boy who is now four years old. They would never have met, but for the memory of one very special man.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: When this baby boy entered the world in Lafayette, Louisiana in 2018, his parents Connie Despanie and Benjamin Hall named him Kingston, but they didn`t have a middle name.\par \par \uc2 It came to them by chance in the form of a handwritten letter that they had received at the hospital. Kingston was just minutes old.\par \par \uc2 A surveillance camera at Lafayette General Medical Center captured a stranger asking for directions so that she could deliver that letter. Who was she?\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE (Kingston`s mother): To my dad`s Angel: Even though I will never know your name, you are the first child born here after my dad`s passing. When one life is taken, another is given. Please keep my dad in your prayers.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT (Loss father): His name is James, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: This was that stranger, Jamie Fontenot.\par \par \uc2 You see shortly after her father, 86-year-old James Lee Grimmett died in the hospital. Jamie heard sweet music.\par \par \uc2 It`s a lullaby that plays throughout the hospital. Families of newborns come here and they press that button in the Labor and Delivery Ward, playing the lullaby, letting everybody know a newborn just arrived.\par \par \uc2 On January 12, 2018, Kingston Hall was born right after Mr. Grimmett died. The timing seemed more than coincidental.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: Somebody said, "Well, then that`s Dad`s angel."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So with no escort, you just took your letter and --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: I just took my letter and I went to the OB unit, I said my dad passed away about 10:40. So give this to the first baby that was born after that time.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Dr. Jennifer Pugliese and nurse Sydney Begnaud received Jamie`s letter. Dr. Pugliese gave it to Connie.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER PUGLIESE (Obstetrics & Gynecology Specialist): And she started reading it silently and she just had tears streaming down her face and it was really beautiful.\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY BEGNAUD (Nurse): And I came in and then the dad says "Oh, we found our middle name," and I`m like "Great. What is it?" And he says "Well, it`s James, of course." And I`m like, "Oh, yes of course."\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: My nurse followed me that entire time and she was like "Well, do you mind if we get your information and give it to Miss Jamie?"\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We asked Connie, do you remember who that nurse was?\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: I know her name was I think, Cindy?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Sidney.\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: Sidney. Yes. I think it was Sidney.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So that`s my mom.\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: Really? What a coincidence. That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Thank you, mom.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it was my mom who helped to deliver baby Kingston, and then told me the story of how a stranger wrote a letter to Connie, who then decided that the best middle name for Kingston was James named in honor of Mr. Grimmett.\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: I am so happy you came.\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: I am happy to meet you. I didn`t think I was going to meet you.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: I didn`t think I`d ever see you, either.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The best part for Jamie, holding, Kingston James.\par \par \uc2 What are we to take away from a story like this?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: Family is everything and if you don`t have faith, you don`t have anything.\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: You just never know when a blessing is going to come and fall on your lap.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So you all will stay in touch?\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: Of course. Of course.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We all want to clap. So we`re just going to clap.\par \par \uc2 Well, that was four years ago.\par \par \uc2 How are you now?\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: Good.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: Good. Great.\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: Great. Everybody is great. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: Blessed.\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: Blessed.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: Hi. Oh, how big you are.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: This is actually the first time they`ve seen each other since the pandemic started.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: He`s got it mastered mom.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Baby Kingston just started pre-K.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: There you go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: When you see him, do you think about your dad?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: Always.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: Yes. Always.\par \par \uc2 KINGSTON JAMES HALL (Namesake of Jamie Fontenot`s father): And my name is Kingston James Hall.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What does it do for you when you hear him saying his name? Do you think about her dad?\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: Yes. And how far he has come in Kingston. He loves to say his name.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Does he?\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: Yes. He`ll tell you, "I`m Kingston James Hall." I say, "Well, go ahead, baby." I can`t even describe it. Because he`s here and on top of that, he was blessed with an angel.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: Coming into the world.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Jamie has been following every milestone, even putting together a special picture book for Kingston`s baptism.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: He was 86 years old.\par \par \uc2 I wanted something, you know, sort of set-in stone that he could have through the years that he could look back at when he was born and the story behind what happened.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What`s happened between the two of you in the last four years?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: Well, we talk. We text. She sends pictures.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What do you hope this story does for other people?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: Share that good things still happen, that strangers can become family.\par \par \uc2 CONNIE DESPANIE: That like everybody can come on one accord and love one another unconditionally without even knowing each other.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FONTENOT: That`s it? Yes, definitely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Strangers can become a family.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Such a sweet peace piece.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am tempted to clap as you did in the piece.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes, I know, right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a great reunion. What was it like for you going back and seeing your mom and this other family that`s now so connected to yours?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Look, it`s gratifying because it`s my hometown. Right? So I love telling stories from home. You know, I remember when my mom first told me about this story and she didn`t tell it very well. I went to bed that night and I called her --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is not a story mom.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I thought, I called her back the next day and I said, "Wait, you delivered a baby and they named the baby after the man." She goes, "Yes, yes, that`s what happened." I was like, "Mom, that`s a great story." I`ve got to tell the story.\par \par \uc2 So --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love this connection though that they have and this notion of unconditional love between strangers, between your neighbor all wrapped around in this little bundle of joy and this guardian angel.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: We need more of it, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, before he -- the day that he was born. It`s an incredible story, David. Really well done.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Thank you, mama.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you, Mama Begnaud.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On that note, you took care of the storytelling. You got that part down, but she gave you the faith and the love and the belief in family that is the most important thing and you can see that every day in your storytelling.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Thank you. And by the way, mom is now retired. She gave COVID shots at the start of the pandemic, did that, and now she`s just enjoying those grandbabies.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hi, mom.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: So --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re supposed to say hi, mom.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Hi, mama.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, talk up your stuff coming up.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Please. Yes. Tomorrow, by the way, we check in with a South Carolina photographer turned nurse who has been documenting the pandemic through a pretty unique lens. He explains how his deeply emotional photos have changed his life since we first told his story.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It`s great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m bummed I`m going to miss a series. I`m going to be on vacation.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s all right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Completely different time zone.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You can record it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I`m got my --\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Well, CBSnews.com.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right, baby.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Paramount+ app. You won`t be that bummed when the plane lands.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you very much. We`ll see you again tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, important career advice for women of color from news producer and reporter Daniela Pierre-Bravo. She is in our progressive greenroom to talk about harnessing the power of what she calls "The Other."\par \par \uc2 But first it is time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO (News producer and reporter): Being undocumented is more than a lack of status. It`s a feeling and it is always there with you.\par \par \uc2 Am I good enough? Am I worthy? Will I ever belong?\par \par \uc2 And you know most of us feel that way at some point in life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That was news producer and reporter, Daniela Pierre-Bravo in her TED Talk "From Undocumented to Unstoppable."\par \par \uc2 Now she`s on a mission to help other women of color follow in her footsteps and find success in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 As an immigrant from Chile, Pierre-Bravo spent many years undocumented and in the shadows. She worked odd jobs to pay her way through school before finding an opening in the media industry, and eventually becoming a reporter on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 In her new book "The Other: How to Own Your Power at Work as a Woman of Color," she writes about her journey and the lessons she`s learned along the way.\par \par \uc2 Daniela Pierre-Bravo, good morning to you. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Good morning. Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You know, what I love about the book is that you talk about as an immigrant, that there are really great things about entering the workplace of hustling and having this work ethic. But then you talk about how that can change and it is not so great anymore.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes, well, the immigrant ethos is a beautiful thing, right? My mom and my grandma always taught me to put my head down and do the work, right, be grateful for everything. It`s the energy that got me from undocumented to be able to go to college, to getting on an 18-hour bus ride, because I lied on my resume and said that I lived in New York, just so that I could get an opportunity to get an unpaid internship.\par \par \uc2 But you know, by the same token, when we advance in our career, especially for women of color, when we`re constantly leading with that overly grateful mentality to the point where we fear asking for more, because we don`t want to be ungrateful, it can kind of not work in our favor.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, if you put your head down and do the work in corporate America, if you don`t let people know what you`re doing, no one is going to notice.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes, you`ve got to brag about yourself.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s right. Don`t be humble.\par \par \uc2 The book is being not being humble, I`ll not be humble for you. You`ve got a great voice, you`ve got a delicate touch, it`s going to help a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 And you describe a moment in the book is sort of part career advice and then part memoir. And in the memoir part, you talk about a breaking point for yourself, when you started thinking about change. Can you talk about that breaking point?\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes, I think for a lot of women of color, we experience this breaking point of burnout. And you know, for a lot of our communities, we have been in constant survival mode, that fight or flight is very familiar to us. So we tend to find and recreate those moments in our career, to the point where we feel like our worth is predicated on our productivity, and it is hard for us to shift that mindset.\par \par \uc2 And we know that as women go on in their careers, we need to play by different rules. We can`t be constantly in that customer service mentality and it is hard because that means that we need to say no to some things that we advise.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: How do we do that?\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: And it is hard, right? And especially for women of color, because we don`t want to cause that cognitive dissonance. We don`t want to put people at dis-ease, but it`s so important as we move along in our career.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You know, I really try to cheerlead other women, but I even find it hard to cheerlead myself. What are some tips for people that you would, if you`re speaking to other women that you give them?\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes, I mean, I think that, you know, we`re so lucky now, especially Gen Z, as we -- you know, as they come up in the workplace. Gen Z is going to be the most diverse generation out there.\par \par \uc2 And when I think about as a woman of color, who is sometimes the one and only in a room, it`s not just about me, right? It`s about the advocating that I do for myself, but the more room that I leave for other women behind us, and I think that`s important that we`re not just cheerleading ourselves, and it is not just about us, but it`s about the door that we leave even wider for the women after us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Pay matters. Pay negotiation.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What tips you have on that front, and you write about why even if you get a no at first, you shouldn`t stop at no?\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes, well, there`s a lot of great tips on negotiation in the book, but that`s exactly right. It`s so important to not leave that negotiation and just assume the no is forever.\par \par \uc2 So what you want to do is you want to make sure to talk to your manager and say, okay, what are the metrics that I missed? And what are the qualifications and the metrics that you`re looking for, so that I meet them? And make sure that you don`t leave there without putting a time on their calendar, whether it`s three or six months to have this conversation to check in, because you don`t want to wait a full year for the next performance review.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You really have to know your worth though.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Sometimes that can be really hard.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Yes. Well, that`s why it`s so important to talk about it and talk about it often. You know, in this remote work kind of life that we`re going through, it`s even more important. It`s more than okay, it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 And you know, it`s hard to be a manager at this time. There`s a lot of things that they`re juggling, so help them help you. And one way by doing that is to make sure that you`re having consistent conversations, whether it be weekly or biweekly about what are the wins that you`re bringing to the table.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I like that a lot. Daniela Pierre-Bravo, thank you so much for being here. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA PIERRE-BRAVO: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: "The Other: How to Own Your Power at Work as a Woman of Color" goes on sale tomorrow. You can pick it up.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. Easy-peasy.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Pull the trigger. Pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 JAY ELLIS: Ah.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`ve been hit.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Okay, oh my God.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I really did not want to take control of that airplane.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The pilot behind you, he`s got a big smile on his face as you`re going "ah."\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well, he`s done it a time or two.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, the questions you got were, did you throw up?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Which was no. How many G`s did you pull?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Seven and a half almost eight.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Seven and a half. Is Jay Ellis as handsome in person as he appears to be on TV?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: He is even better looking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Even better looking.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh my gosh. He is so handsome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. You know --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: His wife is beautiful too. They`ve got a great marriage.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m sure they do.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: He is not in the market, ladies.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That does it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220822ei8m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Workers Across The Country Are Demanding Higher Pay And Better Work Conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Elise Preston \par \uc2 837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, workers from California to New York are demanding higher wages and better working conditions. They`re staging walkouts and going on strike, amid a Gallup poll showing worker stress is at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Elise Preston reports on growing work dissatisfaction.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voice-over): Fed up and frustrated, more than 150 \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers walked off the job, calling for a $5 raise and safer conditions at this California air freight facility.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: More than 2,000 \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  mental health care workers now on strike, they say they are stretched thin from the workload.\par \par \uc2 And, in Minnesota, nearly 15,000 nurses voted to authorize a strike.\par \par \uc2 BRIANNA HNATH (ICU Nurse, North Memorial Health Hospital): We have been through hell and back. And we will not stop fighting until we are given a good and fair contract.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: According to new Gallup poll, half of workers are stressed, and one in five battle anger or sadness during the day.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This job sucks. Like, this job is not fun. And we`re not getting paid enough.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Dissatisfied and disengaged workers can cost the global economy $7.8 trillion in lost productivity. This summer, more than 415,000 American workers stopped clocking in.\par \par \uc2 What is the message to companies moving forward?\par \par \uc2 ANNELIZABETH KONKEL (Indeed): They need to look at how to retain workers. It`s not just getting workers in the door.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Mother of two Sara Fry is getting called back to her \uc2 AT&T\uc2  office in September after working remotely during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 SARA FRY (Mother): I`m like a lot of folks. And there`s a panic setting in. Some persons have actually said they`re literally weeping every day thinking about how they have to change up their day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: We have not heard back yet from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  says the company has been negotiating with the union for more than a year. Now, with nearly two jobs for each unemployed person, workers are in the driver`s seat. And if employees are not getting what they want, they may consider a job switch -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elise Preston, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead: why hearing aids are about to get a lot cheaper and easier to get.\par \par \uc2 And the U.S. military shows off its firepower in the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A show of U.S. force overnight in the Pacific. The military tested an unarmed Minuteman III ICBM. The missile was launched from California, traveling more than 4,000 miles to the Marshall Islands. The test was delayed to avoid escalating tensions during China`s show of force after House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s Taiwan visit.\par \par \uc2 All right, tonight, we`re learning of a major crackdown on illegal prescription drugs. More than a dozen federal agents and sheriff deputies swarmed a pharmacy this morning in Miramar, Florida, near Miami. It`s suspected of being a so-called pill mill for opioids, possibly the largest in the state. Pill mills have fueled the nation`s opioid crisis.\par \par \uc2 There`s big news tonight for people who have trouble with their hearing. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  will make hearing aids available over the counter without a prescription as soon as mid-October. The agency estimates that could save consumers nearly $3,000 on a pair of hearing aids. About 30 million adults are believed to suffer from hearing loss.\par \par \uc2 That is some good news.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back: a big change for travelers, overseas trips in about half the time.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It looks like supersonic air travel is making a comeback.\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  said it`s buying up to 20 supersonic jets from a company called Boom, as in sonic boom. United previously said it`s buying 15. The jets won`t be in service for at least seven years, but Boom says they will travel twice as fast as today`s passenger jets.\par \par \uc2 And we want to note the passing of director Wolfgang Petersen, whose action movies kept audiences spellbound. His string of hits included The Perfect Storm, plus Air Force One, in which a U.S. president single-handedly thwarts a terrorist plot, and Outbreak about a killer virus 24 years before COVID. Wolfgang Petersen died last Friday of pancreatic cancer. He was 81.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back with a group of volunteers supplying students with a bright future.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220817ei8g000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For August 5, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Nancy Chen,Steven Portnoy, Justin Michaels,  Scott MacFarlane, Kris Van Cleave, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,565 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Tonight: the surprising jobs report. Hiring surges in July, with U.S. employers adding 528,000 new jobs, more than double what economists expected.\par \par \uc2 But underneath the blockbuster numbers, worrying economic signs that inflation may get worse. And some small businesses are still struggling to find workers.\par \par \uc2 Extreme weather. A lightning strike kills three near the White House.\par \par \uc2 A water spout in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the flash flood risk returns to hard-hit Eastern Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news: A jury orders conspiracy theorist Alex Jones to pay tens of millions more in damages.\par \par \uc2 Global tensions rise. China cuts off contacts with the U.S. on vital issues and stages military drills off Taiwan`s coast. The White House warns it`s provocative and irresponsible. Our interview with Taiwan`s top representative to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you ready for what may be coming?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Plus: Russia finally says it`s ready to talk about a prisoner swap and basketball star Brittney Griner.\par \par \uc2 The CDC`s new COVID recommendations. The nation`s top public health agency is expected to ease guidelines just as students return to schools.\par \par \uc2 And On the Road: how one woman`s passion for grammar is taking her across the country?\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the CBS Evening News With Norah O`Donnell, reporting from the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good evening. Thank you for joining us. I`m Margaret Brennan in for Norah.\par \par \uc2 Tonight: America`s economy has recovered from the pandemic. The labor market has regained of tens of millions of jobs lost when COVID first hit U.S. shores. A stunningly strong jobs report shows a hiring surge in July. Employers added 528,000 jobs last month, the biggest gains since February. The unemployment rate ticked down to 3.5 percent, matching the lowest rate in more than 50 years.\par \par \uc2 The gains were across many sectors, including leisure and hospitality, business and professional services, government and construction. But there are still flashing warning signs ahead, as inflation remains high.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of news to get to tonight.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave is here to start us off.\par \par \uc2 Good evening to you, Kris.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Margaret, that 3.5 percent unemployment number comes with a caveat, because the overall labor pool is getting smaller; 63,000 people left the work force last month alone.\par \par \uc2 And while low unemployment is usually a good thing, the recovery remains uneven.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice-over): A summer surprise: the labor market bouncing back, taking 2.5 years to undo what COVID did in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We`re at almost 10 million jobs, almost at 10 million jobs since I took office. That`s the fastest job growth in history.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The stunning July job numbers mean the economy has now recouped all 22 million jobs lost early in the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: It is very difficult to square the fact that we can create a half-a-million jobs in the month of July, and also believe that the U.S. is in a recession. So I think we put the R-word off to the side, at least for another month.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: But the news is not all good. Small businesses, like Ford`s Fish Shack in Loudoun County, Virginia, still cannot find enough workers.\par \par \uc2 TONY STAFFORD (Owner, Ford`s Fish Shack): You know I could take 10 to 12 people right now and start them to work tomorrow in each one of my restaurants.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Even after raising wages, owner Tony Stafford is down at least 20 percent of his work force and his costs have jumped by a third. Customers are also cutting back.\par \par \uc2 TONY STAFFORD: People are not buying another bottle of wine or that extra appetizer, so I really see people kind of closing their wallets up in our restaurants.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: That has to be a concern for you.\par \par \uc2 TONY STAFFORD: Absolutely. We`re just getting hit all the way, and now, all of a sudden, we aren`t getting the guests to come in the front door, which is even more concerning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Wages grew by over 5 percent last month, well behind the rate of inflation, meaning many workers are making more, but living with less.\par \par \uc2 The food pantry at St. Thomas` Episcopal Church in Richmond is seeing triple the number of families in need.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN CUMMINGS (Director, St. Thomas` Episcopal Church Food Pantry): We see people who are working their tails off at jobs every single day coming to our food pantry on the way home from work, picking up food for their children.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: What the employment numbers won`t do is slow down inflation, and that may prompt the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to pursue another substantial rate hike as soon as next month -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you, Kris.\par \par \uc2 In Congress, Senate Democrats are on the verge of a major legislative victory on a spending package aimed at tackling climate change, expanding health care, and capping drug prices.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane is here with why things on Capitol Hill are so fragile.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: No margin for error this weekend, Margaret. Let me take you inside the Capitol and show you why.\par \par \uc2 In the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , where there are 50 Democrats, 50 Republicans, any one Democrat can derail any legislation. And there are two pivotal moderates this weekend. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of West Virginia, he helped broker this deal. Arizona`s Kyrsten Sinema appears to have been the last holdout. And they made a change for her to the tax provisions.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you those. The bill includes a 15 percent tax rate for major corporations, a big boost in funding for the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , and there was a $14 billion plan to change how investments were made for investment funds. But that`s been eliminated. Senator Sinema wanted it out.\par \par \uc2 What`s all the money for? Partly for climate change investments, including making it easier for people to buy electric cars, expanding access to the Affordable Care Act and health insurance, and an attempt to lower prescription drug costs.\par \par \uc2 But this bill is a fraction of the original multitrillion-dollar plan President Biden pitched last year. If they get it, it`s a big win for President Biden and for all those men and women in the blue seats, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Indeed, it will be. We will be tracking that.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Scott.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a line of severe weather is moving across the country, from the Ohio Valley to the Northeast. Heavy rains and thunderstorms will bring threats of flash flooding, strong winds and dangerous lightning.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky is also bracing for more rain in an area already devastated by historic floods.\par \par \uc2 Another storm turned deadly last night outside the White House.\par \par \uc2 Justin Michaels from our partners \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS (National Correspondent, \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ) (voice-over): The lightning bolt split the sky, striking four people in Lafayette Park just across from the White House. Today, we learned the names of two of the three people who died, 76-year-old James Mueller and 75-year-old Donna Mueller, both from Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 One other person is in the hospital. They all were struck near a tree during the storm.\par \par \uc2 KADE CAMPBELL (Witness): All of a sudden, this lightning came. And I`m not exaggerating. It came this close to us. And we were going, whoa.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, no!\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: That storm also spawned this water spout, which made landfall as an EF-1 tornado on Maryland`s Smith Island. High winds ripped through homes, leaving a path of debris.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, hard-hit Eastern Kentucky is bracing for more.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ANDY BESHEAR (D-Kentucky): Biggest concern today is weather. We have slow-moving thunderstorms that could lead to heavy rainfall at times through Saturday.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: Debris from last week`s storm is still piled high, leaving many roads blocked. In the town of Ary, mud still cakes classrooms and the high water mark is still visible on the walls.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We have a community that`s hurting.\par \par \uc2 JAMES FUGATE (Principal, Robinson Elementary): We`re hopeful that we can get school back. We`re hopeful that we can get our community back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: Unwelcome weather here again in Eastern Kentucky tonight with more rain in the forecast, also a flood watch in place until eight tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 Also today, Margaret, we learned the president and first lady will be here Monday -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Justin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Biden is dealing with rising tension on two fronts tonight, Russia and China. As Vladimir Putin`s war in Ukraine and imprisonment of Americans continues, now China`s leaders of warning of the risk of war with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Steven Portnoy is tracking new developments at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re inspired every day by B.G.`s strength.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY (voice-over): Brittney Griner`s WNBA teammates huddled last night in a moment of solidarity after she was sentenced to spend nine years in a Russian prison.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said today he`s hopeful she will be returned.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We`re working hard.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY: The U.S. has proposed a prisoner swap to see Griner and retired Marine Paul Whelan released. It reportedly involves the U.S. sending back convicted arms dealer Viktor Bout.\par \par \uc2 In Cambodia today, the Russian foreign said his side is ready to discuss the proposal, but \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  called public discussion of the swap a problem. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  was also in Cambodia for a Southeast Asia summit, but the two men never met.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of State): They are prepared to engage through channels we have established to do just that.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY: Blinken also blasted China for what he called an overreaction to House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s recent visit. The Chinese have begun a series of missile launches towards Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: There is no justification for this extreme, disproportionate, and escalatory military response.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY: China has also called off planned talks with the U.S. on a range of defense matters, transnational crime, and even climate change.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the White House summoned China`s ambassador for a formal rebuke. Ambassador Qin Gang responded in a "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " op-ed, calling the question of Taiwan one of the very few issues that might take China and the United States to conflict.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY: The way the Chinese see it, the speaker of the House is the third-highest-ranking official in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . They say that Nancy Pelosi`s visit to Taiwan violates America`s longstanding pledge not to have formal relations with Taiwan -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Steven Portnoy, we will be tracking that story.\par \par \uc2 And the U.S. has committed to helping Taiwan defend itself, but it remains unclear if the U.S. would militarily intervene to stop a Chinese invasion of that self-governed island.\par \par \uc2 We spoke earlier with China`s top diplomat in the U.S., Representative Bi- khim Hsiao.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden indicated the military wasn`t enthusiastic about Speaker Pelosi visiting. She spoke openly about the risk of her plane being shot down.\par \par \uc2 Did Taiwan ever have that level of concern that there was a risk here?\par \par \uc2 BI-KHIM HSIAO (Taipei Economic and Cultural Representative Office): Yes, we have been living under the threat from China for decades, and we cannot let their ongoing threats define our desire to make friends internationally.\par \par \uc2 If you have a kid being bullied at school, you don`t say, you don`t go to school. You try to find a way to deal with the bully. And that`s exactly what Taiwan is doing, working on making our society stronger and more resilient, fortifying our defenses.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Beijing has sent 68 warplanes, 16 warships right off your coast. Do you believe that this is just a drill?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE BI-KHIM HSIAO: China`s behavior is unprecedented. And from the scope and the actions, it appears that they have been preparing for this for some time, way before Speaker Pelosi decided to visit Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Taiwan`s defense minister said just last year that China would have the capability to launch an invasion by 2025. Are you ready for what may be coming?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE BI-KHIM HSIAO: We do not intend, nor are we able to engage in an arms race with China. But we are going to be smart and asymmetrical about our own capabilities.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When you talk to Biden administration officials, they will say, \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , the president of China, is taking notes. He is watching what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is doing right now in Ukraine as a test case to see what he can get away with in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE BI-KHIM HSIAO: I think we are all learning lessons. We have to be stronger in our own self-defenses. We have to work hard to galvanize international support in working to deter that tragic scenario from ever happening.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You can watch more of our interview with Representative Hsiao Sunday on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Israel unleashed airstrikes in the Gaza Strip today targeting an Iranian- backed militant group known as Islamic Jihad. Palestinian officials say dozens were wounded and at least 10 people were killed, including a 5-year- old child, as well as a senior militant. Tensions in the region have been on the rise following the arrest of another senior militant in the occupied West Bank.\par \par \uc2 There`s breaking news in the Alex Jones defamation trial. We will have the details when we come back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A Texas jury has ordered conspiracy theorist Alex Jones to pay $45.2 million in punitive damages to the parents of a first grader killed in the 2012 Sandy Hook Elementary School massacre. That`s in addition to $4.1 million in compensatory damages.\par \par \uc2 The right-wing radio host falsely claimed the 2012 mass shooting that killed 20 children and six adults was a hoax.\par \par \uc2 In Northeastern Pennsylvania, a criminal investigation is under way tonight after a house fire killed 10 people, including three children. A volunteer firefighter told a local newspaper, when he arrived at the scene, he realized the victims were his own relatives. He said 14 people were living in that house. The youngest victim was 5 years old.\par \par \uc2 Up next: the CDC is expected to ease its COVID-19 guidelines. What that means for students as they head back to school.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We have learned the CDC is expected to relax some COVID- 19 recommendations in the coming days. Those new guidelines could mean big changes in how schools manage COVID infections.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Chen has those details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Look at the bows.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN (voice-over): As students go back to class, CBS News has obtained a copy of the CDC`s draft document expected to ease code guidelines controlling the spread of the virus.\par \par \uc2 Michael Osterholm\uc2  is an infectious disease expert.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 MICHAEL OSTERHOLM\uc2  (Director, University of Minnesota Center for Infectious Disease Research): This virus is going to be here with us in the days ahead, and we have got to learn to live with it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: While the guidelines are not final yet, we`re learning some of the proposed changes, de-emphasizing six-feet social distancing and removing the so-called test-to-stay recommendations, in which students exposed to COVID-19 take regular tests to stay in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL CORNELL (Superintendent, Hamburg, New York, Central School District): If the CDC is going to say we should go back to school relatively normally with minimal restrictions, then count me in.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Michael Cornell, a superintendent in Western New York, says his students need to get back to normal.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL CORNELL: What we have to focus on is making sure our kids experience joy, value, and connection in school.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Another possible change, unvaccinated people exposed to COVID- 19 will no longer need to quarantine, instead masking for 10 days, and testing five days after exposure.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You`re done.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The proposed changes are raising some concerns, with less than half of school-aged children fully vaccinated, and the majority of Americans living in community with high rates of COVID.\par \par \uc2 But Osterholm says virus is still evolving.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 MICHAEL OSTERHOLM\uc2 : So, as this virus continues to change over time, we might be revising these guidelines again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And the latest guidelines would be part of the agency`s plans to help Americans live with COVID for the long term, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next, and we end this week with a big exclamation point.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You may not know it from the text messages and e-mails you receive, but \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman found out that grammar still matters, at least On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voice-over): What would you think if you were out walking, shopping, or doing some other gerund...\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN (Grammar Table): Hello.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... and out of nowhere...\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: Do you have any grammar questions?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... a woman on the street prepositioned you?\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: Over is a preposition. Under is a preposition.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You might think, whom is this crazy lady?\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: Do you have any grammar questions?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Who sees grammar as such an imperative that she sets up a table to talk about it?\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: Because I love grammar so much. And, also...\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You couldn`t think of anything more interesting?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: But it`s the anchor. Like, right now, we are using words. This wouldn`t even be happening. We`d just be looking at each other.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: As we first reported in 2019, Ellen Jovin runs a company training people in business communications, but her real passion is linguistics.\par \par \uc2 So, to share her knowledge and have some fun, Ellen began setting up her grammar table around New York City, where she lives, and it went so well, she took the table to the collective noun that is America.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: I`m taking it on the road around the country.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: We caught up with her here in New Hope, Pennsylvania, where Ellen reminded people how to diagram sentences...\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: I bet you remember this.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... explained when to use who and whom...\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: Whom. And do you know why?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... and even answered something I have always wondered about. Does the period have to go inside the quotation mark or can it go outside in certain circumstances?\par \par \uc2 How about, "The story Steve did on the grammar lady was interesting"?\par \par \uc2 Where does the period go there, because I`m using interesting in like an ironic way?\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: Yes, I understand that completely.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: But it always goes inside.\par \par \uc2 So, in this sentence...\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But Ellen says her favorite part is settling grammar disputes between husbands and wives.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: In my experience, usually, if a couple comes up, usually, the woman is right, I mean, in my limited experience.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I`m grammarless right now.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: She hung me out like a dangling modifier, but you got to love her passion.\par \par \uc2 Since we first told this story, Ellen has taken her table across the country and even written a book about the experience called "Rebel With a Clause."\par \par \uc2 Ellen says Americans are way more interested in grammar than some cynical reporters would have you believe, and she may be right.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I mean, this guy at a red light just had to know, do you always capitalize after a colon?\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: If it`s only a piece of a sentence, definitely no cap.\par \par \uc2 MAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: That made her day.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: Bye.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: One more proper usage in Ellen`s never-ending quest to make us all gooder at wording stuff.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN JOVIN: Thank you for stopping by. Bye.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Steve Hartman, On the Road, in New Hope, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with an amazing photo that was too good to be true.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Tonight, a prominent French scientist is apologizing after tweeting a photo he claimed was the image of a distant star taken by the James Webb Space Telescope.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of people viewed the image, but it turns out it was actually a close-up photo of a slice of chorizo sausage. He said it was supposed to be a joke.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s CBS Evening News. For Norah O`Donnell, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220927ei8500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Surprising Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Kris Van Cleave \par \uc2 953 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good evening. Thank you for joining us. I`m Margaret Brennan in for Norah.\par \par \uc2 Tonight: America`s economy has recovered from the pandemic. The labor market has regained of tens of millions of jobs lost when COVID first hit U.S. shores. A stunningly strong jobs report shows a hiring surge in July. Employers added 528,000 jobs last month, the biggest gains since February. The unemployment rate ticked down to 3.5 percent, matching the lowest rate in more than 50 years.\par \par \uc2 The gains were across many sectors, including leisure and hospitality, business and professional services, government and construction. But there are still flashing warning signs ahead, as inflation remains high.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of news to get to tonight.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave is here to start us off.\par \par \uc2 Good evening to you, Kris.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Margaret, that 3.5 percent unemployment number comes with a caveat, because the overall labor pool is getting smaller; 63,000 people left the work force last month alone.\par \par \uc2 And while low unemployment is usually a good thing, the recovery remains uneven.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice-over): A summer surprise: the labor market bouncing back, taking 2.5 years to undo what COVID did in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We`re at almost 10 million jobs, almost at 10 million jobs since I took office. That`s the fastest job growth in history.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The stunning July job numbers mean the economy has now recouped all 22 million jobs lost early in the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: It is very difficult to square the fact that we can create a half-a-million jobs in the month of July, and also believe that the U.S. is in a recession. So I think we put the R-word off to the side, at least for another month.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: But the news is not all good. Small businesses, like Ford`s Fish Shack in Loudoun County, Virginia, still cannot find enough workers.\par \par \uc2 TONY STAFFORD (Owner, Ford`s Fish Shack): You know I could take 10 to 12 people right now and start them to work tomorrow in each one of my restaurants.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Even after raising wages, owner Tony Stafford is down at least 20 percent of his work force and his costs have jumped by a third. Customers are also cutting back.\par \par \uc2 TONY STAFFORD: People are not buying another bottle of wine or that extra appetizer, so I really see people kind of closing their wallets up in our restaurants.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: That has to be a concern for you.\par \par \uc2 TONY STAFFORD: Absolutely. We`re just getting hit all the way, and now, all of a sudden, we aren`t getting the guests to come in the front door, which is even more concerning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Wages grew by over 5 percent last month, well behind the rate of inflation, meaning many workers are making more, but living with less.\par \par \uc2 The food pantry at St. Thomas` Episcopal Church in Richmond is seeing triple the number of families in need.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN CUMMINGS (Director, St. Thomas` Episcopal Church Food Pantry): We see people who are working their tails off at jobs every single day coming to our food pantry on the way home from work, picking up food for their children.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: What the employment numbers won`t do is slow down inflation, and that may prompt the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to pursue another substantial rate hike as soon as next month -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you, Kris.\par \par \uc2 In Congress, Senate Democrats are on the verge of a major legislative victory on a spending package aimed at tackling climate change, expanding health care, and capping drug prices.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane is here with why things on Capitol Hill are so fragile.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: No margin for error this weekend, Margaret. Let me take you inside the Capitol and show you why.\par \par \uc2 In the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , where there are 50 Democrats, 50 Republicans, any one Democrat can derail any legislation. And there are two pivotal moderates this weekend. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of West Virginia, he helped broker this deal. Arizona`s Kyrsten Sinema appears to have been the last holdout. And they made a change for her to the tax provisions.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you those. The bill includes a 15 percent tax rate for major corporations, a big boost in funding for the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , and there was a $14 billion plan to change how investments were made for investment funds. But that`s been eliminated. Senator Sinema wanted it out.\par \par \uc2 What`s all the money for? Partly for climate change investments, including making it easier for people to buy electric cars, expanding access to the Affordable Care Act and health insurance, and an attempt to lower prescription drug costs.\par \par \uc2 But this bill is a fraction of the original multitrillion-dollar plan President Biden pitched last year. If they get it, it`s a big win for President Biden and for all those men and women in the blue seats, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Indeed, it will be. We will be tracking that.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Scott.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220806ei8500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 How Honesty And Bravery Helped A Cancer Patient Ace Her Job Interview\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Dr. Jon LaPook \par \uc2 621 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: As we know, employers consider many factors when hiring someone: experience, salary, compatibility, to name just a few. But one company had a lot more to think about with one prospective employee.\par \par \uc2 CBS News chief medical correspondent Dr. Jon LaPook explains.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @katiekickscancer on TikTok) KATIE COLEMAN (MDisrupt): I`m heading to an interview today. DR. JON LAPOOK (voiceover): When 31-year-old Katie Coleman headed to that interview in March...\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @katiekickscancer on TikTok) KATIE COLEMAN: I also have stage four cancer, which I plan to be fully transparent about.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: ... she had a lot on her mind. In December 2020, Coleman was told she had a rare form of kidney cancer that had spread to her liver and was inoperable. But several months later, her surgeon called to say he thought surgery was worth the risk.\par \par \uc2 KATIE COLEMAN: I kind of jumped up and down there in my office a little bit.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: The surgery was successful, but she was left with tiny spots on her liver that, for now, are not growing.\par \par \uc2 KATIE COLEMAN: We`re hoping many years, if not like a full life ahead of me.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: When she received an interview for a computer programming job, she decided to take another risk.\par \par \uc2 KATIE COLEMAN: Several people tell me that I shouldn`t disclose my diagnosis, but I don`t see my diagnosis as a weakness. It`s honestly one of my greatest strengths.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Ruby Gadelrab Tudor is the founder and CEO of digital health company MDisrupt.\par \par \uc2 At what point did she tell you, I have had kidney cancer?\par \par \uc2 RUBY GADELRAB TUDOR (CEO and Founder, MDisrupt): She told me and our engineering manager in that very first interview. I thought, oh, my goodness, this is the most inspiring person I have ever met.\par \par \uc2 KATIE COLEMAN: People from every walk of life just come together to really support people when they`re struggling.\par \par \uc2 RUBY GADELRAB TUDOR: Why would you not hire that amazing person? So we did.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Dr. Jon LaPook, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: When we come back, a tribute to a special member of our \uc2 CBS\uc2  family.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Finally tonight, that last story we showed you about a woman who refused to let cancer define her was produced by beloved EVENING NEWS producer Diane Ronnau, who we lost this weekend to cancer.\par \par \uc2 Diane died following a 16-year battle with pancreatic cancer. Diane wrote and produced many of the stories you have seen on \uc2 CBS\uc2  for the last 30 years. She was curious, compassionate, funny, tough, and kind, everything you would want in a colleague and a friend.\par \par \uc2 Diane loved her job, but she was defined by her family. Devotion to her twin sons simply knew no bounds. Our thoughts are with them tonight as we grieve by their side. Diane will be missed. Her memory will live with us forever.\par \par \uc2 (Graphics onscreen: Diane Ronnau, Producer/CBS News, 1961-2022)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. For Norah O`Donnell, I`m Major Garrett in the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220728ei7p000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pressure On Election Officials\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Scott MacFarlane, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 882 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, a group of election officials targeted by former President Trump after the 2020 election had some harrowing stories to tell on Capitol Hill yesterday at a hearing of the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 Republican officeholders and a poll worker in Georgia describe death threats and other forms of harassment from Trump supporters all because the former President was lying about them.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill for us. Scott, some scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Yes, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Emotional compelling testimony from these local election officials who say despite being targeted by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they stood their ground and paid a price.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In the chaotic days after the 2020 election, and weeks before the January 6th riot --\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "We, the People.)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Arizona State House Speaker Republican, \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2  testified he stood up to Trump and his legal team`s repeated attempts to overturn the election results.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE RUSTY BOWERS (R-Arizona): You`re asking me to do something against my oath and I will not break my oath.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Bowers says former Trump attorney \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was relentless claiming he had evidence of election fraud, but never producing any.\par \par \uc2 RUSTY BOWERS: He said, we`ve got lots of theories, we just don`t have the evidence.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Bowers` resistance has come at an enormous personal cost to him and his family.\par \par \uc2 RUSTY BOWERS: We have various groups come by and they have had video panel trucks with videos of me proclaiming me to be a pedophile and a pervert.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The stories of harassment and pressure were echoed in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Why wouldn`t you want to find the right answer, Brad, instead of keep saying that the numbers are right?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Where Trump and his lawyers asked the state`s officials to decertify their electors and investigate baseless claims of fraud.\par \par \uc2 BRAD RAFFENSPERGER (R) (Georgia Secretary of State): We just followed the law and we followed the Constitution, and at the end of the day, President Trump came up short.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Committee also revealed text messages between a staffer to Wisconsin Senator Ron Johnson and a top aide to then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  suggesting the senator wanted to deliver a slate of fake electors to Pence on the Senate floor January 6th, but I was told not to.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Johnson denied the claim.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RON JOHNSON (R- Wisconsin): I was not involved in this at all.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Some of the most emotional testimony of the day came from Georgia election worker, Shaye Moss, who said she and her mother have experienced harassment after Trump falsely accused them of bringing in fake ballots.\par \par \uc2 WANDREA "SHAYE" MOSS: I don`t want to go anywhere. I second guess everything that I do. It has affected my life in a major way, every way all because of lies for me doing my job.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Her mother, Ruby Freeman testified that there`s nowhere they feel safe now.\par \par \uc2 RUBY FREEMAN (Former Georgia election worker): Do you know how it feels to have the President of the United States to target you? The President of the United States is supposed to represent every American, not to target one.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The hearings resume tomorrow. The Committee says with a focus on the 11th hour interference by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the \uc2 US Justice Department\uc2  -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is holding on to some important decisions at the end of its term, including that abortion case that could strike down Roe v. Wade. Yesterday, the Justices gave a big victory to religious schools in Maine.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford covers the Supreme Court. Jan, a lot of people were watching this case. What did the Justices decide?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean this was the third case in recent years where we`ve seen the Courts strike down laws that exclude religious schools and institutions from state programs that are generally available to everyone else.\par \par \uc2 Now, in this case, the State of Maine was helping families who live in rural areas where there are no public schools pay for their tuition at private schools, but the state wouldn`t allow families to use that money to send their kids to religious affiliated schools, and the Court`s majority said that discriminates against religion.\par \par \uc2 Now, this trend had already been -- it started well before the three most recent conservative Justices joined the Court, but on the horizon now, two major cases like you said whether the Court is going to overturn Roe versus Wade, of course, and then another big case involving gun rights, more decisions next, tomorrow -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. We`re all watching. Thank you, Jan.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220625ei6m0015q\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Americans in the Frontlines; Plane Frustrations; Vaccinating our Youngest; Pushing the Big Lie; A Split in the GOP; Celebrating Juneteenth; Gun Reform Road Block; Forgotten History Honored; Listen to Them; Ringing in a Win; Diversifying the Skies; Fashion`s New Fungus Trend; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Chris Livesay, Elise  Preston, Scott MacFarlane, Robert Costa, Jim Axelrod, Vladimir Duthiers,  Kris Van Cleave; Vladimir Duthiers, Lilia Luciano \par \uc2 13,447 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday morning, June 20th. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s President warns a Russian escalation is coming. We spoke to an American who fought alongside the captured US veterans.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Letting them know that they were missing were without a doubt the hardest phone calls I`ve ever had to make in my life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thousands of flights canceled or delayed and it could be like this all summer. What you need to know.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Sad to miss Father`s Day with my daughter because I`m stuck here in the airport.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A deadly shooting in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A 15-year-old was killed and three others were wounded including a police officer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The January 6 Committee hearings resume even as members face threats from Trump supporters.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-IL): Threatens to execute me, as well as my wife and five-month-old child.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The international governing body for swimming voted to restrict transgender athletes from competing at the professional level.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is deep. And he did it again.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Jack Suwinski.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The first ever rookie to hit three homeruns in a game including a walk.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Suwinski wins it for the Pirates.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Bird for three.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Sue Bird went out with a bang in her New York finale, delivering the dagger with under 20 seconds to play.\par \par \uc2 SUE BIRD (American-Israeli professional basketball player): Gave my final shot in my final game in New York. It didn`t look like it was going in for a second.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The US Open comes down to a final putt and a surprise winner, Matt Fitzpatrick went from never having won a pro golf tournament in the US to winning the US Open.\par \par \uc2 MAN: On Father`s Day, how can it get any sweeter, joining Jack Nicklaus as the only players to win the US amateur in the US Open on the same course.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Every day is special, but it`s got to be really nice if you`re an athlete, you went on Father`s Day. I`m assuming that was his dad that he was by being. Very nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dad has taken him to all these golf tournaments over the years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, when you`re growing up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Helping him get there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Big win -- many for him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of eyes on him.\par \par \uc2 All right, thanks for joining us on this Monday morning. A Federal holiday. We`ve got a big show for you.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to look at the levers of justice this morning, including one bank`s efforts to slow the spread of gun violence in this country with the idea being to flag suspicious gun purchases the way we already flag things like identity theft. Our CBS News investigation found opposition however, to the idea from some surprising places. \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  will be here with that story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, we`re marking Juneteenth by sharing a mostly forgotten piece of history still relevant today. Enslaved people fought in Court to win their freedom. Now, there is a new monument to represent their courage, two centuries in the making.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this morning in Ukraine -- we don`t like this -- where a brutal battle is still raging for a strategic city in the eastern part of the country. Ukraine`s President predicts that Russia will escalate attacks this week.\par \par \uc2 Americans as you know, have been part of this fight as well, including two who were captured by Russian forces. Chris Livesay is in Ukraine where he spoke to another American who fought alongside them.\par \par \uc2 Chris, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 New video has emerged of those two Americans captured in Ukraine, along with a new voice from an American who fought with them and spoke to us here in Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: They wound up out here by accident. They wanted to do good and this is sort of, I suppose the unfortunate result of that.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: An American on the frontline insisting he remain anonymous because he fought alongside Andy Huynh and Alex Drueke before they were captured and resurfaced on Russian media speaking under duress.\par \par \uc2 In newly released footage, Drueke he says he has been repeatedly beaten and at one point, the two were bound, blindfolded, and forced on their knees. Drueke said he thought they were going to be executed.\par \par \uc2 What do you know about prisoners of war in this conflict?\par \par \uc2 MAN: So we were advised a while back that execution could be a possibility. I`m very happy that they did not follow through with that.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Though their whereabouts remain uncertain. At one point in this video, Drueke`s interrogator says they are in an area held by pro- Russian separatists. In that same area earlier this month two Brits and a Moroccan were found guilty of fighting alongside Ukrainians and sentenced to death.\par \par \uc2 Anything you would say to Andy and Alex?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I would apologize.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Why would you apologize?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Because they sort of followed me out here.\par \par \uc2 You know, we all agreed, there was no there was no leader in the group, but I feel -- I definitely feel a bit guilty, without a doubt. We should have taken a closer look at more humanitarian options or training options. If we did, then they wouldn`t be in the situation they`re in.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Now that fighter says he is in touch with the State Department which told him they`re doing everything they can. He also says the best case scenario for his imprisoned friends, is a prisoner swap with Russia -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Heartbreaking stuff. Chris Livesay in Ukraine. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to a frustrating situation for thousands of Americans trying to go on vacation and for people like Tony traveling to and from LA for his wife`s book event. We`re talking about thousands of passengers stranded in part because of staffing issues for airlines in the air and on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston is at New Jersey`s Newark Liberty Airport.\par \par \uc2 Elise, there were some big, big problems over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Absolutely frustrating, Nate. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Within the past 72 hours, more than 14,000 flights have been canceled or delayed. Here at Newark, 40 flights have been grounded already just this morning. This, as airports across the country are desperate to get back on schedule.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Understaffed airports and airlines are being slammed and it is taking a toll on travelers.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: At this point, I don`t have any high hopes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are talking 10 hours.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: More than 900 flights were canceled on Sunday alone. Delta getting hit the hardest with more than 200 cancellations. Delta apologized saying in a statement: "A variety of factors continue to impact our operations, including challenges with air traffic control, weather, and unscheduled absences. Canceling a flight is always our last resort."\par \par \uc2 Since the start of June, US airports have seen an average of 2.2 million flyers every day.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: They didn`t tell them why it was canceled, but they canceled everybody`s flight.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The industry has been struggling receiving tens of billions in Federal aid since the start of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg met with airline executives right before the weekend, telling them to stick to their flight schedules and to hire more customer service agents, giving them until the Fourth of July weekend to do better or face potential fines.\par \par \uc2 CBS News senior travel adviser, Peter Greenberg.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: You can hire as many customer service agents you want to say "I`m sorry." But if you don`t fix the root problems, the apologies will continue.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got to be able to find pilots. There`s a shortage. You have to be able to find people who work under the wing, ground handlers, baggage loaders.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Until staffing increases as a whole, Greenberg expects more disruptions through September.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now after that meeting with airline executive, Secretary Buttigieg had his own flight canceled. He had to drive from Washington, DC to New York.\par \par \uc2 Now some tips to keep in mind for your own air travel, trying to book the first flight out and arrive at least three hours early -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh boy. Elise, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Nate, since you brought it up. I did try to travel this week and I had a flight canceled on Friday, two-hour delay on Saturday, and a three-hour delay on Sunday. What was supposed to be a pleasure trip, a little bit of book tour, involved was just work, work, work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you in a good mood when you got there?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so frustrating, I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know. It was tough. It was tough. I missed all of Father`s Day, actually. I got home it was dark, all the kids were asleep. My grandma had made some cards. I got to greet them in the dark.\par \par \uc2 A lot of dads missed --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But we`re glad you`re back, even though it took a while.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 All right, now to a big deal for little kids and a relief for many parents. Children six months of age to under five years of age can now be vaccinated for COVID after the CDC recommended \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and \uc2 Moderna\uc2  shots for babies and toddlers.\par \par \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 `s requires three shots at present; \uc2 Moderna\uc2  just two.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, this is the last age group to become eligible nearly a year and a half since the first vaccines were approved.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Dr. Ashish Jha. He`s a White House COVID-19 Response Coordinator. Dr. Jha, always good to have you here, especially today.\par \par \uc2 You know, a lot of parents seem to be taking a wait and see attitude when it comes to their children. What can you say to ease their concerns about getting this vaccine for the little one?\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA (White House Coronavirus Response Coordinator): Yes. Good morning, Gayle. First of all, thanks for having me back.\par \par \uc2 What I would say to folks, and what I`ve been saying is these vaccines have been thoroughly tested. Millions of children above the age of five who have gotten these vaccines are exceedingly safe, and we expect confidence in these vaccines to build over time in this population.\par \par \uc2 And so what I`ve encouraged parents to do is talk to their family physician talk to their pediatrician, you know the people who take care of their kids and have that conversation and I think over time, we`re going to see a majority of parents ending up getting their children protected with these vaccines.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How common is COVID been in the little guys, number one? And number two, if you`ve already had COVID as a child, should you still get this vaccine?\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA: So COVID has been quite common in children, actually, we think maybe almost 70 percent of kids have ended up getting infected with COVID. Still worth getting the vaccine. It really offers an extra level of protection, an extra layer of protection.\par \par \uc2 What vaccines do is they keep kids out of the hospital, and that`s why they are so effective, and everybody deserves that protection. So even if your child has been previously infected, the strong recommendation here is to go ahead and get that -- get your child protected.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, Dr. Jha. It`s Tony.\par \par \uc2 A point of clarification here. You said talk to your pediatrician if you`re a parent about these shots, but the pediatrician won`t be the one actually administering it, is that right?\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA: No, many pediatricians will, Tony. This is one of the things that`s going to look different in this campaign. You know, we know that parents are going to want to get their children vaccinated in pediatrician`s offices; some people will go to a pharmacy, some people will go to a children`s hospital or some sort of community health center.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is, I think, a majority of parents are going to want to get their child vaccinated in their pediatrician`s office, so many pediatricians are going to be offering the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And do you see these vaccines keeping up with variants so parents get a two-shot or three-shot regimen this year. And then next year, it`s another shot and then onward and upward?\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA Well, we don`t know. I mean, we don`t know what the long term play on these things are. What we know is right now, these vaccines, despite the fact that we have various variants out there, are doing an extraordinary job keeping kids out of the hospital. That`s why both CDC and \uc2 FDA\uc2  have been so enthusiastic. The scientists have been so enthusiastic about recommending these things, they`re really making a difference.\par \par \uc2 Over the long run, whether the vaccines will need tweaking or changing, we`ll have to see as the virus evolves and as the pandemic evolves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Any side effects for your kids if you get it?\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA You know, it`s very similar to the to the side effects we`ve seen for older kids or for adults, that 24 hours of some kids just you know, they kind of don`t feel as well, they feel tired. They don`t have the same appetite. But thankfully, there have not been any serious side effects of these vaccines.\par \par \uc2 And again, after giving these vaccines to millions of children, it`s really reassuring to know that for young kids, these vaccines are exceedingly safe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, well, I`ve heard that from reliable sources. My favorite grandson, Luca, will be getting one. So Dr. Jha, we thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA Excellent. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Former President Trump`s spent the weekend repeating the same lies that are now the focus of the January 6 Committee hearings.\par \par \uc2 At events in Tennessee and Mississippi, he argued without evidence that the 2020 election was stolen. He also attacked his former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  again.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill. Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 We understand that there are two more public hearings this week?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Yes, Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 They are scheduled for Tuesday and Thursday, but the focus is shifting after showing the pressure campaign from former President Trump on then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  last week. This week, the January 6 Committee says it is focusing on Trump`s pressing of local election officials to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Four days before the attack on the Capitol, former President Trump tried to pressure the man who administers elections in Georgia to change the results.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Then President of the United States): I just want to find 11,780 votes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: On Tuesday, the recipient of that call, Secretary of State, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  will be a witness as the committee investigating the Capitol insurrection focuses its fourth public hearing on Trump`s interference with local election officials focusing on Georgia and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): We will show evidence of the President`s involvement in the scheme. We`ll also again show evidence about what his own lawyers came to think about this scheme.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Tomorrow`s hearing comes after a weekend in which Trump continued to make baseless claims the election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We had a bad election. We had a rigged and stolen election.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And continue to go after his former Vice President.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  : \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  had a chance to be great.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Despite last week`s testimony that the riotous crowd got within 40 feet of Pence and the Committee`s claims Trump fueled the violence.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): He`s essentially saying, yes, I did it and I`ll do it again.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Over the weekend, we learned a violent threat was directed at a member of the Committee itself.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-IL): This threat that came in, it was mailed to my house. We got it a couple of days ago and it threatens to execute me, as well as my wife and five-month-old child.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Congressman Kinzinger says he is not worried about his personal safety, but he did predict future political violence.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, members of the Committee confirmed they have asked to speak with Ginni Thomas, wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  about her interactions with local election officials and with the California lawyer who devised the scheme to overturn the January 6 counting of the votes -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Scott, got it. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Former Vice President Pence is about to make his first public appearance since Thursday`s hearing. And for more on that, let`s bring in chief election and campaign correspondent, Robert Costa. He`s in Chicago, where Pence is giving that speech later today.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you. What do we expect from Vice President Pence`s remarks today? And what might they tell us about this split clearly between the former President and the former Vice President?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Tony, good morning. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 Later today, just across the street at the University Club in Chicago, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  will deliver what is being billed as an economic address. Like so many Republicans he wants to move on from January 6, but former President Trump clearly does not want to move on, still battling his former VP and rallying his supporters to echo his own false claims about the election.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bob, we`re having this national conversation, but there is a state level conversation going on as well. What are you seeing at the state level with regard to belief in the Big Lie about the 2020 election?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Part of the reason Pence is here in Chicago is he wants to try to run for President in 2024. He wants to be a leader of the GOP, but it is becoming increasingly hard for someone like Pence to be in that position because across the country, the Big Lie remains front and center inside the party.\par \par \uc2 Just over the weekend, the Texas GOP passed a resolution saying that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , his presidency is illegitimate and this has been repeated in other states in similar ways.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is the big worry going to 2024.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And if you`re wondering why you have a day off today, here you go. It`s the day the government celebrates Juneteenth. It`s our newest national holiday. Millions of Americans honored the end of legal slavery on Sunday. That`s the actual date.\par \par \uc2 Galveston, Texas marked the day with a parade. It was there on June 19, 1865, two years after the Emancipation Proclamation had been signed that Union soldiers arrived to order state`s enslaved people be freed.\par \par \uc2 Ninety-five-year-old, Opal Lee, there she is front and center. The so- called grandmother of Juneteenth, who helped lead the fight for a national holiday says that orders still resonates a century and a half later.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OPAL LEE (Grandmother of Juneteenth): And when they learned they were free, they started celebrating and we`ve been celebrating ever since.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Miss Lee. Fewer than half the states recognize the holiday for their government workers. Lawmakers in Mississippi, South Carolina, Tennessee and other states failed to pass the needed legislation to give their public employees a paid day off. Hopefully that`ll change one of these days.\par \par \uc2 But I keep thinking about the people that for two years didn`t know that they had been freed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s amazing. It`s astounding.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s certainly worth celebrating.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And speaking of not knowing, I didn`t learn about Juneteenth when I was in elementary or middle school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. I didn`t know about it until I was adult.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I knew a little bit about it in high school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But as an adult is where I learned about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Which is why, we need to celebrate it and in form.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s why we have an annual history lesson.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a problem, too, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is. It is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it is our job to continue to educate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, disappointing news from Naomi Osaka. See what the tennis champion is telling her fans about missing a big tournament for the second year in a world. We`d love to see her on the court, but it might not happen for a while.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, a proposal to flag suspicious gun buys the same way we track identity theft, but it was met with pushback from the financial industry. See what we found in our CBS News investigation.\par \par \uc2 This is \uc2 CBS\uc2  mornings.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming right up, Tony has your 7:30 headlines. Plus, we continue to celebrate Juneteenth, some forgotten heroes who stood up for justice are finally being honored.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Good morning. I`m Kris Van Cleave in St. Louis. We`re talking about the freedom suits. Hundreds of lawsuits brought by slaves trying to win their freedom here in Missouri, in a court of law.\par \par \uc2 Those cases lost to history for over a century are now the focus of a new monument that has been a 14-year effort to get it built. We`ll show it to you coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Better late than never. You`re going to like this story.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s get a check now of your 7:30 headlines. It is Monday, June 20th, starting with the investigation into the police response in Uvalde, Texas.\par \par \uc2 A new report says that not only did police wait more than an hour to engage the gunman, they did not even check the doors to see if they were locked outside classrooms 111 and 112 where the massacre happened. That`s according to "The San Antonio Express News" citing a person familiar with the surveillance footage, no comment yet from police.\par \par \uc2 To our second story in sports now, the controversy surrounding transgender athletes. Swimming`s international governing body has voted to restrict and effectively to ban trans athletes in elite women`s swimming competitions.\par \par \uc2 According to the policy, trans-women who have gone through all or even the initial part of male puberty will not be allowed to compete. Officials said it`s an effort to protect "competitive fairness." They also cited the "performance gap" that emerges at puberty between men and women.\par \par \uc2 The move was slammed by transgender advocacy groups.\par \par \uc2 Finally, workers at an \uc2 Apple\uc2  store in Maryland have voted to unionize by a margin of nearly two to one. Here they are in this picture celebrating, Towson, Maryland, that`s near Baltimore. They say they want better workplace protections and they are not alone.\par \par \uc2 It`s part of a big trend of retail locations unionizing of late including an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse and more than 150 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  locations.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  is not commenting on this vote exactly. But they`ve been against this sort of thing in the past. Up next, getting the votes certified and then let the negotiations begin.\par \par \uc2 Unions are having a moment in this country, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They are. Workplace protection is key.\par \par \uc2 Yes, speaking of protection. This week, Senators from both parties head back to the negotiating table over new gun legislation. Not far from Capitol Hill, a shooting last night killed a 15-year-old and injured three adults including a police officer. That happened after a fight broke out at an unpermitted music event that drew hundreds of people.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we found search for solutions is hitting other roadblocks. A CBS News investigation shows credit card industry, they`re blocking an effort to track certain firearm and ammunition purchases that deprives law enforcement of potentially identifying and stopping illegal gun crimes.\par \par \uc2 Chief investigative correspondent, \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  has the story.\par \par \uc2 Jim, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Frustrated by legislative failures, gun reform advocates in 2018 looked to the financial system to stop guns from falling into the hands of dangerous individuals. What they found weren`t politicians standing in the way, but a little known technical committee, whose members include employees of some of the biggest names in business -- \uc2 Visa\uc2 , \uc2 Mastercard\uc2 , and \uc2 American Express\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN (\uc2 Amalgamated Bank\uc2  CEO): So much illegal gun activity depends on your ability to use the financial system to buy the guns.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Priscilla Sims Brown is the CEO of \uc2 Amalgamated Bank\uc2 , founded by union workers nearly a hundred years ago. The bank bills itself as the nation`s oldest socially responsible bank.\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: We believe you can do well and do good.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: That urge to do good struck after the bank notice some of the deadliest mass shootings were being financed with credit cards.\par \par \uc2 911: People are running out of the theater. They`re shot.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: The shooter who terrorized a Colorado movie theater in 2012 charged more than $9,000.00 worth of guns, ammo, and tactical gear in two months.\par \par \uc2 And man who shot up the Pulse Nightclub in Orlando put more than $26,000.00 on credit cards.\par \par \uc2 And the shooter who killed 59 at a Las Vegas Music Festival charged almost $95,000.00.\par \par \uc2 People aren`t bringing sacks of cash --\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: They use credit cards.\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: They use credit cards.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Amalgamated believe it could run software to detect suspect purchases in the same way it detected evidence of other suspicious activity like fraud and human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: Where there may be gun sales that are intended for black markets, where we see patterns of gun purchases being made at multiple gun shops.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: So there are algorithms that could be written?\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: Exactly. And we could provide that information to the authorities to investigate.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: They`re called Suspicious Activity Reports and banks and credit unions made more than 1.4 million of them in 2021 flagging transactions that might suggest everything from identity theft to terrorist financing.\par \par \uc2 But when it came to guns, amalgamated discovered it was missing what it says was a key piece of information to report suspicious purchases, an individual merchant code unique to firearm sellers.\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: You might be a nail salon, you might be a sporting goods store. There is a merchant category code assigned to you so that we can see that purchase has been made.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: And while even shoeshine parlors have their own merchant code, the nearly 9,000 standalone US gun sellers do not.\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: We actually submitted an application to try and get that changed.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: That application went here last year, to a Geneva, Switzerland based organization, which sets standards across the financial services industry.\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: You apply to a panel, a committee of sorts, it includes credit card companies.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: \uc2 Mastercard\uc2 , \uc2 Visa\uc2 , \uc2 American Express\uc2  -- they all have representatives on this?\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: Correct.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: This Committee.\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Documents obtained by CBS News show the credit card industry pushed back. The Swiss agency said card company representatives only acted as advisors, and were there in a personal capacity.\par \par \uc2 But Amalgamated`s application was rejected -- twice. The bank was told the code for gun and ammo sellers wouldn`t identify the sales at sporting goods stores, and the burden primarily would fall on small retailers.\par \par \uc2 Wait a minute, there`s a code literally for a shoe shine stall?\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: And there`s not a code, gun and ammunition store?\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: Yes. It`s just so obvious. It`s hard to deny the logic.\par \par \uc2 JIM YURGEALITIS (Former senior Special Agent): Financial institutions provide information to law enforcement on a regular basis.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Former senior Special Agent Jim Yurgealitis spent more than two decades investigating gun crime for the ATF.\par \par \uc2 Would law enforcement benefit from a standalone merchant category code for firearms?\par \par \uc2 JIM YURGEALITIS: I believe so especially if you`re talking about large purchase amounts.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Visa, \uc2 Mastercard\uc2 , and \uc2 American Express\uc2  all didn`t say if they supported creating a merchant code for firearm sellers when asked by CBS News.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Mastercard\uc2  said it was up to elected officials to address the issue of gun violence.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of credit card companies that say, "It`s not our responsibility?"\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: Well, this is our responsibility. We have an obligation to address crime that is being facilitated through our system.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: And if somebody says, but it`s just going to, at best, make a small difference.\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: What price do you put on a live if it stops two people from dying, isn`t that worth it?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: In statements to CBS News, \uc2 American Express\uc2  and \uc2 Mastercard\uc2  said they are working to ensure only lawful purchases are permitted on their networks. The Swiss standards organization said the card industry reps who pushed back on Amalgamated`s application acted based on their expertise and do not represent the views of their employer.\par \par \uc2 After we began reaching out, Amalgamated also heard from the standards organization telling the bank that it could now reapply for the code, which Amalgamated said it plans to do this week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I liked that Priscilla Sims Brown, number one, she did not stutter when she said "It is our responsibility." I`d never heard of that merchant code category. Shoe places have it.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s nuts.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: And to her point, she said look, whether you`re a gun owner or not, if you`re a law-abiding citizen, why wouldn`t you support something?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: That could keep the guns out of the hands of people who shouldn`t have.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or buying them in a suspicious fashion, and as it currently stands, you can`t tell if the person is buying footballs and hockey equipment or firearms.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: And if it`s used for human trafficking, if it`s used to root out mortgage fraud, why can`t we?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And like she said it, if it just saves one life, it is worth it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People need to call Priscilla Sims Brown. She`s got some good ideas.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jim. Good to have you at the table, too.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Good to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Vlad Duthiers, we always like having him at the table. He`s got stories you`re going to be talking about later today. He is coming on a little early in case you`re clocking your watch by Vlad, I know a lot of people do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of people do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of people do.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, we also call this an invitation. You can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. What do you get? Today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes and we call that a deal, people.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Mr. Duthiers is here a little bit early.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A little bit early.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So glad to have you here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We will play a Beach Boys to celebrate Brian Wilson`s birthday. He is 80 years old today. Happy Birthday, Mr. Wilson. Deep voice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He sounds good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He does sound -- in fact when he was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know that`s old. I know, I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- a young teenager recording that song with the Beach Boys.\par \par \uc2 All right, let us begin this morning.\par \par \uc2 Wimbledon begins one week from today and it will be missing another star player, Naomi Osaka. The four-time Grand Slam winner has pulled out of the tournament. The 24-year-old fan told her fans -- she`s not a fan, she is a player -- the 24-year-old told her fans on social media, "My Achilles still isn`t right, so I will see you next time."\par \par \uc2 Osaka did not play at Wimbledon last year as part of a mental health break. Last month, Osaka struggled at the French Open with an injury losing in the first round. We just hope she gets better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. We just want her in tip top shape so whenever she comes back, we will be there waving our Naomi pompons, but Serena is playing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. Serena is playing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So that`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Unranked Serena.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, what`s up with that? That`s like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because she hasn`t played in a while.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s temporary.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, right. That`s the way I feel about it today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They will use it as motivation.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She will not be unranked afterwards.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay. Remember, "Be kind rewind."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Of course.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I bet you there`s people on our staff who are like, "What are they talking about?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Some of the babies.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a generation gap thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What does that mean? So let me explain.\par \par \uc2 A VHS copy of a classic movie just fetched a historic price.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Back to the Future.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That of course is "Back to the Future" starring Michael J. Fox and Christopher Lloyd. Earlier this month, a rare mint condition VHS tape of the movie -- Nate is looking at this like "Wait, what am I looking at?"\par \par \uc2 It was auctioned, Nate, for $75,000.00 a VHS tape, right? According to Heritage Auctions, it is the highest price ever paid at auction for a sealed, graded VHS tape. The seller, none other than Biff Tannen himself --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tom Wilson, who was in all three of the "Back to the Future" flicks.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Of course, he played the bully bit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s because he saw the future with the sports --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dude, exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The movie is going to get $75,000.00 for it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nate, like a tree, "Get out of here." It`s such a great line.\par \par \uc2 I have a Vladism, and you`re going to tease me, it`s one of those stories from the boring life of Vlad. In 2015, I interviewed Bob Gale, who is the writer of "Back to the Future" along with Robert Zemeckis.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Vladimir Duthiers in October 21, 2015.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And during the interview, I was telling them how "Back to the Future" was such an important film. There I am in the DeLorean. Remember, this piece, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is from 2015.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I was saying to him when I was 15, I went to see "Back to the Future" like 30 times in a row.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Back to the Future.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because I had no friends, and I was like, you know, just loving this film and he said, "Vlad, I wrote `Back to the Future` for kids like you."\par \par \uc2 And I cried.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And look how you --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I had to stop the interview.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look how you turned out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know, look how I turned out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Vlad, you`ve got lots of things.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I do have now.\par \par \uc2 All right, drumroll please. We recently told you about tribute concerts for the late Foo Fighters drummer, Taylor Hawkins. Now, 12-year-old phenom, Nandi Bushell revealed some news to us. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANDI BUSHELL (South African-British musician and social media celebrity): Vlad, I saw you talked about the new upcoming tribute concert for Taylor. Well, I have some news.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to be performing in Wembley Stadium with Mr. Grohl and I`m super excited.\par \par \uc2 It`s a privilege and I`m honored.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much \uc2 CBS\uc2  for your support and kindness. I have to head off for school. Peace out.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Nandi Bushell playing the drums.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. Nandi will be joining the Foo Fighters once again, joining the lineup for a tribute