sued for their freedom long before the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Millions of people arrived in this country as refugees, suddenly forced to flee their homelands. Today, we`ll hear just a few of those stories from those who lived them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Fight climate change and look good doing it. Why one company says the leather of the future is made of mushrooms.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, chew on that for a second.\par \par \uc2 But first here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Russia releases video of two American fighters captured in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They wanted to do good. This is sort of, I suppose the unfortunate result of that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thousands were stranded by canceled flights over the weekend. One big reason: Short staffing.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: But if you don`t fix the root problems, your apologies will continue. You`ve got to be able to find pilots. There`s a shortage.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  repeated false claims of election fraud over the weekend. As the January 6 hearings continue.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): He is essentially saying, yes, I did it and I`ll do it again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A CBS News investigation finds the credit card industry has been pushing back against efforts to flag suspicious gun purchases.\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA SIMS BROWN: We have an obligation to address crime that is being facilitated through our system.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Neil Diamond makes a rare public appearance since announcing he has Parkinson`s and joined fans at Fenway Park for a traditional eighth inning sing along.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Neil Diamond and Fenway Park crowd singing "Sweet Caroline.")\par \par \uc2 MAN: The man himself here for "Sweet Caroline." A beautiful noise.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a really sweet moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And it is a song that just makes you feel good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a very happy song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So good. So good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Better than the seventh inning stretch for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s nice. I like it.\par \par \uc2 Well, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 St. Louis is how we`re going to start today. They`re marking this Juneteenth holiday by dedicating a memorial to an important and often forgotten chapter of American history. It involves Dred Scott, the key figure in an infamous Supreme Court case.\par \par \uc2 Scott was an enslaved man who claimed that he and his family should be freed because they had lived for a time in a free state. He sued under a Missouri law and he won leading to an 11-year legal battle.\par \par \uc2 In 1857, the Supreme Court threw out that verdict, a decision that helped put the North and South on a collision course towards Civil War.\par \par \uc2 Now, here is something I bet you didn`t know -- we certainly didn`t -- Scot was not the only person to file a freedom suit.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave met a Judge descended from slavery, who fought for years to establish a memorial to them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The sound of history being remembered, a story of freedom fighters being honored, cast in more than 8,000 pounds of bronze and standing nearly two storeys tall -- this monument installation in front of the St. Louis Civil Courts Building is the emotional end to a story that began two centuries ago.\par \par \uc2 Missouri law held if a person was once free, they were always free. It allowed slaves like Dred and Harriet Scott to sue for their freedom. The Scott story spelled out in these handwritten affidavits convinced an all- White jury to free them.\par \par \uc2 LYNN JACKSON (The Dred Scott Heritage Foundation President and founder): This was really based on family preservation.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Lynn Jackson is their great, great granddaughter.\par \par \uc2 LYNN JACKSON: They were there to save their daughters. They hid them away for possibly up to two years just to keep them from being sold during this process.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH WINN (Historian): For about 20 years. There is a sort of a golden age of slave freedom suits.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Historian, Kenneth Winn was the Missouri State archivist when a court clerk made a startling discovery: The Scotts we`re not alone.\par \par \uc2 Buried in old Court records and lost to history until the late 1990s where at least 306 so-called freedom suits brought by slaves in St. Louis seeking their freedom. Winn estimates more than 120 cases were successful.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH WINN: It shows the collective effort of a people trying to be free, who did not rest and just accept their lot and slavery, but resisted it. It really speaks in some ways to people who believed in the integrity of the law.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Tucked inside this case file from 1821, the jury`s findings, handwritten by its foreman, "We, all the jurors, find that the plaintiff is free."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MASON (Circuit Court Judge): This is something that St. Louis should honor. You had to be pretty brave and courageous just to file the suit, because if you lost, the consequences could be dire.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Circuit Court Judge, David Mason, a descendant of slaves spent the last 14 years pushing for a Freedom Suit Memorial. Its installation brought him to tears.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MASON: And when I`m reading these files and reading the affidavits of the slaves, I`m hearing them. I am feeling them. I`m hearing my people. These were some of the strongest people over 500 years that have ever been produced in American society.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: These freedoms suits were hard fought legal victories. Remember, it`s the middle of the 1800s, so a slave walking into a St. Louis courtroom wasn`t allowed to read or write, couldn`t even testify in a court of law.\par \par \uc2 The Judge and jury consisted solely of White men, and Missouri was a slave state.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a time when the jury was likely biased against --\par \par \uc2 DAVID MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Against the person suing for their freedom. The Judge was likely biased in some ways against them and that they still -- the system still worked.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: What does that say about justice?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MASON: That means that it can work. Truth is powerful. Facts are powerful. Having the strength to look falsehood in the eye and call it out for what it is, is powerful.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Funded by private donations, the towering sculpture entitled "Freedom`s Home" is set to be officially unveiled as the nation celebrates Juneteenth.\par \par \uc2 The names of all 330 known freedom suit plaintiffs will be added around its base.\par \par \uc2 What do you think Dred and Harriet would say?\par \par \uc2 LYNN JACKSON: They would be happy and they would be proud, but they would totally feel like they did not deserve all of this recognition.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: A piece of American history once largely lost, well worth recognizing and remembering.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicely done, Kris Van Cleave. Talk about an uphill battle. They persisted and persevered.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the courage involved in that in that age, because if you lose, it`s a real bad scene, already is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, you speak on the odds being stacked against you. You walk into a Courtroom, you`re not supposed to be able to read or write, a Courtroom full of all White men in a slave state.\par \par \uc2 But like he said, "The truth is power."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Facts are powerful. Yes, you`re not even viewed as a human being back then.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that? No doubt about it. Some of the strongest people.\par \par \uc2 Okay, coming up, a groundbreaking leader in the military, General Richard Clark, the first Black man to take charge the \uc2 US Air Force Academy\uc2  talks with us about the importance of diversity in combat.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:07. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The \uc2 United Nations\uc2  is celebrating World Refugee Day today commemorating the strength, courage, and perseverance of refugees. And if you needed a reminder, well, the war in Ukraine is showing us once again the horror that people face when they are forced out of their homes by conflict or persecution.\par \par \uc2 Since 1975, the United States has welcomed more than three million refugees from all over the world and we asked three of them to share their stories.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA (Washington, DC): My name is Jessi Calzado-Esponda.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI (Syracuse, New York): My name is Kumari Regmi.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR (Staten Island, New York): My name is George Tarr.\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA: And this is a story --\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: of how I became --\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: A refugee.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Some of the worst fighting and the endless Civil War broke out near the American Embassy.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: I came to the United States in 1999 due to a 14-year Civil War in Liberia, my birth country.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of fighting in Liberia.)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: I was six years old when we fled Liberia. I lost a few family members, mainly my grandfather and my father. I personally almost got killed during the Civil War. I was standing on top of a hill and a bullet just came and you know, passed right by my ear.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: I was six years old when my family were forcefully evicted from Bhutan.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The country`s former ruler, King Jigme Singye Wangchuck imposed tough new citizenship laws on ethnic Nepalese.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: All people have to practice Buddhism and then speak their language and then wear their clothing, and many families have been tortured. People were taken from the community and were beaten at night, so we could hear them crying at night.\par \par \uc2 So my parents and my grandparents were talking saying that, you know, I think someone will come and we`ll be killed, so we need to leave home right now.\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA: In a blink of an eye and no fault of my own, I became a refugee.\par \par \uc2 I came to the US when I was seven years old and this happened in 1995 and we had huge migration -- after a crisis from Cuba coming to America.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thirteen Cuban boat people picked up in the Caribbean last week.\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA: People who really prefer to just risk your life and going across the sea than to spend one more day in like, economically collapsed Cuba, chaos erupted. They grabbed me, put me in this raft and I actually became an accidental refugee by being placed in this raft.\par \par \uc2 With 16 people in a dog, I could never really fully describe the intensity of the sea. But then years later, I saw a movie "Cast Away."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Cast Away.")\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA: And there was a scene where it is the water and it`s the ocean and it was thundering. It brought me back to that moment, it was exactly what I experienced.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: We made our way to Ghana where we ended up living, in the Buduburam refugee camp.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: Well, I spent 17 years in the refugee camp.\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA: After we were rescued, we were transferred to Guantanamo Bay.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: There were many days where, you know, we went to bed hungry.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: We used to eat rice in the morning and rice at night, there was no snacks or no fruits or anything. I`ve seen so many little kids dying because of malnutrition.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: Staten Island was the first place that we got resettled.\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA: I lived in Miami for a little bit, and then in Tampa, Florida.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: The first day when I came to America was the day that \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was inaugurated.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States): So help me, God.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Congratulations, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: I saw on the news everywhere like "Obama, Obama," I`ll never forget that day.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: it took a while to adjust to life in the United States.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: I think the biggest challenge was the first thing was the language, the language barrier.\par \par \uc2 CHILD: Dad.\par \par \uc2 FAMILY: Dad.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: And the other was the culture, the food, the clothing.\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA: While the community was very supportive, the children could be very cruel.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: When we first started school, due to the fact that we had a heavy accent, we were always made fun of.\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA: Sometimes I was bullied because of my accent, or because I didn`t look like everyone else. And when people found out that I wasn`t from America, children just were not educated and as children do, like they`re like, "Well, why aren`t you from America? That`s weird," right?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: We were called different African name. The ethnic question, like if I used to walk around naked? It used to really upset me which back then, I was just taking it upon myself to educate people in Africa.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: I do want it to tell people out there, listen to refugees, listen to their stories, and if you could help them, please help them.\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA: No one is a refugee by choice.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: A refugee can be anybody.\par \par \uc2 JESSI CALZADO-ESPONDA: Despite some people`s beliefs, we`re not murderers. We`re not people and women who are trying to steal your jobs. These are all people that prior to something happening to them were just regular day people, just like every day Americans, living their lives, going to school, getting married, having fun.\par \par \uc2 But it just takes a moment, you know, like the invasion of Russia into Ukraine for you to become a refugee.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TARR: I think we saw that a lot recently in Ukraine. A lot of people are saying, "Oh, this is not something you see in Europe. This is something you see in Africa." It just tells you that they don`t really know what a refugee is.\par \par \uc2 KUMARI REGMI: If you only listen to them, you will know what they`re going through and just give them the place to live, like us.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, and on this World Refugee Day, I think it is such a good reminder. I like what Jessi said, no one is a refugee by choice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right. There`s a famous poem.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is a good point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It has a wisdom in it. It says that you don`t put your child or your family in a boat unless the water is somehow safer than the land you`re stepping of off.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So true. And it`s worth reminding us that, as you know we have three million refugees since 1975, but the numbers have declined in recent years as we put new limits on who can come and it`s a reminder that they`re not coming by choice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And just to remind our kids, our children to embrace them, because we were talking about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not to be so mean.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not to be so mean.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not to be so mean.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s the type of thing that we take so much for granted, those of us who are born and raised here. So the fact that Jessi said that that scene from "Cast Away" reminded her what it took to get here, something we should think about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the country was founded by people fleeing persecution, let`s recall as well, you know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The only difference is time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do you think it`s one of the greatest places to be, but sometimes you need a reminder what it takes to get here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very nice job to you, Chris Z.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Chris Zawistowski. I know I screwed it up. We`ll call him Chris Z. And to you the editor --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s why we use the Chris Z.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, Zawistowski -- Chris Zawistowski is that right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Zawistowski -- because you know his parents are watching and I don`t want to screw up his name on national TV. Chris Z, bravo. Daniel Drake. Those pieces are very hard to do, where there`s no track, where you`re just putting together people`s stories and music and clips.\par \par \uc2 Bravo, bravo, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, the "Uplift" featuring a five-year-old boy who has every reason to look forward to summer vacation -- don`t we all -- but this little guy made it through a terrible ordeal.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have the story --\par \par \uc2 Look at his face. We will have his story right after the break.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for the "Uplift at Eight." The stories that bring you some joy.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, I want some new smile lines.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, you`re going to get some smile lines today. You`re going to be smiling about this all day long, I promise.\par \par \uc2 We are bringing you a very, very, very big celebration. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Henry Fuhrman ringing a bell.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So that is five-year-old Henry Fuhrman. He is ringing the bell after winning a two and a half year battle with leukemia. It`s a tradition for cancer patients to ring a bell to mark the end of their treatment.\par \par \uc2 Get this, guys. Henry went through 45 rounds of chemotherapy at Children`s Hospital in Tennessee. He celebrated with his parents, his nurses, and the doctors who helped him along the way. And Henry shared a message for other kids battling cancer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HENRY FUHRMAN (Five-year-old): If I can get, you can do it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pretty simple.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "I can do it, you can do it." Henry plans to spend his summer hiking, swimming, and bowling. Congratulations, Henry.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great guy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Smile lines for sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right, you`re going to be smiling all day long. I promise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he rang that bell with gusto, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is bringing it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is awesome. Oh, man. That was heart. Thank you, Vlad. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, General History. Three-Star General Richard Clark is in our progressive greenroom. We will talk about his goals as the Air Force`s Academy`s first Black superintendent. That`s right. That`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you -- yes, Harry Styles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this song. The whole album is good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The album is so good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, but that`s not our "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: ... "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who`s up first? We`ve got Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll jump right in, with another musician producer that we`re all familiar with for real. My "Talk of the Table" is Pharrell Williams surprising five NAACP youth leaders by paying off their student debt.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at what happened when they found out?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You are our next generation and we want to give you since you all love to be able to pursue your dream, be able to pursue your passions, and be able to enjoy that way of life that not only you desire, but that you deserve.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tears of joy, that`s for sure. Five HBCU students and recent graduates, they got their student debt paid off by Pharrell.\par \par \uc2 We all know his hit song, the one that we all love "Happy."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Happy."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And they are absolutely happy. And you know what else happened? NAACP President Derek Johnson, he tweeted at the President and said: "It`s your turn now to do the same for all Americans plagued by student debt."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Talk with Symone. Very nice, Pharrell, very nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, well done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He always does it right.\par \par \uc2 My Talk of table is this year`s Power Lunch with Warren Buffett. So it was just auctioned off. What do you think it went for? $5 million? $10 million?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With Warren Buffett?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With Warrant Buffett.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, people want some financial advice at the moment. So I think, invest a lot in getting that meeting so you can get -- make more money. I don`t know, a million bucks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s your number? A million?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I would say about a million or two at the most.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is I think a valuable meeting is what I am saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A million or two at the most? How about $19 million. That`s one nine. And it`s all going towards a good cause.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: An anonymous bidder paid that record amount for a private steak lunch with a legendary businessman, not even dinner, a lunch. Buffett has been doing this for 21 years. The money for the auction goes to a nonprofit, it is called Glide Foundation, which is awesome.\par \par \uc2 It works to overcome poverty, hunger, and homelessness in the San Francisco area. The annual Power Lunch auction has raised $53 million for that group throughout the years. Now, before that, the previous record was $4.5 million in 2019.\par \par \uc2 But the thing is, the winner who remains anonymous can bring seven people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, okay. All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s pretty good. Right?\par \par \uc2 So what would you ask? What would you sit and talk to Warren Buffett about?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s going to happen? What`s going to happen? Are we going to have a recession?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Save us, Warren. Save us. But I thought that was nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow. $19 million.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That lunch can change your life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Contribution.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope the refills are free.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, your turn.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, my "Talk of the Table" -- I imagine you`re in New Zealand. It is Sunday night, darkness falls and you walk outside and you look at the night sky and you see this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It looks like a blue Galaxy floating across the sky.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It looks like a painting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it looks like a painting. So some people snapped some pictures. It turns out the going theory is, of course, immediately people were like "Aliens at it again."\par \par \uc2 The going theory is that it is exhaust from a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  rocket catching sunlight in outer space illuminated in that spiral looking like a galaxy. Isn`t that cool?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Very.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is very cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Initially, I thought it was a painting, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or maybe aliens at it again. You know, would have been.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know what, Tony, I have somebody that might be able to answer that question. I`m glad you asked that question.\par \par \uc2 All right, as part of our Juneteenth coverage, we have a history making pilot with us. That`s right. Lieutenant General Richard Clark has been superintendent of the \uc2 US Air Force Academy\uc2  for nearly two years. He is the first Black General appointed to that prestigious post.\par \par \uc2 General Clark is also a highly decorated command pilot with more than 400 hours of combat experience.\par \par \uc2 A few years ago, he commanded the Eighth Air Force where the Tuskegee Airmen served during World War Two.\par \par \uc2 General, how`re you doing? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK (\uc2 US Air Force Academy\uc2  Superintendent): I`m great, Nate. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, as we noted, you are the first Black Superintendent of the Air Force Academy. What has it been like? Because you`ve been at this position for about two years, right?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: That`s correct. Yes. It`s been interesting. It`s been great though.\par \par \uc2 I love being back at my Alma Mater, but COVID was tough. We fought through that. Imagine being at a service academy during a global pandemic, but it`s really an honor to be there. So ...\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And your Air Force Chief of Staff and the Vice Superintendent are also Black.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is it like for you and the cadets to see that representation in positions of power and influence?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: Well, I think it`s a representation really the diversity in our Armed Forces, our Air Force, and we constantly work on that. We think it`s important.\par \par \uc2 It is a strategic imperative for us because if we`re going to have an Armed Forces, we can`t just build that Armed Forces on 30 percent of our population.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: We need to be represented by the entire population. If we`re going to defend America, we need to look like America.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that a matter of principle and philosophy or a matter of actual effectiveness in combat?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: It`s a strategic imperative. If we`re going to be effective, we need the power of our population and diversity is an inherently American strength. And in the military, we`re taught to capitalize on our strengths. So that`s one that we have and we work hard to capitalize on it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I look forward to the day where we don`t have to have the first Black anything. I look forward to that day. We`re certainly not there yet that`s why we wanted to celebrate you on this Juneteenth.\par \par \uc2 Did you feel or do you feel stress or pressure on yourself? Because you are a first in that position?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: I think at times the pressure for me though, is to perform. I mean, I have a job to do, and I need to do it well, but there are so many people that have made this opportunity possible for me. I don`t want to let them down.\par \par \uc2 People like the Tuskegee Airmen, like you mentioned, Nate. I remember this one event I was at. It was the Tuskegee Airmen Convention and I had showed up and our Chief of Staff, \uc2 Mark Welsh\uc2 , at the time, General \uc2 Mark Welsh\uc2 , he met me there. He told me that I was going to be promoted to three-star, but to keep it quiet, and I spent that cocktail hour time just talking to these original Tuskegee Airmen, didn`t tell them I was going to be promoted.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: But I was just on cloud nine, but then when General Welsh, he was the keynote speaker. It was when I was the Commander of Eighth Air Force, which is all of our bomber forces. He gave this great speech about the Tuskegee Airmen.\par \par \uc2 And then he goes, "Where`s Rich Clark? Are you sitting in the audience? Where are you, Rich?" And he asked me to come up, and I had no idea what he was going to do. And he brought me up and he looked out to these original Tuskegee Airmen, he goes, "Would you have ever imagined when you were flying missions in World War Two that one day, the commander of the bombers that you`re escorting would be a Black man?" And those Tuskegee Airmen, it was so humbling, they started to stand up and clap.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And clap. Yes.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: And I realized it wasn`t about me, it was about one person who was a part of their legacy. And it put some, honestly some pressure on me to pay it forward.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we`re clapping for you today, and clearly you take the job very seriously. I was reading that when we first got COVID -- when COVID hit, you spent 28 days, General, sir, in the dorms with the cadets.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sleeping on the cots.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And 28 days, why did you do that?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: They were going through some tough times. And I wanted them to know that their whole staff, all of our faculty, our instructors, our coaches, that we`re all with them, and I felt like I was probably the best representative of our staff and that we`re in this together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have core priorities for them.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The cadets?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: Well, first to develop leaders of character. And we talked about that all the time. That is most important in any -- any realm of life, was to defeat COVID-19. I think we`re in a good place there. I hope -- I hope we are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: The next one is to prepare them for future conflict, not for next year, not for two years from now, but for five or 15 years, so that they`re ready for whatever our country might face.\par \par \uc2 And then lastly, to really cultivate a culture of dignity and respect, which is crucial in our Armed Forces.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, Air Force Academy also trains cadets for Space Force.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Talking about the future.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Your hours, hours of experience and your knowledge in Space Force, I have to ask, it`s got to feel like a lot of people at home are wondering, what`s all this talk about UFOs? Are they real?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Does alien life form exist?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can`t believe --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You didn`t see that question.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t let you leave the set without asking you that question. A lot of Americans want to know.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: So if I told you, I`d have --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man. Well, listen, I might have to sacrifice myself for the people. They want to know.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: I wish I knew. I`ll be honest. You went outside my space there because --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you go see "Top Gun." Did you like it?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: Oh I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you like it? Was it believable to you?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: I thought it was a great movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How realistic is it?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: Well, I thought it was realistic enough for me to enjoy that movie. I thought it was a great movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because we loved it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We loved it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Talk about great, it is June 20th today. I`ve heard from reliable sources. This is a really good day for you. Why?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: It`s a big day. I`ve got to give a shout out to my anniversary. My wife, Amy and I have been married 24 years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay, Amy.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: So any chance I get to make some points with her, I am taking it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. That`s great.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: Happy Anniversary.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations to you and Amy. Do you have a brother, just asking for a friend?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: I do. I do. He is taken though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: I have a son though, but he is only 20, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, thank you. No, thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What? Come on, Stella, you want to get your groove back?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no, no. Absolutely not. Amy, I would never. I would never. Congratulations. We are very happy for you.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lieutenant General Clark, thank you very much for being here. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD CLARK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, leather you can grow. How the material made from mushroom promises to help fight climate change and already adds a bit of bling to luxury brands.\par \par \uc2 But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want one.\par \par \uc2 One of the hottest new ideas in fashion comes from fungus, yes, fungus. We`re talking about leather that`s made from mushrooms. What?\par \par \uc2 This all natural-plant based alternative has none of the environmental cruelty concerns that you have with animal skins. Lilia Luciano, one of the best TV names in the business, visited a lab outside San Francisco where this mushroom leather is growing and making an impact on fashion and beyond.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Out west where cattle ranches have long held sway, cowhide leather may be losing its luster.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIA WANG (MycoWorks co-founder and chief of culture): You can`t grow a cow to spec.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yes, cows don`t have that --\par \par \uc2 SOPHIA WANG: Cows just do what they do.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIA WANG: So that was what was remarkable about natural material that you can grow to form.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: But this material is made from mushrooms under the watchful eyes of Bay Area technicians.\par \par \uc2 Definitely, it feels like leather.\par \par \uc2 Bolt Threads is one company engineering mushroom leather through mycelium, the underground network of fibers that is the basis of mushrooms.\par \par \uc2 From \uc2 Lululemon\uc2  to \uc2 Stella McCartney\uc2  and Adidas, the company is turning heads with what their plant-based leather is capable of.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BRESLAUER (Bolt Threads co-founder and CEO): It`s a phenomenal material. Everybody loves their leather, whether it`s for their clothes or their car.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: CTO, David Breslauer is approaching the issue from a scientist`s perspective. He says leather is a huge industry to take on, but the fashion world has always embraced innovation, especially the kind that helps fight climate change.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BRESLAUER: The fewer cows we grow industrially, the less emissions we`re going to have. So there is a huge impact climate change and for almost all major fashion brands. Their biggest contributor to greenhouse gas emissions are from the cows that make their leather.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And then there`s deforestation.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BRESLAUER: And there`s deforestation, biodiversity loss, ecological collapse. The list goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: But in order for consumers to adopt mushroom leather, it needs to be durable, workable, and most importantly, look and feel like the real thing.\par \par \uc2 BEATRICE AMBLARD (Master leather artisan): So we`re getting really, really close with this material to having something completely looking like leather.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Beatrice Amblard is a leather artisan and with four decades of experience and says this keeps with the trend of finding plant-based alternatives to what we eat, and where.\par \par \uc2 BEATRICE AMBLARD: I believe people are ready for changes, ready for innovation. For me, combining all techniques with biomaterials is an incredible opportunity.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: What`s that like for you?\par \par \uc2 BEATRICE AMBLARD This is amazing. It`s an incredible feeling. It`s combining the two.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: She works with MycoWorks, another Bay Area company that`s got the fashion industry doing double takes.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIA WANG: I see this material working everywhere where leather goes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Sophia Wang and her co-founder, Phil Ross have been pushing the boundaries of leathers since 2003. Their proprietary Reishi Leather appears close to the real thing.\par \par \uc2 What kind of testing are you guys doing to make sure that it is durable?\par \par \uc2 SOPHIA WANG: We do, do accelerated wear testing, exposing Reishi to the elements just to demonstrate that this material is competitive to leather in terms of durability, and strength, and resistance.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Their product finds purpose from potentially life-saving sheets, interwoven with bulletproof Kevlar to incorporating conductive wiring in the fibers, allowing for smart fashion that connects to your devices, and even leather enmeshed with gold thread for well, you know --\par \par \uc2 One of those $100,000.00 designer bags they have to justify.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIA WANG: Redefining luxury.\par \par \uc2 We work with our partners to actually grow our materials to those customized needs, which is not something you can do with any other natural material.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: She says that if you can think of a luxury brand, they`ve probably worked with them like \uc2 Hermes\uc2 , the High Priest of French leather products.\par \par \uc2 The company worked with the story brand on an iconic bag in 2021. It all points to a future where guilt-free fashion could be the norm and instead of traditional leather on our back or in our hand, we`ll be making room for mushrooms.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Lilia Luciano, Berkeley, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like the way they`re thinking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m all for it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it`s very cool and if you`re plane goes down in a remote location, you know you could always eat your leather shoe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, is that where your mind went?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I just -- you know, I eat a lot of mushrooms, a little rosemary, olive oil, 40 minutes in the oven, 400 degrees.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, but it doesn`t have a bad smell.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, it doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If you can do this and not hurt animals.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If this saves the animals, it is worth it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if you lose a bet, you can eat your shoe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s happening to Tony?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back. I don`t know what he is thinking about this --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I make a lot of bad bets. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They`re eaten shoes and mushrooms. We`ll see you in a bit.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got one order in for an all-leather mushroom suit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are having mushrooms.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still thinking about the General. He left us all a coin.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It says -- I like it when people bring gifts -- "Integrity, service, excellence."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Challenge --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How do I --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He embodies all of that -- all of that.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow right here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I lost mine, Lieutenant General.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Already?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know what happened.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220620ei6k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 May The 4th Be With You\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Gayle King \par \uc2 1,104 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Today is apparently celebrated around the world as a special day for fans of "Star Wars." It`s May 4th, and you may hear those fans say, "May the Fourth be with you," which is a play on a line from the movie, I guess.\par \par \uc2 Vlad Duthiers is one of those fans and he`s here with a story about trying to share the passion with someone else. I`m just kidding, Vlad, I know what it is. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good morning. Good to see you again. So Tony, the Fourth of May or May the Fourth as in May the Fourth be with you is in fact Star Wars Day.\par \par \uc2 We here at CBS MORNINGS discovered that one of our favorite co-workers who`s actually celebrating a birthday today had never seen "Star Wars." Fix it, I have.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: I`m Chris Stover. I`m a supervising producer at CBS MORNINGS and I have never seen "Star Wars."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is astounding to me that in 2022 there is someone walking around, working in media who had never seen "Star Wars."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Wars.")\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: My birthday happens to be May 4th. May the Fourth be with you -- and it is a constant stressor. Every day, friends, family colleagues, constant ridicule.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Wars.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He doesn`t know what "Use the Force" means. He doesn`t even know what the Force is.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Wars.")\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: I know a lightsaber is a thing. I can name you some of the characters. Chewbacca is the furry one. R2-D2 is the robot.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s go. Let`s go on this adventure. Hop on my X-Wing. Take you to Tatooine, introduce you to Luke Skywalker.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: I suppose it`s time. I am entering with an open mind and we`ll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Now or never.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am so excited. I know, I`m so excited.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Oh, be careful. Yes.\par \par \uc2 So people first start seeing this and they see Episode Four and they`re thinking what?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What am I missing?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Wars.")\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: I have so many questions already.\par \par \uc2 I think I missed something.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You just witnessed the power of the Force. Right? Obi- Wan is a Jedi.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: The Force caused the mind trick.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Obi-Wan`s mastery of the Force is what allowed him to - -\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Conduct the mind trick.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The mind trip.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Wars.")\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Who is that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Princess Leia.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Oh.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Wars.")\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: It was a lot different than I expected. It`s funnier. I was not expecting the kind of sarcastic humor.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Do you get a little bit of why it entered into the zeitgeist the way that it did?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: I`m struggling a little bit. Like I just don`t understand -- forgive me, the obsession. Maybe when I -- in 35 years, I`ll see the next one. I don`t know. But --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know you`re not waiting. 35 years. We`re starting now.\par \par \uc2 Roll that "Empire Strikes Back."\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: I have to go work.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Chris is here. Happy Birthday first of all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is your birthday. Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Thank you. Thank you very much. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you`re ready for "Empire Strikes Back?"\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: I`ve still got some questions.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: First of all, you told David Begnaud when David Begnaud told you -- I`d never seen "Star Wars" before you said what?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: I said yes, it might not be completely worth it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You ain`t missing much is what he said.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Okay. You know, I still have a lot of questions. I think that`s kind of the fascination with this is that this came out so long ago, you could not \uc2 Google\uc2  beforehand, afterward to find out the answers to your questions and I still haven`t done that, frankly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So it sounds like one of your questions is why do people love this so much?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They really do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They do.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: They do, and I understand as a whole like the series as a whole, the franchise as a whole, I get that. But having this one movie spawn all of that, I don`t understand.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So we spawned something for you in honor of your birthday. We got a little something for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What have we got?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a DVD stand.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I want to show you. But one thing -- I`ll answer that question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, here is Brian. All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Aren`t we supposed to sing though?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And, Chris, I`ve got you a gift.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow. Check this out. Can you name --\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: All right, I was going to say.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay. Who`s that?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: That is R2-D2.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: That is the Mark Hamill.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Luke Skywalker.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Luke Skywalker. That`s Chewbacca. That`s Harrison Ford, still working on it. I`m still working on it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hans Solo.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Hans Solo and then that`s Princess Leia.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s for you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I have one, too. Don`t open it though. You`ll ruin the values.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is true. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Happy birthday, Chris. Vlad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us. We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s goodbye?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think that`s it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s goodbye, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220505ei54000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Right Work-Life Balance\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,426 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 April 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here`s a question for all you Type A people out there. Have you ever worked so hard for a dream job or a promotion that you pushed yourself beyond your limits and you sacrificed your health? Chloe Benjamin knows what that`s like.\par \par \uc2 She went from being an assistant to a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestselling author with her book, "The Immortalists." It was a moment she dreamed of, but behind the scenes though, she was grappling with stress and chronic migraines. She ended up in the ER.\par \par \uc2 In a recent article for "The Cut," she wrote this about her physical breakdown saying: "I had put my writing, my ambition, and my success over not just my health, but my experience of life."\par \par \uc2 Wow. She joins us now with her story and some advice for a less stressful life. Chloe, I`m so glad to see you, because I so related to this story because I`m a big believer I`m pushing through, plowing through. You said that you did that almost to the expense, not almost -- to the expense of your health.\par \par \uc2 When did you realize, okay, I have a real problem here, and then I can`t push through?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN ("The Immortalists" author): Well, I think it`s interesting because it was a slow realization. And in fact, it was -- you know, it was hearing these cues from my body, the body speaks through pain.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: When you`re not listening, you know, to anything else. And slowly, I started to realize I could no longer ignore those signals, but I did for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what were the signals specifically that were speaking to you?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Really pain. Pain, yes, just increasing migraines. You know, I was so grateful to go on a book tour that was quite extensive. I was so honored that this book found a wide audience. But every time I did an event, I would get a migraine and I didn`t listen to that until it was too late.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what just listen to your body really mean?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Well, we hear this phrase "listen to your body."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Listen to the body. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: And for me, you know, it became very granular. It was, how am I holding my tension? How am I breathing? I realized that I held so much tension in my shoulders, I think a lot of us do. And so I actually put stickers around my house. And when I saw the sticker, that was my cue to relax my shoulders.\par \par \uc2 I almost have this detective quality of what makes my body feel good, what makes my body feel bad. And I`m really getting to know myself in that way so that my body could trust my mind, and my mind could trust my body.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s so much pressure on people, and people put it on themselves to push through that kind of pain. So on the one hand, there is listen to your body. But on the other hand, there`s -- we get this cultural message or maybe you can tell me where you think it comes from, to push through. Could you trace that back to some origin?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because Tony, sometimes I think, is it really that bad? Is pain really that bad? That I wonder about this, too?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Well, I think we live in a world in which productivity is prized over almost anything else. And you know, there is this idea of grind culture right now, you know, no days off, push.\par \par \uc2 And I think that can be so harmful, because it means that again, we`re privileging our work over our experience of life. And why are we doing that? And how can we change it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you said you`ve changed. You`ve changed your life.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: I have, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How you live.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: It`s taken a few years to make those changes, and honestly, I never thought I could. I`ve been ambitious and perfectionistic since childhood, and those qualities got me to a place of success, but they also hurt me.\par \par \uc2 So you know, the way that I live is totally different. And one thing that I hope might be helpful to others is an idea of pacing. So rather than waiting until I finish a scene, or a book, or a tour to rest, I`m resting throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`ll take a five minute break to meditate. I`ll walk around the block. Even just looking out the window for a few minutes when I`m on a screen, and this way, my energy moves like a wave instead of building to a point of collapse.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can imagine younger people, particularly younger, creative people listening to you and saying it`s very easy for Chloe to say this now, because she`s already made it. She wrote a bestseller.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s so true, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you`re starting out, it`s very difficult.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right. When you`re still trying to make it, make it, you`re still trying to push through, right?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: It is. It`s true. Although when I look back, I think maybe I didn`t have to do as much touring as I did for the book to be successful, and maybe it`s okay to say no, because when you say no, what are you saying yes to? That might be your health. That might be creating a way of working that is sustainable.\par \par \uc2 Because the thing is, you don`t want to have one book that is a success and then not be able to write or promote for years. So actually, if -- you know, I think maybe it`s a fallacy that you have to push and push and push until you make it because then you crash.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You definitely would.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Maybe you can make it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If you say no, what are you saying yes to? I like that.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Yes, and maybe you know, you can make it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Chloe, I am sorry.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: That`s okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Finish us off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Finish the sentence. Just finish it.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: You know, maybe you can do it in a more sustainable way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think it`s definitely true. I`m going to read "The Immortalists." Thank you very much, Chloe Benjamin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, we have to leave you with some very sad news for us here at CBS News. We`ve lost a very dear colleague, Michael Hopkins was our Director of Special Events and Logistics who died very suddenly yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Whenever -- look at his face -- whenever CBS News sent our teams to cover events like presidential debate or a natural disaster, it was Hops, "Hoppy" they called him, I like to call him "Mike" who made sure everyone and everything was where it needed to be.\par \par \uc2 He has been behind the scenes in all around the world for CBS News over the past 38 years. Remotes as we call them, won`t be the same without him.\par \par \uc2 Our hearts go out to Mike`s wife, Kathy, and their three children.\par \par \uc2 I got the news yesterday, last night, and I felt like I`d been kicked in the stomach.\par \par \uc2 Mike Hopkins was somebody you could call about anything and everything and he was always there to help with that raspy voice. He would say, "What do you need, dear?" And he would always figure it out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Way too soon. Still a still young man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Way too soon. You will be -- END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220421ei4k000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Final Stand in Mariupol; Russia`s Relentless Offensive; Unmasking Uncertainty; Fake Agents Indicted; Emergency Wildfire Evacuations; Drought Relief in the West; Fake Agents Indicted; Fleeing Russia`s Crackdown; Johnny Depp Takes the Stand; The Great Green North; Forests and Climate Change; The Art of the Interview; Death on Safari; Jordan Klepper Live; The Right Work-Life Balance, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Charlie D`Agata,  Errol Barnett, Lilia Luciano, Carter Evans, Ben Tracy, Debra Patta \par \uc2 13,948 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 April 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off, but we`re in good hands. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Russia pounds Ukraine overnight as holdouts refuse to surrender in Mariupol, the latest developments in the fierce battles for territory.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL JOHN KIRBY (RET.) (Pentagon Press Secretary): Our assessment is that the Mariupol still remains contested, that the Russians have not taken it, that the Ukrainians are still fighting for it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The White House weighs in amid uncertainty following the end of the travel mask mandate.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Should people continue to wear masks on planes.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): That`s up to them.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hundreds of homes have been evacuated in Arizona as fire crews work to contain a fast moving wildfire.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Johnny Depp takes the stand saying he never hit e- wife Amber Heard.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY DEPP (Actor): Nor have I ever struck anyone in my life.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Netflix\uc2  reported its first loss of worldwide subscribers sending in stock plunging below pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Kyrie Irving was fined $50,000.00 by the \uc2 NBA\uc2  for flipping off \uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2  fans.\par \par \uc2 MAN: President Biden reportedly told former President Obama he plans to seek re-election in 2024 when he is 81 years old.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL\uc2  ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): It`s like when a woman tells her friends she is planning to get bangs. It`s the kind of the friend`s job to say, "Are you sure about that?"\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): When it comes to wearing masks, not everyone is having the same experience that you are. My hope is that we can get to a point where we`re not villainizing each other over a tiny piece of cloth. We should be saving our anger for the real enemy, all those people who think that a tuna sandwich is appropriate to eat on a plane.\par \par \uc2 That`s who we fight, together. N95 ain`t going to save you from that.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trevor Noah once again making a very good point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, he`s very right about that. The air circulation is great on a plane, but not that great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I was going to say, you think you wouldn`t smell it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You wouldn`t smell it, I will say.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a great show for you today. Lots of talk about including another fascinating installment of our climate series, it is called "Earth 365." We`re going to be looking at how the world`s forests are critical, absolutely critical to cooling the planet, and may even predict rising temperatures.\par \par \uc2 Ben Tracy takes us on a trip to the world`s largest forest system right here in the U.S. where scientists think they may be able to see the future of our planet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And "The Daily Show`s" Jordan Klepper is here to discuss his latest special, it`s about an unusual fascination shared by some supporters of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Jordan`s journey takes him all the way to Europe in the authoritarian government in Hungary. You won`t want to miss this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. He has got an interesting spin on this, but we`re going to begin with the last Ukrainian holdouts against a Russian offensive in a crucial city in Southern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Russia has shared this video of smoke rising from a steelworks in Mariupol, where it`s estimated thousands of Ukrainians troops and civilians are under siege.\par \par \uc2 Russia bombed the facility overnight, but it is vast and strongly fortified. The rest of Mariupol has more or less been destroyed. Most Russian forces are now pivoting to a new phase of this war in Ukraine when all-out offensive in the east of the country.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in Ukraine with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Another ultimatum, another deadline for Ukrainian forces to surrender in Mariupol has just come and gone, both sides have agreed on a humanitarian corridor to allow civilians to leave. But fighting rages on in cities in the south and the east.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Drone footage shows a city torn apart after weeks of bombardment. Residential neighborhoods flattened.\par \par \uc2 Beneath the Azovstal Iron and Steel Works Plant, women and children huddle together in bomb shelters too terrified to attempt an escape.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: A Ukrainian Marine makes a desperate plea, "We`re facing our last days, if not last hours. The enemy outnumbers us 10 to one." And the enemy is closing in fast. Russian soldiers surrounding the steel plant hunting down remaining fighters.\par \par \uc2 Russia has intensified attacks along a -00 mile frontline advancing forward and reinforcing regions all already under occupation including the hard fought Zaporizhzhia nuclear facility, Europe`s largest.\par \par \uc2 The nuclear power plant remains under Russian control in Russian-held territory on that side of the river. This is Ukrainian-held territory. Nearby, they`ve been counting the cost of the war.\par \par \uc2 A Russian anti-tank mine took the life of 29-year-old paratrooper Vyacheslav Dimov (sp?) on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 How important was it for your son to fight for his country?\par \par \uc2 (ALA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "It was very important. He dreamed like crazy of becoming a soldier," his mother, Ala (sp?) said.\par \par \uc2 (ALA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "But I didn`t bring him into this world for war, but for a peaceful life, so he could live with his beloved wife, Ivana.\par \par \uc2 His beloved wife, Ivana, and he had only been married for 18 months. They were planning to start a family when the war broke out. The 23-year-old widow told us about their last video call, saying she knew that every call might be the last one.\par \par \uc2 (IVANA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "Every time we spoke, I told him, `I love you` look after yourself," she said. "Now I say, I love you, look after me."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Ivana told us when she first heard the news, she didn`t want to believe it was true, but when it sunk in, her world shattered like a jigsaw puzzle -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Unimaginable and heartbreaking. Charlie D`Agata in Ukraine for us. Thank you, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 In this country, the Biden administration says it will wait for the C.D.C.`s guidance on whether to appeal a judge`s ruling that struck down mass mandates on airplanes and public transit. So far, the C.D.C. hasn`t given any guidance on that. The judge`s decision has caused uncertainty especially among the most vulnerable to COVID.\par \par \uc2 Errol Barnett is at Reagan National Airport outside of Washington.\par \par \uc2 Errol, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Hey there, Vlad. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 One thing to keep in mind here is that the original mask mandate was set to expire in 13 days anyway. So even if the C.D.C. made a new determination and the Department of Justice appealed the Florida Federal Judge`s decision, it would only extend the rule by a matter of days.\par \par \uc2 And while yes, certainly many are excited to see the end of this rule, CBS News is also finding others and now having to make difficult decision and are upset to see the end of the mask mandate.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: In public transportation hubs across the country, travelers now have the option to go massless. \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  joined all U.S. airlines and \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  saying masks are now optional.\par \par \uc2 All cities like Philadelphia and New York say the mandate will remain in place.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think it should be up to what the person wants. I will probably wear it until I get on the plane and then when I sit in my seat, I will take it off.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: President Biden carefully weighed in Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Mr. President, should people continue to wear masks on planes?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : That`s up to them.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Genet Abate is an \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver in the D.C. area thrilled to learn the rideshare service dropped its requirements.\par \par \uc2 GENET ABATE (\uc2 Uber\uc2  driver): I just want to feel free and breathe freely. I very safe to not wear a mask.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Still the 47 percent spike in COVID cases and just the last 14 days has some high risk travelers on edge.\par \par \uc2 GRETCHEN MUSA (Immunocompromised individual): I`m concerned. I`m especially concerned about who else sit next to and if they`ll be willing to wear a mask.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Gretchen Musa is one of seven million Americans who are immunocompromised, but despite the risks, Musa is going to stick with her plans to visit her grandparents who are both in their 90s in California,\par \par \uc2 GRETCHEN MUSA: I needed to weigh whether if I didn`t go see them, which might be the safer choice for my physical health, how much regret I would have later in life if I go through my life knowing that I wasn`t able to be there for them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: It`s a tough choice, and we should note the C.D.C. still recommends mask use while traveling on aircraft, but now, all the major airlines have to decide what to do about all of those passengers banned for failure to comply with this Federal rule. For \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , that`s about a thousand people. It tells CBS News, it will decide if those folks can get back on board, Gayle, on a case by case basis.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there is an interesting question, Errol. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Dr. \uc2 Tom Frieden\uc2 . He is former Director of the C.D.C., he is currently President and CEO of Resolve to Save Lives whose mission is to save 100 million lives by preventing heart disease and epidemics.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Frieden, we`re really glad you`re here at the table. Listen, we are all COVID exhausted, we`re over it. The COVID is not over us. Does it make sense to you this ruling? And should we leave it up to people to make their own decisions to mask or not mask?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 TOM FRIEDEN\uc2  (Former Director, \uc2 U.S. Centers for Disease Control\uc2 ): Well, I`m a doctor, not a lawyer, so I can`t comment on the legal case. But we rely on the society to make sure that our kid doesn`t get hit by a drunk driver, that the food that we eat in a restaurant doesn`t make us terribly sick, that the rest -- that the medicines we pick up at the pharmacy are not contaminated. In the same way, we need the government and society need to be able to protect us if more dangerous variant comes or another health threat comes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So how much does this really matter? We heard from the immunocompromised person in Errol`s piece, if you`re wearing a mask yourself, one way masking as it is called, and everyone else is not, how much of a difference does that make?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 TOM L. FRIEDEN\uc2 : You`re a lot safer if everyone is masking, that`s why we are all connected, and that connection means that my health depends on what you do. But if other people around aren`t masks and if you`re immunocompromised, older, then up your mask game to an N95. It`s not perfect, but it`s more protective than a cloth mask or a surgical mask.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How concerned are you about immunocompromised people traveling on planes? My father, for example, we`ve talked about bringing our families to South Africa. It`s a 12 to -hour flight. No one is masked, but he is masked, what would you recommend?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 TOM L. FRIEDEN\uc2 : Unfortunately, if you`re immunosuppressed or otherwise medically vulnerable, flying just got less safe for you. Now, it`s really hard to wear a mask for 10 to 12 to 14 hours, but that`s what you might have to do if you want to reduce your risk for yourself personally. If all of us were masked up, all of us would be safer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So it sounds like you`re saying he probably shouldn`t get on a plane for that long.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 TOM L. FRIEDEN\uc2 : You know, it`s a risk benefit calculation. How important is that trip to you? How important is that meeting or that meal? And if it`s important, then you do it staying as safe as possible meaning, an N95 mask for the whole way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cases are already going up now. Don`t you think this will mean cases will continue to go up?\par \par \uc2 Here`s what I struggle with and I struggle with people making the decision, I`m not going to mask. If you could not wear a mask and it only affects you, good on you, but it affects so many other people.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 TOM L. FRIEDEN\uc2 : With cases going up, the idea of masks coming down doesn`t make a lot of sense. But we don`t know what`s going to happen yet, either with the severity of this particular wave in terms of hospitalization, but we have great vaccines, great treatments. We need more people to get them.\par \par \uc2 But the biggest concern is what is going to happen in the coming months? What if we have a more dangerous variant, deadly, that gets away from our vaccine induced immunity? We really need these kinds of tools to protect people not just to save lives, but also to keep our economy growing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What about kids that can`t be vaccinated? Can`t get the vaccine and aren`t wearing the masks.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 TOM L. FRIEDEN\uc2 : We`ve seen just yesterday new data out from the C.D.C., very severe illness in hundreds of kids almost all of them unvaccinated between the ages of six and 11. So very important to get vaccinated, boosted up-to-date and to mask up, recommended at least, and when disease is spreading a lot to require 