an families are now camping out at the Southern border seeking asylum in this country from Vladimir Putin`s regime.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, Florida`s governor threatens \uc2 Disney\uc2 `s privileged position in that state after the company protests the so-called Don`t Say Gay Law.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Johnny Depp gives testimony in his high-profile defamation trial against his ex-wife, Amber Heard. What we`re learning about their troubled relationship.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is marijuana being grown and today is April 20th, aka 4/20, historically a day of celebration for cannabis enthusiast, also protest against marijuana prohibition.\par \par \uc2 So we are sharing our latest polling on that subject. Here it is. Two- thirds of those surveyed in a new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll say they want recreational aka just for fun marijuana to be legal both in their home states and nationwide. The vast majority in the poll across all age groups support legalization at the Federal level; however, people over the age of 65 notably less on board.\par \par \uc2 Those who are on board in the big way are between 30 and 44. They`re the most supportive. Recreational marijuana currently legal in 18 states and Washington, D.C., and it is legal for medicinal use. Some say, it is all medicinal, in 37 states and the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 The House of Representatives recently passed a bill to end the Federal prohibition on pot, but the Senate has yet to weigh in and we inch ever closer, folks.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, it`s got to happen. Look, if you look at the polling, it`s got to happen.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There`s a lot of support. I do think if our Representatives represent the people, clearly, the polling is on the side of legalization.\par \par \uc2 All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Smiles in the room.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Smiles in the room.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a big announcement from \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , why the streaming giant is not smiling, saying it is losing subscribers and could change its lineup as a result.\par \par \uc2 Also, Ben Tracy takes us to a forest in Minnesota to talk climate change. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Here inside this chamber in Northern Minnesota, scientists are simulating different levels of global warming to see how the forest reacts.\par \par \uc2 We`ll tell you what they found and what it means for the health of our planet.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re looking at Moscow, where the Russian government has been cracking down on protests against the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 This footage shows one protests being broken up earlier in the war, but anti-war demonstrations are now rare as the punishment for telling the truth has become far more severe.\par \par \uc2 To escape the crackdown, hundreds of thousands of Russians have left their country. Lilia Luciano shows us the painful struggle of one Russian family to get out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yulia (sp?), her two children, and the few things she carries with her are now tucked into small spaces at her sister Olga`s home near San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 When did you start suspecting that you didn`t really have a future in Russia?\par \par \uc2 YULIA (Left Russia with two children): I think the strangest thing is I could even now have a future in Russia, building my career, raising my kids, but I don`t want to be scared for them to be persecuted for something.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In the largest country in the world, there is little space for dissent.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Russian protest.)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Protest against the invasion had met harsh crackdowns, news outlets are being silenced, and new laws threaten jail time for spreading so called misinformation about the Russian military.\par \par \uc2 YULIA: I`m actually very angry that I had to go, but what did it for me was another of Putin`s speeches when he said, okay, when he mentioned atomic weapons and things I was like, "Now, I`m scared."\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yulia, her sister Olga and her brother Yakov are all U.S. citizens. Their parents fled the Soviet Union in the 80s as political refugees, but after college, Yulia returned.\par \par \uc2 YULIA: Russia was very exciting. It was new. There was so much happening and it just seemed very free, cut 20 years later, I`m a refugee again.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Retracing the steps of her parents.\par \par \uc2 YULIA: I`m trying to fight the feeling of being a failure because like my parents did so much to get us out, and here I am again.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yulia and her two children are dual citizens. That American passport makes them the exception, giving them an easier way out.\par \par \uc2 But she left behind her 93-year-old grandmother who didn`t have a visa. Her brother Yakov visiting Moscow from his home in Massachusetts, took over to try to get her out, but he soon realized what he was up against.\par \par \uc2 YAKOV KRONROD (American citizen in Russia): We talked to almost every U.S. Embassy in Europe.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Seeking freedom or fleeing the crushing consequences of sanctions, many Russians are trying to escape, but where Europe is opening doors to Ukrainian refugees, Russians are finding a dead end.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s really tough for Russians.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Anaida Zadykyan is 