ay a little bit more to watch it ad-free.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I don`t want to pay a little bit more. I don`t want to pay a little bit more, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, Vlad, 220 million worldwide subscribers is a huge number.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, but you know, when Wall Street sees a loss of subscribers, that`s a flag.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But eventually they`re going to run out of people. I mean, there`s just going to be at their --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The whole world is \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and chilling.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s end with Michelle Obama on "The Ellen DeGeneres Show." The former First Lady talked about spending time with her grown up daughter, Sasha and Malia during the pandemic. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELLEN DEGENERES (Talk show host): Your daughters were home and now they`re gone. So it`s an empty house.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA (Former First Lady of the United States): Bye.\par \par \uc2 Being with them as adults, is -- it is fun. I mean, I love them at every age.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN DEGENERES: Before your husband was President even, you came here with them. They were eight and 10.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: They were --\par \par \uc2 ELLEN DEGENERES: And just to see the Jonas Brothers.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: They love the Jonas Brothers.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN DEGENERES: You brought them to see the Jonas Brothers.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Now they`re bringing grown men home. Before, it was just like a pop band. Now, they have boyfriends and real lives and all that stuff.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sasha and Malia are 20 and 23. Can you imagine you`re coming to visit to pick up one of them to take them out on a date?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Barack Obama answers the door?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is there answering the door.\par \par \uc2 