e the year?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s pretty good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, we`re going to begin with this as we count down to Earth Day. It`s this Friday, our "Earth 365" series is focusing on issues affecting the health of our planet year-round.\par \par \uc2 Now scientists have long known the value of trees as vital warriors in this fight against climate change. The new research shows that world`s forests help cool the planet by about two degrees, which is a lot in global terms for historic planet warming carbon dioxide and cool the air helping to prevent the droughts.\par \par \uc2 Senior national and environmental correspondent, that`s Ben Tracy, visited a unique research site in Northern Minnesota. Scientists there are learning more about these potentially planet saving ecosystems, which could help predict rise in temperatures.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: In the forests of Northern Minnesota, where winter often overstays its welcome, hidden among the trees is something rather unusual. This is the Marcell Experimental Forest, or you could just as easily call it a Climate Change Time Machine.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is such a unique place to come to work.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WESTON (\uc2 Oak Ridge National Laboratory\uc2  senior scientist): It`s incredible. Each one of these is giving you a manipulation of what the future may be hold.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: David Weston is a senior scientist at \uc2 Oak Ridge National Laboratory\uc2 . He and more than 100 other researchers come here to study how different degrees of global warming might impact the world`s northern forests.\par \par \uc2 I noticed above the door on the way in here it says "Welcome to a warmer future." That seems kind of ominous.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WESTON: It is giving us some idea of how far we can push the system and how resilient this system may be. It`s absolutely amazing to have a future scenario that you can measure right now today.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: They pump heat and carbon dioxide into these chambers. The temperature simulations range from no warming all the way up to a frightening nine degrees Celsius or more than 16 degrees Fahrenheit.\par \par \uc2 That`s pretty extreme.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WESTON: It is extreme and so this is the upper end of what we`re trying to simulate.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And so at this level of warming, clearly, this doesn`t react very well.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WESTON: No, we`re not doing very well here.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: These hexagon-shaped chambers here at the experimental forest are open to the sky. Each one is more than 30 feet tall, 40 feet wide. And there are 10 of them here on this massive seven-acre site, each one providing a different glimpse into the future.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just any forest they`re studying. It`s what`s known as the boreal forest, the world`s largest forest system. It wraps around the upper third of the earth, from North America, through Scandinavia and much of Russia, like some sort of planetary headband.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN SEBESTYEN (Marcell Experimental Forest): The hair or the bald spot exposed on the top, and then you have this band that extends around the forehead or the more curved places on the globe.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Stephen Sebestyen is a researcher with the National Forest Service. He says the boreal includes several species of trees and crucial mossy peatland that play an outsized role in regulating the Earth`s temperature by sequestering planet warming carbon dioxide.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN SEBESTYEN: The peatlands are only three percent of the land surface area of the planet, but they hold up to a third to a half of the global soil carbon pool.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Unfortunately, what they`re discovering here inside these chambers, is that the hotter the planet gets, the boreal forest ecosystem dries out. The critical peat moss is eaten up by bacteria and replaced by shrubs. That carbon once captured in the ground is released into the atmosphere accelerating the devastating impacts of climate change.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN SEBESTYEN: Every single year has led to loss of carbon from these ecosystems. That`s the crisis that we face. Taking carbon from the solid form and a stable form stored in these peatlands to be in a gas in the atmosphere. That`s a greenhouse gas.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And the real world impacts are already painfully clear. These drier northern forests are now more prone to wildfires. Record breaking blazes tore through the boreal forests of Siberia in the past two years, charring millions of acres and spewing record amounts of carbon dioxide into the sky.\par \par \uc2 Scientists say we need to quickly reduce our planet warming emissions before these forest systems reach a tipping point, switching from accumulating carbon to emitting it, potentially even rapidly in a so-called carbon bomb.\par \par \uc2 And in some ways, this place is kind of a giant warning to humans about the future.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WESTON: Maybe it gives us a reason to act. Right? I`m a bit of an optimist. But why roll the dice. Let`s solve the problem now.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Because the dire future foretold here in this forest is one we can still change.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ben Tracy, in Northern Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like how he is thinking, why roll the dice. I don`t like rolling the dice on anything, especially when he says why don`t we solve the problem now?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s the butterfly effect that Ben describes, right? The fossil fuels make the planet hotter, which in turn dries out the forest, which in turn, you know, creates a hotter planet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that`s where we are right now, and if we can just reduce those emissions, maybe we can save the planet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We need those forests. It`s not just climate change, it is air pollution now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, ahead, tips and strategies for nailing that job interview. We will speak with a human behavior expert about how to look and sound confident.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Doing great, Vlad. You`re doing great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I need a lot of her help. I can`t wait to talk to her. But first, it is 8:08, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jordan Klepper of "The Daily Show" is known for his interviews at political rallies and protests in the U.S., but for a new special, he traveled all the way to Hungary to find out why so many American conservatives looks at that Central European country for inspiration.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER ("The Today Show" contributor): Today is the Revolution Day. It celebrates 1848 when they pushed back against the Hapsburgs and I should have bought a guidebook instead of trying to watch "Tenet" on the plane.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, Jordan will share what he eventually learned about Hungary`s brand of authoritarian politics and what it says about us.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Internship.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Of course that is a scene from "The Internship." And it`s an example of what not to do if you want to nail a job interview.\par \par \uc2 Millions of people have been rethinking their careers during this so-called Great Resignation. If you are one of them, researcher Vanessa Van Edwards has some tips for you. She`s the author of "Cues: Master the Secret Language of Charismatic Communication." And she is here with us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS (Bestselling author and researcher): Good morning. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you say, Vanessa, that making a good first impression begins with how you say "hello"?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: Yes. So, most people don`t realize what you say is not important, it is how you say it. And the very first thing we`re doing in our first impression is we`re looking for confidence.\par \par \uc2 We like to be around confident people. We like to hire confident people. And this is a weird measurement, but the space between your ear and your shoulder is the fastest way we indicate confidence. Why? When we`re nervous, we bring our shoulders, we tilt our chin down.\par \par \uc2 So I see people on video, they`ll go, "Hey, good morning," or they hunch over their computer or they start an interview and they have their shoulders up. The first thing I want you to do is maximize this distance. It is a really weird measurement, but it actually signals confidence and it also helps you speak more confidently.\par \par \uc2 How you say "hello" can give away all your vocal confidence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s many ways to say hello, like what?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: Yes, okay, so here`s what happens. We get nervous. We`re hearing the phone ring, we`re waiting for the video to pop up. We`re walking around we, "\uc2 Hello\uc2 ," all the way up here, all the way up into our high octave and we say "hi" at the top of our breath.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that gives away all your confidence. What I want you to do instead is say "hello" on the outbreath. Listen to the difference. This is me at the top of anxiety. "Hey, nice to see you." Versus "Hey, good to see you."\par \par \uc2 So when you speak on the outbreath, it forces your vocal cords to relax. You lower your shoulders and it signals such nice confidence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, before you sat down, you said, we`re going to help people get jobs today. So people out there are saying, "Thank you, Vanessa Van Edwards."\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: We`re going to help you get a job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But one of the things you said was colors. Much to my chagrin, you said yellow, which is my favorite color, by the way, feel no pressure. I said yellow is not a good color to wear on an interview. Why is it? Why is it not and what is a good color?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: Okay, so with colors, what you want to think about is what it triggers for most humans. So yellow is a very bright color. It`s high energy, but it`s your signature color.\par \par \uc2 So here`s a weird way to think about colors. You can create a signature color or you can wear the color of the brand. So if you`re interviewing at Coke, wear red. It looks like you fit in. If you`re interviewing at Pepsi, wear blue. Sprite, wear green.\par \par \uc2 My favorite color though is blue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: This is my favorite color today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony got the job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tony is hired.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: I was so happy when I saw your suit today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Someone is wearing blue. Pretty good chance on a given day. But isn`t it a little bit boring? Blue?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: No. No. So blue. So blue. What does it remind humans of? Clear blue skies, clear blue water. It makes us feel calm, it makes us feel relaxed. So that`s a great color to wear.\par \par \uc2 Red. Now, I chose red today. This is a high energy color. Intensity, passion. You only want to wear red if you are bringing energy. Do not let the red wear you.\par \par \uc2 So, today, I`m trying to bring it, so it`s a high energy color. Black is actually the most boring color. No one is in black.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But I do wear black outside of like my suits. I`m pretty much all black all the time.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: So black is the most formal color, and if you have answers that can speak for themselves, you want to focus on your content, black is a great color to wear because it can make you blend in, your answers can stand out.\par \par \uc2 The last color I want you to consider is green. Green, human we think of spring, we think of fresh, we think of new. If you`re looking for --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We think of money.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: We think of money. I mean, that`s good, too. Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think of growth and new beginnings.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: Growth and new beginnings. Yes. And so if you`re looking for a role with transition or change, green can be a very refreshing color to wear.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this is all good, what to wear, how to say it, but what to say. Words of connection in virtual interviews. What are those?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: So we`ve actually found, we wondered if you could make up for the lack of a handshake in a virtual interview. Could you actually say the word that is that touchy feely word? And what they found was if you say, "Oh, I`m sending a handshake from here," or "I`m sending a virtual high five" or "It`s so good to see you," saying those words actually creates more of a physiological response.\par \par \uc2 We actually measure that using smartwatch technology, and so saying positive words especially not saying, "Can you see me?" "Can you hear me?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: The first 10 words you say in an interview can you set you up for success. So think about those first 10 words to make them slightly positive.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So let me ask you, we were talking backstage, I had a mentor who used to work at CBS News who constantly told me to advocate for myself, which I`m not very good at doing, believe it or not.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: It`s so hard.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I know that there are people who go into interviews and they say, "I`m the next greatest thing you`re hiring, you`ve got to hire me. If you don`t, you`re doing the wrong thing," but I could never do that.\par \par \uc2 But he said --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That can come off as obnoxious.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: Obnoxious.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: Braggy, okay, so, I want you to advocate for yourself, but not in terms of labeling yourself. So I don`t wait to say, I`m successful, I`m going to be the next best thing for this role. I want you to do it with stories.\par \par \uc2 So instead of saying you`re successful or productive, and say, you know, in my last role, I was able to create a system. I was productive, and it was super successful at that company.\par \par \uc2 So using stories to advocate for yourself, is a way of talking about yourself, but it also feels comfortable, not like you`re bragging.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s such good advice. Your book is so good, Vanessa, because there is a way you have to sell yourself. But that braggy word is never good.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: And soft. Yes, can I give one more kind of subtle tip.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jon Towers says hurry up.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: Okay, M.I.T. found that if you subtly mirror your interviewer, you`re going to earn 20 to 30 percent more in your salary.\par \par \uc2 So if they`re leaning forward, you lean forward. If they`re nodding, I`m going to nod with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, I`ve had mirroring in marital counseling, didn`t go so well.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: This is a job interview.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have to think about that. Okay --\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAN EDWARDS: This is a job interview.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. Thanks Vanessa Van Edwards, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "48 Hours" is investigating the mysterious death of an American woman in Africa. Bianca Rudolph suffered a deadly gunshot wound while she was visiting Zambia with her husband, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 The case centers on the crucial question: Did Bianca accidentally shoot herself?\par \par \uc2 That`s what Zambian Police say, but as "48 Hours" contributor Debora Patta reports, American investigators believe Lawrence Rudolph murdered his wife.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: In 2016, Lawrence Rudolph and his wife, Bianca, were preparing to return home from a hunting trip in Zambia. Lawrence says he was in the cabin`s bathroom when he heard a gunshot and his wife`s scream, finding her dead shot in the chest, he would tell authorities, Bianca, must have shot herself while packing up their gun in its case.\par \par \uc2 JAMES GAGLIANO: Gun safety rules and regulations stipulate, once the hunting trip was ended, the weapons should have been rendered safe.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: James Gagliano, a retired F.B.I. special agent and CBS News consultant has questions over why the gun would have even been loaded.\par \par \uc2 JAMES GAGLIANO: To think that they would have left the weapon off safe, loaded, and then a finger could have gotten in there trigger, it just does not make sense.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: It is especially puzzling, says Gagliano, because of the Rudolph`s background\par \par \uc2 JAMES GAGLIANO: Lawrence Rudolph and his wife, Bianca, were avid hunters. These were not rookies and they understand gun safety rules and precautions.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lawrence Rudolph for his part, has maintained innocence throughout this investigation. You can watch Debora`s full report tonight at eight Eastern, seven Central on the CBS News streaming network.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And still ahead, "The Daily Show`s" Jordan Klepper there he is in our progressive greenroom. Hey, Jordan.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Hello.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here for his new special. We`re good. Hope you`re good, too.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Time how to bring you some stories that are talk of our low coffee table this morning. Mr. Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m in pole position. So my "Talk of the Table" is a new survey showing two out of three people are not afraid of what happens after death.\par \par \uc2 The survey commissioned by Spirit Shack, which is a British company that sells ghost hunting gear.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, so there`s that, but they did find out that half of the 1,000 people surveyed believe in an afterlife, 55 percent of those who believe say they will go to heaven, while 58 percent worry about going to hell.\par \par \uc2 Two out of five people believe they will be reunited with their pets in the afterlife, and the majority of people believe that their pets will be reincarnated.\par \par \uc2 I found this study really, really fascinating because it`s always been interesting to me the psychology of how we exist on this Earth. When we think about our daily existence, which is painful, everybody`s existence is painful. So we create this notion for us -- for ourselves that the pain ends once we go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like the idea that most people think they`re going to heaven. It`s like most people think they`re a good driver.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Better than --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No one is saying, "I think I`m going to hell." No, everybody is like, "I`m going to heaven" but also my pets are going to heaven. I like that. I like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not ready for it, but I`m not afraid of it. But I`m certainly not looking forward to it anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I think the survey -- the key, I think is that the majority of people are not scared of what happens in the afterlife.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like that. All right, Gayle, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is a fond farewell to TV`s Johnson family.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Blackish.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. The series "Blackish" ended its network run last night. Did you see it after eight seasons and 176 episodes?\par \par \uc2 Star, Anthony Anderson called, "Blackish" one of his proudest moments of his life. He wrote on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 : "What this show has done, and has shown for the culture and Black families, really all-American families overall, will never be forgotten." A lot of fans say that "Blackish" gave them a family, they could finally see themselves in.\par \par \uc2 I know that`s true for certainly at my house. We all love "Blackish." But the thing about the show that made it so good, regardless of what your ethnicity was, the problems were so relatable.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The highs and the lows are so relatable, and I think that that`s -- I think that`s a very good thing.\par \par \uc2 Tracee Ellis Ross said, "We really changed the landscape of modern primetime TV, more reflective of who we are as human beings." So bravo to the team of "Blackish." The show is going to be next --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bravo. It is one of the most incredible shows. One of the greatest shows of television. The acting was impeccable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I think so, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m going to miss it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am going to miss it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But they all say they`re ready to go on to their next chapter. So good for them, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good for them. I haven`t seen it. I`m going to look forward to it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, my "Talk of the Table" looking forward to right now is "The Daily Show`s" Jordan Klepper, best known for interviewing Trump supporters at rallies all across America. His new special takes things much, much further.\par \par \uc2 Last month, after speaking with people at the conservative political convention, CPAC, Klepper went to the European country of Hungary.\par \par \uc2 At CPAC, he had asked U.S. conservatives why so many seem to look to Hungary and its authoritarian leader as a model for America`s future.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: What do you think about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when he called \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  a genius? Was that him being tough?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think he has a way of playing with words.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes, definitely like saying the opposite of what a Patriot would say?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, and then he gets the media riled up.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: He disregards his own Intelligence Community, and instead he kisses [bleep] \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . But that`s him just being smart.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Being a wise guy.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: He is being a wise guy.\par \par \uc2 The MAGA crowd`s obsessions were predictable.\par \par \uc2 Is this a little much?\par \par \uc2 But there was a new infatuation I was surprised to hear because for the first time at CPAC, people were applauding another country.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hungary is on the forefront.\par \par \uc2 MAN: When you see people like leaders in Hungary, talking and speaking the way that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did when he was in office, it inspires me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The special is called "Jordan Klepper Fingers the Globe: Hungary for Democracy," later on \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 . Why do I we feel like a practical joke has been played on that title.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: You shouldn`t have comedians on your show. It is more respectable.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do enjoy it. And the lawyers required me to say that \uc2 CBS\uc2  is a division of the \uc2 Paramount Global Company\uc2 . "Daily Show" is part of that and you`ve convinced our fine corporate leaders to send you on a European vacation where you also did some work.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Exactly. I think that`s the little trick I pulled there. It`s been a long couple of years, send me to Eastern Europe and it turns out, democracy is crumbling there, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Which had a serious point, exactly. So why did you do it?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And why Hungary? That is what I think is so interesting.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: You know, Hungary has suddenly been a part of the cultural conversation here. \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  has done a special over there. We went to CPAC, where a lot of conservatives get their marching orders for what`s next. And they`re literally holding a CPAC in Budapest.\par \par \uc2 So, we were curious, what is it about Hungary that conservatives see as a potential American future? So we went.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you surprised to see that people there say that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is fake? That his presidency is not legit? Somebody even brought up Hunter Biden`s laptop. I was so -- I was blown away by that.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was a topic of conversation in Hungary.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: I thought I was escaping those conversations for a mere 10 days. But when we landed in their March 15th, they have a giant event, and I was shocked, I thought we`re going to talk about what was happening in Ukraine, which is literally next door to Hungary, Hunter Biden`s laptop came out of their mouths, so it felt like I hadn`t gotten very far.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the other really amazing thing is how Hungary has declined from a near democracy to what has been qualified as an authoritarian state by many observers.\par \par \uc2 In your piece, I learned that the opposition leader, the guy trying to replace the current leader got five minutes of state television over a four-year period.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s astounding.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes, you go --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Early in the morning early.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes, it was early in the morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Actually, same time slot you`re having right now.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Which is a great time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wonderful.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: You`ve got to utilize that timestamp, but you look at it, essentially, the amount of time that I`m going to be on this program is too little. I`ve been telling you producers this, but the Hungarians have been talking about that as well.\par \par \uc2 Like, if you go to Hungary, they`re having an election. It is a democracy. But what you see is, there`s only one candidate that anybody is hearing from us and that`s affecting the outcome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that`s my question, which is it is a democracy. It`s part of the E.U. These things don`t happen overnight. It`s not that all of a sudden Hungary woke up and it`s in the situation that you`re seeing on the ground now. How did it happen? And how do you see the antecedents here in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Well, it`s a slide from democracy towards autocracy to go from free to partly free. And basically what happens is they affect -- make voting harder. There`s a lot of gerrymandering, they control the media. Most of the media there is owned by wealthy oligarchs who have connections to the ruling party, and they vilify certain minority groups, the LGBTQ community is being vilified right now to rev up voters.\par \par \uc2 And you look at what`s happening in Florida right now, and you start to see that thing happening here. And so when you see this democracy slide, in a European country, it`s that frog boiling in water. Democracy is fragile. And so when you`re back here in America, look, it might look a lot like what`s happening there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before you realize -- the frog realizes, hey, I`m dying in here.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Getting really hot. I mean, you said in this special that you wanted to go to get some sense of where America is heading. You briefly touched on that. What conclusions did you draw?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Democracy is fragile, and you have to stay vigilant. When people talk about the downfall of American democracy, it can be very hyperbolic and hard to imagine. And you look at Hungary, it`s beautiful. Travel to Budapest, people are wonderful.\par \par \uc2 And they do vote. They do have a media system that looks on the outside, like it`s an open free democracy. But underneath, people who are in power are making it harder and harder for things to change. I think America needs to look very closely at that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How about misinformation? What role does that play? We know what it plays here in the United States. What role does it play in countries like Hungary?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Well, when I`m talking to people on the streets, and I`m talking to folks about \uc2 George Soros\uc2  being the boogeyman who controls things, talking about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  being a fake President, and they`re getting some of our misinformation. They are in bubbles, like we`re in bubbles over there.\par \par \uc2 And again, if all you`re exposed to day in and day out is one party`s view of the world and the destruction that is happening, literally in the world, literally next door, you`re going to be caught up in kind of this echo chamber.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You also talked about Ukraine with a spokesperson from the government, from President Orban. It was very interesting to see their reaction to Putin. Could you talk about that for a minute?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Well, like Orban --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They did not want to call Putin out.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes. Their leader, \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , is very cozy with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He said, it`s too early to tell.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Too early to tell, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who is responsible here?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Well, I think, you know, you start to see a lot of this rhetoric echoed in the conversation here. They make no -- \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  was very cozy with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and to his supporters. That`s a tough time to be cozy with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . So when you address, is that an issue now? They point fingers somewhere else.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got to be getting recognized in a lot of these conferences, right? Like when you`re on the street, people still talk to you?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes. Occasionally. I will say this though --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a good question, Tony.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The people who go to those rallies, watch your show and watch "The Daily Show."\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: They watch it.\par \par \uc2 I went to CPAC, full of conservatives and I kind of expected to be a public enemy number one, and I took selfies the entire time, and I pulled --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They turned into dartboards.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: They turn the dartboards really quick, but I think for many people, even if you`re the heel in this world, they view it as entertainment so they want to be a part of the world, even if you`re not supposedly on the side of the person they support.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great. Amusing ourselves to death. I love it.\par \par \uc2 Jordan Klepper, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Jordan`s special premieres tomorrow on \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`re going to talk with another author, Chloe Benjamin, she was riding a wave of success after her book hit "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestseller list, but it came with a physical cost.\par \par \uc2 Chloe will share her story and offer advice on how to redefine success.\par \par \uc2 But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here`s a question for all you Type A people out there. Have you ever worked so hard for a dream job or a promotion that you pushed yourself beyond your limits and you sacrificed your health? Chloe Benjamin knows what that`s like.\par \par \uc2 She went from being an assistant to a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestselling author with her book, "The Immortalists." It was a moment she dreamed of, but behind the scenes though, she was grappling with stress and chronic migraines. She ended up in the ER.\par \par \uc2 In a recent article for "The Cut," she wrote this about her physical breakdown saying: "I had put my writing, my ambition, and my success over not just my health, but my experience of life."\par \par \uc2 Wow. She joins us now with her story and some advice for a less stressful life. Chloe, I`m so glad to see you, because I so related to this story because I`m a big believer I`m pushing through, plowing through. You said that you did that almost to the expense, not almost -- to the expense of your health.\par \par \uc2 When did you realize, okay, I have a real problem here, and then I can`t push through?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN ("The Immortalists" author): Well, I think it`s interesting because it was a slow realization. And in fact, it was -- you know, it was hearing these cues from my body, the body speaks through pain.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: When you`re not listening, you know, to anything else. And slowly, I started to realize I could no longer ignore those signals, but I did for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what were the signals specifically that were speaking to you?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Really pain. Pain, yes, just increasing migraines. You know, I was so grateful to go on a book tour that was quite extensive. I was so honored that this book found a wide audience. But every time I did an event, I would get a migraine and I didn`t listen to that until it was too late.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what just listen to your body really mean?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Well, we hear this phrase "listen to your body."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Listen to the body. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: And for me, you know, it became very granular. It was, how am I holding my tension? How am I breathing? I realized that I held so much tension in my shoulders, I think a lot of us do. And so I actually put stickers around my house. And when I saw the sticker, that was my cue to relax my shoulders.\par \par \uc2 I almost have this detective quality of what makes my body feel good, what makes my body feel bad. And I`m really getting to know myself in that way so that my body could trust my mind, and my mind could trust my body.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s so much pressure on people, and people put it on themselves to push through that kind of pain. So on the one hand, there is listen to your body. But on the other hand, there`s -- we get this cultural message or maybe you can tell me where you think it comes from, to push through. Could you trace that back to some origin?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because Tony, sometimes I think, is it really that bad? Is pain really that bad? That I wonder about this, too?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Well, I think we live in a world in which productivity is prized over almost anything else. And you know, there is this idea of grind culture right now, you know, no days off, push.\par \par \uc2 And I think that can be so harmful, because it means that again, we`re privileging our work over our experience of life. And why are we doing that? And how can we change it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you said you`ve changed. You`ve changed your life.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: I have, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How you live.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: It`s taken a few years to make those changes, and honestly, I never thought I could. I`ve been ambitious and perfectionistic since childhood, and those qualities got me to a place of success, but they also hurt me.\par \par \uc2 So you know, the way that I live is totally different. And one thing that I hope might be helpful to others is an idea of pacing. So rather than waiting until I finish a scene, or a book, or a tour to rest, I`m resting throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`ll take a five minute break to meditate. I`ll walk around the block. Even just looking out the window for a few minutes when I`m on a screen, and this way, my energy moves like a wave instead of building to a point of collapse.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can imagine younger people, particularly younger, creative people listening to you and saying it`s very easy for Chloe to say this now, because she`s already made it. She wrote a bestseller.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s so true, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you`re starting out, it`s very difficult.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right. When you`re still trying to make it, make it, you`re still trying to push through, right?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: It is. It`s true. Although when I look back, I think maybe I didn`t have to do as much touring as I did for the book to be successful, and maybe it`s okay to say no, because when you say no, what are you saying yes to? That might be your health. That might be creating a way of working that is sustainable.\par \par \uc2 Because the thing is, you don`t want to have one book that is a success and then not be able to write or promote for years. So actually, if -- you know, I think maybe it`s a fallacy that you have to push and push and push until you make it because then you crash.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You definitely would.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Maybe you can make it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If you say no, what are you saying yes to? I like that.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Yes, and maybe you know, you can make it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Chloe, I am sorry.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: That`s okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Finish us off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Finish the sentence. Just finish it.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: You know, maybe you can do it in a more sustainable way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think it`s definitely true. I`m going to read "The Immortalists." Thank you very much, Chloe Benjamin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE BENJAMIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, we have to leave you with some very sad news for us here at CBS News. We`ve lost a very dear colleague, Michael Hopkins was our Director of Special Events and Logistics who died very suddenly yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Whenever -- look at his face -- whenever CBS News sent our teams to cover events like presidential debate or a natural disaster, it was Hops, "Hoppy" they called him, I like to call him "Mike" who made sure everyone and everything was where it needed to be.\par \par \uc2 He has been behind the scenes in all around the world for CBS News over the past 38 years. Remotes as we call them, won`t be the same without him.\par \par \uc2 Our hearts go out to Mike`s wife, Kathy, and their three children.\par \par \uc2 I got the news yesterday, last night, and I felt like I`d been kicked in the stomach.\par \par \uc2 Mike Hopkins was somebody you could call about anything and everything and he was always there to help with that raspy voice. He would say, "What do you need, dear?" And he would always figure it out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Way too soon. Still a still young man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Way too soon. You will be --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220420ei4k0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Battle Rages in Eastern Ukraine; New Images of Russian Warship; Pope Condemns Russia`s War; Warnings of a Cyberattack; Weekend of Violence; Tackling High Gas Prices; Wrongfully Convicted; Laser Strikes on the Rise; The Nuclear Energy Option; Having Career Regret; Janelle Monae Live; A Lesson in Compassion, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Charlie D`Agata,  Elizabeth Palmer, Weijia Jiang, Kris Van Cleave, Catherine Herridge, Erin  Moriarty, Ben Tracy, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 13,236 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 April 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday, your taxes are due today. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is off, but Nate is back. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 New missile attacks from Russia as the last Ukrainian defenders in Mariupol try to stop the city from falling into Russian hands.\par \par \uc2 DMYTRO KULEBA (Ukrainian Foreign Minister): The situation in Mario is heartbreaking. The city doesn`t exist anymore.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Officials say we could soon face cyberattacks from Russia as the war rages on where we`re most vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 JEN EASTERLY (United States Director of the \uc2 Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\uc2 ): We have to assume there`s going to be an incident, there`s going to be an attack.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Another day of violent clashes between Israeli Security Forces and Palestinians at Holy sites in Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And multiple mass shootings over the holiday weekend, why gun crimes are increasing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m going to go wash the blood off my wife`s car, which is like a really depressing way to spend Easter.\par \par \uc2 MAN: North Korea says it has tested a new type of tactical-guided missile that can deliver a nuclear strike.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Body camera footage shows the stunning moment police officers located a California teenager missing since 2019.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Jordan Spieth won the RBC Heritage in a sudden death playoff over Patrick Cantlay, his first win since becoming a father.\par \par \uc2 MAN: So, this is as sweet as it gets.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The \uc2 NBA\uc2  playoffs are full swing and they are not disappointing us.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Celtics in an instant classic as they take game one.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Eight seconds, kick out, less of that smart optics, finds the cutter, Tatum spins, lays it up in to beat the buzzer. It is over. It is over.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Celtics win. Celtics beating the buzzer and they`re going absolutely wild here at TD Garden.\par \par \uc2 There is somebody`s grandma who just made a sweet potato pie -- damn, I can`t believe it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did he say about sweet potato pie -- that was an amazing game.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was like what?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He said, "There is somebody`s grandma who just make a sweet potato pie, damn." You know, he was excited about that last second shot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And actually, that sort of worked with what happened there yesterday because listen, I was pulling for the Nets of course, but they were down 15 They came back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And almost won the game, but listen, it is only game one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not counting Kevin Durant out, ever.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Only Game one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And they insist though because grandma`s sweet potato pie is sweet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pretty good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was a sweet last second shot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A good way to describe it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We have a big show to start the week, including a look at the so-called nuclear option to fight climate change.\par \par \uc2 Nuclear power has been out of fashion with many people worried about meltdowns and radioactive waste. But the nuclear industry says it`s ready to be part of the solution to global warming and new reactors are being built in America for the first time in decades. Ben Tracy takes us on a visit.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And today`s edition of "Kindness 101" with Steve Hartman is all about compassion, specifically, a little boy named Robbie with a special bond for dogs in need of tender loving care. As Steve and his kids explain, Robbie`s kindness started someone being kind to him when all hope was lost.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kindness always works. Unfortunately, there`s nothing kind about what`s happening in Ukraine. And that of course, is where we begin again this week.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news there, at least seven people are dead after a missile strikes on the City of Lviv, but despite this attack in the west of Ukraine, Russia appears to be shifting its focus to the east.\par \par \uc2 Now this map shows a territory that Russia occupied about three weeks ago, including areas near the capital City of Kyiv. Here`s what they control today.\par \par \uc2 As you can see, the Russian troops are now concentrated mostly in the eastern and southern parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that`s where a fierce battle is still raging for the southern port city of Mariupol, which has been mostly destroyed. President Zelenskyy vows Ukrainians will fight absolutely to the end to defend the city.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in Ukraine. Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s Prime Minister said forces there continue to make a stand in Mariupol, and they will do until the end, this despite renewed threats from Russian forces that any remaining fighters will be eliminated.\par \par \uc2 We need to point out that this report contains images that some might find disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: There`s not much left of Mariupol after weeks of an unrelenting Russian onslaught, but within this sprawling steel plant and the series of tunnels beneath it, a number of Ukrainian forces are still holding out defying the ultimatum from Russia to surrender or die.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s Foreign Minister, \uc2 Dmytro Kuleba\uc2  told "Face the Nation" Russia is razing the city to the ground at any cost.\par \par \uc2 DMYTRO KULEBA: The situation in Mariupol is most dire, militarily and heartbreaking. The city doesn`t exist anymore.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: In Kharkiv, medics treating an injured woman were forced to dive for cover to save their own lives in the middle of an artillery attack.\par \par \uc2 Amid this suffering and fear, worshippers gathered for the start of Holy Week in Ukraine, Eastern is celebrated next Sunday. In Vinnytsia, southwest of the capital, Protodeacon Vladislav (sp?) prayed for the lives of the injured and souls of the dead.\par \par \uc2 But the war has turned a church into a battleground, too after Russian Orthodox leaders gave their blessings to the war and President Putin, too.\par \par \uc2 (FATHER VLADISLAV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Father Vladislav says that makes the Russian Orthodox Church an accomplice.\par \par \uc2 "It`s a position no church should ever take," he said. "Russia started this war, not defending their territory, but attacking the territory of our country."\par \par \uc2 Even as the war rages on, he is praying for a peaceful Easter.\par \par \uc2 Is it more difficult to hope under the circumstances?\par \par \uc2 (FATHER VLADISLAV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "When people undergo all this grief and atrocities, and they still hope, still believe still say they feel the hand of God that guides them, this belief becomes their reality."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And new images have emerged of that Russian warship that went down in the Black Sea last week, the Moskva, take a look at this, it is smoke billowing and starting to sink shortly after allegedly being struck by Ukrainian missiles.\par \par \uc2 Now naval experts have identified that that is the ship, but it can`t be confirmed where or when those images were taken. The Kremlin insists -- continue to insist that it went down by accident -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still, a lot of questions there, Charlie, and of course, we, around the world join the deacon in praying for the lives of the injured and the souls of the dead.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata in Ukraine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  spoke about the ongoing conflict. In his Easter Sunday message from St. Peter`s Square, he expressed his anguish over the war and hope that peace can prevail.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave reports from London.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: In clear discomfort from a lingering knee problem, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  slowly made his way to the balcony of St. Peter`s Basilica to deliver his traditional Easter speech, urging the world`s leaders to strive for peace.\par \par \uc2 The 85-year-old pontiff described this as an Easter of war, condemning the violence in Ukraine, calling it cruel and senseless, implicitly criticizing Russia: A message clearly resonating with the crowd of tens of thousands.\par \par \uc2 (POPE FRANCIS speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: "We have seen all too much blood, all too much violence," in what is being interpreted as a reference to the threat of nuclear war, France has quoted from a 1955 manifesto by philosopher Bertrand Russell and physicist Albert Einstein that warns of putting an end to the human race unless mankind renounces war.\par \par \uc2 To hear this stark warning, that fateful returned for the first Easter weekend since the pandemic. After two years of an empty St Peter`s Square, the crowd flooded into the street beyond the Square to listen and once again see \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  up close.\par \par \uc2 But the Vatican is facing criticism for its traditional Good Friday procession at Rome`s Colosseum, which included both the Ukrainian and Russian cross bearer.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: U.S. officials are warning about potential cyberattacks from Russia as the war in Ukraine drags on. They say everything from the public transit systems to banks and the energy sector could be targeted in this country.\par \par \uc2 Senior investigative correspondent Catherine Herridge has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: With the Russian invasion of Ukraine in its eighth week, a top U.S. cybersecurity official told "60 Minutes" \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  may resort to digital warfare against American targets.\par \par \uc2 JEN EASTERLY: We are seeing evolving Intelligence about Russian planning for potential attacks and we have to assume that there is going to be a breach, there is going to be an incident, there is going to be an attack.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: A March F.B.I. alert obtained by CBS News warned of increased reconnaissance by foreign actors of U.S. energy networks searching for vulnerabilities. And in recent weeks, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 `s Shields Up Campaign said every organization large and small must be prepared to respond to disruptive cyber incidents.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN HENRY\uc2  (\uc2 CrowdStrike\uc2  President): Think about energy companies financial services, sector transportation or communication, key organizations that are part of our day-to-day lives.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Sean Henry is the President of cybersecurity firm \uc2 CrowdStrike\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHAWN HENRY\uc2 : If they were shut down for weeks or months with the inability to service their consumers, that would be catastrophic to the United States.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: So you`re saying you go to the ATM, the ATM is not working. You try and get on public transit and the trains are not running.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN HENRY\uc2 : That is exactly what I`m saying.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The Justice Department recently unsealed criminal charges for Russian hackers alleging a past campaign against the energy sector that targeted victims in the United States and in more than 135 other countries.\par \par \uc2 LISA MONACO (U.S. Deputy Attorney General): It is very much the type of activity that we are warning about today when it comes to Russia`s response to the world`s response to the horror in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Why do you think Russia has not unleashed a major cyberattack at this point?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN HENRY\uc2 : I think that it`s an arrow in their quiver. I think that there may be some concern about what might happen in a retaliatory response.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: And cybersecurity experts tell CBS News, the U.S. has a robust capability to respond, but there can be unintended consequences and the risk of further escalation with potentially damaging fallout for all involved -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Catherine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now, we turn to a wave of gun violence over the weekend in this country, two shootings with multiple victims in South Carolina and a deadly attack in Pittsburgh, add to a growing list of more than 100 mass shootings so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Think about those numbers for just a second -- Nikki Battiste looks at the latest violence and what could be behind it. We have to warn you, words you`ve heard many times before, some of the video may be very tough to watch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @ThompsonFoto12 of Pittsburgh shooting.)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: This terrifying video captures that chaotic moments when shooters opened fire on partygoers in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, leaving two teenagers dead and almost a dozen others injured.\par \par \uc2 Police say at least 90 rounds were fired into the crowd of roughly 200 people, mostly miners at a party held at an \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  rental property. Video shows broken windows where people jumped in an attempt to escape the gunfire.\par \par \uc2 A massive manhunt is still underway to find the shooters.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We owe it to everybody in the city of Pittsburgh to get those that are responsible for this.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The violence was just one of multiple deadly mass shootings this holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 In Columbia, South Carolina. 22-year-old, Jewayne M. Price was arrested in connection with Saturday`s shooting at a mall that sent shoppers fleeing for safety, fourteen people were injured.\par \par \uc2 Price is charged with unlawful carrying of a pistol.\par \par \uc2 A separate South Carolina town was the site of another mass shooting when gunshots rang out at a club early Sunday morning. Nine were injured.\par \par \uc2 The violent weekend is part of a broader trend in violence. This year, there have already been 142 mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROMAN (\uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  Senior Fellow at NORC): There is a huge increase in the number of guns purchased by Americans and there has been some reductions in the requirements that people have about how they access and carry and hold those guns.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: John Roman, Senior Fellow at NORC at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  says access to guns is one of a combination of factors behind the violence.\par \par \uc2 How much of a role has the pandemic played in these increases?\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROMAN: The pandemic played a huge role in increasing gun violence in the United States, especially among young men who were separated from all their social sports, from school, from work, from community, from church. They lived in places in some -- where the gun violence is very concentrated and that led to a cycle of shootings and retaliations and we`re still seeing the repercussions of that cycle.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: For CBS MORNINGS, Nikki Battiste, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know you hear the story and you go "another one." I just don`t want us to become desensitized to this. I almost feel like we`re Russian -- playing Russian roulette with all of our lives. It doesn`t feel like you`re safe anywhere in this country, and I don`t know what`s going to take for us to get it right when it comes to guns. We don`t have it right yet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let me echo what you said, we need to resist the urge to become numb to the stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There used to be a time when we`d be shocked when we hear about any shooting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now it`s like, "Oh, another one."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Another one," and "Where is it?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, but you when you see that number, 142 mass shootings this year alone, that is stark.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is April.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is April.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. North Korea claims it successfully test fired a new type of nuclear missile over the weekend just before the United States and South Korea began a joint military exercise.\par \par \uc2 The North`s official news agency released a photo claiming to show dictator, Kim Jong-un celebrating the test.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer has more from Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 This is the 12th missile test in three and a half months and proof that North Korea`s leader Kim Jong on is fast tracking a serious upgrade to his already fearsome arsenal.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Official North Korean pictures shows the launch of what South Korean analysts say were two short range missiles, at least one of which landed on a tiny rocky island.\par \par \uc2 Kim Jong-un is among the observers looking especially pleased.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen variations of this, the launches and the happy leader every few weeks since January. They may have a cartoon quality, but they are deadly serious.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: On Friday, there was a huge parade in Pyongyang on a public holiday to mark the 110th Anniversary of the birth of North Korea`s founder, Kim Il-sung.\par \par \uc2 Unusually, the own weapons on show were neon lit models. But behind the spectacle, preparations were already underway for the very real weapons test with ominous overtones.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: It`s not the hardware that has got alarm bells ringing this time so much as the rhetoric. The North Koreans are saying the missile is meant to carry nuclear warheads, which it already has. An analyst think we`ll be seeing a new nuclear test sometime very soon.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Elizabeth Palmer, Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Millions of Americans who hit the road for Easter or Passover found it cost a little less to drive. That`s good news.\par \par \uc2 AAA reports the average price of gas nationwide has fallen about 24 cents in the last five weeks to $4.09 a gallon. President Biden has taken steps to bring that number down because it is so considered high.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang shows us the latest effort to control gas prices and why some of the President`s allies still don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: As the Russian invasion causes instability in the global energy market, President Biden wants to ramp up domestic oil production. He is already tapping the nation`s Strategic Petroleum Reserve to help slash gas prices, and now, the administration plans to resume selling leases for new oil and gas drilling on 144,000 acres of public lands in nine states even though Mr. Biden made this promise on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): No more drilling on Federal lands. Period. Period. Period. Period.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The Department of Interior called the decision to open up more Federal land for drilling a balanced approach to energy development, but climate groups are furious. One group says while the Biden administration talks a good talk on climate action, the reality is they are in bed with the oil and gas industry.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Of course, another big priority is getting back to normal. So here on the South Lawn this morning, the White House is getting ready to welcome about 30,000 visitors for the Annual Easter Egg Roll, which came to a halt in 2020 because of the pandemic, but very lucky children are already waiting outside and inside, there are 50,000 of these hard boiled eggs for the Easter Egg Roll races with wooden spoons and decorating, some egg hunts.\par \par \uc2 But Gayle, with this bump I have, I am about to roll myself down the lawn.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: It is a little bump here, but it will work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, your bump looks great. I was thinking, Weijia, you`ve got to breathe. I remember those days, but baby --\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Baby squeezes our organs, but it is worth everything it takes to get there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Weijia looks great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You look fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You look fantastic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re doing a great job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hear Ed O`Keefe is in that line with his two little kids. I hope we get video of that.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Weijia. Thank you and baby.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right now, it`s 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, Erin Moriarty has the remarkable story of Joyce Watkins convicted more than 30 years ago of a crime she did not commit.\par \par \uc2 It turns out, it was bad forensic evidence that put her behind bars. Of course, we`ll share her long struggle to prove her innocence.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, Ben Tracy shows us why our future could be nuclear power.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: This is the cooling tower for the newest nuclear power reactor in the country. We`ll tell you why nuclear power may be safer than you think and why scientists and even environmentalists are now saying it is critical to combating climate change.\par \par \uc2 That story coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a remarkable story today and "remarkable" is a word here -- of perseverance in the face of great injustice.\par \par \uc2 Joyce Watkins spent decades in prison for a crime she did not commit because of bad science. A former boyfriend also wrongly convicted died in prison.\par \par \uc2 According to the Innocence Project, false or misleading forensic evidence is a factor in nearly a quarter of all wrongful convictions in this country.\par \par \uc2 Erin Moriarty is here with this particular story.\par \par \uc2 Erin, this is just heartbreaking on many levels.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: But it was an honor to meet Joyce.\par \par \uc2 What happened to Joyce Watkins and her boyfriend, Charlie Dunn is scary because it could happen to anyone. Hundreds of people have been convicted because of so called forensic sciences that are later discredited, and as you will see, in this case, it`s an error that can take years or even a lifetime to correct.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOYCE WATKINS (Wrongfully convicted of crime): My family is my life. My nieces, my nephew, that`s my life.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Late on the night of June 26, 1987. Joyce Watkins drove from her home in Tennessee to pick up her great niece who had been staying with a relative in Kentucky for two months.\par \par \uc2 Almost immediately, Watkins could tell something was wrong and took the child in the morning to the emergency room. The four-year-old seen here as she was transferred to Vanderbilt Medical Center in Nashville was suffering from head and vaginal injuries and died a day later.\par \par \uc2 Joyce, did it ever occur to you at that moment that you would be accused of her death?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE WATKINS: No.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: But they questioned you?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE WATKINS: Yes. They asked me what happened to her. I told them I didn`t know. I couldn`t tell them something that I didn`t know because I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Despite evidence that the child had been injured while in Kentucky. Watkins and her boyfriend, Charlie Dunn, became suspects after an Assistant Medical Examiner visually observing the child`s bruises mistakenly determined that she had been injured during the nine hours she was with the couple.\par \par \uc2 JASON GICHNER (Tennessee Innocence Project senior attorney): Everybody just got laser focused on Joyce and Charlie.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Jason Gichner is a senior attorney with the Tennessee Innocence Project.\par \par \uc2 JASON GICHNER: It didn`t matter that they cooperated with the investigation, that they kept meeting with the police over and over again without a lawyer and saying, we didn`t do this. Come take whatever evidence you want.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Watkins and Dunn were both convicted and sent to prison for life.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE WATKINS: It took everything away from us. It took us from our families, him from his kids, took us from everything we`ve worked for.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Over the years, they tried and failed to get their convictions overturned. In 2015, while still incarcerated, Charlie Dunn died of throat cancer. Later that year, after 27 years in prison, Watkins was released on parole.\par \par \uc2 What was your mission at that point?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE WATKINS: To prove our innocence.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Last summer, the Conviction Review Unit of the Davidson County District Attorney`s Office released a report finding that the methods used by the Medical Examiner have been largely discredited and determined that Joyce Watkins and Charlie Dunn are actually innocent.\par \par \uc2 When you heard at the hearing last December, just how wrong her medical evidence was in 1987, and that it was the main evidence against you and Charlie, how did you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE WATKINS: Broken, real heartbroken, because it is something that should have never happened. When I was incarcerated --\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: It took 35 years and the unwavering faith of Joyce Watkins, now 74 years old, but in January of this year, the Court finally got it right.\par \par \uc2 ANGELITA BLACKSHEAR DALTON (Davidson County Criminal Court Judge of Division II): Miss Watkins, this charge against you is dismissed, and to the family of Charlie Dunn, the charge against Charlie Dunn is dismissed.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE WATKINS: It was a happy day. It was a happy day.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: But bittersweet, too.\par \par \uc2 You never got to grieve either. You never had time to grieve the loss of Brandi.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE WATKINS: No, I haven`t, but I`m going to go and visit her grave in a few months.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Both Joyce Watkins and Charlie Dunn lost everything they had. Under Tennessee law, Joyce has to first apply for and then get a pardon from the Governor to be eligible for any compensation and there is likely nothing for the family of Charlie Dunn.\par \par \uc2 I think the real takeaway on this story is the fact that there was too much weight put on one witness, that Assistant Medical Examiner, because they ignored all the evidence that this child have had problems in Kentucky before Joyce and Charlie ever picked them up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I`m looking at Joyce who lost such a big chunk of her life, Charlie, who`s no longer with us, will they reopen the investigation because somebody did something to this little girl and it wasn`t them?\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: I think there is a demand for that, but it has been 35 years. And so you know, witnesses are gone. I think it could be very, very difficult.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You told us just before we went on the air, she was in the process of adopting a child herself, because she lost many things.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: She did. You know, she owned her own home and she had savings, but I think what hurts her the most is the fact that she was in the process of adopting a child. She`s all about family. And I think that`s what weighs on her the most, the biggest loss and that can`t be compensated for.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was she like, Erin?\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Well I have to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m in awe of her.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: The word "grace" comes to mind. Every time when you -- everyone says that when they meet her. She doesn`t appear to have anger. She is just determined.\par \par \uc2 And here is what I loved about her the most, that it wasn`t enough for her to be exonerated, even though Charlie had gone, she insisted he be exonerated as well.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow. And you pointed out yesterday, Erin, the killer/killers still at large.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Well, have never been -- if someone killed her and they don`t think these injuries are accidental, that person has never been brought to justice. No question.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tough story. Erin Moriarty --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There has to be compensation here though, Erin. That strikes me, too. No compensation --\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: Well, under the law, she has to get a pardon first.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pardon first.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORIARTY: And then it`s limited to a million which may sound like a lot, but it`s not considering all that she lost.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, it`s not. Erin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, new urgency and the effort to crack down on laser lights that can leave pilots blinded.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, we focus on something that no pilot ever wants to see, a laser light beaming right into the cockpit.\par \par \uc2 New numbers from the F.A.A. show incidents of people hitting aircrafts with these blinding lights are on pace for a new record. The number of reported strikes jumped by 41 percent last year prompting an F.A.A. alert and a plea for people to stop aiming handheld laser pointers at planes.\par \par \uc2 Pilots tell Kris Van Cleave it can literally be a matter of life and death.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: We just got hit with a laser.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Flying low over Palmetto, Florida, Manatee Sheriffs watch one helicopter got lit up by a blinding bright green laser aimed at the crew from the ground.\par \par \uc2 BEN SEEHORN (Deputy): All you see is green. So it`s very disorientating.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Deputy Ben Seehorn (sp?) was at the controls.\par \par \uc2 BEN SEEHORN: It`s almost like a disco ball. Once the laser itself hits the cockpit, it bounces everywhere and it`s very blinding.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, There it is, okay. Right from the river.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Laser strikes on aircraft are soaring, up 41 percent last year to more than 9,700. That`s more than one an hour.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Southwest 4465, use caution, just had an unauthorized laser illumination event.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: California, Texas, and Florida lead the nation in incidents, 47 pilots reported injuries from the strikes last year. The lasers can cause temporary blindness, and in some cases, lasting eye injuries and they often occur just as pilots are about to land.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Departure Delta 1037.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Go ahead, sir.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, we got a laser that was pointed at us when we were at the turn.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: In Seattle, the F.B.I. is now offering a $10,000.00 reward after a dramatic increase in laser incidents. Pilots reported more than 100 strikes since January, 28 occurred in just three days while airliners were taking off or landing.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER (Commercial Airline Pilot): We absolutely have to be able to see to land the aircraft.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: It happened to Captain Laura Einsetler as she was landing at LAX.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: I was flying as the Captain. The first officer exclaimed, "ah" and said "Laser" as the entire cockpit had lit up bright green.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The F.A.A. thinks part of the reason these strikes are surging the way they are is the laser pointers are powerful and cheap. We ordered this one online for about 20 bucks.\par \par \uc2 Pilots are trained to look away from the lasers if they see a strike coming. In the last year, \uc2 Washington State Patrol\uc2  pilots were first in the nation to test new protective glasses developed by the Air Force Research Lab aimed at filtering out the laser light to reduce the impact of these strikes.\par \par \uc2 While the number of incidents continues to climb, arrests are rare.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They are directly at the duplex.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: But Watch One was able to hone in on the guy with the laser, directing officers to a 17-year-old who is now facing felony charges and the potential of thousands in fines from the F.A.A.\par \par \uc2 BEN SEEHORN: It`s not worth it. You`re going to have a bad day if you get caught.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: A warning from police that a beam of light could have you headed to jail in a flash.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Sarasota, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Job of a pilot is tough enough.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you will be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:44, it is time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Every song applies to Vlad, I`m telling you.\par \par \uc2 All right, it is time for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, baby.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With Vlad Duthiers aka "Austin Powers."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s up? Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Glad to be back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 All right, so let`s start with some sports here. Game one of the playoff matchup between the Nets and the Celtics was exciting, for more reasons than one. Of course, Boston beat Brooklyn 115 to 114 with this buzzer beater layup by Jason Tatum.\par \par \uc2 During the game though, Nets guard Kyrie Irving flipped the bird, now there you go. A little circle there and not once, but twice to some in the crowd.\par \par \uc2 Now, he said he is used to getting booed by Celtics fans ever since he left Boston after playing two seasons there. The second time Irving apparently flipped off the crowd was when he appeared to scratch his head.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at the fans. I love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The dude in the back.\par \par \uc2 No word yet on any fines for Irving. He was fined $25,000.00 back in January for directing obscene language towards a fan, so we were talking about this a little earlier. He says, look, I`m going to direct the energy that I`m getting directly back to the crowd.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they had been harassing him all game.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All game long, which but you said if you`re going to be on the road --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If you`re going to do that, it`s going to be exhausting as an athlete. The one thing I do respect about Kyrie, he has his zen about him and he is locked in when he steps on the court.\par \par \uc2 I feel like yesterday he lost his cool a little bit and I`m not taking a shot at him. This is coming from an athlete who would talk trash every time somebody talks trash to me. Football is a little bit different though.\par \par \uc2 On the basketball court, all eyes are on the superstars. And another thing I`ll say, giving that same energy back to the crowd, flipping the bird, it seems good in theory when you`re going back and forth with an adult, but there are also kids out there ...\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: ... sitting in the stands.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I agree with you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But I love Kyrie though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think you`re right. I think you`re right, and he did say that he`s not directing it at all at the fans, only the ones that are booing him, but you`re right, you`re an athlete.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And how much are you supposed to take though, too? I look at that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I`ll say Kyrie, "Just let it go."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s the job, though, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Keep balling out, he had a great game. Kyrie, just let fans be fans.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you tune it out, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can have tunnel vision, yes, and put those blinders on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Good advice. Good advice.\par \par \uc2 All right, this inflight video is getting a lot of attention on social media with commenters wondering if other passengers are okay with the performance. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Jack Jensz, Jr. via \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let me explain what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 If you haven`t seen this because millions of people already have. A youth pastor shared this video of a group of people singing Christian songs earlier this month, obviously on a plane. It was captioned "Worshiping Jesus 30,000 feet in the air."\par \par \uc2 It`s unclear if this was a commercial flight or if anyone asked permission from other travelers or crew before singing, but of course everybody`s zeroed in on this one dude, right on the scene, a picture of a man, like cue the "Curb Your Enthusiasm" music.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "National Lampoon`s Animal House.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And also a lot of people shared this John Belushi video. We all remember the late Great John Belushi in "Animal House." And here is the scene where the guys play "Gave my Love a Cherry." That had no stone, but the larger -- and of course -- bam.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m sure not everybody liked it. But I think there are a lot of people that will appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If it wasn`t any other language, if it was an Arabic, would people have the same reaction?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My man was like, Hallelujah. Hallelujah. Sit down. All right.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, is there anything the multitalented Janelle Monae cannot do? It doesn`t seem like it. She is here today to talk about her new book of short stories.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. Nuclear power could be making a comeback as a way to fight climate change. Ben Tracy looks at what might be next in our new series "Earth 365."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Steve Hartman is back with another edition of "Kindness 101" and he talks to one very smart kid whose love for older dogs is personal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Steve, kids, and dogs -- a winner.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s eye opener at eight.\par \par \uc2 Russian attacks intensify overnight in Kharkiv and other cities while Ukrainians make their last stand against Russian troops in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 DMYTRO KULEBA: From the way Russian army behaved, they decided to raze the city to the ground at any cost.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: U.S. officials warned transit systems, banks, and the energy sector could potentially be targeted soon by Russian hackers.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN HENRY\uc2 : Key organizations that are part of our day-to-day lives, that would be catastrophic to the United States.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A manhunt is underway after a mass shooting in Pittsburgh, part of a surge of gun violence in America.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A woman is cleared after decades in prison for a crime she did not commit, a conviction based on false scientific evidence.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE WATKINS: It took everything away from us. It took us from our families.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Paul on Nantz, the hesitation and the drive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And there was no blocking out the \uc2 Phoenix Suns\uc2  in Game One of their playoff series against the \uc2 New Orleans Pelicans\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BARKLEY (\uc2 ESPN\uc2  sports commentator): The Suns are going to whoop these boys, plain and simple. Guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Off to the races, Paul will accelerate and take it in. Good.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Here comes Bridges plummeting into the paint. Oh, what a shot, and the Suns are up, one nothing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go, go, go Chris Paul and Charles Barkley has spoken.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can always count on him keeping it real.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Always.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is a former Phoenix Sun, too, as well\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know that`s true. But always keep --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, a little detail there. There might be a little bias, just a little.\par \par \uc2 As we count down to Earth Day, this Friday, CBS News is launching our "Earth 365" series. It`s focusing on issues affecting the health of our planet year round.\par \par \uc2 The latest U.N. Climate Report warns that the world needs to move very quickly away from planet-warming fossil fuels like coal, oil, and natural gas, stuff a lot of us use. The idea is to switch to cleaner forms of energy like wind and solar power, which are now cheaper and more abundant than ever before.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But to ditch fossil fuels rapidly, a lot of experts and even some environmentalists say we also need to consider the nuclear option.\par \par \uc2 Senior national and environmental correspondent, Ben Tracy, visited America`s first new reactors in more than 30 years and learned that nuclear power could be poised for a comeback.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: So this thing, that`s the new reactor?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WOMACK\uc2  (\uc2 Georgia Power Company\uc2 ): That is -- that`s it.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: That is the newest nuclear reactor in the United States. There are two of them being built here outside Augusta, Georgia. When combined with these other reactors built in the 1980s, this will be the largest nuclear power plant in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WOMACK\uc2 : We`re building the future of energy and we think nuclear power is a major part of it.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: \uc2 Chris Womack\uc2  runs the \uc2 Georgia Power Company\uc2 , which is overseeing the final construction of the first nuclear reactors in the country in more than three decades. It`s taken 10 years and costs more than $28 billion.\par \par \uc2 So that is nuclear fuel in there.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WOMACK\uc2 : That is nuclear fuel there.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: He showed us this 47-foot deep sapphire pool loaded with nuclear fuel rods containing uranium that will enter the reactor and power a million homes.\par \par \uc2 Why do we need this?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WOMACK\uc2 : Our customers need energy on a 24/7 basis. There needs to be a foundation of our electric grid that`s available all the time.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Right now, that foundation is fossil fuels, which are rapidly warming the planet, having devastating and deadly changes to the climate.\par \par \uc2 Coal, oil, and natural gas make up 61 percent of our energy supply in the U.S., renewables such as wind and solar are 20 percent and the nation`s 56 nuclear plants make up the rest.\par \par \uc2 Nuclear power creates no planet warming emissions, and when the wind isn`t blowing and the sun isn`t shining, the atoms are still splitting.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WOMACK\uc2 : It`s reliable. It`s safe. It`s affordable, and it`s very, very clean.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: If this is such a great form of power generation, why are these the only two reactors being built in the country?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WOMACK\uc2 : You know, we got away from it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of anti-nuclear power protests.)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Because the public turned against. It in the 1970s, concerns over nuclear waste insecurity fueled a nuclear power backlash.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The China Syndrome.")\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: The 1979 film "The China Syndrome" stoked fears with a fictional meltdown at a nuclear power plant. Twelve days after it hit theaters --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Radioactive xenon gas is still being discharged.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Life imitated art in Pennsylvania at the Three Mile Island nuclear facility. No one was killed, but then came 1986.\par \par \uc2 MAN: U.S. officials believe the disaster at Chernobyl began on Friday with --\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: The meltdown of Reactor Four at Chernobyl in Ukraine when it was part of the Soviet Union became the worst nuclear disaster in history, directly killing 31 people and exposing millions more to the fallout.\par \par \uc2 These accidents made nuclear power itself radioactive.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Nuclear power is dead. It died at Three Mile Island.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Dozens of planned nuclear projects were canceled, and the U.S. double down on fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 But now nuclear power is once again making headlines as a way to potentially save the world from climate change.\par \par \uc2 ALEX TREMBATH (\uc2 The Breakthrough Institute\uc2  Deputy Director): Electricity demand in the United States and really all around the world is only going up, up, up, up, up.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: \uc2 Alex Trembath\uc2  is Deputy Director of \uc2 The Breakthrough Institute\uc2 , a climate think tank. He says to electrify everything from our vehicles to our homes and businesses, we will need nuclear.\par \par \uc2 ALEX TREMBATH: There is no grid on the planet that relies overwhelmingly on wind and solar for more than even half of its electricity generation. So, everywhere in the world where nuclear gets shut down, it`s replaced largely by fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: After the 2011 meltdown of Japan`s Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant, the Japanese and the Germans shut down most of their nuclear power, coal and gas filled much of the void.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. is on track to decommission nine nuclear power units in just eight years, including California`s Diablo Canyon Plant, which generates 10 percent of the state`s electricity, far more than wind power provides.\par \par \uc2 ALEX TREMBATH: I don`t know why we would take this enormous piece of our clean electricity pie and just throw it in the garbage.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Trembath argues the impacts of fossil fuels are killing far more people than nuclear power ever has. And nearly 80 scientists and academics recently signed a letter urging California`s Governor to keep Diablo Canyon open warning that closing it is irresponsible and potentially catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 RALPH CAVANAGH (\uc2 Natural Resources Defense Council\uc2 ): Nuclear power has been getting a shot in the United States for at least five decades.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Ralph Cavanagh is with the \uc2 Natural Resources Defense Council\uc2 . He argues nuclear is too costly and takes too long to build to effectively help fight climate change, and there is also that other issue.\par \par \uc2 RALPH KAVANAGH: Our failure to find a nuclear waste solution, which means that any community that hosts a nuclear power plant has to be prepared to also host its radioactive waste for centuries, if not longer.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: The nuclear industry is now touting a next generation of smaller, safer, and less expensive plants. \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  is funding a company called \uc2 TerraPower\uc2 , which plans to build its first compact nuclear plant in Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WOMACK\uc2 : I think people can come around.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: \uc2 Chris Womack\uc2  says he is excited to show that nuclear power has a future despite being billions over budget.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WOMACK\uc2 : Nuclear power will be a reliable stable fuel source for many, many years to come.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: In what could be a new Atomic Age.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ben Tracy, in Waynesboro, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, something for everybody to mull over. I think Mr. Womack and Mr. Trembath make some really interesting points about nuclear, but a lot of people are still holding on to those movies, "The China Syndrome."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then in the early 80s, it was "The Day After."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I remember that as kid. That`s right. Every child saw that movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember that, too. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I think there still needs to be a reeducation on nuclear energy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So hearing Ben Tracy being told it`s reliable, safe, affordable, and clean, it does help put people at ease.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that point that, you know, fossil fuels are killing far more people than nuclear power.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought that was interesting, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It has, I mean, I lived in France, 70 percent or two- thirds of France`s energy comes from nuclear power. There are ways to do it properly. There are ways to do it, so that you don`t see the things that we saw in Chernobyl or even Three Mile Island.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  career expert, Catherine Fisher, who is in our greenroom right now will join us to discuss why people are feeling regret after leaving jobs during the pandemic, and what to do if you are one of them.\par \par \uc2 Don`t worry, we`ve got your back.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:09, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Many of us have done things that we regret, like skipping a trip, leaving a relationship, or walking away from a good job.\par \par \uc2 A recent survey focuses on some of the 47 million people who quit their jobs during last year`s so called Great Resignation. About one in five of those people who resigned during the pandemic regretted it. Many of them wanted more pay and responsibility then realize their new gig wasn`t the dream job they wanted.\par \par \uc2 So what do they do now? What do you do now if this is you?\par \par \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  career expert, Catherine Fisher is here with some advice to help workers bounce back from a wrong move.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, how you doing?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (\uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  career expert): Good morning. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Now, why are people regretting leaving their jobs during the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: So those of you who are, you know, unhappy with the move, it`s probably because that you were just moving too fast both on the company side and on the employee side.\par \par \uc2 So what had happened is that these companies were really trying to fill vacant roles and had been sitting for a long time, so they shortcut the system in terms of you know, we saw some companies who are taking two weeks to hire people, it`s really fast to make a decision on an employment both for the person taking the job and for the company.\par \par \uc2 And some people also we saw on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 , you know, a woman was talking about how she was lured to that really big salary, but didn`t take into account what is the job? What`s my manager going to be like? You know, how much accountability will I have?\par \par \uc2 And so because of this hiring process has really changed, you know, all of a sudden, they didn`t ask those the right questions that you typically do in the hiring process.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are the right questions, Catherine, to ask?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If you`re sitting out here on this limb, and it is thawing, so now, what are the right questions? Because you don`t want to make the same mistake twice?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes. And what you want to do is make sure that you`re not asking really general questions of like: Hey, do you like working here? Of course, their job is to sell you on the job. So what you should be asking is, how do you celebrate wins? How do you celebrate failures? Like what happens if you fail? Give me an example of a time when the team didn`t have enough resources to do a job, how did you manage through that?\par \par \uc2 So being really specific about what`s important to you to give you greater insight into the culture. But I`m also --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Culture. I never when I was looking for a job, ever asked about the workplace. Did you?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No. No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you ever workplace culture, ever?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, well, culture is very important, and something that many companies are really investing in and a great thing to do, though, is beyond just, you know, the people that you`re talking to on the interview panel is talk to people outside of that interview panel.\par \par \uc2 You can go to their, you know, company page, you can go figure out, what is it that you know -- see if you have any one in common that you worked with at that company or you know people and talk to them about really what is it like to work there, you`ll get the more honest answer and take time to reflect, really ask yourself, what do you need to be happy?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, if you took a job during the pandemic, and now you realize it`s not the right fit, which is what a lot of people have realized, what should you do? Because I would think that as a manager, we were talking about impulse, you`d look at the person coming back to a job or trying to get another job and go, "Hmm, you strike me like an impulsive person not a thoughtful individual." That could be a red flag.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes. Well, you can be thoughtful. So, what is really important is to say, you know, I took this job, I didn`t ask all the right questions that I needed to and what I`ve discovered during this, you know, three-month job that I had is this is actually what`s really important to me, and so if you use that time to be self-reflective, and be honest about it. I actually think it makes you a strong candidate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s still a red flag though. I mean, if you didn`t ask --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, not every employer is going to say, "Okay."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, yes, we will just forget about that period of your life. And I mean, I think it`s a little tough for people if you`re going to make a decision about bringing somebody in, and you want to assess the character of that person. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do you add that to the resume? If you`re going to look for a new job, do you say, "Oh, I quit my old job and I was only there for a short time, but it`s not what it looks like."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I would not add that part.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: And I actually wouldn`t say you should.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: See.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You should?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes. Because I think --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, you`re being honest, right?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: If you`re being honest and if you`re saying I took this job, because this is what I was looking for, I realized that actually what I need to be successful is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I made a mistake.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think people appreciate it when you say you made a mistake.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes. Just don`t do it all the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s -- just don`t do it all the time. There`s a lot of mistakes to be made. But you say despite these numbers, the people looking for work still have the upper hand today.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: They do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You do? You do think that. How so?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: There`s still a lot of companies looking to hire people, and I think that also companies are doing a lot to attract employees. So they`re doing things obviously, like with salary, sign-on bonuses. We`re also hearing a lot of companies doing things like upskilling their workforce, so paying for their education, so it really is still a great time to look for a job.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I will say though, that for the people that leave, that creates opportunities for the people who stay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ah. This is true.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also important not to burn bridges. If you`re going to leave, don`t do that rage quit and put it on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, the worst thing you can do is rage.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what I`m hearing, finding a new job is like finding a new lover. Be honest with what you want, right?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: It`s absolutely is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I haven`t thought of that, but now I am thinking about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you looking for a new lover?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, I am just saying. That`s what I am hearing. It`s like a relationship you`re getting into.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, it is absolutely. You`ve got to know how much you`re paid.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. It`s not "50 Shades of Grey," Gayle. I`m just saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m taking notes. Taking notes. Thank you, Catherine Fisher.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Steve Hartman gets a lesson and compassion from a big hearted little boy in our series, we call it "Kindness 101."\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are launching a new series, we`re calling it "Rebuilding America" to showcase the ingenuity of individuals and communities who are working together to build a better world, one project at a time.\par \par \uc2 So tomorrow, we`re going to take you out to New Mexico where an eco-minded group is battling climate change by building so called Earthships. The self-sustaining homes are made largely from recycled materials. Look at this.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to introduce you to a recent convert who gave up life here in New York City for nights in the desert.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: I love the way this house functions. I love being off grid. I love the stars at night.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think he loves it. We will show you what`s driving the popularity of these other worldly homes. That`s tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And just ahead, we will talk with singer-songwriter, actor and now, author, Janelle Monae about writing fiction and how it compares to writing music.\par \par \uc2 Your local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a table, we`re talking. Gayle, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m first. Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m starting my "Talk of the Table," unfortunately, with some very troubling news from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I hear another people speaking my ear, do you guys hear that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I do hear a little bit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that`s Janelle getting mic`ed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Janelle Monae.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Janelle getting mic`ed so that`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 A community kitchen run by Chef Jose Andres was destroyed in an apparent Russian missile strike in the City of Kharkiv over the weekend. Now, four of the kitchen staff members were injured in that attack, but they are okay.\par \par \uc2 Chef Andres says the team in Kharkiv has already moved and started cooking in another location and he is asking people please, please, please stop the war.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of other locations, actor director, this is Liev Schreiber, has been helping \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  feed Ukrainian refugees at a kitchen in Poland during Passover.\par \par \uc2 So last week, they made a batch of borscht for 1,500 people. Schreiber posted this video on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  over the weekend for his fans during a lunch break.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LIEV SCHREIBER (Actor-director): I have an exciting announcement to make that in honor of Passover, we are going to be cooking 900 kilos of brisket.\par \par \uc2 The Master Chef -- just chef -- Marc Murphy here.\par \par \uc2 MARC MURPHY (Chef): Hey, how are you?\par \par \uc2 LIEV SCHREIBER: On the wheels of steel, and the amazing --\par \par \uc2 ACE LICHTENSTEIN (Ideal Management President): Sous chef.\par \par \uc2 LIEV SCHREIBER: Sous chef, Ace Lichtenstein.\par \par \uc2 To everyone who donated, thank you so much. Have an amazing Easter. Have an amazing Passover. Chag Sameach. And please donate if you can. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love Liev Schreiber. He says, you know, his dad is Protestant, his mom is Jewish. His grandfather`s maternal grandfather emigrated from Ukraine, and he just wanted to do what he can to help and he is encouraging others to do the same.\par \par \uc2 I`ve just known him as Ray Donovan and I like everything about that, but there, he looks super cool. He`s very in the moment and he`s working very hard.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So many people are doing that. But it`s so horrific to know that the place where people are just trying to get some food is hit by --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is hit by a missile.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right, and I just love Liev Schreiber. He has a million dollar voice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He has influence, and he is using it for good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is awesome. That was great job, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 All right, so my talk at the table is an international celebration that is finally back after the pandemic, pillow fights. Yes, you heard me right, pillow fights.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pillow fights?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Toronto kicked off the celebration earlier this month with dozens of people battling it out. Now, the fight is typically held during the first Saturday in April. But New Yorkers, since apparently it`s always about us, waited until this weekend to get in on the fun.\par \par \uc2 I just think this is a healthy way --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love pillow fights.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To get your energy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know what I am saying? As an adult, there is not too many ways you can get out maybe yelling in a room by yourself, breaking some plates and pillow fighting, so I am with this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you know what, Nate? You can get hit in the head with a pillow as I have before. That hurts.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got to keep your head on the swivel, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m sorry, I`m like really laughing too loud for that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that hurts.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got to dodge, duck, dip, dive, dodge, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pillow fights --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve got to remember that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are like all foam.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll remember that in my next pillow fight.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You get hit with a foam pillow. That would definitely knock your block off.\par \par \uc2 All right, my talk of the table is Janelle Monae.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is singer, songwriter, actor, and fashion icon. And now she`s adding author to her resume. Her new book is called "The Memory Librarian: And Other Stories of Dirty Computer." It`s a collection of short stories about the same futuristic world she created for her Grammy- nominated album "Dirty Computer."\par \par \uc2 The stories explore the ideas that appear in songs like "Crazy Classic Lifeless."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Crazy Classic Lifeless.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, Monae collaborated with authors to write the book. They address themes of race, gender, and sexuality with a futuristic sci-fi twist, and Janelle Monae joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE (Singer-songwriter): Good morning. I`m so happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re so happy that you are here. I`m so -- I love this book because I`m a huge sci-fi nerd. And I just wanted to ask you, first of all, you created this futuristic world, right, where there is a totalitarian regime that can control people`s thoughts and memories. And you play -- you are a character in one of these books. You are Jane 57-821. Where did this come to you? I read that it was like a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Yes, it was a nightmare that I had before I wrote the album "Dirty Computer." This book is inspired from the same soil and I had a nightmare that I was kidnapped at a movie theater. I wanted to eat popcorn and sit down and an usher was trying to help me with like, hey, come backstage. We come through this secret way, because they`re kidnapping people. I didn`t listen. And I was one of them that they caught and they swiped all of my memories clean and made me into somebody different.\par \par \uc2 And so this book deals with them literally, taking people`s identities, erasing our pasts, erasing the people that we once were, and making us into something that they can control.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But you know, sci-fi is often an allegory for the world that we`re living in today. What you just described, somebody might describe as reality.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Yes, I know, it is pretty meta. I hate to say that, but it is pretty meta, especially when we`re thinking about, you know, Greg Abbott, who put into law in Texas, a law that bans us talking about the LGBTQIA-plus communities in books, bans critical race theory, like there`s a lot of erasure happening in our very country, and I hope this book can serve as a warning sign of what that will look like and feel like if we don`t fight against those abuse of power.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because the stories feature Black and queer protagonists.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In a futuristic world. Why did you want to tell that particular story at this particular time?\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Because we are so cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because we`re cool.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: We are so just like -- and for so many years, and so many centuries, you know, rooted, it`s deep that there`s something wrong with us, like, soon as we come out, we`re considered a dirty computer. And, you know, it`s this whole culture of assimilating, and, you know, how do we thrive in a society that just tells us that we`re too Black, we`re too this or too that, we`re too -- but we are so beautiful and I wanted to represent that in the literary world.\par \par \uc2 The story doesn`t stay in that totalitarian society. It really does, I think embrace hope. It embraces you picking love, your self-love, like how do we overcome those things? Because we`re going to be dealing with these things, right? But my message is to Black and Brown and marginalized folks, like we will prevail in the end, we will.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You mentioned the word "beauty." I have to say this, you look incredible.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That outfit is just stunning.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Thank you. I am wearing --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I just had to say it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Incredible, incredible.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Look, I have my mic back here. Stay here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let me just say very --\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Dead Lotus Couture.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is it?\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Dead Lotus Couture and yes, I just --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s fire. That`s fire.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I said, not very many people could pull that look off.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Look at you. Yes, look.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, Gayle always looks great.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Gayle --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Shrek over here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You heard that.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: You are lovely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But Janelle, you know, I want to talk about these stories. Whenever I write, I feel like I learn something about myself. As you`re writing these stories, what did you learn about yourself?\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Well, one, writing a book and being in a literary space is different. You know, I`m --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve been writing music.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: I`ve been writing music. It`s actually -- so, the cool thing about this book is that I collaborated with incredible writers. I collaborated with Alaya Dawn Johnson on the short story, "The Memory Librarian;" Danny Lore on "Never Mind." Sheree Renee Thomas on "Time Box Alternative." There are so many people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I like Nate`s question, what did you learn about yourself when you were --\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: I know. So what I learned? I know, look at you, look trying to --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Answer that question, Janelle.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Shout out to, you know --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. There are many shout outs.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Yohanca Delgado, and one last, Eve L. Ewing, all of these Black and Brown --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Time Box."\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: "Time Box" yes. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 But anyways, I learned -- what did I learn? I wrote this book in the pandemic. So as I was writing it, I will not lie, like this is when we were still spraying Lysol on cardboard boxes. We didn`t know what this was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, what we were dealing with.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: So I went through -- you know, I went through some depression, some anxiety, some isolation, and --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A lot of us did.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Honestly, this was a really good way for me to just like, go inward and see like: How can I still help people and reach people? And I think what I learned about myself is that one, it`s important that I stay present.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: You know, stay present. I think that when I`m writing about the future, I`m in the future so much. I think now is my moment where you will see me through music, through art being more present. Being more present and having --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Being exactly where your feet are.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Having, yes, like rooted, just like, what`s around me? I`m in the most like carefree, having fun, I have nothing to prove space that I`ve ever been in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I just tell you one critic says: "This book confirms Monae`s incredible power of imagination and creativity. Whatever medium she`s working in, the ability and talent of her co-writers in bringing her vision alive on the page."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Co-signing that.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: It`s so you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Janelle Monae.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One of the most versatile talents on the business.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Thank you, guys. Oh my goodness, you guys, look --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: I am just trying I`m just trying to do the Lord`s work.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re doing it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Amen.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re doing it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Amen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And looking good doing it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Janelle Monae, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So good to see you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So good to meet you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "The Memory Librarian" goes on sale.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Yes. Pick it up.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tomorrow, "Knives Out 2" also, Janelle.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve got that coming up.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: That`s this year, the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s going to be huge.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Time now for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Doing the Lord`s work.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, we continue our special and I mean that special series "Kindness 101" with Steve Hartman, who is sharing stories that are built around themes of kindness and character and the people who have mastered those qualities.\par \par \uc2 And today`s lesson, it`s about compassion.\par \par \uc2 Steve and his kids show us how one boy found his own home which inspired him to help others find theirs.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good morning. I`m here with my daughter, Merrill.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: Hi.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And she`s going to help me teach a lesson about --\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: Compassion. What is compassion? For that, we going to my brother, Emmett at the "Dictionary Desk," Emmett.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Good morning, everyone. \uc2 Compassion\uc2  is to feel sympathy for someone who is sad or disappointed.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Yes, it`s kind of that feeling you get inside that makes you want to help someone in need, or some animal in need.\par \par \uc2 We got a dog recently. I did not want to get a dog. But you know why I went along with it.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Because mommy really wanted one, and she wears the pants.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Yes, but also because pets teach compassion. And if you treat a pet with compassion, there is a good chance you`re going to grow up to treat people that same way, too.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got a story this morning I want to share with you that illustrates this point perfectly.\par \par \uc2 It`s a classic from the archives featuring the boy who has become my role model for compassion.\par \par \uc2 Eight-year-old Robbie Gay loves an underdog.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY (Loves dogs): Hey.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Bring him to the Flagler County Humane Society in Palm Coast, Florida as we did and ask him to find a favorite.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: Can I get in here?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He will seek out the oldest, mangiest, least adoptable mutt of the lot.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: There is something about old dogs that I just like.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Do you see yourself in these dogs?\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY (Adoptive mother of Robbie Gay): He knows what it feels like not to be loved and cared for. He`s the most hopeful, optimistic, and genuinely caring kid who has absolutely no reason to be that way.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Robbie`s adoptive mom, Maria, says before he entered the foster system, Robbie was a holy terror, so badly abused, he was twice hospitalized with brain injuries.\par \par \uc2 Then two years ago, Maria and her husband, Charles, adopted him.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: It is just to the day.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: What did that day mean to you?\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: Everything.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He has come a long way, except in this one respect. Maria says he could not cry despite the horrors of his past or maybe because of them, the kid was a stone.\par \par \uc2 Then one day, one of his old dogs, Buffy, had to be put down. He insisted on holding her until the very end, perhaps because he knew it was the release he needed.\par \par \uc2 After Robbie finally let go, he told his mom, "I know how it feels not to be loved or cared for, and I don`t want any animal of mine to feel that way." Nor does he want any foster kid to feel that way.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: Because people don`t want older people and older dogs. They only want babies and puppies.\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY: He is so aware that it could have gone totally differently for him, and in these older dogs, Robbie has found a place to practice compassion.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Someday, Robbie wants to adopt older foster children himself.\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY: Go up and knock on the door.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But until then, to show his commitment and do what he can, he has vowed to adopt as many old dogs as his parents will allow.\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY: Do you love her?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Today, it is a lame snaggletooth shih tzu named Molly. Molly`s owner had to go into assisted living, but Molly got a new home, thanks to the sweet little boy who sees his reflection in the eyes of the suffering.\par \par \uc2 Robbie and his mom, Maria, join us now. \uc2 Hello\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY: Hi.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: Hello.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Robbie, I know you just lost Molly not long ago. I`m sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: It`s okay.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But your mom also surprised you with a new old dog and we have some video of that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Robbie Gay meeting new old dog.)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You seem like you were pretty surprised when you got off the bus.\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY: Yes, he had no idea.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Robbie Gay meeting new old dog.)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Is this rare that you have a dog that can see and hear?\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY: Totally.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: Very.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And what`s that dog`s name?\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY: Chewy Lewis and the News.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: So we call him Chewy Lewis.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I only have one dog. How many dogs are you allowed to have?\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: A lot.\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY: As many as possible.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY: Some of these older dogs, we might only have them for --\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: A month.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So if you only have them for a little while, what makes you want to keep doing it?\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: Because all dogs deserve to be loved. I don`t think of when they`re gone, I think of how they feel right now.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Thank you so much for what you`ve done, and thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MARIA GAY: It was really nice to meet you, kiddos.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE GAY: Bye.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Robbie has made a place in his heart to love the lonely and if you do the same, compassion will always follow.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Until then, stay kind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Steve does it again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, man. It`s hard to pick a favorite with Steve, but that`s really in my top.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Man, it just pulls at the heartstrings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How about the little boy when he said "I know how it feels not to feel loved and cared for." Nobody should feel that. Now, he wants to give that back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He couldn`t cry. Can you imagine that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I can`t.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The boy is so badly abused that he couldn`t cry, and finally did when he lost his dog. What a story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now, he`s got Chewy Lewis.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done, Steve.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well done, indeed.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Awesome. On today`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNING`s podcast, we`re going to take a look at how brands are entering the Metaverse. You knew this had to happen.\par \par \uc2 Ann Mukherjee, she is the North American Chairwoman and CEO of \uc2 Pernod Ricard\uc2 . That`s the world`s second largest wine and spirits company. Did you know that?\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to discuss how the pandemic has affected her business, and how Absolut Vodka -- a lot of people like Absolut Vodka here -- is entering the Metaverse with Absolut Blend at Coachella.\par \par \uc2 Nate, doesn`t that seem like a good place to introduce it? At Coachella?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh yes. You know, they`re drinking out there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. A little libation going on.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow right here on CBS MORNINGS. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220418ei4i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Story About Work-Life Balance, The Four-Day Workweek\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Kris Van Cleave \par \uc2 424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We turn now to a story about work-life balance, the four-day workweek. That`s a full week`s pay for 32 hours on the job. Dozens of companies are trying it and finding surprising results.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): When Emily Lutyens started at Uncharted in 2020, the Denver-based nonprofit was running an experiment: Could they maintain 100 percent productivity and pay while working 20 percent less?\par \par \uc2 EMILY LUTYENS (Uncharted): It`s an opportunity to take a step back and think about how you can be smarter with her work.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: CEO Banks Benitez hired an analyst to track productivity and the impact of a four-day workweek on employees.\par \par \uc2 How has that changed how you guys do business?\par \par \uc2 BANKS BENITEZ (CEO, Uncharted): It`s changed everything. I mean, it reduced workplace stress, reduced burnout. We saw no reduction in productivity, and every Friday was off.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: A recent survey finds 92 percent of Americans want a four-day workweek. Nearly three-quarters believe they can maintain the same workload, but may have to work longer hours to do it.\par \par \uc2 Crowdfunding site Kickstarter is among the dozens of companies in North America and the U.K. trying out a four-day week.\par \par \uc2 Bill Schaninger\uc2  from consulting firm \uc2 McKinsey & Company\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 BILL SCHANINGER (\uc2 McKinsey & Company\uc2 ): The challenge is, for the portion of the work force that has gotten a taste of flexibility, flexibility full stop is way more attractive than still the mandate, but on a shorter number of days.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: For Lutyens, working fewer days makes balancing her career and being a new mom easier.\par \par \uc2 Could you see yourself ever going back to a job that was Monday through Friday?\par \par \uc2 EMILY LUTYENS: Umm...\par \par \uc2 It would be hard.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Living her new normal with more time for herself.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, CBS News, Denver.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Now to something we have all dreamed of in our traveling future: flying cars.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220418ei4f0008e\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Subway Shooting Suspect Arrested; In the Firing Line; Plea for More Weapons; White House Approves $800 Million Aid to Ukraine; Deadly Police Shooting; Travel Mask Mandate Extended; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 `s Twitter Takeover; Dangers in the Metaverse; Rethinking the Work Week; Last Minute Tax Tips; Creating Chocolate Masterpieces; Historically Black Fashion; A Lesson in Fortitude, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Mola Lenghi, Holly  Williams, Errol Barnett, David Martin, Adriana Diaz, Anna Werner, Jill  Schlesinger, Kris Van Cleave, Mark Strassmann, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 13,641 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Thursday, the 14th day of April, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Officers arrested New York City subway shooting suspect after he calls police on himself. We have new details in this investigation.\par \par \uc2 KEECHANT SEWELL\uc2  (New York City Police Commissioner): We used every resource at our disposal. We were able to shrink his world quickly there was nowhere left for him to run.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Russia`s navy suffers a major setback while the U.S. steps up the shipment of weapons to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL JOHN KIRBY (RET.) (Pentagon Press Secretary): Some of them are new capabilities that we have not provided Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: With COVID cases now on the rise, the Biden administration has extended its masking mandate on planes and public transit.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Powerful storms sweep across the country for a third straight day.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Tornadoes, hail in excess of two inches in diameter, extreme winds, it could be over 70 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Accused of violating three women at a New York nightclub, actor, Cuba Gooding, Jr. pleaded guilty to one count of forcible touching.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A position player pitching. Yes. Hits Braves` Travis d`Arnaud with a 52-mile an hour pitch.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: During President Joe Biden`s speech in an Iowa barn, something drops on his shoulder. Some are saying that it`s bird droppings.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): According to an NBC political reporter. "I was in the room, corn powder particles were dripping on all of us periodically." This guy sure seems eager to convince us it wasn`t bird poop. Can we see a photo of that reporter? A-ha.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Zack Tahhan helped find that suspect accused of opening fire in a Brooklyn subway.\par \par \uc2 ZACK TAHHAN (21-year-old security camera installation): I called the police. Yo, this is that guy. He got (INAUDIBLE) man from the Brooklyn. We need to catch him today. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): That`s what I am talking about. Zak, everyone should be cheering his name. Zack, Zack, Zack, Zack, Zack, Zack, Zack, Zak. Whatever you do, don`t check his old tweets. We don`t care. We want to know. He`s our hero. We`re keeping him.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I`m with you, Trevor. I`m all for that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re keeping him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because that`s exactly what happens. People start digging up your old stuff and find something and then it`s all done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t do it. Zack is a proud New Yorker. We are proud of him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: American hero, an immigrant.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Zack.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re going to have more on the arrest of that suspect and on Zack and what his role in all this, but we`ve got a lot to get to beginning with a story about some potential dangers of the so-called Metaverse.\par \par \uc2 Experts are already warning that scammers are gathering, coming up with ideas to exploit people including children. Anna Werner is going to show us what can happen when others find new ways to control your perception of reality.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, Mark Strassman shows us some incredible creations that are sweet enough to eat.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: All of these 100 percent chocolate.\par \par \uc2 CHEF AMAURY GUICHON (Swiss-French pastry chef): Absolutely. Yes. You can actually turn the steering wheel which is also a fun added feature. You know making it a little bit playful. It`s quite amazing chocolates is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, that really was chocolate. Chef Amaury Guichon was a failed student who later found his passion. He tells Mark why his chocolate art is more than just dessert.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think he`s right. Chocolate is beautiful for many reason.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For many reasons.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin of course with the arrest of a suspect in Tuesday`s mass shooting in a New York subway train station. His name is Frank James. He is 62 years old. He is charged with terrorism aboard a mass transit system. He is due to appear in Federal Court later today.\par \par \uc2 Mola Lenghi is near the crime scene in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 Mola, good morning to you. It sure didn`t take long to get him.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Yes, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Police say they arrested James without incident yesterday afternoon. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  and a few Federal agencies assisted in the search, but in the end, it took the help of a few eagle-eyed citizens and even James himself to bring him in.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Aleksei Korobow of Frank James arrest.)\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: At approximately 1:42 PM, New York Police officers arrested Frank James in lower Manhattan bringing a 30-hour manhunt to a close.\par \par \uc2 KEECHANT SEWELL: We were able to shrink his world quickly. There was nowhere left for him to run.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Surveillance video shows the alleged gunman entering a subway station early Tuesday morning wearing an orange worker`s vest, a construction helmet, and carrying a bag.\par \par \uc2 Police say he rode a Manhattan bound train eight stops --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Will B. Wylde of NYC Subway shooting.)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Yav Montano of NYC Subway shooting.)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Prateek Pratap of NYC Subway shooting.)\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Before detonating smoke bombs and firing 33 shots into a subway are injuring at least 20 people.\par \par \uc2 JAMES ESSIG (\uc2 NYPD\uc2  Chief of Detectives): Mr. James boarded an R train than pulled into the station, went one stop up and exited at 25th Street Station.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: But it was apparently James himself who tipped police to his location in Lower Manhattan. Investigators say he called them saying his phone was dying and he was charging it at a McDonald`s.\par \par \uc2 Cops were pointed in his direction by concerned locals like Zack Tahhan.\par \par \uc2 ZACK TAHHAN: The security cameras inside, and I had seen that guy, he walking from the screen, I see him from the cameras. So I thought oh, [bleep], this guy. Let me call the police. And I call them and we got him.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: James was charged with terrorism on board a mass transit system. If convicted, he could face up to life in prison. His arrest record spans at least 15 years in several states.\par \par \uc2 It includes nine misdemeanor arrests in New York between 1992 and 1998 for possession of burglary tools and a criminal sex act. Investigators described him as a lone wolf who posted hours of violent racist rants online, including threats to Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  and a declared desire to kill.\par \par \uc2 JAMES ESSIG: Investigation is continuing into the motive, into his social media postings.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Once in police custody, sources tell CBS News that James was calm and cool, but made no statements. His first Court appearance is scheduled for later today -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Mola, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to Ukraine and reports of a major new setback for Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian military says it has badly damaged the flagship of Russia`s Black Sea Fleet in a missile attack. The Russians say the cruiser was not hit by Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, residents Chernihiv in Northern Ukraine are returning to what`s left of their homes after a devastating campaign of shelling and airstrikes.\par \par \uc2 The local mayor says 70 percent of the city was destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is in Ukraine this morning.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, Ukrainian forces have so far put up a much tougher resistance than many people expected, aided by some notable Russian military failures.\par \par \uc2 But now Ukraine is bracing for a fresh Russian attack in this country`s east.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: These people have fled Russia`s brutal invasion. At this evacuation center, they register and get whatever help available, but they are running short here of even basic supplies like soap and toothbrushes.\par \par \uc2 The pain and the anxiety is written in their faces.\par \par \uc2 (GISLENA YAGAROVA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Gislena Yagarova (sp?) is distraught. She made it out of far-eastern Ukraine before and expected Russian assault, but her 73-year- old husband refused to come.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine civilians know they are in the firing line. This residential apartment block was hit in the City of Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 (VALENTINA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Valentina says her next door neighbor was killed.\par \par \uc2 And in the shattered city of Mariupol, bombarded for weeks on end, Ukrainian officials say more than 20,000 are believed dead.\par \par \uc2 Russia claims over a thousand Ukrainian troops surrendered, though Ukraine denies it.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s military with a fraction of Moscow`s forces is still punching above its weight. Ukraine claims it once again successfully targeted Russia`s Navy, this time the flagship of Russia`s Black Sea Fleet, the same ship that Ukrainian troops famously told to go to hell when they were ordered to surrender at the start of the war.\par \par \uc2 But there is growing frustration in this country that they haven`t been given more weapons by the West.\par \par \uc2 (COL. IGOR IGAMBERDIEV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Colonel Igor Igamberdiev (sp?), a veteran of Ukraine`s military told us earlier this week that the U.S. has given Ukraine enough weapons to prevent its own destruction, but not enough to win the war.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (Ukrainian President): Freedom must be armed better than tyranny. Western countries have ever seem to make it happen.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  spelled out what Ukraine wants yesterday with a wish list of weaponry --\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY: Artillery, 155 millimeters; artillery shells, 152 to millimeters as many as possible.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: And a reminder of what he says is at stake.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY: Arm Ukraine now to defend freedom.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: President Zelenskyy has praised President Biden for accusing Russia of genocide here in Ukraine, saying quote, "Calling things by their names is essential to stand up to evil" -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Holly Williams on another tense day in Ukraine. Holly Thank you very much. Back in this country on a call with President Zelenskyy, President Biden said America is ramping up its military aid to Ukraine with another $800 million of equipment, and for the first time, the U.S. will be sending heavy artillery to the Ukrainian military along with more javelin anti-tank missiles and other types of weaponry.\par \par \uc2 David Martin got an exclusive look at a shipment of javelins headed from this country to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Five hundred javelin anti-tank missiles are included in the latest round of military aid approved by President Biden. Javelins, which are proving the single most effective weapon against Russian tanks arrive in unmarked trucks at Dover Air Force Base in Delaware, which is the hub of resupply flights for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 There are 24 javelins and each stack, several hundred in all. Once they`re all loaded on the 747, it is a seven-hour-52-minute flight to Poland, where they will all be turned over to the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 Moving supplies through a giant \uc2 Amazon\uc2  like warehouse at Dover, the U.S. is one of 15 countries sending military aid to Ukraine in an operation run out of Germany by the European Commands Chief Logistics Officer.\par \par \uc2 How much have you moved so far?\par \par \uc2 MAN: We have moved almost 400 aircraft across U.S. and our allies.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: On a daily basis, how many flights are going into these countries on the border with Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Between eight and 10 flights a day, and that`s from approximately five to seven donating countries.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Javelins, combined with the skill and bravery of Ukrainian soldiers to win the battle for Kyiv, but now the Russians are massing their forces for a bigger battle in the east of the country.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. will now start rushing bigger, more expensive equipment to the front, including artillery pieces along with 40,000 shells and armored personnel carriers, even Russian-made helicopters.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are in it to win the top priority.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: How long can you keep this up?\par \par \uc2 MAN: We can keep it up for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: The Ukrainians do not know how to use all of the American made equipment included in this latest round of aid, so the U.S. will have to start a training program to teach them, but with this major battle looming in the east, it will have to be a crash course -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, they seem ready to learn. Thank you very much, David.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, severe weather battered parts of the south and the Midwest for a third straight day.\par \par \uc2 Overnight storms in Greene County, Alabama, that`s south of Birmingham damaged at least 10 homes and littered streets with debris and downed trees.\par \par \uc2 The storms turned deadly in Cleveland County, Arkansas. That`s near Pine Bluff. A 20-year-old woman was killed in strong winds blew a tree onto her mobile home.\par \par \uc2 The National Weather Service says an EF-3 tornado touchdown in Texas, north of Austin. Officials say Tuesday`s twister injured at least 23 people and damaged at least 63 homes and buildings.\par \par \uc2 Since Monday at least two dozen confirmed tornadoes struck across nine different states. Forecasters do expect the thunderstorms to flare up again today from the Florida Panhandle to New England. But the good news is that tornadoes are not expected there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A deadly police shooting in Grand Rapids, Michigan has led to outrage and an investigation by State Police. Twenty-six-year-old Patrick Lyoya was killed during a struggle after a traffic stop. He was shot in the back of the head as he was on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m stopping you. Do you have a license?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK LYOYA (26-year-old killed in traffic stop): No, I don`t.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The fatal encounter between the unidentified officer and 26- year-old Patrick Lyoya began as a traffic stop over a license plate that didn`t match the vehicle`s registration.\par \par \uc2 Lyoya appears to walk away from the officer and then resist before running.\par \par \uc2 Multiple videos including from the officers body camera, his dash cam, a cell phone and a security camera captured what happened next. The two appear to wrestle over control of the officer`s Taser. It deploys twice into the ground.\par \par \uc2 Then as this video shot by the car`s passenger shows, while on top of Lyoya`s back, the officer fires a single shot striking Lyoya in the head.\par \par \uc2 The Grand Rapids Police Chief released the video despite an ongoing investigation, saying the community needed to see what happened.\par \par \uc2 ERIC WINSTROM (Grand Rapids Police Chief): So a \uc2 Taser\uc2  is not per se a deadly weapon. The \uc2 Taser\uc2  is what would be known as an intermediate weapon.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Under Michigan law, an officer 