at least two more weeks. That`s through May 3rd.\par \par \uc2 The measure was originally set to expire on Monday. Errol Barnett is at Reagan National Airport, just outside of Washington, D.C. Errol, good morning. What`s the thinking here?\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Hey there. Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Look, this decision comes despite fears and ongoing resistance from the airlines, the travel industry, and some Republican lawmakers as well.\par \par \uc2 But it also means that air travelers are now experiencing two full years of having to mask up while traveling. Now, the spread of the recent BA.2 subvariant of omicron is why the C.D.C. says this extension is needed, announcing in a statement that the move gives them time to assess the potential impact the rise of cases has on severe disease including hospitalizations, and deaths.\par \par \uc2 Now the spread of the subvariant has caused an uptick in reported cases since early April driving outbreaks in the northeast and parts of Florida as we`ve been reporting. It now makes up more than 85 percent of all U.S. COVID infections.\par \par \uc2 And this is now the fifth extension. And there are lawsuits filed in mostly Republican-led states seeking to overturn the Federal mask requirement all together, but we cannot exclude the American public here. A recent Harris poll in late March and early April found 60 percent of respondents are okay and support an extension of the mandate.\par \par \uc2 And when we came here to Reagan National yesterday speaking with travelers, they agreed. So despite that ongoing resistance, most Americans are okay with masking up, at least for the moment these next few weeks until we know more about this subvariant -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Errol, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got some breaking news from the financial world. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the world`s richest man wants to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , all 100 percent of it. The \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  CEO recently revealed that he has bought more than nine percent of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  stock, then rejected an invitation to join its Board of Directors. The offer was revealed in a filing with the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a letter to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s Chairman, Musk wrote, "I invested in \uc2 Twitter\uc2  as I believe in its potential to be the platform for free speech around the globe, and I believe free speech is a societal imperative for a functioning democracy." He also wrote: "\uc2 Twitter\uc2  has extraordinary potential, I will unlock it."\par \par \uc2 Musk has repeatedly criticized \uc2 Twitter\uc2  over the years asking his online followers to suggest changes in the platform. He`s offering the pay $54.20 a share for the rest of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  stock. That is a significant markup over yesterday`s closing price of $45.85.\par \par \uc2 According to their 2021 fourth quarter filing, the platform has 217 million daily active users worldwide.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Which is not that large. Most people in America do not tweet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: True, but I do -- I`m intrigued by this offer to buy the entire company.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, me, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which will be interesting to see if the shareholders go for it. We`re going to have jobs. I`ve got a \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  little later.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I got on board --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I know you do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  always keeps it interesting.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`ll have much more on his offer to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Plus, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 , what it could mean for users and the shareholders, whether some people who have been banned could be allowed back on.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, we`ll look at how criminals are trying to exploit vulnerabilities in the Metaverse. Yes, if you go there, it could cost you. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead in our series "Kindness 101," don`t we need that? Yes, we do. Steve Hartman shows us how a woman who has been beating the odds her entire life is now fighting for others just like --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is 7:30 where we`re continuing our series "Mornings in the Metaverse." We`ll look at some of the potential risks that people could face in that emerging virtual world.\par \par \uc2 The Metaverse can look and feel more real than the online social interactions and gaming experiences that we`re used to, but that can leave consumers potentially vulnerable, and there is a lot of money at stake.\par \par \uc2 Researchers predict that the global Metaverse market could be worth about $680 billion in just a few years, by 2030.\par \par \uc2 Consumer investigative correspondent, Anna Werner spoke with cybersecurity experts who say that where the money goes, the criminal soon follow.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: If you want to know what can be great about the Metaverse, talk to 12-year-old Cooper Stone.\par \par \uc2 COOPER STONE (12-year-old gamer): The Metaverse is a virtual reality world where you can go to concerts, you can see famous and really cool people around the world.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Cooper plays video games like "Fortnight" and uses his virtual reality headset to play sports. And in the future, those experiences will likely seem much more real, as Jason Rubin of \uc2 Meta\uc2  formerly called \uc2 Facebook\uc2  told CBS News in February in an interview conducted in their virtual reality program.\par \par \uc2 JASON RUBIN (\uc2 Meta\uc2 ): It`s slowly but surely, I think the things you can do in the Metaverse, and the amount of time you spend in virtual reality in the Metaverse will slowly increase to the point where we really become an immersive world.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So it`s no surprise that companies are seeking ways to create experiences and make money in the Metaverse, but where there`s money to be made, the criminals won`t be far behind.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN GOSSCHALK (Arkose Labs Founder and CEO): We totally expect them to move into this.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Kevin Gosschalk is with cybersecurity firm, Arkose Labs.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN GOSSCHALK: The moment there`s a valid Metaverse spun up that has a real money mechanism where they can make money, they will be in there day one.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: He showed us how in chat rooms, conversations are already going on about how to exploit the Metaverse for fraud.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN GOSSCHALK: To sell identities, to sell Social Security numbers.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Essentially, what you`re saying is that when it comes to scams, everything old is new again, it`s just in the Metaverse now.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN GOSSCHALK: Right, the way they do it may be slightly different, but what they`re trying to achieve -- money -- and the goal of doing it, duping somebody into giving them money, they`re going to be aligned.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Cooper Stone and his parents say they already got scammed when hackers broke into his "Fortnight" account last year and charged up his parents` credit cards.\par \par \uc2 COOPER STONE: They were like, "Cooper, did you spend $25.00 or $50.00 on this game without my permission?" I`m like, "No."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We investigated a little bit more and found out that he had not done it.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE WONG (Cobalt.io Chief Strategy Officer): Everyone is looking to make money off the Metaverse and the hackers go where the money is.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: And Caroline Wong with cybersecurity firm Cobalt.io says criminals` attacks can take on more disturbing forms. For example, if a hacker gains access to your VR headset.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE WONG: If a hacker can access that camera, they can see into your office, they can see into your bedroom, they can see inside of your home. That`s called the camera attack.\par \par \uc2 Another one is called the overlay attack, when they can control what you`re seeing and what you`re hearing.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So in other words, they`re going to change what you see and what you hear.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE WONG: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Into what they want you to see and hear.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE WONG: Yes. And because they`re so immersed, people may actually have a trauma response.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Not only that, some content in the Metaverse might be disturbing to some users, especially children, as Wong and I saw when we donned headsets to visit some VR worlds.\par \par \uc2 In a program called VRChat, we found users, some who clearly sounded like children making crude drawings we can`t show here.\par \par \uc2 Oh, okay. Yes, graffiti.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE WONG: I think I know what these folks are trying to draw.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: And in a user created room in Meta`s Horizon Worlds, we were able to pick up guns lying randomly around the house as other users fired them off.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE WONG: That`s not -- that`s not comforting.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Another user who sounded like a young child played with one of those guns.\par \par \uc2 Put the gun down. Put the gun down.\par \par \uc2 CHILD: I`m the officer.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: No, don`t push it. You`re going to kill it.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So I guess the question is whether you want your child in here?\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE WONG: I would say, definitively no.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: \uc2 Meta\uc2  told us \uc2 Quest\uc2  headsets are designed for children ages 13 and up with some experiences only for people 18 and up. The company says it has set up safe zones inside the software so users can exit situations they might find uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 And later this month will offer a way for parents to lock specific apps that aren`t age appropriate for teens.\par \par \uc2 VRChat told us, underage users are not permitted and are banned if detected, and that user safety is a top priority.\par \par \uc2 You know, I hear the company saying, "Well, it`s just like the real world. You can`t protect against everything."\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE WONG: You can`t protect against everything, but there are things that you can do.\par \par \uc2 They build the world. They create the experiences. Everything is being monitored already. All of that is being monitored. It can be monitored.\par \par \uc2 It is the appropriate thing to do. It is absolutely possible and it costs money and resources to do so.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So they have to be willing to step up and do it.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE WONG: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: The goal both she and the companies say is to create safe, enjoyable experiences for all in a new virtual world.\par \par \uc2 I`m Anna Werner for CBS MORNINGS in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anna, thank you. We should let our viewers know that the company that owns "Fortnight," that`s \uc2 Epic Games\uc2  told us it has bot controls for parents to manage "Fortnight" purchases and quote "checks all accounts for signs of compromise" continuously to help stop fraud.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 , for its part, also told us in May, they will offer additional parental supervision tools to monitor their teen`s activities.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know I sound like a boomer even though I`m not, but I`m like, I`ll just hang out in the real world for now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am a Boomer and I`m going to say, I just want to hang out in the real world.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As a parent --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That overlay attack, Tony, where it says they can control what you`re seeing and what you`re hearing. I thought Miss Wong was very good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, she is good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what -- the heads up that she was giving everybody, clearly work to be done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`m already sweating bullets as a parent here. When they put it on parents to police it. It`s difficult because I can barely -- you know, you can barely figure out your own phone and keep the fraud out of that from the real world, let alone keeping control of what your kids are doing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, and the fact that kids are in the Metaverse like spraying obscene graffiti in the virtual world. I mean, they do that in the real world. So why would we expect that that behavior would not transfer in the Metaverse?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Actually, it`s a choice between writing on a real building and writing on a fake building, go for the fake building --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Go for the fake building, right? Yes, at least you won`t get in trouble.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To be continued for sure.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Vlad. This Vlad right here will have the stories we think you`re going to be talking about today. And there is a special guest this time, and a reminder, aka an invitation, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast.\par \par \uc2 We like it. Because what do you get? Today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes, and we think that`s the deal.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  wants \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but he doesn`t got it yet. Time for "What to Watch." We`ve got Jill here, our money expert to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 And Vlad, you`re going to run us through the top of the introduction to this whole conversation.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He doesn`t have it yet until I say he has it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, really?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am only kidding. I am only kidding, Elon.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we are going to talk --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Elon, on line two for you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "What to Watch." I`m buying that, too, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 So more on billionaire, Elon Musk`s bombshell offer to buy 100 percent of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . This morning`s announcement came just days after he bought more than nine percent of the social media platform, and then backed out of an offer to join \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s Board of Directors.\par \par \uc2 Musk says \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , this is a quote now, "\uc2 Twitter\uc2  needs to be transformed as a private company." So yes, we`ve got Jill here to break this all down.\par \par \uc2 I find this absolutely fascinating that he rejected the offer to join the Board and then decided in this filing with the S.E.C. that he wanted to buy the company outright to take it private.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Right. He doesn`t want to be, I think guided by S.E.C. rules. Now remember, when you`re a publicly traded company, the S.E.C. has a series of rules about Board Directors, the number of shares and percentage of shares you own, the disclosures you have to make, and you have what`s called a fiduciary responsibility to your shareholders.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got to do what`s right for the shareholders first, before anyone else. And by --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If he takes it private, what will that mean?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : If he takes it private, he doesn`t have that. He has a responsibility to who? Himself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He could do whatever he wants when he wants.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Whatever he wants, exactly. And so I think this is a really interesting moment of a hostile takeover of a company to bring it private, so he has control over it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So who decides whether this hostile takeover is successful? Will he actually take control the company?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, I mean, the shareholders. So here is what`s really fascinating --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because they don`t have to sell it to him.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : They don`t have to.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, they don`t.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : But --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the shareholders -- and they have to be the ones that --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is dangling a lot of money in there for them.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Absolutely. And you`ll need to get a certain percentage of those shareholders to say yes, before doing it.\par \par \uc2 So a few numbers for you, guys. If you look back, I looked at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s IPO price back in 2013, it was 26 bucks a share. It went up to 45 after the first day of trading. It has had a bumpy ride.\par \par \uc2 In fact, if you look at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , say compared to \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2  stocks has crushed it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what is he offering now?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : And he is now offering $54.00 a share.\par \par \uc2 Now listen, there are some folks that I just spoke to very quickly on my ride into work who said 54 is too low, the stock just traded at 70 a couple of -- you know, within the last 12 months.\par \par \uc2 So there are shareholders who could hold out for more. But remember, this is a very compelling offer to just get out of a stock that has been underperforming almost 10 years.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He did say that his offer is 54 -- it`s a 54 percent premium over the share price before he started buying the shares and the 38 percent premium over the day he made it public.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Can I just say one thing about that though? Because he didn`t disclose it, he was able to buy shares before -- if you knew \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  was buying, you`d be like, let me sit back and see what happens.\par \par \uc2 But people who sold it to him say, a few weeks ago, and they didn`t know they were selling to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , they missed the first 38 percent rise in the stock. And now they may say, oh, I want that money back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, you`re a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  user and you`re sitting here today, what does this mean to you if he buys it? Pros -- if you have pros and cons.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I think what he has said is he wants to make this a subscription based model.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is fascinating to me that he would -- what`s the - - I don`t get it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Like, if you`re all about free speech, and the public is saying what it needs to say, why would you take it private and make it subscription based?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Because I think he wants to make money, and I think there are a lot of analysts who look at this company and say it hasn`t made as much money as its potential because you need an ad base, but also --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he is the richest man in the world, why are you saying he needs to make money?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Because he -- I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It has to be something more than that.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, I think he also wants to influence the conversation. We were talking before we came on the air, this is a product, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , that is not so much a moneymaker, but it does influence media, government politics. You`re in the conversation when you own --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Under the current configuration, does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  come back if \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I think that there`s a possibility that that gets reversed. I really do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s a tug and pull between making money and free speech, right? Either you`re all about you want to make it this platform for free speech, or you`re saying to yourself, I want to make it about --\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : But you could have two different levels. You could have an ad supported one and you could also have one that is a subscription base. And it`s going to be interesting to see. This story is not over yet. It`s the beginning. The filing is the beginning of the battle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But remember, this free speech would be as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  see`s free speech.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : It`s Musk`s speech.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, Musk`s speech.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re looking at some hot tips from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  decking out. \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a famous fashion line`s new inspiration. Two designers tell Vlad how they dug into the history of Black Colleges and Universities to come up with their latest product.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:46, time check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Office Space.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t you love this guy? Yes. We know people like this. Some of us are people like this. Classic comedy "Office Space" had a lot to say about working life.\par \par \uc2 Ron Livingston`s character may have benefited from a four-day workweek, a growing number of Americans have it already.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Kris Van Cleave takes us inside one startup to show us how employees can be more productive by working fewer days every week.\par \par \uc2 I actually understand that, don`t you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is a lot to this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think so, too, especially during the pandemic. People learned, "We could do this."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In France in 2000, I was living and working in France. They made it a 35-hour work week. Now they`re talking about a 32-hour work week, which is four days, but people still work more.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We keep getting productive. Let`s get productive, and then do less and more with our free time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are on to something here.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, how would you like to work a four-day workweek? Yes, I would, \uc2 CBS\uc2 . We`re going to visit a company showing what a better life balance could look like.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil, on day four of our workweek and Steve Hartman returns with his series "Kindness 101." A woman who spent decades breaking down barriers gives us an inspiring lesson in fortitude.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. We`re also talking fashion today with a new clothing line based on Historically Black Colleges and Universities, how to change the world and look great doing it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it. Go, Ralph Lauren.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Police arrest a suspect in Tuesday`s New York subway shooting and he will be in Court today.\par \par \uc2 KEECHANT SEWELL: We were able to shrink his world quickly. There was nowhere left for him to run.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Ukraine is bracing for a fresh Russian attack in this country`s east.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ukraine says it has hit the flagship vessel of Russia`s Black Sea Fleet as the U.S. steps up military aid.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Twenty four javelin each stack, several hundred in all, where they will be turned over to the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Severe weather batters parts of the South and the Midwest for a third straight day. The storms turn deadly in Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As the so called Metaverse expands, there are many potential dangers, including scam artists.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE WONG: Everyone is looking to make money off the Metaverse, and the hackers go where the money is.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I thought it`s just another typical New York day, just a random arrest. Right? And then when I saw it, I was really in shock.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is still facing questions over its handling of this week`s mass shooting, even after the suspect`s arrest.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): It`s fortunate that James left behind plenty of evidence because none of the station security cameras were in full operation at the time of the shooting. It does explain the new subway safety posters. "If you see something, that`s cool. We didn`t."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s funny, but it`s not funny. They`re working on it. That`s New York humor. They have to be working on it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`ve got to be working on it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To fix this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But maybe only for four days a week. Right? This is Thursday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s early.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So that`s what we`re talking about right now. How would you -- I know how you feel, Gayle. It`s like work only four days a week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is the question?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The question is, how would you, the viewer, like to work fewer hours, but for the same amount of money?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that a trick question? Come on, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like, yes. That`s the reality for a growing number of American workers.\par \par \uc2 California lawmakers are considering a bill to make the standard workweek 32 hours instead of 40. If it becomes law, it would apply to workers in companies with 500 or more employees. Many private businesses across the country already trying a four-day workweek hoping to reduce stress without reducing productivity.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave went to Colorado to see how one of them is doing it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: When Emily Lutyens started at Uncharted in 2020, the Denver-based nonprofit was running an experiment. Could they maintain 100 percent productivity in pay while working 20 percent less?\par \par \uc2 EMILY LUTYENS (Uncharted Managing Director): That`s an opportunity to take a step back and think about how you can be smarter with your work.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Are you happier working four days a week instead of five?\par \par \uc2 EMILY LUTYENS: I`m definitely happier, and I think that I`m just living my life better.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Nationally, 92 percent of employees favor a four-day workweek. But CEO Banks Benitez wanted to know his business wouldn`t suffer. So he brought in an analyst to track productivity and the impact of a four-day week on employees.\par \par \uc2 How has that changed how you guys do business?\par \par \uc2 BANKS BENITEZ (Uncharted CEO and founder): It`s changed everything. I mean, reduced workplace stress, reduced burnout. We saw no reduction in productivity, and every Friday was off.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Henry Ford`s assembly line ushered in the standard five- day work week in 1926, and while that remains the norm, today`s workforce is demanding better life balance, a trend sped up by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 In Iceland, roughly 85 percent of the country works or will soon work four days a week. Earlier this year, Belgium announced employees can opt to work for 10-hour work days, and across North America and the U.K., dozens of companies including crowdfunding site, Kickstarter are testing a four-day week.\par \par \uc2 Can the four-day workweek work for everyone?\par \par \uc2 BILL SCHANINGER (\uc2 McKinsey & Company\uc2 ): That`s unlikely to work for everyone.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 Bill Schaninger\uc2  from consulting firm, \uc2 McKinsey & Company\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BILL SCHANINGER: The challenge is for the portion of the workforce that has gotten a taste of flexibility. Flexibility full stop is way more attractive than still the mandate on a shorter number of days.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: California Congressman Mark Takano has introduced legislation to make workers eligible for overtime after 32 hours instead of 40.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARK TAKANO (D-CA): It`s time to re-examine, you know what the workweek should look like in the 21st Century.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: At Uncharted, the new norm means prioritizing what`s important.\par \par \uc2 BANKS BENITEZ: So we`re really thoughtful about what is the most essential work that we`re doing? Is this meeting really critical for us to have? Can we cancel this meeting?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: You`re cancelling meetings?\par \par \uc2 BANKS BENITEZ: Any given workweek, we will cancel 10 percent of the meetings, 15 percent of the meetings, because we don`t need them. They`re not essential or we just shorten them.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Are you hiring?\par \par \uc2 For Emily Lutyens, the extra time is making balancing her career with being a new mom a little easier.\par \par \uc2 Could you see yourself ever going back to a job that was Monday through Friday?\par \par \uc2 EMILY LUTYENS: It will be hard.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Working smarter, to get more time for herself.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Denver.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I hope this is a trend that catches on, Tony and Vlad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s kind of a no brainer. You know, we`ve got all this technology, you can get a lot more done in a shorter amount of time and people want it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like I said, in 2000 when France made it a 35-hour workweek, there were some people who were able to take advantage of that. Not everybody, there were still people working 40 hours, 45 hours if the job demanded it, but the option was there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you can do 40 hours in four days, and you can work four long days and still have all that time relax and recuperate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, you know, people keep smirking when they talk about this, but it`s serious and it can really help a lot of folks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And as long as you get the work done, I don`t know what the issue is. I think it`s harder for us in these jobs.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. There are certain jobs you can`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there are certain jobs you can`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we`re still looking for a five-day week in this job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are we?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Did you hear the sudden chill in the room?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Iceberg.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And speaking on behalf of our producing staff, I think they`d love a five-day week.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, tax deadline is days away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is a 24/7 job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a 24/7 job, and you love it because it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a 24/7 job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you get to -- you know you`re on the forefront of things, but it can be exhausting as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Taxes are also exhausting. Deadline is just days away. If you haven`t filed your taxes yet, though, you might want to.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, we`ll tell you how much money the average person is getting back in their bank accounts, and look who`s back with us? \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  in our progressive greenroom with some last minute tips on how to spend it if you got it.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:07, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, Mark Strassmann visits the Las Vegas chef behind these dazzling works of edible art. Look at this, guys, this is a chocolate bird.\par \par \uc2 The chef shares his unlikely journey from struggling student in France to chocolate connoisseur.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In today`s "Money Watch," we`ve got some timely advice on taxes if you`re filing at the last minute, and if you`re listening to me now and you haven`t filed, you are.\par \par \uc2 The deadline to submit your taxes for 2021 or ask for an extension is just a few days away, Monday, April 18th.\par \par \uc2 As of April 1st, more than 63 million of your fellow Americans have got refunds from the I.R.S. and the average refund -- get this -- more than $3,200.00. That is up 11.5 percent from last year and it could help with inflation.\par \par \uc2 CBS News` business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Jill, thank you for being here. If you haven`t filed your taxes yet, what should you do?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Take a deep breath file electronically, and you know this, check, double check, triple track. You know the biggest errors are things like inverting a Social Security number or a signature.\par \par \uc2 If you are clinging to your paper returns, it`s going to take longer, so please try to do electronic. But if you are doing paper, check the mailing address. The I.R.S. as people send their returns to the wrong place.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If people have plans this weekend other than collating receipts and filing taxes, what should they do about an extension?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So you file for an extension right on the I.R.S. website, irs.gov. It`s a great website and the form that you are looking for, and it`ll be right there is, 4868. That is giving you six months extra to file your taxes.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the little caveat.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We react about this part, yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Okay, you have to pay if you think you owe taxes, even if you`re on extension, so you`ve got to do this work anyway.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if you don`t, the government will charge you interest on what you owe.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : And penalties.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: However, if they owe you money, there is no interest. So you might as well grab it. Tell me about that.\par \par \uc2 People are getting a lot of money this year.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes, I mean, so you have $3,200.00 that`s coming in on average, and people keep saying to me, "What do I do with this money?"\par \par \uc2 So number one, you may want to keep it on hand. We have four-decade highs in inflation, and according to government data, this is costing the average American, an extra 350 or 400 bucks a month.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, that`s $3,200.00 a year.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Exactly. So you may need that. But let`s say you`ve got enough money. You know, my big three, I say this all the time, please have an emergency reserve fund. You know, things are going to continue to be expensive. Two, pay down debt. Get ready to pay down those student loans, that`s going to happen at some point. And three, try to put money into a retirement account. I think this is an important thing.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people by the way, if you`re going on an extension, you don`t get an extension to put money into an IRA or a Roth IRA. You still have to do that by the April 18th deadline. So please, pay attention to that. If you can, do it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So people could pour it into a new business they`ve started. A lot of Americans have done that during the pandemic. If you started a new business in the 2021 tax year, what should you keep in mind?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, what you should keep in mind is no one is withholding taxes for you. And that means you may need to make quarterly estimates of your tax bill and it`s a drag, I get it, but go on to the website, you`ll have worksheets there. You can figure out either how much you owe, what your actual estimate should be. Four quarterly estimates will keep you out of problems.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you say don`t call the I.R.S. if you have questions. What do you do then?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You go on to the -- you go onto the website, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  received 282 million calls last year and they answered 11 percent of that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, all right.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So let`s not call them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So go to the website. Websites they`re catching on.\par \par \uc2 Jill, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Maybe you need a sweet treat after all that tax talk. Well, up next, we will meet the Las Vegas based chef creating incredible sculptures, all of them edible, all of them made from chocolate. They look great. How do they taste?\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Calling all chocolate lovers. Yes, I`m talking to you, Clark, on the Steadicam with his camera because he says, "I grew up in Switzerland."\par \par \uc2 Before I say anything, this is all chocolate. We`ve got a big treat for you. Take a look at these dazzling works of edible art. They are the creations of French pastry Amaury Guichon is his name.\par \par \uc2 His designs are featured on the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  reality show. It`s called "School of Chocolate."\par \par \uc2 In his unlikely journey from struggling student to chocolate connoisseur is now an inspiration for many people. This morning, we can reveal the show has been renewed for a second season -- yay to that -- Mark Strassmann got the sweet assignment you could say to visit the chef at his Las Vegas Culinary Academy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Amaury Guichon, he is part Picasso, part Willy Wonka, a celebrated pastry chef becoming world famous for bending chocolate to his will and his imagination.\par \par \uc2 All of these 100 percent chocolate\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: Absolutely yes. You can actually turn the steering wheel, which is also a fun added feature, you know, making it a little bit playful. It`s quite amazing. Chocolate is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Look at his sculpted masterworks: A roaring gorilla, a motorcycle, this elephant -- elaborate, intricate, each of them 100 percent chocolate.\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: There is 1,500 feathers. All of them were done by hand with the tip of a knife and then assembled one by one. This one was very challenging.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Meticulous.\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Kind of draining.\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: Oh, I was dead at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "School of Chocolate.")\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Guichon shares his secrets and skills on his hit \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series, "School of Chocolate."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "School of Chocolate.")\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Eight contestants, all accomplished pastry chefs, but they`ve never been challenged like this.\par \par \uc2 You`re really reimagining chocolate.\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: I`m trying to push the boundaries, yes, of what`s doable. Chocolate is so precious. It`s so unique.\par \par \uc2 I still learn things about chocolates. It sounds stupid, right? It`s just a liquid blob of brown matter, and yet you find beauty every single time you work with it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Like his creations. Guichon`s story feels like a chocolate fairy tale.\par \par \uc2 He floundered as a student in France. When Guichon was 14, the school system pushed him to learn a trade.\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: I was pushed into choosing either culinary or hairstylist, so I choose culinary.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You`re telling me, you became a pastry genius because you were a bad student.\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: Yes, and funny enough, I`m deep into education, so I`m on the other side. My old teenage years, I was told that I was too stupid to pursue any other thing. It`s sad, but it`s true. And I was told that I would never do anything good with my life.\par \par \uc2 And now I`m 16, I`m in a field where people consider lame, so I`m like, let`s be the best at it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: A decade ago, 21 and speaking no English, the chef`s moved to Las Vegas. His showmanship sparkled in this City of Glitz, and he eventually started showcasing his work on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now 31, the chocolate charmer has more than six million followers.\par \par \uc2 His eye-popping images are produced by his wife and co-CEO, Fiona.\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: That`s perfect.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This masterwork is called Father Nature.\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: This is my favorite piece. You know, like every piece of art, you know, you look at it, it gives you an emotion.\par \par \uc2 The way I was able to capture the look on his face, like an old soul, you know, wise and calming. I didn`t know I had it in me.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Even his most intricate creations take no more than a week. Under the right conditions, Guichon says they can last about 20 years, although admittedly losing a bit of flavor.\par \par \uc2 So if I snapped off his head, they would taste the same.\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You might punch me but --\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: It is milk chocolate and it is delicious.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Guichon also founded this Las Vegas Pastry Academy with Chef Michel Ernots.\par \par \uc2 Over 10 weeks, students learn the craft and chemistry behind original desserts.\par \par \uc2 He gave me a shot at building something simpler, lemon tacos.\par \par \uc2 Sorry.\par \par \uc2 Clearly some people should stay out of the kitchen.\par \par \uc2 But for Guichon, this is the place where a chocolate genius transforms the mundane into the magical.\par \par \uc2 AMAURY GUICHON: I often say I`m not the best sculpture. You see some clay sculpture or woodcarving, people who do some crazy amazing things. The only thing that makes me stand out, it is chocolate.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go, Amaury Guichon.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very impressive. Love the guy. Great backstory. Wishing him the most success in the world. But we do a piece on chocolate, and no one ate any chocolate? Zero chocolate eaten in the whole piece. How does it actually taste?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I wouldn`t want to eat it. I think it`s -- I love that -- that`s what art is, taking something that`s ordinary and mundane and making something beautiful and spectacular. He`s done that. That`s really cool. I wouldn`t want to eat it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That said, I wouldn`t want to eat it, but I was curious, Tony, to your point about what it tastes like. I was wondering that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tastes like chocolate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But still I love the chef though. I got caught up in his in his story to be told that you`re not going to amount to anything, that you`re worthless.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, it`s just another great example of, you can`t let somebody else define who you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is an artist.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But give me a Hershey bar for everyday chocolate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You get one after the show.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Ralph Lauren unveils a new collection drawn from the archives of two Historically Black Colleges.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, bet you remember that song. Hello, Hanson`s.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you some of the stories that are talk of table this morning. Vlad, you are in the pole position.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So my "Talk of the Table" is a controversial concert.\par \par \uc2 John Hinckley, Jr., the man who tried to assassinate President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  is set to play a sold-out acoustic show in Brooklyn, New York.\par \par \uc2 In 1981, Hinckley wounded Reagan and three others. He was found not guilty by reason of insanity and spent decades in a psychiatric institution. He was allowed to leave custody in 2016 to live with his mother in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Hinckley is now 66 years old and he is exploring a career in music. There he is. He tweeted that he will perform at a venue in Brooklyn on July 8th, a month after Court ordered restrictions he has been living under are lifted.\par \par \uc2 The Reagan Foundation tweeted it was concerned and saddened by the news and strongly opposes it, quote: "His release in to society where he apparently seeks to make a profit from his infamy." The venue that he will be playing at --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They said it is sold out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is sold out. The venue says that he paid his debt to society, and so that`s why they`re allowing him to perform.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He certainly has paid his debt to society and I also believe in second chances, but there is something about this that really rubs me the wrong way.\par \par \uc2 The actions that he took on that terrible day where it was \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , it was James Brady, it was a Secret Service agent. Fortunately, they all survived and I love what Patti Reagan said when someone you loved his gun down, you don`t forget about that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And time really does stand still. So, I think he got a second chance because he`s out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is out, right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I just think, take your second chance and live your life, but it bothers me that now there is a concert and he could possibly profit from it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what the Reagan Foundation is saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am with them on that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are saying, he is going to profit from something, because he is only known to the public because of that heinous act.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, what do you do? Perform under a false name? I mean, he likes to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, no, he is performing under his own name.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, I know, but what`s the solution here? Right? Like so clearly all of these people in Brooklyn are going to the concert not because he`s a great up and coming guitar player, but because of the name.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s a novelty to it. But what are you going to require him to do?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Speaking of novelties, Tony Dokoupil. You`re "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How dare? How do you dare call Hanson a novelty?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh man. I am going to get burned.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How dare you?\par \par \uc2 We are on the eve of a major milestone in music. Twenty-five years ago, tomorrow, the iconic song "Mmmbop" by Hanson came into the world. Here is a reminder of what it sounds like.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mmmbop.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The pop philosophers from Oklahoma, the Hanson brothers wrote that song 25 years ago, released it, three Grammy nominations, 10 million sales.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m not hating. I love the song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A question that I have long had since sitting in the back of mom`s car riding into high school, I am thinking like, what are they singing about? What is "Mmmbop"?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I only know "Mmmbop." I don`t know anything else about that song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, it`s true. We have an answer now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "The New York Post" tracked them down and ask Zac Hanson the meaning of the song and he says quote -- and this is quite deep, so get ready for it.\par \par \uc2 "The song is all about the passage of time. How very few things will last in an `Mmmbop,` they`re gone. In an "Mmmbop" we`re not here."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And an "Mmmbop" they`re gone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have chills.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have chills.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, the lyrics, they are -- so hold on to the words you really care --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have goosebumps, Tony? Of all the songs, Vlad, would you ever thought that Tony Dokoupil would like Hanson "Mmmbop"?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that he loves it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love how deep -- and then I went looked at the lyrics. It actually is kind of neat.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is very deep the lyrics of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you just give me a line -- a lyric from the song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does it say?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "And when get when you get old and start losing your hair, can you tell me who will care? Who will care?"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Mmmbop."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Mmmbop."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In an "Mmmbop" it`s gone. And then my favorite part of the conversation is when you call them The Hanson`s, just like "The Simpsons."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s like --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are. Like they`re a family.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, they are just Hanson. All right, all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That song is going to be with you all day, America.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is. I`ve been dancing all day, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re welcome. You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is about an extraordinary young guy who couldn`t decide what college he wanted to go to. So guess what? He applied to 27. Well, now he got lots and lots of options. He is 18 years old. His name is Jonathan Walker.\par \par \uc2 He was accepted by -- you guessed it, to all 27 schools. You go, Jonathan, including Yale, Harvard, and Penn, just to name a few.\par \par \uc2 He`s also been offered a total of $4 million in scholarships.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Walker, by the way is not a nerd alert. Nothing wrong with that. He is so well-rounded.\par \par \uc2 Listen to this. He`s on the school`s football team. He was Homecoming King. In his spare time, he invented a device to help people who are blind and/or deaf communicate with others.\par \par \uc2 Here is what he said about writing 27 college applications what that was like --\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN WALKER (Accepted to 27 colleges): It`s really just like writing like a set of essays for one college and then from there like tweaking them specifically for the colleges.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So see what he did? He applied to a college and tweaked every application. I really liked this guy.\par \par \uc2 He is narrowing down his choices. He still is not saying where he`s going to go this fall. He has lots and lots of options. I think he`s narrowed it down very closely, but he`s not telling us yet.\par \par \uc2 But his advice to kids who are going on this path. Keep grinding.\par \par \uc2 I like the fact that even says keep grinding. Keep grinding and get it done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because he really is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because he tried all schools.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because he really is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because really is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Jonathan Walker.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: His family must be over the moon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, we turn now to a new clothing inspired by Historically Black Colleges from one of America`s most iconic fashion brands.\par \par \uc2 The new collection from Ralph Lauren draws on the archives and the yearbooks of Morehouse and Spelman Colleges. It leans heavily on campus fashion from the 1920s through the 1950s, and the idea was created by two Ralph Lauren employees who are alumni of those schools, and I sat down with them to find out the source of their inspiration and their dreams for what the collection might represent.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RALPH LAUREN (Fashion designer): I`ve always believed in the American dream.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: For over 50 years, Ralph Lauren`s line of clothing has celebrated elegance and style with a romanticized vision of America.\par \par \uc2 Now, its latest chapter of Americana, perhaps overdue, is a collection inspired by the archives of two Historically Black Colleges and Universities featuring an all-Black production team and models from Spelman and Morehouse Colleges.\par \par \uc2 DARA DOUGLAS (Ralph Lauren library inspirational content director): Previously, you know, the way that we showed collegiate style to the world was really through a White Ivy League lens.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Alumni Dara Douglas and James Jeter were behind that collaboration. They drew inspiration from the clothing worn by students attending HBCUs throughout the decades.\par \par \uc2 DARA DOUGLAS: This was a love letter to Spelman and Morehouse. It was also a love letter to our HBCUs.\par \par \uc2 JAMES JETER (Ralph Lauren Director, Concept Design and Special Projects Lead): But it was really more about putting a spotlight on the incredible style of individuals at these HBCUs in the 20s, 30s, 40s and 50s.\par \par \uc2 This wasn`t Black style. This wasn`t White style. It was American style.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jeter conceived the idea of following the events of summer 2020.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of George Floyd`s murder, you were questioning whether or not you even wanted to stay here, even though you`d spent your entire career at Ralph Lauren.\par \par \uc2 JAMES JETER: Absolutely. And you know, I think a lot of brands make commitments and 2020 Ralph Lauren included, but 2021, it is really about doing the actual work.\par \par \uc2 None of my heroes looked like me and I said that throughout.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Jeter says, Lauren listened.\par \par \uc2 Though the label historically included Black models like Naomi Campbell and Tyson Beckford when other campaigns lagged on diversity, through the ages, Ralph Lauren has been seen as the paragon of Ivy League prep, elite society and some have even said waspiness.\par \par \uc2 But its creator was none of those things. He was a Jewish kid from the Bronx.\par \par \uc2 When Ralph Lauren, Ralph Lipschitz started this company, he was an outsider himself.\par \par \uc2 DARA DOUGLAS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JAMES JETER: Ralph didn`t grow up as a part of the Ivy League school, so he made that world for himself and he built a dream.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What do you say to people who will say, well, yes, there`s an opportunity to make some money here.\par \par \uc2 JAMES JETER: It`s so much bigger than again, the money, I think it`s about a little girl walking by a billboard and seeing herself up in the sky.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How important is it to see yourself represented in these spaces?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WALL RICE (Morehouse College Professor of Psychology): It`s profoundly important.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Professor David Wall Rice was in the campaign. He says Jeter`s vision included an open dialogue about complex issues.\par \par \uc2 Some historians have said, well, this clothing that the brand represents is clothing that Black people have had to wear to occupy White spaces. And if anything, this might be a way to simply make money.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WALL RICE: I think that`s fair. Now, one of the things that I would say is that, as we`re talking about how it is that Black folks show up in the world, there`s no singular way of doing that. In suggesting how it is that Black folk have to wear clothes to do a certain thing, I think that that divorces us from agency.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: While Rice says Black Americans have spent 400 years adapting and finding ways to navigate the American cultural landscape.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WALL RICE: I`m thinking now of Dubois` classic note and construct of double consciousness where there is the Black cultural and there`s also the American cultural, and in many times, they`re at loggerheads.\par \par \uc2 These were some of the clothes that we built America in. We also picked cotton, and so we had clothing for that as well, and I think that this collaboration was an effort of realizing us doing things more boldly, more fully, and telling, you know, more accurate stories.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And James Jeter says, there are more chapters ahead.\par \par \uc2 JAMES JETER: And so this is just the beginning. Ralph has told so many stories over the last 54 years, but has so many stories to tell.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nice line.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So many bravos and kudos in that story. Bravo to James and Dara who brought it to Ralph`s attention, the fact that they felt comfortable enough and challenging him to say, you know, I`m not so sure I want to be here.\par \par \uc2 And then big bravos and kudos to Ralph Lauren who listened.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And said: What can we do about it? And then really put out a really beautiful, gorgeous collection.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: James is 30 years old.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, and Dara is also fairly young for Ralph Lauren, a guy who is, you know, in his 80s, who listened to these two young Black employees of his.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Didn`t have to do anything, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And turned it over to them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Turned it over to them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So the models were people of color.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The photographers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Photographers, the whole production were people of color. And I should point out, I want to thank Vita (sp?) and Jenny Camen our producer, and Mary Swarms (sp?). First day back in the office yesterday for Mary. She produced that piece, edited that piece. Really well done. It was beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All well said, beautifully done, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Up next, our "Kindness 101" series gives us all a lesson in fortitude.\par \par \uc2 A woman living with Down syndrome shows Steve Hartman how she never gave up, whether she`s learning to drive or looking for change at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Local weather, next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning, we`re continuing our special series "Kindness 101" with Steve Hartman who is sharing stories that are built around themes of kindness and character and focusing on the people who have mastered those qualities.\par \par \uc2 Today`s lesson: Fortitude.\par \par \uc2 Steve and his kids show us what one woman made possible with a little patience and a whole lot of grit.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good morning, everyone. This is my daughter, Merrill.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: Hi.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And as you can see, we have two forts on our desk. This one Merrill made out of stone, and this one I made out of carts.\par \par \uc2 Now, Merrill, which fort would you rather live in?\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: The stone one.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Right. Because this fort has more.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: Fortitude.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: The word "fort" is right in there. What`s that word mean, though? For that, let`s go over to Emmett at the Dictionary Desk -- Emmett.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Good morning. Fortitude is to have or feel strength.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Yes, in people, it is the emotional strength you need to get through tough times, so that whenever anything bad blows your way, fortitude helps you withstand the adversity.\par \par \uc2 And there`s no better example of this than the woman in the story we are about to show you. A story pulled from our archive of amazing Americans, the living embodiment of fortitude.\par \par \uc2 Kayla McKeon may be Washington`s most unlikely power broker.\par \par \uc2 As a lobbyist, the only registered lobbyist with Down syndrome, Kayla roams the Capitol advocating for the \uc2 National Down Syndrome Society\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON (\uc2 National Down Syndrome Society\uc2  advocate): I`m good.\par \par \uc2 SARA HART WEIR (Former President and CEO of the \uc2 National Down Syndrome Society\uc2 ): She`s an incredible asset to this organization.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Sara Hart Weir is her boss.\par \par \uc2 SARA HART WEIR: She is extremely articulate, and she is quick on her feet.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: And I`m not going to take no for an answer.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: They hired you for your communication skills.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: That, too.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And your charm.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: You`re good at this.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It takes a schmoozer to know a schmoozer.\par \par \uc2 But Kayla also has a certain sincerity that can turn almost any politician into putty.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: I need your help.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Okay, what do you need? You need a sponsor?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: I definitely need you.\par \par \uc2 MARK MCKEON (Kayla McKeon`s father): Am I surprised that she is in Washington, D.C. calling on senators and congressmen? Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: For parents, Mark and Patti say, although their daughter continues to surprise, Kayla never really let Down syndrome slow her down.\par \par \uc2 They say even at two, Kayla had already decided she would drive a car someday.\par \par \uc2 MARK MCKEON: To pass the permit test, we said you have to be able to read. So that gave her encouragement to knuckle down and start reading and right now, she still reads a book a week.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And she got her driver`s license, too. That`s her celebrating after passing the road test.\par \par \uc2 She is now one of just a handful of people with Down syndrome to have a license.\par \par \uc2 What about that parallel parking?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: You don`t want to do know.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Really?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: That`s challenging.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Fortunately, today she does more flying than driving. A couple times a month she leaves her home in Syracuse for her office in D.C. where Kayla is focused on passing a law that would make it illegal to pay people with disabilities anything less than minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: I got it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: She says if it passes, it would be a monumental achievement, but a thrill regardless, just to be part of the process.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: I just love the feeling of, wow, I`m here. I`m making history.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Hard to believe?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Kayla McKeon, lily in the swamp.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, my friend and my inspiration, Kayla.\par \par \uc2 Hello\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Hi, Steve. Hi, Merrill. Hi, Emmett.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Hi, Kayla.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Is it true? You`re getting your college degree now?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Actually, I`m two classes away from my Associate`s Degree in Dental Studies, and I just passed my Meteorology class last time.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You passed your Meteorology class?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Yes, with a C.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I dropped out of that class.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: That`s hard.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Yes. Very hard. That`s a mess.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: Did you pass your driving test the first time?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Not my first, sorry, but my fifth time.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: How do you not give up?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: I am very persistent. Yes, I sometimes did, but then I`ll go, no I`ll have it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Seems like a big part of fortitude is patience and budging Congress on anything I think would take a lot of patience, right?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Oh, absolutely. It does.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Where does that last stand now?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Talk about patience, because it has to go through many channels.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And you`re not going to give up because you have --\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Fortitude, darn it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Okay. Well, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MCKEON: Absolutely. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And good luck to you powering through whatever obstacles you face today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: Until then, stay kind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Merrill, we will do our best to stay kind. That`s not hard to do.\par \par \uc2 Didn`t we love Kayla?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We love Kayla. I love these Steve Hartman stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re so good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like what Kayla said to Steve, "You`re good at this." You go Kayla, you`re good at this, too.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Day four of our workweek and we are still talking about the possibility about this being the new Friday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are still trying to figure out how we can make that work, \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`re open to all suggestions. Help us.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Shawna just said, "We can`t."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220414ei4e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 One On One With Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 720 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , who is working from home after testing positive for COVID, said the city would not allow New Yorkers to be terrorized, even by a single individual.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with the mayor about the investigation, and he vowed that the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  will catch the suspect.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Is the suspect known to the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D-Mayor of New York City): As of this time, we have not made a positive identification. Again, we`re following a number of leads.\par \par \uc2 And what we don`t want to do is put out any misinformation. We want to be thorough in the information we give, so that the public can assist us in making this apprehension.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So, you`re saying that, at this hour, the suspect is still at large, and you believe him to be armed and dangerous?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 We`re telling anyone that -- approach anyone that they believe is suspicious to notify the police department. But, at this time, the person is not apprehended. There`s no reason for to us believe that he`s not still armed.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Why hasn`t the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  released a photo of the suspect?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: In cases like these -- I was a former transit police officer, and, as you know, a former member of the \uc2 New York City Police Department\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You put out a photo of the suspect that is incorrect, you could create a real crisis in the city.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Do you have a good photo of the suspect?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: All of this is part of the ongoing investigation. And as soon as we get one, we`re going to release it to the public.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Can you confirm that there was a malfunction with the camera system? Was it just one camera or all the cameras in that subway station?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: No, I can`t confirm. I know we were having a feed problem. We`re attempting to retrieve whatever video that`s possible.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What concerns you the most, given that this suspect is still on the loose?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: It`s larger than this suspect.\par \par \uc2 What concerns me most is what I have been talking about for several months now, that we have many rivers that are feeding the sea of violence in our city and cities across America. And it`s time for all lawmakers to be on the same page.\par \par \uc2 The overproliferation of guns. We removed 1,800 guns off our streets in a little over three months, similar to the gun that was used. It`s time for us to get serious about the guns in our city, including those guns.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: More than three million people use the subway every day in New York City. What are you doing to make sure that commuters feel safe?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: Well, number one, I`m one of them.\par \par \uc2 I believe our subway system is one of the finest systems in the country. And it`s going to play a vital role in our recovery effort. Today, immediately, we`re going to double down on our patrol strength.\par \par \uc2 If I was not here recovering from COVID, I would be on that subway system today, because I think, as the mayor, you have to lead from the front.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And that was our interview with New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to the economy, with those stunning new inflation numbers out today. The Labor Department`s Consumer Price Index rose by 8.5 percent in March compared to a year ago. That is the fastest pace in more than 40 years. Gas prices accounted for over half the monthly increase.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220414ei4c0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Deadly Tornado Outbreak; Tornado Threat Continues; Judge Brown Jackson Testimony; Ukraine`s Fight for Freedom; Biden`s High-Stakes Summit; NATO`s Historic Test; Why are Gas Prices Still High?; Amanda Bynes Freed from Conservatorship; Venus Williams Live; Being Transgender in America, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Vladimir Duthiers, Nate Burleson, David Begnaud, Omar  Villafranca, Nikole Killion, Holly Williams, Ed O`Keefe, Major Garrett,  Robert Costa, Jan Crawford, Jonathan Vigliotti \par \uc2 13,678 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is off today. But let`s go to today`s Eye Opener. It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Another day of deadly tornadoes, this time in New Orleans and the suburbs nearby. We are there as people reckon with the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DICKERSON (Arabi, Louisiana resident): When it hit, I could feel it and I could hear it. It sounded like a train. When it pulled the roof off, it was pulling me off the ground.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The President heads to Europe to shore up support from our allies for new actions against Russia.\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE (\uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  Spokesperson): This war will not end easily or rapidly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Republicans step up the pressure in the confirmation hearing of Judge \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): So you wouldn`t say that you`re an activist, Judge.\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Associate Justice nominee): I would not say that.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The omicron sub variant known as BA.2 is causing about one-third of COVID-19 cases in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2  (\uc2 National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases\uc2  Director): I don`t really see that there would be a major surge.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  has tested positive for COVID and White House Press Secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  has just tested positive.\par \par \uc2 MAN: So big news in women`s tennis, world number one Ash Barty has announced for retirement from the sport at the age of 25.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Trae Young put on the show. He going 45.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Point six on the clock. He is going to heave with full court.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And Trae Young beats the buzzer.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You could try that a hundred times, I don`t think you`d throw that perfectly. Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: James Corden surprised Drew Carey on the set of "The Price is Right" in honor of the show`s 50th anniversary. Then he gave him a chance to win in his own game.\par \par \uc2 DREW CAREY ("The Price is Right" host): I really want that stroganoff.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show with James Corden" host): Alexis?\par \par \uc2 We cannot let you go home empty handed.\par \par \uc2 DREW CAREY: No you can`t.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: So guess what? Tonight, you can take that stroganoff.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Drew Carey. I remember him from "The Drew Carey Show" and I thought, how is he going to be able to do "Price is Right"?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it working out okay for him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. And he is really awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I needed not to worry about Drew Carey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it. The price is always right on the show because we`re giving you information for free.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of the show, we have some big stories for you today. Record high gas prices have many Americans asking why not just drill for more oil, more supply and lower prices? Well, Omar Villafranca visited oil producers and found out it is not quite that simple. He will show us how the pain at the oil pump affects everyone`s bottom line.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we`ll talk to one of the great ones in the world of tennis. That is the one and only Venus Williams. She is trying to help women get what they need to succeed through a new collaboration. She will tell us all about that.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got to ask about the Oscars. You know, it`s just a few days away. "King Richard" the movie about per family is up for six Academy Awards. Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to start with the weather, you`ve heard about it. The severe weather that is pounding the south. At least two tornadoes, including this one, tore through New Orleans and the surrounding area. At least one person was killed and many more were injured.\par \par \uc2 The town of Arabi, that`s just east of New Orleans was hit especially hard. The homes knocked off their foundations that debris scattered far and wide. Our lead national correspondent, David Begnaud is in Arabi with more on the story.\par \par \uc2 David, I know this is your home state. How`s it looking? What are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Listen, my friend, we were driving around assessing the damage when we rolled up on this, a school bus toss like toy. In fact, there are two on the street right behind Arabi Elementary School.\par \par \uc2 Now on average, a school bus weighs about 25,000 pounds. That`s how powerful that tornado was. And then watch this across the street. I said: Wait a minute. Where did those stairs lead to, right? The steps? Where would that go? And somebody said: Oh, that used to be somebody`s front door. You`d come here and walk in the house and I said, "Well, where`s the house?" They said, "That`s it."\par \par \uc2 The tornado picked it up, moved it off its blocks and literally shoved it about five yards with the people who live there inside.\par \par \uc2 The first tornado hit the New Orleans area right after dinnertime, about 7:30 PM local time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: [Bleep], dude. Holy [bleep].\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That is what it looked like, a tornado moving through a major American city, New Orleans. This is what cut through parts of New Orleans and moved into nearby St. Bernard Parish.\par \par \uc2 The twister moved through New Orleans`s lower Ninth Ward without doing much damage, but as it touched down in St. Bernard Parish, it was devastating.\par \par \uc2 James Dickerson was in his home in Arabi, Louisiana with his five-year-old son.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DICKERSON: And the house started shaking and then when it pulled the roof off, I was, you know, sheltering him and then it was pulling me off the ground.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: His house is now ruined.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DICKERSON: The dining room here. You know a bookcase. Anyway, there is the front door, it was there.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Where the hell is the church?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The damage in St. Bernard Parish is widespread.\par \par \uc2 Power lines are down everywhere, streets are littered with debris. That is actually pieces of people`s homes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The porch of my friend`s house doesn`t have a roof anymore.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: This tornadoes spawned from the same weather system that originally started in Texas on Monday and caused extensive damage there and in Oklahoma, and now three other states along the Gulf Coast as the line of weather moves eastward.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We heard it coming down the street. It was a lot of whistling.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Back here where we are in Arabi, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You know, I`m just glad, mostly everybody seems to be accounted for. I think there was a couple of issues a block away from us. But other than that, you know, we`re safe.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Look, meteorologists here in the New Orleans area say surveying the damage preliminarily, it looks like the tornado could have been an EF2-EF3. That would put winds near 165 miles per hour. Nothing official yet, crews will be back out today. But that`s how powerful it appears to have been.\par \par \uc2 Nate, back to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Those severe storms are headed east threatening some 30 million Americans today. For what to expect, let us bring in meteorologist, Jim Cantore from \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 `s global headquarters in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Jim, a busy day yesterday and it leads to another one today.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Do we need to remind everybody it is only the fourth day of spring? And three out of those four days have had either tornadoes on the ground or warnings like we have already this morning. Holy smokes.\par \par \uc2 You can see the kind of storm reports that we`ve been looking at, and if you just take the reports over the last couple of days, 32 on Monday, and then 26 yesterday, let`s say all those become tornadoes. That`s over half of what we see for the whole month of March.\par \par \uc2 So holy smokes, we are off to a heck of a start.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go back and revisit this New Orleans tornado though because, of course last night, the sun is about to set and all of a sudden, we get this what we call long track supercell. Those are the types of storms that produce the tornadoes moving up through New Orleans, all right, Arabi, the Ninth Ward and eventually all the way up toward the eastern side of Lake Pontchartrain, at times, showing some very, very strong rotation and that is one of the things about the new radar that we can look at.\par \par \uc2 We can actually see the rotation, you see that with the red green couplet. This is going to reset for you in just a second. Look at what`s going on, Jean Lafitte, then just southeast of the city center in New Orleans, boom, that rotation maximized itself out and we had those ominous looking pictures, a lot, of course from the local TV stations showing that on their cameras.\par \par \uc2 Today, our focus shifting to the east, also up to the north. Don`t forget about Ohio and Michigan, some of the same parameters that exist for tornadoes in the region across the south also exist on up to the north.\par \par \uc2 So here`s the radar. It looks like it`s going to be an active afternoon, the atmosphere will continue to moisten from North Carolina, all the way down to Florida. So it`s going to be one of those humid days, but it`s not as humid in Ohio and Michigan, but we have better parameters in terms of cold air loft and spin in the atmosphere and that is where we`re going to see some of those isolated tornadoes, hail producers as well. All hazards are in play.\par \par \uc2 Of course it is, it`s springtime, and that is what we get with thunderstorms. As I mentioned March getting off to a big star here with spring. We only go up from here, Nate, the biggest months for tornadoes will be likely April and May.\par \par \uc2 You could catch live coverage of severe weather all day on The Weather Channel Television Network.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As always, Jim Cantore on the show with everything we need to know. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: My pleasure, buddy. My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now to Capitol Hill where Judge \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  did as much listening as speaking yesterday during her Supreme Court nomination.\par \par \uc2 Several Republican senators questioned her on issues that matter to the party`s voters including critical race theory. Over 13 hours, Jackson was also asked about her philosophy.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion was on Capitol Hill where the hearing continues this morning.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 I went out to dinner last night. I got home at nine. I couldn`t believe she was still sitting there. What a very long day for the Judge.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, and it could be another long day for Judge Jackson, Gayle. But look, the White House and Democrats are dismissing some of these questions from Republican senators. They say they crossed the line and are even pushing on smears based on conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Out of the gate, Judge \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  addressed the elephant in the room.\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Associate Justice nominee): As a mother and a judge, I was thinking that nothing could be further from the truth.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Jackson refuted debunked claims by Missouri Republican Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  that she issued lenient sentences in child pornography cases.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): We`re talking about eight-year-olds and nine- year-olds and 11-year-olds and 12-year-olds. He`s got images of these the government said added up to over 600 images. Help me understand that. What word would you use if it`s not heinous or egregious?\par \par \uc2 What -- how would you describe it?\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 : It is heinous. It is egregious. What a judge has to do is determine how to sentence defendants proportionately.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: It wasn`t the only issue Senate Republicans raised as Texas Senator Ted Cruz also brought up critical race theory questioning Jackson`s role on a private school board.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Are you comfortable with these ideas being taught to children?\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 : Senator, I have not reviewed any of those books.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: When Senator Cruz was asked how this is relevant to the Supreme Court. He said critical race theory is divisive.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): There`s too much division in our country, and I think it is concerning if we have justices who are embracing that view.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But by and large --\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 DIANNE FEINSTEIN\uc2  (D-CA): I think you`re doing very well. And as you can see, this is a bit of a tough place.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The majority of questions from both sides of the aisle centered on Judge Jackson`s judicial philosophy.\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 : I do not believe that there is a living Constitution.\par \par \uc2 I must admit that I don`t really have a justice that I`ve molded myself after or that I would. What I have is a record.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: A record that almost brought the judge to tears, as she talked about the challenges of balancing motherhood.\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 : I said in my opening that girls, you know, you`ve had to deal with me juggling motherhood and job responsibilities. And I didn`t always get the balance right.\par \par \uc2 And so I would hope for them, seeing me move to the Supreme Court that they can know that you don`t have to be perfect in your career trajectory, and you can still end up doing what you want to do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Back to some of those questions from Senator Hawley, he doubled down telling me, he thought Judge Jackson`s responses were excuses and that he can`t vote for someone like that. But in the end, it may not matter because Democrats don`t necessarily need GOP votes to confirm her -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And there will be more questions today.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion for us. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 There are new signs of progress this morning in Ukraine`s fight against Russia. Russian forces are still devastating communities by air, but citizens are banding together to keep them at bay on the ground. Holly Williams is about a hundred miles west of Kyiv talking with some of those people who are fighting back.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 From the air, Russia continues to reign death and destruction on this country. This school was hit by a Russian air strike earlier this month according to Ukrainian officials, though nobody was killed. But on the ground, Ukrainian forces are on the counteroffensive while Russia is struggling to supply its troops with fuel and food according to a senior U.S. defense official.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: This video appears to show a column of Russian military vehicles being lured into a trap in the town of Voznesens`k earlier this month. Helping with the ambush was a shopkeeper, Alexander (sp?).\par \par \uc2 (ALEXANDER speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "You little beauties," he shouts.\par \par \uc2 Nearly a month after Russia invaded its neighbor, its ground forces have made surprisingly slow progress. Ukraine`s military says it recaptured the town of Makariv on Monday, a key strategic gateway to the capital Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 And across Ukraine, ordinary people have mobilized to defend their homeland.\par \par \uc2 At this checkpoint, we met Anatoly (sp?) a pig farmer.\par \par \uc2 Okay, load it.\par \par \uc2 Armed with his own hunting rifle.\par \par \uc2 (ANATOLY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: He told us the biggest thing he ever shot before was a goat. But now, he is ready to fight the Russians with an improvised bomb shelter dug out by the roadside.\par \par \uc2 This is your protection from missiles and airstrikes.\par \par \uc2 They`ve got tire spikes and other obstacles to stop a Russian advance.\par \par \uc2 These are to throw in the way of the tanks and disable them.\par \par \uc2 VITALIY (Green grocer): Yes, yes. I`ll show you --\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Vitaliy (sp?), a green grocer showed us their homemade arsenal of Molotov cocktails.\par \par \uc2 Would you be willing to give up some of Ukraine`s territory for peace?\par \par \uc2 (VITALIY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "It is impossible to give our territory," away he told us. "I want to agree to anything else."\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s fighting spirit is breathtaking. The ingenuity and bravery of its people extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 But from the air, Russia is leaving carnage in its path.\par \par \uc2 This hospital was decimated by a Russian missile strike on Monday say Ukrainian officials, killing three.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: And Russia has even deadlier weapons at its disposal. In an interview yesterday, the Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov repeatedly refused to rule out using nuclear weapons if Russia faced a quote, "existential threat" -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Holly, I am so in awe of the Ukrainian people. The love and the passion they have for their country is like nothing I`ve ever seen before. I am so glad you`re telling these stories.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Holly.\par \par \uc2 President Biden heads to Western Europe this morning where he will meet with \uc2 NATO\uc2 , \uc2 European Union\uc2 , and \uc2 G7\uc2  allies to find a solution to the growing crisis there.\par \par \uc2 The President will also visit Poland where more than two million Ukrainian refugees have fled since the Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is in Brussels. He is with the President where the President will start his trip.\par \par \uc2 Ed, this trip seems to have come together very quickly from those on the outside looking in.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It sure has, Gayle. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 In fact, it`s come together so quickly that \uc2 NATO\uc2  is officially calling this an extraordinary summit because it normally takes months, if not a year, to arrange for the 30 world leaders to get together here.\par \par \uc2 There will also be meetings, as you mentioned of the \uc2 G7\uc2  and the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 . And what`s on the agenda? Well, among other things, they are going to be talking about new sanctions against Russia and also finding ways to bolster the ones that have already been put in place to make sure that oligarchs and others being targeted don`t evade the punishments.\par \par \uc2 The President is also expected to talk about making sure that Europe becomes less dependent on Russian oil and natural gas. Also likely to be some talk about military deployments by \uc2 NATO\uc2  closer to the region and Ukraine`s leader, Zelenskyy is expected to virtually address the meetings.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Ukraine has been pushing to become a member of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and \uc2 NATO\uc2 , but is not currently a member of either Alliance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hey, Ed, the President is also heading to Ukraine`s neighbor Poland. I know you know that, but what do we expect from that part of the trip?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes, remember, they are on the frontlines and they`ve absorbed more than two million refugees since this crisis began. So the visit is in essence, a "thank you" to Poland for shouldering so much of this humanitarian crisis.\par \par \uc2 The White House says the President will meet with Poland`s President. He will give formal remarks about how the free world is responding to the crisis and helping Ukraine and don`t be surprised though nothing has been officially announced that he meets at some point with Ukrainian refugees.\par \par \uc2 The irony of it, many Americans back home of course are starting to go back to work after the pandemic. Well, this is a President who says he prefers face-to-face diplomacy over those virtual calls he has been holding over the last several months. He will have plenty of time for gripping, grinning, handshakes, and face-to-face conversation in the coming days -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Face-to-face, always very important. Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 The number one women`s tennis player sent shockwaves through the sports world overnight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up why Ash Barty says it is game set match for her career and she is only 25. Why is she doing that? She will explain, but first it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, what is driving the surging price of crude oil? We go to the heart of oil country where the rubber meets the well.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Major Garrett looks at the long history of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and how the Western alliance was on shaky ground before Vladimir Putin`s invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When you rent a bounce house for your kid`s birthday party, the house is supposed to bounce. So look what happened over the weekend in Hickory, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 This video shows an inflatable castle flying through the air nearly hitting a child after strong winds broke the straps that were supposed to hold it steady.\par \par \uc2 The child`s mother, Jennifer Bean said the inflatable grazed her son, Michael`s head and back. No one was hurt, thankfully. But we hear that Michael has requested a no bouncy house policy for future birthday parties.\par \par \uc2 I feel you on that, Michael.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You see too many stories like this, Nate, with these bounce houses. If you`re going to do it, please make sure it is professionally installed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is nuts to me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very true.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re going to get a visit from Venus Williams. She will talk about a new initiative to give working women more money and we`ll see what she thinks of tennis star, Ash Barty retiring at just 25.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS as President Biden travels to Belgium to meet with \uc2 NATO\uc2  members. He says the Alliance is more united today than ever before. The reason: \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This morning, chief Washington correspondent, that`s Major Garrett, looks at the history of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and its many challenges over the decades. He is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Major, I`m thinking, a lot of people could use a little history lesson right about now. You are the perfect person for that.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good morning, Gayle. Good morning, everyone.\par \par \uc2 President Biden leaves for Brussels this morning knowing \uc2 NATO\uc2  has never faced a test like this, a Hot War in Europe with terrifying conventional weapons and the possibility of worse, nuclear, biological, and chemical weapons.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 NATO\uc2  is not defending a member country, but it has grown unified in the minds of some surprisingly unified in defense of everything this alliance was created to preserve.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: On April 4, 1949, the North Atlantic Treaty was signed.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: \uc2 NATO\uc2  grew out of World War Two, a defensive alliance to shield Western Europe from the Soviet Union.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The treaty members realized that real peace is more than an absence of war.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: America and Europe also wanted to contain Germany, the aggressor nation in both world wars.\par \par \uc2 MARY SAROTTE (Author, "Not One Inch."): The unofficial purpose as it was quietly rumored at the time was to keep the Soviets out, to keep the Americans in, and to keep the Germans down.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The late 40s, the Soviets controlled East Germany marking the beginning of the Cold War and NATO`s formation.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): The United States together with our allies will defend every inch of \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The key to \uc2 NATO\uc2  is Article V, "An attack on one member nation is an attack on the alliance and all nations must respond in common defense." That kept the peace for decades.\par \par \uc2 In 1979, when the Soviets invaded Afghanistan, \uc2 NATO\uc2  deployed nuclear missiles as a deterrent. When the Berlin Wall fell in 1989, Germany reunified and Pan European peace seemed achievable.\par \par \uc2 Then came 9/11, a new kind of war. \uc2 NATO\uc2  came to Americans defense, invoking Article V for the first time with \uc2 NATO\uc2  forces joining the U.S. in Afghanistan for the next 20 years.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  (Former President of the United States): This was an act of great friendship in a time of great need.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: In 2004, \uc2 NATO\uc2  added seven former Soviet client states and has gradually expanded since then to its current size of 30 member nations.\par \par \uc2 This expansion alarmed and infuriated \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , who saw \uc2 NATO\uc2  a defensive alliance as a military and political rival, one that might deprive Russia of global prominence.\par \par \uc2 KAY BAILEY HUTCHISON (Former \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  member): I think it was absolutely the right decision to expand.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Kay Bailey Hutchison was U.S. Ambassador to \uc2 NATO\uc2  during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 KAY BAILEY HUTCHISON: If we hadn`t gone out and brought those former Soviet satellite countries in with us, I think they`d be the ones that would be invaded right now.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: While it expanded, the Alliance drifted. Member nations declined to meet defense spending requirements, an issue pressed by the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States): If we`ve got collective defense, that means that everybody has got to chip in.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And amplified by President Trump.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): They will have to pay more frankly. They`re going to have to pay more.\par \par \uc2 KAY BAILEY HUTCHISON: They didn`t like the tone that President Trump used, of course, but they never ever said one time that they didn`t know they had to do more.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Trump says he helped revive the Alliance though critics said, threatening to pull out of it did the opposite.\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2  has talked off and on of adding Ukraine, but never did. That`s why \uc2 NATO\uc2  did not intervene when Putin took Crimea in 2014 and isn`t fighting Russia now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: This war is now about Russia taking control of all of Ukraine if it can. If that happens, of course, Ukraine would never become a part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As we`ve seen, Ukraine is fighting for its very survival and its future, and if it prevails, it may not need or want common defense protection or it may want it more than ever. Either way, \uc2 NATO\uc2  is not far from the bloody center of this conflict -- guys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, Major, I always love a history lesson from you. And as you know, General \uc2 Dwight Eisenhower\uc2  was the first Supreme Allied Commander, and he said in a speech in 1957, "We want freedom, pay any price necessary to preserve freedom." Is \uc2 NATO\uc2  still willing to do that?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: It appears it is, and there is one really important part of this process. That reunification of Germany, when Germany reunified, then Secretary of State James Baker said to \uc2 Mikhail Gorbachev\uc2 , if you let Germany reunify on our terms, we won`t expand \uc2 NATO\uc2  one inch to the east. \uc2 Mikhail Gorbachev\uc2  said okay, that`s what Putin seizes upon.\par \par \uc2 That was an informal agreement, the actual treaty that did that though said \uc2 NATO\uc2  could expand. So Putin fixates on that informal process, ignores the actual treaty itself.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Major, thank you. Always a history lesson when you come on in. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That`s right. The world is watching. Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up. With gas prices soaring, we visit an Oklahoma oil producer to see why it is hard for the industry to pump as much oil as we want.\par \par \uc2 And here`s a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: With drivers facing sticker shock right now, we wanted to take a closer look at how U.S. oil production relates to soaring gas prices. AAA says the national average for a gallon of gas is now $4.24. But the price of crude oil is much more important to oil producers.\par \par \uc2 This morning, the benchmark price is over $115.00 a barrel, a lot higher than last year.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca went to Oklahoma to show the impact of oil prices on local operators and the people who depend on them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DARLENE WALLACE (Columbus Oil Company CEO/owner): That`s Carol`s house.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Is that how you identify things?\par \par \uc2 DARLENE WALLACE: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Hidden between the barns and buried 3,000 feet underground is Darlene Wallace`s fortune.\par \par \uc2 Wallace operates 13 small oil wells near Seminole, Oklahoma known as stripper wells, producing up to 15 barrels a day for Columbus Oil Company. There are about 400,000 operational stripper wells in the United States.\par \par \uc2 It`s not a high producing -- well, it`s not a gusher.\par \par \uc2 DARLENE WALLACE: Well, that`s why they call them stripper rails because we are stripping that last bit of oil out of the whale that we can possibly get.\par \par \uc2 TIM POPLIN (Superintendent): Anything under $50.00 a barrel, we lose money.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Superintendent Tim Poplin (sp?) showed us a sample of what they collect.\par \par \uc2 TIM POPLIN: This is the salt water and this is Wilcox oil. And if you look, it`s really not all the way across the top.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: As long as oil is over $50.00 a barrel, it is worth doing the work for.\par \par \uc2 TIM POPLIN: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Oil prices have been especially volatile in the last five years. And the boom and bust of the oil patch can have a major impact on small producers like Columbus Oil.\par \par \uc2 DARLENE WALLACE: All of our wells shut down when it went to a minus number in April and we shut everything down because you can`t pay someone to come pick up your oil.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: You had to shut down for two months.\par \par \uc2 DARLENE WALLACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: What was that like?\par \par \uc2 DARLENE WALLACE: It was like not getting a paycheck.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Now, that number has reached about $115.00 a barrel, a pain at the pump for the consumer, but a boom for her small business.\par \par \uc2 DARLENE WALLACE: This is my rainy day fund. So I`m going to bank money and wait for that crash to come because it will come. This business is very cyclic.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Even though it may be 10 to 15 barrels a day. Do you think you guys can help the American economy when it comes to oil and the oil supply?\par \par \uc2 DARLENE WALLACE: Well, we produce 10 percent of the American oil. That`s what -- you know, that`s what stripper wells do.\par \par \uc2 ED HIRS (\uc2 University of Houston\uc2  energy fellow): It takes mobilization --\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Ed Hirs, an energy fellow at the \uc2 University of Houston\uc2  says even though oil is in a boom, it`s not easy to just turn on the pumps. It can take months to get them online and the workforce, while rebounding has taken a beating.\par \par \uc2 In the last 10 years, the sector has lost more than 50,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 So when people are saying, you know we have enough domestic supply to bring out of the ground and alleviate the pain at the pump. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 ED HIRS: Oh, that`s absolutely true. It just requires capital.\par \par \uc2 Our wells are not the most productive in the world. Our wells are the most expensive oil wells in the world to drill because we don`t get the huge output that say a Saudi well does.\par \par \uc2 It`s just a matter of dollars and cents and no one is going to start drilling unless they can see a path forward to getting revenue and profits.\par \par \uc2 DARLENE WALLACE: The oil had gone --\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Wallace won`t slow down and plans to keep pushing production of her small wells.\par \par \uc2 DARLENE WALLACE: Wells are in the rural area and they`re not in urban areas and we give people in rural areas jobs. We can`t let our rural areas dissipate because there are no jobs.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: She says it may not help Wall Street, but it helps Main Street.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Omar Villafranca, Seminole, Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a new term I hadn`t heard before, stripper wells.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Vlad is going to have the stories we think you`re going to be talking about today. He`s got some good stuff on the docket.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he does.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I try.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He makes all of our dreams come. It`s time for Vlad and "What to Watch." What`s up Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s going on? What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What do you have for us today?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve got some really, really good interesting talkers today.\par \par \uc2 Let`s begin with this tennis shocker. The world`s number one women`s player, that is Ash Barty. She announced her retirement overnight. She, guys, is only 25 years old and she is at the top of her game.\par \par \uc2 The Australian broke the news yesterday on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  in an emotional interview with her former doubles partner, Casey Dellacqua. Barty says she started thinking of hanging up her racket for good after winning Wimbledon last year, which was a lifelong dream. She says the real tipping point was winning this year`s Australian Open.\par \par \uc2 Barty is the first Australian champion in nearly half a century. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEIGH BARTY (Australian tennis player): I think that for me just feels like the most perfect way, my perfect way to celebrate what an amazing journey my tennis career has been.\par \par \uc2 As a person, this is what I want. I want to chase after some other dreams that I`ve always wanted to do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow, I so admire that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Me too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The idea that she says "I want to chase after other dreams," she said, Ash Barty, the person has so many dreams that she wants to chase after that don`t necessarily involve traveling the world, being away from my family, being away from my home, which is where I`ve always wanted to be.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to see what those dreams are.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is good to see at 25 to know with such conviction.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s a certain guilt that athletes have when they walk away too soon from the game because they feel like they have to stay in the game because they are living somebody else`s dream. We hear all the time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s fascinating.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man, it must be amazing. You know what I would do to play in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 ? You know what I`ll do to be a tennis, I would give my left arm.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right, at that level.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: At that level, and you think to yourself, I can`t walk away. I`ve been blessed with these gifts I have to play this game. She is saying, you know what? I don`t want to live somebody else`s dream. I already lived this dream. It was amazing. I had a great run.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how many athletes do you see that retire at the top of their game? Isn`t that rare?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is rare, it is. But I do believe it`s going to happen more often. One, they are making a lot more money, so they can walk away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But we have to keep this in mind as well. She didn`t just luck into being the best tennis player in the world.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is the best.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is and she worked her butt off since she was a kid.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She`s probably fatigued and tired. She is like, you know what, I want to chase something else.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think you`re right. I think that this is just where we are today, especially after this pandemic, where now young people are leading the way in saying, I don`t have to do the one thing that I was meant to do for my entire life. I can do other things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I call that the new rich.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Ash Barty, you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Whichever you want.\par \par \uc2 All right, we are also cheering on Rachel Zegler. We brought this up --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Big time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? We talked to you about this yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this story.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Rachel Zegler may go to the Oscars after all.\par \par \uc2 The young actress who plays Maria in the remake of "West Side Story" had said she was not invited to Sunday`s Academy Awards. Well, fans on social media were outraged. We were kind of outraged. But she`s got millions of fans on social media.\par \par \uc2 And she is not nominated, but the movie did pick up seven nods including Best Picture. Now, "The Hollywood Reporter" says the \uc2 Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences\uc2  has invited her to be a presenter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We reached out to the Academy and Zegler`s reps, we haven`t heard back because she isn`t a nominee. It was up to 20th Century Studios, and the parent company which is \uc2 Disney\uc2  to offer Zegler a seat at the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So \uc2 Disney\uc2  got hammered.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Disney\uc2  got hammered, and a spokesperson told "Entertainment Weekly" that Zegler wasn`t invited due to a scheduling issue because she is filming "Snow White" in London, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: News flash, they have these things called planes. People go. You get onboard and you get on a plane.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am so glad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel like we probably we played a role in that, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I feel like we probably did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am so glad it worked out though. Will Packer is producing the show this year. I think this is a great edition.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And she is going to be a presenter, which is awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, instead of being in sweats and in her boyfriend`s flannel on the couch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On the coach.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She can now walk the red carpet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly. I am so excited. I just love her story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Chosen out of 30,000 to play Maria. We love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love some --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Great job, Vlad. All right, coming up, tennis great. That`s right tennis great, Venus William joins us to talk about fighting for equal pay for women. And of course, we have to talk "King Richard" at the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, Day Three of confirmation hearings for Supreme Court nominee, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 . We`ll ask our Jan Crawford and Robert Costa, how is it going so far?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. Actress Amanda Bynes, has been freed from her conservatorship. What it means for her and how this case is different from Britney Spears.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. We are talking to a legend today. Venus Williams joins us to talk tennis and serves up a new effort to address the gender pay gap.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I see what you did there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did, too. She is on the case, but first, here is today`s Eye Opener at Eight.\par \par \uc2 Destructive tornadoes tear through Louisiana killing one person near New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Where`s the house? They said, that`s it. The tornado picked it up, moved it off its blocks and literally shoved it about five yards with the people who live there inside.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: President Biden travels to Europe today to meet with \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies about Russia`s war on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: From the air, Russia continues to reign death and destruction on this country.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Supreme Court nominee, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  continues her Senate testimony today after a marathon day and night of questioning.\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 : What a judge has to do is determined how to sentence defendants proportionately.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: More than one-third of all new COVID cases in the U.S. now come from the new omicron sub variant. Overall infections remain very low.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 : But I think it will be that we do see an uptick. Hopefully, it won`t be accompanied by an increase in hospitalizations.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the airline industry faces a pilot shortage and one young man wants to help, just as soon as he is old enough.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Do you want to tell you out the key difference from a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and an Airbus? You see out the window, they look pretty much square.\par \par \uc2 So you want to turn left, you put the left engines down or you increase power on the right.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You got it, man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He seems to know what he is doing in the cockpit.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You increase power.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you`re good to go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It looks like he might earn his wings pretty soon. That`s for sure. Fly high, young man, fly high.\par \par \uc2 All right, we begin this hour with devastation near New Orleans after two tornadoes hit the area last night. At least one person was killed, many more were injured throughout the area.\par \par \uc2 Here, you can see just some of the damage in Arabi, Louisiana just east of New Orleans. Our lead national correspondent, David Begnaud is in one of those homes that was moved by the high winds.\par \par \uc2 David, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Nate, good morning. It is 7:00 AM.\par \par \uc2 We were outside the home and I told you this is a home that literally the tornado picked up and moved about five yards. Well, the lady who owns the home invited us inside to see it.\par \par \uc2 This is Gabriella Rivarde, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 Tell me where you were last night with your son when the tornado hit.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLA RIVARDE (Arabi, Louisiana homeowner): Right in this living room right here. We -- I was there with my three dogs and my son, just kind of like watching the weather, just trying to see in the news like what you know, like because my phone kept going off with tornado warnings. So ...\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What was that moment like where the house literally gets picked up and moved?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLA RIVARDE: So, the first gust of wind just opened like the back door and one of my dogs run out, and the second -- and so that`s when I knew that the tornado was coming.\par \par \uc2 So then I started grabbing my son and my two dogs that were still in the house and running into that bedroom right there, trying to get them. I think I dislocated my son`s shoulder by like just trying to like pull him into that bedroom and then it just felt literally like we were being shoved and that everything was exploding around us kind of like just like a bomb went off inside the house.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Like a bomb went off. And when you looked at the floor, I mean everything is just scattered like it was tossed.\par \par \uc2 Her son, she took to the hospital. He`s got a hairline fracture. The dogs, by the way are okay.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about this tornado, as Gabriella knows that first touched down in New Orleans at about 7:30 PM local time, rolled through the Ninth Ward, which was so badly hit during Hurricane Katrina back in 2005, didn`t cause a lot of damage in New Orleans, but then moved eastward into St. Bernard Parish and this is where a majority of the damage is.\par \par \uc2 Two deaths so far, Gayle, and really a swath of damage that goes for several miles. We should find out today how strong the tornado was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Mother Nature is a mighty force.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And Gabriella, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it moved so quickly, too.\par \par \uc2 Yes, thank you. Thank you, both.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Supreme Court nominee, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  is back on Capitol Hill this morning for a second day of Senate questioning.\par \par \uc2 Senators quizzed the Federal Judge for 13 hours yesterday on a wide range of topics. Republicans pointed to her sentencing record in child pornography cases and for work defending detainees at Guantanamo Bay.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Do you support the idea -- did you support them the idea that indefinite detention of an enemy combatant is unlawful?\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 : Respectfully, Senator, when you are an attorney, and you have clients who come to you whether they pay or not, you represent their positions before the Court.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Do you believe the voice of the children is heard when a hundred percent of the time you`re sentencing child -- those in possession of child pornography to far below what the prosecutor is asking for?\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 : Yes, Senator, I do. Your chart does not include all of the factors that Congress has told Judges to consider including the probation officer`s recommendation in these cases.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Joining us now from Washington, our chief legal correspondent, Jan Crawford and chief election and campaign correspondent, Robert Costa. They`ve each covered at least half a dozen Supreme Court confirmation hearings.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you both.\par \par \uc2 Jan, let me begin with you. This is the first time we`ve seen Judge Jackson defend herself against Republican lawmakers during these hearings. Senator Ted Cruz, his line of query to Judge Jackson seemed to indicate, to the first Black woman ever to be nominated to the Supreme Court that she is soft on crime, she is soft on pedophiles, and that she holds views that Whites are somehow oppressors.\par \par \uc2 What did you make of her reaction to those questions?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, look, I mean, I think throughout the day, I mean, she was this mix of forceful when she needed to defend herself and you know, then compassionate when she was talking about her family, and some of her hard decisions that she`s had to make as a Judge, including in those child pornography cases, which she has handled as a Federal Trial Court Judge.\par \par \uc2 You saw Senator Cruz, Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  really kind of go after her on the allegation that she was lenient in sentencing these horrific child pornography offenders. But Vlad, I mean, there is just no there, there.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you look at her sentencing in those cases, it`s not out of whack with how other Federal Judges approach these cases. It does not take into account what we heard from those Republican senators yesterday. It does not take account that her sentences were actually in line with what probation officers were recommending in those cases.\par \par \uc2 So I think today, they are going to move on, and focus more generally on how she is maybe a lenient sentencer in other cases, but you know, there was no -- I mean, they`ve got no traction with that. And I thought what was interesting is that, you know, you saw her personality really come through in that questioning.\par \par \uc2 You know, she was forceful, but then she was compassionate in talking about how hard those cases were. It`s been very effective. I mean, it`s like when you`re trying, I thought yesterday, you know, when you`re trying to strike a match, and it won`t light, you know, there`s just no spark.\par \par \uc2 You saw the Republicans trying to strike that match, but there weren`t any sparks, which I mean, from her perspective, I think that is a very good thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I mean -- but Robert, I saw a headline in the "National Review."\par \par \uc2 The "National Review" that said Josh Hawley`s attacks on the judge are disingenuous.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: That is the Republican consensus, at least among some senators behind the scenes, and when I pull them aside, they say that these hearings are not just about Judge Jackson, they are a prism for the upcoming midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 So many of the issues that Republicans want to be talking about on the campaign trail beyond Ukraine, beyond the economy, crime, matters of race, matters of gender, they are bringing up in these hearings. It is a reflection of issues they believe, will motivate their own voters ahead of the November elections.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so glad that you made that point, Robert, because I was wondering if some of that line of questioning was even relevant to why she was in the room yesterday, but I know you`ve been speaking with lawmakers on both sides of the aisle about this.\par \par \uc2 Do you get the sense that there will be some Republicans -- it is so partisan these days in Washington -- do you get the sense that any Republicans will vote for her?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Here is who I`m paying attention to Gayle. Two of the Republicans senators who voted for her confirmation last year to the Federal bench, Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine and Senator Lisa Murkowski of Alaska and late last night, Gayle, I pulled aside a few Republican senators and asked who else?\par \par \uc2 They say pay attention to retiring Republican senators who may not feel the same obligations politically to vote against Judge Jackson. They could come out in support of her in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jan, what do you make of Senator Cory Booker`s exchange with Judge Jackson asking about our parents and our children, getting a bit emotional? Do you believe that strategy for the Democrats is going to work?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Oh, I mean, Nate, I mean that was striking. You know sometimes in these hearings, there is going to be this moment where you know, you can just feel the fight just leaves the room and we saw that in the confirmation hearings for Justice Alito for example. His wife burst into tears and rushed out.\par \par \uc2 And I thought Senator Booker`s questioning came pretty close to a moment. I mean, it was among the most powerful today. Not only that exchange, you mentioned about her parents, but also when he asked about the high murder rates of Black men, her worries for her own family. She called it anguishing, something I know she said all too well, that revealed that personal side of her.\par \par \uc2 And then of course, on the other side of the aisle, there was a misstep by Republican Senator Kennedy from Louisiana who described her as articulate as if that is somehow you know, surprising for a star debater Harvard, Harvard Law grad who has been a Federal Judge for a decade.\par \par \uc2 But I think that`s going to be interesting to see how all of this, you know, what we saw with a powerful questioning from Senator Booker and then that misstep by Senator Kennedy, how that affects some of the tone of today`s questioning?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, we know the numbers are in her favor. You know, she certainly is going to be confirmed. Do you think this is still the best way to do this, guys, knowing what the outcome? We already know what the outcome is going to be. It seems to lend itself to a lot of grandstanding.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: That`s always been the case. I mean, Robert knows that news as well as anybody. I mean, it`s always more about hearing the senators talk, but we do, the American people do get to see Judge Jackson and it is like that that`s really valuable. I think, Bob, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Gayle, it is the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . I`m never surprised to see a little grand standing in this chamber.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That sort of sums it up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Spot on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very accurately, I should say. Spot on. Thank you, Bob. Thank you, Jan.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you Jan and Robert. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 You can follow the confirmation hearings for Judge Jackson throughout the day on CBS News streaming.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, Tennis great, that is Venus Williams will talk with us about a new push to help women get equal pay. She says it is especially important for Black women.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:11. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, another ex-child star is freed from her conservatorship. After nearly nine years, a Judge says that Amanda Bynes can now take care of herself, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 See why this story is not quite the same as Britney Spears` very long legal fight. It`s a story though. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Amanda Show.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: America got to know Amanda Bynes as a child star on shows like "All That" and "The Amanda Show" on Nickelodeon, a division of Paramount. Now, she is 35 and a judge has just released her from a nearly nine-year long conservatorship. Bynes says she feels grateful and excited about her future.\par \par \uc2 This case follows last year`s highly publicized end of Britney Spears` conservatorship.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti shows us how Bynes` situation was much different.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "All That.")\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Former child actress, Amanda Bynes, was released from a court conservatorship, Tuesday, which controlled her life for nearly nine years.\par \par \uc2 A Judge granted Bynes` request to end it saying, "She has done everything the Court has asked over a long period of time."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Amanda Bynes is free.\par \par \uc2 GROUP: Yay.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Woo.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Although Bynes was not present at the hearing, some of her former "All That" costars were there and celebrated the decision.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY KNOWINGS (Former costar): Amanda has brought joy to so many people throughout her career.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: In a statement, Bynes said: "I would like to thank my fans, my lawyer, and my parents for their support. I`ve been working hard to improve my health so that I can live and work independently and I will continue to prioritize my wellbeing in this next chapter."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "All That.")\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: By age 13, Bynes` headlined her own show on Nickelodeon, and she quickly graduated on to bigger roles in movies like "She`s the Man."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "She the Man.")\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And "Easy A."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Easy A.")\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: But she has not acted since.\par \par \uc2 In 2012 and 2013, she had a series of run-ins with the law. She was arrested and accused of driving under the influence and possession of drugs. She was also held involuntarily in a psychiatric hospital, which ultimately led to her being ordered into a conservatorship controlled by her mother.\par \par \uc2 SARAH J. WENTZ (Fox Rothschild`s attorney): I think it is interesting to everyone that both the Amanda Bynes and the Britney Spears` conservatorship came about from a psychiatric hold initially.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Attorney Sarah Wentz specializes in conservatorships.\par \par \uc2 When you look at Britney Spears and Amanda Bynes, the beginnings of their conservatorship were very similar, but the way they ended vastly different.\par \par \uc2 SARAH J. WENTZ: Yes, vastly different and I think that was because of the support of Amanda Bynes` parents. I think that part of that was that in the Britney case, you had people that were making money and profiting from the conservatorship.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Amanda Bynes` mother, Lynn, says she is thrilled that the ruling according to her attorney, Tamar Arminak.\par \par \uc2 TAMAR ARMINAK (Attorney): She is looking forward to resuming her relationship with Amanda, as a mother and daughter, not a conservator and conservatee.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: There are about 1.3 million active adult guardianship or conservatorship cases in the U.S., and that figure is based on the handful of states that track those numbers.\par \par \uc2 How does the future of a conservatorship look because of these very high profile cases?\par \par \uc2 SARAH J. WENTZ: I think that maybe the Courts need to look at whether or not they handle emergency situations where there is a mental health crisis, different form of long-term diagnosis of dementia, for example. There is nothing to show that it`s going to be ongoing.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: For CBS MORNINGS, Jonathan Vigliotti, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It looks like there is a happy ending to that story. She thanked her lawyer, her fans, and her parents at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Britney story, you know, is still going on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good for Amanda.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It feels like this is the way it`s supposed to work.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And as opposed to the way it worked for Britney, which is the way it`s not supposed to work because people were profiting off of Britney Spears and it doesn`t sound like that was happening here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For me, I hear these stories and I keep going back to the impact of the industry that it has on children`s stars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And how they deal with so much that they need help and they end up having situations like this as adults.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. And here, apparently her mom is a big supporter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It helps. That helps.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, transgender rights are a hot topic in many states right now. The groundbreaking success of college swimmer, Lia Thomas raises some important questions, but some new laws would block her from competing against other women.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, activist Raquel Willis talks with us about her new TV series that shows us what it`s like to be transgender in America. Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, tennis legend and overall Superwoman, Venus Williams is ready to talk with us about equal pay for women, and also, what about the Oscars and "King Richard"? She is going to enjoy that and we`re going to enjoy her.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a very perfect song for this segment. Let`s give them something to talk about. We call this "Talk of the Table" because it`s trying to bring you some of the stories that we like to share with you.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, you`re in pole position.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am in pole position. So my "Talk of the Table" is about a dog who accidentally caused quite a mix up.\par \par \uc2 Rosie is her name. She plopped her belly on her owner`s phone after the man known as Rye fell asleep while texting his fiancee Sunday night. Now, you could see the dog was able to text some gibberish before sending the phrase "The brain hurts."\par \par \uc2 Well, Rye, the fiancee thought that Rye suffered a stroke texting back, "Are you okay?" "Baby?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the fiancee who is out of town called 9/11 because you see the response from Rosie. It says, "N." no. Okay, so she called the cops to go check on him.\par \par \uc2 He was fine of course and explained the confusion to the officer. Rye then tweeted a photo of the naughty dog labeled the culprit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a cool picture.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I should just point out that they did everything right. The C.D.C. says every minute counts. If you think someone is having a stroke, call the police. So they did everything right. It`s Rosie who should not be lying on a phone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do we know how the dog did this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She apparently loves lying on electronics. She is always lying on any electronic thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was an accident.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was an accident.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s a dog version of a butt dial.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a dog dial.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, a dog dial. There you go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You got it, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was good. All right, so I have something pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I heard this from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It will make you smile, that`s for sure. My "Talk of the Table" is a little girl. Her adorable reaction to seeing her grandmother after -- check this out -- only one night apart.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Purity Nderitu via Storyful.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now she was told we`re going to get grandma in 24 hours. Don`t worry. We`ll see her in a day. But it was as if she hasn`t seen grandma in years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I know. But is there anything better, honestly, than children`s laughter?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That laughter. I heard it before I even saw the video. We were talking about it during the commercial on --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Pure joy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s bliss. We like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is that how little Luca is with you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope so. Right now, he is not saying much, but I hope so.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s how you are when you see Luca.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Luca.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Luca. Luca baby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is so true. It`s ridiculous. I`m very proud of that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I won`t -- thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not apologizing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nothing with it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is tennis superstar, Venus Williams. Love her. This seven-time Grand Slam winner -- did you hear that? Seven times -- has fought for women to get equal pay throughout her legendary career.\par \par \uc2 Now, in honor of Women`s History Month, Williams and her company, EleVen are spearheading the privilege tax initiative to draw attention to the gender pay gap.\par \par \uc2 Listen to these numbers. White women in this country make 82 cents for every dollar men made in 2020 and the gap is even greater for women of color. Oh no.\par \par \uc2 During this campaign, people who buy something from participating companies can choose to donate a dollar at checkout 100 percent -- did you get that part -- 100 percent of the proceeds go to the nonprofit group, \uc2 Girls Inc\uc2 ., which works to create more opportunities for girls. It`s a great organization and recently, rightly honored Venus Williams, Tina Fey, and some others.\par \par \uc2 Venus Williams, it`s so good to see you. \uc2 Hello\uc2 . \uc2 Hello\uc2 . \uc2 Hello\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So you have been fighting this battle for --\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS (Tennis champion): Hi, good morning. Hi, everybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning. You`ve been fighting this fight for a very long time. Did you have a I`m-mad-as-hell-and-I`m-not-going-to-take-it-anymore- moment that you finally said: Okay, that`s enough. Because I`m thinking about the letter that you wrote back in the Wimbledon days to say "This ain`t right."\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: Yeah, I came into inequality at the young age of 16 when I played my first Grand Slam, and that`s a rude awakening. So I don`t want any other young women to have to face that. And you have the stats up there, women are earning 82 cents the dollar that men are earning. And if you`re minority, if you`re living out of the country of U.S.A. then it gets even worse.\par \par \uc2 So it is important to call attention to this that people know this because women not only need equal opportunity, but it gives them opportunity to grow and become strong and to help their families and all these things are so important.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you wrote back in 2006 to "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 "With power and status comes responsibility. Wimbledon has power and status. The time has come for them to do the right thing by paying men and women the same prize money." And you were the first woman, Venus, who got the same prize money as the man, Roger Federer at the time.\par \par \uc2 What were your thoughts back then when that happened? Did you say okay, finally, finally.\par \par \uc2 But we`re still having this conversation.\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: Exactly. It was a conversation we`ve been having for 30 years, and we`d been rejected over 30 years when we talked to the Grand Slam Committee.\par \par \uc2 So many amazing things have happened since that time. Recently, we saw women`s soccer garner equal pay. So that was super exciting time. Now, it`s Women`s History Month and so it is a very important time to talk about this.\par \par \uc2 And I partnered with my active wear brand, EleVen to create an initiative called Privilege Tax where we talk about this very subject. We have some amazing partners, like you had on the screen, Tracy Anderson, WTA charities, Captivate and I`ve also partnered with \uc2 Credit Karma\uc2  as well on this, to make sure that companies that are giving resources, time, education to their managers, and make sure they`re not hiring with biases, to make sure they`re giving leadership opportunities to women.\par \par \uc2 And I love that companies like \uc2 Credit Karma\uc2  have taken that opportunity to make that happen in their organization, but there has got to be more organizations. And people do want to do this, but it takes time. It doesn`t happen overnight. So that`s why we`re talking about it. So people know and organizations can get involved.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Venus, how you doing? Nate Burleson here. I have to say this as an \uc2 NFL\uc2  player, former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player, I would look up to you, because you stood for so much, so much more than your sport. You transcended tennis.\par \par \uc2 And now as a father of a young girl, I want to say I appreciate you, because you are one of the women that I point to and tell her you can accomplish anything, not just what people tell you, you can do.\par \par \uc2 But you also noted that that sexism isn`t just a woman`s issue. How can men get involved and help, you know, change what is this gap in payment in wage?\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: Absolutely, and I`m so excited to partner with \uc2 Girls Inc\uc2 ., a national organization that helps girls to be bold and strong, and girls need to know that they have the same opportunities and it is just so important that this happened. So I`m very excited, and I forgot part of the question now. So I was just thinking about it, I am so sorry.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How can men be allies?\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: What are we talking about?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, how can men be allies?\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: Oh, absolutely. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How can we get involved with helping close that gap?\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: Because that is an important question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re here to help, Venus.\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: That is very important, so thank you, I needed. It`s early for me. I`m not an early riser.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: It`s important because men like you said, men are the fathers and men are the husbands, or the brothers. Those are our moms. Those are our friends, those are our colleagues. Men are an integral part of this solution.\par \par \uc2 And at this moment, there are definitely more men in power than women. I just read a statistic that there`s eight percent of women that are CEOs of Fortune 500 companies.\par \par \uc2 So men, it`s important that men are a part of the solution and part of this conversation and actually taking a role and leading this, so it is so important and I love that you asked that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Venus, it`s Vlad. The pay gap is even worse. It`s more stark for Black women. How has your experience as a Black woman shape your perspective on this issue?\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: Absolutely. It is very true that that gap widens when you`re a Black woman, a woman of minority and it is important that we talk about that as well.\par \par \uc2 I`m just very happy that as an African-American woman that I can speak to this and make this known. So it`s a very important role that I never thought I`d play. I just wanted to win Wimbledon. I got there, it wasn`t equal and it has just led me to just this place where I`m able to do more than I ever could have thought I could do for myself.\par \par \uc2 It`s just been a dream to be a part of it to be honest.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you are still doing it. You know, the Oscars are Sunday night, Venus Williams. Who are you rooting for in the Best Actor category? Who are you rooting for?\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: Maybe Will Smith. Just maybe, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Will Smith said playing your dad was one of the great honors of his life and to sit at the table with you when he won a big award recently, he said was one of the highlights of his career.\par \par \uc2 I`m wondering if your dad has seen the movie and what he thought and if you`re going to be going to the Oscars?\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: Yes, I`m so happy to call Will not only the person who has memorialized my family story, but also my friend. And he just portrayed my dad so closely. They were moments. And you know all this was filmed during COVID. So there will be moments where we were listening to the livestream or watching the livestream being filmed, and I would hear my dad`s voice and it was so -- it gave me goosebumps how close he got to my father.\par \par \uc2 And of course we can`t forget and Aunjanue and how she played my mom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How she played your mom.\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: She got it so right. We cannot forget the conversation. Then the girls who learned to play tennis, that`s not easy to do. They learned and they made it realistic, and I`m so excited that we can share this moment at the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think they nailed it.\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: If we win.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Has your dad seen the movie? Did your dad see the movie? Did he watch?\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: You know my dad and I, we don`t talk a lot about tennis, but he is definitely really proud of what that has been said about the film. It`s weird, we don`t talk about work, but no he`s very proud.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Venus Williams. Thank you. We are always cheering you on. Thank you so much for coming on this morning.\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Transgender activist, that`s Raquel Willis has just arrived. She`s in our progressive green room. There she is, hey, Raquel.\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS ("Trans Youth Town Hall" Executive producer and host): Hi.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She has got a new TV series about that community that she wants to share, coming up. She`ll talk about creating a safe space for voices like hers.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:40. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There has been a very hot debate in the past year over transgender rights and some backlash in the area of women`s sports.\par \par \uc2 Swimmer Lia Thomas made headlines this month when she became the first openly transgender person to win a Division One National title.\par \par \uc2 Now, some women criticized her participation saying that she had an unfair advantage growing up as a biological male. The issue is playing out in state legislatures. Eleven states now have laws restricting transgender girls from playing female sports and this week, the Republican Governors of Utah and Indiana vetoed similar new restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Utah legislators are expected to override that veto.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A new three-part series highlights what it`s like to be transgender in America right now. It`s called "Trans Youth Town Hall." Executive producer and host, Raquel Willis interviews people of all ages about their experiences.\par \par \uc2 In this clip, the panelists describe what trans-joy means to them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My trans-joy is my art.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: When people know us as who we are and not what we are.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Trans-joy for me is all about the love and care that flows between friends, family, community, and me.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Definitely doing things I love with the people I love and everything is just happy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The series will air on Logo, a division of Paramount. Raquel Willis joins us right now. How are you doing? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Good morning. So glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning. Glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Town hall creates this safe space for conversations about the transgender community. How does it feel to be part of something like this?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: It feels beautiful. I mean, I think one of the most amazing parts of the series is that it sort of cuts through all of the fodder, all of the rhetoric that`s out there and talk directly to trans-youth. We are organizing, we are letting people empathize with their stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I think you know, this may sound silly, but I think many people need a Trans 101 class, if you will, Raquel.\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Could you just briefly explain exactly what it means? And tell us a little bit about your own personal story?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Absolutely. I mean, being trans, it`s really not identifying with the gender I was assigned at birth. So for instance, I was assigned male at birth. I am a woman. And it`s been a journey for me.\par \par \uc2 I think, we all have struggles around gender because there are so many expectations that we`re all kind of forced to meet. And for many of us, those boxes that were put in don`t fit for us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When did you realize for yourself? You said you were born a male. When did you realize that this isn`t really who I am?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Well, I --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How did you struggle with that?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Yes, well, I was assigned male at birth, and I really had a lot of struggles growing up, because I never quite fit in with all of the standards that were put out there for boys, essentially.\par \par \uc2 And so my mom, you know, is one of my biggest supporters. And when I told her I was trans and I was figuring out my gender identity. Once I was in college, she was like, oh, okay, this makes a lot of sense, because I`ve seen your journey along the years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, we hear you mention the word struggle quite a bit. And some of those struggles we hear about in the transgender community, it is violence and tragedy. But you decided in this Town Hall to bring up the words trans-joy, because you wanted to show some positivity within the community. Explain what trans-joy is?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Yes, I mean, trans-joy is really kind of the backbone of our community. A lot of people don`t know, trans-people have been around for centuries. This is not a new phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We just didn`t know. Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Absolutely. And so now we have more language than ever before and we know more about our history. Some of these legendary figures like Marsha P. Johnson, Sylvia Rivera, and so many more.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For education.\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Absolutely. So it`s about education. It`s about us communing together and understanding that there is something beautiful about knowing your truth and being able to fully stand in it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, one of the things that I thought was so great is the panel you have is knockout and you all cover a lot of different topics, including sports, including health. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Yes, well, I think one of the things that people will hear in the news right now, obviously, all the talk about the anti-trans legislation. But what does it really mean to be a young trans-person right now? And what are the things that trans-people need to access?\par \par \uc2 I mean, these young people are just like every other young person. They need to be able to have access to healthcare, to education, and to their passions, whether it`s sports, the arts, and beyond.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle mentioned earlier, Lia Thomas, the swimmer who just won a Division One title, I believe, and there are people on both sides saying support her, she can play any sport that she wants. And then there are other people saying, well, my daughter was a daughter -- she was a young lady since birth and she fought her whole life to compete, and now she`s competing with somebody who might have an unfair advantage. What are your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Well, I think we have to understand that what makes any athlete elite is different across the board. So there are a lot of these ideas around looking at certain factors of trans-people`s bodies, trans- people`s hormone levels all of these different things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s an advantage and what`s not.\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: But we have to be looking at people holistically, and I think one of the more important things for us to think about is what about access? When I think about young Black trans-athletes, have they had access to all of the training that maybe their White counterparts have been able to? So a lot of times we`re also thinking about class as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. What do you want people to take away real quick from the Town Hall?\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: Yes, well, I want people to understand that trans-people are just like every other person. You know, we`re all in our lives trying to figure out the best path forward.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Trying to live the best live.\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL WILLIS: And we all deserve to live our best life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Amen to that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are all trying to figure it out. That is so true.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you. Appreciate you bringing that joy to the show this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Raquel Willis, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 You can watch her Town Hall series on Logo. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On today`s CBS MORNINGS podcast, we`ll talk with Director McDowell about his new \uc2 Netflix\uc2  movie. What? "Windfall." You know we had his wife on yesterday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, that was amazing. Gayle, I`m digging that pink today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you tomorrow. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220414ei3n000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 How One School District Is Solving The Teacher Shortage\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Meg Oliver \par \uc2 528 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Today, Sacramento`s teachers went on strike in protest over low pay and more work due to a teacher shortage. Labor Department data shows there are more than 300,000 fewer teachers compared to before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 So, in tonight`s Eye on America, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver looks at what one Tennessee school district is doing to address the problem.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEMETRIUS WINN (Teacher): All right, here we go.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): At Kenwood Middle School, an hour north of Nashville, Tennessee...\par \par \uc2 DEMETRIUS WINN: The primary wave and the secondary wave.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: ... Demetrius Winn tries to explain seismic waves to this eighth grade class.\par \par \uc2 DEMETRIUS WINN: All right, so we know it`s 8:30 because this is our 8:30 block.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Two years ago, the 42-year-old father of four was spending his days mopping floors as a school custodian.\par \par \uc2 DEMETRIUS WINN: This is definitely a change.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Do you feel it`s a calling?\par \par \uc2 DEMETRIUS WINN: Oh, definitely. Definitely, it`s a calling, because, like I said, it was -- it was something I wanted to do. Just didn`t know how it was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Facing a growing teach shortage, the Clarksville-Montgomery County School District launched an innovative teacher residency program in 2018, recently registered as the first of its kind in the country.\par \par \uc2 special education Sean Impeartrice is the district`s chief academic officer.\par \par \uc2 If you didn`t have this program in place right now, how bad would the teacher shortage be?\par \par \uc2 SEAN IMPEARTRICE (Chief Academic Officer, Clarksville-Montgomery School System): We would have had about 150 teachers short.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The program squeezes a four-year degree into three. Night school is free, and they co-teach during the day with a mentor, earning up to $27,000 a year. When they graduate, their salary almost doubles.\par \par \uc2 How will that change your life?\par \par \uc2 DEMETRIUS WINN: Oh, it will open up a lot of opportunities for me.\par \par \uc2 OK, so here we go.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The program is funded through the district`s regular budget, state, and federal grants.\par \par \uc2 Is this a model the rest of the country could follow to solve the teacher shortage?\par \par \uc2 SEAN IMPEARTRICE: Now that it`s registered, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Would you give Mr. Winn an A?\par \par \uc2 Nice.\par \par \uc2 Can anyone tell me -- you feel good, right?\par \par \uc2 DEMETRIUS WINN: They`re generous.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Winn will graduate with a bachelor`s in education and a minor in in 2023.\par \par \uc2 How does the future look?\par \par \uc2 DEMETRIUS WINN: The future is bright. I know that, once I become a full- time teacher, that, like, the opportunities are endless.\par \par \uc2 The primary wave first hits.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Endless opportunities helping fulfill educational dreams.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Meg Oliver, CBS News, Clarksville, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, let`s support our teachers.\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead on tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS: big news for parents in the fight against COVID, what \uc2 Moderna\uc2  is saying about its vaccine for kids under 6 years old.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220327ei3n0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Google\uc2  Accused Of Bias\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Lilia Luciano, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A new lawsuit accuses tech giant, \uc2 Google\uc2 , of racial bias against its Black workers.\par \par \uc2 April Curley, a former \uc2 Google\uc2  employee claims in her lawsuit that the company steered Black hires toward lower paying jobs, using their knowledge while denying them chances to advance.\par \par \uc2 She says that she was fired after she spoke out.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano spoke to Curley about her experience working for \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 APRIL CURLEY (Former \uc2 Google\uc2  employee): It was my dream job.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: That dream for April Curley who joined \uc2 Google\uc2  in 2014 meant leading the change she wished to see in Big Tech.\par \par \uc2 APRIL CURLEY: My role was to engage with Historically Black Colleges and universities and recruit some of the brightest, most talented Black students.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And she did.\par \par \uc2 APRIL CURLEY: I brought over 500 talented qualified students to \uc2 Google\uc2  to be hired.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: But in a lawsuit she filed against \uc2 Google\uc2 , she claims, she and those student recruits were pigeon holed into dead-end jobs, received lower pay and were often dismissed as being not "Googly" enough.\par \par \uc2 What were they saying?\par \par \uc2 APRIL CURLEY: It is a fit test and it is to see if the wording normally is would you grab a beer with that person? And it is a very unfair metric.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: She claims in the suit that she and other Black employees endured micro and macro aggressions.\par \par \uc2 APRIL CURLEY: I was told that I was intimidating and that I was never tapped for leadership opportunities because my White managers said that they were afraid of me.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Curley says after she complained about her treatment, she was put on a performance review and ultimately lost her job.\par \par \uc2 CBS legal analyst, Rikki Klieman --\par \par \uc2 What should be most concerning to \uc2 Google\uc2  about what this case lays out?\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: The worst fact for \uc2 Google\uc2  on the face of the complaint is that they terminated her after she raised issues of racial discrimination. So it looks like it`s retaliatory.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Civil rights attorney, \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2  is representing Curley in the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP\uc2  (Attorney for April Curley): \uc2 Google\uc2  has been referred to as one of the greatest disruptors in the past century. Well, it is not enough to disrupt technology if you don`t also disrupt racism and discrimination.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Beyond the lawsuit, Reuters reported that California`s Department of Fair Employment and Housing last year launched its own investigation into the company`s treatment of Black women. In response, \uc2 Google\uc2  said they would " ... investigate any concerns to make sure our workplace is representative and equitable."\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the social justice protests of 2020, \uc2 Google\uc2  launched a multimillion dollar initiative to hire more Black workers. \uc2 Google\uc2  didn`t respond to a request for comment, but it told "The Washington Post" in the past it doesn`t agree with Curley`s description of her termination.\par \par \uc2 The company`s annual diversity report shows the number of Black employees hired went from 5.5 percent in 2020 to 8.8 percent last year, but Curley says, representation isn`t just about hiring, it is about retention.\par \par \uc2 And what does it do not having people of color in the room?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP\uc2 : Their core value number one, to work with great people. Well, great people include Black and Brown people.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: For CBS MORNINGS, Lilia Luciano, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So quite a story there, quite a lawsuit. There may be more to it. So Reuters, in an exclusive back in December, I spoke to seven current and former Black employees, female, at \uc2 Google\uc2  who all felt marginalized. The turnover rate is higher than other groups. And as Lilia mentioned, the Civil Rights regulator there in California is investigating. So things may be happening.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead in are "Changing the Game" series, Ava DuVernay talks about her barrier breaking career, changing Hollywood, and her advice for the next generation.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220331ei3m0015q\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CNET`s Dan Ackerman On Technology To Save You Time And Money\b0\par\par\uc2 Michelle Miller, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 1,738 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: As the work from home trend continues, there is a good chance many of you are thinking about some upgrades around the house.\par \par \uc2 CNET`s Dan Ackerman is here to help. Our resident tech guru has some smart home tech that could help you save some money and make your day a little bit brighter and he brought in what else? A toaster?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A toaster.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A toaster.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: A toaster?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN (CNET Editorial Director): If you`re going to go smart home, you`ve got to start with the small things, these little appliances.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: What does he do?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: All right, listen, I`m not going to lie. You don`t need a smart toaster, but if you`re going to get one, they`re kind of fun. This is the Revolution InstaGLO, and I love that it has this big giant touchscreen on the side, very bright, and then you pick what you`re doing.\par \par \uc2 You can get a waffle, you can get regular toast and then you select how dark you want it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pop tarts.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: There is a pop tart.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can go --\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: You can go super dark if you`re a maniac or you can just go get a regular for us normal folks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Just put the bread in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wait, wait.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: What did you bring?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I got the bread here from home. I don`t kid around. No, this is for real.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Wait, I`m going to go to the settings for that. Look, I have the settings.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is my normal toast at home. I wanted a perfect comparison.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Reach over.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I have a $25.00 --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Smuggling in toast for work.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: I am not saying this is going to be any better, but you get to see a picture.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have a $25.00 toaster at home.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: I`m going to go middle on that. How about that? I am going to hit the button. Oh there it goes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: And then, it`s got the timer. The timer is going to go down and we`ll come back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. We will talk about other things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So let`s move along. You`ve got a gadget that can help us start our day with some sunshine. So, what have you got for us?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Okay, I always wanted smart blinds. To me that sounds like a great idea. They just go up automatically. We`re not quite there yet. This SwitchBot, however, connects to your curtains like a little robot and it just pulls them this way, pulls them that way. You can have your Alexa do it, you can do it on an app.\par \par \uc2 And you just throw a couple of these on and we`re not in the future where all the blinds are perfect and smart and everything, but this is a good intermediate step and I love the concept.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, so we`re spending money. Can we save money with that plug over there?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: I use these on everything in my house that is not already a smart device. This is a TPLink Smart Plug. A lot of companies make these. You just plug your device into this, plug this into the wall and then again, Alexa or your \uc2 Google\uc2  Assistant or your app can turn stuff off and on.\par \par \uc2 A lot of these had Energy Monitoring built in so you can see from an app on your phone, how much power you`re using, and if you go outside and leave stuff on, you can just tell it, oh turn off that light, turn off that you know electronic thing I left plugged in.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That`s an upgraded clapper.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow. An upgraded clapper.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: It`s a verbal clapper. You just go, Alexa, do the clapper thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a clapper.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, what have we got on the toast here?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It is not ready yet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Working our way down.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: We`re still counting down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, we`re still counting down. Okay, so do you recommend the Amazon Blink Outdoor Security Camera?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: You know, I think we`re making a lot of progress here in these cameras, indoor ones, outdoor ones. We used to have to -- if you wanted the doorbell or a camera, you had to wire it in and that was a -- that was a no go for a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 Now, they are wireless and you can put a couple of batteries in this. It lasts for like two years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you can pop it up anywhere.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: ... with batteries. Just put some double-sided tape on the back. Stick it anywhere.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No wires.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t believe that.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Listen, I`ve got a place up in the country and bears come and go to the garbage occasionally and I literally got this to put outside. I have my kids, so I put it up there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you can sit in your office like Scarface and have cameras everywhere just looking at every angle of your house.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: If you choose to do that, that`s one way to use this. I`m just looking out for the bears.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Okay. Is the toast ready yet?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It smells delicious.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: IT does smell really good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You said this is something you might not need, but this is like about a want. These items are about want.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: You want to upgrade. You know, it`s a small indulgence, a small luxury and then you go, "I`ve got a smart toaster."\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: What does this set you back on?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Okay, this is where it gets a little -- 400 bucks.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Four hundred bucks?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And again --\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: But look at that cool color of touchscreen? It`s got a waffle mode.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It does have waffle mode.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It smells like it is burning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What setting did you have that on? I think you got that on --\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: I think I get a little aggressive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, we might want to pump that up because, I think you got that --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. No. No. It`s smart. Let it do its work.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: It`s going to go to final countdown. The final countdown is about to start.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re saying, it is smarter than me?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s smarter than all of us.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And those devices over there. How much is that?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: These are about $80.00 to $90.00, $15.00 to $20.00. These are about 70 to a hundred bucks.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, we got a pop and now we`ve got --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Pop it out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Five, four, three, two, one.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Wait, wait.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What do you say?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s pretty good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know if it is better than my --\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Thirty dollar toaster?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know if it is better than my Target toaster, my $25.00 Oster toaster.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But look at the modes though. You have bread, waffles. You have Pop Tarts. You have all kinds of stuff in there.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Gluten free mode. There is a gluten free button on that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I thought we were doing a count down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A gluten free mode?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, you were counting down the toaster?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: The toaster has its own countdown.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, I thought you were --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You know what?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I thought we were going to commercial. That`s why I was five and --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Can I show you something?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You have this puppy, right? Okay, to do its thing, you know for nothing, with this 80 bucks for nothing. How about that?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: My arm.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: A child could come do that for you. A spouse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s pretty good. That`s pretty good.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: But sometimes, you just want a little technology help to open your curtains.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dan Ackerman, you are the best.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much for stopping by.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, I look like a maniac.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You`re King.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Tony, would you keep this puppy on your counter?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would not. I believe the toaster is like a utensil and it goes in a drawer. I want it off the counter.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wait, wait. Did you have that piece of bread in your inside pocket the entire show?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have a pop tart in that jacket right now. I know. Nate`s got a slice of pizza in his wallet.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Whose is that?\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: This is -- I am low carb, so this one is not for me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Low carbon and. What? You put your finger prints on it, man.\par \par \uc2 DAN ACKERMAN: I`m fine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`ll do it for us. We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Thank you for watching. Enjoy that bread.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220316ei2h000um\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Why Americans Are Quitting Their Jobs In Record Numbers\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Jill Schlesinger \par \uc2 2,511 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Seinfeld.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s George Costanza from "Seinfeld" quitting his job in frustration. You know what? He`s not alone.\par \par \uc2 In today`s "Money Watch" we`re looking at what`s driving millions of Americans to leave their jobs.\par \par \uc2 A new government report shows that 4.3 million people quit in December. That`s down slightly from a high of four and a half million in November. CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  joins us right now. Dollar, dollar Jill, you all, how are you doing?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so let`s talk about this great resignation. We`ve been speaking on this for last several months. Do you think that there is a change coming soon?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, we know that the number peaked in November. It was down slightly in December. But when you have more than four million people quitting every month, month after month, that does not seem to be going away anytime soon. We`ll have to see.\par \par \uc2 We`re sort of still in the beginning stages of this big labor market change.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, we`re all calling it The Great Resignation, but you`re giving it a new term.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s that?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I think it`s the reshuffling because it`s not as if someone goes in and says, you know, like Costanza, I quit and they don`t have another job. Most of these people are going from one job to another. So they are job changers.\par \par \uc2 And we see this really early in the COVID recession. People said: Hey, I`m a frontline worker. I work, let`s say, in hospitality. It`s kind of scary. Maybe I`ll go to a job with better pay, with benefits, and maybe with a regular scheduled like warehousing.\par \par \uc2 If you look at like right before the COVID recession began in February of 2020, through the end of last year, leisure and hospitality lost 1.2 million jobs. Meanwhile, warehousing up more than 200,000 jobs. So that`s a reshuffling more than a flat out I quit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How is inflation impacting the reshuffling?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, I think that when people are not making enough money to keep up with prices, they`re saying: Wow, I`ve got to do something. So this is a real big issue. Like think about this. If you even just go back to that leisure and hospitality, if you`re not paying enough to get to seven percent better, then you`re behind.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, if we look at the Labor Department report from last December, we say: Hey, people after you factor in inflation, guess what? These are actually down. Meanwhile, leisure and hospitality wants all those people back. Wages up about 16 percent from a year ago, and warehousing up eight percent, so they are keeping track with inflation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So George Costanza didn`t really walk away gracefully? There`s a lot of people quitting their jobs resigning or retiring. There`s one big name recently we`ve been talking about, is there a way to do it the right way?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Not like George Costanza, and with all due respect to our friends in New England, I don`t think the way that Tom Brady did it was great. I mean, you`ve got to thank the team you played for, for 20 years, the company.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The company you worked for, right? Twenty years.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Right. So you want to be gracious, you want to say, look, thank you so much for this opportunity. You want to say, let`s stay in touch. Let`s stay connected on LinkedIn. Let`s do all of that.\par \par \uc2 And you want to walk away with your head held high. Give your two weeks` notice. Be a team player to the end.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You don`t want to burn any bridges is what you`re saying? Right?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : No. Definitely not.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we still have a lot more to talk about, because I want to get your idea on how people can walk away and resign or quit their job without burning those bridges?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Next time, we`ll talk about it.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Next time. All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dollar, dollar Jill, you all. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up first on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Washington Commanders` head coach, Ron Rivera talks with us about the \uc2 NFL\uc2  team`s new name and his push to change the culture of that franchise.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Picking up the pieces.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Picking up the pieces.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Special late edition of "What to Watch." if you schedule your morning around "What to Watch," you`re extremely late for work right now, so get out the door. But we`ve got you. We`ve got you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve got you. We`ve got you. We`ve got you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I`m here to deliver the news that you all have been waiting for on "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 So let`s begin with the fallout over Whoopi Goldberg`s Holocaust comment.\par \par \uc2 ABC News announced last night that Goldberg is suspended from "The View" for two weeks. Goldberg apologized on yesterday`s show for saying a day earlier that the Holocaust was not about race, but about man`s inhumanity to man. Those remarks were widely condemned. Critics pointed out the Nazis did in fact consider Jews to be an inferior race. The controversy follows a documented rise in anti-Semitism.\par \par \uc2 The President of ABC News said in part: "While Whoopi has apologized, I`ve asked her to take time to reflect and learn about the impact of her comments." I think it`s important to point out too, that Jonathan Greenblatt, the head of the ADL was on CNN last night with Don Lemon, and he said he accepted her apology.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that he talked about the concept of redemption in Judaism, which is a not a concept unique to Judaism. But he said this, "It means all of us have the power to admit when we do wrong and to commit to doing better. I heard Whoopi say she`s committed to doing better. I accept the apology with sincerity she delivered."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we should also point out that he was also on "The View" yesterday at Whoopi`s invitation because he wanted to continue the conversation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He likes to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She has made it clear, she is very sorry for how this has turned out and what she said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Like Nick Cannon`s idea not of cancel culture, but of counsel culture.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Counsel --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Counsel culture, which I thought was a really interesting concept. Counsel, meaning to help somebody understand the error of their ways and to learn from learn it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, learn and reflect.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. All right, don`t know what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I thought I would escape this in my career because I didn`t go through local news, so I never had to cover Punxsutawney Phil.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I have covered him. I have covered him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We need to find one of those clips in the closer on Friday. We have some breaking news. I can`t believe I am saying this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Please don`t. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We have some breaking news from Groundhog Day from Gobblers Knob, home of Punxsutawney Phil. Did the famous groundhog see a shadow this morning?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did he? Did he? Did he?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He did. He did, which means six more weeks of winter according to folklore, when Phil sees a shadow, winter does not end early, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Brings back memories?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does Phil even know that he is Phil? Don`t they just pull up some random rodent and say --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a very existential question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you see your shadow?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t think he knows -- those are all real beards?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He doesn`t know what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. No, he does not. But I like the tradition of it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know, I am still driving a pickup truck in the movies.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. Let me share this. This is really cool. You might recognize the new number one song for Hot 100. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Encanto.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So that is "We don`t talk about Bruno" from the movie "Encanto" which is number one. It`s only the second time that a song from a \uc2 Disney\uc2  animated movie has topped 100 chart. The first time was back in 1993 with "A Whole New World" from "Aladdin."\par \par \uc2 "We don`t talk about Bruno" is also the first song by "Hamilton" creator Lin Manuel Miranda to hit number one. He, of course wrote the music for "Encanto."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did. You know, it`s the first time that I now want to see a movie based on a song. I saw an interview with Lin where he said everybody has a family member where we go, we don`t talk about. We don`t talk about Tony.\par \par \uc2 Everybody has a family member like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And now, they are singing it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why you`ve got to say Tony`s name?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony is just looking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I said we don`t talk about Nate. We don`t talk about Tony.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, Jericka`s daughter was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We don`t talk about Patti. We don`t talk about --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a great soundtrack though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Every kid -- every kid knows it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a great soundtrack.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got to go. Coming up -- we`ve got to go.\par \par \uc2 You`re local news is coming up.\par \par \uc2 Next, we`ve got another great story from Vlad. Don`t leave.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Adele.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is Adele. This is "Talk of the Table" and Gayle is singing along first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because I love all things Adele. I love her. So my "Talk of the Table" is about Adele. A week and a half after pulling all the shows in Vegas. Remember, she said the show wasn`t ready. There were some COVID issues.\par \par \uc2 Adele says she will perform in London at the BRIT Awards and she has tweeted about this. She says: "I`m really happy to say that I`m performing at the BRIT`s next week, and I`ll also be popping in to see Graham for a chat on the couch while I`m in town." Graham Norton is a very, very famous chat show host there. That`s what they call them.\par \par \uc2 And she also seemed to shoot down a little bit of gossip because at the very end she says: "Oh, and Rich sends his love."\par \par \uc2 She says she is looking forward to it because heard all the chatter that there are problems in paradise with Rich Paul and Adele and Adele wants to make that clear that that`s not true. And I think Rich Paul is also saying that`s not true.\par \par \uc2 You know how people in the streets just talk about stuff they know nothing about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They know zero about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They know zero about, but the good news is, she is going to sing again. Hopefully fingers crossed that this will work out in Vegas, who knows, hope so. That she and Rich -- when she said, "Rich sends his love" or Rich says hi making it clear, we`re still together. So back-back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In other words, mind your own business.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mind your own business, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re doing just fine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, I`ll jump right in. I think I`ll take this from here. We`ve been talking about there are too many jobs and not enough workers. So my "Talk of the Table" is Home Depot`s new strategy to fill those jobs, and they announced that that`s exactly what they plan to do, and they`re talking about filling 100,000 jobs coming up.\par \par \uc2 And they`re saying that you can be hired within a day after applying, which is great because a lot of people are spending time at home. So there`s a lot of home improvement going on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, a lot of people walking around \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  like, I don`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You and me, both.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You want that orange apron.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You want somebody to come up to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where is the apron?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll take a random dude in an orange shirt. Like can you help me?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are you contractor? You look like you know what you`re doing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Nate says, no, I don`t work here, sir.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I am looking for a guy shopping there with were calluses on his hands and say: Hey, man, I`m trying to fix something at home.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Work gloves in the back pocket. Which one of this should I get?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re saying you can apply and then the next day you could get hired?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And then the next day and they`re talking about filling spaces, flexible full time, part time, customer service, sales, store support, freight merchandising, and warehouse positions, which is great. So shout out to \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 . Yes, yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So it is actually a great job. My uncle was a mail carrier for like 35 years and then when he retired, his dream job was \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, word.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Helping people out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uncle who?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Helping people out using your hands.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Uncle Jimmy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uncle Jimmy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shout out to Uncle Jimmy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220211ei22000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Voting Rights Bill Fails; The President`s First Year; Escalating Russia Tension; Desperate COVID Battle; George Floyd Civil Rights Trial; Havana Syndrome Report; The Vice President`s View; Quitting Grac efully; Farewell Yellow Brick Road; A Jurassic Giant, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Scott Macfarlane, Ed  O`Keefe, Carter Evans, Mola Lenghi, Anthony Mason, Christina Ruffini,  Catherine Herridge, Vladimir Duthiers, Joel Payne, Charlie D`Agata \par \uc2 13,722 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 January 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS on this Thursday. I`m Gayle King,\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We are all here. Let`s go to today`s Eye Opener. It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Did you over promise in your first year in office?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): I didn`t over promise, but I have probably outperformed what anybody thought would happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: President Biden strikes a defiant tone at a news conference to mark one year since he first took office.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A new voting rights bill fails in the Senate after two Democrats join Republicans and refused to push it through.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WY): The \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  has never been able to end debate on legislation with a simple majority vote.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The U.S. military has released new video of a drone strike that mistakenly killed 10 Afghan civilians including seven children.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`re inside an overwhelmed hospital in Missouri, a state where the vaccination rate is low and the COVID cases are skyrocketing.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN SOLLARS (Nurse): We`re all going to need therapy for the next decade to handle this.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Jury selection starts in the trial for three former police officers involved in the death of George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Rapper, Cardi B is offering to pay funeral expenses for all 17 victims of a Bronx apartment fire that happened earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s just a regular day in New York City. \uc2 Bill Murray\uc2 , just stopping by to sing "I feel pretty."\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show with James Corden" host): President Biden gets his first solo press conference in months, one day before his presidency hits the one year mark. At one point, Biden said he didn`t expect so much Republican pushback to his agenda. I mean, to be fair, we should give the man some time to figure out how Washington works. He`s only been there for 48 years.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But there`s always new lessons to learn in Washington.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I know. That was an interesting talk though from the President. I mean, during the Obama years when he was Vice President, a big theme was obstructionism.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One year in the book. Now, year two begins.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It does.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can we just say something about \uc2 Bill Murray\uc2 , who we just saw there in the Eye Opener who was here yesterday. So, did he leave here and then they go over to the park?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Clearly, he did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And started singing?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Made good on his statement that he is unafraid and unaware when it comes to signing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When he sings.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Keep singing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You go, \uc2 Bill Murray\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. We`ve got a lot to discuss today, including Happy Anniversary to the Biden administration because it was exactly one year into the Biden presidency today.\par \par \uc2 So we have a conversation with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  after another tough year for Americans during this pandemic. We talked about what she thinks the administration has accomplished, and the vast amount that she acknowledges still needs to be done.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we have something good for you only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. Anthony Mason speaks with Sir Elton John.\par \par \uc2 (Video clip of Elton John performance.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: After years of delays, the singer kicked off the final leg of his Farewell Tour last night in New Orleans. That`s right. He`s actually retiring. He tells Anthony how it feels after more than 50 years on the road.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but he has a three-year retirement plan.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say, I`ll believe it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: COVID has delayed it a bit. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ll believe the retirement when I see it.\par \par \uc2 All right, but we`re going to begin with a major setback for Democrats on Capitol Hill. What would have been their landmark voting rights bill failed 52 to 48 in the Senate last night with two Democrats joining all 50 Republicans in opposing a rules change to push it through.\par \par \uc2 The vote came after hours of passionate speeches from both sides of the aisle. And in the statement, President Biden reacted saying, quote, he is, " ... profoundly disappointed that the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  has failed to stand up for our democracy," closed quote.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane was inside the chamber and he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Scott, this is a stinging defeat for Democrats. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Tony. Good morning. Supporters said this voting rights law would have made it easier to vote and stop suppression at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 Critics said it was a power grab to rewrite the rule books, but in the end, it was Senate Democrats here at the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  who couldn`t wrangle enough votes to make it law.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: On this vote, the yays are 52, the nays are 48.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: President Biden`s landmark voting legislation failed in the Senate late Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): This is a moral moment in America --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Because after nearly 12 hours of heated debate over changing filibuster rules to get the bill through --\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY): They would shatter the soul of the Senate for short term power.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JACKY ROSEN\uc2  (D-NV): This is an unprecedented coordinated attack to make voting harder for eligible citizens.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Facing a wall of unanimous Republican opposition, Democrats were dealt back-to-back losses without being able to change Senate procedures, eliminate the filibuster, and pass sweeping voting rights law.\par \par \uc2 One of the holdouts, West \uc2 Virginia`s Joe Manchin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): Eliminating the filibuster would be the easy way out. IT wasn`t meant to be easy.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The proposed voting legislation would have allowed for early voting and no excuse mail-in voting nationwide, and it would have made Election Day a Federal Holiday.\par \par \uc2 Two of the Senate`s three black members clashed over accusations that any opposition was an attempt to deny minorities the vote.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I felt frustration and irritation rising in my soul, as I keep hearing the references to Jim Crow. I asked myself how many Americans understand what Jim Crow was.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER\uc2  (D-NJ): Don`t lecture me about Jim Crow. I know this is not 1965. That`s what makes me feel outraged. It`s 2022 and they are blatantly removing more polling places from the counties where black and Latinos are over represented.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Members of the Congressional Black Caucus took a last minute stand outside the Senate, but walked away knowing the Voting Rights Law was set to fail.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , that pivotal senator from West Virginia said yesterday he is open to changing the Electoral College Count Act, the law by which Congress counts the electoral votes after a presidential election. That was the process interrupted by the Capitol attack, January 6th.\par \par \uc2 But the outlook for that is cloudy. Democrats have been emphatic. They want much bigger change -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Cloudy, normally never a good thing. Thank you very much, Scott.\par \par \uc2 Now the vote came just hours after a marathon news conference by President Biden to mark one year since his Inauguration. He took questions for nearly two hours.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is at the White House with more on the story.\par \par \uc2 Ed, it was one of the longest news conferences people have seen. Clearly, he had a lot to say.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: He sure did. He sure did, Gayle. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 He took dozens of questions on issues ranging from the pandemic to the future of his legislative agenda. But on that issue of voting rights, he became the second consecutive American President to raise concerns about future election 