e military drills in Belarus.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, CBS News confirmed the U.S. has given permission for \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies to rush additional American made weapons to Ukraine. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is in Germany today meeting with European leaders trying to get everyone on the same page about sanctions. Then he will come here to Geneva where even though tensions are high, he is already trying to set the bar low, saying not to expect any major breakthroughs -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Christina Ruffini for us in Geneva. Christina, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We have new information on a high stakes mystery we`ve been covering for years now. The C.I.A. says that most cases of the neurological illness known Havana Syndrome -- remember that -- were not -- were not caused by a foreign adversary.\par \par \uc2 Senior investigative correspondent, Catherine Herridge has the latest on all this. Catherine. I think this was surprising news for a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Tony, it was for many.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The C.I.A. has released new interim findings overnight on hundreds of reported cases of Havana Syndrome, ultimately ruling out Russia, Cuba, or any other foreign group as the cause of most of these illnesses.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the majority of reports can be reasonably explained my medical conditions or environmental and technical factors, including previously undiagnosed illnesses. After a cluster of cases was first reported in Cuba, U.S. diplomats and personnel stationed overseas said they experienced Havana Syndrome symptoms, including in China, Austria, and Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 Some told CBS News they heard ringing or loud piercing sounds and felt intense pressure in the face with pain, nausea, and dizziness. A recent study conducted for the State Department found the most plausible cause was directed pulsed radio frequency energy.\par \par 