y and do the best of and that`ll be what I`m listening to as I chop the vegetables for dinner tonight.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and we oftentimes see a different side.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Of whoever he is talking to.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Anthony doesn`t miss. He just pulls something out of them that we haven`t heard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. It`s always a little deeper. I know nothing going in, but I learn --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I like anything Elton John. I really want to go to one of those shows before it`s all over. I think he`s a great showman.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I want that jacket.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where are you going?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s making jackets out of my ties.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The local news is coming up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re looking at a shot of the White House and with good reason. We welcome you back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS because today marks one year since President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  took office.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference yesterday, the President announced Harris will be on the ticket with him in 2024 if he decides to run again. Now, that`s the first time that we`ve heard that they could run together for re-election.\par \par \uc2 Harris`s vice presidency has been historic, as you know, because she`s the first woman and the first person of color to serve in this position. She joined us earlier this morning to discuss the highs and lows of the last year.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now Vice President Harris joins us. Good morning to you, Madam Vice President, it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (United States Vice President): Good morning, Gayle. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, I know it was a very long night in the Senate last night. As expected, the voting rights legislation did not pass last night, and in part because of two of your own party members. But the President says he can take action to protect the voting rights. What specifically are you going to do?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : Well, thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 First of all, it`s really important for everyone to understand what`s at stake. Around our country, there have been purposeful and direct attempts to make it more difficult for people to vote, access to the ballot. And so that`s the issue when we talk about voting rights, attacks on our democracy.\par \par \uc2 If you boil it down to it, one of the specific issues is that folks are making it -- people in elected offices in states are making it more difficult for their citizens to vote. So for example, they are making it more difficult for people to vote by mail.\par \par \uc2 Well, think about folks who have disabilities, parents who have, you know, obligations every day who work two or three shifts, voting by mail is the best way they can vote.\par \par \uc2 They are passing laws, eliminating or reducing the number of drop boxes. Again, if you`re a single parent, a mom or dad, you`ve got those three kids in the backseat and you want to vote and it is much easier for you to fill out your ballot at home and drop it -- drive by, drop it in the drop box than to have those kids stand with you in line.\par \par \uc2 What we need to do around --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we`ve heard the case Madam Vice President, so what are you going to do? I mean, you all have laid out the case very, very nicely about what`s at stake. And now we`re here and it didn`t pass.\par \par \uc2 So what will you do?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : Well, what we will do is we will keep fighting to get the legislation passed, because that is critical. So we are not giving up on that, and then it is a matter of continuing to do the work of executive orders, doing the work through the Department of Justice, which has been litigating these cases in the various states, because we believe that they are a violation of the spirit of the Constitution of the United States.\par \par \uc2 It is going to be about continuing to elevate this conversation, because Gayle, frankly, you`d be surprised how many people in the burden of everyday life are unaware of what`s happening in these States.\par \par \uc2 But when they become aware of laws that make it illegal to give people food and water, if they`re standing in line, they`re outraged.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We heard the President say that he intends to get out of the bubble of the White House and go and take the message directly to the people. But at this point, I know it`s only one year, it`s your one year anniversary today. You`ve got three years to go, but the polling is not good so far.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling out today shows only 44 percent of Americans approve of your job, in particular, that you all have failed to pass the Build Back Better, voting rights, and police reform. What`s your new strategy to get this done?\par \par \uc2 And I know you`ve got three years to go, what`s the new strategy?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : So, well, let`s just say it to your -- the point, I think, the spirit of your question, polls go up and down, but we have to remain consistent in fighting for the American people and their needs, and so that is the strategy about staying focused.\par \par \uc2 I`m not going to give up on the issue of affordable childcare.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you do that without Congress?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : I know that many parents.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Madam Vice President, can you do that without Congress?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : You know, I will tell you, and you`ll appreciate this, Gayle, last week, I was in Atlanta, and I had a visit with Ambassador Andy Young, who is probably one of the last living members of those leaders who marched with and advised, Dr. King.\par \par \uc2 And he said to me, you know, we had ups and downs in the fight for civil rights and for progress in this country, but you can`t give up. And you can`t give up on the spirit of the American people to want things to be better.\par \par \uc2 And that`s how I feel. I take that seriously.\par \par \uc2 So yes, we`re not going to give up on the affordable childcare.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all do. We all do.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : We`re not going to give up on some of these issues that really get to the heart of what people need and they want -- they want solutions. They want things to be fixed. They want to enjoy their families and not have to worry about some of these issues.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s turn to Ukraine and Russia because I`m wondering if you all are having a cleanup in aisle three in the State Department today where the President raised a couple of eyebrows when he seemed to make the distinction between a minor incursion and an all-out invasion.\par \par \uc2 And so the people in Ukraine today, we understand, are very upset with that, because they believe that the President has given -- Biden -- has given rather, President Putin the greenlight. What do you say to that? Because it does seem to be very confusing.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : So the President, let me just first tell you, I start every morning reading the presidential daily briefing, which contains classified information about threats to our National Security and hotspots around the world.\par \par \uc2 I`m often in meetings in the Situation Room, where the President and I and members of our leadership of our military, our Intelligence Community convene on these issues.\par \par \uc2 The President of the United States, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been very clear on the subject of Ukraine, which is that if Russia takes aggressive action, there will be severe and swift consequences, that there will be a price to pay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He didn`t seem to say that yesterday, though, at the news conference, that`s why there is some -- that`s why I think clarification is needed.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : Well, we are very clear, and we`ve been very clear with Putin that any aggressive action taken by Russia will meet a cost -- a severe cost.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s talk about COVID because you all ran on, we`re going to get COVID under control, we are going to get this lit. And as we sit here today, as you know, we`re going into year three, we are still talking about COVID.\par \par \uc2 You all admit it, listen, we`ve made some mistakes, we`re going to do better, we`re going to be better.\par \par \uc2 The schools, can the kids go to schools? In New Mexico, the National Guard is being asked to be substitute teachers, all because of COVID. So it is not under control. So I`m wondering, what is your strategy here? And have you come to the point -- have you come to the point that we`ve all got to figure out how to live with COVID?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : So I`m going to try to unpack the various ...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Unpack it -- yes, I know, there is a lot. But this is --\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : ... important points that you`ve made. No, but they are important points.\par \par \uc2 So let`s start with our children, okay, which should always be where we start, frankly. If you look at where we were last year, where we were certainly at the beginning of this pandemic, we know that comparing them to now, we`ve seen great progress, we`re over 95 percent of our schools are open.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : We now have vaccines for our children who are over the age of five. So we have seen great progress. We, as an administration put $310 billion into the Schools of America to help upgrade ventilation systems and make sure that they have the resources they need to keep both our children and their teachers safe in the school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People will say, Madam Vice President, it`s not enough. And have you reached the point that you have to say, we`ve got to figure out a way to live with this? Or do you still believe we can get this under control?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : There is still more work to be done. We started out with very few people getting vaccinated. And we now have 210 million Americans who have been vaccinated. We need everyone to take seriously and take advantage of the tools that are available to them.\par \par \uc2 You know when I -- I will tell you, Gayle, that one of the thing that inspires me, about this moment in the midst of everything is the incredible power of the American people to take advantage of the tools that are available to them and say that they`re not going to let things happen to them. They`re going to actually do what they have in their power, and that includes getting vaccinated includes wearing masks.\par \par \uc2 It includes doing the work that we must do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is still more work to do.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : Absolutely, absolutely, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, the White House admits they are just as frustrated as we all are with COVID. But the bottom line is what you -- what are you going to do? What are you going to do?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and the same is true when it comes to international affairs and the situation in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t think Americans have an appetite for troops on the ground there, and so it`s got to be sanctions and it doesn`t seem like Putin cares about sanctions. So what are they going to do?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And on the other hand, people say, look, this is only one year, it`s in the middle of a pandemic. There`s three years to go. There`s still work to be done and they want to do the work.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know, but there`s that midterm vote.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Up next, Vlad Duthiers has got the stories we`ll be talking about.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mr. Duthiers. This is a jazzy kind of --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Some Jay-Z.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Like a whiskey bar kind of action.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s your point, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good to see you all. I want to begin with a really, really interesting story and I want your reactions to it.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with people rallying around TV news anchor Michelle Li.\par \par \uc2 So here`s what happened. On New Year`s Day, she said on the air during her morning show that she eats dumpling soup to mark the holiday. That brought a racist response from one viewer who let the voice message that Li shared on social media and watch Michelle`s face that she`s listening to this. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I don`t think it was appropriate that she said that and she was being very Asian and I don`t know. She can keep her Korean to herself.\par \par \uc2 All right, sorry --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Michelle is a longtime anchor at KSDK TV in St. Louis and she said the comment about being very Asian made her upset as you can imagine.\par \par \uc2 She then went on the "Ellen" show yesterday and she called the whole incident a gift because her phone blew up with messages of support. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LI (KSDK TV anchor): It just felt like the world saw it and the world stood up to it. You know not just the St. Louis community. I mean the St. Louis community came out very strong, but who gets to say that the world stood up for you in a moment?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So her story created the hashtag, #VeryAsian. She and Gia Vang, who is another news anchor, they started selling merchandise to raise money for the \uc2 Asian-American Journalists Association\uc2 , which they`ve raised thousands of dollars for.\par \par \uc2 She actually spoke to the woman afterwards.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She did?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to hear what she said.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Spoke to the woman and the woman apologized, and Michelle accepted her apology and she hasn`t shared her name or any details on who she is because she doesn`t want the world going after this woman.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s very nice of her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a private conversation.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s a private conversation. She says that she hopes to meet with her after the pandemic is over to talk about this. Still, a long way to go, but we all do those stories on morning shows, right?\par \par \uc2 Here is what people eat around the holidays. And Michelle is Korean American, she said this is what I eat and somebody left that mess.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was wondering though, how it was going to be a gift, and now I see and the fact that she spoke to the woman and good on her, she is not releasing her name.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because she doesn`t want that woman to get the hatred that the woman gave to her. I think it`s such a beautiful thing that has happened.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would like to know more about the woman`s apology and what made her realize that it was something to apologize for. What was the turn in understanding --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And why she sees things the way that she does because that is a learned behavior.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So if she could speak on why she felt like she was compelled to make that call, maybe that would allow other people to think on how they judge other races.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: #VeryAsian.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But has -- you can find somebody or you ask somebody, why did you say that? And they will say, "Oh, I`m so sorry."\par \par \uc2 Haven`t you heard that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, all the time. All the time. It happens all the time for all of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shout out to my #VeryAsian.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Getting you all the gear. Getting you all the gear.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Michelle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Go Michelle Li.\par \par \uc2 All right, TV reporting can have some unexpected danger. Watch what happened during a live shot in West Virginia last night. Don`t worry, everyone`s okay, but watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TIM IRR (News anchor): Now, we`re starting to experience unfortunately and freeze, thaw we see this water main breaks.\par \par \uc2 TORI YORGEY (WSAZ-TV reporter): Oh my God. I just got hit by a car, but I am okay. I just got hit by a car, but I`m okay, Tim.\par \par \uc2 TIM IRR: Well, that`s a first for you on TV, Tori.\par \par \uc2 TORI YORGEY: We`re all good. I`m okay. Yes. You know, that`s live TV for you. It`s all good. I actually got hit by a car in college, too, just like that.\par \par \uc2 TIM IRR: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TORI YORGEY: I am so glad, I`m okay. You`re okay.\par \par \uc2 TIM IRR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TORI YORGEY: You`re okay. We`re all good. This is -- you know what, it`s one moment --\par \par \uc2 TIM IRR: Are you sure you`re okay, Tori?\par \par \uc2 TORI YORGEY: We`re good, Tim. Ma`am, you are so sweet. And you`re okay. It is all good. You know -- oh, Lord. You know, it`s my last week on the job and I think this is what happened.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this is Tori Yorgey of WSAZ TV and she is fine. So shout out to MMJ. This is what they do, man. She is by herself.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right, right. She is a one-man band.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She set up the camera shot, that`s why there is no crew.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: no crew, no producer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the anchor had no return. He could only hear. He couldn`t see what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. That is incredible.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The danger that MMJ`s face.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Indispensable. I`m glad she is okay. Vlad, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is 7:47 Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Video clip of Elton John`s performance.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is a one and only Sir Elton John kicking off the final leg of his farewell tour last night. He`s in New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, he tells Anthony why he took a time from the road.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up at this hour, we are exactly one year into the Biden presidency. We will get a report card from two of the smartest political analysts around.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Millions of Americans left their jobs last year and many more are still looking for a change. You know who you are. We`re going to look at the right and wrong ways to quit and key questions to ask in an interview.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Anthony Mason talks with Sir Elton John, as the singer begins the final leg of his farewell tour after decades on the road.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to go. But first, here`s today`s Eye Opener at 8.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We didn`t over promise, but I have probably outperformed what anybody thought would happen.\par \par \uc2 We have made enormous progress.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Today marks a year since President Biden took office. We spoke to Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : You can`t give up on the spirit of the American people to want things to be better, and that`s how I feel. I take that seriously.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JACKY ROSEN\uc2  (D-NV): This is an unprecedented coordinated attack to make voting harder for eligible citizens.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A new voting rights bill collapses after two Democrats refuse to get it through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): Eliminating the filibuster would be the easy way out. It wasn`t meant to be easy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Omicron cases rise as misinformation continues to endanger people`s lives.\par \par \uc2 PAMELA MAPLES: I had no clue it would be like this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A big blow to former President Trump, the Supreme Court rules he cannot hide his records from the House Committee looking into the January 6 attack.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the \uc2 Denver Nuggets\uc2  were as good as gold in an exhilarating overtime win over the \uc2 LA Clippers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAN: Five on the shot clock. They double team in Lake, cross court, Gordon for the lead.\par \par \uc2 Energy. Yes.\par \par \uc2 Jackson got it off in time, and that is the ball game.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good as gold. I see what you did there, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that was a pretty good -- nice crosscourt pass there. It helps to be tall. You can see over there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I love people`s enthusiasm when they see the reaction when the shot goes in that cleanly. It`s nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nothing like a buzzer beater.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with a look at the biggest accomplishments, the setbacks, and the challenges during President Joe Biden`s first year in office.\par \par \uc2 Today is anniversary and the President gave himself high marks at his news conference yesterday afternoon saying he quote, "Probably outperformed what anybody thought would happen."\par \par \uc2 Now one year ago today, Mr. Biden was sworn in after a very contentious election that divided the country and amid a pandemic that has now dominated American life, and continues to do so unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keeffe shows us all that has happened over the last 365 days.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : My whole soul is in this, bringing America together, uniting our people.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: During his first speech as Commander-in-Chief, President Biden promised to tackle the nation`s many challenges.\par \par \uc2 But unity quickly proved hard to find. Racial justice protests continue, while teachers, parents, and students bristle plans to keep schools safe from the coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 The new administration ramped up vaccine distribution and passed trillions of dollars in financial relief.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We`re closer than ever to declaring our independence from this deadly virus.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Over the summer, the pandemic took a temporary backseat to foreign policy as a controversial decision to end America`s involvement in Afghanistan sparked chaos.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I was not going to extend this Forever War, and I was not extending a forever exit.\par \par \uc2 Here we go.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The administration succeeded in enacting major infrastructure legislation.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Let`s believe in possibilities. Let`s believe in one another.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But plans for an ambitious spending bill remains stalled.\par \par \uc2 As fall turned to winter, supply chain disruptions and inflation sent consumer prices soaring, and a new COVID variant sparked long lines at testing and vaccination sites once again.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We have to do more. We have to do better, and we will.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Still, the President kicked off the New Year pushing for voting reform and blasting his predecessor`s supporters.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : They want chaos to rein. We want the people to rule.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And unity in Congress, like in much of the country continues to remain elusive.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. I`m Ed O`Keefe at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For more on what happened and what it means, we are joined now by CBS News political contributor and Democratic strategist, Joel Payne and Steve Hayes. He is co-founder, CEO, and editor of "The Dispatch."\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, good morning to both of you. That was a nearly two-hour long press conference yesterday. There were questions from conservative outlets, liberal outlets, middle of the road outlets, foreign language outlets, international outlets.\par \par \uc2 If it went any longer, dogs and children were going to be asking questions. So if you could, Joel, off the top help us take away kind of the headline, what was the big message that you got from those two hours?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, I think some of the message was nonverbal, which is the President can stand and take the heat and I`ve got the endurance to do the job and I think that`s part of what his team wanted to do.\par \par \uc2 They wanted to reflect that you have an engaged President, you have a President that is focused on the job at hand. He did make some news for his team. Unfortunately, they had to do some cleanup afterwards, related to issues in Russia. He made some comments about potentially Russia invading the Ukraine, which his team again had to clean up afterwards.\par \par \uc2 And I think he also made news around the Build Back Better agenda, revealing that that is going to have to be broken up into multiple pieces of legislation.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, press conferences like that, they really don`t move the needle, I think that they more reflect the direction, and I think the direction of this President is to attempt to reset as we look at the beginning of 2022.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, Steve, the President, his inaugural address promised to be a unifier for the country. He campaigned that way as well.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, for the administration, people tend to be unifying around disapproval at the moment. Do you think that press conference changed the narrative in any way?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HAYES ("The Dispatch co-founder, CEO, and editor): I do. I mean, I agree with Joel that, you know, if endurance was one of the measuring sticks, he did well. I mean, I think people were surprised that he was able to stand up there for two hours and answer questions.\par \par \uc2 But I think he raised as many questions as he answered in several different ways. I mean, if you talk about -- look at the -- his comment about a minor incursion by the Russians into Ukraine, you saw immediate reaction from overseas, particularly in Kiev, where officials said, boy, that seems like a green light for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You also had the President`s comments on the possibility of an illegitimate election in 2022, when he was asked a question about whether if these voting reforms that have been debated in Congress don`t pass that would contribute to the illegitimacy of the 2022 elections?\par \par \uc2 I think the right answer would be to reaffirm the strength of our electoral system while you know, expressing disappointment that that the reforms were unlikely to pass. Instead, he said, yes, it`s possible that the 2022 elections could be illegitimate, which I think is not what we need, whether you`re a Republican or a Democrat, or a liberal or a conservative.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. So Steve, how is that helpful? And doesn`t that set up a tit-for-tat dynamic that I think is very troubling on many issues?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HAYES: Yes, I mean, I think it`s worrisome. I mean, look, if you`re a conservative, and you`ve got questions or problems with the way that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  conducted himself in the aftermath of the 2020 election, lying about whether the election was stolen, trying to remain in office, what his supporters have been doing in states and localities across the country, to basically put themselves in a position where they could muscle through an illegitimate election.\par \par \uc2 The last thing you wanted to hear was to hear from President Biden, that there are questions about the legitimacy of the elections. You needed the President to say, no, we`ve got the best electoral system in the world, the safest and the most secure and one thing we don`t need to worry about, even if I`m frustrated that Republicans aren`t supporting me on these voting reforms. The one thing that we can be sure of is that the elections will be legitimate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Joel, you mentioned it briefly. The President said that he is considering breaking up the Build Back Better plan. Is it as simple as just giving in and trying to pass it in chunks as you said?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, here`s the thing. I mean, part of that would be, you`d still need to get 60 votes for some of that. Now, there are parts of that agenda that if you may be offered up piecemeal, you could maybe get to that 60-vote threshold.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got folks like Senator Romney who might support parts of that agenda. You`ve got other moderates in that Republican Caucus who might support part of that agenda.\par \par \uc2 And I think for probably some of the President`s allies, they are looking forward to an opportunity for him again to reset and to focus more on a unity message, getting back to the messages of the bipartisan infrastructure bill, right, bipartisan -- bringing Republicans along.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, for a lot of Democrats, you`ve got a lot of frustration that some of the core progressive agenda items, voting rights reform, some of the more, you know, expansive parts of the Build Back Better agenda are probably not going to get passed in this cycle of Congress.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Joel, the two big parts of Build Back Better that the President cares about a lot, he said, are free community college, so the country guaranteeing an education, two years beyond primary school, and also a child tax credit, which can lift millions of kids out of hunger.\par \par \uc2 If those are broken off and passed, does that change the narrative for the midterms? I mean, it looks like they`re going to lose at the moment. Could those bills, those separate pieces change that?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, Democrats have to decide whether or not they want to tell a positive story about this presidency.\par \par \uc2 If you look on one hand you could say, you passed a major infrastructure bill, you already passed the big COVID Relief Bill which had some child care tax credit in it last year, and you`ve got a chance to pass a little bit more going into 2022, and that`s something you could sell to voters as you look at the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 You could tell the story like that. You could also tell a story in a way to say we`re not getting as much as we want, and I think what the President is probably trying to do, among many things, bringing the country together is also bucking up his party and getting his party to feel good about the work that they`ve done over the last 13 months.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think part of the President`s job is psychological here to get his party on board.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Payne and Hayes, a political consultancy. You guys can work together. There is a ring to that, Payne and Hayes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that. There we go.\par \par \uc2 All right, we appreciate you guys. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now, Americans have been quitting jobs in record numbers. Coming up, we will find out why and also the best way that you can walk away from your job gracefully.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:10 time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, Sir Elton John recently made history as the first artist to have a top 10 single in the U.K. in six different decades.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN (Singer-songwriter): It feels quite surreal, and I`m very grateful and it`s all come as a bit of a surprise really because last year, I wasn`t going to do any music whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he`s changed his mind on that, only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Sir Elton talks with Anthony about the surprising collaborations on his latest album and finishing up that farewell tour.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Video clip from "Jerry Maguire.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, stealing the office goldfish is a great way to burn bridges if it is time to leave your job, like Tom Cruise`s character in "Jerry Maguire."\par \par \uc2 "Who`s coming with me?"\par \par \uc2 Now, if you want to exit your job more gracefully, listen up. A record four and a half million Americans quit their jobs in November, and a recent \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  survey shows The Great Resignation will likely continue.\par \par \uc2 Sixty two percent of working Americans say that they are considering a job change in 2022 with 24 percent looking right now.\par \par \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  career expert, Catherine Fisher joins us right now. How are you doing this morning?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (\uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  career expert): Good. Thanks for having me, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So why are so many people quitting their jobs?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Well, there`s a lot of jobs out there to be had. And what`s happened is over the last couple of years, people have really, you know, sat down and thought to themselves, does my job match my values? Is this giving me what I need out of my life?\par \par \uc2 You combine that with the record number of jobs available. Plus, now you know, with omicron, it is a little different, but with the pandemic, you know, people now are feeling more comfortable to make a change.\par \par \uc2 So whereas you saw people sheltering in place, and that included their jobs, they are now more willing to go out there and make a change.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was raised in the mantra that you don`t quit a job without a job. And I remember saying to my son who was unhappy at the time, don`t quit a job without a job. He didn`t listen to me and quit. He said, I`ll just figure it out.\par \par \uc2 But do you think that that -- more people are thinking that way. They`re quitting and they don`t even have another job?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that okay to do?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: I think obviously, it does depend on personal circumstances. It`s okay if you`re in a position that you can wait a little while to get the right job.\par \par \uc2 What you don`t want to do is be forced to like jump into the wrong job, so you make mistakes that you`ve made in the past.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So if you can afford to sit --\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, I`ve the good news though is because there are so many job openings, you`ll likely get one pretty quickly. But it`s important to really take stock of what`s important to you, what values and then match up the companies that really align with your values as opposed to like I`m just going to get the first job that I can get.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do employees have the upper hand these days, do you think?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: I do think so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You do.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: And I think what you`re also seeing is that the employers, companies are really taking a look at not only how do I get people to come join the company, because we have all these job openings, how do I get them to stay?\par \par \uc2 So it`s become a culture of more human-centric. And if you see, like we just released our jobs on the rise, and a lot of the jobs that are you know, rising to the top are ones that really indicate that companies are looking for this.\par \par \uc2 So diversity inclusion managers, chief H.R. officers, and talent acquisition specialists, so they`re starting to really invest in those poles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But these are jobs with college degrees involved.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, generally speaking, but they`re also like in logistics, which is we see is an industry that is hiring a lot. And what`s great about that is that they offer training, and they pay well.\par \par \uc2 So this is an area where people who are shifting industries, maybe they don`t want to be burned by the hospitality industry.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do want to deliver on the promise of that "Jerry Maguire" clip, how should one gracefully exit a job they no longer want to be in?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, you have to keep in mind that the relationships that you`ve built during your career and at that job are going to carry you through your job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t walk out singing, "Take this job and shove it," is that good?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: I would not do that. That is not good advice. And so you really want to, you know, leave a job just as you joined it -- gracefully and with the intention to build and maintain those relationships.\par \par \uc2 And let make sure your boss is the first to know. Give adequate, you know, notice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But the demand for jobs that require you to work from home or allow you to work from home.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Which states are hiring?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Well, we`re seeing a lot of interest in Denver, and Cleveland and Austin. And some of this could be because you`re seeing some, you know, tech companies build hubs there.\par \par \uc2 Also, we`re seeing a rise in entrepreneurship. And even some people are saying, listen, I can work and live where I want to. So I`m going to go to a place that maybe is a little bit more affordable, and gives me the quality of life that I`m looking for.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So somebody listening to you right now, Catherine, is going, you know, I really don`t like my job. I don`t like the people I work with. I really want to make a change. What are the steps they should do?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: They should figure out what is it that they don`t like about their job and before just jumping ship, talk to your manager and say, this is what I need from my job and the company, are you able to deliver this?\par \par \uc2 And if the answer is no, start looking, but again, look more for the companies and their culture, because right now those companies are focusing on culture.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of people have that resignation letter already in the draft vault.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony is just waiting to pull it out.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Not now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Catherine, thank you very much. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. We`re going to show you an amazing giant sea dragon fossil from 180 million years ago and find out what it might reveal about our changing climate today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead and only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, music legend Sir Elton John talks with Anthony Mason about being nervous before his farewell tour and collaborating with younger artists and why he`s ready to step away from touring.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that we like to call "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 And Nate, you`re in pole position.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, let`s start this --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It means, you go first.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. I appreciate that, Gayle. Thanks for the clarification. My "Talk of the Table," go ahead and mark your calendars.\par \par \uc2 This is some high fashion, the sneakers that are being auctioned off and who do I mean? I`m talking about Virgil Abloh, who passed away in late 2021. And now we know he is one of the best fashion designers to ever set foot on Earth.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of feet, he left his footprints in many ways, but sneakers -- sneakers is a big part of his resume. Now, 200 pairs of limited edition Louis Vuitton Air Force One sneakers will be auctioned off, and we`re talking about they start at $2,000.00.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s the retail price.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the retail price. So, they`re going on auction from Sotheby`s.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They were good looking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes, and here is the thing about Virgil. When he first started, a lot of people paid attention to his shoes, and that is where the artists, the athletes, and the influencers, really got to know him.\par \par \uc2 So this is going to be an auction that will also benefit the scholarship fund that was made in his name.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think it`s wild that, you know, Hollywood keeps remaking franchises from our childhood, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel like the shoes from our childhood are also --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They all come back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Like they`re back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They come back in style.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No one has invented a new shoe. They haven`t improved on it yet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you think? How much do you think those will go for?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, they start at $2,000.00.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it is going to -- yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They`ll go up to anywhere from 10, 20, even $50,000.00.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think so, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you know, he is an artist. These fashion designers or artists. So when an artist passes away, there are usually the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, the value goes up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For sure. For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they were also good looking because there are some you see and you go, okay, I don`t know what they were doing there. But those look nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is a follow up on that T-Rex story we told you about the other day involving Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson. A lot of people are wondering if the action star was the anonymous buyer, who paid $31 million for this fossil of Stan, that`s a T-Rex from a few years back.\par \par \uc2 So Johnson pointed out Stan in his office during a TV appearance on Monday night`s \uc2 NFL\uc2  playoff game, and so that got everybody talking because he called him Stan and it was very noticeable in the shot.\par \par \uc2 So the next day he wrote on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . "This is my replica cast of Stan." He did call him Stan. "If I was a proud owner of the real Stan, I sure as hell wouldn`t keep him in my office. I`d keep him in a museum so that the world could enjoy, study, and learn from him," which I thought was good.\par \par \uc2 Because listen, $31 million, we know is a lot of money and a replica can cost you like $12,000.00 -- eleven to twelve thousand for the head.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A hundred and twenty two thousand for the whole body. But it didn`t seem out of the realm of possibility that he would own the real thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Definitely not.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it is nice that he said I`d put it in a museum. I thought that was nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is kind of calling out whoever does own the real Stan --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Saying, it shouldn`t be in your house.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We don`t know where that is right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s why they`re anonymous. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s not far-fetched though. We`ve seen celebrities and actors spend big money on some wild things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For sure. For sure. It can happen, and speaking of big money on wild things, my "Talk of the Table" is about a trend among some celebrities and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  influencers.\par \par \uc2 It`s a trend I did not believe at first, but apparently is real. They are cloning their beloved pets. So we have a gallery of photos here. Each photo is a litter of puppies cloned from the dead puppy that the influencer wanted to recreate.\par \par \uc2 There`s a company that does it in Texas. I wanted to talk about this because I`m not sure yet how I feel about it though I understand the influence or the desire to do it.\par \par \uc2 When I was a kid we had a dog die on Thanksgiving Day, a lab, and we were devastated. And I remember two weeks later, my parents went out and they got the same dog, we named it the same thing. And so I get it, but at the same time I`m like, "Really"?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I have to be honest, I didn`t know that this was legal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I didn`t know it was either, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is legal. I know a couple of wealthy people who have done it and can range anywhere from $150,000.00 to $250,000.00. But they don`t regret it because they said that dog that they have is exactly like their other dog, exactly the way they look --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sometimes the personality can vary though. That`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, but that`s the thing about the clone. They say even the personality is the same.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Barbra Streisand said you can clone the dog, but not the soul. So --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I remember watching the movie "Pet Sematary" and that`s -- all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Nate will not do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I leave that there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate will not be cloned.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No cloning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Switching gears now after more than half a century of touring, Sir Elton John is -- he is going to retire from the road. Really, he says.\par \par \uc2 The iconic singer kicked off the final leg of his twice postponed Farewell Yellow Brick Road tour last night at a packed stadium. He is in New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 Fans had been waiting for two years to see him take the stage one last time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: You have been holding on to these tickets for 745 days, and I can`t thank you enough for being so patient. We live in funny times.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sir Elton. He is worth waiting for, I say, only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Anthony recently spoke with Sir Elton virtually from his home.\par \par \uc2 He lives in Windsor, England where he was in his pre tour COVID bubble. Anthony, can`t wait to hear the two of you together. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Sir Elton has had another huge year despite being stuck at home like the rest of us with his 32nd Studio album, "The Lockdown Sessions," he became the first ever artist to have a top 10 single in the U.K. in six different decades.\par \par \uc2 Sir Elton has worked hard to get back on the road so he can finally finish his big farewell.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Before he returned to the stage last night in New Orleans, Sir Elton was feeling a little anxious.\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: I haven`t played for two years.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Does that feel strange?\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: It does. It feels strange for everybody who is a performer.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes/\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: I have been to a couple of shows and the audience go nuts. So, I`m looking forward to it from that point of view, and I hope I`m not too rusty. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Elton had to halt his farewell tour during the pandemic and postponed it again to have hip surgery --\par \par \uc2 But you`ve enjoyed being at home.\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: Oh boy. I`m never home. This house in Windsor, I`ve been in since 1975. I barely spent three or four weeks at a time here before I`m going somewhere else.\par \par \uc2 So I got to spend lockdown with David, my husband and Zachary and Elijah, our sons, and it was just the best time possible. It was great --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And lockdown didn`t stop Elton from having one of his best years in decades on the charts.\par \par \uc2 Three of his recordings reached number one in the U.K. last year. "Cold Heart" with Dua Lipa and two collaborations with \uc2 Ed Sheeran\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How does that feel?\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: It feels quite surreal and I`m very grateful and it`s all come as a bit of a surprise really, because last year I wasn`t going to do any music whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But younger musicians started reaching out to collaborate with the legend. Charlie Puth on the song "After All."\par \par \uc2 The Texas duo "Surfaces" asked Elton to work with them on "Learn to Fly."\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: But I just want to thank you from the bottom of my heart for your time and for working with me on this album.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He also collaborated with Miley Cyrus, Lil NAS X, and Dua Lipa on what became "The Lockdown Sessions."\par \par \uc2 I think it was your producer, Andrew Watt, who said in a way you kind of went back to your roots as a session musician.\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: Yes, I did. I mean, when I was in Abbey Road playing on Lil NAS X and singing on Glen Campbell, 54 years before that I was in the same studio playing on the Hollies record of "He Ain`t Heavy, He`s my brother."\par \par \uc2 And I thought, you know what, 54 years later, the studio is exactly the same, the desk, the stuffing coming out of the desk is still tatty.\par \par \uc2 And here I am 54 years later doing the same thing, and I thought the circle has been closed and it feels pretty good.\par \par \uc2 My name is Elton John.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: At the height of the pandemic, Elton shot a video for Britain`s \uc2 National Health Service\uc2  to promote COVID vaccinations --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Just kind of pretend that you`re having the vaccine from a nurse.\par \par \uc2 What was that, Elton?\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: That was me acting.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Okay, let`s cut there.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Sir Michael Caine also joined in.\par \par \uc2 SIR MICHAEL CAINE (Actor): Hello, my name is Michael Caine.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I thought you did a pretty good Michael Caine impression.\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: Oh, yes. My name is Michael Caine. Yes, we had a lot of fun. And you know, he`s a dear friend anyway, and he`s a lovely man.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And in the middle of it all, Elton, known for his outrageous eyeglasses also put together a collection of eyewear with \uc2 Walmart\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I`ve got actually some samples here. I like this one particularly.\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: Oh that looks cool on you, yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How did you pick these?\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: We went through all the old eyeglasses I have upstairs and my old looks from photographs, and we put them into categories and it was a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Sales will benefit the \uc2 Elton John AIDS Foundation\uc2 . Elton was honored by Prince Charles in November for his charity work and for his music.\par \par \uc2 This is the final leg of the farewell tour.\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Are you looking forward to the end?\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Why?\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: Because I want to be with my family.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: I mean, I`ve been touring since I was 17 in the back of a van. I`ve had the most incredible life. I`ve been so lucky, and I`ve loved every single minute of it.\par \par \uc2 But I`ve had enough of that applause and I really want -- I`ll be 76 years of age when I stop the touring in 2023. I want to do something different with the rest of my life.\par \par \uc2 But we have to finish this tour. You know, it`s been postponed so many times. I want to finish it in the best way possible, in the most triumphant way possible.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, if it gets postponed anymore, I`d be 85 before I finished.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: There are some musicians who play until they`re 85, Elton.\par \par \uc2 SIR ELTON JOHN: I know, but I don`t want to, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And so he won`t. Last night in New Orleans, fans had to either show proof of vaccination or a negative COVID test to enter the arena, which is standard for concerts in the COVID era.\par \par \uc2 Of course, you can see more of Sir Elton`s "Inside the Lockdown Sessions" on Apple Music. They`re in a very tight bubble, he said, you know, which is one reason why we couldn`t be with him. No backstage visitors, things like that. Extra careful.\par \par \uc2 But he`ll be playing here through the spring. Then he goes to Europe and he comes back finishes in the U.S. in November at Dodger Stadium where of course he had those famous concerts back in 1971.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still an amazing conversation. Have you ever heard a musician say to you, I`ve had enough of the applause?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is so rare.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That struck me, too.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s so rare. It`s so rare. But on the other hand, if you`re 75 you understand it. It takes a lot to get up and do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you know -- but Anthony, I appreciate him saying it number one.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I also understand when he says, I just want to be with my family and do something else because his kids are really young.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, you could feel how much he enjoyed being home during COVID.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you can see that he`s ready, but with the younger artists, that younger generation reaching out, it is almost like they`re not ready to let him go.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s exactly true, and he has found an entirely new audience, Nate, through these collaborations. All sorts of people, going back to "The Lion King."\par \par \uc2 You know, he was talking about that with me. You know, all these younger audience have found him through younger artists in many cases.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I got the album when it came out. It`s good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, the Elton John sunglasses --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The collaborations --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The \uc2 Walmart\uc2  line that you tried on there.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I didn`t get to keep those --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really? I was going to look at --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You couldn`t spring the budget for that?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I might have to go back to them for those.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Anthony thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, it`s been called the jackpot of fossil discoveries. How paleontologists unearthed the remains of a 180-million year old sea dragon. They were real. First, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are sharing the story with you this morning from back in time and deep under the sea. Take a look at this giant fossil of a sea reptile recently found in the middle of England in near pristine condition.\par \par \uc2 It`s a skeleton thought to be 180-million years old and as our Charlie D`Agata data shows us, it could help us understand our own future.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: It didn`t look like much at first. A conservation team thought they found maybe an old broken pipe sticking out of the mud after draining a reservoir.\par \par \uc2 But what Joe Davis stumbled upon has been described as one of the greatest fossil finds in the history of British paleontology.\par \par \uc2 JOE DAVIS (U.K. Rutland Conservation leader): A lot of people spend their careers looking for something like this. My kids have now named me Joe- Rassic.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: What Joe-Rassic discovered lurking beneath the mud were the remains of a monumental 180-million-year-old beast, a beast paleontologist, Dean Lomax has spent a career hunting down.\par \par \uc2 DEAN LOMAX (Paleontologist): Not only is this the largest ichthyosaur skeleton ever found in Britain, but it`s also the largest complete skeleton of any prehistoric reptile found here in the U.K. as well.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Known as the sea dragon, the air breathing apex predator ruled the seas like a huge bloodthirsty dolphin.\par \par \uc2 Raising the monster from the deep was a complex and delicate operation that meant covering it up in plaster and transporting it to a warehouse.\par \par \uc2 And here it is, and it is actually the length of a double decker bus, around 33 feet long. The skull alone is more than six feet long and weighs nearly a ton.\par \par \uc2 Paleontologist Nigel Larkin helped lead the excavation.\par \par \uc2 What was going through your mind as you started to uncover the segments and thought, wow.\par \par \uc2 NIGEL LARKIN (Paleontologist): We thought, is it going to be 10 percent complete? Twenty percent? Hey, it could be 50 percent complete. So as we were digging away, it was just getting bigger and bigger. We couldn`t believe it.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Jackpot.\par \par \uc2 NIGEL LARKIN: Yes, fantastic.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: A jackpot that goes far beyond what will eventually be a pretty impressive museum piece.\par \par \uc2 What was the reason they died out? What can we learn?\par \par \uc2 NIGEL LARKIN: We can learn a lot from fossils, generally. We can only predict future climate change by understanding past climate change and how animals like this adapted to climate change or failed to adapt.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Unlocking the secrets of the deep and the deep past may help us learn to adapt to our own future.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Charlie D`Agata, Shrewsbury, England.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s pretty amazing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I once caught a fish that big down in Florida. It got away though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A Florida man almost caught a fish that big.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And you said that with a straight face.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I never caught a fish in my life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That makes two of us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. Wildwood, New Jersey. Always good when you`re closing with Bruce Springsteen.\par \par \uc2 We`re still thinking about Elton John though and Anthony`s conversation with Elton John.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I really appreciate him saying I`m ready to go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve done my job\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Although he said -- Anthony said, he didn`t have the glasses anymore. I think his son stole them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to see those glasses again. We`ll see you tomorrow. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220120ei1k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  CEO \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2  On The Future Of Travel And Remote Work\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 1,248 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 January 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  says nearly half of the nights booked from last July to September were for stays of seven days or longer. CEO, that`s \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2  announced this yesterday. Guess what he`s going to do? He is going to be traveling and staying in Airbnb`s over the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 He joins us first on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS from his \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  in Atlanta. Hey, \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2 . I`m so glad to see you, Brian.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHESKY\uc2  (\uc2 Airbnb\uc2  CEO): Good to see you, Gayle, I am so happy to be here from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, so I`m curious. Listen, all of us have stayed in Airbnb`s. I know you`ve stayed in Airbnb`s yourself, but what is the point you`re making? Why are you doing this now?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHESKY\uc2 : Well, Gayle, I think that the pandemic has very likely caused one of the biggest changes to travel since the advent of commercial flying and that is because for the first time, millions of people, not everyone, but millions people don`t have to go to the office five days a week.\par \par \uc2 And because many of these people don`t go to the office five days a week, they`re not tethered to the city they live in, and so they can now potentially live anywhere.\par \par \uc2 And so some people are choosing long weekends, some people are choosing to go away for the entire summers if they have families. But others like me, I can actually live kind of nomadically.\par \par \uc2 And so every couple of weeks, I`m going to be traveling to different cities across the country. And you know, even around the world, because we`re seeing millions of people on \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  interested in doing this, and I want to really test the experience, optimize and show that you can actually run a really big company doing this and it means a lot of people can do this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So Brian, you`re telling me that you went on to the \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  website, clicked around through all the pictures, and then picked the place that you`re in right now?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHESKY\uc2 : Yes. I did and you know what I`m looking for is -- I don`t really want to feel like I`m traveling. I want to feel like I actually live somewhere, even if I`m there just for a couple of weeks. And so I`m looking for a place in a local community. I`m here in Reynoldstown in Atlanta. This is an incredibly nice home by this really wonderful host named Kimberly.\par \par \uc2 And I really love great design, good Wi-Fi, so I can do interviews, things like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wi-Fi is key. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I want to talk about a new policy that you guys are rolling out in Oregon, to fight racial bias. I know that there was a lawsuit settled. Tell us about that policy.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHESKY\uc2 : Well, I think just to step back, you know, \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  is all about connecting people all over the world. On New Year`s Eve, four and a half million people were staying in \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 . So if we`re going to bring people together, the opposite of bringing people together is discrimination. We don`t stand for it. We make every member on \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  sign a community commitment.\par \par \uc2 Now one of the things we did is we work with the State of Oregon to limit how much information a guest shows about them, so we don`t show their full name or photo, just their initials. We think this could reduce discrimination of hosts, deciding they want to host somebody because of the color of their skin or their name.\par \par \uc2 Now the key thing is we`re constantly innovating and testing different ways to reduce discrimination. We have an entire team dedicated to this. And so, I think that for \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  to work, we cannot have discrimination because that`s an existential threat to the company.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How has omicron changed the game for you, Brian? Because I got my favorite daughter, Kirby just had a little baby and she`s looking for childcare, and one of the things she told me last night is they`re going to look for \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  to quarantine someone before they bring them into the house because omicron is so off the charts.\par \par \uc2 I actually thought that`s a really great idea. How has that changed the game? Or has Omicron changed the game for you?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHESKY\uc2 : Yes, I mean, basically, the longer the pandemic goes on, and every new strain keeps the pandemic going on longer, the more people don`t travel the way they used to. They used to go to a city for a couple of days, typically for business travel, often staying in a hotel district.\par \par \uc2 And the longer it goes on, Gayle, you`re totally right. People are now looking to stay longer. Almost half of our business is longer than a week. A fifth of our business by nights are people staying longer than a month. We have people staying for entire seasons.\par \par \uc2 They are obviously quarantining, they`re getting away. So it`s really changing the game. The longer this pandemic goes on, the more the world never goes back to the way it was and that means travel isn`t going back to the way it was. It means that traveling and living are starting to blur and people are truly living in homes all over the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what happens if you find something, Brian, that`s not cool in an \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  property, what are you going to do?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHESKY\uc2 : What I am going to do is get right on the phone with my team and say we need to figure out the root causes so no one else experiences this.\par \par \uc2 And I`m already creating a list of ideas based on this experience. And I think that like one of the big risks of a CEO is you`re stuck in your ivory tower and I feel like you`ve got to get on the field and be constantly using your product.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well Brian --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s great, Brian.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t forget to leave a review. It`s part of being part of the community.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We appreciate you being on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Great to see you, Brian.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Being part of our community today.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHESKY\uc2 : Thank you. Good to see you, Gayle. Good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bye.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, and first on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, author and activist Kimberly Jones is here to share the message from her powerful viral video that used the game Monopoly to illustrate black Americans economic inequality.\par \par \uc2 But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220121ei1j000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Conversation With Antonio Barbera\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Gayle King \par \uc2 1,696 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another four and a half million of your fellow Americans quit their jobs in November according to the latest Federal data. It is part of what`s being called the Great Resignation.\par \par \uc2 Are you among those Americans? If you are, guess what? We are revealing the U.S. News and World Report List of the best jobs for 2022.\par \par \uc2 This is a first on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, so here we go.\par \par \uc2 Coming in at number three, physician`s assistant. Congratulations if you already have that job. Number two, nurse practitioner. And the best job in the U.S. for 2022, information security analyst. We`re going to find out what that is.\par \par \uc2 We are joined now by Antonio Barbera. He is a Senior Editor for Consumer Advice at U.S. News and World Report.\par \par \uc2 Antonio, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 If you ask a fifth grader, what do you want to be when you grow up? They don`t say information security analyst, but that`s your number one. What is it? Why`d you pick it?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO BARBERA (Consumer Advice at U.S. News and World Report Senior Editor): It`s important for the fifth graders and it is important for anybody. It is -- they are the gatekeepers of our digital information. They protect companies and individuals from hackers and cyberattacks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Antonio, so why do nurse practitioners and physician assistants rank at numbers two and three, respectively?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO BARBERA: So healthcare jobs always do extremely well in our rankings, and these are two positions that have low unemployment rates, excellent growth opportunities, strong median salaries of over $100,000.00 per year, and they have lower barriers to entry than some other healthcare jobs.\par \par \uc2 They don`t require Medical School, so you can start working much earlier and you won`t be saddled with hundreds of thousands of dollars of potential student debt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Maybe that explains why four out of the top 10 jobs are in healthcare. You know, we`ve been hearing from frontline workers how burned out they`re feeling, how overwhelmed they`re feeling. But yet you`re saying it is still a very appealing job.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO BARBERA: It doesn`t go away. The scores for these positions do not do very well in stress level, in work life balance, but they just dominate in terms of the low unemployment rates, extremely strong salaries, and just excellent expectations of job growth over the next 10 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Antonio, what kind of things do you look for when you`re putting the list together? Because we all made note that nobody -- TV news anchors is not on your list of the top 100.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Although, actor is number seven.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Actors, bartenders, actuary is all on the top 100. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO BARBERA: Journalists, you can imagine the stress levels and the work life balance for that and unemployment rates. So some work -- some work to be done there yet.\par \par \uc2 But overall, for the methodology, we look at what we think job seekers look for most in a new job and that`s median salary. That`s unemployment rate. That`s stress level, work life balance, as I just mentioned, and whether or not the job is going to grow over the next 10 years.\par \par \uc2 So we take all of these characteristics, weigh them to give us a score between one and 10 and then give it a corresponding ranking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Antonio, so I`m pretty sure we have some viewers that are at home, maybe some friends or family members that are sitting back saying I`m looking to switch careers, but I don`t want to go back to school for a long time. I don`t want to sit and do a whole bunch of new training.\par \par \uc2 So what type of jobs on the list would you recommend?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I want to make some money?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, money.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO BARBERA: Well, look, you mentioned at the top, there is four and a half million resignations happened recently, there`s 10.6 million job openings. So we`ve reached an inflection point where there are significantly more job openings than applicants. And this is a real advantage to job seekers who now have a bit more selectivity in making a decision of what they want their next job to be.\par \par \uc2 Whether it be something that has a higher salary, a better work life balance, maybe the opportunity to work remotely.\par \par \uc2 So we invite all those job seekers to usnews.com to check out not only our rankings and not only our job profiles, but also subcategories for different topics such as business and healthcare.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. Antonio Barbera.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have a very cool name. You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Editor is not on the list either.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that we call talk of the table this morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You good with your pillow?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I always find that annoying. I always feel like, I meant to take it off before we got on camera. Tony, you`re in pole position.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Redecorating right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m in pole position, you do with everything else. I`m in pole position with my long awaited "Talk of the Table" about this amazing research on dogs and how well they understand us even in different languages.\par \par \uc2 So we have a picture of Kun-Kun, I believe let`s see. Yes, there he is. That is a dog from Mexico that moved with its owner to Hungary. The owner is a scientist who wondered, does the dog realize that people here in Hungary are speaking a different language?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So he was trained along with 17 other dogs to sit in that little MRI machine, and guess what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When the dog is hearing Spanish, a different part of the brain lights up than when the dog is hearing Hungarian suggesting they can tell the difference.\par \par \uc2 Also, and this is what really got me -- the dog knows whether it is hearing real speech, regardless of language or just sounds.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: really?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So keep this in mind, never been proven before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So when you have a dog at home, and you`re talking about that dog, and it gives you the little tilt head with the ear up, it can hear you, it knows what you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I knows when you are speaking gibberish.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It knows if it`s gibberish, and it knows if you`re saying "get out of here" or "wipe those feet" or "go outside."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it`s interesting they can tell different languages. I think that`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you`re saying dogs are bilingual?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It could be.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are as lingual as we are. They know all the languages we do, and I have to underscore again, the scientists just wondered this and we found for the first time that a non-human brain can distinguish between languages.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so glad you got to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that. That was a good one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s been wanting to tell that story, Nate, as you know, for over a week. So it was worth the wait.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I grew up with Labradors, which is a lot of confused dog looks. You know what I`m saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love all things dog.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is a spicy dare for you. How about this? Arby? Arby`s has just launched the new Diablo Dare Sandwich they`re calling it. The fast food maker calls it the spiciest sandwich on the market.\par \par \uc2 So you get a choice of crispy chicken or smoked brisket. And here is how it works.\par \par \uc2 Made with ghost pepper, jack cheese -- I like that -- fiery hot seasoning, fire roasted jalapenos and Diablo barbeque sauce served on a red Chipotle bun.\par \par \uc2 Now Arby`s is so worried about you being able to accept this sandwich they`re going to give you a free vanilla shake to cool down your mouth in between bites. The challenge, how much of the Diablo Dare Sandwich can you take before reaching for the vanilla shake.\par \par \uc2 The sandwich is available for a limited time only and they`re doing it because you know there was a survey with fast food restaurants and say they don`t make it spicy enough. So Arby`s says okay, we`re going to rise to the challenge.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So do that what you will.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It might be delicious on the way down though. You`re going to social distance everybody on the way out.\par \par \uc2 So my Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have experience of this Mr. Burleson?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m messing with that, Gayle. All right, so my "Talk --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a milkshake story --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure. My "Talk of the Table` is a movie franchise that we all grew up with, 25 years ago. The killer ghost face. He terrified audiences in the original "Scream" movie and it was amazing.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220112ei1b000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Omicron`s Travel Impact; More Kids Battling COVID; America`s Omicron Fight; Denver Shooting Rampage; Deadly Police Confrontation; Severe Weather Threat; Workers on Demand; Saving the Manatees; The Year in Travel Trends; Honoring \uc2 Debbie Allen\uc2 ; Lox of Love; Posts of Positivity, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson; Vladimir Duthiers, Jamie Yuccas, Errol Barnett, Meg  Oliver, Jeff Berardelli, Jonathan Vigliotti, Peter Greenberg, Ben Tracy,  Michelle Miller, Carter Evans, Jamie Wax, Anthony Mason \par \uc2 13,413 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 December 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And I`m Jamie Yuccas.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here is today`s Eye Opener, your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Waited on the phone for two and a half hours, didn`t sleep.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Airlines can`t operate flights without the required number of pilots and flight attendants.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: More cancellations at America`s airports as COVID sidelines crews. Will the new C.D.C. guidelines make a difference?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The C.D.C. shortening the recommended quarantine time for people who test positive from 10 days down to five days if asymptomatic.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2  (National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases Director): We want to get people back to the jobs particularly those with essential jobs.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hospitalizations climb among kids because of the omicron variant. What parents need to know.\par \par \uc2 DR. SALLIE PERMAR (New York Presbyterian Pediatrician-in-chief): The vaccine is so much safer than getting the virus itself.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And an app is aiming to bridge the gap between short staffed employers and people who need work.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are an economic opportunity at the touch of a button.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Here in San Diego, the small jet plane crashes in the middle of a residential neighborhood, the investigation is ongoing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A highway patrol officer in Northern California had to take off after a runaway bull charged at him.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Monday Night Football, the Dolphins taking on the Saints.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Dolphins become the first ever team to have a seven-game losing streak and a seven-game win streak in the same season.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Jaylen Waddle into the end zone.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is not the future of basketball. FIBA, the \uc2 International Basketball Federation\uc2  posted video of this six foot 10 inch basketball playing robot. He showed off some pretty, well, frankly awful dribbling skills and looked very creepy while doing so.\par \par \uc2 MAN: However from the free throw line, awkward motion, but effective.\par \par \uc2 Just hoping I`m not around long enough to see five on five robot ball.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Nate I know you`re terrified of robots, but when you see how bad that one is. Are you okay with it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so robots shooting jumpers and dribbling basketballs. It`s all fun and games until we all end up like Will Smith in "\uc2 iRobot\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: But did you see how bad that robot is?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That looked like me. That`s how I dribble. I`m like nobody come near me. Nobody can hear me.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: But he`s got a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, you`re right about that, Jamie.\par \par \uc2 All right, welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Gayle King and Tony Dokoupil are off, but we are in great hands. We also have a great show for you, including a serious threat of one of nature`s most serene and beautiful creatures, the manatee.\par \par \uc2 Ben Tracy wrote rode with conservationists who warn you that the animals are in growing danger right now from another species, humans. Yes, that says. He`ll explain why that is and also what can be done about it.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Plus, it`s a lot more fun being places -- Vlad knows this because he just got back from Hawaii -- than trying to get places right now. There are flight delays, crowds, cancellations, changing rules and restrictions. Not to mention the anxiety of traveling during a pandemic wondering if it`s safe.\par \par \uc2 We want to know, will it get any better in 2022? We will ask the expert our own Peter Greenberg, what to expect and I`m really hoping it is good news, guys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Me, too. Very much so.\par \par \uc2 My flight was not canceled though. My flight was not canceled.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Darn it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. Thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s begin this morning with new C.D.C. guidance for people who test positive for COVID-19. The government health agency says if people are COVID positive and have no symptoms. They can now isolate for five days instead of 10, and they don`t need a negative test to end that isolation period.\par \par \uc2 They should still wear masks around others for another five days. New research shows the omicron variant, the most common COVID strain in America right now is most infectious roughly two days before and three days after the first symptoms.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Vlad, there are also new guidance for people who have been around someone with COVID, but have not tested positive themselves. If you`re vaccinated with a booster and you were exposed to the virus, you will no longer need to try to stay at home or quarantine, but you should wear a mask around others for 10 days.\par \par \uc2 If you are unvaccinated, you still need to try to quarantine for five full days after exposure.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: The new guidance comes as millions of Americans return from seeing family for the Holidays. At least people are trying to get home. Thousands of flights have been canceled in the last week in part because COVID has left airlines with a shortage of crew members.\par \par \uc2 Now as Errol Barnett reports there are questions about weighing passenger safety against the needs of travelers and the airlines.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: The shorter isolation and quarantine windows from the C.D.C. are welcome news for airlines, which lobbied for the change to five days arguing that vaccines and other mitigating measures are widely available and that the 10-day window worsen personnel shortages.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Sara Nelson\uc2 , the President of the Flight Attendants Union suggests this new guidance meets corporate needs, saying quote, "If any business pressures a worker to return before they feel better, it will cause much greater disruption than any staff shortages."\par \par \uc2 White House medical adviser, \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  says the decision is safe.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 : If you look at the chance of getting a transmission in that second half of that 10-day period, it is considerably less than in those first few days. So on balance, if you look at the safety of the public, and the need to have society not disrupted, this was a good choice.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Earlier, Dr. Fauci suggested the Federal government should consider requiring vaccines for domestic flights. COVID exposure isn`t the only risk for flight attendants, they have borne the brunt of unruly passengers this year, mostly over mask compliance.\par \par \uc2 On this Christmas Eve flight, two passengers fight over wearing masks, and neither was properly masked during the argument.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Really just a bizarre incident and the woman you saw standing in that video was taken into F.B.I. custody.\par \par \uc2 Now back to the C.D.C. guidelines, it should start to ease these airline staffing shortages immediately and we are seeing airline schedules gradually return to normal anyway. There are almost 700 flights canceled across the country today. Tomorrow night, that number drops to just a few dozen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, as people plan the trips, they should also have a plan B. Errol, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now Americans are not being hospitalized with the omicron variant at the same rate as other strains, but it is still dangerous including for kids. The U.S. now averages 260 pediatric COVID hospitalizations a day. That is up nearly 30 percent from last week.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver is in Paterson, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Meg, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Nate, good morning. That rise in cases and hospitalizations has some school districts like here in Paterson switching to all remote for two weeks after winter break. Some say the solution isn`t keeping kids out of the classroom, but getting shots in the arms.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: As the omicron variant continues to sweep across the Northeast, unvaccinated of all ages are at increased risk.\par \par \uc2 In New York State, health officials say pediatric hospitalizations in New York City rose nearly fivefold from the start of December, and almost all of the children were unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DR. MARY T. BASSETT (\uc2 New York State Department of Health\uc2  Acting Commissioner: We need to get child vaccinations up. We need to get them higher than they are particularly in the five to 11 year old age group.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: In New York State, roughly 27 percent of five to 11-year-olds are vaccinated. Nationwide, that number falls to roughly 23 percent. New York Presbyterian chief pediatrician, Dr. Sallie Permar.\par \par \uc2 DR. SALLIE PERMAR: The vaccine is so much safer than getting the virus itself. And so giving your child the vaccine keeps them safer than letting them get infected with this virus without any immunity from a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: While hospitals typically see a rise in pediatric admissions this time of year. Dr. Permar says parents should take omicron seriously.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: How serious are their symptoms?\par \par \uc2 DR. SALLIE PERMAR: Well, serious enough to be admitted to the hospital, which again, is an indication that this is not just a disease of adults.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: What do you think should happen in terms of reopening schools now that you`re seeing the spike in cases among kids?\par \par \uc2 DR. SALLIE PERMAR: Yes, I think we know so much more about how to keep our children safe, and we also learned how devastating it is to keep children out of school. So, I think we should use all the tools we have, implement vaccination for all the school kids.\par \par \uc2 Also use testing, also use masks, and our typical hand washing and social distancing to keep kids in school even with this rising cases.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  also announced yesterday, she is urging all superintendents to keep their classrooms open. She says she is prepared to send any additional resources they may need to prevent any interruptions to in-person learning -- Jamie.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Meg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is \uc2 Dr. Tom Frieden\uc2 , the former Director of the C.D.C. He is now President and CEO of Resolved to Save Lives, an initiative working to protect people from cardiovascular disease and prevent epidemics.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Frieden, thank you so much for being with us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 You know, we just heard from Meg. I think this is so confusing for parents. You hear that hospitalizations are up. You hear the new C.D.C. guidance. If you have little kids, you`re wondering what to do with child care. What advice do you have?\par \par \uc2 DR. TOM FRIEDEN\uc2  (Former C.D.C. Director): Well, first and foremost, get vaccinated and if it`s time, get boosted. We`re going to be talking about whether you`re up to date with your COVID vaccinations. And as we learn more what that means, it may change with time, but vaccination is by far the most important thing that we need to do.\par \par \uc2 Also, what your correspondent just covered is very important. We all need to work together to keep kids in school and learning. And we can do that by vaccinating, boosting, and masking.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Dr. Frieden, you will agree that this is sort of confusing for a lot of people because the C.D.C. is now saying Americans who test positive for COVID can stop isolating after five days, if they no longer have symptoms, but they should still wear a mask. What do you think prompted this move?\par \par \uc2 Because I suspect that there will be a lot of people who will see this guidance and wonder what this really means and what this has meant over the course of the entire pandemic.\par \par \uc2 DR. TOM FRIEDEN\uc2 : Here`s the bottom line, the virus is adapting. Omicron is different, very different from prior strains, and we need to adapt to. So appropriately, the C.D.C. is adapting their guidance for the current situation. They`re doing so in real time.\par \par \uc2 We wish we knew more. We wish we knew how severe omicron is going to be, but we won`t know that for a few weeks. We wish we were able to protect kids under age five, but currently, we don`t have a vaccine for them. That is why it is so important that everyone, five and over get vaccinated and boosted to protect all of us.\par \par \uc2 Remember, over 50 American kids have been killed by COVID so far, and thousands have suffered from the inflammatory condition that rarely follows it, but can be quite severe and life threatening.\par \par \uc2 We only have got to work together, and if we do that, we can prevent COVID from dominating our lives in 2022.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Frieden, you mentioned being boosted. Now, the C.D.C. says individuals that have received their booster shot should not need -- shouldn`t need to quarantine. So if that is the case, then them wearing a mask for 10 days around individuals, is that enough time or do they need to wear a mask after that?\par \par \uc2 DR. TOM FRIEDEN\uc2 : Ten days is a conservative amount. And frankly, anytime you`re indoors with a lot of other people, mask up. It protects you and it protects others around you especially where omicron is spreading a lot, and especially if people there or people they have contact with are older or have weakened immune systems.\par \par \uc2 Masks are a very small price to pay to keep our economy moving, keep our schools learning in person, and save lives.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I think there`s a lot of questions about testing, too. I know a lot of people who got their positive test, they now hear these new guidelines. Part of the guidelines is not to test negative out of this. One expert calls that reckless. Should a negative test be part of this five day new rule? Or what do you think about this?\par \par \uc2 DR. TOM FRIEDEN\uc2 : The C.D.C. does say that if you`ve been exposed, best practice would be to test at five days before leaving quarantine. But you know, C.D.C. makes guidance and recommendations in the real world, not the world that we wish existed and right now, there aren`t enough tests.\par \par \uc2 And furthermore, the data suggests that with omicron, you`re most infectious a day or two before you get sick and for the first day or two after you`ve gotten sick. So, it`s a reasonable approach to keep our economy moving, save lives, to be practical in a way that we can make sure that COVID doesn`t dominate our lives and the lives of people around us through 2022.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is certainly the wish. Dr. Frieden, thank you so much for the clarity as always, we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DR. TOM FRIEDEN\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now we turn to Colorado where police are investigating a deadly series of shootings yesterday. Police say the rampage began around 5:00 PM and stretched across multiple locations in Denver and Lakewood.\par \par \uc2 Four people were killed and at least three others were injured including a police officer. Officials believe a single suspect shot all of those people. The gunman who has not been identified was killed near a busy shopping center in a shootout with police.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: From Denver to Los Angeles where the LAPD has released a video -- a video evidence shooting -- police shooting last week where a 14- year-old girl was killed. It happened at a department store after a police officers responded to reports that a man with a gun was assaulting people and the officer confronted that man, shot the suspect, but also hit the girl who was trying on clothes with her mother.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti has that story, and we warn you that some of the video is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Police body camera footage shows multiple LAPD officers arriving at these North Hollywood Burlington Department Store with their guns drawn after receiving calls of an assault and a possible shooting in progress.\par \par \uc2 EMPLOYEE: Evacuate. Get out.\par \par \uc2 CUSTOMER: There is a guy with a gun.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Moments before police arrived, surveillance video captured the suspect attacking multiple customers with what appeared to be a bicycle lock. The officers moved quickly to the second floor where they found 24-year-old suspect, Daniel Elena Lopez near a woman he was beating with the lock.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hey, she`s bleeding. She`s bleeding.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: An officer then opens fire killing Lopez. Unknown to all at the time, one shot pierced a wall behind the suspect and struck and killed a 14-year-old Valentino Orellana-Peralta, who was in a dressing room with her mother.\par \par \uc2 Officers recovered the bicycle lock at the scene, but no gun was found on the suspect.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN HIGGINS (John Jay College of Criminal Justice professor): What exactly did the officer see? And why did the officer choose to fire his firearm?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Law enforcement expert, Brian Higgins says there are more questions than answers right now.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN HIGGINS: The agency I`m sure is going to investigate how three shots are fired with a long weapon. Only one hit the suspect and maybe one that missed hit the innocent victim.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: LAPD Police Chief Michael Moore told \uc2 CBS\uc2  affiliate, KCBS that the use of deadly force is always the last resort.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF MICHAEL MOORE (\uc2 Los Angeles Police Department\uc2 ): We`re doing what we can to gather as much, to be as transparent as possible.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The officer who fired the rounds has been placed on paid administrative leave pending an internal investigation.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Jonathan Vigliotti, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It seems that LAPD wanted to clear this up very quickly releasing that video so soon. Usually, they wait a period of time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it`s just so unfortunate. A young lady with her mother trying on clothes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Senseless. Tragic.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, our heart goes out to Valentina and her family.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: This morning, severe weather is still pummeling parts of the West. California`s Sierra Nevada Mountains have had up to 14 feet of snow in recent days. Look at that. It is blocking many of the local roads and more snow is coming.\par \par \uc2 CBS News meteorologists and climate specialist, Jeff Berardelli is tracking the Western storms.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JEFF BERARDELLI: Hey, good morning, Jamie. Good morning, everybody.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got to tell you, it is rare that you see a pattern as extreme across the U.S. as we have seen so far this December, but it will continue, at least through the New Year. Let`s start out with the radar.\par \par \uc2 So we have a storm system we`re tracking in the Midwest, and it`s going to produce some snow today. Chicago northward, Wisconsin, Minnesota could see as much as two to five inches of snow or so. And across the West Coast, the big storm is done, but it is still precipitating, if you will. We have some rain. We have mostly snow parts of the Sierra Nevada, the most snow they`ve ever seen in December.\par \par \uc2 What`s going on? Huge ridge of high pressure across Alaska get to 67 degrees yesterday, in Kodiak, Alaska, that`s the warmest it`s ever been in the winter in all of Alaska. Well, that has repercussions.\par \par \uc2 Notice the cold air is barreling south into the West, especially across British Columbia. Record highs during the day today, but even more so, record warmth in the southeast. It has been the warmest December across much of the country and that will continue today.\par \par \uc2 High temperatures near 80 degrees, about 15 to 25 degrees above normal, and when you have all that heat, and all that humidity, we`re going to have a storm system move through tomorrow and that`s going to generate severe weather in the south, could see even the possibility of some isolated tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 This is just now, but as we head through the week and into the weekend and beyond, we`re going to see more extreme weather across the country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jeff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ve talked a lot about the pandemic and its impact on American workforce. Coming up, a company that`s trying to change the way employers connect with the new workers.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:17, to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, travel trends in 2022. Our senior travel adviser, Peter Greenberg will show you what to look for when planning your next trip. Part of our series "What`s New in `22."\par \par \uc2 Plus, scientists highlight a danger to Florida`s manatees. They are starving in record numbers. Our Ben Tracy looks at why it`s happening and how scientists are trying to save them.\par \par \uc2 This is \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, we celebrate the work of legendary dancer and choreographer \uc2 Debbie Allen\uc2 , a 2020 Kennedy Center honoree. Our Michelle Miller talks with Allen about her groundbreaking career and the family that inspires her to do great things. Love me some \uc2 Debbie Allen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: She`s pretty amazing. Been dancing since she was three.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I can`t wait to see this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And not just dancing, actress as well.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Does it all. Does it all.\par \par \uc2 Your local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. Work, work, work and that`s what we`re talking about or lack thereof.\par \par \uc2 The United States has had a shortage of workers this year leading businesses struggling to find new employees. Some of them are using an app called Instawork to deal with that problem. This is how it works.\par \par \uc2 Instawork connects businesses with people who are willing to work day-to- day as needed. As Carter Evans reports, some of the customers say this app is a godsend during the pandemic, and especially during the holidays.\par 