\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That`s the big part of it right, though is that you get to pick the hours, you get to pick the job, you get to pick where you`re going.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. We`ve talked about this before. The pandemic will really sort of realign how people work, how people live, and there will be some positives that come with it. This is one of them.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well, it is not only that, but if you think about the pandemic, childcare has been up in the air for so many people so maybe, you work one week, but your child is in quarantine the next week.\par \par \uc2 So you kind of have to pick and choose the job sometimes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So it works as somewhat of a temp agency. Like you said, it allows the individual to be their own boss.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That`s empowering, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think it`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Working at a BTS concert? I mean, that`s pretty cool. Working -- JAMIE YUCCAS: Can we do that later? Is that what is happening? You`re signing up?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sign me up. Sign me up.\par \par \uc2 All right, as a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS on the go podcast. You will hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, pollutions deadly impact on one of Florida`s most beloved sea creatures. We`re going to tell you why manatees are dying in record numbers and what scientists are doing to try and save them. This is \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: The \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  faces a potential lawsuit from conservation groups over the fate of the Florida manatee. The gentle sea creatures have been dying in record numbers this year.\par \par \uc2 Federal wildlife officials call the die off an unusual mortality event. Those conservation groups claim the F.D.A. is not taking necessary action to protect manatees from water pollution.\par \par \uc2 Ben Tracy went to Florida to find out what`s threatening these beloved mammals and what`s being done to save them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: The manatees, they really are everywhere. They`re all around us.\par \par \uc2 CORA BERCHEM (Save the Manatee): The manatees are literally everywhere this morning.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: It`s a flotilla of Florida manatees just hanging out in the morning mist.\par \par \uc2 There`s like six or seven right there.\par \par \uc2 CORA BERCHEM: Yes, they are literally like stacked on top of each other here.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: It`s a manatee traffic jam.\par \par \uc2 CORA BERCHEM: It is a manatee traffic jam.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Cora Berchem works for the nonprofit, Save the Manatee. Nearly every morning here at Florida`s Blue Spring State Park, she hand counts the manatees that flock here in the winter for the warm water.\par \par \uc2 CORA BERCHEM: We`ve had days in here where we`ve had over 600 manatees.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CORA BERCHEM: Oh, that`s Harry. That was Harry.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Harry?\par \par \uc2 CORA BERCHEM: Harry the manatee.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: She knows most by name because of their distinctive scars from being hit by boat propellers.\par \par \uc2 CORA BERCHEM: They`re like family, you know, they are like family coming back.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Berchem is now worried that many of them may not survive. Manatees have been starving to death. Their bones even washing up on shore while zoos in places such as SeaWorld rush to rescue the emaciated marine mammals.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK ROSE (Save the Manatee Executive Director): Having lost hundreds of manatees to starvation, that`s never happened before.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Patrick Rose is Executive Director of Save the Manatee and says starvation is the leading cause of a drastic spike in manatee deaths. More than 1,000 of them have died so far this year. That`s more than any year in state history. It is especially grim considering there are just about 6,000 manatees in all of Florida`s waters.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK ROSE: Many of those manatees that did finally succumb to the starvation, their organs were really deteriorated. It was just really an agonizing death.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: More than half of the manatees have died here inside the Indian River Lagoon, which stretches for more than 150 miles along Florida`s East Coast, and the reason they`re starving is because their food is disappearing.\par \par \uc2 Manatees are vegetarians and often called Sea Cows because they eat a lot of seagrass, more than 100 pounds a day. But for about a decade now, the seagrass has been disappearing. That`s because of human caused water pollution from Florida`s development boom, mainly sewage from septic systems and fertilizer runoff from nearby farms that helped fuel a recent series of algae super blooms in Florida. They darken the water and block out the sun, killing the seagrass.\par \par \uc2 The manatees are left struggling to find food.\par \par \uc2 DENNIS HANISAK\uc2  (Harbor Branch Oceanographic Institute researcher): We think we`re right at the tipping point.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: \uc2 Dennis Hanisak\uc2  is a researcher at the Harbor Branch Oceanographic Institute where they are experimenting with growing seagrass in these large tanks and then transplanting it into the lagoon to try to restore some of the loss seagrass beds.\par \par \uc2 How much seagrass has been lost?\par \par \uc2 DENNIS HANISAK\uc2 . Sixty to eighty percent if you look maybe at the whole lagoon.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: By some estimates, repairing the massive lagoon will require $5 billion and much tougher pollution rules.\par \par \uc2 So for now, desperate animal welfare organizations have even resorted to hand feeding manatees romaine lettuce.\par \par \uc2 More than 130 manatees had been rescued this year and slowly nursed back to help. This manatee, Anastasia, had lost much of her body weight, but in October, she was strong enough to be released. Berchem says it is humans that caused this problem and we need to fix it.\par \par \uc2 CORA BERCHEM: And it`s not going to be a quick fix. I strongly believe if we all work together and we all do our part, we can still make a difference.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Ben Tracy, Vero Beach, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I lived in Florida for a long time and fishermen are usually the ones to first notice some of these issues and with this case, in particular, with the seagrass and that type of thing, they also say that things like blue crabs, sea trout also being impacted. So it`s not just the manatee, it`s having widespread impacts across the whole ecosystem.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve got to pull it back from the tipping point. That`s the key.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, these stories are a reality check. But I love hearing that we can do something about it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re the ones who caused it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly, all right, up next we`re going to talk stories you`re talking about today. But first, it is 7:43. It is time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wait a second, I just thought of something. You know, it is time now for "What to Watch" and Jamie Wax, you are in for me.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: I`m in for you. But you`re here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Guys, this is -- this entire table has hosted "What to Watch" --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: All of us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: These are -- we are along.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURL