avel in the first quarter, and that`s what they`re hoping to do.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, they have pulled out of a number of cities. Americans pulled out of 26 cities. Delta and United about 10 cities each. So, they`re trying to get back to a more stable schedule.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Peter, here is something that a lot of people want to know. Will airlines start to mandate vaccines for passengers?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, there`s some precedent for this. In Canada right now, you cannot get on an airplane unless you can show proof of vaccination. The same is in place in Australia, a number of the CEOs that I`ve talked to in this country want desperately to have that rule, but no one airline wants to take the lead because they`re worried about losing market share. They`re waiting for the Biden administration to make that rule.\par \par \uc2 They are not about to make that rule right now, because their other vaccination mandate is being challenged in the courts all the way up to the Supreme Court. So until that`s resolved, it`s dead on arrival.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Peter, you`ve really probably can`t see this, but the guys can. I have more than 600,000 SkyMiles I need to use. I know a lot of people have frequent flyer miles, especially if they have been traveling during the pandemic. What should I be doing with these and why redeem them now?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Jamie, you want to run not walk through redeem those miles. I am giving you all a red flag warning here because during the pandemic, the airlines were desperate to raise cash and what did they realize? Their frequent flyer programs are actually worth more than the airline`s themselves and they actually mortgaged their programs for between $6 billion and $10 billion each airline, that`s a lot of debt.\par \par \uc2 How they`re going to handle that debt? They`re going to start devaluing your miles. They are making it harder to earn them and harder to redeem them. So what you want to do is start today, look out about 330 days as far out as that and redeem miles as much as you can, because starting in January, that devaluation parade is going to start, and it`s not going to be pretty.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Peter, speaking of value, you say we should expect to pay more for hotels in the New Year, but that rates will be negotiable. Who are they? What are these hotel brands that will negotiate rates? Because I`ve never had that experience. Tell me how to do it.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, the reason why you haven`t had that experience is chances are you made your reservations online. You need to have a conversation. Remember, it is the law of supply and demand. It`s not the posted rate that counts, it`s all the ancillary rates, about whether you`re not going to have to pay for the water, or the Wi-Fi or the parking.\par \par \uc2 And not only that, as more hotels open with the same continuing staff shortages, it`s going to be a much more competitive marketplace. They will be much more willing to negotiate because any revenue is better than no revenue.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so how do you expect the restaurant business to have an impact on what`s going to happen in 2022?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, you`d expect to pay more for your menu. Every menu item price is going up, and a lot of things that never used to be on the menu as a charged item, they are going to be on that.\par \par \uc2 Remember, they have staffing issues that are going to continue through the first quarter. The only way to get they`re going to get there people back is do what -- incentivize them -- higher wages, perks, benefits -- and that`s going to all translate into higher menu prices, not just on the entrees, but what used to come on your dish is now going to be charged as a side order.\par \par \uc2 Even the breadbasket is going to be a paid item, so be prepared for that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: No free bread?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The bread and butter has always been free, Peter.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You just ruined my morning. No, I`m joking. Peter Greenberg, thank you so much. You`re awesome with valuable information that we need.\par \par \uc2 Now, here is a look at what`s coming up this week.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, we`re going to look ahead to politics in 2022 on the crucial midterm elections. Thursday, we examine the big health trends of the New Year, and on Friday, New Year`s Eve look at the economy in the year ahead.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: All right, guys, still ahead, a story that means a lot to me. I`m going to introduce you to two authors who are helping millions of people rediscover their light in these dark times. That`s a story we need.\par \par \uc2 I love this conversation. I hope you will, too. It`s coming up but first, it is 8:08 times check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, 2020 Kennedy Center honoree, \uc2 Debbie Allen\uc2  talks with Michelle Miller about her lifetime commitment to dance and drama and helping others follow in her path.\par \par \uc2 Plus, meet the 92-year-old lox slicer who returns to the counter after the pandemic forced him to stay home. See what this man shows about New York City spirit.\par \par \uc2 That`s coming up next.\par \par \uc2 This is \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The coronavirus pandemic forced the 2020 Kennedy Center Honors to be delayed until 2021. The honorees included legendary actress, dancer, and choreographer, \uc2 Debbie Allen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She has spent decades as a champion of the arts around the world, collecting three Emmy Awards and 18 nominations.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 CBS\uc2  Morning Saturday" cohost, Michelle Miller spoke to Allen about her groundbreaking career, which almost never happened.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: \uc2 Debbie Allen\uc2  has spent the last half century dancing, acting, and directing her way through the full spectrum of Fine Arts.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN (Kennedy Center honoree): I think it`s a combination of courage and curiosity. I`m not afraid to not know something, and I`m always interested in solving and figuring something out.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: A trait she says she inherited from her mother, Pulitzer Prize nominated poet, Vivian Ayers.\par \par \uc2 VIVIAN AYERS (Pulitzer Prize nominated poet): Well, I`m very proud of her inspiration.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN She has been for me, Phylicia, my brother, my brother Tex, my brother Huey, our greatest fan and our biggest critic and a guide for us to stay on the path.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: A path that took them from the segregated south to Mexico when she was 12 years old.\par \par \uc2 She took you out of Houston, Texas, and moved you to another country.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: Mama raised us believing we were children of the universe. Growing up as I was, with so many boundaries and limitations, it was dance that kept me believing everything my mother told me.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: But there was a turning point in your life at 16.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: Yes, there was a turning point when it was time to go to college. I was auditioning for the North Carolina School of the Arts and I was the hope of my whole community until the head teacher gave me the verdict that I was not going to be accepted, that I was not the right body type for dance.\par \par \uc2 It was the most painful. It was hard. Because everybody thought I was joking.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: They thought you were joking.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: They thought I was joking when I called to say they weren`t accepting me, and that was so hard. It was as if I had failed. And it`s what my mom said to me when I got off the plane.\par \par \uc2 Mommy told me I had failed, which is really hard.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Your mother said you failed.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: She said that to me. And -- but what I realized years later that she was loving me, that she was making me responsible. I had to get back on track somehow.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That track led her to \uc2 Howard University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Had you given up on dance at that point?\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: I had done that my freshman year until I went to a party and I was tearing that party up, girl. They called me dancing snack. That was my nickname.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Dancing snack.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: That was my nickname. And Mike Malone was at the party, and he said wait, you can really dance. He said, no. I want to show you something. He showed me his picture in "Dance" Magazine. I had never seen a black man in "Dance" Magazine. And it just made me woke again about who I am and what I should be doing.\par \par \uc2 He said I want you to come and join my company.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: She made out for the Big Apple to join her big sister, Phylicia Allen Rashad, also trying to make it in show business.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: We struggled together. Sometimes we only had one can of tuna fish between us and our two cats.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That`s not much tuna fish.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: But it was okay. We made it.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Then came Broadway and in 1980, a Tony nom for her performance as Anita in the revival of "West Side Story." But it was a role for TV where \uc2 Debbie Allen\uc2  found --\par \par \uc2 (Video clip from "Fame.")\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: Then they decided to do the series, they called and asked if I would be the teacher? I said I`m fine with that if I can do the choreography and they said okay, we don`t care about that. I`m like, "Ciao, let`s go."\par \par \uc2 That was everything.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: But when she stepped behind the camera to direct a "Cosby Show" spin off, her world was well -- different.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: I got the call from Bill Cosby to go in and he said, "Miss." He called me "Miss Trash."\par \par \uc2 "Miss Trash going in and clean house." I said, "I`m ready."\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Why did it need to be cleaned?\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: It needed to be clean because it didn`t have any cultural identity. They didn`t have someone that understood Historically Black College.\par \par \uc2 (Video clip from "A Different World.")\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: We have a place at Howard called The Punch Out.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Is it still there?\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: Yes. The Punch Out. There was no waitresses waiting on you. But there was hot sauce on every table.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Every table.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: So if that tells you anything, I basically brought the hot sauce and put it on the table.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: She continued to bring that heat to Hollywood, producing her first motion picture for Director Steven Spielberg, and simultaneously acting in and directing hit TV shows, like "Grey`s Anatomy," but she`s never too big to give back.\par \par \uc2 In 2001, Allen founded the Debbie Allen Dance Academy.\par \par \uc2 You`re teaching to itty-bitties.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: I love it because I feel that I have something to give them. They look up to me. When they look at all the things that I do they know they can do anything I do and more.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I can`t let you go without talking about the Kennedy Center.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And this honor.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE ALLEN: It`s really something because I`ve worked with the Kennedy Center for like 20 years. I`m still in the making, and that my work is significant enough for this honor to be part of that. That tribe, that Kennedy Center Honor tribe is a fierce group. It is a fierce group.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What can`t she do?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is every woman.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, and you and I were reacting when she was explaining to Michelle, what happened to her as a young child, that rejection. This is a woman who has won every dance and acting award that you know. She`s one of the dozens of the most --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One of the most revered in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And yet that one moment still sticks with her. It still brings out that emotion. It just says something about how we should think about children, young people, when we`re guiding them along their path without realizing that we may damage them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And also as I saw her tear up, I`m thinking about all the awards she might have sitting at home on the shelves, but what we don`t see are the wounds.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the scars and all the things that she`s been through.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: But it propelled her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It did propel.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It motivated her, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Michelle Miller with a great interview as always.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Great job, Michelle. Well done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Getting inspiration out there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, don`t you go anywhere. Coming up, New York City needs to replace more than 9,000 crosswalk signals. Why a Judge said it had to be done. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Coming up, one of our favorite characters of the year, the kind you`ll only find in New York City. Anthony Mason meets a 92-year-old lox slicer who takes his work to heart.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What`s the secret to being a good slicer?\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK (Lox slicer): Love. I think it`s really the secret to being good at anything, if you love what to do.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: It`s wonderful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: He does have some lessons on life that we could all use right now.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you some of the stories that we`re going to be talking about today. "Talk at the Table" this morning. Nate, you have an important one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, and that song is fitting because it`s about New York.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My "Talk at the Table" is a change for the better here in New York City. A Federal Judge ordered the city to install more than 9,000 new crosswalk signals at intersections across the city. Now, not for any old reason these new devices will use sounds and vibrations to make it easier for blind and visually impaired pedestrians to safely cross the street.\par \par \uc2 Now, here`s the thing. They use different ways to cross the street, you know, trying to listen to cars, which can be difficult nowadays with all these electric and silent vehicles.\par \par \uc2 So the Judge said, you know what, let`s make this more convenient and comfortable. It`s going to cost the city $672 million, but who cares about the money? This is about making sure that everybody is safe even those that you know, might be visually impaired.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And everybody can access the city.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The beauty of New York, 