his from "Say Anything."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: One of my favorite movies ever. I think there`s another one we might have two. "You" Season 2. He`s staring over across the street.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, the stalker.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You know what`s interesting. Kanye actually paid more than $400,000.00 over ask for this house.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Four hundred grand?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Over the asking price of the house. It is a $4.5 million house to make sure that he is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, he is right across the street.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Correct. And during their marriage, he was -- you know in other -- branch in Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maybe this doesn`t have anything to do with Kim. Maybe it`s more just being closer to the kids.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I think that is what it`s about. According to "Online," he wants to be able to co-parent with her, but it is a little -- I mean, I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I mean, he is going to peak out the blinds and keep going like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s like you wake up in the morning and you open up the blinds like that, and you just see Kanye --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sipping his coffee. Is that Pete Davidson? Yes, that`s an interesting story.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Right. By the way, the house has not been for sale in like more than 60 years so he got the chance and he snatched it up.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Kanye West is full of surprises.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: You never know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, I want to everybody back to New York for a second this morning.\par \par \uc2 We have a story about lox. Okay, not the ones you put on a door. We`re talking about the cured smoke salmon considered a delicacy, especially here in New York City at a well-known local market known as Zabar`s, who as Vlad said, I really need to try.\par \par \uc2 You`ll find a 92-year-old, Len Berk, one of the last of the city`s authentic lox slicers. He was laid off because of COVID, but now, he is vaccinated and slicing fish again proving even the pandemic can`t crush the soul of New York.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Twice a week for 26 years, Len Berk and his wife Llewellyn have made the trip from the suburbs into New York City, where Len has gone to work at Zabar`s, the famous gourmet food emporium.\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: This place is like a second home to me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So when Zabar`s sent him home for a year during the pandemic, it was Len says --\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: Kind of traumatic.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He`s back slicing lox, cutting paper thin slices of smoked salmon with his 18-inch knife.\par \par \uc2 Did you think you`d still be working at 92?\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: I never thought I would be 92. It`s a very strange place to be, let me tell you. Things change.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: They don`t get better. But --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But you`re still here.\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: I`m still plugging away.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: A native New Yorker, Len was an accountant in his first career. Then after he sold his practice.\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: A friend of mine called me up and said there was an ad in the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " that Zabar`s was looking for a slicer. And I`ve been here ever since.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What`s your most memorable day here been?\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: The day I had kind of a little argument with Itzhak Perlman.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Len and the Grammy winning conductor fought over cookies. It`s a long story.\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: We agreed to disagree. He`s a lovely man.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He`s also served to Jerry Seinfeld, and Glenn Close.\par \par \uc2 Anybody else?\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: Lauren Bacall.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He missed all of that when he was away.\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: It was not good.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: To pass the time, Len wrote a song about his eventual return to work, sung to the tune of "The Second Time Around."\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: Lox is, lox here, the second time around. Come Tuesday by and buy yourself a pound.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What`s the secret to being a good slicer?\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: Love. I think it`s really the secret to being good at anything. If you love what you do.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: It`s wonderful.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So you`ve got to be in love with the salmon?\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: Well, that`s pretty tricky. I do continue my search for the perfect slice.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s elusive, is it?\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: Very elusive. If I see it, I`ll know it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is that the day you retire?\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: No, I`m not going to retire. No.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No. For Len Berk, the lure of lox is simply too strong.\par \par \uc2 LEN BERK: I feel at times, I`m one with the salmon.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One with the salmon.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what you get when you visit places like Zabar`s here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: The authenticity and the love.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The consistent -- the love -- saying that he does it with love.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We should all do that. Like I love that.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I wish everybody had that kind of passion for their work.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, he said you`ve got to do it with love and you should do everything with love, which is words we should all live by.\par \par \uc2 So you`re saying I should visit Zabar`s.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nate Burleson needs to go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Can you give me a list?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All of New York City`s great delicatessens --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because I`m a transplant, I`m a West Coast person.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, Russ and Daughters, Murray`s Sturgeon Shop, Zabar`s. The lox. Try the cream cheese. Try the liver.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I love that it is coming a dining guide.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Can we go as a trio this week?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll wear a mask though.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Of course.\par \par \uc2 We`ve all been looking for some inspiration during the pandemic, right? Coming up, a pair of poets who are teaching self-love and finding power and pain and they have millions of followers.\par \par \uc2 But first, a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: There is a debate going on in this country over social media`s impact on young women. Research shows \uc2 Instagram\uc2  can have a negative impact on mental health and body image, especially for teen girls.\par \par \uc2 A growing community though of writers and poets is trying to turn the platform into a force for positive change.\par \par \uc2 Two authors involved in that group are helping their millions of followers see light during dark times.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LALAH DELIA (Poet): Diamonds form under pressure.\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ (Poet): We normally come together with many unresolved issues because healing simply takes time.\par \par \uc2 LALAH DELIA: Whenever you feel crushed, under pressure, or in darkness, you`re in a powerful place of transformation and transmutation.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Diego Perez who uses the pen name Yung Pueblo, and Lalah Delia live on opposite coasts, but are connected through a community of authors turning the page on what it means to be a writer.\par \par \uc2 LALAH DELIA: We must continually outgrow who we have been in order to continually develop into who we were created to become.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: They both use social media to spread positive messages of hope and self-discovery, gaining power through pain and musings of what it means to love yourself.\par \par \uc2 LALAH DELIA: The way I write is from the heart and from the soul. And so the fact that people are gravitating to that message, that inner calling, a lot of people are looking for another way of being in the world.\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: Misery is going out of style. People are tired of being miserable. You know you can have money you can like have beautiful things in your life. But if your mind is constantly reacting, it`s very easy for you to ruin things for yourself.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yung Pueblo`s posts are simple.\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: Next time you feel agitated because you`re falling back into past patterns --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: His words powerful and insightful.\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: Remember that simply being aware that you are repeating the past is a sign of progress.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And are resonating with the millions of people following him on social media.\par \par \uc2 So we`re in Prospect Park which is where you started writing.\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: I would spend so many precious quiet moments here just trying to one, figure out my life and just figure out what I was actually understanding about myself.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: He likes to call himself and explorer, not a teacher. His latest "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestselling book lays out how to find clarity and connection, something he struggled with for much of his life.\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: I never wanted to admit to myself that I actually felt pretty sad and depressed inside.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What was the rock bottom moment for you?\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: Just partying way too much, doing too many different drugs and just pushing myself way too far. I felt like my life was like slipping from reality. So, I took the drugs, I flushed them down the toilet, and from that day forward, that`s when all the honestly started.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: He says Vipassana meditation, which means to see things as they really are, changed his life. It`s now the biggest part of his identity and backbone of his work.\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: Meditation made me realize that I don`t need to cause myself this extra mental pain.\par \par \uc2 LALAH DELIA: The moment you allow what happened to strengthen you, you win.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Vulnerability and pain are also at the center of Delia`s work.\par \par \uc2 LALAH DELIA: I was at the threshold of taking my own life. I was in depression coming from back-to-back domestic violence partnerships. It was a lot and I didn`t know how to express that pain, and I didn`t know how to stop down spiraling.\par \par \uc2 And so breaking that cycle for me was the turning point. So, many of us come to our higher selves through pain, through that portal.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Delia has amassed more than a half million followers doing what she loves as a spiritual writer.\par \par \uc2 What`s it like for you to be back at the beach, a place you like to write?\par \par \uc2 LALAH DELIA: It feels like home.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: She encourages people to vibrate higher daily, aptly the title of her new book.\par \par \uc2 LALAH DELIA: It just means that day after day that you have this commitment, and your actions are an affirmation. They`re a meditation. They are offerings to this commitment to live at a higher level.\par \par \uc2 Just remember who you are and remember your practice.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It seems like for a lot of people, part of the practice now is going to \uc2 Instagram\uc2  to writers spaces like yours and looking at it and feeling inspired for the day.\par \par \uc2 LALAH DELIA: Absolutely, like getting messages from people saying that I was going to give up, you know, your words helped me. This right here, it lets me know that I`m doing my part to help the world vibrate higher in some way.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: There are now hundreds, if not thousands of accounts on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  that post positive messages. And while many of these writers have secured book deals through their success, the underlying goal and hope is simply to help other people find their happiness from within.\par \par \uc2 You talk a lot about happiness doesn`t come from a relationship.\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: No.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: A job, your family. It comes from yourself.\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Can you help people understand that idea?\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: A lot of happiness, you know, it has to be cultivated from within. You loving yourself, you being aligned with your values, and you also having a deep appreciation of impermanence.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of the times the mental struggles that we have are because we just reject the fact that things are constantly changing. Life is this constant movement of change that we don`t really have control over.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Do you think that`s why your writings are speaking to so many people right now?\par \par \uc2 DIEGO PEREZ: Absolutely. I mean, the world is in massive transition, everything is changing. There have always been problems historically, that people had to deal with. But now we have a greater amount of accessibility in regards to accessing tools that can deeply heal ourselves and that`s what`s always been missing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That`s what I love about both writers is that they believe that we are all connected. And if you love yourself, you are then going to spread love out into the world, which then you vibrate higher, or you have a deeper connection, and that we`re all learning and growing.\par \par \uc2 You can start anywhere, right? Sometimes you have to start at the very bottom, but the work is hard. It`s worth it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is all about those vibrations, you know, because that creates almost a magnet and then you attract what you want in this world.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like what he said about being thankful for that. I`ve talked to you about this, being thankful for the pain. I think when you`re experiencing pain, you think to yourself, this is the worst thing that could happen to me.\par \par \uc2 But then later in life, you realize --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What a lesson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You were for having had that one.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Nate, you wrote something?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, well, when I saw this piece -- it was well done, by the way. You know, I write, it`s therapeutic for me. And here`s what I wrote. Poetry is food for thought. If your life is a movie, then you should choose the plot. So script every scene so you can steal the show. This way in dark times, nobody can steal your glow. I know. I know it is kind of deep, right? But if your expectations are loose, how do you sleep tight? It`s just a little piece of what I wrote.\par \par \uc2 I`ll share the rest later.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey, everybody should have --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: A round of applause.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But I was motivated by that piece.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Thank you, bud.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That does it for us. Jamie, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It feels great to be back.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Welcome back, brother.\par \par \uc2 All right, see you tomorrow on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211228ehcs00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Labor Shortage Across America\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Carter Evans \par \uc2 581 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, there is a labor shortage across America, as businesses are struggling to find and hire employees.\par \par \uc2 Well, now, a popular app that connects businesses with hourly workers has become indispensable during the pandemic, both for those businesses and those who have recently lost their jobs.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Carter Evans has the amazing story of American ingenuity.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LIRAZ BIRNBAUM (CEO, Lula`s Garden): So, this is one of our most popular gifts.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voice-over): At Lula`s Garden in Los Angeles, `tis the season for succulents.\par \par \uc2 But CEO Liraz Birnbaum says getting help during the pandemic worker shortage is a challenge. Now, instead of placing a want ad, she orders Instaworkers on an app.\par \par \uc2 Do you have to send them a paycheck?\par \par \uc2 LIRAZ BIRNBAUM: No. That`s the best part. Once you put the credit card on the app, it just charges automatically. You don`t need to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So, what makes this different from, say, a temp agency?\par \par \uc2 SUMIR MEGHANI (CEO & Co-Founder, Instawork): We`re economic opportunity at the touch of a button.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Sumir Meghani co-founded Instawork in 2016 in a stroke of American ingenuity, but business took off when COVID hit.\par \par \uc2 SUMIR MEGHANI: The whole work force changed how they work during the pandemic. They`re not just looking for better jobs or higher pay. They`re looking for more flexible work.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: That appealed to Chester Lemon (ph), who lost his job during the pandemic. Now he works at a clothing designer warehouse one day and, the next, a concert.\par \par \uc2 CHESTER LEMON (Worker): I did BTS this past weekend.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The jobs don`t provide benefits or overtime, but the best part for him? Getting paid immediately.\par \par \uc2 So, let me get this straight. When you go home today, the money from this job will be in your bank account?\par \par \uc2 CHESTER LEMON: Before I get to my car.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Instawork has more than 1.5 million workers in 25 cities. And, just since April this year, wages on the app have increased 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 SUMIR MEGHANI: About half our professionals aren`t willing to work for less than $15 an hour.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The company charges employers a commission.\par \par \uc2 And what would you say to critics who say Instawork is just another middleman that adds costs?\par \par \uc2 SUMIR MEGHANI: Our workers speak with their swipe.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Do you ever see yourself having a regular 9:00-to-5:00 job again at one place?\par \par \uc2 CHESTER LEMON: It`s going to be hard, because I have gotten to used to this now, when you know you can make money tonight, tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Most Instaworkers are considered independent contractors, so they have to pay their own taxes quarterly.\par \par \uc2 The company does background checks. And, on the app, workers only see the jobs they`re qualified to do -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s really interesting.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211217ehcg000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  Has Announced A New Set Of COVID Restrictions\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Ramy Inocencio, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 581 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Moving overseas now to England where Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  has announced a new set of COVID restrictions starting tomorrow. At the same time, he is facing a scandal that could undermine his authority overall, we`ve got Ramy Inocencio for us outside 10 Downing Street. That`s the Prime Minister`s home in London where he had a party last Christmas, facing some criticism for that.\par \par \uc2 Ramy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 And the mood here is one of resignation that this is a setback in terms of the freedoms and the mobility that Britons have been able to enjoy for much of the past year. But Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  is now saying that these COVID restrictions are for the protection of the people, this Plan B and it is not a prelude to another lockdown.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2  (British Prime Minister): But from Monday, you should work from home if you can go to work, if you must work from home if you can, all right. I know this will be hard for many people.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: U.K. Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  announcing the strictest COVID prevention measures since this summer after a dire forecast of an exponential rise from about 500 known omicron cases now to more than one million by New Year`s in just three weeks.\par \par \uc2 Starting Friday, facemasks will be legally required in most public indoor venues and starting next week, double vaccinations or a negative test will be required at large events.\par \par \uc2 But Johnson said people should not cancel their Christmas parties just as he is suffering a crisis in confidence after a leaked video from a rehearsal press conference inside 10 Downing showing a staff strongly implied they flouted COVID restrictions with a Christmas party there in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It wasn`t a party. It was cheese and wine.\par \par \uc2 ALLEGRA STRATTON (Former \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  Press Secretary): Is cheese and wine all right? It was a business meeting.\par \par \uc2 MAN: No. Joking.\par \par \uc2 ALLEGRA STRATTON: This is recorded. This fictional party was a business meeting and it was not socially distanced.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Allegra Stratton, Johnson`s Press Secretary at the time, tearfully resigning late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 ALLEGRA STRATTON: I will regret those remarks for the rest of my days and now, for my profound apologies to all of you at home.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And forcing an apology from the Prime Minister.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 : I`m sickened myself and furious about that.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: But sorry, may not be enough for those who did follow the rules, but lost loved ones during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And all of the U.K.`s new COVID restrictions will be coming into force by next week, but the question is whether the people will actually follow it, especially after Prime Minister Boris Johnson`s government advisers this time last year, apparently flouted those rules then -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ramy Inocencio in London, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211210ehc90002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New COVID Vaccine Requirements Are Announced For Private Employers In New York City\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nikki Battiste \par \uc2 967 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: the country`s strictest vaccine mandate, the new requirements for private employers in New York City, why the move is described as a preemptive strike against the Omicron variant.\par \par \uc2 The Big Apple`s big mandate, impacting nearly 200,000 private businesses -- plus, what we`re learning about the symptoms of the Omicron variant, as a new wave of Delta infections dominate.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER HOTEZ\uc2  (\uc2 Baylor College of Medicine\uc2 ): If you haven`t gotten vaccinated yet, you`re playing with fire.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Behind bars. The parents of the accused gunman in that deadly school shooting in Michigan now in the same jail as their 15-year-old son. Could school officials face charges next for allegedly ignoring the warning signs?\par \par \uc2 American hostages. Three more Christian missionaries are released in Haiti -- tonight, the latest on their condition.\par \par \uc2 Boycotting China, the big announcement from the White House about the Winter Olympics, what it means for U.S. athletes.\par \par \uc2 Plus: Biden prepares for a crucial meeting with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jussie Smollett testifies -- what the actor accused of staging a racist attack on himself said on the stand.\par \par \uc2 A final salute to Bob Dole, flags at half-staff tonight, as we look back on the life and legacy of an American hero.\par \par \uc2 And a time-honored tradition, the Kennedy Center Honors, here in the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the CBS EVENING NEWS With Norah O`Donnell, reporting from the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We are going to begin with an alarming surge in new COVID infections in the U.S. For the first time in two months, the country is again averaging more than 100,000 new cases per day. More than 1, 100 Americans are dying each day from COVID, and nearly 50,000 are being treated in hospitals.\par \par \uc2 While the Delta variant remains the dominant strain of the coronavirus, the new Omicron variant is also spreading rapidly. Cases have now been detected in at least 18 states. But scientists still aren`t sure if it is more dangerous than previous variants. And scientists in South Africa, where Omicron was first detected, say that symptoms in most cases have been mild.\par \par \uc2 Well, the mayor of New York City today announced one of the nation`s toughest COVID vaccination mandates in the country, forcing all private businesses to require their workers to get their shots by December 27. That`s actually three weeks from now.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Nikki Battiste is going to lead off our coverage tonight in New York with all those details.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Nikki.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Norah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Not only is it one of the toughest mandates in the country; New York is now believed to be the first city to require vaccines for all workers. And now children here as young as 5 must be vaccinated before any indoor dining or entertainment.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voice-over): The New York mayor today called his mandate requiring all private workers be fully vaccinated a preemptive strike against the cold weather and the new Omicron variant.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO\uc2  (D-New York City): Look, this is how we put health and safety first, by ensuring that there is a vaccine mandate that reaches everyone universally in the private sector.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The mandate will affect 184,000 businesses, but will not apply to people working from home. And there will be no testing alternative.\par \par \uc2 Early data shows that the new Omicron variant produces mild symptoms, at worst. In fact, it is believed to be a Delta variant driving a new wave of cases across the U.S., averaging more than 100,000 a day, with an increase in hospitalizations as well.\par \par \uc2 With much about Omicron still unknown, \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  urged Americans not to get complacent.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2  (Chief Medical Adviser to President Biden): But we have really got to be careful before we make any determinations that it is less severe or it really doesn`t cause any severe illness comparable to Delta.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The new variant has now been detected in at least 18 states, including Louisiana, where 17 new COVID cases were discovered on a \uc2 Norwegian Cruise Line\uc2  ship that had just returned to port on Sunday. The cruise line said all crew and guests had been required to be fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 One of the cases involves a South African crew member who is suspected to have the Omicron variant.\par \par \uc2 Is there any data yet to show how effective our current vaccines are when it comes to Omicron?\par \par \uc2 PETER HOTEZ\uc2 : All of the vaccines have shown some degree of cross- protection in terms of virus- neutralizing antibodies. So I would expect the same will be true of Omicron.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: \uc2 Dr. Peter Hotez\uc2  says vaccination remains the best protection.\par \par \uc2 PETER HOTEZ\uc2 : If you haven`t gotten vaccinated yet, you`re playing with fire.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr. Hotez says a real concern with Omicron is reinfection, especially for anyone who is unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Here in New York City, more than 10 percent of eligible adults have not gotten a vaccine yet -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikki Battiste, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211207ehc600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Labor Strike By More Than 1,000 Kellogg`s Cereal Plant Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 501 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 November 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tonight, a labor strike by more than 1,000 Kellogg`s cereal plant workers is in week seven, and it`s part of a recent wave of work stoppages as union employees fight for better pay and better benefits.\par \par \uc2 Here`s CBS` Mark Strassmann.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: They`re going to drop you guys like a bad habit.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Brouhaha in Omaha.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You know, you got that? Look, I walked in front (ph) and he hit me.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Striking union workers, busloads of replacement workers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You have no future here!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Around the clock, two-month labor stare-down at Kellogg`s, makers of Corn Flakes and Rice Krispies.\par \par \uc2 Is this hardball?\par \par \uc2 DAN OSBORN (Mechanic): It`s absolutely hardball.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Mechanic Dan Osborn (ph) is among 1,400 striking workers.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Union --\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Power!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: At Kellogg`s four U.S. plants, they demand an end to two- tier pay structure the union conceded to in 2015. New hires make less indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 DAN OSBORN: We`re professional cereal makers. We`ve been doing it our whole lives and they`re not going to get anybody better than that.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: This is what democracy looks like!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Part of America`s great resignation is a great repudiation, workers rising up, demanding better.\par \par \uc2 MAN: No contract!\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No coal!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Ongoing walkouts by Alabama coal miners. Healthcare workers in northern California. John Deere recently settled with 10,000 striking workers, as did Mercy Hospital in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 It`s brought out the tiger in these makers of Frosted Flakes.\par \par \uc2 JAMES JACKSON (Kellogg Employee): I feel we have the upper hand right now.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Striking workers James Jackson and Robert Jensen point to Nebraska`s unemployment rate -- 1.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT JENSEN (Kellogg Employee): Just isn`t enough skilled craftsmen to - - to fill all these openings.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You`re not that easy to replace.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT JENSEN: No.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Kellogg`s told us in a statement: The prolonged work stoppage has left us no choice but to begin to hire some permanent employees to replace those currently on strike.\par \par \uc2 DAN OSBORN: We`ll stay out here one day longer than they`re willing to.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: One day longer! One day stronger!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: That`s at least through next Tuesday when negotiations resume.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, CBS News, Omaha.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A warning tonight for online shoppers looking for Black Friday and Cyber Monday deals.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211127ehbq0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 America`s Hiring Boom\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Manuel Bojorquez \par \uc2 659 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: America`s economy is showing signs of healing from the pandemic. U.S. companies added 531,000 jobs last month, and the unemployment rate dropped to 4.6 percent. But as more Americans re-enter the workforce, many are finding that their resumes are falling through the cracks of online hiring systems.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Manuel Bojorquez has more on this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Joey Holz`s search for part-time work in Fort Myers, Florida, turned into a full-time experiment. He wanted to see what would happen if he submitted 60 online applications for entry-level work over one month. And how many calls back did you get?\par \par \uc2 JOEY HOLZ (Job Seeker): Four of those turned into phone calls where I actually spoke to a person. And one of those turned into an interview.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: His post about the experience has gone viral, with people sharing similar stories.\par \par \uc2 JOEY HOLZ: By removing humans from the application process, you`re removing the ability to find the people that are actually going to be right for the job.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Harvard business professor Joseph Fuller agrees.\par \par \uc2 While the pandemic has upended the job market, he says algorithms used by some companies to screen applicants may be filtering out the very workers they need.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH FULLER (Harvard Business Professor): So, it`s very, very easy to end up on the wayside of a candidate who is just missing one attribute and triggered one filter.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Nationwide there`s more than one job opening for every American who wants to work. But a survey by job search web sited in shows 77 percent of applicants have had employers stop communication during the interview process, and 28 percent have stopped communication themselves.\par \par \uc2 One ongoing challenge for employers: a low participation rate in the job market means people are dropping out or looking outside traditional channels.\par \par \uc2 JAY JOHNSON (Restaurant Owner): It`s very rare that I find an owner of any business that tells me they are fully staffed.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Jay Johnson is seeing it firsthand at his Cape Coral restaurant Bubba`s Roadhouse and Saloon, where he has six unfilled positions.\par \par \uc2 JAY JOHNSON: For every 10 applications we get, we might hire two. And of the two, maybe one will show up for the first day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Manny joins us now. Number one, the food looks delicious at that restaurant. Number two, what can be done to bridge the gap between these employers and job seekers?\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Well, Norah, for one, jay Johnson says he`s offering a higher hourly rate, trying to attract better candidates, though it`s still a challenge. For job seekers, experts suggest working back channels out of the online process, trying to get in touch with someone who already works at that company and make a connection that way -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manny Bojorquez, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, there`s big news tonight on several COVID fronts. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  said today its experimental pill to treat the virus cut rates of hospitalizations and deaths by nearly 90 percent in high-risk adults. Studies have shown that women who are vaccinated during their pregnancies pass the antibodies to their babies through the placenta and breast milk. But there is new research showing women vaccinated post-partum do not pass the antibodies through breast milk, reimposing the recommendation for pregnant women, get vaccinated. It helps with your child.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211106ehb50005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Latest From New York City, Where A Vaccine Mandate Leaves Only 6 Percent Of City Workers On Unpaid Leave\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nikki Battiste \par \uc2 469 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, the global death toll from COVID has surpassed 5 million people. The U.S. alone approaching 750,000 confirmed deaths, accounts for about 15 percent of all COVID fatalities.\par \par \uc2 Later this week, the U.S. is expected to begin vaccinating children with the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  shot as young as five, but there`s a setback for Moderna`s COVID vaccine for kids.\par \par \uc2 Here`s CBS`s Nikki Battiste.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The FDA is delaying the Moderna vaccine for 12 to 17-year- olds, so it can take more time to look into reports of the rare side effect of myocarditis, an inflammation of the heart muscle in some boys and young men.\par \par \uc2 Now the vaccine may not be available until January, but, by the end of the week, the first \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  shots for children five to eleven could be available. A CDC advisory committee is meeting tomorrow to decide whether to recommend them.\par \par \uc2 JEFF ZIENTS (White House COVID-19 Response Coordinator): We have enough vaccine, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine, for every child age five to eleven.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: As some parents anticipate vaccinating their children, in New York City today, roughly 9,000 municipal workers were placed on unpaid leave after failing to get at least one COVID shot, missing the mandatory deadline. That`s 6 percent of the workforce, 15 percent of police, 19 percent of fire and 17 percent of sanitation department workers are still unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 But many have applied for medical and religious exemptions, temporarily keeping them on payroll.\par \par \uc2 The firefighter`s union says some members want more time to comply with the mandate. Others are considering leaving the department.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ANSBRO (Uniformed Firefighters Association President): There are plenty of firefighters who have made the decision and just say they don`t want it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Here in New York City, more than 2,000 firefighters are out sick today, but the FDNY says it has not closed any firehouses. We also learned today the Biden administration`s vaccine mandate for businesses with more than 100 employees will take effect within days. That means those companies will have to require either a COVID vaccine or weekly testing -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikki Battiste, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211102ehb10005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 City Workers Battle Covid-19 Vaccine Mandates\b0\par\par\uc2 Irika Sargent, Michael George \par \uc2 633 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 IRIKA SARGENT: Tensions are rising across the country over vaccine mandates, as unvaccinated workers face deadlines to get their shots. CBS` Michael George in New York, where first responders are in the spotlight. Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE (CBS News Correspondent): Erika, good evening. Right now, the FDNY`s vaccination rate is at seventy-seven percent and the NYPD`s is at eighty-four percent. And that could rise over the weekend but there`s a lot of concern about what happens if thousands of officers, firefighters, and city workers are put on unpaid leave.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: Garbage is piling up in some New York neighborhoods. Sanitation crews apparently skipping pickups to protest one of the toughest vaccine mandates in the nation.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): We the people will not comply.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: Angry protests broke out this week leading up to Monday`s deadline. City workers, including police and firefighters, have to prove they`re at least partially vaccinated or go on unpaid leave.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Response times are going to go through the roof.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: Union leaders predict up to twenty percent of fire companies will close. The mayor says he has contingency plans.\par \par \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO\uc2  (Mayor of New York): A mandatory overtime, scheduling changes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: Mandate battles are raging in other cities. The Los Angeles Police Union is suing over details of the city`s vaccine requirements. While in Chicago, the mandate for city workers to report their vaccine status survived challenges in federal court and in the city council, where Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  made an impassioned speech.\par \par \uc2 LORI LIGHTFOOT\uc2  (Mayor of Chicago): The people that are unvaccinated are playing Russian roulette with their life and they`re playing Russian roulette with the lives of their families, their neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: Chicago`s police superintendent says fewer than two dozen officers have been placed on no-pay status for refusing to comply.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: And on Friday, the FDNY suspended six of its own members for a month after they allegedly drove a fire truck to a state senator`s office and threatened his staff in protest over the vaccine mandate. Erika.\par \par \uc2 IRIKA SARGENT: Michael George, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Today, actor Alec Baldwin addressed the deadly prop gun shooting on the set of his latest film Rust. For the first time on camera, speaking to paparazzi in Vermont, Baldwin says he`s been ordered by investigators not to discuss the fatal shooting, and that he`s cooperating with them.\par \par \uc2 ALEC BALDWIN (Backgrid): It`s an active investigation in terms of a woman dying. She was my friend. She was my friend.\par \par \uc2 IRIKA SARGENT: Baldwin did say he fully supports tightening up safety measures and possibly banning real guns and live ammunition on movie sets.\par \par \uc2 Three people were killed when an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train crashed into their car at a railroad crossing early today. It happened in North Charleston, South Carolina. A fourth person in the car was hospitalized but no one on the train was injured. The crash is under investigation.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  and the John Deere company reached a tentative agreement on a new contract today. It covers more than ten thousand workers at facilities in Iowa, Illinois, and Kansas. Workers went on strike two weeks ago. John Deere is expected to report record profits this year.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020211208ehau0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New York City Workers Protest Vaccine Mandate\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mola Lenghi \par \uc2 520 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, New York City is bracing for a possible shortage of police officers and firefighters, as a COVID vaccination mandate looms. Hundreds of first responders took their complaints about the mandate to the mayor`s mansion today. We have more now from CBS` Mola Lenghi.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: This is about anti-mandate and anti-mandate only.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI (CBS News Correspondent): The divide over New York City`s vaccine mandate for city workers spilled into the streets of Manhattan today.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): We, the people--\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Firefighters, EMTs, and sanitation workers--largely unmasked-- rallied outside the mayor`s official residence just one day before the deadline for municipal employees to get at least one COVID shot or be placed on unpaid leave.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Our members do have a choice about their health.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: All but roughly thirty-eight thousand city workers are vaccinated but about a quarter of the police force and a third of firefighters are not.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: You can`t force a vaccine on somebody, it should be a choice.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Six of the country`s ten largest cities have or are planning vaccine mandates for municipal workers. Of those, New York, Los Angeles, and San Diego do not have a testing alternative.\par \par \uc2 (Crowd protesting)\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: New York City`s largest police union sued this week to block the mayor`s vaccine mandate but a state judge rejected that request. The police are asking the city to adopt a weekly testing option in lieu of vaccination, even though compared to health care workers, first responders are at higher risk of contracting COVID.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ANSBRO (FDNY Uniformed Firefighters Association President): We`re going to show up to work and fulfill our oath. If the mayor or commissioner sends them home, what happens is on them.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: But you`re going to be ready to work that day?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ANSBRO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: But today, the mayor doubled down.\par \par \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO\uc2  (New York): You want to protest. Go protest. People want these jobs. Anyone who doesn`t want to do the job anymore, I know someone else will step up and fill it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Now, if unvaccinated first responders are sent home Monday, according to the firefighters union, up to twenty percent of fire houses could have to close, that of course would impact the city`s ambulance fleet and, in turn, response times. But the mayor says he has a contingency plan in place. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. We`ll be watching that closely. Mola Lenghi, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211029ehas0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Millions Quit Their Jobs Due To Pandemic Burnout\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 453 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. Tonight, we`re taking a closer look at what`s being called the Great Resignation. 4.3 million Americans quit their jobs in August alone. That`s actually a record. The biggest reason--burnout. And rather than complain about the boss, thousands have become one by starting their own companies. Here`s CBS` Mark Strassmann.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Nick Folmar had pandemic panic--furloughed by his janitorial company thirteen months ago when no one else was hiring.\par \par \uc2 NICK FOLMAR: If I`m going to have something, I`m going to have to create it.\par \par \uc2 Hi, I`m Nicholas with Jet Stream Clean.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Folmar gambled with his family`s savings to start Jet Stream Clean, his carpet cleaning business, along the Alabama-Georgia border.\par \par \uc2 Has it paid off?\par \par \uc2 NICK FOLMAR: Well, I`ve doubled my money and my salary.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Doubled?\par \par \uc2 NICK FOLMAR: Doubled.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: COVID America has become a nation of quitters, job quitters. Millions of workers like Folmar leaving the job market for good, often to become their own boss. In one survey, nearly one third of workers who quit started their own business. Ominously for employers, in another survey, ninety-five percent of workers said they were thinking about quitting.\par \par \uc2 TOM SMITH: I`m done with the cubicle. I`m done commuting. I`m done sitting in an office.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Professor Tom Smith studies the pandemic`s labor market trends at Emory University`s business school.\par \par \uc2 What explains why people are willing to take that leap now?\par \par \uc2 TOM SMITH: Maybe the looking craziness in the eye, and coming out on the other end of it, has made people reevaluate how much risk is actually involved.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Hustling in a crisis, Folmar found pandemic prosperity-- more money, more time with his family, better life balance.\par \par \uc2 NICK FOLMAR: I took this dream and ran with it. And with it, I cured my family.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: With a paycheck he created, Folmar can take this job--\par \par \uc2 NICK FOLMAR: Look at the color difference here.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: --and love it.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, CBS News, Fort Mitchell, Alabama .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Glad to see they`re doing so well.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211023eham000dx\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/10/2026\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Source\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 CBS Network - All sourc